(39, 'a', '\nAbject', ' adj. Miserable, wretched; degraded; despicable.  abjection n. [latin jacio -ject- throw]'),
(40, 'a', '\nAbjure', ' v. (-ring) renounce on oath (an opinion, cause, etc.).  abjuration n. [latin juro swear]'),
(41, 'a', '\nAblative', ' gram. â€”n. Case (in latin) of nouns and pronouns indicating an agent, instrument, or location. â€”adj. Of or in the ablative. [latin ablatus taken away]'),
(42, 'a', '\nAblaze', ' predic. Adj. & adv. 1 on fire. 2 glittering, glowing. 3 greatly excited.'),
(43, 'a', '\nAble', ' adj. (abler, ablest) 1 (often foll. By to + infin.; used esp. In is able, will be able, etc., replacing tenses of can) having the capacity or power (not able to come). 2 talented, clever.  ably adv. [latin habilis]'),
(44, 'a', '\n-able', ' suffix forming adjectives meaning: 1 that may or must be (eatable; payable). 2 that can be made the subject of (dutiable; objectionable). 3 relevant to or in accordance with (fashionable; seasonable). [latin -abilis]'),
(45, 'a', '\nAble-bodied', ' adj. Fit, healthy.'),
(46, 'a', '\nAble-bodied', 'seaman  n. Ordinary trained seaman.'),
(47, 'a', '\nAblution', ' n. (usu. In pl.) 1 ceremonial washing of the hands, sacred vessels, etc. 2 colloq. A ordinary bodily washing. B place for this. [latin ablutio from luo lut- wash]'),
(48, 'a', '\n-ably', ' suffix forming adverbs corresponding to adjectives in -able.'),
