(74, 'a', '\nAbound', ' v. 1 be plentiful. 2 (foll. By in, with) be rich; teem. [latin unda wave]'),
(75, 'a', '\nAbout', ' â€”prep. 1 a on the subject of (a book about birds). B relating to (glad about it). C in relation to (symmetry about a plane). 2 at a time near to (about six). 3 a in, round (walked about the town; a scarf about her neck). B all round from a centre (look about you). 4 at points in (strewn about the house). 5 carried with (no money about me). 6 occupied with (about her business). â€”adv. 1 a approximately (about ten miles). B colloq. In an understatement (just about had enough). 2 nearby (a lot of flu about). 3 in every direction (look about). 4 on the move; in action (out and about). 5 in rotation or succession (turn and turn about).  be about (or all about) colloq. Have as its essential nature (life is all about having fun). Be about to be on the point of (was about to laugh). [old english]'),
(76, 'a', '\nAbout-face', ' n. & int. = *about-turn, about turn.'),
(77, 'a', '\nAbout-turn', ' â€”n. 1 turn made so as to face the opposite direction. 2 change of opinion or policy etc. â€”int. (about turn) mil. Command to make an about-turn.'),
(78, 'a', '\nAbove', ' â€”prep. 1 over; on the top of; higher than; over the surface of (head above water; above the din). 2 more than (above twenty people). 3 higher in rank, importance, etc., than. 4 a too great or good for (not above cheating). B beyond the reach of (above my understanding; above suspicion). â€”adv. 1 at or to a higher point; overhead (the floor above; the sky above). 2 earlier on a page or in a book (as noted above). â€”adj. Preceding (the above argument). â€”n. (prec. By the) preceding text (the above shows).  above all most of all, more than anything else. Above oneself conceited, arrogant. [old english: related to *a2]'),
(79, 'a', '\nAbove-board', ' adj. & adv. Without concealment; open or openly.'),
(80, 'a', '\nAbracadabra', ' â€”int. Supposedly magic word used in conjuring. â€”n. Spell or charm. [latin from greek]'),
(81, 'a', '\nAbrade', ' v. (-ding) scrape or wear away (skin, rock, etc.) By rubbing. [latin rado scrape]'),
