(147, 'a', '\nAccept', ' v. 1 (also absol.) Willingly receive (a thing offered). 2 (also absol.) Answer affirmatively (an offer etc.). 3 regard favourably; treat as welcome (felt accepted). 4 believe, receive (an opinion, explanation, etc.) As adequate or valid. 5 take as suitable (does accept cheques). 6 undertake (an office or duty). [latin capio take]'),
(148, 'a', '\nAcceptable', ' adj. 1 worth accepting, welcome. 2 tolerable.  acceptability n. Acceptably adv. [french: related to *accept]'),
(149, 'a', '\nAcceptance', ' n. 1 willingness to accept. 2 affirmative answer to an invitation etc. 3 approval, belief (found wide acceptance).'),
(150, 'a', '\nAccess', ' â€”n. 1 way of approach or entry (shop with rear access). 2 a right or opportunity to reach or use or visit; admittance (access to secret files, to the prisoner). B accessibility. 3 archaic outburst (an access of anger). â€”v. 1 computing gain access to (data etc.). 2 accession. [french: related to *accede]'),
(151, 'a', '\nAccessible', ' adj. (often foll. By to) 1 reachable or obtainable; readily available. 2 easy to understand.  accessibility n.'),
