(738, 'a', '\nAllotropy', ' n. Existence of two or more different physical forms of a chemical element.  allotropic adj. [greek allos different, tropos manner]'),
(739, 'a', '\nAllow', ' v. 1 (often foll. By to + infin.) Permit. 2 assign a limited amount etc. (was allowed Â£500). 3 (usu. Foll. By for) provide or set aside for a purpose; add or deduct in consideration (allow Â£50 for expenses; allow for wastage). [originally = commend, from french: related to *ad-, latin laudo praise, loco place]'),
(740, 'a', '\nAllowance', ' n. 1 amount or sum allowed, esp. Regularly for a stated purpose. 2 amount allowed in reckoning. 3 deduction or discount.  make allowances (often foll. By for) 1 consider (mitigating circumstances). 2 make excuses for (a person, bad behaviour, etc.).'),
(741, 'a', '\nAlloy', ' â€”n. 1 mixture of two or more metals. 2 inferior metal mixed esp. With gold or silver. â€”v. 1 mix (metals). 2 debase by admixture. 3 moderate (pleasure alloyed with pain). [french: related to *ally]'),
(742, 'a', '\nAll-purpose', ' attrib. Adj. Having many uses.'),
