(1050, 'a', '\nAnimal', ' â€”n. 1 living organism, esp. Other than man, which feeds and usu. Has sense-organs and a nervous system and can move quickly. 2 brutish person. â€”adj. 1 of or like an animal. 2 bestial; carnal. [latin animalis having breath]'),
(1051, 'a', '\nAnimalism', ' n. 1 nature and activity of animals. 2 belief that humans are mere animals.'),
(1052, 'a', '\nAnimality', ' n. 1 the animal world. 2 animal behaviour.'),
(1053, 'a', '\nAnimalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make (a person) bestial, sensualize.'),
(1054, 'a', '\nAnimate', ' â€”adj. 1 having life. 2 lively. â€”v. (-ting) 1 enliven. 2 give life to. [latin anima breath]'),
(1055, 'a', '\nAnimated', ' adj. 1 lively, vigorous. 2 having life. 3 (of a film etc.) Using animation.'),
(1056, 'a', '\nAnimation', ' n. 1 vivacity, ardour. 2 being alive. 3 technique of producing a moving picture from a sequence of drawings or puppet poses etc.'),
(1057, 'a', '\nAnimism', ' n. Belief that inanimate and natural phenomena have souls.  animist n. Animistic adj.'),
(1058, 'a', '\nAnimosity', ' n. (pl. -ies) spirit or feeling of hostility. [latin: related to *animus]'),
(1059, 'a', '\nAnimus', ' n. Animosity, ill feeling. [latin, = spirit, mind]'),
(1060, 'a', '\nAnion', ' n. Negatively charged ion.  anionic adj. [greek ana up, *ion]'),
(1061, 'a', '\nAnise', ' n. Plant with aromatic seeds. [greek anison]'),
(1062, 'a', '\nAniseed', ' n. Seed of the anise, used for flavouring.'),
(1063, 'a', '\nAnkle', ' n. 1 joint connecting the foot with the leg. 2 this part of the leg. [old norse]'),
(1064, 'a', '\nAnklet', ' n. Ornament or fetter worn round the ankle.'),
(1065, 'a', '\nAnkylosis', ' n. Stiffening of a joint by fusion of the bones. [greek agkulos crooked]'),
(1066, 'a', '\nAnnals', ' n.pl. 1 narrative of events year by year. 2 historical records.  annalist n. [latin annus year]'),
(1067, 'a', '\nAnnatto', ' n. (also anatto) orange-red dye from the pulp of a tropical fruit, used for colouring foods. [carib name of the fruit-tree]'),
(1068, 'a', '\nAnneal', ' v. Heat (metal or glass) and cool slowly, esp. To toughen it. [old english Ã¦lan bake]'),
(1069, 'a', '\nAnnelid', ' n. Segmented worm, e.g. The earthworm. [latin anulus ring]'),
(1070, 'a', '\nAnnex', ' v. 1 (often foll. By to) add as a subordinate part. 2 incorporate (territory) into one''s own. 3 add as a condition or consequence. 4 colloq. Take without right.  annexation n. [latin necto bind]'),
(1071, 'a', '\nAnnexe', ' n. 1 separate or added building. 2 addition to a document.'),
(1072, 'a', '\nAnnihilate', ' v. (-ting) completely destroy or defeat.  annihilation n. [latin nihil nothing]'),
(1073, 'a', '\nAnniversary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 date of an event in a previous year. 2 celebration of this. [latin annus year, verto vers- turn]'),
(1074, 'a', '\nAnno', 'domini  adv. Years after christ''s birth. [latin, = in the year of the lord]'),
(1075, 'a', '\nAnnotate', ' v. (-ting) add explanatory notes to.  annotation n. [latin nota mark]'),
(1076, 'a', '\nAnnounce', ' v. (-cing) 1 make publicly known. 2 make known the arrival or imminence of (a guest, dinner, etc.). 3 be a sign of.  announcement n. [latin nuntius messenger]'),
(1077, 'a', '\nAnnouncer', ' n. Person who announces, esp. In broadcasting.'),
(1078, 'a', '\nAnnoy', ' v. 1 (often in passive) anger or distress slightly (am annoyed with you). 2 molest, harass.  annoyance n. [latin in odio hateful]'),
(1079, 'a', '\nAnnual', ' â€”adj. 1 reckoned by the year. 2 occurring yearly. 3 living or lasting (only) a year. â€”n. 1 book etc. Published yearly. 2 plant that lives only a year.  annually adv. [latin annus year]'),
(1080, 'a', '\nAnnualized', ' adj. (of rates of interest etc.) Calculated on an annual basis, as a projection from figures obtained for a shorter period.'),
(1081, 'a', '\nAnnuity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 yearly grant or allowance. 2 investment yielding a fixed annual sum.'),
(1082, 'a', '\nAnnul', ' v. (-ll-) 1 declare invalid. 2 cancel, abolish.  annulment n. [latin nullus none]'),
(1083, 'a', '\nAnnular', ' adj. Ring-shaped. [latin anulus ring]'),
(1084, 'a', '\nAnnular', 'eclipse  n. Solar eclipse in which a ring of light remains visible.'),
(1085, 'a', '\nAnnulate', ' adj. Marked with or formed of rings.'),
(1086, 'a', '\nAnnunciation', ' n. 1 announcement, esp. (annunciation) that made by the angel gabriel to mary. 2 festival of this. [latin: related to *announce]'),
(1087, 'a', '\nAnode', ' n. Positive electrode in an electrolytic cell etc. [greek anodos way up]'),
(1088, 'a', '\nAnodize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) coat (metal) with a protective layer by electrolysis.'),
(1089, 'a', '\nAnodyne', ' â€”adj. 1 pain-relieving. 2 mentally soothing. â€”n. Anodyne drug etc. [greek an- without, odune pain]'),
(1090, 'a', '\nAnoint', ' v. 1 apply oil or ointment to, esp. Ritually. 2 (usu. Foll. By with) smear. [latin inungo anoint]'),
(1091, 'a', '\nAnomalous', ' adj. Irregular, deviant, abnormal. [greek an- not, homalos even]'),
(1092, 'a', '\nAnomaly', ' n. (pl. -ies) anomalous thing; irregularity.'),
(1093, 'a', '\nAnon', ' adv. Archaic soon, shortly. [old english on an into one]'),
(1094, 'a', '\nAnon.', ' Abbr. Anonymous.'),
(1095, 'a', '\nAnonymous', ' adj. 1 of unknown name or authorship. 2 without character; featureless.  anonymity n. [greek an- without, onoma name]'),
(1096, 'a', '\nAnorak', ' n. Waterproof usu. Hooded jacket. [eskimo]'),
(1097, 'a', '\nAnorexia', ' n. Lack of appetite, esp. (in full anorexia nervosa) an obsessive desire to lose weight by refusing to eat.  anorexic adj. & n. [greek an- without, orexis appetite]'),
(1098, 'a', '\nAnother', ' â€”adj. 1 an additional; one more (another cake). 2 person like (another hitler). 3 a different (another matter). 4 some other (another man''s work). â€”pron. Additional, other, or different person or thing. [earlier an other]'),
(1099, 'a', '\nAnswer', ' â€”n. 1 something said or done in reaction to a question, statement, or circumstance. 2 solution to a problem. â€”v. 1 make an answer or response (to) (answer the door). 2 suit (a purpose or need). 3 (foll. By to, for) be responsible (you will answer to me for your conduct). 4 (foll. By to) correspond, esp. To a description.  answer back answer insolently. [old english, = swear against (a charge)]'),
(1100, 'a', '\nAnswerable', ' adj. 1 (usu. Foll. By to, for) responsible (answerable to them for any accident). 2 that can be answered.'),
(1101, 'a', '\nAnswering', 'machine  n. Tape recorder which answers telephone calls and takes messages.'),
(1102, 'a', '\nAnswerphone', ' n. = *answering machine.'),
(1103, 'a', '\nAnt', ' n. Small usu. Wingless insect living in complex social colonies and proverbial for industry. [old english]'),
(1104, 'a', '\n-ant', ' suffix 1 forming adjectives denoting attribution of an action (repentant) or state (arrogant). 2 forming agent nouns (assistant). [latin -ant-, present participial stem of verbs]'),
(1105, 'a', '\nAntacid', ' â€”adj. Preventing or correcting acidity. â€”n. Antacid agent.'),
(1106, 'a', '\nAntagonism', ' n. Active hostility. [french: related to *agony]'),
(1107, 'a', '\nAntagonist', ' n. Opponent or adversary.  antagonistic adj.'),
(1108, 'a', '\nAntagonize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make hostile; provoke.'),
(1109, 'a', '\nAntarctic', ' â€”adj. Of the south polar regions. â€”n. This region. [latin: related to *arctic]'),
(1110, 'a', '\nAntarctic', 'circle  n. Parallel of latitude 66Â° 32Â´ s., forming an imaginary line round the antarctic region.'),
(1111, 'a', '\nAnte', ' â€”n. 1 stake put up by a player in poker etc. Before receiving cards. 2 amount payable in advance. â€”v. (-tes, -ted) 1 put up as an ante. 2 us a bet, stake. B (foll. By up) pay.'),
(1112, 'a', '\nAnte-', ' prefix before, preceding. [latin, = before]'),
(1113, 'a', '\nAnteater', ' n. Any of various mammals feeding on ants and termites.'),
(1114, 'a', '\nAntecedent', ' â€”n. 1 preceding thing or circumstance. 2 gram. Word or phrase etc. To which another word (esp. A relative pronoun) refers. 3 (in pl.) Person''s past history or ancestors. â€”adj. Previous. [latin cedo go]'),
(1115, 'a', '\nAntechamber', ' n. Ante-room.'),
(1116, 'a', '\nAntedate', ' v. (-ting) 1 precede in time. 2 assign an earlier than actual date to.'),
(1117, 'a', '\nAntediluvian', ' adj. 1 of the time before the flood. 2 colloq. Very old or out of date. [from *ante-, latin diluvium deluge]'),
(1118, 'a', '\nAntelope', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) swift-moving deerlike ruminant, e.g. The gazelle and gnu. [greek antholops]'),
(1119, 'a', '\nAntenatal', ' adj. 1 before birth. 2 of pregnancy.'),
(1120, 'a', '\nAntenna', ' n. 1 (pl. -tennae) each of a pair of feelers on the heads of insects, crustaceans, etc. 2 (pl. -s) = *aerial n. [latin, = sail-yard]'),
(1121, 'a', '\nAntepenultimate', ' adj. Last but two.'),
(1122, 'a', '\nAnte-post', ' adj. (of betting) done at odds determined at the time of betting, in advance of the event concerned. [from *ante-, *post1]'),
(1123, 'a', '\nAnterior', ' adj. 1 nearer the front. 2 (often foll. By to) prior. [latin from ante before]'),
(1124, 'a', '\nAnte-room', ' n. Small room leading to a main one.'),
(1125, 'a', '\nAnthem', ' n. 1 elaborate choral composition usu. Based on a passage of scripture. 2 solemn hymn of praise etc., esp. = *national anthem. [latin: related to *antiphon]'),
(1126, 'a', '\nAnther', ' n. Part of a stamen containing pollen. [greek anthos flower]'),
(1127, 'a', '\nAnthill', ' n. Moundlike nest built by ants or termites.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(1128, 'a', '\nAnthology', ' n. (pl. -ies) collection of poems, essays, stories, etc.  anthologist n. [greek anthos flower, -logia collection]'),
(1129, 'a', '\nAnthracite', ' n. Hard type of coal burning with little flame and smoke. [greek: related to *anthrax]'),
(1130, 'a', '\nAnthrax', ' n. Disease of sheep and cattle transmissible to humans. [greek, = coal, carbuncle]'),
(1131, 'a', '\nAnthropocentric', ' adj. Regarding mankind as the centre of existence. [greek anthropos man]'),
(1132, 'a', '\nAnthropoid', ' â€”adj. Human in form. â€”n. Anthropoid ape.'),
(1133, 'a', '\nAnthropology', ' n. The study of mankind, esp. Its societies and customs.  anthropological adj. Anthropologist n.'),
(1134, 'a', '\nAnthropomorphism', ' n. Attribution of human characteristics to a god, animal, or thing.  anthropomorphic adj. [greek morphe form]'),
(1135, 'a', '\nAnthropomorphous', ' adj. Human in form.'),
(1136, 'a', '\nAnti', ' â€”prep. Opposed to. â€”n. (pl. -s) person opposed to a policy etc.'),
(1137, 'a', '\nAnti-', ' prefix 1 opposed to (anticlerical). 2 preventing (antifreeze). 3 opposite of (anticlimax). 4 unconventional (anti-hero). [greek]'),
(1138, 'a', '\nAnti-abortion', ' adj. Opposing abortion.  anti-abortionist n.'),
(1139, 'a', '\nAnti-aircraft', ' adj. (of a gun or missile) used to attack enemy aircraft.'),
(1140, 'a', '\nAntibiotic', ' â€”n. Substance (e.g. Penicillin) that can inhibit or destroy susceptible micro-organisms. â€”adj. Functioning as an antibiotic. [greek bios life]'),
(1141, 'a', '\nAntibody', ' n. (pl. -ies) a blood protein produced in response to and then counteracting antigens. [translation of german antikÃ¶rper]'),
(1142, 'a', '\nAntic', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Foolish behaviour or action. [italian antico *antique]'),
(1143, 'a', '\nAntichrist', ' n. Enemy of christ.  antichristian adj.'),
(1144, 'a', '\nAnticipate', ' v. (-ting) 1 deal with or use before the proper time. 2 expect, foresee (did not anticipate a problem). 3 forestall (a person or thing). 4 look forward to.  anticipation n. Anticipatory adj. [latin anti- before, capio take]'),
(1145, 'a', '\nUsage', ' the use of anticipate in sense 2, â€˜expectâ€™, â€˜foreseeâ€™, is well-established in informal use, but is regarded as incorrect by some people.'),
(1146, 'a', '\nAnticlerical', ' adj. Opposed to clerical influence, esp. In politics.'),
(1147, 'a', '\nAnticlimax', ' n. Disappointingly trivial conclusion to something significant.'),
(1148, 'a', '\nAnticlockwise', ' adj. & adv. Moving in a curve opposite in direction to the hands of a clock.'),
(1149, 'a', '\nAnticyclone', ' n. System of winds rotating outwards from an area of high pressure, producing fine weather.'),
(1150, 'a', '\nAntidepressant', ' â€”n. Drug etc. That alleviates depression. â€”adj. Alleviating depression.'),
(1151, 'a', '\nAntidote', ' n. 1 medicine etc. Used to counteract poison. 2 anything counteracting something unpleasant. [greek antidotos given against]'),
(1152, 'a', '\nAntifreeze', ' n. Substance added to water to lower its freezing point, esp. In a vehicle''s radiator.'),
(1153, 'a', '\nAntigen', ' n. Foreign substance (e.g. Toxin) which causes the body to produce antibodies. [greek -genes of a kind]'),
(1154, 'a', '\nAnti-hero', ' n. (pl. -es) central character in a story, lacking conventional heroic qualities.'),
(1155, 'a', '\nAntihistamine', ' n. Drug that counteracts the effects of histamine, used esp. In treating allergies.'),
(1156, 'a', '\nAntiknock', ' n. Substance added to motor fuel to prevent premature combustion.'),
(1157, 'a', '\nAnti-lock', ' attrib. Adj. (of brakes) set up so as to prevent locking and skidding when applied suddenly.'),
(1158, 'a', '\nAntilog', ' n. Colloq. = *antilogarithm. [abbreviation]'),
(1159, 'a', '\nAntilogarithm', ' n. Number to which a logarithm belongs.'),
(1160, 'a', '\nAntimacassar', ' n. Detachable protective cloth for the back of a chair etc.'),
(1161, 'a', '\nAntimatter', ' n. Matter composed solely of antiparticles.'),
(1162, 'a', '\nAntimony', ' n. Brittle silvery metallic element used esp. In alloys. [medieval latin]'),
(1163, 'a', '\nAntinomian', ' â€”adj. Believing that christians need not obey the moral law. â€”n. (antinomian) hist. Person believing this. [greek nomos law]'),
(1164, 'a', '\nAntinomy', ' n. (pl. -ies) contradiction between two reasonable beliefs or conclusions.'),
(1165, 'a', '\nAntinovel', ' n. Novel avoiding the conventions of the form.'),
(1166, 'a', '\nAnti-nuclear', ' adj. Opposed to the development of nuclear weapons or power.'),
(1167, 'a', '\nAntiparticle', ' n. Elementary particle with the same mass but opposite charge etc. To another particle.'),
(1168, 'a', '\nAntipathy', ' n. (pl. -ies) (often foll. By to, for, between) strong aversion or dislike.  antipathetic adj. [greek: related to *pathetic]'),
(1169, 'a', '\nAntiperspirant', ' n. Substance preventing or reducing perspiration.'),
(1170, 'a', '\nAntiphon', ' n. 1 hymn sung alternately by two groups. 2 versicle or phrase from this.  antiphonal adj. [greek phone sound]'),
(1171, 'a', '\nAntipodes', ' n.pl. Places diametrically opposite to one another on the earth, esp. (also antipodes) australasia in relation to europe.  antipodean adj. & n. [greek, = having the feet opposite]'),
(1172, 'a', '\nAntipope', ' n. Pope set up in opposition to one chosen by canon law.'),
(1173, 'a', '\nAntipyretic', ' â€”adj. Preventing or reducing fever. â€”n. Antipyretic drug.'),
(1174, 'a', '\nAntiquarian', ' â€”adj. Of or dealing in antiques or rare books. â€”n. Antiquary.  antiquarianism n.'),
(1175, 'a', '\nAntiquary', ' n. (pl. -ies) student or collector of antiques etc. [latin: related to *antique]'),
(1176, 'a', '\nAntiquated', ' adj. Old-fashioned.'),
(1177, 'a', '\nAntique', ' â€”n. Old object, esp. A piece of furniture, of high value. â€”adj. 1 of or from an early date. 2 old-fashioned. [latin antiquus]'),
(1178, 'a', '\nAntiquity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 ancient times, esp. Before the middle ages. 2 great age. 3 (usu. In pl.) Relics from ancient times. [latin: related to *antique]'),
(1179, 'a', '\nAntirrhinum', ' n. Plant with two-lipped flowers, esp. The snapdragon. [greek, = snout]'),
(1180, 'a', '\nAnti-semite', ' n. Person who is prejudiced against jews.  anti-semitic adj. Anti-semitism n.'),
(1181, 'a', '\nAntiseptic', ' â€”adj. 1 counteracting sepsis, esp. By destroying germs. 2 sterile, uncontaminated. 3 lacking character. â€”n. Antiseptic agent.'),
(1182, 'a', '\nAntiserum', ' n. Serum with a high antibody content.'),
(1183, 'a', '\nAntisocial', ' adj. 1 opposed or harmful to society. 2 not sociable.'),
(1184, 'a', '\nUsage', ' antisocial is sometimes used mistakenly instead of unsocial in the phrase unsocial hours. This should be avoided.'),
(1185, 'a', '\nAntistatic', ' adj. Counteracting the effects of static electricity.'),
(1186, 'a', '\nAnti-tank', ' attrib. Adj. Used against tanks.'),
(1187, 'a', '\nAntitetanus', ' adj. Effective against tetanus.'),
(1188, 'a', '\nAntithesis', ' n. (pl. -theses) 1 (foll. By of, to) direct opposite. 2 contrast. 3 rhetorical use of strongly contrasted words.  antithetical adj. [greek antitithemi set against]'),
(1189, 'a', '\nAntitoxin', ' n. Antibody counteracting a toxin.  antitoxic adj.'),
(1190, 'a', '\nAntitrades', ' n.pl. Winds blowing in the opposite direction to (and usu. Above) trade winds.'),
(1191, 'a', '\nAntiviral', ' adj. Effective against viruses.'),
(1192, 'a', '\nAntler', ' n. Branched horn of a stag or other deer.  antlered adj. [french]'),
(1193, 'a', '\nAntonym', ' n. Word opposite in meaning to another. [greek onoma name]'),
(1194, 'a', '\nAntrum', ' n. (pl. Antra) natural cavity in the body, esp. In a bone. [greek, = cave]'),
(1195, 'a', '\nAnus', ' n. (pl. Anuses) excretory opening at the end of the alimentary canal. [latin]'),
(1196, 'a', '\nAnvil', ' n. Iron block on which metals are worked. [old english]'),
(1197, 'a', '\nAnxiety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being anxious. 2 worry or concern. 3 eagerness, troubled desire. [latin anxietas from ango choke]'),
(1198, 'a', '\nAnxious', ' adj. 1 mentally troubled. 2 causing or marked by anxiety (anxious moment). 3 eager, uneasily wanting (anxious to please).  anxiously adv. [latin anxius]'),
(1199, 'a', '\nAny', ' â€”adj. 1 a one, no matter which, of several (cannot find any answer). B some, no matter how much or many or of what sort (if any books arrive; have you any sugar?). 2 a minimal amount of (hardly any difference). 3 whichever is chosen (any fool knows). 4 an appreciable or significant (did not stay for any length of time; has any amount of money). â€”pron. 1 any one (did not know any of them). 2 any number or amount (are any of them yours?). â€”adv. (usu. With neg. Or interrog.) At all (is that any good?). [old english Ã¦nig: related to *one, *-y1]'),
(1200, 'a', '\nAnybody', ' n. & pron. 1 any person. 2 person of importance (is he anybody?).'),
(1201, 'a', '\nAnyhow', ' adv. 1 anyway. 2 in a disorderly manner or state (does his work anyhow).'),
(1202, 'a', '\nAnyone', ' pron. Anybody.'),
(1203, 'a', '\nUsage', ' anyone is written as two words to emphasize a numerical sense, as in any one of us can do it.'),
(1204, 'a', '\nAnything', ' pron. Any thing; thing of any sort.  anything but not at all.'),
(1205, 'a', '\nAnyway', ' adv. 1 in any way or manner. 2 at any rate. 3 to resume (anyway, as i was saying).'),
(1206, 'a', '\nAnywhere', ' â€”adv. In or to any place. â€”pron. Any place (anywhere will do).'),
(1207, 'a', '\nAob', ' abbr. Any other business.'),
(1208, 'a', '\nAorta', ' n. (pl. -s) main artery, giving rise to the arterial network carrying oxygenated blood to the body from the heart.  aortic adj. [greek aeiro raise]'),
(1209, 'a', '\nApace', ' adv. Literary swiftly. [french Ã  pas]'),
(1210, 'a', '\nApache', ' n. Member of a n. American indian tribe. [mexican spanish]'),
(1211, 'a', '\nApart', ' adv. 1 separately, not together (keep your feet apart). 2 into pieces (came apart). 3 to or on one side. 4 to or at a distance.  apart from 1 excepting, not considering. 2 in addition to (apart from roses we grow irises). [french Ã  part to one side]'),
(1212, 'a', '\nApartheid', ' n. (esp. In s. Africa) racial segregation or discrimination. [afrikaans]'),
(1213, 'a', '\nApartment', ' n. 1 (in pl.) Suite of rooms. 2 single room. 3 us flat. [italian a parte, apart]'),
(1214, 'a', '\nApathy', ' n. Lack of interest; indifference.  apathetic adj. [greek a- without, *pathos]'),
(1215, 'a', '\nApe', ' â€”n. 1 tailless monkey-like primate, e.g. The gorilla, chimpanzee, orang-utan, or gibbon. 2 imitator. â€”v. (-ping) imitate, mimic. [old english]'),
(1216, 'a', '\nApeman', ' n. Extinct primate held to be the forerunner of present-day man.'),
(1217, 'a', '\nAperient', ' â€”adj. Laxative. â€”n. Laxative medicine. [latin aperio open]'),
(1218, 'a', '\nAperitif', ' n. Alcoholic drink taken before a meal. [latin aperio open]'),
(1219, 'a', '\nAperture', ' n. Opening or gap, esp. A variable opening in a camera for admitting light. [latin aperio open]'),
(1220, 'a', '\nApex', ' n. (also apex) (often attrib.) System of reduced fares for scheduled flights. [advance purchase excursion]'),
(1221, 'a', '\nApex', ' n. (pl. -es) 1 highest point. 2 tip or pointed end. [latin]'),
(1222, 'a', '\nAphasia', ' n. Loss of verbal understanding or expression, owing to brain damage. [greek aphatos speechless]'),
(1223, 'a', '\nAphelion', ' n. (pl. -lia) point in a celestial body''s orbit where it is furthest from the sun. [greek aph''heliou from the sun]'),
(1224, 'a', '\nAphid', ' n. Small insect infesting and damaging plants, e.g. The greenfly.'),
(1225, 'a', '\nAntitrades', ' n.pl. Winds blowing in the opposite direction to (and usu. Above) trade winds.'),
(1226, 'a', '\nAntiviral', ' adj. Effective against viruses.'),
(1227, 'a', '\nAntler', ' n. Branched horn of a stag or other deer.  antlered adj. [french]'),
(1228, 'a', '\nAntonym', ' n. Word opposite in meaning to another. [greek onoma name]'),
(1229, 'a', '\nAntrum', ' n. (pl. Antra) natural cavity in the body, esp. In a bone. [greek, = cave]'),
(1230, 'a', '\nAnus', ' n. (pl. Anuses) excretory opening at the end of the alimentary canal. [latin]'),
(1231, 'a', '\nAnvil', ' n. Iron block on which metals are worked. [old english]'),
(1232, 'a', '\nAnxiety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being anxious. 2 worry or concern. 3 eagerness, troubled desire. [latin anxietas from ango choke]'),
(1233, 'a', '\nAnxious', ' adj. 1 mentally troubled. 2 causing or marked by anxiety (anxious moment). 3 eager, uneasily wanting (anxious to please).  anxiously adv. [latin anxius]'),
(1234, 'a', '\nAny', ' â€”adj. 1 a one, no matter which, of several (cannot find any answer). B some, no matter how much or many or of what sort (if any books arrive; have you any sugar?). 2 a minimal amount of (hardly any difference). 3 whichever is chosen (any fool knows). 4 an appreciable or significant (did not stay for any length of time; has any amount of money). â€”pron. 1 any one (did not know any of them). 2 any number or amount (are any of them yours?). â€”adv. (usu. With neg. Or interrog.) At all (is that any good?). [old english Ã¦nig: related to *one, *-y1]'),
