(1532, 'a', '\nArm1', ' n. 1 upper limb of the human body from shoulder to hand. 2 forelimb or tentacle of an animal. 3 a sleeve of a garment. B arm support of a chair etc. C thing branching from a main stem (an arm of the sea). D control, means of reaching (arm of the law).  arm in arm with arms linked. At arm''s length at a distance. With open arms cordially.  armful n. (pl. -s). [old english]'),
(1533, 'a', '\nArm2', ' â€”n. 1 (usu. In pl.) Weapon. 2 (in pl.) Military profession. 3 branch of the military (e.g. Infantry, cavalry). 4 (in pl.) Heraldic devices (coat of arms). â€”v. 1 supply, or equip oneself, with weapons etc., esp. In preparation for war. 2 make (a bomb etc.) Ready.  take up arms go to war. Under arms equipped for war. Up in arms (usu. Foll. By against, about) actively resisting, highly indignant. [latin arma arms]'),
(1534, 'a', '\nArmada', ' n. Fleet of warships, esp. (armada) that sent by spain against england in 1588. [spanish from romanic]'),
(1535, 'a', '\nArmadillo', ' n. (pl. -s) s. American mammal with a plated body and large claws. [spanish armado armed man]'),
(1536, 'a', '\nArmageddon', ' n. Huge battle or struggle, esp. Marking the end of the world. [rev. 16:16]'),
(1537, 'a', '\nArmament', ' n. 1 (often in pl.) Military equipment. 2 equipping for war. 3 force equipped. [latin: related to *arm2]'),
(1538, 'a', '\nArmature', ' n. 1 rotating coil or coils of a dynamo or electric motor. 2 iron bar placed across the poles of a horseshoe magnet to preserve its power. 3 metal framework on which a sculpture is moulded. [latin armatura, = armour]'),
(1539, 'a', '\nArmband', ' n. Band worn around the upper arm to hold up a shirtsleeve, or as identification, or to aid swimming.'),
(1540, 'a', '\nArmchair', ' n. 1 chair with arm supports. 2 (attrib.) Theoretical rather than active (armchair critic).'),
(1541, 'a', '\nArmhole', ' n. Each of two holes for arms in a garment.'),
(1542, 'a', '\nArmhole', ' n. Each of two holes for arms in a garment.'),
(1543, 'a', '\nArmistice', ' n. Truce, esp. Permanent. [latin arma arms, sisto make stand]'),
(1544, 'a', '\nArmistice', 'day  n. Anniversary of the armistice of 11 nov. 1918.'),
(1545, 'a', '\nArmlet', ' n. Ornamental band worn round the arm.'),
(1546, 'a', '\nArmor', ' (brit. Armour) â€”n. 1 protective usu. Metal covering formerly worn in fighting. 2 a (in full armour-plate) protective metal covering for an armed vehicle, ship, etc. B armed vehicles collectively. 3 protective covering or shell of an animal or plant. 4 heraldic devices. â€”v. (usu. Asarmoured adj.) Provide with protective covering, and often guns (armoured car; armoured train). [latin armatura: related to *arm2]'),
(1547, 'a', '\nArmorial', ' adj. Of heraldry or coats of arms. [related to *armour]'),
(1548, 'a', '\nArmour', ' â€”n. 1 protective usu. Metal covering formerly worn in fighting. 2 a (in full armour-plate) protective metal covering for an armed vehicle, ship, etc. B armed vehicles collectively. 3 protective covering or shell of an animal or plant. 4 heraldic devices. â€”v. (usu. As armoured adj.) Provide with protective covering, and often guns (armoured car; armoured train). [latin armatura: related to *arm2]'),
