(2751, 'b', '\r\nBlade', ' n. 1 cutting part of a knife etc. 2 flattened part of an oar, propeller, etc. 3 a flat narrow leaf of grass etc. B broad thin part of a leaf. 4 flat bone, e.g. In the shoulder. [old english]'),
(2752, 'b', '\r\nBlame', ' â€”v. (-ming) 1 assign fault or responsibility to. 2 (foll. By on) fix responsibility for (an error etc.) On (blamed it on his brother). â€”n. 1 responsibility for an error etc. 2 blaming or attributing of responsibility (got all the blame).  be to blame be responsible; deserve censure.  blameable adj. Blameless adj. Blameworthy adj. [french: related to *blaspheme]'),
(2753, 'b', '\r\nBlanch', ' v. 1 make or become white or pale. 2 a peel (almonds etc.) By scalding. B immerse (vegetables etc.) Briefly in boiling water. 3 whiten (a plant) by depriving it of light. [french: related to *blank]'),
(2754, 'b', '\r\nBlancmange', ' n. Sweet opaque jelly of flavoured cornflour and milk. [french, = white food]'),
(2755, 'b', '\r\nBland', ' adj. 1 a mild, not irritating. B tasteless; insipid. 2 gentle in manner; suave.  blandly adv. Blandness n. [latin blandus smooth]'),
(2756, 'b', '\r\nBlandish', ' v. Flatter; coax.  blandishment n. (usu. In pl.). [latin: related to *bland]'),
(2757, 'b', '\r\nBlank', ' â€”adj. 1 a (of paper) not written or printed on. B (of a document) with spaces left for a signature or details. 2 a empty (blank space). B unrelieved (blank wall). 3 a without interest, result, or expression (blank face). B having (temporarily) no knowledge etc. (mind went blank). 4 complete (a blank refusal; blank despair). â€”n. 1 a unfilled space, esp. In a document. B document having blank spaces. 2 (in full blank cartridge) cartridge containing gunpowder but no bullet. 3 dash written instead of a word or letter. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By off, out) screen, obscure.  draw a blank get no response; fail.  blankly adv. Blankness n. [french blanc white, from germanic]'),
(2758, 'b', '\r\nBlank', 'cheque  n. 1 cheque left for the payee to fill in. 2 colloq. Unlimited freedom of action.'),
(2759, 'b', '\r\nBlanket', ' â€”n. 1 large esp. Woollen sheet used as a bed-covering etc. 2 thick covering mass or layer. â€”attrib. Adj. Covering everything; inclusive. â€”v. (-t-) 1 cover. 2 stifle, suppress. [french: related to *blank]'),
(2760, 'b', '\r\nBlanket', 'bath  n. Body wash given to a bedridden patient.'),
(2761, 'b', '\r\nBlanket', 'stitch  n. Stitch used to finish the edges of a blanket etc.'),
(2762, 'b', '\r\nBlank', 'verse  n. Unrhymed verse, esp. Iambic pentameters.'),
(2763, 'b', '\r\nBlare', ' â€”v. (-ring) 1 sound or utter loudly. 2 make the sound of a trumpet. â€”n. Blaring sound. [low german or dutch, imitative]'),
(2764, 'b', '\r\nBlarney', ' â€”n. Cajoling talk; flattery. â€”v. (-eys, -eyed) flatter, cajole. [blarney, castle near cork]'),
(2765, 'b', '\r\nBlasÃ©', ' adj. Bored or indifferent through over-familiarity. [french]'),
(2766, 'b', '\r\nBlaspheme', ' v. (-ming) 1 use religious names irreverently; treat a religious or sacred subject irreverently. 2 talk irreverently about; use blasphemy against. [greek blasphemeo]'),
(2767, 'b', '\r\nBlasphemy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 irreverent talk or treatment of a religious or sacred thing. 2 instance of this.  blasphemous adj.'),
(2768, 'b', '\r\nBlast', ' â€”n. 1 strong gust of air. 2 a explosion. B destructive wave of air from this. 3 loud note from a wind instrument, car horn, etc. 4 colloq. Severe reprimand. â€”v. 1 blow up with explosives. 2 wither, blight (blasted oak; blasted her hopes). 3 (cause to) make a loud noise. â€”int. Expressing annoyance.  at full blast colloq. At maximum volume, speed, etc. Blast off take off from a launching site. [old english]'),
(2769, 'b', '\r\nBlasted', ' colloq. â€”attrib. Adj. Damned; annoying. â€”adv. Damned; extremely.'),
