(2781, 'b', '\r\nBleary-eyed', ' adj. Having dim sight.'),
(2782, 'b', '\r\nBleat', ' â€”v. 1 (of a sheep, goat, or calf) make a wavering cry. 2 (often foll. By out) speak or say plaintively. â€”n. Bleating cry. [old english]'),
(2783, 'b', '\r\nBleed', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Bled) 1 emit blood. 2 draw blood from surgically. 3 colloq. Extort money from. 4 (often foll. By for) suffer wounds or violent death. 5 a emit sap. B (of dye) come out in water. 6 empty (a system) of excess air or fluid. â€”n. Act of bleeding.  one''s heart bleeds usu. Iron. One is very sorrowful. [old english]'),
(2784, 'b', '\r\nBleeder', ' n. Coarse slang unpleasant or contemptible person.'),
(2785, 'b', '\r\nBleeding', ' adj. & adv. Coarse slang expressing annoyance or antipathy.'),
(2786, 'b', '\r\nBleep', ' â€”n. Intermittent high-pitched electronic sound. â€”v. 1 make a bleep. 2 summon with a bleeper. [imitative]'),
(2787, 'b', '\r\nBleeper', ' n. Small electronic device bleeping to contact the carrier.'),
(2788, 'b', '\r\nBlemish', ' â€”n. Flaw, defect, or stain. â€”v. Spoil, mark, or stain. [french]'),
(2789, 'b', '\r\nBlench', ' v. Flinch, quail. [old english]'),
(2790, 'b', '\r\nBlend', ' â€”v. 1 mix together as required. 2 become one. 3 (often foll. By with, in) mingle; mix thoroughly. 4 (esp. Of colours) merge imperceptibly; harmonize. â€”n. Mixture. [old norse]'),
(2791, 'b', '\r\nBlender', ' n. Machine for liquidizing, chopping, or purÃ©eing food.'),
(2792, 'b', '\r\nBlenny', ' n. (pl. -ies) small spiny-finned scaleless marine fish. [greek blennos mucus]'),
(2793, 'b', '\r\nBless', ' v. (past and past part. Blessed, poet. Blest) 1 ask god to look favourably on, esp. By making the sign of the cross over. 2 consecrate (food etc.). 3 glorify (god). 4 attribute one''s good luck to (stars etc.); thank. 5 (usu. In passive) make happy or successful (blessed with children).  bless me (or my soul) exclamation of surprise etc. Bless you! Exclamation of endearment, gratitude, etc., or to a person who has just sneezed. [old english]'),
(2794, 'b', '\r\nBlessed', ' adj. (also poet. Blest) 1 holy. 2 euphem. Cursed (blessed nuisance!). 3 rc ch. Beatified.  blessedness n.'),
(2795, 'b', '\r\nBlessing', ' n. 1 invocation of (esp. Divine) favour. 2 grace said at a meal. 3 benefit.'),
(2796, 'b', '\r\nBlether', ' var. Of *blather.'),
(2797, 'b', '\r\nBlew', ' past of *blow1.'),
(2798, 'b', '\r\nBlether', ' var. Of *blather.'),
(2799, 'b', '\r\nBlew', ' past of *blow1.'),
(2800, 'b', '\r\nBlight', ' â€”n. 1 plant disease caused by insects etc. 2 such an insect etc. 3 harmful or destructive force. 4 ugly urban area. â€”v. 1 affect with blight. 2 harm, destroy. 3 spoil. [origin unknown]'),
(2801, 'b', '\r\nBlighter', ' n. Colloq. Contemptible or annoying person.'),
