(2803, 'b', '\r\nBlimey', ' int. Coarse slang expression of surprise, contempt, etc. [(god) blind me!]'),
(2804, 'b', '\r\nBlimp', ' n. 1 (also (colonel) blimp) reactionary person. 2 small non-rigid airship. 3 soundproof cover for a cine-camera. [origin uncertain]'),
(2805, 'b', '\r\nBlind', ' â€”adj. 1 lacking the power of sight. 2 a without adequate foresight, discernment, or information (blind effort). B (often foll. By to) unwilling or unable to appreciate a factor etc. (blind to argument). 3 not governed by purpose or reason (blind forces). 4 reckless (blind hitting). 5 a concealed (blind ditch). B closed at one end. 6 (of flying) using instruments only. 7 cookery (of a flan case etc.) Baked without a filling. â€”v. 1 deprive of sight. 2 rob of judgement; deceive; overawe. 3 slang go recklessly. â€”n. 1 screen for a window; awning. 2 thing used to hide the truth. 3 obstruction to sight or light. â€”adv. Blindly.  blindly adv. Blindness n. [old english]'),
(2806, 'b', '\r\nBlind', 'alley  n. 1 alley closed at one end. 2 futile course.'),
(2807, 'b', '\r\nBlind', 'date  n. Colloq. Date between two people who have not previously met.'),
(2808, 'b', '\r\nBlind', 'drunk  adj. Colloq. Extremely drunk.'),
(2809, 'b', '\r\nBlindfold', ' â€”v. Cover the eyes of (a person) with a tied cloth etc. â€”n. Cloth etc. So used. â€”adj. & adv. 1 with eyes covered. 2 without due care. [originally blindfelled = struck blind]'),
(2810, 'b', '\r\nBlind', 'man''s buff  n. Game in which a blindfold player tries to catch others.'),
(2811, 'b', '\r\nBlind', 'spot  n. 1 point on the retina insensitive to light. 2 area where vision or understanding is lacking.'),
(2812, 'b', '\r\nBlindworm', ' n. = *slow-worm.'),
(2813, 'b', '\r\nBlink', ' â€”v. 1 shut and open the eyes quickly. 2 (often foll. By back) prevent (tears) by blinking. 3 shine unsteadily, flicker. â€”n. 1 act of blinking. 2 momentary gleam or glimpse.  blink at 1 look at while blinking. 2 ignore, shirk. On the blink slang not working properly; out of order. [dutch, var. Of *blench]'),
(2814, 'b', '\r\nBlinker', ' â€”n. 1 (usu. In pl.) Each of two screens on a bridle preventing lateral vision. 2 device that blinks. â€”v. 1 obscure with blinkers. 2 (as blinkered adj.) Having narrow and prejudiced views.'),
(2815, 'b', '\r\nBlinking', ' adj. & adv. Slang expressing annoyance etc. (it''s blinking stupid).'),
(2816, 'b', '\r\nBlip', ' â€”n. 1 minor deviation or error. 2 quick popping sound. 3 small image on a radar screen. â€”v. (-pp-) make a blip. [imitative]'),
(2817, 'b', '\r\nBliss', ' n. 1 perfect joy. 2 being in heaven.  blissful adj. Blissfully adv. [old english]'),
(2818, 'b', '\r\nBlister', ' â€”n. 1 small bubble on the skin filled with watery fluid and caused by heat or friction. 2 similar swelling on plastic, wood, etc. â€”v. 1 come up in blisters. 2 raise a blister on. 3 attack sharply. [origin uncertain]'),
(2819, 'b', '\r\nBlithe', ' adj. 1 cheerful, happy. 2 careless, casual.  blithely adv. [old english]'),
(2820, 'b', '\r\nBlithering', ' attrib. Adj. Colloq. Hopeless; contemptible (esp. In blithering idiot). [blither, var. Of *blather]'),
(2821, 'b', '\r\nBlitt.', ' Abbr. Bachelor of letters. [latin baccalaureus litterarum]'),
(2822, 'b', '\r\nBlitz', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 a intensive or sudden (esp. Aerial) attack. B intensive period of work etc. (must have a blitz on this room). 2 (the blitz) german air raids on london in 1940. â€”v. Inflict a blitz on. [abbreviation of *blitzkrieg]'),
