(2824, 'b', '\r\nBlizzard', ' n. Severe snowstorm. [origin unknown]'),
(2825, 'b', '\r\nBloat', ' v. 1 inflate, swell. 2 (as bloated adj.) Inflated with pride, wealth, or food. 3 cure (a herring) by salting and smoking lightly. [old norse]'),
(2826, 'b', '\r\nBloater', ' n. Bloated herring.'),
(2827, 'b', '\r\nBlob', ' n. Small drop or spot. [imitative]'),
(2828, 'b', '\r\nBloc', ' n. Group of governments etc. Sharing a common purpose. [french: related to *block]'),
(2829, 'b', '\r\nBlock', ' â€”n. 1 solid piece of hard material, esp. Stone or wood. 2 this as a base for chopping etc., as a stand, or for mounting a horse from. 3 a large building, esp. When subdivided. B group of buildings between streets. 4 obstruction. 5 two or more pulleys mounted in a case. 6 piece of wood or metal engraved for printing. 7 slang head. 8 (often attrib.) Number of things as a unit, e.g. Shares, theatre seats (block booking). 9 sheets of paper glued along one edge. â€”v. 1 a (often foll. By up) obstruct. B impede. 2 restrict the use of. 3 cricket stop (a ball) with a bat defensively.  block in 1 sketch roughly; plan. 2 confine. Block out 1 shut out (light, noise, a memory, view, etc.). 2 sketch roughly; plan. Block up confine; enclose. [low german or dutch]'),
(2830, 'b', '\r\nBlockade', ' â€”n. Surrounding or blocking of a place by an enemy to prevent entry and exit. â€”v. (-ding) subject to a blockade.'),
(2831, 'b', '\r\nBlockage', ' n. Obstruction.'),
(2832, 'b', '\r\nBlock', 'and tackle  n. System of pulleys and ropes, esp. For lifting.'),
(2833, 'b', '\r\nBlockbuster', ' n. Slang 1 thing of great power, esp. A very successful film, book, etc. 2 highly destructive bomb.'),
(2834, 'b', '\r\nBlock', 'capitals  n.pl. (also block letters) letters printed without serifs, or written with each letter separate and in capitals.'),
(2835, 'b', '\r\nBlockhead', ' n. Stupid person.'),
(2836, 'b', '\r\nBlockhouse', ' n. 1 reinforced concrete shelter. 2 hist. Small fort of timber.'),
(2837, 'b', '\r\nBlock', 'vote  n. Vote proportional in power to the number of people a delegate represents.'),
(2838, 'b', '\r\nBloke', ' n. Slang man, fellow. [shelta]'),
(2839, 'b', '\r\nBlond', ' (of a woman usu. Blonde) â€”adj. (of a person, hair, or complexion) light-coloured, fair. â€”n. Blond person. [latin blondus yellow]'),
(2840, 'b', '\r\nBlood', ' â€”n. 1 usu. Red fluid circulating in the arteries and veins of animals. 2 bloodshed, esp. Killing. 3 passion, temperament. 4 race, descent, parentage (of the same blood). 5 relationship; relations (blood is thicker than water). 6 dandy. â€”v. 1 give (a hound) a first taste of blood. 2 initiate (a person).  in one''s blood inherent in one''s character. [old english]'),
(2841, 'b', '\r\nBlood', 'bank  n. Store of blood for transfusion.'),
