(2873, 'b', '\r\nBlooming', ' â€”adj. 1 flourishing; healthy. 2 slang an intensifier (blooming miracle). â€”adv. Slang an intensifier (blooming difficult).'),
(2874, 'b', '\r\nBlossom', ' â€”n. 1 flower or mass of flowers, esp. Of a fruit-tree. 2 promising stage (blossom of youth). â€”v. 1 open into flower. 2 mature, thrive. [old english]'),
(2875, 'b', '\r\nBlot', ' â€”n. 1 spot or stain of ink etc. 2 disgraceful act or quality. 3 blemish. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 make a blot on, stain. 2 dry with blotting-paper.  blot one''s copybook damage one''s reputation. Blot out 1 obliterate. 2 obscure (a view, sound, etc.). [probably scandinavian]'),
(2876, 'b', '\r\nBlotch', ' â€”n. 1 discoloured or inflamed patch on the skin. 2 irregular patch of colour. â€”v. Cover with blotches.  blotchy adj. (-ier, -iest). [obsolete plotch, *blot]'),
(2877, 'b', '\r\nBlotter', ' n. Pad of blotting-paper.'),
(2878, 'b', '\r\nBlotting-paper', ' n. Absorbent paper for drying wet ink.'),
(2879, 'b', '\r\nBlotto', ' adj. Slang very drunk. [origin uncertain]'),
(2880, 'b', '\r\nBlouse', ' â€”n. 1 woman''s garment like a shirt. 2 upper part of a military uniform. â€”v. (-sing) make (a bodice etc.) Full like a blouse. [french]'),
(2881, 'b', '\r\nBlouson', ' n. Short blouse-shaped jacket. [french]'),
(2882, 'b', '\r\nBlow1', ' â€”v. (past blew; past part. Blown) 1 direct a current of air (at) esp. From the mouth. 2 drive or be driven by blowing (blew the door open). 3 (esp. Of the wind) move rapidly. 4 expel by breathing (blew smoke). 5 sound or be sounded by blowing. 6 (past part. Blowed) slang (esp. In imper.) Curse, confound (i''m blowed if i know; blow it!). 7 clear (the nose) by blowing. 8 puff, pant. 9 slang depart suddenly (from). 10 shatter etc. By an explosion. 11 make or shape (glass or a bubble) by blowing. 12 a melt from overloading (the fuse has blown). B break or burst suddenly. 13 (of a whale) eject air and water. 14 break into with explosives. 15 slang a squander (blew Â£20). B bungle (an opportunity etc.). C reveal (a secret etc.). â€”n. 1 act of blowing. 2 a gust of wind or air. B exposure to fresh air.  be blowed if one will colloq. Be unwilling to. Blow a gasket slang lose one''s temper. Blow hot and cold colloq. Vacillate. Blow in 1 break inwards by an explosion. 2 colloq. Arrive unexpectedly. Blow a person''s mind slang cause to have hallucinations etc.; astound. Blow off 1 escape or allow (steam etc.) To escape forcibly. 2 slang break wind noisily. Blow out 1 extinguish by blowing. 2 send outwards by an explosion. Blow over (of trouble etc.) Fade away. Blow one''s top colloq. Explode in rage. Blow up 1 explode. 2 colloq. Rebuke strongly. 3 inflate (a tyre etc.). 4 colloq. A enlarge (a photograph). B exaggerate. 5 colloq. Arise, happen. 6 colloq. Lose one''s temper. [old english]'),
(2883, 'b', '\r\nBlow2', ' n. 1 hard stroke with a hand or weapon. 2 sudden shock or misfortune. [origin unknown]'),
(2884, 'b', '\r\nBlow-by-blow', ' attrib. Adj. (of a narrative etc.) Detailed.'),
(2885, 'b', '\r\nBlow-dry', ' â€”v. Arrange (the hair) while drying it. â€”n. Act of doing this.'),
(2886, 'b', '\r\nBlower', ' n. 1 device for blowing. 2 colloq. Telephone.'),
(2887, 'b', '\r\nBlowfly', ' n. Bluebottle.'),
(2888, 'b', '\r\nBlow-hole', ' n. 1 nostril of a whale. 2 hole (esp. In ice) for breathing or fishing through. 3 vent for air, smoke, etc.'),
(2889, 'b', '\r\nBlow-job', ' n. Coarse slang instance of fellatio or cunnilingus.'),
(2890, 'b', '\r\nBlowlamp', ' n. Device with a very hot flame for burning off paint, plumbing, etc.'),
(2891, 'b', '\r\nBlown', ' past part. Of *blow1.'),
(2892, 'b', '\r\nBlow-out', ' n. Colloq. 1 burst tyre. 2 melted fuse. 3 huge meal.'),
(2893, 'b', '\r\nBlowpipe', ' n. 1 tube for blowing air through, esp. To intensify a flame or to blow glass. 2 tube for propelling poisoned darts etc. By blowing.'),
(2894, 'b', '\r\nBlowtorch', ' n. Us = *blowlamp.'),
(2895, 'b', '\r\nBlow-up', ' n. 1 colloq. Enlargement (of a photograph etc.). 2 explosion.'),
(2896, 'b', '\r\nBlowy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) windy.'),
(2897, 'b', '\r\nBlowzy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 coarse-looking; red-faced. 2 slovenly. [obsolete blowze beggar''s wench]'),
(2898, 'b', '\r\nBlub', ' v. (-bb-) slang sob. [shortening of *blubber]'),
(2899, 'b', '\r\nBlubber', ' â€”n. Whale fat. â€”v. 1 sob loudly. 2 sob out (words). â€”adj. Swollen, thick. [probably imitative]'),
(2900, 'b', '\r\nBludgeon', ' â€”n. Heavy club. â€”v. 1 beat with this. 2 coerce. [origin unknown]'),
(2901, 'b', '\r\nBlue', ' â€”adj. (bluer, bluest) 1 having the colour of a clear sky. 2 sad, depressed. 3 pornographic (a blue film). 4 politically conservative. â€”n. 1 blue colour or pigment. 2 blue clothes or material (dressed in blue). 3 person who represents a university in a sport, esp. Oxford or cambridge. 4 conservative party supporter. â€”v. (blues, blued, bluing or blueing) 1 make blue. 2 slang squander.  once in a blue moon very rarely. Out of the blue unexpectedly. [french from germanic]'),
(2902, 'b', '\r\nBlue', 'baby  n. Baby with a blue complexion due to a congenital heart defect.'),
(2903, 'b', '\r\nBluebell', ' n. Woodland plant with bell-shaped blue flowers.'),
