(3374, 'b', '\r\nBreeches', ' n.pl. Short trousers, esp. Fastened below the knee.'),
(3375, 'b', '\r\nBreeches', 'buoy  n. Lifebuoy with canvas breeches for the user''s legs.'),
(3376, 'b', '\r\nBreed', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Bred) 1 (of animals) produce young. 2 propagate; raise (animals). 3 yield; result in. 4 arise; spread. 5 bring up; train. 6 create (fissile material) by nuclear reaction. â€”n. 1 stock of similar animals or plants within a species, usu. Developed by deliberate selection. 2 race; lineage. 3 sort, kind.  breeder n. [old english]'),
(3377, 'b', '\r\nBreeder', 'reactor  n. Nuclear reactor creating surplus fissile material.'),
(3378, 'b', '\r\nBreeding', ' n. 1 raising of offspring; propagation. 2 social behaviour; ancestry.'),
(3379, 'b', '\r\nBreeze1', ' â€”n. 1 gentle wind. 2 colloq. Quarrel. 3 esp. Us colloq. Easy task. â€”v. (-zing) (foll. By in, out, along, etc.) Colloq. Saunter casually. [probably spanish and portuguese briza]'),
