(7035, 'c', '\r\nCajole', ' v. (-ling) persuade by flattery, deceit, etc.  cajolery n. [french]'),
(7036, 'c', '\r\nCake', ' â€”n. 1 mixture of flour, butter, eggs, sugar, etc., baked in the oven and often iced and decorated. 2 other food in a flat round shape (fish cake). 3 flattish compact mass (cake of soap). â€”v. (-king) 1 form into a compact mass. 2 (usu. Foll. By with) cover (with a hard or sticky mass).  have one''s cake and eat it colloq. Enjoy both of two mutually exclusive alternatives. A piece of cake colloq. Something easily achieved. Sell (or go) like hot cakes colloq. Be sold (or go) quickly; be popular. [old norse]'),
(7037, 'c', '\r\nCakewalk', ' n. 1 obsolete american black dance. 2 colloq. Easy task. 3 fairground entertainment consisting of a promenade moved by machinery.'),
(7038, 'c', '\r\nCal', ' abbr. Large calorie(s).'),
(7039, 'c', '\r\nCal', ' abbr. Small calorie(s).'),
