(7530, 'c', '\r\nCastrate', ' v. (-ting) 1 remove the testicles of; geld. 2 deprive of vigour.  castration n. [latin castro]'),
(7531, 'c', '\r\nCastrato', ' n. (pl. -ti) hist. Castrated male soprano or alto singer. [italian: related to *castrate]'),
(7532, 'c', '\r\nCasual', ' â€”adj. 1 accidental; chance. 2 not regular or permanent (casual work). 3 a unconcerned. B careless; unthinking. 4 (of clothes) informal. â€”n. 1 casual worker. 2 (usu. In pl.) Casual clothes or shoes.  casually adv. Casualness n. [french and latin: related to *case1]'),
(7533, 'c', '\r\nCasualty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 person killed or injured in a war or accident. 2 thing lost or destroyed. 3 = *casualty department. 4 accident, mishap. [medieval latin: related to *casual]'),
(7534, 'c', '\r\nCasualty', 'department  n. Part of a hospital where casualties are dealt with.'),
(7535, 'c', '\r\nCasuist', ' n. 1 person who uses clever but false reasoning in matters of conscience etc. 2 sophist, quibbler.  casuistic adj. Casuistry n. [latin: related to *case1]'),
(7536, 'c', '\r\nCat', ' n. 1 small soft-furred four-legged domesticated animal. 2 wild animal of the same family, e.g. Lion, tiger. 3 colloq. Malicious or spiteful woman. 4 = *cat-o''-nine-tails.  the cat''s whiskers colloq. Excellent person or thing. Let the cat out of the bag reveal a secret. Like a cat on hot bricks very agitated. Put (or set) the cat among the pigeons cause trouble. Rain cats and dogs rain hard. [latin cattus]'),
(7537, 'c', '\r\nCata-', ' prefix 1 down. 2 wrongly. [greek]'),
(7538, 'c', '\r\nCatabolism', ' n. Breakdown of complex molecules in living organisms to release energy; destructive metabolism.  catabolic adj. [greek katabole throwing down]'),
(7539, 'c', '\r\nCatachresis', ' n. (pl. -chreses) incorrect use of words.  catachrestic adj. [greek khraomai use]'),
(7540, 'c', '\r\nCataclysm', ' n. 1 a violent upheaval or disaster. B great change. 2 great flood.  cataclysmic adj. [greek kluzo wash]'),
(7541, 'c', '\r\nCatacomb', ' n. (often in pl.) Underground cemetery, esp. Roman. [french from latin]'),
(7542, 'c', '\r\nCatafalque', ' n. Decorated bier, used esp. In state funerals or for lying in state. [french from italian]'),
(7543, 'c', '\r\nCatalan', ' â€”n. Native or language of catalonia in spain. â€”adj. Of catalonia. [french from spanish]'),
(7544, 'c', '\r\nCatalepsy', ' n. Trance or seizure with unconsciousness and rigidity of the body.  cataleptic adj. & n. [greek lepsis seizure]'),
(7545, 'c', '\r\nCatalog', ' (brit. Catalogue) â€”n. 1 complete alphabetical or otherwise ordered list of items, often with a description of each. 2 extensive list (catalog of disasters). â€”v. (-logs, -loged, -loging; brit. -logues, -logued, -loguing) 1 make a catalog of. 2 enter in a catalog. [greek lego choose]'),
(7546, 'c', '\r\nCatalogue', ' (us catalog) â€”n. 1 complete alphabetical or otherwise ordered list of items, often with a description of each. 2 extensive list (catalogue of disasters). â€”v. (-logues, -logued, -loguing; us -logs, -loged, -loging) 1 make a catalogue of. 2 enter in a catalogue. [greek lego choose]'),
(7547, 'c', '\r\nCatalpa', ' n. Tree with long pods and showy flowers. [n. American indian]'),
(7548, 'c', '\r\nCatalyse', ' v. (us -yze) (-sing or -zing) produce (a reaction) by catalysis.'),
(7549, 'c', '\r\nCatalysis', ' n. (pl. -lyses) acceleration of a chemical reaction by a catalyst. [greek luo set free]'),
(7550, 'c', '\r\nCatalyst', ' n. 1 substance that does not itself change, but speeds up a chemical reaction. 2 person or thing that precipitates change.'),
(7551, 'c', '\r\nCatalytic', ' adj. Of or involving catalysis.'),
(7552, 'c', '\r\nCatalytic', 'converter  n. Device incorporated in a vehicle''s exhaust system, with a catalyst for converting pollutant gases into harmless products.'),
(7553, 'c', '\r\nCatalyze', ' v. (brit. -yse) produce (a reaction) by catalysis.'),
(7554, 'c', '\r\nCatamaran', ' n. 1 boat with parallel twin hulls. 2 raft of yoked logs or boats. [tamil]'),
(7555, 'c', '\r\nCatamite', ' n. Passive partner (esp. A boy) in homosexual practices. [latin, = ganymede]'),
(7556, 'c', '\r\nCat-and-dog', ' adj. (of a relationship etc.) Quarrelsome.'),
(7557, 'c', '\r\nCatapult', ' â€”n. 1 forked stick etc. With elastic for shooting stones. 2 mil hist. Machine for hurling large stones etc. 3 device for launching a glider etc. â€”v. 1 a hurl from or launch with a catapult. B fling forcibly. 2 leap or be hurled forcibly. [latin from greek]'),
(7558, 'c', '\r\nCataract', ' n. 1 a large waterfall. B downpour; rush of water. 2 eye condition in which the lens becomes progressively opaque. [greek katarrhaktes, = down-rushing]'),
(7559, 'c', '\r\nCatarrh', ' n. 1 inflammation of the mucous membrane of the nose, air-passages, etc. 2 mucus caused by this.  catarrhal adj. [greek rheo flow]'),
(7560, 'c', '\r\nCatastrophe', ' n. 1 great and usu. Sudden disaster. 2 denouement of a drama.  catastrophic adj. Catastrophically adv. [greek strepho turn]'),
(7561, 'c', '\r\nCatatonia', ' n. 1 schizophrenia with intervals of catalepsy and sometimes violence. 2 catalepsy.  catatonic adj. & n. [greek: related to *cata-, *tone]'),
(7562, 'c', '\r\nCat', 'burglar  n. Burglar who enters by climbing to an upper storey.'),
(7563, 'c', '\r\nCatcall', ' â€”n. Shrill whistle of disapproval. â€”v. Make a catcall.'),
(7564, 'c', '\r\nCatch', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Caught) 1 capture in a trap, one''s hands, etc. 2 detect or surprise (esp. A guilty person). 3 a intercept and hold (a moving thing) in the hands etc. B cricket dismiss (a batsman) by catching the ball before it reaches the ground. 4 a contract (a disease) from an infected person. B acquire (a quality etc.) From another. 5 a reach in time and board (a train, bus, etc.). B be in time to see etc. (a person or thing about to leave or finish). 6 apprehend with the senses or mind (esp. A thing occurring quickly or briefly). 7 (of an artist etc.) Reproduce faithfully. 8 a (cause to) become fixed, entangled, or checked. B (often foll. By on) hit, deal a blow to (caught his elbow on the table). 9 draw the attention of; captivate (caught his eye; caught her fancy). 10 begin to burn. 11 reach or overtake (a person etc. Ahead). 12 (foll. By at) try to grasp. â€”n. 1 a act of catching. B cricket etc. Chance or act of catching the ball. 2 a amount of a thing caught, esp. Of fish. B thing or person caught or worth catching, esp. In marriage. 3 a question, trick, etc., intended to deceive, incriminate, etc. B unexpected or hidden difficulty or disadvantage. 4 device for fastening a door or window etc. 5 mus. Round, esp. With words arranged to produce a humorous effect.  catch fire see *fire. Catch hold of grasp, seize. Catch it slang be punished. Catch on colloq. 1 become popular. 2 understand what is meant. Catch out 1 detect in a mistake etc. 2 take unawares. 3 = sense 3b of v. Catch up 1 a (often foll. By with) reach a person etc. Ahead (caught us up; caught up with us). B (often foll. By with, on) make up arrears. 2 pick up hurriedly. 3 (often in passive) a involve; entangle (caught up in crime). B fasten up (hair caught up in a ribbon). [latin capto try to catch]'),
(7565, 'c', '\r\nCatch-all', ' n. (often attrib.) Thing designed to be all-inclusive.'),
(7566, 'c', '\r\nCatch-as-catch-can', ' n. Wrestling with few holds barred.'),
(7567, 'c', '\r\nCatching', ' adj. (of a disease, practice, etc.) Infectious.'),
(7568, 'c', '\r\nCatchline', ' n. Short line of type, esp. At the head of copy or as a running headline.'),
(7569, 'c', '\r\nCatchment', ' n. Collection of rainfall.'),
(7570, 'c', '\r\nCatchment', 'area  n. 1 area served by a school, hospital, etc. 2 area from which rainfall flows into a river etc.'),
(7571, 'c', '\r\nCatchpenny', ' attrib. Adj. Intended merely to sell quickly; superficially attractive.'),
(7572, 'c', '\r\nCatch-phrase', ' n. Phrase in frequent use.'),
(7573, 'c', '\r\nCatch-22', ' n. (often attrib.) Colloq. Unresolvable situation containing conflicting or mutually dependent conditions.'),
(7574, 'c', '\r\nCatchweight', ' â€”adj. Unrestricted as regards weight. â€”n. Unrestricted weight category in sports.'),
(7575, 'c', '\r\nCatchword', ' n. 1 phrase, word, or slogan in frequent current use. 2 word so placed as to draw attention.'),
(7576, 'c', '\r\nCatchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (of a tune) easy to remember, attractive.'),
(7577, 'c', '\r\nCat', 'door  var. Of *cat flap.'),
(7578, 'c', '\r\nCatechism', ' n. 1 a principles of a religion in the form of questions and answers. B book containing this. 2 series of questions. [church latin: related to *catechize]'),
(7579, 'c', '\r\nCatechist', ' n. Religious teacher, esp. One using a catechism.'),
(7580, 'c', '\r\nCatechize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) instruct by using a catechism. [greek katekheo cause to hear]'),
(7581, 'c', '\r\nCatechumen', ' n. Christian convert under instruction before baptism. [church latin catechumenus]'),
(7582, 'c', '\r\nCategorical', ' adj. Unconditional, absolute; explicit.  categorically adv. [related to *category]'),
(7583, 'c', '\r\nCategorize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) place in a category.  categorization n.'),
(7584, 'c', '\r\nCategory', ' n. (pl. -ies) class or division (of things, ideas, etc.). [greek, = statement]'),
(7585, 'c', '\r\nCater', ' v. 1 supply food. 2 (foll. By for) provide what is needed or desired (caters for all tastes). 3 (foll. By to) pander to (esp. Low tastes). [anglo-french acatour buyer, from latin capto: related to *catch]'),
(7586, 'c', '\r\nCaterer', ' n. Professional supplier of food for social events.'),
(7587, 'c', '\r\nCaterpillar', ' n. 1 larva of a butterfly or moth. 2 (caterpillar) a (in full caterpillar track or tread) propr. Steel band passing round the wheels of a tractor etc. For travel on rough ground. B vehicle with these. [anglo-french, = hairy cat]'),
(7588, 'c', '\r\nCaterwaul', ' â€”v. Make the shrill howl of a cat. â€”n. This noise. [from *cat, -waul imitative]'),
(7589, 'c', '\r\nCatfish', ' n. (pl. Same) freshwater fish with whisker-like barbels round the mouth.'),
(7590, 'c', '\r\nCat', 'flap  n. (also cat door) small swinging flap in an outer door, for a cat to pass in and out.'),
(7591, 'c', '\r\nCatgut', ' n. Material used for the strings of musical instruments and surgical sutures, made of intestines of the sheep, horse, etc. (but not cat).');
