(7831, 'c', '\r\nChain-smoke', ' v. Smoke continually, esp. By lighting the next cigarette etc. From the previous one.  chain-smoker n.'),
(7832, 'c', '\r\nChain', 'store  n. One of a series of similar shops owned by one firm.'),
(7833, 'c', '\r\nChair', ' â€”n. 1 seat for one person usu. With a back. 2 professorship. 3 a chairperson. B seat or office of a chairperson. 4 us = *electric chair. â€”v. 1 preside over (a meeting). 2 carry (a person) aloft in triumph.  take the chair preside over a meeting. [greek kathedra]'),
(7834, 'c', '\r\nChair-lift', ' n. Series of chairs on a looped cable, for carrying passengers up and down a mountain etc.'),
(7835, 'c', '\r\nChairman', ' n. (fem. Also chairwoman) 1 person chosen to preside over a meeting. 2 permanent president of a committee, board of directors, etc.'),
(7836, 'c', '\r\nChairperson', ' n. Chairman or chairwoman.'),
(7837, 'c', '\r\nChaise', ' n. Esp. Hist. Horse-drawn usu. Open carriage for one or two persons. [french]'),
(7838, 'c', '\r\nChaise', 'longue  n. (pl. Chaise longues or chaises longues pronunc. Same) sofa with only one arm rest. [french, = long chair]'),
(7839, 'c', '\r\nChalcedony', ' n. (pl. -ies) type of quartz with many varieties, e.g. Onyx. [latin from greek]'),
(7840, 'c', '\r\nChalet', ' n. 1 swiss mountain hut or cottage with overhanging eaves. 2 house in a similar style. 3 small cabin in a holiday camp etc. [swiss french]'),
(7841, 'c', '\r\nChalice', ' n. 1 goblet. 2 eucharistic cup. [latin *calix]'),
(7842, 'c', '\r\nChalk', ' â€”n. 1 white soft limestone. 2 a similar substance, sometimes coloured, for writing or drawing. B piece of this. â€”v. 1 rub, mark, draw, or write with chalk. 2 (foll. By up) a write or record with chalk. B register or gain (success etc.).  by a long chalk by far.  chalky adj. (-ier, -iest). Chalkiness n. [latin *calx]'),
(7843, 'c', '\r\nChallenge', ' â€”n. 1 summons to take part in a contest etc. Or to prove or justify something. 2 demanding or difficult task. 3 objection made to a jury member. 4 call to respond. â€”v. (-ging) 1 issue a challenge to. 2 dispute, deny. 3 (as challenging adj.) Stimulatingly difficult. 4 object to (a jury member, evidence, etc.).  challenger n. [latin calumnia calumny]'),
(7844, 'c', '\r\nChalybeate', ' adj. (of water etc.) Impregnated with iron salts. [latin chalybs steel, from greek]'),
(7845, 'c', '\r\nChamber', ' n. 1 a hall used by a legislative or judicial body. B body that meets in it, esp. Any of the houses of a parliament. 2 (in pl.) A rooms used by a barrister or barristers, esp. In inns of court. B judge''s room for hearing cases not needing to be taken in court. 3 archaic room, esp. A bedroom. 4 mus. (attrib.) Of or for a small group of instruments. 5 cavity or compartment in the body, machinery, etc. (esp. The part of a gun-bore that contains the charge). [greek kamara vault]'),
(7846, 'c', '\r\nChamberlain', ' n. 1 officer managing a royal or noble household. 2 treasurer of a corporation etc. [germanic: related to *chamber]'),
(7847, 'c', '\r\nChambermaid', ' n. Woman who cleans hotel bedrooms.'),
(7848, 'c', '\r\nChamber', 'of commerce  n. Association to promote local commercial interests.'),
(7849, 'c', '\r\nChamber-pot', ' n. Receptacle for urine etc., used in the bedroom.'),
(7850, 'c', '\r\nChameleon', ' n. 1 small lizard able to change colour for camouflage. 2 variable or inconstant person. [greek, = ground-lion]'),
(7851, 'c', '\r\nChamfer', ' â€”v. Bevel symmetrically (a right-angled edge or corner). â€”n. Bevelled surface at an edge or corner. [french chant edge, fraint broken]'),
(7852, 'c', '\r\nChamois', ' n. (pl. Same) 1  agile european and asian mountain antelope. 2  (in full chamois leather) a soft leather from sheep, goats, deer, etc. B piece of this. [french]'),
(7853, 'c', '\r\nChamomile', ' var. Of *camomile.'),
(7854, 'c', '\r\nChamp1', ' â€”v. Munch or chew noisily. â€”n. Chewing noise.  champ at the bit be restlessly impatient. [imitative]'),
(7855, 'c', '\r\nChamp2', ' n. Slang champion. [abbreviation]'),
(7856, 'c', '\r\nChampagne', ' n. 1 a white sparkling wine from champagne. B similar wine from elsewhere. 2 pale cream colour. [champagne, former province in e. France]'),
(7857, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of this word in sense 1b is, strictly speaking, incorrect.'),
(7858, 'c', '\r\nChampers', ' n. Slang champagne.'),
(7859, 'c', '\r\nChampion', ' â€”n. 1 (often attrib.) Person or thing that has defeated or surpassed all rivals. 2 person who fights or argues for a cause or another person. â€”v. Support the cause of, defend. â€”adj. Colloq. Splendid. â€”adv. Colloq. Splendidly. [medieval latin campio fighter]'),
(7860, 'c', '\r\nChampionship', ' n. 1 (often in pl.) Contest to decide the champion in a sport etc. 2 position of champion.'),
(7861, 'c', '\r\nChance', ' â€”n. 1 possibility. 2 (often in pl.) Probability. 3 unplanned occurrence. 4 opportunity. 5 fortune; luck. 6 (often chance) course of events regarded as a power; fate. â€”attrib. Adj. Fortuitous, accidental. â€”v. (-cing) 1 colloq. Risk. 2 happen (i chanced to find it).  by any chance perhaps. By chance fortuitously. Chance one''s arm try though unlikely to succeed. Chance on (or upon) happen to find, meet, etc. Game of chance one decided by luck, not skill. On the off chance just in case (the unlikely occurs). Stand a chance have a prospect of success etc. Take a chance (or chances) risk failure; behave riskily. Take a (or one''s) chance on (or with) risk the consequences of. [latin cado fall]'),
(7862, 'c', '\r\nChancel', ' n. Part of a church near the altar. [latin cancelli grating]'),
(7863, 'c', '\r\nChancellery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 chancellor''s department, staff, or residence. 2 us office attached to an embassy or consulate.'),
(7864, 'c', '\r\nChancellor', ' n. 1 state or legal official. 2 head of government in some european countries. 3 non-resident honorary head of a university. [latin cancellarius secretary]'),
(7865, 'c', '\r\nChancellor', 'of the exchequer  n. Uk finance minister.'),
(7866, 'c', '\r\nChancery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 (chancery) lord chancellor''s division of the high court of justice. 2 records office. 3 chancellery. [contraction of *chancellery]'),
(7867, 'c', '\r\nChancy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) uncertain; risky.  chancily adv.'),
(7868, 'c', '\r\nChandelier', ' n. Ornamental branched hanging support for lighting. [french: related to *candle]'),
(7869, 'c', '\r\nChandler', ' n. Dealer in candles, oil, soap, paint, etc. [french: related to *candle]'),
(7870, 'c', '\r\nChange', ' â€”n. 1 a making or becoming different. B alteration or modification. 2 a money exchanged for money in larger units or a different currency. B money returned as the balance of that given in payment. 3 new experience; variety (need a change). 4 substitution of one thing for another (change of scene). 5 (in full change of life) colloq. Menopause. 6 (usu. In pl.) One of the different orders in which bells can be rung. â€”v. (-ging) 1 undergo, show, or subject to change; make or become different. 2 a take or use another instead of; go from one to another (change one''s socks; changed trains). B (usu. Foll. By for) give up or get rid of in exchange (changed the car for a van). 3 give or get money in exchange for. 4 put fresh clothes or coverings on. 5 (often foll. By with) give and receive, exchange. 6 change trains etc. 7 (of the moon) arrive at a fresh phase.  change down engage a lower gear. Change gear engage a different gear. Change hands 1 pass to a different owner. 2 substitute one hand for the other. Change one''s mind adopt a different opinion or plan. Change over change from one system or situation to another.'),
(7871, 'c', '\r\nChangeable', ' adj. 1 inconstant. 2 that can change or be changed.'),
(7872, 'c', '\r\nChangeling', ' n. Child believed to be substituted for another.'),
(7873, 'c', '\r\nChange', 'of clothes  n. Second outfit in reserve.'),
(7874, 'c', '\r\nChange', 'of heart  n. Conversion to a different view.'),
(7875, 'c', '\r\nChange-over', ' n. Change from one system to another.'),
(7876, 'c', '\r\nChannel', ' â€”n. 1 a piece of water wider than a strait, joining esp. Two seas. B (the channel) the english channel. 2 medium of communication; agency. 3 band of frequencies used in radio and television transmission, esp. By a particular station. 4 course in which anything moves. 5 a hollow bed of water. B navigable part of a waterway. 6 passage for liquid. 7 lengthwise strip on recording tape etc. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 guide, direct. 2 form channel(s) in. [latin: related to *canal]'),
(7877, 'c', '\r\nChant', ' â€”n. 1 spoken singsong phrase. 2 a simple tune used for singing unmetrical words, e.g. Psalms. B song, esp. Monotonous or repetitive. â€”v. 1 talk or repeat monotonously. 2 sing or intone (a psalm etc.). [latin canto from cano sing]'),
(7878, 'c', '\r\nChanter', ' n. Melody-pipe of bagpipes.'),
(7879, 'c', '\r\nChanticleer', ' n. Name given to a domestic cock in stories. [french: related to *chant, *clear]'),
(7880, 'c', '\r\nChantry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 endowment for the singing of masses. 2 priests, chapel, etc., so endowed. [french: related to *chant]'),
(7881, 'c', '\r\nChaos', ' n. 1 utter confusion. 2 formless matter supposed to have existed before the creation of the universe.  chaotic adj. Chaotically adv. [latin from greek]'),
(7882, 'c', '\r\nChap1', ' n. Colloq. Man, boy, fellow. [abbreviation of *chapman]'),
(7883, 'c', '\r\nChap2', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (esp. Of the skin) develop cracks or soreness. 2 (of the wind, cold, etc.) Cause to chap. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Crack in the skin etc. [origin uncertain]'),
(7884, 'c', '\r\nChaparral', ' n. Us dense tangled brushwood. [spanish]'),
(7885, 'c', '\r\nChapatti', ' n. (also chapati, chupatty) (pl. Chapat(t)is or chupatties) flat thin cake of unleavened bread. [hindi]'),
(7886, 'c', '\r\nChapel', ' n. 1 a place for private christian worship in a cathedral or large church, with its own altar. B this attached to a private house etc. 2 a place of worship for nonconformists. B chapel service.  3 members or branch of a printers'' trade union at a place of work. [medieval latin cappa cloak: the first chapel was a sanctuary in which st martin''s cloak (cappella) was preserved]'),
(7887, 'c', '\r\nChaperon', ' â€”n. Person, esp. An older woman, ensuring propriety by accompanying a young unmarried woman on social occasions. â€”v. Act as chaperon to.  chaperonage n. [french from chape cope: related to *cape1]'),
(7888, 'c', '\r\nChaplain', ' n. Member of the clergy attached to a private chapel, institution, ship, regiment, etc.  chaplaincy n. (pl. -ies). [latin: related to *chapel]'),
(7889, 'c', '\r\nChaplet', ' n. 1 garland or circlet for the head. 2 short string of beads; rosary. [latin: related to *cap]'),
(7890, 'c', '\r\nChapman', ' n. Hist. Pedlar. [old english: related to *cheap, *man]'),
(7891, 'c', '\r\nChappie', ' n. Colloq. = *chap1.'),
(7892, 'c', '\r\nChapter', ' n. 1 main division of a book. 2 period of time (in a person''s life etc.). 3 a canons of a cathedral or members of a religious community. B meeting of these. [latin diminutive of caput head]'),
(7893, 'c', '\r\nChapter', 'and verse  n. Exact reference or details.'),
(7894, 'c', '\r\nChapter', 'of accidents  n. Series of misfortunes.'),
(7895, 'c', '\r\nChar1', ' v. (-rr-) 1 make or become black by burning; scorch. 2 burn to charcoal. [from *charcoal]'),
(7896, 'c', '\r\nChar2', ' colloq. â€”n. = *charwoman. â€”v. (-rr-) work as a charwoman. [old english, = turn]'),
(7897, 'c', '\r\nChar3', ' n. Slang tea. [chinese cha]'),
(7898, 'c', '\r\nChar4', ' n. (pl. Same) a kind of small trout. [origin unknown]'),
(7899, 'c', '\r\nCharabanc', ' n. Hist. Early form of motor coach. [french char Ã  bancs seated carriage]'),
(7900, 'c', '\r\nCharacter', ' n. 1 collective qualities or characteristics that distinguish a person or thing. 2 a moral strength. B reputation, esp. Good reputation. 3 a person in a novel, play, etc. B part played by an actor; role. 4 colloq. Person, esp. An eccentric one. 5 printed or written letter, symbol, etc. 6 written description of a person''s qualities. 7 characteristic (esp. Of a biological species).  in (or out of) character consistent (or inconsistent) with a person''s character.  characterless adj. [greek kharakter]'),
(7901, 'c', '\r\nCharacteristic', ' â€”adj. Typical, distinctive. â€”n. Characteristic feature or quality.  characteristically adv.'),
(7902, 'c', '\r\nCharacterize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 a describe the character of. B (foll. By as) describe as. 2 be characteristic of. 3 impart character to.  characterization n.'),
(7903, 'c', '\r\nCharade', ' n. 1 (usu. In pl., treated as sing.) Game of guessing a word from acted clues. 2 absurd pretence. [provenÃ§al charra chatter]'),
(7904, 'c', '\r\nCharcoal', ' n. 1 a form of carbon consisting of black residue from partially burnt wood etc. B piece of this for drawing. C a drawing in charcoal. 2 (in full charcoal grey) dark grey. [origin unknown]'),
(7905, 'c', '\r\nCharge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 a ask (an amount) as a price. B ask (a person) for an amount as a price. 2 a (foll. By to, up to) debit the cost of to (a person or account). B debit (a person or account). 3 a (often foll. By with) accuse (of an offence). B (foll. By that + clause) make an accusation that. 4 (foll. By to + infin.) Instruct or urge. 5 (foll. By with) entrust with. 6 make a rushing attack (on). 7 (often foll. By up) a give an electric charge to. B store energy in (a battery). 8 (often foll. By with) load or fill (a vessel, gun, etc.) To the full or proper extent. 9 (usu. As charged adj.) A (foll. By with) saturated with. B (usu. Foll. By with) pervaded (with strong feelings etc.). â€”n. 1 a price asked for services or goods. B financial liability or commitment. 2 accusation. 3 a task, duty, commission. B care, custody. C person or thing entrusted. 4 a impetuous rush or attack, esp. In battle. B signal for this. 5 appropriate amount of material to be put into a receptacle, mechanism, etc. At one time, esp. Of explosive for a gun. 6 a property of matter causing electrical phenomena. B quantity of this carried by the body. C energy stored chemically for conversion into electricity. 7 exhortation; directions, orders. 8 heraldic device or bearing.  in charge having command. Take charge (often foll. By of) assume control.  chargeable adj. [latin carrus *car]'),
(7906, 'c', '\r\nCharge-capping', '  n. Imposition of an upper limit on the community charge leviable by a local authority.'),
(7907, 'c', '\r\nCharge', 'card  n. = *credit card.'),
(7908, 'c', '\r\nChargÃ©', 'd''affaires  n. (pl. ChargÃ©s pronunc. Same) 1 ambassador''s deputy. 2 envoy to a minor country. [french]'),
(7909, 'c', '\r\nCharger', ' n. 1 cavalry horse. 2 apparatus for charging a battery.'),
(7910, 'c', '\r\nChariot', ' n. Hist. Two-wheeled vehicle drawn by horses, used in ancient warfare and racing. [french: related to *car]'),
(7911, 'c', '\r\nCharioteer', ' n. Chariot-driver.'),
(7912, 'c', '\r\nCharisma', ' n. 1 power to inspire or attract others; exceptional charm. 2 divinely conferred power or talent.  charismatic adj. [greek kharis grace]'),
(7913, 'c', '\r\nCharitable', ' adj. 1 generous in giving to those in need. 2 of or relating to a charity or charities. 3 generous in judging others.  charitably adv.'),
(7914, 'c', '\r\nCharity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 giving voluntarily to those in need. 2 organization set up to help those in need or for the common good. 3 a kindness, benevolence. B tolerance in judging others. C love of fellow men. [latin caritas from carus dear]'),
(7915, 'c', '\r\nCharlady', ' n. = *charwoman.'),
(7916, 'c', '\r\nCharlatan', ' n. Person falsely claiming knowledge or skill.  charlatanism n. [italian, = babbler]'),
(7917, 'c', '\r\nCharleston', ' n. (also charleston) lively dance of the 1920s with side-kicks from the knee. [charleston in s. Carolina]'),
(7918, 'c', '\r\nCharlotte', ' n. Pudding of stewed fruit covered with bread etc. [french]'),
(7919, 'c', '\r\nCharm', ' â€”n. 1 power or quality of delighting, arousing admiration, or influencing; fascination, attractiveness. 2 trinket on a bracelet etc. 3 object, act, or word(s) supposedly having magic power. â€”v. 1 delight, captivate. 2 influence or protect as if by magic (a charmed life). 3 obtain or gain by charm (charmed his way into the bbc).  charmer n. [latin carmen song]'),
(7920, 'c', '\r\nCharming', ' adj. Delightful.  charmingly adv.'),
(7921, 'c', '\r\nCharnel-house', ' n. Repository of corpses or bones. [latin: related to *carnal]'),
(7922, 'c', '\r\nChart', ' â€”n. 1 geographical map or plan, esp. For navigation. 2 sheet of information in the form of a table, graph, or diagram. 3 (usu. In pl.) Colloq. Listing of the currently best-selling pop records. â€”v. Make a chart of, map. [latin charta: related to *card1]'),
(7923, 'c', '\r\nCharter', ' â€”n. 1 a document granting rights, issued esp. By a sovereign or legislature. B written constitution or description of an organization''s functions etc. 2 contract to hire an aircraft, ship, etc., for a special purpose. â€”v. 1 grant a charter to. 2 hire (an aircraft, ship, etc.). [latin chartula: related to *chart]'),
(7924, 'c', '\r\nChartered', ' attrib. Adj. (of an accountant, engineer, librarian, etc.) Qualified member of a professional body that has a royal charter.'),
(7925, 'c', '\r\nCharter', 'flight  n. Flight by chartered aircraft.'),
(7926, 'c', '\r\nChartism', ' n. Hist. Uk parliamentary reform movement of 1837â€“48.  chartist n. [from *charter: name taken from â€˜people''s charterâ€™]'),
(7927, 'c', '\r\nChartreuse', ' n. Pale green or yellow brandy-based liqueur. [chartreuse, monastery in s. France]'),
(7928, 'c', '\r\nCharwoman', ' n. Woman employed as a cleaner in a house.'),
(7929, 'c', '\r\nChary', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 cautious, wary. 2 sparing; ungenerous. [old english: related to *care]'),
(7930, 'c', '\r\nCharybdis', ' see *scylla and charybdis.'),
(7931, 'c', '\r\nChase1', ' â€”v. (-sing) 1 run after; pursue. 2 (foll. By from, out of, to, etc.) Force to run away or flee. 3 a (foll. By after) hurry in pursuit of. B (foll. By round etc.) Colloq. Act or move about hurriedly. 4 (usu. Foll. By up) colloq. Pursue (a thing overdue). 5 colloq. A try to attain. B court persistently. â€”n. 1 pursuit. 2 unenclosed hunting-land. 3 (prec. By the) hunting, esp. As a sport. [latin capto: related to *catch]'),
(7932, 'c', '\r\nChase2', ' v. (-sing) emboss or engrave (metal). [french: related to *case2]'),
(7933, 'c', '\r\nChaser', ' n. 1 horse for steeplechasing. 2 colloq. Drink taken after another of a different kind.'),
(7934, 'c', '\r\nChasm', ' n. 1 deep cleft or opening in the earth, rock, etc. 2 wide difference of feeling, interests, etc. [latin from greek]'),
(7935, 'c', '\r\nChassis', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 base-frame of a motor vehicle, carriage, etc. 2 frame to carry radio etc. Components. [latin: related to *case2]'),
(7936, 'c', '\r\nChaste', ' adj. 1 abstaining from extramarital, or from all, sexual intercourse. 2 pure, virtuous. 3 simple, unadorned.  chastely adv. Chasteness n. [latin castus]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(7937, 'c', '\r\nChasten', ' v. 1 (esp. As chastening, chastened adjs.) Subdue, restrain. 2 discipline, punish.'),
(7938, 'c', '\r\nChastise', ' v. (-sing) 1 rebuke severely. 2 punish, esp. By beating.  chastisement n.'),
(7939, 'c', '\r\nChastity', ' n. Being chaste.'),
(7940, 'c', '\r\nChasuble', ' n. Loose sleeveless usu. Ornate outer vestment worn by a celebrant at mass or the eucharist. [latin casubla]'),
(7941, 'c', '\r\nChat', ' â€”v. (-tt-) talk in a light familiar way. â€”n. 1 pleasant informal talk. 2 any of various songbirds.  chat up colloq. Chat to, esp. Flirtatiously or with an ulterior motive. [shortening of *chatter]'),
(7942, 'c', '\r\nChÃ¢teau', ' n. (pl. -x) large french country house or castle. [french: related to *castle]'),
(7943, 'c', '\r\nChatelaine', ' n. 1 mistress of a large house. 2 hist. Set of short chains attached to a woman''s belt, for carrying keys etc. [medieval latin castellanus: related to *castle]'),
(7944, 'c', '\r\nChatline', ' n. Telephone service which sets up a conference call among youngsters.'),
(7945, 'c', '\r\nChat', 'show  n. Television or radio broadcast in which celebrities are interviewed informally.'),
(7946, 'c', '\r\nChattel', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Movable possession. [french: related to *cattle]'),
(7947, 'c', '\r\nChatter', ' â€”v. 1 talk quickly, incessantly, trivially, or indiscreetly. 2 (of a bird, monkey, etc.) Emit short quick sounds. 3 (of teeth) click repeatedly together. â€”n. Chattering talk or sounds. [imitative]'),
(7948, 'c', '\r\nChatterbox', ' n. Talkative person.'),
(7949, 'c', '\r\nChatty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 fond of chatting. 2 resembling chat.  chattily adv. Chattiness n.'),
(7950, 'c', '\r\nChauffeur', ' â€”n. (fem. Chauffeuse) person employed to drive a car. â€”v. Drive (a car or person) as a chauffeur. [french, = stoker]'),
(7951, 'c', '\r\nChauvinism', ' n. 1 exaggerated or aggressive patriotism. 2 excessive or prejudiced support or loyalty for one''s cause or group. [chauvin, name of a character in a french play 1831]'),
(7952, 'c', '\r\nChauvinist', ' n. 1 person exhibiting chauvinism. 2 (in full male chauvinist) man who shows prejudice against women.  chauvinistic adj. Chauvinistically adv.'),
(7953, 'c', '\r\nCheap', ' â€”adj. 1 low in price; worth more than its cost. 2 charging low prices; offering good value. 3 of poor quality; inferior. 4 costing little effort and hence of little worth. â€”adv. Cheaply.  on the cheap cheaply.  cheaply adv. Cheapness n. [old english, = price, bargain]'),
(7954, 'c', '\r\nCheapen', ' v. Make or become cheap; depreciate, degrade.'),
(7955, 'c', '\r\nCheapjack', ' â€”n. Seller of inferior goods at low prices. â€”adj. Inferior, shoddy.'),
(7956, 'c', '\r\nCheapskate', ' n. Esp. Us colloq. Stingy person.'),
(7957, 'c', '\r\nCheat', ' â€”v. 1 a (often foll. By into, out of) deceive or trick. B (foll. By of) deprive of. 2 gain an unfair advantage by deception or breaking rules. â€”n. 1 person who cheats. 2 trick, deception.  cheat on colloq. Be sexually unfaithful to. [from *escheat]'),
(7958, 'c', '\r\nCheck', ' â€”v. 1 a examine the accuracy or quality of. B make sure, verify. 2 a stop or slow the motion of; curb. B colloq. Rebuke. 3 chess directly threaten (the opposing king). 4 us agree on comparison. 5 us mark with a tick etc. 6 us deposit (luggage etc.). â€”n. 1 means or act of testing or ensuring accuracy, quality, etc. 2 a stopping or slowing of motion. B rebuff or rebuke. C person or thing that restrains. 3 a pattern of small squares. B fabric so patterned. C (attrib.) So patterned. 4 (also as int.) Chess exposure of a king to direct attack. 5 us restaurant bill. 6 us = *cheque. 7 esp. Us token of identification for left luggage etc. 8 us cards counter used in games. 9 temporary loss of the scent in hunting.  check in 1 arrive or register at a hotel, airport, etc. 2 record the arrival of. Check into register one''s arrival at (a hotel etc.). Check off mark on a list etc. As having been examined. Check on examine, verify, keep watch on. Check out 1 (often foll. By of) leave a hotel etc. With due formalities. 2 esp. Us investigate. Check up make sure, verify. Check up on = check on. [persian, = king]'),
(7959, 'c', '\r\nChecked', ' adj. Having a check pattern.'),
(7960, 'c', '\r\nChecker1', ' n. Person etc. That examines, esp. In a factory etc.'),
(7961, 'c', '\r\nChecker2', ' n. 1 var. Of *chequer. 2 us a (in pl., usu. Treated as sing.) Draughts. B piece used in this game.'),
(7962, 'c', '\r\nCheck-in', ' n. Act or place of checking in.'),
(7963, 'c', '\r\nCheckmate', ' â€”n. (also as int.) Chess check from which a king cannot escape. â€”v. (-ting) 1 chess put into checkmate. 2 frustrate. [french: related to *check, persian mat is dead]'),
(7964, 'c', '\r\nCheckout', ' n. 1 act of checking out. 2 pay-desk in a supermarket etc.'),
(7965, 'c', '\r\nCheckpoint', ' n. Place, esp. A barrier or entrance, where documents, vehicles, etc., are inspected.'),
(7966, 'c', '\r\nCheck-up', ' n. Thorough (esp. Medical) examination.'),
(7967, 'c', '\r\nCheddar', ' n. A kind of firm smooth cheese. [cheddar in somerset]'),
(7968, 'c', '\r\nCheek', ' â€”n. 1 a side of the face below the eye. B side-wall of the mouth. 2 a impertinence; cool confidence. B impertinent speech. 3 slang buttock. â€”v. Be impertinent to.  cheek by jowl close together; intimate. [old english]'),
(7969, 'c', '\r\nCheek-bone', ' n. Bone below the eye.'),
(7970, 'c', '\r\nCheeky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) impertinent.  cheekily adv. Cheekiness n.'),
(7971, 'c', '\r\nCheep', ' â€”n. Weak shrill cry of a young bird. â€”v. Make such a cry. [imitative]'),
(7972, 'c', '\r\nCheer', ' â€”n. 1 shout of encouragement or applause. 2 mood, disposition (full of good cheer). 3 (in pl.; as int.) Colloq. A expressing good wishes on parting or before drinking. B expressing gratitude. â€”v. 1 a applaud with shouts. B (usu. Foll. By on) urge with shouts. 2 shout for joy. 3 gladden; comfort.  cheer up make or become less depressed. [latin cara face, from greek]'),
(7973, 'c', '\r\nCheerful', ' adj. 1 in good spirits, noticeably happy. 2 bright, pleasant.  cheerfully adv. Cheerfulness n.'),
(7974, 'c', '\r\nCheerio', ' int. Colloq. Expressing good wishes on parting.'),
(7975, 'c', '\r\nCheer-leader', ' n. Person who leads cheers of applause etc.'),
(7976, 'c', '\r\nCheerless', ' adj. Gloomy, dreary.'),
(7977, 'c', '\r\nCheery', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) cheerful.  cheerily adv. Cheeriness n.'),
(7978, 'c', '\r\nCheese', ' n. 1 a food made from curds of milk. B cake of this with rind. 2 conserve with the consistency of soft cheese.  cheesy adj. [latin caseus]'),
(7979, 'c', '\r\nCheeseburger', ' n. Hamburger with cheese in or on it.'),
(7980, 'c', '\r\nCheesecake', ' n. 1 tart filled with sweetened curds etc. 2 slang portrayal of women in a sexually stimulating manner.'),
(7981, 'c', '\r\nCheesecloth', ' n. Thin loosely-woven cloth.'),
(7982, 'c', '\r\nCheesed', ' adj. Slang (often foll. By off) bored, fed up. [origin unknown]'),
(7983, 'c', '\r\nCheese-paring', ' adj. Stingy.'),
(7984, 'c', '\r\nCheese', 'plant  n. Climbing plant with holes in its leaves.'),
(7985, 'c', '\r\nCheetah', ' n. Swift-running spotted leopard-like feline. [hindi]'),
(7986, 'c', '\r\nChef', ' n. (usu. Male) cook, esp. The chief cook in a restaurant. [french]'),
(7987, 'c', '\r\nChelsea', 'bun  n. Currant bun in the form of a flat spiral. [chelsea in london]'),
(7988, 'c', '\r\nChelsea', 'pensioner  n. Inmate of the chelsea royal hospital for old or disabled soldiers.'),
(7989, 'c', '\r\nChemical', ' â€”adj. Of, made by, or employing chemistry or chemicals. â€”n. Substance obtained or used in chemistry.  chemically adv. [french or medieval latin: related to *alchemy]'),
(7990, 'c', '\r\nChemical', 'engineering  n. Creation and operation of industrial chemical plants.'),
(7991, 'c', '\r\nChemical', 'warfare  n. Warfare using poison gas and other chemicals.'),
(7992, 'c', '\r\nChemise', ' n. Hist. Woman''s loose-fitting undergarment or dress. [latin camisia shirt]'),
(7993, 'c', '\r\nChemist', ' n. 1 dealer in medicinal drugs etc. 2 expert in chemistry. [french: related to *alchemy]'),
(7994, 'c', '\r\nChemistry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 branch of science dealing with the elements and the compounds they form and the reactions they undergo. 2 chemical composition and properties of a substance. 3 colloq. Sexual attraction.'),
(7995, 'c', '\r\nChemotherapy', ' n. Treatment of disease, esp. Cancer, by chemical substances.'),
(7996, 'c', '\r\nChenille', ' n. 1 tufty velvety cord or yarn. 2 fabric of this. [french, = caterpillar, from latin canicula little dog]'),
(7997, 'c', '\r\nCheque', ' n. 1 written order to a bank to pay the stated sum from the drawer''s account. 2 printed form on which this is written. [from *check]'),
