(8525, 'c', '\r\nClub-root', ' n. Disease of cabbages etc. With swelling at the base of the stem.'),
(8526, 'c', '\r\nClub', 'sandwich  n. Sandwich with two layers of filling between three slices of toast or bread.'),
(8527, 'c', '\r\nCluck', ' â€”n. Guttural cry like that of a hen. â€”v. Emit cluck(s). [imitative]'),
(8528, 'c', '\r\nClue', ' â€”n. 1 fact or idea that serves as a guide, or suggests a line of inquiry, in a problem or investigation. 2 piece of evidence etc. In the detection of a crime. 3 verbal formula as a hint to what is to be inserted in a crossword. â€”v. (clues, clued, cluing or clueing) provide a clue to.  clue in (or up) slang inform. Not have a clue colloq. Be ignorant or incompetent. [var. Of old english clew]'),
(8529, 'c', '\r\nClueless', ' adj. Colloq. Ignorant, stupid.'),
(8530, 'c', '\r\nClump', ' â€”n. (foll. By of) cluster or mass, esp. Of trees. â€”v. 1 a form a clump. B heap or plant together. 2 (also clomp) walk with a heavy tread. [low german or dutch]'),
(8531, 'c', '\r\nClumsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 awkward in movement or shape; ungainly. 2 difficult to handle or use. 3 tactless.  clumsily adv. Clumsiness n. [obsolete clumse be numb with cold]'),
(8532, 'c', '\r\nClung', ' past and past part. Of *cling.'),
(8533, 'c', '\r\nClunk', ' â€”n. Dull sound as of thick pieces of metal meeting. â€”v. Make such a sound. [imitative]'),
(8534, 'c', '\r\nCluster', ' â€”n. Close group or bunch of similar people or things growing or occurring together. â€”v. 1 bring into, come into, or be in cluster(s). 2 (foll. By round, around) gather. [old english]'),
(8535, 'c', '\r\nClutch1', ' â€”v. 1 seize eagerly; grasp tightly. 2 (foll. By at) try desperately to seize. â€”n. 1 tight grasp. 2 (in pl.) Grasping hands; cruel or relentless grasp or control. 3 a (in a vehicle) device for connecting and disconnecting the engine and the transmission. B pedal operating this. [old english]'),
(8536, 'c', '\r\nClutch2', ' n. 1 set of eggs for hatching. 2 brood of chickens. [old norse, = hatch]'),
(8537, 'c', '\r\nClutch', 'bag  n. Slim flat handbag without handles.'),
(8538, 'c', '\r\nClutter', ' â€”n. 1 crowded and untidy collection of things. 2 untidy state. â€”v. (often foll. By up, with) crowd untidily, fill with clutter. [related to *clot]'),
(8539, 'c', '\r\nCm', ' symb. Curium.'),
