(9117, 'c', '\r\nCondign', ' adj. (of a punishment etc.) Severe and well-deserved. [latin dignus worthy]'),
(9118, 'c', '\r\nCondiment', ' n. Seasoning or relish for food. [latin condio pickle]'),
(9119, 'c', '\r\nCondition', ' â€”n. 1 stipulation; thing upon the fulfilment of which something else depends. 2 a state of being or fitness of a person or thing. B ailment, abnormality (heart condition). 3 (in pl.) Circumstances, esp. Those affecting the functioning or existence of something (good working conditions). â€”v. 1 a bring into a good or desired state. B make fit (esp. Dogs or horses). 2 teach or accustom. 3 a impose conditions on. B be essential to.  in (or out of) condition in good (or bad) condition. On condition that with the stipulation that. [latin dico say]'),
(9120, 'c', '\r\nConditional', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By on) dependent; not absolute; containing a condition. 2 gram. (of a clause, mood, etc.) Expressing a condition.  conditionally adv. [latin: related to *condition]'),
(9121, 'c', '\r\nConditioned', 'reflex  n. Reflex response to a non-natural stimulus, established by training.'),
(9122, 'c', '\r\nConditioner', ' n. Agent that conditions, esp. The hair.'),
(9123, 'c', '\r\nCondole', ' v. (-ling) (foll. By with) express sympathy with (a person) over a loss etc. [latin condoleo grieve with another]'),
(9124, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' condole is often confused with console1.'),
(9125, 'c', '\r\nCondolence', ' n. (often in pl.) Expression of sympathy.'),
(9126, 'c', '\r\nCondom', ' n. Contraceptive sheath worn by men. [origin unknown]'),
(9127, 'c', '\r\nCondominium', ' n. 1 joint rule or sovereignty. 2 us building containing individually owned flats. [latin dominium lordship]'),
(9128, 'c', '\r\nCondone', ' v. (-ning) forgive or overlook (an offence or wrongdoing). [latin dono give]'),
(9129, 'c', '\r\nCondor', ' n. Large s. American vulture. [spanish from quechua]'),
(9130, 'c', '\r\nConduce', ' v. (-cing) (foll. By to) contribute to (a result). [latin: related to *conduct]'),
(9131, 'c', '\r\nConducive', ' adj. (often foll. By to) contributing or helping (towards something).'),
(9132, 'c', '\r\nConduct', ' â€”n. 1 behaviour. 2 activity or manner of directing or managing (a business, war, etc.). â€”v. 1 lead or guide. 2 direct or manage (a business etc.). 3 (also absol.) Be the conductor of (an orchestra etc.). 4 transmit (heat, electricity, etc.) By conduction. 5 refl. Behave. [latin duco duct- lead]'),
(9133, 'c', '\r\nConductance', ' n. Power of a specified material to conduct electricity.'),
(9134, 'c', '\r\nConduction', ' n. Transmission of heat, electricity, etc. Through a substance. [latin: related to *conduct]'),
(9135, 'c', '\r\nConductive', ' adj. Transmitting (esp. Heat, electricity, etc.).  conductivity n.'),
(9136, 'c', '\r\nConductor', ' n. 1 person who directs an orchestra etc. 2 (fem. Conductress) person who collects fares in a bus etc. 3 thing that conducts heat or electricity. [latin: related to *conduct]'),
