(9159, 'c', '\r\nConfide', ' v. (-ding) 1 (foll. By in) talk confidentially to. 2 (usu. Foll. By to) tell (a secret etc.) In confidence. 3 (foll. By to) entrust (an object of care, a task, etc.) To. [latin confido trust]'),
(9160, 'c', '\r\nConfidence', ' n. 1 firm trust. 2 a feeling of reliance or certainty. B sense of self-reliance; boldness. 3 something told as a secret.  in confidence as a secret. In a person''s confidence trusted with a person''s secrets. Take into one''s confidence confide in. [latin: related to *confide]'),
(9161, 'c', '\r\nConfidence', 'trick  n. Swindle in which the victim is persuaded to trust the swindler.  confidence trickster n.'),
(9162, 'c', '\r\nConfident', ' adj. Feeling or showing confidence; bold.  confidently adv. [italian: related to *confide]'),
(9163, 'c', '\r\nConfidential', ' adj. 1 spoken or written in confidence. 2 entrusted with secrets (confidential secretary). 3 confiding.  confidentiality n. Confidentially adv.'),
(9164, 'c', '\r\nConfiguration', ' n. 1 arrangement in a particular form. 2 form or figure resulting from this. 3 computing hardware and its arrangement of connections etc.  configure v. (-ring). [latin: related to *figure]'),
