(9166, 'c', '\r\nConfinement', ' n. 1 confining or being confined. 2 time of childbirth.'),
(9167, 'c', '\r\nConfirm', ' v. 1 provide support for the truth or correctness of. 2 (foll. By in) encourage (a person) in (an opinion etc.). 3 establish more firmly (power, possession, etc.). 4 make formally valid. 5 administer the religious rite of confirmation to.  [latin: related to *firm1]'),
(9168, 'c', '\r\nConfirmation', ' n. 1 confirming or being confirmed. 2 rite confirming a baptized person as a member of the christian church.'),
(9169, 'c', '\r\nConfirmed', ' adj. Firmly settled in some habit or condition (confirmed bachelor).'),
(9170, 'c', '\r\nConfiscate', ' v. (-ting) take or seize by authority.  confiscation n. [latin: related to *fiscal]'),
(9171, 'c', '\r\nConflagration', ' n. Great and destructive fire. [latin: related to *flagrant]'),
(9172, 'c', '\r\nConflate', ' v. (-ting) blend or fuse together (esp. Two variant texts into one).  conflation n. [latin flo flat- blow]'),
(9173, 'c', '\r\nConflict', ' â€”n. 1 a state of opposition. B fight, struggle. 2 (often foll. By of) clashing of opposed interests etc. â€”v. Clash; be incompatible. [latin fligo flict- strike]'),
(9174, 'c', '\r\nConfluence', ' n. 1 place where two rivers meet. 2 a coming together. B crowd of people. [latin fluo flow]'),
