(9186, 'c', '\r\nConfusion', ' n. Confusing or being confused.'),
(9187, 'c', '\r\nConfute', ' v. (-ting) prove (a person or argument) to be in error.  confutation n. [latin]'),
(9188, 'c', '\r\nConga', ' â€”n. 1 latin-american dance, with a line of dancers one behind the other. 2 tall narrow drum beaten with the hands. â€”v. (congas, congaed or conga''d, congaing) perform the conga. [spanish conga (feminine), = of the congo]'),
(9189, 'c', '\r\nCongeal', ' v. 1 make or become semi-solid by cooling. 2 (of blood etc.) Coagulate.  congelation n. [french from latin gelo freeze]'),
(9190, 'c', '\r\nCongenial', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By with, to) pleasant because like-minded. 2 (often foll. By to) suited or agreeable.  congeniality n. Congenially adv. [from *com-, *genial]'),
(9191, 'c', '\r\nCongenital', ' adj. 1 (esp. Of disease) existing from birth. 2 as such from birth (congenital liar).  congenitally adv. [latin: related to *com-]'),
(9192, 'c', '\r\nConger', ' n. (in full conger eel) large marine eel. [greek goggros]'),
(9193, 'c', '\r\nCongeries', ' n. (pl. Same) disorderly collection; mass, heap. [latin congero heap together]'),
(9194, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' the form congery, formed under the misapprehension that congeries is plural only, is incorrect.'),
(9195, 'c', '\r\nCongest', ' v. (esp. As congested adj.) Affect with congestion. [latin congero -gest- heap together]'),
