(9236, 'c', '\r\nConnive', ' v. (-ving) 1 (foll. By at) disregard or tacitly consent to (a wrongdoing). 2 (usu. Foll. By with) conspire.  connivance n. [latin conniveo shut the eyes]'),
(9237, 'c', '\r\nConnoisseur', ' n. (often foll. By of, in) expert judge in matters of taste. [french connaÃ®tre know]'),
(9238, 'c', '\r\nConnote', ' v. (-ting) 1 (of a word etc.) Imply in addition to the literal or primary meaning. 2 mean, signify.  connotation n. Connotative adj. [medieval latin: related to *note]'),
