(9431, 'c', '\r\nConvector', ' n. Heating appliance that circulates warm air by convection.'),
(9432, 'c', '\r\nConvene', ' v. (-ning) 1 summon or arrange (a meeting etc.). 2 assemble. [latin venio vent- come]'),
(9433, 'c', '\r\nConvener', ' n. (also convenor) 1 person who convenes a meeting. 2 senior trade union official at a workplace.'),
(9434, 'c', '\r\nConvenience', ' n. 1 state of being convenient; suitability.  2 useful thing. 3 advantage. 4 lavatory, esp. A public one.  at one''s convenience at a time or place that suits one. [latin: related to *convene]'),
(9435, 'c', '\r\nConvenience', 'food  n. Food requiring little preparation.'),
(9436, 'c', '\r\nConvenient', ' adj. 1 a serving one''s comfort or interests. B suitable. C free of trouble or difficulty. 2 available or occurring at a suitable time or place. 3 well situated (convenient for the shops).  conveniently adv.'),
(9437, 'c', '\r\nConvent', ' n. 1 religious community, esp. Of nuns, under vows. 2 premises occupied by this. [latin: related to *convene]'),
