(9469, 'c', '\r\nCoo', ' â€”n. Soft murmuring sound as of a dove. â€”v. (coos, cooed) 1 emit a coo. 2 talk or say in a soft or amorous voice. â€”int. Slang expressing surprise or disbelief. [imitative]'),
(9470, 'c', '\r\nCooee', ' n. & int. Colloq. Call used to attract attention. [imitative]'),
(9471, 'c', '\r\nCook', ' â€”v. 1 prepare (food) by heating it. 2 (of food) undergo cooking. 3 colloq. Falsify (accounts etc.). 4 (as be cooking) colloq. Be happening or about to happen. â€”n. Person who cooks, esp. Professionally or in a specified way (a good cook).  cook up colloq. Concoct (a story, excuse, etc.). [latin coquus]'),
(9472, 'c', '\r\nCookbook', ' n. Us cookery book.'),
(9473, 'c', '\r\nCook-chill', ' attrib. Adj. (of food, meals, etc.) Sold in pre-cooked and refrigerated form.'),
(9474, 'c', '\r\nCooker', ' n. 1 appliance or vessel for cooking food. 2 fruit (esp. An apple) suitable for cooking.'),
(9475, 'c', '\r\nCookery', ' n. Art or practice of cooking.'),
(9476, 'c', '\r\nCookery', 'book  n. Book containing recipes.'),
(9477, 'c', '\r\nCookie', ' n. Us 1 sweet biscuit. 2 colloq. Person (a tough cookie). [dutch koekje]'),
(9478, 'c', '\r\nCool', ' â€”adj. 1 of or at a fairly low temperature, fairly cold. 2 suggesting or achieving coolness. 3 calm, unexcited. 4 lacking enthusiasm. 5 unfriendly (a cool reception). 6 calmly audacious. 7 (prec. By a) colloq. At least (cost a cool thousand). 8 slang esp. Us marvellous. â€”n. 1 coolness. 2 cool air or place. 3 slang calmness, composure. â€”v. (often foll. By down, off) make or become cool.  cool it slang relax, calm down.  coolly adv. Coolness n. [old english]'),
(9479, 'c', '\r\nCoolant', ' n. Cooling agent, esp. Fluid.'),
(9480, 'c', '\r\nCool-bag', ' n. (also cool-box) insulated container for keeping food cool.'),
(9481, 'c', '\r\nCooler', ' n. 1 vessel in which a thing is cooled. 2 us refrigerator. 3 slang prison cell.'),
