(9555, 'c', '\r\nCorker', ' n. Slang excellent person or thing.'),
(9556, 'c', '\r\nCorkscrew', ' â€”n. 1 spiral device for extracting corks from bottles. 2 (often attrib.) Thing with a spiral shape. â€”v. Move spirally; twist.'),
(9557, 'c', '\r\nCorm', ' n. Underground swollen stem base of some plants. [greek kormos lopped tree-trunk]'),
(9558, 'c', '\r\nCormorant', ' n. Diving sea bird with black plumage. [latin corvus marinus sea-raven]'),
(9559, 'c', '\r\nCorn1', 'n. 1 a cereal before or after harvesting, esp. The chief crop of a region. B grain or seed of a cereal plant. 2 colloq. Something corny or trite. [old english]'),
(9560, 'c', '\r\nCorn2', ' n. Small tender area of horny skin, esp. On the toe. [latin cornu horn]'),
(9561, 'c', '\r\nCorn-cob', ' n. Cylindrical centre of a maize ear on which the grains grow.'),
(9562, 'c', '\r\nCorncrake', ' n. Rail inhabiting grassland and nesting on the ground.'),
(9563, 'c', '\r\nCorn', 'dolly  n. Figure of plaited straw.'),
(9564, 'c', '\r\nCornea', ' n. Transparent circular part of the front of the eyeball.  corneal adj. [medieval latin: related to *corn2]'),
(9565, 'c', '\r\nCorned', ' adj.  (esp. Of beef) preserved in salt or brine. [from *corn1]'),
(9566, 'c', '\r\nCornelian', ' n. (also carnelian) dull red variety of chalcedony. [french]'),
(9567, 'c', '\r\nCorner', ' â€”n. 1 place where converging sides or edges meet. 2 projecting angle, esp. Where two streets meet. 3 internal space or recess formed by the meeting of two sides, esp. Of a room. 4 difficult position, esp. One with no escape. 5 secluded place. 6 region or quarter, esp. A remote one. 7 action or result of buying or controlling the whole stock of a commodity. 8 boxing & wrestling corner of the ring where a contestant rests between rounds. 9 football & hockey free kick or hit from the corner of a pitch. â€”v. 1 force into a difficult or inescapable position. 2 establish a corner in (a commodity). 3 (esp. Of or in a vehicle) go round a corner. [latin: related to *corn2]'),
(9568, 'c', '\r\nCornerstone', ' n. 1 a stone in the projecting angle of a wall. B foundation-stone. 2 indispensable part or basis.'),
(9569, 'c', '\r\nCornet', ' n. 1 brass instrument resembling a trumpet but shorter and wider. 2 conical wafer for holding ice-cream.  cornetist n. (also cornettist). [latin cornu: related to *corn2]'),
(9570, 'c', '\r\nCornflake', ' n. 1 (in pl.) Breakfast cereal of toasted maize flakes. 2 flake of this cereal.'),
(9571, 'c', '\r\nCornflour', ' n. Fine-ground flour, esp. Of maize or rice.'),
(9572, 'c', '\r\nCornflower', ' n. Plant with deep-blue flowers originally growing among corn.'),
(9573, 'c', '\r\nCornice', ' n. Ornamental moulding, esp. Round a room just below the ceiling or as the topmost part of an entablature. [french from italian]'),
(9574, 'c', '\r\nCornish', ' â€”adj. Of cornwall. â€”n. Celtic language of cornwall.'),
(9575, 'c', '\r\nCornish', 'pasty  n. Pastry envelope containing meat and vegetables.'),
(9576, 'c', '\r\nCorn', 'on the cob  n. Maize cooked and eaten from the corn-cob.'),
(9577, 'c', '\r\nCornucopia', ' n. 1 horn overflowing with flowers, fruit, and corn, as a symbol of plenty. 2 abundant supply. [latin: related to *corn2, *copious]'),
(9578, 'c', '\r\nCorny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 banal. 2 feebly humorous. 3 sentimental.  cornily adv. Corniness n. [from *corn1]'),
(9579, 'c', '\r\nCorolla', ' n. Whorl of petals forming the inner envelope of a flower. [latin diminutive of *corona]'),
(9580, 'c', '\r\nCorollary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 proposition that follows from one already proved. 2 (often foll. By of) natural consequence. [latin, = gratuity: related to *corolla]'),
(9581, 'c', '\r\nCorona', ' n. (pl. -nae) 1 a halo round the sun or moon. B gaseous envelope of the sun, seen as an area of light around the moon during a total solar eclipse. 2 anat. Crownlike structure. 3 crownlike outgrowth from the inner side of a corolla. 4 glow around an electric conductor.  coronal adj. [latin, = crown]'),
(9582, 'c', '\r\nCoronary', ' â€”adj. Anat. Resembling or encircling like a crown. â€”n. (pl. -ies) = *coronary thrombosis. [latin: related to *corona]'),
(9583, 'c', '\r\nCoronary', 'artery  n. Artery supplying blood to the heart.'),
(9584, 'c', '\r\nCoronary', 'thrombosis  n. Blockage caused by a blood clot in a coronary artery.'),
(9585, 'c', '\r\nCoronation', ' n. Ceremony of crowning a sovereign or consort. [medieval latin: related to *corona]'),
(9586, 'c', '\r\nCoroner', ' n. Official holding inquests on deaths thought to be violent or accidental. [anglo-french: related to *crown]'),
(9587, 'c', '\r\nCoronet', ' n. 1 small crown. 2 circlet of precious materials, esp. As a headdress. [french diminutive: related to *crown]'),
(9588, 'c', '\r\nCorpora', ' pl. Of *corpus.'),
(9589, 'c', '\r\nCorporal1', ' n. Non-commissioned army or air-force officer ranking next below sergeant. [french from italian]'),
(9590, 'c', '\r\nCorporal2', ' adj. Of the human body.  corporality n. [latin corpus body]'),
(9591, 'c', '\r\nCorporal', 'punishment  n. Physical punishment.'),
(9592, 'c', '\r\nCorporate', ' adj. 1 forming a corporation. 2 of, belonging to, or united in a group. [latin: related to *corporal2]'),
(9593, 'c', '\r\nCorporation', ' n. 1 group of people authorized to act as an individual, esp. In business. 2 municipal authorities of a borough, town, or city. 3 joc. Large stomach.'),
(9594, 'c', '\r\nCorporative', ' adj. 1 of a corporation. 2 governed by or organized in corporations.'),
(9595, 'c', '\r\nCorporeal', ' adj. Bodily, physical, material.  corporeality n. Corporeally adv. [latin: related to *corporal2]'),
(9596, 'c', '\r\nCorps', ' n. (pl. Corps) 1 a body of troops with special duties (intelligence corps). B main subdivision of an army in the field. 2 body of people engaged in a special activity (diplomatic corps). [french: related to *corpse]'),
(9597, 'c', '\r\nCorps', 'de ballet  n. Group of ensemble dancers in a ballet. [french]'),
(9598, 'c', '\r\nCorpse', ' n. Dead body. [latin: related to *corpus]'),
(9599, 'c', '\r\nCorpulent', ' adj. Physically bulky, fat.  corpulence n. [latin: related to *corpus]'),
(9600, 'c', '\r\nCorpus', ' n. (pl. -pora) body or collection of writings, texts, etc. [latin, = body]'),
(9601, 'c', '\r\nCorpuscle', ' n. Minute body or cell in an organism, esp. (in pl.) The red or white cells in the blood of vertebrates.  corpuscular adj. [latin diminutive of *corpus]'),
(9602, 'c', '\r\nCorral', ' â€”n. 1 us pen for cattle, horses, etc. 2 enclosure for capturing wild animals. â€”v. (-ll-) put or keep in a corral. [spanish and portuguese: related to *kraal]'),
(9603, 'c', '\r\nCorrect', ' â€”adj. 1 true, accurate. 2 proper, in accordance with taste or a standard. â€”v. 1 set right; amend. 2 mark errors in. 3 substitute a right thing for (a wrong one). 4 a admonish (a person). B punish (a person or fault). 5 counteract (a harmful quality). 6 adjust (an instrument etc.).  correctly adv. Correctness n. Corrector n. [latin rego rect- guide]'),
(9604, 'c', '\r\nCorrection', ' n. 1 correcting or being corrected. 2 thing substituted for what is wrong. 3 archaic punishment.  correctional adj. [latin: related to *correct]'),
(9605, 'c', '\r\nCorrectitude', ' n. Consciously correct behaviour. [from *correct, *rectitude]'),
(9606, 'c', '\r\nCorrective', ' â€”adj. Serving to correct or counteract something harmful. â€”n. Corrective measure or thing. [latin: related to *correct]'),
(9607, 'c', '\r\nCorrelate', ' â€”v. (-ting) (usu. Foll. By with, to) have or bring into a mutual relation or dependence. â€”n. Each of two related or complementary things.  correlation n. [medieval latin correlatio]'),
(9608, 'c', '\r\nCorrelative', ' â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By with, to) having a mutual relation. 2 (of words) corresponding to each other and used together (as neither and nor). â€”n. Correlative word or thing.'),
(9609, 'c', '\r\nCorrespond', ' v. 1 a (usu. Foll. By to) be similar or equivalent. B (usu. Foll. By with, to) be in agreement, not contradict. 2 (usu. Foll. By with) exchange letters.  correspondingly adv. [french from medieval latin]'),
(9610, 'c', '\r\nCorrespondence', ' n. 1 agreement or similarity. 2 a exchange of letters. B letters.'),
(9611, 'c', '\r\nCorrespondence', 'course  n. Course of study conducted by post.'),
(9612, 'c', '\r\nCorrespondent', ' n. 1 person who writes letters. 2 person employed to write or report for a newspaper or for broadcasting etc.'),
(9613, 'c', '\r\nCorridor', ' n. 1 passage giving access into rooms. 2 passage in a train giving access into compartments. 3 strip of territory of one state passing through that of another. 4 route which an aircraft must follow, esp. Over a foreign country. [french from italian]'),
(9614, 'c', '\r\nCorridors', 'of power  n.pl. Places where covert influence is said to be exerted in government.'),
(9615, 'c', '\r\nCorrigendum', ' n. (pl. -da) error to be corrected. [latin corrigo: related to *correct]'),
(9616, 'c', '\r\nCorrigible', ' adj. 1 able to be corrected. 2 submissive.  corrigibly adv. [medieval latin: related to *corrigendum]'),
(9617, 'c', '\r\nCorroborate', ' v. (-ting) confirm or give support to (a statement or belief etc.).  corroboration n. Corroborative adj. Corroborator n. [latin robur strength]'),
(9618, 'c', '\r\nCorrode', ' v. (-ding) 1 a wear away, esp. By chemical action. B decay. 2 destroy gradually. [latin rodo ros- gnaw]'),
(9619, 'c', '\r\nCorrosion', ' n. 1 corroding or being corroded. 2 corroded area.  corrosive adj. & n.'),
(9620, 'c', '\r\nCorrugate', ' v. (-ting) (esp. As corrugated adj.) Form into alternate ridges and grooves, esp. To strengthen (corrugated iron).  corrugation n. [latin ruga wrinkle]'),
(9621, 'c', '\r\nCorrupt', ' â€”adj. 1 dishonest, esp. Using bribery. 2 immoral; wicked. 3 (of a text etc.) Made unreliable by errors or alterations. â€”v. Make or become corrupt.  corruptible adj. Corruptibility n. Corruption n. Corruptive adj. Corruptly adv. Corruptness n. [latin rumpo rupt- break]'),
(9622, 'c', '\r\nCorsage', ' n. Small bouquet worn by women. [french: related to *corpse]'),
(9623, 'c', '\r\nCorsair', ' n. 1 pirate ship. 2 pirate. [french: related to *course]'),
(9624, 'c', '\r\nCorselette', ' n. Combined corset and bra. [french corslet armour covering trunk]'),
(9625, 'c', '\r\nCorset', ' n. Closely-fitting undergarment worn to shape the body or to support it after injury.  corsetry n. [french diminutive: related to *corpse]'),
(9626, 'c', '\r\nCortÃ¨ge', ' n. Procession, esp. For a funeral. [french]'),
(9627, 'c', '\r\nCortex', ' n. (pl. -tices) outer part of an organ, esp. Of the brain or kidneys.  cortical adj. [latin, = bark]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(9628, 'c', '\r\nCortisone', ' n. Hormone used esp. In treating inflammation and allergy. [abbreviation of chemical name]'),
(9629, 'c', '\r\nCorundum', ' n. Extremely hard crystallized alumina, used esp. As an abrasive. [tamil from sanskrit]'),
(9630, 'c', '\r\nCoruscate', ' v. (-ting) sparkle.  coruscation n. [latin]'),
(9631, 'c', '\r\nCorvette', ' n. 1 small naval escort-vessel. 2 hist. Warship with one tier of guns. [french from dutch]'),
(9632, 'c', '\r\nCorymb', ' n. Flat-topped cluster of flowers with the outer flower-stalks proportionally longer. [latin from greek]'),
(9633, 'c', '\r\nCos1', ' n. Lettuce with crisp narrow leaves. [kos, greek island]'),
(9634, 'c', '\r\nCos2', ' abbr. Cosine.'),
(9635, 'c', '\r\nCos3', ' conj. Colloq. Because. [abbreviation]'),
(9636, 'c', '\r\nCosec', ' abbr. Cosecant.'),
(9637, 'c', '\r\nCosecant', ' n. Math. Ratio of the hypotenuse (in a right-angled triangle) to the side opposite an acute angle.'),
(9638, 'c', '\r\nCosh1', ' colloq. â€”n. Heavy blunt weapon. â€”v. Hit with a cosh. [origin unknown]'),
(9639, 'c', '\r\nCosh2', ' abbr. Hyperbolic cosine.'),
(9640, 'c', '\r\nCo-signatory', ' n. (pl. -ies) person or state signing a treaty etc. Jointly with others.'),
(9641, 'c', '\r\nCosine', ' n. Ratio of the side adjacent to an acute angle (in a right-angled triangle) to the hypotenuse.'),
(9642, 'c', '\r\nCosmetic', ' â€”adj. 1 beautifying, enhancing. 2 superficially improving or beneficial. 3 (of surgery or a prosthesis) imitating, restoring, or enhancing normal appearance. â€”n. Cosmetic preparation, esp. For the face.  cosmetically adv. [greek, = ornament]'),
(9643, 'c', '\r\nCosmic', ' adj. 1 of the cosmos or its scale; universal (of cosmic significance). 2 of or for space travel.'),
(9644, 'c', '\r\nCosmic', 'rays  n.pl. High-energy radiations from space etc.'),
(9645, 'c', '\r\nCosmogony', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 origin of the universe. 2 theory about this. [greek -gonia -begetting]'),
(9646, 'c', '\r\nCosmology', ' n. Science or theory of the universe.  cosmological adj. Cosmologist n. [from *cosmos, *-logy]'),
(9647, 'c', '\r\nCosmonaut', ' n. Soviet astronaut. [from *cosmos, greek nautes sailor]'),
(9648, 'c', '\r\nCosmopolitan', ' â€”adj. 1 of, from, or knowing many parts of the world. 2 free from national limitations or prejudices. â€”n. Cosmopolitan person.  cosmopolitanism n. [greek polites citizen]'),
(9649, 'c', '\r\nCosmos', ' n. The universe as a well-ordered whole. [greek]'),
(9650, 'c', '\r\nCossack', ' n. Member of a people of southern russia. [turki quzzaq]'),
(9651, 'c', '\r\nCosset', ' v. (-t-) pamper. [dialect cosset = pet lamb, probably from old english, = cottager]'),
(9652, 'c', '\r\nCost', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Cost) 1 be obtainable for (a sum of money); have as a price. 2 involve as a loss or sacrifice (it cost him his life). 3 (past and past part. Costed) fix or estimate the cost of. â€”n. 1 what a thing costs; price. 2 loss or sacrifice. 3 (in pl.) Legal expenses.  at all costs (or at any cost) whatever the cost or risk may be. [latin consto stand at a price]'),
(9653, 'c', '\r\nCostal', ' adj. Of the ribs. [latin costa rib]'),
(9654, 'c', '\r\nCost-effective', ' adj. Effective in relation to its cost.'),
(9655, 'c', '\r\nCostermonger', ' n. Person who sells produce from a barrow. [costard large apple: related to *costal]'),
(9656, 'c', '\r\nCosting', ' n. Estimation of cost(s).'),
(9657, 'c', '\r\nCostive', ' adj. Constipated. [latin: related to *constipate]'),
(9658, 'c', '\r\nCostly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) costing much; expensive.  costliness n.'),
(9659, 'c', '\r\nCost', 'of living  n. Level of prices esp. Of basic necessities.'),
(9660, 'c', '\r\nCost', 'price  n. Price paid for a thing by one who later sells it.'),
(9661, 'c', '\r\nCostume', ' â€”n. 1 style of dress, esp. Of a particular place or time. 2 set of clothes. 3 clothing for a particular activity (swimming-costume). 4 actor''s clothes for a part. â€”v. (-ming) provide with a costume. [latin: related to *custom]'),
(9662, 'c', '\r\nCostume', 'jewellery  n. Artificial jewellery.'),
(9663, 'c', '\r\nCostumier', ' n. Person who makes or deals in costumes. [french: related to *costume]'),
(9664, 'c', '\r\nCosy', ' (us cozy) â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) comfortable and warm; snug. â€”n. (pl. -ies) cover to keep a teapot etc. Hot.  cosily adv. Cosiness n. [origin unknown]'),
(9665, 'c', '\r\nCot1', ' n. 1 small bed with high sides for a baby. 2 small light bed. [hindi]'),
(9666, 'c', '\r\nCot2', ' n. 1 small shelter; cote. 2 poet. Cottage. [old english]'),
(9667, 'c', '\r\nCot3', ' abbr. Cotangent.'),
(9668, 'c', '\r\nCotangent', ' n. Ratio of the side adjacent to an acute angle (in a right-angled triangle) to the opposite side.'),
(9669, 'c', '\r\nCot-death', ' n. Unexplained death of a sleeping baby.'),
(9670, 'c', '\r\nCote', ' n. Shelter for animals or birds. [old english]'),
(9671, 'c', '\r\nCoterie', ' n. Exclusive group of people sharing interests. [french]'),
(9672, 'c', '\r\nCotoneaster', ' n. Shrub bearing usu. Bright red berries. [latin cotoneum *quince]'),
(9673, 'c', '\r\nCottage', ' n. Small simple house, esp. In the country. [anglo-french: related to *cot2]'),
(9674, 'c', '\r\nCottage', 'cheese  n. Soft white lumpy cheese made from skimmed milk curds.'),
(9675, 'c', '\r\nCottage', 'industry  n. Business activity carried on at home.'),
(9676, 'c', '\r\nCottage', 'pie  n. Dish of minced meat topped with mashed potato.'),
(9677, 'c', '\r\nCottager', ' n. Person who lives in a cottage.'),
(9678, 'c', '\r\nCotter', ' n. 1 bolt or wedge for securing parts of machinery etc. 2 (in full cotter pin) split pin that can be opened after passing through a hole. [origin unknown]'),
(9679, 'c', '\r\nCotton', ' n. 1 soft white fibrous substance covering the seeds of certain plants. 2 such a plant. 3 thread or cloth from this.  cotton on (often foll. By to) colloq. Begin to understand. [french from arabic]'),
(9680, 'c', '\r\nCotton', 'wool  n. Fluffy wadding of a kind orig. Made from raw cotton.'),
(9681, 'c', '\r\nCotyledon', ' n. Embryonic leaf in seed-bearing plants. [greek kotule cup]'),
(9682, 'c', '\r\nCouch1', ' â€”n. 1 upholstered piece of furniture for several people; sofa. 2 long padded seat with a headrest at one end. â€”v. 1 (foll. By in) express in (certain terms). 2 archaic (of an animal) lie, esp. In its lair. [latin colloco lay in place]'),
(9683, 'c', '\r\nCouch2', ' n. (in full couch grass) a grass with long creeping roots. [var. Of *quitch]'),
(9684, 'c', '\r\nCouchette', ' n. 1 railway carriage with seats convertible into sleeping-berths. 2 berth in this. [french, = little bed]'),
(9685, 'c', '\r\nCouch', 'potato  n. Us slang person who likes lazing at home.'),
(9686, 'c', '\r\nCougar', ' n. Us puma. [french from guarani]'),
(9687, 'c', '\r\nCough', ' â€”v. 1 expel air etc. From the lungs with a sudden sharp sound. 2 (of an engine etc.) Make a similar sound. 3 slang confess. â€”n. 1 act of coughing. 2 condition of respiratory organs causing coughing.  cough up 1 eject with coughs. 2 slang bring out or give (money or information) reluctantly. [imitative, related to dutch kuchen]'),
(9688, 'c', '\r\nCough', 'mixture  n. Liquid medicine to relieve a cough.'),
(9689, 'c', '\r\nCould', ' past of *can1. â€”v. Colloq. Feel inclined to (i could murder him).'),
(9690, 'c', '\r\nCouldn''t', ' contr. Could not.'),
(9691, 'c', '\r\nCoulomb', ' n. Si unit of electric charge. [coulomb, name of a physicist]'),
(9692, 'c', '\r\nCoulter', ' n. (us colter) vertical blade in front of a ploughshare. [latin culter knife]'),
(9693, 'c', '\r\nCouncil', ' n. 1 a advisory, deliberative, or administrative body. B meeting of such a body. 2 a local administrative body of a parish, district, town, etc. B (attrib.) Provided by a local council (council flat). [latin concilium]'),
(9694, 'c', '\r\nCouncillor', ' n. Member of a (esp. Local) council.'),
(9695, 'c', '\r\nCouncil', 'tax  n. Proposed new local tax based on the value of a property and the number of people living in it, to replace the community charge.'),
(9696, 'c', '\r\nCounsel', ' â€”n. 1 advice, esp. Formally given. 2 consultation for advice. 3 (pl. Same) legal adviser, esp. A barrister; body of these. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 advise (a person). 2 give esp. Professional advice to (a person) on personal problems. 3 recommend (a course of action).  keep one''s own counsel not confide in others. Take counsel (usu. Foll. By with) consult.  counselling n. [latin consilium]'),
(9697, 'c', '\r\nCounsellor', ' n. (us counselor) 1 adviser. 2 person giving professional guidance on personal problems. 3 us barrister.'),
(9698, 'c', '\r\nCounsel', 'of perfection  n. Ideal but impracticable advice.'),
(9699, 'c', '\r\nCounselor', ' n. (brit. Counsellor) 1 adviser. 2 person giving professional guidance on personal problems. 3 us barrister.'),
(9700, 'c', '\r\nCount1', ' â€”v. 1 determine the total number of, esp. By assigning successive numbers. 2 repeat numbers in ascending order. 3 (often foll. By in) include or be included in one''s reckoning or plan. 4 consider or regard to be (lucky etc.). 5 (often foll. By for) have value; matter (my opinion counts for little). â€”n. 1 a counting or being counted. B total of reckoning. 2 law each charge in an indictment.  count against be reckoned to the disadvantage of. Count one''s blessings be grateful for what one has. Count on (or upon) rely on; expect. Count out 1 count while taking from a stock. 2 complete a count of ten seconds over (a fallen boxer etc.). 3 colloq. Exclude, disregard. 4 polit. Procure the adjournment of (the house of commons) when fewer than 40 members are present. Count up find the sum of. Keep count take note of how many there have been etc. Lose count forget the number etc. Counted. Out for the count 1 defeated. 2 unconscious; asleep. [latin: related to *compute]'),
(9701, 'c', '\r\nCount2', ' n. Foreign noble corresponding to an earl. [latin comes companion]'),
(9702, 'c', '\r\nCountable', ' adj. 1 that can be counted. 2 gram. (of a noun) that can form a plural or be used with the indefinite article.'),
(9703, 'c', '\r\nCountdown', ' n. 1 act of counting backwards to zero, esp. At the launching of a rocket etc. 2 period immediately before an event.'),
(9704, 'c', '\r\nCountenance', ' â€”n. 1 the face or facial expression. 2 composure. 3 moral support. â€”v. (-cing) support, approve. [french: related to *contain]'),
(9705, 'c', '\r\nComposure.', '3 moral support. â€”v. (-cing) support, approve. [french: related to *contain]'),
(9706, 'c', '\r\nCounter1', ' n. 1 long flat-topped fitment in a shop etc., across which business is conducted. 2 a small disc for playing or scoring in board-games etc. B token representing a coin. 3 apparatus for counting.  under the counter surreptitiously, esp. Illegally. [related to *count1]'),
(9707, 'c', '\r\nCounter2', ' â€”v. 1 a oppose, contradict. B meet by countermove. 2 boxing give a return blow while parrying. â€”adv. In the opposite direction or manner. â€”adj. Opposite. â€”n. Parry; countermove. [related to *counter-]'),
(9708, 'c', '\r\nCounter-', ' comb. Form denoting: 1 retaliation, opposition, or rivalry (counter-threat). 2 opposite direction (counter-clockwise). 3 correspondence (counterpart; countersign). [latin contra against]'),
(9709, 'c', '\r\nCounteract', ' v. Hinder or neutralize by contrary action.  counteraction n. Counteractive adj.'),
(9710, 'c', '\r\nCounter-attack', ' â€”n. Attack in reply to a preceding attack. â€”v. Attack in reply.'),
(9711, 'c', '\r\nCounterbalance', ' â€”n. Weight or influence balancing another. â€”v. (-cing) act as a counterbalance to.'),
(9712, 'c', '\r\nCounter-clockwise', ' adv. & adj. Us = *anticlockwise.'),
(9713, 'c', '\r\nCounter-espionage', ' n. Action taken against enemy spying.'),
(9714, 'c', '\r\nCounterfeit', ' â€”adj. Made in imitation; not genuine; forged. â€”n. A forgery or imitation. â€”v. Imitate fraudulently; forge. [french]'),
(9715, 'c', '\r\nCounterfoil', ' n. Part of a cheque, receipt, etc., retained by the payer as a record. [from *foil2]'),
(9716, 'c', '\r\nCounter-intelligence', ' n. = *counter-espionage.'),
(9717, 'c', '\r\nCountermand', ' â€”v. 1 revoke (a command). 2 recall by a contrary order. â€”n. Order revoking a previous one. [latin: related to *mandate]'),
(9718, 'c', '\r\nCountermeasure', ' n. Action taken to counteract a danger, threat, etc.'),
(9719, 'c', '\r\nCountermove', ' n. Move or action in opposition to another.'),
(9720, 'c', '\r\nCounterpane', ' n. Bedspread. [medieval latin culcita puncta quilted mattress]'),
(9721, 'c', '\r\nCounterpart', ' n. 1 person or thing like another or forming the complement or equivalent to another. 2 duplicate.'),
(9722, 'c', '\r\nCounterpoint', ' n. 1 a art or practice of combining melodies according to fixed rules. B melody combined with another. 2 contrasting argument, plot, literary theme, etc. [medieval latin contrapunctum marked opposite]'),
(9723, 'c', '\r\nCounterpoise', ' â€”n. 1 counterbalance. 2 state of equilibrium. â€”v. (-sing) counterbalance. [latin pensum weight]'),
(9724, 'c', '\r\nCounter-productive', ' adj. Having the opposite of the desired effect.'),
(9725, 'c', '\r\nCounter-revolution', ' n. Revolution opposing a former one or reversing its results.'),
(9726, 'c', '\r\nCountersign', ' â€”v. Add a signature to (a document already signed by another). â€”n. 1 password spoken to a person on guard. 2 mark used for identification etc. [italian: related to *sign]'),
(9727, 'c', '\r\nCountersink', ' v. (past and past part. -sunk) 1 shape (the rim of a hole) so that a screw or bolt can be inserted flush with the surface. 2 sink (a screw etc.) In such a hole.'),
(9728, 'c', '\r\nCounter-tenor', ' n. 1 male alto singing-voice. 2 singer with this voice. [italian: related to *contra-]'),
(9729, 'c', '\r\nCountervail', ' v. Literary 1 counterbalance. 2 (often foll. By against) oppose, usu. Successfully. [latin valeo have worth]'),
(9730, 'c', '\r\nCounterweight', ' n. Counterbalancing weight.'),
(9731, 'c', '\r\nCountess', ' n. 1 wife or widow of a count or earl. 2 woman holding the rank of count or earl. [latin comitissa: related to *count2]'),
(9732, 'c', '\r\nCountless', ' adj. Too many to be counted.'),
(9733, 'c', '\r\nCountrified', ' adj. Rustic in manner or appearance.'),
(9734, 'c', '\r\nCountry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 territory of a nation; state. 2 (often attrib.) Rural districts as opposed to towns or the capital. 3 land of a person''s birth or citizenship. 4 region with regard to its aspect, associations, etc. (mountainous country; hardy country). 5 national population, esp. As voters. [medieval latin contrata (terra) (land) lying opposite]'),
(9735, 'c', '\r\nCountry-and-western', ' n. Type of folk music originated by whites in the southern us.'),
(9736, 'c', '\r\nCountry', 'club  n. Sporting and social club in a rural setting.'),
(9737, 'c', '\r\nCountry', 'dance  n. Traditional dance, esp. English, usu. With couples facing each other in lines.'),
(9738, 'c', '\r\nCountryman', ' n. (fem. Countrywoman) 1 person living in a rural area. 2 (also fellow-countryman) person of one''s own country.'),
(9739, 'c', '\r\nCountry', 'music  n. = *country-and-western.'),
(9740, 'c', '\r\nCountryside', ' n. Rural areas.'),
(9741, 'c', '\r\nCountry-wide', ' adj. & adv. Extending throughout a nation.'),
(9742, 'c', '\r\nCounty', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 territorial division in some countries, forming the chief unit of local administration. 2 us political and administrative division of a state. â€”adj. Of or like the gentry. [latin comitatus: related to *count2]'),
(9743, 'c', '\r\nCounty', 'council  n. Elected governing body of an administrative county.'),
(9744, 'c', '\r\nCounty', 'court  n. Judicial court for civil cases.'),
(9745, 'c', '\r\nCounty', 'town  n. Administrative capital of a county.'),
