(9785, 'c', '\r\nCoventry', ' n.  send a person to coventry refuse to associate with or speak to a person. [coventry in england]'),
(9786, 'c', '\r\nCover', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By with) protect or conceal with a cloth, lid, etc. 2 a extend over; occupy the whole surface of. B (often foll. By with) strew thickly or thoroughly. C lie over. 3 a protect; clothe. B (as covered adj.) Wearing a hat; having a roof. 4 include; comprise; deal with. 5 travel (a specified distance). 6 describe as a reporter. 7 be enough to defray (Â£20 should cover it). 8 a refl. Take measures to protect oneself. B (absol.; foll. By for) stand in for. 9 a aim a gun etc. At. B (of a fortress, guns, etc.) Command (territory). C protect (an exposed person etc.) By being able to return fire. 10 a esp. Cricket stand behind (another player) to stop any missed balls. B mark (an opposing player). 11 (of a stallion etc.) Copulate with. â€”n. 1 thing that covers, esp.: a lid. B book''s binding. C either board of this. D envelope or wrapping (under separate cover). 2 shelter. 3 a pretence; screen. B pretended identity. C mil. Supporting force protecting an advance party from attack. 4 a funds, esp. Obtainable from insurance to meet a liability or secure against loss. B insurance protection (third-party cover). 5 person acting as a substitute. 6 place-setting at table. 7 cricket = *cover-point.  cover up completely cover or conceal. Take cover find shelter. [latin cooperio]'),
(9787, 'c', '\r\nCoverage', ' n. 1 area or amount covered. 2 amount of publicity received by an event etc.'),
(9788, 'c', '\r\nCoverall', ' n. Esp. Us 1 thing that covers entirely. 2 (usu. In pl.) Full-length protective garment.'),
(9789, 'c', '\r\nCover', 'charge  n. Service charge per head in a restaurant, nightclub, etc.'),
(9790, 'c', '\r\nCover', 'girl  n. Female model appearing on magazine covers etc.'),
