(9882, 'c', '\r\nCrash1', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) make a loud smashing noise. 2 throw, drive, move, or fall with a loud smash. 3 (often foll. By into) collide or fall, or cause (a vehicle etc.) To collide or fall, violently; overturn at high speed. 4 collapse financially. 5 colloq. Gatecrash. 6 computing (of a machine or system) fail suddenly. 7 colloq. Pass (a red traffic-light etc.). 8 (often foll. By out) slang sleep, esp. On a floor etc. â€”n. 1 loud and sudden smashing noise. 2 violent collision or fall, esp. Of a vehicle. 3 ruin, esp. Financial. 4 computing sudden failure of a machine or system. 5 (attrib.) Done rapidly or urgently (crash course in first aid). â€”adv. With a crash (go crash). [imitative]'),
(9883, 'c', '\r\nCrash2', ' n. Coarse plain fabric of linen, cotton, etc. [russian]'),
(9884, 'c', '\r\nCrash', 'barrier  n. Barrier at the side or centre of a road etc.'),
(9885, 'c', '\r\nCrash-dive', ' â€”v. 1 a (of a submarine or its pilot) dive hastily in an emergency. B (of an aircraft or airman) dive and crash. 2 cause to crash-dive. â€”n. Such a dive.'),
(9886, 'c', '\r\nCrash-helmet', ' n. Helmet worn esp. By motor cyclists.'),
(9887, 'c', '\r\nCrashing', ' adj. Colloq. Overwhelming (crashing bore).'),
(9888, 'c', '\r\nCrash-land', ' v. Land or cause (an aircraft etc.) To land hurriedly with a crash.  crash landing n.'),
