(9898, 'c', '\r\nCraw', ' n. Crop of a bird or insect.  stick in one''s craw be unacceptable. [low german or dutch]'),
(9899, 'c', '\r\nCrawfish', ' n. (pl. Same) large marine spiny lobster. [var. Of *crayfish]'),
(9900, 'c', '\r\nCrawl', ' â€”v. 1 move slowly, esp. On hands and knees or with the body close to the ground etc. 2 walk or move slowly. 3 colloq. Behave obsequiously. 4 (often foll. By with) be or appear to be covered or filled with crawling or moving things or people. 5 (esp. Of the skin) creep. â€”n. 1 crawling. 2 slow rate of movement. 3 high-speed overarm swimming stroke. [origin unknown]'),
(9901, 'c', '\r\nCrayfish', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 small lobster-like freshwater crustacean. 2 crawfish. [french crevice]'),
(9902, 'c', '\r\nCrayon', ' â€”n. Stick or pencil of coloured chalk, wax, etc. â€”v. Draw with crayons. [french craie chalk]'),
(9903, 'c', '\r\nCraze', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 (usu. As crazed adj.) Make insane (crazed with grief). 2 produce fine surface cracks on (pottery glaze etc.); develop such cracks. â€”n. 1 usu. Temporary enthusiasm (craze for skateboarding). 2 object of this. [perhaps from old norse]'),
(9904, 'c', '\r\nCrazy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 colloq. Insane or mad; foolish. 2 (usu. Foll. By about) colloq. Extremely enthusiastic. 3 (attrib.) (of paving etc.) Made up of irregular pieces.  crazily adv. Craziness n.'),
(9905, 'c', '\r\nCreak', ' â€”n. Harsh scraping or squeaking sound. â€”v. 1 make a creak. 2 a move stiffly or with a creaking noise. B be poorly constructed (plot creaks). [imitative]'),
(9906, 'c', '\r\nCreaky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 liable to creak. 2 a stiff or frail. B decrepit, outmoded.  creakiness n.'),
(9907, 'c', '\r\nCream', ' â€”n. 1 fatty part of milk. 2 its yellowish-white colour. 3 creamlike cosmetic etc. 4 food or drink like or containing cream. 5 (usu. Prec. By the) best part of something. â€”v. 1 take cream from (milk). 2 make creamy. 3 treat (the skin etc.) With cosmetic cream. 4 form a cream or scum. â€”adj. Pale yellowish white.  cream off take (esp. The best part) from a whole. [latin cramum and church latin chrisma oil for anointing]'),
(9908, 'c', '\r\nCream', 'cheese  n. Soft rich cheese made from cream and unskimmed milk.'),
(9909, 'c', '\r\nCreamer', ' n. 1 cream-substitute for adding to coffee. 2 jug for cream.'),
(9910, 'c', '\r\nCreamery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 factory producing butter and cheese. 2 dairy.'),
(9911, 'c', '\r\nCream', 'of tartar  n. Purified tartar, used in medicine, baking powder, etc.'),
(9912, 'c', '\r\nCream', 'soda  n. Carbonated vanilla-flavoured soft drink.'),
(9913, 'c', '\r\nCream', 'tea  n. Afternoon tea with scones, jam, and cream.'),
(9914, 'c', '\r\nCreamy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like cream. 2 rich in cream.  creamily adv. Creaminess n.'),
(9915, 'c', '\r\nCrease', ' â€”n. 1 line caused by folding or crushing. 2 cricket line marking the position of a bowler or batsman. â€”v. (-sing) 1 make creases in. 2 develop creases. 3 slang (often foll. By up) make or become incapable through laughter. [from *crest]'),
(9916, 'c', '\r\nCreate', ' v. (-ting) 1 bring into existence; cause. 2 originate (actor creates a part). 3 invest with rank (created him a lord). 4 slang make a fuss. [latin creo]'),
(9917, 'c', '\r\nCreation', ' n. 1 creating or being created. 2 a (usu. The creation) god''s creating of the universe. B (usu. Creation) all created things, the universe. 3 product of the imagination, art, fashion, etc.'),
(9918, 'c', '\r\nCreative', ' adj. 1 inventive, imaginative. 2 able to create.  creatively adv. Creativeness n. Creativity n.'),
(9919, 'c', '\r\nCreator', ' n. 1 person who creates. 2 (as the creator) god.'),
(9920, 'c', '\r\nCreature', ' n. 1 any living being, esp. An animal. 2 person of a specified kind (poor creature). 3 subservient person.  creaturely adj. [french from latin: related to *create]'),
(9921, 'c', '\r\nCreature', 'comforts  n.pl. Good food, warmth, etc.'),
(9922, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨che', ' n. Day nursery. [french]'),
(9923, 'c', '\r\nCredence', ' n. Belief.   give credence to believe. [medieval latin: related to *credo]'),
(9924, 'c', '\r\nCredential', ' n. (usu. In pl.) 1 certificates, references, etc., attesting to a person''s education, character, etc. 2 letter(s) of introduction. [medieval latin: related to *credence]'),
(9925, 'c', '\r\nCredibility', ' n. 1 being credible. 2 reputation, status.'),
(9926, 'c', '\r\nCredibility', 'gap  n. Apparent difference between what is said and what is true.'),
(9927, 'c', '\r\nCredible', ' adj. Believable or worthy of belief. [latin: related to *credo]'),
(9928, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' credible is sometimes confused with credulous.'),
(9929, 'c', '\r\nCredit', ' â€”n. 1 source of honour, pride, etc. (is a credit to the school). 2 acknowledgement of merit. 3 good reputation. 4 belief or trust. 5 a person''s financial standing, esp. As regards money in the bank etc. B power to obtain goods etc. Before payment. 6 (usu. In pl.) Acknowledgement of a contributor''s services to a film etc. 7 grade above pass in an examination. 8 reputation for solvency and honesty in business. 9 a entry in an account of a sum paid into it. B sum entered. C side of an account recording such entries. 10 educational course counting towards a degree. â€”v. (-t-) 1 believe (cannot credit it). 2 (usu. Foll. By to, with) enter on the credit side of an account.  credit a person with ascribe (a good quality) to a person. Do credit to (or do a person credit) enhance the reputation of. On credit with an arrangement to pay later. To one''s credit in one''s favour. [italian or latin: related to *credo]'),
(9930, 'c', '\r\nCreditable', ' adj. Bringing credit or honour.  creditably adv.'),
(9931, 'c', '\r\nCredit', 'card  n. Plastic card from a bank etc. Authorizing the purchase of goods on credit.'),
(9932, 'c', '\r\nCredit', 'note  n. Note with a specific monetary value given by a shop etc. For goods returned.'),
(9933, 'c', '\r\nCreditor', ' n. Person to whom a debt is owing. [latin: related to *credit]'),
(9934, 'c', '\r\nCredit', 'rating  n. Estimate of a person''s suitability for commercial credit.'),
(9935, 'c', '\r\nCreditworthy', ' adj. Considered suitable to receive commercial credit.  creditworthiness n.'),
(9936, 'c', '\r\nCredo', ' n. (pl. -s) creed. [latin, = i believe]'),
(9937, 'c', '\r\nCredulous', ' adj. Too ready to believe; gullible.  credulity n. Credulously adv. [latin: related to *credo]'),
(9938, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' credulous is sometimes confused with credible.'),
(9939, 'c', '\r\nCreed', ' n. 1 set of principles or beliefs. 2 system of religious belief. 3 (often the creed) formal summary of christian doctrine. [latin: related to *credo]'),
(9940, 'c', '\r\nCreek', ' n. 1 a inlet on a sea-coast. B short arm of a river. 2 esp. Us, austral., & nz tributary of a river; stream.  up the creek slang 1 in difficulties. 2 crazy. [old norse and dutch]'),
(9941, 'c', '\r\nCreel', ' n. Fisherman''s large wicker basket. [origin unknown]'),
(9942, 'c', '\r\nCreep', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Crept) 1 move with the body prone and close to the ground. 2 move stealthily or timidly. 3 advance very gradually (a feeling crept over her). 4 colloq. Act obsequiously in the hope of advancement. 5 (of a plant) grow along the ground or up a wall etc. 6 (as creeping adj.) Developing slowly and steadily. 7 (of flesh) shiver or shudder from fear, horror, etc. â€”n. 1 act or spell of creeping. 2 (in pl.; prec. By the) colloq. Feeling of revulsion or fear. 3 slang unpleasant person. 4 (of metals etc.) Gradual change of shape under stress. [old english]'),
(9943, 'c', '\r\nCreeper', ' n. 1 climbing or creeping plant. 2 bird that climbs, esp. The treecreeper. 3 slang soft-soled shoe.'),
(9944, 'c', '\r\nCreepy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Feeling or causing horror or fear.  creepily adv. Creepiness n.'),
(9945, 'c', '\r\nCreepy-crawly', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Small crawling insect etc.'),
(9946, 'c', '\r\nCremate', ' v. (-ting) burn (a corpse etc.) To ashes.  cremation n. [latin cremo burn]'),
(9947, 'c', '\r\nCrematorium', ' n. (pl. -ria or -s) place where corpses are cremated.'),
(9948, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', ' n. 1 = *cream n. 4. 2 liqueur (crÃ¨me de cassis). [french, = cream]'),
(9949, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', 'brÃ»lÃ©e  n. Baked cream or custard pudding coated with caramel.'),
(9950, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', 'caramel  n. Custard coated with caramel.'),
(9951, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', 'de cassis  n. Blackcurrant liqueur.'),
(9952, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', 'de la crÃ¨me  n. Best part; Ã©lite.'),
(9953, 'c', '\r\nCrÃ¨me', 'de menthe  n.  Peppermint liqueur.'),
(9954, 'c', '\r\nCrenelate', ' v. (brit. Crenellate) (-ting) provide (a tower etc.) With battlements.  crenellation n. [french crenel embrasure]'),
(9955, 'c', '\r\nCrenellate', ' v. (us crenelate) (-ting) provide (a tower etc.) With battlements.  crenellation n. [french crenel embrasure]'),
(9956, 'c', '\r\nCreole', ' â€”n. 1 a descendant of european settlers in the w. Indies or central or s. America. B white descendant of french settlers in the southern us. C person of mixed european and black descent. 2 language formed from a european language and another (esp. African) language. â€”adj. 1 of creoles. 2 (usu. Creole) of creole origin etc. (creole cooking). [french from spanish]'),
(9957, 'c', '\r\nCreosote', ' â€”n. 1 dark-brown oil distilled from coal tar, used as a wood-preservative. 2 oily fluid distilled from wood tar, used as an antiseptic. â€”v. (-ting) treat with creosote. [greek kreas flesh, soter preserver, because of its antiseptic properties]'),
(9958, 'c', '\r\nCrÃªpe', ' n. 1 fine gauzy wrinkled fabric. 2 thin pancake with a savoury or sweet filling. 3 hard-wearing wrinkled sheet rubber used for the soles of shoes etc.  crÃªpey adj. CrÃªpy adj. [latin: related to *crisp]'),
(9959, 'c', '\r\nCrÃªpe', 'de chine  n. Fine silk crÃªpe.'),
(9960, 'c', '\r\nCrÃªpe', 'paper  n. Thin crinkled paper.'),
(9961, 'c', '\r\nCrÃªpe', 'suzette  n. Small dessert pancake flamed in alcohol.'),
(9962, 'c', '\r\nCrept', ' past and past part. Of *creep.'),
(9963, 'c', '\r\nCrepuscular', ' adj. 1 a of twilight. B dim. 2 zool. Appearing or active in twilight. [latin crepusculum twilight]'),
(9964, 'c', '\r\nCres.', ' Abbr. Crescent.'),
(9965, 'c', '\r\nCresc.', ' Abbr. (also cres.) Mus. = *crescendo.'),
(9966, 'c', '\r\nCrescendo', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 mus. Gradual increase in loudness. 2 progress towards a climax. â€”adv. & adj. Increasing in loudness. [italian: related to *crescent]'),
(9967, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' crescendo is sometimes wrongly used to mean the climax itself rather than progress towards it.'),
(9968, 'c', '\r\nCrescent', ' â€”n. 1 curved sickle shape as of the waxing or waning moon. 2 thing of this shape, esp. A street forming an arc. â€”adj. Crescent-shaped. [latin cresco grow]'),
(9969, 'c', '\r\nCress', ' n. Any of various plants with pungent edible leaves. [old english]'),
(9970, 'c', '\r\nCrest', ' â€”n. 1 a comb or tuft etc. On a bird''s or animal''s head. B plume etc. On a helmet etc. 2 top of a mountain, wave, roof, etc. 3 heraldry a device above a coat of arms. B such a device on writing-paper etc. â€”v. 1 reach the crest of. 2 provide with a crest or serve as a crest to. 3 (of a wave) form a crest.  crested adj. [latin crista]'),
(9971, 'c', '\r\nCrestfallen', ' adj. Dejected, dispirited.'),
(9972, 'c', '\r\nCretaceous', ' â€”adj. 1 of or like chalk. 2 (cretaceous) geol. Of the last period of the mesozoic era, with deposits of chalk. â€”n. (cretaceous) geol. This era or system. [latin creta chalk]'),
(9973, 'c', '\r\nCretin', ' n. 1 deformed and mentally retarded person, esp. As the result of thyroid deficiency. 2 colloq. Stupid person.  cretinism n. Cretinous adj. [french crÃ©tin: related to *christian]'),
(9974, 'c', '\r\nCretonne', ' n. (often attrib.) Heavy cotton upholstery fabric, usu. With a floral pattern. [creton in normandy]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(9975, 'c', '\r\nCrevasse', ' n. Deep open crack, esp. In a glacier. [latin crepo crack]'),
(9976, 'c', '\r\nCrevice', ' n. Narrow opening or fissure, esp. In rock etc. [french: related to *crevasse]'),
(9977, 'c', '\r\nCrew1', ' â€”n. (often treated as pl.) 1 a people manning a ship, aircraft, train, etc. B these as distinct from the captain or officers. C people working together; team. 2 colloq. Gang. â€”v. 1 supply or act as a crew or crew member for. 2 act as a crew. [latin cresco increase]'),
(9978, 'c', '\r\nCrew2', ' past of *crow2.'),
(9979, 'c', '\r\nCrew', 'cut  n. Close-cropped hairstyle.'),
(9980, 'c', '\r\nCrewel', ' n. Thin worsted yarn for tapestry and embroidery. [origin unknown]'),
(9981, 'c', '\r\nCrewel-work', ' n. Design in crewel.'),
(9982, 'c', '\r\nCrew', 'neck  n. Round close-fitting neckline.'),
(9983, 'c', '\r\nCrib', ' â€”n. 1 a baby''s small bed or cot. B model of the nativity with a manger. 2 rack for animal fodder. 3  colloq. A translation of a text used by students. B plagiarized work etc. 4 colloq. A cribbage. B set of cards given to the dealer at cribbage. â€”v. (-bb-) (also absol.) 1 colloq. Copy unfairly. 2 confine in a small space. 3 colloq. Pilfer. [old english]'),
(9984, 'c', '\r\nCribbage', ' n. Card-game for up to four players. [origin unknown]'),
(9985, 'c', '\r\nCrick', ' â€”n. Sudden painful stiffness, esp. In the neck. â€”v. Cause this in. [origin unknown]'),
(9986, 'c', '\r\nCricket1', ' n. Team game played on a grass pitch, with bowling at a wicket defended by a batting player of the other team.  not cricket colloq. Unfair behaviour.  cricketer n. [origin uncertain]'),
(9987, 'c', '\r\nCricket2', ' n. Grasshopper-like chirping insect. [french, imitative]'),
(9988, 'c', '\r\nCri', 'de cÅ“ur  n. (pl. Cris de cÅ“ur pronunc. Same) passionate appeal, protest, etc. [french, = cry from the heart]'),
(9989, 'c', '\r\nCried', ' past and past part. Of *cry.'),
(9990, 'c', '\r\nCrier', ' n. (also cryer) 1 person who cries. 2 official making public announcements in a lawcourt or street. [related to *cry]'),
(9991, 'c', '\r\nCrikey', ' int. Slang expression of astonishment. [from *christ]'),
(9992, 'c', '\r\nCrime', ' n. 1 a offence punishable by law. B illegal acts (resorted to crime). 2 evil act (crime against humanity). 3 colloq. Shameful act. [latin crimen]'),
(9993, 'c', '\r\nCriminal', ' â€”n. Person guilty of a crime. â€”adj. 1 of, involving, or concerning crime. 2 guilty of crime. 3 law of or concerning criminal offences (criminal code; criminal lawyer). 4 colloq. Scandalous, deplorable.  criminality n. Criminally adv. [latin: related to *crime]'),
(9994, 'c', '\r\nCriminology', ' n. The study of crime.  criminologist n.'),
(9995, 'c', '\r\nCrimp', ' â€”v. 1 press into small folds; corrugate. 2 make waves in (hair). â€”n. Crimped thing or form. [low german or dutch]'),
(9996, 'c', '\r\nCrimplene', ' n. Propr. Synthetic crease-resistant fabric.'),
(9997, 'c', '\r\nCrimson', ' â€”adj. Of a rich deep red. â€”n. This colour. [ultimately from arabic: related to *kermes]'),
(9998, 'c', '\r\nCringe', ' v. (-ging) 1 shrink in fear; cower. 2 (often foll. By to) behave obsequiously. [related to *crank]'),
(9999, 'c', '\r\nCrinkle', ' â€”n. Wrinkle or crease. â€”v. (-ling) form crinkles (in).  crinkly adj. [related to *cringe]'),
(10000, 'c', '\r\nCrinkle-cut', ' adj. (of vegetables) with wavy edges.'),
(10001, 'c', '\r\nCrinoline', ' n. 1 hist. Stiffened or hooped petticoat. 2 stiff fabric of horsehair etc. Used for linings, hats, etc. [french from latin crinis hair, linum thread]'),
(10002, 'c', '\r\nCripple', ' â€”n. Permanently lame person. â€”v. (-ling) 1 make a cripple of; lame. 2 disable, weaken, or damage seriously (crippled by strikes). [old english]'),
(10003, 'c', '\r\nCrisis', ' n. (pl. Crises) 1 time of danger or great difficulty. 2 decisive moment; turning-point. [greek, = decision]'),
(10004, 'c', '\r\nCrisp', ' â€”adj. 1 hard but brittle. 2 a (of air) bracing. B (of style or manner) lively, brisk and decisive. C (of features etc.) Neat, clear-cut. D (of paper) stiff and crackling. E (of hair) closely curling. â€”n. (in full potato crisp) potato sliced thinly, fried, and sold in packets. â€”v. Make or become crisp.  crisply adv. Crispness n. [latin crispus curled]'),
(10005, 'c', '\r\nCrispbread', ' n. 1 thin crisp biscuit of crushed rye etc. 2 these collectively (packet of crispbread).'),
(10006, 'c', '\r\nCrispy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) crisp.  crispiness n.'),
(10007, 'c', '\r\nCriss-cross', ' â€”n. Pattern of crossing lines. â€”adj. Crossing; in cross lines. â€”adv. Crosswise; at cross purposes. â€”v. 1 a intersect repeatedly. B move crosswise. 2 mark or make with a criss-cross pattern. [christ''s cross]'),
(10008, 'c', '\r\nCriterion', ' n. (pl. -ria) principle or standard of judgement. [greek, = means of judging]'),
(10009, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' the plural form of criterion, criteria, is often used incorrectly as the singular. In the singular criterion should always be used.'),
(10010, 'c', '\r\nCritic', ' n. 1 person who criticizes. 2 person who reviews literary, artistic, etc. Works. [latin criticus from greek krites judge]'),
(10011, 'c', '\r\nCritical', ' adj. 1 a fault-finding, censorious. B expressing or involving criticism. 2 skilful at or engaged in criticism. 3 providing textual criticism (critical edition of milton). 4 a of or at a crisis; dangerous, risky (in a critical condition). B decisive, crucial (at the critical moment). 5 a math. & physics marking a transition from one state etc. To another (critical angle). B (of a nuclear reactor) maintaining a self-sustaining chain reaction.  critically adv. Criticalness n.'),
(10012, 'c', '\r\nCritical', 'path  n. Sequence of stages determining the minimum time needed for an operation.'),
(10013, 'c', '\r\nCriticism', ' n. 1 a fault-finding; censure. B critical remark etc. 2 a work of a critic. B analytical article, essay, etc.'),
(10014, 'c', '\r\nCriticize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) (also absol.) 1 find fault with; censure. 2 discuss critically.'),
(10015, 'c', '\r\nCritique', ' n. Critical analysis. [french: related to *critic]'),
(10016, 'c', '\r\nCroak', ' â€”n. Deep hoarse sound, esp. Of a frog. â€”v. 1 utter or speak with a croak. 2 slang die. [imitative]'),
(10017, 'c', '\r\nCroaky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) croaking; hoarse.  croakily adv. Croakiness n.'),
(10018, 'c', '\r\nCroat', ' (also croatian) â€”n. 1 a native of croatia in se europe. B person of croatian descent. 2 slavonic dialect of the croats. â€”adj. Of the croats or their dialect. [serbo-croatian hrvat]'),
(10019, 'c', '\r\nCrochet', ' â€”n. Needlework in which yarn is hooked to make a lacy patterned fabric. â€”v. (crocheted; crocheting) (also absol.) Make using crochet. [french: related to *crotchet]'),
(10020, 'c', '\r\nCrock1', ' n. Colloq. Old or worn-out person or vehicle. [originally scots]'),
(10021, 'c', '\r\nCrock2', ' n. 1 earthenware pot or jar. 2 broken piece of this. [old english]'),
(10022, 'c', '\r\nCrockery', ' n. Earthenware or china dishes, plates, etc. [related to *crock2]'),
(10023, 'c', '\r\nCrocodile', ' n. 1 a large tropical amphibious reptile with thick scaly skin, a long tail, and long jaws. B (often attrib.) Its skin. 2 colloq. Line of schoolchildren etc. Walking in pairs. [greek krokodilos]'),
(10024, 'c', '\r\nCrocodile', 'tears  n.pl. Insincere grief.'),
(10025, 'c', '\r\nCrocus', ' n. (pl. -cuses) small plant with white, yellow, or purple flowers, growing from a corm. [latin from greek]'),
(10026, 'c', '\r\nCroesus', ' n. Person of great wealth. [name of a king of ancient lydia]'),
(10027, 'c', '\r\nCroft', ' â€”n. 1 enclosed piece of (usu. Arable) land. 2 small rented farm in scotland or n. England. â€”v. Farm a croft; live as a crofter. [old english]'),
(10028, 'c', '\r\nCrofter', ' n. Person who farms a croft.'),
(10029, 'c', '\r\nCrohn''s', 'disease  n. Chronic inflammatory disease of the alimentary tract. [e. Crohn, name of a us pathologist]'),
(10030, 'c', '\r\nCroissant', ' n. Crescent-shaped breakfast roll. [french: related to *crescent]'),
(10031, 'c', '\r\nCromlech', ' n. 1 dolmen. 2 prehistoric stone circle. [welsh]'),
(10032, 'c', '\r\nCrone', ' n. Withered old woman. [dutch croonje carcass]'),
(10033, 'c', '\r\nCrony', ' n. (pl. -ies) friend, companion. [greek khronios long-lasting]'),
(10034, 'c', '\r\nCrook', ' â€”n. 1 hooked staff of a shepherd or bishop. 2 a bend, curve, or hook. B hooked or curved thing. 3 colloq. Rogue; swindler; criminal. â€”v. Bend, curve. [old norse]'),
(10035, 'c', '\r\nCrooked', ' adj. (-er, -est) 1 not straight or level; bent. 2 colloq. Not straightforward; dishonest, criminal.  crookedly adv. Crookedness n.'),
(10036, 'c', '\r\nCroon', ' â€”v. Sing, hum, or say in a low sentimental voice. â€”n. Such singing etc.  crooner n. [low german or dutch]'),
(10037, 'c', '\r\nCrop', ' â€”n. 1 a produce of cultivated plants, esp. Cereals. B season''s yield. 2 group, yield, etc., of one time or place (a new crop of students). 3 handle of a whip. 4 a very short haircut. B cropping of hair. 5 pouch in a bird''s gullet where food is prepared for digestion. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 a cut off. B bite off. 2 cut (hair etc.) Short. 3 (foll. By with) sow or plant (land) with a crop. 4 (of land) bear a crop.  crop up occur unexpectedly. [old english]'),
(10038, 'c', '\r\nCrop', 'circle  n. Circle of crops that has been inexplicably flattened.'),
(10039, 'c', '\r\nCrop-eared', ' adj. With the ears (esp. Of animals) or hair cut short.'),
(10040, 'c', '\r\nCropper', ' n. Crop-producing plant of a specified quality.  come a cropper slang fall heavily; fail badly.'),
(10041, 'c', '\r\nCroquet', ' â€”n. 1 lawn game in which wooden balls are driven through hoops with mallets. 2 act of croqueting a ball. â€”v. (croqueted; croqueting) drive away (an opponent''s ball) by placing and then striking one''s own against it. [perhaps a dial. Form of french crochet hook]'),
(10042, 'c', '\r\nCroquette', ' n. Ball of breaded and fried mashed potato etc. [french croquer crunch]'),
(10043, 'c', '\r\nCrosier', ' n. (also crozier) bishop''s ceremonial hooked staff. [french croisier cross-bearer and crossier crook-bearer]'),
(10044, 'c', '\r\nCross', ' â€”n. 1 upright post with a transverse bar, as used in antiquity for crucifixion. 2 a (the cross) cross on which christ was crucified. B representation of this as an emblem of christianity. C = *sign of the cross. 3 staff surmounted by a cross, carried in a religious procession. 4 thing or mark like a cross, esp. Two short intersecting lines (+ or x). 5 cross-shaped military etc. Decoration. 6 a hybrid. B crossing of breeds etc. 7 (foll. By between) mixture of two things. 8  crosswise movement, pass in football, etc. 9 trial or affliction. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By over) go across. 2 intersect; (cause to) be across (roads cross; cross one''s legs). 3 a draw line(s) across. B mark (a cheque) with two parallel lines to indicate that it cannot be cashed. 4 (foll. By off, out, through) cancel etc. By drawing lines across. 5 (often refl.) Make the sign of the cross on or over. 6 a pass in opposite or different directions. B (of letters etc.) Be sent at the same time. C (of telephone lines) be connected to an unwanted conversation. 7 a cause to interbreed. B cross-fertilize (plants). 8 oppose or thwart (crossed in love). â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By with) peevish, angry. 2 (usu. Attrib.) Transverse; reaching from side to side. 3 (usu. Attrib.) Intersecting. 4 (usu. Attrib.) Contrary, opposed, reciprocal.  at cross purposes misunderstanding; conflicting. Cross one''s fingers (or keep one''s fingers crossed) 1 put one finger across another to ward off bad luck. 2 trust in good luck. Cross one''s heart make a solemn pledge, esp. By crossing one''s front. Cross one''s mind occur to one, esp. Transiently. Cross swords (often foll. By with) argue or dispute. Cross wires (or get one''s wires crossed) 1 become wrongly connected by telephone. 2 have a misunderstanding. On the cross diagonally.  crossly adv. Crossness n. [latin crux]'),
(10045, 'c', '\r\nCrossbar', ' n. Horizontal bar, esp. That on a man''s bicycle.'),
(10046, 'c', '\r\nCross-bench', ' n. Seat in the house of lords for non-party members.  cross-bencher n.'),
(10047, 'c', '\r\nCrossbill', ' n. Finch with a bill with crossed mandibles for opening pine cones.'),
(10048, 'c', '\r\nCrossbones', ' see *skull and crossbones.'),
(10049, 'c', '\r\nCrossbow', ' n. Bow fixed on a wooden stock, with a groove for an arrow.'),
(10050, 'c', '\r\nCrossbreed', ' â€”n. 1 hybrid breed of animals or plants. 2 individual hybrid. â€”v. Produce by crossing.'),
(10051, 'c', '\r\nCross-check', ' â€”v. Check by alternative method(s). â€”n. Such a check.'),
(10052, 'c', '\r\nCross-country', ' â€”adj. & adv. 1 across open country. 2 not keeping to main roads. â€”n. (pl. -ies) cross-country race.'),
(10053, 'c', '\r\nCross-cut', ' â€”adj. Cut across the main grain. â€”n. Diagonal cut, path, etc.'),
(10054, 'c', '\r\nCross-cut', 'saw  n. Saw for cross-cutting.'),
(10055, 'c', '\r\nCross-dressing', ' n. Practice of dressing in the clothes of the opposite sex.  cross-dress v.'),
(10056, 'c', '\r\nCrosse', ' n. Lacrosse stick. [french]'),
(10057, 'c', '\r\nCross-examine', ' v. Question (esp. An opposing witness in a lawcourt).  cross-examination n.'),
(10058, 'c', '\r\nCross-eyed', ' adj. Having one or both eyes turned inwards.'),
(10059, 'c', '\r\nCross-fertilize', ' v. (also -ise) 1 fertilize (an animal or plant) from one of a different species. 2 interchange ideas etc.  cross-fertilization n.'),
(10060, 'c', '\r\nCrossfire', ' n. 1 firing in two crossing directions simultaneously. 2 a attack or criticism from all sides. B combative exchange of views etc.'),
(10061, 'c', '\r\nCross-grain', ' n. Grain in timber, running across the regular grain.'),
(10062, 'c', '\r\nCross-grained', ' adj. 1 having a cross-grain. 2 perverse, intractable.'),
(10063, 'c', '\r\nCross-hatch', ' v. Shade with crossing parallel lines.'),
(10064, 'c', '\r\nCrossing', ' n. 1 place where things (esp. Roads) cross. 2 place for crossing a street etc. 3 journey across water.'),
(10065, 'c', '\r\nCross-legged', ' adj. (sitting) with legs folded one across the other.'),
(10066, 'c', '\r\nCrossover', ' â€”n. 1 point or place of crossing. 2 process of crossing over, esp. From one style or genre to another. â€”attrib. Adj. That crosses over, esp. From one style or genre to another.'),
(10067, 'c', '\r\nCrosspatch', ' n. Colloq. Bad-tempered person.'),
(10068, 'c', '\r\nCrosspiece', ' n. Transverse beam etc.'),
(10069, 'c', '\r\nCross-ply', ' adj. (of a tyre) having fabric layers with crosswise cords.'),
(10070, 'c', '\r\nCross-question', ' v. = *cross-examine.'),
(10071, 'c', '\r\nCross-refer', ' v. (-rr-) refer from one part of a book etc. To another.'),
(10072, 'c', '\r\nCross-reference', ' â€”n. Reference from one part of a book etc. To another. â€”v. Provide with cross-references.'),
(10073, 'c', '\r\nCrossroad', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Intersection of two or more roads.  at the crossroads at the critical point.'),
(10074, 'c', '\r\nCross-section', ' n. 1 a a cutting across a solid. B plane surface so produced. C drawing etc. Of this. 2 representative sample.  cross-sectional adj.'),
(10075, 'c', '\r\nCross-stitch', ' n. Cross-shaped stitch.'),
(10076, 'c', '\r\nCrosstalk', ' n. 1 unwanted signals between communication channels. 2 witty repartee.'),
(10077, 'c', '\r\nCrossways', ' adv. = *crosswise.'),
(10078, 'c', '\r\nCrosswind', ' n. Wind blowing across one''s path etc.'),
(10079, 'c', '\r\nCrosswise', ' adj. & adv. 1 in the form of a cross; intersecting. 2 diagonal or diagonally.'),
(10080, 'c', '\r\nCrossword', ' n. (also crossword puzzle) printed grid of squares and blanks for vertical and horizontal words to be filled in from clues.'),
(10081, 'c', '\r\nCrotch', ' n. Fork, esp. Between legs (of a person, trousers, etc.). [related to *crook]'),
(10082, 'c', '\r\nCrotchet', ' n. Mus. Note equal to a quarter of a semibreve and usu. One beat. [french diminutive of croc: related to *crook]'),
(10083, 'c', '\r\nCrotchety', ' adj. Peevish, irritable.'),
(10084, 'c', '\r\nCrouch', ' â€”v. Lower the body with limbs close to the chest; be in this position. â€”n. Crouching; crouching position. [old norse: related to *crook]'),
(10085, 'c', '\r\nCroup1', ' n. Childhood inflammation of the larynx etc., with a hard cough. [imitative]'),
(10086, 'c', '\r\nCroup2', ' n. Rump, esp. Of a horse. [french: related to *crop]'),
(10087, 'c', '\r\nCroupier', ' n. Person running a gaming-table, raking in and paying out money etc. [french: related to *croup2]'),
(10088, 'c', '\r\nCroÃ»ton', ' n. Small cube of fried or toasted bread served with soup etc. [french: related to *crust]'),
(10089, 'c', '\r\nCrow1', ' n. 1 large black bird with a powerful black beak. 2 similar bird, e.g. The raven, rook, and jackdaw.  as the crow flies in a straight line. [old english]'),
(10090, 'c', '\r\nCrow2', ' â€”v. 1 (past crowed or crew) (of a cock) utter a loud cry. 2 (of a baby) utter happy cries. 3 (usu. Foll. By over) gloat; show glee. â€”n. Cry of a cock or baby. [old english]'),
(10091, 'c', '\r\nCrowbar', ' n. Iron bar with a flattened end, used as a lever.'),
(10092, 'c', '\r\nCrowd', ' â€”n. 1 large gathering of people. 2 spectators; audience. 3 colloq. Particular set of people. 4 (prec. By the) majority. â€”v. 1 a (cause to) come together in a crowd. B force one''s way (crowded into the cinema). 2 a (foll. By into) force or compress into a confined space. B (often foll. By with; usu. In passive) fill or make full of. 3 colloq. Come aggressively close to.  crowd out exclude by crowding.  crowdedness n. [old english]'),
(10093, 'c', '\r\nCrown', ' â€”n. 1 monarch''s jewelled headdress. 2 (the crown) a monarch as head of state. B power or authority of the monarchy. 3 a wreath for the head as an emblem of victory. B award or distinction, esp. In sport. 4 crown-shaped ornament etc. 5 top part of the head, a hat, etc. 6 a highest or central part (crown of the road). B thing that completes or forms a summit. 7 a part of a tooth visible outside the gum. B artificial replacement for this. 8 former british coin worth five shillings. â€”v. 1 put a crown on (a person or head). 2 invest with a royal crown or authority. 3 be a crown to; rest on top of. 4 a (often as crowning adj.) (cause to) be the reward, summit, or finishing touch to (crowning glory). B bring to a happy outcome. 5 fit a crown to (a tooth). 6 slang hit on the head. 7 promote (a piece in draughts) to king. [latin corona]'),
(10094, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'colony  n. British colony controlled by the crown.'),
(10095, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'court  n. Court of criminal jurisdiction in england and wales.'),
(10096, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'derby  n. Porcelain made at derby and often marked with a crown.'),
(10097, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'glass  n. Glass without lead or iron used formerly in windows, now as optical glass of low refractive index.'),
(10098, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'jewels  n.pl. Sovereign''s state regalia etc.'),
(10099, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'prince  n. Male heir to a throne.'),
(10100, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'princess  n. 1 wife of a crown prince. 2 female heir to a throne.'),
(10101, 'c', '\r\nCrown', 'wheel  n. Wheel with teeth at right angles to its plane.'),
(10102, 'c', '\r\nCrow''s-foot', ' n. Wrinkle near the eye.'),
(10103, 'c', '\r\nCrow''s-nest', ' n. Shelter at a sailing-ship''s masthead for a lookout man.'),
(10104, 'c', '\r\nCrozier', ' var. Of *crosier.'),
(10105, 'c', '\r\nCrt', ' abbr. Cathode-ray tube.'),
(10106, 'c', '\r\nCru', ' n. 1 french vineyard or wine region. 2 grade of wine. [french crÃ» grown]'),
(10107, 'c', '\r\nCruces', ' pl. Of *crux.'),
(10108, 'c', '\r\nCrucial', ' adj. 1 decisive, critical. 2 very important.  crucially adv. [latin crux crucis cross]'),
(10109, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of crucial in sense 2 should be restricted to informal contexts.'),
(10110, 'c', '\r\nCrucible', ' n. 1 melting-pot for metals etc. 2 severe test. [medieval latin: related to *crucial]'),
(10111, 'c', '\r\nCruciferous', ' adj. Having flowers with four petals arranged in a cross. [latin: related to *crucial]'),
(10112, 'c', '\r\nCrucifix', ' n. Model of a cross with the figure of christ on it. [latin cruci fixus fixed to a cross]'),
(10113, 'c', '\r\nCrucifixion', ' n. 1 crucifying or being crucified. 2 (crucifixion) crucifixion of christ. [church latin: related to *crucifix]'),
(10114, 'c', '\r\nCruciform', ' adj. Cross-shaped. [latin crux crucis cross]'),
(10115, 'c', '\r\nCrucify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 put to death by fastening to a cross. 2 persecute, torment. 3 slang defeat thoroughly; humiliate. [french: related to *crucifix]'),
(10116, 'c', '\r\nCrud', ' n. Slang 1 deposit of grease etc. 2 unpleasant person.  cruddy adj. (-ier, -iest). [var. Of *curd]'),
(10117, 'c', '\r\nCrude', ' â€”adj. 1 a in the natural state; not refined. B unpolished; lacking finish. 2 a rude, blunt. B offensive, indecent. 3 inexact. â€”n. Natural mineral oil.  crudely adv. Crudeness n. Crudity n. [latin crudus raw]'),
(10118, 'c', '\r\nCruditÃ©s', ' n.pl. Hors d''Å“uvre of mixed raw vegetables. [french]'),
(10119, 'c', '\r\nCruel', ' adj. (crueller, cruellest or crueler, cruelest) 1 causing pain or suffering, esp. Deliberately. 2 harsh, severe (a cruel blow).  cruelly adv. Cruelness n. Cruelty n. (pl. -ies). [latin: related to *crude]'),
(10120, 'c', '\r\nCruet', ' n. 1 set of small salt, pepper, etc. Containers for use at table. 2 such a container. [anglo-french diminutive: related to *crock2]'),
(10121, 'c', '\r\nCruise', ' â€”v. (-sing) 1 a travel by sea for pleasure, calling at ports. B sail about. 2 travel at a relaxed or economical speed. 3 achieve an objective, esp. Win a race etc. With ease. 4 slang search for a sexual (esp. Homosexual) partner in bars, streets, etc. â€”n. Cruising voyage. [dutch: related to *cross]'),
(10122, 'c', '\r\nCruise', 'missile  n. One able to fly low and guide itself.'),
(10123, 'c', '\r\nCruiser', ' n. 1 high-speed warship. 2 = *cabin cruiser.'),
(10124, 'c', '\r\nCruiserweight', ' n. = *light heavyweight.'),
(10125, 'c', '\r\nCrumb', ' â€”n. 1 a small fragment, esp. Of bread. B small particle (crumb of comfort). 2 bread without crusts. 3 slang objectionable person. â€”v. Cover with or break into breadcrumbs. [old english]'),
(10126, 'c', '\r\nCrumble', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 break or fall into small fragments. 2 (of power etc.) Gradually disintegrate. â€”n. Dish of stewed fruit with a crumbly topping.'),
(10127, 'c', '\r\nCrumbly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) consisting of, or apt to fall into, crumbs or fragments.  crumbliness n.'),
(10128, 'c', '\r\nCrumbs', ' int. Slang expressing dismay or surprise. [euphemism for *christ]'),
(10129, 'c', '\r\nCrumby', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like or covered in crumbs. 2 = *crummy.'),
(10130, 'c', '\r\nCrumhorn', ' var. Of *krummhorn.'),
(10131, 'c', '\r\nCrummy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang dirty, squalid; inferior, worthless.  crumminess n. [var. Of *crumby]'),
(10132, 'c', '\r\nCrumpet', ' n. 1 soft flat yeasty cake toasted and buttered. 2 joc. Or offens. Sexually attractive woman or women. [origin uncertain]'),
(10133, 'c', '\r\nCrumple', ' â€”v. (-ling) (often foll. By up) 1 crush or become crushed into creases or wrinkles. 2 collapse, give way. â€”n. Crease or wrinkle. [obsolete crump curl up]'),
(10134, 'c', '\r\nCrunch', ' â€”v. 1 a crush noisily with the teeth. B grind under foot, wheels, etc. 2 (often foll. By up, through) make a crunching sound. â€”n. 1 crunching; crunching sound. 2 colloq. Decisive event or moment. [imitative]'),
(10135, 'c', '\r\nCrunchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) hard and crisp.  crunchiness n.'),
(10136, 'c', '\r\nCrupper', ' n. 1 strap looped under a horse''s tail to hold the harness back. 2 hindquarters of a horse. [french: related to *croup2]'),
(10137, 'c', '\r\nCrusade', ' â€”n. 1 hist. Any of several medieval military expeditions made by europeans to recover the holy land from the muslims. 2 vigorous campaign for a cause. â€”v. (-ding) engage in a crusade.  crusader n. [french: related to *cross]'),
(10138, 'c', '\r\nCruse', ' n. Archaic earthenware pot. [old english]'),
(10139, 'c', '\r\nCrush', ' â€”v. 1 compress with force or violence, so as to break, bruise, etc. 2 reduce to powder by pressure. 3 crease or crumple. 4 defeat or subdue completely. â€”n. 1 act of crushing. 2 crowded mass of people. 3 drink from the juice of crushed fruit. 4 (usu. Foll. By on) colloq. Infatuation. [french]'),
(10140, 'c', '\r\nCrust', ' â€”n. 1 a hard outer part of bread. B hard dry scrap of bread. C slang livelihood. 2 pastry covering of a pie. 3 hard casing over a soft thing. 4 outer portion of the earth. 5 deposit, esp. From wine on a bottle. â€”v. Cover or become covered with or form into a crust. [latin crusta rind, shell]'),
(10141, 'c', '\r\nCrustacean', ' â€”n. Esp. Aquatic arthropod with a hard shell, e.g. The crab, lobster, and shrimp. â€”adj. Of crustaceans.'),
(10142, 'c', '\r\nCrusty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having a crisp crust. 2 irritable, curt.  crustily adv. Crustiness n.'),
(10143, 'c', '\r\nCrutch', ' n. 1 usu. T-shaped support for a lame person fitting under the armpit. 2 support, prop. 3 crotch. [old english]'),
(10144, 'c', '\r\nCrux', ' n. (pl. Cruxes or cruces) decisive point at issue. [latin, = cross]'),
(10145, 'c', '\r\nCruzado', ' n. (pl. -s) chief monetary unit of brazil. [portuguese]'),
(10146, 'c', '\r\nCruzeiro', ' n. (pl. -s) one-thousandth of a cruzado. [portuguese]'),
(10147, 'c', '\r\nCry', ' â€”v. (cries, cried) 1 (often foll. By out) make a loud or shrill sound, esp. To express pain, grief, etc., or to appeal for help. 2 shed tears; weep. 3 (often foll. By out) say or exclaim loudly or excitedly. 4 (foll. By for) appeal, demand, or show a need for. 5 (of an animal, esp. A bird) make a loud call. â€”n. (pl. Cries) 1 loud shout or scream of grief, pain, etc. 2 spell of weeping. 3 loud excited utterance. 4 urgent appeal. 5 a public demand or opinion. B rallying call. 6 call of an animal.  cry down disparage. Cry off withdraw from an undertaking. Cry out for need as an obvious requirement or solution. Cry wolf see *wolf. [latin quirito]'),
(10148, 'c', '\r\nCry-baby', ' n. Person who weeps frequently.'),
(10149, 'c', '\r\nCryer', ' var. Of *crier.'),
(10150, 'c', '\r\nCrying', ' attrib. Adj. (of injustice etc.) Flagrant, demanding redress.'),
(10151, 'c', '\r\nCryogenics', ' n. Branch of physics dealing with very low temperatures.  cryogenic adj. [greek kruos frost, -genes born]'),
(10152, 'c', '\r\nCrypt', ' n. Vault, esp. Beneath a church, used usu. As a burial-place. [latin crypta from greek kruptos hidden]'),
(10153, 'c', '\r\nCryptic', ' adj. Obscure in meaning; secret, mysterious.  cryptically adv.'),
(10154, 'c', '\r\nCryptogam', ' n. Plant with no true flowers or seeds, e.g. Ferns, mosses, and fungi.  cryptogamous adj. [as *crypt, greek gamos marriage]'),
(10155, 'c', '\r\nCryptogram', ' n. Text written in cipher. [related to *crypt]'),
(10156, 'c', '\r\nCryptography', ' n. Art of writing or solving ciphers.  cryptographer n. Cryptographic adj.'),
(10157, 'c', '\r\nCrystal', ' â€”n. 1 a transparent colourless mineral, esp. Rock crystal. B piece of this. 2 a highly transparent glass; flint glass. B articles of this. 3 crystalline piece of semiconductor. 4 aggregation of molecules with a definite internal structure and the external form of a solid enclosed by symmetrically arranged plane faces. â€”adj. (usu. Attrib.) Made of, like, or clear as crystal. [greek krustallos]'),
(10158, 'c', '\r\nCrystal', 'ball  n. Glass globe used in crystal-gazing.'),
(10159, 'c', '\r\nCrystal-gazing', ' n. Supposed foretelling of the future by gazing into a crystal ball.'),
(10160, 'c', '\r\nCrystalline', ' adj. 1 of, like, or clear as crystal. 2 having the structure and form of a crystal.  crystallinity n.'),
(10161, 'c', '\r\nCrystallize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 form into crystals. 2 (often foll. By out) (of ideas or plans) make or become definite. 3 make or become coated or impregnated with sugar (crystallized fruit).  crystallization n.'),
(10162, 'c', '\r\nCrystallography', ' n. Science of crystal formation and structure.  crystallographer n.'),
(10163, 'c', '\r\nCrystalloid', ' n. Substance that in solution is able to pass through a semipermeable membrane.'),
(10164, 'c', '\r\nCs', ' symb. Caesium.'),
(10165, 'c', '\r\nC/s', ' abbr. Cycles per second.'),
(10166, 'c', '\r\nCse', ' abbr. Hist. Certificate of secondary education.'),
(10167, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' the cse examination was replaced in 1988 by gcse.'),
(10168, 'c', '\r\nCs', 'gas  n. Tear-gas used to control riots etc. [corson and stoughton, names of chemists]'),
(10169, 'c', '\r\nCtc', ' abbr.  City technology college.'),
(10170, 'c', '\r\nCu', ' symb. Copper. [latin cuprum]'),
(10171, 'c', '\r\nCu.', ' Abbr. Cubic.'),
(10172, 'c', '\r\nCub', ' â€”n. 1 young of a fox, bear, lion, etc. 2 (cub) (in full cub scout) junior scout. 3 colloq. Young newspaper reporter. â€”v. (-bb-) (also absol.) Give birth to (cubs). [origin unknown]'),
(10173, 'c', '\r\nCubby-hole', ' n. 1 very small room. 2 snug space. [low german]'),
(10174, 'c', '\r\nCube', ' â€”n. 1 solid contained by six equal squares. 2 cube-shaped block. 3 product of a number multiplied by its square. â€”v. (-bing) 1 find the cube of (a number). 2 cut (food etc.) Into small cubes. [latin from greek]'),
(10175, 'c', '\r\nCube', 'root  n. Number which produces a given number when cubed.'),
(10176, 'c', '\r\nCubic', ' adj. 1 cube-shaped. 2 of three dimensions. 3 involving the cube (and no higher power) of a number (cubic equation).'),
(10177, 'c', '\r\nCubical', ' adj. Cube-shaped.'),
(10178, 'c', '\r\nCubicle', ' n. 1 small screened space. 2 small separate sleeping-compartment. [latin cubo lie]'),
(10179, 'c', '\r\nCubic', 'metre  etc. N. Volume of a cube whose edge is one metre etc.'),
(10180, 'c', '\r\nCubism', ' n. Style in art, esp. Painting, in which objects are represented geometrically.  cubist n. & adj.'),
(10181, 'c', '\r\nCubit', ' n. Ancient measure of length, approximating to the length of a forearm. [latin cubitum elbow]'),
(10182, 'c', '\r\nCuboid', ' â€”adj. Cube-shaped; like a cube. â€”n. Geom. Rectangular parallelepiped.'),
(10183, 'c', '\r\nCuckold', ' â€”n. Husband of an adulteress. â€”v. Make a cuckold of.  cuckoldry n. [french]'),
(10184, 'c', '\r\nCuckoo', ' â€”n. Bird having a characteristic cry, and laying its eggs in the nests of small birds. â€”predic. Adj. Slang crazy. [french, imitative]'),
(10185, 'c', '\r\nCuckoo', 'clock  n. Clock with the figure of a cuckoo emerging to make a call on the hour.'),
(10186, 'c', '\r\nCuckoo-pint', ' n. Wild arum.'),
(10187, 'c', '\r\nCuckoo-spit', ' n. Froth exuded by insect larvae on leaves, stems, etc.'),
(10188, 'c', '\r\nCucumber', ' n. 1 long green fleshy fruit, used in salads. 2 climbing plant yielding this. [french from latin]'),
(10189, 'c', '\r\nCud', ' n. Half-digested food returned to the mouth of ruminants for further chewing. [old english]'),
(10190, 'c', '\r\nCuddle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 hug, fondle. 2 nestle together, lie close and snug. â€”n. Prolonged and fond hug.  cuddlesome adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(10191, 'c', '\r\nCuddly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a person, toy, etc.) Soft and yielding. 2 given to cuddling.'),
(10192, 'c', '\r\nCudgel', ' â€”n. Short thick stick used as a weapon. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) beat with a cudgel. [old english]'),
(10193, 'c', '\r\nCue1', ' â€”n. 1 a last words of an actor''s speech as a signal to another to enter or speak. B similar signal to a musician etc. 2 a stimulus to perception etc. B signal for action. C hint on appropriate behaviour. 3 cueing audio equipment (see sense 2 of v.). â€”v. (cues, cued, cueing or cuing) 1 give a cue to. 2 put (audio equipment) in readiness to play a particular section.  cue in 1 insert a cue for. 2 give information to. On cue at the correct moment. [origin unknown]'),
(10194, 'c', '\r\nCue2', ' billiards etc. â€”n. Long rod for striking a ball. â€”v. (cues, cued, cueing or cuing) strike (a ball) with or use a cue. [var. Of *queue]'),
(10195, 'c', '\r\nCue-ball', ' n. Ball to be struck with a cue.'),
(10196, 'c', '\r\nCuff1', ' n. 1 end part of a sleeve. 2 us trouser turn-up. 3 (in pl.) Colloq. Handcuffs.  off the cuff colloq. Without preparation, extempore. [origin unknown]'),
(10197, 'c', '\r\nCuff2', ' â€”v. Strike with an open hand. â€”n. Such a blow. [perhaps imitative]'),
(10198, 'c', '\r\nCuff-link', ' n. Two joined studs etc. For fastening a cuff.'),
(10199, 'c', '\r\nCufic', ' var. Of *kufic.'),
(10200, 'c', '\r\nCuirass', ' n. Armour breastplate and back-plate fastened together. [latin corium leather]'),
(10201, 'c', '\r\nCuisine', ' n. Style or method of cooking. [french]'),
(10202, 'c', '\r\nCul-de-sac', ' n. (pl. Culs-de-sac pronunc. Same, or cul-de-sacs) 1 road etc. With a dead end. 2 futile course. [french, = sack-bottom]'),
(10203, 'c', '\r\n-cule', ' suffix forming (orig. Diminutive) nouns (molecule). [latin -culus]'),
(10204, 'c', '\r\nCulinary', ' adj. Of or for cooking. [latin culina kitchen]'),
(10205, 'c', '\r\nCull', ' â€”v. 1 select or gather (knowledge culled from books). 2 gather (flowers etc.). 3 a select (animals), esp. For killing. B reduce the population of (an animal) by selective slaughter. â€”n. 1 culling or being culled. 2 animal(s) culled. [french: related to *collect1]'),
(10206, 'c', '\r\nCulminate', ' v. (-ting) (usu. Foll. By in) reach its highest or final point (culminate in war).  culmination n. [latin culmen top]'),
(10207, 'c', '\r\nCulottes', ' n.pl. Women''s trousers cut like a skirt. [french, = knee-breeches]'),
(10208, 'c', '\r\nCulpable', ' adj. Deserving blame.  culpability n. [latin culpo blame]'),
(10209, 'c', '\r\nCulprit', ' n. Guilty person. [perhaps from anglo-french culpable: see *culpable]'),
(10210, 'c', '\r\nCult', ' n. 1 religious system, sect, etc., esp. Ritualistic. 2 a devotion to a person or thing (cult of aestheticism). B fashion. C (attrib.) Fashionable (cult film). [latin: related to *cultivate]'),
(10211, 'c', '\r\nCultivar', ' n. Plant variety produced by cultivation. [from *cultivate, *variety]'),
(10212, 'c', '\r\nCultivate', ' v. (-ting) 1 prepare and use (soil etc.) For crops or gardening. 2 a raise (crops). B culture (bacteria etc.). 3 a (often as cultivated adj.) Improve (the mind, manners, etc.). B nurture (a person, friendship, etc.).  cultivable adj. Cultivation n. [latin colo cult- till, worship]'),
(10213, 'c', '\r\nCultivator', ' n. 1 mechanical implement for breaking up the ground etc. 2 person or thing that cultivates.'),
(10214, 'c', '\r\nCultural', ' adj. Of or relating to intellectual or artistic matters, or to a specific culture.  culturally adv.'),
(10215, 'c', '\r\nCulture', ' â€”n. 1 a intellectual and artistic achievement or expression (city lacking in culture). B refined appreciation of the arts etc. (person of culture). 2 customs, achievements, etc. Of a particular civilization or group (chinese culture). 3 improvement by mental or physical training. 4 cultivation of plants; rearing of bees etc. 5 quantity of micro-organisms and nutrient material supporting their growth. â€”v. (-ring) maintain (bacteria etc.) In suitable growth conditions. [latin: related to *cultivate] '),
(10216, 'c', '\r\nCultured', ' adj. Having refined taste etc.'),
(10217, 'c', '\r\nCultured', 'pearl  n. Pearl formed by an oyster after the insertion of a foreign body into its shell.'),
(10218, 'c', '\r\nCulture', 'shock  n. Disorientation felt by a person subjected to an unfamiliar way of life.'),
(10219, 'c', '\r\nCulture', 'vulture  n. Colloq. Person eager for cultural pursuits.'),
(10220, 'c', '\r\nCulvert', ' n. Underground channel carrying water under a road etc. [origin unknown]'),
(10221, 'c', '\r\nCum', ' prep. (usu. In comb.) With, combined with, also used as (bedroom-cum-study). [latin]'),
(10222, 'c', '\r\nCumbersome', ' adj. (also cumbrous) inconveniently bulky etc.; unwieldy. [cumber hinder]'),
(10223, 'c', '\r\nCumin', ' n. (also cummin) 1 plant with aromatic seeds. 2 these as flavouring. [greek kuminon]'),
(10224, 'c', '\r\nCummerbund', ' n. Waist sash. [hindustani and persian]'),
(10225, 'c', '\r\nCumquat', ' var. Of *kumquat.'),
(10226, 'c', '\r\nCumulative', ' adj. 1 increasing or increased progressively in amount, force, etc. (cumulative evidence). 2 formed by successive additions (learning is a cumulative process).  cumulatively adv.'),
(10227, 'c', '\r\nCumulus', ' n. (pl. -li) cloud formation of rounded masses heaped up on a flat base. [latin, = heap]'),
(10228, 'c', '\r\nCuneiform', ' â€”adj. 1 wedge-shaped. 2 of or using wedge-shaped writing. â€”n. Cuneiform writing. [latin cuneus wedge]'),
(10229, 'c', '\r\nCunnilingus', ' n. Oral stimulation of the female genitals. [latin cunnus vulva, lingo lick]'),
(10230, 'c', '\r\nCunning', ' â€”adj. (-er, -est) 1 deceitful, clever, or crafty. 2 ingenious (cunning device). 3 us attractive, quaint. â€”n. 1 craftiness; deception. 2 skill, ingenuity.  cunningly adv. [old norse: related to *can1]'),
(10231, 'c', '\r\nCunt', ' n. Coarse slang 1 female genitals. 2 offens. Unpleasant person. [origin uncertain]'),
(10232, 'c', '\r\nCup', ' â€”n. 1 small bowl-shaped container for drinking from. 2 a its contents. B = *cupful. 3 cup-shaped thing. 4 flavoured wine, cider, etc., usu. Chilled. 5 cup-shaped trophy as a prize. 6 one''s fate or fortune (a bitter cup). â€”v. (-pp-) 1 form (esp. The hands) into the shape of a cup. 2 take or hold as in a cup.  one''s cup of tea colloq. What interests or suits one. [medieval latin cuppa]'),
(10233, 'c', '\r\nCupboard', ' n. Recess or piece of furniture with a door and (usu.) Shelves.'),
(10234, 'c', '\r\nCupboard', 'love  n. False affection for gain.'),
(10235, 'c', '\r\nCup', 'final  n. Final match in a (esp. Football) competition.'),
(10236, 'c', '\r\nCupful', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 amount held by a cup, esp. Us a half-pint or 8-ounce measure. 2 full cup.'),
(10237, 'c', '\r\nUsage', ' a cupful is a measure, and so three cupfuls is a quantity regarded in terms of a cup; three cups full denotes the actual cups as in brought us three cups full of water.'),
(10238, 'c', '\r\nCupid', ' n. 1 roman god of love, represented as a naked winged boy archer. 2 (also cupid) representation of cupid. [latin cupio desire]'),
(10239, 'c', '\r\nCupidity', ' n. Greed; avarice. [latin: related to *cupid]'),
(10240, 'c', '\r\nCupid''s', 'bow  n. Upper lip etc. Shaped like an archery bow.'),
(10241, 'c', '\r\nCupola', ' n. 1 dome forming or adorning a roof. 2 revolving dome protecting mounted guns. 3 furnace for melting metals.  cupolaed adj. [italian from latin cupa cask]'),
(10242, 'c', '\r\nCuppa', ' n. Colloq. 1 cup of. 2 cup of tea. [corruption]'),
(10243, 'c', '\r\nCupreous', ' adj. Of or like copper. [latin: related to *copper1]'),
(10244, 'c', '\r\nCupric', ' adj. Of copper.'),
(10245, 'c', '\r\nCupro-nickel', ' n. Alloy of copper and nickel.'),
(10246, 'c', '\r\nCup-tie', ' n. Match in a competition for a cup.'),
(10247, 'c', '\r\nCur', ' n. 1 mangy ill-tempered dog. 2 contemptible person. [perhaps from old norse kurr grumbling]'),
(10248, 'c', '\r\nCurable', ' adj. Able to be cured.  curability n.'),
(10249, 'c', '\r\nCuraÃ§ao', ' n. (pl. -s) orange-flavoured liqueur. [curaÃ§ao, caribbean island]'),
(10250, 'c', '\r\nCuracy', ' n. (pl. -ies) curate''s office or tenure of it.'),
(10251, 'c', '\r\nCurare', ' n. Extract of various plants, used by american indians to poison arrows. [carib]'),
(10252, 'c', '\r\nCurate', ' n. Assistant to a parish priest. [medieval latin curatus: related to *cure]'),
(10253, 'c', '\r\nCurate''s', 'egg  n. Thing that is good in parts.'),
(10254, 'c', '\r\nCurative', ' â€”adj. Tending or able to cure. â€”n. Curative agent. [medieval latin: related to *curate]'),
(10255, 'c', '\r\nCurator', ' n. Keeper or custodian of a museum etc.  curatorship n. [anglo-latin: related to *cure]'),
(10256, 'c', '\r\nCurb', ' â€”n. 1 check, restraint. 2 strap etc. Passing under a horse''s lower jaw, used as a check. 3 enclosing border, e.g. The frame round a well or a fender round a hearth. 4 = *kerb. â€”v. 1 restrain. 2 put a curb on (a horse). [french: related to *curve]'),
(10257, 'c', '\r\nCurd', ' n. (often in pl.) Coagulated acidic milk product made into cheese or eaten as food. [origin unknown]'),
(10258, 'c', '\r\nCurd', 'cheese  n. Soft smooth cheese made from skimmed milk curds.'),
(10259, 'c', '\r\nCurdle', ' v. (-ling) form into curds; congeal.  make one''s blood curdle horrify one. [from *curd]'),
(10260, 'c', '\r\nCure', ' â€”v. (-ring) 1 (often foll. By of) restore to health; relieve (cured of pleurisy). 2 eliminate (disease, evil, etc.). 3 preserve (meat, fruit, etc.) By salting, drying, etc. 4 vulcanize (rubber); harden (plastic etc.). â€”n. 1 restoration to health. 2 thing effecting a cure. 3 course of treatment. 4 curacy. [latin cura care]'),
(10261, 'c', '\r\nCurÃ©', ' n. Parish priest in france etc. [french]'),
(10262, 'c', '\r\nCure-all', ' n. Panacea.'),
(10263, 'c', '\r\nCurette', ' â€”n. Surgeon''s small scraping-instrument. â€”v. (-tting) clean or scrape with this.  curettage n. [french: related to *cure]'),
(10264, 'c', '\r\nCurfew', ' n. 1 signal or time after which people must remain indoors. 2 hist. Signal for extinction of fires at a fixed hour. [french: related to *cover, latin *focus]'),
(10265, 'c', '\r\nCuria', ' n. (also curia) papal court; government departments of the vatican. [latin]'),
(10266, 'c', '\r\nCurie', ' n. Unit of radioactivity. [p. Curie, name of a scientist]'),
(10267, 'c', '\r\nCurio', ' n. (pl. -s) rare or unusual object. [abbreviation of *curiosity]'),
(10268, 'c', '\r\nCuriosity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 eager desire to know; inquisitiveness. 2 strange, rare, etc. Object. [latin: related to *curious]'),
(10269, 'c', '\r\nCurious', ' adj. 1 eager to learn; inquisitive. 2 strange, surprising, odd.  curiously adv. [latin: related to *cure]'),
(10270, 'c', '\r\nCurium', ' n. Artificial radioactive metallic element. [m. And p. Curie, name of scientists]'),
(10271, 'c', '\r\nCurl', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) bend or coil into a spiral. 2 move in a spiral form. 3 a (of the upper lip) be raised contemptuously. B cause (the lip) to do this. 4 play curling. â€”n. 1 lock of curled hair. 2 anything spiral or curved inwards. 3 a curling movement. B being curled.  curl one''s lip express scorn. Curl up 1 lie or sit with the knees drawn up. 2 colloq. Writhe in embarrassment etc. [dutch]'),
(10272, 'c', '\r\nCurler', ' n. Pin or roller etc. For curling the hair.'),
(10273, 'c', '\r\nCurlew', ' n. Wading bird, usu. With a long slender bill. [french]'),
(10274, 'c', '\r\nCurlicue', ' n. Decorative curl or twist. [from *curly, *cue2 or *q1]'),
(10275, 'c', '\r\nCurling', ' n. Game resembling bowls, played on ice with round flat stones.'),
(10276, 'c', '\r\nCurly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having or arranged in curls. 2 moving in curves.  curliness n.'),
(10277, 'c', '\r\nCurly', 'kale  n. = *kale.'),
(10278, 'c', '\r\nCurmudgeon', ' n. Bad-tempered person.  curmudgeonly adj. [origin unknown]'),
(10279, 'c', '\r\nCurrant', ' n. 1 small seedless dried grape. 2 a any of various shrubs producing red, white, or black berries. B such a berry. [anglo-french from corinth in greece]'),
(10280, 'c', '\r\nCurrency', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a money in use in a country. B other commodity used as money. 2 being current; prevalence (e.g. Of words or ideas).'),
(10281, 'c', '\r\nCurrent', ' â€”adj. 1 belonging to the present; happening now (current events). 2 (of money, opinion, rumour, etc.) In general circulation or use. â€”n. 1 body of moving water, air, etc., esp. Passing through still water etc. 2 a ordered movement of electrically charged particles. B quantity representing the intensity of this. 3 (usu. Foll. By of) general tendency or course (of events, opinions, etc.).  currentness n. [latin curro curs- run]'),
(10282, 'c', '\r\nCurrent', 'account  n. Instantly accessible bank account.'),
(10283, 'c', '\r\nCurrently', ' adv. At the present time; now.'),
(10284, 'c', '\r\nCurriculum', ' n. (pl. -la) subjects included in a course of study. [latin, = course]'),
(10285, 'c', '\r\nCurriculum', 'vitae  n. Brief account of one''s education, career, etc.'),
(10286, 'c', '\r\nCurry1', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) meat, vegetables, etc., cooked in a spicy sauce, usu. Served with rice. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) prepare or flavour with a curry sauce. [tamil]'),
(10287, 'c', '\r\nCurry2', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 groom (a horse) with a curry-comb. 2 treat (tanned leather) to improve it.  curry favour ingratiate oneself. [germanic: related to *ready]'),
(10288, 'c', '\r\nCurry-comb', ' n. Metal serrated device for grooming horses.'),
(10289, 'c', '\r\nCurry-powder', ' n. Mixture of turmeric, cumin, etc. For making curry.'),
(10290, 'c', '\r\nCurse', ' â€”n. 1 solemn invocation of divine wrath on a person or thing. 2 supposed resulting evil. 3 violent or profane exclamation or oath. 4 thing causing evil or harm. 5 (prec. By the) colloq.'),
(10291, 'c', '\r\nCursed', ' attrib. Adj. Damned.'),
(10292, 'c', '\r\nCursive', ' â€”adj. (of writing) with joined characters. â€”n. Cursive writing. [medieval latin, = running: related to *current]'),
(10293, 'c', '\r\nCursor', ' n. 1 math. Etc. Transparent slide with a hairline, forming part of a slide-rule. 2 computing indicator on a vdu screen identifying esp. The position that the program will operate on with the next keystroke. [latin, = runner: related to *cursive]'),
(10294, 'c', '\r\nCursory', ' adj. Hasty, hurried.  cursorily adv. Cursoriness n. [latin: related to *cursor]'),
(10295, 'c', '\r\nCurt', ' adj. Noticeably or rudely brief.  curtly adv. Curtness n. [latin curtus short]'),
(10296, 'c', '\r\nCurtail', ' v. Cut short; reduce.  curtailment n. [corruption of obsolete adj. Curtal: related to *curt]'),
(10297, 'c', '\r\nCurtain', ' â€”n. 1 piece of cloth etc. Hung as a screen, esp. At a window. 2 a rise or fall of a stage curtain between acts or scenes. B = *curtain-call. 3 partition or cover. 4 (in pl.) Slang the end. â€”v. 1 provide or cover with curtain(s). 2 (foll. By off) shut off with curtain(s). [latin cortina]'),
(10298, 'c', '\r\nCurtain-call', ' n. Audience''s applause summoning actors to take a bow.'),
(10299, 'c', '\r\nCurtain-raiser', ' n. 1 short play before the main performance. 2 preliminary event.'),
(10300, 'c', '\r\nCurtilage', ' n. Esp. Law area attached to a house and forming one enclosure with it. [french: related to *court]'),
(10301, 'c', '\r\nCurtsy', ' (also curtsey) â€”n. (pl. -ies or -eys) bending of the knees and lowering of the body made by a girl or woman in acknowledgement of applause or as a respectful greeting etc. â€”v. (-ies, -ied or -eys, -eyed) make a curtsy. [var. Of *courtesy]'),
(10302, 'c', '\r\nCurvaceous', ' adj. Colloq. (esp. Of a woman) having a shapely figure.'),
(10303, 'c', '\r\nCurvature', ' n. 1 curving. 2 curved form. 3 deviation of a curve or curved surface from a plane. [french from latin: related to *curve]'),
(10304, 'c', '\r\nCurve', ' â€”n. 1 line or surface of which no part is straight or flat. 2 curved form or thing. 3 curved line on a graph. â€”v. (-ving) bend or shape to form a curve.  curved adj. [latin curvus curved]'),
(10305, 'c', '\r\nCurvet', ' â€”n. Horse''s frisky leap. â€”v. (-tt- or -t-) perform a curvet. [italian diminutive: related to *curve]'),
(10306, 'c', '\r\nCurvilinear', ' adj. Contained by or consisting of curved lines.  curvilinearly adv. [from *curve after rectilinear]'),
(10307, 'c', '\r\nCurvy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having many curves. 2 (of a woman''s figure) shapely.  curviness n.'),
(10308, 'c', '\r\nCushion', ' â€”n. 1 bag stuffed with soft material, for sitting or leaning on etc. 2 protection against shock; measure to soften a blow. 3 padded rim of a billiard-table etc. 4 air supporting a hovercraft etc. â€”v. 1 provide or protect with cushion(s). 2 mitigate the adverse effects of. [latin culcita mattress]'),
(10309, 'c', '\r\nCushy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. (of a job etc.) Easy and pleasant. [hindi khush pleasant]'),
(10310, 'c', '\r\nCusp', ' n. Point at which two curves meet, e.g. The horn of a crescent moon etc. [latin cuspis -id- point, apex]'),
(10311, 'c', '\r\nCuss', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 curse. 2 usu. Derog. Person; creature. â€”v. Curse. [var. Of *curse]'),
(10312, 'c', '\r\nCussed', ' adj. Colloq. Stubborn.  cussedness n.'),
(10313, 'c', '\r\nCustard', ' n. Pudding or sweet sauce of eggs or flavoured cornflour and milk. [obsolete crustade: related to *crust]'),
(10314, 'c', '\r\nCustodian', ' n. Guardian or keeper.  custodianship n.'),
(10315, 'c', '\r\nCustody', ' n. 1 guardianship; protective care. 2 imprisonment.  take into custody arrest.  custodial adj. [latin custos -od- guard]'),
(10316, 'c', '\r\nCustom', ' n. 1 a usual behaviour. B particular established way of behaving. 2 law established usage having the force of law. 3 regular business dealings or customers. 4 (in pl.; also treated as sing.) A duty on imports and exports. B official department administering this. C area at a port, frontier, etc., dealing with customs etc. [latin consuetudo]'),
(10317, 'c', '\r\nCustomary', ' adj. In accordance with custom, usual.  customarily adv. Customariness n. [medieval latin: related to *custom]'),
(10318, 'c', '\r\nCustom-built', ' adj. (also custom-made) made to order.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(10319, 'c', '\r\nCustomer', ' n. 1 person who buys goods or services from a shop or business. 2 colloq. Person of a specified kind (awkward customer). [anglo-french: related to *custom]'),
(10320, 'c', '\r\nCustom-house', ' n. Customs office at a port or frontier etc.'),
(10321, 'c', '\r\nCustomize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make or modify to order; personalize.'),
(10322, 'c', '\r\nCut', ' â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Cut) 1 (also absol.) Penetrate or wound with a sharp-edged instrument. 2 (often foll. By into) divide or be divided with a knife etc. 3 trim or detach by cutting. 4 (foll. By loose, open, etc.) Loosen etc. By cutting. 5 (esp. As cutting adj.) Wound (cutting remark). 6 (often foll. By down) reduce (wages, time, etc.) Or cease (services etc.). 7 a make (a coat, gem, key, record, etc.) By cutting. B make (a path, tunnel, etc.) By removing material. 8 perform, make (cut a caper; cut a sorry figure). 9 (also absol.) Cross, intersect. 10 (foll. By across, through, etc.) Traverse, esp. As a shorter way (cut across the grass). 11 a deliberately ignore (a person one knows). B renounce (a connection). 12 esp. Us deliberately miss (a class etc.). 13 cards a divide (a pack) into two parts. B do this to select a dealer etc. 14 a edit (film or tape). B (often in imper.) Stop filming or recording. C (foll. By to) go quickly to (another shot). 15 switch off (an engine etc.). 16 chop (a ball). â€”n. 1 cutting. 2 division or wound made by cutting. 3 stroke with a knife, sword, whip, etc. 4 a reduction (in wages etc.). B cessation (of power supply etc.). 5 removal of lines etc. From a play, film, etc. 6 wounding remark or act. 7 style of hair, garment, etc. Achieved by cutting. 8 particular piece of butchered meat. 9 colloq. Commission; share of profits. 10 stroke made by cutting. 11 deliberate ignoring of a person. 12 = *woodcut.  a cut above colloq. Noticeably superior to. Be cut out (foll. By for, or to + infin.) Be suited. Cut across 1 transcend (normal limitations etc.). 2 see sense 10 of v. Cut and run slang run away. Cut back 1 reduce (expenditure etc.). 2 prune (a tree etc.). Cut both ways 1 serve both sides of an argument etc. 2 (of an action) have both good and bad effects. Cut a corner go across it. Cut corners do perfunctorily or incompletely, esp. To save time. Cut a dash make a brilliant show. Cut a person dead deliberately ignore (a person one knows). Cut down 1 a bring or throw down by cutting. B kill by sword or disease. 2 see sense 6 of v. 3 reduce the length of (cut down trousers to make shorts). 4 (often foll. By on) reduce consumption (cut down on beer). Cut a person down to size colloq. Deflate a person''s pretensions. Cut in 1 interrupt. 2 pull in too closely in front of another vehicle. Cut it fine allow very little margin of time etc. Cut it out (usu. In imper.) Slang stop doing that. Cut one''s losses abandon an unprofitable scheme. Cut no ice slang have no influence. Cut off 1 remove by cutting. 2 a (often in passive) bring to an abrupt end or (esp. Early) death. B intercept, interrupt. C disconnect (a person on the telephone). 3 a prevent from travelling. B (as cut off adj.) Isolated or remote. 4 disinherit. Cut out 1 remove from inside by cutting. 2 make by cutting from a larger whole. 3 omit. 4 colloq. Stop doing or using (something) (cut out chocolate). 5 (cause to) cease functioning (engine cut out). 6 outdo or supplant (a rival). Cut short interrupt; terminate. Cut one''s teeth on acquire experience from.'),
(10323, 'c', '\r\nCut', 'and dried  adj. 1 completely decided; inflexible. 2 (of opinions etc.) Ready-made, lacking freshness.'),
(10324, 'c', '\r\nCut', 'and thrust  n. Lively argument etc.'),
(10325, 'c', '\r\nCutaneous', ' adj. Of the skin. [latin: related to *cuticle]'),
(10326, 'c', '\r\nCutaway', ' attrib. Adj. (of a diagram etc.) With parts of the exterior left out to reveal the interior.'),
(10327, 'c', '\r\nCut-back', ' n. Cutting back, esp. A reduction in expenditure.'),
(10328, 'c', '\r\nCute', ' adj. Colloq. 1 esp. Us attractive, quaint. 2 clever, ingenious.  cutely adv. Cuteness n. [shortening of *acute]'),
(10329, 'c', '\r\nCut', 'glass  n. (often hyphenated when attrib.) Glass with patterns cut on it.'),
(10330, 'c', '\r\nCuticle', ' n. Dead skin at the base of a fingernail or toenail. [latin diminutive of cutis skin]'),
(10331, 'c', '\r\nCutis', ' n. True skin, beneath the epidermis. [latin]'),
(10332, 'c', '\r\nCutlass', ' n. Hist. Short sword with a slightly curved blade. [latin cultellus: related to *cutler]'),
(10333, 'c', '\r\nCutler', ' n. Person who makes or deals in knives etc. [latin cultellus diminutive: related to *coulter]'),
(10334, 'c', '\r\nCutlery', ' n. Knives, forks, and spoons for use at table. [anglo-french: related to *cutler]'),
(10335, 'c', '\r\nCutlet', ' n. 1 neck-chop of mutton or lamb. 2 small piece of veal etc. For frying. 3 flat cake of minced meat or nuts and breadcrumbs etc. [french diminutive from latin costa rib]'),
(10336, 'c', '\r\nCut-off', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Point at which something is cut off. 2 device for stopping a flow.'),
(10337, 'c', '\r\nCut-out', ' n. 1 figure cut out of paper etc. 2 device for automatic disconnection, the release of exhaust gases, etc.'),
(10338, 'c', '\r\nCut-price', ' adj. (also cut-rate) at a reduced price.'),
(10339, 'c', '\r\nCutter', ' n. 1 a person or thing that cuts. B (in pl.) Cutting tool. 2 a small fast sailing-ship. B small boat carried by a large ship.'),
(10340, 'c', '\r\nCutthroat', ' â€”n. 1 murderer. 2 (in full cutthroat razor) razor with a long unguarded blade set in a handle. â€”adj. 1 (of competition) ruthless and intense. 2 (of a card-game) three-handed.'),
(10341, 'c', '\r\nCutting', ' â€”n. 1 piece cut from a newspaper etc. 2 piece cut from a plant for propagation. 3 excavated channel in a hillside etc. For a railway or road. â€”adj. See *cut v. 5.  cuttingly adv.'),
(10342, 'c', '\r\nCuttlefish', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) mollusc with ten arms and ejecting a black fluid when threatened. [old english]'),
(10343, 'c', '\r\nCutwater', ' n. 1 forward edge of a ship''s prow. 2 wedge-shaped projection from a pier or bridge.'),
