(11292, 'd', '\r\nDictate', ' â€”v. (-ting) 1 say or read aloud (material to be written down or recorded). 2 state or order authoritatively or peremptorily. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Authoritative instruction or requirement (dictates of conscience, fashion).  dictation n. [latin dicto from dico say]'),
(11293, 'd', '\r\nDictator', ' n. 1 usu. Unelected omnipotent ruler. 2 omnipotent person in any sphere. 3 domineering person.  dictatorship n. [latin: related to *dictate]'),
(11294, 'd', '\r\nDictatorial', ' adj. 1 of or like a dictator. 2 overbearing.  dictatorially adv. [latin: related to *dictator]'),
(11295, 'd', '\r\nDiction', ' n. Manner of enunciation in speaking or singing. [latin dictio from dico dict- say]'),
(11296, 'd', '\r\nDictionary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 book listing (usu. Alphabetically) and explaining the words of a language or giving corresponding words in another language. 2 reference book explaining the terms of a particular subject. [medieval latin: related to *diction]'),
(11297, 'd', '\r\nDictum', ' n. (pl. Dicta or -s) 1 formal expression of opinion. 2 a saying. [latin, neuter past part. Of dico say]'),
(11298, 'd', '\r\nDid', ' past of *do1.'),
(11299, 'd', '\r\nDidactic', ' adj. 1 meant to instruct. 2 (of a person) tediously pedantic.  didactically adv. Didacticism n. [greek didasko teach]'),
(11300, 'd', '\r\nDiddle', ' v. (-ling) colloq. Swindle. [probably from diddler, name of a character in a 19th-c. Play]'),
(11301, 'd', '\r\nDiddums', ' int. Often iron. Expressing commiseration. [= did ''em, i.e. Did they (tease you etc.)?]'),
(11302, 'd', '\r\nDiddums', ' int. Often iron. Expressing commiseration. [= did ''em, i.e. Did they (tease you etc.)?]'),
(11303, 'd', '\r\nDidgeridoo', ' n. Long tubular australian aboriginal musical instrument. [imitative]'),
(11304, 'd', '\r\nDidn''t', ' contr. Did not.'),
(11305, 'd', '\r\nDie1', ' v. (dies, died, dying) 1 cease to live; expire, lose vital force. 2 a come to an end, fade away (his interest died). B cease to function. C (of a flame) go out. 3 (foll. By on) die or cease to function while in the presence or charge of (a person). 4 (usu. Foll. By of, from, with) be exhausted or tormented (nearly died of boredom).  be dying (foll. By for, or to + infin.) Wish for longingly or intently (was dying for a drink). Die away fade to the point of extinction. Die back (of a plant) decay from the tip towards the root. Die down become fainter or weaker. Die hard die reluctantly (old habits die hard). Die off die one after another. Die out become extinct, cease to exist. [old norse]'),
(11306, 'd', '\r\nDie2', ' n. 1 = *dice 1b. 2 (pl. Dies) a engraved device for stamping coins, medals, etc. B device for stamping, cutting, or moulding material.  the die is cast an irrevocable step has been taken. [latin datum from do give]'),
(11307, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' dice, rather than die, is now the standard singular as well as plural form in the games sense (one dice, two dice).'),
(11308, 'd', '\r\nDie-casting', ' n. Process or product of casting from metal moulds.'),
(11309, 'd', '\r\nDie-hard', ' n. Conservative or stubborn person.'),
(11310, 'd', '\r\nDielectric', ' â€”adj. Not conducting electricity. â€”n. Dielectric substance.'),
(11311, 'd', '\r\nDieresis', ' n. (pl. Diereses) (brit. Diaeresis) mark (as in naÃ¯ve) over a vowel to indicate that it is sounded separately. [greek, = separation]'),
(11312, 'd', '\r\nDiesel', ' n. 1 (in full diesel engine) internal-combustion engine in which heat produced by the compression of air in the cylinder ignites the fuel. 2 vehicle driven by a diesel engine. 3 fuel for a diesel engine. [diesel, name of an engineer]'),
(11313, 'd', '\r\nDiesel-electric', ' adj. (of a locomotive etc.) Driven by an electric current from a diesel-engined generator.'),
(11314, 'd', '\r\nDiesel', 'oil  n. Heavy petroleum fraction used in diesel engines.'),
(11315, 'd', '\r\nDie-sinker', ' n. Engraver of dies.'),
(11316, 'd', '\r\nDie-stamping', ' n. Embossing paper etc. With die.'),
(11317, 'd', '\r\nDiet1', ' â€”n. 1 range of foods habitually eaten by a person or animal. 2 limited range of food to which a person is restricted. 3 thing regularly offered (diet of half-truths). â€”v. (-t-) restrict oneself to a special diet, esp. To slim.  dietary adj. Dieter n. [greek diaita way of life]'),
(11318, 'd', '\r\nDiet2', ' n. 1 legislative assembly in certain countries. 2 hist. Congress. [latin dieta]'),
(11319, 'd', '\r\nDietetic', ' adj. Of diet and nutrition. [greek: related to *diet1]'),
(11320, 'd', '\r\nDietetics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) The study of diet and nutrition.'),
(11321, 'd', '\r\nDietitian', ' n. (also dietician) expert in dietetics.'),
(11322, 'd', '\r\nDif-', ' prefix = *dis-.'),
(11323, 'd', '\r\nDiffer', ' v. 1 (often foll. By from) be unlike or distinguishable. 2 (often foll. By with) disagree. [latin differo, dilat- bring apart]'),
(11324, 'd', '\r\nDifference', ' n. 1 being different or unlike. 2 degree of this. 3 way in which things differ. 4 a quantity by which amounts differ. B remainder left after subtraction. 5 disagreement, dispute.  make a (or all the, no, etc.) Difference have a significant (or a very significant, or no etc.) Effect. With a difference having a new or unusual feature.'),
(11325, 'd', '\r\nDifferent', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By from or to) unlike, of another nature. 2 distinct, separate. 3 unusual.  differently adv.'),
(11326, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1, different from is more widely acceptable than different to, which is common in less formal use.'),
(11327, 'd', '\r\nDifferential', ' â€”adj. 1 of, exhibiting, or depending on a difference. 2 math. Relating to infinitesimal differences. 3 constituting or relating to a specific difference. â€”n. 1 difference between things of the same kind. 2 difference in wages between industries or categories of employees in the same industry. 3 difference between rates of interest etc.'),
(11328, 'd', '\r\nDifferential', 'calculus  n. Method of calculating rates of change, maximum or minimum values, etc.'),
(11329, 'd', '\r\nDifferential', 'gear  n. Gear enabling a vehicle''s rear wheels to revolve at different speeds on corners.'),
(11330, 'd', '\r\nDifferentiate', ' v. (-ting) 1 constitute a difference between or in. 2 recognize as different; distinguish. 3 become different during development. 4 math. Calculate the derivative of.  differentiation n.'),
(11331, 'd', '\r\nDifficult', ' adj. 1 a needing much effort or skill. B troublesome, perplexing. 2 (of a person) demanding. 3 problematic.'),
(11332, 'd', '\r\nDifficulty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being difficult. 2 a difficult thing; problem, hindrance. B (often in pl.) Distress, esp. Financial (in difficulties). [latin difficultas: related to *faculty]'),
(11333, 'd', '\r\nDiffident', ' adj. Shy, lacking self-confidence; excessively reticent.  diffidence n. Diffidently adv. [latin diffido distrust]'),
(11334, 'd', '\r\nDiffract', ' v. Break up (a beam of light) into a series of dark and light bands or coloured spectra, or (a beam of radiation or particles) into a series of high and low intensities.  diffraction n. Diffractive adj. [latin diffringo: related to *fraction]'),
(11335, 'd', '\r\nDiffuse', ' â€”adj. 1 spread out, not concentrated. 2 not concise, wordy. â€”v. (-sing) 1 disperse or spread widely. 2 intermingle by diffusion.  diffusible adj. Diffusive adj. [latin: related to *found3]'),
(11336, 'd', '\r\nDiffusion', ' n. 1 diffusing or being diffused. 2 interpenetration of substances by natural movement of their particles. [latin: related to *diffuse]'),
(11337, 'd', '\r\nDig', ' â€”v. (-gg-; past and past part. Dug) 1 (also absol.) Break up and remove or turn over (ground etc.). 2 (foll. By up) break up the soil of (fallow land). 3 make (a hole, tunnel, etc.) By digging. 4 (often foll. By up, out) a obtain by digging. B (foll. By up, out) find or discover. C (foll. By into) search for information in (a book etc.). 5 (also absol.) Excavate (an archaeological site). 6 slang like; understand. 7 (foll. By in, into) thrust (a sharp object); prod or nudge. 8 (foll. By into, through, under) make one''s way by digging. â€”n. 1 piece of digging. 2 thrust or poke. 3 colloq. Pointed remark. 4 archaeological excavation. 5 (in pl.) Colloq. Lodgings.  dig one''s heels in be obstinate. Dig in colloq. Begin eating. Dig oneself in 1 prepare a defensive trench or pit. 2 establish one''s position. [old english]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(11338, 'd', '\r\nDigest', ' â€”v. 1 assimilate (food) in the stomach and bowels. 2 understand and assimilate mentally. 3 summarize. â€”n. 1 periodical synopsis of current literature or news. 2 methodical summary, esp. Of laws.  digestible adj. [latin digero -gest-]'),
(11339, 'd', '\r\nDigestion', ' n. 1 process of digesting. 2 capacity to digest food.'),
(11340, 'd', '\r\nDigestive', ' â€”adj. Of or aiding digestion. â€”n. 1 substance aiding digestion. 2 (in full digestive biscuit) wholemeal biscuit.'),
(11341, 'd', '\r\nDigger', ' n. 1 person or machine that digs, esp. A mechanical excavator. 2 colloq. Australian or new zealander.'),
(11342, 'd', '\r\nDigit', ' n. 1 any numeral from 0 to 9. 2 finger or toe. [latin, = finger, toe]'),
(11343, 'd', '\r\nDigital', ' adj. 1 of digits. 2 (of a clock, watch, etc.) Giving a reading by displayed digits. 3 (of a computer) operating on data represented by a series of digits. 4 (of a recording) with sound-information represented by digits for more reliable transmission.  digitally adv. [latin: related to *digit]'),
(11344, 'd', '\r\nDigital', 'audio tape  n. Magnetic tape on which sound is recorded digitally.'),
(11345, 'd', '\r\nDigitalis', ' n. Drug prepared from foxgloves, used to stimulate the heart. [related to *digit, from the form of the flowers]'),
(11346, 'd', '\r\nDigitize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) convert (data etc.) Into digital form, esp. For a computer.  digitization n.'),
(11347, 'd', '\r\nDignified', ' adj. Having or showing dignity.'),
(11348, 'd', '\r\nDignify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 confer dignity on; ennoble. 2 give a fine name to. [latin dignus worthy]'),
(11349, 'd', '\r\nDignitary', ' n. (pl. -ies) person of high rank or office. [from *dignity]'),
(11350, 'd', '\r\nDignity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 composed and serious manner. 2 worthiness, nobleness (dignity of work). 3 high rank or position.  beneath one''s dignity not worthy enough for one. Stand on one''s dignity insist on being treated with respect. [latin dignus worthy] '),
(11351, 'd', '\r\nDigraph', ' n. Two letters representing one sound, e.g. Ph, ey as in phone, key. [from *di-1, *-graph]'),
(11352, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' digraph is sometimes confused with ligature, which means two or more letters joined together.'),
(11353, 'd', '\r\nDigress', ' v. Depart from the main subject in speech or writing.  digression n. [latin digredior -gress-]'),
(11354, 'd', '\r\nDigs', ' see *dig n. 5.'),
(11355, 'd', '\r\nDike1', ' var. Of *dyke1.'),
(11356, 'd', '\r\nDike2', ' var. Of *dyke2.'),
(11357, 'd', '\r\nDiktat', ' n. Categorical statement or decree. [german, = *dictate]'),
(11358, 'd', '\r\nDilapidated', ' adj. In disrepair or ruin.  dilapidation n. [latin: related to *di-2, lapis stone]'),
(11359, 'd', '\r\nDilatation', ' n. 1 dilating of the cervix, e.g. For surgical curettage. 2 dilation. [from *dilate]'),
(11360, 'd', '\r\nDilate', ' v. (-ting) 1 make or become wider or larger. 2 speak or write at length.  dilation n. [latin latus wide]'),
(11361, 'd', '\r\nDilatory', ' adj. Given to or causing delay. [latin dilatorius: related to *differ]'),
(11362, 'd', '\r\nDildo', ' n. (pl. -s) artificial erect penis for sexual stimulation. [origin unknown]'),
(11363, 'd', '\r\nDilemma', ' n. 1 situation in which a difficult choice has to be made. 2 difficult situation, predicament. [greek lemma premiss]'),
(11364, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of dilemma in sense 2 is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(11365, 'd', '\r\nDilettante', ' n. (pl. Dilettanti or -s) dabbler in a subject.  dilettantism n. [italian dilettare *delight]'),
(11366, 'd', '\r\nDiligent', ' adj. 1 hard-working. 2 showing care and effort.  diligence n. Diligently adv. [french from latin diligo love]'),
(11367, 'd', '\r\nDill', ' n. Herb with aromatic leaves and seeds. [old english]'),
(11368, 'd', '\r\nDilly-dally', ' v. (-ies, -ied) colloq. 1 dawdle. 2 vacillate. [reduplication of *dally]'),
(11369, 'd', '\r\nDilute', ' â€”v. (-ting) 1 reduce the strength of (a fluid) by adding water etc. 2 weaken or reduce in effect. â€”adj. Diluted.  dilution n. [latin diluo -lut- wash away]'),
(11370, 'd', '\r\nDiluvial', ' adj. Of a flood, esp. Of the flood in genesis. [latin: related to *deluge]'),
(11371, 'd', '\r\nDim', ' â€”adj. (dimmer, dimmest) 1 a faintly luminous or visible; not bright. B indistinct. 2 not clearly perceived or remembered. 3 colloq. Stupid. 4 (of the eyes) not seeing clearly. â€”v. (-mm-) make or become dim.  take a dim view of colloq. Disapprove of.  dimly adv. Dimness n. [old english]'),
(11372, 'd', '\r\nDime', ' n. Us ten-cent coin. [latin decima tenth (part)]'),
(11373, 'd', '\r\nDimension', ' â€”n. 1 measurable extent, as length, breadth, depth, etc. 2 (in pl.) Size (of huge dimensions). 3 aspect, facet (gained a new dimension). â€”v. (usu. As dimensioned adj.) Mark dimensions on (a diagram etc.).  dimensional adj. [latin metior mens- measure]'),
(11374, 'd', '\r\nDiminish', ' v. 1 make or become smaller or less. 2 (often as diminished adj.) Lessen the reputation of (a person); humiliate.  law of diminishing returns fact that expenditure etc. Beyond a certain point ceases to produce a proportionate yield. [latin: related to *minute1]'),
(11375, 'd', '\r\nDiminuendo', ' mus. â€”n. (pl -s) gradual decrease in loudness. â€”adv. & adj. Decreasing in loudness. [italian: related to *diminish]'),
(11376, 'd', '\r\nDiminution', ' n. 1 diminishing or being diminished. 2 decrease. [latin: related to *diminish]'),
(11377, 'd', '\r\nDiminutive', ' â€”adj. 1 tiny. 2 (of a word or suffix) implying smallness or affection. â€”n. Diminutive word or suffix.'),
(11378, 'd', '\r\nDimmer', ' n. 1 (in full dimmer switch) device for varying the brightness of an electric light. 2 us a (in pl.) Small parking lights on a vehicle. B headlight on low beam.'),
(11379, 'd', '\r\nDimple', ' â€”n. Small hollow, esp. In the cheek or chin. â€”v. (-ling) form dimples (in).  dimply adj. [probably old english]'),
(11380, 'd', '\r\nDim-wit', ' n. Colloq. Stupid person.  dim-witted adj.'),
(11381, 'd', '\r\nDin', ' n. Any of a series of german technical standards designating electrical connections, film speeds, and paper sizes. [german, from deutsche industrie-norm]'),
(11382, 'd', '\r\nDin', ' â€”n. Prolonged loud confused noise. â€”v. (-nn-) (foll. By into) force (information) into a person by constant repetition; make a din. [old english]'),
(11383, 'd', '\r\nDinar', ' n. Chief monetary unit of yugoslavia and several countries of the middle east and n. Africa. [arabic and persian from latin *denarius]'),
(11384, 'd', '\r\nDine', ' v. (-ning) 1 a eat dinner. B (foll. By on, upon) eat for dinner. 2 (esp. In phr. Wine and dine) entertain with food.  dine out dine away from home. [french diner as *dis-, latin jejunus fasting]'),
(11385, 'd', '\r\nDiner', ' n. 1 person who dines. 2 dining-car. 3 us small restaurant. 4 small dining-room.'),
(11386, 'd', '\r\nDinette', ' n. Small room or alcove for eating meals.'),
(11387, 'd', '\r\nDing', ' â€”v. Make a ringing sound. â€”n. Ringing sound. [imitative]'),
(11388, 'd', '\r\nDingbat', ' n. Slang us & austral. Stupid or eccentric person. [perhaps from ding to beat + *bat1]'),
(11389, 'd', '\r\nDing-dong', ' n. 1 sound of two chimes, esp. As a doorbell. 2 colloq. Heated argument or fight. [imitative]'),
(11390, 'd', '\r\nDinghy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 small boat carried by a ship. 2 small pleasure-boat. 3 small inflatable rubber boat. [hindi]'),
(11391, 'd', '\r\nDingle', ' n. Deep wooded valley or dell. [origin unknown]'),
(11392, 'd', '\r\nDingo', ' n. (pl. -es) wild australian dog. [aboriginal]'),
(11393, 'd', '\r\nDingy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) dirty-looking, drab.  dingily adv. Dinginess n. [origin uncertain]'),
(11394, 'd', '\r\nDining-car', ' n. Restaurant on a train.'),
(11395, 'd', '\r\nDining-room', ' n. Room in which meals are eaten.'),
(11396, 'd', '\r\nDinkum', ' adj. Austral. & nz colloq. Genuine, honest, true.  dinkum oil the honest truth. Fair dinkum 1 fair play. 2 genuine(ly), honest(ly), true, truly. [origin unknown]'),
(11397, 'd', '\r\nDinky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Neat and attractive; small, dainty. [scots dink]'),
(11398, 'd', '\r\nDinner', ' n. 1 main meal of the day, either at midday or in the evening. 2 (in full dinner-party) formal evening meal, esp. With guests. [french: related to *dine]'),
(11399, 'd', '\r\nDinner-dance', ' n. Formal dinner followed by dancing.'),
(11400, 'd', '\r\nDinner-jacket', ' n. Man''s short usu. Black formal jacket for evening wear.'),
(11401, 'd', '\r\nDinner', 'lady  n. Woman who supervises school dinners.'),
(11402, 'd', '\r\nDinner', 'service  n. Set of matching crockery for dinner.'),
(11403, 'd', '\r\nDinosaur', ' n. 1 extinct, often enormous, reptile of the mesozoic era. 2 unwieldy or unchanging system or organization. [greek deinos terrible, *saurian]'),
(11404, 'd', '\r\nDint', ' â€”n. Dent. â€”v. Mark with dints.  by dint of by force or means of. [old english and old norse]'),
(11405, 'd', '\r\nDiocese', ' n. District under the pastoral care of a bishop.  diocesan adj. [greek dioikesis administration]'),
(11406, 'd', '\r\nDiode', ' n. 1 semiconductor allowing the flow of current in one direction only and having two terminals. 2 thermionic valve having two electrodes. [from *di-1, *electrode]'),
(11407, 'd', '\r\nDionysian', ' adj. Wildly sensual; unrestrained. [greek dionusos god of wine]'),
(11408, 'd', '\r\nDiopter', ' n. (brit. Dioptre) unit of refractive power of a lens. [greek: related to *dia-, opsis sight]'),
(11409, 'd', '\r\nDioptre', ' n. (us diopter) unit of refractive power of a lens. [greek: related to *dia-, opsis sight]'),
(11410, 'd', '\r\nDiorama', ' n. 1 scenic painting lit to simulate sunrise etc. 2 small scene with three-dimensional figures, viewed through a window etc. 3 small-scale model or film-set. [from *dia-, greek horao see]'),
(11411, 'd', '\r\nDioxide', ' n. Oxide containing two atoms of oxygen in the molecule.'),
(11412, 'd', '\r\nDip', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 put or lower briefly into liquid etc.; immerse. 2 a go below a surface or level. B (of income, activity, etc.) Decline slightly, esp. Briefly. 3 slope or extend downwards (road dips). 4 go under water and emerge quickly. 5 (foll. By into) look cursorily into (a book, subject, etc.). 6 a (foll. By into) put a hand, ladle, etc., into (a container) to take something out. B use part of (one''s resources) (dipped into our savings). 7 lower or be lowered, esp. In salute. 8 lower the beam of (headlights) to reduce dazzle. 9 colour (a fabric) by immersing it in dye. 10 wash (sheep) in disinfectant. â€”n. 1 dipping or being dipped. 2 liquid for dipping.'),
(11413, 'd', '\r\nDip.', 'Ed.  Abbr. Diploma in education.'),
(11414, 'd', '\r\nDiphtheria', ' n. Acute infectious bacterial disease with inflammation of a mucous membrane esp. Of the throat. [greek diphthera skin, hide]'),
(11415, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(11416, 'd', '\r\nDiphthong', ' n. Two written or spoken vowels pronounced in one syllable (as in coin, loud, toy). [greek phthoggos voice]'),
(11417, 'd', '\r\nDiplodocus', ' n. (pl. -cuses) giant plant-eating dinosaur with a long neck and tail. [greek diplous double, dokos wooden beam]'),
(11418, 'd', '\r\nDiploma', ' n. 1 certificate of qualification awarded by a college etc. 2 document conferring an honour or privilege. [greek, = folded paper, from diplous double]'),
(11419, 'd', '\r\nDiplomacy', ' n. 1 a management of international relations. B skill in this. 2 tact. [french: related to *diplomatic]'),
(11420, 'd', '\r\nDiplomat', ' n. 1 member of a diplomatic service. 2 tactful person.'),
(11421, 'd', '\r\nDiplomatic', ' adj. 1 of or involved in diplomacy. 2 tactful.  diplomatically adv. [french: related to *diploma]'),
(11422, 'd', '\r\nDiplomatic', 'bag  n. Container for dispatching official mail etc. To or from an embassy, usu. Exempt from customs inspection.'),
(11423, 'd', '\r\nDiplomatic', 'immunity  n. Exemption of diplomatic staff abroad from arrest, taxation, etc.'),
(11424, 'd', '\r\nDiplomatic', 'service  n. Branch of the civil service concerned with the representation of a country abroad.'),
(11425, 'd', '\r\nDiplomatist', ' n. Diplomat.'),
(11426, 'd', '\r\nDipole', ' n. 1 two equal and oppositely charged or magnetized poles separated by a distance. 2 molecule in which a concentration of positive charges is separated from a concentration of negative charges. 3 aerial consisting of a horizontal metal rod with a connecting wire at its core.'),
(11427, 'd', '\r\nDipper', ' n. 1 diving bird, esp. The water ouzel. 2 ladle.'),
(11428, 'd', '\r\nDippy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang crazy, silly. [origin uncertain]'),
(11429, 'd', '\r\nDipso', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Alcoholic. [abbreviation]'),
(11430, 'd', '\r\nDipsomania', ' n. Alcoholism.  dipsomaniac n. [greek dipsa thirst]'),
(11431, 'd', '\r\nDipstick', ' n. Rod for measuring the depth of esp. Oil in a vehicle''s engine.'),
(11432, 'd', '\r\nDip-switch', ' n. Switch for dipping a vehicle''s headlights.'),
(11433, 'd', '\r\nDipterous', ' adj. (of an insect) having two wings. [greek pteron wing]'),
(11434, 'd', '\r\nDiptych', ' n. Painting, esp. An altarpiece, on two hinged panels closing like a book. [greek, = pair of writing-tablets, from ptukhe fold]'),
(11435, 'd', '\r\nDire', ' adj. 1 a calamitous, dreadful. B ominous. C (predic) colloq. Very bad. 2 urgent (in dire need). [latin]'),
(11436, 'd', '\r\nDirect', ' â€”adj. 1 extending or moving in a straight line or by the shortest route; not crooked or circuitous. 2 straightforward; frank. 3 with nothing or no-one in between; personal (direct line). 4 (of descent) lineal, not collateral. 5 complete, greatest possible (the direct opposite). â€”adv. 1 in a direct way or manner (dealt with them direct). 2 by the direct route (sent direct to london). â€”v. 1 control; govern or guide (duty directs me). 2 (foll. By to + infin., or that + clause) order (a person) to. 3 (foll. By to) a address (a letter etc.). B tell or show (a person) the way to (a place). 4 (foll. By at, to, towards) point, aim, or turn (a blow, attention, or remark). 5 (also absol.) Supervise the performing, staging, etc., of (a film, play, etc.).  directness n. [latin dirigo from rego rect- guide]'),
(11437, 'd', '\r\nDirect', 'current  n. Electric current flowing in one direction only.'),
(11438, 'd', '\r\nDirect', 'debit  n. Regular debiting of a bank account at the request of the payee.'),
(11439, 'd', '\r\nDirect-grant', 'school  n. School funded by the government and not a local authority.'),
(11440, 'd', '\r\nDirection', ' n. 1 directing; supervision. 2 (usu. In pl.) Order or instruction. 3 line along which, or point to or from which, a person or thing moves or looks. 4 tendency or scope of a theme, subject, etc.'),
(11441, 'd', '\r\nDirectional', ' adj. 1 of or indicating direction. 2 sending or receiving radio or sound waves in one particular direction.'),
(11442, 'd', '\r\nDirective', ' â€”n. Order from an authority. â€”adj. Serving to direct.'),
(11443, 'd', '\r\nDirectly', ' â€”adv. 1 a at once; without delay, immediately (directly after lunch). B presently, shortly. 2 exactly (directly opposite). 3 in a direct manner. â€”conj. Colloq. As soon as (will tell you directly they come).'),
