(11605, 'd', '\r\nDismay', ' â€”n. Intense disappointment or despair. â€”v. Fill with dismay. [french from germanic: related to *dis-, *may]'),
(11606, 'd', '\r\nDismember', ' v. 1 remove the limbs from. 2 partition or divide up.  dismemberment n.'),
(11607, 'd', '\r\nDismiss', ' v. 1 send away, esp. From one''s presence; disperse. 2 terminate the employment of, esp. Dishonourably; sack. 3 put from one''s mind or emotions. 4 consider not worth talking or thinking about; treat summarily. 5 law refuse further hearing to (a case). 6 cricket put (a batsman or side) out (usu. For a stated score).  dismissal n. [latin mitto miss- send]'),
(11608, 'd', '\r\nDismissive', ' adj. Dismissing rudely or casually; disdainful.  dismissively adv. Dismissiveness n.'),
(11609, 'd', '\r\nDismount', ' v. 1 a alight from a horse, bicycle, etc. B unseat. 2 remove (a thing) from its mounting.'),
(11610, 'd', '\r\nDisobedient', ' adj. Disobeying; rebellious.  disobedience n. Disobediently adv.'),
(11611, 'd', '\r\nDisobey', ' v. Refuse or fail to obey.'),
(11612, 'd', '\r\nDisoblige', ' v. (-ging) refuse to help or cooperate with (a person).'),
(11613, 'd', '\r\nDisorder', ' n. 1 lack of order; confusion. 2 public disturbance; riot. 3 ailment or disease.  disordered adj.'),
(11614, 'd', '\r\nDisorderly', ' adj. 1 untidy; confused. 2 riotous, unruly.  disorderliness n.'),
(11615, 'd', '\r\nDisorganize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 throw into confusion or disorder. 2 (as disorganized adj.) Badly organized; untidy.  disorganization n.'),
(11616, 'd', '\r\nDisorient', ' v. = *disorientate.'),
(11617, 'd', '\r\nDisorientate', ' v. (also disorient) (-ting) confuse (a person), esp. As to his or her bearings.  disorientation n.'),
(11618, 'd', '\r\nDisown', ' v. Deny or give up any connection with; repudiate.'),
(11619, 'd', '\r\nDisparage', ' v. (-ging) 1 criticize; belittle. 2 bring discredit on.  disparagement n. [french: related to *dis-, parage rank]'),
(11620, 'd', '\r\nDisparate', ' adj. Essentially different; not comparable.  disparateness n. [latin disparo separate]'),
(11621, 'd', '\r\nDisparity', ' n. (pl. -ies) inequality; difference; incongruity.'),
(11622, 'd', '\r\nDispassionate', ' adj. Free from emotion; impartial.  dispassionately adv.'),
(11623, 'd', '\r\nDispatch', ' (also despatch) â€”v. 1 send off to a destination or for a purpose. 2 perform (a task etc.) Promptly; finish off. 3 kill, execute. 4 colloq. Eat quickly. â€”n. 1 dispatching or being dispatched. 2 a official written message, esp. Military. B news report to a newspaper etc. 3 promptness, efficiency. [italian dispacciare or spanish despachar]'),
(11624, 'd', '\r\nDispatch-box', ' n. Case for esp. Parliamentary documents.'),
(11625, 'd', '\r\nDispatch-rider', ' n. Messenger on a motor cycle.'),
(11626, 'd', '\r\nDispel', ' v. (-ll-) drive away; scatter (fears etc.). [latin pello drive]'),
(11627, 'd', '\r\nDispensable', ' adj. That can be dispensed with.'),
(11628, 'd', '\r\nDispensary', ' n. (pl. -ies) place where medicines are dispensed.'),
(11629, 'd', '\r\nDispensation', ' n. 1 dispensing or distributing. 2 exemption from penalty, rule, etc. 3 ordering or management of the world by providence.'),
(11630, 'd', '\r\nDispense', ' v. (-sing) 1 distribute; deal out. 2 administer. 3 make up and give out (medicine etc.). 4 (foll. By with) do without; make unnecessary. [french from latin pendo pens- weigh]'),
(11631, 'd', '\r\nDispenser', ' n. 1 person or thing that dispenses e.g. Medicine, good advice. 2 automatic machine dispensing a specific amount.'),
(11632, 'd', '\r\nDisperse', ' v. (-sing) 1 go, send, drive, or scatter widely or in different directions. 2 send to or station at different points. 3 disseminate. 4 chem. Distribute (small particles) in a medium. 5 divide (white light) into its coloured constituents.  dispersal n. Dispersive adj. [latin: related to *dis-, *sparse]'),
(11633, 'd', '\r\nDispersion', ' n. 1 dispersing or being dispersed. 2 (the dispersion) = *diaspora.'),
(11634, 'd', '\r\nDispirit', ' v. (esp. As dispiriting, dispirited adjs.) Make despondent, deject.'),
(11635, 'd', '\r\nDisplace', ' v. (-cing) 1 move from its place. 2 remove from office. 3 take the place of; oust.'),
(11636, 'd', '\r\nDisplaced', 'person  n. Refugee in war etc., or from persecution.'),
(11637, 'd', '\r\nDisplacement', ' n. 1 displacing or being displaced. 2 amount of fluid displaced by an object floating or immersed in it.'),
(11638, 'd', '\r\nDisplay', ' â€”v. 1 exhibit; show. 2 reveal; betray. â€”n. 1 displaying. 2 a exhibition or show. B thing(s) displayed. 3 ostentation. 4 mating rituals of some birds etc. 5 what is shown on a visual display unit etc. [latin plico fold]'),
(11639, 'd', '\r\nDisplease', ' v. (-sing) make upset or angry; annoy.  displeasure n.'),
(11640, 'd', '\r\nDisport', ' v. (often refl.) Play, frolic, enjoy oneself. [anglo-french porter carry, from latin]'),
(11641, 'd', '\r\nDisposable', ' â€”adj. 1 intended to be used once and discarded. 2 able to be disposed of. â€”n. Disposable article.'),
(11642, 'd', '\r\nDisposable', 'income  n. Income after tax and other fixed payments.'),
(11643, 'd', '\r\nDisposal', ' n. Disposing of, e.g. Waste.  at one''s disposal available.'),
(11644, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' disposal is the noun corresponding to the verb dispose of (get rid of, deal with, etc.). Disposition is the noun from dispose (arrange, incline).'),
(11645, 'd', '\r\nDispose', ' v. (-sing) 1 (usu. Foll. By to, or to + infin.) A make willing; incline (was disposed to agree). B tend (wheel was disposed to buckle). 2 arrange suitably. 3 (as disposed adj.) Have a specified inclination (ill-disposed; well-disposed). 4 determine events (man proposes, god disposes).  dispose of 1 a deal with. B get rid of. C finish. D kill. 2 sell. 3 prove (an argument etc.) Incorrect. [french: related to *pose]'),
(11646, 'd', '\r\nDisposition', ' n. 1 natural tendency; temperament. 2 a ordering; arrangement (of parts etc.). B arrangement. 3 (usu. In pl.) Preparations; plans.'),
(11647, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' see note at disposal.'),
(11648, 'd', '\r\nDispossess', ' v. 1 (usu. Foll. By of) (esp. As dispossessed adj.) Deprive (a person) of. 2 dislodge; oust.  dispossession n.'),
(11649, 'd', '\r\nDisproof', ' n. Refutation.'),
(11650, 'd', '\r\nDisproportion', ' n. Lack of proportion; being out of proportion.  disproportional adj. Disproportionally adv.'),
(11651, 'd', '\r\nDisproportionate', ' adj. 1 out of proportion. 2 relatively too large or small etc.  disproportionately adv.'),
(11652, 'd', '\r\nDisprove', ' v. (-ving) prove false.'),
(11653, 'd', '\r\nDisputable', ' adj. Open to question; uncertain.  disputably adv.'),
(11654, 'd', '\r\nDisputant', ' n. Person in a dispute.'),
(11655, 'd', '\r\nDisputation', ' n. 1 debate, esp. Formal. 2 argument; controversy.'),
(11656, 'd', '\r\nDisputatious', ' adj. Argumentative.'),
(11657, 'd', '\r\nDispute', ' â€”v. (-ting) 1 debate, argue. 2 discuss, esp. Heatedly; quarrel. 3 question the truth or validity of (a statement etc.). 4 contend for (disputed territory). 5 resist, oppose. â€”n. 1 controversy; debate. 2 quarrel. 3 disagreement leading to industrial action.  in dispute 1 being argued about. 2 (of a workforce) involved in industrial action. [latin puto reckon]'),
(11658, 'd', '\r\nDisqualify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 debar from a competition or pronounce ineligible as a winner. 2 make or pronounce ineligible, unsuitable, or unqualified (disqualified from driving).  disqualification n.'),
(11659, 'd', '\r\nDisquiet', ' â€”v. Make anxious. â€”n. Anxiety; uneasiness.'),
(11660, 'd', '\r\nDisquietude', ' n. Disquiet.'),
(11661, 'd', '\r\nDisquisition', ' n. Discursive treatise or discourse. [latin quaero quaesit- seek]'),
(11662, 'd', '\r\nDisregard', ' â€”v. 1 ignore. 2 treat as unimportant. â€”n. Indifference; neglect.'),
(11663, 'd', '\r\nDisrepair', ' n. Poor condition due to lack of repairs.'),
(11664, 'd', '\r\nDisreputable', ' adj. 1 of bad reputation. 2 not respectable in character or appearance.  disreputably adv.'),
(11665, 'd', '\r\nDisrepute', ' n. Lack of good reputation; discredit.'),
(11666, 'd', '\r\nDisrespect', ' n. Lack of respect; discourtesy.  disrespectful adj. Disrespectfully adv.'),
(11667, 'd', '\r\nDisrobe', ' v. (-bing) literary undress.'),
(11668, 'd', '\r\nDisrupt', ' v. 1 interrupt the continuity of; bring disorder to. 2 break apart.  disruption n. Disruptive adj. Disruptively adv. [latin: related to *rupture]'),
(11669, 'd', '\r\nDissatisfy', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make discontented; fail to satisfy.  dissatisfaction n.'),
(11670, 'd', '\r\nDissect', ' v. 1 cut into pieces, esp. For examination or post mortem. 2 analyse or criticize in detail.  dissection n. [latin: related to *section]'),
(11671, 'd', '\r\nDissemble', ' v. (-ling) 1 be hypocritical or insincere. 2 disguise or conceal (a feeling, intention, etc.). [latin simulo *simulate]'),
(11672, 'd', '\r\nDisseminate', ' v. (-ting) scatter about, spread (esp. Ideas) widely.  dissemination n. [latin: related to *dis-, *semen]'),
(11673, 'd', '\r\nDissension', ' n. Angry disagreement. [latin: related to *dissent]'),
(11674, 'd', '\r\nDissent', ' â€”v. (often foll. By from) 1 disagree, esp. Openly. 2 differ, esp. From the established or official opinion. â€”n. 1 such difference. 2 expression of this. [latin: related to *dis-, sentio feel]'),
(11675, 'd', '\r\nDissenter', ' n. 1 person who dissents. 2 (dissenter) protestant dissenting from the church of england.'),
(11676, 'd', '\r\nDissentient', ' â€”adj. Disagreeing with the established or official view. â€”n. Person who dissents.'),
(11677, 'd', '\r\nDissertation', ' n. Detailed discourse, esp. One submitted towards an academic degree. [latin disserto discuss]'),
(11678, 'd', '\r\nDisservice', ' n. Harmful action, harm.'),
(11679, 'd', '\r\nDissident', ' â€”adj. Disagreeing, esp. With the established government, system, etc. â€”n. Dissident person.  dissidence n. [latin: related to *dis-, sedeo sit]'),
(11680, 'd', '\r\nDissimilar', ' adj. Unlike, not similar.  dissimilarity n. (pl. -ies).'),
(11681, 'd', '\r\nDissimulate', ' v. (-ting) dissemble.  dissimulation n. [latin: related to *dissemble]'),
(11682, 'd', '\r\nDissipate', ' v. (-ting) 1 disperse, disappear, dispel. 2 squander. 3 (as dissipated adj.) Dissolute. [latin dissipare -pat-]'),
(11683, 'd', '\r\nDissipation', ' n. 1 dissolute way of life. 2 dissipating or being dissipated.'),
(11684, 'd', '\r\nDissociate', ' v. (-ting) 1 disconnect or separate. 2 become disconnected.  dissociate oneself from declare oneself unconnected with.  dissociation n. Dissociative adj. [latin: related to *dis-, *associate]'),
(11685, 'd', '\r\nDissoluble', ' adj. That can be disintegrated, loosened, or disconnected.'),
(11686, 'd', '\r\nDissolute', ' adj. Lax in morals; licentious. [latin: related to *dissolve]'),
(11687, 'd', '\r\nDissolution', ' n. 1 dissolving or being dissolved, esp. Of a partnership or of parliament for a new election. 2 breaking up, abolition (of an institution). 3 death.'),
(11688, 'd', '\r\nDissolve', ' v. (-ving) 1 make or become liquid, esp. By immersion or dispersion in a liquid. 2 (cause to) disappear gradually. 3 dismiss (an assembly, esp. Parliament). 4 annul or put an end to (a partnership, marriage, etc.). 5 (often foll. By into) be overcome (by tears, laughter, etc.). [latin: related to *dis-, solvo solut- loosen]'),
(11689, 'd', '\r\nDissonant', ' adj. 1 harsh-toned; unharmonious. 2 incongruous.  dissonance n. [latin: related to *dis-, sono *sound1]'),
(11690, 'd', '\r\nDissuade', ' v. (-ding) (often foll. By from) discourage (a person); persuade against.  dissuasion n. Dissuasive adj. [latin: related to *dis-, suadeo advise]'),
(11691, 'd', '\r\nDissyllable', ' var. Of *disyllable.'),
(11692, 'd', '\r\nDistaff', ' n. Cleft stick holding wool or flax for spinning by hand. [old english]'),
(11693, 'd', '\r\nDistaff', 'side  n. Female branch of a family.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(11694, 'd', '\r\nDistance', ' â€”n. 1 being far off; remoteness. 2 space between two points. 3 distant point or place. 4 aloofness; reserve. 5 remoter field of vision (in the distance). 6 interval of time. â€”v. (-cing) (often refl.) 1 place or cause to seem far off; be aloof. 2 leave far behind in a race etc.  at a distance far off. Keep one''s distance remain aloof. [latin: related to *dis-, sto stand]'),
(11695, 'd', '\r\nDistant', ' adj. 1 far away; at a specified distance (three miles distant). 2 remote in time, relationship, etc. (distant prospect; distant relation). 3 aloof. 4 abstracted (distant stare). 5 faint (distant memory).  distantly adv.'),
(11696, 'd', '\r\nDistaste', ' n. (usu. Foll. By for) dislike; aversion.  distasteful adj. Distastefully adv. Distastefulness n.'),
(11697, 'd', '\r\nDistemper1', ' hist â€”n. Paint using glue or size as a base, for use on walls. â€”v. Paint with this. [latin, = soak: see *distemper2]'),
(11698, 'd', '\r\nDistemper2', ' n. Disease of esp. Dogs, with coughing and weakness. [latin: related to *dis-, tempero mingle]'),
(11699, 'd', '\r\nDistend', ' v. Swell out by pressure from within (distended stomach).  distensible adj. Distension n. [latin: related to *tend1]'),
(11700, 'd', '\r\nDistich', ' n. Verse couplet. [greek stikhos line]'),
(11701, 'd', '\r\nDistil', ' v. (us distill) (-ll-) 1 purify or extract the essence from (a substance) by vaporizing and condensing it and collecting the resulting liquid. 2 extract the essential meaning of (an idea etc.). 3 make (whisky, essence, etc.) By distilling raw materials. 4 fall or cause to fall in drops.  distillation n. [latin: related to *de-, stillo drip]'),
(11702, 'd', '\r\nDistill', ' v. (brit. Distil) (-ll-) 1 purify or extract the essence from (a substance) by vaporizing and condensing it and collecting the resulting liquid. 2 extract the essential meaning of (an idea etc.). 3 make (whisky, essence, etc.) By distilling raw materials. 4 fall or cause to fall in drops.  distillation n. [latin: related to *de-, stillo drip]'),
(11703, 'd', '\r\nDistiller', ' n. Person who distils, esp. Alcoholic liquor.'),
(11704, 'd', '\r\nDistillery', ' n. (pl. -ies) place where alcoholic liquor is distilled.'),
(11705, 'd', '\r\nDistinct', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By from) not identical; separate; different. 2 clearly perceptible. 3 unmistakable, decided (distinct advantage).  distinctly adv. [latin: related to *distinguish]'),
(11706, 'd', '\r\nDistinction', ' n. 1 discriminating or distinguishing. 2 difference between two things. 3 thing that differentiates or distinguishes. 4 special consideration or honour (treat with distinction). 5 excellence (person of distinction). 6 title or mark of honour. [latin: related to *distinguish]'),
(11707, 'd', '\r\nDistinctive', ' adj. Distinguishing, characteristic.  distinctively adv. Distinctiveness n.'),
(11708, 'd', '\r\nDistinguÃ©', ' adj. Distinguished in appearance, manner, etc. [french]'),
(11709, 'd', '\r\nDistinguish', ' v. 1 (often foll. By from, between) differentiate; see or draw distinctions. 2 be a mark or property of; characterize. 3 discover by listening, looking, etc. 4 (usu. Refl.; often foll. By by) make prominent (distinguished himself by winning).  distinguishable adj. [latin: related to *dis-, stinguo stinct- extinguish]'),
(11710, 'd', '\r\nDistinguished', ' adj. 1 eminent; famous. 2 dignified.'),
(11711, 'd', '\r\nDistort', ' v. 1 pull or twist out of shape. 2 misrepresent (facts etc.). 3 transmit (sound etc.) Inaccurately.  distortion n. [latin torqueo tort- twist]'),
(11712, 'd', '\r\nDistract', ' v. 1 (often foll. By from) draw away the attention of. 2 bewilder, perplex. 3 (as distracted adj.) Confused, mad, or angry. 4 amuse, esp. To divert from pain etc. [latin: related to *dis-, traho tract- draw]'),
(11713, 'd', '\r\nDistraction', ' n. 1 a distracting or being distracted. B thing that distracts. 2 relaxation; amusement. 3 confusion; frenzy, madness.'),
(11714, 'd', '\r\nDistrain', ' v. (usu. Foll. By upon) impose distraint (on a person, goods, etc.). [latin: related to *dis-, stringo strict- draw tight]'),
(11715, 'd', '\r\nDistraint', ' n. Seizure of goods to enforce payment.'),
(11716, 'd', '\r\nDistrait', ' adj. (fem. Distraite) inattentive; distraught. [french: related to *distract]'),
(11717, 'd', '\r\nDistraught', ' adj. Distracted with worry, fear, etc.; extremely agitated. [related to *distrait]'),
(11718, 'd', '\r\nDistress', ' â€”n. 1 anguish or suffering caused by pain, sorrow, worry, etc. 2 poverty. 3 law = *distraint. â€”v. Cause distress to, make unhappy.  in distress suffering or in danger.  distressful adj. [romanic: related to *distrain]'),
(11719, 'd', '\r\nDistressed', ' adj. 1 suffering from distress. 2 impoverished. 3 (of furniture, clothing, etc.) Aged, torn, etc. Artificially.'),
(11720, 'd', '\r\nDistressed', 'area  n. Region of high unemployment and poverty.'),
(11721, 'd', '\r\nDistribute', ' v. (-ting) 1 give shares of; deal out. 2 scatter; put at different points. 3 arrange; classify. [latin tribuo -but- assign]'),
(11722, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation given, with the stress on the first syllable, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(11723, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation given, with the stress on the first syllable, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(11724, 'd', '\r\nDistribution', ' n. 1 distributing or being distributed. 2 a commercial dispersal of goods etc. B extent to which different classes etc. Share in a nation''s total wealth etc.'),
(11725, 'd', '\r\nDistributive', ' â€”adj. 1 of or produced by distribution. 2 logic & gram. Referring to each individual of a class, not to the class collectively (e.g. Each, either). â€”n. Gram. Distributive word.'),
(11726, 'd', '\r\nDistributor', ' n. 1 person or thing that distributes, esp. Goods. 2 device in an internal-combustion engine for passing current to each spark-plug in turn.'),
(11727, 'd', '\r\nDistrict', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Area regarded as a geographical or administrative unit (the peak district; postal district; wine-growing district). 2 administrative division of a county etc. [latin: related to *distrain]'),
(11728, 'd', '\r\nDistrict', 'attorney  n. (in the us) prosecuting officer of a district.'),
(11729, 'd', '\r\nDistrict', 'nurse  n. Nurse who makes home visits in an area.'),
(11730, 'd', '\r\nDistrust', ' â€”n. Lack of trust; suspicion. â€”v. Have no trust in.  distrustful adj. Distrustfully adv.'),
(11731, 'd', '\r\nDisturb', ' v. 1 break the rest, calm, or quiet of. 2 agitate; worry. 3 move from a settled position (disturbed my papers). 4 (as disturbed adj.) Emotionally or mentally unstable. [latin: related to *dis-, turba tumult]'),
(11732, 'd', '\r\nDisturbance', ' n. 1 disturbing or being disturbed. 2 tumult; uproar; agitation.'),
(11733, 'd', '\r\nDisunion', ' n. Lack of union; separation; dissension.'),
(11734, 'd', '\r\nDisunite', ' v. (-ting) 1 remove the unity from. 2 separate.  disunity n.'),
(11735, 'd', '\r\nDisuse', ' â€”n. Disused state. â€”v. (-sing) cease to use.'),
(11736, 'd', '\r\nDisyllable', ' n. (also dissyllable) prosody word or metrical foot of two syllables.  disyllabic adj.'),
(11737, 'd', '\r\nDitch', ' â€”n. Long narrow excavation esp. For drainage or as a boundary. â€”v. 1 make or repair ditches (hedging and ditching). 2 slang abandon; discard.   dull as ditch-water extremely dull. [old english]'),
(11738, 'd', '\r\nDither', ' â€”v. 1 hesitate; be indecisive. 2 tremble; quiver. â€”n. Colloq. State of agitation or hesitation.  ditherer n. Dithery adj. [var. Of didder *dodder1]'),
(11739, 'd', '\r\nDithyramb', ' n. 1 wild choral hymn in ancient greece. 2 passionate or inflated poem etc.  dithyrambic adj. [latin from greek]'),
(11740, 'd', '\r\nDitto', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 (in accounts, inventories, etc.) The aforesaid, the same. 2 colloq. (used to avoid repetition) the same (came late today and ditto yesterday). [latin *dictum]'),
(11741, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1, the word ditto is often replaced by " under the word or sum to be repeated.'),
(11742, 'd', '\r\nDitto', 'marks  n.pl. Inverted commas etc. Representing â€˜dittoâ€™.'),
(11743, 'd', '\r\nDitty', ' n. (pl. -ies) short simple song. [latin: related to *dictate]'),
(11744, 'd', '\r\nDiuretic', ' â€”adj. Causing increased output of urine. â€”n. Diuretic drug. [greek: related to *dia-, oureo urinate]'),
(11745, 'd', '\r\nDiurnal', ' adj. 1 of the day or daytime. 2 daily. 3 occupying one day.  diurnally adv. [latin diurnalis from dies day]'),
(11746, 'd', '\r\nDiva', ' n. (pl. -s) great woman opera singer; prima donna. [italian from latin, = goddess]'),
(11747, 'd', '\r\nDivalent', ' adj. Chem. Having a valency of two.'),
(11748, 'd', '\r\nDivan', ' n. Low couch or bed without a back or ends. [ultimately from persian divan bench]'),
(11749, 'd', '\r\nDive', ' â€”v. (-ving) 1 plunge head first into water. 2 a (of an aircraft, person, etc.) Plunge steeply downwards. B (of a submarine) submerge; go deeper. 3 (foll. By into) colloq. A put one''s hand into (a pocket, handbag, etc.). B become enthusiastic about (a subject, meal, etc.). 4 move suddenly (dived into a shop). â€”n. 1 act of diving; plunge. 2 steep descent or fall. 3 colloq. Disreputable nightclub, bar, etc. [old english]'),
(11750, 'd', '\r\nDive-bomb', ' v. Bomb (a target) from a diving aircraft.  dive-bomber n.'),
(11751, 'd', '\r\nDiver', ' n. 1 person who dives, esp. Working under water. 2 diving bird.'),
(11752, 'd', '\r\nDiverge', ' v. (-ging) 1 a spread out from a central point, become dispersed. B take different courses (their interests diverged). 2 a (often foll. By from) depart from a set course.'),
(11753, 'd', '\r\nDivers', ' adj. Archaic various; several. [latin: related to *diverse]'),
(11754, 'd', '\r\nDiverse', ' adj. Varied. [latin: related to *di-2, verto vers- turn]'),
(11755, 'd', '\r\nDiversify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 make diverse; vary. 2 spread (investment) over several enterprises. 3 (often foll. By into) expand one''s range of products.  diversification n.'),
(11756, 'd', '\r\nDiversion', ' n. 1 diverting or being diverted. 2 a diverting of attention. B stratagem for this. 3 recreation, pastime. 4 alternative route when a road is temporarily closed.  diversionary adj.'),
(11757, 'd', '\r\nDiversity', ' n. Variety.'),
(11758, 'd', '\r\nDivert', ' v. 1 a turn aside; deflect. B distract (attention). 2 (often as diverting adj.) Entertain; amuse. [latin: related to *diverse]'),
(11759, 'd', '\r\nDivest', ' v. (usu. Foll. By of) 1 unclothe; strip. 2 deprive, rid. [latin: related to *vest]'),
(11760, 'd', '\r\nDivide', ' â€”v. (-ding) 1 (often foll. By in, into) separate into parts; break up; split. 2 (often foll. By out) distribute; deal; share. 3 a separate (one thing) from another. B classify into parts or groups. 4 cause to disagree. 5 a find how many times (a number) contains or is contained in another (divide 20 by 4; divide 4 into 20). B (of a number) be contained in (a number) without remainder (4 divides into 20). 6 parl. Vote (by members entering either of two lobbies) (the house divided). â€”n. 1 dividing line. 2 watershed. [latin divido -vis-]'),
(11761, 'd', '\r\nDividend', ' n. 1 share of profits paid to shareholders or to winners in a football pool etc. 2 number to be divided. 3 benefit from an action. [anglo-french: related to *divide]'),
(11762, 'd', '\r\nDivider', ' n. 1 screen etc. Dividing a room. 2 (in pl.) Measuring-compasses.'),
(11763, 'd', '\r\nDivination', ' n. Supposed supernatural insight into the future etc. [latin: related to *divine]'),
