(11996, 'd', '\r\nDower', 'house  n. Smaller house near a big one, as part of a widow''s dower.'),
(11997, 'd', '\r\nDow-jones', 'index  n. (also dow-jones average) a figure indicating the relative price of shares on the new york stock exchange. [dow and jones, names of american economists]'),
(11998, 'd', '\r\nDown1', ' â€”adv. 1 into or towards a lower place, esp. To the ground (fall down). 2 in a lower place or position (blinds were down). 3 to or in a place regarded as lower, esp.: a southwards. B away from a major city or a university. 4 a in or into a low or weaker position or condition (hit a man when he''s down; down with a cold). B losing by (three goals down; Â£5 down). C (of a computer system) out of action. 5 from an earlier to a later time (down to 1600). 6 to a finer or thinner consistency or smaller amount or size (grind down; water down; boil down). 7 cheaper (bread is down; shares are down). 8 into a more settled state (calm down). 9 in writing or recorded form (copy it down; down on tape; down to speak next). 10 paid or dealt with as a deposit or part (Â£5 down, Â£20 to pay; three down, six to go). 11 with the current or wind. 12 (of a crossword clue or answer) read vertically (five down). â€”prep. 1 downwards along, through, or into. 2 from the top to the bottom of. 3 along (walk down the road). 4 at or in a lower part of (lives down the road). â€”attrib. Adj. 1 directed downwards (a down draught). 2 from a capital or centre (down train; down platform). â€”v. Colloq. 1 knock or bring down. 2 swallow. â€”n. 1 act of putting down. 2 reverse of fortune (ups and downs). 3 colloq.'),
(11999, 'd', '\r\nDown3', ' n. 1 open rolling land. 2 (in pl.) Chalk uplands, esp. In s. England. [old english]'),
(12000, 'd', '\r\nDown-and-out', ' n. Destitute person.  down and out predic. Adj.'),
(12001, 'd', '\r\nDownbeat', ' â€”n. Mus. Accented beat, usu. The first of the bar. â€”adj. 1 pessimistic, gloomy. 2 relaxed.'),
(12002, 'd', '\r\nDowncast', ' adj. 1 dejected. 2 (of eyes) looking downwards.'),
(12003, 'd', '\r\nDowner', ' n. Slang 1 depressant or tranquillizing drug. 2 depressing person or experience; failure. 3 = *downturn.'),
(12004, 'd', '\r\nDownfall', ' n. 1 fall from prosperity or power. 2 cause of this.'),
