(12073, 'd', '\r\nDrat', ' colloq. â€”v. (-tt-) (usu. As int.) Curse (drat the thing!). â€”int. Expressing anger or annoyance.  dratted adj. [(go)d rot]'),
(12074, 'd', '\r\nDraught', ' n. (us draft) 1 current of air in a room or chimney etc. 2 pulling, traction. 3 depth of water needed to float a ship. 4 drawing of liquor from a cask etc. 5 a single act of drinking or inhaling. B amount drunk thus. 6 (in pl.) Game for two with 12 pieces each on a draughtboard. 7 a drawing in of a fishing-net. B fish so caught.  feel the draught colloq. Suffer from esp. Financial hardship. [related to *draw]'),
(12075, 'd', '\r\nDraught', 'beer  n. Beer from the cask, not bottled or canned.'),
(12076, 'd', '\r\nDraughtboard', ' n. = *chessboard.'),
(12077, 'd', '\r\nDraught-horse', ' n. Horse for heavy work.'),
(12078, 'd', '\r\nDraughtsman', ' n. 1 person who makes drawings, plans, or sketches. 2 piece in draughts.  draughtsmanship n.'),
(12079, 'd', '\r\nDraughty', ' adj. (us drafty) (-ier, -iest) (of a room etc.) Letting in sharp currents of air.  draughtiness n.'),
(12080, 'd', '\r\nDraw', ' â€”v. (past drew; past part. Drawn) 1 pull or cause to move towards or after one. 2 pull (a thing) up, over, or across. 3 pull (curtains etc.) Open or shut. 4 take (a person) aside. 5 attract; bring; take in (drew a deep breath; felt drawn to her; drew my attention; drew a crowd). 6 (foll. By at, on) inhale from (a cigarette, pipe, etc.). 7 (also absol.) Take out; remove (a tooth, gun, cork, card, etc.). 8 obtain or take from a source (draw a salary; draw inspiration; drew Â£100 out). 9 a (also absol.) Make (a line or mark). B produce (a picture) thus. C represent (something) thus. 10 (also absol.) Finish (a contest or game) with equal scores. 11 proceed (drew near the bridge; draw to a close; drew level). 12 infer (a conclusion).'),
(12081, 'd', '\r\nDrawback', ' n. Disadvantage.'),
(12082, 'd', '\r\nDrawbridge', ' n. Hinged retractable bridge, esp. Over a moat.'),
(12083, 'd', '\r\nDrawer', ' n. 1 person or thing that draws, esp. A cheque etc. 2 also lidless boxlike storage compartment, sliding in and out of a table etc. (chest of drawers). 3 (in pl.) Knickers, underpants.'),
(12084, 'd', '\r\nDrawing', ' n. 1 art of representing by line with a pencil etc. 2 picture etc. Made thus.'),
(12085, 'd', '\r\nDrawing-board', ' n. Board on which paper is fixed for drawing on.'),
(12086, 'd', '\r\nDrawing-pin', ' n. Flat-headed pin for fastening paper etc. To a surface.'),
(12087, 'd', '\r\nDrawing-room', ' n. 1 room in a private house for sitting or entertaining in. 2 (attrib.) Restrained, polite (drawing-room manners). [earlier withdrawing-room]'),
(12088, 'd', '\r\nDrawl', ' â€”v. Speak with drawn-out vowel sounds. â€”n. Drawling utterance or way of speaking. [low german or dutch]'),
(12089, 'd', '\r\nDrawn', ' adj. Looking strained and tense.'),
(12090, 'd', '\r\nDrawstring', ' n. String or cord threaded through a waistband, bag opening, etc.'),
(12091, 'd', '\r\nDray', ' n. Low cart without sides for heavy loads, esp. Beer-barrels. [related to *draw]'),
(12092, 'd', '\r\nDread', ' â€”v. Fear greatly, esp. In advance. â€”n. Great fear or apprehension. â€”adj. 1 dreaded. 2 archaic awe-inspiring, dreadful. [old english]'),
(12093, 'd', '\r\nDreadful', ' adj. 1 terrible. 2 colloq. Very annoying, very bad.  dreadfully adv.'),
(12094, 'd', '\r\nDreadlocks', ' n.pl. Rastafarian hairstyle with hair hanging in tight braids on all sides.'),
(12095, 'd', '\r\nDream', ' â€”n. 1 series of scenes or feelings in the mind of a sleeping person. 2 day-dream or fantasy. 3 ideal, aspiration. 4 beautiful or ideal person or thing. â€”v. (past and past part. Dreamt or dreamed) 1 experience a dream. 2 imagine as in a dream. 3 (with neg.) Consider possible (never dreamt that he would come; would not dream of it). 4 (foll. By away) waste (time). 5 be inactive or unpractical.  dream up imagine, invent. Like a dream colloq. Easily, effortlessly.  dreamer n. [old english]'),
(12096, 'd', '\r\nDreamboat', ' n. Colloq. Sexually attractive or ideal person.'),
(12097, 'd', '\r\nDreamland', ' n. Ideal or imaginary land.'),
(12098, 'd', '\r\nDreamy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 given to day-dreaming or fantasy. 2 dreamlike; vague. 3 colloq. Delightful.  dreamily adv. Dreaminess n.'),
(12099, 'd', '\r\nDreary', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) dismal, dull, gloomy.  drearily adv. Dreariness n. [old english]'),
(12100, 'd', '\r\nDredge1', ' â€”n. Apparatus used to scoop up oysters etc., or to clear mud etc., from a river or sea bed. â€”v. (-ging) 1 (often foll. By up) a bring up or clear (mud etc.) With a dredge. B bring up (something forgotten) (dredged it all up). 2 clean with or use a dredge. [origin uncertain]'),
(12101, 'd', '\r\nDredge2', ' v. (-ging) sprinkle with flour, sugar, etc. [earlier = sweetmeat, from french]'),
(12102, 'd', '\r\nDredger1', ' n. 1 boat with a dredge. 2 dredge.'),
(12103, 'd', '\r\nDredger2', ' n. Container with a perforated lid, for sprinkling flour, sugar, etc.'),
(12104, 'd', '\r\nDregs', ' n.pl. 1 sediment; grounds, lees. 2 = *scum n. 2 (dregs of humanity). [old norse]'),
(12105, 'd', '\r\nDrench', ' â€”v. 1 wet thoroughly. 2 force (an animal) to take medicine. â€”n. Dose of medicine for an animal. [old english]'),
(12106, 'd', '\r\nDress', ' â€”v. 1 a (also absol.) Put clothes on. B have and wear clothes (dresses well). 2 put on evening dress. 3 arrange or adorn (hair, a shop window, etc.). 4 treat (a wound) esp. With a dressing. 5 a prepare (poultry, crab, etc.) For cooking or eating. B add dressing to (a salad etc.). 6 apply manure to. 7 finish the surface of (fabric, leather, stone, etc.). 8 correct the alignment of (troops). 9 make (an artificial fly) for fishing. â€”n. 1 woman''s garment of a bodice and skirt. 2 clothing, esp. A whole outfit. 3 formal or ceremonial costume. 4 external covering; outward form.  dress down colloq. 1 reprimand or scold. 2 dress informally. Dress up 1 put on special clothes. 2 make (a thing) more attractive or interesting. [french dresser, ultimately related to *direct]'),
(12107, 'd', '\r\nDressage', ' n. Training of a horse in obedience and deportment; display of this. [french]'),
(12108, 'd', '\r\nDress', 'circle  n. First gallery in a theatre.'),
(12109, 'd', '\r\nDress', 'coat  n. Man''s swallow-tailed evening coat.'),
(12110, 'd', '\r\nDresser1', ' n. Kitchen sideboard with shelves for plates etc. [french dresser prepare]'),
(12111, 'd', '\r\nDresser2', ' n. 1 person who helps to dress actors or actresses. 2 surgeon''s assistant in operations. 3 person who dresses in a specified way (snappy dresser).'),
(12112, 'd', '\r\nDressing', ' n. 1 putting one''s clothes on. 2 a sauce, esp. Of oil and vinegar etc., for salads (french dressing). B sauce or stuffing etc. For food. 3 bandage, ointment, etc., for a wound. 4 size or stiffening used to coat fabrics. 5 compost etc. Spread over land.'),
(12113, 'd', '\r\nDressing-down', ' n. Colloq. Scolding.'),
(12114, 'd', '\r\nDressing-gown', ' n. Loose robe worn when one is not fully dressed.'),
(12115, 'd', '\r\nDressing-room', ' n. Room for changing one''s clothes, esp. In a theatre, or attached to a bedroom.'),
(12116, 'd', '\r\nDressing-table', ' n. Table with a flat top, mirror, and drawers, used while applying make-up etc.'),
(12117, 'd', '\r\nDressmaker', ' n. Person who makes women''s clothes, esp. For a living.  dressmaking n.'),
(12118, 'd', '\r\nDress', 'rehearsal  n. Final rehearsal in full costume.'),
(12119, 'd', '\r\nDress-shield', ' n. Waterproof material in the armpit of a dress to protect it from sweat.'),
(12120, 'd', '\r\nDress-shirt', ' n. Man''s shirt worn with evening dress, usu. White with concealed buttons or studs.'),
(12121, 'd', '\r\nDressy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. (of clothes or a person) smart, elaborate, elegant.  dressiness n.'),
(12122, 'd', '\r\nDrew', ' past of *draw.'),
(12123, 'd', '\r\nDrey', ' n. Squirrel''s nest. [origin unknown]'),
(12124, 'd', '\r\nDribble', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 allow saliva to flow from the mouth. 2 flow or allow to flow in drops. 3 (also absol.) Esp. Football & hockey move (the ball) forward with slight touches of the feet or stick. â€”n. 1 act of dribbling. 2 dribbling flow. [obsolete drib = *drip]'),
(12125, 'd', '\r\nDriblet', ' n. Small quantity.'),
(12126, 'd', '\r\nDribs', 'and drabs  n.pl. Colloq. Small scattered amounts.'),
(12127, 'd', '\r\nDried', ' past and past part. Of *dry.'),
(12128, 'd', '\r\nDrier1', ' compar. Of *dry.'),
(12129, 'd', '\r\nDrier2', ' n. (also dryer) device for drying hair, laundry, etc.'),
(12130, 'd', '\r\nDriest', ' superl. Of *dry.'),
(12131, 'd', '\r\nDrift', ' â€”n. 1 a slow movement or variation. B this caused by a current. 2 intention, meaning, etc. Of what is said etc. 3 mass of snow etc. Heaped up by the wind. 4 esp. Derog. State of inaction. 5 slow deviation of a ship, aircraft, etc., from its course. 6 fragments of rock heaped up (glacial drift). 7 s.afr. Ford. â€”v. 1 be carried by or as if by a current of air or water. 2 progress casually or aimlessly (drifted into teaching). 3 pile or be piled into drifts. 4 (of a current) carry, cause to drift. [old norse and germanic trift movement of cattle]'),
(12132, 'd', '\r\nDrifter', ' n. 1 aimless person. 2 boat used for drift-net fishing.'),
(12133, 'd', '\r\nDrift-net', ' n. Net for sea fishing, allowed to drift.'),
(12134, 'd', '\r\nDriftwood', ' n. Wood floating on moving water or washed ashore.'),
(12135, 'd', '\r\nDrill1', ' â€”n. 1 tool or machine for boring holes, sinking wells, etc. 2 instruction in military exercises. 3 routine procedure in an emergency (fire-drill). 4 thorough training, esp. By repetition. 5 colloq. Recognized procedure (what''s the drill?). â€”v. 1 a make a hole in or through with a drill. B make (a hole) with a drill. 2 train or be trained by drill. [dutch]'),
(12136, 'd', '\r\nDrill2', ' â€”n. 1 machine for making furrows, sowing, and covering seed. 2 small furrow. 3 row of seeds sown by a drill. â€”v. Plant in drills. [origin unknown]'),
(12137, 'd', '\r\nDrill3', ' n. Coarse twilled cotton or linen fabric. [latin trilix having three threads]'),
(12138, 'd', '\r\nDrill4', ' n. W. African baboon related to the mandrill. [probably native]'),
(12139, 'd', '\r\nDrily', ' adv. (also dryly) in a dry manner.'),
(12140, 'd', '\r\nDrink', ' â€”v. (past drank; past part. Drunk) 1 a (also absol.) Swallow (liquid). B swallow the contents of (a vessel). 2 take alcohol, esp. To excess. 3 (of a plant, sponge, etc.) Absorb (moisture). 4 bring (oneself etc.) To a specified condition by drinking. 5 wish (a person good health etc.) By drinking (drank his health). â€”n. 1 a liquid for drinking. B draught or specified amount of this. 2 a alcoholic liquor. B portion, glass, etc. Of this. C excessive use of alcohol (took to drink). 3 (the drink) colloq. The sea.  drink in listen eagerly to. Drink to toast; wish success to. Drink up (also absol.) Drink all or the remainder of.  drinkable adj. Drinker n.'),
(12141, 'd', '\r\nDrink-driver', ' n. Person who drives with excess alcohol in the blood.  drink-driving n.'),
(12142, 'd', '\r\nDrip', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 fall or let fall in drops. 2 (often foll. By with) be so wet as to shed drops. â€”n. 1 a liquid falling in drops (steady drip of rain). B drop of liquid. C sound of dripping. 2 colloq. Dull or ineffectual person. 3 = *drip-feed.  be dripping with be full of or covered with. [danish: cf. *drop]'),
(12143, 'd', '\r\nDrip-dry', ' â€”v. Dry or leave to dry crease-free when hung up. â€”adj. Able to be drip-dried.'),
(12144, 'd', '\r\nDrip-feed', ' â€”v. Feed intravenously in drops. â€”n. 1 feeding thus. 2 apparatus for doing this.'),
(12145, 'd', '\r\nDripping', ' n. Fat melted from roasted meat.'),
(12146, 'd', '\r\nDrippy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang ineffectual; sloppily sentimental.'),
(12147, 'd', '\r\nDrive', ' â€”v. (-ving; past drove; past part. Driven) 1 urge forward, esp. Forcibly. 2 a compel (was driven to complain). B force into a specified state (drove him mad). C (often refl.) Urge to overwork. 3 a operate and direct (a vehicle, locomotive, etc.). B convey or be conveyed in a vehicle. C be competent to drive (a vehicle) (does he drive?). D travel in a private vehicle. 4 (of wind etc.) Carry along, propel, esp. Rapidly (driven snow; driving rain). 5 a (often foll. By into) force (a stake, nail, etc.) Into place by blows. B bore (a tunnel etc.). 6 effect or conclude forcibly (drove a hard bargain; drove his point home). 7 (of power) operate (machinery). 8 (usu. Foll. By at) work hard; dash, rush. 9 hit (a ball) forcibly. â€”n. 1 journey or excursion in a vehicle. 2 a (esp. Scenic) street or road. B private road through a garden to a house. 3 a motivation and energy. B inner urge (sex-drive). 4 forcible stroke of a bat etc. 5 organized effort (membership drive). 6 a transmission of power to machinery, wheels, etc. B position of the steering-wheel in a vehicle (left-hand drive). C computing = *disk drive. 7 organized whist, bingo, etc. Competition.  drive at seek, intend, or mean (what is he driving at?). [old english]'),
(12148, 'd', '\r\nDrive-in', ' â€”attrib. Adj. (of a bank, cinema, etc.) Used while sitting in one''s car. â€”n. Such a bank, cinema, etc.'),
(12149, 'd', '\r\nDrivel', ' â€”n. Silly talk; nonsense. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-, -ling) 1 talk drivel. 2 run at the mouth or nose. [old english]'),
(12150, 'd', '\r\nDriven', ' past part. Of *drive.'),
(12151, 'd', '\r\nDrive-on', ' adj. (of a ship) on to which vehicles may be driven.'),
(12152, 'd', '\r\nDriver', ' n. 1 person who drives a vehicle. 2 golf-club for driving from a tee.'),
(12153, 'd', '\r\nDriveway', ' n. = *drive n. 2b.'),
(12154, 'd', '\r\nDriving-licence', ' n. Licence permitting one to drive a vehicle.'),
(12155, 'd', '\r\nDriving', 'test  n. Official test of competence to drive.'),
(12156, 'd', '\r\nDriving-wheel', ' n. Wheel communicating motive power in machinery.'),
(12157, 'd', '\r\nDrizzle', ' â€”n. Very fine rain. â€”v. (-ling) (of rain) fall in very fine drops.  drizzly adj. [old english]'),
(12158, 'd', '\r\nDroll', ' adj. Quaintly amusing; strange, odd.  drollery n. (pl. -ies). Drolly adv. [french]'),
(12159, 'd', '\r\nDromedary', ' n. (pl. -ies) one-humped (esp. Arabian) camel bred for riding. [greek dromas -ados runner]'),
(12160, 'd', '\r\nDrone', ' â€”n. 1 non-working male of the honey-bee. 2 idler. 3 deep humming sound. 4 monotonous speaking tone. 5 bass-pipe of bagpipes or its continuous note. â€”v. (-ning) 1 make a deep humming sound. 2 speak or utter monotonously. [old english]'),
(12161, 'd', '\r\nDrool', ' v. 1 slobber, dribble. 2 (often foll. By over) admire extravagantly. [from *drivel]'),
(12162, 'd', '\r\nDroop', ' â€”v. 1 bend or hang down, esp. From weariness; flag. 2 (of the eyes) look downwards. â€”n. 1 drooping attitude. 2 loss of spirit.  droopy adj. [old norse: related to *drop]'),
(12163, 'd', '\r\nDrop', ' â€”n. 1 a globule of liquid that hangs, falls, or adheres to a surface. B very small amount of liquid (just a drop left). C glass etc. Of alcohol. 2 a abrupt fall or slope. B amount of this (drop of fifteen feet). C act of dropping. D fall in prices, temperature, etc. E deterioration (drop in status). 3 drop-shaped thing, esp. A pendant or sweet. 4 curtain or scenery let down on to a stage. 5 (in pl.) Liquid medicine used in drops (eye drops). 6 minute quantity. 7 slang hiding-place for stolen goods etc. 8 slang bribe. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 fall or let fall in drops, shed (tears, blood). 2 fall or allow to fall; let go. 3 a sink down from exhaustion or injury. B die. C fall naturally (drop asleep; drop into the habit). 4 a (cause to) cease or lapse; abandon. B colloq. Cease to associate with or discuss. 5 set down (a passenger etc.) (drop me here). 6 utter or be uttered casually (dropped a hint). 7 send casually (drop a line). 8 a fall or allow to fall in direction, amount, condition, degree, pitch, etc. (voice dropped; wind dropped; we dropped the price). B (of a person) jump down lightly; let oneself fall. C allow (trousers etc.) To fall to the ground. 9 omit (a letter) in speech (drop one''s h''s). 10 (as dropped adj.) In a lower position than usual (dropped handlebars; dropped waist). 11 give birth to (esp. A lamb). 12 lose (a game, point, etc.). 13 deliver by parachute etc. 14 football send (a ball), or score (a goal), by a drop-kick. 15 colloq. Dismiss or omit (dropped from the team).  at the drop of a hat promptly, instantly. Drop back (or behind) fall back; get left behind. Drop a brick colloq. Make an indiscreet or embarrassing remark. Drop a curtsy curtsy. Drop in (or by) colloq. Visit casually. Drop off 1 fall asleep. 2 drop (a passenger). Drop out colloq. Cease to participate.  droplet n. [old english]'),
(12164, 'd', '\r\nDrop-curtain', ' n. Painted curtain lowered on to a stage.'),
(12165, 'd', '\r\nDrop-kick', ' n. Football kick as the ball touches the ground having been dropped.'),
(12166, 'd', '\r\nDrop-out', ' n. Colloq. Person who has dropped out of conventional society, a course of study, etc.'),
(12167, 'd', '\r\nDropper', ' n. Device for releasing liquid in drops.'),
(12168, 'd', '\r\nDroppings', ' n.pl. 1 dung of animals or birds. 2 thing that falls or has fallen in drops.'),
(12169, 'd', '\r\nDrop', 'scone  n. Scone made by dropping a spoonful of mixture into the pan etc.'),
(12170, 'd', '\r\nDrop-shot', ' n. (in tennis) shot dropping abruptly over the net.'),
(12171, 'd', '\r\nDropsy', ' n. = *oedema.  dropsical adj. [earlier hydropsy from greek hudrops: related to *hydro-]'),
(12172, 'd', '\r\nDrosophila', ' n. Fruit fly used in genetic research. [greek, = dew-loving]'),
(12173, 'd', '\r\nDross', ' n. 1 rubbish. 2 a scum from melted metals. B impurities. [old english]'),
(12174, 'd', '\r\nDrought', ' n. Prolonged absence of rain. [old english]'),
(12175, 'd', '\r\nDrove1', ' past of *drive.'),
(12176, 'd', '\r\nDrove2', ' n. 1 a moving crowd. B (in pl.) Colloq. Great number (people arrived in droves). 2 herd or flock driven or moving together. [old english: related to *drive]'),
(12177, 'd', '\r\nDrover', ' n. Herder of cattle.'),
(12178, 'd', '\r\nDrown', ' v. 1 kill or die by submersion in liquid. 2 submerge; flood; drench. 3 deaden (grief etc.) By drinking. 4 (often foll. By out) overpower (sound) with louder sound. [probably old english]'),
(12179, 'd', '\r\nDrowse', ' v. (-sing) be lightly asleep. [from *drowsy]'),
(12180, 'd', '\r\nDrowsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) very sleepy, almost asleep.  drowsily adv. Drowsiness n. [probably old english]'),
(12181, 'd', '\r\nDrub', ' v. (-bb-) 1 beat, thrash. 2 defeat thoroughly.  drubbing n. [arabic daraba beat]'),
(12182, 'd', '\r\nDrudge', ' â€”n. Person who does dull, laborious, or menial work. â€”v. (-ging) work laboriously, toil.  drudgery n. [origin uncertain]'),
(12183, 'd', '\r\nDrug', ' â€”n. 1 medicinal substance. 2 (esp. Addictive) narcotic, hallucinogen, or stimulant. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 add a drug to (food or drink). 2 a give a drug to. B stupefy. [french]'),
(12184, 'd', '\r\nDrugget', ' n. Coarse woven fabric used for floor coverings etc. [french]'),
(12185, 'd', '\r\nDruggist', ' n. Pharmacist. [related to *drug]'),
(12186, 'd', '\r\nDrugstore', ' n. Us combined chemist''s shop and cafÃ©.'),
(12187, 'd', '\r\nDruid', ' n. 1 priest of an ancient celtic religion. 2 member of a modern druidic order, esp. The gorsedd.  druidic adj. Druidism n. [latin from celtic]'),
(12188, 'd', '\r\nDrum', ' â€”n. 1 hollow esp. Cylindrical percussion instrument covered at the end(s) with plastic etc. 2 (often in pl.) Percussion section of an orchestra etc. 3 sound made by a drum. 4 thing resembling a drum, esp. A container, etc. 5 segment of a pillar. 6 eardrum. â€”v. (-mm-) 1 play a drum. 2 beat or tap continuously with the fingers etc. 3 (of a bird or insect) make a loud noise with the wings.  drum into drive (a lesson or facts) into (a person) by persistence. Drum out dismiss with ignominy. Drum up summon or get by vigorous effort (drum up support). [low german]'),
(12189, 'd', '\r\nDrumbeat', ' n. Stroke or sound of a stroke on a drum.'),
(12190, 'd', '\r\nDrum', 'brake  n. Brake in which brake shoes on a vehicle press against the brake drum on a wheel.'),
(12191, 'd', '\r\nDrumhead', ' n. Part of a drum that is hit.'),
(12192, 'd', '\r\nDrum', 'kit  n. Set of drums in a band etc.'),
(12193, 'd', '\r\nDrum', 'machine  n. Electronic device that simulates percussion.'),
(12194, 'd', '\r\nDrum', 'major  n. Leader of a marching band.'),
(12195, 'd', '\r\nDrum', 'majorette  n. Female baton-twirling member of a parading group.'),
(12196, 'd', '\r\nDrummer', ' n. Player of drums.'),
(12197, 'd', '\r\nDrumstick', ' n. 1 stick for beating drums. 2 lower leg of a dressed fowl.'),
(12198, 'd', '\r\nDrunk', ' â€”adj. 1 lacking control from drinking alcohol. 2 (often foll. By with) overcome with joy, success, power, etc. â€”n. Person who is drunk, esp. Habitually. [past part. Of *drink]'),
(12199, 'd', '\r\nDrunkard', ' n. Person who is habitually drunk.'),
(12200, 'd', '\r\nDrunken', ' adj. (usu. Attrib.) 1 = *drunk 1. 2 caused by or involving drunkenness (drunken brawl). 3 often drunk.  drunkenly adv. Drunkenness n.'),
(12201, 'd', '\r\nDrupe', ' n. Fleshy stone-fruit, e.g. The olive and plum. [latin from greek]'),
(12202, 'd', '\r\nDry', ' â€”adj. (drier; driest) 1 free from moisture, esp.: a with moisture having evaporated, drained away, etc. (clothes are not dry yet). B (of eyes) free from tears. C (of a climate etc.) With insufficient rain; not rainy (dry spell). D (of a river, well, etc.) Dried up. E using or producing no moisture (dry shampoo; dry cough). F (of a shave) with an electric razor. 2 (of wine) not sweet (dry sherry). 3 a plain, unelaborated (dry facts). B uninteresting (dry book). 4 (of a sense of humour) subtle, ironic, understated. 5 prohibiting the sale of alcohol (a dry state). 6 (of bread) without butter etc. 7 (of provisions etc.) Solid, not liquid. 8 impassive. 9 (of a cow) not yielding milk. 10 colloq. Thirsty (feel dry). â€”v. (dries, dried) 1 make or become dry. 2 (usu. As dried adj.) Preserve (food etc.) By removing moisture. 3 (often foll. By up) colloq. Forget one''s lines. â€”n. (pl. Dries) 1 act of drying. 2 dry ginger ale. 3 dry place (come into the dry).  dry out 1 make or become fully dry. 2 treat or be treated for alcoholism. Dry up 1 make or become utterly dry. 2 dry dishes. 3 colloq. (esp. In imper.) Cease talking. 4 become unproductive. 5 (of supplies) run out.  dryness n. [old english]'),
(12203, 'd', '\r\nDryad', ' n. Wood nymph. [greek drus tree]'),
(12204, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'battery  n. (also dry cell) electric battery or cell in which electrolyte is absorbed in a solid.'),
(12205, 'd', '\r\nDry-clean', ' v. Clean (clothes etc.) With solvents without water.  dry-cleaner n.'),
(12206, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'dock  n. Dock that can be pumped dry for building or repairing ships.'),
(12207, 'd', '\r\nDryer', ' var. Of *drier2.'),
(12208, 'd', '\r\nDry-fly', ' attrib. Adj. (of fishing) with a floating artificial fly.'),
(12209, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'ice  n. Solid carbon dioxide used as a refrigerant.'),
(12210, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'land  n. Land as distinct from sea etc.'),
(12211, 'd', '\r\nDryly', ' var. Of *drily.'),
(12212, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'measure  n. Measure for dry goods.'),
(12213, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'rot  n. Decayed state of unventilated wood; fungi causing this.'),
(12214, 'd', '\r\nDry', 'run  n. Colloq. Rehearsal.'),
(12215, 'd', '\r\nDry-shod', ' adj. & adv. Without wetting one''s shoes.'),
(12216, 'd', '\r\nDrystone', ' attrib. Adj. (of a wall etc.) Built without mortar.'),
(12217, 'd', '\r\nDsc', ' abbr. Distinguished service cross.'),
(12218, 'd', '\r\nD.sc.', ' Abbr. Doctor of science.'),
(12219, 'd', '\r\nDsm', ' abbr. Distinguished service medal.'),
(12220, 'd', '\r\nDso', ' abbr. Distinguished service order.'),
(12221, 'd', '\r\nDss', ' abbr. Department of social security (formerly dhss).'),
(12222, 'd', '\r\nDt', ' abbr. (also dt''s) delirium tremens.'),
(12223, 'd', '\r\nDti', ' abbr. Department of trade and industry.'),
(12224, 'd', '\r\nDual', ' â€”adj. 1 in two parts; twofold. 2 double (dual ownership). â€”n. Gram. Dual number or form.  duality n. [latin duo two]'),
(12225, 'd', '\r\nDual', 'carriageway  n. Road with a dividing strip between traffic flowing in opposite directions.'),
(12226, 'd', '\r\nDual', 'control  n. Two linked sets of controls, enabling operation by either of two persons.'),
(12227, 'd', '\r\nDub1', ' v. (-bb-) 1 make (a person) a knight by touching his shoulders with a sword. 2 give (a person) a name, nickname, etc. 3 smear (leather) with grease. [french]'),
(12228, 'd', '\r\nDub2', ' v. (-bb-) 1 provide (a film etc.) With an, esp. Translated, alternative soundtrack. 2 add (sound effects or music) to a film or broadcast. 3 transfer or make a copy of (recorded sound or images). [abbreviation of *double]'),
(12229, 'd', '\r\nDubbin', ' n. (also dubbing) thick grease for softening and waterproofing leather. [see *dub1 3]'),
(12230, 'd', '\r\nDubiety', ' n. Literary doubt. [latin: related to *dubious]'),
(12231, 'd', '\r\nDubious', ' adj. 1 hesitating, doubtful. 2 questionable; suspicious. 3 unreliable.  dubiously adv. Dubiousness n. [latin dubium doubt]'),
(12232, 'd', '\r\nDucal', ' adj. Of or like a duke. [french: related to *duke]'),
(12233, 'd', '\r\nDucat', ' n. Gold coin, formerly current in most of europe. [medieval latin ducatus *duchy]'),
(12234, 'd', '\r\nDuchess', ' n. 1 duke''s wife or widow. 2 woman holding the rank of duke. [medieval latin ducissa: related to *duke]'),
(12235, 'd', '\r\nDuchesse', 'potatoes  n.pl. Mashed potatoes mixed with egg, baked or fried, and served as small cakes or used as piping. [french]'),
(12236, 'd', '\r\nDuchy', ' n. (pl. -ies) territory of a duke or duchess; royal dukedom of cornwall or lancaster. [medieval latin ducatus: related to *duke]'),
(12237, 'd', '\r\nDuck1', ' â€”n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 a swimming-bird, esp. The domesticated form of the mallard or wild duck. B female of this. C its flesh as food. 2 score of 0 in cricket. 3 (also ducks) colloq. (esp. As a form of address) dear. â€”v. 1 bob down, esp. To avoid being seen or hit. 2 a dip one''s head briefly under water. B plunge (a person) briefly in water. 3 colloq. Dodge (a task etc.).  like water off a duck''s back colloq. Producing no effect. [old english]'),
(12238, 'd', '\r\nDuck2', ' n. 1 strong linen or cotton fabric. 2 (in pl.) Trousers made of this. [dutch]'),
(12239, 'd', '\r\nDuckbill', ' n. (also duck-billed platypus) = *platypus.'),
(12240, 'd', '\r\nDuckboard', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Path of wooden slats over muddy ground, in a trench, etc.'),
(12241, 'd', '\r\nDuckling', ' n. Young duck.'),
(12242, 'd', '\r\nDucks', 'and drakes  n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Game of making a flat stone skim the surface of water.  play ducks and drakes with colloq. Squander.'),
(12243, 'd', '\r\nDuckweed', ' n. Any of various plants growing on the surface of still water.'),
(12244, 'd', '\r\nDucky', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. (esp. As a form of address) dear.'),
(12245, 'd', '\r\nDuct', ' â€”n. Channel or tube for conveying a fluid, cable, bodily secretions, etc. (tear ducts). â€”v. Convey through a duct. [latin ductus from duco duct- lead]'),
(12246, 'd', '\r\nDuctile', ' adj. 1 (of metal) capable of being drawn into wire; pliable. 2 easily moulded. 3 docile.  ductility n. [latin: related to *duct]'),
(12247, 'd', '\r\nDuctless', 'gland  n. Gland secreting directly into the bloodstream.'),
(12248, 'd', '\r\nDud', ' slang â€”n. 1 useless or broken thing. 2 counterfeit article. 3 (in pl.) Clothes, rags. â€”adj. Useless, defective. [origin unknown]'),
(12249, 'd', '\r\nDude', ' n. Slang 1 fellow. 2 us dandy. 3 us city-dweller staying on a ranch. [german dial. Dude fool]'),
(12250, 'd', '\r\nDudgeon', ' n. Resentment, indignation.  in high dudgeon very angry. [origin unknown]'),
(12251, 'd', '\r\nDue', ' â€”adj. 1 owing or payable. 2 (often foll. By to) merited; appropriate. 3 (foll. By to) that ought to be given or ascribed to (a person, cause, etc.) (first place is due to milton; difficulty due to ignorance). 4 (often foll.'),
(12252, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of due to to mean â€˜because ofâ€™ as in the example given is regarded as unacceptable by some people and could be avoided by substituting his lateness was due to an accident. Alternatively, owing to could be used.'),
(12253, 'd', '\r\nDuel', ' â€”n. 1 armed contest between two people, usu. To the death. 2 two-sided contest. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) fight a duel.  duellist n. [latin duellum war]'),
(12254, 'd', '\r\nDuenna', ' n. Older woman acting as a chaperon to girls, esp. In spain. [spanish from latin domina *don1]'),
(12255, 'd', '\r\nDuet', ' n. Musical composition for two performers.  duettist n. [latin duo two]'),
(12256, 'd', '\r\nDuff1', ' â€”n. Boiled pudding. â€”adj. Slang worthless, counterfeit, useless. [var. Of *dough]'),
(12257, 'd', '\r\nDuff2', ' v.  duff up slang beat; thrash. [perhaps from *duffer]'),
(12258, 'd', '\r\nDuffer', ' n. Colloq. Inefficient or stupid person; dunce. [origin uncertain]'),
(12259, 'd', '\r\nDuffle', ' n. (also duffel) heavy woollen cloth. [duffel in belgium]'),
(12260, 'd', '\r\nDuffle', 'bag  n. Cylindrical canvas bag closed by a drawstring.'),
(12261, 'd', '\r\nDuffle-coat', ' n. Hooded overcoat of duffle, fastened with toggles.'),
(12262, 'd', '\r\nDug1', ' past and past part. Of *dig.'),
(12263, 'd', '\r\nDug2', ' n. Udder, teat. [origin unknown]'),
(12264, 'd', '\r\nDugong', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) asian sea-mammal. [malay]'),
(12265, 'd', '\r\nDugout', ' n. 1 a roofed shelter, esp. For troops in trenches. B underground shelter. 2 canoe made from a tree-trunk.'),
(12266, 'd', '\r\nDuke', ' n. 1 person holding the highest hereditary title of the nobility. 2 sovereign prince ruling a duchy or small state.  dukedom n. [latin dux leader]'),
(12267, 'd', '\r\nDulcet', ' adj. Sweet-sounding. [latin dulcis sweet]'),
(12268, 'd', '\r\nDulcimer', ' n. Metal stringed instrument struck with two hand-held hammers. [latin: related to *dulcet, melos song]'),
(12269, 'd', '\r\nDull', ' â€”adj. 1 tedious; not interesting. 2 (of the weather) overcast. 3 (of colour, light, sound, etc.) Not bright, vivid, or clear. 4 (of a pain) indistinct; not acute (a dull ache). 5 slow-witted; stupid. 6 (of a knife-edge etc.) Blunt. 7 a (of trade etc.) Sluggish, slow. B listless; depressed. 8 (of the ears, eyes, etc.) Lacking keenness. â€”v. Make or become dull.  dullness n. Dully adv. [low german or dutch]'),
(12270, 'd', '\r\nDullard', ' n. Stupid person.'),
(12271, 'd', '\r\nDuly', ' adv. 1 in due time or manner. 2 rightly, properly.'),
(12272, 'd', '\r\nDumb', ' adj. 1 a unable to speak. B (of an animal) naturally dumb. 2 silenced by surprise, shyness, etc. 3 taciturn, reticent (dumb insolence). 4 suffered or done in silence (dumb agony). 5 colloq. Stupid; ignorant. 6 disenfranchised; inarticulate (dumb masses). 7 (of a computer terminal etc.) Able to transmit or receive but unable to process data. 8 giving no sound. [old english]'),
(12273, 'd', '\r\nDumb-bell', ' n. 1 short bar with a weight at each end, for muscle-building etc. 2 slang stupid person, esp. A woman.'),
(12274, 'd', '\r\nDumbfound', ' v. Nonplus, make speechless with surprise. [from *dumb, *confound]'),
(12275, 'd', '\r\nDumbo', ' n. (pl. -s) slang stupid person. [from *dumb, *-o]'),
(12276, 'd', '\r\nDumb', 'show  n. Gestures; mime.'),
(12277, 'd', '\r\nDumbstruck', ' adj. Speechless with surprise.'),
(12278, 'd', '\r\nDumb', 'waiter  n. Small hand-operated lift for conveying food from kitchen to dining-room.'),
(12279, 'd', '\r\nDumdum', ' n. (in full dumdum bullet) soft-nosed bullet that expands on impact. [dum-dum in india]'),
(12280, 'd', '\r\nDummy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 model of a human figure, esp. As used to display clothes or by a ventriloquist or as a target. 2 (often attrib.) Imitation object used to replace a real or normal one. 3 baby''s rubber or plastic teat. 4 colloq. Stupid person. 5 figurehead. 6 imaginary player in bridge etc., whose cards are exposed and played by a partner. â€”attrib. Adj.'),
(12281, 'd', '\r\nDummy', 'run  n. Trial attempt; rehearsal.'),
(12282, 'd', '\r\nDump', ' â€”n. 1 place or heap for depositing rubbish. 2 colloq. Unpleasant or dreary place. 3 temporary store of ammunition etc. â€”v. 1 put down firmly or clumsily. 2 deposit as rubbish. 3 colloq. Abandon or get rid of. 4 sell (excess goods) to a foreign market at a low price. 5 copy (the contents of a computer memory etc.) As a diagnostic aid or for security.  dump on esp. Us criticize or abuse; get the better of. [origin uncertain]'),
(12283, 'd', '\r\nDumpling', ' n. 1 ball of dough boiled in stew or containing apple etc. 2 small fat person. [dump small round object]'),
(12284, 'd', '\r\nDumps', ' n.pl. (usu. In down in the dumps) colloq. Low spirits. [low german or dutch: related to *damp]'),
(12285, 'd', '\r\nDump', 'truck  n. Truck that tilts or opens at the back for unloading.'),
(12286, 'd', '\r\nDumpy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) short and stout.  dumpily adv. Dumpiness n. [related to *dumpling]'),
(12287, 'd', '\r\nDun', ' â€”adj. Greyish-brown. â€”n. 1 dun colour. 2 dun horse. [old english]'),
(12288, 'd', '\r\nDunce', ' n. Person slow at learning; dullard. [duns scotus, name of a philosopher]'),
(12289, 'd', '\r\nDunce''s', 'cap  n. Paper cone worn by a dunce.'),
(12290, 'd', '\r\nDunderhead', ' n. Stupid person. [origin unknown]'),
(12291, 'd', '\r\nDune', ' n. Drift of sand etc. Formed by the wind. [dutch: related to *down3]'),
(12292, 'd', '\r\nDung', ' â€”n. Excrement of animals; manure. â€”v. Apply dung to (land). [old english]'),
(12293, 'd', '\r\nDungaree', ' n. 1 coarse cotton cloth. 2 (in pl.) Overalls or trousers of this. [hindi]'),
(12294, 'd', '\r\nDung-beetle', ' n. Beetle whose larvae develop in dung.'),
(12295, 'd', '\r\nDungeon', ' n. Underground prison cell. [earlier donjon keep of a castle; ultimately from latin dominus lord]'),
(12296, 'd', '\r\nDunghill', ' n. Heap of dung or refuse.'),
(12297, 'd', '\r\nDunk', ' v. 1 dip (food) into liquid before eating. 2 immerse. [german tunken dip]'),
(12298, 'd', '\r\nDunlin', ' n. Red-backed sandpiper. [probably from *dun]'),
(12299, 'd', '\r\nDunnock', ' n. Hedge sparrow. [apparently from *dun]'),
(12300, 'd', '\r\nDuo', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 pair of performers. 2 duet. [italian from latin, = two]'),
(12301, 'd', '\r\nDuodecimal', ' adj. 1 of twelfths or twelve. 2 in or by twelves. [latin duodecim twelve]'),
(12302, 'd', '\r\nDuodenum', ' n. (pl. -s) first part of the small intestine immediately below the stomach.  duodenal adj. [medieval latin: related to *duodecimal]'),
(12303, 'd', '\r\nDuologue', ' n. Dialogue between two people. [from *duo, *monologue]'),
(12304, 'd', '\r\nDupe', ' â€”n. Victim of deception. â€”v. (-ping) deceive, trick. [french]'),
(12305, 'd', '\r\nDuple', ' adj. Of two parts. [latin duplus]'),
(12306, 'd', '\r\nDuple', 'time  n. Mus. Rhythm with two beats to the bar.'),
(12307, 'd', '\r\nDuplex', ' â€”n. (often attrib.) Esp. Us 1 flat on two floors. 2 house subdivided for two families; semi-detached house. â€”adj. 1 of two parts. 2 computing (of a circuit) allowing simultaneous two-way transmission of signals. [latin, = double]'),
(12308, 'd', '\r\nDuplicate', ' â€”adj. 1 identical. 2 a having two identical parts. B doubled. 3 (of card-games) with the same hands played by different players. â€”n. 1 identical thing, esp. A copy. 2 copy of a letter etc. â€”v. (-ting) 1 multiply by two; double. 2 make or be an exact copy of. 3 repeat (an action etc.), esp. Unnecessarily.  in duplicate in two exact copies.  duplication n. [latin: related to *duplex]'),
(12309, 'd', '\r\nDuplicator', ' n. Machine for making multiple copies of a text etc.'),
(12310, 'd', '\r\nDuplicity', ' n. Double-dealing; deceitfulness.  duplicitous adj. [latin: related to *duplex]'),
(12311, 'd', '\r\nDurable', ' â€”adj. 1 lasting; hard-wearing. 2 (of goods) with a relatively long useful life. â€”n. (in pl.) Durable goods.  durability n. [latin durus hard]'),
(12312, 'd', '\r\nDura', 'mater  n. Tough outermost membrane enveloping the brain and spinal cord. [medieval latin = hard mother, translation of arabic]'),
(12313, 'd', '\r\nDuration', ' n. 1 time taken by an event. 2 specified length of time (duration of a minute).  for the duration 1 until the end of an event. 2 for a very long time. [medieval latin: related to *durable]'),
(12314, 'd', '\r\nDuress', ' n. 1 compulsion, esp. Illegal use of threats or violence (under duress). 2 imprisonment. [latin durus hard]'),
(12315, 'd', '\r\nDurex', ' n. Propr. Condom. [origin uncertain]'),
(12316, 'd', '\r\nDuring', ' prep. Throughout or at some point in. [latin: related to *durable]'),
(12317, 'd', '\r\nDusk', ' n. Darker stage of twilight. [old english]'),
(12318, 'd', '\r\nDusky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 shadowy; dim. 2 dark-coloured; dark-skinned.  duskily adv. Duskiness n.'),
(12319, 'd', '\r\nDust', ' â€”n. 1 finely powdered earth or other material etc. (pollen dust). 2 dead person''s remains. 3 confusion, turmoil. â€”v. 1 wipe the dust from (furniture etc.). 2 a sprinkle with powder, sugar, etc. B sprinkle (sugar, powder, etc.).  dust down 1 dust the clothes of. 2 colloq. Reprimand. 3 = dust off. Dust off 1 remove the dust from. 2 use again after a long period. When the dust settles when things quieten down. [old english]'),
(12320, 'd', '\r\nDustbin', ' n. Container for household refuse.'),
(12321, 'd', '\r\nDust', 'bowl  n. Desert made by drought or erosion.'),
(12322, 'd', '\r\nDustcart', ' n. Vehicle collecting household refuse.'),
(12323, 'd', '\r\nDust', 'cover  n. 1 = *dust-sheet. 2 = *dust-jacket.'),
(12324, 'd', '\r\nDuster', ' n. Cloth for dusting furniture etc.'),
(12325, 'd', '\r\nDust-jacket', ' n. Paper cover on a hardback book.'),
(12326, 'd', '\r\nDustman', ' n. Person employed to collect household refuse.'),
(12327, 'd', '\r\nDustpan', ' n. Pan into which dust is brushed from the floor.'),
(12328, 'd', '\r\nDust-sheet', ' n. Protective cloth over furniture.'),
(12329, 'd', '\r\nDust-up', ' n. Colloq. Fight, disturbance.'),
(12330, 'd', '\r\nDusty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of or covered with dust. 2 (of a colour) dull or muted.  not so dusty slang fairly good.  dustily adv. Dustiness n.'),
(12331, 'd', '\r\nDusty', 'answer  n. Colloq. Curt refusal.'),
(12332, 'd', '\r\nDutch', ' â€”adj. Of the netherlands or its people or language. â€”n. 1 the dutch language. 2 (prec. By the; treated as pl.) The people of the netherlands.  go dutch share expenses on an outing etc. [dutch]'),
