(12340, 'd', '\r\nDutch', 'oven  n. 1 metal box with the open side facing a fire. 2 covered cooking-pot for braising etc.'),
(12341, 'd', '\r\nDutch', 'treat  n. Party, outing, etc., at which people pay for themselves.'),
(12342, 'd', '\r\nDutch', 'uncle  n. Kind but firm adviser.'),
(12343, 'd', '\r\nDuteous', ' adj. Literary dutiful.  duteously adv.'),
(12344, 'd', '\r\nDutiable', ' adj. Requiring the payment of duty.'),
(12345, 'd', '\r\nDutiful', ' adj. Doing one''s duty; obedient.  dutifully adv.'),
(12346, 'd', '\r\nDuty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a moral or legal obligation; responsibility. B binding force of what is right. 2 tax on certain goods, imports, etc. 3 job or function arising from a business or office (playground duty). 4 deference; respect due to a superior.  do duty for serve as or pass for (something else). On (or off) duty working (or not working). [anglo-french: related to *due]'),
(12347, 'd', '\r\nDuty-bound', ' adj. Obliged by duty.'),
(12348, 'd', '\r\nDuty-free', ' adj. (of goods) on which duty is not payable.'),
(12349, 'd', '\r\nDuty-free', 'shop  n. Shop at an airport etc. Selling duty-free goods.'),
(12350, 'd', '\r\nDuvet', ' n. Thick soft quilt used instead of sheets and blankets. [french]'),
(12351, 'd', '\r\nDwarf', ' â€”n. (pl. -s or dwarves) 1 person, animal, or plant much below normal size. 2 small mythological being with magical powers. 3 small usu. Dense star. â€”v. 1 stunt in growth. 2 make seem small.  dwarfish adj. [old english]'),
(12352, 'd', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1, with regard to people, the term person of restricted growth is now often preferred.'),
(12353, 'd', '\r\nDwell', ' v. (past and past part. Dwelt or dwelled) live, reside.  dwell on (or upon) think, write, or speak at length on.  dweller n. [old english, = lead astray]'),
(12354, 'd', '\r\nDwelling', ' n. House, residence.'),
(12355, 'd', '\r\nDwindle', ' v. (-ling) 1 become gradually less or smaller. 2 lose importance. [old english]'),
(12356, 'd', '\r\nDy', ' symb. Dysprosium.'),
(12357, 'd', '\r\nDye', ' â€”n. 1 substance used to change the colour of hair, fabric, etc. 2 colour so produced. â€”v. (dyeing, dyed) 1 colour with dye. 2 dye a specified colour (dyed it yellow).  dyer n. [old english]'),
(12358, 'd', '\r\nDyed-in-the-wool', ' adj. (usu. Attrib.) Out and out; unchangeable.'),
(12359, 'd', '\r\nDying', ' attrib. Adj. Of, or at the time of, death (dying words).'),
(12360, 'd', '\r\nDyke1', ' (also dike) â€”n. 1 embankment built to prevent flooding. 2 low wall of turf or stone. â€”v. (-king) provide or protect with dyke(s). [related to *ditch]'),
(12361, 'd', '\r\nDyke2', ' n. (also dike) slang lesbian. [origin unknown]'),
(12362, 'd', '\r\nDynamic', ' adj. 1 energetic; active. 2 physics a of motive force. B of force in actual operation. 3 of dynamics.  dynamically adv. [greek dunamis power]'),
(12363, 'd', '\r\nDynamics', ' n.pl. 1 (usu. Treated as sing.) A mathematical study of motion and the forces causing it. B branch of any science concerned with forces or changes. 2 motive forces in any sphere. 3 mus. Variation in loudness.'),
(12364, 'd', '\r\nDynamism', ' n. Energy; dynamic power.'),
(12365, 'd', '\r\nDynamite', ' â€”n. 1 high explosive mixture containing nitroglycerine. 2 potentially dangerous person etc. â€”v. (-ting) charge or blow up with dynamite.'),
(12366, 'd', '\r\nDynamo', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 machine converting mechanical into electrical energy, esp. By rotating coils of copper wire in a magnetic field. 2 colloq. Energetic person. [abbreviation of dynamo-electric machine]'),
(12367, 'd', '\r\nDynamometer', ' n. Instrument measuring energy expended. [greek: related to *dynamic]'),
(12368, 'd', '\r\nDynast', ' n. 1 ruler. 2 member of a dynasty. [latin from greek]'),
(12369, 'd', '\r\nDynasty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 line of hereditary rulers. 2 succession of leaders in any field.  dynastic adj. [latin from greek]'),
(12370, 'd', '\r\nDyne', ' n. Physics force required to give a mass of one gram an acceleration of one centimetre per second per second. [greek dunamis force]'),
(12371, 'd', '\r\nDys-', ' prefix bad, difficult. [greek]'),
(12372, 'd', '\r\nDysentery', ' n. Inflammation of the intestines, causing severe diarrhoea. [greek entera bowels]'),
(12373, 'd', '\r\nDysfunction', ' n. Abnormality or impairment of functioning.'),
(12374, 'd', '\r\nDyslexia', ' n. Abnormal difficulty in reading and spelling.  dyslectic adj. & n. Dyslexic adj. & n. [greek lexis speech]'),
(12375, 'd', '\r\nDysmenorrhoea', ' n. Painful or difficult menstruation.'),
(12376, 'd', '\r\nDyspepsia', ' n. Indigestion.  dyspeptic adj. & n. [greek peptos digested]'),
(12377, 'd', '\r\nDysphasia', ' n. Lack of coordination in speech, owing to brain damage. [greek dusphatos hard to utter]'),
(12378, 'd', '\r\nDysprosium', ' n. Metallic element of the lanthanide series. [greek dusprositos hard to get at]'),
(12379, 'd', '\r\nDystrophy', ' n. Defective nutrition. [greek -trophe nourishment]'),
(13705, 'e', 'E1', ' n. (also e) (pl. Es or e''s) 1 fifth letter of the alphabet. 2 mus. Third note of the diatonic scale of c major.'),
(13706, 'e', '\r\nE2', ' abbr. (also e.) 1 east, eastern. 2 see *e-number.'),
(13707, 'e', '\r\nE-', ' prefix see *ex-1 before some consonants.'),
(13708, 'e', '\r\nEach', ' â€”adj. Every one of two or more persons or things, regarded separately (five in each class). â€”pron. Each person or thing (each of us). [old english]'),
(13709, 'e', '\r\nEach', 'other  pron. One another.'),
(13710, 'e', '\r\nEach', 'way  adj. (of a bet) backing a horse etc. To win or to come second or third.'),
(13711, 'e', '\r\nEager', ' adj. Keen, enthusiastic (eager to learn; eager for news).  eagerly adv. Eagerness n. [latin acer keen]'),
(13712, 'e', '\r\nEager', 'beaver  n. Colloq. Very diligent person.'),
(13713, 'e', '\r\nEagle', ' n. 1 a large bird of prey with keen vision and powerful flight. B this as a symbol, esp. Of the us. 2 score of two strokes under par at any hole in golf. [latin aquila]'),
(13714, 'e', '\r\nEagle', 'eye  n. Keen sight, watchfulness.  eagle-eyed adj.'),
(13715, 'e', '\r\nEaglet', ' n. Young eagle.'),
(13716, 'e', '\r\nE.', '& o. E.  Abbr. Errors and omissions excepted.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(13717, 'e', '\r\nEar1', ' n. 1 organ of hearing, esp. Its external part. 2 faculty for discriminating sounds (an ear for music).  3 attention, esp. Sympathetic (give ear to; have a person''s ear).  all ears listening attentively. Have (or keep) an ear to the ground be alert to rumours or trends. Up to one''s ears (often foll. By in) colloq. Deeply involved or occupied. [old english]'),
(13718, 'e', '\r\nEar2', ' n. Seed-bearing head of a cereal plant. [old english]'),
(13719, 'e', '\r\nEarache', ' n. Pain in the inner ear.'),
(13720, 'e', '\r\nEardrum', ' n. Membrane of the middle ear.'),
(13721, 'e', '\r\nEarful', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. 1 prolonged amount of talking. 2 strong reprimand.'),
(13722, 'e', '\r\nEarl', ' n. British nobleman ranking between marquis and viscount.  earldom n. [old english]'),
(13723, 'e', '\r\nEarl', 'marshal  n. President of the college of heralds, with ceremonial duties.'),
(13724, 'e', '\r\nEarly', ' â€”adj. & adv. (-ier, -iest) 1 before the due, usual, or expected time. 2 a not far on in the day or night, or in time (early evening; at the earliest opportunity). B prompt (early payment appreciated). 3 not far on in a period, development, or process of evolution; being the first stage (early english architecture; early spring). 4 forward in flowering, ripening, etc. (early peaches). â€”n. (pl. -ies) (usu. In pl.) Early fruit or vegetable.  earliness n. [old english: related to *ere]'),
(13725, 'e', '\r\nEarly', 'bird  n. Colloq. Person who arrives, gets up, etc. Early.'),
(13726, 'e', '\r\nEarly', 'days  n.pl. Too soon to expect results etc.'),
(13727, 'e', '\r\nEarly', 'on  adv. At an early stage.'),
(13728, 'e', '\r\nEarmark', ' â€”v. Set aside for a special purpose. â€”n. Identifying mark.'),
(13729, 'e', '\r\nEarn', ' v. 1 bring in as income or interest. 2 be entitled to or obtain as the reward for work or merit.  earner n. [old english]'),
(13730, 'e', '\r\nEarnest', ' adj. Intensely serious.  in earnest serious, seriously, with determination.  earnestly adv. Earnestness n. [old english]'),
(13731, 'e', '\r\nEarnings', ' n.pl. Money earned.'),
(13732, 'e', '\r\nEarphone', ' n. Device applied to the ear to receive a radio etc. Communication.'),
(13733, 'e', '\r\nEarpiece', ' n. Part of a telephone etc. Applied to the ear.'),
(13734, 'e', '\r\nEar-piercing', ' â€”adj. Shrill. â€”n. Piercing of the ears for wearing earrings.'),
(13735, 'e', '\r\nEarplug', ' n. Piece of wax etc. Placed in the ear to protect against water, noise, etc.'),
(13736, 'e', '\r\nEarring', ' n. Jewellery worn on the ear.'),
(13737, 'e', '\r\nEarshot', ' n. Hearing-range (within earshot).'),
(13738, 'e', '\r\nEar-splitting', ' adj. Excessively loud.'),
(13739, 'e', '\r\nEarth', ' â€”n. 1 a (also earth) the planet on which we live. B land and sea, as distinct from sky. 2 a the ground (fell to earth). B soil, mould. 3 relig. This world, as distinct from heaven or hell. 4 connection to the earth as the completion of an electrical circuit. 5 hole of a fox etc. 6 (prec. By the) colloq. Huge sum; everything (cost the earth; want the earth). â€”v. 1 cover (plant-roots) with earth. 2 connect (an electrical circuit) to the earth.  come back (or down) to earth return to realities. Gone to earth in hiding. On earth colloq. Existing anywhere; emphatically (the happiest man on earth; looked like nothing on earth; what on earth have you done?). Run to earth find after a long search.  earthward adj. & adv. Earthwards adv. [old english]'),
(13740, 'e', '\r\nEarthbound', ' adj. 1 attached to the earth or earthly things. 2 moving towards the earth.'),
(13741, 'e', '\r\nEarthen', ' adj. Made of earth or baked clay.'),
(13742, 'e', '\r\nEarthenware', ' n. Pottery made of fired clay.'),
(13743, 'e', '\r\nEarthling', ' n. Inhabitant of the earth, esp. In science fiction.'),
(13744, 'e', '\r\nEarthly', ' adj. 1 of the earth or human life on it; terrestrial. 2 (usu. With neg.) Colloq. Remotely possible (is no earthly use; there wasn''t an earthly reason).  not an earthly colloq. No chance or idea whatever.'),
(13745, 'e', '\r\nEarth', 'mother  n. Sensual and maternal woman.'),
(13746, 'e', '\r\nEarthquake', ' n. Convulsion of the earth''s surface as a result of faults in strata or volcanic action.'),
(13747, 'e', '\r\nEarth', 'sciences  n.pl. Those concerned with the earth or part of it.'),
(13748, 'e', '\r\nEarth-shattering', ' adj. Colloq. Traumatic, devastating.  earth-shatteringly adv.'),
(13749, 'e', '\r\nEarthwork', ' n. Artificial bank of earth in fortification or road-building etc.'),
(13750, 'e', '\r\nEarthworm', ' n. Common worm living in the ground.'),
(13751, 'e', '\r\nEarthy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like earth or soil. 2 coarse, crude (earthy humour).  earthiness n.'),
(13752, 'e', '\r\nEar-trumpet', ' n. Trumpet-shaped device formerly used as a hearing-aid.'),
(13753, 'e', '\r\nEarwig', ' n. Small insect with pincers at its rear end. [from *ear1, because they were once thought to enter the head through the ear]'),
(13754, 'e', '\r\nEase', ' â€”n. 1 facility, effortlessness. 2 a freedom from pain or trouble. B freedom from constraint. â€”v. (-sing) 1 relieve from pain or anxiety. 2 (often foll. By off, up) a become less burdensome or severe. B begin to take it easy. C slow down; moderate one''s behaviour etc. 3 a relax; slacken; make a less tight fit. B move or be moved carefully into place (eased it into position).  at ease 1 free from anxiety or constraint. 2 mil. In a relaxed attitude, with the feet apart. [latin: related to *adjacent]'),
(13755, 'e', '\r\nEasel', ' n. Stand for an artist''s work, a blackboard, etc. [dutch ezel ass]'),
(13756, 'e', '\r\nEasement', ' n. Legal right of way or similar right over another''s land. [french: related to *ease]'),
(13757, 'e', '\r\nEasily', ' adv. 1 without difficulty. 2 by far (easily the best). 3 very probably (it could easily snow).'),
(13758, 'e', '\r\nEast', ' â€”n. 1 a point of the horizon where the sun rises at the equinoxes. B compass point corresponding to this. C direction in which this lies. 2 (usu. The east) a countries to the east of europe. B states of eastern europe. 3 eastern part of a country, town, etc. â€”adj. 1 towards, at, near, or facing the east. 2 from the east (east wind). â€”adv. 1 towards, at, or near the east. 2 (foll. By of) further east than.  to the east (often foll. By of) in an easterly direction. [old english]'),
(13759, 'e', '\r\nEastbound', ' adj. Travelling or leading eastwards.'),
(13760, 'e', '\r\nEast', 'end  n. Part of london east of the city.  east ender n.'),
(13761, 'e', '\r\nEaster', ' n. Festival (held on a variable sunday in march or april) commemorating christ''s resurrection. [old english]'),
(13762, 'e', '\r\nEaster', 'egg  n. Artificial usu. Chocolate egg given at easter.'),
(13763, 'e', '\r\nEasterly', ' â€”adj. & adv. 1 in an eastern position or direction. 2 (of a wind) from the east. â€”n. (pl. -ies) such a wind.'),
(13764, 'e', '\r\nEastern', ' adj. Of or in the east.  easternmost adj.'),
(13765, 'e', '\r\nEastern', 'church  n. Orthodox church.'),
(13766, 'e', '\r\nEasterner', ' n. Native or inhabitant of the east.'),
(13767, 'e', '\r\nEast-north-east', ' n. Point or direction midway between east and north-east.'),
(13768, 'e', '\r\nEast-south-east', ' n. Point or direction midway between east and south-east.'),
(13769, 'e', '\r\nEastward', ' â€”adj. & adv. (also eastwards) towards the east. â€”n. Eastward direction or region.'),
(13770, 'e', '\r\nEasy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 not difficult; not requiring great effort. 2 free from pain, trouble, or anxiety. 3 free from constraint; relaxed and pleasant. 4 compliant. â€”adv. With ease; in an effortless or relaxed manner. â€”int. Go or move carefully.  easy on the eye (or ear etc.) Colloq. Pleasant to look at (or listen to etc.). Go easy (foll. By with, on) be sparing or cautious. I''m easy colloq. I have no preference. Take it easy 1 proceed gently. 2 relax; work less.  easiness n. [french: related to *ease]'),
(13771, 'e', '\r\nEasy', 'chair  n. Large comfortable armchair.'),
(13772, 'e', '\r\nEasygoing', ' adj. Placid and tolerant.'),
(13773, 'e', '\r\nEasy', 'street  n. Colloq. Affluence.'),
(13774, 'e', '\r\nEat', ' â€”v. (past ate; past part. Eaten) 1 a take into the mouth, chew, and swallow (food). B consume food; take a meal. C devour (eaten by a lion). 2 (foll. By away, at, into) a destroy gradually, esp. By corrosion, disease, etc. B begin to consume or diminish (resources etc.). 3 colloq. Trouble, vex (what''s eating you?). â€”n. (in pl.) Colloq. Food.  eat one''s heart out suffer from excessive longing or envy. Eat out have a meal away from home, esp. In a restaurant. Eat up 1 eat completely.'),
(13775, 'e', '\r\nEatable', ' â€”adj. Fit to be eaten. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Food.'),
(13776, 'e', '\r\nEater', ' n. 1 person who eats (a big eater). 2 eating apple etc.'),
(13777, 'e', '\r\nEating', 'apple  etc. N. Apple etc. Suitable for eating raw.'),
(13778, 'e', '\r\nEau-de-cologne', ' n. Toilet water orig. From cologne. [french, = water of cologne]'),
(13779, 'e', '\r\nEaves', ' n.pl. Underside of a projecting roof. [old english]'),
(13780, 'e', '\r\nEavesdrop', ' v. (-pp-) listen to a private conversation.  eavesdropper n.'),
(13781, 'e', '\r\nEbb', ' â€”n. Movement of the tide out to sea. â€”v. (often foll. By away) 1 flow out to sea; recede. 2 decline (life was ebbing away). [old english]'),
(13782, 'e', '\r\nEbonite', ' n. Vulcanite. [from *ebony]'),
(13783, 'e', '\r\nEbony', ' â€”n. Heavy hard dark wood of a tropical tree. â€”adj. 1 made of ebony. 2 black like ebony. [greek ebenos ebony tree]'),
(13784, 'e', '\r\nEbullient', ' adj. Exuberant.  ebullience n. Ebulliency n. Ebulliently adv. [latin: related to *boil1]'),
(13785, 'e', '\r\nEc', ' abbr. 1 east central. 2 european community.'),
(13786, 'e', '\r\nEccentric', ' â€”adj. 1 odd or capricious in behaviour or appearance. 2 (also excentric) a not placed, not having its axis placed, centrally. B (often foll. By to) (of a circle) not concentric (to another). C (of an orbit) not circular. â€”n. 1 eccentric person. 2 disc at the end of a shaft for changing rotatory into backward-and-forward motion.  eccentrically adv. Eccentricity n. [greek: related to *centre]'),
(13787, 'e', '\r\nEccles', 'cake  n. Round cake of pastry filled with currants etc. [eccles in n. England]'),
(13788, 'e', '\r\nEcclesiastic', ' â€”n. Clergyman. â€”adj. = *ecclesiastical. [greek ekklesia church]'),
(13789, 'e', '\r\nEcclesiastical', ' adj. Of the church or clergy.'),
(13790, 'e', '\r\nEcg', ' abbr. Electrocardiogram.'),
(13791, 'e', '\r\nEchelon', ' n. 1 level in an organization, in society, etc.; those occupying it (often in pl.: upper echelons). 2 wedge-shaped formation of troops, aircraft, etc. [french, = ladder, from latin scala]'),
(13792, 'e', '\r\nEchidna', ' n. Australian egg-laying spiny mammal. [greek, = viper]'),
(13793, 'e', '\r\nEchinoderm', ' n. (usu. Spiny) sea animal of the group including the starfish and sea urchin. [greek ekhinos sea-urchin, derma skin]'),
(13794, 'e', '\r\nEcho', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) 1 a repetition of a sound by the reflection of sound waves. B sound so produced. 2 reflected radio or radar beam. 3 close imitation or imitator. 4 circumstance or event reminiscent of an earlier one. â€”v. (-es, -ed) 1 a (of a place) resound with an echo. B (of a sound) be repeated; resound. 2 repeat (a sound) thus. 3 a repeat (another''s words). B imitate the opinions etc. Of. [latin from greek]'),
(13795, 'e', '\r\nEcho', 'chamber  n. Enclosure with sound-reflecting walls.'),
(13796, 'e', '\r\nEchoic', ' adj. (of a word) onomatopoeic.'),
(13797, 'e', '\r\nEcholocation', ' n. Location of objects by reflected sound.'),
