(13823, 'e', '\r\nEctoplasm', ' n. Supposed viscous substance exuding from the body of a spiritualistic medium during a trance. [from *ecto-, *plasma]'),
(13824, 'e', '\r\nEcu', ' n. (also ecu) (pl. -s) european currency unit. [abbreviation]'),
(13825, 'e', '\r\nEcumenical', ' adj. 1 of or representing the whole christian world. 2 seeking worldwide christian unity.  ecumenically adv. Ecumenism n. [greek oikoumenikos of the inhabited earth]'),
(13826, 'e', '\r\nEczema', ' n. Inflammation of the skin, with itching and discharge. [latin from greek]'),
(13827, 'e', '\r\nEd.', ' Abbr. 1 edited by. 2 edition. 3 editor. 4 educated.'),
(13828, 'e', '\r\n-ed1', ' suffix forming adjectives: 1 from nouns, meaning â€˜having, wearing, etc.â€™ (talented; trousered). 2 from phrases of adjective and noun (good-humoured). [old english]'),
(13829, 'e', '\r\n-ed2', ' suffix forming: 1 past tense and past participle of weak verbs (needed). 2 participial adjectives (escaped prisoner). [old english]'),
(13830, 'e', '\r\nEdam', ' n. Round dutch cheese with a red rind. [edam in holland]'),
(13831, 'e', '\r\nEddy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 circular movement of water causing a small whirlpool. 2 movement of wind, smoke, etc. Resembling this. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) whirl round in eddies. [old english ed- again, back]'),
(13832, 'e', '\r\nEdelweiss', ' n. Alpine plant with white flowers. [german, = noble-white]'),
(13833, 'e', '\r\nEdema', ' n. (brit. Oedema) accumulation of excess fluid in body tissues, causing swelling. [greek oideo swell]'),
(13834, 'e', '\r\nEden', ' n. Place or state of great happiness, with reference to the abode of adam and eve at the creation. [hebrew, originally = delight]'),
(13835, 'e', '\r\nEdentate', ' â€”adj. Having no or few teeth. â€”n. Such a mammal. [latin dens dent- tooth]'),
(13836, 'e', '\r\nEdge', ' â€”n. 1 boundary-line or margin of an area or surface. 2 narrow surface of a thin object. 3 meeting-line of surfaces. 4 a sharpened side of a blade. B sharpness. 5 brink of a precipice. 6 edge-like thing, esp. The crest of a ridge. 7 effectiveness, incisiveness; excitement. â€”v. (-ging) 1 advance, esp. Gradually or furtively. 2 a provide with an edge or border. B form a border to. 3 sharpen (a tool etc.).  have the edge on (or over) have a slight advantage over. On edge tense and irritable. Set a person''s teeth on edge (of taste or sound) cause an unpleasant nervous sensation. Take the edge off make less intense. [old english]'),
(13837, 'e', '\r\nEdgeways', ' adv. (also edgewise) with edge uppermost or foremost.  get a word in edgeways contribute to a conversation when the dominant speaker pauses.'),
(13838, 'e', '\r\nEdging', ' n. Thing forming an edge or border.'),
(13839, 'e', '\r\nEdgy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) irritable; anxious.  edgily adv. Edginess n.'),
(13840, 'e', '\r\nEdible', ' adj. Fit to be eaten.  edibility n. [latin edo eat]'),
(13841, 'e', '\r\nEdict', ' n. Order proclaimed by authority. [latin edico proclaim]'),
(13842, 'e', '\r\nEdifice', ' n. Building, esp. An imposing one. [latin aedis dwelling]'),
(13843, 'e', '\r\nEdify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) improve morally or intellectually.  edification n. [latin aedifico build]'),
(13844, 'e', '\r\nEdit', ' v. (-t-) 1 assemble, prepare, or modify (written material for publication). 2 be editor of (a newspaper etc.). 3 take extracts from and collate (a film etc.) To form a unified sequence. 4 a prepare (data) for processing by a computer. B alter (a text entered in a word processor etc.). 5 a reword in order to correct, or to alter the emphasis. B (foll. By out) remove (a part) from a text etc. [latin edo edit- give out]'),
(13845, 'e', '\r\nEdition', ' n. 1 edited or published form of a book etc. 2 copies of a book, newspaper, etc. Issued at one time. 3 instance of a regular broadcast. 4 person or thing similar to another (a miniature edition of her mother).'),
(13846, 'e', '\r\nEditor', ' n. 1 person who edits. 2 person who directs the preparation of a newspaper or broadcast news programme or a particular section of one (sports editor). 3 person who selects or commissions material for publication. 4 computer program for entering and modifying textual data.  editorship n.'),
(13847, 'e', '\r\nEditorial', ' â€”adj. 1 of editing or editors. 2 written or approved by an editor. â€”n. Article giving a newspaper''s views on a current topic.  editorially adv.'),
(13848, 'e', '\r\nEdp', ' abbr. Electronic data processing.'),
(13849, 'e', '\r\nEducate', ' v. (-ting) 1 give intellectual, moral, and social instruction to. 2 provide education for.  educable adj. Educability n. Educative adj. Educator n. [latin educo -are rear]'),
(13850, 'e', '\r\nEducated', ' adj. 1 having had an (esp. Good) education. 2 resulting from this (educated accent). 3 based on experience or study (educated guess).'),
(13851, 'e', '\r\nEducation', ' n. 1 systematic instruction. 2 particular kind of or stage in education (a classical education; further education). 3 development of character or mental powers.  educational adj. Educationally adv.'),
(13852, 'e', '\r\nEducationist', ' n. (also educationalist) expert in educational methods.'),
(13853, 'e', '\r\nEduce', ' v. (-cing) literary bring out or develop from latency.  eduction n. [latin educo -ere draw out]'),
(13854, 'e', '\r\nEdwardian', ' â€”adj. Of or characteristic of the reign of edward vii (1901â€“10). â€”n. Person of this period.'),
(13855, 'e', '\r\n-ee', ' suffix forming nouns denoting: 1 person affected by the verbal action (employee; payee). 2 person concerned with or described as (absentee; refugee). 3 object of smaller size (bootee). [french -Ã© in past part.]'),
(13856, 'e', '\r\nEec', ' abbr. European economic community.'),
(13857, 'e', '\r\nUsage', ' ec is the more correct term.'),
(13858, 'e', '\r\nEeg', ' abbr. Electroencephalogram.'),
(13859, 'e', '\r\nEel', ' n. Snakelike fish. [old english]'),
(13860, 'e', '\r\n-eer', ' suffix forming: 1 nouns meaning â€˜person concerned withâ€™ (auctioneer). 2 verbs meaning â€˜be concerned withâ€™ (electioneer). [french -ier from latin -arius]'),
(13861, 'e', '\r\nEerie', ' adj. (eerier, eeriest) gloomy and strange; weird (eerie silence).  eerily adv. Eeriness n. [old english]'),
(13862, 'e', '\r\nEf-', ' see *ex-1.'),
(13863, 'e', '\r\nEfface', ' v. (-cing) 1 rub or wipe out (a mark, recollection, etc.). 2 surpass, eclipse. 3 refl. (usu. As self-effacing adj.) Treat oneself as unimportant.  effacement n. [french: related to *face]'),
(13864, 'e', '\r\nEffect', ' â€”n. 1 result or consequence of an action etc. 2 efficacy (had little effect). 3 impression produced on a spectator, hearer, etc. (lights gave a pretty effect; said it just for effect). 4 (in pl.) Property. 5 (in pl.) Lighting, sound, etc., giving realism to a play, film, etc. 6 physical phenomenon (doppler effect; greenhouse effect). â€”v. Bring about (a change, cure, etc.).  bring (or carry) into effect accomplish. Give effect to make operative. In effect for practical purposes. Take effect become operative. To the effect that the gist being that. To that effect having that result or implication. With effect from coming into operation at (a stated time). [latin: related to *fact]'),
(13865, 'e', '\r\nUsage', ' effect should not be confused with affect which, as a verb, has more meanings and is more common, but which does not exist as a noun.'),
(13866, 'e', '\r\nEffective', ' adj. 1 producing the intended result. 2 impressive, striking. 3 actual, existing. 4 operative.  effectively adv. Effectiveness n.'),
(13867, 'e', '\r\nEffectual', ' adj. 1 producing the required effect. 2 valid.  effectually adv.'),
(13868, 'e', '\r\nEffeminate', ' adj. (of a man) womanish in appearance or manner.  effeminacy n. Effeminately adv. [latin femina woman]'),
(13869, 'e', '\r\nEffervesce', ' v. (-cing) 1 give off bubbles of gas. 2 be lively.  effervescence n. Effervescent adj. [latin: related to *fervent]'),
(13870, 'e', '\r\nEffete', ' adj. Feeble, lanquid; effeminate.  effeteness n. [latin]'),
(13871, 'e', '\r\nEfficacious', ' adj. Producing the desired effect.  efficacy n. [latin efficax: related to *efficient]'),
(13872, 'e', '\r\nEfficient', ' adj. 1 productive with minimum waste or effort. 2 (of a person) capable; acting effectively.  efficiency n. Efficiently adv. [latin facio make]'),
(13873, 'e', '\r\nEffigy', ' n. (pl. -ies) sculpture or model of a person.  burn in effigy burn a model of a person. [latin effigies from fingo fashion]'),
(13874, 'e', '\r\nEffloresce', ' v. (-cing) 1 burst into flower. 2 a (of a substance) turn to a fine powder on exposure to air. B (of salts) come to the surface and crystallize. C (of a surface) become covered with salt particles.  efflorescence n. Efflorescent adj. [latin flos flor- *flower]'),
(13875, 'e', '\r\nEffluence', ' n. 1 flowing out of light, electricity, etc. 2 that which flows out. [latin fluo flux- flow]'),
(13876, 'e', '\r\nEffluent', ' â€”adj. Flowing out. â€”n. 1 sewage or industrial waste discharged into a river etc. 2 stream or lake flowing from a larger body of water.'),
