(14516, 'e', '\r\nEscapade', ' n. Piece of reckless behaviour. [french from provenÃ§al or spanish: related to *escape]'),
(14517, 'e', '\r\nEscape', ' â€”v. (-ping) 1 (often foll. By from) get free of restriction or control. 2 (of gas etc.) Leak. 3 succeed in avoiding punishment etc. 4 get free of (a person, grasp, etc.). 5 avoid (a commitment, danger, etc.). 6 elude the notice or memory of (nothing escapes you; name escaped me). 7 (of words etc.) Issue unawares from (a person etc.). â€”n. 1 act or instance of escaping. 2 means of escaping (often attrib.: escape hatch). 3 leakage of gas etc. 4 temporary relief from unpleasant reality. [latin cappa cloak]'),
(14518, 'e', '\r\nEscape', 'clause  n. Law clause specifying conditions under which a contracting party is free from an obligation.'),
(14519, 'e', '\r\nEscapee', ' n. Person who has escaped.'),
