(14553, 'e', '\r\nExtemporize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) improvise.  extemporization n.'),
(14554, 'e', '\r\nExtend', ' v. 1 lengthen or make larger in space or time. 2 stretch or lay out at full length. 3 (foll. By to, over) reach or be or make continuous over a specified area. 4 (foll. By to) have a specified scope (permit does not extend to camping). 5 offer or accord (an invitation, hospitality, kindness, etc.). 6 (usu. Refl. Or in passive) tax the powers of (an athlete, horse, etc.).  extendible adj. (also extensible). [latin extendo -tens-: related to *tend1]'),
(14555, 'e', '\r\nExtended', 'family  n. Family including relatives living near.'),
(14556, 'e', '\r\nExtended-play', ' adj. (of a gramophone record) playing for somewhat longer than most singles.'),
(14557, 'e', '\r\nExtension', ' n. 1 extending or being extended. 2 part enlarging or added on to a main building etc. 3 additional part. 4 a subsidiary telephone on the same line as the main one. B its number. 5 additional period of time. 6 extramural instruction by a university or college.'),
(14558, 'e', '\r\nExtensive', ' adj. 1 covering a large area. 2 far-reaching.  extensively adv. Extensiveness n. [latin: related to *extend]'),
(14559, 'e', '\r\nExtent', ' n. 1 space over which a thing extends. 2 range, scope, degree. [anglo-french: related to *extend]'),
(14560, 'e', '\r\nExtenuate', ' v. (often as extenuating adj.) Make (guilt or an offence) seem less serious by reference to another factor.  extenuation n. [latin tenuis thin]'),
