(14586, 'e', '\r\nExtramural', ' adj. Additional to normal teaching or studies, esp. For non-resident students.'),
(14587, 'e', '\r\nExtraneous', ' adj. 1 of external origin. 2 (often foll. By to) a separate from the object to which it is attached etc. B irrelevant, unrelated. [latin extraneus]'),
(14588, 'e', '\r\nExtraordinary', ' adj. 1 unusual or remarkable. 2 unusually great. 3 (of a meeting, official, etc.) Additional; specially employed.  extraordinarily adv. [latin]'),
(14589, 'e', '\r\nExtrapolate', ' v. (-ting) (also absol.) Calculate approximately from known data etc. (others which lie outside the range of those known).  extrapolation n. [from *extra-, *interpolate]'),
(14590, 'e', '\r\nExtrasensory', ' adj. Derived by means other than the known senses, e.g. By telepathy.'),
(14591, 'e', '\r\nExtraterrestrial', ' â€”adj. Outside the earth or its atmosphere. â€”n. (in science fiction) being from outer space.'),
(14592, 'e', '\r\nExtravagant', ' adj. 1 spending money excessively. 2 excessive; absurd. 3 costing much.  extravagance n. Extravagantly adv. [latin vagor wander]'),
(14593, 'e', '\r\nExtravaganza', ' n. 1 spectacular theatrical or television production. 2 fanciful literary, musical, or dramatic composition. [italian]'),
(14594, 'e', '\r\nExtreme', ' â€”adj. 1 of a high, or the highest, degree (extreme danger). 2 severe (extreme measures). 3 outermost. 4 on the far left or right of a political party. 5 utmost; last. â€”n. 1 (often in pl.) Either of two things as remote or as different as possible. 2 thing at either end. 3 highest degree. 4 math. First or last term of a ratio or series.  go to extremes take an extreme course of action. In the extreme to an extreme degree.  extremely adv. [french from latin]'),
(14595, 'e', '\r\nExtreme', 'unction  n. Last rites in the roman catholic and orthodox churches.'),
(14596, 'e', '\r\nExtremist', ' n. (also attrib.) Person with extreme views.  extremism n.'),
(14597, 'e', '\r\nExtremity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 extreme point; very end. 2 (in pl.) The hands and feet. 3 condition of extreme adversity. [latin: related to *extreme]'),
