(14919, 'e', '\r\nExpenditure', ' n. 1 spending or using up. 2 thing (esp. Money) expended.'),
(14920, 'e', '\r\nExpense', ' n. 1 cost incurred; payment of money. 2 (usu. In pl.) A costs incurred in doing a job etc. B amount paid to reimburse this. 3 thing on which money is spent.  at the expense of so as to cause loss or harm to; costing. [latin expensa: related to *expend]'),
(14921, 'e', '\r\nExpense', 'account  n. List of an employee''s expenses payable by the employer.'),
(14922, 'e', '\r\nExpensive', ' adj. Costing or charging much.  expensively adv. Expensiveness n.'),
(14923, 'e', '\r\nExperience', ' â€”n. 1 observation of or practical acquaintance with facts or events. 2 knowledge or skill resulting from this. 3 event or activity participated in or observed (a rare experience). â€”v. (-cing) 1 have experience of; undergo. 2 feel. [latin experior -pert- try]'),
(14924, 'e', '\r\nExperienced', ' adj. 1 having had much experience. 2 skilled from experience (experienced driver).'),
(14925, 'e', '\r\nExperiential', ' adj. Involving or based on experience.  experientially adv.'),
(14926, 'e', '\r\nExperiment', ' â€”n. Procedure adopted in the hope of success, or for testing a hypothesis etc., or to demonstrate a known fact. â€”v. (often foll. By on, with) make an experiment.  experimentation n. Experimenter n. [latin: related to *experience]'),
(14927, 'e', '\r\nExperimental', ' adj. 1 based on or making use of experiment. 2 used in experiments.  experimentalism n. Experimentally adv.'),
(14928, 'e', '\r\nExpert', ' â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By at, in) having special knowledge of or skill in a subject. 2 (attrib.) Involving or resulting from this (expert advice). â€”n. (often foll. By at, in) person with special knowledge or skill.  expertly adv. [latin: related to *experience]'),
(14929, 'e', '\r\nExpertise', ' n. Expert skill, knowledge, or judgement. [french]'),
(14930, 'e', '\r\nExpiate', ' v. (-ting) pay the penalty for or make amends for (wrongdoing).  expiable adj. Expiation n. Expiatory adj. [latin expio: related to *pious]'),
(14931, 'e', '\r\nExpire', ' v. (-ring) 1 (of a period of time, validity, etc.) Come to an end. 2 cease to be valid. 3 die. 4 (also absol.) Breathe out (air etc.).  expiration n. Expiratory adj. (in sense 4). [latin spirare breathe]'),
(14932, 'e', '\r\nExpiry', ' n. End of validity or duration.'),
(14933, 'e', '\r\nExplain', ' v. 1 a make clear or intelligible (also absol.: let me explain). B make known in detail. 2 (foll. By that) say by way of explanation. 3 account for (one''s conduct etc.).  explain away minimize the significance of by explanation. Explain oneself 1 make one''s meaning clear. 2 give an account of one''s motives or conduct. [latin explano from planus flat]'),
