(15033, 'f', '\r\nFailure', ' n. 1 lack of success; failing. 2 unsuccessful person or thing. 3 non-performance. 4 breaking down or ceasing to function (heart failure). 5 running short of supply etc. [anglo-french: related to *fail]'),
(15034, 'f', '\r\nFain', ' archaic â€”predic. Adj. (foll. By to + infin.) Willing or obliged to. â€”adv. Gladly (esp. Would fain). [old english]'),
(15035, 'f', '\r\nFaint', ' â€”adj. 1 indistinct, pale, dim. 2 weak or giddy. 3 slight. 4 feeble; timid. 5 (also feint) (of paper) with inconspicuous ruled lines. â€”v. 1 lose consciousness. 2 become faint. â€”n. Act or state of fainting.  faintly adv. Faintness n. [french: related to *feign]'),
(15036, 'f', '\r\nFaint-hearted', ' adj. Cowardly, timid.'),
(15037, 'f', '\r\nFair1', ' â€”adj. 1 just, equitable; in accordance with the rules. 2 blond; light or pale. 3 a moderate in quality or amount. B satisfactory. 4 (of weather) fine; (of the wind) favourable. 5 clean, clear (fair copy). 6 archaic beautiful. â€”adv. 1 in a just manner. 2 exactly, completely.  in a fair way to likely to.  fairness n. [old english]'),
(15038, 'f', '\r\nFair2', ' n. 1 stalls, amusements, etc., for public entertainment. 2 periodic market, often with entertainments. 3 exhibition, esp. Commercial. [latin feriae holiday]'),
(15039, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'and square  adv. Exactly; straightforwardly.'),
(15040, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'dinkum  see *dinkum.'),
(15041, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'dos  n.pl. (esp. As int.) Colloq. Fair shares; fair treatment.'),
(15042, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'game  n. Legitimate target or object.'),
(15043, 'f', '\r\nFairground', ' n. Outdoor area where a fair is held.'),
(15044, 'f', '\r\nFairing', ' n. Streamlining structure added to a ship, aircraft, vehicle, etc.'),
(15045, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'isle  n. (also attrib.) Multicoloured knitwear design characteristic of fair isle. [fair isle in the shetlands]'),
(15046, 'f', '\r\nFairly', ' adv. 1 in a fair manner. 2 moderately (fairly good). 3 quite, rather (fairly narrow).'),
(15047, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'play  n. Just treatment or behaviour.'),
(15048, 'f', '\r\nFair', 'sex  n. (prec. By the) women.'),
(15049, 'f', '\r\nFairway', ' n. 1 navigable channel. 2 part of a golf-course between a tee and its green, kept free of rough grass.'),
(15050, 'f', '\r\nFair-weather', 'friend  n. Unreliable friend or ally.'),
(15051, 'f', '\r\nFairy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 (often attrib.) Small winged legendary being. 2 slang offens. Male homosexual. [french: related to *fay, *-ery]'),
(15052, 'f', '\r\nFairy', 'cake  n. Small iced sponge cake.'),
(15053, 'f', '\r\nFairy', 'godmother  n. Benefactress.'),
(15054, 'f', '\r\nFairyland', ' n. 1 home of fairies. 2 enchanted region.'),
(15055, 'f', '\r\nFairy', 'lights  n.pl. Small decorative coloured lights.'),
(15056, 'f', '\r\nFairy', 'ring  n. Ring of darker grass caused by fungi.'),
(15057, 'f', '\r\nFairy', 'story  n. (also fairy tale) 1 tale about fairies. 2 incredible story; lie.'),
(15058, 'f', '\r\nFait', 'accompli  n. Thing that has been done and is not capable of alteration. [french]'),
(15059, 'f', '\r\nFaith', ' n. 1 complete trust or confidence. 2 firm, esp. Religious, belief. 3 religion or creed (christian faith). 4 loyalty, trustworthiness. [latin fides]'),
(15060, 'f', '\r\nFaithful', ' adj. 1 showing faith. 2 (often foll. By to) loyal, trustworthy. 3 accurate (faithful account). 4 (the faithful) the believers in a religion.  faithfulness n.'),
(15061, 'f', '\r\nFaithfully', ' adv. In a faithful manner.  yours faithfully formula for ending a formal letter when it begins â€˜dear sirâ€™ or â€˜dear madamâ€™.'),
(15062, 'f', '\r\nFaithless', ' adj. 1 false, unreliable, disloyal. 2 without religious faith.'),
(15063, 'f', '\r\nFake', ' â€”n. False or counterfeit thing or person. â€”adj. Counterfeit; not genuine. â€”v. (-king) 1 make a fake or imitation of (faked my signature). 2 feign (a feeling, illness, etc.). [german fegen sweep]'),
(15064, 'f', '\r\nFakir', ' n. Muslim or (rarely) hindu religious beggar or ascetic. [arabic, = poor man]'),
(15065, 'f', '\r\nFalcon', ' n. Small hawk sometimes trained to hunt. [latin falco]'),
(15066, 'f', '\r\nFalconry', ' n. Breeding and training of hawks.'),
(15067, 'f', '\r\nFall', ' â€”v. (past fell; past part. Fallen) 1 go or come down freely; descend. 2 (often foll. By over) come suddenly to the ground from loss of balance etc. 3 a hang or slope down. B (foll. By into) (of a river etc.) Discharge into. 4 a sink lower; decline, esp. In power, status, etc. B subside. 5 occur (falls on a monday). 6 (of the face) show dismay or disappointment. 7 yield to temptation. 8 take or have a particular direction or place (his eye fell on me; accent falls on the first syllable). 9 a find a place; be naturally divisible. B (foll. By under, within) be classed among. 10 come by chance or duty (it fell to me to answer). 11 a pass into a specified condition (fell ill). B become (fall asleep). 12 be defeated or captured. 13 die. 14 (foll. By on, upon) a attack. B meet with. C embrace or embark on avidly. 15 (foll. By to + verbal noun) begin (fell to wondering). â€”n. 1 act of falling. 2 that which falls or has fallen, e.g. Snow. 3 recorded amount of rainfall etc. 4 overthrow (fall of rome). 5 a succumbing to temptation. B (the fall) adam''s sin and its results. 6 (also fall) us autumn. 7 (esp. In pl.) Waterfall etc. 8 wrestling-bout; throw in wrestling.  fall about colloq. Be helpless with laughter. Fall away 1 (of a surface) incline abruptly. 2 become few or thin; gradually vanish. 3 desert. Fall back retreat. Fall back on have recourse to in difficulty. Fall behind 1 be outstripped; lag. 2 be in arrears. Fall down (often foll. By on) colloq. Fail. Fall for colloq. Be captivated or deceived by. Fall foul of come into conflict with. Fall in 1 take one''s place in military formation. 2 collapse inwards. Fall in with 1 meet by chance. 2 agree with. 3 coincide with. Fall off 1 become detached. 2 decrease, deteriorate. Fall out 1 quarrel. 2 (of the hair, teeth, etc.) Become detached. 3 mil. Come out of formation. 4 result; occur. Fall over backwards see *backwards. Fall over oneself colloq. 1 be eager. 2 stumble through haste, confusion, etc. Fall short be deficient. Fall short of fail to reach or obtain. Fall through fail; miscarry. Fall to begin, e.g. Eating or working. [old english]'),
(15068, 'f', '\r\nFallacy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 mistaken belief. 2 faulty reasoning; misleading argument.  fallacious adj. [latin fallo deceive]'),
(15069, 'f', '\r\nFall', 'guy  n. Slang easy victim; scapegoat.'),
(15070, 'f', '\r\nFallible', ' adj. Capable of making mistakes.  fallibility n. Fallibly adv. [medieval latin: related to *fallacy]'),
(15071, 'f', '\r\nFalling', 'star  n. Meteor.'),
(15072, 'f', '\r\nFallopian', 'tube  n. Either of two tubes along which ova travel from the ovaries to the womb. [fallopius, name of an anatomist]'),
(15073, 'f', '\r\nFallout', ' n. Radioactive nuclear debris.');
