(15113, 'f', '\r\nFanfare', ' n. Short showy or ceremonious sounding of trumpets etc. [french]'),
(15114, 'f', '\r\nFang', ' n. 1 canine tooth, esp. Of a dog or wolf. 2 tooth of a venomous snake. 3 root of a tooth or its prong. [old english]'),
(15115, 'f', '\r\nFan-jet', ' n. = *turbofan.'),
(15116, 'f', '\r\nFanlight', ' n. Small, orig. Semicircular, window over a door or another window.'),
(15117, 'f', '\r\nFan', 'mail  n. Letters from fans.'),
(15118, 'f', '\r\nFanny', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 coarse slang the female genitals. 2 us slang the buttocks. [origin unknown]'),
(15119, 'f', '\r\nFantail', ' n. Pigeon with a broad tail.'),
(15120, 'f', '\r\nFantasia', ' n. Free or improvisatory musical or other composition, or one based on familiar tunes. [italian: related to *fantasy]'),
(15121, 'f', '\r\nFantasize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 day-dream. 2 imagine; create a fantasy about.'),
(15122, 'f', '\r\nFantastic', ' adj. 1 colloq. Excellent, extraordinary. 2 extravagantly fanciful. 3 grotesque, quaint.  fantastically adv. [greek: related to *fantasy]'),
(15123, 'f', '\r\nFantasy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 imagination, esp. When unrelated to reality (lives in the realm of fantasy). 2 mental image, day-dream. 3 fantastic invention or composition. [greek phantasia appearance]'),
(15124, 'f', '\r\nFar', ' (further, furthest or farther, farthest) â€”adv. 1 at, to, or by a great distance (far away; far off; far out). 2 a long way (off) in space or time (are you travelling far?). 3 to a great extent or degree; by much (far better; far too early). â€”adj. 1 remote; distant (far country). 2 more distant (far end of the hall). 3 extreme (far left).  as far as 1 right up to (a place). 2 to the extent that. By far by a great amount. A far cry a long way. Far from very different from being; almost the opposite of (far from being fat). Go far 1 achieve much. 2 contribute greatly. Go too far overstep the limit (of propriety etc.). So far 1 to such an extent; to this point. 2 until now. So (or in so) far as (or that) to the extent that. So far so good satisfactory up to now. [old english]'),
(15125, 'f', '\r\nFarad', ' n. Si unit of capacitance. [faraday, name of a physicist]'),
(15126, 'f', '\r\nFar', 'and away  adv. By a very large amount.'),
(15127, 'f', '\r\nFar', 'and wide  adv. Over a large area.'),
(15128, 'f', '\r\nFar-away', ' adj. 1 remote. 2 (of a look or voice) dreamy, distant.'),
(15129, 'f', '\r\nFarce', ' n. 1 a low comedy with a ludicrously improbable plot. B this branch of drama. 2 absurdly futile proceedings; pretence.  farcical adj. [latin farcio to stuff, used metaphorically of interludes etc.]'),
(15130, 'f', '\r\nFare', ' â€”n. 1 a price of a journey on public transport. B fare-paying passenger. 2 range of food. â€”v. (-ring) progress; get on (how did you fare?). [old english]'),
(15131, 'f', '\r\nFar', 'east  n. (prec. By the) china, japan, and other countries of e. Asia.'),
(15132, 'f', '\r\nFare-stage', ' n. 1 section of bus etc. Route for which a fixed fare is charged. 2 stop marking this.'),
(15133, 'f', '\r\nFarewell', ' â€”int. Goodbye. â€”n. Leave-taking.'),
