(15174, 'f', '\r\nFastness', ' n. Stronghold. [old english]'),
(15175, 'f', '\r\nFast', 'neutron  n. Neutron with high kinetic energy.'),
(15176, 'f', '\r\nFast', 'worker  n. Colloq. Person who rapidly makes esp. Sexual advances.'),
(15177, 'f', '\r\nFat', ' â€”n. 1 natural oily or greasy substance found esp. In animal bodies. 2 part of meat etc. Containing this. â€”adj. (fatter, fattest) 1 corpulent; plump. 2 containing much fat. 3 fertile. 4 a thick (fat book). B substantial (fat cheque). 5 colloq. Iron. Very little; not much (a fat chance; a fat lot). â€”v. (-tt-) make or become fat.  the fat is in the fire trouble is imminent. Kill the fatted calf celebrate, esp. At a prodigal''s return (luke 15). Live off (or on) the fat of the land live luxuriously.  fatless adj. Fatness n. Fattish adj. [old english]'),
(15178, 'f', '\r\nFatal', ' adj. 1 causing or ending in death (fatal accident). 2 (often foll. By to) ruinous (fatal mistake). 3 fateful.  fatally adv. [latin: related to *fate]'),
(15179, 'f', '\r\nFatalism', ' n. 1 belief in predetermination. 2 submissive acceptance.  fatalist n. Fatalistic adj. Fatalistically adv.'),
(15180, 'f', '\r\nFatality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 death by accident or in war etc. 2 fatal influence. 3 predestined liability to disaster.'),
(15181, 'f', '\r\nFate', ' â€”n. 1 supposed power predetermining events. 2 a the future so determined. B individual''s destiny or fortune. 3 death, destruction. â€”v. (-ting) 1 (usu. In passive) preordain (fated to win). 2 (as fated adj.) Doomed.  fate worse than death see *death. [italian and latin fatum]'),
(15182, 'f', '\r\nFateful', ' adj. 1 important, decisive. 2 controlled by fate.  fatefully adv.'),
(15183, 'f', '\r\nFat-head', ' n. Colloq. Stupid person.'),
(15184, 'f', '\r\nFat-headed', ' adj. Stupid.'),
(15185, 'f', '\r\nFather', ' â€”n. 1 male parent. 2 (usu. In pl.) Forefather. 3 originator, early leader. 4 (fathers or fathers of the church) early christian theologians. 5 (also father) (often as a title or form of address) priest. 6 (the father) (in christian belief) first person of the trinity. 7 (father) venerable person, esp. As a title in personifications (father time). 8 (usu. In pl.) Elders (city fathers). â€”v. 1 beget. 2 originate (a scheme etc.).  fatherhood n. Fatherless adj. [old english]'),
(15186, 'f', '\r\nFather-figure', ' n. Older man respected and trusted like a father.'),
(15187, 'f', '\r\nFather-in-law', ' n. (pl. Fathers-in-law) father of one''s husband or wife.'),
(15188, 'f', '\r\nFatherland', ' n. One''s native country.'),
(15189, 'f', '\r\nFatherly', ' adj. Like or of a father.'),
(15190, 'f', '\r\nFather''s', 'day  n. Day on which cards and presents are given to fathers.'),
(15191, 'f', '\r\nFathom', ' â€”n. (pl. Often fathom when prec. By a number) measure of six feet, esp. In depth soundings. â€”v. 1 comprehend. 2 measure the depth of (water).  fathomable adj. [old english]'),
(15192, 'f', '\r\nFathomless', ' adj. Too deep to fathom.'),
(15193, 'f', '\r\nFatigue', ' â€”n. 1 extreme tiredness. 2 weakness in metals etc. Caused by repeated stress. 3 a non-military army duty. B (in pl.) Clothing worn for this. â€”v. (-gues, -gued, -guing) cause fatigue in. [latin fatigo exhaust]'),
