(15227, 'f', '\r\nFe', ' symb. Iron. [latin ferrum]'),
(15228, 'f', '\r\nFealty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 hist. Fidelity to a feudal lord. 2 allegiance. [latin: related to *fidelity]'),
(15229, 'f', '\r\nFear', ' â€”n. 1 a panic or distress caused by a sense of impending danger, pain, etc. B cause of this. C state of alarm (in fear). 2 (often foll. By of) dread, awe (towards) (fear of heights). 3 danger (little fear of failure). â€”v. 1 feel fear about or towards. 2 (foll. By for) feel anxiety about (feared for my life). 3 (often foll. By that) foresee or expect with unease, fear, or regret (fear the worst; i fear that you are wrong). 4 (foll. By verbal noun) shrink from (feared meeting his ex-wife). 5 revere (esp. God).  for fear of (or that) to avoid the risk of (or that). No fear colloq. Certainly not! [old english]'),
(15230, 'f', '\r\nFearful', ' adj. 1 (usu. Foll. By of or that) afraid. 2 terrible, awful. 3 colloq. Extreme, esp. Unpleasant (fearful row).  fearfully adv. Fearfulness n.'),
(15231, 'f', '\r\nFearless', ' adj. (often foll. By of) not afraid, brave.  fearlessly adv. Fearlessness n.'),
(15232, 'f', '\r\nFearsome', ' adj. Frightening.  fearsomely adv.'),
(15233, 'f', '\r\nFeasible', ' adj. Practicable, possible.  feasibility n. Feasibly adv. [latin facio do]'),
(15234, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' feasible should not be used to mean â€˜possibleâ€™ or â€˜probableâ€™ in the sense â€˜likelyâ€™. â€˜possibleâ€™ or â€˜probableâ€™ should be used instead.'),
(15235, 'f', '\r\nFeast', ' â€”n. 1 large or sumptuous meal. 2 sensual or mental pleasure. 3 religious festival. 4 annual village festival. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By on) partake of a feast; eat and drink sumptuously. 2 regale.  feast one''s eyes on look with pleasure at. [latin festus joy]'),
(15236, 'f', '\r\nFeat', ' n. Remarkable act or achievement. [latin: related to *fact]'),
(15237, 'f', '\r\nFeather', ' â€”n. 1 one of the structures forming a bird''s plumage, with a horny stem and fine strands. 2 (collect.) A plumage. B game-birds. â€”v. 1 cover or line with feathers. 2 turn (an oar) edgeways through the air.  feather in one''s cap a personal achievement. Feather one''s nest enrich oneself. In fine (or high) feather colloq. In good spirits.  feathery adj. [old english]'),
(15238, 'f', '\r\nFeather', 'bed  n. Bed with a feather-stuffed mattress.'),
(15239, 'f', '\r\nFeather-bed', ' v. (-dd-) cushion, esp. Financially.'),
(15240, 'f', '\r\nFeather-brain', ' n. (also feather-head) silly or absent-minded person.  feather-brained adj. (also feather-headed).'),
(15241, 'f', '\r\nFeathering', ' n. 1 bird''s plumage. 2 feathers of an arrow. 3 feather-like structure or marking.'),
(15242, 'f', '\r\nFeatherweight', ' n. 1 a weight in certain sports between bantamweight and lightweight, in amateur boxing 54â€“7kg. B sportsman of this weight. 2 very light person or thing. 3 (usu. Attrib.) Unimportant thing.'),
(15243, 'f', '\r\nFeature', ' â€”n. 1 distinctive or characteristic part of a thing. 2 (usu. In pl.) Part of the face. 3 (esp. Specialized) article in a newspaper etc. 4 (in full feature film) main film in a cinema programme. â€”v. (-ring) 1 make a special display of; emphasize. 2 have as or be a central participant or topic in a film, broadcast, etc.  featureless adj. [latin factura formation: related to *fact]'),
(15244, 'f', '\r\nFeb.', ' Abbr. February.'),
(15245, 'f', '\r\nFebrifuge', ' n. Medicine or treatment for fever. [latin febris fever]'),
(15246, 'f', '\r\nFebrile', ' adj. Of fever; feverish. [latin febris fever]'),
(15247, 'f', '\r\nFebruary', ' n. (pl. -ies) second month of the year. [latin februa purification feast]'),
(15248, 'f', '\r\nFecal', ' us var. Of faecal (*feces).'),
(15249, 'f', '\r\nFeces', ' n.pl. (brit. Faeces) waste matter discharged from the bowels.  faecal adj. [latin]'),
(15250, 'f', '\r\nFeckless', ' adj. 1 feeble, ineffective. 2 unthinking, irresponsible. [scots feck from effeck var. Of *effect]'),
(15251, 'f', '\r\nFecund', ' adj. 1 prolific, fertile. 2 fertilizing.  fecundity n. [latin]'),
(15252, 'f', '\r\nFecundate', ' v. (-ting) 1 make fruitful. 2 fertilize.  fecundation n.'),
(15253, 'f', '\r\nFed', ' past and past part. Of *feed.  fed up (often foll. By with) discontented or bored.'),
(15254, 'f', '\r\nFederal', ' adj. 1 of a system of government in which self-governing states unite for certain functions etc. 2 of such a federation (federal laws). 3 of or favouring centralized government. 4 (federal) us of the northern states in the civil war. 5 comprising an association of largely independent units.  federalism n. Federalist n. Federalize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). Federalization n. Federally adv. [latin foedus covenant]'),
(15255, 'f', '\r\nFederal', 'reserve  n. (in the us) reserve cash available to banks.'),
(15256, 'f', '\r\nFederate', ' â€”v. (-ting) unite on a federal basis. â€”adj. Federally organized.  federative adj.'),
(15257, 'f', '\r\nFederation', ' n. 1 federal group. 2 act of federating. [latin: related to *federal]'),
(15258, 'f', '\r\nFee', ' n. 1 payment made for professional advice or services etc. 2 a charge for a privilege, examination, admission to a society, etc. (enrolment fee). B money paid for the transfer to another employer of a footballer etc. 3 (in pl.) Regular payments (esp. To a school). 4 law inherited estate, unlimited (fee simple) or limited (fee tail) as to category of heir. [medieval latin feudum]'),
(15259, 'f', '\r\nFeeble', ' adj. (feebler, feeblest) 1 weak, infirm. 2 lacking strength, energy, or effectiveness.  feebly adv. [latin flebilis lamentable]'),
(15260, 'f', '\r\nFeeble-minded', ' adj. Mentally deficient.'),
(15261, 'f', '\r\nFeeble-minded', ' adj. Mentally deficient.'),
(15262, 'f', '\r\nFeed', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Fed) 1 a supply with food. B put food into the mouth of. 2 give as food, esp. To animals. 3 (usu. Foll. By on) (esp. Of animals, or colloq. Of people) eat. 4 (often foll. By on) nourish or be nourished by; benefit from. 5 a keep (a fire, machine, etc.) Supplied with fuel etc. B (foll. By into) supply (material) to a machine etc. C (often foll. By into) (of a river etc.) Flow into a lake etc. D keep (a meter) supplied with coins to ensure continuity. 6 slang supply (an actor etc.) With cues. 7 sport send passes to (a player). 8 gratify (vanity etc.). 9 provide (advice, information, etc.) To. â€”n. 1 food, esp. For animals or infants. 2 feeding; giving of food. 3 colloq. Meal. 4 a raw material for a machine etc. B provision of or device for this.  feed back produce feedback. Feed up fatten. [old english]'),
(15263, 'f', '\r\nFeedback', ' n. 1 public response to an event, experiment, etc. 2 electronics a return of a fraction of an output signal to the input. B signal so returned.'),
(15264, 'f', '\r\nFeeder', ' n. 1 person or thing that feeds, esp. In specified manner. 2 baby''s feeding-bottle. 3 bib. 4 tributary stream. 5 branch road, railway line, etc. Linking with a main system. 6 main carrying electricity to a distribution point. 7 feeding apparatus in a machine.'),
(15265, 'f', '\r\nFeel', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Felt) 1 a examine or search by touch. B (absol.) Have the sensation of touch (unable to feel). 2 perceive or ascertain by touch (feel the warmth). 3 experience, exhibit, or be affected by (an emotion, conviction, etc.) (felt strongly about it; felt the rebuke). 4 (foll. By that) have an impression (i feel that i am right). 5 consider, think (i feel it useful). 6 seem (air feels chilly). 7 be consciously; consider oneself (i feel happy). 8 (foll. By for, with) have sympathy or pity. 9 (often foll. By up) slang fondle sexually. â€”n. 1 feeling; testing by touch. 2 sensation characterizing a material, situation, etc. 3 sense of touch.  feel like have a wish or inclination for. Feel up to be ready to face or deal with. Feel one''s way proceed cautiously. Get the feel of become accustomed to using. [old english]'),
(15266, 'f', '\r\nFeeler', ' n. 1 organ in certain animals for touching or searching for food. 2 tentative proposal (put out feelers).'),
(15267, 'f', '\r\nFeeling', ' â€”n. 1 a capacity to feel; sense of touch (lost all feeling). B physical sensation. 2 a (often foll. By of) emotional reaction (feeling of despair). B (in pl.) Emotional susceptibilities (hurt my feelings). 3 particular sensitivity (feeling for literature). 4 a opinion or notion (had a feeling she would). B general sentiment. 5 sympathy or compassion. 6 emotional sensibility or intensity (played with feeling). â€”adj. Sensitive, sympathetic; heartfelt.  feelingly adv.'),
(15268, 'f', '\r\nFeet', ' pl. Of *foot.'),
(15269, 'f', '\r\nFeign', ' v. Simulate; pretend (feign madness). [latin fingo fict- mould, contrive]'),
(15270, 'f', '\r\nFeint', ' â€”n. 1 sham attack or diversionary blow. 2 pretence. â€”v. Make a feint. â€”adj. = *faint adj. 5. [french: related to *feign]'),
(15271, 'f', '\r\nFeldspar', ' n. (also felspar) common aluminium silicate of potassium, sodium, or calcium.  feldspathic adj. [german feld field, spat(h) *spar3]'),
(15272, 'f', '\r\nFelicitate', ' v. (-ting) formal congratulate.  felicitation n. (usu. In pl.). [latin felix happy]'),
(15273, 'f', '\r\nFelicitous', ' adj. Formal apt; pleasantly ingenious; well-chosen.'),
(15274, 'f', '\r\nFelicity', ' n. (pl. -ies) formal 1 intense happiness. 2 a capacity for apt expression. B well-chosen phrase. [latin felix happy]'),
(15275, 'f', '\r\nFeline', ' â€”adj. 1 of the cat family. 2 catlike. â€”n. Animal of the cat family.  felinity n. [latin feles cat]'),
(15276, 'f', '\r\nFell1', ' past of *fall v.'),
(15277, 'f', '\r\nFell2', ' v. 1 cut down (esp. A tree). 2 strike or knock down. 3 stitch down (the edge of a seam). [old english]'),
(15278, 'f', '\r\nFell3', ' n. N.engl. 1 hill. 2 stretch of hills or moorland. [old norse]'),
(15279, 'f', '\r\nFell4', ' adj. Poet. Or rhet. Ruthless, destructive.  at (or in) one fell swoop in a single (orig. Deadly) action. [french: related to *felon]'),
(15280, 'f', '\r\nFell5', ' n. Animal''s hide or skin with its hair. [old english]'),
(15281, 'f', '\r\nFellatio', ' n. Oral stimulation of the penis. [latin fello suck]'),
(15282, 'f', '\r\nFeller', ' n. = *fellow 1.'),
