(15284, 'f', '\r\nFellow', ' n. 1 colloq. Man or boy (poor fellow!). 2 (usu. In pl.) Person in a group etc.; comrade (separated from their fellows). 3 counterpart; one of a pair. 4 equal; peer. 5 a incorporated senior member of a college. B elected graduate paid to do research. 6 member of a learned society. 7 (attrib.) Of the same group etc. (fellow-countryman). [old english from old norse]'),
(15285, 'f', '\r\nFellow-feeling', ' n. Sympathy.'),
(15286, 'f', '\r\nFellowship', ' n. 1 friendly association with others, companionship. 2 body of associates. 3 status or income of a fellow of a college or society.'),
(15287, 'f', '\r\nFellow-traveller', ' n. 1 person who travels with another. 2 sympathizer with the communist party.'),
(15288, 'f', '\r\nFelly', ' var. Of *felloe.'),
(15289, 'f', '\r\nFelon', ' n. Person who has committed a felony. [medieval latin fello]'),
