(15435, 'f', '\r\nFig-leaf', ' n. 1 leaf of a fig-tree. 2 concealing device, esp. For the genitals (gen. 3:7).'),
(15436, 'f', '\r\nFigment', ' n. Invented or imaginary thing. [latin: related to *feign]'),
(15437, 'f', '\r\nFiguration', ' n. 1 a act or mode of formation; form. B shape or outline. 2 ornamentation. [latin: related to *figure]'),
(15438, 'f', '\r\nFigurative', ' adj. 1 metaphorical, not literal. 2 characterized by figures of speech. 3 of pictorial or sculptural representation.  figuratively adv. [latin: related to *figure]'),
(15439, 'f', '\r\nFigure', ' â€”n. 1 external form or bodily shape. 2 a silhouette, human form (figure on the lawn). B person of a specified kind or appearance (public figure; cut a poor figure). 3 a human form in drawing, sculpture, etc. B image or likeness. 4 two- or three-dimensional space enclosed by lines or surface(s), e.g. A triangle or sphere. 5 a numerical symbol or number, esp. 0â€“9. B amount; estimated value (cannot put a figure on it). C (in pl.) Arithmetical calculations. 6 diagram or illustration. 7 decorative pattern. 8 movement or sequence in a set dance etc. 9 mus. Succession of notes from which longer passages are developed. 10 (in full figure of speech) metaphor, hyperbole, etc. â€”v. (-ring) 1 appear or be mentioned, esp. Prominently. 2 represent pictorially. 3 imagine; picture mentally. 4 embellish with a pattern etc. (figured satin). 5 calculate; do arithmetic. 6 symbolize. 7 esp. Us a understand, consider. B colloq. Make sense; be likely (that figures).  figure on us count on, expect. Figure out work out by arithmetic or logic. [latin figura: related to *feign]'),
(15440, 'f', '\r\nFigured', 'bass  n. Mus. = *continuo.'),
(15441, 'f', '\r\nFigurehead', ' n. 1 nominal leader. 2 wooden bust or figure at a ship''s prow.'),
(15442, 'f', '\r\nFigure-skating', ' n. Skating in prescribed patterns.  figure-skater n.'),
(15443, 'f', '\r\nFigurine', ' n. Statuette. [italian: related to *figure]'),
(15444, 'f', '\r\nFilament', ' n. 1 threadlike body or fibre. 2 conducting wire or thread in an electric bulb etc.  filamentous adj. [latin filum thread]'),
(15445, 'f', '\r\nFilbert', ' n. 1 the cultivated hazel with edible nuts. 2 this nut. [anglo-french, because ripe about st philibert''s day]'),
(15446, 'f', '\r\nFilch', ' v. Pilfer, steal. [origin unknown]'),
(15447, 'f', '\r\nFile1', ' â€”n. 1 folder, box, etc., for holding loose papers. 2 papers kept in this. 3 computing collection of (usu. Related) data stored under one name. 4 line of people or things one behind another. â€”v. (-ling) 1 place (papers) in a file or among (esp. Public) records. 2 submit (a petition for divorce, a patent application, etc.). 3 (of a reporter) send (copy) to a newspaper. 4 walk in a line. [latin filum thread]'),
(15448, 'f', '\r\nFile2', ' â€”n. Tool with a roughened surface for smoothing or shaping wood, fingernails, etc. â€”v. (-ling) smooth or shape with a file. [old english]'),
(15449, 'f', '\r\nFilial', ' adj. Of or due from a son or daughter.  filially adv. [latin filius, -a son, daughter]'),
(15450, 'f', '\r\nFilibuster', ' â€”n. 1 obstruction of progress in a legislative assembly, esp. By prolonged speaking. 2 esp. Us person who engages in this. â€”v. Act as a filibuster (against).  filibusterer n. [dutch: related to *freebooter]'),
(15451, 'f', '\r\nFiligree', ' n. 1 fine ornamental work in gold etc. Wire. 2 similar delicate work.  filigreed adj. [latin filum thread, granum seed]'),
(15452, 'f', '\r\nFiling', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Particle rubbed off by a file.'),
(15453, 'f', '\r\nFiling', 'cabinet  n. Cabinet with drawers for storing files.'),
(15454, 'f', '\r\nFilipino', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) native or national of the philippines. â€”adj. Of the philippines or filipinos. [spanish, = philippine]'),
(15455, 'f', '\r\nFill', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By with) make or become full. 2 occupy completely; spread over or through. 3 block up (a cavity in a tooth); drill and put a filling into (a decayed tooth). 4 appoint a person to hold or (of a person) hold (a post). 5 hold (an office). 6 carry out or supply (an order, commission, etc.). 7 occupy (vacant time). 8 (of a sail) be distended by wind. 9 (usu. As filling adj.) (esp. Of food) satisfy, satiate. â€”n. 1 as much as one wants or can bear (eat your fill). 2 enough to fill something.  fill the bill be suitable or adequate. Fill in 1 complete (a form, document, etc.). 2 a complete (a drawing etc.) Within an outline. B fill (an outline) in this way. 3 fill (a hole etc.) Completely. 4 (often foll. By for) act as a substitute. 5 occupy oneself during (spare time). 6 colloq. Inform (a person) more fully. 7 slang thrash, beat. Fill out 1 enlarge to the required size. 2 become enlarged or plump. 3 us fill in (a document etc.). Fill up 1 make or become completely full. 2 fill in (a document etc.). 3 fill the petrol tank of (a car etc.). [old english]'),
(15456, 'f', '\r\nFiller', ' n. 1 material used to fill a cavity or increase bulk. 2 small item filling space in a newspaper etc.'),
(15457, 'f', '\r\nFillet', ' â€”n. 1 a boneless piece of meat or fish. B (in full fillet steak) undercut of a sirloin. 2 ribbon etc. Binding the hair. 3 thin narrow strip or ridge. 4 narrow flat band between mouldings. â€”v. (-t-) 1 remove bones from (fish or meat) or divide into fillets. 2 bind or provide with fillet(s). [latin filum thread]'),
(15458, 'f', '\r\nFilling', ' n. Material that fills a tooth, sandwich, pie, etc.'),
(15459, 'f', '\r\nFilling-station', ' n. Garage selling petrol etc.'),
(15460, 'f', '\r\nFillip', ' â€”n. 1 stimulus, incentive. 2 flick with a finger or thumb. â€”v. (-p-) 1 stimulate. 2 flick. [imitative]'),
(15461, 'f', '\r\nFilly', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 young female horse. 2 colloq. Girl or young woman. [old norse]'),
(15462, 'f', '\r\nFilm', ' â€”n. 1 thin coating or covering layer. 2 strip or sheet of plastic etc. Coated with light-sensitive emulsion for exposure in a camera. 3 a story, episode, etc., on film, with the illusion of movement. B (in pl.) The cinema industry. 4 slight veil or haze etc. 5 dimness or morbid growth affecting the eyes. â€”v. 1 make a photographic film of (a scene, story, etc.). 2 cover or become covered with or as with a film. [old english]'),
(15463, 'f', '\r\nFilm-goer', ' n. Person who frequents the cinema.'),
(15464, 'f', '\r\nFilmsetting', ' n. Typesetting by projecting characters on to photographic film.  film-set v. Film-setter n.'),
(15465, 'f', '\r\nFilm', 'star  n. Celebrated film actor or actress.'),
(15466, 'f', '\r\nFilm-strip', ' n. Series of transparencies in a strip for projection.'),
(15467, 'f', '\r\nFilmy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 thin and translucent. 2 covered with or as with a film.'),
(15468, 'f', '\r\nFilofax', ' n. Propr. A type of loose-leaf personal organizer. [from *file1, *fact]'),
(15469, 'f', '\r\nFilo', 'pastry  n. (also phyllo pastry) leaved pastry like strudel pastry. [greek phullon leaf]'),
(15470, 'f', '\r\nFilter', ' â€”n. 1 porous device for removing impurities etc. From a liquid or gas passed through it. 2 = *filter tip. 3 screen or attachment for absorbing or modifying light, x-rays, etc. 4 device for suppressing unwanted electrical or sound waves. 5 arrangement for filtering traffic. â€”v. 1 (cause to) pass through a filter. 2 (foll. By through, into, etc.) Make way gradually. 3 (foll. By out) (cause to) leak. 4 allow (traffic) or (of traffic) be allowed to pass to the left or right at a junction. [germanic: related to *felt1]'),
(15471, 'f', '\r\nFilter-paper', ' n. Porous paper for filtering.'),
(15472, 'f', '\r\nFilter', 'tip  n. 1 filter on a cigarette removing some impurities. 2 cigarette with this.  filter-tipped adj.'),
(15473, 'f', '\r\nFilth', ' n. 1 repugnant or extreme dirt. 2 obscenity. [old english: related to *foul]'),
(15474, 'f', '\r\nFilthy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 extremely or disgustingly dirty. 2 obscene. 3 colloq. (of weather) very unpleasant. â€”adv. 1 filthily (filthy dirty). 2 colloq. Extremely (filthy rich).  filthily adv. Filthiness n.'),
(15475, 'f', '\r\nFilthy', 'lucre  n. 1 dishonourable gain. 2 joc. Money.'),
(15476, 'f', '\r\nFiltrate', ' â€”v. (-ting) filter. â€”n. Filtered liquid.  filtration n. [related to *filter]'),
(15477, 'f', '\r\nFin', ' n. 1 (usu. Thin) flat external organ of esp. Fish, for propelling, steering, etc. (dorsal fin). 2 similar stabilizing projection on an aircraft, car, etc. 3 underwater swimmer''s flipper.  finned adj. [old english]'),
(15478, 'f', '\r\nFinagle', ' v. (-ling) colloq. Act or obtain dishonestly.  finagler n. [dial. Fainaigue cheat]'),
(15479, 'f', '\r\nFinal', ' â€”adj. 1 situated at the end, coming last. 2 conclusive, decisive. â€”n. 1 last or deciding heat or game in sports etc. (cup final). 2 last daily edition of a newspaper. 3 (usu. In pl.) Examinations at the end of a degree course.  finally adv. [latin finis end]'),
(15480, 'f', '\r\nFinal', 'cause  n. Philos. Ultimate purpose.'),
(15481, 'f', '\r\nFinal', 'clause  n. Gram. Clause expressing purpose.'),
(15482, 'f', '\r\nFinale', ' n. Last movement or section of a piece of music or drama etc. [italian: related to *final]'),
(15483, 'f', '\r\nFinalist', ' n. Competitor in the final of a competition etc.'),
(15484, 'f', '\r\nFinality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 fact of being final. 2 final act etc. [latin: related to *final]'),
(15485, 'f', '\r\nFinalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) put into final form; complete.  finalization n.'),
(15486, 'f', '\r\nFinal', 'solution  n. Nazi policy (1941â€“5) of exterminating european jews.'),
(15487, 'f', '\r\nFinance', ' â€”n. 1 management of (esp. Public) money. 2 monetary support for an enterprise. 3 (in pl.) Money resources of a state, company, or person. â€”v. (-cing) provide capital for. [french: related to *fine2]'),
(15488, 'f', '\r\nFinance', 'company  n. (also finance house) company providing money, esp. For hire-purchase transactions.'),
(15489, 'f', '\r\nFinancial', ' adj. Of finance.  financially adv.'),
(15490, 'f', '\r\nFinancial', 'year  n. Year as reckoned for taxing or accounting, esp. From 6 april.'),
(15491, 'f', '\r\nFinancier', ' n. Capitalist; entrepreneur. [french: related to *finance]'),
(15492, 'f', '\r\nFinch', ' n. Small seed-eating bird, esp. A crossbill, canary, or chaffinch. [old english]'),
(15493, 'f', '\r\nFind', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Found) 1 a discover or get by chance or effort (found a key). B become aware of. 2 a obtain, succeed in obtaining; receive (idea found acceptance). B summon up (found courage). 3 seek out and provide or supply (will find you a book; finds his own meals). 4 discover by study etc. (find the answer). 5 a perceive or experience (find no sense in it). B (often in passive) discover to be present (not found in shakespeare). C discover from experience (finds england too cold). 6 law (of a jury, judge, etc.) Decide and declare (found him guilty). 7 reach by a natural process (water finds its own level). â€”n. 1 discovery of treasure etc. 2 valued thing or person newly discovered.  all found (of wages) with board and lodging provided free. Find fault see *fault. Find favour prove acceptable. Find one''s feet 1 become able to walk. 2 develop independence. Find oneself 1 discover that one is (found herself agreeing).'),
(15494, 'f', '\r\nFinder', ' n. 1 person who finds. 2 small telescope attached to a large one to locate an object. 3 viewfinder.'),
(15495, 'f', '\r\nFinding', ' n. (often in pl.) Conclusion reached by an inquiry etc.'),
(15496, 'f', '\r\nFine1', ' â€”adj. 1 a of high quality; excellent (fine painting). B good, satisfactory (that will be fine). 2 a pure, refined. B (of gold or silver) containing a specified proportion of pure metal. 3 imposing, dignified (fine buildings). 4 in good health (i''m fine). 5 (of weather etc.) Bright and clear. 6 a thin; sharp. B in small particles. C worked in slender thread. 7 euphemistic; flattering (fine words). 8 ornate, showy. 9 fastidious, affectedly refined. â€”adv. 1 finely. 2 colloq. Very well (suits me fine). â€”v. (-ning) 1 (often foll. By away, down, off) make or become finer, thinner, more tapering, or less coarse. 2 (often foll. By down) make or become clear (esp. Of beer etc.).  not to put too fine a point on it to speak bluntly.  finely adv. Fineness n. [french fin from latin finio *finish]'),
(15497, 'f', '\r\nFine2', ' â€”n. Money to be paid as a penalty. â€”v. (-ning) punish by a fine (fined him Â£5).  in fine in short. [french fin settlement of a dispute, from latin finis end]'),
(15498, 'f', '\r\nFine', 'arts  n.pl. Poetry, music, and the visual arts, esp. Painting, sculpture, and architecture.'),
(15499, 'f', '\r\nFinery', ' n. Showy dress or decoration. [from *fine1]'),
(15500, 'f', '\r\nFines', 'herbes  n.pl. Mixed herbs used in cooking. [french, = fine herbs]'),
(15501, 'f', '\r\nFine-spun', ' adj. 1 delicate. 2 (of theory etc.) Too subtle, unpractical.'),
(15502, 'f', '\r\nFinesse', ' â€”n. 1 refinement. 2 subtle manipulation. 3 artfulness; tact. 4 cards attempt to win a trick with a card that is not the highest held. â€”v. (-ssing) 1 use or achieve by finesse. 2 cards a make a finesse. B play (a card) as a finesse. [french: related to *fine1]'),
(15503, 'f', '\r\nFine-tooth', 'comb  n. Comb with close-set teeth.  go over with a fine-tooth comb check or search thoroughly.'),
(15504, 'f', '\r\nFine-tune', ' v. Make small adjustments to (a mechanism etc.).'),
(15505, 'f', '\r\nFinger', ' â€”n. 1 any of the terminal projections of the hand (usu. Excluding the thumb). 2 part of a glove etc. For a finger. 3 finger-like object or structure (fish finger). 4 colloq. Small measure of liquor. â€”v. Touch, feel, or turn about with the fingers.  get (or pull) one''s finger out slang start to act. Lay a finger on touch, however slightly. Put one''s finger on locate or identify exactly.  fingerless adj. [old english]'),
(15506, 'f', '\r\nFinger-board', ' n. Part of the neck of a stringed instrument on which the fingers press to vary the pitch.'),
(15507, 'f', '\r\nFinger-bowl', ' n. (also finger-glass) small bowl for rinsing the fingers during a meal.'),
(15508, 'f', '\r\nFinger-dry', ' v. Dry and style (the hair) by running one''s fingers through it.'),
(15509, 'f', '\r\nFingering', ' n. 1 technique etc. Of using the fingers, esp. In playing music. 2 indication of this in a musical score.'),
(15510, 'f', '\r\nFinger-mark', ' n. Mark left by a finger.'),
(15511, 'f', '\r\nFingernail', ' n. Nail of each finger.'),
(15512, 'f', '\r\nFinger-plate', ' n. Plate fixed to a door to prevent finger-marks.'),
(15513, 'f', '\r\nFingerprint', ' â€”n. Impression of a fingertip on a surface, used in detecting crime. â€”v. Record the fingerprints of.'),
(15514, 'f', '\r\nFinger-stall', ' n. Protective cover for an injured finger.'),
(15515, 'f', '\r\nFingertip', ' n. Tip of a finger.  have at one''s fingertips be thoroughly familiar with (a subject etc.).'),
(15516, 'f', '\r\nFinial', ' n. Ornamental top or end of a roof, gable, etc. [anglo-french: related to *fine1]'),
(15517, 'f', '\r\nFinicky', ' adj. (also finical, finicking) 1 over-particular, fastidious. 2 detailed; fiddly.  finickiness n. [perhaps from *fine1]'),
(15518, 'f', '\r\nFinis', ' n. End, esp. Of a book. [latin]'),
(15519, 'f', '\r\nFinish', ' â€”v. 1 a (often foll. By off) bring or come to an end or the end of; complete; cease. B (usu. Foll. By off) colloq. Kill; vanquish. C (often foll. By off, up) consume or complete consuming (food or drink). 2 treat the surface of (cloth, woodwork, etc.). â€”n. 1 a end, last stage, completion. B point at which a race etc. Ends. 2 method, material, etc. Used for surface treatment of wood, cloth, etc. (mahogany finish).  finish up (often foll. By in, by) end (finished up by crying). Finish with have no more to do with, complete using etc. [latin finis end]'),
(15520, 'f', '\r\nFinishing-school', ' n. Private college preparing girls for fashionable society.'),
(15521, 'f', '\r\nFinishing', 'touch  n. (also finishing touches) final enhancing details.'),
(15522, 'f', '\r\nFinite', ' adj. 1 limited; not infinite. 2 (of a part of a verb) having a specific number and person. [latin: related to *finish]'),
(15523, 'f', '\r\nFinn', ' n. Native or national of finland; person of finnish descent. [old english]'),
(15524, 'f', '\r\nFinnan', ' n. (in full finnan haddock) smoke-cured haddock. [findhorn, findon, in scotland]'),
(15525, 'f', '\r\nFinnic', ' adj. Of the group of peoples or languages related to the finns or finnish.'),
(15526, 'f', '\r\nFinnish', ' â€”adj. Of the finns or their language. â€”n. Language of the finns.'),
(15527, 'f', '\r\nFino', ' n. (pl. -s) light-coloured dry sherry. [spanish, = fine]'),
(15528, 'f', '\r\nFiord', ' n. (also fjord) long narrow sea inlet, as in norway. [norwegian]'),
(15529, 'f', '\r\nFipple', ' n. Plug at the mouth-end of a wind instrument. [origin unknown]'),
(15530, 'f', '\r\nFipple', 'flute  n. Flute played by blowing endwise, e.g. A recorder.'),
(15531, 'f', '\r\nFir', ' n. 1 (in full fir-tree) evergreen coniferous tree with needles growing singly on the stems. 2 its wood.  firry adj. [old norse]'),
(15532, 'f', '\r\nFir-cone', ' n. Fruit of the fir.'),
(15533, 'f', '\r\nFire', ' â€”n. 1 a combustion of substances with oxygen, giving out light and heat. B flame or incandescence. 2 destructive burning (forest fire). 3 a burning fuel in a grate, furnace, etc. B = *electric fire. C = *gas fire. 4 firing of guns. 5 a fervour, spirit, vivacity. B poetic inspiration. 6 burning heat, fever. â€”v. (-ring) 1 (often foll. By at, into, on) a shoot (a gun, missile, etc.). B shoot a gun or missile etc. 2 produce (a broadside, salute, etc.) By shooting guns etc. 3 (of a gun etc.) Be discharged. 4 explode or kindle (an explosive). 5 deliver or utter rapidly (fired insults at us). 6 slang dismiss (an employee). 7 set fire to intentionally. 8 catch fire. 9 (of esp. An internal-combustion engine) undergo ignition. 10 supply (a furnace, engine, etc.) With fuel. 11 stimulate; enthuse. 12 bake, dry, or cure (pottery, bricks, tea, tobacco, etc.). 13 become or cause to become heated, excited, red, or glowing.  catch fire begin to burn. Fire away colloq. Begin; go ahead. On fire 1 burning. 2 excited. Set fire to (or set on fire) ignite, kindle. Set the world (or thames) on fire do something remarkable or sensational. Under fire 1 being shot at. 2 being rigorously criticized or questioned. [old english]'),
(15534, 'f', '\r\nFire-alarm', ' n. Device warning of fire.'),
(15535, 'f', '\r\nFire', 'and brimstone  n. Supposed torments of hell.'),
(15536, 'f', '\r\nFirearm', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Gun, pistol, or rifle.'),
(15537, 'f', '\r\nFire-ball', ' n. 1 large meteor. 2 ball of flame or lightning. 3 energetic person.'),
(15538, 'f', '\r\nFire-bomb', ' n. Incendiary bomb.'),
(15539, 'f', '\r\nFirebox', ' n. Place where fuel is burned in a steam engine or boiler.'),
(15540, 'f', '\r\nFirebrand', ' n. 1 piece of burning wood. 2 person causing trouble or unrest.'),
(15541, 'f', '\r\nFire-break', ' n. Obstacle to the spread of fire in a forest etc., esp. An open space.'),
(15542, 'f', '\r\nFire-brick', ' n. Fireproof brick in a grate.'),
(15543, 'f', '\r\nFire', 'brigade  n. Body of professional firefighters.'),
(15544, 'f', '\r\nFireclay', ' n. Clay used to make fire-bricks.'),
(15545, 'f', '\r\nFirecracker', ' n. Us explosive firework.'),
(15546, 'f', '\r\nFiredamp', ' n. Miners'' name for methane, which is explosive when mixed with air.'),
(15547, 'f', '\r\nFiredog', ' n. Andiron.'),
(15548, 'f', '\r\nFire', 'door  n. Fire-resistant door preventing the spread of fire.'),
(15549, 'f', '\r\nFire-drill', ' n. Rehearsal of the procedures to be used in case of fire.'),
(15550, 'f', '\r\nFire-eater', ' n. 1 conjuror who appears to swallow fire. 2 quarrelsome person.'),
(15551, 'f', '\r\nFire-engine', ' n. Vehicle carrying hoses, firefighters, etc.'),
(15552, 'f', '\r\nFire-escape', ' n. Emergency staircase etc. For use in a fire.'),
(15553, 'f', '\r\nFire', 'extinguisher  n. Apparatus discharging foam etc. To extinguish a fire.'),
(15554, 'f', '\r\nFirefighter', ' n. = *fireman 1.'),
(15555, 'f', '\r\nFirefly', ' n. Beetle emitting phosphorescent light, e.g. The glow-worm.'),
(15556, 'f', '\r\nFire-guard', ' n. Protective screen placed in front of a fireplace.'),
(15557, 'f', '\r\nFire-irons', ' n.pl. Tongs, poker, and shovel for a domestic fire.'),
(15558, 'f', '\r\nFirelight', ' n. Light from a fire in a fireplace.'),
(15559, 'f', '\r\nFire-lighter', ' n. Inflammable material used to start a fire in a grate.'),
(15560, 'f', '\r\nFireman', ' n. 1 member of a fire brigade. 2 person who tends a steam engine or steamship furnace.'),
(15561, 'f', '\r\nFireplace', ' n. 1 place for a domestic fire, esp. A recess in a wall. 2 structure surrounding this.'),
(15562, 'f', '\r\nFire-power', ' n. Destructive capacity of guns etc.'),
(15563, 'f', '\r\nFire-practice', ' n. Fire-drill.'),
(15564, 'f', '\r\nFireproof', ' â€”adj. Able to resist fire or great heat. â€”v. Make fireproof.'),
(15565, 'f', '\r\nFire-raiser', ' n. Arsonist.  fire-raising n.'),
(15566, 'f', '\r\nFire-screen', ' n. 1 ornamental screen for a fireplace. 2 screen against the direct heat of a fire. 3 fire-guard.'),
(15567, 'f', '\r\nFire-ship', ' n. Hist. Ship set on fire and directed against an enemy''s ships etc.'),
(15568, 'f', '\r\nFireside', ' n. 1 area round a fireplace. 2 home or home-life.'),
(15569, 'f', '\r\nFire', 'station  n. Headquarters of a fire brigade.'),
(15570, 'f', '\r\nFire-storm', ' n. High wind or storm following a fire caused by bombs.'),
(15571, 'f', '\r\nFire-trap', ' n. Building without fire-escapes etc.'),
(15572, 'f', '\r\nFire-watcher', ' n. Person keeping watch for fires, esp. Those caused by bombs.'),
(15573, 'f', '\r\nFire-water', ' n. Colloq. Strong alcoholic liquor.'),
(15574, 'f', '\r\nFirewood', ' n. Wood as fuel.'),
(15575, 'f', '\r\nFirework', ' n. 1 device that burns or explodes spectacularly when lit. 2 (in pl.) Outburst of passion, esp. Anger.'),
(15576, 'f', '\r\nFiring', ' n. 1 discharge of guns. 2 fuel.'),
(15577, 'f', '\r\nFiring-line', ' n. 1 front line in a battle. 2 centre of activity etc.'),
(15578, 'f', '\r\nFiring-squad', ' n. 1 soldiers ordered to shoot a condemned person. 2 group firing the salute at a military funeral.'),
(15579, 'f', '\r\nFirm1', ' â€”adj. 1 a solid or compact. B fixed, stable, steady. 2 a resolute, determined. B steadfast, constant (firm belief; firm friend). 3 (of an offer etc.) Definite; not conditional. â€”adv. Firmly (stand firm). â€”v. (often foll. By up) make or become firm, secure, compact, or solid.  firmly adv. Firmness n. [latin firmus]'),
(15580, 'f', '\r\nFirm2', ' n. Business concern or its partners. [latin firma: cf. *firm1]'),
(15581, 'f', '\r\nFirmament', ' n. Literary the sky regarded as a vault or arch. [latin: related to *firm1]'),
(15582, 'f', '\r\nFirmware', ' n. Computing permanent kind of software.'),
(15583, 'f', '\r\nFirry', ' see *fir.'),
(15584, 'f', '\r\nFirst', ' â€”adj. 1 earliest in time or order (took the first bus). 2 foremost in rank or importance (first lord of the treasury). 3 most willing or likely (the first to admit it). 4 basic or evident (first principles). â€”n. 1 (prec. By the) person or thing first mentioned or occurring. 2 first occurrence of something notable. 3 place in the first class in an examination. 4 first gear. 5 a first place in a race. B winner of this. â€”adv. 1 before any other person or thing (first of all; first and foremost). 2 before someone or something else (get this done first). 3 for the first time (when did you first see her?). 4 in preference; rather (will see him damned first).  at first at the beginning. At first hand directly from the original source.'),
(15585, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'aid  n. Emergency medical treatment.'),
(15586, 'f', '\r\nFirst-born', ' â€”adj. Eldest. â€”n. Person''s eldest child.'),
(15587, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'class  â€”n. 1 best group or category. 2 best accommodation in a train, ship, etc. 3 mail given priority. 4 highest division in an examination. â€”adj. & adv. (first-class) 1 of or by the first class. 2 excellent.'),
(15588, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'cousin  see *cousin.'),
(15589, 'f', '\r\nFirst-day', 'cover  n. Envelope with stamps postmarked on their first day of issue.'),
(15590, 'f', '\r\nFirst-degree', ' adj. Denoting non-serious surface burns.'),
(15591, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'finger  n. Finger next to the thumb.'),
(15592, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'floor  n. (us second floor) floor above the ground floor.'),
(15593, 'f', '\r\nFirst-foot', ' scot. â€”n. First person to cross a threshold in the new year. â€”v. Be a first-foot.'),
(15594, 'f', '\r\nFirst-fruit', ' n. (usu. In pl.) 1 first agricultural produce of a season, esp. As offered to god. 2 first results of work etc.'),
(15595, 'f', '\r\nFirsthand', ' adj. & adv. From the original source; direct.'),
(15596, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'lady  n. (in the us) wife of the president.'),
(15597, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'light  n. Dawn.'),
(15598, 'f', '\r\nFirstly', ' adv. In the first place, first (cf. *first adv.).'),
(15599, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'mate  n. (on a merchant ship) second in command.'),
(15600, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'name  n. Personal or christian name.'),
(15601, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'night  n. First public performance of a play etc.'),
(15602, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'offender  n. Criminal without previous convictions.'),
(15603, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'officer  n. = *first mate.'),
(15604, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'person  see *person.'),
(15605, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'post  n. (also last post) bugle-call as a signal to retire for the night.'),
(15606, 'f', '\r\nFirst-rate', ' adj. 1 excellent. 2 colloq. Very well (feeling first-rate).'),
(15607, 'f', '\r\nFirst', 'thing  adv. Colloq. Before anything else; very early.'),
(15608, 'f', '\r\nFirth', ' n. (also frith) 1 narrow inlet of sea. 2 estuary. [old norse: related to *fiord]'),
(15609, 'f', '\r\nFiscal', ' â€”adj. Of public revenue. â€”n. 1 legal official in some countries. 2 scot. = *procurator fiscal. [latin fiscus treasury]'),
(15610, 'f', '\r\nFiscal', 'year  n. = *financial year.'),
(15611, 'f', '\r\nFish1', ' â€”n. (pl. Same or -es) 1 vertebrate cold-blooded animal with gills and fins living wholly in water. 2 any of various non-vertebrate animals living wholly in water, e.g. The cuttlefish, shellfish, and jellyfish. 3 fish as food. 4 colloq. Person of a specified, usu. Unpleasant, kind (an odd fish). 5 (the fish or fishes) sign or constellation pisces. â€”v. 1 try to catch fish. 2 fish in (a certain river, pool, etc.). 3 (foll. By for) a search for. B seek indirectly (fishing for compliments). 4 (foll. By up, out, etc.) Retrieve with effort.  drink like a fish drink alcohol excessively. Fish out of water person out of his or her element. Other fish to fry other matters to attend to. [old english]'),
(15612, 'f', '\r\nFish2', ' n. Flat or curved plate of iron, wood, etc., used to strengthen a beam, joint, or mast. [french ficher fix, from latin figere *fix]'),
(15613, 'f', '\r\nFish-bowl', ' n. (usu. Round) glass bowl for pet fish.'),
(15614, 'f', '\r\nFish', 'cake  n. Breaded cake of fish and mashed potato, usu. Fried.'),
(15615, 'f', '\r\nFisher', ' n. 1 animal that catches fish. 2 archaic fisherman. [old english]'),
(15616, 'f', '\r\nFisherman', ' n. Man who catches fish as a livelihood or for sport.'),
(15617, 'f', '\r\nFishery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 place where fish are caught or reared. 2 industry of fishing or breeding fish.'),
(15618, 'f', '\r\nFish-eye', 'lens  n. Very wide-angle lens with a highly-curved front.'),
(15619, 'f', '\r\nFish', 'farm  n. Place where fish are bred for food.'),
(15620, 'f', '\r\nFish', 'finger  n. Small oblong piece of fish in batter or breadcrumbs.'),
(15621, 'f', '\r\nFish-hook', ' n. Barbed hook for catching fish.'),
(15622, 'f', '\r\nFishing', ' n. Catching fish.'),
(15623, 'f', '\r\nFishing-line', ' n. Thread with a baited hook etc. For catching fish.'),
(15624, 'f', '\r\nFishing-rod', ' n. Tapering usu. Jointed rod for fishing.'),
(15625, 'f', '\r\nFish-kettle', ' n. Oval pan for boiling fish.'),
(15626, 'f', '\r\nFish-knife', ' n. Knife for eating or serving fish.'),
(15627, 'f', '\r\nFish-meal', ' n. Ground dried fish as fertilizer or animal feed.'),
(15628, 'f', '\r\nFishmonger', ' n. Dealer in fish.'),
(15629, 'f', '\r\nFishnet', ' n. (often attrib.) Open-meshed fabric (fishnet stockings).'),
(15630, 'f', '\r\nFish-plate', ' n. Flat piece of iron etc. Connecting railway rails or positioning masonry.'),
(15631, 'f', '\r\nFish-slice', ' n. Flat slotted cooking utensil.'),
(15632, 'f', '\r\nFishtail', ' n. Device etc. Shaped like a fish''s tail.'),
(15633, 'f', '\r\nFishwife', ' n. 1 coarse-mannered or noisy woman. 2 woman who sells fish.'),
(15634, 'f', '\r\nFishy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like fish. 2 slang dubious, suspect.  fishily adv. Fishiness n.'),
(15635, 'f', '\r\nFissile', ' adj. 1 capable of undergoing nuclear fission. 2 tending to split. [latin: related to *fissure]'),
(15636, 'f', '\r\nFission', ' â€”n. 1 splitting of a heavy atomic nucleus, with a release of energy. 2 cell division as a mode of reproduction. â€”v. (cause to) undergo fission.  fissionable adj. [latin: related to *fissure]'),
(15637, 'f', '\r\nFission', 'bomb  n. Atomic bomb.'),
(15638, 'f', '\r\nFissure', ' â€”n. Crack or split, usu. Long and narrow. â€”v. (-ring) split, crack. [latin findo fiss- cleave]'),
(15639, 'f', '\r\nFist', ' n. Tightly closed hand.  fistful n. (pl. -s). [old english]'),
(15640, 'f', '\r\nFisticuffs', ' n.pl. Fighting with the fists. [probably from obsolete fisty (from *fist), *cuff2]'),
(15641, 'f', '\r\nFistula', ' n. (pl. -s or -lae) abnormal or artificial passage between an organ and the body surface or between two organs.  fistular adj. Fistulous adj. [latin, = pipe]'),
(15642, 'f', '\r\nFit1', ' â€”adj. (fitter, fittest) 1 a well suited. B qualified, competent, worthy. C in suitable condition, ready. D (foll. By for) good enough (fit for a king). 2 in good health or condition. 3 proper, becoming, right (it is fit that). â€”v. (-tt-) 1 a (also absol.) Be of the right shape and size for (dress fits her; key doesn''t fit). B (often foll. By in, into) be correctly positioned (that bit fits here). C find room for (fit another on here). 2 make suitable or competent; adapt (fitted for battle). 3 (usu. Foll. By with) supply. 4 fix in place (fit a lock on the door). 5 = fit on. 6 befit, become (it fits the occasion). â€”n. Way in which a garment, component, etc., fits (tight fit). â€”adv. (foll. By to + infin.) Colloq. So that; likely (laughing fit to bust).  fit the bill = fill the bill. Fit in 1 (often foll. By with) be compatible; accommodate (tried to fit in with their plans). 2 find space or time for (dentist fitted me in). Fit on try on (a garment). Fit out (or up) (often foll. By with) equip. See (or think) fit (often foll. By to + infin.) Decide or choose (a specified action).  fitly adv. Fitness n. [origin unknown]'),
(15643, 'f', '\r\nFit2', ' n. 1 sudden esp. Epileptic seizure with unconsciousness or convulsions. 2 sudden brief bout or burst (fit of giggles; fit of coughing).  by (or in) fits and starts spasmodically. Have a fit colloq. Be greatly surprised or outraged. In fits laughing uncontrollably. [old english]'),
(15644, 'f', '\r\nFitful', ' adj. Spasmodic or intermittent.  fitfully adv.'),
(15645, 'f', '\r\nFitment', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Fixed item of furniture.'),
(15646, 'f', '\r\nFitted', ' adj. 1 made to fit closely or exactly (fitted carpet). 2 provided with built-in fittings etc. (fitted kitchen). 3 built-in (fitted cupboards).'),
(15647, 'f', '\r\nFitter', ' n. 1 mechanic who fits together and adjusts machinery. 2 supervisor of the cutting, fitting, etc. Of garments.'),
(15648, 'f', '\r\nFitting', ' â€”n. 1 trying-on of a garment etc. For adjustment before completion. 2 (in pl.) Fixtures and fitments of a building. â€”adj. Proper, becoming, right.  fittingly adv.'),
(15649, 'f', '\r\nFive', ' adj. & n. 1 one more than four. 2 symbol for this (5, v, v). 3 size etc. Denoted by five. 4 set or team of five. 5 five o''clock (is it five yet?). 6 cricket hit scoring five runs. [old english]'),
(15650, 'f', '\r\nFivefold', ' adj. & adv. 1 five times as much or as many. 2 consisting of five parts.'),
(15651, 'f', '\r\nFive', 'o''clock shadow  n. Beard-growth visible in the latter part of the day.'),
(15652, 'f', '\r\nFiver', ' n. Colloq. Five-pound note.'),
(15653, 'f', '\r\nFives', ' n. Game in which a ball is hit with a gloved hand or bat against the walls of a court.'),
(15654, 'f', '\r\nFive-star', ' adj. Of the highest class.'),
(15655, 'f', '\r\nFivestones', ' n. Jacks played with five pieces of metal etc. And usu. No ball.'),
(15656, 'f', '\r\nFix', ' â€”v. 1 make firm or stable; fasten, secure. 2 decide, settle, specify (a price, date, etc.). 3 mend, repair. 4 implant in the mind. 5 a (foll. By on, upon) direct (the eyes etc.) Steadily, set. B attract and hold (the attention, eyes, etc.). C (foll. By with) single out with one''s look etc. 6 place definitely, establish. 7 determine the exact nature, position, etc., of; refer (a thing) to a definite place or time; identify, locate. 8 a make (the eyes, features, etc.) Rigid. B (of eyes, features, etc.) Become rigid. 9 us colloq. Prepare (food or drink). 10 congeal or become congealed. 11 colloq. Punish, kill, deal with (a person). 12 colloq. A bribe or threaten into supporting. B gain a fraudulent result of (a race etc.). 13 slang inject a narcotic. 14 make (a colour, photographic image, etc.) Fast or permanent. 15 (of a plant etc.) Assimilate (nitrogen or carbon dioxide). â€”n. 1 colloq. Dilemma, predicament. 2 a finding one''s position by bearings etc. B position found in this way. 3 slang dose of an addictive drug.  be fixed (usu. Foll. By for) colloq. Be situated (regarding) (how is he fixed for money?). Fix on (or upon) choose, decide on. Fix up 1 arrange, organize. 2 accommodate. 3 (often foll. By with) provide (a person) (fixed me up with a job).  fixable adj. [latin figo fix-]'),
(15657, 'f', '\r\nFixate', ' v. (-ting) 1 direct one''s gaze on. 2 psychol. (usu. In passive; often foll. By on, upon) cause (a person) to become abnormally attached to a person or thing. [latin: related to *fix]'),
(15658, 'f', '\r\nFixation', ' n. 1 state of being fixated. 2 obsession, monomania. 3 coagulation. 4 process of assimilating a gas to form a solid compound.'),
(15659, 'f', '\r\nFixative', ' â€”adj. Tending to fix or secure. â€”n. Fixative substance.'),
(15660, 'f', '\r\nFixedly', ' adv. Intently.'),
(15661, 'f', '\r\nFixed', 'star  n. Astron. Seemingly motionless star.'),
(15662, 'f', '\r\nFixer', ' n. 1 person or thing that fixes. 2 photog. Substance for fixing a photographic image etc. 3 colloq. Person who makes esp. Illicit deals.'),
(15663, 'f', '\r\nFixings', ' n.pl. Us 1 apparatus or equipment. 2 trimmings for a dish, dress, etc.'),
(15664, 'f', '\r\nFixity', ' n. Fixed state; stability; permanence.'),
(15665, 'f', '\r\nFixture', ' n. 1 a something fixed in position. B colloq. Seemingly immovable person or thing (seems to be a fixture). 2 a sporting event, esp. A match, race, etc. B date agreed for this. 3 (in pl.) Articles attached to a house or land and regarded as legally part of it.'),
(15666, 'f', '\r\nFizz', ' â€”v. 1 make a hissing or spluttering sound. 2 (of a drink) effervesce. â€”n. 1 effervescence. 2 colloq. Effervescent drink, esp. Champagne. [imitative]'),
(15667, 'f', '\r\nFizzle', ' â€”v. (-ling) make a feeble hiss. â€”n. Such a sound.  fizzle out end feebly. [imitative]'),
(15668, 'f', '\r\nFizzy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) effervescent.  fizziness n.'),
(15669, 'f', '\r\nFjord', ' var. Of *fiord.'),
(15670, 'f', '\r\nFl.', ' Abbr. 1 floruit. 2 fluid.'),
(15671, 'f', '\r\nFlab', ' n. Colloq. Fat; flabbiness. [imitative, or from *flabby]'),
(15672, 'f', '\r\nFlabbergast', ' v. (esp. As flabbergasted adj.) Colloq. Astonish; dumbfound. [origin uncertain]'),
(15673, 'f', '\r\nFlabby', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of flesh etc.) Limp; flaccid. 2 feeble.  flabbiness n. [alteration of flappy: related to *flap]'),
(15674, 'f', '\r\nFlaccid', ' adj. Limp, flabby, drooping.  flaccidity n. [latin flaccus limp]'),
(15675, 'f', '\r\nFlag1', ' â€”n. 1 a usu. Oblong or square piece of cloth, attachable by one edge to a pole or rope as a country''s emblem or standard, a signal, etc. B small toy etc. Resembling a flag. 2 adjustable strip of metal etc. Indicating a taxi''s availability for hire. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 a grow tired; lag (was soon flagging). B hang down; droop. 2 mark out with or as if with a flag or flags. 3 (often foll. By that) inform or communicate by flag-signals.  flag down signal to stop. [origin unknown]'),
(15676, 'f', '\r\nFlag2', ' â€”n. (also flagstone) 1 flat usu. Rectangular paving stone. 2 (in pl.) Pavement of these. â€”v. (-gg-) pave with flags. [probably scandinavian]'),
(15677, 'f', '\r\nFlag3', ' n. Plant with a bladed leaf (esp. The iris). [origin unknown]'),
(15678, 'f', '\r\nFlag-day', ' n. Fund-raising day for a charity, esp. With the sale of small paper flags etc. In the street.'),
(15679, 'f', '\r\nFlagellant', ' â€”n. Person who scourges himself, herself, or others as a religious discipline or as a sexual stimulus. â€”adj. Of flagellation. [latin flagellum whip]'),
(15680, 'f', '\r\nFlagellate', ' v. (-ting) scourge, flog.  flagellation n.'),
(15681, 'f', '\r\nFlagellum', ' n. (pl. -gella) 1 long lashlike appendage on some microscopic organisms. 2 runner; creeping shoot. [latin, = whip]'),
(15682, 'f', '\r\nFlageolet', ' n. Small flute blown at the end. [french from provenÃ§al]'),
(15683, 'f', '\r\nFlag', 'of convenience  n. Foreign flag under which a ship is registered, usu. To avoid regulations or financial charges.'),
(15684, 'f', '\r\nFlag-officer', ' n. Admiral, vice admiral, or rear admiral, or the commodore of a yacht-club.'),
(15685, 'f', '\r\nFlag', 'of truce  n. White flag requesting a truce.'),
(15686, 'f', '\r\nFlagon', ' n. 1 large bottle, usu. Holding a quart (1.13 litres), esp. Of wine, cider, etc. 2 large vessel for wine etc., usu. With a handle, spout, and lid. [latin flasco *flask]'),
(15687, 'f', '\r\nFlag-pole', ' n. = *flagstaff.'),
(15688, 'f', '\r\nFlagrant', ' adj. Blatant; notorious; scandalous.  flagrancy n. Flagrantly adv. [latin flagro blaze]'),
(15689, 'f', '\r\nFlagship', ' n. 1 ship with an admiral on board. 2 leader in a category etc.; exemplar.'),
(15690, 'f', '\r\nFlagstaff', ' n. Pole on which a flag may be hoisted.'),
(15691, 'f', '\r\nFlagstone', ' n. = *flag2.'),
(15692, 'f', '\r\nFlag-waving', ' n. Populist agitation, chauvinism.'),
(15693, 'f', '\r\nFlail', ' â€”n. Wooden staff with a short heavy stick swinging from it, used for threshing. â€”v. 1 wave or swing wildly. 2 beat with or as with a flail. [latin flagellum whip]'),
(15694, 'f', '\r\nFlair', ' n. 1 natural talent in a specific area (flair for languages). 2 style, finesse. [french flairer to smell]'),
(15695, 'f', '\r\nFlak', ' n. 1 anti-aircraft fire. 2 adverse criticism; abuse. [german, fliegerabwehrkanone, â€˜aviator-defence-gunâ€™]'),
(15696, 'f', '\r\nFlake', ' â€”n. 1 small thin light piece of snow etc. 2 thin broad piece peeled or split off. 3 dogfish etc. As food. â€”v. (-king) (often foll. By away, off) 1 take off or come away in flakes. 2 sprinkle with or fall in flakes.  flake out colloq. Fall asleep or drop from exhaustion; faint. [origin unknown]'),
(15697, 'f', '\r\nFlak', 'jacket  n. Protective reinforced military jacket.'),
(15698, 'f', '\r\nFlaky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of, like, or in flakes. 2 esp. Us slang crazy, eccentric.'),
(15699, 'f', '\r\nFlaky', 'pastry  n. Crumblier version of puff pastry.'),
(15700, 'f', '\r\nFlambÃ©', ' adj. (of food) covered with alcohol and set alight briefly (following a noun: pancakes flambÃ©). [french: related to *flame]'),
(15701, 'f', '\r\nFlamboyant', ' adj. 1 ostentatious; showy. 2 floridly decorated or coloured.  flamboyance n. Flamboyantly adv. [french: related to *flambÃ©]'),
(15702, 'f', '\r\nFlame', ' â€”n. 1 a ignited gas. B portion of this (flame flickered; burst into flames). 2 a bright light or colouring. B brilliant orange-red colour. 3 a strong passion, esp. Love (fan the flame). B colloq. Sweetheart. â€”v. (-ming) 1 (often foll. By away, forth, out, up) burn; blaze. 2 (often foll. By out, up) a (of passion) break out. B (of a person) become angry. 3 shine or glow like flame. [latin flamma]'),
(15703, 'f', '\r\nFlamenco', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 style of spanish gypsy guitar music with singing. 2 dance performed to this. [spanish, = flemish]'),
(15704, 'f', '\r\nFlame-thrower', ' n. Weapon for throwing a spray of flame.'),
(15705, 'f', '\r\nFlaming', ' adj. 1 emitting flames. 2 very hot (flaming june). 3 colloq. A passionate (flaming row). B expressing annoyance (that flaming dog). 4 bright-coloured.'),
(15706, 'f', '\r\nFlamingo', ' n. (pl. -s or -es) tall long-necked wading bird with mainly pink plumage. [provenÃ§al: related to *flame]'),
(15707, 'f', '\r\nFlammable', ' adj. Inflammable.  flammability n. [latin: related to *flame]'),
(15708, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' flammable is often used because inflammable can be mistaken for a negative (the true negative being non-flammable).'),
(15709, 'f', '\r\nFlan', ' n. 1 pastry case with a savoury or sweet filling. 2 sponge base with a sweet topping. [medieval latin flado -onis]'),
(15710, 'f', '\r\nFlange', ' n. Projecting flat rim etc., for strengthening or attachment. [origin uncertain]'),
(15711, 'f', '\r\nFlank', ' â€”n. 1 side of the body between ribs and hip. 2 side of a mountain, building, etc. 3 right or left side of an army etc. â€”v. (often in passive) be at or move along the side of (road flanked by mountains). [french from germanic]'),
(15712, 'f', '\r\nFlannel', ' â€”n. 1 a woven woollen usu. Napless fabric. B (in pl.) Flannel garments, esp. Trousers. 2 face-cloth, esp. Towelling. 3 slang nonsense; flattery. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 slang flatter. 2 wash with a flannel. [welsh gwlanen from gwlan wool]'),
(15713, 'f', '\r\nFlannelette', ' n. Napped cotton fabric like flannel.'),
(15714, 'f', '\r\nFlap', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 move or be moved up and down; beat. 2 colloq. Be agitated or panicky. 3 sway; flutter. 4 (usu. Foll. By away, off) strike (flies etc.) With flat object; drive. 5 colloq. (of ears) listen intently. â€”n. 1 piece of cloth, wood, etc. Attached by one side esp. To cover a gap, e.g. A pocket-cover, the folded part of an envelope, a table-leaf. 2 motion of a wing, arm, etc. 3 colloq. Agitation; panic (in a flap). 4 aileron. 5 light blow with something flat.  flappy adj. [probably imitative]'),
(15715, 'f', '\r\nFlapdoodle', ' n. Colloq. Nonsense. [origin unknown]'),
(15716, 'f', '\r\nFlapjack', ' n. 1 sweet oatcake. 2 esp. Us pancake.'),
(15717, 'f', '\r\nFlapper', ' n. 1 person apt to panic. 2 slang (in the 1920s) young unconventional woman.'),
(15718, 'f', '\r\nFlare', ' â€”v. (-ring) 1 widen gradually (flared trousers). 2 (cause to) blaze brightly and unsteadily. 3 burst out, esp. Angrily. â€”n. 1 a dazzling irregular flame or light. B sudden outburst of flame. 2 flame or bright light used as a signal or to illuminate a target etc. 3 a gradual widening, esp. Of a skirt or trousers. B (in pl.) Wide-bottomed trousers.  flare up burst into a sudden blaze, anger, activity, etc. [origin unknown]'),
(15719, 'f', '\r\nFlare-path', ' n. Line of lights on a runway to guide aircraft.'),
(15720, 'f', '\r\nFlare-up', ' n. Sudden outburst.'),
(15721, 'f', '\r\nFlash', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) emit a brief or sudden light; (cause to) gleam. 2 send or reflect like a sudden flame (eyes flashed fire). 3 a burst suddenly into view or perception (answer flashed upon me). B move swiftly (train flashed past). 4 a send (news etc.) By radio, telegraph, etc. B signal to (a person) with lights. 5 colloq. Show ostentatiously (flashed her ring). 6 slang indecently expose oneself. â€”n. 1 sudden bright light or flame, e.g. Of lightning. 2 an instant (in a flash). 3 sudden brief feeling, display of wit, etc. (flash of hope). 4 = *newsflash. 5 photog. = *flashlight 1. 6 mil. Coloured cloth patch on a uniform. 7 bright patch of colour. â€”adj. Colloq. Gaudy; showy; vulgar (flash car). [imitative]'),
(15722, 'f', '\r\nFlashback', ' n. Scene set in an earlier time than the main action.'),
(15723, 'f', '\r\nFlash', 'bulb  n. Photog. Bulb for a flashlight.'),
(15724, 'f', '\r\nFlash-cube', ' n. Photog. Set of four flash bulbs in a cube, operated in turn.'),
(15725, 'f', '\r\nFlasher', ' n. 1 slang man who indecently exposes himself. 2 automatic device for switching lights rapidly on and off.'),
(15726, 'f', '\r\nFlash-gun', ' n. Device operating a camera flashlight.'),
(15727, 'f', '\r\nFlashing', ' n. (usu. Metal) strip used to prevent water penetration at a roof joint etc. [dial.]'),
(15728, 'f', '\r\nFlash', 'in the pan  n. Promising start followed by failure.'),
(15729, 'f', '\r\nFlash-lamp', ' n. Portable flashing electric lamp.'),
(15730, 'f', '\r\nFlashlight', ' n. 1 light giving an intense flash, used for night or indoor photography. 2 us electric torch.'),
(15731, 'f', '\r\nFlashpoint', ' n. 1 temperature at which vapour from oil etc. Will ignite in air. 2 point at which anger etc. Is expressed.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(15732, 'f', '\r\nFlashy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) showy; gaudy; cheaply attractive.  flashily adv. Flashiness n.'),
(15733, 'f', '\r\nFlask', ' n. 1 narrow-necked bulbous bottle for wine etc. Or used in chemistry. 2 = *hip-flask. 3 = *vacuum flask. [latin flasca, flasco: cf. *flagon]'),
(15734, 'f', '\r\nFlat1', ' â€”adj. (flatter, flattest) 1 a horizontally level. B even; smooth; unbroken. C level and shallow (flat cap). 2 unqualified; downright (flat refusal). 3 a dull; lifeless; monotonous (in a flat tone). B dejected. 4 (of a fizzy drink) having lost its effervescence. 5 (of an accumulator, battery, etc.) Having exhausted its charge. 6 mus. A below true or normal pitch (violins are flat). B (of a key) having a flat or flats in the signature. C (as b, e, etc. Flat) semitone lower than b, e, etc. 7 (of a tyre) punctured; deflated. â€”adv. 1 at full length; spread out (lay flat; flat against the wall). 2 colloq. A completely, absolutely (flat broke). B exactly (in five minutes flat). 3 mus. Below the true or normal pitch (sings flat). â€”n. 1 flat part or thing (flat of the hand). 2 level ground, esp. A plain or swamp. 3 mus. A note lowered a semitone below natural pitch. B sign indicating this. 4 (as the flat) flat racing or its season. 5 theatr. Flat scenery on a frame. 6 esp. Us colloq. Flat tyre.  flat out 1 at top speed. 2 using all one''s strength etc. That''s flat colloq. That is definite.  flatly adv. Flatness n. Flattish adj. [old norse]'),
(15735, 'f', '\r\nFlat2', ' n. Set of rooms, usu. On one floor, as a residence.  flatlet n. [obsolete flet floor, dwelling, from germanic: related to *flat1]'),
(15736, 'f', '\r\nFlat-fish', ' n. Sole, plaice, etc. With both eyes on one side of a flattened body.'),
(15737, 'f', '\r\nFlat', 'foot  n. Foot with a flattened arch.'),
(15738, 'f', '\r\nFlat-footed', ' n. 1 having flat feet. 2 colloq. A uninspired. B unprepared. C resolute.'),
(15739, 'f', '\r\nFlat-iron', ' n. Hist. Domestic iron heated on a fire etc.'),
(15740, 'f', '\r\nFlatmate', ' n. Person sharing a flat.'),
(15741, 'f', '\r\nFlat', 'race  n. Horse race without jumps, over level ground.  flat racing n.'),
(15742, 'f', '\r\nFlat', 'rate  n. Unvarying rate or charge.'),
(15743, 'f', '\r\nFlat', 'spin  n. 1 aeron. A nearly horizontal spin. 2 colloq. State of panic.'),
(15744, 'f', '\r\nFlatten', ' v. 1 make or become flat. 2 colloq. A humiliate. B knock down.'),
(15745, 'f', '\r\nFlatter', ' v. 1 compliment unduly, esp. For gain or advantage. 2 (usu. Refl.; usu. Foll. By that) congratulate or delude (oneself etc.) (he flatters himself that he can sing). 3 (of colour, style, portrait, painter etc.) Enhance the appearance of (that blouse flatters you). 4 cause to feel honoured.  flatterer n. Flattering adj. Flatteringly adv. [french]'),
(15746, 'f', '\r\nFlattery', ' n. Exaggerated or insincere praise.'),
(15747, 'f', '\r\nFlatulent', ' adj. 1 a causing intestinal wind. B caused by or suffering from this. 2 (of speech etc.) Inflated, pretentious.  flatulence n. [latin flatus blowing]'),
(15748, 'f', '\r\nFlatworm', ' n. Worm with a flattened body, e.g. Flukes.'),
(15749, 'f', '\r\nFlaunt', ' v. (often refl.) Display proudly; show off; parade. [origin unknown]'),
(15750, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' flaunt is often confused with flout which means â€˜to disobey contemptuouslyâ€™.'),
(15751, 'f', '\r\nFlautist', ' n. Flute-player. [italian: related to *flute]'),
(15752, 'f', '\r\nFlavor', ' (brit. Flavour) â€”n. 1 mingled sensation of smell and taste (cheesy flavour). 2 characteristic quality (romantic flavour). 3 (usu. Foll. By of) slight admixture (flavour of failure). â€”v. Give flavour to; season.  flavourless adj. Flavoursome adj. [french]'),
(15753, 'f', '\r\nFlavoring', ' n. (brit. Flavouring) substance used to flavour food or drink.'),
(15754, 'f', '\r\nFlavour', ' (us flavor) â€”n. 1 mingled sensation of smell and taste (cheesy flavour). 2 characteristic quality (romantic flavour). 3 (usu. Foll. By of) slight admixture (flavour of failure). â€”v. Give flavour to; season.  flavourless adj. Flavoursome adj. [french]'),
(15755, 'f', '\r\nFlavouring', ' n. (us flavoring) substance used to flavour food or drink.'),
(15756, 'f', '\r\nFlavour', 'of the month  n. (also flavour of the week) temporary trend or fashion.'),
(15757, 'f', '\r\nFlaw1', ' â€”n. 1 imperfection; blemish. 2 crack, chip, etc. 3 invalidating defect. â€”v. Crack; damage; spoil.  flawless adj. Flawlessly adv. [old norse]'),
(15758, 'f', '\r\nFlaw2', ' n. Squall of wind. [low german or dutch]'),
(15759, 'f', '\r\nFlax', ' n. 1 blue-flowered plant cultivated for its textile fibre and its seeds. 2 flax fibres. [old english]'),
(15760, 'f', '\r\nFlaxen', ' adj. 1 of flax. 2 (of hair) pale yellow.'),
(15761, 'f', '\r\nFlax-seed', ' n. Linseed.'),
(15762, 'f', '\r\nFlay', ' v. 1 strip the skin or hide off, esp. By beating. 2 criticize severely. 3 peel off (skin, bark, peel, etc.). 4 extort money etc. From. [old english]'),
(15763, 'f', '\r\nFlea', ' n. Small wingless jumping parasitic insect.  a flea in one''s ear sharp reproof. [old english]'),
(15764, 'f', '\r\nFleabag', ' n. Slang shabby or unattractive person or thing.'),
(15765, 'f', '\r\nFlea-bite', ' n. 1 bite of a flea. 2 trivial injury or inconvenience.'),
(15766, 'f', '\r\nFlea-bitten', ' adj. 1 bitten by or infested with fleas. 2 shabby.'),
(15767, 'f', '\r\nFlea', 'market  n. Street market selling second-hand goods etc.'),
(15768, 'f', '\r\nFlea-pit', ' n. Dingy dirty cinema etc.'),
(15769, 'f', '\r\nFleck', ' â€”n. 1 small patch of colour or light. 2 particle, speck. â€”v. Mark with flecks. [old norse, or low german or dutch]'),
(15770, 'f', '\r\nFlection', ' n. (brit. Flexion) 1 bending or being bent, esp. Of a limb or joint. 2 bent part; curve. [latin flexio: related to *flex1]'),
(15771, 'f', '\r\nFled', ' past and past part. Of *flee.'),
(15772, 'f', '\r\nFledge', ' v. (-ging) 1 provide or deck (an arrow etc.) With feathers. 2 bring up (a young bird) until it can fly. 3 (as fledged adj.) A able to fly. B independent; mature. [obsolete adj. Fledge fit to fly]'),
(15773, 'f', '\r\nFledgling', ' n. (also fledgeling) 1 young bird. 2 inexperienced person.'),
(15774, 'f', '\r\nFlee', ' v. (past and past part. Fled) 1 (often foll. By from, before) a run away (from); leave abruptly (fled the room). B seek safety by fleeing. 2 vanish. [old english]'),
(15775, 'f', '\r\nFleece', ' â€”n. 1 a woolly coat of a sheep etc. B wool sheared from a sheep at one time. 2 thing resembling a fleece, esp. Soft fabric for lining etc. â€”v. (-cing) 1 (often foll. By of) strip of money, valuables, etc.; swindle. 2 shear (sheep etc.). 3 cover as if with a fleece (sky fleeced with clouds).  fleecy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(15776, 'f', '\r\nFleet', ' â€”n. 1 a warships under one commander-in-chief. B (prec. By the) nation''s warships etc.; navy. 2 number of vehicles in one company etc. â€”adj. Poet. Literary swift, nimble. [old english]'),
(15777, 'f', '\r\nFleeting', ' adj. Transitory; brief.  fleetingly adv.'),
(15778, 'f', '\r\nFleming', ' n. 1 native of medieval flanders. 2 member of a flemish-speaking people of n. And w. Belgium. [old english]'),
(15779, 'f', '\r\nFlemish', ' â€”adj. Of flanders. â€”n. Language of the flemings. [dutch]'),
(15780, 'f', '\r\nFlesh', ' n. 1 a soft, esp. Muscular, substance between the skin and bones of an animal or a human. B plumpness; fat. 2 the body, esp. As sinful. 3 pulpy substance of a fruit etc. 4 a visible surface of the human body. B (also flesh-colour) yellowish pink colour. 5 animal or human life.  all flesh all animate creation. Flesh out make or become substantial. In the flesh in person. One''s own flesh and blood near relatives. [old english]'),
(15781, 'f', '\r\nFlesh', 'and blood  â€”n. 1 the body or its substance. 2 humankind. 3 human nature, esp. As fallible. â€”adj. Real, not imaginary.'),
(15782, 'f', '\r\nFleshly', ' adj. (-lier, -liest) 1 bodily; sensual. 2 mortal. 3 worldly.'),
(15783, 'f', '\r\nFleshpots', ' n.pl. Luxurious living.'),
(15784, 'f', '\r\nFlesh-wound', ' n. Superficial wound.'),
(15785, 'f', '\r\nFleshy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) of flesh; plump, pulpy.  fleshiness n.'),
(15786, 'f', '\r\nFleur-de-lis', ' n. (also fleur-de-lys) (pl. Fleurs- pronunc. Same) 1 iris flower. 2 heraldry a lily of three petals. B former royal arms of france. [french, = flower of lily]'),
(15787, 'f', '\r\nFlew', ' past of *fly1.'),
(15788, 'f', '\r\nFlews', ' n.pl. Hanging lips of a bloodhound etc. [origin unknown]'),
(15789, 'f', '\r\nFlex1', ' v. 1 bend (a joint, limb, etc.) Or be bent. 2 move (a muscle) or (of a muscle) be moved to bend a joint. [latin flecto flex- bend]'),
(15790, 'f', '\r\nFlex2', ' n. Flexible insulated electric cable. [abbreviation of *flexible]'),
(15791, 'f', '\r\nFlexible', ' adj. 1 capable of bending without breaking; pliable. 2 manageable. 3 adaptable; variable (works flexible hours).  flexibility n. Flexibly adv. [latin flexibilis: related to *flex1]'),
(15792, 'f', '\r\nFlexion', ' n. (us flection) 1 bending or being bent, esp. Of a limb or joint. 2 bent part; curve. [latin flexio: related to *flex1]'),
(15793, 'f', '\r\nFlexitime', ' n. System of flexible working hours. [from *flexible]'),
(15794, 'f', '\r\nFlibbertigibbet', ' n. Gossiping, frivolous, or restless person. [imitative]'),
(15795, 'f', '\r\nFlick', ' â€”n. 1 a light sharp blow with a whip etc. B sudden release of a bent digit, esp. To propel a small object. 2 sudden movement or jerk, esp. Of the wrist in throwing etc. 3 colloq. A cinema film. B (in pl.; prec. By the) the cinema. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By away, off) strike or move with a flick (flicked the ash off). 2 give a flick with (a whip etc.).  flick through 1 turn over (cards, pages, etc.). 2 a turn over the pages etc. Of, by a rapid movement of the fingers. B glance through (a book etc.). [imitative]'),
(15796, 'f', '\r\nFlicker', ' â€”v. 1 (of light or flame) shine or burn unsteadily. 2 flutter. 3 (of hope etc.) Waver. â€”n. 1 flickering movement or light. 2 brief spell (of hope etc.).  flicker out die away. [old english]'),
(15797, 'f', '\r\nFlick-knife', ' n. Knife with a blade that springs out when a button is pressed.'),
(15798, 'f', '\r\nFlier', ' var. Of *flyer.'),
(15799, 'f', '\r\nFlight1', ' n. 1 a act or manner of flying. B movement or passage through the air. 2 a journey through the air or in space. B timetabled airline journey. 3 flock of birds, insects, etc. 4 (usu. Foll. By of) series, esp. Of stairs. 5 imaginative excursion or sally (flight of fancy). 6 (usu. Foll. By of) volley (flight of arrows). 7 tail of a dart. [old english: related to *fly1]'),
(15800, 'f', '\r\nFlight2', ' n. Fleeing, hasty retreat.  put to flight cause to flee. Take (or take to) flight flee. [old english]'),
(15801, 'f', '\r\nFlight', 'bag  n. Small zipped shoulder bag for air travel.'),
(15802, 'f', '\r\nFlight-deck', ' n. 1 deck of an aircraft-carrier. 2 control room of a large aircraft.'),
(15803, 'f', '\r\nFlightless', ' adj. (of a bird etc.) Unable to fly.'),
(15804, 'f', '\r\nFlight', 'lieutenant  n. Raf officer next below squadron leader.'),
(15805, 'f', '\r\nFlight', 'path  n. Planned course of an aircraft etc.'),
(15806, 'f', '\r\nFlight-recorder', ' n. Device in an aircraft recording technical details of a flight.'),
(15807, 'f', '\r\nFlight', 'sergeant  n. Raf rank next above sergeant.'),
(15808, 'f', '\r\nFlighty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (usu. Of a girl) frivolous, fickle, changeable.  flightiness n.'),
(15809, 'f', '\r\nFlimsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 insubstantial, rickety (flimsy structure). 2 (of an excuse etc.) Unconvincing. 3 (of clothing) thin.  flimsily adv. Flimsiness n. [origin uncertain]'),
(15810, 'f', '\r\nFlinch', ' v. Draw back in fear etc.; wince. [french from germanic]'),
(15811, 'f', '\r\nFling', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Flung) 1 throw or hurl forcefully or hurriedly. 2 (foll. By on, off) put on or take off (clothes) carelessly or rapidly. 3 put or send suddenly or violently (was flung into jail). 4 rush, esp. Angrily (flung out of the room). 5 (foll. By away) discard rashly. â€”n. 1 act of flinging; throw. 2 bout of wild behaviour. 3 whirling scottish dance, esp. The highland fling. [old norse]'),
(15812, 'f', '\r\nFlint', ' n. 1 a hard grey siliceous stone. B piece of this, esp. As a primitive tool or weapon. 2 piece of hard alloy used to give a spark. 3 anything hard and unyielding.  flinty adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(15813, 'f', '\r\nFlintlock', ' n. Hist. Old type of gun fired by a spark from a flint.'),
(15814, 'f', '\r\nFlip1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 flick or toss (a coin, pellet, etc.) So that it spins in the air. 2 turn (a small object) over; flick. 3 slang = flip one''s lid. â€”n. 1 act of flipping. 2 colloq. Short trip. â€”adj. Colloq. Glib; flippant.  flip one''s lid slang lose self-control; go mad. Flip through = flick through. [probably from *fillip]'),
(15815, 'f', '\r\nFlip2', ' n. 1 = *egg-flip. 2 drink of heated beer and spirit. [perhaps from *flip1]'),
(15816, 'f', '\r\nFlip', 'chart  n. Large pad of paper on a stand.'),
(15817, 'f', '\r\nFlip-flop', ' n. (usu. Rubber) sandal with a thong between the toes. [imitative]'),
(15818, 'f', '\r\nFlippant', ' adj. Frivolous; disrespectful; offhand.  flippancy n. Flippantly adv. [from *flip1]'),
(15819, 'f', '\r\nFlipper', ' n. 1 broad flat limb of a turtle, penguin, etc., used in swimming. 2 similar rubber foot attachment for underwater swimming. 3 slang hand.'),
(15820, 'f', '\r\nFlipping', ' adj. & adv. Slang expressing annoyance, or as an intensifier.'),
(15821, 'f', '\r\nFlip', 'side  n. Colloq. 1 reverse side of a gramophone record. 2 reverse or less important side of something.'),
(15822, 'f', '\r\nFlirt', ' â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By with) try to attract sexually but without serious intent. 2 (usu. Foll. By with) superficially engage in; trifle. â€”n. Person who flirts.  flirtation n. Flirtatious adj. Flirtatiously adv. Flirtatiousness n. [imitative]'),
(15823, 'f', '\r\nFlit', ' â€”v. (-tt-) 1 move lightly, softly, or rapidly. 2 make short flights. 3 colloq. Disappear secretly to escape creditors etc. â€”n. Act of flitting. [old norse: related to *fleet]'),
(15824, 'f', '\r\nFlitch', ' n. Side of bacon. [old english]'),
(15825, 'f', '\r\nFlitter', ' v. Flit about; flutter. [from *flit]'),
(15826, 'f', '\r\nFlitter-mouse', ' n. = *bat2.'),
(15827, 'f', '\r\nFloat', ' â€”v. 1 a (cause to) rest or move on the surface of a liquid. B set (a stranded ship) afloat. 2 colloq. A move in a leisurely way. B (often foll. By before) hover before the eye or mind. 3 (often foll. By in) move or be suspended freely in a liquid or gas. 4 a start or launch (a company, scheme, etc.). B offer (stock, shares, etc.) On the stock market. 5 commerce cause or allow to have a fluctuating exchange rate. 6 circulate or cause (a rumour or idea) to circulate. â€”n. 1 thing that floats, esp.: a a raft. B a light object as an indicator of a fish biting or supporting a fishing-net. C a hollow structure enabling an aircraft to float on water. D a floating device on water, petrol, etc., controlling the level. 2 small esp. Electrically-powered vehicle or cart (milk float). 3 decorated platform or tableau on a lorry in a procession etc. 4 a supply of loose change in a shop, at a fÃªte, etc. B petty cash. 5 theatr. (in sing. Or pl.) Footlights. 6 tool for smoothing plaster.  floatable adj. [old english]'),
(15828, 'f', '\r\nFloatation', ' var. Of *flotation.'),
(15829, 'f', '\r\nFloating', ' adj. Not settled; variable (floating population).'),
(15830, 'f', '\r\nFloating', 'dock  n. Floating structure usable as a dry dock.'),
(15831, 'f', '\r\nFloating', 'kidney  n. Abnormally movable kidney.'),
(15832, 'f', '\r\nFloating', 'rib  n. Lower rib not attached to the breastbone.'),
(15833, 'f', '\r\nFloating', 'voter  n. Voter without fixed allegiance.'),
(15834, 'f', '\r\nFloaty', ' adj. (esp. Of fabric) light and airy. [from *float]'),
(15835, 'f', '\r\nFlocculent', ' adj. Like or in tufts of wool etc.; downy.  flocculence n. [related to *flock2]'),
(15836, 'f', '\r\nFlock1', ' â€”n. 1 animals of one kind as a group or unit. 2 large crowd of people. 3 people in the care of a priest or teacher etc. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By to, in, out, together) congregate; mass; troop. [old english]'),
(15837, 'f', '\r\nFlock2', ' n. 1 lock or tuft of wool, cotton, etc. 2 (also in pl.; often attrib.) Wool-refuse etc. Used for quilting and stuffing. [latin floccus]'),
(15838, 'f', '\r\nFlock-paper', ' n. (also flock-wallpaper) wallpaper with a raised flock pattern.'),
(15839, 'f', '\r\nFloe', ' n. Sheet of floating ice. [norwegian]'),
(15840, 'f', '\r\nFlog', ' v. (-gg-) 1 a beat with a whip, stick, etc. B make work through violent effort (flogged the engine). 2 (often foll. By off) slang sell.  flog a dead horse waste one''s efforts. Flog to death colloq. Talk about or promote at tedious length. [origin unknown]'),
(15841, 'f', '\r\nFlood', ' â€”n. 1 a overflowing or influx of water, esp. Over land; inundation. B the water that overflows. 2 outpouring; torrent (flood of tears). 3 inflow of the tide (also in comb.: flood-tide).'),
(15842, 'f', '\r\nFloodgate', ' n. 1 gate for admitting or excluding water, esp. In a lock. 2 (usu. In pl.) Last restraint against tears, rain, anger, etc.'),
(15843, 'f', '\r\nFloodlight', ' â€”n. Large powerful light (usu. One of several) to illuminate a building, sports ground, etc. â€”v. Illuminate with floodlights.  floodlit adj.'),
(15844, 'f', '\r\nFlood-tide', ' n. Exceptionally high tide caused esp. By the moon.'),
(15845, 'f', '\r\nFloor', ' â€”n. 1 lower supporting surface of a room. 2 a bottom of the sea, a cave, etc. B any level area. 3 all the rooms etc. On one level of a building; storey. 4 a (in a legislative assembly) place where members sit and speak. B right to speak next in a debate (gave him the floor). 5 minimum of prices, wages, etc. 6 colloq. Ground. â€”v. 1 provide with a floor; pave. 2 knock or bring (a person) down. 3 colloq. Confound, baffle. 4 colloq. Overcome. 5 serve as the floor of (lino floored the hall).  from the floor (of a speech etc.) Given by a member of the audience. Take the floor 1 begin to dance. 2 speak in a debate. [old english]'),
(15846, 'f', '\r\nFloorboard', ' n. Long wooden board used for flooring.'),
(15847, 'f', '\r\nFloorcloth', ' n. Cloth for washing the floor.'),
(15848, 'f', '\r\nFlooring', ' n. Material of which a floor is made.'),
(15849, 'f', '\r\nFloor', 'manager  n. Stage-manager of a television production.'),
(15850, 'f', '\r\nFloor', 'plan  n. Diagram of the rooms etc. On one storey.'),
(15851, 'f', '\r\nFloor', 'show  n. Nightclub entertainment.'),
(15852, 'f', '\r\nFloozie', ' n. (also floozy) (pl. -ies) colloq. Esp. Disreputable girl or woman. [origin unknown]'),
(15853, 'f', '\r\nFlop', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 sway about heavily or loosely. 2 (often foll. By down, on, into) fall or sit etc. Awkwardly or suddenly. 3 slang fail; collapse (play flopped). 4 make a dull soft thud or splash. â€”n. 1 flopping movement or sound. 2 slang failure. â€”adv. With a flop. [var. Of *flap]'),
(15854, 'f', '\r\nFloppy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) tending to flop; flaccid. â€”n. (pl. -ies) (in full floppy disk) computing flexible disc for the storage of data.  floppiness n.'),
(15855, 'f', '\r\nFlora', ' n. (pl. -s or florae) 1 plant life of a region or period. 2 list or book of these. [latin flora, name of the goddess of flowers]'),
(15856, 'f', '\r\nFloral', ' adj. Of, decorated with, or depicting flowers.  florally adv. [latin]'),
(15857, 'f', '\r\nFlorentine', ' â€”adj. Of florence in italy. â€”n. Native or citizen of florence. [latin]'),
(15858, 'f', '\r\nFloret', ' n. 1 each of the small flowers making up a composite flower-head. 2 each stem of a head of cauliflower, broccoli, etc. 3 small flower. [latin flos *flower]'),
