(15860, 'f', '\r\nFlorid', ' adj. 1 ruddy (florid complexion). 2 elaborately ornate; showy.  floridly adv. Floridness n. [latin: related to *flower]'),
(15861, 'f', '\r\nFlorin', ' n. Hist. 1 british two-shilling coin now worth 10 pence. 2 english or foreign gold or silver coin. [italian fiorino: related to *florist]'),
(15862, 'f', '\r\nFlorist', ' n. Person who deals in or grows flowers. [latin flos *flower]'),
(15863, 'f', '\r\nFloruit', ' â€”v. Flourished; lived and worked (of a painter, writer, etc., whose exact dates are unknown). â€”n. Period or date of working etc. [latin, = he or she flourished]'),
(15864, 'f', '\r\nFloss', ' â€”n. 1 rough silk of a silkworm''s cocoon. 2 silk thread used in embroidery. 3 = *dental floss. â€”v. (also absol.) Clean (teeth) with dental floss.  flossy adj. [french floche]'),
(15865, 'f', '\r\nFlotation', ' n. (also floatation) launching or financing of a commercial enterprise etc. [from *float]'),
(15866, 'f', '\r\nFlotilla', ' n. 1 small fleet. 2 fleet of small ships. [spanish]'),
(15867, 'f', '\r\nFlotsam', ' n. Wreckage found floating. [anglo-french: related to *float]'),
(15868, 'f', '\r\nFlotsam', 'and jetsam  n. 1 odds and ends. 2 vagrants.'),
(15869, 'f', '\r\nFlounce1', ' â€”v. (-cing) (often foll. By away, about, off, out) go or move angrily or impatiently (flounced out in a huff). â€”n. Flouncing movement. [origin unknown]'),
(15870, 'f', '\r\nFlounce2', ' â€”n. Frill on a dress, skirt, etc. â€”v. (-cing) trim with flounces. [alteration of frounce pleat, from french]'),
(15871, 'f', '\r\nFlounder1', ' â€”v. 1 struggle helplessly as if wading in mud. 2 do a task clumsily. â€”n. Act of floundering. [imitative]'),
(15872, 'f', '\r\nFlounder2', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 edible european flat-fish. 2 n. American flat-fish. [anglo-french, probably scandinavian]'),
(15873, 'f', '\r\nFlour', ' â€”n. 1 meal or powder from ground wheat etc. 2 any fine powder. â€”v. Sprinkle with flour.  floury adj. (-ier, -iest). Flouriness n. [different spelling of *flower â€˜best partâ€™]'),
(15874, 'f', '\r\nFlourish', ' â€”v. 1 a grow vigorously; thrive. B prosper. C be in one''s prime. 2 wave, brandish. â€”n. 1 showy gesture. 2 ornamental curve in handwriting. 3 mus. Ornate passage or fanfare. [latin floreo from flos *flower]'),
(15875, 'f', '\r\nFlout', ' â€”v. Disobey (the law etc.) Contemptuously; mock; insult. â€”n. Flouting speech or act. [dutch fluiten whistle: related to *flute]'),
(15876, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' flout is often confused with flaunt which means â€˜to display proudly, show offâ€™.'),
(15877, 'f', '\r\nFlow', ' â€”v. 1 glide along as a stream. 2 (of liquid, blood, etc.) Gush out; be spilt. 3 (of blood, money, electric current, etc.) Circulate. 4 move smoothly or steadily. 5 (of a garment, hair, etc.) Hang gracefully. 6 (often foll. By from) be caused by. 7 (esp. Of the tide) be in flood. 8 (of wine) be plentiful. 9 (foll. By with) archaic be plentifully supplied with (flowing with milk and honey). â€”n. 1 a flowing movement or mass. B flowing liquid (stop the flow). C outpouring; stream (flow of complaints). 2 rise of a tide or river (ebb and flow). [old english]'),
(15878, 'f', '\r\nFlow', 'chart  n. (also flow diagram or flow sheet) diagram of the movement or action in a complex activity.'),
(15879, 'f', '\r\nFlower', ' â€”n. 1 part of a plant from which the fruit or seed is developed. 2 blossom, esp. Used for decoration. 3 plant cultivated for its flowers. â€”v. 1 bloom or cause (a plant) to bloom; blossom. 2 reach a peak.  the flower of the best of. In flower blooming.  flowered adj. [latin flos flor-]'),
(15880, 'f', '\r\nFlower-bed', ' n. Garden bed for flowers.'),
(15881, 'f', '\r\nFlower-head', ' n. = *head n. 3 c.'),
(15882, 'f', '\r\nFlower', 'people  n. Hippies with flowers as symbols of peace and love.'),
(15883, 'f', '\r\nFlowerpot', ' n. Pot for growing a plant in.'),
(15884, 'f', '\r\nFlower', 'power  n. Peace and love, esp. As a political idea.'),
(15885, 'f', '\r\nFlowers', 'of sulphur  n. Fine powder produced when sulphur evaporates and condenses.'),
(15886, 'f', '\r\nFlowery', ' adj. 1 florally decorated. 2 (of style, speech, etc.) High-flown; ornate. 3 full of flowers.  floweriness n.'),
(15887, 'f', '\r\nFlowing', ' adj. 1 (of style etc.) Fluent; easy. 2 (of a line, curve, etc.) Smoothly continuous. 3 (of hair etc.) Unconfined.  flowingly adv.'),
(15888, 'f', '\r\nFlown', ' past part. Of *fly1.'),
(15889, 'f', '\r\nFlu', ' n. Colloq. Influenza. [abbreviation]'),
(15890, 'f', '\r\nFluctuate', ' v. (-ting) vary irregularly; rise and fall.  fluctuation n. [latin fluctus wave]'),
(15891, 'f', '\r\nFlue', ' n. 1 smoke-duct in a chimney. 2 channel for conveying heat. [origin unknown]'),
(15892, 'f', '\r\nFluent', ' adj. 1 (of speech, style, etc.) Flowing, natural. 2 verbally facile, esp. In a foreign language (fluent in german).  fluency n. Fluently adv. [latin fluo flow]'),
(15893, 'f', '\r\nFluff', ' â€”n. 1 soft fur, feathers, or fabric particles etc. 2 slang mistake in a performance etc. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) shake into or become a soft mass. 2 colloq. Make a fluff; bungle.  bit of fluff slang offens. Attractive woman.  fluffy adj. (-ier, -iest). Fluffiness n. [probably dial. Alteration of flue fluff]'),
(15894, 'f', '\r\nFlugelhorn', ' n. Valved brass wind instrument like a cornet. [german flÃ¼gel wing, horn horn]'),
(15895, 'f', '\r\nFluid', ' â€”n. 1 substance, esp. A gas or liquid, whose shape is determined by its confines. 2 fluid part or secretion. â€”adj. 1 able to flow and alter shape freely. 2 constantly changing (situation is fluid).'),
(15896, 'f', '\r\nFluid', 'ounce  n. One-twentieth, or us one-sixteenth, of a pint.'),
(15897, 'f', '\r\nFluke1', ' â€”n. Lucky accident (won by a fluke). â€”v. (-king) achieve by a fluke.  fluky adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin uncertain]'),
(15898, 'f', '\r\nFluke2', ' n. 1 parasitic flatworm, e.g. The liver fluke. 2 flat-fish, esp. A flounder. [old english]'),
(15899, 'f', '\r\nFluke3', ' n. 1 broad triangular plate on an anchor arm. 2 lobe of a whale''s tail. [perhaps from *fluke2]'),
(15900, 'f', '\r\nFlummery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 flattery; nonsense. 2 sweet dish made with beaten eggs, sugar, etc. [welsh llymru]'),
(15901, 'f', '\r\nFlummox', ' v. Colloq. Bewilder, disconcert. [origin unknown]'),
(15902, 'f', '\r\nFlung', ' past and past part. Of *fling.'),
(15903, 'f', '\r\nFlunk', ' v. Us colloq. Fail (esp. An exam). [origin unknown]'),
(15904, 'f', '\r\nFlunkey', ' n. (also flunky) (pl. -eys or -ies) usu. Derog. 1 liveried footman. 2 toady; snob. 3 us cook, waiter, etc. [origin uncertain]'),
(15905, 'f', '\r\nFluoresce', ' v. (-scing) be or become fluorescent. [from *fluorescent]'),
(15906, 'f', '\r\nFluorescence', ' n. 1 light radiation from certain substances. 2 property of absorbing invisible light and emitting visible light. [from *fluorspar, after opalescence]'),
(15907, 'f', '\r\nFluorescent', ' adj. Of, having, or showing fluorescence.'),
(15908, 'f', '\r\nFluorescent', 'lamp  n. (also fluorescent bulb) esp. Tubular lamp or bulb radiating largely by fluorescence.'),
(15909, 'f', '\r\nFluoridate', ' v. (-ting) add fluoride to (drinking-water etc.), esp. To prevent tooth decay.  fluoridation n.'),
(15910, 'f', '\r\nFluoride', ' n. Binary compound of fluorine.'),
(15911, 'f', '\r\nFluorinate', ' v. (-ting) 1 = *fluoridate. 2 introduce fluorine into (a compound).  fluorination n.'),
(15912, 'f', '\r\nFluorine', ' n. Poisonous pale-yellow gaseous element. [french: related to *fluorspar]'),
(15913, 'f', '\r\nFluorite', ' n. Mineral form of calcium fluoride. [italian: related to *fluorspar]'),
(15914, 'f', '\r\nFluorocarbon', ' n. Compound of a hydrocarbon with fluorine atoms.'),
(15915, 'f', '\r\nFluorspar', ' n. = *fluorite. [fluor a mineral used as flux, from latin fluo flow]'),
(15916, 'f', '\r\nFlurry', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 gust or squall (of snow, rain, etc.). 2 sudden burst of activity, excitement, etc.; commotion. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) confuse; agitate. [imitative]'),
(15917, 'f', '\r\nFlush1', ' â€”v. 1 blush, redden, glow warmly (he flushed with embarrassment). 2 (usu. As flushed adj.) Cause to glow or blush (often foll. By with: he was flushed with pride). 3 a cleanse (a drain, lavatory, etc.) By a flow of water. B (often foll. By away, down) dispose of in this way. 4 rush out, spurt. â€”n. 1 blush or glow. 2 a rush of water. B cleansing of a drain, lavatory, etc. Thus. 3 rush of esp. Elation or triumph. 4 freshness; vigour. 5 a (also hot flush) sudden feeling of heat during menopause. B feverish redness or temperature etc. â€”adj. 1 level, in the same plane. 2 colloq. Having plenty of money. [perhaps = *flush3]'),
(15918, 'f', '\r\nFlush2', ' n. Hand of cards all of one suit, esp. In poker. [latin fluxus *flux]'),
(15919, 'f', '\r\nFlush3', ' v. 1 cause (esp. A game-bird) to fly up. 2 (of a bird) fly up and away.  flush out 1 reveal. 2 drive out. [imitative]'),
(15920, 'f', '\r\nFluster', ' â€”v. 1 make or become nervous or confused (he flusters easily). 2 bustle. â€”n. Confused or agitated state. [origin unknown]'),
(15921, 'f', '\r\nFlute', ' â€”n. 1 a high-pitched woodwind instrument held sideways. B any similar wind instrument. 2 ornamental vertical groove in a column. â€”v. (-ting) 1 play, or play (a tune etc.) On, the flute. 2 speak or sing etc. In a high voice. 3 make grooves in.  fluting n. Fluty adj. (in sense 1a of n.). [french]'),
(15922, 'f', '\r\nFlutter', ' â€”v. 1 flap (the wings) in flying or trying to fly. 2 fall quiveringly (fluttered to the ground). 3 wave or flap quickly. 4 move about restlessly. 5 (of a pulse etc.) Beat feebly or irregularly. â€”n. 1 act of fluttering. 2 tremulous excitement (caused a flutter). 3 slang small bet, esp. On a horse. 4 abnormally rapid heartbeat. 5 rapid variation of pitch, esp. Of recorded sound. [old english]'),
(15923, 'f', '\r\nFluvial', ' adj. Of or found in rivers. [latin fluvius river]'),
(15924, 'f', '\r\nFlux', ' n. 1 process of flowing or flowing out. 2 discharge. 3 continuous change (state of flux). 4 substance mixed with a metal etc. To aid fusion. [latin fluxus from fluo flux- flow]'),
(15925, 'f', '\r\nFly1', ' â€”v. (flies; past flew; past part. Flown) 1 a (of an aircraft, bird, etc.) Move through the air or space under control, esp. With wings. B travel through the air or space. 2 control the flight of or transport in (esp. An aircraft). 3 a cause to fly or remain aloft. B (of a flag, hair, etc.) Wave or flutter. 4 pass, move, or rise quickly. 5 a flee; flee from. B colloq. Depart hastily. 6 be driven, forced, or scattered (sent me flying). 7 (foll. By at, upon) a hasten or spring violently. B attack or criticize fiercely. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 (usu. In pl.) A concealing flap, esp. Over a trouser-fastening. B this fastening. 2 flap at a tent entrance. 3 (in pl.) Space above a stage where scenery and lighting are suspended. 4 act of flying.  fly high be ambitious; prosper. Fly in the face of disregard or disobey. Fly a kite test opinion. Fly off the handle colloq. Lose one''s temper. [old english]'),
(15926, 'f', '\r\nFly2', ' n. (pl. Flies) 1 insect with two usu. Transparent wings. 2 other winged insect, e.g. A firefly. 3 disease of plants or animals caused by flies. 4 (esp. Artificial) fly as bait in fishing.  like flies in large numbers (usu. Of people dying etc.). No flies on (him etc.) Colloq. (he is) very astute. [old english]'),
(15927, 'f', '\r\nFly3', ' adj. Slang knowing, clever, alert. [origin unknown]'),
(15928, 'f', '\r\nFly-away', ' adj. (of hair) fine and difficult to control.'),
(15929, 'f', '\r\nFly-blown', ' adj. Tainted, esp. By flies.'),
(15930, 'f', '\r\nFly-by-night', ' â€”adj. Unreliable. â€”n. Unreliable person.'),
(15931, 'f', '\r\nFlycatcher', ' n. Bird catching insects during short flights from a chosen perch.'),
(15932, 'f', '\r\nFlyer', ' n. (also flier) colloq. 1 airman or airwoman. 2 thing that flies in a specified way (poor flyer). 3 fast-moving animal or vehicle. 4 ambitious or outstanding person. 5 small handbill.'),
(15933, 'f', '\r\nFly-fish', ' v. Fish with a fly.'),
(15934, 'f', '\r\nFly-half', ' n. Rugby stand-off half.'),
(15935, 'f', '\r\nFlying', ' â€”adj. 1 fluttering, waving, or hanging loose. 2 hasty, brief (flying visit). 3 designed for rapid movement. 4 (of an animal) leaping with winglike membranes etc. â€”n. Flight, esp. In an aircraft.  with flying colours with distinction.'),
(15936, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'boat  n. Boatlike seaplane.'),
(15937, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'buttress  n. (usu. Arched) buttress running from the upper part of a wall to an outer support and transmitting the thrust of the roof or vault.'),
(15938, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'doctor  n. Doctor who uses an aircraft to visit patients.'),
(15939, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'fish  n. Tropical fish with winglike fins for gliding through the air.'),
(15940, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'fox  n. Fruit-eating bat with a foxlike head.'),
(15941, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'officer  n. Raf rank next below flight lieutenant.'),
(15942, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'picket  n. Mobile industrial strike picket.'),
(15943, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'saucer  n. Supposed alien spaceship.'),
(15944, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'squad  n. Rapidly mobile police detachment etc.'),
(15945, 'f', '\r\nFlying', 'start  n. 1 start (of a race etc.) In which the starting-point is crossed at full speed. 2 vigorous start (of an enterprise etc.).'),
(15946, 'f', '\r\nFly', 'in the ointment  n. Minor irritation or setback.'),
(15947, 'f', '\r\nFlyleaf', ' n. Blank leaf at the beginning or end of a book.'),
(15948, 'f', '\r\nFly', 'on the wall  n. Unnoticed observer.'),
(15949, 'f', '\r\nFlyover', ' n. Bridge carrying one road or railway over another.'),
(15950, 'f', '\r\nFly-paper', ' n. Sticky treated paper for catching flies.'),
(15951, 'f', '\r\nFly-past', ' n. Ceremonial flight of aircraft.'),
(15952, 'f', '\r\nFly-post', ' v. Fix (posters etc.) Illegally on walls etc.'),
(15953, 'f', '\r\nFlysheet', ' n. 1 canvas cover over a tent for extra protection. 2 short tract or circular.'),
(15954, 'f', '\r\nFly-tip', ' v. Illegally dump (waste).  fly-tipper n.'),
(15955, 'f', '\r\nFly-trap', ' n. Plant that catches flies.'),
(15956, 'f', '\r\nFlyweight', ' n. 1 weight in certain sports between light flyweight and bantamweight, in amateur boxing 48â€“51 kg. 2 sportsman of this weight.'),
(15957, 'f', '\r\nFlywheel', ' n. Heavy wheel on a revolving shaft to regulate machinery or accumulate power.'),
(15958, 'f', '\r\nFm', ' abbr. 1 field marshal. 2 frequency modulation.'),
(15959, 'f', '\r\nFm', ' symb. Fermium.'),
(15960, 'f', '\r\nF-number', ' n. Ratio of the focal length to the effective diameter of a camera lens. [from focal]'),
(15961, 'f', '\r\nFo', ' abbr. Flying officer.'),
(15962, 'f', '\r\nFoal', ' â€”n. Young of a horse or related animal. â€”v. Give birth to (a foal).  in (or with) foal (of a mare etc.) Pregnant. [old english]'),
(15963, 'f', '\r\nFoam', ' â€”n. 1 mass of small bubbles formed on or in liquid by agitation, fermentation, etc. 2 froth of saliva or sweat. 3 substance resembling these, e.g. Spongy rubber or plastic. â€”v. Emit or run with foam; froth.  foam at the mouth be very angry.  foamy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(15964, 'f', '\r\nFob1', ' n. 1 chain of a pocket-watch. 2 small pocket for a watch etc. 3 tab on a key-ring. [german]'),
(15965, 'f', '\r\nFob2', ' v. (-bb-)  fob off 1 (often foll. By with a thing) deceive into accepting something inferior. 2 (often foll. By on or on to a person) offload (an unwanted thing). [cf. Obsolete fop dupe]'),
(15966, 'f', '\r\nFocal', ' adj. Of or at a focus. [latin: related to *focus]'),
(15967, 'f', '\r\nFocal', 'distance  n. (also focal length) distance between the centre of a mirror or lens and its focus.'),
(15968, 'f', '\r\nFocal', 'point  n. 1 = *focus n. 1. 2 centre of interest or activity.'),
(15969, 'f', '\r\nFo''c''s''le', ' var. Of *forecastle.'),
(15970, 'f', '\r\nFocus', ' â€”n. (pl. Focuses or foci) 1 a point at which rays or waves meet after reflection or refraction. B point from which rays etc. Appear to proceed. 2 a point at which an object must be situated for a lens or mirror to give a well-defined image. B adjustment of the eye or a lens to give a clear image. C state of clear definition (out of focus). 3 = *focal point 2. â€”v. (-s- or -ss-) 1 bring into focus. 2 adjust the focus of (a lens or eye). 3 concentrate or be concentrated on. 4 converge or make converge to a focus. [latin, = hearth]'),
(15971, 'f', '\r\nFodder', ' n. Dried hay or straw etc. As animal food. [old english]'),
(15972, 'f', '\r\nFoe', ' abbr. Friends of the earth.'),
(15973, 'f', '\r\nFoe', ' n. Esp. Poet. Enemy. [old english]'),
(15974, 'f', '\r\nFoetid', ' var. Of *fetid.'),
(15975, 'f', '\r\nFoetus', ' n. (us fetus) (pl. -tuses) unborn mammalian offspring, esp. A human embryo of eight weeks or more.  foetal adj. [latin fetus offspring]'),
(15976, 'f', '\r\nFog', ' â€”n. 1 thick cloud of water droplets or smoke suspended at or near the earth''s surface. 2 cloudiness on a photographic negative etc. 3 uncertain or confused position or state. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 cover or become covered with or as with fog. 2 perplex. [perhaps a back-formation from *foggy]'),
(15977, 'f', '\r\nFog-bank', ' n. Mass of fog at sea.'),
(15978, 'f', '\r\nFog-bound', ' adj. Unable to travel because of fog.'),
(15979, 'f', '\r\nFogey', ' var. Of *fogy.'),
(15980, 'f', '\r\nFoggy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of fog. 2 of or like fog. 3 vague, indistinct.  not have the foggiest colloq. Have no idea at all.  fogginess n. [perhaps from fog long grass]'),
(15981, 'f', '\r\nFoghorn', ' n. 1 horn warning ships in fog. 2 colloq. Loud penetrating voice.'),
(15982, 'f', '\r\nFog-lamp', ' n. Powerful lamp for use in fog.'),
(15983, 'f', '\r\nFogy', ' n. (also fogey) (pl. -ies or -eys) dull old-fashioned person (esp. Old fogy). [origin unknown]'),
(15984, 'f', '\r\nFoible', ' n. Minor weakness or idiosyncrasy. [french: related to *feeble]'),
(15985, 'f', '\r\nFoil1', ' v. Frustrate, baffle, defeat. [perhaps from french fouler trample]'),
(15986, 'f', '\r\nFoil2', ' n. 1 metal rolled into a very thin sheet. 2 person or thing setting off another to advantage. [latin folium leaf]'),
(15987, 'f', '\r\nFoil3', ' n. Light blunt fencing sword. [origin unknown]'),
(15988, 'f', '\r\nFoist', ' v. (foll. By on) force (a thing or oneself) on to an unwilling person. [dutch vuisten take in the hand]'),
(15989, 'f', '\r\nFold1', ' â€”v. 1 a bend or close (a flexible thing) over upon itself. B (foll. By back, over, down) bend part of (a thing) (fold down the flap). 2 become or be able to be folded. 3 (foll. By away, up) make compact by folding. 4 (often foll. By up) colloq. Collapse, cease to function. 5 enfold (esp. Fold in the arms or to the breast). 6 (foll. By about, round) clasp (the arms). 7 (foll. By in) mix (an ingredient with others) gently. â€”n. 1 folding. 2 line made by folding. 3 folded part. 4 hollow among hills. 5 curvature of geological strata.'),
(15990, 'f', '\r\nFold2', ' â€”n. 1 = *sheepfold. 2 religious group or congregation. â€”v. Enclose (sheep) in a fold. [old english]'),
(15991, 'f', '\r\n-fold', ' suffix forming adjectives and adverbs from cardinal numbers, meaning: 1 in an amount multiplied by (repaid tenfold). 2 with so many parts (threefold blessing). [originally = â€˜folded in so many layersâ€™]'),
(15992, 'f', '\r\nFolder', ' n. Folding cover or holder for loose papers.'),
(15993, 'f', '\r\nFoliaceous', ' adj. 1 of or like leaves. 2 laminated. [latin: related to *foil2]'),
(15994, 'f', '\r\nFoliage', ' n. Leaves, leafage. [french feuillage from feuille leaf]'),
(15995, 'f', '\r\nFoliar', ' adj. Of leaves. [as *foliate]'),
(15996, 'f', '\r\nFoliar', 'feed  n. Fertilizer supplied to the leaves of plants.'),
(15997, 'f', '\r\nFoliate', ' â€”adj. 1 leaflike. 2 having leaves. â€”v. (-ting) split or beat into thin layers.  foliation n. [latin folium leaf]'),
(15998, 'f', '\r\nFolio', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 leaf of paper etc., esp. Numbered only on the front. 2 sheet of paper folded once making two leaves of a book. 3 book of such sheets. â€”adj. (of a book) made of folios, of the largest size.  in folio made of folios. [latin, ablative of folium leaf]'),
(15999, 'f', '\r\nFolk', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 (treated as pl.) People in general or of a specified class (few folk about; townsfolk). 2 (in pl.) (usu. Folks) one''s parents or relatives. 3 (treated as sing.) A people or nation. 4 (in full folk-music) (treated as sing.) Colloq. Traditional music or modern music in this style. 5 (attrib.) Of popular origin (folk art). [old english]'),
(16000, 'f', '\r\nFolk-dance', ' n. Dance of popular origin.'),
(16001, 'f', '\r\nFolklore', ' n. Traditional beliefs and stories of a people; the study of these.'),
(16002, 'f', '\r\nFolk-singer', ' n. Singer of folk-songs.'),
(16003, 'f', '\r\nFolk-song', ' n. Song of popular or traditional origin or style.'),
(16004, 'f', '\r\nFolksy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like folk art, culture, etc. 2 friendly, unpretentious.  folksiness n.'),
(16005, 'f', '\r\nFolk-tale', ' n. Traditional story.'),
(16006, 'f', '\r\nFolkweave', ' n. Rough loosely woven fabric.'),
(16007, 'f', '\r\nFollicle', ' n. Small sac or vesicle in the body, esp. One containing a hair-root.  follicular adj. [latin diminutive of follis bellows]'),
(16008, 'f', '\r\nFollow', ' v. 1 (often foll. By after) go or come after (a person or thing ahead). 2 go along (a road etc.). 3 come after in order or time (dessert followed; proceed as follows). 4 take as a guide or leader. 5 conform to. 6 practise (a trade or profession). 7 undertake (a course of study etc.). 8 understand (a speaker, argument, etc.). 9 take an interest in (current affairs etc.). 10 (foll. By with) provide with a sequel or successor. 11 happen after something else; ensue. 12 a be necessarily true as a consequence. B (foll. By from) result.  follow on 1 continue. 2 (of a cricket team) have to bat twice in succession. Follow out carry out (instructions etc.). Follow suit 1 play a card of the suit led. 2 conform to another''s actions. Follow through 1 continue to a conclusion. 2 continue the movement of a stroke after hitting the ball. Follow up (foll. By with) 1 develop, supplement. 2 investigate further. [old english]'),
(16009, 'f', '\r\nFollower', ' n. 1 supporter or devotee. 2 person who follows.'),
(16010, 'f', '\r\nFollowing', ' â€”prep. After in time; as a sequel to. â€”n. Supporters or devotees. â€”adj. That follows or comes after.  the following 1 what follows. 2 now to be given or named (answer the following).'),
(16011, 'f', '\r\nFollow-on', ' n. Cricket instance of following on.'),
(16012, 'f', '\r\nFollow-through', ' n. Action of following through.'),
(16013, 'f', '\r\nFollow-up', ' n. Subsequent or continued action.'),
(16014, 'f', '\r\nFolly', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 foolishness. 2 foolish act, behaviour, idea, etc. 3 fanciful ornamental building created for display. [french folie from fol mad, *fool1]'),
(16015, 'f', '\r\nFoment', ' v. Instigate or stir up (trouble, discontent, etc.).  fomentation n. [latin foveo heat, cherish]'),
(16016, 'f', '\r\nFond', ' adj. 1 (foll. By of) liking. 2 a affectionate. B doting. 3 (of beliefs etc.) Foolishly optimistic or credulous.  fondly adv. Fondness n. [obsolete fon fool, be foolish]'),
(16017, 'f', '\r\nFondant', ' n. Soft sugary sweet. [french = melting: related to *fuse1]'),
(16018, 'f', '\r\nFondle', ' v. (-ling) caress. [related to *fond]'),
(16019, 'f', '\r\nFondue', ' n. Dish of melted cheese. [french, = melted: related to *fuse1]'),
(16020, 'f', '\r\nFont1', ' n. Receptacle in a church for baptismal water. [latin fons font- fountain]'),
(16021, 'f', '\r\nFont2', ' var. Of *fount2.'),
(16022, 'f', '\r\nFontanel', ' n. (brit. Fontanelle) membranous space in an infant''s skull at the angles of the parietal bones. [latin fontanella little *fountain]'),
(16023, 'f', '\r\nFontanelle', ' n. (us fontanel) membranous space in an infant''s skull at the angles of the parietal bones. [latin fontanella little *fountain]'),
(16024, 'f', '\r\nFood', ' n. 1 a substance taken in to maintain life and growth. B solid food (food and drink). 2 mental stimulus (food for thought). [old english]'),
(16025, 'f', '\r\nFood', 'additive  n. Substance added to food to colour or flavour it etc.'),
(16026, 'f', '\r\nFood-chain', ' n. Series of organisms each dependent on the next for food.'),
(16027, 'f', '\r\nFoodie', ' n. Colloq. Person who makes a cult of food; gourmet.'),
(16028, 'f', '\r\nFood', 'poisoning  n. Illness due to bacteria etc. In food.'),
(16029, 'f', '\r\nFood', 'processor  n. Machine for chopping and mixing food.'),
(16030, 'f', '\r\nFoodstuff', ' n. Substance used as food.'),
(16031, 'f', '\r\nFood', 'value  n. Nourishing power of a food.'),
(16032, 'f', '\r\nFool1', ' â€”n. 1 rash, unwise, or stupid person. 2 hist. Jester; clown. 3 dupe. â€”v. 1 deceive. 2 (foll. By into or out of) trick; cheat. 3 joke or tease. 4 (foll. By about, around) play or trifle.  act (or play) the fool behave in a silly way. Be no (or nobody''s) fool be shrewd or prudent. Make a fool of make (a person or oneself) look foolish; trick, deceive. [latin follis bellows]'),
(16033, 'f', '\r\nFool2', ' n. Dessert of fruit purÃ©e with cream or custard. [perhaps from *fool1]'),
(16034, 'f', '\r\nFoolery', ' n. Foolish behaviour.'),
(16035, 'f', '\r\nFoolhardy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) rashly or foolishly bold; reckless.  foolhardily adv. Foolhardiness n.'),
(16036, 'f', '\r\nFoolish', ' adj. Lacking good sense or judgement; unwise.  foolishly adv. Foolishness n.'),
(16037, 'f', '\r\nFoolproof', ' adj. (of a procedure, mechanism, etc.) Incapable of misuse or mistake.'),
(16038, 'f', '\r\nFoolscap', ' n. Large size of paper, about 330 x 200 (or 400) mm. [from a watermark of a fool''s cap]'),
(16039, 'f', '\r\nFool''s', 'paradise  n. Illusory happiness.'),
(16040, 'f', '\r\nFoot', ' â€”n. (pl. Feet) 1 a part of the leg below the ankle. B part of a sock etc. Covering this. 2 a lowest part of a page, stairs, etc. B end of a bed where the feet rest. C part of a chair, appliance, etc. On which it rests. 3 step, pace, or tread (fleet of foot). 4 (pl. Feet or foot) linear measure of 12 inches (30.48 cm). 5 metrical unit of verse forming part of a line. 6 hist. Infantry. â€”v. 1 pay (a bill). 2 (usu. As foot it) go or traverse on foot.  feet of clay fundamental weakness in a respected person. Have one''s (or both) feet on the ground be practical. Have one foot in the grave be near death or very old. My foot! Int. Expressing strong contradiction. On foot walking. Put one''s feet up colloq. Take a rest. Put one''s foot down colloq. 1 insist firmly. 2 accelerate a vehicle. Put one''s foot in it colloq. Make a tactless blunder. Under one''s feet in the way. Under foot on the ground.  footless adj. [old english]'),
(16041, 'f', '\r\nFootage', ' n. 1 a length of tv or cinema film etc. 2 length in feet.'),
(16042, 'f', '\r\nFoot-and-mouth', 'disease  n. Contagious viral disease of cattle etc.'),
(16043, 'f', '\r\nFootball', ' n. 1 large inflated ball of leather or plastic. 2 outdoor team game played with this.  footballer n.'),
(16044, 'f', '\r\nFootball', 'pool  n. (also football pools pl.) Large-scale organized gambling on the results of football matches.'),
(16045, 'f', '\r\nFootbrake', ' n. Foot-operated brake on a vehicle.'),
(16046, 'f', '\r\nFootbridge', ' n. Bridge for pedestrians.'),
(16047, 'f', '\r\nFootfall', ' n. Sound of a footstep.'),
(16048, 'f', '\r\nFoot-fault', ' n. (in tennis) placing of the foot over the baseline while serving.'),
(16049, 'f', '\r\nFoothill', ' n. Any of the low hills at the base of a mountain or range.'),
(16050, 'f', '\r\nFoothold', ' n. 1 secure place for a foot when climbing etc. 2 secure initial position.'),
(16051, 'f', '\r\nFooting', ' n. 1 foothold; secure position (lost his footing). 2 operational basis. 3 relative position or status (on an equal footing). 4 (often in pl.) Foundations of a wall.'),
(16052, 'f', '\r\nFootle', ' v. (-ling) (usu. Foll. By about) colloq. Potter or fiddle about. [origin uncertain]'),
(16053, 'f', '\r\nFootlights', ' n.pl. Row of floor-level lights at the front of a stage.'),
(16054, 'f', '\r\nFootling', ' adj. Colloq. Trivial, silly.'),
(16055, 'f', '\r\nFootloose', ' adj. Free to act as one pleases.'),
(16056, 'f', '\r\nFootman', ' n. Liveried servant.'),
(16057, 'f', '\r\nFootmark', ' n. Footprint.'),
(16058, 'f', '\r\nFootnote', ' n. Note printed at the foot of a page.'),
(16059, 'f', '\r\nFootpad', ' n. Hist. Unmounted highwayman.'),
(16060, 'f', '\r\nFootpath', ' n. Path for pedestrians; pavement.'),
(16061, 'f', '\r\nFootplate', ' n. Platform for the crew in a locomotive.'),
(16062, 'f', '\r\nFootprint', ' n. Impression left by a foot or shoe.'),
(16063, 'f', '\r\nFootrest', ' n. Stool, rail, etc. For the feet.'),
(16064, 'f', '\r\nFootsie', ' n. = *ft-se. [respelling of *ft-se]'),
(16065, 'f', '\r\nFootsie', ' n. Colloq. Amorous play with the feet.'),
(16066, 'f', '\r\nFootsore', ' adj. With sore feet, esp. From walking.'),
(16067, 'f', '\r\nFootstep', ' n. 1 step taken in walking. 2 sound of this.  follow in a person''s footsteps do as another did before.'),
(16068, 'f', '\r\nFootstool', ' n. Stool for resting the feet on when sitting.'),
(16069, 'f', '\r\nFootway', ' n. Path for pedestrians.'),
(16070, 'f', '\r\nFootwear', ' n. Shoes, socks, etc.'),
(16071, 'f', '\r\nFootwork', ' n. Use or agility of the feet in sports, dancing, etc.'),
(16072, 'f', '\r\nFop', ' n. Dandy.  foppery n. Foppish adj. [perhaps from obsolete fop fool]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(16073, 'f', '\r\nFor', ' â€”prep. 1 in the interest or to the benefit of; intended to go to (did it all for my country; these flowers are for you). 2 in defence, support, or favour of. 3 suitable or appropriate to (a dance for beginners; not for me to say). 4 in respect of or with reference to; regarding (usual for ties to be worn; ready for bed). 5 representing or in place of (mp for lincoln; here for my uncle). 6 in exchange with, at the price of, corresponding to (swapped it for a cake; give me Â£5 for it; bought it for Â£5; word for word). 7 as a consequence of (fined for speeding; decorated for bravery; here''s Â£5 for your trouble). 8 a with a view to; in the hope or quest of; in order to get (go for a walk; send for a doctor; did it for the money). B on account of (could not speak for laughing). 9 to reach; towards (left for rome). 10 so as to start promptly at (meet at seven for eight). 11 through or over (a distance or period); during (walked for miles). 12 as being (for the last time; i for one refuse). 13 in spite of; notwithstanding (for all your fine words). 14 considering or making due allowance in respect of (good for a beginner). â€”conj. Because, since, seeing that.  be for it colloq. Be about to be punished etc. For all (that) in spite of, although. For ever for all time (cf. *forever). [old english reduced form of *fore]'),
(16074, 'f', '\r\nFor-', ' prefix forming verbs etc. Meaning: 1 away, off (forget; forgive). 2 prohibition (forbid). 3 abstention or neglect (forgo; forsake). [old english]'),
(16075, 'f', '\r\nForage', ' â€”n. 1 food for horses and cattle. 2 searching for food. â€”v. 1 search for food; rummage. 2 collect food from. 3 get by foraging. [germanic: related to *fodder]'),
(16076, 'f', '\r\nForage', 'cap  n. Infantry undress cap.'),
(16077, 'f', '\r\nForasmuch', 'as  conj. Archaic because, since. [from for as much]'),
(16078, 'f', '\r\nForay', ' â€”n. Sudden attack; raid. â€”v. Make a foray. [french: related to *fodder]'),
(16079, 'f', '\r\nForbade', ' (also forbad) past of *forbid.'),
(16080, 'f', '\r\nForbear1', ' v. (past forbore; past part. Forborne) formal abstain or desist (from) (could not forbear (from) speaking out; forbore to mention it). [old english: related to *bear1]'),
(16081, 'f', '\r\nForbear2', ' var. Of *forebear.'),
(16082, 'f', '\r\nForbearance', ' n. Patient self-control; tolerance.'),
(16083, 'f', '\r\nForbid', ' v. (forbidding; past forbade or forbad; past part. Forbidden) 1 (foll. By to + infin.) Order not (i forbid you to go). 2 refuse to allow (a thing, or a person to have a thing). 3 refuse a person entry to.  god forbid! May it not happen! [old english: related to *bid]'),
(16084, 'f', '\r\nForbidden', 'degrees  n.pl. (also prohibited degrees) family relationship too close for marriage to be permitted.'),
(16085, 'f', '\r\nForbidden', 'fruit  n. Something desired esp. Because not allowed.'),
(16086, 'f', '\r\nForbidding', ' adj. Stern, threatening.  forbiddingly adv.'),
(16087, 'f', '\r\nForbore', ' past of *forbear1.'),
(16088, 'f', '\r\nForborne', ' past part. Of *forbear1.'),
(16089, 'f', '\r\nForce1', ' â€”n. 1 power; strength, impetus; intense effort. 2 coercion, compulsion. 3 a military strength. B organized body of soldiers, police, etc. 4 a moral, intellectual, or legal power, influence, or validity. B person etc. With such power (force for good). 5 effect; precise significance. 6 a influence tending to cause a change in the motion of a body. B intensity of this. â€”v. (-cing) 1 compel or coerce (a person) by force. 2 make a forcible entry into; break open by force. 3 drive or propel violently or against resistance. 4 make (a way) by force. 5 (foll. By on, upon) impose or press on (a person). 6 cause, produce, or attain by effort (forced a smile; forced an entry). 7 strain or increase to the utmost. 8 artificially hasten the growth of (a plant). 9 seek quick results from; accelerate (force the pace).  force a person''s hand make a person act prematurely or unwillingly. Force the issue make an immediate decision necessary. In force 1 valid (laws now in force). 2 in great strength or numbers (attacked in force). [latin fortis strong]'),
(16090, 'f', '\r\nForce2', ' n. N.engl. Waterfall. [old norse]'),
(16091, 'f', '\r\nForced', 'labour  n. Compulsory labour, esp. In prison.'),
(16092, 'f', '\r\nForced', 'landing  n. Emergency landing of an aircraft.'),
(16093, 'f', '\r\nForced', 'march  n. Long and vigorous march, esp. By troops.'),
(16094, 'f', '\r\nForce-feed', ' v. Force (esp. A prisoner) to take food.'),
(16095, 'f', '\r\nForceful', ' adj. Vigorous, powerful, impressive.  forcefully adv. Forcefulness n.'),
(16096, 'f', '\r\nForce', 'majeure  n. 1 irresistible force. 2 unforeseeable circumstances excusing a person from the fulfilment of a contract. [french]'),
(16097, 'f', '\r\nForcemeat', ' n. Minced seasoned meat for stuffing or garnish. [related to *farce]'),
(16098, 'f', '\r\nForceps', ' n. (pl. Same) surgical pincers. [latin]'),
(16099, 'f', '\r\nForcible', ' adj. Done by or involving force; forceful.  forcibly adv. [french: related to *force1]'),
(16100, 'f', '\r\nFord', ' â€”n. Shallow place where a river or stream may be crossed by wading, in a vehicle, etc. â€”v. Cross (water) at a ford.  fordable adj. [old english]'),
(16101, 'f', '\r\nFore', ' â€”adj. Situated in front. â€”n. Front part; bow of a ship. â€”int. (in golf) warning to a person in the path of a ball.  to the fore in or into a conspicuous position. [old english]'),
(16102, 'f', '\r\nFore-', ' prefix forming: 1 verbs meaning: a in front (foreshorten). B beforehand (forewarn). 2 nouns meaning: a situated in front of (forecourt). B front part of (forehead). C of or near the bow of a ship (forecastle). D preceding (forerunner).'),
(16103, 'f', '\r\nFore', 'and aft  â€”adv. At bow and stern; all over the ship. â€”adj. (fore-and-aft) (of a sail or rigging) lengthwise.'),
(16104, 'f', '\r\nForearm1', ' n. The arm from the elbow to the wrist or fingertips.'),
(16105, 'f', '\r\nForearm2', ' v. Arm beforehand, prepare.'),
(16106, 'f', '\r\nForebear', ' n. (also forbear) (usu. In pl.) Ancestor. [from *fore, obsolete beer: related to *be]'),
(16107, 'f', '\r\nForebode', ' v. (-ding) 1 be an advance sign of, portend. 2 (often foll. By that) have a presentiment of (usu. Evil).'),
(16108, 'f', '\r\nForeboding', ' n. Expectation of trouble.'),
(16109, 'f', '\r\nForecast', ' â€”v. (past and past part. -cast or -casted) predict; estimate beforehand. â€”n. Prediction, esp. Of weather.  forecaster n.'),
(16110, 'f', '\r\nForecastle', ' n. (also fo''c''s''le) forward part of a ship, formerly the living quarters.'),
(16111, 'f', '\r\nForeclose', ' v. (-sing) 1 stop (a mortgage) from being redeemable. 2 repossess the mortgaged property of (a person) when a loan is not duly repaid. 3 exclude, prevent.  foreclosure n. [latin foris outside, *close2]'),
(16112, 'f', '\r\nForecourt', ' n. 1 part of a filling-station with petrol pumps. 2 enclosed space in front of a building.'),
(16113, 'f', '\r\nForefather', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Ancestor of a family or people.'),
(16114, 'f', '\r\nForefinger', ' n. Finger next to the thumb.'),
(16115, 'f', '\r\nForefoot', ' n. Front foot of an animal.'),
(16116, 'f', '\r\nForefront', ' n. 1 leading position. 2 foremost part.'),
(16117, 'f', '\r\nForego', ' var. Of *forgo.'),
(16118, 'f', '\r\nForegoing', ' adj. Preceding; previously mentioned.'),
(16119, 'f', '\r\nForegone', 'conclusion  n. Easily predictable result.'),
(16120, 'f', '\r\nForeground', ' n. 1 part of a view or picture nearest the observer. 2 most conspicuous position. [dutch: related to *fore-, *ground1]'),
(16121, 'f', '\r\nForehand', ' n. 1 (in tennis etc.) Stroke played with the palm of the hand facing forward. 2 (attrib.) (also forehanded) of or made with a forehand.'),
(16122, 'f', '\r\nForehead', ' n. The part of the face above the eyebrows.'),
(16123, 'f', '\r\nForeign', ' adj. 1 of, from, in, or characteristic of, a country or language other than one''s own. 2 dealing with other countries (foreign service). 3 of another district, society, etc. 4 (often foll. By to) unfamiliar, alien. 5 coming from outside (foreign body).  foreignness n. [latin foris outside]'),
(16124, 'f', '\r\nForeign', 'and commonwealth office  n. British government department dealing with foreign affairs.'),
(16125, 'f', '\r\nForeigner', ' n. Person born in or coming from another country.'),
(16126, 'f', '\r\nForeign', 'legion  n. Body of foreign volunteers in the (esp. French) army.'),
(16127, 'f', '\r\nForeign', 'minister  n. (also foreign secretary) government minister in charge of foreign affairs.'),
(16128, 'f', '\r\nForeign', 'office  n. Hist. Or informal = *foreign and commonwealth office.'),
(16129, 'f', '\r\nForeknow', ' v. (past -knew, past part. -known) literary know beforehand.  foreknowledge n.'),
(16130, 'f', '\r\nForeland', ' n. Cape, promontory.'),
(16131, 'f', '\r\nForeleg', ' n. Front leg of an animal.'),
(16132, 'f', '\r\nForelimb', ' n. Front limb of an animal.'),
(16133, 'f', '\r\nForelock', ' n. Lock of hair just above the forehead.  touch one''s forelock defer to a person of higher social rank.'),
(16134, 'f', '\r\nForeman', ' n. 1 worker supervising others. 2 president and spokesman of a jury.'),
(16135, 'f', '\r\nForemast', ' n. Mast nearest the bow of a ship.'),
(16136, 'f', '\r\nForemost', ' â€”adj. 1 most notable, best. 2 first, front. â€”adv. Most importantly (first and foremost). [old english]'),
(16137, 'f', '\r\nForename', ' n. First or christian name.'),
(16138, 'f', '\r\nForenoon', ' n. Morning.'),
(16139, 'f', '\r\nForensic', ' adj. 1 of or used in courts of law (forensic science; forensic medicine). 2 of or involving forensic science (sent for forensic examination). forensically adv. [latin forensis: related to *forum]'),
(16140, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' use of forensic in sense 2 is common but considered an illogical extension of sense 1 by some people.'),
(16141, 'f', '\r\nForeordain', ' v. Destine beforehand.'),
(16142, 'f', '\r\nForepaw', ' n. Front paw of an animal.'),
(16143, 'f', '\r\nForeplay', ' n. Stimulation preceding sexual intercourse.'),
(16144, 'f', '\r\nForerunner', ' n. 1 predecessor. 2 herald.'),
(16145, 'f', '\r\nForesail', ' n. Principal sail on a foremast.'),
(16146, 'f', '\r\nForesee', ' v. (past -saw; past part. -seen) see or be aware of beforehand.  foreseeable adj.'),
(16147, 'f', '\r\nForeshadow', ' v. Be a warning or indication of (a future event).'),
(16148, 'f', '\r\nForeshore', ' n. Shore between high- and low-water marks.'),
(16149, 'f', '\r\nForeshorten', ' v. Show or portray (an object) with the apparent shortening due to visual perspective.'),
(16150, 'f', '\r\nForesight', ' n. 1 regard or provision for the future. 2 foreseeing. 3 front sight of a gun.'),
(16151, 'f', '\r\nForeskin', ' n. Fold of skin covering the end of the penis.'),
(16152, 'f', '\r\nForest', ' â€”n. 1 (often attrib.) Large area of trees and undergrowth. 2 trees in this. 3 large number or dense mass. â€”v. 1 plant with trees. 2 convert into a forest. [latin forestis: related to *foreign]'),
(16153, 'f', '\r\nForestall', ' v. 1 prevent by advance action. 2 deal with beforehand. [from *fore-, *stall1]'),
(16154, 'f', '\r\nForester', ' n. 1 person managing a forest or skilled in forestry. 2 dweller in a forest.'),
(16155, 'f', '\r\nForestry', ' n. Science or management of forests.'),
(16156, 'f', '\r\nForetaste', ' n. Small preliminary experience of something.'),
(16157, 'f', '\r\nForetell', ' v. (past and past part. -told) 1 predict, prophesy. 2 indicate the approach of.'),
(16158, 'f', '\r\nForethought', ' n. 1 care or provision for the future. 2 deliberate intention.'),
(16159, 'f', '\r\nForever', ' adv. Continually, persistently (is forever complaining) (cf. For ever).'),
(16160, 'f', '\r\nForewarn', ' v. Warn beforehand.'),
(16161, 'f', '\r\nForewoman', ' n. 1 female worker with supervisory responsibilities. 2 president and spokeswoman of a jury.'),
(16162, 'f', '\r\nForeword', ' n. Introductory remarks at the beginning of a book, often not by the author.'),
(16163, 'f', '\r\nForfeit', ' â€”n. 1 penalty. 2 thing surrendered as a penalty. â€”adj. Lost or surrendered as a penalty. â€”v. (-t-) lose the right to, surrender as a penalty.  forfeiture n. [french forfaire transgress, from latin foris outside, facio do]'),
(16164, 'f', '\r\nForgather', ' v. Assemble; associate. [dutch]'),
(16165, 'f', '\r\nForgave', ' past of *forgive.'),
(16166, 'f', '\r\nForge1', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 make or write in fraudulent imitation. 2 shape (metal) by heating and hammering. â€”n. 1 furnace or workshop etc. For melting or refining metal. 2 blacksmith''s workshop; smithy.  forger n. [latin fabrica: related to *fabric]'),
(16167, 'f', '\r\nForge2', ' v. (-ging) move forward gradually or steadily.  forge ahead 1 take the lead. 2 progress rapidly. [perhaps an alteration of *force1]'),
(16168, 'f', '\r\nForgery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 act of forging. 2 forged document etc.'),
(16169, 'f', '\r\nForget', ' v. (forgetting; past forgot; past part. Forgotten or us forgot) 1 (often foll. By about) lose remembrance of; not remember. 2 neglect or overlook. 3 cease to think of.  forget oneself 1 act without dignity. 2 act selflessly.  forgettable adj. [old english]'),
(16170, 'f', '\r\nForgetful', ' adj. 1 apt to forget, absent-minded. 2 (often foll. By of) neglectful.  forgetfully adj. Forgetfulness n.'),
(16171, 'f', '\r\nForget-me-not', ' n. Plant with small blue flowers.'),
(16172, 'f', '\r\nForgive', ' v. (-ving; past forgave; past part. Forgiven) 1 cease to feel angry or resentful towards; pardon. 2 remit (a debt).  forgivable adj. [old english]'),
(16173, 'f', '\r\nForgiveness', ' n. Forgiving or being forgiven.'),
(16174, 'f', '\r\nForgiving', ' adj. Inclined to forgive.'),
(16175, 'f', '\r\nForgo', ' v. (also forego) (-goes; past -went; past part. -gone) go without; relinquish. [old english]'),
(16176, 'f', '\r\nForgot', ' past of *forget.'),
(16177, 'f', '\r\nForgotten', ' past part. Of *forget.'),
(16178, 'f', '\r\nFork', ' â€”n. 1 pronged item of cutlery. 2 similar large tool used for digging, lifting, etc. 3 forked support for a bicycle wheel. 4 a divergence of a branch, road, etc. Into two parts. B place of this. C either part. â€”v. 1 form a fork or branch by separating into two parts. 2 take one road at a fork. 3 dig, lift, etc., with a fork.  fork out slang pay, esp. Reluctantly. [latin furca pitchfork]'),
(16179, 'f', '\r\nFork-lift', 'truck  n. Vehicle with a fork for lifting and carrying loads.'),
(16180, 'f', '\r\nForlorn', ' adj. 1 sad and abandoned. 2 in a pitiful state.  forlornly adv. [lorn = past part. Of obsolete leese *lose]'),
(16181, 'f', '\r\nForlorn', 'hope  n. Faint remaining hope or chance. [dutch verloren hoop lost troop]'),
(16182, 'f', '\r\nForm', ' â€”n. 1 shape; arrangement of parts; visible aspect. 2 person or animal as visible or tangible. 3 mode of existence or manifestation. 4 kind or variety (a form of art). 5 printed document with blank spaces for information to be inserted. 6 class in a school. 7 customary method. 8 set order of words. 9 etiquette or specified adherence to it (good or bad form). 10 (prec. By the) correct procedure (knows the form). 11 a (of an athlete, horse, etc.) Condition of health and training. B racing history of a horse etc. 12 state or disposition (in great form). 13 any of the spellings, inflections, etc. Of a word. 14 arrangement and style in a literary or musical composition. 15 long low bench. 16 hare''s lair. â€”v. 1 make or be made (formed a straight line; puddles formed). 2 make up or constitute. 3 develop or establish as a concept, institution, or practice (form an idea; form a habit). 4 (foll. By into) mould or organize to become (formed ourselves into a circle). 5 (often foll. By up) (of troops etc.) Bring or move into formation. 6 train or instruct.  off form not playing or performing well. On form playing or performing well. Out of form not fit for racing etc. [latin forma]'),
(16183, 'f', '\r\nForm', ' comb. Form (usu. As -iform) forming adjectives meaning: 1 having the form of (cruciform). 2 having so many forms (multiform).'),
(16184, 'f', '\r\nFormal', ' adj. 1 in accordance with rules, convention, or ceremony (formal dress; formal occasion). 2 precise or symmetrical (formal garden). 3 prim or stiff. 4 perfunctory, in form only. 5 drawn up etc. Correctly; explicit (formal agreement). 6 of or concerned with (outward) form, not content or matter.  formally adv. [latin: related to *form]'),
(16185, 'f', '\r\nFormaldehyde', ' n. Colourless pungent gas used as a disinfectant and preservative. [from *formic acid, *aldehyde]'),
(16186, 'f', '\r\nFormalin', ' n. Solution of formaldehyde in water.'),
(16187, 'f', '\r\nFormalism', ' n. Strict adherence to external form without regard to content, esp. In art.  formalist n.'),
(16188, 'f', '\r\nFormality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a formal, esp. Meaningless, act, regulation, or custom. B thing done simply to comply with a rule. 2 rigid observance of rules or convention.'),
(16189, 'f', '\r\nFormalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 give definite (esp. Legal) form to. 2 make formal.  formalization n.'),
(16190, 'f', '\r\nFormat', ' â€”n. 1 shape and size (of a book, etc.). 2 style or manner of procedure etc. 3 computing arrangement of data etc. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 arrange or put into a format. 2 computing prepare (a storage medium) to receive data. [latin formatus shaped: related to *form]'),
(16191, 'f', '\r\nFormation', ' n. 1 forming. 2 thing formed. 3 particular arrangement (e.g. Of troops). 4 rocks or strata with a common characteristic. [latin: related to *form]'),
(16192, 'f', '\r\nFormative', ' adj. Serving to form or fashion; of formation (formative years).'),
(16193, 'f', '\r\nForme', ' n. Printing body of type secured in a chase ready for printing. [var. Of *form]'),
(16194, 'f', '\r\nFormer', ' attrib. Adj. 1 of the past, earlier, previous (in former times). 2 (the former) (often absol.) The first or first-mentioned of two. [related to *foremost]'),
(16195, 'f', '\r\n-former', ' comb. Form pupil in a specified form (fourth-former).'),
(16196, 'f', '\r\nFormerly', ' adv. In former times.'),
(16197, 'f', '\r\nFormica', ' n. Propr. Hard durable plastic laminate used for surfaces. [origin uncertain]'),
(16198, 'f', '\r\nFormic', 'acid  n. Colourless irritant volatile acid contained in fluid emitted by ants; methanoic acid. [latin formica ant]'),
(16199, 'f', '\r\nFormidable', ' adj. 1 inspiring dread, awe, or respect. 2 hard to overcome or deal with.  formidably adv. [latin formido fear]'),
(16200, 'f', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation given, with the stress on the second syllable, is common but considered incorrect by some people.'),
