(16527, 'f', '\r\nFug', ' n. Colloq. Close stuffy atmosphere.  fuggy adj. [origin unknown]'),
(16528, 'f', '\r\nFugitive', ' â€”n. (often foll. By from) person who flees, e.g. From justice or an enemy. â€”adj. 1 fleeing. 2 transient, fleeting. [latin fugio flee]'),
(16529, 'f', '\r\nFugue', ' n. Piece of music in which a short melody or phrase is introduced by one part and taken up and developed by others.  fugal adj. [latin fuga flight]'),
(16530, 'f', '\r\nFÃ¼hrer', ' n. (also fuehrer) tyrannical leader. [german]'),
(16531, 'f', '\r\n-ful', ' comb. Form forming: 1 adjectives from a nouns, meaning full of or having qualities of (beautiful; masterful). B adjectives (direful). C verbs, meaning â€˜apt toâ€™ (forgetful). 2 nouns (pl. -fuls) meaning â€˜amount that fillsâ€™ (handful; spoonful).'),
(16532, 'f', '\r\nFulcrum', ' n. (pl. -s or -cra) point on which a lever is supported. [latin fulcio to prop]'),
(16533, 'f', '\r\nFulfil', ' v. (us fulfill) (-ll-) 1 carry out (a task, prophecy, promise, etc.). 2 a satisfy (conditions, a desire, prayer, etc.). B (as fulfilled adj.) Completely happy. 3 answer (a purpose).  fulfil oneself realize one''s potential.  fulfilment n. [old english: related to *full1, *fill]'),
(16534, 'f', '\r\nFulfill', ' v. (brit. Fulfil) (-ll-) 1 carry out (a task, prophecy, promise, etc.). 2 a satisfy (conditions, a desire, prayer, etc.). B (as fulfilled adj.) Completely happy. 3 answer (a purpose).  fulfil oneself realize one''s potential.  fulfilment n. [old english: related to *full1, *fill]'),
(16535, 'f', '\r\nFull1', ' â€”adj. 1 holding all it can (bucket is full; full of water). 2 having eaten all one can or wants. 3 abundant, copious, satisfying (a full life; full details). 4 (foll. By of) having an abundance of (full of vitality). 5 (foll. By of) engrossed in (full of himself). 6 complete, perfect (full membership; in full bloom). 7 (of tone) deep and clear. 8 plump, rounded (full figure). 9 (of clothes) ample, hanging in folds. â€”adv. 1 very (knows full well). 2 quite, fully (full six miles). 3 exactly (full on the nose).  full up colloq. Completely full. In full 1 without abridgement. 2 to or for the full amount'),
(16536, 'f', '\r\nFull2', ' v. Clean and thicken (cloth). [from *fuller]'),
(16537, 'f', '\r\nFull', 'back  n. Defensive player near the goal in football, hockey, etc.'),
(16538, 'f', '\r\nFull-blooded', ' adj. 1 vigorous, hearty, sensual. 2 not hybrid.'),
