(16668, 'g', '\r\nGag', ' â€”n. 1 thing thrust into or tied across the mouth, esp. To prevent speaking or crying out. 2 joke or comic scene. 3 parliamentary closure. 4 thing restricting free speech. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 apply a gag to. 2 silence; deprive of free speech. 3 choke, retch. 4 make gags as a comedian etc. [origin uncertain]'),
(16669, 'g', '\r\nGaga', ' adj. Slang 1 senile. 2 slightly crazy. [french]'),
(16670, 'g', '\r\nGage1', ' n. 1 pledge; thing deposited as security. 2 symbol of a challenge to fight, esp. A glove thrown down. [germanic: related to *wed, *wage]'),
(16671, 'g', '\r\nGage2', ' (brit. Gauge: see also sense 6) â€”n. 1 standard measure, esp. Of the capacity or contents of a barrel, fineness of a textile, diameter of a bullet, or thickness of sheet metal. 2 instrument for measuring pressure, width, length, thickness, etc. 3 distance between rails or opposite wheels. 4 capacity, extent. 5 criterion, test. 6 (usu. Gage) naut. Position relative to the wind. â€”v. (-ging) 1 measure exactly. 2 measure the capacity or content of. 3 estimate (a person, situation, etc.). [french]'),
(16672, 'g', '\r\nGaggle', ' n. 1 flock of geese. 2 colloq. Disorganized group of people. [imitative]'),
(16673, 'g', '\r\nGaiety', ' n. (us gayety) 1 being gay; mirth. 2 merrymaking. 3 bright appearance. [french: related to *gay]'),
(16674, 'g', '\r\nGaily', ' adv. In a gay or careless manner (gaily decorated; gaily announced their departure).'),
(16675, 'g', '\r\nGain', ' â€”v. 1 obtain or win (gain advantage; gain recognition). 2 acquire as profits etc., earn. 3 (often foll. By in) get more of, improve (gain momentum; gain in experience). 4 benefit, profit. 5 (of a clock etc.) Become fast; become fast by (a specified amount of time). 6 (often foll. By on, upon) come closer to a person or thing pursued. 7 a reclaim (land from the sea). B win (a battle). 8 reach (a desired place). â€”n. 1 increase of wealth etc.; profit, improvement. 2 (in pl.) Sums of money got by trade etc. 3 increase in amount.  gain ground 1 advance. 2 (foll. By on) catch up (a person pursued). [french from germanic]'),
(16676, 'g', '\r\nGainful', ' adj. 1 (of employment) paid. 2 lucrative.  gainfully adv.'),
(16677, 'g', '\r\nGainsay', ' v. Deny, contradict. [old norse: related to *against, *say]'),
(16678, 'g', '\r\nGait', ' n. Manner of walking or forward motion. [old norse]'),
(16679, 'g', '\r\nGaiter', ' n. Covering of cloth, leather, etc., for the lower leg. [french guÃªtre]'),
(16680, 'g', '\r\nGal', ' n. Slang girl. [representing a variant pronunciation]'),
(16681, 'g', '\r\nGal.', ' Abbr. (also gall.) Gallon(s).'),
(16682, 'g', '\r\nGala', ' n. Festive occasion or gathering (swimming gala). [ultimately from french gale rejoicing from germanic]'),
(16683, 'g', '\r\nGalactic', ' adj. Of a galaxy or galaxies.'),
(16684, 'g', '\r\nGalantine', ' n. White meat boned, stuffed, spiced, etc., and served cold. [french from latin]'),
(16685, 'g', '\r\nGalaxy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 independent system of stars, gas, dust, etc., in space. 2 (the galaxy) milky way. 3 (foll. By of) brilliant company (galaxy of talent). [greek gala milk]'),
(16686, 'g', '\r\nGale', ' n. 1 very strong wind or storm. 2 outburst, esp. Of laughter. [origin unknown]'),
(16687, 'g', '\r\nGall', 'bladder  n. Organ storing bile.'),
(16688, 'g', '\r\nGalleon', ' n. Hist. Warship (usu. Spanish). [french or spanish: related to *galley]'),
(16689, 'g', '\r\nGalleria', ' n. Collection of small shops under a single roof. [italian]'),
(16690, 'g', '\r\nGallery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 room or building for showing works of art. 2 balcony, esp. In a church, hall, etc. (minstrels'' gallery). 3 highest balcony in a theatre. 4 a covered walk partly open at the side; colonnade. B narrow passage in the thickness of a wall or on corbels, open towards the interior of the building. 5 long narrow room or passage (shooting-gallery). 6 horizontal underground passage in a mine etc. 7 group of spectators at a golf-match etc.  play to the gallery seek to win approval by appealing to popular taste. [french galerie]'),
(16691, 'g', '\r\nGalley', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 hist. A long flat single-decked vessel usu. Rowed by slaves or criminals. B ancient greek or roman warship. 2 ship''s or aircraft''s kitchen. 3 printing (in full galley proof) proof in continuous form before division into pages. [latin galea]'),
(16692, 'g', '\r\nGalley-slave', ' n. Drudge.'),
(16693, 'g', '\r\nGallic', ' adj. 1 french or typically french. 2 of gaul or the gauls. [latin gallicus]'),
(16694, 'g', '\r\nGallicism', ' n. French idiom. [related to *gallic]'),
(16695, 'g', '\r\nGallinaceous', ' adj. Of the order including domestic poultry, pheasants, etc. [latin gallina hen]'),
(16696, 'g', '\r\nGallium', ' n. Soft bluish-white metallic element. [latin gallia france: so named patriotically by its discoverer lecoq]'),
(16697, 'g', '\r\nGallivant', ' v. Colloq. Gad about. [origin uncertain]'),
(16698, 'g', '\r\nGallo-', ' comb. Form french. [latin]'),
(16699, 'g', '\r\nGallon', ' n. 1 measure of capacity equal to eight pints (4.5 litres; for wine, or us, 3.8 litres). 2 (in pl.) Colloq. Large amount. [french]'),
(16700, 'g', '\r\nGallop', ' â€”n. 1 fastest pace of a horse etc., with all the feet off the ground together in each stride. 2 ride at this pace. â€”v. (-p-) 1 a (of a horse etc. Or its rider) go at a gallop. B make (a horse etc.) Gallop. 2 read, talk, etc., fast. 3 progress rapidly (galloping inflation). [french: related to *wallop]'),
(16701, 'g', '\r\nGallows', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Structure, usu. Of two uprights and a crosspiece, for hanging criminals. [old norse]'),
(16702, 'g', '\r\nGallstone', ' n. Small hard mass forming in the gall-bladder.'),
(16703, 'g', '\r\nGallup', 'poll  n. = *opinion poll. [gallup, name of a statistician]'),
(16704, 'g', '\r\nGalore', ' adv. In plenty (whisky galore). [irish]'),
(16705, 'g', '\r\nGalosh', ' n. (also golosh) (usu. In pl.) Overshoe, usu. Of rubber. [french]'),
(16706, 'g', '\r\nGalumph', ' v. (esp. As galumphing adj.) Colloq. Move noisily or clumsily. [coined by lewis carroll, perhaps from *gallop, *triumph]'),
(16707, 'g', '\r\nGalvanic', ' adj. 1 a producing an electric current by chemical action. B (of electricity) produced by chemical action. 2 a sudden and remarkable (had a galvanic effect). B stimulating; full of energy.  galvanically adv.'),
(16708, 'g', '\r\nGalvanize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (often foll. By into) rouse forcefully, esp. By shock or excitement (was galvanized into action). 2 stimulate by or as by electricity. 3 coat (iron) with zinc to protect against rust.  galvanization n. [galvani, name of a physiologist]'),
(16709, 'g', '\r\nGalvanometer', ' n. Instrument for detecting and measuring small electric currents.  galvanometric adj.'),
(16710, 'g', '\r\nGambit', ' n. 1 chess opening in which a player sacrifices a piece or pawn to secure an advantage. 2 opening move in a discussion etc. 3 trick or device. [italian gambetto tripping up]'),
(16711, 'g', '\r\nGamble', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 play games of chance for money. 2 a bet (a sum of money) in gambling. B (often foll. By away) lose by gambling. 3 risk much in the hope of great gain. 4 (foll. By on) act in the hope of. â€”n. 1 risky undertaking. 2 spell of gambling.  gambler n.'),
(16712, 'g', '\r\nGamboge', ' n. Gum resin used as a yellow pigment and as a purgative. [cambodia in se asia]'),
(16713, 'g', '\r\nGambol', ' â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) skip or jump about playfully. â€”n. Frolic, caper. [french gambade leap, from italian gamba leg]'),
(16714, 'g', '\r\nGame1', ' â€”n. 1 form of play or sport, esp. A competitive one with rules. 2 portion of play forming a scoring unit, e.g. In bridge or tennis. 3 (in pl.) Series of athletic etc. Contests (olympic games). 4 a piece of fun, jest (didn''t mean to upset you; it was only a game). B (in pl.) Dodges, tricks (none of your games!). 5 colloq. A scheme (so that''s your game). B type of activity or business (have been in the antiques game a long time). 6 a wild animals or birds hunted for sport or food. B their flesh as food. â€”adj. Spirited; eager and willing (are you game for a walk?). â€”v. (-ming) gamble for money stakes.  the game is up scheme is revealed or foiled. On the game slang involved in prostitution.  gamely adv. [old english]'),
(16715, 'g', '\r\nGame2', ' adj. Colloq. (of a leg, arm, etc.) Crippled. [origin unknown]'),
(16716, 'g', '\r\nGamecock', ' n. Cock bred and trained for cock-fighting.'),
(16717, 'g', '\r\nGamekeeper', ' n. Person employed to breed and protect game.'),
(16718, 'g', '\r\nGamelan', ' n. 1 se asian orchestra mainly of percussion instruments. 2 type of xylophone used in this. [javanese]'),
(16719, 'g', '\r\nGamesmanship', ' n. Art of winning games by gaining psychological advantage.'),
(16720, 'g', '\r\nGamester', ' n. Gambler.'),
(16721, 'g', '\r\nGamete', ' n. Mature germ cell able to unite with another in sexual reproduction.  gametic adj. [greek, = wife]'),
(16722, 'g', '\r\nGamin', ' n. 1 street urchin. 2 impudent child. [french]'),
(16723, 'g', '\r\nGamine', ' n. 1 girl gamin. 2 girl with mischievous charm. [french]'),
(16724, 'g', '\r\nGamma', ' n. 1 third letter of the greek alphabet (g, g). 2 third-class mark for a piece of work etc. [greek]'),
(16725, 'g', '\r\nGamma', 'radiation  n. (also gamma rays) electromagnetic radiation of shorter wavelength than x-rays.'),
(16726, 'g', '\r\nGammon', ' n. 1 bottom piece of a flitch of bacon including a hind leg. 2 ham of a pig cured like bacon. [french: related to *jamb]'),
(16727, 'g', '\r\nGammy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang = *game2. [dial. Form of *game2]'),
(16728, 'g', '\r\nGamut', ' n. Entire range or scope.  run the gamut of experience or perform the complete range of. [latin gamma ut, words arbitrarily taken as names of notes]'),
(16729, 'g', '\r\nGamy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) smelling or tasting like high game.'),
(16730, 'g', '\r\nGander', ' n. 1 male goose. 2 slang look, glance (take a gander). [old english]'),
(16731, 'g', '\r\nGang', ' n. 1 band of persons associating for some (usu. Antisocial or criminal) purpose. 2 set of workers, slaves, or prisoners.  gang up colloq. 1 (often foll. By with) act together. 2 (foll. By on) combine against. [old norse]'),
(16732, 'g', '\r\nGanger', ' n. Foreman of a gang of workers.'),
(16733, 'g', '\r\nGangling', ' adj. (of a person) loosely built; lanky. [frequentative of old english gang go]'),
(16734, 'g', '\r\nGanglion', ' n. (pl. -lia or -s) structure containing an assemblage of nerve cells.  ganglionic adj. [greek]'),
(16735, 'g', '\r\nGangly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) = *gangling.'),
(16736, 'g', '\r\nGangplank', ' n. Movable plank for boarding or disembarking from a ship etc.'),
(16737, 'g', '\r\nGangrene', ' n. Death of body tissue, usu. Resulting from obstructed circulation.  gangrenous adj. [greek gaggraina]'),
(16738, 'g', '\r\nGangster', ' n. Member of a gang of violent criminals.'),
(16739, 'g', '\r\nGangue', ' n. Valueless earth etc. In which ore is found. [german: related to *gang]'),
(16740, 'g', '\r\nGangway', ' n. 1 passage, esp. Between rows of seats. 2 a opening in a ship''s bulwarks. B bridge from ship to shore.'),
(16741, 'g', '\r\nGannet', ' n. 1 large diving sea bird. 2 slang greedy person. [old english]'),
(16742, 'g', '\r\nGantry', ' n. (pl. -ies) structure supporting a travelling crane, railway or road signals, rocket-launching equipment, etc. [probably gawn, a dial. Form of *gallon, + *tree]'),
(16743, 'g', '\r\nGaol', ' var. Of *jail.'),
(16744, 'g', '\r\nGaolbird', ' var. Of *jailbird.'),
(16745, 'g', '\r\nGaolbreak', ' var. Of *jailbreak.'),
(16746, 'g', '\r\nGaoler', ' var. Of *jailer.'),
(16747, 'g', '\r\nGap', ' n. 1 empty space, interval; deficiency. 2 breach in a hedge, fence, etc. 3 wide divergence in views etc.  gappy adj. [old norse]'),
(16748, 'g', '\r\nGape', ' â€”v. (-ping) 1 a open one''s mouth wide. B be or become wide open; split. 2 (foll. By at) stare at. â€”n. 1 open-mouthed stare; open mouth. 2 rent, opening. [old norse]'),
(16749, 'g', '\r\nGarage', ' â€”n. 1 building for housing a vehicle. 2 establishment selling petrol etc., or repairing and selling vehicles. â€”v. (-ging) put or keep in a garage. [french]'),
(16750, 'g', '\r\nGarb', ' â€”n. Clothing, esp. Of a distinctive kind. â€”v. (usu. In passive or refl.) Dress. [germanic: related to *gear]'),
(16751, 'g', '\r\nGarbage', ' n. 1 esp. Us refuse. 2 colloq. Nonsense. [anglo-french]'),
(16752, 'g', '\r\nGarble', ' v. (-ling) 1 (esp. As garbled adj.) Unintentionally distort or confuse (facts, messages, etc.). 2 make a (usu. Unfair) selection from (facts, statements, etc.). [italian from arabic]'),
(16753, 'g', '\r\nGarden', ' â€”n. 1 piece of ground for growing flowers, fruit, or vegetables, and as a place of recreation. 2 (esp. In pl.) Grounds laid out for public enjoyment. 3 (attrib.) Cultivated (garden plants). â€”v. Cultivate or tend a garden.  gardening n. [germanic: related to *yard2]'),
(16754, 'g', '\r\nGarden', 'centre  n. Place where plants and garden equipment are sold.'),
(16755, 'g', '\r\nGarden', 'city  n. Town spaciously laid out with parks etc.'),
(16756, 'g', '\r\nGardener', ' n. Person who gardens, esp. For a living.'),
(16757, 'g', '\r\nGardenia', ' n. Tree or shrub with large fragrant flowers. [garden, name of a naturalist]'),
(16758, 'g', '\r\nGarden', 'party  n. Party held on a lawn or in a garden.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(16759, 'g', '\r\nGarfish', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) fish with a long spearlike snout. [old english, = spear-fish]'),
(16760, 'g', '\r\nGargantuan', ' adj. Gigantic. [from the name gargantua, a giant in rabelais]'),
(16761, 'g', '\r\nGargle', ' â€”v. (-ling) wash (the throat) with a liquid kept in motion by breathing through it. â€”n. Liquid for gargling. [french: related to *gargoyle]'),
(16762, 'g', '\r\nGargoyle', ' n. Grotesque carved face or figure, esp. As a spout from the gutter of a building. [french, = throat]'),
(16763, 'g', '\r\nGaribaldi', ' n. (pl. -s) biscuit containing a layer of currants. [garibaldi, name of an italian patriot]'),
(16764, 'g', '\r\nGarish', ' adj. Obtrusively bright; showy; gaudy.  garishly adv. Garishness n. [obsolete gaure stare]'),
(16765, 'g', '\r\nGarland', ' â€”n. Wreath of flowers etc., worn on the head or hung as a decoration. â€”v. Adorn or crown with a garland or garlands. [french]'),
(16766, 'g', '\r\nGarlic', ' n. Plant of the onion family with a pungent bulb used in cookery.  garlicky adj. [old english, = spear-leek]'),
(16767, 'g', '\r\nGarment', ' n. 1 article of dress. 2 outward covering. [french: related to *garnish]'),
(16768, 'g', '\r\nGarner', ' â€”v. 1 collect. 2 store. â€”n. Literary storehouse or granary. [latin: related to *granary]'),
(16769, 'g', '\r\nGarnet', ' n. Glassy silicate mineral, esp. A red kind used as a gem. [medieval latin granatum *pomegranate]'),
(16770, 'g', '\r\nGarnish', ' â€”v. Decorate (esp. Food). â€”n. Decoration, esp. To food. [french garnir from germanic]'),
(16771, 'g', '\r\nGarotte', ' var. Of *garrotte.'),
(16772, 'g', '\r\nGarret', ' n. Attic or room in a roof. [french, = watch-tower: related to *garrison]'),
(16773, 'g', '\r\nGarrison', ' â€”n. Troops stationed in a town etc. To defend it. â€”v. (-n-) 1 provide with or occupy as a garrison. 2 place on garrison duty. [french garir defend, from germanic]'),
(16774, 'g', '\r\nGarrote', ' (brit. Garrotte) â€”v. (-ting) execute or kill by strangulation, esp. With a wire collar. â€”n. Device used for this. [french or spanish]'),
(16775, 'g', '\r\nGarrotte', ' (also garotte; us garrote) â€”v. (-ting) execute or kill by strangulation, esp. With a wire collar. â€”n. Device used for this. [french or spanish]'),
(16776, 'g', '\r\nGarrulous', ' adj. Talkative.  garrulity n. Garrulousness n. [latin]'),
(16777, 'g', '\r\nGarter', ' n. 1 band worn to keep a sock or stocking up. 2 (the garter) a highest order of english knighthood. B badge or membership of this. [french]'),
(16778, 'g', '\r\nGarter', 'stitch  n. Plain knitting stitch.'),
(16779, 'g', '\r\nGas', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) 1 any airlike substance (i.e. Not solid or liquid) moving freely to fill any space available. 2 such a substance (esp. Found naturally or extracted from coal) used as fuel (also attrib.: gas cooker; gas industry). 3 nitrous oxide or other gas as an anaesthetic. 4 poisonous gas used in war. 5 us colloq. Petrol, gasoline. 6 slang idle talk; boasting. 7 slang enjoyable or amusing thing or person. â€”v. (gases, gassed, gassing) 1 expose to gas, esp. To kill. 2 colloq. Talk idly or boastfully.'),
(16780, 'g', '\r\nGall1', ' n. 1 slang impudence. 2 rancour. 3 bitterness. 4 bile. [old norse]'),
(16781, 'g', '\r\nGasbag', ' n. Slang idle talker.'),
(16782, 'g', '\r\nGas', 'chamber  n. Room filled with poisonous gas to kill people or animals.'),
(16783, 'g', '\r\nGaseous', ' adj. Of or like gas.'),
(16784, 'g', '\r\nGas', 'fire  n. Domestic heater burning gas.'),
(16785, 'g', '\r\nGas-fired', ' adj. Using gas as fuel.'),
(16786, 'g', '\r\nGash', ' â€”n. Long deep slash, cut, or wound. â€”v. Make a gash in; cut. [french]'),
(16787, 'g', '\r\nGasholder', ' n. Large receptacle for storing gas; gasometer.'),
(16788, 'g', '\r\nGasify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) convert into gas.  gasification n.'),
(16789, 'g', '\r\nGasket', ' n. Sheet or ring of rubber etc., shaped to seal the junction of metal surfaces. [french garcette]'),
(16790, 'g', '\r\nGaslight', ' n. Light from burning gas.'),
(16791, 'g', '\r\nGasman', ' n. Man who installs or services gas appliances, or reads gas meters.'),
(16792, 'g', '\r\nGas', 'mask  n. Respirator as a protection against poison gas.'),
(16793, 'g', '\r\nGasoline', ' n. (also gasolene) us petrol.'),
(16794, 'g', '\r\nGasometer', ' n. Large tank from which gas is distributed by pipes. [french gazomÃ¨tre: related to *gas, *-meter]'),
(16795, 'g', '\r\nGasp', ' â€”v. 1 catch one''s breath with an open mouth as in exhaustion or astonishment. 2 utter with gasps. â€”n. Convulsive catching of breath. [old norse]'),
(16796, 'g', '\r\nGas', 'ring  n. Hollow ring perforated with gas jets, for cooking etc.'),
(16797, 'g', '\r\nGassy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 a of or like gas. B full of gas. 2 colloq. Verbose.'),
(16798, 'g', '\r\nGasteropod', ' var. Of *gastropod.'),
(16799, 'g', '\r\nGastric', ' adj. Of the stomach. [french: related to *gastro-]'),
(16800, 'g', '\r\nGastric', 'flu  n. Colloq. Intestinal disorder of unknown cause.'),
(16801, 'g', '\r\nGastric', 'juice  n. Digestive fluid secreted by the stomach glands.'),
(16802, 'g', '\r\nGastritis', ' n. Inflammation of the stomach.'),
(16803, 'g', '\r\nGastro-', ' comb. Form stomach. [greek gaster stomach]'),
(16804, 'g', '\r\nGastro-enteritis', ' n. Inflammation of the stomach and intestines.'),
(16805, 'g', '\r\nGastronome', ' n. Gourmet. [greek gaster stomach, nomos law]'),
(16806, 'g', '\r\nGastronomy', ' n. Science or art of good eating and drinking.  gastronomic adj. Gastronomical adj. Gastronomically adv.'),
(16807, 'g', '\r\nGastropod', ' n. (also gasteropod) mollusc that moves by means of a ventral muscular organ, e.g. A snail. [from *gastro-, greek pous pod- foot]'),
(16808, 'g', '\r\nGasworks', ' n. Place where gas is manufactured for lighting and heating.'),
(16809, 'g', '\r\nGate', ' â€”n. 1 barrier, usu. Hinged, used to close an opening made for entrance and exit through a wall, fence, etc. 2 such an opening. 3 means of entrance or exit. 4 numbered place of access to aircraft at an airport. 5 device regulating the passage of water in a lock etc. 6 a number of people entering by payment at the gates of a sports ground etc. B amount of money taken thus. 7  a electrical signal that causes or controls the passage of other signals. B electrical circuit with an output that depends on the combination of several inputs. â€”v. (-ting) confine to college or school as a punishment.  gated adj. [old english]'),
(16810, 'g', '\r\nGateau', ' n. (pl. -s or -x) large rich cake filled with cream etc. [french]'),
(16811, 'g', '\r\nGatecrasher', ' n. Uninvited guest at a party etc.  gatecrash v.'),
(16812, 'g', '\r\nGatehouse', ' n. House standing by or over a gateway, esp. To a large house or park.'),
(16813, 'g', '\r\nGateleg', ' n. (in full gateleg table) table with folding flaps supported by legs swung open like a gate.  gatelegged adj.'),
(16814, 'g', '\r\nGatepost', ' n. Post at either side of a gate.'),
(16815, 'g', '\r\nGateway', ' n. 1 opening which can be closed with a gate. 2 means of access (gateway to the south; gateway to success).'),
(16816, 'g', '\r\nGather', ' â€”v. 1 bring or come together; accumulate. 2 pick or collect as harvest. 3 infer or deduce. 4 a increase (gather speed). B collect (gather dust). 5 summon up (energy etc.). 6 draw together in folds or wrinkles. 7 (often as gathering adj.) Come to a head (gathering storm).'),
(16817, 'g', '\r\nGall2', ' â€”n. 1 sore made by chafing. 2 mental soreness or its cause. 3 place rubbed bare. â€”v. 1 rub sore. 2 vex, humiliate. [low german or dutch galle]'),
(16818, 'g', '\r\nGall3', ' n. Growth produced by insects etc. On plants and trees, esp. On oak. [latin galla]'),
(16819, 'g', '\r\nGall.', ' Abbr. Var. Of *gal.'),
(16820, 'g', '\r\nGallant', ' â€”adj. 1 brave. 2 fine, stately. 3  very attentive to women. â€”n. Ladies'' man.  gallantly adv. [french galer make merry]'),
(16821, 'g', '\r\nGallantry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 bravery. 2 devotion to women. 3 polite act or speech.'),
(16822, 'g', '\r\nGathering', ' n. 1 assembly. 2 purulent swelling. 3 group of leaves taken together in bookbinding.'),
(16823, 'g', '\r\nGatt', ' abbr. General agreement on tariffs and trade.'),
(16824, 'g', '\r\nGauche', ' adj. 1 socially awkward. 2 tactless.  gauchely adv. Gaucheness n. [french]'),
(16825, 'g', '\r\nGaucherie', ' n. Gauche manners or act. [french: related to *gauche]'),
(16826, 'g', '\r\nGaucho', ' n. (pl. -s) cowboy from the s. American pampas. [spanish from quechua]'),
(16827, 'g', '\r\nGaudy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) tastelessly showy.  gaudily adv. Gaudiness n. [obsolete gaud ornament, from latin gaudeo rejoice]'),
(16828, 'g', '\r\nGauge', ' (us gage: see also sense 6) â€”n. 1 standard measure, esp. Of the capacity or contents of a barrel, fineness of a textile, diameter of a bullet, or thickness of sheet metal. 2 instrument for measuring pressure, width, length, thickness, etc. 3 distance between rails or opposite wheels. 4 capacity, extent. 5 criterion, test. 6 (usu. Gage) naut. Position relative to the wind. â€”v. (-ging) 1 measure exactly. 2 measure the capacity or content of. 3 estimate (a person, situation, etc.). [french]'),
