(16994, 'g', '\r\nGesticulate', ' v. (-ting) 1 use gestures instead of, or to reinforce, speech. 2 express thus.  gesticulation n. [latin: related to *gesture]'),
(16995, 'g', '\r\nGesture', ' â€”n. 1 significant movement of a limb or the body. 2 use of such movements, esp. As a rhetorical device. 3 action to evoke a response or convey intention, usu. Friendly. â€”v. (-ring) gesticulate. [latin gestura from gero wield]'),
(16996, 'g', '\r\nGet', ' v. (getting; past got; past part. Got or us gotten) (and in comb.) 1 come into possession of; receive or earn (get a job; got Â£200 a week; got first prize). 2 fetch or procure (get my book for me; got a new car). 3 go to reach or catch (a bus, train, etc.). 4 prepare (a meal etc.). 5 (cause to) reach some state or become (get rich; get married; get to be famous; got them ready; got him into trouble). 6 obtain as a result of calculation. 7 contract (a disease etc.). 8 establish contact by telephone etc. With; receive (a broadcast signal). 9 experience or suffer; have inflicted on one; receive as one''s lot or penalty (got four years in prison). 10 a succeed in bringing, placing, etc. (get it round the corner; get it on to the agenda). B (cause to) succeed in coming or going (will get you there somehow; got absolutely nowhere; got home). 11 (prec. By have) a possess (have not got a penny). B (foll. By to + infin.) Be bound or obliged (have got to see you). 12 (foll. By to + infin.) Induce; prevail upon (got them to help me). 13 colloq. Understand (a person or an argument) (have you got that?; i get your point; do you get me?). 14 colloq. Harm, injure, kill, esp. In retaliation (i''ll get you for that). 15 colloq. A annoy. B affect emotionally. C attract. 16 (foll. By to + infin.) Develop an inclination (am getting to like it). 17 (foll. By verbal noun) begin (get going). 18 establish (an idea etc.) In one''s mind. 19 archaic beget.  get about 1 travel extensively or fast; go from place to place. 2 begin walking etc. (esp. After illness). Get across 1 communicate (an idea etc.). 2 (of an idea etc.) Be communicated. Get ahead make progress (esp. In a career etc.). Get along (or on) (foll. By together, with) live harmoniously. Get around = get about. Get at 1 reach; get hold of. 2 colloq. Imply. 3 colloq. Nag, criticize. Get away 1 escape, start. 2 (as int.) Colloq. Expressing disbelief or scepticism. 3 (foll. By with) escape blame or punishment for. Get back at colloq. Retaliate against. Get by colloq. Manage, even if with difficulty. Get cracking see *crack. Get down 1 alight, descend (from a vehicle, ladder, etc.). 2 record in writing.'),
(16997, 'g', '\r\nGet-at-able', ' adj. Colloq. Accessible.'),
(16998, 'g', '\r\nGetaway', ' n. Escape, esp. After a crime.'),
(16999, 'g', '\r\nGet-out', ' n. Means of avoiding something.'),
(17000, 'g', '\r\nGet-together', ' n. Colloq. Social gathering.'),
(17001, 'g', '\r\nGet-up', ' n. Colloq. Style or arrangement of dress etc.'),
(17002, 'g', '\r\nGet-up-and-go', ' n. Colloq. Energy, enthusiasm.'),
(17003, 'g', '\r\nGeyser', ' n. 1 intermittent hot spring. 2 apparatus for heating water. [icelandic geysir from geysa to gush]'),
(17004, 'g', '\r\nGhastly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 horrible, frightful. 2 colloq. Unpleasant. 3 deathlike, pallid.   ghastliness n. [obsolete gast terrify]'),
(17005, 'g', '\r\nGhee', ' n. Indian clarified butter. [hindi from sanskrit]'),
(17006, 'g', '\r\nGherkin', ' n. Small pickled cucumber. [dutch]'),
(17007, 'g', '\r\nGhetto', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 part of a city occupied by a minority group. 2 hist. Jewish quarter in a city. 3 segregated group or area. [italian]'),
(17008, 'g', '\r\nGhetto-blaster', ' n. Slang large portable radio, esp. For playing loud pop music.'),
(17009, 'g', '\r\nGhillie', ' var. Of *gillie.'),
(17010, 'g', '\r\nGhost', ' â€”n. 1 supposed apparition of a dead person or animal; disembodied spirit. 2 shadow or semblance (not a ghost of a chance). 3 secondary image in a defective telescope or television picture. â€”v. (often foll. By for) act as ghost-writer of (a work).  ghostliness n. Ghostly adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(17011, 'g', '\r\nGhosting', ' n. Appearance of a â€˜ghostâ€™ image in a television picture.'),
(17012, 'g', '\r\nGhost', 'town  n. Town with few or no remaining inhabitants.'),
(17013, 'g', '\r\nGhost', 'train  n. (at a funfair) open-topped miniature railway in which the rider experiences ghoulish sights, sounds, etc.'),
(17014, 'g', '\r\nGhost-writer', ' n. Person who writes on behalf of the credited author.'),
(17015, 'g', '\r\nGhoul', ' n. 1 person morbidly interested in death etc. 2 evil spirit or phantom. 3 spirit in muslim folklore preying on corpses.  ghoulish adj. Ghoulishly adv. [arabic]'),
(17016, 'g', '\r\nGhq', ' abbr. General headquarters.'),
(17017, 'g', '\r\nGhyll', ' var. Of *gill3.'),
(17018, 'g', '\r\nGi', ' n. (often attrib.) Soldier in the us army. [abbreviation of government (or general) issue]'),
(17019, 'g', '\r\nGiant', ' â€”n. 1 (fem. Giantess) imaginary or mythical being of human form but superhuman size. 2 person or thing of great size, ability, courage, etc. â€”attrib. Adj. 1 gigantic. 2 of a very large kind. [greek gigas gigant-]'),
(17020, 'g', '\r\nGibber', ' v. Jabber inarticulately. [imitative]'),
(17021, 'g', '\r\nGibberish', ' n. Unintelligible or meaningless speech; nonsense.'),
(17022, 'g', '\r\nGibbet', ' â€”n. Hist. 1 a gallows. B post with an arm on which an executed criminal was hung. 2 (prec. By the) death by hanging. â€”v. (-t-) 1 put to death by hanging. 2 expose or hang up on a gibbet. [french gibet]'),
(17023, 'g', '\r\nGibbon', ' n. Long-armed se asian anthropoid ape. [french]'),
(17024, 'g', '\r\nGibbous', ' adj. 1 convex. 2 (of a moon or planet) having the bright part greater than a semicircle and less than a circle. 3 humpbacked. [latin gibbus hump]'),
(17025, 'g', '\r\nGibe', ' (also jibe) â€”v. (-bing) (often foll. By at) jeer, mock. â€”n. Jeering remark, taunt. [perhaps from french giber handle roughly]'),
(17026, 'g', '\r\nGiblets', ' n.pl. Edible organs etc. Of a bird, removed and usu. Cooked separately. [french gibelet game stew]'),
(17027, 'g', '\r\nGiddy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 dizzy, tending to fall or stagger. 2 a mentally intoxicated (giddy with success). B excitable, frivolous, flighty. 3 making dizzy (giddy heights).  giddily adv. Giddiness n. [old english]'),
(17028, 'g', '\r\nGift', ' n. 1 thing given; present. 2 natural ability or talent. 3 the power to give (in his gift). 4 giving. 5 colloq. Easy task. [old norse: related to *give]'),
(17029, 'g', '\r\nGifted', ' adj. Talented; intelligent.'),
(17030, 'g', '\r\nGift', 'of the gab  n. Colloq. Eloquence, loquacity.'),
(17031, 'g', '\r\nGift', 'token  n. (also gift voucher) voucher used as a gift and exchangeable for goods.'),
(17032, 'g', '\r\nGift-wrap', ' v. Wrap attractively as a gift.'),
(17033, 'g', '\r\nGig1', ' n. 1 light two-wheeled one-horse carriage. 2 light ship''s boat for rowing or sailing. 3 rowing-boat esp. For racing. [probably imitative]'),
(17034, 'g', '\r\nGig2', ' colloq. â€”n. Engagement to play music etc., usu. For one night. â€”v. (-gg-) perform a gig. [origin unknown]'),
(17035, 'g', '\r\nGiga-', ' comb. Form one thousand million (109). [greek: related to *giant]'),
(17036, 'g', '\r\nGigantic', ' adj. Huge, giant-like.  gigantically adv. [latin: related to *giant]'),
(17037, 'g', '\r\nGiggle', ' â€”v. (-ling) laugh in half-suppressed spasms. â€”n. 1 such a laugh. 2 colloq. Amusing person or thing; joke (did it for a giggle).  giggly adj. (-ier, -iest). [imitative]'),
(17038, 'g', '\r\nGigolo', ' n. (pl. -s) young man paid by an older woman to be her escort or lover. [french]'),
(17039, 'g', '\r\nGild1', ' v. (past part. Gilded or as adj. In sense 1 gilt) 1 cover thinly with gold. 2 tinge with a golden colour. 3 give a false brilliance to.  gild the lily try to improve what is already satisfactory. [old english: related to *gold]'),
(17040, 'g', '\r\nGild2', ' var. Of *guild.'),
(17041, 'g', '\r\nGill1', ' n. (usu. In pl.) 1 respiratory organ in a fish etc. 2 vertical radial plate on the underside of a mushroom etc. 3 flesh below a person''s jaws and ears. [old norse]'),
(17042, 'g', '\r\nGill2', ' n. Unit of liquid measure equal to 1/4 pint. [french]'),
(17043, 'g', '\r\nGill3', ' n. (also ghyll) 1 deep usu. Wooded ravine. 2 narrow mountain torrent. [old norse]'),
(17044, 'g', '\r\nGillie', ' n. (also ghillie) scot. Man or boy attending a person hunting or fishing. [gaelic]'),
(17045, 'g', '\r\nGillyflower', ' n. Clove-scented flower, e.g. A wallflower or the clove-scented pink. [french gilofre]'),
(17046, 'g', '\r\nGilt1', ' â€”adj. 1 thinly covered with gold. 2 gold-coloured. â€”n. 1 gilding. 2 gilt-edged security. [from *gild1]'),
(17047, 'g', '\r\nGilt2', ' n. Young sow. [old norse]'),
(17048, 'g', '\r\nGilt-edged', ' adj. (of securities, stocks, etc.) Having a high degree of reliability.'),
(17049, 'g', '\r\nGimbals', ' n.pl. Contrivance of rings and pivots for keeping instruments horizontal in ships, aircraft, etc. [var. Of gimmal from french gemel double finger-ring]'),
(17050, 'g', '\r\nGimcrack', ' â€”adj. Showy but flimsy and worthless. â€”n. Showy ornament; knick-knack. [origin unknown]'),
(17051, 'g', '\r\nGimlet', ' n. Small tool with a screw-tip for boring holes. [french]'),
(17052, 'g', '\r\nGimlet', 'eye  n. Eye with a piercing glance.'),
(17053, 'g', '\r\nGimmick', ' n. Trick or device, esp. To attract attention or publicity.  gimmickry n. Gimmicky adj. [origin unknown]'),
(17054, 'g', '\r\nGimp', ' n. (also gymp) 1 twist of silk etc. With cord or wire running through it. 2 fishing-line of silk etc. Bound with wire. [dutch]'),
(17055, 'g', '\r\nGin1', ' n. Spirit made from grain or malt and flavoured with juniper berries. [dutch geneva: related to *juniper]'),
(17056, 'g', '\r\nGin2', ' â€”n. 1 snare, trap. 2 machine separating cotton from its seeds. 3 a kind of crane and windlass. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 treat (cotton) in a gin. 2 trap. [french: related to *engine]'),
(17057, 'g', '\r\nGinger', ' â€”n. 1 a hot spicy root usu. Powdered for use in cooking, or preserved in syrup, or candied. B plant having this root. 2 light reddish-yellow. 3 spirit, mettle. â€”adj. Of a ginger colour. â€”v. 1 flavour with ginger. 2 (foll. By up) enliven.  gingery adj. [old english and french, ultimately from sanskrit]'),
(17058, 'g', '\r\nGinger', 'ale  n. Ginger-flavoured non-alcoholic drink.'),
(17059, 'g', '\r\nGinger', 'beer  n. Mildly alcoholic or non-alcoholic cloudy drink made from fermented ginger and syrup.'),
(17060, 'g', '\r\nGingerbread', ' â€”n. Ginger-flavoured treacle cake. â€”attrib. Adj. Gaudy, tawdry.'),
(17061, 'g', '\r\nGinger', 'group  n. Group urging a party or movement to stronger policy or action.'),
(17062, 'g', '\r\nGingerly', ' â€”adv. In a careful or cautious manner. â€”adj. Showing great care or caution. [perhaps from french gensor delicate]'),
(17063, 'g', '\r\nGinger-nut', ' n. Ginger-flavoured biscuit.'),
(17064, 'g', '\r\nGingham', ' n. Plain-woven cotton cloth, esp. Striped or checked. [dutch from malay]'),
(17065, 'g', '\r\nGingivitis', ' n. Inflammation of the gums. [latin gingiva *gum2, *-itis]'),
(17066, 'g', '\r\nGinkgo', ' n. (pl. -s) tree with fan-shaped leaves and yellow flowers. [chinese, = silver apricot]'),
(17067, 'g', '\r\nGinormous', ' adj. Slang enormous. [from *giant, *enormous]'),
(17068, 'g', '\r\nGin', 'rummy  n. Form of the card-game rummy.'),
(17069, 'g', '\r\nGinseng', ' n. 1 plant found in e. Asia and n. America. 2 root of this used as a medicinal tonic. [chinese]'),
(17070, 'g', '\r\nGippy', 'tummy  n. Colloq. Diarrhoea affecting visitors to hot countries. [from *egyptian]'),
(17071, 'g', '\r\nGipsy', ' var. Of *gypsy.'),
(17072, 'g', '\r\nGiraffe', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) large four-legged african animal with a long neck and forelegs. [french, ultimately from arabic]'),
(17073, 'g', '\r\nGird', ' v. (past and past part. Girded or girt) 1 encircle, attach, or secure, with a belt or band. 2 enclose or encircle. 3 (foll. By round) place (a cord etc.) Round.  gird (or gird up) one''s loins prepare for action. [old english]'),
(17074, 'g', '\r\nGirder', ' n. Iron or steel beam or compound structure for bridge-building etc.'),
(17075, 'g', '\r\nGirdle1', ' â€”n. 1 belt or cord worn round the waist. 2 corset. 3 thing that surrounds. 4 bony support for the limbs (pelvic girdle). â€”v. (-ling) surround with a girdle. [old english]'),
(17076, 'g', '\r\nGirdle2', ' n. Scot. & n.engl. Var. Of *griddle.'),
(17077, 'g', '\r\nGirl', ' n. 1 female child, daughter. 2 colloq. Young woman. 3 colloq. Girlfriend. 4 female servant.  girlhood n. Girlish adj. Girly adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(17078, 'g', '\r\nGirl', 'friday  n. Female helper or follower.'),
(17079, 'g', '\r\nGirlfriend', ' n. 1 person''s regular female companion or lover. 2 female friend.'),
(17080, 'g', '\r\nGirlie', ' adj. Colloq. (of a magazine etc.) Depicting young women in erotic poses.'),
(17081, 'g', '\r\nGirl', 'scout  n. = *scout n. 4.'),
(17082, 'g', '\r\nGiro', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 system of credit transfer between banks, post offices, etc. 2 cheque or payment by giro. â€”v. (-es, -ed) pay by giro. [german from italian]'),
(17083, 'g', '\r\nGirt', ' see *gird.'),
(17084, 'g', '\r\nGirth', ' n. 1 distance round a thing. 2 band round the body of a horse to secure the saddle etc. [old norse: related to *gird]'),
(17085, 'g', '\r\nGismo', ' n. (also gizmo) (pl. -s) slang gadget. [origin unknown]'),
(17086, 'g', '\r\nGist', ' n. Substance or essence of a matter. [latin jaceo *lie1]'),
(17087, 'g', '\r\nGit', ' n. Slang silly or contemptible person. [get (noun), = fool]'),
(17088, 'g', '\r\nGÃ®te', ' n. Furnished holiday house in the french countryside. [french]'),
(17089, 'g', '\r\nGive', ' â€”v. (-ving; past gave; past part. Given) 1 transfer the possession of freely; hand over as a present; donate. 2 a transfer temporarily; provide with (gave him the dog to hold; gave her a new hip). B administer (medicine). C deliver (a message). 3 (usu. Foll. By for) make over in exchange or payment. 4 a confer; grant (a benefit, honour, etc.). B accord; bestow (love, time, etc.). C pledge (gave his word). 5 a perform (an action etc.) (gave a jump; gave a performance; gave an interview). B utter; declare (gave a shriek; gave the batsman out). 6 (in passive; foll. By to) be inclined to or fond of (is given to boasting; is given to strong drink). 7 yield to pressure; collapse. 8 yield as a product or result (gives an average of 7). 9 a consign, put (gave him into custody). B sanction the marriage of (a daughter etc.). 10 devote; dedicate (gave his life to the cause). 11 present; offer; show; hold out (gives no sign of life; gave her his arm; give me an example). 12 impart; be a source of; cause (gave me a cold; gave me trouble; gave much pain). 13 concede (i give you the benefit of the doubt). 14 deliver (a judgement etc.) Authoritatively. 15 provide (a party, meal, etc.) As host. 16 (in past part.) Assume or grant or specify (given the circumstances; in a given situation; given that we earn so little). 17 (absol.) Colloq. Tell what one knows. â€”n. Capacity to yield or comply; elasticity.  give and take 1 exchange of words, ideas, blows, etc. 2 ability to compromise. Give away 1 transfer as a gift. 2 hand over (a bride) to a bridegroom. 3 reveal (a secret etc.). Give the game (or show) away reveal a secret or intention. Give in 1 yield; acknowledge defeat. 2 hand in (a document etc.) To an official etc. Give it to a person colloq. Scold or punish. Give me i prefer (give me greece any day). Give off emit (fumes etc.). Give oneself up to 1 abandon oneself to (despair etc.). 2 addict oneself to. Give on to (or into) (of a window, corridor, etc.) Overlook or lead into. Give or take colloq. Accepting as a margin of error in estimating. Give out 1 announce; emit; distribute. 2 be exhausted. 3 run short. Give over 1 colloq. Stop or desist. 2 hand over. 3 devote. Give rise to cause. Give a person to understand inform or assure. Give up 1 resign; surrender. 2 part with.'),
(17090, 'g', '\r\nGive-away', ' n. Colloq. 1 unintentional revelation. 2 thing given as a gift or at a low price.'),
(17091, 'g', '\r\nGizmo', ' var. Of *gismo.'),
(17092, 'g', '\r\nGizzard', ' n. 1 second part of a bird''s stomach, for grinding food. 2 muscular stomach of some fish etc. [french]'),
(17093, 'g', '\r\nGlacÃ©', ' adj. 1 (of fruit, esp. Cherries) preserved in sugar. 2 (of cloth etc.) Smooth; polished. [french]'),
(17094, 'g', '\r\nGlacÃ©', 'icing  n. Icing made with icing sugar and water.'),
(17095, 'g', '\r\nGlacial', ' adj. 1 of ice. 2 geol. Characterized or produced by ice. [latin glacies ice]'),
(17096, 'g', '\r\nGlacial', 'period  n. Period when an exceptionally large area was covered by ice.'),
(17097, 'g', '\r\nGlaciated', ' adj. 1 marked or polished by the action of ice. 2 covered by glaciers or ice sheets.  glaciation n. [glaciate freeze, from latin: related to *glacial]'),
(17098, 'g', '\r\nGlacier', ' n. Mass of land ice formed by the accumulation of snow on high ground. [french: related to *glacial]'),
(17099, 'g', '\r\nGlad', ' adj. (gladder, gladdest) 1 (predic.) Pleased. 2 expressing or causing pleasure (glad cry; glad news). 3 ready and willing (am glad to help).  be glad of find useful.  gladly adv. Gladness n. [old english]'),
(17100, 'g', '\r\nGladden', ' v. Make or become glad.'),
(17101, 'g', '\r\nGlade', ' n. Open space in a forest. [origin unknown]'),
(17102, 'g', '\r\nGlad', 'eye  n. (prec. By the) colloq. Amorous glance.'),
(17103, 'g', '\r\nGlad', 'hand  n. Colloq. Hearty welcome.'),
(17104, 'g', '\r\nGladiator', ' n. Hist. Trained fighter in ancient roman shows.  gladiatorial adj. [latin gladius sword]'),
(17105, 'g', '\r\nGladiolus', ' n. (pl. -li) plant of the lily family with sword-shaped leaves and flower-spikes. [latin, diminutive of gladius sword]'),
(17106, 'g', '\r\nGlad', 'rags  n.pl. Colloq. Best clothes.'),
(17107, 'g', '\r\nGladsome', ' adj. Poet. Cheerful, joyous.'),
(17108, 'g', '\r\nGladstone', 'bag  n. Bag with two compartments joined by a hinge. [gladstone, name of a statesman]'),
(17109, 'g', '\r\nGlair', ' n. 1 white of egg. 2 adhesive preparation made from this. [french]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(17110, 'g', '\r\nGlam', ' adj. Colloq. Glamorous. [abbreviation]'),
(17111, 'g', '\r\nGlamor', ' n. (brit. Glamour) 1 physical, esp. Cosmetic, attractiveness. 2 alluring or exciting beauty or charm.  glamorous adj. Glamorously adv. [var. Of *grammar in obsolete sense â€˜magicâ€™]'),
(17112, 'g', '\r\nGlamorize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make glamorous or attractive.'),
(17113, 'g', '\r\nGlamour', ' n. (us glamor) 1 physical, esp. Cosmetic, attractiveness. 2 alluring or exciting beauty or charm.  glamorous adj. Glamorously adv. [var. Of *grammar in obsolete sense â€˜magicâ€™]'),
(17114, 'g', '\r\nGlance', ' â€”v. (-cing) 1 (often foll. By down, up, over, etc.) Look briefly, direct one''s eye. 2 strike at an angle and glide off an object (glancing blow; ball glanced off his bat). 3 (usu. Foll. By over) refer briefly or indirectly to a subject or subjects. 4 (of light etc.) Flash or dart. â€”n. 1 brief look. 2 flash or gleam. 3 glancing stroke in cricket.  at a glance immediately upon looking. [origin uncertain]'),
(17115, 'g', '\r\nGland', ' n. 1 organ or similar structure secreting substances for use in the body or for ejection. 2 bot. Similar organ in a plant. [latin glandulae pl.]'),
(17116, 'g', '\r\nGlanders', ' n.pl. Contagious disease of horses. [french glandre: related to *gland]'),
(17117, 'g', '\r\nGlandular', ' adj. Of a gland or glands.'),
(17118, 'g', '\r\nGlandular', 'fever  n. Infectious disease with swelling of the lymph glands.'),
(17119, 'g', '\r\nGlare', ' â€”v. (-ring) 1 look fiercely or fixedly. 2 shine dazzlingly or oppressively. â€”n. 1 a strong fierce light, esp. Sunshine. B oppressive public attention (glare of publicity). 2 fierce or fixed look. 3 tawdry brilliance. [low german or dutch]'),
(17120, 'g', '\r\nGlaring', ' adj. 1 obvious, conspicuous (glaring error). 2 shining oppressively.  glaringly adv.'),
(17121, 'g', '\r\nGlasnost', ' n. (in the former soviet union) policy of more open government and access to information. [russian, = openness]'),
(17122, 'g', '\r\nGlass', ' â€”n. 1 a (often attrib.) Hard, brittle, usu. Transparent substance, made by fusing sand with soda and lime etc. B substance of similar properties. 2 glass objects collectively. 3 a glass drinking vessel. B its contents. 4 mirror. 5 glazed frame for plants. 6 barometer. 7 covering of a watch-face. 8 lens. 9 (in pl.) A spectacles. B binoculars. â€”v. (usu. As glassed adj.) Fit with glass.  glassful n. (pl. -s). [old english]'),
(17123, 'g', '\r\nGlass-blowing', ' n. Blowing semi-molten glass to make glassware.'),
(17124, 'g', '\r\nGlass', 'fibre  n. Filaments of glass made into fabric or embedded in plastic as reinforcement.'),
(17125, 'g', '\r\nGlasshouse', ' n. 1 greenhouse. 2 slang military prison.'),
(17126, 'g', '\r\nGlass-paper', ' n. Paper coated with glass particles, for smoothing and polishing.'),
(17127, 'g', '\r\nGlassware', ' n. Articles made of glass.'),
(17128, 'g', '\r\nGlass', 'wool  n. Mass of fine glass fibres for packing and insulation.'),
(17129, 'g', '\r\nGlassy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like glass. 2 (of the eye, expression, etc.) Abstracted; dull; fixed.'),
(17130, 'g', '\r\nGlaswegian', ' â€”adj. Of glasgow. â€”n. Native of glasgow. [after norwegian]'),
(17131, 'g', '\r\nGlaucoma', ' n. Eye-condition with increased pressure in the eyeball and gradual loss of sight.  glaucomatous adj. [greek glaukos greyish blue]'),
(17132, 'g', '\r\nGlaze', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 fit (a window etc.) With glass or (a building) with windows. 2 a cover (pottery etc.) With a glaze. B fix (paint) on pottery thus. 3 cover (pastry, cloth, etc.) With a glaze. 4 (often foll. By over) (of the eyes) become glassy. 5 give a glassy surface to. â€”n. 1 vitreous substance for glazing pottery. 2 smooth shiny coating on food etc. 3 thin coat of transparent paint to modify underlying tone. 4 surface formed by glazing. [from *glass]'),
(17133, 'g', '\r\nGlazier', ' n. Person whose trade is glazing windows etc.'),
(17134, 'g', '\r\nGleam', ' â€”n. Faint or brief light or show. â€”v. Emit gleams, shine. [old english]'),
(17135, 'g', '\r\nGlean', ' v. 1 acquire (facts etc.) In small amounts. 2 gather (corn left by reapers). [french]'),
(17136, 'g', '\r\nGleanings', ' n.pl. Things gleaned, esp. Facts.'),
(17137, 'g', '\r\nGlebe', ' n. Piece of land as part of a clergyman''s benefice and providing income. [latin gl(a)eba clod, soil]'),
(17138, 'g', '\r\nGlee', ' n. 1 mirth; delight. 2 part-song for three or more (esp. Male) voices. [old english]'),
(17139, 'g', '\r\nGleeful', ' adj. Joyful.  gleefully adv. Gleefulness n.'),
