(17627, 'g', '\r\nGrin', ' â€”v. (-nn-) 1 a smile broadly, showing the teeth. B make a forced, unrestrained, or stupid smile. 2 express by grinning. â€”n. Act of grinning.  grin and bear it take pain etc. Stoically. [old english]'),
(17628, 'g', '\r\nGrind', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Ground) 1 reduce to small particles or powder by crushing. 2 a sharpen or smooth by friction. B rub or rub together gratingly. 3 (often foll. By down) oppress; harass with exactions. 4 a (often foll. By away) work or study hard. B (foll. By out) produce with effort. â€”n. 1 act or instance of grinding. 2 colloq. Hard dull work (the daily grind). 3 size of ground particles.  grind to a halt stop laboriously.'),
(17629, 'g', '\r\nGrinder', ' n. 1 person or thing that grinds, esp. A machine. 2 molar tooth.'),
(17630, 'g', '\r\nGrindstone', ' n. 1 thick revolving disc used for grinding, sharpening, and polishing. 2 a kind of stone used for this.  keep one''s nose to the grindstone work hard and continuously.'),
(17631, 'g', '\r\nGrip', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 a grasp tightly. B take a firm hold, esp. By friction. 2 compel the attention of. â€”n. 1 a firm hold; tight grasp. B manner of grasping or holding. 2 power of holding attention. 3 a intellectual mastery. B effective control of one''s behaviour etc. (lose one''s grip). 4 a part of a machine that grips. B part by which a weapon etc. Is held. 5 = *hairgrip. 6 travelling bag.  come (or get) to grips with approach purposefully; begin to deal with. [old english]'),
(17632, 'g', '\r\nGripe', ' â€”v. (-ping) 1 colloq. Complain. 2 affect with gastric pain. â€”n. 1 (usu. In pl.) Colic. 2 colloq. Complaint. 3 grip, clutch. [old english]'),
(17633, 'g', '\r\nGripe', 'water  n. Propr. Preparation to relieve colic in infants.'),
(17634, 'g', '\r\nGrisly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) causing horror, disgust, or fear.  grisliness n. [old english]'),
(17635, 'g', '\r\nGrist', ' n. Corn to grind.  grist to the (or a person''s) mill source of profit or advantage. [old english: related to *grind]'),
(17636, 'g', '\r\nGristle', ' n. Tough flexible animal tissue; cartilage.  gristly adj. [old english]'),
(17637, 'g', '\r\nGrit', ' â€”n. 1 particles of stone or sand, esp. As irritating or hindering. 2 coarse sandstone. 3 colloq. Pluck, endurance. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 spread grit on (icy roads etc.). 2 clench (the teeth). 3 make a grating sound.  gritter n. Gritty adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(17638, 'g', '\r\nGrits', ' n.pl. 1 coarsely ground grain, esp. Oatmeal. 2 oats that have been husked but not ground. [old english]'),
