(17815, 'g', '\r\nGush', ' â€”v. 1 emit or flow in a sudden and copious stream. 2 speak or behave effusively. â€”n. 1 sudden or copious stream. 2 effusive manner. [probably imitative]'),
(17816, 'g', '\r\nGusher', ' n. 1 oil well from which oil flows without being pumped. 2 effusive person.'),
(17817, 'g', '\r\nGusset', ' n. 1 piece let into a garment etc. To strengthen or enlarge it. 2 bracket strengthening an angle of a structure. [french]'),
(17818, 'g', '\r\nGust', ' â€”n. 1 sudden strong rush of wind. 2 burst of rain, smoke, emotion, etc. â€”v. Blow in gusts.  gusty adj. (-ier, -iest). [old norse]'),
(17819, 'g', '\r\nGusto', ' n. Zest; enjoyment. [latin gustus taste]'),
(17820, 'g', '\r\nGut', ' â€”n. 1 the intestine. 2 (in pl.) The bowel or entrails. 3 (in pl.) Colloq. Personal courage and determination; perseverance. 4 slang stomach, belly. 5 (in pl.) A contents. B essence. 6 a material for violin strings etc. B material for fishing-lines made from the silk-glands of silkworms. 7 (attrib.) A instinctive (a gut reaction). B fundamental (a gut issue). â€”v. (-tt-) 1 remove or destroy the internal fittings of (a house etc.). 2 remove the guts of (a fish).  hate a person''s guts colloq. Dislike a person intensely. [old english]'),
(17821, 'g', '\r\nGutless', ' adj. Colloq. Lacking courage or energy.'),
(17822, 'g', '\r\nGutsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 courageous. 2 greedy.'),
(17823, 'g', '\r\nGutta-percha', ' n. Tough rubbery substance obtained from latex. [malay]'),
(17824, 'g', '\r\nGutted', ' adj. Slang utterly exhausted or fed-up.'),
(17825, 'g', '\r\nGutter', ' â€”n. 1 shallow trough below the eaves of a house, or a channel at the side of a street, to carry off rainwater. 2 (prec. By the) poor or degraded background or environment. 3 open conduit. 4 groove. â€”v. (of a candle) burn unsteadily and melt away rapidly. [latin gutta drop]'),
(17826, 'g', '\r\nGuttering', ' n. 1 gutters of a building etc. 2 material for gutters.'),
(17827, 'g', '\r\nGutter', 'press  n. Sensational newspapers.'),
(17828, 'g', '\r\nGuttersnipe', ' n. Street urchin.'),
(17829, 'g', '\r\nGuttural', ' â€”adj. 1 throaty, harsh-sounding. 2 phonet. (of a consonant) produced in the throat or by the back of the tongue and palate. 3 of the throat. â€”n. Phonet. Guttural consonant (e.g. K, g).  gutturally adv. [latin guttur throat]'),
(17830, 'g', '\r\nGuv', ' n. Slang = *governor 7. [abbreviation]'),
(17831, 'g', '\r\nGuy1', ' â€”n. 1 colloq. Man; fellow. 2 effigy of guy fawkes burnt on 5 nov.  â€”v. Ridicule. [guy fawkes, name of a conspirator]'),
(17832, 'g', '\r\nGuy2', ' â€”n. Rope or chain to secure a tent or steady a crane-load etc. â€”v. Secure with a guy or guys. [probably low german]'),
(17833, 'g', '\r\nGuzzle', ' v. (-ling) eat or drink greedily. [probably french gosiller from gosier throat]'),
(17834, 'g', '\r\nGybe', ' v. (us jibe) (-bing) 1 (of a fore-and-aft sail or boom) swing across. 2 cause (a sail) to do this. 3 (of a ship or its crew) change course so that this happens. [dutch]'),
(17835, 'g', '\r\nGym', ' n. Colloq. 1 gymnasium. 2 gymnastics. [abbreviation]'),
(17836, 'g', '\r\nGymkhana', ' n. Horse-riding competition. [hindustani gendkhana ball-house, assimilated to *gymnasium]'),
(17837, 'g', '\r\nGymnasium', ' n. (pl. -s or -sia) room or building equipped for gymnastics. [greek gumnos naked]'),
