(17875, 'h', '\r\nHacienda', ' n. (in spanish-speaking countries) estate with a dwelling-house. [spanish, from latin facienda things to be done]'),
(17876, 'h', '\r\nHack1', ' â€”v. 1 cut or chop roughly. 2 football etc. Kick the shin of (an opponent). 3 (often foll. By at) deliver cutting blows. 4 cut (one''s way) through foliage etc. 5 colloq. Gain unauthorized access to (data in a computer). 6 slang manage, cope with; tolerate. 7 (as hacking adj.) (of a cough) short, dry, and frequent. â€”n. 1 kick with the toe of a boot. 2 gash or wound, esp. From a kick. 3 a mattock. B miner''s pick. [old english]'),
(17877, 'h', '\r\nHack2', ' â€”n. 1 a = *hackney. B horse let out for hire. 2 person hired to do dull routine work, esp. Writing. â€”attrib. Adj. 1 used as a hack. 2 typical of a hack; commonplace (hack work). â€”v. Ride on horseback on a road at an ordinary pace. [abbreviation of *hackney]'),
(17878, 'h', '\r\nHacker', ' n. 1 person or thing that hacks or cuts roughly. 2 colloq. A person whose hobby is computing or computer programming. B person who uses a computer to gain unauthorized access to a computer network.'),
(17879, 'h', '\r\nHackle', ' n. 1 a (in pl.) Erectile hairs on an animal''s neck, rising when it is angry or alarmed. B feather(s) on the neck of a domestic cock etc. 2 steel comb for dressing flax.  make one''s hackles rise cause one to be angry or indignant. [old english]'),
(17880, 'h', '\r\nHackney', ' n. (pl. -s) horse for ordinary riding. [hackney in london]'),
(17881, 'h', '\r\nHackney', 'carriage  n. Taxi.'),
(17882, 'h', '\r\nHackneyed', ' adj. (of a phrase etc.) Made trite by overuse.'),
