(17904, 'h', '\r\nHagio-', ' comb. Form of saints. [greek hagios holy]'),
(17905, 'h', '\r\nHagiography', ' n. Writing about saints'' lives.  hagiographer n.'),
(17906, 'h', '\r\nHagiology', ' n. Literature dealing with the lives and legends of saints.'),
(17907, 'h', '\r\nHagridden', ' adj. Afflicted by nightmares or anxieties.'),
(17908, 'h', '\r\nHah', ' var. Of *ha1.'),
(17909, 'h', '\r\nHa', 'ha  int. Representing laughter (iron. When spoken). [old english]'),
(17910, 'h', '\r\nHa-ha', ' n. Ditch with a wall in it, forming a boundary or fence without interrupting the view. [french]'),
(17911, 'h', '\r\nHahnium', ' n. Artificially produced radioactive element. [hahn, name of a chemist]'),
(17912, 'h', '\r\nHaiku', ' n. (pl. Same) japanese three-part poem of usu. 17 syllables. [japanese]'),
(17913, 'h', '\r\nHail1', ' â€”n. 1 pellets of frozen rain. 2 (foll. By of) barrage or onslaught. â€”v. 1 a (prec. By it as subject) hail falls. B come down forcefully. 2 pour down (blows, words, etc.). [old english]'),
(17914, 'h', '\r\nHail2', ' â€”v. 1 signal to (a taxi etc.) To stop. 2 greet enthusiastically. 3 acclaim (hailed him king). 4 (foll. By from) originate or come (hails from leeds). â€”int. Archaic or joc. Expressing greeting. â€”n. Act of hailing. [old norse heill: related to *wassail]'),
(17915, 'h', '\r\nHail-fellow-well-met', ' adj. Friendly, esp. Too friendly towards strangers.'),
(17916, 'h', '\r\nHail', 'mary  n. The ave maria (see *ave).'),
(17917, 'h', '\r\nHailstone', ' n. Pellet of hail.'),
(17918, 'h', '\r\nHailstorm', ' n. Period of heavy hail.'),
(17919, 'h', '\r\nHair', ' n. 1 a any of the fine threadlike strands growing from the skin of mammals, esp. From the human head. B these collectively (has long hair). 2 thing resembling a hair. 3 elongated cell growing from a plant. 4 very small quantity or extent (also attrib.: hair crack).  get in a person''s hair colloq. Annoy a person. Keep one''s hair on colloq. Keep calm; not get angry. Let one''s hair down colloq. Enjoy oneself by abandoning restraint. Make one''s hair stand on end colloq. Horrify one. Not turn a hair remain unmoved or unaffected.  hairless adj. [old english]'),
(17920, 'h', '\r\nHairbrush', ' n. Brush for tidying the hair.'),
(17921, 'h', '\r\nHaircloth', ' n. Stiff cloth woven from hair.'),
(17922, 'h', '\r\nHaircut', ' n. 1 act of cutting the hair (needs a haircut). 2 style in which the hair is cut.'),
(17923, 'h', '\r\nHairdo', ' n. (pl. -s) style of or act of styling the hair.'),
(17924, 'h', '\r\nHairdresser', ' n. 1 person who cuts and styles the hair, esp. For a living. 2 hairdresser''s shop.  hairdressing n.'),
(17925, 'h', '\r\nHair-drier', ' n. (also hair-dryer) device for drying the hair with warm air.'),
(17926, 'h', '\r\nHairgrip', ' n. Flat hairpin with the ends close together.'),
(17927, 'h', '\r\nHairline', ' n. 1 edge of a person''s hair, esp. On the forehead. 2 very narrow line, crack (usu. Hairline crack), etc.'),
(17928, 'h', '\r\nHairnet', ' n. Piece of netting for confining the hair.'),
(17929, 'h', '\r\nHair', 'of the dog  n. Further alcoholic drink taken to cure the effects of drink.'),
(17930, 'h', '\r\nHairpiece', ' n. Quantity of hair augmenting a person''s natural hair.'),
(17931, 'h', '\r\nHairpin', ' n. U-shaped pin for fastening the hair.'),
(17932, 'h', '\r\nHairpin', 'bend  n. Sharp u-shaped bend in a road.'),
(17933, 'h', '\r\nHair-raising', ' adj. Terrifying.'),
(17934, 'h', '\r\nHair''s', 'breadth  n. A tiny amount or margin.'),
(17935, 'h', '\r\nHair', 'shirt  n. Shirt of haircloth, worn formerly by penitents and ascetics.'),
(17936, 'h', '\r\nHair-slide', ' n. Clip for keeping the hair in place.'),
(17937, 'h', '\r\nHair-splitting', ' adj. & n. Quibbling.'),
(17938, 'h', '\r\nHairspray', ' n. Liquid sprayed on the hair to keep it in place.'),
(17939, 'h', '\r\nHairspring', ' n. Fine spring regulating the balance-wheel in a watch.'),
(17940, 'h', '\r\nHairstyle', ' n. Particular way of arranging the hair.  hairstylist n.'),
(17941, 'h', '\r\nHair-trigger', ' n. Trigger of a firearm set for release at the slightest pressure.'),
(17942, 'h', '\r\nHairy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 covered with hair. 2 slang frightening, dangerous.  hairiness n.'),
(17943, 'h', '\r\nHajj', ' n. (also hadj) islamic pilgrimage to mecca. [arabic]'),
(17944, 'h', '\r\nHajji', ' n. (also hadji) (pl. -s) muslim who has made the pilgrimage to mecca. [persian from arabic]'),
(17945, 'h', '\r\nHaka', ' n. Nz 1 maori ceremonial war dance with chanting. 2 imitation of this by a sports team before a match. [maori]'),
(17946, 'h', '\r\nHake', ' n. (pl. Same) marine fish resembling the cod, used as food. [origin uncertain]'),
(17947, 'h', '\r\nHalal', ' n. (also hallal) (often attrib.) Meat from an animal killed according to muslim law. [arabic]'),
(17948, 'h', '\r\nHalberd', ' n. Hist. Combined spear and battleaxe. [french from german]'),
(17949, 'h', '\r\nHalcyon', ' adj. Calm, peaceful, happy (halcyon days). [greek, = kingfisher, because it was reputed to calm the sea at midwinter]'),
(17950, 'h', '\r\nHale', ' adj. Strong and healthy (esp. In hale and hearty). [var. Of *whole]'),
(17951, 'h', '\r\nHalf', ' â€”n. (pl. Halves) 1 either of two (esp. Equal) parts into which a thing is divided. 2 colloq. Half a pint, esp. Of beer. 3 sport either of two equal periods of play. 4 colloq. Half-price fare or ticket, esp. For a child. 5 colloq. = *half-back. â€”adj. 1 amounting to half (half the men). 2 forming a half (a half share). â€”adv. 1 (often in comb.) To the extent of half; partly (half cooked). 2 to some extent (esp. In idiomatic phrases: half dead; am half convinced). 3 (in reckoning time) by the amount of half (an hour etc.) (half past two).  at half cock see *cock1. By half (prec. By too + adj.) Excessively (too clever by half). By halves imperfectly or incompletely (does nothing by halves). Half a mind see *mind. Half the time see *time. Not half 1 slang extremely, violently (he didn''t half swear). 2 not nearly (not half long enough). 3 colloq. Not at all (not half bad). [old english]'),
(17952, 'h', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 3 of the adverb, the word â€˜pastâ€™ is often omitted in colloquial usage, e.g. Came at half two. In some parts of scotland and ireland this means â€˜half past oneâ€™.'),
(17953, 'h', '\r\nHalf-and-half', ' adj. Being half one thing and half another.'),
(17954, 'h', '\r\nHalf-back', ' n. Sport player between the forwards and full backs.'),
(17955, 'h', '\r\nHalf-baked', ' adj. Colloq. 1 not thoroughly thought out; foolish. 2 (of enthusiasm etc.) Only partly committed.'),
(17956, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'board  n. Provision of bed, breakfast, and one main meal at a hotel etc.'),
(17957, 'h', '\r\nHalf-breed', ' n. Offens. = *half-caste.'),
(17958, 'h', '\r\nHalf-brother', ' n. Brother with whom one has only one parent in common.'),
(17959, 'h', '\r\nHalf-caste', ' n. Offens. Person of mixed race.'),
(17960, 'h', '\r\nHalf-crown', ' n. (also half a crown) former coin and monetary unit worth 2s. 6d. (121/2p).'),
(17961, 'h', '\r\nHalf-cut', ' adj.  Slang fairly drunk.'),
(17962, 'h', '\r\nHalf-dozen', ' n. (also half a dozen) colloq. Six, or about six.'),
(17963, 'h', '\r\nHalf-duplex', ' n. Computing (of a circuit) allowing the two-way transmission of signals but not simultaneously.'),
(17964, 'h', '\r\nHalf-hardy', ' adj. (of a plant) able to grow in the open except in severe frost.'),
(17965, 'h', '\r\nHalf-hearted', ' adj. Lacking enthusiasm.  half-heartedly adv. Half-heartedness n.'),
(17966, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'hitch  n. Knot formed by passing the end of a rope round its standing part and then through the loop.'),
(17967, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'holiday  n. Half a day as holiday.'),
(17968, 'h', '\r\nHalf-hour', ' n. 1 (also half an hour) period of 30 minutes. 2 point of time 30 minutes after any hour o''clock.  half-hourly adj. & adv.'),
(17969, 'h', '\r\nHalf-life', ' n. Time taken for radioactivity etc. To fall to half its original value.'),
(17970, 'h', '\r\nHalf-light', ' n. Dim imperfect light.'),
(17971, 'h', '\r\nHalf-mast', ' n. Position of a flag halfway down a mast, as a mark of respect for a deceased person.'),
(17972, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'measures  n.pl. Unsatisfactory compromise or inadequate policy.'),
(17973, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'moon  n. 1 moon when only half its surface is illuminated. 2 time when this occurs. 3 semicircular object.'),
(17974, 'h', '\r\nHalf', 'nelson  see *nelson.'),
(17975, 'h', '\r\nHalfpenny', ' n. (pl. -pennies or -pence) former coin worth half a penny.'),
(17976, 'h', '\r\nUsage', ' the halfpenny was withdrawn from circulation in 1984.'),
(17977, 'h', '\r\nHalf-sister', ' n. Sister with whom one has only one parent in common.'),
(17978, 'h', '\r\nHalf-term', ' n. Short holiday halfway through a school term.'),
(17979, 'h', '\r\nHalf-timbered', ' adj. Having walls with a timber frame and a brick or plaster filling.'),
(17980, 'h', '\r\nHalf-time', ' n. 1 mid-point of a game or contest. 2 short break occurring at this time.'),
(17981, 'h', '\r\nHalf-title', ' n. Title or short title of a book printed on the front of the leaf preceding the title-page.'),
(17982, 'h', '\r\nHalftone', ' n. Photographic illustration in which various tones of grey are produced from small and large black dots.'),
(17983, 'h', '\r\nHalf-truth', ' n. Statement that (esp. Deliberately) conveys only part of the truth.'),
(17984, 'h', '\r\nHalf-volley', ' n. (in ball games) playing of the ball as soon as it bounces off the ground.'),
(17985, 'h', '\r\nHalfway', ' â€”adv. 1 at a point midway between two others (halfway to rome). 2 to some extent, more or less (is halfway acceptable). â€”adj. Situated halfway (reached a halfway point).'),
(17986, 'h', '\r\nHalfway', 'house  n. 1 compromise. 2 halfway point in a progression. 3 centre for rehabilitating ex-prisoners etc. 4 inn midway between two towns.'),
(17987, 'h', '\r\nHalfwit', ' n. Foolish or stupid person.  halfwitted adj.'),
(17988, 'h', '\r\nHalibut', ' n. (pl. Same) large marine flat-fish used as food. [from *holy (perhaps because eaten on holy days), butt flat-fish]'),
(17989, 'h', '\r\nHalitosis', ' n. = *bad breath. [latin halitus breath]'),
(17990, 'h', '\r\nHall', ' n. 1 area into which the front entrance of a house etc. Opens. 2 large room or building for meetings, concerts, etc. 3 large country house or estate. 4 (in full hall of residence) residence for students. 5 (in a college etc.) Dining-room. 6 premises of a guild (fishmongers'' hall). 7 large public room in a palace etc. [old english]'),
(17991, 'h', '\r\nHallal', ' var. Of *halal.'),
(17992, 'h', '\r\nHallelujah', ' var. Of *alleluia.'),
(17993, 'h', '\r\nHalliard', ' var. Of *halyard.'),
(17994, 'h', '\r\nHallmark', ' â€”n. 1 mark indicating the standard of gold, silver, and platinum. 2 distinctive feature. â€”v. Stamp with a hallmark.'),
(17995, 'h', '\r\nHallo', ' var. Of *hello.'),
(17996, 'h', '\r\nHalloo', ' int. Inciting dogs to the chase or calling attention. [perhaps from hallow pursue with shouts]'),
(17997, 'h', '\r\nHallow', ' v. 1 make holy, consecrate. 2 honour as holy. [old english: related to *holy]'),
(17998, 'h', '\r\nHallowe''en', ' n. Eve of all saints'' day, 31 oct.'),
(17999, 'h', '\r\nHallucinate', ' v. (-ting) experience hallucinations.  hallucinant adj. & n. [greek alusso be uneasy]'),
(18000, 'h', '\r\nHallucination', ' n. Illusion of seeing or hearing something not actually present.  hallucinatory adj.'),
(18001, 'h', '\r\nHallucinogen', ' n. Drug causing hallucinations.  hallucinogenic adj.'),
(18002, 'h', '\r\nHallway', ' n. Entrance-hall or corridor.'),
(18003, 'h', '\r\nHalm', ' var. Of *haulm.'),
(18004, 'h', '\r\nHalo', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) 1 disc or circle of light shown surrounding the head of a sacred person. 2 glory associated with an idealized person etc. 3 circle of white or coloured light round a luminous body, esp. The sun or moon. â€”v. (-es, -ed) surround with a halo. [greek halos threshing-floor, disc of the sun or moon]'),
(18005, 'h', '\r\nHalogen', ' n. Any of the non-metallic elements (fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine, and astatine) which form a salt (e.g. Sodium chloride) when combined with a metal. [greek hals halos salt, *-gen]'),
(18006, 'h', '\r\nHalon', ' n. Any of various gaseous compounds of carbon, bromine, and other halogens, used to extinguish fires. [related to *halogen]'),
(18007, 'h', '\r\nHalt1', ' â€”n. 1 stop (usu. Temporary) (come to a halt). 2 minor stopping-place on a local railway line. â€”v. Stop; come or bring to a halt.  call a halt (to) decide to stop. [german: related to *hold1]'),
(18008, 'h', '\r\nHalt2', ' â€”v. (esp. As halting adj.) Proceed hesitantly. â€”adj. Archaic lame.  haltingly adv. [old english]'),
(18009, 'h', '\r\nHalter', ' n. 1 headstall and rope for leading or tying up a horse etc. 2 a strap round the neck holding a dress etc. Up and leaving the shoulders and back bare. B (also halterneck) dress etc. Held by this. [old english]'),
(18010, 'h', '\r\nHalva', ' n. Confection of sesame flour and honey etc. [yiddish from turkish helva from arabic halwa]'),
(18011, 'h', '\r\nHalve', ' v. (-ving) 1 divide into two halves or parts; share equally between two. 2 reduce by half. 3 golf use the same number of strokes as one''s opponent in (a hole or match).'),
(18012, 'h', '\r\nHalves', ' pl. Of *half.'),
(18013, 'h', '\r\nHalyard', ' n. (also halliard) rope or tackle for raising or lowering a sail, yard, etc. [archaic hale drag forcibly]'),
(18014, 'h', '\r\nHam', ' â€”n. 1 a upper part of a pig''s leg salted and dried or smoked for food. B meat from this. 2 back of the thigh; thigh and buttock. 3 colloq. (often attrib.) Inexpert or unsubtle actor or piece of acting. 4 colloq. Operator of an amateur radio station. â€”v. (-mm-) (usu. In ham it up) colloq. Overact. [old english]'),
(18015, 'h', '\r\nHamburger', ' n. Cake of minced beef, usu. Eaten in a soft bread roll. [hamburg in germany]'),
(18016, 'h', '\r\nHam-fisted', ' adj. (also ham-handed) colloq. Clumsy.'),
(18017, 'h', '\r\nHamitic', ' â€”n. Group of african languages including ancient egyptian and berber. â€”adj. Of this group. [from the name ham (gen. 10:6 ff.)]'),
(18018, 'h', '\r\nHamlet', ' n. Small village, esp. Without a church. [french hamelet diminutive]'),
(18019, 'h', '\r\nHammer', ' â€”n. 1 a tool with a heavy metal head at right angles to its handle, used for driving nails etc. B similar device, as for exploding the charge in a gun, striking the strings of a piano, etc. 2 auctioneer''s mallet. 3 metal ball attached to a wire for throwing in an athletic contest. â€”v. 1 a hit or beat with or as with a hammer. B strike loudly. 2 a drive in (nails) with a hammer. B fasten or secure by hammering (hammered the lid down). 3 (usu. Foll. By in) inculcate (ideas, knowledge, etc.) Forcefully or repeatedly. 4 colloq. Defeat utterly; beat up. 5 (foll. By at, away at) work hard or persistently at.  come under the hammer be sold at auction. Hammer out 1 make flat or smooth by hammering. 2 work out details of (a plan etc.) Laboriously. 3 play (a tune, esp. On the piano) loudly or clumsily.  hammering n. (esp. In sense 4 of v.). [old english]'),
(18020, 'h', '\r\nHammer', 'and sickle  n. Symbols of the industrial worker and peasant used as an emblem of the former ussr and international communism.'),
(18021, 'h', '\r\nHammer', 'and tongs  adv. Colloq. With great vigour and commotion.'),
(18022, 'h', '\r\nHammerhead', ' n. Shark with a flattened head and with eyes in lateral extensions of it.'),
(18023, 'h', '\r\nHammerlock', ' n. Wrestling hold in which the arm is twisted and bent behind the back.'),
(18024, 'h', '\r\nHammer-toe', ' n. Toe bent permanently downwards.'),
(18025, 'h', '\r\nHammock', ' n. Bed of canvas or rope network suspended by cords at the ends. [spanish from carib]'),
(18026, 'h', '\r\nHammy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Over-theatrical.'),
(18027, 'h', '\r\nHamper1', ' n. Large basket, usu. With a hinged lid and containing food. [french hanap goblet]'),
(18028, 'h', '\r\nHamper2', ' v. Prevent the free movement of; hinder. [origin unknown]'),
(18029, 'h', '\r\nHamster', ' n. Mouselike rodent with a short tail and large cheek-pouches for storing food. [german]'),
(18030, 'h', '\r\nHamstring', ' â€”n. 1 each of five tendons at the back of the knee. 2 great tendon at the back of the hock in quadrupeds. â€”v. (past and past part. -strung or -stringed) 1 cripple by cutting the hamstrings of (a person or animal). 2 impair the activity or efficiency of.'),
(18031, 'h', '\r\nHand', ' â€”n. 1 a end part of the human arm beyond the wrist. B (in other primates) end part of a forelimb. 2 a (often in pl.) Control, management, custody, disposal (is in good hands). B agency or influence (suffered at their hands). C share in an action; active support (had a hand in it; give me a hand). 3 thing like a hand, esp. The pointer of a clock. 4 right or left side or direction relative to a person or thing. 5 a skill (has a hand for making pastry). B person skilful in some respect. 6 person who does or makes something, esp. Distinctively (picture by the same hand). 7 person''s writing or its style. 8 person etc. As a source (at first hand). 9 pledge of marriage. 10 manual worker, esp. At a factory or farm; member of a ship''s crew. 11 a playing-cards dealt to a player. B round of play. 12 colloq. Burst of applause. 13 unit of measure of a horse''s height, 4 inches (10.16 cm). 14 forehock of pork. 15 (attrib.) A operated by or held in the hand (hand-drill). B done by hand, not machine (hand-knitted). â€”v. 1 (foll. By in, to, over, etc.) Deliver; transfer by hand or otherwise. 2 colloq. Give away too readily (handed them the advantage).  all hands entire crew or workforce. At hand 1 close by. 2 about to happen. By hand 1 by a person, not a machine. 2 delivered privately, not by post. From hand to mouth satisfying only one''s immediate needs. Get (or have or keep) one''s hand in become (or be or remain) in practice. Hand down 1 pass ownership or use of to a later generation etc. 2 a transmit (a decision) from a higher court etc. B us express (an opinion or verdict). Hand it to colloq. Award deserved praise to. Hand on pass (a thing) to the next in a series. Hand out 1 serve, distribute.'),
(18032, 'h', '\r\nHandbag', ' n. Small bag carried esp. By a woman.'),
(18033, 'h', '\r\nHandball', ' n. 1 game with a ball thrown by hand among players or against a wall. 2 football intentional touching of the ball, constituting a foul.'),
(18034, 'h', '\r\nHandbell', ' n. Small bell for ringing by hand, esp. One of a set.'),
(18035, 'h', '\r\nHandbill', ' n. Printed notice distributed by hand.'),
(18036, 'h', '\r\nHandbook', ' n. Short manual or guidebook.'),
(18037, 'h', '\r\nHandbrake', ' n. Brake operated by hand.'),
(18038, 'h', '\r\nH.', '& c.  Abbr. Hot and cold (water).'),
(18039, 'h', '\r\nHandcart', ' n. Small cart pushed or drawn by hand.'),
(18040, 'h', '\r\nHandclap', ' n. Clapping of the hands.'),
(18041, 'h', '\r\nHandcraft', ' â€”n. = *handicraft. â€”v. Make by handicraft.'),
(18042, 'h', '\r\nHandcuff', ' â€”n. Each of a pair of linked metal rings for securing a prisoner''s wrist(s). â€”v. Put handcuffs on.'),
(18043, 'h', '\r\nHandful', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 quantity that fills the hand. 2 small number or amount. 3 colloq. Troublesome person or task.'),
(18044, 'h', '\r\nHand-grenade', ' see *grenade.'),
(18045, 'h', '\r\nHandgun', ' n. Small firearm held in and fired with one hand.'),
(18046, 'h', '\r\nHandhold', ' n. Something for the hand to grip on (in climbing etc.).'),
(18047, 'h', '\r\nHandicap', ' â€”n. 1 physical or mental disability. 2 thing that makes progress or success difficult. 3 a disadvantage imposed on a superior competitor to make chances more equal. B race etc. In which this is imposed. 4 number of strokes by which a golfer normally exceeds par for a course. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 impose a handicap on. 2 place at a disadvantage. [hand i'' (= in) cap describing a kind of sporting lottery]'),
(18048, 'h', '\r\nHandicapped', ' adj. Suffering from a physical or mental disability.'),
(18049, 'h', '\r\nHandicraft', ' n. Work requiring manual and artistic skill. [from earlier *handcraft]'),
(18050, 'h', '\r\nHand', 'in glove  adj. In collusion or association.'),
(18051, 'h', '\r\nHand', 'in hand  adv. 1 in close association (power and money go hand in hand). 2 (hand-in-hand) holding hands.'),
(18052, 'h', '\r\nHandiwork', ' n. Work done or a thing made by hand, or by a particular person. [old english]'),
(18053, 'h', '\r\nHandkerchief', ' n. (pl. -s or -chieves) square of cloth for wiping one''s nose etc.'),
(18054, 'h', '\r\nHandle', ' â€”n. 1 part by which a thing is held, carried, or controlled. 2 fact that may be taken advantage of (gave a handle to his critics). 3 colloq. Personal title. â€”v. (-ling) 1 touch, feel, operate, or move with the hands. 2 manage, deal with (can handle people). 3 deal in (goods). 4 treat (a subject). [old english: related to *hand]'),
(18055, 'h', '\r\nHandlebar', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Steering-bar of a bicycle etc.'),
(18056, 'h', '\r\nHandlebar', 'moustache  n. Thick moustache with curved ends.'),
(18057, 'h', '\r\nHandler', ' n. 1 person who handles or deals in something. 2 person who trains and looks after an animal (esp. A police dog).'),
(18058, 'h', '\r\nHandmade', ' adj. Made by hand (as opposed to machine).'),
(18059, 'h', '\r\nHandmaid', ' n. (also handmaiden) archaic female servant.'),
(18060, 'h', '\r\nHand-me-down', ' n. Article of clothing etc. Passed on from another person.'),
(18061, 'h', '\r\nHand-out', ' n. 1 thing given free to a needy person. 2 statement given to the press etc.; notes given out in a class etc.'),
(18062, 'h', '\r\nHand-over', ' n. Handing over.'),
(18063, 'h', '\r\nHand-over-fist', ' adv. Colloq. With rapid progress.'),
(18064, 'h', '\r\nHand-pick', ' v. Choose carefully or personally.'),
(18065, 'h', '\r\nHandrail', ' n. Narrow rail for holding as a support.'),
(18066, 'h', '\r\nHandsaw', ' n. Saw worked by one hand.'),
(18067, 'h', '\r\nHandset', ' n. Telephone mouthpiece and earpiece as one unit.'),
(18068, 'h', '\r\nHandshake', ' n. Clasping of a person''s hand as a greeting etc.'),
(18069, 'h', '\r\nHands', 'off  â€”int. Warning not to touch or interfere with something. â€”adj. & adv. (also hands-off) not requiring the manual use of controls.'),
(18070, 'h', '\r\nHandsome', ' adj. (handsomer, handsomest) 1 (usu. Of a man) good-looking. 2 (of an object) imposing, attractive. 3 a generous, liberal (handsome present). B (of a price, fortune, etc.) Considerable.  handsomely adv.'),
(18071, 'h', '\r\nHands', 'on  (also hands-on) â€”adj. & adv. Of or requiring personal operation at a keyboard. â€”attrib. Adj. Practical rather than theoretical (lacks hands-on experience).'),
(18072, 'h', '\r\nHandspring', ' n. Gymnastic feat consisting of a handstand, somersaulting, and landing in a standing position.'),
(18073, 'h', '\r\nHandstand', ' n. Supporting oneself on one''s hands with one''s feet in the air.'),
(18074, 'h', '\r\nHand-to-hand', ' adj. (of fighting) at close quarters.'),
(18075, 'h', '\r\nHandwork', ' n. Work done with the hands.  handworked adj.'),
(18076, 'h', '\r\nHandwriting', ' n. 1 writing done with a pen, pencil, etc. 2 person''s particular style of this.  handwritten adj.'),
(18077, 'h', '\r\nHandy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 convenient to handle or use; useful. 2 ready to hand. 3 clever with the hands.  handily adv. Handiness n.'),
(18078, 'h', '\r\nHandyman', ' n. Person able to do occasional repairs etc.; odd-job man.'),
(18079, 'h', '\r\nHang', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Hung except in sense 7) 1 a secure or cause to be supported from above, esp. With the lower part free. B (foll. By up, on, on to, etc.) Attach by suspending from the top. 2 set up (a door etc.) On hinges. 3 place (a picture) on a wall or in an exhibition. 4 attach (wallpaper) to a wall. 5 (foll. By on) colloq. Blame (a thing) on (a person) (can''t hang that on me). 6 (foll. By with) decorate by suspending pictures etc. (hall hung with tapestries). 7 (past and past part. Hanged) a suspend or be suspended by the neck with a noosed rope until dead, esp. As a form of capital punishment. B as a mild oath (hang the expense). 8 let droop (hang one''s head). 9 suspend (meat or game) from a hook and leave until dry, tender, or high. 10 be or remain hung (in various senses). 11 remain static in the air. 12 (often foll. By over) be present or imminent, esp. Oppressively or threateningly (a hush hung over the room). 13 (foll. By on) a be contingent or dependent on (everything hangs on his reply). B listen closely to (hangs on my every word). â€”n. Way a thing hangs or falls.  get the hang of colloq. Understand the technique or meaning of. Hang about (or around) 1 a stand about or spend time aimlessly; not move away. B linger near (a person or place). 2 (often foll. By with) colloq. Associate with. Hang back show reluctance to act or move. Hang fire be slow in taking action or in progressing. Hang heavily (or heavy) (of time) seem to pass slowly. Hang in us colloq. 1 persist, persevere. 2 linger. Hang on 1 (often foll. By to) continue to hold or grasp. 2 (foll. By to) retain; fail to give back. 3 colloq. A wait for a short time. B (in telephoning) not ring off during a pause in the conversation. 4 colloq. Continue; persevere. Hang out 1 suspend from a window, clothes-line, etc. 2 a protrude downwards (shirt hanging out). B (foll. By of) lean out of (a window etc.). 3 slang frequent or live in a place. Hang together 1 make sense. 2 remain associated. Hang up 1 hang from a hook etc. 2 (often foll. By on) end a telephone conversation by replacing the receiver (he hung up on me). 3 (usu. In passive, foll. By on) slang be a psychological problem or obsession for (is hung up on her father). Not care (or give) a hang colloq. Not care at all. [old english] '),
(18080, 'h', '\r\nHangar', ' n. Building for housing aircraft etc. [french]'),
(18081, 'h', '\r\nHangdog', ' adj. Shamefaced.'),
