(18114, 'h', '\r\nHarbor', ' (brit. Harbour) â€”n. 1 place of shelter for ships. 2 shelter; refuge. â€”v. 1 give shelter to (esp. A criminal). 2 keep in one''s mind (esp. Resentment etc.). [old english, = army shelter]'),
(18115, 'h', '\r\nHarbour', ' (us harbor) â€”n. 1 place of shelter for ships. 2 shelter; refuge. â€”v. 1 give shelter to (esp. A criminal). 2 keep in one''s mind (esp. Resentment etc.). [old english, = army shelter]'),
(18116, 'h', '\r\nHarbour-master', ' n. Official in charge of a harbour.'),
(18117, 'h', '\r\nHard', ' â€”adj. 1 (of a substance etc.) Firm and solid. 2 a difficult to understand, explain, or accomplish. B (foll. By to + infin.) Not easy to (hard to please). 3 difficult to bear (a hard life). 4 unfeeling; severely critical. 5 (of a season or the weather) severe. 6 unpleasant to the senses, harsh (hard colours). 7 a strenuous, enthusiastic, intense (a hard worker). B severe, uncompromising (a hard bargain). C polit. Extreme; most radical (the hard right). 8 a (of liquor) strongly alcoholic. B (of drugs) potent and addictive. C (of pornography) highly obscene. 9 (of water) containing mineral salts that make lathering difficult. 10 established; not disputable (hard facts). 11 (of currency, prices, etc.) High; not likely to fall in value. 12 (of a consonant) guttural (as c in cat, g in go). â€”adv. Strenuously, intensely, copiously (try hard; raining hard).  be hard on 1 be difficult for. 2 be severe in one''s treatment or criticism of. 3 be unpleasant to (the senses). Be hard put to it (usu. Foll. By to + infin.) Find it difficult. Hard by close by. Hard on (or upon) close to in pursuit etc.  hardish adj. Hardness n. [old english]'),
(18118, 'h', '\r\nHard', 'and fast  adj. (of a rule or distinction) definite, unalterable, strict.'),
(18119, 'h', '\r\nHardback', ' â€”adj. Bound in boards covered with cloth etc. â€”n. Hardback book.'),
(18120, 'h', '\r\nHardbitten', ' adj. Colloq. Tough and cynical.'),
(18121, 'h', '\r\nHardboard', ' n. Stiff board made of compressed and treated wood pulp.'),
(18122, 'h', '\r\nHard-boiled', ' adj. 1 (of an egg) boiled until the white and yolk are solid. 2 colloq. (of a person) tough, shrewd.'),
(18123, 'h', '\r\nHard', 'cash  n. Negotiable coins and banknotes.'),
(18124, 'h', '\r\nHard', 'copy  n. Material printed by a computer on paper.'),
(18125, 'h', '\r\nHardcore', ' n. Solid material, esp. Rubble, as road-foundation.'),
(18126, 'h', '\r\nHard', 'core  n. 1 irreducible nucleus. 2 colloq. A the most committed members of a society etc. B conservative or reactionary minority (see also *hardcore).'),
(18127, 'h', '\r\nHard-core', ' adj. 1 forming a nucleus. 2 blatant, uncompromising. 3 (of pornography) explicit, obscene.'),
(18128, 'h', '\r\nHard', 'disk  n. Computing large-capacity rigid usu. Magnetic storage disk.'),
(18129, 'h', '\r\nHard-done-by', ' adj. Unfairly treated.');
