(18182, 'h', '\r\nHarridan', ' n. Bad-tempered old woman. [origin uncertain]'),
(18183, 'h', '\r\nHarrier', ' n. 1 hound used for hunting hares. 2 group of cross-country runners. 3 hawklike bird of prey. [from *hare, *harry]'),
(18184, 'h', '\r\nHarrow', ' â€”n. Heavy frame with iron teeth dragged over ploughed land to break up clods etc. â€”v. 1 draw a harrow over (land). 2 (usu. As harrowing adj.) Distress greatly. [old norse hervi]'),
