(18303, 'h', '\r\nHeavier-than-air', ' attrib. Adj. (of an aircraft) weighing more than the air it displaces.'),
(18304, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of great or unusually high weight; difficult to lift. 2 of great density (heavy metal). 3 abundant, considerable (heavy crop; heavy traffic). 4 severe, intense, extensive (heavy fighting; a heavy sleep). 5 doing a thing to excess (heavy drinker). 6 striking or falling with force; causing strong impact (heavy blows; heavy rain; heavy sea; a heavy fall). 7 (of machinery, artillery, etc.) Very large of its kind; large in calibre etc. 8 needing much physical effort (heavy work). 9 carrying heavy weapons (the heavy brigade). 10 serious or sombre in tone or attitude; dull, tedious. 11 a hard to digest. B hard to read or understand. 12 (of bread etc.) Too dense from not having risen. 13 (of ground) difficult to traverse or work. 14 oppressive; hard to endure (heavy demands). 15 a coarse, ungraceful (heavy features). B unwieldy. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 colloq. Large violent person; thug (esp. Hired). 2 villainous or tragic role or actor. 3 (usu. In pl.) Colloq. Serious newspaper. 4 anything large or heavy of its kind, e.g. A vehicle. â€”adv. Heavily (esp. In comb.: heavy-laden).'),
(18305, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-duty', ' adj. Intended to withstand hard use.'),
(18306, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'going  n. Slow or difficult progress.'),
(18307, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-handed', ' adj. 1 clumsy. 2 overbearing, oppressive.  heavy-handedly adv. Heavy-handedness n.'),
(18308, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-hearted', ' adj. Sad, doleful.'),
(18309, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'hydrogen  n. = *deuterium.'),
(18310, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'industry  n. Industry producing metal, machinery, etc.'),
(18311, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'metal  n. 1 heavy guns. 2 metal of high density. 3 colloq. Loud kind of rock music with a pounding rhythm.'),
(18312, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'petting  n. Erotic fondling that stops short of intercourse.'),
(18313, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'water  n. Water composed of deuterium and oxygen.'),
(18314, 'h', '\r\nHeavyweight', ' n. 1 a weight in certain sports, in amateur boxing over 81 kg. B sportsman of this weight. 2 person etc. Of above average weight. 3 colloq. Person of influence or importance.'),
(18315, 'h', '\r\nHebdomadal', ' adj. Formal weekly, esp. Meeting weekly. [greek hepta seven]'),
(18316, 'h', '\r\nHebe', ' n. Evergreen flowering shrub from new zealand. [greek goddess hebe]'),
(18317, 'h', '\r\nHebraic', ' adj. Of hebrew or the hebrews.'),
(18318, 'h', '\r\nHebrew', ' â€”n. 1 member of a semitic people orig. Centred in ancient palestine. 2 a their language. B modern form of this, used esp. In israel. â€”adj. 1 of or in hebrew. 2 of the hebrews or the jews. [hebrew, = one from the other side of the river]'),
(18319, 'h', '\r\nHeck', ' int. Colloq. Mild exclamation of surprise or dismay. [a form of *hell]'),
