(18336, 'h', '\r\nHeel1', ' â€”n. 1 back of the foot below the ankle. 2 a part of a sock etc. Covering this. B part of a shoe etc. Supporting this. 3 thing like a heel in form or position. 4 crust end of a loaf of bread. 5 colloq. Scoundrel. 6 (as int.) Command to a dog to walk close to its owner''s heel. â€”v. 1 fit or renew a heel on (a shoe etc.). 2 touch the ground with the heel as in dancing. 3 (foll. By out) rugby pass the ball with the heel.  at heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. At (or on) the heels of following closely after (a person or event). Cool (or kick) one''s heels be kept waiting. Down at heel 1 (of a shoe) with the heel worn down. 2 (of a person) shabby. Take to one''s heels run away. To heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. Turn on one''s heel turn sharply round. [old english]'),
(18337, 'h', '\r\nHeel2', ' â€”v. (often foll. By over) 1 (of a ship etc.) Lean over. 2 cause (a ship etc.) To do this. â€”n. Act or amount of heeling. [obsolete heeld, from germanic]'),
(18338, 'h', '\r\nHeel3', ' var. Of *hele.'),
(18339, 'h', '\r\nHeelball', ' n. 1 mixture of hard wax and lampblack used by shoemakers for polishing. 2 this or a similar mixture used in brass-rubbing.'),
(18340, 'h', '\r\nHefty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a person) big and strong. 2 (of a thing) large, heavy, powerful.  heftily adv. Heftiness n. [heft weight: related to *heave]'),
