(18710, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'first  adv. 1 with the head foremost. 2 precipitately.'),
(18711, 'h', '\r\nHeadgear', ' n. Hat or headdress.'),
(18712, 'h', '\r\nHead-hunting', ' n. 1 collecting of the heads of dead enemies as trophies. 2 seeking of (esp. Senior) staff by approaching people employed elsewhere.  head-hunt v. Head-hunter n.'),
(18713, 'h', '\r\nHeading', ' n. 1 a title at the head of a page or section of a book etc. B section of a subject of discourse etc. 2 horizontal passage made in preparation for building a tunnel, or in a mine.'),
(18714, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'in the sand  n. Refusal to acknowledge danger or difficulty.'),
(18715, 'h', '\r\nHeadlamp', ' n. = *headlight.'),
(18716, 'h', '\r\nHeadland', ' n. Promontory.'),
(18717, 'h', '\r\nHeadlight', ' n. 1 strong light at the front of a vehicle. 2 beam from this.'),
(18718, 'h', '\r\nHeadline', ' n. 1 heading at the top of an article or page, esp. In a newspaper. 2 (in pl.) Summary of the most important items in a news bulletin.'),
(18719, 'h', '\r\nHeadlock', ' n. Wrestling hold with an arm round the opponent''s head.'),
(18720, 'h', '\r\nHeadlong', ' adv. & adj. 1 with the head foremost. 2 in a rush.'),
(18721, 'h', '\r\nHeadman', ' n. Chief man of a tribe etc.'),
(18722, 'h', '\r\nHeadmaster', ' n. (fem. Headmistress) = *head teacher.'),
(18723, 'h', '\r\nHead-on', ' adj. & adv. 1 with the front foremost (head-on crash). 2 in direct confrontation.'),
(18724, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'over heels  â€”n. Turning over completely in forward motion as in a somersault etc. â€”adv. Utterly (head over heels in love).'),
(18725, 'h', '\r\nHeadphones', ' n.pl. Set of earphones fitting over the head, for listening to audio equipment etc.'),
(18726, 'h', '\r\nHeadquarters', ' n. (as sing. Or pl.) Administrative centre of an organization.'),
(18727, 'h', '\r\nHeadrest', ' n. Support for the head, esp. On a seat.'),
(18728, 'h', '\r\nHeadroom', ' n. Space or clearance above a vehicle, person''s head, etc.'),
(18729, 'h', '\r\nHeadscarf', ' n. Scarf worn round the head and tied under the chin.'),
(18730, 'h', '\r\nHeadset', ' n. Headphones, often with a microphone attached.'),
(18731, 'h', '\r\nHeadship', ' n. Position of head or chief, esp. In a school.'),
(18732, 'h', '\r\nHeadshrinker', ' n. Slang psychiatrist.'),
(18733, 'h', '\r\nHeadstall', ' n. Part of a halter or bridle fitting round a horse''s head.'),
(18734, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'start  n. Advantage granted or gained at an early stage.'),
(18735, 'h', '\r\nHeadstone', ' n. Stone set up at the head of a grave.'),
(18736, 'h', '\r\nHeadstrong', ' adj. Self-willed.'),
(18737, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'teacher  n. Teacher in charge of a school.'),
(18738, 'h', '\r\nHeadwaters', ' n.pl. Streams flowing from the sources of a river.'),
(18739, 'h', '\r\nHeadway', ' n. 1 progress. 2 ship''s rate of progress. 3 headroom.'),
(18740, 'h', '\r\nHead', 'wind  n. Wind blowing from directly in front.'),
(18741, 'h', '\r\nHeadword', ' n. Word forming a heading.'),
(18742, 'h', '\r\nHeady', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of liquor) potent. 2 intoxicating, exciting. 3 impulsive, rash. 4 headachy.  headily adv. Headiness n.'),
(18743, 'h', '\r\nHeal', ' v. 1 (often foll. By up) become sound or healthy again. 2 cause to heal. 3 put right (differences etc.). 4 alleviate (sorrow etc.).  healer n. [old english: related to *whole]'),
(18744, 'h', '\r\nHealth', ' n. 1 state of being well in body or mind. 2 person''s mental or physical condition. 3 soundness, esp. Financial or moral. [old english: related to *whole]'),
(18745, 'h', '\r\nHealth', 'centre  n. Building containing various local medical services and doctors'' practices.'),
(18746, 'h', '\r\nHealth', 'farm  n. Establishment offering improved health by a regime of dieting, exercise, etc.'),
(18747, 'h', '\r\nHealth', 'food  n. Natural food, thought to promote good health.'),
(18748, 'h', '\r\nHealthful', ' adj. Conducive to good health; beneficial.'),
(18749, 'h', '\r\nHealth', 'service  n. Public service providing medical care.'),
(18750, 'h', '\r\nHealth', 'visitor  n. Trained nurse who visits mothers and babies, or the sick or elderly, at home.'),
(18751, 'h', '\r\nHealthy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having, showing, or promoting good health. 2 indicative of (esp. Moral or financial) health (a healthy sign). 3 substantial (won by a healthy 40 seconds).  healthily adv. Healthiness n.'),
(18752, 'h', '\r\nHeap', ' â€”n. 1 disorderly pile. 2 (esp. In pl.) Colloq. Large number or amount. 3 slang dilapidated vehicle. â€”v. 1 (foll. By up, together, etc.) Collect or be collected in a heap. 2 (foll. By with) load copiously with. 3 (foll. By on, upon) give or offer copiously (heaped insults on them). [old english]'),
(18753, 'h', '\r\nHear', ' v. (past and past part. Heard) 1 (also absol.) Perceive with the ear. 2 listen to (heard them on the radio). 3 listen judicially to (a case etc.). 4 be told or informed. 5 (foll.'),
(18754, 'h', '\r\nHearing', ' n. 1 faculty of perceiving sounds. 2 range within which sounds may be heard (within hearing). 3 opportunity to state one''s case (a fair hearing). 4 trial of a case before a court.'),
(18755, 'h', '\r\nHearing-aid', ' n. Small device to amplify sound, worn by a partially deaf person.'),
(18756, 'h', '\r\nHearken', ' v. Archaic (often foll. By to) listen. [old english: related to *hark]'),
(18757, 'h', '\r\nHearsay', ' n. Rumour, gossip.'),
(18758, 'h', '\r\nHearse', ' n. Vehicle for conveying the coffin at a funeral. [french herse harrow, from latin hirpex large rake]'),
(18759, 'h', '\r\nHeart', ' n. 1 hollow muscular organ maintaining the circulation of blood by rhythmic contraction and dilation. 2 region of the heart; the breast. 3 a centre of thought, feeling, and emotion (esp. Love). B capacity for feeling emotion (has no heart). 4 a courage or enthusiasm (take heart). B mood or feeling (change of heart). 5 a central or innermost part of something. B essence (heart of the matter). 6 compact tender inner part of a lettuce etc. 7 a heart-shaped thing. B conventional representation of a heart with two equal curves meeting at a point at the bottom and a cusp at the top. 8 a playing-card of the suit denoted by a red figure of a heart. B (in pl.) This suit.  at heart 1 in one''s inmost feelings. 2 basically. Break a person''s heart overwhelm a person with sorrow. By heart from memory. Give (or lose) one''s heart (often foll. By to) fall in love (with). Have the heart (usu. With neg.; foll. By to + infin.) Be insensitive or hard-hearted enough (didn''t have the heart to ask him). Take to heart be much affected by. To one''s heart''s content see *content1. With all one''s heart sincerely; with all goodwill. [old english]'),
(18760, 'h', '\r\nHeartache', ' n. Mental anguish.'),
(18761, 'h', '\r\nHeart', 'attack  n. Sudden occurrence of coronary thrombosis.'),
(18762, 'h', '\r\nHeartbeat', ' n. Pulsation of the heart.'),
(18763, 'h', '\r\nHeartbreak', ' n. Overwhelming distress.  heartbreaking adj. Heartbroken adj.'),
(18764, 'h', '\r\nHeartburn', ' n. Burning sensation in the chest from indigestion.'),
(18765, 'h', '\r\nHearten', ' v. Make or become more cheerful.  heartening adj.'),
(18766, 'h', '\r\nHeart', 'failure  n. Failure of the heart to function properly, esp. As a cause of death.'),
(18767, 'h', '\r\nHeartfelt', ' adj. Sincere; deeply felt.'),
(18768, 'h', '\r\nHearth', ' n. 1 floor of a fireplace. 2 the home. [old english]'),
(18769, 'h', '\r\nHearthrug', ' n. Rug laid before a fireplace.'),
(18770, 'h', '\r\nHeartily', ' adv. 1 in a hearty manner. 2 very (am heartily sick of it).'),
(18771, 'h', '\r\nHeartland', ' n. Central part of an area.'),
(18772, 'h', '\r\nHeartless', ' adj. Unfeeling, pitiless.  heartlessly adv.'),
(18773, 'h', '\r\nHeart-lung', 'machine  n. Machine that temporarily takes over the functions of the heart and lungs.'),
(18774, 'h', '\r\nHeart-rending', ' adj. Very distressing.'),
(18775, 'h', '\r\nHeart-searching', ' n. Examination of one''s own feelings and motives.'),
(18776, 'h', '\r\nHeartsick', ' adj. Despondent.'),
(18777, 'h', '\r\nHeartstrings', ' n.pl. One''s deepest feelings.'),
(18778, 'h', '\r\nHeartthrob', ' n. Colloq. Person for whom one has (esp. Immature) romantic feelings.'),
(18779, 'h', '\r\nHeart-to-heart', ' â€”attrib. Adj. (of a conversation etc.) Candid, intimate. â€”n. Candid or personal conversation.'),
(18780, 'h', '\r\nHeart-warming', ' adj. Emotionally rewarding or uplifting.'),
(18781, 'h', '\r\nHeartwood', ' n. Dense inner part of a tree-trunk, yielding the hardest timber.'),
(18782, 'h', '\r\nHearty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 strong, vigorous. 2 (of a meal or appetite) large. 3 warm, friendly.  heartiness n.'),
(18783, 'h', '\r\nHeat', ' â€”n. 1 condition of being hot. 2 physics form of energy arising from the motion of bodies'' molecules. 3 hot weather. 4 warmth of feeling; anger or excitement. 5 (foll. By of) most intense part or period of activity (heat of battle). 6 (usu. Preliminary or trial) round in a race etc. â€”v. 1 make or become hot or warm. 2 inflame.  on heat (of mammals, esp.'),
(18784, 'h', '\r\nHeated', ' adj. Angry; impassioned.  heatedly adv.'),
(18785, 'h', '\r\nHeater', ' n. Stove or other heating device.'),
(18786, 'h', '\r\nHeath', ' n. 1 area of flattish uncultivated land with low shrubs. 2 plant growing on a heath, esp. Heather. [old english]'),
(18787, 'h', '\r\nHeathen', ' â€”n. 1 person not belonging to a predominant religion, esp. Not a christian, jew, or muslim. 2 person regarded as lacking culture or moral principles. â€”adj. 1 of heathens. 2 having no religion. [old english]'),
(18788, 'h', '\r\nHeather', ' n. Any of various shrubs growing esp. On moors and heaths. [origin unknown]'),
(18789, 'h', '\r\nHeath', 'robinson  adj. Absurdly ingenious and impracticable. [name of a cartoonist]'),
(18790, 'h', '\r\nHeating', ' n. 1 imparting or generation of heat. 2 equipment used to heat a building etc.'),
(18791, 'h', '\r\nHeatproof', ' â€”adj. Able to resist great heat. â€”v. Make heatproof.'),
(18792, 'h', '\r\nHeat', 'shield  n. Device to protect (esp. A spacecraft) from excessive heat.'),
(18793, 'h', '\r\nHeatwave', ' n. Period of unusually hot weather.'),
(18794, 'h', '\r\nHeave', ' â€”v. (-ving; past and past part. Heaved or esp. Naut. Hove) 1 lift or haul with great effort. 2 utter with effort (heaved a sigh). 3 colloq. Throw. 4 rise and fall rhythmically or spasmodically. 5 naut. Haul by rope. 6 retch. â€”n. Heaving.  heave in sight come into view. Heave to esp. Naut. Bring or be brought to a standstill. [old english]'),
(18795, 'h', '\r\nHeaven', ' n. 1 place regarded in some religions as the abode of god and the angels, and of the blessed after death. 2 place or state of supreme bliss. 3 colloq. Delightful thing. 4 (usu. Heaven) god, providence (often as an exclamation or mild oath: heavens). 5 (the heavens) esp. Poet. The sky as seen from the earth, in which the sun, moon, and stars appear.  heavenward adv. (also heavenwards). [old english]'),
(18796, 'h', '\r\nHeavenly', ' adj. 1 of heaven; divine. 2 of the heavens or sky. 3 colloq. Very pleasing; wonderful.'),
(18797, 'h', '\r\nHeavenly', 'bodies  n.pl. The sun, stars, planets, etc.'),
(18798, 'h', '\r\nHeaven-sent', ' adj. Providential.'),
(18799, 'h', '\r\nHeavier-than-air', ' attrib. Adj. (of an aircraft) weighing more than the air it displaces.'),
(18800, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of great or unusually high weight; difficult to lift. 2 of great density (heavy metal). 3 abundant, considerable (heavy crop; heavy traffic). 4 severe, intense, extensive (heavy fighting; a heavy sleep). 5 doing a thing to excess (heavy drinker). 6 striking or falling with force; causing strong impact (heavy blows; heavy rain; heavy sea; a heavy fall). 7 (of machinery, artillery, etc.) Very large of its kind; large in calibre etc. 8 needing much physical effort (heavy work). 9 carrying heavy weapons (the heavy brigade). 10 serious or sombre in tone or attitude; dull, tedious. 11 a hard to digest. B hard to read or understand. 12 (of bread etc.) Too dense from not having risen. 13 (of ground) difficult to traverse or work. 14 oppressive; hard to endure (heavy demands). 15 a coarse, ungraceful (heavy features). B unwieldy. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 colloq. Large violent person; thug (esp. Hired). 2 villainous or tragic role or actor. 3 (usu. In pl.) Colloq. Serious newspaper. 4 anything large or heavy of its kind, e.g. A vehicle. â€”adv. Heavily (esp. In comb.: heavy-laden).  heavy on using a lot of (heavy on petrol). Make heavy weather of see *weather.  heavily adv. Heaviness n. Heavyish adj. [old english]'),
(18801, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-duty', ' adj. Intended to withstand hard use.'),
(18802, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'going  n. Slow or difficult progress.'),
(18803, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-handed', ' adj. 1 clumsy. 2 overbearing, oppressive.  heavy-handedly adv. Heavy-handedness n.'),
(18804, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-hearted', ' adj. Sad, doleful.'),
(18805, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'hydrogen  n. = *deuterium.'),
(18806, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'industry  n. Industry producing metal, machinery, etc.'),
(18807, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'metal  n. 1 heavy guns. 2 metal of high density. 3 colloq. Loud kind of rock music with a pounding rhythm.'),
(18808, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'petting  n. Erotic fondling that stops short of intercourse.'),
(18809, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'water  n. Water composed of deuterium and oxygen.'),
(18810, 'h', '\r\nHeavyweight', ' n. 1 a weight in certain sports, in amateur boxing over 81 kg. B sportsman of this weight. 2 person etc. Of above average weight. 3 colloq. Person of influence or importance.'),
(18811, 'h', '\r\nHebdomadal', ' adj. Formal weekly, esp. Meeting weekly. [greek hepta seven]'),
(18812, 'h', '\r\nHebe', ' n. Evergreen flowering shrub from new zealand. [greek goddess hebe]'),
(18813, 'h', '\r\nHebraic', ' adj. Of hebrew or the hebrews.'),
(18814, 'h', '\r\nHebrew', ' â€”n. 1 member of a semitic people orig. Centred in ancient palestine. 2 a their language. B modern form of this, used esp. In israel. â€”adj. 1 of or in hebrew. 2 of the hebrews or the jews. [hebrew, = one from the other side of the river]'),
(18815, 'h', '\r\nHeck', ' int. Colloq. Mild exclamation of surprise or dismay. [a form of *hell]'),
(18816, 'h', '\r\nHeckle', ' â€”v. (-ling) interrupt and harass (a public speaker). â€”n. Act of heckling.  heckler n. [var. Of *hackle]'),
(18817, 'h', '\r\nHectare', ' n. Metric unit of square measure, 100 ares (2.471 acres or 10,000 square metres). [french: related to *hecto-, *are2]'),
(18818, 'h', '\r\nHectic', ' adj. 1 busy and confused; excited. 2 feverish.  hectically adv. [greek hektikos habitual]'),
(18819, 'h', '\r\nHecto-', ' comb. Form hundred. [greek hekaton]'),
(18820, 'h', '\r\nHectogram', ' n. (also hectogramme) metric unit of mass equal to 100 grams.'),
(18821, 'h', '\r\nHector', ' â€”v. Bully, intimidate. â€”n. Bully. [from the name hector in the iliad]'),
(18822, 'h', '\r\nHe''d', ' contr. 1 he had. 2 he would.'),
(18823, 'h', '\r\nHedge', ' â€”n. 1 fence or boundary of dense bushes or shrubs. 2 protection against possible loss. â€”v. (-ging) 1 surround or bound with a hedge. 2 (foll. By in) enclose. 3 a reduce one''s risk of loss on (a bet or speculation) by compensating transactions on the other side. B avoid committing oneself. [old english]'),
(18824, 'h', '\r\nHedgehog', ' n. Small insect-eating mammal with a piglike snout and a coat of spines, rolling itself up into a ball when attacked.'),
(18825, 'h', '\r\nHedge-hop', ' v. Fly at a very low altitude.'),
(18826, 'h', '\r\nHedgerow', ' n. Row of bushes etc. Forming a hedge.'),
(18827, 'h', '\r\nHedge', 'sparrow  n. Common grey and brown bird; the dunnock.'),
(18828, 'h', '\r\nHedonism', ' n. 1 belief in pleasure as mankind''s proper aim. 2 behaviour based on this.  hedonist n. Hedonistic adj. [greek hedone pleasure]'),
(18829, 'h', '\r\nHeebie-jeebies', ' n.pl. (prec. By the) slang nervous anxiety, tension. [origin unknown]'),
(18830, 'h', '\r\nHeed', ' â€”v. Attend to; take notice of. â€”n. Careful attention.  heedful adj. Heedless adj. Heedlessly adv. [old english]'),
(18831, 'h', '\r\nHee-haw', ' â€”n. Bray of a donkey. â€”v. Make a braying sound. [imitative]'),
(18832, 'h', '\r\nHeel1', ' â€”n. 1 back of the foot below the ankle. 2 a part of a sock etc. Covering this. B part of a shoe etc. Supporting this. 3 thing like a heel in form or position. 4 crust end of a loaf of bread. 5 colloq. Scoundrel. 6 (as int.) Command to a dog to walk close to its owner''s heel. â€”v. 1 fit or renew a heel on (a shoe etc.). 2 touch the ground with the heel as in dancing. 3 (foll. By out) rugby pass the ball with the heel.  at heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. At (or on) the heels of following closely after (a person or event). Cool (or kick) one''s heels be kept waiting. Down at heel 1 (of a shoe) with the heel worn down. 2 (of a person) shabby. Take to one''s heels run away. To heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. Turn on one''s heel turn sharply round. [old english]'),
(18833, 'h', '\r\nHeel2', ' â€”v. (often foll. By over) 1 (of a ship etc.) Lean over. 2 cause (a ship etc.) To do this. â€”n. Act or amount of heeling. [obsolete heeld, from germanic]'),
(18834, 'h', '\r\nHeel3', ' var. Of *hele.'),
(18835, 'h', '\r\nHeelball', ' n. 1 mixture of hard wax and lampblack used by shoemakers for polishing. 2 this or a similar mixture used in brass-rubbing.'),
(18836, 'h', '\r\nHefty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a person) big and strong. 2 (of a thing) large, heavy, powerful.  heftily adv. Heftiness n. [heft weight: related to *heave]'),
(18837, 'h', '\r\nHegemony', ' n. Leadership, esp. By one state of a confederacy. [greek hegemon leader]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(18838, 'h', '\r\nHegira', ' n. (also hejira) 1 muhammad''s flight from mecca in ad 622. 2 muslim era reckoned from this date. [arabic hijra departure]'),
(18839, 'h', '\r\nHeifer', ' n. Young cow, esp. One that has not had more than one calf. [old english]'),
(18840, 'h', '\r\nHeight', ' n. 1 measurement from base to top or head to foot. 2 elevation above the ground or a recognized level. 3 considerable elevation (situated at a height). 4 high place or area. 5 top. 6 a most intense part or period (battle was at its height). B extreme example (the height of fashion). [old english]'),
(18841, 'h', '\r\nHeighten', ' v. Make or become higher or more intense.'),
(18842, 'h', '\r\nHeinous', ' adj. Utterly odious or wicked. [french haÃ¯r hate]'),
(18843, 'h', '\r\nHeir', ' n. (fem. Heiress) person entitled to property or rank as the legal successor of its former holder. [latin heres hered-]'),
(18844, 'h', '\r\nHeir', 'apparent  n. Heir whose claim cannot be set aside by the birth of another heir.'),
(18845, 'h', '\r\nHeirloom', ' n. 1 piece of personal property that has been in a family for several generations. 2 piece of property as part of an inheritance.'),
(18846, 'h', '\r\nHeir', 'presumptive  n. Heir whose claim may be set aside by the birth of another heir.'),
(18847, 'h', '\r\nHejira', ' var. Of *hegira.'),
(18848, 'h', '\r\nHeld', ' past and past part. Of *hold1.'),
(18849, 'h', '\r\nHele', ' v. (-ling) (also heel) (foll. By in) set (a plant) in the ground temporarily and cover its roots. [old english]'),
(18850, 'h', '\r\nHelical', ' adj. Having the form of a helix.'),
(18851, 'h', '\r\nHelices', ' pl. Of *helix.'),
(18852, 'h', '\r\nHelicopter', ' n. Wingless aircraft obtaining lift and propulsion from horizontally revolving overhead blades. [greek: related to *helix, pteron wing]'),
(18853, 'h', '\r\nHelio-', ' comb. Form sun. [greek helios sun]'),
(18854, 'h', '\r\nHeliocentric', ' adj. 1 regarding the sun as centre. 2 considered as viewed from the sun''s centre.'),
(18855, 'h', '\r\nHeliograph', ' â€”n. 1 signalling apparatus reflecting sunlight in flashes. 2 message sent by means of this. â€”v. Send (a message) by heliograph.'),
(18856, 'h', '\r\nHeliotrope', ' n. Plant with fragrant purple flowers. [greek: related to *helio-, trepo turn]'),
(18857, 'h', '\r\nHeliport', ' n. Place where helicopters take off and land.'),
(18858, 'h', '\r\nHelium', ' n. Light inert gaseous element used in airships and as a refrigerant. [related to *helio-]'),
(18859, 'h', '\r\nHelix', ' n. (pl. Helices) spiral curve (like a corkscrew) or coiled curve (like a watch spring). [latin from greek]'),
(18860, 'h', '\r\nHell', ' â€”n. 1 place regarded in some religions as the abode of the dead, or of devils and condemned sinners. 2 place or state of misery or wickedness. â€”int. Expressing anger, surprise, etc.  the hell (usu. Prec. By what, where, who, etc.) Expressing anger, disbelief, etc. (who the hell is this?; the hell you are!). Beat etc. The hell out of colloq. Beat etc. Without restraint. Come hell or high water no matter what the difficulties. For the hell of it colloq. Just for fun. Get hell colloq. Be severely scolded or punished. Give a person hell colloq. Scold or punish a person. A (or one) hell of a colloq. Outstanding example of (a hell of a mess; one hell of a party). Like hell colloq. 1 not at all. 2 recklessly, exceedingly. [old english]'),
(18861, 'h', '\r\nHe''ll', ' contr. He will; he shall.'),
(18862, 'h', '\r\nHell-bent', ' adj. (foll. By on) recklessly determined.'),
(18863, 'h', '\r\nHellebore', ' n. Evergreen plant with usu. White, purple, or green flowers, e.g. The christmas rose. [greek (h)elleborus]'),
(18864, 'h', '\r\nHellene', ' n. 1 native of modern greece. 2 ancient greek.  hellenic adj. [greek]'),
(18865, 'h', '\r\nHellenism', ' n. (esp. Ancient) greek character or culture.  hellenist n.'),
(18866, 'h', '\r\nHellenistic', ' adj. Of greek history, language, and culture of the late 4th to the late 1st c. Bc.'),
(18867, 'h', '\r\nHell-fire', ' n. Fire(s) regarded as existing in hell.'),
(18868, 'h', '\r\nHell', 'for leather  adv. At full speed.'),
(18869, 'h', '\r\nHell-hole', ' n. Oppressive or unbearable place.'),
(18870, 'h', '\r\nHellish', ' â€”adj. 1 of or like hell. 2 colloq. Extremely difficult or unpleasant. â€”adv. Colloq. Extremely (hellish expensive).  hellishly adv.'),
(18871, 'h', '\r\nHello', ' (also hallo, hullo) â€”int. Expression of informal greeting, or of surprise, or to call attention. â€”n. (pl. -s) cry of â€˜helloâ€™. [var. Of earlier hollo]'),
(18872, 'h', '\r\nHell''s', 'angel  n. Member of a gang of male motor-cycle enthusiasts notorious for outrageous and violent behaviour.'),
(18873, 'h', '\r\nHelm', ' n. Tiller or wheel for controlling a ship''s rudder.  at the helm in control; at the head of an organization etc. [old english]'),
(18874, 'h', '\r\nHelmet', ' n. Protective head-covering worn by a policeman, motor cyclist, etc. [french from germanic]'),
(18875, 'h', '\r\nHelmsman', ' n. Person who steers a ship.'),
(18876, 'h', '\r\nHelot', ' n. Serf, esp. (helot) of a class in ancient sparta. [latin from greek]'),
(18877, 'h', '\r\nHelp', ' â€”v. 1 provide with the means towards what is needed or sought (helped me with my work; helped me (to) pay my debts; helped him on with his coat). 2 (often absol.) Be of use or service to (does that help?). 3 contribute to alleviating (a pain or difficulty). 4 prevent or remedy (it can''t be helped). 5 (usu. With neg.) A refrain from (can''t help it; could not help laughing). B refl. Refrain from acting (couldn''t help himself). 6 (often foll. By to) serve (a person with food). â€”n. 1 helping or being helped (need your help; came to our help). 2 person or thing that helps. 3 colloq. Domestic assistant or assistance. 4 remedy or escape (there is no help for it).  help oneself (often foll. By to) 1 serve oneself (with food etc.). 2 take without permission. Help a person out give a person help, esp. In difficulty.  helper n. [old english]'),
(18878, 'h', '\r\nHelpful', ' adj. Giving help; useful.  helpfully adv. Helpfulness n.'),
(18879, 'h', '\r\nHelping', ' n. Portion of food at a meal.'),
