(19038, 'h', '\r\nHearsay', ' n. Rumour, gossip.'),
(19039, 'h', '\r\nHearse', ' n. Vehicle for conveying the coffin at a funeral. [french herse harrow, from latin hirpex large rake]'),
(19040, 'h', '\r\nHeart', ' n. 1 hollow muscular organ maintaining the circulation of blood by rhythmic contraction and dilation. 2 region of the heart; the breast. 3 a centre of thought, feeling, and emotion (esp. Love). B capacity for feeling emotion (has no heart). 4 a courage or enthusiasm (take heart). B mood or feeling (change of heart). 5 a central or innermost part of something. B essence (heart of the matter). 6 compact tender inner part of a lettuce etc. 7 a heart-shaped thing. B conventional representation of a heart with two equal curves meeting at a point at the bottom and a cusp at the top. 8 a playing-card of the suit denoted by a red figure of a heart. B (in pl.) This suit.  at heart 1 in one''s inmost feelings. 2 basically. Break a person''s heart overwhelm a person with sorrow. By heart from memory. Give (or lose) one''s heart (often foll. By to) fall in love (with). Have the heart (usu. With neg.; foll. By to + infin.) Be insensitive or hard-hearted enough (didn''t have the heart to ask him). Take to heart be much affected by. To one''s heart''s content see *content1. With all one''s heart sincerely; with all goodwill. [old english]'),
(19041, 'h', '\r\nHeartache', ' n. Mental anguish.'),
(19042, 'h', '\r\nHeart', 'attack  n. Sudden occurrence of coronary thrombosis.'),
(19043, 'h', '\r\nHeartbeat', ' n. Pulsation of the heart.'),
(19044, 'h', '\r\nHeartbreak', ' n. Overwhelming distress.  heartbreaking adj. Heartbroken adj.'),
(19045, 'h', '\r\nHeartburn', ' n. Burning sensation in the chest from indigestion.'),
(19046, 'h', '\r\nHearten', ' v. Make or become more cheerful.  heartening adj.'),
(19047, 'h', '\r\nHeart', 'failure  n. Failure of the heart to function properly, esp. As a cause of death.'),
(19048, 'h', '\r\nHeartfelt', ' adj. Sincere; deeply felt.'),
(19049, 'h', '\r\nHearth', ' n. 1 floor of a fireplace. 2 the home. [old english]'),
(19050, 'h', '\r\nHearthrug', ' n. Rug laid before a fireplace.'),
(19051, 'h', '\r\nHeartily', ' adv. 1 in a hearty manner. 2 very (am heartily sick of it).'),
(19052, 'h', '\r\nHeartland', ' n. Central part of an area.'),
(19053, 'h', '\r\nHeartless', ' adj. Unfeeling, pitiless.  heartlessly adv.'),
(19054, 'h', '\r\nHeart-lung', 'machine  n. Machine that temporarily takes over the functions of the heart and lungs.'),
(19055, 'h', '\r\nHeart-rending', ' adj. Very distressing.'),
(19056, 'h', '\r\nHeart-searching', ' n. Examination of one''s own feelings and motives.'),
(19057, 'h', '\r\nHeartsick', ' adj. Despondent.'),
(19058, 'h', '\r\nHeartstrings', ' n.pl. One''s deepest feelings.'),
(19059, 'h', '\r\nHeartthrob', ' n. Colloq. Person for whom one has (esp. Immature) romantic feelings.'),
(19060, 'h', '\r\nHeart-to-heart', ' â€”attrib. Adj. (of a conversation etc.) Candid, intimate. â€”n. Candid or personal conversation.'),
(19061, 'h', '\r\nHeart-warming', ' adj. Emotionally rewarding or uplifting.'),
(19062, 'h', '\r\nHeartwood', ' n. Dense inner part of a tree-trunk, yielding the hardest timber.'),
(19063, 'h', '\r\nHearty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 strong, vigorous. 2 (of a meal or appetite) large. 3 warm, friendly.  heartiness n.'),
(19064, 'h', '\r\nHeat', ' â€”n. 1 condition of being hot. 2 physics form of energy arising from the motion of bodies'' molecules. 3 hot weather. 4 warmth of feeling; anger or excitement. 5 (foll. By of) most intense part or period of activity (heat of battle). 6 (usu. Preliminary or trial) round in a race etc. â€”v. 1 make or become hot or warm. 2 inflame.  on heat (of mammals, esp. Females) sexually receptive. [old english]'),
(19065, 'h', '\r\nHeated', ' adj. Angry; impassioned.  heatedly adv.'),
(19066, 'h', '\r\nHeater', ' n. Stove or other heating device.'),
(19067, 'h', '\r\nHeath', ' n. 1 area of flattish uncultivated land with low shrubs. 2 plant growing on a heath, esp. Heather. [old english]'),
(19068, 'h', '\r\nHeathen', ' â€”n. 1 person not belonging to a predominant religion, esp. Not a christian, jew, or muslim. 2 person regarded as lacking culture or moral principles. â€”adj. 1 of heathens. 2 having no religion. [old english]'),
(19069, 'h', '\r\nHeather', ' n. Any of various shrubs growing esp. On moors and heaths. [origin unknown]'),
(19070, 'h', '\r\nHeath', 'robinson  adj. Absurdly ingenious and impracticable. [name of a cartoonist]'),
(19071, 'h', '\r\nHeating', ' n. 1 imparting or generation of heat. 2 equipment used to heat a building etc.'),
(19072, 'h', '\r\nHeatproof', ' â€”adj. Able to resist great heat. â€”v. Make heatproof.'),
(19073, 'h', '\r\nHeat', 'shield  n. Device to protect (esp. A spacecraft) from excessive heat.'),
(19074, 'h', '\r\nHeatwave', ' n. Period of unusually hot weather.'),
(19075, 'h', '\r\nHeave', ' â€”v. (-ving; past and past part. Heaved or esp. Naut. Hove) 1 lift or haul with great effort. 2 utter with effort (heaved a sigh). 3 colloq. Throw. 4 rise and fall rhythmically or spasmodically. 5 naut. Haul by rope. 6 retch. â€”n. Heaving.  heave in sight come into view. Heave to esp. Naut. Bring or be brought to a standstill. [old english]'),
(19076, 'h', '\r\nHeaven', ' n. 1 place regarded in some religions as the abode of god and the angels, and of the blessed after death. 2 place or state of supreme bliss. 3 colloq. Delightful thing. 4 (usu. Heaven) god, providence (often as an exclamation or mild oath: heavens). 5 (the heavens) esp. Poet. The sky as seen from the earth, in which the sun, moon, and stars appear.  heavenward adv. (also heavenwards). [old english]'),
(19077, 'h', '\r\nHeavenly', ' adj. 1 of heaven; divine. 2 of the heavens or sky. 3 colloq. Very pleasing; wonderful.'),
(19078, 'h', '\r\nHeavenly', 'bodies  n.pl. The sun, stars, planets, etc.'),
(19079, 'h', '\r\nHeaven-sent', ' adj. Providential.'),
(19080, 'h', '\r\nHeavier-than-air', ' attrib. Adj. (of an aircraft) weighing more than the air it displaces.'),
(19081, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of great or unusually high weight; difficult to lift. 2 of great density (heavy metal). 3 abundant, considerable (heavy crop; heavy traffic). 4 severe, intense, extensive (heavy fighting; a heavy sleep). 5 doing a thing to excess (heavy drinker). 6 striking or falling with force; causing strong impact (heavy blows; heavy rain; heavy sea; a heavy fall). 7 (of machinery, artillery, etc.) Very large of its kind; large in calibre etc. 8 needing much physical effort (heavy work). 9 carrying heavy weapons (the heavy brigade). 10 serious or sombre in tone or attitude; dull, tedious. 11 a hard to digest. B hard to read or understand. 12 (of bread etc.) Too dense from not having risen. 13 (of ground) difficult to traverse or work. 14 oppressive; hard to endure (heavy demands). 15 a coarse, ungraceful (heavy features). B unwieldy. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 colloq. Large violent person; thug (esp. Hired). 2 villainous or tragic role or actor. 3 (usu. In pl.) Colloq. Serious newspaper. 4 anything large or heavy of its kind, e.g. A vehicle. â€”adv. Heavily (esp. In comb.: heavy-laden).'),
(19082, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-duty', ' adj. Intended to withstand hard use.'),
(19083, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'going  n. Slow or difficult progress.'),
(19084, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-handed', ' adj. 1 clumsy. 2 overbearing, oppressive.  heavy-handedly adv. Heavy-handedness n.'),
(19085, 'h', '\r\nHeavy-hearted', ' adj. Sad, doleful.'),
(19086, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'hydrogen  n. = *deuterium.'),
(19087, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'industry  n. Industry producing metal, machinery, etc.'),
(19088, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'metal  n. 1 heavy guns. 2 metal of high density. 3 colloq. Loud kind of rock music with a pounding rhythm.'),
(19089, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'petting  n. Erotic fondling that stops short of intercourse.'),
(19090, 'h', '\r\nHeavy', 'water  n. Water composed of deuterium and oxygen.'),
(19091, 'h', '\r\nHeavyweight', ' n. 1 a weight in certain sports, in amateur boxing over 81 kg. B sportsman of this weight. 2 person etc. Of above average weight. 3 colloq. Person of influence or importance.'),
(19092, 'h', '\r\nHebdomadal', ' adj. Formal weekly, esp. Meeting weekly. [greek hepta seven]'),
(19093, 'h', '\r\nHebe', ' n. Evergreen flowering shrub from new zealand. [greek goddess hebe]'),
(19094, 'h', '\r\nHebraic', ' adj. Of hebrew or the hebrews.'),
(19095, 'h', '\r\nHebrew', ' â€”n. 1 member of a semitic people orig. Centred in ancient palestine. 2 a their language. B modern form of this, used esp. In israel. â€”adj. 1 of or in hebrew. 2 of the hebrews or the jews. [hebrew, = one from the other side of the river]'),
(19096, 'h', '\r\nHeck', ' int. Colloq. Mild exclamation of surprise or dismay. [a form of *hell]'),
(19097, 'h', '\r\nHeckle', ' â€”v. (-ling) interrupt and harass (a public speaker). â€”n. Act of heckling.  heckler n. [var. Of *hackle]'),
(19098, 'h', '\r\nHectare', ' n. Metric unit of square measure, 100 ares (2.471 acres or 10,000 square metres). [french: related to *hecto-, *are2]'),
(19099, 'h', '\r\nHectic', ' adj. 1 busy and confused; excited. 2 feverish.  hectically adv. [greek hektikos habitual]'),
(19100, 'h', '\r\nHecto-', ' comb. Form hundred. [greek hekaton]'),
(19101, 'h', '\r\nHectogram', ' n. (also hectogramme) metric unit of mass equal to 100 grams.'),
(19102, 'h', '\r\nHector', ' â€”v. Bully, intimidate. â€”n. Bully. [from the name hector in the iliad]'),
(19103, 'h', '\r\nHe''d', ' contr. 1 he had. 2 he would.'),
(19104, 'h', '\r\nHedge', ' â€”n. 1 fence or boundary of dense bushes or shrubs. 2 protection against possible loss. â€”v. (-ging) 1 surround or bound with a hedge. 2 (foll. By in) enclose. 3 a reduce one''s risk of loss on (a bet or speculation) by compensating transactions on the other side. B avoid committing oneself. [old english]'),
(19105, 'h', '\r\nHedgehog', ' n. Small insect-eating mammal with a piglike snout and a coat of spines, rolling itself up into a ball when attacked.'),
(19106, 'h', '\r\nHedge-hop', ' v. Fly at a very low altitude.'),
(19107, 'h', '\r\nHedgerow', ' n. Row of bushes etc. Forming a hedge.'),
(19108, 'h', '\r\nHedge', 'sparrow  n. Common grey and brown bird; the dunnock.'),
(19109, 'h', '\r\nHedonism', ' n. 1 belief in pleasure as mankind''s proper aim. 2 behaviour based on this.  hedonist n. Hedonistic adj. [greek hedone pleasure]'),
(19110, 'h', '\r\nHeebie-jeebies', ' n.pl. (prec. By the) slang nervous anxiety, tension. [origin unknown]'),
(19111, 'h', '\r\nHeed', ' â€”v. Attend to; take notice of. â€”n. Careful attention.  heedful adj. Heedless adj. Heedlessly adv. [old english]'),
(19112, 'h', '\r\nHee-haw', ' â€”n. Bray of a donkey. â€”v. Make a braying sound. [imitative]'),
(19113, 'h', '\r\nHeel1', ' â€”n. 1 back of the foot below the ankle. 2 a part of a sock etc. Covering this. B part of a shoe etc. Supporting this. 3 thing like a heel in form or position. 4 crust end of a loaf of bread. 5 colloq. Scoundrel. 6 (as int.) Command to a dog to walk close to its owner''s heel. â€”v. 1 fit or renew a heel on (a shoe etc.). 2 touch the ground with the heel as in dancing. 3 (foll. By out) rugby pass the ball with the heel.  at heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. At (or on) the heels of following closely after (a person or event). Cool (or kick) one''s heels be kept waiting. Down at heel 1 (of a shoe) with the heel worn down. 2 (of a person) shabby. Take to one''s heels run away. To heel 1 (of a dog) close behind. 2 (of a person etc.) Under control. Turn on one''s heel turn sharply round. [old english]'),
(19114, 'h', '\r\nHeel2', ' â€”v. (often foll. By over) 1 (of a ship etc.) Lean over. 2 cause (a ship etc.) To do this. â€”n. Act or amount of heeling. [obsolete heeld, from germanic]'),
(19115, 'h', '\r\nHeel3', ' var. Of *hele.'),
(19116, 'h', '\r\nHeel3', ' var. Of *hele.'),
(19117, 'h', '\r\nHeelball', ' n. 1 mixture of hard wax and lampblack used by shoemakers for polishing. 2 this or a similar mixture used in brass-rubbing.'),
(19118, 'h', '\r\nHefty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a person) big and strong. 2 (of a thing) large, heavy, powerful.  heftily adv. Heftiness n. [heft weight: related to *heave]'),
(19119, 'h', '\r\nHegemony', ' n. Leadership, esp. By one state of a confederacy. [greek hegemon leader]'),
(19120, 'h', '\r\nHegira', ' n. (also hejira) 1 muhammad''s flight from mecca in ad 622. 2 muslim era reckoned from this date. [arabic hijra departure]'),
(19121, 'h', '\r\nHeifer', ' n. Young cow, esp. One that has not had more than one calf. [old english]'),
(19122, 'h', '\r\nHeight', ' n. 1 measurement from base to top or head to foot. 2 elevation above the ground or a recognized level. 3 considerable elevation (situated at a height). 4 high place or area. 5 top. 6 a most intense part or period (battle was at its height). B extreme example (the height of fashion). [old english]'),
(19123, 'h', '\r\nHeighten', ' v. Make or become higher or more intense.'),
(19124, 'h', '\r\nHeinous', ' adj. Utterly odious or wicked. [french haÃ¯r hate]'),
(19125, 'h', '\r\nHeir', ' n. (fem. Heiress) person entitled to property or rank as the legal successor of its former holder. [latin heres hered-]'),
(19126, 'h', '\r\nHeir', 'apparent  n. Heir whose claim cannot be set aside by the birth of another heir.'),
(19127, 'h', '\r\nHeirloom', ' n. 1 piece of personal property that has been in a family for several generations. 2 piece of property as part of an inheritance.'),
(19128, 'h', '\r\nHeir', 'presumptive  n. Heir whose claim may be set aside by the birth of another heir.'),
(19129, 'h', '\r\nHejira', ' var. Of *hegira.'),
(19130, 'h', '\r\nHeld', ' past and past part. Of *hold1.'),
(19131, 'h', '\r\nHele', ' v. (-ling) (also heel) (foll. By in) set (a plant) in the ground temporarily and cover its roots. [old english]'),
(19132, 'h', '\r\nHelical', ' adj. Having the form of a helix.'),
(19133, 'h', '\r\nHelices', ' pl. Of *helix.'),
(19134, 'h', '\r\nHelicopter', ' n. Wingless aircraft obtaining lift and propulsion from horizontally revolving overhead blades. [greek: related to *helix, pteron wing]'),
(19135, 'h', '\r\nHelio-', ' comb. Form sun. [greek helios sun]'),
(19136, 'h', '\r\nHeliocentric', ' adj. 1 regarding the sun as centre. 2 considered as viewed from the sun''s centre.'),
(19137, 'h', '\r\nHeliograph', ' â€”n. 1 signalling apparatus reflecting sunlight in flashes. 2 message sent by means of this. â€”v. Send (a message) by heliograph.'),
(19138, 'h', '\r\nHeliotrope', ' n. Plant with fragrant purple flowers. [greek: related to *helio-, trepo turn]'),
(19139, 'h', '\r\nHeliport', ' n. Place where helicopters take off and land.'),
(19140, 'h', '\r\nHelium', ' n. Light inert gaseous element used in airships and as a refrigerant. [related to *helio-]'),
(19141, 'h', '\r\nHelix', ' n. (pl. Helices) spiral curve (like a corkscrew) or coiled curve (like a watch spring). [latin from greek]'),
(19142, 'h', '\r\nHell', ' â€”n. 1 place regarded in some religions as the abode of the dead, or of devils and condemned sinners. 2 place or state of misery or wickedness. â€”int. Expressing anger, surprise, etc.  the hell (usu. Prec. By what, where, who, etc.) Expressing anger, disbelief, etc. (who the hell is this?; the hell you are!). Beat etc. The hell out of colloq. Beat etc. Without restraint.'),
(19143, 'h', '\r\nHe''ll', ' contr. He will; he shall.'),
(19144, 'h', '\r\nHell-bent', ' adj. (foll. By on) recklessly determined.'),
(19145, 'h', '\r\nHellebore', ' n. Evergreen plant with usu. White, purple, or green flowers, e.g. The christmas rose. [greek (h)elleborus]'),
(19146, 'h', '\r\nHellene', ' n. 1 native of modern greece. 2 ancient greek.  hellenic adj. [greek]'),
(19147, 'h', '\r\nHellenism', ' n. (esp. Ancient) greek character or culture.  hellenist n.'),
(19148, 'h', '\r\nHellenistic', ' adj. Of greek history, language, and culture of the late 4th to the late 1st c. Bc.'),
(19149, 'h', '\r\nHell-fire', ' n. Fire(s) regarded as existing in hell.'),
(19150, 'h', '\r\nHell', 'for leather  adv. At full speed.'),
(19151, 'h', '\r\nHell-hole', ' n. Oppressive or unbearable place.'),
(19152, 'h', '\r\nHellish', ' â€”adj. 1 of or like hell. 2 colloq. Extremely difficult or unpleasant. â€”adv. Colloq. Extremely (hellish expensive).  hellishly adv.'),
(19153, 'h', '\r\nHello', ' (also hallo, hullo) â€”int. Expression of informal greeting, or of surprise, or to call attention. â€”n. (pl. -s) cry of â€˜helloâ€™. [var. Of earlier hollo]'),
(19154, 'h', '\r\nHell''s', 'angel  n. Member of a gang of male motor-cycle enthusiasts notorious for outrageous and violent behaviour.'),
(19155, 'h', '\r\nHelm', ' n. Tiller or wheel for controlling a ship''s rudder.  at the helm in control; at the head of an organization etc. [old english]'),
(19156, 'h', '\r\nHelmet', ' n. Protective head-covering worn by a policeman, motor cyclist, etc. [french from germanic]'),
(19157, 'h', '\r\nHelmsman', ' n. Person who steers a ship.'),
(19158, 'h', '\r\nHelot', ' n. Serf, esp. (helot) of a class in ancient sparta. [latin from greek]'),
(19159, 'h', '\r\nHelp', ' â€”v. 1 provide with the means towards what is needed or sought (helped me with my work; helped me (to) pay my debts; helped him on with his coat). 2 (often absol.) Be of use or service to (does that help?). 3 contribute to alleviating (a pain or difficulty). 4 prevent or remedy (it can''t be helped). 5 (usu. With neg.) A refrain from (can''t help it; could not help laughing). B refl. Refrain from acting (couldn''t help himself). 6 (often foll. By to) serve (a person with food). â€”n. 1 helping or being helped (need your help; came to our help). 2 person or thing that helps. 3 colloq. Domestic assistant or assistance. 4 remedy or escape (there is no help for it).  help oneself (often foll. By to) 1 serve oneself (with food etc.). 2 take without permission. Help a person out give a person help, esp. In difficulty.  helper n. [old english]'),
(19160, 'h', '\r\nHelpful', ' adj. Giving help; useful.  helpfully adv. Helpfulness n.'),
(19161, 'h', '\r\nHelping', ' n. Portion of food at a meal.'),
(19162, 'h', '\r\nHelpless', ' adj. 1 lacking help or protection; defenceless. 2 unable to act without help.  helplessly adv. Helplessness n.'),
(19163, 'h', '\r\nHelpline', ' n. Telephone service providing help with problems.'),
(19164, 'h', '\r\nHelpmate', ' n. Helpful companion or partner.'),
(19165, 'h', '\r\nHelter-skelter', ' â€”adv. & adj. In disorderly haste. â€”n. (at a fairground) external spiral slide round a tower. [imitative]'),
(19166, 'h', '\r\nHem1', ' â€”n. Border of cloth where the edge is turned under and sewn down. â€”v. (-mm-) turn down and sew in the edge of (cloth etc.).  hem in confine; restrict the movement of. [old english]'),
(19167, 'h', '\r\nHem2', ' â€”int. Calling attention or expressing hesitation by a slight cough. â€”n. Utterance of this. â€”v. (-mm-) say hem; hesitate in speech.  hem and haw = hum and haw (see *hum). [imitative]'),
(19168, 'h', '\r\nHemal', ' adj. (brit. Haem-) of the blood. [greek haima blood]'),
(19169, 'h', '\r\nHe-man', ' n. Masterful or virile man.'),
(19170, 'h', '\r\nHematite', ' n. (brit. Haem-) a ferric oxide ore. [latin: related to *haemal]'),
(19171, 'h', '\r\nHematology', ' n. (brit. Haem-) the study of the blood.  haematologist n.'),
(19172, 'h', '\r\nHemi-', ' comb. Form half. [greek, = latin semi-]'),
(19173, 'h', '\r\nHemipterous', ' adj. Of the insect order including aphids, bugs, and cicadas, with piercing or sucking mouthparts. [greek pteron wing]'),
(19174, 'h', '\r\nHemisphere', ' n. 1 half a sphere. 2 half of the earth, esp. As divided by the equator (into northern and southern hemisphere) or by a line passing through the poles (into eastern and western hemisphere).  hemispherical adj. [greek: related to *hemi-, *sphere]'),
(19175, 'h', '\r\nHemline', ' n. Lower edge of a skirt etc.'),
(19176, 'h', '\r\nHemlock', ' n. 1 poisonous plant with fernlike leaves and small white flowers. 2 poison made from this. [old english]'),
(19177, 'h', '\r\nHemoglobin', ' n. (brit. Haem-) oxygen-carrying substance in the red blood cells of vertebrates. [from *globulin]'),
(19178, 'h', '\r\nHemophilia', ' n. (brit. Haem-) hereditary failure of the blood to clot normally with the tendency to bleed severely from even a slight injury. [greek haima blood, philia loving]'),
(19179, 'h', '\r\nHemophiliac', ' n. (brit. Haem-) person with haemophilia.'),
(19180, 'h', '\r\nHemorrhage', ' (brit. Haem-) â€”n. 1 profuse loss of blood from a ruptured blood-vessel. 2 damaging loss, esp. Of people or assets. â€”v. (-ging) suffer a haemorrhage. [greek haima blood, rhegnumi burst]'),
(19181, 'h', '\r\nHemorrhoids', ' n.pl. (brit. Haem-) swollen veins in the wall of the anus; piles. [greek haima blood, -rhoos -flowing]'),
(19182, 'h', '\r\nHemp', ' n. 1 (in full indian hemp) asian herbaceous plant. 2 its fibre used to make rope and stout fabrics. 3 narcotic drug made from the hemp plant. [old english]'),
(19183, 'h', '\r\nHempen', ' adj. Made of hemp.'),
(19184, 'h', '\r\nHemstitch', ' â€”n. Decorative stitch. â€”v. Hem with this stitch.'),
(19185, 'h', '\r\nHen', ' n. Female bird, esp. Of a domestic fowl. [old english]'),
(19186, 'h', '\r\nHenbane', ' n. Poisonous hairy plant with an unpleasant smell.'),
(19187, 'h', '\r\nHence', ' adv. 1 from this time (two years hence). 2 for this reason (hence we seem to be wrong). 3 archaic from here. [old english]'),
(19188, 'h', '\r\nHenceforth', ' adv. (also henceforward) from this time onwards.'),
(19189, 'h', '\r\nHenchman', ' n. Usu. Derog. Trusted supporter. [old english hengst horse, *man]'),
(19190, 'h', '\r\nHenge', ' n. Prehistoric monument consisting of a circle of stone or wood uprights. [stonehenge in s. England]'),
(19191, 'h', '\r\nHenna', ' â€”n. 1 tropical shrub. 2 reddish dye made from it and used to colour hair. â€”v. (hennaed, hennaing) dye with henna. [arabic]'),
(19192, 'h', '\r\nHen-party', ' n. Colloq. Social gathering of women only.'),
(19193, 'h', '\r\nHenpeck', ' v. (usu. In passive) (of a wife) constantly nag her husband.');
