(19493, 'h', '\r\nHock2', ' n. German white wine from the rhineland. [hochheim in germany]'),
(19494, 'h', '\r\nHock3', ' v. Esp. Us colloq. Pawn; pledge.  in hock 1 in pawn. 2 in debt. 3 in prison. [dutch]'),
(19495, 'h', '\r\nHockey', ' n. Team game with hooked sticks and a small hard ball. [origin unknown]'),
(19496, 'h', '\r\nHocus-pocus', ' n. Deception; trickery. [sham latin]'),
(19497, 'h', '\r\nHod', ' n. 1 v-shaped trough on a pole used for carrying bricks etc. 2 portable receptacle for coal. [french hotte pannier]'),
(19498, 'h', '\r\nHodgepodge', ' var. Of *hotchpotch.'),
(19499, 'h', '\r\nHodgkin''s', 'disease  n. Malignant disease of lymphatic tissues, usu. Characterized by enlargement of the lymph nodes. [hodgkin, name of a physician]'),
(19500, 'h', '\r\nHoe', ' â€”n. Long-handled tool with a blade, used for weeding etc. â€”v. (hoes, hoed, hoeing) weed (crops); loosen (earth); dig up with a hoe. [french from germanic]'),
(19501, 'h', '\r\nHog', ' â€”n. 1 castrated male pig. 2 colloq. Greedy person. â€”v. (-gg-) colloq. Take greedily; hoard selfishly; monopolize.  go the whole hog colloq. Do something completely or thoroughly.  hoggish adj. [old english]'),
(19502, 'h', '\r\nHogmanay', ' n. Scot. New year''s eve. [probably french]'),
(19503, 'h', '\r\nHogshead', ' n. 1 large cask. 2 liquid or dry measure (about 50 gallons). [from *hog: the reason for the name is unknown]'),
(19504, 'h', '\r\nHogwash', ' n. Colloq. Nonsense, rubbish.'),
(19505, 'h', '\r\nHo-ho', ' int. 1 representing a deep jolly laugh. 2 expressing surprise, triumph, or derision. [reduplication of *ho]'),
(19506, 'h', '\r\nHoick', ' v. Colloq. (often foll. By out) lift or pull, esp. With a jerk. [perhaps var. Of *hike]'),
(19507, 'h', '\r\nHoi', 'polloi  n. The masses; the common people. [greek, = the many]'),
(19508, 'h', '\r\nUsage', ' this phrase is often preceded by the, which is, strictly speaking, unnecessary, since hoi means â€˜theâ€™.'),
(19509, 'h', '\r\nHoist', ' â€”v. 1 raise or haul up. 2 raise by means of ropes and pulleys etc. â€”n. 1 act of hoisting, lift. 2 apparatus for hoisting.  hoist with one''s own petard caught by one''s own trick etc. [earlier hoise, probably from low german]'),
(19510, 'h', '\r\nHoity-toity', ' adj. Haughty. [obsolete hoit romp]'),
(19511, 'h', '\r\nHokum', ' n. Esp. Us slang 1 sentimental, sensational, or unreal material in a film or play etc. 2 bunkum; rubbish. [origin unknown]'),
(19512, 'h', '\r\nHold1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Held) 1 a keep fast; grasp (esp. In the hands or arms). B (also refl.) Keep or sustain (a thing, oneself, one''s head, etc.) In a particular position. C grip so as to control (hold the reins). 2 have the capacity for, contain (holds two pints). 3 possess, gain, or have, esp.: a be the owner or tenant of (land, property, stocks, etc.). B gain or have gained (a qualification, record, etc.). C have the position of (a job or office). D keep possession of (a place etc.), esp. Against attack. 4 remain unbroken; not give way (roof held under the storm). 5 celebrate or conduct (a meeting, festival, conversation, etc.). 6 a keep (a person etc.) In a place or condition (held him in suspense). B detain, esp. In custody. 7 a engross (book held him for hours). B dominate (held the stage). 8 (foll. By to) keep (a person etc.) To (a promise etc.). 9 (of weather) continue fine. 10 think, believe; assert (held it to be plain; held that the earth was flat). 11 regard with a specified feeling (held him in contempt). 12 cease; restrain (hold your fire). 13 keep or reserve (please hold our seats). 14 be able to drink (alcohol) without effect (can''t hold his drink). 15 (of a court etc.) Lay down; decide. 16 mus. Sustain (a note). 17 = hold the line. â€”n. 1 (foll. By on, over) influence or power over (has a strange hold over me). 2 manner of holding in wrestling etc. 3 grasp (take hold of him). 4 (often in comb.) Thing to hold by (seized the handhold).  hold (a thing) against (a person) resent or regard it as discreditable to (a person). Hold back 1 impede the progress of; restrain. 2 keep for oneself. 3 (often foll. By from) hesitate; refrain. Hold one''s breath see *breath. Hold down 1 repress. 2 colloq. Be competent enough to keep (one''s job etc.). Hold the fort 1 act as a temporary substitute. 2 cope in an emergency. Hold forth speak at length or tediously. Hold one''s ground see *ground1. Hold hands grasp one another by the hand as a sign of affection or for support or guidance. Hold it cease action or movement. Hold the line not ring off (in a telephone connection).'),
(19513, 'h', '\r\nHold2', ' n. Cavity in the lower part of a ship or aircraft for cargo. [old english: related to *hollow]'),
(19514, 'h', '\r\nHoldall', ' n. Large soft travelling bag.'),
(19515, 'h', '\r\nHolding', ' n. 1 tenure of land. 2 stocks, property, etc. Held.'),
(19516, 'h', '\r\nHolding', 'company  n. Company created to hold the shares of other companies, which it then controls.'),
(19517, 'h', '\r\nHold-up', ' n. 1 stoppage or delay. 2 robbery by force.'),
(19518, 'h', '\r\nHole', ' n. 1 a empty space in a solid body. B opening in or through something. 2 animal''s burrow. 3 (in games) cavity or receptacle for a ball. 4 colloq. Small or dingy place. 5 colloq. Awkward situation. 6 golf a point scored by a player who gets the ball from tee to hole with the fewest strokes. B terrain or distance from tee to hole.  hole up us colloq. Hide oneself. Make a hole in use a large amount of.  holey adj. [old english]'),
(19519, 'h', '\r\nHole-and-corner', ' adj. Secret; underhand.'),
(19520, 'h', '\r\nHole', 'in the heart  n. Colloq. Congenital defect in the heart membrane.'),
(19521, 'h', '\r\nHoliday', ' â€”n. 1 (often in pl.) Extended period of recreation, esp. Spent away from home or travelling; break from work. 2 day of festivity or recreation when no work is done, esp. A religious festival etc. â€”v. Spend a holiday. [old english: related to *holy, *day]'),
(19522, 'h', '\r\nHoliday', 'camp  n. Place for holiday-makers with facilities on site.'),
(19523, 'h', '\r\nHoliday-maker', ' n. Person on holiday.'),
(19524, 'h', '\r\nHolier-than-thou', ' adj. Colloq. Self-righteous.'),
(19525, 'h', '\r\nHoliness', ' n. 1 being holy or sacred. 2 (holiness) title used when addressing or referring to the pope. [old english: related to *holy]'),
(19526, 'h', '\r\nHolism', ' n. (also wholism) 1 philos. Theory that certain wholes are greater than the sum of their parts. 2 med. Treating of the whole person rather than the symptoms of a disease.  holistic adj. [greek holos whole]'),
(19527, 'h', '\r\nHollandaise', 'sauce  n. Creamy sauce of melted butter, egg-yolks, vinegar, etc. [french]'),
(19528, 'h', '\r\nHoller', ' v. & n. Us colloq. Shout. [french holÃ  hello!]'),
(19529, 'h', '\r\nHollow', ' â€”adj. 1 a having a cavity; not solid. B sunken (hollow cheeks). 2 (of a sound) echoing. 3 empty; hungry. 4 meaningless (hollow victory). 5 insincere (hollow laugh). â€”n. 1 hollow place; hole. 2 valley; basin. â€”v. (often foll. By out) make hollow; excavate. â€”adv. Colloq. Completely (beaten hollow).  hollowly adv. Hollowness n. [old english]'),
(19530, 'h', '\r\nHolly', ' n. (pl. -ies) evergreen shrub with prickly leaves and red berries. [old english]'),
(19531, 'h', '\r\nHollyhock', ' n. Tall plant with showy flowers. [from *holy, obsolete hock mallow]'),
(19532, 'h', '\r\nHolm', ' n. (in full holm-oak) evergreen oak with holly-like young leaves. [dial. Holm holly]'),
(19533, 'h', '\r\nHolmium', ' n. Metallic element of the lanthanide series. [latin holmia stockholm]'),
(19534, 'h', '\r\nHolocaust', ' n. 1 large-scale destruction, esp. By fire or nuclear war. 2 (the holocaust) mass murder of the jews by the nazis 1939â€“45. [greek holos whole, kaustos burnt]'),
(19535, 'h', '\r\nHologram', ' n. Photographic pattern that gives a three-dimensional image when illuminated by coherent light. [greek holos whole, *-gram]'),
(19536, 'h', '\r\nHolograph', ' â€”adj. Wholly written by hand by the person named as the author. â€”n. Holograph document. [greek holos whole, *-graph]'),
(19537, 'h', '\r\nHolography', ' n. The study or production of holograms.'),
(19538, 'h', '\r\nHols', ' n.pl. Colloq. Holidays. [abbreviation]'),
(19539, 'h', '\r\nHolster', ' n. Leather case for a pistol or revolver, worn on a belt etc. [dutch]'),
(19540, 'h', '\r\nHoly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 morally and spiritually excellent or perfect, and to be revered. 2 belonging to or devoted to god. 3 consecrated, sacred. [old english: related to *whole]'),
(19541, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'communion  see *communion.'),
(19542, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'ghost  n. = *holy spirit.'),
(19543, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'grail  see *grail.'),
(19544, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'land  n. Area between the river jordan and the mediterranean sea.'),
(19545, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'of holies  n. 1 sacred inner chamber of the jewish temple. 2 thing regarded as most sacred.'),
(19546, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'orders  n.pl. The status of a bishop, priest, or deacon.'),
(19547, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'roman empire  n. Western part of the roman empire as revived by charlemagne in 800 ad.'),
(19548, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'see  n. Papacy or papal court.'),
(19549, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'spirit  n. Third person of the trinity, god as spiritually acting.'),
(19550, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'week  n. Week before easter.'),
(19551, 'h', '\r\nHoly', 'writ  n. Holy writings, esp. The bible.'),
(19552, 'h', '\r\nHomage', ' n. Tribute, expression of reverence (pay homage to). [latin homo man]'),
(19553, 'h', '\r\nHomburg', ' n. Man''s felt hat with a narrow curled brim and a lengthwise dent in the crown. [homburg in germany]'),
(19554, 'h', '\r\nHome', ' â€”n. 1 a place where one lives; fixed residence. B dwelling-house. 2 family circumstances (comes from a good home). 3 native land. 4 institution caring for people or animals. 5 place where a thing originates, is kept, or is native or most common. 6 a finishing-point in a race. B (in games) place where one is safe; goal. 7 sport home match or win. â€”attrib. Adj. 1 a of or connected with one''s home. B carried on, done, or made, at home. 2 in one''s own country (home industries; the home market). 3 sport played on one''s own ground etc. (home match). â€”adv. 1 to, at, or in one''s home or country (go home; is he home yet?).'),
(19555, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'and dry  predic. Adj. Having achieved one''s aim.'),
(19556, 'h', '\r\nHome-brew', ' n. Beer or other alcoholic drink brewed at home.'),
(19557, 'h', '\r\nHome-coming', ' n. Arrival at home.'),
(19558, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'counties  n.pl. The counties closest to london.'),
(19559, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'economics  n.pl. The study of household management.'),
(19560, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'farm  n. Principal farm on an estate, providing produce for the owner.'),
(19561, 'h', '\r\nHome-grown', ' adj. Grown or produced at home.'),
(19562, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'guard  n. Hist. British citizen army organized for defence in 1940.'),
(19563, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'help  n. Person helping with housework etc., esp. One provided by a local authority.'),
(19564, 'h', '\r\nHomeland', ' n. 1 one''s native land. 2 any of several partially self-governing areas in s. Africa reserved for black south africans (the official name for a bantustan).'),
(19565, 'h', '\r\nHomeless', ' adj. Lacking a home.  homelessness n.'),
(19566, 'h', '\r\nHomely', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 simple, plain, unpretentious. 2 us (of facial appearance) plain, unattractive. 3 comfortable, cosy.  homeliness n.'),
(19567, 'h', '\r\nHome-made', ' adj. Made at home.'),
(19568, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'office  n. British government department dealing with law and order, immigration, etc., in england and wales.'),
(19569, 'h', '\r\nHomeopathy', ' n. (brit. Homoeopathy) treatment of disease by minute doses of drugs that in a healthy person would produce symptoms of the disease.  homoeopath n. Homoeopathic adj. [greek homoios like: related to *pathos]'),
(19570, 'h', '\r\nHomeric', ' adj. 1 of, or in the style of, homer. 2 of bronze age greece as described in homer''s poems.'),
(19571, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'rule  n. Government of a country or region by its own citizens.'),
(19572, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'secretary  n. Secretary of state in charge of the home office.'),
(19573, 'h', '\r\nHomesick', ' adj. Depressed by absence from home.  homesickness n.'),
(19574, 'h', '\r\nHomespun', ' â€”adj. 1 made of yarn spun at home. 2 plain, simple. â€”n. Homespun cloth.'),
(19575, 'h', '\r\nHomestead', ' n. House, esp. A farmhouse, and outbuildings.'),
(19576, 'h', '\r\nHome', 'truth  n. Basic but unwelcome information about oneself.'),
(19577, 'h', '\r\nHomeward', ' â€”adv. (also homewards) towards home. â€”adj. Going towards home.'),
(19578, 'h', '\r\nHomework', ' n. 1 work to be done at home, esp. By a school pupil. 2 preparatory work or study.'),
(19579, 'h', '\r\nHomey', ' adj. (also homy) (-mier, -miest) suggesting home; cosy.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(19580, 'h', '\r\nHomicide', ' n. 1 killing of a human being by another. 2 person who kills a human being.  homicidal adj. [latin homo man]'),
(19581, 'h', '\r\nHomily', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 sermon. 2 tedious moralizing discourse.  homiletic adj. [greek homilia]'),
(19582, 'h', '\r\nHoming', ' attrib. Adj. 1 (of a pigeon) trained to fly home. 2 (of a device) for guiding to a target etc.'),
(19583, 'h', '\r\nHominid', ' â€”adj. Of the primate family including humans and their fossil ancestors. â€”n. Member of this family. [latin homo homin- man]'),
(19584, 'h', '\r\nHominoid', ' â€”adj. Like a human. â€”n. Animal resembling a human.'),
(19585, 'h', '\r\nHomo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Offens. Homosexual. [abbreviation]'),
(19586, 'h', '\r\nHomo-', ' comb. Form same. [greek homos same]'),
(19587, 'h', '\r\nHomoeopathy', ' n. (us homeopathy) treatment of disease by minute doses of drugs that in a healthy person would produce symptoms of the disease.  homoeopath n. Homoeopathic adj. [greek homoios like: related to *pathos]'),
(19588, 'h', '\r\nHomogeneous', ' adj. 1 of the same kind. 2 consisting of parts all of the same kind; uniform.  homogeneity n. Homogeneously adv. [from *homo-, greek genos kind]'),
(19589, 'h', '\r\nUsage', ' homogeneous is often confused with homogenous which is a term in biology meaning â€˜similar owing to common descentâ€™.'),
(19590, 'h', '\r\nHomogenize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 make homogeneous. 2 treat (milk) so that the fat droplets are emulsified and the cream does not separate.'),
(19591, 'h', '\r\nHomograph', ' n. Word spelt like another but of different meaning or origin (e.g. *pole1, *pole2).'),
(19592, 'h', '\r\nHomologous', ' adj. 1 a having the same relation, relative position, etc. B corresponding. 2 biol. (of organs etc.) Similar in position and structure but not necessarily in function. [from *homo-, greek logos ratio]'),
(19593, 'h', '\r\nHomology', ' n. Homologous state or relation; correspondence.'),
(19594, 'h', '\r\nHomonym', ' n. 1 word spelt or pronounced like another but of different meaning; homograph or homophone. 2 namesake. [from *homo-, onoma name]'),
(19595, 'h', '\r\nHomophobia', ' n. Hatred or fear of homosexuals.  homophobe n. Homophobic adj.'),
(19596, 'h', '\r\nHomophone', ' n. Word pronounced like another but of different meaning or origin (e.g. Pair, pear). [from *homo-, greek phone sound]'),
(19597, 'h', '\r\nHomo', 'sapiens  n. Modern humans regarded as a species. [latin, = wise man]'),
(19598, 'h', '\r\nHomosexual', ' â€”adj. Feeling or involving sexual attraction only to people of the same sex. â€”n. Homosexual person.  homosexuality n. [from *homo-, *sexual]'),
(19599, 'h', '\r\nHomy', ' var. Of *homey.'),
(19600, 'h', '\r\nHon.', ' Abbr. 1 honorary. 2 honourable.'),
(19601, 'h', '\r\nHone', ' â€”n. Whetstone, esp. For razors. â€”v. (-ning) sharpen on or as on a hone. [old english]'),
(19602, 'h', '\r\nHonest', ' â€”adj. 1 fair and just; not cheating or stealing. 2 free of deceit and untruthfulness; sincere. 3 fairly earned (an honest living). 4 blameless but undistinguished. â€”adv. Colloq. Genuinely, really. [latin honestus]'),
(19603, 'h', '\r\nHonestly', ' adv. 1 in an honest way. 2 really (i don''t honestly know).'),
(19604, 'h', '\r\nHonesty', ' n. 1 being honest. 2 truthfulness. 3 plant with purple or white flowers and flat round semi-transparent seed-pods.'),
(19605, 'h', '\r\nHoney', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 sweet sticky yellowish fluid made by bees from nectar. 2 colour of this. 3 a sweetness. B sweet thing. 4 esp. Us (usu. As a form of address) darling. [old english]'),
(19606, 'h', '\r\nHoney-bee', ' n. Common hive-bee.'),
(19607, 'h', '\r\nHoneycomb', ' â€”n. 1 bees'' wax structure of hexagonal cells for honey and eggs. 2 pattern arranged hexagonally. â€”v. 1 fill with cavities or tunnels, undermine. 2 mark with a honeycomb pattern. [old english]'),
(19608, 'h', '\r\nHoneydew', ' n. 1 sweet sticky substance excreted by aphids on leaves and stems. 2 variety of melon.'),
(19609, 'h', '\r\nHoneyed', ' adj. (of words, flattery, etc.) Sweet, sweet-sounding.'),
(19610, 'h', '\r\nHoneymoon', ' â€”n. 1 holiday taken by a newly married couple. 2 initial period of enthusiasm or goodwill. â€”v. Spend a honeymoon.  honeymooner n.'),
(19611, 'h', '\r\nHoneysuckle', ' n. Climbing shrub with fragrant yellow or pink flowers.'),
(19612, 'h', '\r\nHonk', ' â€”n. 1 sound of a car horn. 2 cry of a wild goose. â€”v. (cause to) make a honk. [imitative]'),
(19613, 'h', '\r\nHonky-tonk', ' n. Colloq. 1 ragtime piano music. 2 cheap or disreputable nightclub etc. [origin unknown]'),
(19614, 'h', '\r\nHonor', ' (brit. Honour) â€”n. 1 high respect, public regard. 2 adherence to what is right or an accepted standard of conduct. 3 nobleness of mind, magnanimity (honour among thieves). 4 thing conferred as a distinction, esp. An official award for bravery or achievement. 5 privilege, special right (had the honour of being invited). 6 a exalted position. B (honour) (prec. By your, his, etc.) Title of a circuit judge etc. 7 (foll. By to) person or thing that brings honour (an honour to her profession). 8 a chastity (of a woman). B reputation for this. 9 (in pl.) Specialized degree course or special distinction in an examination. 10 (in card-games) the four or five highest-ranking cards. 11 golf the right of driving off first. â€”v. 1 respect highly.'),
(19615, 'h', '\r\nHonorable', ' adj. (brit. Honourable) 1 deserving, bringing, or showing honour. 2 (honourable) title indicating distinction, given to certain high officials, the children of certain ranks of the nobility, and (in the house of commons) to mps.  honourably adv.'),
(19616, 'h', '\r\nHonorarium', ' n. (pl. -s or -ria) fee, esp. A voluntary payment for professional services rendered without the normal fee. [latin: related to *honour]'),
(19617, 'h', '\r\nHonorary', ' adj. 1 conferred as an honour (honorary degree). 2 (of an office or its holder) unpaid.'),
(19618, 'h', '\r\nHonorific', ' adj. 1 conferring honour. 2 implying respect.'),
(19619, 'h', '\r\nHonour', ' (us honor) â€”n. 1 high respect, public regard. 2 adherence to what is right or an accepted standard of conduct. 3 nobleness of mind, magnanimity (honour among thieves). 4 thing conferred as a distinction, esp. An official award for bravery or achievement. 5 privilege, special right (had the honour of being invited). 6 a exalted position. B (honour) (prec. By your, his, etc.) Title of a circuit judge etc. 7 (foll. By to) person or thing that brings honour (an honour to her profession). 8 a chastity (of a woman). B reputation for this. 9 (in pl.) Specialized degree course or special distinction in an examination. 10 (in card-games) the four or five highest-ranking cards. 11 golf the right of driving off first. â€”v. 1 respect highly. 2 confer honour on. 3 accept or pay (a bill or cheque) when due.  do the honours perform the duties of a host to guests etc. In honour of as a celebration of. On one''s honour (usu. Foll. By to + infin.) Under a moral obligation. [latin honor repute]'),
(19620, 'h', '\r\nHonourable', ' adj. (us honorable) 1 deserving, bringing, or showing honour. 2 (honourable) title indicating distinction, given to certain high officials, the children of certain ranks of the nobility, and (in the house of commons) to mps.  honourably adv.'),
(19621, 'h', '\r\nHooch', ' n. Us colloq. Alcoholic liquor, esp. Inferior or illicit whisky. [alaskan]'),
(19622, 'h', '\r\nHood1', ' â€”n. 1 a covering for the head and neck, esp. As part of a garment. B separate hoodlike garment. 2 folding top of a car etc. 3 us bonnet of a car etc. 4 protective cover. â€”v. Cover with or as with a hood. [old english]'),
(19623, 'h', '\r\nHood2', ' n. Us slang gangster, gunman. [abbreviation of *hoodlum]'),
(19624, 'h', '\r\n-hood', ' suffix forming nouns: 1 of condition or state (childhood; falsehood). 2 designating a group (sisterhood; neighbourhood). [old english]'),
(19625, 'h', '\r\nHooded', ' adj. 1 having a hood. 2 (of an animal) having a hoodlike part (hooded crow).'),
(19626, 'h', '\r\nHoodlum', ' n. 1 street hooligan, young thug. 2 gangster. [origin unknown]'),
(19627, 'h', '\r\nHoodoo', ' n. Esp. Us 1 a bad luck. B thing or person that brings this. 2 voodoo. [alteration of *voodoo]'),
(19628, 'h', '\r\nHoodwink', ' v. Deceive, delude. [from *hood1: originally = â€˜blindfoldâ€™]'),
(19629, 'h', '\r\nHoof', ' n. (pl. -s or hooves) horny part of the foot of a horse etc.  hoof it slang go on foot. [old english]'),
(19630, 'h', '\r\nHoo-ha', ' n. Slang commotion. [origin unknown]'),
(19631, 'h', '\r\nHook', ' â€”n. 1 a bent or curved piece of metal etc. For catching hold or for hanging things on. B (in full fish-hook) bent piece of wire for catching fish. 2 curved cutting instrument (reaping-hook). 3 bend in a river, curved strip of land, etc. 4 a hooking stroke. B boxing short swinging blow. â€”v. 1 grasp or secure with hook(s). 2 catch with or as with a hook. 3 slang steal. 4 (in sports) send (the ball) in a curve or deviating path. 5 rugby secure (the ball) and pass it backward with the foot in the scrum.  by hook or by crook by one means or another. Off the hook 1 colloq. Out of difficulty or trouble. 2 (of a telephone receiver) not on its rest.  [old english]'),
(19632, 'h', '\r\nHookah', ' n. Oriental tobacco-pipe with a long tube passing through water for cooling the smoke as it is drawn through. [urdu from arabic, = casket]'),
(19633, 'h', '\r\nHook', 'and eye  n. Small metal hook and loop as a fastener on a garment.'),
(19634, 'h', '\r\nHooked', ' adj. 1 hook-shaped. 2 (often foll. By on) slang addicted or captivated.'),
(19635, 'h', '\r\nHooker', ' n. 1 rugby player in the front row of the scrum who tries to hook the ball. 2 slang prostitute.'),
(19636, 'h', '\r\nHookey', ' n. Us  play hookey slang play truant. [origin unknown]'),
(19637, 'h', '\r\nHook,', 'line, and sinker  adv. Entirely.'),
(19638, 'h', '\r\nHook-up', ' n. Connection, esp. Of broadcasting equipment.'),
(19639, 'h', '\r\nHookworm', ' n. Worm with hooklike mouthparts, infesting humans and animals.'),
(19640, 'h', '\r\nHooligan', ' n. Young ruffian.  hooliganism n. [origin unknown]'),
(19641, 'h', '\r\nHoop', ' â€”n. 1 circular band of metal, wood, etc., esp. As part of a framework. 2 ring bowled along by a child, or for circus performers to jump through. 3 arch through which balls are hit in croquet. â€”v. Bind or encircle with hoop(s).  be put (or go) through the hoop (or hoops) undergo rigorous testing. [old english]'),
(19642, 'h', '\r\nHoop-la', ' n. Fairground game with rings thrown to encircle a prize.'),
(19643, 'h', '\r\nHoopoe', ' n. Salmon-pink bird with black and white wings and a large erectile crest. [latin upupa (imitative of its cry)]'),
(19644, 'h', '\r\nHooray', ' int. = *hurrah.'),
(19645, 'h', '\r\nHooray', 'henry  n. Slang loud upper-class young man.'),
(19646, 'h', '\r\nHoot', ' â€”n. 1 owl''s cry. 2 sound made by a car''s horn etc. 3 shout expressing scorn or disapproval. 4 colloq. A laughter. B cause of this. 5 (also two hoots) slang anything at all, in the slightest degree (don''t care a hoot; doesn''t matter two hoots). â€”v. 1 utter or make hoot(s). 2 greet or drive away with scornful hoots. 3 sound (a car horn etc.). [imitative]'),
(19647, 'h', '\r\nHooter', ' n. 1 thing that hoots, esp. A car''s horn or a siren. 2 slang nose.'),
(19648, 'h', '\r\nHoover', ' â€”n. Propr. Vacuum cleaner. â€”v. (hoover) 1 (also absol.) Clean with a vacuum cleaner. 2 (foll. By up) a suck up with a vacuum cleaner. B clean a room etc. With a vacuum cleaner. [name of the manufacturer]'),
(19649, 'h', '\r\nHooves', ' pl. Of *hoof.'),
(19650, 'h', '\r\nHop1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (of a bird, frog, etc.) Spring with two or all feet at once. 2 (of a person) jump on one foot. 3 move or go quickly (hopped over the fence). 4 cross (a ditch etc.) By hopping. â€”n. 1 hopping movement. 2 colloq. Informal dance. 3 short journey, esp. A flight.  hop in (or out) colloq. Get into (or out of) a car etc. Hop it slang go away. On the hop colloq. Unprepared (caught on the hop). [old english]'),
(19651, 'h', '\r\nHop2', ' n. 1 climbing plant bearing cones. 2 (in pl.) Its ripe cones, used to flavour beer. [low german or dutch]'),
(19652, 'h', '\r\nHope', ' â€”n. 1 expectation and desire for a thing. 2 person or thing giving cause for hope. 3 what is hoped for. â€”v. (-ping) 1 feel hope. 2 expect and desire. 3 feel fairly confident.  hope against hope cling to a mere possibility. [old english]'),
(19653, 'h', '\r\nHopeful', ' â€”adj. 1 feeling hope. 2 causing or inspiring hope. 3 likely to succeed, promising. â€”n. Person likely to succeed.'),
(19654, 'h', '\r\nHopefully', ' adv. 1 in a hopeful manner. 2 it is to be hoped (hopefully, we will succeed).'),
(19655, 'h', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of hopefully in sense 2 is common, but is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(19656, 'h', '\r\nHopeless', ' adj. 1 feeling no hope. 2 admitting no hope (hopeless case). 3 incompetent.  hopelessly adv. Hopelessness n.'),
(19657, 'h', '\r\nHopper1', ' n. 1 container tapering downward to an opening for discharging its contents. 2 hopping insect.'),
(19658, 'h', '\r\nHopper2', ' n. Hop-picker.'),
(19659, 'h', '\r\nHopping', 'mad  predic. Adj. Colloq. Very angry.'),
(19660, 'h', '\r\nHopscotch', ' n. Children''s game of hopping over squares marked on the ground to retrieve a stone etc. [from *hop1, *scotch]'),
(19661, 'h', '\r\nHorde', ' n. Usu. Derog. Large group, gang. [turkish ordu camp]'),
(19662, 'h', '\r\nHorehound', ' n. Herbaceous plant yielding a bitter aromatic juice used against coughs etc. [old english, = hoary herb]'),
(19663, 'h', '\r\nHorizon', ' n. 1 line at which the earth and sky appear to meet. 2 limit of mental perception, experience, interest, etc.  on the horizon (of an event) just imminent or becoming apparent. [greek horizo bound]'),
(19664, 'h', '\r\nHorizontal', ' â€”adj. 1 parallel to the plane of the horizon, at right angles to the vertical. 2 of or concerned with the same work, status, etc. (it was a horizontal move rather than promotion). â€”n. Horizontal line, plane, etc.  horizontality n. Horizontally adv.'),
(19665, 'h', '\r\nHormone', ' n. 1 regulatory substance produced in an organism and transported in tissue fluids to stimulate cells or tissues into action. 2 similar synthetic substance.  hormonal adj. [greek hormao impel]'),
(19666, 'h', '\r\nHormone', 'replacement therapy  n. Treatment to relieve menopausal symptoms by boosting a woman''s oestrogen levels.'),
(19667, 'h', '\r\nHorn', ' n. 1 a hard outgrowth, often curved and pointed, on the head of esp. Hoofed animals. B each of two branched appendages on the head of (esp. Male) deer. C hornlike projection on animals, e.g. A snail''s tentacle. 2 substance of which horns are made. 3 mus. A = *french horn. B wind instrument played by lip vibration, orig. Made of horn, now usu. Of brass. 4 instrument sounding a warning. 5 receptacle or instrument made of horn. 6 horn-shaped projection. 7 extremity of the moon or other crescent. 8 arm of a river etc.  horn in slang intrude, interfere.  horned adj. Hornist n. (in sense 3 of n.). [old english]'),
(19668, 'h', '\r\nHornbeam', ' n. Tree with a hard tough wood.'),
(19669, 'h', '\r\nHornbill', ' n. Bird with a hornlike excrescence on its large curved bill.'),
(19670, 'h', '\r\nHornblende', ' n. Dark-brown, black, or green mineral occurring in many rocks. [german]'),
(19671, 'h', '\r\nHornet', ' n. Large wasp capable of inflicting a serious sting. [low german or dutch]'),
(19672, 'h', '\r\nHorn', 'of plenty  n. A cornucopia.'),
(19673, 'h', '\r\nHornpipe', ' n. 1 lively dance (esp. Associated with sailors). 2 music for this.'),
(19674, 'h', '\r\nHorn-rimmed', ' adj. (esp. Of spectacles) having rims made of horn or a similar substance.'),
(19675, 'h', '\r\nHorny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like horn. 2 hard like horn. 3 slang sexually excited.  horniness n.'),
(19676, 'h', '\r\nHorology', ' n. Art of measuring time or making clocks, watches, etc.  horological adj. [greek hora time]'),
(19677, 'h', '\r\nHoroscope', ' n. 1 forecast of a person''s future from a diagram showing the relative positions of the stars and planets at his or her birth. 2 such a diagram. [greek hora time, skopos observer]'),
(19678, 'h', '\r\nHorrendous', ' adj. Horrifying.  horrendously adv. [latin: related to *horrible]'),
(19679, 'h', '\r\nHorrible', ' adj. 1 causing or likely to cause horror. 2 colloq. Unpleasant.  horribly adv. [latin horreo bristle, shudder at]'),
(19680, 'h', '\r\nHorrid', ' adj. 1 horrible, revolting. 2 colloq. Unpleasant (horrid weather).'),
(19681, 'h', '\r\nHorrific', ' adj. Horrifying.  horrifically adv.'),
(19682, 'h', '\r\nHorrify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) arouse horror in; shock.  horrifying adj.'),
(19683, 'h', '\r\nHorror', ' â€”n. 1 painful feeling of loathing and fear. 2 a (often foll. By of) intense dislike. B (often foll. By at) colloq. Intense dismay. 3 a person or thing causing horror. B colloq. Bad or mischievous person etc. 4 (in pl.; prec. By the) fit of depression, nervousness, etc. â€”attrib. Adj. (of films etc.) Designed to interest by arousing feelings of horror.'),
(19684, 'h', '\r\nHors', 'd''Å“uvre  n. Food served as an appetizer at the start of a meal. [french, = outside the work]'),
(19685, 'h', '\r\nHorse', ' â€”n. 1 a large four-legged mammal with flowing mane and tail, used for riding and to carry and pull loads. B adult male horse; stallion or gelding. C (collect.; as sing.) Cavalry. 2 vaulting-block. 3 supporting frame (clothes-horse). â€”v. (-sing) (foll. By around) fool about.  from the horse''s mouth colloq. (of information etc.) From the original or an authoritative source. [old english]'),
(19686, 'h', '\r\nHorseback', ' n.  on horseback mounted on a horse.'),
(19687, 'h', '\r\nHorsebox', ' n. Closed vehicle for transporting horse(s).'),
(19688, 'h', '\r\nHorse-brass', ' n. Brass ornament orig. For a horse''s harness.'),
(19689, 'h', '\r\nHorse', 'chestnut  n. 1 large tree with upright conical clusters of flowers. 2 dark brown fruit of this.'),
(19690, 'h', '\r\nHorse-drawn', ' adj. (of a vehicle) pulled by a horse or horses.'),
(19691, 'h', '\r\nHorseflesh', ' n. 1 flesh of a horse, esp. As food. 2 horses collectively.'),
(19692, 'h', '\r\nHorsefly', ' n. Any of various biting insects troublesome esp. To horses.'),
(19693, 'h', '\r\nHorse', 'guards  n.pl. Cavalry brigade of the household troops.'),
(19694, 'h', '\r\nHorsehair', ' n. Hair from the mane or tail of a horse, used for padding etc.'),
(19695, 'h', '\r\nHorseman', ' n. 1 rider on horseback. 2 skilled rider.  horsemanship n.'),
