(19874, 'h', '\r\nHumility', ' n. 1 humbleness, meekness. 2 humble condition. [french: related to *humiliate]'),
(19875, 'h', '\r\nHummingbird', ' n. Small tropical bird that makes a humming sound with its wings when it hovers.'),
(19876, 'h', '\r\nHummock', ' n. Hillock or hump. [origin unknown]'),
(19877, 'h', '\r\nHummus', ' n. (also houmous) dip or appetizer made from ground chick-peas, sesame oil, lemon, and garlic. [turkish]'),
(19878, 'h', '\r\nHumor', ' (brit. Humour) â€”n. 1 a quality of being amusing or comic. B the expression of humour in literature, speech, etc. 2 (in full sense of humour) ability to perceive or express humour. 3 state of mind; inclination (bad humour). 4 (in full cardinal humour) hist. Each of the four fluids (blood, phlegm, choler, melancholy), thought to determine a person''s physical and mental qualities. â€”v. Gratify or indulge (a person or taste etc.).  out of humour displeased.  humourless adj. [latin humor moisture]'),
(19879, 'h', '\r\nHumoresque', ' n. Short lively piece of music. [german humoreske]'),
(19880, 'h', '\r\nHumorist', ' n. Humorous writer, talker, or actor.'),
(19881, 'h', '\r\nHumorous', ' adj. Showing humour or a sense of humour.  humorously adv.'),
(19882, 'h', '\r\nHumour', ' (us humor) â€”n. 1 a quality of being amusing or comic. B the expression of humour in literature, speech, etc. 2 (in full sense of humour) ability to perceive or express humour. 3 state of mind; inclination (bad humour). 4 (in full cardinal humour) hist. Each of the four fluids (blood, phlegm, choler, melancholy), thought to determine a person''s physical and mental qualities. â€”v. Gratify or indulge (a person or taste etc.).  out of humour displeased.  humourless adj. [latin humor moisture]'),
(19883, 'h', '\r\nHump', ' â€”n. 1 rounded protuberance on a camel''s back, or as an abnormality on a person''s back. 2 rounded raised mass of earth etc. 3 critical point in an undertaking. 4 (prec. By the) slang fit of depression or vexation (gave me the hump). â€”v. 1 (often foll. By about) colloq. Lift or carry (heavy objects etc.) With difficulty. 2 make hump-shaped. [probably low german or dutch]'),
(19884, 'h', '\r\nHumpback', ' n. 1 a deformed back with a hump. B person with this. 2 whale with a dorsal fin forming a hump.  humpbacked adj.'),
(19885, 'h', '\r\nHumpback', 'bridge  n. Small bridge with a steep ascent and descent.'),
(19886, 'h', '\r\nHumph', ' int. & n. Inarticulate sound of doubt or dissatisfaction. [imitative]'),
(19887, 'h', '\r\nHumus', ' n. Organic constituent of soil formed by the decomposition of vegetation. [latin, = soil]'),
(19888, 'h', '\r\nHun', ' n. 1 offens. German (esp. In military contexts). 2 member of a warlike asiatic nomadic people who ravaged europe in the 4thâ€“5th c. 3 vandal.  hunnish adj. [old english]'),
(19889, 'h', '\r\nHunch', ' â€”v. Bend or arch into a hump. â€”n. 1 intuitive feeling or idea. 2 hump. [origin unknown]'),
(19890, 'h', '\r\nHunchback', ' n. = *humpback 1.  hunchbacked adj.'),
(19891, 'h', '\r\nHundred', ' adj. & n. (pl. Hundreds or (in sense 1) hundred) (in sing., prec. By a or one) 1 ten times ten. 2 symbol for this (100, c, c). 3 (in sing. Or pl.) Colloq. A large number. 4 (in pl.) The years of a specified century (the seventeen hundreds). 5 hist. Subdivision of a county or shire, having its own court.  hundredfold adj. & adv. Hundredth adj. & n. [old english]'),
(19892, 'h', '\r\nHundreds', 'and thousands  n.pl. Tiny coloured sweets for decorating cakes etc.'),
(19893, 'h', '\r\nHundredweight', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 unit of weight equal to 112 lb, or us equal to 100 lb. 2 unit of weight equal to 50 kg.'),
(19894, 'h', '\r\nHung', ' past and past part. Of *hang.'),
(19895, 'h', '\r\nHungarian', ' â€”n. 1 a native or national of hungary. B person of hungarian descent. 2 language of hungary. â€”adj. Of hungary or its people or language. [medieval latin]'),
(19896, 'h', '\r\nHunger', ' â€”n. 1 a lack of food. B feeling of discomfort or exhaustion caused by this. 2 (often foll. By for, after) strong desire. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By for, after) crave or desire. 2 feel hunger. [old english]'),
(19897, 'h', '\r\nHunger', 'strike  n. Refusal of food as a protest.'),
(19898, 'h', '\r\nHung-over', ' adj. Colloq. Suffering from a hangover.'),
(19899, 'h', '\r\nHung', 'parliament  n. Parliament in which no party has a clear majority.'),
(19900, 'h', '\r\nHungry', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 feeling or showing hunger; needing food. 2 inducing hunger (hungry work). 3 craving (hungry for news).  hungrily adv. [old english]'),
(19901, 'h', '\r\nHunk', ' n. 1 large piece cut off (hunk of bread). 2 colloq. Sexually attractive man.  hunky adj. (-ier, -iest). [probably dutch]'),
(19902, 'h', '\r\nHunky-dory', ' adj. Esp. Us colloq. Excellent. [origin unknown]'),
(19903, 'h', '\r\nHunt', ' â€”v. 1 (also absol.) A pursue and kill (wild animals, esp. Foxes, or game) for sport or food. B use (a horse or hounds) for hunting. C (of an animal) chase (its prey). 2 (foll. By after, for) seek, search. 3 (of an engine etc.) Run alternately too fast and too slow. 4 scour (a district) for game. 5 (as hunted adj.) (of a look etc.) Terrified as if being hunted. â€”n. 1 practice or instance of hunting. 2 a association of people hunting with hounds. B area for hunting.  hunt down pursue and capture.  hunting n. [old english]'),
(19904, 'h', '\r\nHunter', ' n. 1 a (fem. Huntress) person or animal that hunts. B horse used in hunting. 2 person who seeks something. 3 pocket-watch with a hinged cover protecting the glass.'),
(19905, 'h', '\r\nHunter''s', 'moon  n. Next full moon after the harvest moon.'),
(19906, 'h', '\r\nHuntsman', ' n. 1 hunter. 2 hunt official in charge of hounds.'),
(19907, 'h', '\r\nHurdle', ' â€”n. 1 a each of a series of light frames to be cleared by athletes in a race. B (in pl.) Hurdle-race. 2 obstacle or difficulty. 3 portable rectangular frame used as a temporary fence etc. â€”v. (-ling) 1 run in a hurdle-race. 2 fence off etc. With hurdles. [old english]'),
(19908, 'h', '\r\nHurdler', ' n. 1 athlete who runs in hurdle-races. 2 maker of hurdles.'),
(19909, 'h', '\r\nHurdy-gurdy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 droning musical instrument played by turning a handle. 2 colloq. Barrel-organ. [imitative]'),
(19910, 'h', '\r\nHurl', ' â€”v. 1 throw with great force. 2 utter (abuse etc.) Vehemently. â€”n. Forceful throw. [imitative]'),
(19911, 'h', '\r\nHurley', ' n. 1 (also hurling) irish game resembling hockey. 2 stick used in this.'),
(19912, 'h', '\r\nHurly-burly', ' n. Boisterous activity; commotion. [a reduplication of *hurl]'),
(19913, 'h', '\r\nHurrah', ' int. & n. (also hurray) exclamation of joy or approval. [earlier huzza, origin uncertain]'),
(19914, 'h', '\r\nHurricane', ' n. 1 storm with a violent wind, esp. A w. Indian cyclone. 2 meteorol. Wind of 65 knots (75 m.p.h.) Or more, force 12 on the beaufort scale. [spanish and portuguese from carib]'),
(19915, 'h', '\r\nHurricane-lamp', ' n. Oil-lamp designed to resist a high wind.'),
(19916, 'h', '\r\nHurry', ' â€”n. 1 great or eager haste. 2 (with neg. Or interrog.) Need for haste (there is no hurry; what''s the hurry?). â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 move or act hastily. 2 cause to hurry.'),
(19917, 'h', '\r\nHurt', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Hurt) 1 (also absol.) Cause pain or injury to. 2 cause mental pain or distress to. 3 suffer pain (my arm hurts). â€”n. 1 injury. 2 harm, wrong. [french hurter knock]'),
(19918, 'h', '\r\nHurtful', ' adj. Causing (esp. Mental) hurt.  hurtfully adv.'),
(19919, 'h', '\r\nHurtle', ' v. (-ling) 1 move or hurl rapidly or noisily. 2 come with a crash. [from *hurt in the obsolete sense â€˜strike hardâ€™]'),
(19920, 'h', '\r\nHusband', ' â€”n. Married man, esp. In relation to his wife. â€”v. Use (resources) economically; eke out. [old english, = house-dweller]'),
(19921, 'h', '\r\nHusbandry', ' n. 1 farming. 2 management of resources.'),
(19922, 'h', '\r\nHush', ' â€”v. Make or become silent or quiet. â€”int. Calling for silence. â€”n. Expectant stillness or silence.  hush up suppress public mention of (an affair). [husht, an obsolete exclamation, taken as a past part.]'),
(19923, 'h', '\r\nHush-hush', ' adj. Colloq. Highly secret, confidential.'),
(19924, 'h', '\r\nHush', 'money  n. Slang money paid to ensure discretion.');
