(20889, 'i', '\r\nInsinuate', ' v. (-ting) 1 hint obliquely, esp. Unpleasantly. 2 (often refl.; usu. Foll. By into) a introduce (a person etc.) Into favour etc., by subtle manipulation. B introduce (a thing, oneself, etc.) Deviously into a place.  insinuation n. [latin sinuo curve]'),
(20890, 'i', '\r\nInsipid', ' adj. 1 lacking vigour or character; dull. 2 tasteless.  insipidity n. Insipidly adv. [latin sapio have savour]'),
(20891, 'i', '\r\nInsist', ' v. (usu. Foll. By on or that; also absol.) Maintain or demand assertively (insisted on my going; insisted that he was innocent). [latin sisto stand]'),
(20892, 'i', '\r\nInsistent', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By on) insisting. 2 forcing itself on the attention.  insistence n. Insistently adv.'),
