(20922, 'i', '\r\nInstil', ' v. (us instill) (-ll-) (often foll. By into) 1 introduce (a feeling, idea, etc.) Into a person''s mind etc. Gradually. 2 put (a liquid) into something in drops.  instillation n. Instilment n. [latin stillo drop]'),
(20923, 'i', '\r\nInstill', ' v. (brit. Instil) (-ll-) (often foll. By into) 1 introduce (a feeling, idea, etc.) Into a person''s mind etc. Gradually. 2 put (a liquid) into something in drops.  instillation n. Instilment n. [latin stillo drop]'),
(20924, 'i', '\r\nInstinct', ' â€”n. 1 a innate pattern of behaviour, esp. In animals. B innate impulse. 2 intuition. â€”predic. Adj. (foll. By with) imbued, filled (with life, beauty, etc.).  instinctive adj. Instinctively adv. Instinctual adj. [latin stinguo prick]'),
(20925, 'i', '\r\nInstitute', ' â€”n. 1 society or organization for the promotion of science, education, etc. 2 its premises. â€”v. (-ting) 1 establish; found. 2 initiate (an inquiry etc.). 3 (usu. Foll. By to, into) appoint (a person) as a cleric in a church etc. [latin statuo set up]'),
(20926, 'i', '\r\nInstitution', ' n. 1 organization or society founded for a particular purpose. 2 established law, practice, or custom. 3 colloq. (of a person etc.) Familiar object. 4 instituting or being instituted.'),
