(21003, 'i', '\r\nIntercommunion', ' n. 1 mutual communion. 2 mutual action or relationship, esp. Between christian denominations.'),
(21004, 'i', '\r\nInterconnect', ' v. Connect with each other.  interconnection n.'),
(21005, 'i', '\r\nIntercontinental', ' adj. Connecting or travelling between continents.'),
(21006, 'i', '\r\nIntercourse', ' n. 1 communication or dealings between individuals, nations, etc. 2 = *sexual intercourse. [latin: related to *course]'),
(21007, 'i', '\r\nInterdenominational', ' adj. Concerning more than one (religious) denomination.'),
(21008, 'i', '\r\nInterdepartmental', ' adj. Concerning more than one department.'),
(21009, 'i', '\r\nInterdependent', ' adj. Dependent on each other.  interdependence n.'),
(21010, 'i', '\r\nInterdict', ' â€”n. 1 authoritative prohibition. 2 rc ch. Sentence debarring a person, or esp. A place, from ecclesiastical functions and privileges. â€”v. 1 prohibit (an action). 2 forbid the use of. 3 (usu. Foll. By from + verbal noun) restrain (a person). 4 (usu. Foll. By to) forbid (a thing) to a person.  interdiction n. Interdictory adj. [latin dico say]'),
(21011, 'i', '\r\nInterdisciplinary', ' adj. Of or between more than one branch of learning.'),
(21012, 'i', '\r\nInterest', ' â€”n. 1 a concern; curiosity (have no interest in fishing). B quality exciting curiosity etc. (this book lacks interest). 2 subject, hobby, etc., in which one is concerned. 3 advantage or profit (it is in my interest to go). 4 money paid for the use of money lent. 5 a thing in which one has a stake or concern (business interests). B financial stake (in an undertaking etc.). C legal concern, title, or right (in property). 6 a party or group with a common interest (the brewing interest). B principle or cause with which this is concerned. â€”v. 1 excite the curiosity or attention of. 2 (usu. Foll. By in) cause (a person) to take a personal interest. 3 (as interested adj.) Having a private interest; not impartial or disinterested. [latin, = it matters]'),
(21013, 'i', '\r\nInteresting', ' adj. Causing curiosity; holding the attention.  interestingly adv.'),
(21014, 'i', '\r\nInterface', ' â€”n. 1 surface forming a boundary between two regions. 2 means or place of interaction between two systems etc.; interaction (the interface between psychology and education). 3 esp. Computing apparatus for connecting two pieces of equipment so that they can be operated jointly. â€”v. (-cing) (often foll. By with) 1 connect with (another piece of equipment etc.) By an interface. 2 interact.'),
(21015, 'i', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of the noun and verb in sense 2 is deplored by some people.'),
(21016, 'i', '\r\nInterfacing', ' n. Stiffish material between two layers of fabric in collars etc.'),
(21017, 'i', '\r\nInterfere', ' v. (-ring) 1 (usu. Foll. By with) a (of a person) meddle; obstruct a process etc. B (of a thing) be a hindrance. 2 (usu. Foll. By in) intervene, esp. Without invitation or necessity. 3 (foll. By with) euphem. Molest or assault sexually. 4 (of light or other waves) combine so as to cause interference. [latin ferio strike]'),
(21018, 'i', '\r\nInterference', ' n. 1 act of interfering. 2 fading or disturbance of received radio signals. 3 physics combination of two or more wave motions to form a resultant wave in which the displacement is reinforced or cancelled.');
