(21535, 'j', '\r\nJingle', ' â€”n. 1 mixed ringing or clinking noise. 2 a repetition of sounds in a phrase etc. B short catchy verse or song in advertising etc. â€”v. (-ling) 1 (cause to) make a jingling sound. 2 (of writing) be full of alliteration, rhymes, etc. [imitative]'),
(21536, 'j', '\r\nJingo', ' n. (pl. -es) supporter of war; blustering patriot.  by jingo! Mild oath.  jingoism n. Jingoist n. Jingoistic adj. [conjuror''s word]'),
(21537, 'j', '\r\nJink', ' â€”v. 1 move elusively; dodge. 2 elude by dodging. â€”n. Dodging or eluding. [originally scots: imitative]'),
(21538, 'j', '\r\nJinnee', ' n. (also jinn, djinn) (pl. Jinn or djinn) (in muslim mythology) spirit in human or animal form having power over people. [arabic]'),
(21539, 'j', '\r\nJinx', ' colloq. â€”n. Person or thing that seems to cause bad luck. â€”v. (esp. As jinxed adj.) Subject to bad luck. [perhaps var. Of jynx wryneck, charm]'),
(21540, 'j', '\r\nJitter', ' colloq. â€”n. (the jitters) extreme nervousness. â€”v. Be nervous; act nervously.  jittery adj. Jitteriness n. [origin unknown]'),
(21541, 'j', '\r\nJitterbug', ' â€”n. 1 nervous person. 2 hist. Fast popular dance. â€”v. (-gg-) hist. Dance the jitterbug.'),
(21542, 'j', '\r\nJiu-jitsu', ' var. Of *ju-jitsu.'),
(21543, 'j', '\r\nJive', ' â€”n. 1 lively dance popular esp. In the 1950s. 2 music for this. â€”v. (-ving) dance to or play jive music.  jiver n. [origin uncertain]'),
(21544, 'j', '\r\nJnr.', ' Abbr. Junior.'),
(21545, 'j', '\r\nJob', ' â€”n. 1 piece of work to be done; task. 2 position in, or piece of, paid employment. 3 colloq. Difficult task (had a job to find it). 4 slang crime, esp. A robbery. 5 state of affairs etc. (bad job). â€”v. (-bb-) 1 do jobs; do piece-work. 2 deal in stocks; buy and sell (stocks or goods). 3 deal corruptly with (a matter).  just the job colloq. Exactly what is wanted. Make a job (or good job) of do well. On the job colloq. 1 at work. 2 engaged in sexual intercourse. Out of a job unemployed. [origin unknown]'),
(21546, 'j', '\r\nJobber', ' n. 1 person who jobs. 2 hist. Principal or wholesaler on the stock exchange.'),
(21547, 'j', '\r\nUsage', ' up to oct. 1986 jobbers were permitted to deal only with brokers, not directly with the public. From oct. 1986 the name ceased to be in official use (see *broker 2).'),
(21548, 'j', '\r\nJobbery', ' n. Corrupt dealing.'),
(21549, 'j', '\r\nJobbing', ' attrib. Adj. Freelance; pieceworking (jobbing gardener).'),
(21550, 'j', '\r\nJobcentre', ' n. Local government office advertising available jobs.'),
(21551, 'j', '\r\nJob-hunt', ' v. Colloq. Seek employment.'),
(21552, 'j', '\r\nJobless', ' adj. Unemployed.  joblessness n.'),
(21553, 'j', '\r\nJob', 'lot  n. Mixed lot bought at auction etc.'),
(21554, 'j', '\r\nJob''s', 'comforter  n. Person who intends to comfort but increases distress. [job in the old testament]'),
(21555, 'j', '\r\nJobs', 'for the boys  n.pl. Colloq. Appointments for members of one''s own group etc.'),
(21556, 'j', '\r\nJob-sharing', ' n. Sharing of a full-time job by two or more people.  job-share n. & v.'),
(21557, 'j', '\r\nJobsheet', ' n. Sheet for recording details of jobs done.'),
(21558, 'j', '\r\nJock', ' n. Slang scotsman. [scots form of the name jack]'),
(21559, 'j', '\r\nJockey', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) rider in horse-races, esp. Professional. â€”v. (-eys, -eyed) 1 trick, cheat, or outwit. 2 (foll. By away, out, into, etc.) Manoeuvre (a person).  jockey for position manoeuvre for advantage. [diminutive of *jock]'),
(21560, 'j', '\r\nJockstrap', ' n. Support or protection for the male genitals, worn esp. In sport. [slang jock genitals]'),
(21561, 'j', '\r\nJocose', ' adj. Playful; jocular.  jocosely adv. Jocosity n. (pl. -ies). [latin jocus jest]'),
