(21573, 'j', '\r\nJohnny', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 slang condom. 2 colloq. Fellow; man. [diminutive of john]'),
(21574, 'j', '\r\nJohnny-come-lately', ' n. Colloq. Newcomer; upstart.'),
(21575, 'j', '\r\nJoie', 'de vivre  n. Exuberance; high spirits. [french, = joy of living]'),
(21576, 'j', '\r\nJoin', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By to, together) put together; fasten, unite (with one or several things or people). 2 connect (points) by a line etc. 3 become a member of (a club, organization, etc.). 4 a take one''s place with (a person, group, etc.). B (foll. By in, for, etc.) Take part with (others) in an activity etc. (joined them in prayer). 5 (often foll. By with, to) come together; be united. 6 (of a river etc.) Be or become connected or continuous with. â€”n. Point, line, or surface at which things are joined.  join battle begin fighting. Join forces combine efforts. Join hands 1 clasp hands. 2 combine in an action etc. Join in (also absol.) Take part in (an activity). Join up 1 enlist for military service. 2 (often foll. By with) unite, connect. [latin jungo junct-]'),
(21577, 'j', '\r\nJoiner', ' n. 1 maker of finished wood fittings. 2 colloq. Person who joins an organization or who readily joins societies etc.  joinery n. (in sense 1).'),
(21578, 'j', '\r\nJoint', ' â€”n. 1 place at which two or more things or parts of a structure are joined; device for joining these. 2 point at which two bones fit together. 3 division of an animal carcass as meat. 4 slang restaurant, bar, etc. 5 slang marijuana cigarette. 6 geol. Crack in rock. â€”adj. 1 held, done by, or belonging to, two or more persons etc. (joint mortgage; joint action). 2 sharing with another (joint author; joint favourite). â€”v. 1 connect by joint(s). 2 divide at a joint or into joints.  out of joint 1 (of a bone) dislocated. 2 out of order.  jointly adv. [french: related to *join]'),
(21579, 'j', '\r\nJoint', 'stock  n. Capital held jointly; common fund.'),
(21580, 'j', '\r\nJoint-stock', 'company  n. Company formed on the basis of a joint stock.'),
(21581, 'j', '\r\nJointure', ' â€”n. Estate settled on a wife by her husband for use after his death. â€”v. Provide with a jointure. [latin: related to *join]'),
(21582, 'j', '\r\nJoist', ' n. Supporting beam in a floor, ceiling, etc. [french giste from latin jaceo lie]'),
(21583, 'j', '\r\nJojoba', ' n. Plant with seeds yielding an oily extract used in cosmetics etc. [mexican spanish]'),
(21584, 'j', '\r\nJoke', ' â€”n. 1 thing said or done to cause laughter; witticism. 2 ridiculous person or thing. â€”v. (-king) make jokes; tease (only joking).  no joke colloq. Serious matter.  jokingly adv. Joky adj. (also jokey). Jokily adv. Jokiness n. [probably latin jocus jest]'),
(21585, 'j', '\r\nJoker', ' n. 1 person who jokes. 2 slang person. 3 playing-card used in some games.'),
(21586, 'j', '\r\nJollify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make merry.  jollification n.'),
(21587, 'j', '\r\nJollity', ' n. (pl. -ies) merrymaking; festivity. [french jolivetÃ©: related to *jolly1]'),
(21588, 'j', '\r\nJolly1', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 cheerful; merry. 2 festive, jovial. 3 colloq. Pleasant, delightful. â€”adv. Colloq. Very. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) (usu. Foll. By along) colloq. Coax or humour in a friendly way. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Party or celebration.  jollily adv. Jolliness n. [french jolif gay, pretty: perhaps related to *yule]'),
(21589, 'j', '\r\nJolly2', ' n. (pl. -ies) (in full jolly boat) clinker-built ship''s boat smaller than a cutter. [origin unknown: perhaps related to *yawl]'),
