(21653, 'j', '\r\nJumble', ' â€”v. (-ling) (often foll. By up) confuse; mix up; muddle. â€”n. 1 confused state or heap; muddle. 2 articles in a jumble sale. [probably imitative]'),
(21654, 'j', '\r\nJumble', 'sale  n. Sale of second-hand articles, esp. For charity.'),
(21655, 'j', '\r\nJumbo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. 1 (often attrib.) Large animal (esp. An elephant), person, or thing (jumbo packet). 2 (in full jumbo jet) large airliner for several hundred passengers. [probably from *mumbo-jumbo]'),
(21656, 'j', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 2, jumbo is usu. Applied specifically to the boeing 747.'),
(21657, 'j', '\r\nJump', ' â€”v. 1 rise off the ground etc. By sudden muscular effort in the legs. 2 (often foll. By up, from, in, out, etc.) Move suddenly or hastily (jumped into the car). 3 jerk or twitch from shock or excitement etc. 4 a change, esp. Advance in status or rise, rapidly (prices jumped). B cause to do this. 5 (often foll. By about) change the subject etc. Rapidly. 6 pass over (an obstacle etc.) By jumping. 7 skip (a passage in a book etc.). 8 cause (a horse etc.) To jump. 9 (foll. By to, at) reach (a conclusion) hastily. 10 (of a train) leave (the rails). 11 pass (a red traffic-light etc.). 12 get on or off (a train etc.) Quickly, esp. Illegally or dangerously. 13 attack (a person) unexpectedly. â€”n. 1 act of jumping. 2 sudden jerk caused by shock or excitement. 3 abrupt rise in amount, value, status, etc. 4 obstacle to be jumped. 5 a sudden transition. B gap in a series, logical sequence, etc.  jump at accept eagerly. Jump bail fail to appear for trial having been released on bail. Jump down a person''s throat colloq. Reprimand or contradict a person fiercely. Jump the gun colloq. Begin prematurely. Jump on colloq. Attack or criticize severely. Jump out of one''s skin colloq. Be extremely startled. Jump the queue take unfair precedence. Jump ship (of a seaman) desert. Jump to it colloq. Act promptly. One jump ahead one stage further on than a rival etc. [imitative]'),
(21658, 'j', '\r\nJumped-up', ' adj. Colloq. Upstart.'),
(21659, 'j', '\r\nJumper1', ' n. 1 knitted pullover. 2 loose outer jacket worn by sailors. 3 us pinafore dress. [probably jump short coat]'),
(21660, 'j', '\r\nJumper2', ' n. 1 person or animal that jumps. 2 short wire used to make or break an electrical circuit.'),
(21661, 'j', '\r\nJumping', 'bean  n. Seed of a mexican plant that jumps with the movement of a larva inside.'),
(21662, 'j', '\r\nJump-jet', ' n. Vertical take-off jet aircraft.'),
(21663, 'j', '\r\nJump-lead', ' n. Cable for conveying current from the battery of one vehicle to that of another.'),
(21664, 'j', '\r\nJump-off', ' n. Deciding round in showjumping.'),
(21665, 'j', '\r\nJump-start', ' â€”v. Start (a vehicle) by pushing it or with jump-leads. â€”n. Act of jump-starting.'),
(21666, 'j', '\r\nJump', 'suit  n. One-piece garment for the whole body.'),
(21667, 'j', '\r\nJumpy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 nervous; easily startled. 2 making sudden movements.  jumpiness n.'),
(21668, 'j', '\r\nJun.', ' Abbr. 1 june. 2 junior.'),
(21669, 'j', '\r\nJunction', ' n. 1 joint; joining-point. 2 place where railway lines or roads meet. 3 joining. [latin: related to *join]'),
(21670, 'j', '\r\nJunction', 'box  n. Box containing a junction of electric cables etc.'),
(21671, 'j', '\r\nJuncture', ' n. 1 critical convergence of events; point of time (at this juncture). 2 joining-point. 3 joining.'),
(21672, 'j', '\r\nJune', ' n. Sixth month of the year. [latin junius from juno, name of a goddess]'),
(21673, 'j', '\r\nJungian', ' â€”adj. Of the swiss psychologist carl jung or his theories. â€”n. Supporter of jung or of his theories.'),
(21674, 'j', '\r\nJungle', ' n. 1 a land overgrown with tangled vegetation, esp. In the tropics. B an area of this. 2 wild tangled mass. 3 place of bewildering complexity, confusion, or struggle.  law of the jungle state of ruthless competition.  jungly adj. [hindi from sanskrit]'),
(21675, 'j', '\r\nJunior', ' â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By to) inferior in age, standing, or position. 2 the younger (esp. Appended to the name of a son for distinction from his father). 3 of the lower or lowest position (junior partner). 4  (of a school) for younger pupils, usu. Aged 7â€“11. â€”n. 1 junior person. 2 person at the lowest level (in an office etc.). [latin, comparative of juvenis young]'),
(21676, 'j', '\r\nJunior', 'common room  n. (also junior combination room) 1 common-room for undergraduates in a college. 2 undergraduates of a college collectively.'),
(21677, 'j', '\r\nJuniper', ' n. Evergreen shrub or tree with prickly leaves and dark-purple berry-like cones. [latin juniperus]'),
(21678, 'j', '\r\nJunk1', ' â€”n. 1 discarded articles; rubbish. 2 anything regarded as of little value. 3 slang narcotic drug, esp. Heroin. â€”v. Discard as junk. [origin unknown]'),
(21679, 'j', '\r\nJunk2', ' n. Flat-bottomed sailing-vessel in the china seas. [javanese djong]'),
(21680, 'j', '\r\nJunk', 'bond  n. Bond bearing high interest but deemed to be a risky investment.'),
(21681, 'j', '\r\nJunket', ' â€”n. 1 pleasure outing. 2 official''s tour at public expense. 3 sweetened and flavoured milk curds. 4 feast. â€”v. (-t-) feast, picnic. [french jonquette rush-basket (used for junket 3 and 4), from latin juncus rush]'),
(21682, 'j', '\r\nJunk', 'food  n. Food, such as sweets and crisps, with low nutritional value.'),
(21683, 'j', '\r\nJunkie', ' n. Slang drug addict.'),
(21684, 'j', '\r\nJunk', 'mail  n. Unsolicited advertising matter sent by post.'),
(21685, 'j', '\r\nJunk', 'shop  n. Second-hand or cheap antiques shop.'),
(21686, 'j', '\r\nJunta', ' n. (usu. Military) clique taking power in a coup d''Ã©tat. [spanish: related to *join]'),
(21687, 'j', '\r\nJural', ' adj. 1 of law. 2 of rights and obligations. [latin jus jur- law, right]'),
(21688, 'j', '\r\nJurassic', ' geol. â€”adj. Of the second period of the mesozoic era. â€”n. This era or system. [french from jura mountains]'),
(21689, 'j', '\r\nJuridical', ' n. 1 of judicial proceedings. 2 relating to the law. [latin jus jur- law, dico say]'),
(21690, 'j', '\r\nJurisdiction', ' n. 1 (often foll. By over, of) administration of justice. 2 a legal or other authority. B extent of this; territory it extends over.  jurisdictional adj.'),
(21691, 'j', '\r\nJurisprudence', ' n. Science or philosophy of law.  jurisprudential adj.'),
(21692, 'j', '\r\nJurist', ' n. Expert in law.  juristic adj.'),
(21693, 'j', '\r\nJuror', ' n. 1 member of a jury. 2 person taking an oath.'),
(21694, 'j', '\r\nJury', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 body of usu. Twelve people giving a verdict in a court of justice. 2 body of people awarding prizes in a competition.'),
(21695, 'j', '\r\nJury-box', ' n. Enclosure for the jury in a lawcourt.'),
(21696, 'j', '\r\nJury-rigged', ' adj. Naut. Having temporary makeshift rigging. [origin uncertain]'),
(21697, 'j', '\r\nJust', ' â€”adj. 1 morally right or fair. 2 (of treatment etc.) Deserved (just reward). 3 well-grounded; justified (just anger). 4 right in amount etc.; proper. â€”adv. 1 exactly (just what i need). 2 a little time ago; very recently (has just seen them). 3 colloq. Simply, merely (just good friends; just doesn''t make sense). 4 barely; no more than (just managed it). 5 colloq. Positively; indeed (just splendid; won''t i just tell him!). 6 quite (not just yet).  just about colloq. Almost exactly; almost completely. Just in case as a precaution. Just now 1 at this moment. 2 a little time ago. Just the same = all the same. Just so 1 exactly arranged (everything just so). 2 it is exactly as you say.  justly adv. Justness n. [latin justus from jus right]'),
(21698, 'j', '\r\nJustice', ' n. 1 justness, fairness. 2 authority exercised in the maintenance of right. 3 judicial proceedings (brought to justice; court of justice). 4 magistrate; judge.  do justice to 1 treat fairly. 2 appreciate properly. Do oneself justice perform at one''s best. With justice reasonably. [latin justitia]'),
(21699, 'j', '\r\nJustice', 'of the peace  n. Unpaid lay magistrate appointed to hear minor cases.'),
(21700, 'j', '\r\nJustifiable', ' adj. Able to be justified.  justifiably adv.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(21701, 'j', '\r\nJustify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 show the justice or correctness of (a person, act, assertion, etc.). 2 (esp. In passive) cite or constitute adequate grounds for (conduct, a claim, etc.); vindicate. 3 (as justified adj.) Just, right (justified in assuming). 4 printing adjust (a line of type) to give even margins.  justification n. Justificatory adj.'),
(21702, 'j', '\r\nJut', ' â€”v. (-tt-) (often foll. By out, forth) protrude, project. â€”n. Projection. [var. Of *jet1]'),
(21703, 'j', '\r\nJute', ' n. 1 fibre from the bark of an e. Indian plant, used esp. For sacking, mats, etc. 2 plant yielding this. [bengali]'),
(21704, 'j', '\r\nJuvenile', ' â€”adj. 1 a youthful. B of or for young people. 2 often derog. Immature (juvenile behaviour). â€”n. 1 young person. 2 actor playing a juvenile part. [latin juvenis young]'),
(21705, 'j', '\r\nJuvenile', 'court  n. Court for children under 17.'),
(21706, 'j', '\r\nJuvenile', 'delinquency  n. Offences committed by people below the age of legal responsibility.  juvenile delinquent n.'),
(21707, 'j', '\r\nJuvenilia', ' n.pl. Author''s or artist''s youthful works.'),
(21708, 'j', '\r\nJuxtapose', ' v. (-sing) 1 place (things) side by side. 2 (foll. By to, with) place (a thing) beside another.  juxtaposition n. Juxtapositional adj. [latin juxta next, pono put]'),
(21709, 'k', 'K1', ' n. (also k) (pl. Ks or k''s) eleventh letter of the alphabet.'),
(21710, 'k', '\r\nK2', ' abbr. (also k.) 1 kelvin(s). 2 king, king''s. 3 kÃ¶chel (catalogue of mozart''s works). 4 (also k) (prec. By a numeral) a computing unit of 1,024 (i.e. 210) bytes or bits, or loosely 1,000. B 1,000. [sense 4 as abbreviation of *kilo-]'),
(21711, 'k', '\r\nK3', ' symb. Potassium. [latin kalium]'),
(21712, 'k', '\r\nK', ' abbr. 1 kilo-. 2 knot(s).'),
(21713, 'k', '\r\nKaffir', ' n. 1 hist. Member or language of a s. African people of the bantu family. 2 s.afr. Offens. Any black african. [arabic, = infidel]'),
(21714, 'k', '\r\nKafkaesque', ' adj. Impenetrably oppressive or nightmarish, as in the fiction of franz kafka.'),
(21715, 'k', '\r\nKaftan', ' var. Of *caftan.'),
(21716, 'k', '\r\nKaiser', ' n. Hist. Emperor, esp. Of germany, austria, or the holy roman empire. [latin *caesar]'),
(21717, 'k', '\r\nKalashnikov', ' n. Type of soviet rifle or sub-machine-gun. [russian]'),
(21718, 'k', '\r\nKale', ' n. Variety of cabbage, esp. With wrinkled leaves and no heart. [northern var. Of *cole]'),
(21719, 'k', '\r\nKaleidoscope', ' n. 1 tube containing mirrors and pieces of coloured glass etc. Producing changing reflected patterns when shaken. 2 constantly changing pattern, group, etc.  kaleidoscopic adj. [greek kalos beautiful, eidos form, *-scope]'),
(21720, 'k', '\r\nKalends', ' var. Of *calends.'),
(21721, 'k', '\r\nKaleyard', ' n. Scot. Kitchen garden.'),
(21722, 'k', '\r\nKamikaze', ' â€”n. Hist. 1 explosive-laden japanese aircraft deliberately crashed on a ship etc. During the war of 1939â€“45. 2 pilot of this. â€”attrib. Adj. 1 of a kamikaze. 2 reckless, esp. Suicidal. [japanese, = divine wind]'),
(21723, 'k', '\r\nKangaroo', ' n. (pl. -s) australian marsupial with strong hind legs for jumping. [aboriginal]'),
(21724, 'k', '\r\nKangaroo', 'court  n. Illegal court, e.g. Held by strikers or mutineers.'),
(21725, 'k', '\r\nKaolin', ' n. Fine soft white clay used esp. For porcelain and in medicines. [chinese kao-ling high hill]'),
(21726, 'k', '\r\nKapok', ' n. Fine fibrous cotton-like substance from a tropical tree, used for padding. [malay]'),
(21727, 'k', '\r\nKappa', ' n. Tenth letter of the greek alphabet (k, k). [greek]'),
(21728, 'k', '\r\nKaput', ' predic. Adj. Slang broken, ruined. [german]'),
(21729, 'k', '\r\nKarabiner', ' n. Coupling link used by mountaineers. [german, literally â€˜carbineâ€™]'),
(21730, 'k', '\r\nKarakul', ' n. (also caracul) 1 asian sheep with a dark curled fleece when young. 2 fur of or like this. [russian]'),
(21731, 'k', '\r\nKaraoke', ' n. Entertainment in nightclubs etc. With customers singing to a backing track. [japanese, = empty orchestra]'),
(21732, 'k', '\r\nKarate', ' n. Japanese system of unarmed combat using the hands and feet as weapons. [japanese, = empty hand]'),
(21733, 'k', '\r\nKarma', ' n. Buddhism & hinduism person''s actions in previous lives, believed to decide his or her fate in future existences. [sanskrit, = action, fate]'),
(21734, 'k', '\r\nKauri', ' n. (pl. -s) coniferous new zealand tree yielding timber and resin. [maori]'),
(21735, 'k', '\r\nKayak', ' n. 1 eskimo one-man canoe of wood and sealskins. 2 small covered canoe. [eskimo]'),
(21736, 'k', '\r\nKazoo', ' n. Toy musical instrument into which the player sings or hums. [origin uncertain]'),
(21737, 'k', '\r\nKbe', ' abbr. Knight commander of the order of the british empire.'),
(21738, 'k', '\r\nKc', ' abbr. King''s counsel.'),
(21739, 'k', '\r\nKc/s', ' abbr. Kilocycles per second.'),
(21740, 'k', '\r\nKea', ' n. New zealand parrot with brownish-green and red plumage. [maori, imitative]'),
(21741, 'k', '\r\nKebab', ' n. Pieces of meat, vegetables, etc. Cooked on a skewer (cf. *doner kebab, *shish kebab). [urdu from arabic]'),
(21742, 'k', '\r\nKedge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 move (a ship) with a hawser attached to a small anchor. 2 (of a ship) move in this way. â€”n. (in full kedge-anchor) small anchor for this purpose. [origin uncertain]'),
(21743, 'k', '\r\nKedgeree', ' n. Dish of fish, rice, hard-boiled eggs, etc. [hindi]'),
(21744, 'k', '\r\nKeel', ' â€”n. Main lengthwise member of the base of a ship etc. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By over) (cause to) fall down or over. 2 turn keel upwards.  on an even keel steady; balanced. [old norse]'),
(21745, 'k', '\r\nKeelhaul', ' v. 1 drag (a person) under the keel of a ship as a punishment. 2 scold or rebuke severely.'),
(21746, 'k', '\r\nKeelson', ' n. (also kelson) line of timber fastening a ship''s floor-timbers to its keel. [origin uncertain]'),
(21747, 'k', '\r\nKeen1', ' adj. 1 enthusiastic, eager. 2 (foll. By on) enthusiastic about, fond of. 3 (of the senses) sharp. 4 intellectually acute. 5 (of a knife etc.) Sharp. 6 (of a sound, light, etc.) Penetrating, vivid. 7 (of a wind etc.) Piercingly cold. 8 (of a pain etc.) Acute. 9 (of a price) competitive.  keenly adv. Keenness n. [old english]'),
(21748, 'k', '\r\nKeen2', ' â€”n. Irish wailing funeral song. â€”v. (often foll. By over, for) wail mournfully, esp. At a funeral. [irish caoine from caoinim wail]'),
(21749, 'k', '\r\nKeep', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Kept) 1 have continuous charge of; retain possession of. 2 (foll. By for) retain or reserve for (a future time) (kept it for later). 3 retain or remain in a specified condition, position, place, etc. (keep cool; keep out; keep them happy; knives are kept here). 4 (foll. By from) restrain, hold back. 5 detain (what kept you?). 6 observe, honour, or respect (a law, custom, commitment, secret, etc.) (keep one''s word; keep the sabbath). 7 own and look after (animals). 8 a clothe, feed, maintain, etc. (a person, oneself, etc.). B (foll. By in) maintain (a person) with a supply of. 9 carry on; manage (a business etc.). 10 maintain (a diary, house, accounts, etc.) Regularly and in proper order. 11 normally have on sale (do you keep buttons?). 12 guard or protect (a person or place). 13 preserve (keep order). 14 (foll. By verbal noun) continue; repeat habitually (keeps telling me). 15 continue to follow (a way or course). 16 a (esp. Of food) remain in good condition. B (of news etc.) Not suffer from delay in telling. 17 (often foll. By to) remain in (one''s bed, room, etc.). 18 maintain (a person) as one''s mistress etc. (kept woman). â€”n. 1 maintenance, food, etc. (hardly earn your keep). 2 hist. Tower, esp. The central stronghold of a castle.  for keeps colloq. Permanently, indefinitely. How are you keeping? How are you? Keep at (cause to) persist with. Keep away (often foll. By from) avoid, prevent from being near. Keep back 1 remain or keep at a distance. 2 retard the progress of. 3 conceal. 4 withhold (kept back Â£50). Keep down 1 hold in subjection. 2 keep low in amount. 3 stay hidden. 4 not vomit (food eaten). Keep one''s hair on see *hair. Keep one''s hand in see *hand. Keep in with remain on good terms with. Keep off 1 (cause to) stay away from. 2 ward off. 3 abstain from. 4 avoid (a subject) (let''s keep off religion). Keep on 1 continue; do continually (kept on laughing). 2 continue to employ. 3 (foll. By at) nag. Keep out 1 keep or remain outside. 2 exclude. Keep to 1 adhere to (a course, promise, etc.). 2 confine oneself to. Keep to oneself 1 avoid contact with others. 2 keep secret. Keep track of see *track. Keep under repress. Keep up 1 maintain (progress, morale, etc.). 2 keep in repair etc. 3 carry on (a correspondence etc.). 4 prevent from going to bed. 5 (often foll. By with) not fall behind.'),
(21750, 'k', '\r\nKeeper', ' n. 1 person who looks after or is in charge of animals, people, or a thing. 2 custodian of a museum, forest, etc. 3 a = *wicket-keeper. B = *goalkeeper. 4 a sleeper in a pierced ear. B ring that keeps another on the finger.'),
(21751, 'k', '\r\nKeep-fit', ' n. Regular physical exercises.'),
(21752, 'k', '\r\nKeeping', ' n. 1 custody, charge (in safe keeping). 2 agreement, harmony (esp. In or out of keeping (with)).'),
(21753, 'k', '\r\nKeepsake', ' n. Souvenir, esp. Of a person.'),
(21754, 'k', '\r\nKeg', ' n. Small barrel. [old norse]'),
(21755, 'k', '\r\nKeg', 'beer  n. Beer kept in a metal keg under pressure.'),
(21756, 'k', '\r\nKelp', ' n. 1 large brown seaweed suitable for manure. 2 its calcined ashes, formerly a source of sodium, potassium, etc. [origin unknown]'),
(21757, 'k', '\r\nKelpie', ' n. Scot. 1 malevolent water-spirit, usu. In the form of a horse. 2 australian sheepdog. [origin unknown]'),
(21758, 'k', '\r\nKelson', ' var. Of *keelson.'),
(21759, 'k', '\r\nKelt', ' var. Of *celt.'),
(21760, 'k', '\r\nKelt', ' n. Salmon or sea trout after spawning. [origin unknown]'),
(21761, 'k', '\r\nKelter', ' var. Of *kilter.'),
(21762, 'k', '\r\nKelvin', ' n. Si unit of thermodynamic temperature. [kelvin, name of a physicist]'),
(21763, 'k', '\r\nKelvin', 'scale  n. Scale of temperature with zero at absolute zero.'),
(21764, 'k', '\r\nKen', ' â€”n. Range of knowledge or sight (beyond my ken). â€”v. (-nn-; past and past part. Kenned or kent) scot. & n.engl. 1 recognize at sight. 2 know. [old english, = make known: related to *can1]'),
(21765, 'k', '\r\nKendo', ' n. Japanese fencing with two-handed bamboo swords. [japanese, = sword-way]'),
(21766, 'k', '\r\nKennel', ' â€”n. 1 small shelter for a dog. 2 (in pl.) Breeding or boarding place for dogs. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) put into or keep in a kennel. [french chenil from latin canis dog]'),
(21767, 'k', '\r\nKent', ' past and past part. Of *ken.'),
(21768, 'k', '\r\nKenyan', ' â€”adj. Of kenya in e. Africa. â€”n. 1 native or national of kenya. 2 person of kenyan descent.'),
(21769, 'k', '\r\nKepi', ' n. (pl. -s) french military cap with a horizontal peak. [french kÃ©pi]'),
(21770, 'k', '\r\nKept', ' past and past part. Of *keep.'),
(21771, 'k', '\r\nKeratin', ' n. Fibrous protein in hair, feathers, hooves, claws, horns, etc. [greek keras kerat- horn]'),
(21772, 'k', '\r\nKerb', ' n. Stone edging to a pavement or raised path. [var. Of *curb]'),
(21773, 'k', '\r\nKerb-crawling', ' n. Colloq. Driving slowly in order to engage a prostitute.'),
(21774, 'k', '\r\nKerb', 'drill  n. Precautions before crossing a road.'),
(21775, 'k', '\r\nKerbstone', ' n. Stone forming part of a kerb.'),
(21776, 'k', '\r\nKerchief', ' n. 1 headscarf, neckerchief. 2 poet. Handkerchief. [anglo-french courchef: related to *cover, *chief]'),
(21777, 'k', '\r\nKerfuffle', ' n. Colloq. Fuss, commotion. [originally scots]'),
(21778, 'k', '\r\nKermes', ' n. 1 female of an insect with a berry-like appearance. 2 (in full kermes oak) evergreen oak on which this feeds. 3 red dye made from these insects dried. [arabic]'),
(21779, 'k', '\r\nKernel', ' n. 1 (usu. Soft) edible centre within the hard shell of a nut, fruit stone, seed, etc. 2 whole seed of a cereal. 3 essence of anything. [old english: related to *corn1]'),
(21780, 'k', '\r\nKerosene', ' n. (also kerosine) esp. Us fuel oil for use in jet engines, boilers, etc.; paraffin oil. [greek keros wax]'),
(21781, 'k', '\r\nKestrel', ' n. Small hovering falcon. [origin uncertain]'),
(21782, 'k', '\r\nKetch', ' n. Small two-masted sailing-boat. [probably from *catch]'),
(21783, 'k', '\r\nKetchup', ' n. (us catsup) spicy esp. Tomato sauce used as a condiment. [chinese]'),
(21784, 'k', '\r\nKetone', ' n. Any of a class of organic compounds including propanone (acetone). [german keton, alteration of aketon *acetone]'),
(21785, 'k', '\r\nKettle', ' n. Vessel for boiling water in.  a different kettle of fish a different matter altogether. A fine (or pretty) kettle of fish iron. An awkward state of affairs. [old norse]'),
(21786, 'k', '\r\nKettledrum', ' n. Large bowl-shaped drum.'),
(21787, 'k', '\r\nKey1', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 (usu. Metal) instrument for moving the bolt of a lock. 2 similar implement for operating a switch. 3 instrument for grasping screws, nuts, etc., or for winding a clock etc. 4 (often in pl.) Finger-operated button or lever on a typewriter, piano, computer terminal, etc. 5 means of advance, access, etc. (key to success). 6 (attrib.) Essential (key element). 7 a solution or explanation. B word or system for solving a cipher or code. C explanatory list of symbols used in a map, table, etc. 8 mus. System of notes related to each other and based on a particular note (key of c major).'),
(21788, 'k', '\r\nKey2', ' n. Low-lying island or reef, esp. In the w. Indies. [spanish cayo]'),
(21789, 'k', '\r\nKeyboard', ' â€”n. 1 set of keys on a typewriter, computer, piano, etc. 2 electronic musical instrument with keys arranged as on a piano. â€”v. Enter (data) by means of a keyboard.  keyboarder n. (in sense 1 of n.). Keyboardist n. (in sense 2 of n.).'),
(21790, 'k', '\r\nKeyhole', ' n. Hole in a door etc. For a key.'),
(21791, 'k', '\r\nKeyhole', 'surgery  n. Colloq. Minimally invasive surgery carried out through a very small incision.'),
(21792, 'k', '\r\nKeynesian', ' adj. Of the economic theories of j. M. Keynes, esp. Regarding state intervention in the economy.'),
(21793, 'k', '\r\nKeynote', ' n. 1 (esp. Attrib.) Prevailing tone or idea, esp. In a speech, conference, etc. 2 mus. Note on which a key is based.'),
(21794, 'k', '\r\nKeypad', ' n. Miniature keyboard etc. For a portable electronic device, telephone, etc.'),
(21795, 'k', '\r\nKeypunch', ' â€”n. Device for recording data by means of punched holes or notches on cards or paper tape. â€”v. Record (data) thus.'),
(21796, 'k', '\r\nKey-ring', ' n. Ring for keeping keys on.'),
(21797, 'k', '\r\nKey', 'signature  n. Mus. Any of several combinations of sharps or flats indicating the key of a composition.'),
(21798, 'k', '\r\nKeystone', ' n. 1 central principle of a system, policy, etc. 2 central locking stone in an arch.'),
(21799, 'k', '\r\nKeystroke', ' n. Single depression of a key on a keyboard, esp. As a measure of work.'),
(21800, 'k', '\r\nKeyword', ' n. 1 key to a cipher etc. 2 a word of great significance. B significant word used in indexing.'),
(21801, 'k', '\r\nKg', ' abbr. Knight of the order of the garter.'),
(21802, 'k', '\r\nKg', ' abbr. Kilogram(s).'),
(21803, 'k', '\r\nKgb', ' n. State security police of the former ussr. [russian abbreviation, = committee of state security]'),
(21804, 'k', '\r\nKhaki', ' â€”adj. Dull brownish-yellow. â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 khaki fabric or uniform. 2 dull brownish-yellow colour. [urdu, = dusty]'),
(21805, 'k', '\r\nKhan', ' n. Title of rulers and officials in central asia, afghanistan, etc.  khanate n. [turki, = lord]'),
(21806, 'k', '\r\nKhz', ' abbr. Kilohertz.'),
(21807, 'k', '\r\nKibbutz', ' n. (pl. Kibbutzim) communal esp. Farming settlement in israel. [hebrew, = gathering]'),
(21808, 'k', '\r\nKibosh', ' n. Slang nonsense.  put the kibosh on put an end to. [origin unknown]'),
(21809, 'k', '\r\nKick', ' â€”v. 1 strike, strike out, or propel forcibly, with the foot or hoof. 2 (often foll. By at, against) protest at; rebel against. 3 slang give up (a habit). 4 (often foll. By out etc.) Expel or dismiss forcibly. 5 refl. Be annoyed with oneself. 6 football score (a goal) by a kick. â€”n. 1 kicking action or blow. 2 colloq. A sharp stimulant effect, esp. Of alcohol. B (often in pl.) Thrill (did it for kicks). 3 strength, resilience (no kick left). 4 colloq. Specified temporary interest (on a jogging kick). 5 recoil of a gun when fired.  kick about (or around) colloq. 1 a drift idly from place to place. B be unused or unwanted. 2 a treat roughly. B discuss unsystematically. Kick the bucket slang die. Kick one''s heels see *heel1.'),
(21810, 'k', '\r\nKickback', ' n. Colloq. 1 recoil. 2 (usu. Illegal) payment for help or favours, esp. In business.'),
(21811, 'k', '\r\nKick-off', ' n. Football start or resumption of a match.'),
(21812, 'k', '\r\nKickstand', ' n. Rod for supporting a bicycle or motor cycle when stationary.'),
(21813, 'k', '\r\nKick-start', ' â€”n. (also kick-starter) device to start the engine of a motor cycle etc. By the downward thrust of a pedal. â€”v. Start (a motor cycle etc.) In this way.'),
(21814, 'k', '\r\nKid1', ' â€”n. 1 young goat. 2 leather from this. 3 colloq. Child. â€”v. (-dd-) (of a goat) give birth.  handle with kid gloves treat carefully. [old norse]'),
(21815, 'k', '\r\nKid2', ' v. (also refl.) (-dd-) colloq. Deceive, trick, tease (don''t kid yourself; only kidding).  no kidding slang that is the truth. [origin uncertain]'),
(21816, 'k', '\r\nKiddie', ' n. (also kiddy) (pl. -ies) slang = *kid1 n. 3.'),
(21817, 'k', '\r\nKiddo', ' n. (pl. -s) slang = *kid1 n. 3.'),
(21818, 'k', '\r\nKidnap', ' v. (-pp-; us -p-) 1 abduct (a person etc.), esp. To obtain a ransom. 2 steal (a child).  kidnapper n. [from *kid1, nap = *nab]'),
(21819, 'k', '\r\nKidney', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 either of two organs in the abdominal cavity of vertebrates which remove nitrogenous wastes from the blood and excrete urine. 2 animal''s kidney as food. [origin unknown]'),
(21820, 'k', '\r\nKidney', 'bean  n. Red-skinned dried bean.'),
(21821, 'k', '\r\nKidney', 'machine  n. Machine able to take over the function of a damaged kidney.'),
(21822, 'k', '\r\nKidney-shaped', ' adj. Having one side concave and the other convex.'),
(21823, 'k', '\r\nKill', ' â€”v. 1 (also absol.) Deprive of life or vitality; cause death or the death of. 2 destroy (feelings etc.). 3 refl. Colloq. A overexert oneself (don''t kill yourself trying). B laugh heartily. 4 colloq. Overwhelm with amusement. 5 switch off (a light, engine, etc.). 6 computing colloq. Delete. 7 colloq. Cause pain or discomfort to (my feet are killing me). 8 pass (time, or a specified period) usu. While waiting (an hour to kill before the interview). 9 defeat (a bill in parliament). 10 a tennis etc. Hit (the ball) so that it cannot be returned. B stop (the ball) dead. 11 make ineffective (taste, sound, pain, etc.) (carpet killed the sound). â€”n. 1 act of killing (esp. In hunting). 2 animal(s) killed, esp. By a hunter. 3 colloq. Destruction or disablement of an enemy aircraft etc.  dressed to kill dressed showily or alluringly. In at the kill present at a successful conclusion. Kill off 1 destroy completely. 2 (of an author) bring about the death of (a fictional character). Kill or cure (usu. Attrib.) (of a remedy etc.) Drastic, extreme. Kill two birds with one stone achieve two aims at once. Kill with kindness spoil with overindulgence. [perhaps related to *quell]'),
(21824, 'k', '\r\nKiller', ' n. 1 a person, animal, or thing that kills. B murderer. 2 colloq. A impressive, formidable, or excellent thing. B hilarious joke.'),
(21825, 'k', '\r\nKiller', 'instinct  n. 1 innate tendency to kill. 2 ruthless streak.'),
(21826, 'k', '\r\nKiller', 'whale  n. Dolphin with a prominent dorsal fin.'),
(21827, 'k', '\r\nKilling', ' â€”n. 1 a causing of death. B instance of this. 2 colloq. Great (esp. Financial) success (make a killing). â€”adj. Colloq. 1 very funny. 2 exhausting.'),
(21828, 'k', '\r\nKilljoy', ' n. Gloomy or censorious person, esp. At a party etc.'),
(21829, 'k', '\r\nKiln', ' n. Furnace or oven for burning, baking, or drying, esp. For calcining lime or firing pottery etc. [old english from latin culina kitchen]'),
(21830, 'k', '\r\nKilo', ' n. (pl. -s) kilogram. [french, abbreviation]'),
(21831, 'k', '\r\nKilo-', ' comb. Form 1,000 (esp. In metric units). [greek khilioi]'),
(21832, 'k', '\r\nKilobyte', ' n. Computing 1,024 (i.e. 210) bytes as a measure of memory size etc.'),
(21833, 'k', '\r\nKilocalorie', ' n. = large calorie (see *calorie).'),
(21834, 'k', '\r\nKilocycle', ' n. Hist. Kilohertz.'),
(21835, 'k', '\r\nKilogram', ' n. (also -gramme) si unit of mass, approx. 2.205 lb.'),
(21836, 'k', '\r\nKilohertz', ' n. 1,000 hertz, 1,000 cycles per second.'),
(21837, 'k', '\r\nKilojoule', ' n. 1,000 joules, esp. As a measure of the energy value of foods.'),
(21838, 'k', '\r\nKiloliter', ' n. (brit. -litre) 1,000 litres (220 imperial gallons).'),
(21839, 'k', '\r\nKilolitre', ' n. (us -liter) 1,000 litres (220 imperial gallons).'),
(21840, 'k', '\r\nKilometer', ' n. (brit. -metre) 1,000 metres (approx. 0.62 miles).  kilometric adj.'),
(21841, 'k', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation given, with the stress on the second syllable, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(21842, 'k', '\r\nKilometre', ' n. (us -meter) 1,000 metres (approx. 0.62 miles).  kilometric adj.'),
(21843, 'k', '\r\nUsage', ' the second pronunciation given, with the stress on the second syllable, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(21844, 'k', '\r\nKiloton', ' n. (also kilotonne) unit of explosive power equivalent to 1,000 tons of tnt.'),
(21845, 'k', '\r\nKilovolt', ' n. 1,000 volts.'),
(21846, 'k', '\r\nKilowatt', ' n. 1,000 watts.'),
(21847, 'k', '\r\nKilowatt-hour', ' n. Electrical energy equivalent to a power consumption of 1,000 watts for one hour.'),
(21848, 'k', '\r\nKilt', ' â€”n. Pleated knee-length usu. Tartan skirt, traditionally worn by highland men. â€”v. 1 tuck up (the skirts) round the body. 2 (esp. As kilted adj.) Gather in vertical pleats. [scandinavian]'),
(21849, 'k', '\r\nKilter', ' n. (also kelter) good working order (esp. Out of kilter). [origin unknown]'),
(21850, 'k', '\r\nKimono', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 long sashed japanese robe. 2 similar dressing-gown. [japanese]'),
(21851, 'k', '\r\nKin', ' â€”n. One''s relatives or family. â€”predic. Adj. Related. [old english]'),
(21852, 'k', '\r\n-kin', ' suffix forming diminutive nouns (catkin; manikin). [dutch]'),
(21853, 'k', '\r\nKind', ' â€”n. 1 race, species, or natural group of animals, plants, etc. (human kind). 2 class, type, sort, variety. 3 natural way, fashion, etc. (true to kind). â€”adj. (often foll. By to) friendly, generous, or benevolent.  in kind 1 in the same form, likewise (was insulted and replied in kind). 2 (of payment) in goods or labour, not money. 3 character, quality (differ in degree but not in kind). Kind of colloq. To some extent (i kind of expected it). A kind of loosely resembling (he''s a kind of doctor). [old english]'),
(21854, 'k', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 2 of the noun, these kinds of is usually preferred to these kind of.'),
(21855, 'k', '\r\nKindergarten', ' n. Class or school for very young children. [german, = children''s garden]'),
(21856, 'k', '\r\nKind-hearted', ' adj. Of a kind disposition.  kind-heartedly adv. Kind-heartedness n.'),
(21857, 'k', '\r\nKindle', ' v. (-ling) 1 light, catch, or set on fire. 2 arouse or inspire. 3 become aroused or animated. [old norse]'),
(21858, 'k', '\r\nKindling', ' n. Small sticks etc. For lighting fires.'),
(21859, 'k', '\r\nKindly1', ' adv. 1 in a kind manner (spoke kindly). 2 often iron. Please (kindly go away).  look kindly upon regard sympathetically. Take kindly to be pleased by; like.'),
(21860, 'k', '\r\nKindly2', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 kind, kind-hearted. 2 (of a climate etc.) Pleasant, mild.  kindlily adv. Kindliness n.'),
(21861, 'k', '\r\nKindness', ' n. 1 being kind. 2 kind act.'),
(21862, 'k', '\r\nKindred', ' â€”adj. Related, allied, or similar. â€”n. 1 one''s relations collectively. 2 blood relationship. 3 resemblance in character. [old english, = kinship]'),
(21863, 'k', '\r\nKindred', 'spirit  n. Person like or in sympathy with oneself.'),
(21864, 'k', '\r\nKinematics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Branch of mechanics concerned with the motion of objects without reference to cause.  kinematic adj. [greek kinema -matos motion]'),
(21865, 'k', '\r\nKinetic', ' adj. Of or due to motion.  kinetically adv. [greek kineo move]'),
(21866, 'k', '\r\nKinetic', 'art  n. Sculpture etc. Designed to move.'),
(21867, 'k', '\r\nKinetic', 'energy  n. Energy of motion.'),
(21868, 'k', '\r\nKinetics', ' n.pl. 1 = *dynamics 1a. 2 (usu. Treated as sing.) Branch of physical chemistry measuring and studying the rates of chemical reactions.'),
(21869, 'k', '\r\nKing', ' n. 1 (as a title usu. King) male sovereign, esp. A hereditary ruler. 2 pre-eminent person or thing (oil king). 3 (attrib.) Large (or the largest) kind of plant, animal, etc. (king penguin). 4 chess piece which must be checkmated for a win. 5 crowned piece in draughts. 6 court-card depicting a king. 7 (the king) national anthem when the sovereign is male.  kingly adj. Kingship n. [old english]'),
