(21932, 'k', '\r\nKnack', ' n. 1 acquired faculty or trick of doing a thing. 2 habit (a knack of offending people). [origin unknown]'),
(21933, 'k', '\r\nKnacker', ' â€”n. Buyer of useless horses etc. For slaughter, or of old houses, ships, etc. For the materials. â€”v. Slang (esp. As knackered adj.) Exhaust, wear out. [origin unknown]'),
(21934, 'k', '\r\nKnapsack', ' n. Soldier''s or hiker''s usu. Canvas bag carried on the back. [german knappen bite, *sack1]'),
(21935, 'k', '\r\nKnapweed', ' n. Plant with thistle-like purple flowers. [from knop ornamental knob or tuft]'),
(21936, 'k', '\r\nKnave', ' n. 1 rogue, scoundrel. 2 = *jack n. 2.  knavery n. (pl. -ies). Knavish adj. [old english, originally = boy, servant]'),
(21937, 'k', '\r\nKnead', ' v. 1 a work into a dough, paste, etc. By pummelling. B make (bread, pottery, etc.) Thus. 2 massage (muscles etc.) As if kneading. [old english]'),
(21938, 'k', '\r\nKnee', ' â€”n. 1 a (often attrib.) Joint between the thigh and the lower leg in humans. B corresponding joint in other animals. C area around this. D lap (sat on his knee). 2 part of a garment covering the knee. â€”v. (knees, kneed, kneeing) 1 touch or strike with the knee (kneed him in the groin). 2 colloq. Make (trousers) bulge at the knee.  bring a person to his (or her) knees reduce a person to submission. [old english]'),
(21939, 'k', '\r\nKnee-bend', ' n. Bending of the knee, esp. As a physical exercise.'),
(21940, 'k', '\r\nKnee-breeches', ' n.pl. Close-fitting trousers to the knee or just below.'),
(21941, 'k', '\r\nKneecap', ' â€”n. 1 convex bone in front of the knee. 2 protective covering for the knee. â€”v. (-pp-) slang (of a terrorist) shoot (a person) in the knee or leg as a punishment.'),
(21942, 'k', '\r\nKnee-deep', ' adj. 1 (usu. Foll. By in) a immersed up to the knees. B deeply involved. 2 so deep as to reach the knees.'),
(21943, 'k', '\r\nKnee-high', ' adj. So high as to reach the knees.'),
(21944, 'k', '\r\nKnee-jerk', ' n. 1 sudden involuntary kick caused by a blow on the tendon just below the knee. 2 (attrib.) Predictable, automatic, stereotyped.'),
(21945, 'k', '\r\nKneel', ' v. (past and past part. Knelt or esp. Us kneeled) fall or rest on the knees or a knee. [old english: related to *knee]'),
(21946, 'k', '\r\nKnee-length', ' adj. Reaching the knees.'),
(21947, 'k', '\r\nKneeler', ' n. 1 cushion for kneeling on. 2 person who kneels.'),
(21948, 'k', '\r\nKnees-up', ' n. Colloq. Lively party or gathering.'),
(21949, 'k', '\r\nKnell', ' â€”n. 1 sound of a bell, esp. For a death or funeral. 2 announcement, event, etc., regarded as an ill omen. â€”v. 1 ring a knell. 2 proclaim by or as by a knell. [old english]'),
(21950, 'k', '\r\nKnelt', ' past and past part. Of *kneel.'),
(21951, 'k', '\r\nKnew', ' past of *know.'),
(21952, 'k', '\r\nKnickerbocker', ' n. (in pl.) Loose-fitting breeches gathered at the knee or calf. [the pseudonym of w. Irving, author of history of new york]'),
(21953, 'k', '\r\nKnickerbocker', 'glory  n. Ice-cream served with fruit etc. In a tall glass.'),
(21954, 'k', '\r\nKnickers', ' n.pl. Woman''s or girl''s undergarment for the lower torso. [abbreviation of *knickerbocker]'),
(21955, 'k', '\r\nKnick-knack', ' n. (also nick-nack) trinket or small dainty ornament etc. [from *knack in the obsolete sense â€˜trinketâ€™]'),
(21956, 'k', '\r\nKnife', ' â€”n. (pl. Knives) 1 metal blade for cutting or as a weapon, with usu. One long sharp edge fixed in a handle. 2 cutting-blade in a machine. 3 (as the knife) surgical operation. â€”v. (-fing) cut or stab with a knife.  at knife-point threatened with a knife or an ultimatum etc. Get (or have got) one''s knife into treat maliciously, persecute. [old english]'),
(21957, 'k', '\r\nKnife-edge', ' n. 1 edge of a knife. 2 position of extreme danger or uncertainty.'),
(21958, 'k', '\r\nKnife-pleat', ' n. Narrow flat usu. Overlapping pleat on a skirt etc.'),
(21959, 'k', '\r\nKnight', ' â€”n. 1 man awarded a non-hereditary title (sir) by a sovereign. 2 hist. A man, usu. Noble, raised to honourable military rank after service as a page and squire. B military follower, attendant, or lady''s champion in a war or tournament. 3 man devoted to a cause, woman, etc. 4 chess piece usu. Shaped like a horse''s head. â€”v. Confer a knighthood on.  knighthood n. Knightly adj. Poet. [old english, originally = boy]'),
(21960, 'k', '\r\nKnight', 'commander  see *commander.'),
(21961, 'k', '\r\nKnight', 'errant  n. 1 medieval knight in search of chivalrous adventures. 2 chivalrous or quixotic man.  knight-errantry n.'),
(21962, 'k', '\r\nKnit', ' v. (-tt-; past and past part. Knitted or (esp. In senses 2â€“4) knit) 1 (also absol.) A make (a garment etc.) By interlocking loops of esp. Wool with knitting-needles or a knitting-machine. B make (a plain stitch) in knitting (knit one, purl one). 2 momentarily wrinkle (the forehead) or (of the forehead) become momentarily wrinkled. 3 (often foll. By together) make or become close or compact. 4 (often foll. By together) (of a broken bone) become joined; heal.  knit up make or repair by knitting.  knitter n. [old english]'),
(21963, 'k', '\r\nKnitting', ' n. Work being knitted.'),
(21964, 'k', '\r\nKnitting-machine', ' n. Machine for knitting.'),
(21965, 'k', '\r\nKnitting-needle', ' n. Thin pointed rod used esp. In pairs for knitting by hand.'),
(21966, 'k', '\r\nKnitwear', ' n. Knitted garments.'),
(21967, 'k', '\r\nKnives', ' pl. Of *knife.'),
(21968, 'k', '\r\nKnob', ' n. 1 rounded protuberance, esp. At the end or on the surface of a thing, e.g. The handle of a door, drawer, a radio control, etc. 2 small piece (of butter etc.).  with knobs on slang that and more (same to you with knobs on).  knobby adj. Knoblike adj. [low german knobbe knot, knob]'),
(21969, 'k', '\r\nKnobbly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) hard and lumpy. [knobble, diminutive of *knob]'),
(21970, 'k', '\r\nKnock', ' â€”v. 1 a strike with an audible sharp blow. B (often foll. By at) strike (a door etc.) To gain admittance. 2 make (a hole etc.) By knocking. 3 (usu. Foll. By in, out, off, etc.) Drive (a thing, person, etc.) By striking (knocked the ball into the hole; knocked those ideas out of him). 4 slang criticize. 5 a (of an engine) make a thumping or rattling noise. B = *pink3. 6 coarse slang offens. = knock off 6. â€”n. 1 act or sound of knocking. 2 knocking sound in esp. An engine.  knock about (or around) colloq. 1 strike repeatedly; treat roughly. 2 a wander aimlessly or adventurously. B be present, esp. By chance (a cup knocking about somewhere). C (usu. Foll. By with) be associated socially. Knock back 1 slang eat or drink, esp. Quickly. 2 slang disconcert. Knock down 1 strike (esp. A person) to the ground. 2 demolish. 3 (usu. Foll.'),
(21971, 'k', '\r\nKnockabout', ' attrib. Adj. 1 (of comedy) boisterous; slapstick. 2 (of clothes) hard-wearing.'),
(21972, 'k', '\r\nKnock-down', ' attrib. Adj. 1 overwhelming. 2 (of a price) very low. 3 (of a price at auction) reserve. 4 (of furniture etc.) Easily dismantled and reassembled.'),
(21973, 'k', '\r\nKnocker', ' n. 1 hinged esp. Metal instrument on a door for knocking with. 2 (in pl.) Coarse slang woman''s breasts.'),
(21974, 'k', '\r\nKnocking-shop', ' n. Slang brothel.'),
(21975, 'k', '\r\nKnock', 'knees  n.pl. Abnormal curvature of the legs inwards at the knee.  knock-kneed adj.'),
