(22297, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' laudatory is sometimes confused with laudable.'),
(22298, 'l', '\r\nLaugh', ' â€”v. 1 make the sounds and movements usual in expressing lively amusement, scorn, etc. 2 express by laughing. 3 (foll. By at) ridicule, make fun of. â€”n. 1 sound, act, or manner of laughing. 2 colloq. Comical thing.  laugh off get rid of (embarrassment or humiliation) by joking. Laugh up one''s sleeve laugh secretly. [old english]'),
(22299, 'l', '\r\nLaughable', ' adj. Ludicrous; amusing.  laughably adv.'),
(22300, 'l', '\r\nLaughing', ' n. Laughter.  no laughing matter serious matter.  laughingly adv.'),
(22301, 'l', '\r\nLaughing-gas', ' n. Nitrous oxide as an anaesthetic.'),
(22302, 'l', '\r\nLaughing', 'jackass  n. = *kookaburra.'),
(22303, 'l', '\r\nLaughing', 'stock  n. Person or thing open to general ridicule.'),
(22304, 'l', '\r\nLaughter', ' n. Act or sound of laughing. [old english]'),
(22305, 'l', '\r\nLaunch1', ' â€”v. 1 set (a vessel) afloat. 2 hurl or send forth (a weapon, rocket, etc.). 3 start or set in motion (an enterprise, person, etc.). 4 formally introduce (a new product) with publicity etc. 5 (foll. By out, into, etc.) A make a start on (an enterprise etc.). B burst into (strong language etc.). â€”n. Act of launching. [anglo-norman launcher: related to *lance]'),
(22306, 'l', '\r\nLaunch2', ' n. 1 large motor boat. 2 man-of-war''s largest boat. [spanish lancha]'),
(22307, 'l', '\r\nLauncher', ' n. Structure to hold a rocket during launching.'),
(22308, 'l', '\r\nLaunch', 'pad  n. (also launching pad) platform with a supporting structure, for launching rockets from.'),
(22309, 'l', '\r\nLaunder', ' v. 1 wash and iron (clothes etc.). 2 colloq. Transfer (funds) to conceal their origin. [french: related to *lave]'),
(22310, 'l', '\r\nLaunderette', ' n. (also laundrette) establishment with coin-operated washing-machines and driers for public use.'),
(22311, 'l', '\r\nLaundress', ' n. Woman who launders, esp. Professionally.'),
(22312, 'l', '\r\nLaundry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a place for washing clothes etc. B firm washing clothes etc. Commercially. 2 clothes or linen for laundering or newly laundered.'),
(22313, 'l', '\r\nLaureate', ' â€”adj. Wreathed with laurel as a mark of honour. â€”n. = *poet laureate.  laureateship n. [related to *laurel]'),
(22314, 'l', '\r\nLaurel', ' n. 1 = *bay2. 2 (in sing. Or pl.) Wreath of bay-leaves as an emblem of victory or poetic merit. 3 any of various plants with dark-green glossy leaves.  look to one''s laurels beware of losing one''s pre-eminence. Rest on one''s laurels see *rest1. [latin laurus bay]'),
(22315, 'l', '\r\nLav', ' n. Colloq. Lavatory. [abbreviation]'),
(22316, 'l', '\r\nLava', ' n. Matter flowing from a volcano and solidifying as it cools. [latin lavo wash]'),
(22317, 'l', '\r\nLavatorial', ' adj. Of or like lavatories; (esp. Of humour) relating to excretion.'),
(22318, 'l', '\r\nLavatory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 receptacle for urine and faeces, usu. With a means of disposal. 2 room or compartment containing this. [latin: related to *lava]'),
(22319, 'l', '\r\nLavatory', 'paper  n. = *toilet paper.'),
(22320, 'l', '\r\nLave', ' v. (-ving) literary 1 wash, bathe. 2 (of water) wash against; flow along. [latin lavo wash]'),
(22321, 'l', '\r\nLavender', ' n. 1 a evergreen shrub with purple aromatic flowers. B its flowers and stalks dried and used to scent linen etc. 2 pale mauve colour. [latin lavandula]'),
(22322, 'l', '\r\nLavender-water', ' n. Light perfume made with distilled lavender.'),
(22323, 'l', '\r\nLaver', ' n. Edible seaweed. [latin]'),
(22324, 'l', '\r\nLavish', ' â€”adj. 1 giving or producing in large quantities; profuse. 2 generous. â€”v. (often foll. By on) bestow or spend (money, effort, praise, etc.) Abundantly.  lavishly adv. [french lavasse deluge: related to *lave]'),
(22325, 'l', '\r\nLaw', ' n. 1 a rule enacted or customary in a community and recognized as commanding or forbidding certain actions. B body of such rules. 2 controlling influence of laws; respect for laws. 3 laws collectively as a social system or subject of study. 4 binding force (her word is law). 5 (prec. By the) a the legal profession. B colloq. The police. 6 (in pl.) Jurisprudence. 7 a the judicial remedy. B the lawcourts as providing this (go to law). 8 rule of action or procedure. 9 regularity in natural occurrences (laws of nature; law of gravity). 10 divine commandments.  be a law unto oneself do what one considers right; disregard custom. Lay down the law be dogmatic or authoritarian. Take the law into one''s own hands redress a grievance by one''s own means, esp. By force. [old english from old norse, = thing laid down]'),
(22326, 'l', '\r\nLaw-abiding', ' adj. Obedient to the laws.'),
(22327, 'l', '\r\nLawbreaker', ' n. Person who breaks the law.  lawbreaking n. & adj.'),
(22328, 'l', '\r\nLawcourt', ' n. Court of law.'),
(22329, 'l', '\r\nLawful', ' adj. Conforming with or recognized by law; not illegal.  lawfully adv. Lawfulness n.'),
(22330, 'l', '\r\nLawgiver', ' n. Person who formulates laws; legislator.'),
(22331, 'l', '\r\nLawless', ' adj. 1 having no laws or law enforcement. 2 disregarding laws.  lawlessness n.'),
(22332, 'l', '\r\nLaw', 'lord  n. Member of the house of lords qualified to perform its legal work.'),
(22333, 'l', '\r\nLawmaker', ' n. Legislator.'),
(22334, 'l', '\r\nLawn1', ' n. Piece of closely-mown grass in a garden etc. [french launde glade]'),
(22335, 'l', '\r\nLawn2', ' n. Fine linen or cotton. [probably from laon in france]'),
(22336, 'l', '\r\nLawnmower', ' n. Machine for cutting lawns.'),
(22337, 'l', '\r\nLawn', 'tennis  n. Tennis played with a soft ball on outdoor grass or a hard court.'),
(22338, 'l', '\r\nLawrencium', ' n. Artificially made transuranic metallic element. [lawrence, name of a physicist]'),
(22339, 'l', '\r\nLawsuit', ' n. Bringing of a dispute, claim, etc. Before a lawcourt.'),
(22340, 'l', '\r\nLawyer', ' n. Legal practitioner, esp. A solicitor.'),
(22341, 'l', '\r\nLax', ' adj. 1 lacking care or precision. 2 not strict.  laxity n. Laxly adv. Laxness n. [latin laxus loose]'),
(22342, 'l', '\r\nLaxative', ' â€”adj. Facilitating evacuation of the bowels. â€”n. Laxative medicine. [latin: related to *lax]'),
(22343, 'l', '\r\nLay1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Laid) 1 place on a surface, esp. Horizontally or in the proper or specified place. 2 put or bring into the required position or state (lay carpet). 3 make by laying (lay foundations). 4 (often absol.) (of a hen bird) produce (an egg). 5 cause to subside or lie flat. 6 (usu. Foll. By on); attribute or impute (blame etc.). 7 prepare or make ready (a plan or trap). 8 prepare (a table) for a meal. 9 arrange the material for (a fire). 10 put down as a wager; stake. 11 (foll. By with) coat or strew (a surface). 12 slang offens. Have sexual intercourse with (esp. A woman). â€”n. 1 way, position, or direction in which something lies. 2 slang offens. Partner (esp. Female) in, or act of, sexual intercourse.  lay about one hit out on all sides. Lay aside 1 put to one side. 2 cease to consider. Lay at the door of impute to. Lay bare expose, reveal. Lay claim to claim as one''s own. Lay down 1 put on a flat surface. 2 give up (an office). 3 formulate (a rule). 4 store (wine) for maturing. 5 sacrifice (one''s life). Lay (one''s) hands on obtain, locate. Lay hands on seize or attack. Lay hold of seize. Lay in provide oneself with a stock of. Lay into colloq. Punish or scold harshly. Lay it on thick (or with a trowel) colloq. Flatter or exaggerate grossly. Lay low overthrow or humble. Lay off 1 discharge (unneeded workers) temporarily; make redundant. 2 colloq. Desist. Lay on 1 provide. 2 impose. 3 inflict (blows). 4 spread on (paint etc.). Lay open 1 break the skin of. 2 (foll. By to) expose (to criticism etc.). Lay out 1 spread out, expose to view. 2 prepare (a corpse) for burial. 3 colloq. Knock unconscious. 4 arrange (grounds etc.) According to a design.'),
(22344, 'l', '\r\nLay2', ' adj. 1 a non-clerical. B not ordained into the clergy. 2 a not professionally qualified. B of or done by such persons. [greek laos people]'),
(22345, 'l', '\r\nLay3', ' n. 1 short poem meant to be sung. 2 song. [french]'),
(22346, 'l', '\r\nLay4', ' past of *lie1.'),
(22347, 'l', '\r\nLayabout', ' n. Habitual loafer or idler.'),
(22348, 'l', '\r\nLay-by', ' n. (pl. -bys) area at the side of a road where vehicles may stop.'),
(22349, 'l', '\r\nLayer', ' â€”n. 1 thickness of matter, esp. One of several, covering a surface. 2 person or thing that lays. 3 hen that lays eggs. 4 shoot fastened down to take root while attached to the parent plant. â€”v. 1 arrange in layers. 2 cut (hair) in layers. 3 propagate (a plant) by a layer.'),
(22350, 'l', '\r\nLayette', ' n. Set of clothing etc. For a newborn child. [french from dutch]'),
(22351, 'l', '\r\nLay', 'figure  n. 1 jointed figure of a human body used by artists for arranging drapery on etc. 2 unrealistic character in a novel etc. [dutch led joint]'),
(22352, 'l', '\r\nLayman', ' n. (fem. Laywoman) 1 non-ordained member of a church. 2 person without professional or specialized knowledge.'),
(22353, 'l', '\r\nLay-off', ' n. Temporary discharge of workers; a redundancy.'),
(22354, 'l', '\r\nLayout', ' n. 1 way in which land, a building, printed matter, etc., is arranged or set out. 2 something arranged in a particular way; display.'),
(22355, 'l', '\r\nLay', 'reader  n. Lay person licensed to conduct some religious services.'),
(22356, 'l', '\r\nLaze', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 spend time idly. 2 (foll. By away) pass (time) idly. â€”n. Spell of lazing. [back-formation from *lazy]'),
(22357, 'l', '\r\nLazy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 disinclined to work, doing little work. 2 of or inducing idleness.  lazily adv. Laziness n. [perhaps from low german]'),
(22358, 'l', '\r\nLazybones', ' n. (pl. Same) colloq. Lazy person.'),
(22359, 'l', '\r\nLb', ' abbr. Pound(s) (weight). [latin libra]'),
(22360, 'l', '\r\nLbc', ' abbr. London broadcasting company.'),
(22361, 'l', '\r\nL.b.w.', ' Abbr. Leg before wicket.'),
(22362, 'l', '\r\nL.c.', ' Abbr. 1 = *loc. Cit. 2 lower case.'),
(22363, 'l', '\r\nLcd', ' abbr. 1 liquid crystal display. 2 lowest (or least) common denominator.'),
(22364, 'l', '\r\nLcm', ' abbr. Lowest (or least) common multiple.'),
(22365, 'l', '\r\nL/cpl', ' abbr. Lance-corporal.'),
(22366, 'l', '\r\nLd.', ' Abbr. Lord.'),
(22367, 'l', '\r\nLea', ' abbr. Local education authority.'),
(22368, 'l', '\r\nLea', ' n. Poet. Meadow, field. [old english]'),
(22369, 'l', '\r\nLeach', ' v. 1 make (a liquid) percolate through some material. 2 subject (bark, ore, ash, or soil) to the action of percolating fluid. 3 (foll. By away, out) remove (soluble matter) or be removed in this way. [old english]'),
(22370, 'l', '\r\nLead1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Led) 1 cause to go with one, esp. By guiding or going in front. 2 a direct the actions or opinions of. B (often foll. By to, or to + infin.) Guide by persuasion or example (what led you to think that). 3 (also absol.) Provide access to; bring to a certain position (gate leads you into a field; road leads to lincoln). 4 pass or go through (a life etc. Of a specified kind). 5 a have the first place in. B (absol.) Go first; be ahead in a race etc. C (absol.) Be pre-eminent in some field. 6 be in charge of (leads a team). 7 (also absol.) Play (a card) or a card of (a particular suit) as first player in a round. 8 (foll. By to) result in. 9 (foll. By with) (of a newspaper or news broadcast) have as its main story (led with the royal wedding). 10 (foll. By through) make (a liquid, strip of material, etc.) Pass through a certain course. â€”n. 1 guidance given by going in front; example. 2 a leading place (take the lead). B amount by which a competitor is ahead of the others. 3 clue. 4 strap etc. For leading a dog etc. 5 conductor (usu. A wire) conveying electric current to an appliance. 6 a chief part in a play etc. B person playing this. C (attrib.) Chief performer or instrument of a specified type (lead guitar). 7 cards a act or right of playing first. B card led.  lead by the nose cajole into compliance. Lead off begin. Lead on entice dishonestly. Lead up the garden path colloq. Mislead. Lead up to form a preparation for; direct conversation towards. [old english]'),
(22371, 'l', '\r\nLead2', ' â€”n. 1 heavy bluish-grey soft metallic element. 2 a graphite. B thin length of this in a pencil. 3 lump of lead used in sounding water. 4 (in pl.) A strips of lead covering a roof. B piece of lead-covered roof. 5 (in pl.) Lead frames holding the glass of a lattice etc. 6 blank space between lines of print. â€”v. 1 cover, weight, or frame with lead. 2 space (printed matter) with leads. [old english]'),
(22372, 'l', '\r\nLeaden', ' adj. 1 of or like lead. 2 heavy or slow. 3 lead-coloured.'),
(22373, 'l', '\r\nLeader', ' n. 1 a person or thing that leads. B person followed by others. 2 principal player in a music group or of the first violins in an orchestra. 3 = *leading article. 4 shoot of a plant at the apex of a stem or of the main branch.  leadership n.'),
(22374, 'l', '\r\nLead-free', ' adj. (of petrol) without added lead compounds.'),
(22375, 'l', '\r\nLead-in', ' n. Introduction, opening, etc.'),
(22376, 'l', '\r\nLeading1', ' adj. Chief; most important.'),
(22377, 'l', '\r\nLeading2', ' n. Printing = *lead2 n. 6.'),
(22378, 'l', '\r\nLeading', 'aircraftman  n. Rank above aircraftman in the raf.'),
(22379, 'l', '\r\nLeading', 'article  n. Newspaper article giving editorial opinion.'),
(22380, 'l', '\r\nLeading', 'light  n. Prominent and influential person.'),
(22381, 'l', '\r\nLeading', 'note  n. Mus. Seventh note of a diatonic scale.'),
(22382, 'l', '\r\nLeading', 'question  n. Question prompting the answer wanted.'),
(22383, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' leading question does not mean a â€˜principalâ€™ or â€˜loadedâ€™ or â€˜searchingâ€™ question.'),
(22384, 'l', '\r\nLead', 'pencil  n. Pencil of graphite in wood.'),
(22385, 'l', '\r\nLead-poisoning', ' n. Poisoning by absorption of lead into the body.'),
(22386, 'l', '\r\nLeaf', ' â€”n. (pl. Leaves) 1 each of several flattened usu. Green structures of a plant, growing usu. On the side of a stem. 2 a foliage regarded collectively. B state of bearing leaves (tree in leaf). 3 single thickness of paper. 4 very thin sheet of metal etc. 5 hinged part, extra section, or flap of a table etc. â€”v. 1 put forth leaves. 2 (foll. By through) turn over the pages of (a book etc.).  leafage n. Leafy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(22387, 'l', '\r\nLeaflet', ' â€”n. 1 sheet of paper, pamphlet, etc. Giving information. 2 young leaf. 3 bot. Division of a compound leaf. â€”v. (-t-) distribute leaflets (to).'),
(22388, 'l', '\r\nLeaf-mould', ' n. Soil or compost consisting chiefly of decayed leaves.'),
(22389, 'l', '\r\nLeaf-stalk', ' n. Stalk joining a leaf to a stem.'),
(22390, 'l', '\r\nLeague1', ' â€”n. 1 people, countries, groups, etc., combining for a particular purpose. 2 agreement to combine in this way. 3 group of sports clubs which compete for a championship. 4 class of contestants etc. â€”v. (-gues, -gued, -guing) (often foll. By together) join in a league.  in league allied, conspiring. [latin ligo bind]'),
(22391, 'l', '\r\nLeague2', ' n. Hist. Varying measure of distance, usu. About three miles. [latin from celtic]'),
(22392, 'l', '\r\nLeague', 'table  n. List in ranked order of success etc.'),
(22393, 'l', '\r\nLeak', ' â€”n. 1 a hole through which matter passes accidentally in or out. B matter passing through thus. C act of passing through thus. 2 a similar escape of electrical charge. B charge that escapes. 3 disclosure of secret information. â€”v. 1 a pass through a leak. B lose or admit through a leak. 2 disclose (secret information). 3 (often foll. By out) become known.  have (or take) a leak slang urinate.  leaky adj. (-ier, -iest). [low german or dutch]'),
(22394, 'l', '\r\nLeakage', ' n. Action or result of leaking.'),
(22395, 'l', '\r\nLean1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Leaned or leant) 1 (often foll. By across, back, over, etc.) Be or place in a sloping position; incline from the perpendicular. 2 (foll. By against, on, upon) (cause to) rest for support against etc. 3 (foll. By on, upon) rely on. 4 (foll. By to, towards) be inclined or partial to. â€”n. Deviation from the perpendicular; inclination.  lean on colloq. Put pressure on (a person) to act in a certain way. Lean over backwards see *backwards. [old english]'),
(22396, 'l', '\r\nLean2', ' â€”adj. 1 (of a person or animal) thin; having no superfluous fat. 2 (of meat) containing little fat. 3 meagre. â€”n. Lean part of meat.  leanness n. [old english]'),
(22397, 'l', '\r\nLeaning', ' n. Tendency or partiality.'),
(22398, 'l', '\r\nLean-to', ' n. (pl. -tos) building with its roof leaning against a larger building or a wall.'),
(22399, 'l', '\r\nLean', 'years  n.pl. Years of scarcity.'),
(22400, 'l', '\r\nLeap', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Leaped or leapt) jump or spring forcefully. â€”n. Forceful jump.  by leaps and bounds with startlingly rapid progress. Leap in the dark daring step or enterprise. [old english]'),
(22401, 'l', '\r\nLeap-frog', ' â€”n. Game in which players vault with parted legs over others bending down. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 perform such a vault (over). 2 overtake alternately.'),
(22402, 'l', '\r\nLeap', 'year  n. Year with 366 days (including 29th feb. As an intercalary day).'),
(22403, 'l', '\r\nLearn', ' v. (past and past part. Learned or learnt) 1 gain knowledge of or skill in. 2 commit to memory. 3 (foll. By of) be told about. 4 (foll. By that, how, etc.) Become aware of. 5 receive instruction. 6 archaic or dial. Teach. [old english]'),
(22404, 'l', '\r\nLearned', ' adj. 1 having much knowledge acquired by study. 2 showing or requiring learning (a learned work). 3 (of a publication) academic.'),
(22405, 'l', '\r\nLearner', ' n. 1 person who is learning a subject or skill. 2 (in full learner driver) person who is learning to drive but has not yet passed a driving test.'),
(22406, 'l', '\r\nLearning', ' n. Knowledge acquired by study.'),
(22407, 'l', '\r\nLease', ' â€”n. Contract by which the owner of property allows another to use it for a specified time, usu. In return for payment. â€”v. (-sing) grant or take on lease.  new lease of (us on) life improved prospect of living, or of use after repair. [anglo-french lesser let, from latin laxo loosen]'),
(22408, 'l', '\r\nLeasehold', ' n. 1 holding of property by lease. 2 property held by lease.  leaseholder n.'),
(22409, 'l', '\r\nLeash', ' â€”n. Strap for holding a dog etc.; lead. â€”v. 1 put a leash on. 2 restrain.  straining at the leash eager to begin. [french lesse: related to *lease]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(22410, 'l', '\r\nLeast', ' â€”adj. 1 smallest, slightest. 2 (of a species etc.) Very small. â€”n. The least amount. â€”adv. In the least degree.  at least 1 at any rate. 2 (also at the least) not less than. In the least (or the least) (usu. With neg.) At all (not in the least offended). To say the least putting the case moderately. [old english, superlative of *less]'),
(22411, 'l', '\r\nLeast', 'common denominator  n. = *lowest common denominator.'),
(22412, 'l', '\r\nLeast', 'common multiple  n. = *lowest common multiple.'),
(22413, 'l', '\r\nLeather', ' â€”n. 1 material made from the skin of an animal by tanning etc. 2 piece of leather for polishing with. 3 leather part(s) of a thing. 4 slang cricket-ball or football. 5 (in pl.) Leather clothes. â€”v. 1 beat, thrash. 2 cover with leather. 3 polish or wipe with a leather. [old english]'),
(22414, 'l', '\r\nLeatherback', ' n. Large marine turtle with a leathery shell.'),
(22415, 'l', '\r\nLeather-bound', ' adj. Bound in leather.'),
(22416, 'l', '\r\nLeatherette', ' n. Propr. Imitation leather.'),
(22417, 'l', '\r\nLeather-jacket', ' n. Crane-fly grub with a tough skin.'),
(22418, 'l', '\r\nLeathery', ' adj. 1 like leather. 2 tough.'),
(22419, 'l', '\r\nLeave1', ' v. (-ving; past and past part. Left) 1 a go away from. B (often foll. By for) depart. 2 cause to or let remain; depart without taking. 3 (also absol.) Cease to reside at or belong to or work for. 4 abandon; cease to live with (one''s family etc.). 5 have remaining after one''s death. 6 bequeath. 7 (foll. By to + infin.) Allow (a person or thing) to do something independently. 8 (foll. By to) commit to another person etc. (leave that to me). 9 a abstain from consuming or dealing with. B (in passive; often foll. By over) remain over. 10 a deposit or entrust (a thing) to be attended to in one''s absence (left a message with his secretary). B depute (a person) to perform a function in one''s absence. 11 allow to remain or cause to be in a specified state or position (left the door open; left me exhausted).  leave alone refrain from disturbing, not interfere with. Leave a person cold not impress or excite a person. Leave off 1 come to or make an end. 2 discontinue. Leave out omit; exclude. [old english]'),
(22420, 'l', '\r\nLeave2', ' n. 1 (often foll. By to + infin.) Permission. 2 a (in full leave of absence) permission to be absent from duty. B period for which this lasts.  on leave legitimately absent from duty. Take one''s leave (of) bid farewell (to). Take leave of one''s senses go mad. [old english]'),
(22421, 'l', '\r\nLeaved', ' adj. Having a leaf or leaves, esp. (in comb.) Of a specified kind or number (four-leaved clover).'),
(22422, 'l', '\r\nLeaven', ' â€”n. 1 substance causing dough to ferment and rise. 2 pervasive transforming influence; admixture. â€”v. 1 ferment (dough) with leaven. 2 permeate and transform; modify with a tempering element. [latin levo lift]'),
(22423, 'l', '\r\nLeaves', ' pl. Of *leaf.'),
(22424, 'l', '\r\nLeave-taking', ' n. Act of taking one''s leave.'),
(22425, 'l', '\r\nLeavings', ' n.pl. Things left over.'),
(22426, 'l', '\r\nLebanese', ' â€”adj. Of lebanon. â€”n. (pl. Same) 1 native or national of lebanon. 2 person of lebanese descent.'),
(22427, 'l', '\r\nLech', ' colloq. â€”v. (often foll. By after) lust. â€”n. 1 lecherous man. 2 lust. [back-formation from *lecher]'),
(22428, 'l', '\r\nLecher', ' n. Lecherous man. [french lechier live in debauchery]'),
(22429, 'l', '\r\nLecherous', ' adj. Lustful, having excessive sexual desire.  lecherously adv.'),
(22430, 'l', '\r\nLechery', ' n. Excessive sexual desire.'),
(22431, 'l', '\r\nLectern', ' n. 1 stand for holding a book in a church etc. 2 similar stand for a lecturer etc. [latin lectrum from lego read]'),
(22432, 'l', '\r\nLecture', ' â€”n. 1 talk giving specified information to a class etc. 2 long serious speech, esp. As a reprimand. â€”v. (-ring) 1 (often foll. By on) deliver lecture(s). 2 talk seriously or reprovingly to.  lectureship n. [latin: related to *lectern]'),
(22433, 'l', '\r\nLecturer', ' n. Person who lectures, esp. As a teacher in higher education.'),
(22434, 'l', '\r\nLed', ' abbr. Light-emitting diode.'),
(22435, 'l', '\r\nLed', ' past and past part. Of *lead1.'),
(22436, 'l', '\r\nLederhosen', ' n.pl. Leather shorts as worn by some men in bavaria etc. [german, = leather trousers]'),
(22437, 'l', '\r\nLedge', ' n. Narrow horizontal or shelflike projection. [origin uncertain]'),
(22438, 'l', '\r\nLedger', ' n. Main record of the accounts of a business. [dutch]'),
(22439, 'l', '\r\nLee', ' n. 1 shelter given by a close object (under the lee of). 2 (in full lee side) side away from the wind. [old english]'),
(22440, 'l', '\r\nLeech', ' n. 1 bloodsucking worm formerly much used medically. 2 person who sponges on others. [old english]'),
(22441, 'l', '\r\nLeek', ' n. 1 plant of the onion family with flat leaves forming a cylindrical bulb, used as food. 2 this as a welsh national emblem. [old english]'),
(22442, 'l', '\r\nLeer', ' â€”v. Look slyly, lasciviously, or maliciously. â€”n. Leering look. [perhaps from obsolete leer cheek]'),
