(22596, 'l', '\r\nLeviathan', ' n. 1 bibl. Sea-monster. 2 very large or powerful thing. [latin from hebrew]'),
(22597, 'l', '\r\nLevis', ' n.pl. (also levi''s propr.) Type of (orig. Blue) denim jeans or overalls reinforced with rivets. [levi strauss, name of the manufacturer]'),
(22598, 'l', '\r\nLevitate', ' v. (-ting) 1 rise and float in the air (esp. With reference to spiritualism). 2 cause to do this.  levitation n. [latin levis light, after *gravitate]'),
(22599, 'l', '\r\nLevity', ' n. Lack of serious thought, frivolity. [latin levis light]'),
(22600, 'l', '\r\nLevy', ' â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 impose or collect compulsorily (payment etc.). 2 enrol (troops etc.). 3 wage (war). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 a collecting of a contribution, tax, etc. B contribution etc. Levied. 2 a act of enrolling troops etc. B (in pl.) Troops enrolled. [latin levo raise]'),
(22601, 'l', '\r\nLewd', ' adj. 1 lascivious. 2 obscene. [old english, originally = lay, vulgar]'),
(22602, 'l', '\r\nLexical', ' adj. 1 of the words of a language. 2 of or as of a lexicon. [greek lexikos, lexikon: see *lexicon]'),
(22603, 'l', '\r\nLexicography', ' n. Compiling of dictionaries.  lexicographer n. [from *lexicon, *-graphy]'),
(22604, 'l', '\r\nLexicon', ' n. 1 dictionary, esp. Of greek, hebrew, syriac, or arabic. 2 vocabulary of a person etc. [greek lexis word]'),
(22605, 'l', '\r\nLeyden', 'jar  n. Early capacitor consisting of a glass jar with layers of metal foil on the outside and inside. [leyden (now leiden) in holland]'),
(22606, 'l', '\r\nLf', ' abbr. Low frequency.'),
(22607, 'l', '\r\nLi', ' symb. Lithium.'),
(22608, 'l', '\r\nLiability', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being liable. 2 troublesome responsibility; handicap. 3 (in pl.) Debts etc. For which one is liable.'),
(22609, 'l', '\r\nLiable', ' predic. Adj. 1 legally bound. 2 (foll. By to) subject to. 3 (foll. By to + infin.) Under an obligation. 4 (foll. By to) exposed or open to (something undesirable). 5 (foll. By to + infin.) Apt, likely (it is liable to rain). 6 (foll. By for) answerable. [french lier bind, from latin ligo]'),
(22610, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' use of liable in sense 5, though common, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(22611, 'l', '\r\nLiaise', ' v. (-sing) (foll. By with, between) colloq. Establish cooperation, act as a link. [back-formation from *liaison]'),
(22612, 'l', '\r\nLiaison', ' n. 1 communication or cooperation. 2 illicit sexual relationship. [french lier bind: see *liable]'),
(22613, 'l', '\r\nLiana', ' n. Climbing plant of tropical forests. [french]'),
(22614, 'l', '\r\nLiar', ' n. Person who tells a lie or lies.'),
(22615, 'l', '\r\nLib.', ' Abbr. Liberal.'),
(22616, 'l', '\r\nLib', ' n. Colloq. (in names of political movements) liberation. [abbreviation]'),
(22617, 'l', '\r\nLibation', ' n. 1 pouring out of a drink-offering to a god. 2 such a drink-offering. [latin]'),
(22618, 'l', '\r\nLibel', ' â€”n. 1 law a published false statement that is damaging to a person''s reputation. B act of publishing this. 2 false and defamatory misrepresentation or statement. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 defame by libellous statements. 2 law publish a libel against.  libellous adj. [latin libellus diminutive of liber book]'),
(22619, 'l', '\r\nLiberal', ' â€”adj. 1 abundant, ample. 2 giving freely, generous. 3 open-minded. 4 not strict or rigorous. 5 for the general broadening of the mind (liberal studies). 6 a favouring moderate political and social reform. B (liberal) of or characteristic of liberals. â€”n. 1 person of liberal views. 2 (liberal) supporter or member of a liberal party.  liberalism n. Liberality n. Liberally adv. [latin liber free]'),
(22620, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' in the uk the name liberal was discontinued in official political use in 1988 when the party regrouped to form the social and liberal democrats. In 1989 this name was officially replaced by liberal democratic party.'),
(22621, 'l', '\r\nLiberal', 'democrat  n. Member of the party formed from the liberal party and the social democratic party.'),
(22622, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' see note at liberal.'),
(22623, 'l', '\r\nLiberalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make or become more liberal or less strict.  liberalization n.'),
(22624, 'l', '\r\nLiberate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By from) set free. 2 free (a country etc.) From an oppressor or enemy. 3 (often as liberated adj.) Free (a person) from rigid social conventions.  liberation n. Liberator n. [latin liberare liberat- from liber free]'),
(22625, 'l', '\r\nLibertine', ' â€”n. Licentious person, rake. â€”adj. Licentious. [latin, = freedman, from liber free]'),
(22626, 'l', '\r\nLiberty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 freedom from captivity etc. 2 right or power to do as one pleases. 3 (usu. In pl.) Right or privilege granted by authority.  at liberty 1 free. 2 (foll. By to + infin.) Permitted. Take liberties (often foll. By with) behave in an unduly familiar manner. [latin: related to *liberal]'),
(22627, 'l', '\r\nLibidinous', ' adj. Lustful. [latin: related to *libido]'),
(22628, 'l', '\r\nLibido', ' n. (pl. -s) psychic drive or energy, esp. That associated with sexual desire.  libidinal adj. [latin, = lust]'),
(22629, 'l', '\r\nLibra', ' n. 1 constellation and seventh sign of the zodiac (the scales). 2 person born when the sun is in this sign. [latin, = pound weight]'),
(22630, 'l', '\r\nLibrarian', ' n. Person in charge of or assisting in a library.  librarianship n.'),
(22631, 'l', '\r\nLibrary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 collection of books. 2 room or building where these are kept. 3 a similar collection of films, records, computer routines, etc. B place where these are kept. 4 set of books issued in similar bindings. [latin liber book]'),
(22632, 'l', '\r\nLibretto', ' n. (pl. -ti or -s) text of an opera etc.  librettist n. [italian, = little book]'),
(22633, 'l', '\r\nLice', ' pl. Of *louse.'),
(22634, 'l', '\r\nLicence', ' n. (us license) 1 official permit to own or use something, do something, or carry on a trade. 2 permission. 3 liberty of action, esp. When excessive. 4 writer''s or artist''s deliberate deviation from fact, correct grammar, etc. (poetic licence). [latin licet it is allowed]'),
(22635, 'l', '\r\nLicense1', ' n. (brit. Licence) 1 official permit to own or use something, do something, or carry on a trade. 2 permission. 3 liberty of action, esp. When excessive. 4 writer''s or artist''s deliberate deviation from fact, correct grammar, etc. (poetic licence). [latin licet it is allowed]'),
(22636, 'l', '\r\nLicense2', ' v. (-sing) 1 grant a licence to. 2 authorize the use of (premises) for a certain purpose.'),
(22637, 'l', '\r\nLicensee', ' n. Holder of a licence, esp. To sell alcoholic liquor.'),
(22638, 'l', '\r\nLicentiate', ' n. Holder of a certificate of professional competence. [medieval latin: related to *licence]'),
(22639, 'l', '\r\nLicentious', ' adj. Sexually promiscuous. [latin: related to *licence]'),
(22640, 'l', '\r\nLichee', ' var. Of *lychee.'),
(22641, 'l', '\r\nLichen', ' n. Plant composed of a fungus and an alga in association, growing on and colouring rocks, tree-trunks, etc. [greek leikhen]'),
(22642, 'l', '\r\nLich-gate', ' n. (also lych-gate) roofed gateway to a churchyard where a coffin awaits the clergyman''s arrival. [from lich = corpse]'),
(22643, 'l', '\r\nLicit', ' adj. Formal permitted, lawful. [latin: related to *licence]'),
(22644, 'l', '\r\nLick', ' â€”v. 1 pass the tongue over. 2 bring into a specified condition by licking (licked it all up; licked it clean). 3 (of a flame etc.) Play lightly over. 4 colloq. Defeat. 5 colloq. Thrash. â€”n. 1 act of licking with the tongue. 2 colloq. Fast pace (at a lick). 3 smart blow.  lick a person''s boots be servile. Lick into shape make presentable or efficient. Lick one''s lips (or chops) look forward with relish. Lick one''s wounds be in retirement regaining strength etc. After defeat. [old english]'),
(22645, 'l', '\r\nLick', 'and a promise  n. Colloq. Hasty performance of a task, esp. Washing oneself.'),
(22646, 'l', '\r\nLicorice', ' var. Of *liquorice.'),
(22647, 'l', '\r\nLid', ' n. 1 hinged or removable cover, esp. For a container. 2 = *eyelid.  put the lid on colloq. 1 be the culmination of. 2 put a stop to.  lidded adj. (also in comb.). [old english]'),
(22648, 'l', '\r\nLido', ' n. (pl. -s) public open-air swimming-pool or bathing-beach. [lido, name of a beach near venice]'),
(22649, 'l', '\r\nLie1', ' â€”v. (lies; lying; past lay; past part. Lain) 1 be in or assume a horizontal position on a surface; be at rest on something. 2 (of a thing) rest flat on a surface. 3 remain undisturbed or undiscussed etc. (let matters lie). 4 a be kept, remain, or be in a specified state or place (lie hidden; lie in wait; books lay unread). B (of abstract things) exist; be in a certain position or relation (answer lies in education). 5 a be situated (village lay to the east). B be spread out to view. â€”n. Way, direction, or position in which a thing lies.  lie down assume a lying position; have a short rest. Lie down under accept (an insult etc.) Without protest. Lie in stay in bed late in the morning. Lie low 1 keep quiet or unseen. 2 be discreet about one''s intentions. Lie with be the responsibility of (a person) (decision lies with you). Take lying down (usu. With neg.) Accept (an insult etc.) Without protest. [old english]'),
(22650, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' the transitive use of lie, meaning lay, as in lie her on the bed, is incorrect in standard english.'),
(22651, 'l', '\r\nLie2', ' â€”n. 1 intentionally false statement (tell a lie). 2 something that deceives. â€”v. (lies, lied, lying) 1 tell a lie or lies. 2 (of a thing) be deceptive.  give the lie to show the falsity of (a supposition etc.). [old english]'),
(22652, 'l', '\r\nLied', ' n. (pl. Lieder) german song, esp. Of the romantic period. [german]'),
(22653, 'l', '\r\nLie-detector', ' n. Instrument supposedly determining whether a person is lying, by testing for certain physiological changes.'),
(22654, 'l', '\r\nLie-down', ' n. Short rest.'),
(22655, 'l', '\r\nLiege', ' usu. Hist. â€”adj. Entitled to receive, or bound to give, feudal service or allegiance. â€”n. 1 (in full liege lord) feudal superior or sovereign. 2 (usu. In pl.) Vassal, subject. [medieval latin laeticus, probably from germanic]'),
(22656, 'l', '\r\nLie-in', ' n. Prolonged stay in bed in the morning.'),
(22657, 'l', '\r\nLien', ' n. Law right to hold another''s property until a debt on it is paid. [latin ligo bind]'),
(22658, 'l', '\r\nLie', 'of the land  n. State of affairs.'),
(22659, 'l', '\r\nLieu', ' n.  in lieu 1 instead. 2 (foll. By of) in the place of. [latin locus place]'),
(22660, 'l', '\r\nLieut.', ' Abbr. Lieutenant.'),
(22661, 'l', '\r\nLieutenant', ' n. 1 a army officer next in rank below captain. B naval officer next in rank below lieutenant commander. 2 deputy.  lieutenancy n. (pl. -ies). [french: related to *lieu place, *tenant holder]'),
(22662, 'l', '\r\nLieutenant', 'colonel  n. (also lieutenant commander or general) officers ranking next below colonel, commander, or general.'),
(22663, 'l', '\r\nLife', ' n. (pl. Lives) 1 capacity for growth, functional activity, and continual change until death. 2 living things and their activity (insect life; is there life on mars?). 3 a period during which life lasts, or the period from birth to the present time or from the present time to death (have done it all my life; will regret it all my life). B duration of a thing''s existence or ability to function. 4 a person''s state of existence as a living individual (sacrificed their lives). B living person (many lives were lost). 5 a individual''s actions or fortunes; manner of existence (start a new life). B particular aspect of this (private life). 6 business and pleasures of the world (in paris you really see life). 7 energy, liveliness (full of life). 8 biography. 9 colloq. = *life sentence.  for dear (or one''s) life as if or in order to escape death. For life for the rest of one''s life. Not on your life colloq. Most certainly not. [old english]'),
(22664, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'assurance  n. = *life insurance.'),
(22665, 'l', '\r\nLifebelt', ' n. Buoyant belt for keeping a person afloat.'),
(22666, 'l', '\r\nLifeblood', ' n. 1 blood, as being necessary to life. 2 vital factor or influence.'),
(22667, 'l', '\r\nLifeboat', ' n. 1 special boat for rescuing those in distress at sea. 2 ship''s small boat for use in emergency.'),
(22668, 'l', '\r\nLifebuoy', ' n. Buoyant support for keeping a person afloat.'),
(22669, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'cycle  n. Series of changes in the life of an organism, including reproduction.'),
(22670, 'l', '\r\nLifeguard', ' n. Expert swimmer employed to rescue bathers from drowning.'),
(22671, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'guards  n.pl. Regiment of the royal household cavalry.'),
(22672, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'insurance  n. Insurance for a sum to be paid after a set period or on the death of the insured person if earlier.'),
(22673, 'l', '\r\nLife-jacket', ' n. Buoyant jacket for keeping a person afloat.'),
(22674, 'l', '\r\nLifeless', ' adj. 1 dead. 2 unconscious. 3 lacking movement or vitality.  lifelessly adv. [old english]'),
(22675, 'l', '\r\nLifelike', ' adj. Closely resembling life or the person or thing represented.'),
(22676, 'l', '\r\nLifeline', ' n. 1 rope etc. Used for life-saving. 2 sole means of communication or transport.'),
(22677, 'l', '\r\nLifelong', ' adj. Lasting a lifetime.'),
(22678, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'peer  n. Peer whose title lapses on death.'),
(22679, 'l', '\r\nLife-preserver', ' n. 1 short stick with a heavily loaded end. 2 life-jacket etc.'),
(22680, 'l', '\r\nLifer', ' n. Slang person serving a life sentence.'),
(22681, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'sciences  n.pl. Biology and related subjects.'),
(22682, 'l', '\r\nLife', 'sentence  n. Sentence of imprisonment for an indefinite period.'),
(22683, 'l', '\r\nLife-size', ' adj. (also -sized) of the same size as the person or thing represented.'),
(22684, 'l', '\r\nLifestyle', ' n. Way of life of a person or group.'),
(22685, 'l', '\r\nLife-support', 'machine  n. Respirator.'),
(22686, 'l', '\r\nLifetime', ' n. Duration of a person''s life.'),
(22687, 'l', '\r\nLift', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up, off, out, etc.) Raise or remove to a higher position. 2 go up; be raised; yield to an upward force. 3 give an upward direction to (the eyes or face). 4 elevate to a higher plane of thought or feeling. 5 (of fog etc.) Rise, disperse. 6 remove (a barrier or restriction). 7 transport (supplies, troops, etc.) By air. 8 colloq. A steal. B plagiarize (a passage of writing etc.). 9 dig up (esp. Potatoes etc.). â€”n. 1 lifting or being lifted. 2 ride in another person''s vehicle (gave them a lift).'),
(22688, 'l', '\r\nLift-off', ' n. Vertical take-off of a spacecraft or rocket.'),
(22689, 'l', '\r\nLigament', ' n. Band of tough fibrous tissue linking bones. [latin ligo bind]'),
(22690, 'l', '\r\nLigature', ' â€”n. 1 tie or bandage. 2 mus. Slur, tie. 3 two or more letters joined, e.g. Ã†. 4 bond; thing that unites. â€”v. (-ring) bind or connect with a ligature. [latin ligo bind]'),
(22691, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' sense 3 of this word is sometimes confused with digraph, which means â€˜two separate letters together representing one soundâ€™.'),
(22692, 'l', '\r\nLight1', ' â€”n. 1 the natural agent (electromagnetic radiation) that stimulates sight and makes things visible. 2 the medium or condition of the space in which this is present (just enough light to see). 3 appearance of brightness (saw a distant light). 4 source of light, e.g. The sun, a lamp, fire, etc. 5 (often in pl.) Traffic-light. 6 a flame or spark serving to ignite. B device producing this. 7 aspect in which a thing is regarded (appeared in a new light). 8 a mental illumination. B spiritual illumination by divine truth. 9 vivacity etc. In a person''s face, esp. In the eyes. 10 eminent person (leading light). 11 bright parts of a picture etc. 12 window or opening in a wall to let light in. â€”v. (past lit; past part. Lit or lighted) (attrib.) 1 set burning; begin to burn. 2 (often foll. By up) provide with light or lighting; make prominent by means of light. 3 show (a person) the way or surroundings with a light. 4 (usu. Foll. By up) (of the face or eyes) brighten with animation, pleasure, etc. â€”adj. 1 well provided with light; not dark. 2 (of a colour) pale (light blue; light-blue ribbon).  bring (or come) to light reveal or be revealed. In a good (or bad) light giving a favourable (or unfavourable) impression. In the light of taking account of. Light up 1 colloq. Begin to smoke a cigarette etc. 2 = sense 2 of v. 3 = sense 4 of v.  lightish adj. [old english]'),
(22693, 'l', '\r\nLight2', ' â€”adj. 1 not heavy. 2 a relatively low in weight, amount, density, intensity, etc. (light arms, traffic, metal, rain). B deficient in weight (light coin). 3 a carrying or suitable for small loads (light railway). B (of a ship) unladen. C carrying only light arms, armaments, etc. 4 (of food) easy to digest. 5 (of entertainment, music, etc.) Intended for amusement only; not profound. 6 (of sleep or a sleeper) easily disturbed. 7 easily borne or done (light duties). 8 nimble; quick-moving (light step; light rhythm). 9 (of a building etc.) Graceful, elegant. 10 a free from sorrow; cheerful (light heart). B giddy (light in the head). â€”adv. 1 in a light manner (tread light; sleep light). 2 with a minimum load (travel light). â€”v. (past and past part. Lit or lighted) (foll. By on, upon) come upon or find by chance.  make light of treat as unimportant.  lightish adj. Lightly adv. Lightness n. [old english]'),
(22694, 'l', '\r\nLight-bulb', ' n. Glass bulb containing an inert gas and a metal filament, providing light when an electric current is passed through it.'),
(22695, 'l', '\r\nLighten1', ' v. 1 a make or become lighter in weight. B reduce the weight or load of. 2 bring relief to (the mind etc.). 3 mitigate (a penalty).'),
(22696, 'l', '\r\nLighten2', ' v. 1 shed light on. 2 make or grow bright.'),
(22697, 'l', '\r\nLighter1', ' n. Device for lighting cigarettes etc.'),
(22698, 'l', '\r\nLighter2', ' n. Boat, usu. Flat-bottomed, for transferring goods from a ship to a wharf or another ship. [dutch: related to *light2 in the sense â€˜unloadâ€™]'),
(22699, 'l', '\r\nLighter-than-air', ' attrib. Adj. (of an aircraft) weighing less than the air it displaces.'),
(22700, 'l', '\r\nLight-fingered', ' adj. Given to stealing.'),
(22701, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'flyweight  n. 1 amateur boxing weight up to 48 kg. 2 amateur boxer of this weight.'),
(22702, 'l', '\r\nLight-footed', ' adj. Nimble.'),
(22703, 'l', '\r\nLight-headed', ' adj. Giddy, delirious.  light-headedness n.'),
(22704, 'l', '\r\nLight-hearted', ' adj. 1 cheerful. 2 (unduly) casual.  light-heartedly adv.'),
(22705, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'heavyweight  n. 1 weight in certain sports between middleweight and heavyweight, in amateur boxing 75â€“81 kg: also called *cruiserweight. 2 sportsman of this weight.'),
(22706, 'l', '\r\nLighthouse', ' n. Tower etc. Containing a beacon light to warn or guide ships at sea.'),
(22707, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'industry  n. Manufacture of small or light articles.'),
(22708, 'l', '\r\nLighting', ' n. 1 equipment in a room or street etc. For producing light. 2 arrangement or effect of lights.'),
(22709, 'l', '\r\nLighting-up', 'time  n. Time after which vehicles must show the prescribed lights.'),
(22710, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'meter  n. Instrument for measuring the intensity of the light, esp. To show the correct photographic exposure.'),
(22711, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'middleweight  n. 1 weight in amateur boxing of 67â€“71 kg. 2 amateur boxer of this weight.'),
(22712, 'l', '\r\nLightning', ' â€”n. Flash of bright light produced by an electric discharge between clouds or between clouds and the ground. â€”attrib. Adj. Very quick. [from *lighten2]'),
(22713, 'l', '\r\nLightning-conductor', ' n. (also lightning-rod) metal rod or wire fixed to an exposed part of a building or to a mast to divert lightning into the earth or sea.'),
(22714, 'l', '\r\nLights', ' n.pl. Lungs of sheep, pigs, etc., used as a food esp. For pets. [from *light2: cf. *lung]'),
(22715, 'l', '\r\nLightship', ' n. Moored or anchored ship with a beacon light.'),
(22716, 'l', '\r\nLightweight', ' â€”adj. 1 of below average weight. 2 of little importance or influence. â€”n. 1 lightweight person, animal, or thing. 2 a weight in certain sports between featherweight and welterweight, in amateur boxing 57â€“60 kg. B sportsman of this weight.'),
(22717, 'l', '\r\nLight', 'welterweight  n. 1 weight in amateur boxing of 60â€“63.5 kg. 2 amateur boxer of this weight.'),
(22718, 'l', '\r\nLight-year', ' n. Distance light travels in one year, nearly 6 million million miles.'),
(22719, 'l', '\r\nLigneous', ' adj. 1 (of a plant) woody. 2 of the nature of wood. [latin lignum wood]'),
(22720, 'l', '\r\nLignite', ' n. Brown coal of woody texture.'),
(22721, 'l', '\r\nLignum', 'vitae  n. A hard-wooded tree. [latin, = wood of life]'),
(22722, 'l', '\r\nLikable', ' var. Of *likeable.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(22723, 'l', '\r\nLike1', ' â€”adj. (more like, most like) 1 a having some or all of the qualities of another, each other, or an original. B resembling in some way, such as (good writers like dickens). 2 characteristic of (not like them to be late). 3 in a suitable state or mood for (felt like working; felt like a cup of tea). â€”prep. In the manner of; to the same degree as (drink like a fish; acted like an idiot). â€”adv. 1 slang so to speak (did a quick getaway, like). 2 colloq. Likely, probably (as like as not). â€”conj. Colloq. 1 as (cannot do it like you do). 2 as if (ate like they were starving). â€”n. 1 counterpart; equal; similar person or thing. 2 (prec. By the) thing or things of the same kind (will never do the like again).  and the like and similar things. Like anything colloq. Very much, vigorously. The likes of colloq. A person such as. More like it colloq. Nearer what is required. What is he (or it etc.) Like? What sort of person is he (or thing is it etc.)? [old english]'),
(22724, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of like as a conjunction is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(22725, 'l', '\r\nLike2', ' â€”v. (-king) 1 find agreeable or enjoyable. 2 a choose to have; prefer (like my tea weak). B wish for or be inclined to (would like a nap; should like to come). â€”n. (in pl.) Things one likes or prefers. [old english]'),
(22726, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of like as a conjunction is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(22727, 'l', '\r\nLike2', ' â€”v. (-king) 1 find agreeable or enjoyable. 2 a choose to have; prefer (like my tea weak). B wish for or be inclined to (would like a nap; should like to come). â€”n. (in pl.) Things one likes or prefers. [old english]'),
(22728, 'l', '\r\n-like', ' comb. Form forming adjectives from nouns, meaning â€˜similar to, characteristic ofâ€™ (doglike; shell-like; tortoise-like).'),
(22729, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' in formations not generally current the hyphen should be used. It may be omitted when the first element is of one syllable, unless it ends in -l.'),
(22730, 'l', '\r\nLikeable', ' adj. (also likable) pleasant; easy to like.  likeably adv.'),
(22731, 'l', '\r\nLikelihood', ' n. Probability.  in all likelihood very probably.'),
(22732, 'l', '\r\nLikely', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 probable; such as may well happen or be true. 2 to be reasonably expected (not likely to come now). 3 promising; apparently suitable (a likely spot). â€”adv. Probably.  not likely! Colloq. Certainly not, i refuse. [old norse: related to *like1]'),
(22733, 'l', '\r\nLike-minded', ' adj. Having the same tastes, opinions, etc.'),
(22734, 'l', '\r\nLiken', ' v. (foll. By to) point out the resemblance of (a person or thing to another). [from *like1]'),
(22735, 'l', '\r\nLikeness', ' n. 1 (usu. Foll. By between, to) resemblance. 2 (foll. By of) semblance or guise (in the likeness of a ghost). 3 portrait, representation.'),
(22736, 'l', '\r\nLikewise', ' adv. 1 also, moreover. 2 similarly (do likewise).'),
(22737, 'l', '\r\nLiking', ' n. 1 what one likes; one''s taste (is it to your liking?). 2 (foll. By for) regard or fondness; taste or fancy.'),
(22738, 'l', '\r\nLilac', ' â€”n. 1 shrub with fragrant pinkish-violet or white blossoms. 2 pale pinkish-violet colour. â€”adj. Of this colour. [persian]'),
(22739, 'l', '\r\nLiliaceous', ' adj. Of the lily family. [related to *lily]'),
