(22868, 'l', '\r\nLiterati', ' n.pl. The class of learned people.'),
(22869, 'l', '\r\nLiterature', ' n. 1 written works, esp. Those valued for form and style. 2 writings of a country or period or on a particular subject. 3 literary production. 4 colloq. Printed matter, leaflets, etc.'),
(22870, 'l', '\r\nLithe', ' adj. Flexible, supple. [old english]'),
(22871, 'l', '\r\nLithium', ' n. Soft silver-white metallic element. [greek lithion from lithos stone]'),
(22872, 'l', '\r\nLitho', ' colloq. â€”n. = *lithography. â€”v. (-oes, -oed) lithograph. [abbreviation]'),
(22873, 'l', '\r\nLithograph', ' â€”n. Lithographic print. â€”v. Print by lithography. [greek lithos stone]'),
(22874, 'l', '\r\nLithography', ' n. Process of printing from a plate so treated that ink adheres only to the design to be printed.  lithographer n. Lithographic adj. Lithographically adv.'),
(22875, 'l', '\r\nLithuanian', ' â€”n. 1 a native or national of lithuania in eastern europe. B person of lithuanian descent. 2 language of lithuania. â€”adj. Of lithuania, its people, or language.'),
(22876, 'l', '\r\nLitigant', ' â€”n. Party to a lawsuit. â€”adj. Engaged in a lawsuit. [related to *litigate]'),
(22877, 'l', '\r\nLitigate', ' v. (-ting) 1 go to law. 2 contest (a point) at law.  litigation n. Litigator n. [latin lis lit- lawsuit]'),
(22878, 'l', '\r\nLitigious', ' adj. 1 fond of litigation. 2 contentious. [latin: related to *litigate]'),
(22879, 'l', '\r\nLitmus', ' n. Dye from lichens, turned red by acid and blue by alkali. [old norse, = dye-moss]'),
(22880, 'l', '\r\nLitmus', 'paper  n. Paper stained with litmus, used to test for acids or alkalis.'),
(22881, 'l', '\r\nLitmus', 'test  n. Colloq. Real or ultimate test.'),
(22882, 'l', '\r\nLitotes', ' n. (pl. Same) ironic understatement, esp. Using the negative (e.g. I shan''t be sorry for i shall be glad). [greek litos plain, meagre]'),
(22883, 'l', '\r\nLitre', ' n. (us liter) metric unit of capacity equal to 1 cubic decimetre (1.76 pints). [greek litra]'),
(22884, 'l', '\r\nLitt.d.', ' Abbr. Doctor of letters. [latin litterarum doctor]'),
(22885, 'l', '\r\nLitter', ' â€”n. 1 a refuse, esp. Paper, discarded in a public place. B odds and ends lying about. 2 young animals brought forth at one birth. 3 vehicle containing a couch and carried on men''s shoulders or by animals. 4 a kind of stretcher for the sick and wounded. 5 straw etc., as bedding for animals. 6 granulated material for use as an animal''s, esp. A cat''s, toilet indoors. â€”v. 1 make (a place) untidy with refuse. 2 give birth to (whelps etc.). 3  a provide (a horse etc.) With litter as bedding. B spread straw etc. On (a stable-floor etc.). [latin lectus bed]'),
(22886, 'l', '\r\nLitterbug', ' n. Colloq. Person who drops litter in the street etc.'),
(22887, 'l', '\r\nLitter-lout', ' n. Colloq. = *litterbug.'),
(22888, 'l', '\r\nLittle', ' â€”adj. (littler, littlest; less or lesser, least) 1 small in size, amount, degree, etc.; often used affectionately or condescendingly (friendly little chap; silly little fool). 2 a short in stature. B of short distance or duration. 3 (prec. By a) a certain though small amount of (give me a little butter). 4 trivial (questions every little thing).'),
(22889, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'bear  see *bear2.'),
(22890, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'by little  adv. By degrees; gradually.'),
(22891, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'end  n. The smaller end of a connecting-rod, attached to the piston.'),
(22892, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'grebe  n. Small water-bird of the grebe family.'),
(22893, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'people  n.pl. (prec. By the) fairies.'),
(22894, 'l', '\r\nLittle', 'woman  n. (prec. By the) colloq. Often derog. One''s wife.'),
(22895, 'l', '\r\nLittoral', ' â€”adj. Of or on the shore. â€”n. Region lying along a shore. [latin litus litor- shore]'),
(22896, 'l', '\r\nLiturgy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 prescribed form of public worship. 2 (the liturgy) the book of common prayer.  liturgical adj. Liturgically adv. [greek leitourgia public worship]'),
(22897, 'l', '\r\nLivable', ' var. Of *liveable.'),
(22898, 'l', '\r\nLive1', ' v. (-ving) 1 have life; be or remain alive. 2 have one''s home (lives up the road). 3 (foll. By on) subsist or feed (lives on fruit). 4 (foll. By on, off) depend for subsistence (lives off the state; lives on a pension). 5 (foll. By on, by) sustain one''s position (live on their reputation; lives by his wits). 6 a spend or pass (lived a full life). B express in one''s life (lives his faith). 7 conduct oneself, arrange one''s habits, etc., in a specified way (live quietly). 8 (often foll. By on) (of a person or thing) survive; remain (memory lived on). 9 enjoy life to the full (not really living).  live and let live condone others'' failings so as to be similarly tolerated. Live down cause (past guilt, a scandal, etc.) To be forgotten by blameless conduct thereafter. Live for regard as one''s life''s purpose (lives for her music). Live in (or out) reside on (or off) the premises of one''s work. Live it up colloq. Live gaily and extravagantly. Live a lie keep up a pretence. Live together (esp. Of a couple not married to each other) share a home and have a sexual relationship. Live up to fulfil. Live with 1 share a home with. 2 tolerate. [old english]'),
(22899, 'l', '\r\nLive2', ' â€”adj. 1 (attrib.) That is alive; living. 2 (of a broadcast, performance, etc.) Heard or seen at the time of its performance or with an audience present. 3 of current interest or importance (a live issue). 4 glowing, burning (live coals). 5 (of a match, bomb, etc.) Not yet kindled or exploded. 6 (of a wire etc.) Charged with or carrying electricity. â€”adv. 1 in order to make a live broadcast (going live now to the house of commons). 2 as a live performance etc. (show went out live). [from *alive]'),
(22900, 'l', '\r\nLiveable', ' adj. (also livable) 1 colloq. (usu. Liveable-in) (of a house etc.) Fit to live in. 2 (of a life) worth living. 3 colloq. (usu. Liveable-with) (of a person) easy to live with.'),
(22901, 'l', '\r\nLived-in', ' adj. 1 (of a room etc.) Showing signs of habitation. 2 colloq. (of a face) marked by experience.'),
(22902, 'l', '\r\nLive-in', ' attrib. Adj. (of a sexual partner, employee, etc.) Cohabiting; resident.'),
(22903, 'l', '\r\nLivelihood', ' n. Means of living; job, income. [old english: related to *life]'),
(22904, 'l', '\r\nLivelong', ' adj. In its entire length (the livelong day). [from obsolete lief, assimilated to *live1]'),
(22905, 'l', '\r\nLively', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of life; vigorous, energetic. 2 vivid (lively imagination). 3 cheerful. 4 joc. Exciting, dangerous (made things lively for him).  liveliness n. [old english]'),
(22906, 'l', '\r\nLiven', ' v. (often foll. By up) colloq. Make or become lively, cheer up.'),
(22907, 'l', '\r\nLiver1', ' n. 1 large glandular organ in the abdomen of vertebrates. 2 liver of some animals as food. [old english]'),
(22908, 'l', '\r\nLiver2', ' n. Person who lives in a specified way (a fast liver).'),
(22909, 'l', '\r\nLiveried', ' adj. Wearing livery.'),
(22910, 'l', '\r\nLiverish', ' adj. 1 suffering from a liver disorder. 2 peevish, glum.'),
(22911, 'l', '\r\nLiverpudlian', ' â€”n. Native of liverpool. â€”adj. Of liverpool. [liverpool in nw england]'),
(22912, 'l', '\r\nLiver', 'sausage  n. Sausage of cooked liver etc.'),
(22913, 'l', '\r\nLiverwort', ' n. Small mosslike or leafless plant sometimes lobed like a liver.'),
(22914, 'l', '\r\nLivery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 distinctive uniform of a member of a city company or of a servant. 2 distinctive guise or marking (birds in their winter livery). 3 distinctive colour scheme in which a company''s vehicles etc. Are painted.  at livery (of a horse) kept for the owner for a fixed charge. [anglo-french liverÃ©, past part. Of livrer *deliver]'),
(22915, 'l', '\r\nLivery', 'stable  n. Stable where horses are kept at livery or let out for hire.'),
(22916, 'l', '\r\nLives', ' pl. Of *life.'),
(22917, 'l', '\r\nLivestock', ' n. (usu. Treated as pl.) Animals on a farm, kept for use or profit.'),
(22918, 'l', '\r\nLive', 'wire  n. Spirited person.'),
(22919, 'l', '\r\nLivid', ' adj. 1 colloq. Furious. 2 of a bluish leaden colour (livid bruise). [latin]'),
(22920, 'l', '\r\nLiving', ' â€”n. 1 being alive (that''s what living is all about). 2 livelihood. 3 position held by a clergyman, providing an income. â€”adj. 1 contemporary; now alive. 2 (of a likeness) exact, lifelike. 3 (of a language) still in vernacular use.  within living memory within the memory of people still alive.'),
(22921, 'l', '\r\nLiving-room', ' n. Room for general day use.'),
(22922, 'l', '\r\nLiving', 'wage  n. Wage on which one can live without privation.'),
(22923, 'l', '\r\nLizard', ' n. Reptile with usu. A long body and tail, four legs, and a rough or scaly hide. [latin lacertus]'),
(22924, 'l', '\r\nLj', ' abbr. (pl. L jj) lord justice.'),
(22925, 'l', '\r\n''Ll', ' v. (usu. After pronouns) shall, will (i''ll; that''ll). [abbreviation]'),
(22926, 'l', '\r\nLlama', ' n. S. American ruminant kept as a beast of burden and for its soft woolly fleece. [spanish from quechua]'),
(22927, 'l', '\r\nLl', 'b  abbr. Bachelor of laws. [latin legum baccalaureus]'),
(22928, 'l', '\r\nLl', 'd  abbr. Doctor of laws. [latin legum doctor]'),
(22929, 'l', '\r\nLl', 'm  abbr. Master of laws. [latin legum magister]'),
(22930, 'l', '\r\nLloyd''s', ' n. Incorporated society of underwriters in london. [lloyd, proprietor of the coffee-house where the society originally met]'),
(22931, 'l', '\r\nLloyd''s', 'list  n. Daily publication devoted to shipping news.'),
(22932, 'l', '\r\nLloyd''s', 'register  n. Annual classified list of all ships.'),
(22933, 'l', '\r\nLn', ' abbr. Natural logarithm.'),
(22934, 'l', '\r\nLo', ' int. Archaic look.  lo and behold joc. Formula introducing mention of a surprising fact. [old english]'),
(22935, 'l', '\r\nLoach', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) small freshwater fish. [french]'),
(22936, 'l', '\r\nLoad', ' â€”n. 1 a what is carried or to be carried. B amount usu. Or actually carried (often in comb.: lorry-load of bricks). 2 burden or commitment of work, responsibility, care, etc. 3 colloq. A (in pl.; often foll. By of) plenty, a lot (loads of money, people). B (a load of) a quantity (a load of nonsense). 4 amount of power carried by an electric circuit or supplied by a generating station. â€”v. 1 a put a load on or aboard. B place (a load) aboard a ship, on a vehicle, etc. 2 (often foll. By up) (of a vehicle or person) take a load aboard. 3 (often foll. By with) burden, strain (loaded with food). 4 (also load up) (foll. By with) overburden, overwhelm (loaded us with work, with abuse). 5 a put ammunition in (a gun), film in (a camera), a cassette in (a tape recorder), a program in (a computer), etc. B put (a film, cassette, etc.) Into a device. 6 give a bias to.  get a load of slang take note of. [old english, = way]'),
(22937, 'l', '\r\nLoaded', ' adj. 1 slang a rich. B drunk. C us drugged. 2 (of dice etc.) Weighted. 3 (of a question or statement) carrying some hidden implication.'),
(22938, 'l', '\r\nLoader', ' n. 1 loading-machine. 2 (in comb.) Gun, machine, lorry, etc., loaded in a specified way (breech-loader; front-loader).  -loading adj. (in comb.) (in sense 2).'),
(22939, 'l', '\r\nLoad', 'line  n. = *plimsoll line.'),
(22940, 'l', '\r\nLoadstone', ' var. Of *lodestone.'),
(22941, 'l', '\r\nLoaf1', ' n. (pl. Loaves) 1 unit of baked bread, usu. Of a standard size or shape. 2 other food made in the shape of a loaf and cooked. 3 slang head as the seat of common sense. [old english]'),
(22942, 'l', '\r\nLoaf2', ' v. (often foll. By about, around) spend time idly; hang about. [back-formation from *loafer]'),
(22943, 'l', '\r\nLoafer', ' n. 1 idle person. 2 (loafer) propr. Flat soft-soled leather shoe. [origin uncertain]'),
(22944, 'l', '\r\nLoam', ' n. Rich soil of clay, sand, and humus.  loamy adj. [old english]'),
(22945, 'l', '\r\nLoan', ' â€”n. 1 thing lent, esp. A sum of money. 2 lending or being lent. â€”v. Lend (money, works of art, etc.).  on loan being lent. [old english]'),
(22946, 'l', '\r\nLoan', 'shark  n. Colloq. Person who lends money at exorbitant rates of interest.'),
(22947, 'l', '\r\nLoath', ' predic. Adj. (also loth) disinclined, reluctant (loath to admit it). [old english]'),
(22948, 'l', '\r\nLoathe', ' v. (-thing) detest, hate.  loathing n. [old english]'),
(22949, 'l', '\r\nLoathsome', ' adj. Arousing hatred or disgust; repulsive.'),
(22950, 'l', '\r\nLoaves', ' pl. Of *loaf1.'),
(22951, 'l', '\r\nLob', ' â€”v. (-bb-) hit or throw (a ball etc.) Slowly or in a high arc. â€”n. Such a ball. [probably low german or dutch]'),
(22952, 'l', '\r\nLobar', ' adj. Of a lobe, esp. Of the lung (lobar pneumonia).'),
(22953, 'l', '\r\nLobate', ' adj. Having a lobe or lobes.'),
(22954, 'l', '\r\nLobby', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 porch, ante-room, entrance-hall, or corridor. 2 a (in the house of commons) large hall used esp. For interviews between mps and the public. B (also division lobby) each of two corridors to which mps retire to vote. 3 a body of lobbyists (anti-abortion lobby). B organized rally of lobbying members of the public. 4 (prec. By the) group of journalists who receive unattributable briefings from the government (lobby correspondent). â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 solicit the support of (an influential person). 2 (of members of the public) inform in order to influence (legislators, an mp, etc.). 3 frequent a parliamentary lobby. [latin lobia lodge]'),
(22955, 'l', '\r\nLobbyist', ' n. Person who lobbies an mp etc., esp. Professionally.'),
(22956, 'l', '\r\nLobe', ' n. 1 lower soft pendulous part of the outer ear. 2 similar part of other organs, esp. The brain, liver, and lung.  lobed adj. [greek lobos lobe, pod]'),
(22957, 'l', '\r\nLobelia', ' n. Plant with bright, esp. Blue, flowers. [lobel, name of a botanist]'),
(22958, 'l', '\r\nLobotomy', ' n. (pl. -ies) incision into the frontal lobe of the brain, formerly used in some cases of mental disorder. [from *lobe]'),
(22959, 'l', '\r\nLobscouse', ' n. Sailor''s dish of meat stewed with vegetables and ship''s biscuit. [origin unknown]'),
(22960, 'l', '\r\nLobster', ' n. 1 marine crustacean with two pincer-like claws. 2 its flesh as food. [latin locusta lobster, *locust]'),
(22961, 'l', '\r\nLobster-pot', ' n. Basket for trapping lobsters.'),
(22962, 'l', '\r\nLobworm', ' n. Large earthworm used as fishing-bait. [from *lob in obsolete sense â€˜pendulous objectâ€™]'),
(22963, 'l', '\r\nLocal', ' â€”adj. 1 belonging to, existing in, or peculiar to a particular place (local history). 2 of the neighbourhood (local paper). 3 of or affecting a part and not the whole (local anaesthetic). 4 (of a telephone call) to a nearby place and charged at a lower rate. â€”n. 1 inhabitant of a particular place. 2 (often prec. By the) colloq. Local public house. 3 local anaesthetic.  locally adv. [latin locus place]'),
(22964, 'l', '\r\nLocal', 'authority  n. Administrative body in local government.'),
(22965, 'l', '\r\nLocal', 'colour  n. Touches of detail in a story etc. Designed to provide a realistic background.'),
(22966, 'l', '\r\nLocale', ' n. Scene or locality of an event or occurrence. [french local]'),
(22967, 'l', '\r\nLocal', 'government  n. System of administration of a county, district, parish, etc., by the elected representatives of those who live there.'),
(22968, 'l', '\r\nLocality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 district. 2 site or scene of a thing. 3 thing''s position. [latin: related to *local]'),
(22969, 'l', '\r\nLocalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 restrict or assign to a particular place. 2 invest with the characteristics of a particular place. 3 decentralize.'),
(22970, 'l', '\r\nLocal', 'time  n. Time in a particular place.'),
(22971, 'l', '\r\nLocal', 'train  n. Train stopping at all the stations on its route.'),
(22972, 'l', '\r\nLocate', ' v. (-ting) 1 discover the exact place of. 2 establish in a place; situate. 3 state the locality of. [latin: related to *local]'),
(22973, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' in standard english, it is not acceptable to use locate to mean merely â€˜findâ€™ as in can''t locate my key.'),
(22974, 'l', '\r\nLocation', ' n. 1 particular place. 2 locating. 3 natural, not studio, setting for a film etc. (filmed on location).'),
(22975, 'l', '\r\nLoc.', 'Cit.  Abbr. In the passage cited. [latin loco citato]'),
(22976, 'l', '\r\nLoch', ' n. Scot. Lake or narrow inlet of the sea. [gaelic]'),
(22977, 'l', '\r\nLoci', ' pl. Of *locus.'),
(22978, 'l', '\r\nLock1', ' â€”n. 1 mechanism for fastening a door etc., with a bolt that requires a key of a particular shape to work it. 2 confined section of a canal or river within sluice-gates, for moving boats from one level to another. 3 a turning of a vehicle''s front wheels. B (in full full lock) maximum extent of this. 4 interlocked or jammed state. 5 wrestling-hold that keeps an opponent''s limb fixed. 6 (in full lock forward) player in the second row of a rugby scrum. 7 mechanism for exploding the charge of a gun. â€”v. 1 a fasten with a lock. B (foll. By up) shut (a house etc.) Thus. C (of a door etc.) Be lockable. 2 a (foll. By up, in, into) enclose (a person or thing) by locking. B (foll. By up) colloq. Imprison (a person). 3 (often foll. By up, away) store inaccessibly (capital locked up in land). 4 (foll. By in) hold fast (in sleep, an embrace, a struggle, etc.). 5 (usu. In passive) (of land, hills, etc.) Enclose. 6 make or become rigidly fixed. 7 (cause to) jam or catch.  lock on to (of a missile etc.) Automatically find and then track (a target). Lock out 1 keep out by locking the door. 2 (of an employer) subject (employees) to a lockout. Under lock and key locked up.  lockable adj. [old english]'),
(22979, 'l', '\r\nLock2', ' n. 1 portion of hair that hangs together. 2 (in pl.) The hair of the head (golden locks). [old english]'),
(22980, 'l', '\r\nLocker', ' n. (usu. Lockable) cupboard or compartment, esp. For public use.'),
(22981, 'l', '\r\nLocket', ' n. Small ornamental case for a portrait or lock of hair, worn on a chain round the neck. [french diminutive of loc latch, *lock1]'),
(22982, 'l', '\r\nLockjaw', ' n. Form of tetanus in which the jaws become rigidly closed.'),
(22983, 'l', '\r\nLock-keeper', ' n. Person in charge of a river or canal lock.'),
(22984, 'l', '\r\nLockout', ' n. Employer''s exclusion of employees from the workplace until certain terms are agreed to.'),
(22985, 'l', '\r\nLocksmith', ' n. Maker and mender of locks.'),
(22986, 'l', '\r\nLock,', 'stock, and barrel  adv. Completely.'),
(22987, 'l', '\r\nLock-up', ' â€”n. 1 house or room for the temporary detention of prisoners. 2 premises that can be locked up, esp. A small shop. â€”attrib. Adj. That can be locked up (lock-up garage).'),
(22988, 'l', '\r\nLoco1', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Locomotive engine. [abbreviation]'),
(22989, 'l', '\r\nLoco2', ' predic. Adj. Slang crazy. [spanish]'),
(22990, 'l', '\r\nLocomotion', ' n. Motion or the power of motion from place to place. [latin *locus, *motion]'),
(22991, 'l', '\r\nLocomotive', ' â€”n. Engine for pulling trains. â€”adj. Of, having, or effecting locomotion.'),
(22992, 'l', '\r\nLocum', 'tenens  n. (pl. Locum tenentes) (also colloq. Locum) deputy acting esp. For a doctor or clergyman. [latin, = (one) holding a place]'),
(22993, 'l', '\r\nLocus', ' n. (pl. Loci) 1 position or locality. 2 line or curve etc. Formed by all the points satisfying certain conditions, or by the defined motion of a point, line, or surface. [latin, = place]'),
(22994, 'l', '\r\nLocus', 'classicus  n. (pl. Loci classici) best known or most authoritative passage on a subject. [latin: related to *locus]'),
(22995, 'l', '\r\nLocust', ' n. African or asian grasshopper migrating in swarms and consuming all vegetation. [latin locusta locust, *lobster]'),
(22996, 'l', '\r\nLocution', ' n. 1 word, phrase, or idiom. 2 style of speech. [latin loquor locut- speak]'),
(22997, 'l', '\r\nLode', ' n. Vein of metal ore. [var. Of *load]'),
(22998, 'l', '\r\nLodestar', ' n. 1 star used as a guide in navigation, esp. The pole star. 2 a guiding principle. B object of pursuit. [from *lode in obsolete sense â€˜way, journeyâ€™]'),
(22999, 'l', '\r\nLodestone', ' n. (also loadstone) 1 magnetic oxide of iron. 2 a piece of this used as a magnet. B thing that attracts.'),
(23000, 'l', '\r\nLodge', ' â€”n. 1 small house at the entrance to a park or grounds of a large house, occupied by a gatekeeper etc. 2 small house used in the sporting seasons (hunting lodge). 3 porter''s room at the gate of a college, factory, etc. 4 members or meeting-place of a branch of a society such as the freemasons. 5 beaver''s or otter''s lair. â€”v. (-ging) 1 a reside or live, esp. As a lodger. B provide with temporary accommodation. 2 submit or present (a complaint etc.) For attention. 3 become fixed or caught; stick. 4 deposit (money etc.) For security. 5 (foll. By in, with) place (power etc.) In a person. [french loge: related to *leaf]'),
(23001, 'l', '\r\nLodger', ' n. Person paying for accommodation in another''s house.'),
(23002, 'l', '\r\nLodging', ' n. 1 temporary accommodation (a lodging for the night). 2 (in pl.) Room or rooms rented for lodging in.'),
(23003, 'l', '\r\nLoess', ' n. Deposit of fine wind-blown soil, esp. In the basins of large rivers. [swiss german, = loose]'),
(23004, 'l', '\r\nLoft', ' â€”n. 1 attic. 2 room over a stable. 3 gallery in a church or hall. 4 pigeon-house. 5 backward slope on the face of a golf-club. 6 lofting stroke. â€”v. Send (a ball etc.) High up. [old english, = air, upper room]'),
(23005, 'l', '\r\nLofty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of things) of imposing height. 2 haughty, aloof. 3 exalted, noble (lofty ideals).  loftily adv. Loftiness n.'),
(23006, 'l', '\r\nLog1', ' â€”n. 1 unhewn piece of a felled tree; any large rough piece of wood, esp. Cut for firewood. 2 hist. Floating device for gauging a ship''s speed. 3 record of events occurring during the voyage of a ship or aircraft. 4 any systematic record of deeds, experiences, etc. 5 = *logbook. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 a enter (a ship''s speed, or other transport details) in a logbook. B enter (data etc.) In a regular record. 2 attain (a distance, speed, etc., thus recorded) (had logged over 600 miles). 3 cut into logs.  log in = log on. Log on (or off) open (or close) one''s online access to a computer system. Sleep like a log sleep soundly. [origin unknown]'),
(23007, 'l', '\r\nLog2', ' n. Logarithm. [abbreviation]'),
(23008, 'l', '\r\nLogan', ' n. (in full logan-stone) poised heavy stone rocking at a touch. [= (dial.) Logging, = rocking]'),
(23009, 'l', '\r\nLoganberry', ' n. (pl. -ies) dark red fruit, hybrid of a blackberry and a raspberry. [logan, name of a horticulturalist]'),
(23010, 'l', '\r\nLogarithm', ' n. One of a series of arithmetic exponents tabulated to simplify computation by making it possible to use addition and subtraction instead of multiplication and division.  logarithmic adj. Logarithmically adv. [greek logos reckoning, arithmos number]'),
(23011, 'l', '\r\nLogbook', ' n. 1 book containing a detailed record or log. 2 vehicle registration document.'),
(23012, 'l', '\r\nLog', 'cabin  n. Hut built of logs.'),
(23013, 'l', '\r\nLogger', ' n. Us lumberjack.'),
(23014, 'l', '\r\nLoggerhead', ' n.  at loggerheads (often foll. By with) disagreeing or disputing. [probably dial. From logger wooden block]'),
(23015, 'l', '\r\nLoggia', ' n. Open-sided gallery or arcade. [italian, = *lodge]'),
(23016, 'l', '\r\nLogging', ' n. Work of cutting and preparing forest timber.'),
(23017, 'l', '\r\nLogic', ' n. 1 a science of reasoning. B particular system or method of reasoning. 2 a chain of reasoning (regarded as sound or unsound). B use of or ability in argument. 3 inexorable force, compulsion, or consequence (the logic of events). 4 a principles used in designing a computer etc. B circuits using this.  logician n. [related to *-logic]'),
(23018, 'l', '\r\n-logic', ' comb. Form (also -logical) forming adjectives corresponding esp. To nouns in -logy (pathological; zoological). [greek -logikos]'),
(23019, 'l', '\r\nLogical', ' adj. 1 of or according to logic (the logical conclusion). 2 correctly reasoned. 3 defensible or explicable on the ground of consistency. 4 capable of correct reasoning.  logicality n. Logically adv. [greek logos word, reason]'),
(23020, 'l', '\r\n-logist', ' comb. Form forming nouns meaning â€˜person skilled in -logyâ€™ (geologist).'),
(23021, 'l', '\r\nLogistics', ' n.pl. 1 organization of (orig. Military) services and supplies. 2 organization of any complex operation.  logistic adj. Logistical adj. Logistically adv. [french loger lodge]'),
(23022, 'l', '\r\nLog-jam', ' n. Deadlock.'),
(23023, 'l', '\r\nLogo', ' n. (pl. -s) emblem of an organization used in its display material etc. [abbreviation of logotype from greek logos word]'),
(23024, 'l', '\r\n-logy', ' comb. Form forming nouns denoting: 1 a subject of study (biology). 2 speech or discourse or a characteristic of this (trilogy; tautology; phraseology). [greek -logia from logos word]'),
(23025, 'l', '\r\nLoin', ' n. 1 (in pl.) Side and back of the body between the ribs and the hip-bones. 2 joint of meat from this part of an animal. [french loigne from latin lumbus]'),
(23026, 'l', '\r\nLoincloth', ' n. Cloth worn round the hips, esp. As a sole garment.'),
(23027, 'l', '\r\nLoiter', ' v. 1 stand about idly; linger. 2 go slowly with frequent stops.  loiter with intent linger in order to commit a felony.  loiterer n. [dutch]'),
(23028, 'l', '\r\nLoll', ' v. 1 stand, sit, or recline in a lazy attitude. 2 hang loosely. [imitative]'),
(23029, 'l', '\r\nLollipop', ' n. Hard sweet on a stick. [origin uncertain]'),
(23030, 'l', '\r\nLollipop', 'man  n. (also lollipop lady) colloq. Warden using a circular sign on a pole to stop traffic for children to cross the road.'),
(23031, 'l', '\r\nLollop', ' v. (-p-) colloq. 1 flop about. 2 move in ungainly bounds. [probably from *loll, *trollop]'),
(23032, 'l', '\r\nLolly', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 colloq. Lollipop. 2 = *ice lolly. 3 slang money. [abbreviation]'),
(23033, 'l', '\r\nLondoner', ' n. Native or inhabitant of london.'),
(23034, 'l', '\r\nLondon', 'pride  n. Pink-flowered saxifrage.'),
(23035, 'l', '\r\nLone', ' attrib. Adj. 1 solitary; without companions. 2 isolated. 3 unmarried, single (lone parent). [from *alone]'),
(23036, 'l', '\r\nLone', 'hand  n. 1 hand played or player playing against the rest at cards. 2 person or action without allies.'),
(23037, 'l', '\r\nLonely', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 without companions (lonely existence). 2 sad because of this. 3 unfrequented, isolated, uninhabited.  loneliness n.'),
(23038, 'l', '\r\nLonely', 'hearts  n.pl. People seeking friendship or marriage through a newspaper column, club, etc.'),
(23039, 'l', '\r\nLoner', ' n. Person or animal that prefers to be alone.'),
(23040, 'l', '\r\nLonesome', ' adj. Esp. Us 1 lonely. 2 making one feel forlorn (a lonesome song).'),
(23041, 'l', '\r\nLone', 'wolf  n. Loner.'),
(23042, 'l', '\r\nLong1', ' â€”adj. (longer; longest) 1 measuring much from end to end in space or time. 2 (following a measurement) in length or duration (2 metres long; two months long). 3 a consisting of many items (a long list). B seemingly more than the stated amount; tedious (ten long miles). 4 of elongated shape. 5 lasting or reaching far back or forward in time (long friendship). 6 far-reaching; acting at a distance; involving a great interval or difference. 7 (of a vowel or syllable) having the greater of the two recognized durations. 8 (of odds or a chance) reflecting a low level of probability. 9 (of stocks) bought in large quantities in advance, with the expectation of a rise in price. 10 (foll. By on) colloq. Well supplied with. â€”n. Long interval or period (will not take long; won''t be long). â€”adv. (longer; longest) 1 by or for a long time (long before; long ago). 2 (following nouns of duration) throughout a specified time (all day long). 3 (in compar.) After an implied point of time (shall not wait any longer).  as (or so) long as provided that. Before long soon. In the long run (or term) eventually, ultimately. The long and the short of it 1 all that need be said. 2 the eventual outcome. Not by a long shot (or chalk) by no means.  longish adj. [old english]'),
(23043, 'l', '\r\nLong2', ' v. (foll. By for or to + infin.) Have a strong wish or desire for. [old english, = seem *long1 to]'),
(23044, 'l', '\r\nLong.', ' Abbr. Longitude.'),
(23045, 'l', '\r\nLongboat', ' n. Sailing-ship''s largest boat.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(23046, 'l', '\r\nLongbow', ' n. Bow drawn by hand and shooting a long feathered arrow.'),
(23047, 'l', '\r\nLong-distance', ' â€”attrib. Adj.travelling or operating between distant places. â€”adv. Between distant places (phone long-distance).'),
(23048, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'division  n. Division of numbers with details of the calculations written down.'),
(23049, 'l', '\r\nLong-drawn', ' adj. (also long-drawn-out) prolonged.'),
(23050, 'l', '\r\nLongeron', ' n. Longitudinal member of a plane''s fuselage. [french]'),
(23051, 'l', '\r\nLongevity', ' n. Formal long life. [latin longus long, aevum age]'),
(23052, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'face  n. Dismal expression.'),
(23053, 'l', '\r\nLonghand', ' n. Ordinary handwriting.'),
(23054, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'haul  n. 1 transport over a long distance. 2 prolonged effort or task.'),
(23055, 'l', '\r\nLonging', ' â€”n. Intense desire. â€”adj. Having or showing this.  longingly adv.'),
(23056, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'in the tooth  predic. Adj. Colloq. Old.'),
(23057, 'l', '\r\nLongitude', ' n. 1 angular distance east or west from a standard meridian such as greenwich to the meridian of any place. 2 angular distance of a celestial body, esp. Along the ecliptic. [latin longitudo length, from longus long]'),
(23058, 'l', '\r\nLongitudinal', ' adj. 1 of or in length. 2 running lengthwise. 3 of longitude.  longitudinally adv.'),
(23059, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'johns  n.pl. Colloq. Long underpants.'),
(23060, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'jump  n. Athletic contest of jumping as far as possible along the ground in one leap.'),
(23061, 'l', '\r\nLong-life', ' adj. (of milk etc.) Treated to prolong its period of usability.'),
(23062, 'l', '\r\nLong-lived', ' adj. Having a long life; durable.'),
(23063, 'l', '\r\nLong-lost', ' attrib. Adj. That has been lost for a long time.'),
(23064, 'l', '\r\nLong-playing', ' adj. (of a gramophone record) playing for about 20â€“30 minutes on each side.'),
(23065, 'l', '\r\nLong-range', ' adj. 1 having a long range. 2 relating to a period of time far into the future (long-range weather forecast).'),
(23066, 'l', '\r\nLong-running', ' adj. Continuing for a long time (a long-running musical).'),
(23067, 'l', '\r\nLongshore', ' attrib. Adj. 1 existing on or frequenting the shore. 2 directed along the shore. [from along shore]'),
(23068, 'l', '\r\nLongshoreman', ' n. Us docker.'),
(23069, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'shot  n. 1 wild guess or venture. 2 bet at long odds.'),
(23070, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'sight  n. Ability to see clearly only what is comparatively distant.'),
(23071, 'l', '\r\nLong-sighted', ' adj. 1 having long sight. 2 far-sighted.  long-sightedness n.'),
(23072, 'l', '\r\nLong-standing', ' adj. That has long existed.'),
(23073, 'l', '\r\nLong-suffering', ' adj. Bearing provocation patiently.'),
(23074, 'l', '\r\nLong-term', ' adj. Of or for a long period of time (long-term plans).'),
(23075, 'l', '\r\nLong', 'wave  n. Radio wave of frequency less than 300 khz.'),
(23076, 'l', '\r\nLongways', ' adv. (also longwise) = *lengthways.'),
(23077, 'l', '\r\nLong-winded', ' adj. (of a speech or writing) tediously lengthy.'),
(23078, 'l', '\r\nLoo', ' n. Colloq. Lavatory. [origin uncertain]'),
(23079, 'l', '\r\nLoofah', ' n. Rough bath-sponge made from the dried pod of a type of gourd. [arabic]'),
(23080, 'l', '\r\nLook', ' â€”v. 1 a (often foll. By at, down, up, etc.) Use one''s sight; turn one''s eyes in some direction. B turn one''s eyes on; examine (looked me in the eyes; looked us up and down). 2 a make a visual or mental search (i''ll look in the morning). B (foll. By at) consider, examine (must look at the facts). 3 (foll. By for) search for, seek, be on the watch for. 4 inquire (when one looks deeper). 5 have a specified appearance; seem (look a fool; future looks bleak). 6 (foll. By to) a consider; be concerned about (look to the future). B rely on (look to me for support). 7 (foll. By into) investigate. 8 (foll. By what, where, whether, etc.) Ascertain or observe by sight. 9 (of a thing) face some direction. 10 indicate (emotion etc.) By one''s looks. 11 (foll. By that) take care; make sure. 12 (foll. By to + infin.) Aim (am looking to finish it soon). â€”n. 1 act of looking; gaze, glance. 2 (in sing. Or pl.) Appearance of a face; expression. 3 appearance of a thing (by the look of it). 4 style, fashion (this year''s look; the wet look). â€”int. (also look here!) Calling attention, expressing a protest, etc.  look after attend to; take care of. Look one''s age appear as old as one really is. Look back 1 (foll. By on, to) turn one''s thoughts to (something past). 2 (usu. With neg.) Cease to progress (he''s never looked back). Look down on (or look down one''s nose at) regard with contempt or superiority.'),
(23081, 'l', '\r\nLook-alike', ' n. Person or thing closely resembling another.'),
(23082, 'l', '\r\nLooker', ' n. 1 person of a specified appearance (good-looker). 2 colloq. Attractive woman.'),
(23083, 'l', '\r\nLooker-on', ' n. (pl. Lookers-on) spectator.'),
(23084, 'l', '\r\nLook-in', ' n. Colloq. Chance of participation or success (never gets a look-in).'),
(23085, 'l', '\r\nLooking-glass', ' n. Mirror.'),
(23086, 'l', '\r\nLookout', ' n. 1 watch or looking out (on the lookout). 2 a observation-post. B person etc. Stationed to keep watch. 3 prospect (it''s a bad lookout). 4 colloq. Person''s own concern (that''s your lookout).'),
(23087, 'l', '\r\nLoom1', ' n. Apparatus for weaving. [old english]'),
(23088, 'l', '\r\nLoom2', ' v. 1 appear dimly, esp. As a vague and often threatening shape. 2 (of an event) be ominously close. [probably low german or dutch]'),
(23089, 'l', '\r\nLoon', ' n. 1 a kind of diving bird. 2 colloq. Crazy person (cf. *loony). [old norse]'),
(23090, 'l', '\r\nLoony', ' slang â€”n. (pl. -ies) lunatic. â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) crazy.  looniness n. [abbreviation]'),
(23091, 'l', '\r\nLoony-bin', ' n. Slang offens. Mental home or hospital.'),
(23092, 'l', '\r\nLoop', ' â€”n. 1 a figure produced by a curve, or a doubled thread etc., that crosses itself. B thing, path, etc., forming this figure. 2 similarly shaped attachment used as a fastening. 3 ring etc. As a handle etc. 4 contraceptive coil. 5 (in full loop-line) railway or telegraph line that diverges from a main line and joins it again. 6 skating or aerobatic manoeuvre describing a loop. 7 complete circuit for an electric current. 8 endless band of tape or film allowing continuous repetition. 9 sequence of computer operations repeated until some condition is satisfied. â€”v. 1 form or bend into a loop. 2 fasten with a loop or loops. 3 form a loop. 4 (also loop the loop) fly in a circle vertically. [origin unknown]'),
(23093, 'l', '\r\nLoophole', ' n. 1 means of evading a rule etc. Without infringing it. 2 narrow vertical slit in the wall of a fort etc.'),
(23094, 'l', '\r\nLoopy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang crazy, daft.'),
(23095, 'l', '\r\nLoose', ' â€”adj. 1 not tightly held, fixed, etc. (loose handle; loose stones). 2 free from bonds or restraint. 3 not held together (loose papers). 4 not compact or dense (loose soil). 5 inexact (loose translation). 6 morally lax. 7 (of the tongue) indiscreet. 8 tending to diarrhoea. 9 (in comb.) Loosely (loose-fitting). â€”v. (-sing) 1 free; untie or detach; release. 2 relax (loosed my hold). 3 discharge (a missile).  at a loose end unoccupied. On the loose 1 escaped from captivity. 2 enjoying oneself freely.  loosely adv. Looseness n. Loosish adj. [old norse]'),
(23096, 'l', '\r\nLoose', 'cover  n. Removable cover for an armchair etc.'),
(23097, 'l', '\r\nLoose-leaf', ' adj. (of a notebook etc.) With pages that can be removed and replaced.'),
(23098, 'l', '\r\nLoosen', ' v. Make or become loose or looser.  loosen a person''s tongue make a person talk freely. Loosen up 1 relax. 2 limber up.'),
(23099, 'l', '\r\nLoot', ' â€”n. 1 spoil, booty. 2 slang money. â€”v. 1 rob or steal, esp. After rioting etc. 2 plunder.  looter n. [hindi]'),
(23100, 'l', '\r\nLop', ' v. (-pp-) 1 a (often foll. By off, away) cut or remove (a part or parts) from a whole, esp. Branches from a tree. B remove branches from (a tree). 2 (often foll. By off) remove (items) as superfluous. [old english]'),
(23101, 'l', '\r\nLope', ' â€”v. (-ping) run with a long bounding stride. â€”n. Long bounding stride. [old norse: related to *leap]'),
(23102, 'l', '\r\nLop-eared', ' adj. Having drooping ears. [related to *lob]'),
(23103, 'l', '\r\nLopsided', ' adj. Unevenly balanced.  lopsidedness n. [related to *lob]'),
(23104, 'l', '\r\nLoquacious', ' adj. Talkative.  loquacity n. [latin loquor speak]'),
(23105, 'l', '\r\nLoquat', ' n. 1 small yellow egg-shaped fruit. 2 tree bearing it. [chinese]'),
(23106, 'l', '\r\nLord', ' â€”n. 1 master or ruler. 2 hist. Feudal superior, esp. Of a manor. 3 peer of the realm or person with the title lord. 4 (lord) (often prec. By the) god or christ. 5 (lord) a prefixed as the designation of a marquis, earl, viscount, or baron, or (to the christian name) of the younger son of a duke or marquis. B (the lords) = *house of lords. â€”int. (lord, good lord, etc.) Expressing surprise, dismay, etc.  lord it over domineer. [old english, = bread-keeper: related to *loaf1, *ward]'),
(23107, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'chamberlain  n. Official in charge of the royal household.'),
(23108, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'chancellor  n. (also lord high chancellor) highest officer of the crown, presiding in the house of lords etc.'),
(23109, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'chief justice  n. President of the queen''s bench division.'),
(23110, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'lieutenant  n. 1 chief executive authority and head of magistrates in each county. 2 hist. Viceroy of ireland.'),
(23111, 'l', '\r\nLordly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 haughty, imperious. 2 suitable for a lord.  lordliness n.'),
(23112, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'mayor  n. Title of the mayor in some large cities.'),
(23113, 'l', '\r\nLord', 'privy seal  n. Senior cabinet minister without official duties.'),
(23114, 'l', '\r\nLords', 'and ladies  n. Wild arum.'),
(23115, 'l', '\r\nLord''s', 'day  n. Sunday.'),
(23116, 'l', '\r\nLordship', ' n. 1 (usu. Lordship) title used in addressing or referring to a man with the rank of lord (your lordship; his lordship). 2 (foll. By over) dominion, rule.'),
(23117, 'l', '\r\nLord''s', 'prayer  n. The our father.'),
(23118, 'l', '\r\nLords', 'spiritual  n.pl. Bishops in the house of lords.'),
(23119, 'l', '\r\nLord''s', 'supper  n. Eucharist.'),
(23120, 'l', '\r\nLords', 'temporal  n.pl. Members of the house of lords other than bishops.'),
(23121, 'l', '\r\nLore', ' n. Body of traditions and knowledge on a subject or held by a particular group (bird lore; gypsy lore). [old english: related to *learn]'),
(23122, 'l', '\r\nLorgnette', ' n. Pair of eyeglasses or opera-glasses on a long handle. [french lorgner to squint]'),
(23123, 'l', '\r\nLorn', ' adj. Archaic desolate, forlorn. [old english, past part. Of *lose]'),
(23124, 'l', '\r\nLorry', ' n. (pl. -ies) large vehicle for transporting goods etc. [origin uncertain]'),
(23125, 'l', '\r\nLose', ' v. (-sing; past and past part. Lost) 1 be deprived of or cease to have, esp. By negligence. 2 be deprived of (a person) by death. 3 become unable to find, follow, or understand (lose one''s way). 4 let or have pass from one''s control or reach (lost my chance; lost his composure). 5 be defeated in (a game, lawsuit, battle, etc.). 6 get rid of (lost our pursuers; lose weight). 7 forfeit (a right to a thing). 8 spend (time, efforts, etc.) To no purpose.'),
(23126, 'l', '\r\nLoser', ' n. 1 person or thing that loses, esp. A contest (is a bad loser). 2 colloq. Person who regularly fails.'),
(23127, 'l', '\r\nLoss', ' n. 1 losing or being lost. 2 thing or amount lost. 3 detriment resulting from losing.  at a loss (sold etc.) For less than was paid for it. Be at a loss be puzzled or uncertain. [probably back-formation from *lost]'),
(23128, 'l', '\r\nLoss-leader', ' n. Item sold at a loss to attract customers.'),
(23129, 'l', '\r\nLost', ' past and past part. Of *lose.'),
(23130, 'l', '\r\nLost', 'cause  n. Hopeless undertaking.'),
(23131, 'l', '\r\nLot', ' n. 1 colloq. (prec. By a or in pl.) A a large number or amount (a lot of people; lots of milk). B colloq. Much (a lot warmer; smiles a lot). 2 a each of a set of objects used to make a chance selection. B this method of deciding (chosen by lot). 3 share or responsibility resulting from it. 4 person''s destiny, fortune, or condition. 5 (esp. Us) plot; allotment of land (parking lot). 6 article or set of articles for sale at an auction etc. 7 group of associated persons or things.  cast (or draw) lots decide by lots. Throw in one''s lot with decide to share the fortunes of. The (or the whole) lot the total number or quantity. A whole lot colloq. Very much (is a whole lot better). [old english]'),
(23132, 'l', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1a, a lot of is somewhat informal, but acceptable in serious writing, whereas lots of is not acceptable.'),
(23133, 'l', '\r\nLoth', ' var. Of *loath.'),
(23134, 'l', '\r\nLothario', ' n. (pl. -s) libertine. [name of a character in a play]'),
(23135, 'l', '\r\nLotion', ' n. Medicinal or cosmetic liquid preparation applied externally. [latin lavo lot- wash]'),
(23136, 'l', '\r\nLottery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 means of raising money by selling numbered tickets and giving prizes to the holders of numbers drawn at random. 2 thing whose success is governed by chance. [dutch: related to *lot]'),
(23137, 'l', '\r\nLotto', ' n. Game of chance like bingo, but with numbers drawn by players instead of called. [italian]'),
(23138, 'l', '\r\nLotus', ' n. 1 legendary plant inducing luxurious languor when eaten. 2 a kind of water lily etc., esp. Used symbolically in hinduism and buddhism. [greek lotos]'),
(23139, 'l', '\r\nLotus-eater', ' n. Person given to indolent enjoyment.'),
(23140, 'l', '\r\nLotus', 'position  n. Cross-legged position of meditation with the feet resting on the thighs.'),
(23141, 'l', '\r\nLoud', ' â€”adj. 1 strongly audible, noisy. 2 (of colours etc.) Gaudy, obtrusive. â€”adv. Loudly.  out loud aloud.  loudish adj. Loudly adv. Loudness n. [old english]'),
(23142, 'l', '\r\nLoud', 'hailer  n. Electronic device for amplifying the voice.'),
(23143, 'l', '\r\nLoudspeaker', ' n. Apparatus that converts electrical signals into sound.'),
(23144, 'l', '\r\nLough', ' n. Ir. Lake, arm of the sea. [irish: related to *loch]'),
(23145, 'l', '\r\nLounge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 recline comfortably; loll. 2 stand or move about idly. â€”n. 1 place for lounging, esp.: a a sitting-room in a house. B a public room (e.g. In a hotel). C a place in an airport etc. With seats for waiting passengers. 2 spell of lounging. [origin uncertain]'),
(23146, 'l', '\r\nLounge', 'bar  n. More comfortable bar in a pub etc.'),
(23147, 'l', '\r\nLounge', 'suit  n. Man''s suit for ordinary day (esp. Business) wear.'),
(23148, 'l', '\r\nLour', ' v. (also lower) 1 frown; look sullen. 2 (of the sky etc.) Look dark and threatening. [origin unknown]'),
(23149, 'l', '\r\nLouse', ' â€”n. 1 (pl. Lice) parasitic insect. 2 (pl. Louses) slang contemptible person. â€”v. (-sing) delouse.  louse up slang make a mess of. [old english]'),
(23150, 'l', '\r\nLousy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 colloq. Very bad; disgusting; ill (feel lousy). 2 (often foll. By with) colloq. Well supplied, teeming. 3 infested with lice.  lousily adv. Lousiness n.'),
(23151, 'l', '\r\nLout', ' n. Rough-mannered person.  loutish adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(23152, 'l', '\r\nLouvre', ' n. (also louver) 1 each of a set of overlapping slats designed to admit air and some light and exclude rain. 2 domed structure on a roof with side openings for ventilation etc.  louvred adj. [french lover skylight]'),
(23153, 'l', '\r\nLovable', ' adj. (also loveable) inspiring love or affection.'),
(23154, 'l', '\r\nLovage', ' n. Herb used for flavouring etc. [french levesche from latin ligusticum ligurian]'),
(23155, 'l', '\r\nLovat', ' n. & adj. Muted green. [lovat in scotland]'),
(23156, 'l', '\r\nLove', ' â€”n. 1 deep affection or fondness. 2 sexual passion. 3 sexual relations. 4 a beloved one; sweetheart (often as a form of address). B colloq. Form of address regardless of affection. 5 colloq. Person of whom one is fond. 6 affectionate greetings (give him my love). 7 (in games) no score; nil. â€”v. (-ving) 1 feel love or a deep fondness for. 2 delight in; admire; greatly cherish. 3 colloq. Like very much (loves books). 4 (foll. By verbal noun, or to + infin.) Be inclined, esp. As a habit; greatly enjoy (children love dressing up; loves to run).  fall in love (often foll. By with) suddenly begin to love. For love for pleasure not profit. For the love of for the sake of. In love (often foll. By with) enamoured (of). Make love (often foll. By to) 1 have sexual intercourse (with). 2 archaic pay amorous attention (to). Not for love or money colloq. Not in any circumstances. [old english]'),
(23157, 'l', '\r\nLoveable', ' var. Of *lovable.'),
(23158, 'l', '\r\nLove', 'affair  n. Romantic or sexual relationship between two people.'),
(23159, 'l', '\r\nLove-bird', ' n. Parrot, esp. One seeming to show great affection for its mate.'),
(23160, 'l', '\r\nLove', 'bite  n. Bruise made by a partner''s biting etc. During lovemaking.'),
(23161, 'l', '\r\nLove-child', ' n. Child of unmarried parents.'),
(23162, 'l', '\r\nLove-hate', 'relationship  n. Intense relationship involving ambivalent emotions.'),
(23163, 'l', '\r\nLove-in-a-mist', ' n. Blue-flowered cultivated plant.'),
(23164, 'l', '\r\nLoveless', ' adj. Unloving or unloved or both.'),
(23165, 'l', '\r\nLove-lies-bleeding', ' n. Cultivated plant with drooping spikes of purple-red blooms.'),
(23166, 'l', '\r\nLovelorn', ' adj. Pining from unrequited love.'),
(23167, 'l', '\r\nLovely', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 colloq. Pleasing, delightful. 2 beautiful. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Pretty woman.  lovely and colloq. Delightfully (lovely and warm).  loveliness n. [old english]'),
(23168, 'l', '\r\nLovemaking', ' n. 1 sexual play, esp. Intercourse. 2 archaic courtship.'),
(23169, 'l', '\r\nLove-nest', ' n. Colloq. Secluded retreat for (esp. Illicit) lovers.'),
(23170, 'l', '\r\nLover', ' n. 1 person in love with another. 2 person with whom another is having sexual relations. 3 (in pl.) Unmarried couple in love or having sexual relations. 4 person who likes or enjoys a specified thing (music lover).'),
(23171, 'l', '\r\nLove-seat', ' n. Small sofa in the shape of an s, with two seats facing in opposite directions.'),
(23172, 'l', '\r\nLovesick', ' adj. Languishing with love.'),
(23173, 'l', '\r\nLovey-dovey', ' adj. Colloq. Fondly affectionate, sentimental.'),
(23174, 'l', '\r\nLoving', ' â€”adj. Feeling or showing love; affectionate. â€”n. Affection; love.  lovingly adv.'),
(23175, 'l', '\r\nLoving-cup', ' n. Two-handled drinking-cup.'),
(23176, 'l', '\r\nLow1', ' â€”adj. 1 not high or tall (low wall). 2 a not elevated in position (low altitude). B (of the sun) near the horizon. 3 of or in humble rank or position (of low birth).'),
(23177, 'l', '\r\nLow2', ' â€”n. Sound made by cattle; moo. â€”v. Make this sound. [old english]'),
(23178, 'l', '\r\nLow-born', ' adj. Of humble birth.'),
(23179, 'l', '\r\nLowbrow', ' â€”adj. Not intellectual or cultured. â€”n. Lowbrow person.'),
(23180, 'l', '\r\nLow', 'church  n. Section of the church of england attaching little importance to ritual, priestly authority, and the sacraments.'),
(23181, 'l', '\r\nLow-class', ' adj. Of low quality or social class.'),
(23182, 'l', '\r\nLow', 'comedy  n. Comedy bordering on farce.'),
(23183, 'l', '\r\nLow', 'countries  n.pl. The netherlands, belgium, and luxembourg.'),
(23184, 'l', '\r\nLow-down', ' â€”adj. Mean, dishonourable. â€”n. Colloq. (prec. By the; usu. Foll. By on) relevant information.'),
(23185, 'l', '\r\nLower1', ' â€”adj. (compar. Of *low1). 1 less high in position or status. 2 situated below another part (lower lip). 3 a situated on less high land (lower egypt). B situated to the south (lower california). 4 (of a mammal, plant, etc.) Evolved to only a slight degree. â€”adv. In or to a lower position, status, etc.  lowermost adj.'),
(23186, 'l', '\r\nLower2', ' v. 1 let or haul down. 2 make or become lower. 3 degrade.'),
(23187, 'l', '\r\nLower3', ' var. Of *lour.'),
(23188, 'l', '\r\nLower', 'case  n. Small letters.'),
(23189, 'l', '\r\nLower', 'class  n. Working class.'),
(23190, 'l', '\r\nLower', 'house  n. Larger and usu. Elected body in a legislature, esp. The house of commons.'),
(23191, 'l', '\r\nLowest', ' adj. (superl. Of *low1) least high in position or status.'),
(23192, 'l', '\r\nLowest', 'common denominator  n. 1 math. Lowest common multiple of the denominators of several fractions. 2 the worst or most vulgar common feature of members of a group.'),
(23193, 'l', '\r\nLowest', 'common multiple  n. Math. Least quantity that is a multiple of two or more given quantities.'),
(23194, 'l', '\r\nLow', 'frequency  n. Frequency, esp. In radio, 30 to 300 kilohertz.'),
(23195, 'l', '\r\nLow', 'gear  n. Gear such that the driven end of a transmission revolves slower than the driving end.'),
(23196, 'l', '\r\nLow-grade', ' adj. Of low quality.'),
(23197, 'l', '\r\nLow-key', ' adj. Lacking intensity, restrained.'),
(23198, 'l', '\r\nLowland', ' â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Low-lying country. â€”adj. Of or in lowland.  lowlander n.'),
(23199, 'l', '\r\nLow-level', ' adj. (of a computer language) close in form to machine code.'),
