(23391, 'm', '\r\nMadden', ' v. 1 make or become mad. 2 irritate.  maddening adj. Maddeningly adv.'),
(23392, 'm', '\r\nMadder', ' n. 1 herbaceous plant with yellowish flowers. 2  a red dye from its root. B its synthetic substitute. [old english]'),
(23393, 'm', '\r\nMade', ' past and past part. Of *make. â€”adj. 1 built or formed (well-made). 2 successful (self-made man; be made).  have (or have got) it made colloq. Be sure of success. Made for ideally suited to. Made of consisting of. Made of money colloq. Very rich.'),
(23394, 'm', '\r\nMadeira', ' n. 1 fortified white wine from madeira. 2 (in full madeira cake) a kind of sponge cake.'),
(23395, 'm', '\r\nMademoiselle', ' n. (pl. Mesdemoiselles) 1 miss or madam (used of or to an unmarried french-speaking woman). 2 (mademoiselle) a young frenchwoman. B french governess. [french ma my, demoiselle *damsel]'),
(23396, 'm', '\r\nMade', 'to measure  adj. Tailor-made.'),
(23397, 'm', '\r\nMadhouse', ' n. 1 colloq. Scene of confused uproar. 2 archaic mental home or hospital.'),
(23398, 'm', '\r\nMadly', ' adv. 1 in a mad manner. 2 colloq. A passionately. B extremely.'),
(23399, 'm', '\r\nMadman', ' n. Man who is mad.'),
(23400, 'm', '\r\nMadonna', ' n.  1 (prec. By the) the virgin mary. 2 (madonna) picture or statue of her. [italian, = my lady]'),
