(23504, 'm', '\r\nMajor', ' â€”adj. 1 relatively great in size, intensity, scope, or importance. 2 (of surgery) serious. 3 mus. A (of a scale) having intervals of a semitone above its third and seventh notes. B (of an interval) greater by a semitone than a minor interval (major third). C (of a key) based on a major scale. 4 of full legal age. â€”n. 1  a army officer next below lieutenant-colonel. B officer in charge of a band section (drum major). 2 person of full legal age. 3 us a student''s main subject or course. B student of this. â€”v. (foll. By in) us study or qualify in (a subject) as one''s main subject. [latin, comparative of magnus great]'),
(23505, 'm', '\r\nMajor-domo', ' n. (pl. -s) chief steward of a great household. [medieval latin major domus highest official of the household]'),
(23506, 'm', '\r\nMajorette', ' n. = *drum majorette. [abbreviation]'),
