(23876, 'm', '\r\nMater', ' n. Slang mother. [latin]'),
(23877, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' mater is now only found in jocular or affected use.'),
(23878, 'm', '\r\nMaterial', ' â€”n. 1 matter from which a thing is made. 2 cloth, fabric. 3 (in pl.) Things needed for an activity (building materials). 4 person or thing of a specified kind or suitable for a purpose (officer material). 5 (in sing. Or pl.) Information etc. For a book etc. 6 (in sing. Or pl., often foll. By of) elements, constituent parts, or substance. â€”adj. 1 of matter; corporeal; not spiritual. 2 of bodily comfort etc. (material well-being). 3 (often foll. By to) important, significant, relevant. [latin materia *matter]'),
(23879, 'm', '\r\nMaterialism', ' n. 1 greater interest in material possessions and comfort than in spiritual values. 2 philos. Theory that nothing exists but matter.  materialist n. Materialistic adj. Materialistically adv.'),
(23880, 'm', '\r\nMaterialize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 become actual fact; happen. 2 colloq. Appear or be present. 3 represent in or assume bodily form.  materialization n.'),
