(23944, 'm', '\r\nMay', ' n. 1 fifth month of the year. 2 (may) hawthorn, esp. In blossom. [latin maius of the goddess maia]'),
(23945, 'm', '\r\nMay', ' v.aux. (3rd sing. Present may; past might) 1 expressing: a (often foll. By well for emphasis) possibility (it may be true; you may well lose your way). B permission (may i come in?). C a wish (may he live to regret it). D uncertainty or irony (who may you be?; who are you, may i ask?). 2 in purpose clauses and after wish, fear, etc. (hope he may succeed).  be that as it may (or that is as may be) it is possible (but) (be that as it may, i still want to go). May as well = might as well (see *might1). [old english]'),
(23946, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1b, both can and may are used to express permission; in more formal contexts may is preferred since can also denotes capability (can i move? = am i physically able to move?; may i move? = am i allowed to move?).'),
(23947, 'm', '\r\nMaya', ' n. 1 (pl. Same or -s) member of an ancient indian people of central america. 2 their language.  mayan adj. & n. [native name]'),
