(23988, 'm', '\r\nMean1', ' v. (past and past part. Meant) 1 have as one''s purpose or intention (meant no harm by it; i didn''t mean to break it). 2 design or destine for a purpose (meant to be used). 3 intend to convey or refer to (i mean richmond in surrey). 4 (often foll. By that) entail, involve, portend, signify (this means war; means that he is dead). 5 (of a word) have as its equivalent in the same or another language. 6 (foll. By to) be of specified importance to (that means a lot to me).  mean business colloq. Be in earnest. Mean it not be joking or exaggerating. Mean well have good intentions. [old english]'),
(23989, 'm', '\r\nMean2', ' adj. 1 niggardly; not generous. 2 ignoble, small-minded. 3 (of capacity, understanding, etc.) Inferior, poor. 4 shabby; inadequate (mean hovel). 5 a malicious, ill-tempered. B us vicious or aggressive in behaviour. 6 us colloq. Skilful, formidable (a mean fighter).  no mean a very good (no mean feat).  meanly adv. Meanness n. [old english]'),
(23990, 'm', '\r\nMean3', ' â€”n. 1 median point (mean between modesty and pride). 2  a term midway between the first and last terms of an arithmetical etc. Progression. B quotient of the sum of several quantities and their number; average. â€”adj. 1 (of a quantity) equally far from two extremes. 2 calculated as a mean. [latin medianus *median]'),
(23991, 'm', '\r\nMean2', ' adj. 1 niggardly; not generous. 2 ignoble, small-minded. 3 (of capacity, understanding, etc.) Inferior, poor. 4 shabby; inadequate (mean hovel). 5 a malicious, ill-tempered. B us vicious or aggressive in behaviour. 6 us colloq. Skilful, formidable (a mean fighter).  no mean a very good (no mean feat).  meanly adv. Meanness n. [old english]'),
(23992, 'm', '\r\nMean3', ' â€”n. 1 median point (mean between modesty and pride). 2  a term midway between the first and last terms of an arithmetical etc. Progression. B quotient of the sum of several quantities and their number; average. â€”adj. 1 (of a quantity) equally far from two extremes. 2 calculated as a mean. [latin medianus *median]'),
(23993, 'm', '\r\nMeander', ' â€”v. 1 wander at random. 2 (of a stream) wind about. â€”n. 1 (in pl.) Sinuous windings of a river, path, etc. 2 circuitous journey. [greek maiandros, a winding river in ancient phrygia]'),
(23994, 'm', '\r\nMeanie', ' n. (also meany) (pl. -ies) colloq. Niggardly or small-minded person.'),
