(23997, 'm', '\r\nMeaningless', ' adj. Having no meaning or significance.  meaninglessly adv. Meaninglessness n.'),
(23998, 'm', '\r\nMeans', ' n.pl. 1 (often treated as sing.) Action, agent, device, or method producing a result (means of quick travel). 2 a money resources (live beyond one''s means). B wealth (man of means).  by all means certainly. By means of by the agency etc. Of. By no means certainly not. [from *mean3]'),
(23999, 'm', '\r\nMean', 'sea level  n. Level halfway between high and low water.'),
(24000, 'm', '\r\nMeans', 'test  â€”n. Inquiry into income as a basis for eligibility for state benefit etc. â€”v. (means-test) subject to or base on a means test.'),
(24001, 'm', '\r\nMeant', ' past and past part. Of *mean1.'),
(24002, 'm', '\r\nMeantime', ' â€”adv. = *meanwhile. â€”n. Intervening period (esp. In the meantime).'),
(24003, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' as an adverb, meantime is less common than meanwhile.'),
(24004, 'm', '\r\nMeanwhile', ' â€”adv. 1 in the intervening period of time. 2 at the same time. â€”n. Intervening period (esp. In the meanwhile).'),
(24005, 'm', '\r\nMeany', ' var. Of *meanie.'),
(24006, 'm', '\r\nMeasles', ' n.pl. (also treated as sing.) Infectious viral disease marked by a red rash. [low german masele or dutch masel]'),
(24007, 'm', '\r\nMeasly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Meagre, contemptible.'),
(24008, 'm', '\r\nMeasure', ' â€”n. 1 size or quantity found by measuring. 2 system or unit of measuring (liquid measure; 20 measures of wheat). 3 rod, tape, vessel, etc. For measuring. 4 (often foll. By of) degree, extent, or amount (a measure of wit). 5 factor determining evaluation etc. (sales are the measure of popularity). 6 (usu. In pl.) Suitable action to achieve some end. 7 legislative bill, act, etc. 8 prescribed extent or quantity. 9 poetic metre. 10 mineral stratum (coal measures). â€”v. (-ring) 1 ascertain the extent or quantity of (a thing) by comparison with a known standard. 2 be of a specified size. 3 ascertain the size of (a person) for clothes. 4 estimate (a quality etc.) By some criterion. 5 (often foll. By off) mark (a line etc. Of a given length). 6 (foll. By out) distribute in measured quantities. 7 (foll. By with, against) bring (oneself or one''s strength etc.) Into competition with.  beyond measure excessively. For good measure as a finishing touch. In some measure partly. Measure up 1 take the measurements (of). 2 (often foll.'),
(24009, 'm', '\r\nMeasured', ' adj. 1 rhythmical; regular (measured tread). 2 (of language) carefully considered.'),
(24010, 'm', '\r\nMeasureless', ' adj. Not measurable; infinite.'),
(24011, 'm', '\r\nMeasurement', ' n. 1 measuring. 2 amount measured. 3 (in pl.) Detailed dimensions.'),
(24012, 'm', '\r\nMeat', ' n. 1 animal flesh as food. 2 (often foll. By of) substance; chief part.  meatless adj. [old english]'),
(24013, 'm', '\r\nMeatball', ' n. Small round ball of minced meat.'),
(24014, 'm', '\r\nMeat', 'loaf  n. Minced meat etc. Moulded and baked.'),
(24015, 'm', '\r\nMeat', 'safe  n. Ventilated cupboard for storing meat.'),
(24016, 'm', '\r\nMeaty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of meat; fleshy. 2 of or like meat. 3 substantial, full of interest, satisfying.  meatiness n.'),
(24017, 'm', '\r\nMecca', ' n. Place one aspires to visit. [mecca, muslim holy city in arabia]'),
(24018, 'm', '\r\nMechanic', ' n. Person skilled in using or repairing machinery. [latin: related to *machine]'),
(24019, 'm', '\r\nMechanical', ' adj. 1 of machines or mechanisms. 2 working or produced by machinery. 3 (of an action etc.) Automatic; repetitive. 4 (of an agency, principle, etc.) Belonging to mechanics. 5 of mechanics as a science.  mechanically adv. [latin: related to *mechanic]'),
(24020, 'm', '\r\nMechanical', 'engineer  n. Person qualified in the design, construction, etc. Of machines.'),
(24021, 'm', '\r\nMechanics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) 1 branch of applied mathematics dealing with motion etc. 2 science of machinery. 3 routine technical aspects of a thing (mechanics of local government).'),
(24022, 'm', '\r\nMechanism', ' n. 1 structure or parts of a machine. 2 system of parts working together. 3 process; method (defence mechanism; no mechanism for complaints).  mechanistic adj. [greek: related to *machine]'),
(24023, 'm', '\r\nMechanize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 introduce machines in (a factory etc.). 2 make mechanical. 3 equip with tanks, armoured cars, etc.  mechanization n.'),
(24024, 'm', '\r\nMed', ' n. Colloq. Mediterranean sea. [abbreviation]'),
(24025, 'm', '\r\nMedal', ' n. Commemorative metal disc etc., esp. Awarded for military or sporting prowess. [latin: related to *metal]'),
(24026, 'm', '\r\nMedalist', ' n. (brit. Medallist) winner of a (specified) medal (gold medallist).'),
(24027, 'm', '\r\nMedallion', ' n. 1 large medal. 2 thing so shaped, e.g. A decorative panel etc. [italian: related to *medal]'),
(24028, 'm', '\r\nMedallist', ' n. (us medalist) winner of a (specified) medal (gold medallist).'),
(24029, 'm', '\r\nMeddle', ' v. (-ling) (often foll. By with, in) interfere in others'' concerns.  meddler n. [latin: related to *mix]'),
(24030, 'm', '\r\nMeddlesome', ' adj. Interfering.'),
(24031, 'm', '\r\nMedia', ' n.pl. 1 pl. Of *medium. 2 (usu. Prec. By the) mass communications (esp. Newspapers and broadcasting) regarded collectively.'),
(24032, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' media is commonly used with a singular verb (e.g. The media is biased), but this is not generally accepted (cf. *data).'),
(24033, 'm', '\r\nMediaeval', ' var. Of *medieval.'),
(24034, 'm', '\r\nMedial', ' adj. = *median.  medially adv. [latin medius middle]'),
(24035, 'm', '\r\nMedian', ' â€”adj. Situated in the middle. â€”n. 1 straight line drawn from any vertex of a triangle to the middle of the opposite side. 2 middle value of a series. [latin: related to *medial]'),
(24036, 'm', '\r\nMediate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By between) intervene (between disputants) to settle a quarrel etc. 2 bring about (a result) thus.  mediation n. Mediator n. [latin medius middle]'),
(24037, 'm', '\r\nMedic', ' n. Colloq. Medical practitioner or student. [latin medicus physician]'),
(24038, 'm', '\r\nMedical', ' â€”adj. Of medicine in general or as distinct from surgery (medical ward). â€”n. Colloq. Medical examination.  medically adv.'),
(24039, 'm', '\r\nMedical', 'certificate  n. Certificate of fitness or unfitness for work etc.'),
(24040, 'm', '\r\nMedical', 'examination  n. Examination to determine a person''s physical fitness.'),
(24041, 'm', '\r\nMedical', 'officer  n. Person in charge of the health services of a local authority etc.'),
(24042, 'm', '\r\nMedical', 'practitioner  n. Physician or surgeon.'),
(24043, 'm', '\r\nMedicament', ' n. = *medicine 2.'),
(24044, 'm', '\r\nMedicare', ' n. Us federally funded health insurance scheme for the elderly. [from *medical, *care]'),
(24045, 'm', '\r\nMedicate', ' v. (-ting) 1 treat medically. 2 impregnate with medicine etc.  medicative adj. [latin medicare medicat-]'),
(24046, 'm', '\r\nMedication', ' n. 1 = *medicine 2. 2 treatment using drugs.'),
(24047, 'm', '\r\nMedicinal', ' adj. (of a substance) healing.  medicinally adv.'),
(24048, 'm', '\r\nMedicine', ' n. 1 science or practice of the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of disease, esp. As distinct from surgery. 2 drug etc. For the treatment or prevention of disease, esp. Taken by mouth.  take one''s medicine submit to something disagreeable. [latin medicina]'),
(24049, 'm', '\r\nMedicine', 'man  n. Tribal, esp. N. American indian, witch-doctor.'),
(24050, 'm', '\r\nMedieval', ' adj. (also mediaeval) 1 of the middle ages. 2 colloq. Old-fashioned. [latin medium aevum middle age]'),
(24051, 'm', '\r\nMedieval', 'history  n. History of the 5thâ€“15th c.'),
(24052, 'm', '\r\nMedieval', 'latin  n. Latin of about ad 600â€“1500.'),
(24053, 'm', '\r\nMediocre', ' adj. 1 indifferent in quality. 2 second-rate. [latin mediocris]'),
(24054, 'm', '\r\nMediocrity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being mediocre. 2 mediocre person.'),
(24055, 'm', '\r\nMeditate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By on, upon) engage in (esp. Religious) contemplation. 2 plan mentally.  meditation n. Meditator n. [latin meditor]'),
(24056, 'm', '\r\nMeditative', ' adj. 1 inclined to meditate. 2 indicative of meditation, thoughtful.  meditatively adv. Meditativeness n.'),
(24057, 'm', '\r\nMediterranean', ' adj.  Of the sea bordered by s. Europe, sw asia, and n. Africa, or its surrounding region (mediterranean cookery). [latin mediterraneus inland]'),
(24058, 'm', '\r\nMedium', ' â€”n. (pl. Media or -s) 1 middle quality, degree, etc. Between extremes (find a happy medium). 2 means of communication (medium of television). 3 substance, e.g. Air, through which sense-impressions are conveyed. 4 physical environment etc. Of a living organism. 5 means. 6 material or form used by an artist, composer, etc. 7 liquid (e.g. Oil or gel) used for diluting paints. 8 (pl. -s) person claiming to communicate with the dead. â€”adj. 1 between two qualities, degrees, etc. 2 average (of medium height). [latin medius middle]'),
(24059, 'm', '\r\nMedium-range', ' adj. (of an aircraft, missile, etc.) Able to travel a medium distance.'),
(24060, 'm', '\r\nMedium', 'wave  n. Radio wave of frequency between 300 khz and 3 mhz.'),
(24061, 'm', '\r\nMedlar', ' n. 1 tree bearing small brown apple-like fruits, eaten when decayed. 2 such a fruit. [french medler from greek mespile]'),
(24062, 'm', '\r\nMedley', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 varied mixture. 2 collection of tunes etc. Played as one piece. [french medlee]'),
(24063, 'm', '\r\nMedulla', ' n. 1 inner part of certain organs etc., e.g. The kidney. 2 soft internal tissue of plants.  medullary adj. [latin]'),
(24064, 'm', '\r\nMedulla', 'oblongata  n. Lowest part of the brainstem, formed from a continuation of the spinal cord.'),
(24065, 'm', '\r\nMedusa', ' n. (pl. Medusae or -s) jellyfish. [greek medousa, name of a gorgon]'),
(24066, 'm', '\r\nMeek', ' adj. Humble and submissive or gentle.  meekly adv. Meekness n. [old norse]'),
(24067, 'm', '\r\nMeerkat', ' n. S. African mongoose. [dutch, = sea-cat]'),
(24068, 'm', '\r\nMeerschaum', ' n. 1 soft white clay-like substance. 2 tobacco-pipe with its bowl made from this. [german, = sea-foam]'),
(24069, 'm', '\r\nMeet1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Met) 1 encounter (a person etc.) Or (of two or more people) come together by accident or design; come face to face (with) (met on the bridge). 2 be present by design at the arrival of (a person, train, etc.). 3 come or seem to come together or into contact (with); join (where the sea and the sky meet; jacket won''t meet). 4 make the acquaintance of (delighted to meet you; all met at oxford). 5 come together for business, worship, etc. (union met management). 6 a deal with or answer (a demand, objection, etc.) (met the proposal with hostility). B satisfy or conform with (agreed to meet the new terms). 7 pay (a bill etc.); honour (a cheque) (meet the cost). 8 (often foll. By with) experience, encounter, or receive (met their death; met with hostility). 9 confront in battle etc. â€”n. 1 assembly for a hunt. 2 assembly for sport, esp. Athletics.  make ends meet see *end. Meet the case be adequate. Meet the eye be visible or evident.  Meet a person half way compromise with. Meet up colloq. (often foll. By with) = sense 1 of v. Meet with 1 see sense 8 of v. 2 receive (a reaction) (met with her approval). 3 esp. Us = sense 1 of v. [old english]'),
(24070, 'm', '\r\nMeet2', ' adj. Archaic fitting, proper. [related to *mete]'),
(24071, 'm', '\r\nMeeting', ' n. 1 coming together. 2 assembly of esp. A society, committee, etc. 3 = *race meeting.'),
(24072, 'm', '\r\nMega', ' slang â€”adj. 1 excellent. 2 enormous. â€”adv. Extremely.'),
(24073, 'm', '\r\nMega-', ' comb. Form 1 large. 2 one million (106) in the metric system of measurement. 3 slang extremely; very big (mega-stupid; mega-project). [greek megas great]'),
(24074, 'm', '\r\nMegabuck', ' n. Us slang million dollars.'),
(24075, 'm', '\r\nMegabyte', ' n. Computing 1,048,576 (i.e. 220) bytes as a measure of data capacity, or loosely 1,000,000.'),
(24076, 'm', '\r\nMegadeath', ' n. Death of one million people (in war).'),
(24077, 'm', '\r\nMegahertz', ' n. (pl. Same) one million hertz, esp. As a measure of radio frequency.'),
(24078, 'm', '\r\nMegalith', ' n. Large stone, esp. As a prehistoric monument or part of one.  megalithic adj. [greek lithos stone]'),
(24079, 'm', '\r\nMegalomania', ' n. 1 mental disorder producing delusions of grandeur. 2 passion for grandiose schemes.  megalomaniac adj. & n. [greek megas great, *mania]'),
(24080, 'm', '\r\nMegalosaurus', ' n. (pl. -ruses) large flesh-eating dinosaur with stout hind legs and small forelimbs. [greek megas great, sauros lizard]'),
(24081, 'm', '\r\nMegaphone', ' n. Large funnel-shaped device for amplifying the voice. [greek megas great, phone sound]'),
(24082, 'm', '\r\nMegastar', ' n. Colloq. Very famous entertainer etc.'),
(24083, 'm', '\r\nMegaton', ' n. Unit of explosive power equal to one million tons of tnt.'),
(24084, 'm', '\r\nMegavolt', ' n. One million volts, esp. As a unit of electromotive force.'),
(24085, 'm', '\r\nMegawatt', ' n. One million watts, esp. As a measure of electrical power.'),
(24086, 'm', '\r\nMegohm', ' n. One million ohms.'),
(24087, 'm', '\r\nMeiosis', ' n. (pl. Meioses) 1 cell division that results in gametes with half the normal chromosome number. 2 = *litotes. [greek meion less]'),
(24088, 'm', '\r\nMelamine', ' n. 1 white crystalline compound producing resins. 2 (in full melamine resin) plastic made from this and used esp. For laminated coatings. [from arbitrary melam, *amine]'),
(24089, 'm', '\r\nMelancholia', ' n. Depression and anxiety. [latin: related to *melancholy]'),
(24090, 'm', '\r\nMelancholy', ' â€”n. 1 pensive sadness. 2 a mental depression. B tendency to this. â€”adj. Sad; saddening, depressing; expressing sadness.  melancholic adj. [greek melas black, khole bile]'),
(24091, 'm', '\r\nMÃ©lange', ' n. Mixture, medley. [french mÃªler mix]'),
(24092, 'm', '\r\nMelanin', ' n. Dark pigment in the hair, skin, etc., causing tanning in sunlight. [greek melas black]'),
(24093, 'm', '\r\nMelanoma', ' n. Malignant skin tumour.'),
(24094, 'm', '\r\nMelba', 'toast  n. Very thin crisp toast. [melba, name of a soprano]'),
(24095, 'm', '\r\nMeld', ' v. Merge, blend. [origin uncertain]'),
(24096, 'm', '\r\nMelee', ' n. (brit. MÃªlÃ©e) 1 confused fight, skirmish, or scuffle. 2 muddle. [french: related to *medley]'),
(24097, 'm', '\r\nMÃªlÃ©e', ' n. (us melee) 1 confused fight, skirmish, or scuffle. 2 muddle. [french: related to *medley]'),
(24098, 'm', '\r\nMellifluous', ' adj. (of a voice etc.) Pleasing, musical, flowing.  mellifluously adv. Mellifluousness n. [latin mel honey, fluo flow]'),
(24099, 'm', '\r\nMellow', ' â€”adj. 1 (of sound, colour, light) soft and rich, free from harshness. 2 (of character) gentle; mature. 3 genial, jovial. 4 euphem. Partly intoxicated. 5 (of fruit) soft, sweet, and juicy. 6 (of wine) well-matured, smooth. 7 (of earth) rich, loamy. â€”v. Make or become mellow.  mellowly adv. Mellowness n. [origin unknown]'),
(24100, 'm', '\r\nMelodeon', ' n. (also melodion) 1 small organ similar to the harmonium. 2 small german accordion. [from *melody, *harmonium]'),
(24101, 'm', '\r\nMelodic', ' adj. Of melody; melodious.  melodically adv. [greek: related to *melody]'),
(24102, 'm', '\r\nMelodious', ' adj. 1 of, producing, or having melody. 2 sweet-sounding.  melodiously adv. Melodiousness n. [french: related to *melody]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(24103, 'm', '\r\nMelodrama', ' n. 1 sensational play etc. Appealing blatantly to the emotions. 2 this type of drama. 3 theatrical language, behaviour, etc.  melodramatic adj. Melodramatically adv. [greek melos music, *drama]'),
(24104, 'm', '\r\nMelody', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 single notes arranged to make a distinctive recognizable pattern; tune. 2 principal part in harmonized music. 3 musical arrangement of words. 4 sweet music, tunefulness. [greek melos song: related to *ode]'),
(24105, 'm', '\r\nMelon', ' n. 1 sweet fleshy fruit of various climbing plants of the gourd family. 2 such a gourd. [greek melon apple]'),
(24106, 'm', '\r\nMelt', ' v. 1 become liquefied or change to liquid by the action of heat; dissolve. 2 (as molten adj.) (esp. Of metals etc.) Liquefied by heat (molten lava; molten lead). 3 (of food) be delicious, seeming to dissolve in the mouth. 4 soften, or (of a person, the heart, etc.) Be softened, by pity, love, etc. (a melting look). 5 (usu. Foll. By into) merge imperceptibly; change into (night melted into dawn). 6 (often foll. By away) (of a person) leave or disappear unobtrusively (melted into the background).  melt away disappear by or as if by liquefaction. Melt down 1 melt (esp. Metal) for reuse. 2 become liquid and lose structure. [old english]'),
(24107, 'm', '\r\nMeltdown', ' n. 1 melting of a structure, esp. The overheated core of a nuclear reactor. 2 disastrous event, esp. A rapid fall in share values.'),
(24108, 'm', '\r\nMelting', 'point  n. Temperature at which a solid melts.'),
(24109, 'm', '\r\nMelting-pot', ' n. Place for mixing races, theories, etc.'),
(24110, 'm', '\r\nMember', ' n. 1 person etc. Belonging to a society, team, group, etc. 2 (member) person elected to certain assemblies etc. 3 part of a larger structure, e.g. Of a group of figures or a mathematical set. 4 a part or organ of the body, esp. A limb. B = *penis. [latin membrum limb]'),
(24111, 'm', '\r\nMembership', ' n. 1 being a member. 2 number or body of members.'),
(24112, 'm', '\r\nMembrane', ' n. 1 pliable sheetlike tissue connecting or lining organs in plants and animals. 2 thin pliable sheet or skin.  membranous adj. [latin membrana skin, parchment: related to *member]'),
(24113, 'm', '\r\nMemento', ' n. (pl. -es or -s) souvenir of a person or event. [latin, imperative of memini remember]'),
(24114, 'm', '\r\nMemento', 'mori  n. Skull etc. As a reminder of death. [latin, = remember you must die]'),
(24115, 'm', '\r\nMemo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Memorandum. [abbreviation]'),
(24116, 'm', '\r\nMemoir', ' n. 1 historical account etc. Written from personal knowledge or special sources. 2 (in pl.) Autobiography, esp. Partial or dealing with specific events or people. 3 essay on a learned subject. [french mÃ©moire: related to *memory]'),
(24117, 'm', '\r\nMemorabilia', ' n.pl. Souvenirs of memorable events. [latin: related to *memorable]'),
(24118, 'm', '\r\nMemorable', ' adj. 1 worth remembering. 2 easily remembered.  memorably adv. [latin memor mindful]'),
(24119, 'm', '\r\nMemorandum', ' n. (pl. -da or -s) 1 note or record for future use. 2 informal written message, esp. In business, diplomacy, etc. [see *memorable]'),
(24120, 'm', '\r\nMemorial', ' â€”n. Object etc. Established in memory of a person or event. â€”attrib. Adj. Commemorating (memorial service). [latin: related to *memory]'),
(24121, 'm', '\r\nMemorize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) commit to memory.'),
(24122, 'm', '\r\nMemory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 faculty by which things are recalled to or kept in the mind. 2 a this in an individual (my memory is failing). B store of things remembered (deep in my memory). 3 recollection; remembrance, esp. Of a person etc.; person or thing remembered (memory of better times; his mother''s memory). 4 storage capacity of a computer etc. 5 posthumous reputation (his memory lives on; of blessed memory). 6 length of remembered time of a specific person, group, etc. (within living memory). 7 remembering (deed worthy of memory).  from memory as remembered (without checking). In memory of to keep alive the remembrance of. [latin memoria from memor mindful]'),
(24123, 'm', '\r\nMemory', 'lane  n. (usu. Prec. By down, along) joc. Sentimental remembering.'),
(24124, 'm', '\r\nMemsahib', ' n. Anglo-ind. Hist. Indian name for a european married woman in india. [from *ma''am, *sahib]'),
(24125, 'm', '\r\nMen', ' pl. Of *man.'),
(24126, 'm', '\r\nMenace', ' â€”n. 1 threat. 2 dangerous thing or person. 3 joc. Pest, nuisance. â€”v. (-cing) threaten.  menacingly adv. [latin minax from minor threaten]'),
(24127, 'm', '\r\nMÃ©nage', ' n. Household. [latin: related to *manor]'),
(24128, 'm', '\r\nMÃ©nage', 'Ã  trois  n. (pl. MÃ©nages Ã  trois) household of three, usu. A married couple and a lover. [french, = household of three]'),
(24129, 'm', '\r\nMenagerie', ' n. Small zoo. [french: related to *mÃ©nage]'),
(24130, 'm', '\r\nMend', ' â€”v. 1 restore to good condition; repair. 2 regain health. 3 improve (mend matters). â€”n. Darn or repair in material etc.  mend one''s ways reform oneself. On the mend recovering, esp. In health. [anglo-french: related to *amend]'),
(24131, 'm', '\r\nMendacious', ' adj. Lying, untruthful.  mendacity n. (pl. -ies). [latin mendax]'),
(24132, 'm', '\r\nMendelevium', ' n.  Artificially made transuranic radioactive metallic element. [mendeleev, name of a chemist]'),
(24133, 'm', '\r\nMendelian', ' adj. Of mendel''s theory of heredity by genes. [mendel, name of a botanist]'),
(24134, 'm', '\r\nMendicant', ' â€”adj. 1 begging. 2 (of a friar) living solely on alms. â€”n. 1 beggar. 2 mendicant friar. [latin mendicus beggar]'),
(24135, 'm', '\r\nMending', ' n. 1 action of repairing. 2 things, esp. Clothes, to be mended.'),
(24136, 'm', '\r\nMenfolk', ' n.pl. Men, esp. The men of a family.'),
(24137, 'm', '\r\nMenhir', ' n. Usu. Prehistoric monument of a tall upright stone. [breton men stone, hir long]'),
(24138, 'm', '\r\nMenial', ' â€”adj. (of esp. Work) degrading, servile. â€”n. Domestic servant. [anglo-french meinie retinue]'),
(24139, 'm', '\r\nMeninges', ' n.pl. Three membranes enclosing the brain and spinal cord. [greek menigx membrane]'),
(24140, 'm', '\r\nMeningitis', ' n. (esp. Viral) infection and inflammation of the meninges.'),
(24141, 'm', '\r\nMeniscus', ' n. (pl. Menisci) 1 curved upper surface of liquid in a tube. 2 lens convex on one side and concave on the other. [greek meniskos crescent, from mene moon]'),
(24142, 'm', '\r\nMenopause', ' n. 1 ceasing of menstruation. 2 period in a woman''s life (usu. 45â€“55) when this occurs.  menopausal adj. [greek men month, *pause]'),
(24143, 'm', '\r\nMenorah', ' n. Seven-branched jewish candelabrum. [hebrew, = candlestick]'),
(24144, 'm', '\r\nMenses', ' n.pl. Flow of menstrual blood etc. [latin, pl. Of mensis month]'),
(24145, 'm', '\r\nMens', 'rea  n. Law criminal intent. [latin, = guilty mind]'),
(24146, 'm', '\r\nMenstrual', ' adj. Of menstruation. [latin menstruus monthly]'),
(24147, 'm', '\r\nMenstrual', 'cycle  n. Process of ovulation and menstruation.'),
(24148, 'm', '\r\nMenstruate', ' v. (-ting) undergo menstruation.'),
(24149, 'm', '\r\nMenstruation', ' n. Process of discharging blood etc. From the uterus, usu. At monthly intervals from puberty to menopause.'),
(24150, 'm', '\r\nMensuration', ' n. 1 measuring. 2 measuring of lengths, areas, and volumes. [latin: related to *measure]'),
(24151, 'm', '\r\nMenswear', ' n. Clothes for men.'),
(24152, 'm', '\r\n-ment', ' suffix 1 forming nouns expressing the means or result of verbal action (abridgement; embankment). 2 forming nouns from adjectives (merriment; oddment). [latin -mentum]'),
(24153, 'm', '\r\nMental', ' adj. 1 of, in, or done by the mind. 2 caring for mental patients. 3 colloq. Insane.  mentally adv. [latin mens ment- mind]'),
(24154, 'm', '\r\nMental', 'age  n. Degree of mental development in terms of the average age at which such development is attained.'),
(24155, 'm', '\r\nMental', 'block  n. Inability due to subconscious mental factors.'),
(24156, 'm', '\r\nMental', 'deficiency  n. Abnormally low intelligence.'),
(24157, 'm', '\r\nMentality', ' n. (pl. -ies) mental character or disposition; kind or degree of intelligence.'),
(24158, 'm', '\r\nMental', 'patient  n. Sufferer from mental illness.'),
(24159, 'm', '\r\nMental', 'reservation  n. Silent qualification made while seeming to agree.'),
(24160, 'm', '\r\nMenthol', ' n. Mint-tasting organic alcohol found in oil of peppermint etc., used as a flavouring and to relieve local pain. [latin: related to *mint1]'),
(24161, 'm', '\r\nMentholated', ' adj. Treated with or containing menthol.'),
(24162, 'm', '\r\nMention', ' â€”v. 1 refer to briefly or by name. 2 reveal or disclose (do not mention this to anyone). 3 (usu. As mention in dispatches) award a minor military honour to in war. â€”n. 1 reference, esp. By name. 2 minor military or other honour.  don''t mention it polite reply to an apology or thanks. Not to mention and also. [latin mentio]'),
