(24930, 'm', '\r\nMoo', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) cry of cattle. â€”v. (moos, mooed) make this sound. [imitative]'),
(24931, 'm', '\r\nMooch', ' v. Colloq. 1 (usu. Foll. By about, around) wander aimlessly around. 2 esp. Us cadge; steal. [probably from french muchier skulk]'),
(24932, 'm', '\r\nMood1', ' n. 1 state of mind or feeling. 2 fit of bad temper or depression.  in the mood (usu. Foll. By for, or to + infin.) Inclined. [old english]'),
(24933, 'm', '\r\nMood2', ' n. 1 gram. Form or set of forms of a verb indicating whether it expresses a fact, command, wish, etc. (subjunctive mood). 2 distinction of meaning expressed by different moods. [alteration of *mode]'),
(24934, 'm', '\r\nMoody', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) given to changes of mood; gloomy, sullen. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Bad mood; tantrum.  moodily adv. Moodiness n. [related to *mood1]'),
(24935, 'm', '\r\nMoon', ' â€”n. 1 a natural satellite of the earth, orbiting it monthly, illuminated by the sun and reflecting some light to the earth. B this regarded in terms of its waxing and waning in a particular month (new moon). C the moon when visible (there is no moon tonight). 2 satellite of any planet. 3 (prec. By the) colloq. Something desirable but unattainable (promised me the moon). â€”v. 1 wander about aimlessly or listlessly. 2 slang expose one''s buttocks.  many moons ago a long time ago. Moon over act dreamily thinking about (a loved one). Over the moon colloq. Extremely happy.  moonless adj. [old english]'),
(24936, 'm', '\r\nMoonbeam', ' n. Ray of moonlight.'),
(24937, 'm', '\r\nMoon', 'boot  n. Thickly-padded boot for low temperatures.'),
(24938, 'm', '\r\nMoon-face', ' n. Round face.'),
(24939, 'm', '\r\nMoonie', ' n. Colloq. Offens. Member of the unification church. [sun myung moon, name of its founder]'),
(24940, 'm', '\r\nMoonlight', ' â€”n. 1 light of the moon. 2 (attrib.) Lit by the moon. â€”v. (-lighted) colloq. Have two paid occupations, esp. One by day and one by night.  moonlighter n.'),
(24941, 'm', '\r\nMoonlight', 'flit  n. Hurried departure by night, esp. To avoid paying a debt.'),
(24942, 'm', '\r\nMoonlit', ' adj. Lit by the moon.'),
(24943, 'm', '\r\nMoonscape', ' n. 1 surface or landscape of the moon. 2 area resembling this; wasteland.'),
(24944, 'm', '\r\nMoonshine', ' n. 1 foolish or unrealistic talk or ideas. 2 slang illicitly distilled or smuggled alcohol.'),
(24945, 'm', '\r\nMoonshot', ' n. Launching of a spacecraft to the moon.'),
(24946, 'm', '\r\nMoonstone', ' n. Feldspar of pearly appearance.'),
(24947, 'm', '\r\nMoonstruck', ' adj. Slightly mad.'),
(24948, 'm', '\r\nMoony', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) listless; stupidly dreamy.'),
(24949, 'm', '\r\nMoor', ' n. Member of a muslim people of nw africa.  moorish adj. [greek mauros]'),
(24950, 'm', '\r\nMoor1', ' n. 1 open uncultivated upland, esp. When covered with heather. 2 tract of ground preserved for shooting. [old english]'),
(24951, 'm', '\r\nMoor2', ' v. Attach (a boat etc.) To a fixed object.  moorage n. [probably low german]'),
(24952, 'm', '\r\nMoorhen', ' n. Small waterfowl.'),
(24953, 'm', '\r\nMooring', ' n. 1 (often in pl.) Place where a boat etc. Is moored. 2 (in pl.) Set of permanent anchors and chains.'),
(24954, 'm', '\r\nMoorland', ' n. Extensive area of moor.'),
(24955, 'm', '\r\nMoose', ' n. (pl. Same) n. American deer; elk. [narragansett]'),
(24956, 'm', '\r\nMoot', ' â€”adj. Debatable, undecided (moot point). â€”v. Raise (a question) for discussion. â€”n. Hist. Assembly. [old english]'),
(24957, 'm', '\r\nMop', ' â€”n. 1 bundle of yarn or cloth or a sponge on the end of a stick, for cleaning floors etc. 2 similarly-shaped implement for various purposes. 3 thick mass of hair. 4 mopping or being mopped (gave it a mop). â€”v. (-pp-) 1 wipe or clean with or as with a mop. 2 a wipe tears or sweat etc. From (one''s face etc.). B wipe away (tears etc.).  mop up 1 wipe up with or as with a mop. 2 colloq. Absorb. 3 dispatch; make an end of. 4  a complete the occupation of (a district etc.) By capturing or killing enemy troops left there. B capture or kill (stragglers). [origin uncertain]'),
(24958, 'm', '\r\nMope', ' â€”v. (-ping) 1 be depressed or listless. 2 wander about listlessly. â€”n. Person who mopes.  mopy adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin unknown]'),
(24959, 'm', '\r\nMoped', ' n. Two-wheeled low-powered motor vehicle with pedals. [swedish: related to *motor, *pedal]'),
(24960, 'm', '\r\nMoquette', ' n. Thick pile or looped material used for upholstery etc. [french]'),
(24961, 'm', '\r\nMoraine', ' n. Area of debris carried down and deposited by a glacier. [french]'),
(24962, 'm', '\r\nMoral', ' â€”adj. 1 a concerned with goodness or badness of human character or behaviour, or with the distinction between right and wrong. B concerned with accepted rules and standards of human behaviour. 2 a virtuous in general conduct. B capable of moral action. 3 (of rights or duties etc.) Founded on moral not actual law. 4 associated with the psychological rather than the physical (moral courage; moral support). â€”n. 1 moral lesson of a fable, story, event, etc. 2 (in pl.) Moral behaviour, e.g. In sexual conduct.  morally adv. [latin mos mor- custom]'),
(24963, 'm', '\r\nMorale', ' n. Confidence, determination, etc. Of a person or group. [french moral: related to *moral]'),
(24964, 'm', '\r\nMoralist', ' n. 1 person who practises or teaches morality. 2 person who follows a natural system of ethics.  moralistic adj.'),
(24965, 'm', '\r\nMorality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 degree of conformity to moral principles. 2 right moral conduct. 3 science of morals. 4 particular system of morals (commercial morality).'),
(24966, 'm', '\r\nMorality', 'play  n. Hist. Drama with personified abstract qualities and including a moral lesson.'),
(24967, 'm', '\r\nMoralize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (often foll. By on) indulge in moral reflection or talk. 2 make moral or more moral.  moralization n.'),
(24968, 'm', '\r\nMoral', 'law  n. The conditions to be satisfied by any right course of action.'),
(24969, 'm', '\r\nMoral', 'philosophy  n. Branch of philosophy concerned with ethics.'),
(24970, 'm', '\r\nMoral', 'victory  n. Defeat that has some of the satisfactory elements of victory.'),
(24971, 'm', '\r\nMorass', ' n. 1 entanglement; confusion. 2 literary bog. [french marais related to *marsh]'),
(24972, 'm', '\r\nMoratorium', ' n. (pl. -s or -ria) 1 (often foll. By on) temporary prohibition or suspension (of an activity). 2 a legal authorization to debtors to postpone payment. B period of this postponement. [latin moror delay]'),
(24973, 'm', '\r\nMorbid', ' adj. 1 a (of the mind, ideas, etc.) Unwholesome. B given to morbid feelings. 2 colloq. Melancholy. 3 med. Of the nature of or indicative of disease.  morbidity n. Morbidly adv. [latin morbus disease]'),
(24974, 'm', '\r\nMordant', ' â€”adj. 1 (of sarcasm etc.) Caustic, biting. 2 pungent, smarting. 3 corrosive or cleansing. 4 serving to fix dye. â€”n. Mordant substance. [latin mordeo bite]'),
(24975, 'm', '\r\nMore', ' â€”adj. Greater in quantity or degree; additional (more problems than last time; bring some more water). â€”n. Greater quantity, number, or amount (more than three people; more to it than meets the eye). â€”adv. 1 to a greater degree or extent. 2 forming the comparative of adjectives and adverbs, esp. Those of more than one syllable (more absurd; more easily).  more and more to an increasing degree. More of to a greater extent. More or less approximately; effectively; nearly. What is more as an additional point. [old english]'),
(24976, 'm', '\r\nMoreish', ' adj. (also morish) colloq. (of food) causing a desire for more.'),
(24977, 'm', '\r\nMorello', ' n. (pl. -s) sour kind of dark cherry. [italian, = blackish]'),
(24978, 'm', '\r\nMoreover', ' adv. Besides, in addition to what has been said.'),
(24979, 'm', '\r\nMores', ' n.pl. Customs or conventions of a community. [latin, pl. Of mos custom]'),
(24980, 'm', '\r\nMorganatic', ' adj. 1 (of a marriage) between a person of high rank and one of lower rank, the spouse and children having no claim to the possessions or title of the person of higher rank. 2 (of a spouse) married in this way. [latin morganaticus from germanic, = â€˜morning giftâ€™, from a husband to his wife on the morning after consummation of a marriage]'),
(24981, 'm', '\r\nMorgue', ' n. 1 mortuary. 2 (in a newspaper office) room or file of miscellaneous information. [french, originally the name of a paris mortuary]'),
(24982, 'm', '\r\nMoribund', ' adj. 1 at the point of death. 2 lacking vitality. [latin morior die]'),
(24983, 'm', '\r\nMorish', ' var. Of *moreish.'),
(24984, 'm', '\r\nMormon', ' n. Member of the church of jesus christ of latter-day saints.  mormonism n. [mormon, name of the supposed author of the book on which mormonism is founded]'),
(24985, 'm', '\r\nMorn', ' n. Poet. Morning. [old english]'),
(24986, 'm', '\r\nMornay', ' n. Cheese-flavoured white sauce. [origin uncertain]'),
