(25004, 'm', '\r\nMorse', ' â€”n. (in full morse code) code in which letters are represented by combinations of long and short light or sound signals. â€”v. (-sing) signal by morse code. [morse, name of an electrician]'),
(25005, 'm', '\r\nMorsel', ' n. Mouthful; small piece (esp. Of food). [latin morsus bite]'),
(25006, 'm', '\r\nMortal', ' â€”adj. 1 subject to death. 2 causing death; fatal. 3 (of combat) fought to the death. 4 associated with death (mortal agony). 5 (of an enemy) implacable. 6 (of pain, fear, an affront, etc.) Intense, very serious. 7 colloq. Long and tedious (for two mortal hours). 8 colloq. Conceivable, imaginable (every mortal thing; of no mortal use). â€”n. Human being.  mortally adv. [latin mors mort- death]'),
(25007, 'm', '\r\nMortality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being subject to death. 2 loss of life on a large scale. 3 a number of deaths in a given period etc. B (in full mortality rate) death rate.'),
