(25030, 'm', '\r\nMoss', ' n. 1 small flowerless plant growing in dense clusters in bogs, on the ground, trees, stones, etc. 2 scot. & n.engl. Bog, esp. A peatbog.  mossy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(25031, 'm', '\r\nMost', ' â€”adj. 1 greatest in quantity or degree. 2 the majority of (most people think so). â€”n. 1 greatest quantity or number (this is the most i can do). 2 the majority (most of them are missing). â€”adv. 1 in the highest degree. 2 forming the superlative of adjectives and adverbs, esp. Those of more than one syllable (most absurd; most easily). 3 us colloq. Almost.  at most no more or better than (this is at most a makeshift). At the most 1 as the greatest amount. 2 not more than. For the most part 1 mainly. 2 usually.'),
(25032, 'm', '\r\n-most', ' suffix forming superlative adjectives and adverbs from prepositions and other words indicating relative position (foremost; uttermost). [old english]'),
(25033, 'm', '\r\nMostly', ' adv. 1 mainly. 2 usually.'),
(25034, 'm', '\r\nMost', 'reverend  n. Title of archbishops.'),
(25035, 'm', '\r\nMot', ' abbr. (in full mot test) compulsory annual test of vehicles of more than a specified age. [ministry of transport]'),
(25036, 'm', '\r\nMot', ' n. (pl. Mots pronunc. Same) = *bon mot. [french, = word]'),
(25037, 'm', '\r\nMote', ' n. Speck of dust. [old english]'),
(25038, 'm', '\r\nMotel', ' n. Roadside hotel for motorists. [from motor hotel]'),
