(25290, 'm', '\r\nMuslim', ' (also moslem) â€”n. Follower of the islamic religion. â€”adj. Of the muslims or their religion. [arabic: related to *islam]'),
(25291, 'm', '\r\nMuslin', ' n. Fine delicately woven cotton fabric. [italian mussolo mosul in iraq]'),
(25292, 'm', '\r\nMusquash', ' n. = *muskrat. [algonquian]'),
(25293, 'm', '\r\nMussel', ' n. Bivalve mollusc, esp. Of the kind used for food. [old english: related to *muscle]'),
(25294, 'm', '\r\nMust1', ' â€”v.aux. (present must; past had to or in indirect speech must) (foll. By infin., or absol.) 1 a be obliged to (you must go to school). B in ironic questions (must you slam the door?). 2 be certainly (you must be her sister). 3 ought to (must see what can be done). 4 expressing insistence (must ask you to leave). 5 (foll. By not + infin.) A not be permitted to, be forbidden to (must not smoke). B ought not; need not (mustn''t think he''s angry; must not worry). C expressing insistence that something should not be done (they must not be told). â€”n. Colloq. Thing that should not be missed (this exhibition is a must).  i must say often iron. I cannot refrain from saying (i must say he tries hard; a fine way to behave, i must say). Must needs see *needs. [old english]'),
(25295, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 1a, the negative (i.e. Lack of obligation) is expressed by not have to or need not; must not denotes positive forbidding, as in you must not smoke.'),
(25296, 'm', '\r\nMust2', ' n. Grape juice before fermentation is complete. [old english from latin]'),
(25297, 'm', '\r\nMustache', ' n. (brit. Moustache) hair left to grow on a man''s upper lip. [greek mustax]'),
(25298, 'm', '\r\nMustang', ' n. Small wild horse of mexico and california. [spanish]'),
(25299, 'm', '\r\nMustard', ' n. 1 a plant with slender pods and yellow flowers. B seeds of this crushed into a paste and used as a spicy condiment. 2 plant eaten at the seedling stage, often with cress. 3 brownish-yellow colour. [romanic: related to *must2]'),
(25300, 'm', '\r\nMustard', 'gas  n. Colourless oily liquid, whose vapour is a powerful irritant.'),
(25301, 'm', '\r\nMuster', ' â€”v. 1 collect (orig. Soldiers) for inspection, to check numbers, etc. 2 collect, gather together. 3 summon (courage etc.). â€”n. Assembly of persons for inspection.  pass muster be accepted as adequate. [latin monstro show]'),
(25302, 'm', '\r\nMustn''t', ' contr. Must not.'),
(25303, 'm', '\r\nMustn''t', ' contr. Must not.'),
(25304, 'm', '\r\nMusty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 mouldy, stale. 2 dull, antiquated.  mustily adv. Mustiness n. [perhaps an alteration of moisty: related to *moist]'),
(25305, 'm', '\r\nMutable', ' adj. Literary liable to change.  mutability n. [latin muto change]'),
(25306, 'm', '\r\nMutagen', ' n. Agent promoting genetic mutation.  mutagenic adj. Mutagenesis n. [from *mutation, *-gen]'),
(25307, 'm', '\r\nMutant', ' â€”adj. Resulting from mutation. â€”n. Mutant organism or gene.'),
(25308, 'm', '\r\nMutate', ' v. (-ting) (cause to) undergo mutation.'),
(25309, 'm', '\r\nMutation', ' n. 1 change, alteration. 2 genetic change which, when transmitted to offspring, gives rise to heritable variations. 3 mutant. [latin muto change]'),
(25310, 'm', '\r\nMutatis', 'mutandis  adv. (in comparing cases) making the necessary alterations. [latin]'),
(25311, 'm', '\r\nMute', ' â€”adj. 1 silent, refraining from or temporarily bereft of speech. 2 (of a person or animal) dumb. 3 not expressed in speech (mute protest). 4 (of a letter) not pronounced. â€”n. 1 dumb person. 2 device for damping the sound of a musical instrument. 3 unsounded consonant. â€”v. (-ting) 1 deaden or soften the sound of (esp. A musical instrument). 2 a tone down, make less intense. B (as muted adj.) (of colours etc.) Subdued.  mutely adv. Muteness n.  [latin mutus]'),
(25312, 'm', '\r\nMute', 'button  n. Device on a telephone to temporarily prevent the caller from hearing what is being said at the receiver''s end, or on a television etc. To temporarily turn off the sound.'),
(25313, 'm', '\r\nMute', 'swan  n. Common white swan.'),
(25314, 'm', '\r\nMutilate', ' v. (-ting) 1 a deprive (a person or animal) of a limb or organ. B destroy the use of (a limb or organ). 2 excise or damage part of (a book etc.).  mutilation n. [latin mutilus maimed]'),
(25315, 'm', '\r\nMutineer', ' n. Person who mutinies. [romanic: related to *move]'),
(25316, 'm', '\r\nMutinous', ' adj. Rebellious; ready to mutiny.  mutinously adv.'),
(25317, 'm', '\r\nMutiny', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) open revolt, esp. By soldiers or sailors against their officers. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) (often foll. By against) revolt; engage in mutiny.'),
(25318, 'm', '\r\nMutt', ' n. 1 slang ignorant or stupid person. 2 derog. Dog. [abbreviation of *mutton-head]'),
(25319, 'm', '\r\nMutter', ' â€”v. 1 (also absol.) Utter (words) in a barely audible manner. 2 (often foll. By against, at) murmur or grumble. â€”n. 1 muttered words or sounds. 2 muttering. [related to *mute]'),
(25320, 'm', '\r\nMutton', ' n. Flesh of sheep as food. [medieval latin multo sheep]'),
(25321, 'm', '\r\nMutton', 'dressed as lamb  n. Colloq. Middle-aged or elderly woman dressed to appear younger.'),
(25322, 'm', '\r\nMutton-head', ' n. Colloq. Stupid person.'),
(25323, 'm', '\r\nMutual', ' adj. 1 (of feelings, actions, etc.) Experienced or done by each of two or more parties to or towards the other(s) (mutual affection). 2 colloq. Common to two or more persons (a mutual friend). 3 having the same (specified) relationship to each other (mutual well-wishers).  mutuality n. Mutually adv. [latin mutuus borrowed]'),
(25324, 'm', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of mutual in sense 2, although often found, is considered incorrect by some people, for whom common is preferable.'),
(25325, 'm', '\r\nMuzak', ' n. 1 propr. System of piped music used in public places. 2 (muzak) recorded light background music. [fanciful var. Of *music]'),
(25326, 'm', '\r\nMuzzle', ' â€”n. 1 projecting part of an animal''s face, including the nose and mouth. 2 guard, usu. Of straps or wire, put over an animal''s nose and mouth to stop it biting or feeding. 3 open end of a firearm. â€”v. (-ling) 1 put a muzzle on. 2 impose silence on. [medieval latin musum]'),
(25327, 'm', '\r\nMuzzy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 mentally hazy. 2 blurred, indistinct.  muzzily adv. Muzziness n. [origin unknown]'),
(25328, 'm', '\r\nMw', ' abbr. 1 megawatt(s). 2 medium wave.'),
(25329, 'm', '\r\nMy', ' poss. Pron. (attrib.) 1 of or belonging to me. 2 affectionate, patronizing, etc. Form of address (my dear boy). 3 in expressions of surprise (my god!; oh my!). 4 colloq. Indicating a close relative etc. Of the speaker (my johnny''s ill again).  my lady (or lord) form of address to certain titled persons. [from *mine1]'),
(25330, 'm', '\r\nMyalgia', ' n. Muscular pain.  myalgic adj. [greek mus muscle]'),
(25331, 'm', '\r\nMycelium', ' (pl. -lia) microscopic threadlike parts of a fungus. [greek mukes mushroom]'),
(25332, 'm', '\r\nMycenaean', ' â€”adj. Of the late bronze age civilization in greece (c.1500â€“1100 bc), depicted in the homeric poems. â€”n. Person of this civilization. [latin mycenaeus]'),
(25333, 'm', '\r\nMycology', ' n. 1 the study of fungi. 2 fungi of a particular region.  mycologist n. [greek mukes mushroom]'),
(25334, 'm', '\r\nMyna', ' n. (also mynah, mina) talking bird of the starling family. [hindi]'),
(25335, 'm', '\r\nMyopia', ' n. 1 short-sightedness. 2 lack of imagination or insight.  myopic adj. Myopically adv. [greek muo shut, ops eye]'),
(25336, 'm', '\r\nMyriad', ' literary â€”n. An indefinitely great number. â€”adj. Innumerable. [greek murioi 10,000]'),
(25337, 'm', '\r\nMyrrh', ' n. Gum resin used in perfume, medicine, incense, etc. [latin myrrha from greek]'),
(25338, 'm', '\r\nMyrtle', ' n. Evergreen shrub with shiny leaves and white scented flowers. [greek murtos]'),
(25339, 'm', '\r\nMyself', ' pron. 1 emphat. Form of *i2 or *me1 (i saw it myself). 2 refl. Form of *me1 (i was angry with myself).  be myself see *oneself. I myself i for my part (i myself am doubtful). [old english: related to *me1, *self]'),
(25340, 'm', '\r\nMysterious', ' adj. Full of or wrapped in mystery.  mysteriously adv. [french: related to *mystery]'),
(25341, 'm', '\r\nMystery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 secret, hidden, or inexplicable matter. 2 secrecy or obscurity. 3 (attrib.) Secret, undisclosed (mystery guest). 4 practice of making a secret of things (engaged in mystery and intrigue). 5 (in full mystery story) fictional work dealing with a puzzling event, esp. A murder. 6 a religious truth divinely revealed. 7 (in pl.) A secret religious rites of the ancient greeks, romans, etc. B archaic eucharist. [greek musterion: related to *mystic]'),
(25342, 'm', '\r\nMystery', 'play  n. Miracle play.'),
(25343, 'm', '\r\nMystery', 'tour  n. Pleasure trip to an unspecified destination.'),
(25344, 'm', '\r\nMystic', ' â€”n. Person who seeks by contemplation etc. To achieve unity with the deity, or who believes in the spiritual apprehension of truths that are beyond the understanding. â€”adj. = *mystical.  mysticism n. [greek mustes initiated person]'),
(25345, 'm', '\r\nMystical', ' adj. 1 of mystics or mysticism. 2 mysterious; occult; of hidden meaning. 3 spiritually allegorical or symbolic.  mystically adv.'),
(25346, 'm', '\r\nMystify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 bewilder, confuse. 2 wrap in mystery.  mystification n. [french: related to *mystic or *mystery]'),
(25347, 'm', '\r\nMystique', ' n. Atmosphere of mystery and veneration attending some activity, person, profession, etc. [french: related to *mystic]'),
(25348, 'm', '\r\nMyth', ' n. 1 traditional story usu. Involving supernatural or imaginary persons and embodying popular ideas on natural or social phenomena etc. 2 such narratives collectively. 3 widely held but false notion. 4 fictitious person, thing, or idea. 5 allegory (platonic myth).  mythical adj. Mythically adv. [greek muthos]'),
(25349, 'm', '\r\nMythology', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 body of myths. 2 the study of myths.  mythological adj. Mythologize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). [greek: related to *myth]'),
(25350, 'm', '\r\nMyxomatosis', ' n. Viral disease of rabbits. [greek muxa mucus]'),
(25351, 'n', 'N1', ' n. (also n) (pl. Ns or n''s) 1 fourteenth letter of the alphabet. 2 (usu. N) indefinite number.  to the nth degree to the utmost.'),
(25352, 'n', '\r\nN2', ' abbr. (also n.) 1 north; northern. 2 new.'),
(25353, 'n', '\r\nN3', ' symb. Nitrogen.'),
(25354, 'n', '\r\nN', ' abbr. (also n.) 1 name. 2 neuter.'),
(25355, 'n', '\r\nNa', ' symb. Sodium.'),
(25356, 'n', '\r\nNaafi', ' abbr. 1 navy, army, and air force institutes. 2 canteen for servicemen run by the naafi.'),
(25357, 'n', '\r\nNab', ' v. (-bb-) slang 1 arrest; catch in wrongdoing. 2 grab. [origin unknown]'),
(25358, 'n', '\r\nNacho', ' n. (pl. -s) tortilla chip, usu. Topped with melted cheese and spices etc. [origin uncertain]'),
(25359, 'n', '\r\nNacre', ' n. Mother-of-pearl from any shelled mollusc.  nacreous adj. [french]'),
(25360, 'n', '\r\nNadir', ' n. 1 part of the celestial sphere directly below an observer. 2 lowest point; time of deep despair. [arabic, = opposite]'),
(25361, 'n', '\r\nNaevus', ' n. (us nevus) (pl. Naevi) 1 raised red birthmark. 2 = *mole2. [latin]'),
(25362, 'n', '\r\nNaff', ' adj. Slang 1 unfashionable. 2 rubbishy. [origin unknown]'),
(25363, 'n', '\r\nNag1', ' v. (-gg-) 1 a persistently criticize or scold. B (often foll. By at) find fault or urge, esp. Persistently. 2 (of a pain) be persistent. [originally a dial. Word]'),
(25364, 'n', '\r\nNag2', ' n. Colloq. Horse. [origin unknown]'),
(25365, 'n', '\r\nNaiad', ' n. Water-nymph. [latin from greek]'),
(25366, 'n', '\r\nNail', ' â€”n. 1 small metal spike hammered in to join things together or as a peg or decoration. 2 horny covering on the upper surface of the tip of the human finger or toe. â€”v. 1 fasten with a nail or nails. 2 secure or get hold of (a person or thing). 3 keep (attention etc.) Fixed. 4 expose or discover (a lie or liar).  nail down 1 bind (a person) to a promise etc. 2 define precisely. 3 fasten (a thing) with nails. Nail in a person''s coffin something thought to increase the risk of death. On the nail (esp. Of payment) without delay. [old english]'),
(25367, 'n', '\r\nNail-file', ' n. Roughened metal or emery strip used for smoothing the nails.'),
(25368, 'n', '\r\nNail', 'polish  n. (also nail varnish) varnish, usu. Coloured, applied to the nails.'),
(25369, 'n', '\r\nNaÃ¯ve', ' adj. (also naive) 1 innocent; unaffected. 2 foolishly credulous. 3 (of art) produced in a sophisticated society but lacking conventional expertise.  naÃ¯vely adv. NaÃ¯vety n. (also naÃ¯vetÃ©). [latin nativus *native]'),
(25370, 'n', '\r\nNaked', ' adj. 1 without clothes; nude. 2 without its usual covering. 3 undisguised (the naked truth). 4 (of a light, flame, sword, etc.) Unprotected or unsheathed.  nakedly adv. Nakedness n. [old english]'),
(25371, 'n', '\r\nNaked', 'eye  n. (prec. By the) unassisted vision, e.g. Without a telescope etc.'),
(25372, 'n', '\r\nNamby-pamby', ' â€”adj. Insipidly pretty or sentimental; weak. â€”n. (pl. -ies) namby-pamby person. [fanciful formulation on the name of the writer ambrose philips]'),
(25373, 'n', '\r\nName', ' â€”n. 1 word by which an individual person, family, animal, place, or thing is spoken of etc. 2 a (usu. Abusive) term used of a person etc. (called him names). B word denoting an object or esp. A class of objects etc. (what is the name of those flowers?). 3 famous person. 4 reputation, esp. A good one. â€”v. (-ming) 1 give a name to. 2 state the name of. 3 mention; specify; cite. 4 nominate.  have to one''s name possess. In the name of as representing; by virtue of (in the name of the law). In name only not in reality. Make a name for oneself become famous.  nameable adj. [old english]'),
(25374, 'n', '\r\nName-day', ' n. Feast-day of the saint after whom a person is named.'),
(25375, 'n', '\r\nName-dropping', ' n. Familiar mention of famous people as a form of boasting.'),
(25376, 'n', '\r\nNameless', ' adj. 1 having or showing no name. 2 unnamed (our informant, who shall be nameless). 3 too horrific to be named (nameless vices).'),
(25377, 'n', '\r\nNamely', ' adv. That is to say; in other words.'),
(25378, 'n', '\r\nName-plate', ' n. Plate or panel bearing the name of an occupant of a room etc.'),
(25379, 'n', '\r\nNamesake', ' n. Person or thing having the same name as another. [probably from for the name''s sake]'),
(25380, 'n', '\r\nNan', ' n. (also nana, nanna) colloq. Grandmother. [childish pronunciation]'),
(25381, 'n', '\r\nNancy', ' n. (pl. -ies) (in full nancy boy) slang offens. Effeminate man, esp. A homosexual. [pet form of ann]'),
(25382, 'n', '\r\nNanny', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 child''s nurse. 2 colloq. Grandmother. 3 (in full nanny-goat) female goat. [related to *nancy]'),
(25383, 'n', '\r\nNano-', ' comb. Form denoting a factor of 10-9 (nanosecond). [greek nanos dwarf]'),
(25384, 'n', '\r\nNap1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) sleep lightly or briefly. â€”n. Short sleep or doze, esp. By day.  catch a person napping detect in negligence etc; catch off guard. [old english]'),
(25385, 'n', '\r\nNap2', ' n. Raised pile on textiles, esp. Velvet. [low german or dutch]'),
(25386, 'n', '\r\nNap3', ' â€”n. 1 form of whist in which players declare the number of tricks they expect to take. 2 racing tip claimed to be almost a certainty. â€”v. (-pp-) name (a horse etc.) As a probable winner.  go nap 1 attempt to take all five tricks in nap.'),
(25387, 'n', '\r\nNapalm', ' â€”n. Thick jellied hydrocarbon mixture used in bombs. â€”v. Attack with napalm bombs. [from *naphthalene, *palm1]'),
(25388, 'n', '\r\nNape', ' n. Back of the neck. [origin unknown]'),
(25389, 'n', '\r\nNaphtha', ' n. Inflammable hydrocarbon distilled from coal etc. [latin from greek]'),
(25390, 'n', '\r\nNaphthalene', ' n. White crystalline substance produced by distilling coal tar.'),
(25391, 'n', '\r\nNapkin', ' n. 1 piece of linen etc. For wiping the lips, fingers, etc. At meals. 2 baby''s nappy. [french nappe from latin mappa *map]'),
(25392, 'n', '\r\nNappy', ' n. (pl. -ies) piece of towelling etc. Wrapped round a baby to absorb or retain urine and faeces. [from *napkin]'),
(25393, 'n', '\r\nNarcissism', ' n. Excessive or erotic interest in oneself.  narcissistic adj. [narkissos, name of a youth in greek myth who fell in love with his reflection]'),
(25394, 'n', '\r\nNarcissus', ' n. (pl. -cissi) any of several flowering bulbs, including the daffodil. [latin from greek]'),
(25395, 'n', '\r\nNarcosis', ' n. 1 state of insensibility. 2 induction of this. [greek narke numbness]'),
(25396, 'n', '\r\nNarcotic', ' â€”adj. 1 (of a substance) inducing drowsiness etc. 2 (of a drug) affecting the mind. â€”n. Narcotic substance, drug, or influence. [greek narkotikos]'),
(25397, 'n', '\r\nNark', ' slang â€”n. Police informer or decoy. â€”v. Annoy. [romany nak nose]'),
(25398, 'n', '\r\nNarrate', ' v. (-ting) 1 give a continuous story or account of. 2 provide a spoken accompaniment for (a film etc.).  narration n. Narrator n. [latin narro]'),
(25399, 'n', '\r\nNarrative', ' â€”n. Ordered account of connected events. â€”adj. Of or by narration.'),
(25400, 'n', '\r\nNarrow', ' â€”adj. (-er, -est) 1 a of small width. B confined or confining (within narrow bounds). 2 of limited scope (in the narrowest sense). 3 with little margin (narrow escape). 4 precise; exact. 5 = *narrow-minded. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Narrow part of a strait, river, pass, street, etc. â€”v. Become or make narrow; contract; lessen.  narrowly adv. Narrowness n. [old english]'),
(25401, 'n', '\r\nNarrow', 'boat  n. Canal boat.'),
(25402, 'n', '\r\nNarrow-minded', ' adj. Rigid or restricted in one''s views, intolerant.  narrow-mindedness n.'),
(25403, 'n', '\r\nNarwhal', ' n. Arctic white whale, the male of which has a long tusk. [dutch from danish]'),
(25404, 'n', '\r\nNasa', ' abbr. (in the us) national aeronautics and space administration.'),
(25405, 'n', '\r\nNasal', ' â€”adj. 1 of the nose. 2 (of a letter or a sound) pronounced with the breath passing through the nose, e.g. M, n, ng. 3 (of the voice or speech) having many nasal sounds. â€”n. Nasal letter or sound.  nasalize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). Nasally adv. [latin nasus nose]'),
(25406, 'n', '\r\nNascent', ' adj. 1 in the act of being born. 2 just beginning to be; not yet mature.  nascency n. [latin: related to *natal]'),
(25407, 'n', '\r\nNasturtium', ' n. Trailing plant with edible leaves and bright orange, yellow, or red flowers. [latin]'),
(25408, 'n', '\r\nNasty', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 highly unpleasant. 2 difficult to negotiate. 3 (of a person or animal) ill-natured. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Horror film, esp. One on video and depicting cruelty or killing.  nastily adv. Nastiness n. [origin unknown]'),
(25409, 'n', '\r\nNasty', 'piece of work  n. Colloq. Unpleasant or contemptible person.'),
(25410, 'n', '\r\nNat.', ' Abbr. 1 national. 2 nationalist. 3 natural.'),
(25411, 'n', '\r\nNatal', ' adj. Of or from one''s birth. [latin natalis from nascor nat- be born]'),
(25412, 'n', '\r\nNation', ' n. Community of people of mainly common descent, history, language, etc., forming a state or inhabiting a territory. [latin: related to *natal]'),
(25413, 'n', '\r\nNational', ' â€”adj. 1 of a, or the, nation. 2 characteristic of a particular nation. â€”n. 1 citizen of a specified country. 2 fellow-countryman. 3 (the national) = *grand national.  nationally adv.'),
(25414, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'anthem  n. Song adopted by a nation, intended to inspire patriotism.'),
(25415, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'curriculum  n. Common programme of study for pupils in the maintained schools of england and wales, with tests at specified ages.'),
(25416, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'debt  n. Money owed by a state because of loans to it.'),
(25417, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'front  n. Uk political party with extreme reactionary views on immigration etc.'),
(25418, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'grid  n. 1 network of high-voltage electric power lines between major power stations. 2 metric system of geographical coordinates used in maps of the british isles.'),
(25419, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'health  n. (also national health service) system of national medical care paid for mainly by taxation.'),
(25420, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'insurance  n. System of compulsory payments by employed persons (supplemented by employers) to provide state assistance in sickness etc.'),
(25421, 'n', '\r\nNationalism', ' n. 1 patriotic feeling, principles, etc. 2 policy of national independence.  nationalist n. & adj. Nationalistic adj.'),
(25422, 'n', '\r\nNationality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 status of belonging to a particular nation (has british nationality). 2 condition of being national; distinctive national qualities. 3 ethnic group forming a part of one or more political nations.'),
(25423, 'n', '\r\nNationalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 take (railways, industry, land, etc.) Into state ownership. 2 make national.  nationalization n.'),
(25424, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'park  n. Area of natural beauty protected by the state for the use of the public.'),
(25425, 'n', '\r\nNational', 'service  n. Hist. Conscripted peacetime military service.'),
(25426, 'n', '\r\nNationwide', ' adj. & adv. Extending over the whole nation.'),
(25427, 'n', '\r\nNative', ' â€”n. 1 a (usu. Foll. By of) person born in a specified place. B local inhabitant. 2 often offens. Member of a non-white indigenous people, as regarded by colonial settlers. 3 (usu. Foll. By of) indigenous animal or plant. â€”adj. 1 inherent; innate. 2 of one''s birth (native country). 3 (usu. Foll. By to) belonging to a specified place. 4 (esp. Of a non-european) indigenous; born in a place. 5 (of metal etc.) Found in a pure or uncombined state. [latin: related to *natal]'),
(25428, 'n', '\r\nNativity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 (esp. The nativity) a christ''s birth. B festival of christ''s birth. 2 birth. [latin: related to *native]'),
(25429, 'n', '\r\nNato', ' abbr. (also nato) north atlantic treaty organization.'),
(25430, 'n', '\r\nNatter', ' colloq. â€”v. Chatter idly. â€”n. Aimless chatter. [imitative, originally dial.]'),
(25431, 'n', '\r\nNatterjack', ' n. A kind of small toad. [perhaps from *natter]'),
(25432, 'n', '\r\nNatty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Trim; smart.  nattily adv. [cf. *neat]'),
(25433, 'n', '\r\nNatural', ' â€”adj. 1 a existing in or caused by nature (natural landscape). B uncultivated (in its natural state). 2 in the course of nature (died of natural causes). 3 not surprising; to be expected (natural for her to be upset). 4 unaffected, spontaneous. 5 innate (natural talent for music). 6 not disguised or altered (as by make-up etc.). 7 likely or suited by its or their nature to be such (natural enemies; natural leader). 8 physically existing (the natural world). 9 illegitimate. 10 mus. (of a note) not sharpened or flattened (b natural). â€”n. 1 colloq. (usu. Foll. By for) person or thing naturally suitable, adept, etc. 2 mus. A sign denoting a return to natural pitch. B natural note.  naturalness n. [latin: related to *nature]'),
(25434, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'gas  n. Gas found in the earth''s crust, not manufactured.'),
(25435, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'history  n. The study of animals or plants.'),
(25436, 'n', '\r\nNaturalism', ' n. 1 theory or practice in art and literature of realistic representation. 2 a theory of the world that excludes the supernatural or spiritual. B moral or religious system based on this.  naturalistic adj.'),
(25437, 'n', '\r\nNaturalist', ' n. 1 person who studies natural history. 2 adherent of naturalism.'),
(25438, 'n', '\r\nNaturalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 admit (a foreigner) to citizenship. 2 successfully introduce (an animal, plant, etc.) Into another region. 3 adopt (a foreign word, custom, etc.).  naturalization n.'),
(25439, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'law  n. 1 unchanging moral principles common to all human beings. 2 correct statement of an invariable sequence between specified conditions and a specified phenomenon.'),
(25440, 'n', '\r\nNaturally', ' adv. 1 in a natural manner. 2 (qualifying a whole sentence) as might be expected; of course.'),
(25441, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'number  n. Whole number greater than 0.'),
(25442, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'resources  n.pl. Materials or conditions occurring in nature and capable of economic exploitation.'),
(25443, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'science  n. 1 the study of the natural or physical world. 2 (in pl.) Sciences used for this.'),
(25444, 'n', '\r\nNatural', 'selection  n. Darwinian theory of the survival and propagation of organisms best adapted to their environment.'),
(25445, 'n', '\r\nNature', ' n. 1 thing''s or person''s innate or essential qualities or character. 2 (often nature) a physical power causing all material phenomena. B these phenomena. 3 kind or class (things of this nature). 4 inherent impulses determining character or action.  by nature innately. In (or by) the nature of things 1 inevitable. 2 inevitably. [latin natura: related to *natal]'),
(25446, 'n', '\r\nNatured', ' adj. (in comb.) Having a specified disposition (good-natured).'),
(25447, 'n', '\r\nNature', 'reserve  n. Tract of land managed so as to preserve its flora, fauna, physical features, etc.'),
(25448, 'n', '\r\nNature', 'trail  n. Signposted path through the countryside designed to draw attention to natural phenomena.'),
(25449, 'n', '\r\nNaturism', ' n. Nudism.  naturist n.'),
(25450, 'n', '\r\nNaught', ' archaic or literary â€”n. Nothing, nought. â€”adj. (usu. Predic.) Worthless; useless.  come to naught come to nothing, fail. Set at naught despise. [old english: related to *no2, *wight]'),
(25451, 'n', '\r\nNaughty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (esp. Of children) disobedient; badly behaved. 2 colloq. Joc. Indecent.  naughtily adv. Naughtiness n. [from *naught]'),
(25452, 'n', '\r\nNausea', ' n. 1 inclination to vomit. 2 revulsion. [greek naus ship]'),
(25453, 'n', '\r\nNauseate', ' v. (-ting) affect with nausea.  nauseating adj. Nauseatingly adv.'),
(25454, 'n', '\r\nNauseous', ' adj. 1 causing nausea. 2 inclined to vomit (feel nauseous). 3 disgusting; loathsome.'),
(25455, 'n', '\r\nNautical', ' adj. Of sailors or navigation. [greek nautes sailor]'),
(25456, 'n', '\r\nNautical', 'mile  n. Unit of approx. 2,025 yards (1,852 metres).'),
(25457, 'n', '\r\nNautilus', ' n. (pl. Nautiluses or nautili) cephalopod mollusc with a spiral shell, esp. (pearly nautilus) one having a chambered shell. [greek nautilos: related to *nautical]'),
(25458, 'n', '\r\nNaval', ' adj. 1 of the or a navy. 2 of ships. [latin navis ship]'),
(25459, 'n', '\r\nNave1', ' n. Central part of a church, usu. From the west door to the chancel excluding the side aisles. [latin navis ship]'),
(25460, 'n', '\r\nNave2', ' n. Hub of a wheel. [old english]'),
(25461, 'n', '\r\nNavel', ' n. Depression in the centre of the belly marking the site of attachment of the umbilical cord. [old english]'),
(25462, 'n', '\r\nNavel', 'orange  n. Orange with a navel-like formation at the top.'),
(25463, 'n', '\r\nNavigable', ' adj. 1 (of a river etc.) Suitable for ships to pass through. 2 seaworthy. 3 steerable.  navigability n. [latin: related to *navigate]'),
(25464, 'n', '\r\nNavigate', ' v. (-ting) 1 manage or direct the course of (a ship or aircraft) using maps and instruments. 2 a sail on (a sea, river, etc.). B fly through (the air). 3 (in a car etc.) Assist the driver by map-reading etc. 4 sail a ship; sail in a ship.  navigator n. [latin navigo from navis]'),
(25465, 'n', '\r\nNavigation', ' n. 1 act or process of navigating. 2 art or science of navigating.  navigational adj.'),
(25466, 'n', '\r\nNavvy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) labourer employed in building or excavating roads, canals, etc. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) work as a navvy. [abbreviation of navigator]'),
(25467, 'n', '\r\nNavy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 (often the navy) a whole body of a state''s ships of war, including crews, maintenance systems, etc. B officers and men of a navy. 2 (in full navy blue) dark-blue colour as of naval uniforms. 3 poet. Fleet of ships. [romanic navia ship: related to *naval]'),
(25468, 'n', '\r\nNay', ' â€”adv. 1 or rather; and even; and more than that (large, nay, huge). 2 archaic = *no2 adv. 1. â€”n. Utterance of â€˜nayâ€™; â€˜noâ€™ vote. [old norse, = not ever]'),
(25469, 'n', '\r\nNazarene', ' â€”n. 1 a (prec. By the) christ. B (esp. In jewish or muslim use) christian. 2 native or inhabitant of nazareth. â€”adj. Of nazareth. [latin from greek]'),
(25470, 'n', '\r\nNazi', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) hist. Member of the german national socialist party. â€”adj. Of the nazis or nazism.  nazism n. [representing pronunciation of nati- in german nationalsozialist]'),
(25471, 'n', '\r\nNb', ' abbr. Note well. [latin nota bene]'),
(25472, 'n', '\r\nNb', ' symb. Niobium.'),
(25473, 'n', '\r\nNcb', ' abbr. Hist. National coal board.'),
(25474, 'n', '\r\nUsage', ' since 1987 the official name has been british coal.'),
(25475, 'n', '\r\nNco', ' abbr. Non-commissioned officer.'),
(25476, 'n', '\r\nNcp', ' abbr. National car parks.'),
(25477, 'n', '\r\nNd', ' symb. Neodymium.'),
(25478, 'n', '\r\nNe', ' abbr. 1 north-east. 2 north-eastern.'),
(25479, 'n', '\r\nNe', ' symb. Neon.'),
(25480, 'n', '\r\nNeanderthal', ' adj. Of the type of human widely distributed in palaeolithic europe, with a retreating forehead and massive brow-ridges. [region in w. Germany]'),
(25481, 'n', '\r\nNeap', ' n. (in full neap tide) tide at the times of the month when there is least difference between high and low water. [old english]'),
(25482, 'n', '\r\nNeapolitan', ' â€”n. Native or citizen of naples. â€”adj. Of naples. [greek neapolis naples]'),
(25483, 'n', '\r\nNear', ' â€”adv. 1 (often foll. By to) to or at a short distance in space or time. 2 closely (as near as one can guess). â€”prep. 1 to or at a short distance from (in space, time, condition, or resemblance). 2 (in comb.) Almost (near-hysterical). â€”adj. 1 close (to), not far (in place or time) (my flat''s very near; the man nearest you; in the near future). 2 a closely related. B intimate. 3 (of a part of a vehicle, animal, or road) on the left side. 4 close; narrow (near escape). 5 similar (to) (is nearer the original). 6 colloq. Niggardly. â€”v. Approach; draw near to.  come (or go) near (foll. By verbal noun, or to + verbal noun) be on the point of, almost succeed in. Near at hand within easy reach. Near the knuckle colloq. Verging on the indecent.  nearish adj. Nearness n. [old norse, originally = nigher: related to *nigh]'),
(25484, 'n', '\r\nNearby', ' â€”adj. Near in position. â€”adv. Close; not far away.'),
(25485, 'n', '\r\nNear', 'east  n. (prec. By the) region comprising the countries of the eastern mediterranean.  near eastern adj.'),
(25486, 'n', '\r\nNearly', ' adv. 1 almost. 2 closely.  not nearly nothing like.'),
(25487, 'n', '\r\nNear', 'miss  n. 1 bomb etc. Falling close to the target. 2 narrowly avoided collision. 3 not quite successful attempt.'),
(25488, 'n', '\r\nNearside', ' n. (often attrib.) Left side of a vehicle, animal, etc.'),
(25489, 'n', '\r\nNear-sighted', ' adj. = *short-sighted.'),
(25490, 'n', '\r\nNear', 'thing  n. Narrow escape.'),
(25491, 'n', '\r\nNeat', ' adj. 1 tidy and methodical. 2 elegantly simple. 3 brief, clear, and pointed. 4 a cleverly executed. B dexterous. 5 (of esp. Alcoholic liquor) undiluted.  neatly adv. Neatness n. [french net from latin nitidus shining]'),
(25492, 'n', '\r\nNeaten', ' v. Make neat.'),
(25493, 'n', '\r\nNeath', ' prep. Poet. Beneath. [from *beneath]'),
(25494, 'n', '\r\nNebula', ' n. (pl. Nebulae) cloud of gas and dust seen in the night sky, sometimes glowing and sometimes appearing as a dark silhouette.   nebular adj. [latin, = mist]'),
(25495, 'n', '\r\nNebulous', ' adj. 1 cloudlike. 2 indistinct, vague. [latin: related to *nebula]'),
(25496, 'n', '\r\nNec', ' abbr. National executive committee.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(25497, 'n', '\r\nNecessary', ' â€”adj. 1 requiring to be done; requisite, essential. 2 determined, existing, or happening by natural laws etc., not by free will; inevitable. â€”n. (pl. -ies) (usu. In pl.) Any of the basic requirements of life.  the necessary colloq. 1 money. 2 an action etc. Needed for a purpose.  necessarily adv. [latin necesse needful]'),
(25498, 'n', '\r\nNecessitarian', ' â€”n. Person who holds that all action is predetermined and free will is impossible. â€”adj. Of such a person or theory.  necessitarianism n.'),
(25499, 'n', '\r\nNecessitate', ' v. (-ting) make necessary (esp. As a result) (will necessitate some sacrifice).'),
(25500, 'n', '\r\nNecessitous', ' adj. Poor; needy.'),
(25501, 'n', '\r\nNecessity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 indispensable thing. 2 pressure of circumstances. 3 imperative need. 4 want; poverty. 5 constraint or compulsion regarded as a natural law governing all human action.  of necessity unavoidably.'),
(25502, 'n', '\r\nNeck', ' â€”n. 1 a part of the body connecting the head to the shoulders. B part of a garment round the neck. 2 something resembling a neck; narrow part of a cavity, vessel, or object such as a bottle or violin. 3 length of a horse''s head and neck as a measure of its lead in a race. 4 flesh of an animal''s neck as food. 5 slang impudence. â€”v. Colloq. Kiss and caress amorously.  get it in the neck colloq. 1 be severely reprimanded or punished. 2 suffer a severe blow. Up to one''s neck (often foll. By in) colloq. Very deeply involved; very busy. [old english]'),
(25503, 'n', '\r\nNeck', 'and neck  adj. & adv. (running) level in a race etc.'),
(25504, 'n', '\r\nNeckband', ' n. Strip of material round the neck of a garment.'),
(25505, 'n', '\r\nNeckerchief', ' n. Square of cloth worn round the neck. [from *kerchief]'),
(25506, 'n', '\r\nNecklace', ' n. 1 chain or string of beads, precious stones, etc., worn round the neck. 2 s.afr. Tyre soaked or filled with petrol, placed round a victim''s neck, and set alight.'),
(25507, 'n', '\r\nNeckline', ' n. Edge or shape of a garment-opening at the neck.'),
(25508, 'n', '\r\nNecktie', ' n. Esp. Us = *tie n. 2.'),
(25509, 'n', '\r\nNecro-', ' comb. Form corpse. [greek nekros corpse]'),
(25510, 'n', '\r\nNecromancy', ' n. 1 divination by supposed communication with the dead. 2 magic.  necromancer n. [from *necro-, mantis seer]'),
(25511, 'n', '\r\nNecrophilia', ' n. Morbid and esp. Sexual attraction to corpses.'),
(25512, 'n', '\r\nNecropolis', ' n. Ancient cemetery or burial place. [greek: related to *necro-, polis city]'),
(25513, 'n', '\r\nNecrosis', ' n. Death of tissue.  necrotic adj. [greek nekroo kill]'),
(25514, 'n', '\r\nNectar', ' n. 1 sugary substance produced by plants and made into honey by bees. 2 (in greek and roman mythology) the drink of the gods. 3 drink compared to this.  nectarous adj. [latin from greek]'),
(25515, 'n', '\r\nNectarine', ' n. Smooth-skinned variety of peach. [from *nectar]'),
(25516, 'n', '\r\nNedc', ' abbr. National economic development council.'),
(25517, 'n', '\r\nNeddy', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. 1 donkey. 2 (neddy) = *nedc. [pet form of edward]'),
(25518, 'n', '\r\nNee', ' adj. (brit. NÃ©e) (used in adding a married woman''s maiden name after her surname) born (mrs ann hall, nÃ©e brown). [french, feminine past part. Of naÃ®tre be born]'),
(25519, 'n', '\r\nNÃ©e', ' adj. (us nee) (used in adding a married woman''s maiden name after her surname) born (mrs ann hall, nÃ©e brown). [french, feminine past part. Of naÃ®tre be born]'),
(25520, 'n', '\r\nNeed', ' â€”v. 1 stand in want of; require. 2 (foll. By to + infin.; 3rd sing. Present neg. Or interrog. Need without to) be under the necessity or obligation (needs to be done well; he need not come; need you ask?). â€”n. 1 requirement (my needs are few). 2 circumstances requiring some course of action (no need to worry; if need be).'),
(25521, 'n', '\r\nNeedful', ' adj. Requisite.  needfully adv.'),
(25522, 'n', '\r\nNeedle', ' â€”n. 1 a very thin pointed rod of smooth steel etc. With a slit (â€˜eyeâ€™) for thread at the blunt end, used in sewing. B larger plastic, wooden, etc. Slender rod without an eye, used in knitting etc. 2 pointer on a dial. 3 any of several small thin pointed instruments, esp.: a the end of a hypodermic syringe. B = *stylus 1. 4 a obelisk (cleopatra''s needle). B pointed rock or peak. 5 leaf of a fir or pine tree. 6 (the needle) slang fit of bad temper or nervousness. â€”v. (-ling) colloq. Irritate; provoke. [old english]'),
(25523, 'n', '\r\nNeedlecord', ' n. Fine-ribbed corduroy fabric.'),
(25524, 'n', '\r\nNeedle-point', ' n. 1 lace made with needles, not bobbins. 2 = *gros point or *petit point.'),
(25525, 'n', '\r\nNeedless', ' adj. 1 unnecessary. 2 uncalled for.  needlessly adv.'),
(25526, 'n', '\r\nNeedlewoman', ' n. 1 seamstress. 2 woman or girl with specified sewing skill.'),
(25527, 'n', '\r\nNeedlework', ' n. Sewing or embroidery.'),
(25528, 'n', '\r\nNeeds', ' adv. Archaic (usu. Prec. Or foll. By must) of necessity.'),
(25529, 'n', '\r\nNeedy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) poor; destitute.  neediness n.'),
(25530, 'n', '\r\nNe''er', ' adv. Poet. = *never. [contraction]'),
(25531, 'n', '\r\nNe''er-do-well', ' â€”n. Good-for-nothing person. â€”adj. Good-for-nothing.'),
(25532, 'n', '\r\nNefarious', ' adj. Wicked. [latin nefas wrong n.]'),
(25533, 'n', '\r\nNeg.', ' Abbr. Esp. Photog. Negative.'),
(25534, 'n', '\r\nNegate', ' v. (-ting) 1 nullify. 2 assert or imply the non-existence of. [latin nego deny]'),
(25535, 'n', '\r\nNegation', ' n. 1 absence or opposite of something actual or positive. 2 act of denying. 3 negative statement. 4 negative or unreal thing.'),
(25536, 'n', '\r\nNegative', ' â€”adj. 1 expressing or implying denial, prohibition, or refusal (negative answer). 2 (of a person or attitude) lacking positive attributes. 3 marked by the absence of qualities (negative reaction). 4 of the opposite nature to a thing regarded as positive. 5 (of a quantity) less than zero, to be subtracted from others or from zero. 6 electr. A of the kind of charge carried by electrons. B containing or producing such a charge. â€”n. 1 negative statement or word. 2 photog. A image with black and white reversed or colours replaced by complementary ones, from which positive pictures are obtained. B developed film or plate bearing such an image. â€”v. (-ving) 1 refuse to accept or countenance; veto. 2 disprove. 3 contradict (a statement). 4 neutralize (an effect).  in the negative with negative effect.  negatively adv. Negativity n.'),
(25537, 'n', '\r\nNegativism', ' n. Negative attitude; extreme scepticism.'),
(25538, 'n', '\r\nNeglect', ' â€”v. 1 fail to care for or to do; be remiss about. 2 (foll. By to + infin.) Fail; overlook the need to. 3 not pay attention to; disregard. â€”n. 1 negligence. 2 neglecting or being neglected. 3 (usu. Foll. By of) disregard.  neglectful adj. Neglectfully adv. [latin neglego neglect-]'),
(25539, 'n', '\r\nNegligÃ©e', ' n. (also negligee, nÃ©gligÃ©) woman''s flimsy dressing-gown. [french, past part. Of nÃ©gliger *neglect]'),
(25540, 'n', '\r\nNegligence', ' n. 1 lack of proper care and attention. 2 culpable carelessness.  negligent adj. Negligently adv. [latin: related to *neglect]'),
(25541, 'n', '\r\nNegligible', ' adj. Not worth considering; insignificant.  negligibly adv. [french: related to *neglect]'),
(25542, 'n', '\r\nNegotiable', ' adj. 1 open to discussion. 2 able to be negotiated.'),
(25543, 'n', '\r\nNegotiate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (usu. Foll. By with) confer in order to reach an agreement. 2 arrange (an affair) or bring about (a result) by negotiating. 3 find a way over, through, etc. (an obstacle, difficulty, etc.). 4 convert (a cheque etc.) Into money.  negotiation n. Negotiator n. [latin negotium business]'),
(25544, 'n', '\r\nNegress', ' n. Female negro.'),
(25545, 'n', '\r\nUsage', ' the term negress is often considered offensive; black is usually preferred.'),
(25546, 'n', '\r\nNegritude', ' n. 1 state of being black. 2 affirmation of black culture. [french]'),
(25547, 'n', '\r\nNegro', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) member of a dark-skinned race orig. Native to africa. â€”adj. 1 of negroes. 2 (as negro) zool. Black or dark. [latin niger nigri black]'),
(25548, 'n', '\r\nUsage', ' the term negro is often considered offensive; black is usually preferred.'),
(25549, 'n', '\r\nNegroid', ' â€”adj. (of physical features etc.) Characteristic of black people. â€”n. Black.'),
(25550, 'n', '\r\nNeigh', ' â€”n. Cry of a horse. â€”v. Make a neigh. [old english]'),
(25551, 'n', '\r\nNeighbor', ' (brit. Neighbour) â€”n. 1 person living next door to or near or nearest another. 2 fellow human being. 3 person or thing near or next to another. â€”v. Border on; adjoin. [old english: related to *nigh, *boor]'),
(25552, 'n', '\r\nNeighborhood', ' n. (brit. Neighbourhood) 1 district; vicinity. 2 people of a district.  in the neighbourhood of roughly; about.'),
(25553, 'n', '\r\nNeighborly', ' adj. (brit. Neighbourly) like a good neighbour; friendly; kind.  neighbourliness n.'),
(25554, 'n', '\r\nNeighbour', ' (us neighbor) â€”n. 1 person living next door to or near or nearest another. 2 fellow human being. 3 person or thing near or next to another. â€”v. Border on; adjoin. [old english: related to *nigh, *boor]'),
(25555, 'n', '\r\nNeighbourhood', ' n. (us neighborhood) 1 district; vicinity. 2 people of a district.  in the neighbourhood of roughly; about.'),
(25556, 'n', '\r\nNeighbourhood', 'watch  n. Organized local vigilance by householders to discourage crime.'),
(25557, 'n', '\r\nNeighbourly', ' adj. (us neighborly) like a good neighbour; friendly; kind.  neighbourliness n.'),
(25558, 'n', '\r\nNeither', ' â€”adj. & pron. (foll. By sing. Verb) not the one nor the other (of two things); not either (neither of the accusations is true; neither of them knows; neither wish was granted; neither went to the fair). â€”adv. 1 not either; not on the one hand (foll. By nor; introducing the first of two or more things in the negative: neither knowing nor caring; neither the teachers nor the parents nor the children). 2 also not (if you do not, neither shall i). â€”conj. Archaic nor yet; nor (i know not, neither can i guess). [old english: related to *no2, *whether]'),
(25559, 'n', '\r\nNelson', ' n. Wrestling-hold in which one arm is passed under the opponent''s arm from behind and the hand is applied to the neck (half nelson), or both arms and hands are applied (full nelson). [apparently from the name nelson]'),
(25560, 'n', '\r\nNematode', ' n. Worm with a slender unsegmented cylindrical shape. [greek nema thread]'),
(25561, 'n', '\r\nNem.', 'Con.  Abbr. With no one dissenting. [latin nemine contradicente]'),
(25562, 'n', '\r\nNemesis', ' n. (pl. Nemeses) 1 retributive justice. 2 downfall caused by this. [greek, = retribution]'),
(25563, 'n', '\r\nNeo-', ' comb. Form 1 new, modern. 2 new form of. [greek neos new]'),
(25564, 'n', '\r\nNeoclassicism', ' n. Revival of classical style or treatment in the arts.  neoclassical adj.'),
(25565, 'n', '\r\nNeodymium', ' n. Metallic element of the lanthanide series. [from *neo-, greek didumos twin]'),
(25566, 'n', '\r\nNeolithic', ' adj. Of the later part of the stone age. [greek lithos stone]'),
(25567, 'n', '\r\nNeologism', ' n. 1 new word. 2 coining of new words. [greek logos word]'),
(25568, 'n', '\r\nNeon', ' n. Inert gaseous element giving an orange glow when electricity is passed through it. [greek, = new]'),
(25569, 'n', '\r\nNeophyte', ' n. 1 new convert. 2 rc ch. Novice of a religious order. 3 beginner. [greek phuton plant]'),
(25570, 'n', '\r\nNephew', ' n. Son of one''s brother or sister or of one''s spouse''s brother or sister. [latin nepos]'),
(25571, 'n', '\r\nNephritic', ' adj. 1 of or in the kidneys. 2 of nephritis. [greek nephros kidney]'),
(25572, 'n', '\r\nNephritis', ' n. Inflammation of the kidneys.'),
(25573, 'n', '\r\nNe', 'plus ultra  n. 1 furthest attainable point. 2 acme, perfection. [latin, = not further beyond]'),
(25574, 'n', '\r\nNepotism', ' n. Favouritism shown to relatives in conferring offices. [italian nepote nephew]'),
(25575, 'n', '\r\nNeptunium', ' n. Transuranic metallic element produced when uranium atoms absorb bombarding neutrons. [neptune, name of a planet]'),
(25576, 'n', '\r\nNerd', ' n. (also nurd) esp. Us slang foolish, feeble, or uninteresting person. [origin uncertain]'),
(25577, 'n', '\r\nNereid', ' n. Sea-nymph. [latin from greek]'),
(25578, 'n', '\r\nNerve', ' â€”n. 1 a fibre or bundle of fibres that transmits impulses of sensation or motion between the brain or spinal cord and other parts of the body. B material constituting these. 2 a coolness in danger; bravery. B colloq. Impudence. 3 (in pl.) Nervousness; mental or physical stress. â€”v. (-ving) 1 (usu. Refl.) Brace (oneself) to face danger etc. 2 give strength, vigour, or courage to.  get on a person''s nerves irritate a person. [latin nervus sinew, bowstring]'),
(25579, 'n', '\r\nNerve-cell', ' n. Cell transmitting impulses in nerve tissue.'),
(25580, 'n', '\r\nNerve-centre', ' n. 1 group of closely connected nerve-cells. 2 centre of control.'),
(25581, 'n', '\r\nNerve', 'gas  n. Poisonous gas affecting the nervous system.'),
(25582, 'n', '\r\nNerveless', ' adj. 1 lacking vigour. 2 (of style) diffuse.'),
(25583, 'n', '\r\nNerve-racking', ' adj. Causing mental strain.'),
(25584, 'n', '\r\nNervous', ' adj. 1 easily upset, timid, highly strung. 2 anxious. 3 affecting the nerves. 4 (foll. By of + verbal noun) afraid (am nervous of meeting them).  nervously adv. Nervousness n.'),
(25585, 'n', '\r\nNervous', 'breakdown  n. Period of mental illness, usu. Resulting from severe stress.'),
(25586, 'n', '\r\nNervous', 'system  n. Body''s network of nerve cells.'),
(25587, 'n', '\r\nNervy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Nervous; easily excited.'),
(25588, 'n', '\r\nNescient', ' adj. Literary (foll. By of) lacking knowledge.  nescience n. [latin ne- not, scio know]'),
(25589, 'n', '\r\n-ness', ' suffix forming nouns from adjectives, expressing: 1 state or condition, or an instance of this (happiness; a kindness). 2 something in a certain state (wilderness). [old english]'),
(25590, 'n', '\r\nNest', ' â€”n. 1 structure or place where a bird lays eggs and shelters its young. 2 any creature''s breeding-place or lair. 3 snug retreat or shelter. 4 brood or swarm. 5 group or set of similar objects, often of different sizes and fitting one inside the other (nest of tables). â€”v. 1 use or build a nest. 2 take wild birds'' nests or eggs. 3 (of objects) fit together or one inside another. [old english]'),
(25591, 'n', '\r\nNest', 'egg  n. Sum of money saved for the future.'),
(25592, 'n', '\r\nNestle', ' v. (-ling) 1 (often foll. By down, in, etc.) Settle oneself comfortably. 2 press oneself against another in affection etc. 3 (foll. By in, into, etc.) Push (a head or shoulder etc.) Affectionately or snugly. 4 lie half hidden or embedded. [old english]'),
(25593, 'n', '\r\nNestling', ' n. Bird too young to leave its nest.'),
(25594, 'n', '\r\nNet1', ' â€”n. 1 open-meshed fabric of cord, rope, etc. 2 piece of net used esp. To restrain, contain, or delimit, or to catch fish etc. 3 structure with a net used in various games. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 a cover, confine, or catch with a net. B procure as with a net. 2  hit (a ball) into the net, esp. Of a goal. [old english]'),
(25595, 'n', '\r\nNet2', ' (also nett) â€”adj. 1 (esp. Of money) remaining after all necessary deductions. 2 (of a price) not reducible. 3 (of a weight) excluding that of the packaging etc. 4 (of an effect, result, etc.) Ultimate, actual. â€”v. (-tt-) gain or yield (a sum) as net profit. [french: related to *neat]'),
(25596, 'n', '\r\nNetball', ' n. Team game in which goals are scored by throwing a ball through a high horizontal ring from which a net hangs.'),
(25597, 'n', '\r\nNether', ' adj. Archaic = *lower1. [old english]'),
(25598, 'n', '\r\nNether', 'regions  n.pl. (also nether world) hell; the underworld.'),
(25599, 'n', '\r\nNet', 'profit  n. Actual gain after working expenses have been paid.'),
(25600, 'n', '\r\nNett', ' var. Of *net2.'),
(25601, 'n', '\r\nNetting', ' n. 1 netted fabric. 2 piece of this.'),
(25602, 'n', '\r\nNettle', ' â€”n. 1 plant with jagged leaves covered with stinging hairs. 2 plant resembling this. â€”v. (-ling) irritate, provoke. [old english]'),
(25603, 'n', '\r\nNettle-rash', ' n. Skin eruption like nettle stings.'),
(25604, 'n', '\r\nNetwork', ' â€”n. 1 arrangement of intersecting horizontal and vertical lines. 2 complex system of railways etc. 3 people connected by the exchange of information etc., professionally or socially. 4 system of connected electrical conductors. 5 group of broadcasting stations connected for the simultaneous broadcast of a programme. 6 chain of interconnected computers. â€”v. Broadcast on a network.'),
(25605, 'n', '\r\nNeural', ' adj. Of a nerve or the central nervous system. [greek neuron nerve]'),
(25606, 'n', '\r\nNeuralgia', ' n. Intense pain along a nerve, esp. In the head or face.  neuralgic adj.'),
(25607, 'n', '\r\nNeuritis', ' n. Inflammation of a nerve or nerves.'),
(25608, 'n', '\r\nNeuro-', ' comb. Form nerve or nerves. [greek neuron nerve]'),
(25609, 'n', '\r\nNeurology', ' n. The study of nerve systems.  neurological adj. Neurologist n.'),
(25610, 'n', '\r\nNeuron', ' n. (also neurone) nerve-cell.'),
(25611, 'n', '\r\nNeurosis', ' n. (pl. Neuroses) irrational or disturbed behaviour pattern, associated with nervous distress.'),
