(25925, 'n', '\r\nNorth', 'pole  n. Northernmost point of the earth''s axis of rotation.'),
(25926, 'n', '\r\nNorth', 'star  n. Pole star.'),
(25927, 'n', '\r\nNorthward', ' â€”adj. & adv. (also northwards) towards the north. â€”n. Northward direction or region.'),
(25928, 'n', '\r\nNorth-west', ' n. Part of a country or town to the north-west.'),
(25929, 'n', '\r\nNorth-west', ' â€”n. 1 point of the horizon midway between north and west. 2 direction in which this lies. â€”adj. Of, towards, or coming from the north-west. â€”adv. Towards, at, or near the north-west.'),
(25930, 'n', '\r\nNorthwester', ' n. North-west wind.'),
(25931, 'n', '\r\nNorth-westerly', ' adj. & adv. = *north-west.'),
(25932, 'n', '\r\nNorth-western', ' adj. On the north-west side.'),
(25933, 'n', '\r\nNorwegian', ' â€”n. 1 a native or national of norway. B person of norwegian descent. 2 language of norway. â€”adj. Of or relating to norway. [medieval latin norvegia from old norse, = northway]'),
(25934, 'n', '\r\nNor''wester', ' n. Northwester.'),
(25935, 'n', '\r\nNos.', ' Pl. Of *no.'),
(25936, 'n', '\r\nNose', ' â€”n. 1 organ above the mouth of a human or animal, used for smelling and breathing. 2 a sense of smell. B ability to detect a particular thing (a nose for scandal). 3 odour or perfume of wine etc. 4 front end or projecting part of a thing, e.g. Of a car or aircraft. â€”v. (-sing) 1 (usu. Foll. By about, around, etc.) Pry or search. 2 (often foll. By out) a perceive the smell of, discover by smell. B detect. 3 thrust one''s nose against or into. 4 make one''s way cautiously forward.  by a nose by a very narrow margin.'),
(25937, 'n', '\r\nNosebag', ' n. Bag containing fodder, hung on a horse''s head.'),
(25938, 'n', '\r\nNoseband', ' n. Lower band of a bridle, passing over the horse''s nose.'),
(25939, 'n', '\r\nNosebleed', ' n. Bleeding from the nose.'),
(25940, 'n', '\r\nNosedive', ' â€”n. 1 steep downward plunge by an aeroplane. 2 sudden plunge or drop. â€”v. (-ving) make a nosedive.'),
(25941, 'n', '\r\nNosegay', ' n. Small bunch of flowers.'),
(25942, 'n', '\r\nNose-to-tail', ' adj. & adv. (of vehicles) one close behind another.'),
(25943, 'n', '\r\nNosh', ' slang â€”v. Eat. â€”n. 1 food or drink. 2 us snack. [yiddish]'),
(25944, 'n', '\r\nNosh-up', ' n. Slang large meal.'),
(25945, 'n', '\r\nNostalgia', ' n. 1 (often foll. By for) yearning for a past period. 2 severe homesickness.  nostalgic adj. Nostalgically adv. [greek nostos return home]'),
(25946, 'n', '\r\nNostril', ' n. Either of the two openings in the nose. [old english, = nose-hole]'),
(25947, 'n', '\r\nNostrum', ' n. 1 quack remedy, patent medicine. 2 pet scheme, esp. For political or social reform. [latin, = â€˜of our own makeâ€™]'),
(25948, 'n', '\r\nNosy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Inquisitive, prying.  nosily adv. Nosiness n.'),
(25949, 'n', '\r\nNosy', 'parker  n. Colloq. Busybody.'),
(25950, 'n', '\r\nNot', ' adv. Expressing negation, esp.: 1 (also n''t joined to a preceding verb) following an auxiliary verb or be or (in a question) the subject of such a verb (i cannot say; she isn''t there; am i not right?). 2 used elliptically for a negative phrase etc. (is she coming? â€” i hope not; do you want it? â€” certainly not!).  not at all (in polite reply to thanks) there is no need for thanks. Not half see *half. Not quite 1 almost. 2 noticeably not (not quite proper). [contraction of *nought]'),
(25951, 'n', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of not with verbs other than auxiliaries or be is now archaic except with participles and infinitives (not knowing, i cannot say; we asked them not to come).'),
(25952, 'n', '\r\nNotable', ' â€”adj. Worthy of note; remarkable, eminent. â€”n. Eminent person.  notability n. Notably adv. [latin noto *note]'),
(25953, 'n', '\r\nNotary', ' n. (pl. -ies) (in full notary public) solicitor etc. Who attests or certifies deeds etc.  notarial adj. [latin notarius secretary]'),
(25954, 'n', '\r\nNotation', ' n. 1 representation of numbers, quantities, the pitch and duration of musical notes, etc., by symbols. 2 any set of such symbols. [latin: related to *note]'),
(25955, 'n', '\r\nNotch', ' â€”n. V-shaped indentation on an edge or surface. â€”v. 1 make notches in. 2 (usu. Foll. By up) record or score with or as with notches. [anglo-french]'),
(25956, 'n', '\r\nNote', ' â€”n. 1 brief written record as an aid to memory (often in pl.: make notes). 2 observation, usu. Unwritten, of experiences etc. (compare notes). 3 short or informal letter. 4 formal diplomatic communication. 5 short annotation or additional explanation in a book etc. 6 a = *banknote. B written promise of payment. 7 a notice, attention (worthy of note). B eminence (person of note). 8  a single musical tone of definite pitch. B written sign representing its pitch and duration. C key of a piano etc. 9 quality or tone of speaking, expressing mood or attitude etc. (note of optimism). â€”v. (-ting) 1 observe, notice; give attention to. 2 (often foll. By down) record as a thing to be remembered or observed. 3 (in passive; often foll. By for) be well known.  hit (or strike) the right note speak or act in exactly the right manner. [latin nota mark (n.), noto mark (v.)]'),
(25957, 'n', '\r\nNotebook', ' n. Small book for making notes in.'),
(25958, 'n', '\r\nNotecase', ' n. Wallet for holding banknotes.'),
(25959, 'n', '\r\nNotelet', ' n. Small folded usu. Decorated sheet of paper for an informal letter.'),
(25960, 'n', '\r\nNotepaper', ' n. Paper for writing letters.'),
(25961, 'n', '\r\nNoteworthy', ' adj. Worthy of attention; remarkable.'),
(25962, 'n', '\r\nNothing', ' â€”n. 1 not anything (nothing has been done). 2 no thing (often foll. By compl.: i see nothing that i want). 3 person or thing of no importance. 4 non-existence; what does not exist. 5 no amount; nought. â€”adv. Not at all, in no way.  be (or have) nothing to do with 1 have no connection with. 2 not be involved or associated with. For nothing 1 at no cost. 2 to no purpose. Have nothing on 1 be naked. 2 have no engagements. Nothing doing colloq. 1 no prospect of success or agreement. 2 i refuse. [old english: related to *no1, *thing]'),
(25963, 'n', '\r\nNothingness', ' n. 1 non-existence. 2 worthlessness, triviality.'),
(25964, 'n', '\r\nNotice', ' â€”n. 1 attention, observation (escaped my notice). 2 displayed sheet etc. Bearing an announcement. 3 a intimation or warning, esp. A formal one. B formal announcement or declaration of intention to end an agreement or leave employment at a specified time. 4 short published review of a new play, book, etc. â€”v. (-cing) (often foll. By that, how, etc.) Perceive, observe.  at short (or a moment''s) notice with little warning. Take notice (or no notice) show signs (or no signs) of interest. Take notice of 1 observe. 2 act upon. [latin notus known]'),
(25965, 'n', '\r\nNoticeable', ' adj. Perceptible; noteworthy.  noticeably adv.'),
(25966, 'n', '\r\nNotice-board', ' n. Board for displaying notices.'),
(25967, 'n', '\r\nNotifiable', ' adj. (of a disease etc.) That must be notified to the health authorities.'),
(25968, 'n', '\r\nNotify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 (often foll. By of or that) inform or give formal notice to (a person). 2 make known.  notification n. [latin notus known]'),
(25969, 'n', '\r\nNotion', ' n. 1 a concept or idea; conception. B opinion. C vague view or understanding. 2 inclination or intention. [latin notio: related to *notify]'),
(25970, 'n', '\r\nNotional', ' adj. Hypothetical, imaginary.  notionally adv.'),
(25971, 'n', '\r\nNotorious', ' adj. Well-known, esp. Unfavourably.  notoriety n. Notoriously adv. [latin notus known]'),
(25972, 'n', '\r\nNotwithstanding', ' â€”prep. In spite of; without prevention by. â€”adv. Nevertheless. [from *not, *withstand]'),
(25973, 'n', '\r\nNougat', ' n. Sweet made from sugar or honey, nuts, and egg-white. [french from provenÃ§al]'),
(25974, 'n', '\r\nNought', ' n. 1 digit 0; cipher. 2 poet. Or archaic nothing. [old english: related to *not, *aught]'),
(25975, 'n', '\r\nNoughts', 'and crosses  n.pl. Pencil-and-paper game in which players seek to complete a row of three noughts or three crosses.'),
(25976, 'n', '\r\nNoun', ' n.  Word used to name a person, place, or thing. [latin nomen name]'),
(25977, 'n', '\r\nNourish', ' v. 1 sustain with food. 2 foster or cherish (a feeling etc.).  nourishing adj. [latin nutrio to feed]'),
(25978, 'n', '\r\nNourishment', ' n. Sustenance, food.'),
(25979, 'n', '\r\nNous', ' n. 1 colloq. Common sense; gumption. 2 philos. Mind, intellect. [greek]'),
(25980, 'n', '\r\nNouveau', 'riche  n. (pl. Nouveaux riches pronunc. Same) person who has recently acquired (usu. Ostentatious) wealth. [french, = new rich]'),
(25981, 'n', '\r\nNouvelle', 'cuisine  n. Modern style of cookery avoiding heaviness and emphasizing presentation. [french, = new cookery]'),
(25982, 'n', '\r\nNov.', ' Abbr. November.'),
(25983, 'n', '\r\nNova', ' n. (pl. Novae or -s) star showing a sudden burst of brightness and then subsiding. [latin, = new]'),
