(26010, 'n', '\r\nNuance', ' n. Subtle shade of meaning, feeling, colour, etc. [latin nubes cloud]'),
(26011, 'n', '\r\nNub', ' n. 1 point or gist (of a matter or story). 2 (also nubble) small lump, esp. Of coal.  nubbly adj. [related to *knob]'),
(26012, 'n', '\r\nNubile', ' adj. (of a woman) marriageable or sexually attractive.  nubility n. [latin nubo become the wife of]'),
(26013, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', ' adj. 1 of, relating to, or constituting a nucleus. 2 using nuclear energy.'),
(26014, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'bomb  n. Bomb using the release of energy by nuclear fission or fusion or both.'),
(26015, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'energy  n. Energy obtained by nuclear fission or fusion.'),
(26016, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'family  n. A couple and their child or children.'),
(26017, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'fission  n. Nuclear reaction in which a heavy nucleus splits spontaneously or on impact with another particle, with the release of energy.'),
(26018, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'fuel  n. Source of nuclear energy.'),
(26019, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'fusion  n. Nuclear reaction in which atomic nuclei of low atomic number fuse to form a heavier nucleus with the release of energy.'),
(26020, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'physics  n.pl. (treated as sing.) Physics of atomic nuclei.'),
(26021, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'power  n. 1 power generated by a nuclear reactor. 2 country that has nuclear weapons.'),
(26022, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'reactor  n. Device in which a nuclear fission chain reaction is used to produce energy.'),
(26023, 'n', '\r\nNuclear', 'weapon  n. Weapon using the release of energy by nuclear fission or fusion or both.'),
(26024, 'n', '\r\nNucleate', ' â€”adj. Having a nucleus. â€”v. (-ting) form or form into a nucleus. [latin: related to *nucleus]'),
(26025, 'n', '\r\nNucleic', 'acid  n. Either of two complex organic molecules (dna and rna), present in all living cells.'),
(26026, 'n', '\r\nNucleon', ' n. Proton or neutron.'),
(26027, 'n', '\r\nNucleus', ' n. (pl. Nuclei) 1 a central part or thing round which others are collected. B kernel of an aggregate or mass. 2 initial part meant to receive additions. 3 central core of an atom. 4 large dense part of a cell, containing the genetic material. [latin, = kernel, diminutive of nux nuc- nut]'),
(26028, 'n', '\r\nNude', ' â€”adj. Naked, bare, unclothed. â€”n. 1 painting, sculpture, etc. Of a nude human figure. 2 nude person.  in the nude naked.  nudity n. [latin nudus]'),
(26029, 'n', '\r\nNudge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 prod gently with the elbow to attract attention. 2 push gradually. â€”n. Prod; gentle push. [origin unknown]'),
(26030, 'n', '\r\nNudist', ' n. Person who advocates or practises going unclothed.  nudism n.'),
(26031, 'n', '\r\nNugatory', ' adj. 1 futile, trifling. 2 inoperative; not valid. [latin nugae jests]'),
(26032, 'n', '\r\nNugget', ' n. 1 lump of gold etc., as found in the earth. 2 lump of anything. 3 something valuable. [apparently from dial. Nug lump]'),
(26033, 'n', '\r\nNuisance', ' n. Person, thing, or circumstance causing trouble or annoyance. [french, = hurt, from nuire nuis- injure, from latin noceo to hurt]'),
(26034, 'n', '\r\nNuke', ' colloq. â€”n. Nuclear weapon. â€”v. (-king) attack with nuclear weapons. [abbreviation]'),
(26035, 'n', '\r\nNull', ' adj. 1 (esp. Null and void) invalid. 2 non-existent. 3 without character or expression.  nullity n. [latin nullus none]'),
(26036, 'n', '\r\nNullify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) neutralize, invalidate.  nullification n.'),
(26037, 'n', '\r\nNumb', ' â€”adj. (often foll. By with) deprived of feeling; paralysed. â€”v. 1 make numb. 2 stupefy, paralyse.  numbness n. [obsolete nome past part. Of nim take: related to *nimble]'),
(26038, 'n', '\r\nNumber', ' â€”n. 1 a arithmetical value representing a particular quantity. B word, symbol, or figure representing this. C arithmetical value showing position in a series (registration number). 2 (often foll. By of) total count or aggregate (the number of accidents has decreased). 3 numerical reckoning (the laws of number). 4 a (in sing. Or pl.) Quantity, amount (a large number of people; only in small numbers). B (a number of) several (of). C (in pl.) Numerical preponderance (force of numbers). 5 person or thing having a place in a series, esp. A single issue of a magazine, an item in a programme, etc. 6 company, collection, group (among our number). 7 gram. A classification of words by their singular or plural forms. B such a form. â€”v. 1 include (i number you among my friends). 2 assign a number or numbers to. 3 amount to (a specified number). 4 count.  one''s days are numbered one does not have long to live. Have a person''s number colloq. Understand a person''s real motives, character, etc. One''s number is up colloq. One is doomed to die soon. Without number innumerable. [latin numerus]'),
(26039, 'n', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 4b, a number of is normally used with a plural verb: a number of problems remain.'),
(26040, 'n', '\r\nNumber', 'crunching  n. Colloq. Process of making complex calculations.'),
(26041, 'n', '\r\nNumberless', ' adj. Innumerable.'),
(26042, 'n', '\r\nNumber', 'one  â€”n. Colloq. Oneself. â€”adj. Most important (the number one priority).'),
(26043, 'n', '\r\nNumber-plate', ' n. Plate on a vehicle showing its registration number.'),
(26044, 'n', '\r\nNumerable', ' adj. That can be counted. [latin: related to *number]'),
(26045, 'n', '\r\nNumeral', ' â€”n. Symbol or group of symbols denoting a number. â€”adj. Of or denoting a number. [latin: related to *number]'),
(26046, 'n', '\r\nNumerate', ' adj. Acquainted with the basic principles of mathematics.  numeracy n. [latin numerus number, after literate]'),
(26047, 'n', '\r\nNumeration', ' n. 1 method or process of numbering. 2 calculation. [latin: related to *number]'),
(26048, 'n', '\r\nNumerator', ' n. Number above the line in a vulgar fraction showing how many of the parts indicated by the denominator are taken (e.g. 2 in 2/3). [latin: related to *number]'),
(26049, 'n', '\r\nNumerical', ' adj. Of or relating to a number or numbers.  numerically adv. [medieval latin: related to *number]'),
(26050, 'n', '\r\nNumerology', ' n. The study of the supposed occult significance of numbers.'),
(26051, 'n', '\r\nNumerous', ' adj. 1 many. 2 consisting of many. [latin: related to *number]'),
(26052, 'n', '\r\nNuminous', ' adj. 1 indicating the presence of a divinity. 2 spiritual, awe-inspiring. [latin numen deity]'),
(26053, 'n', '\r\nNumismatic', ' adj. Of or relating to coins or medals. [greek nomisma coin]'),
(26054, 'n', '\r\nNumismatics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) The study of coins or medals.  numismatist n.'),
(26055, 'n', '\r\nNumskull', ' n. Stupid person. [from *numb]'),
(26056, 'n', '\r\nNun', ' n. Member of a religious community of women living under certain vows. [latin nonna]'),
(26057, 'n', '\r\nNuncio', ' n. (pl. -s) papal ambassador. [latin nuntius messenger]'),
(26058, 'n', '\r\nNunnery', ' n. (pl. -ies) religious house of nuns.'),
(26059, 'n', '\r\nNuptial', ' â€”adj. Of marriage or weddings. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Wedding. [latin nubo nupt- wed]'),
(26060, 'n', '\r\nNurd', ' var. Of *nerd.'),
(26061, 'n', '\r\nNurse', ' â€”n. 1 person trained to care for the sick or infirm and assist doctors or dentists. 2 = *nursemaid. â€”v. (-sing) 1 a work as a nurse. B attend to (a sick person). 2 feed or be fed at the breast. 3 hold or treat carefully. 4 a foster; promote the development of. B harbour (a grievance etc.). [latin: related to *nourish]'),
