(26124, 'o', '\r\nObdurate', ' adj. 1 stubborn. 2 hardened.  obduracy n. [latin duro harden]'),
(26125, 'o', '\r\nObe', ' abbr. Officer of the order of the british empire.'),
(26126, 'o', '\r\nObedient', ' adj. 1 obeying or ready to obey. 2 submissive to another''s will.  obedience n. Obediently adv. [latin: related to *obey]'),
(26127, 'o', '\r\nObeisance', ' n. 1 bow, curtsy, or other respectful gesture. 2 homage.  obeisant adj. [french: related to *obey]'),
(26128, 'o', '\r\nObelisk', ' n. Tapering usu. Four-sided stone pillar as a monument or landmark. [greek diminutive: related to *obelus]'),
(26129, 'o', '\r\nObelus', ' n. (pl. Obeli) dagger-shaped reference mark (â€ ). [greek, = pointed pillar, *spit2]'),
(26130, 'o', '\r\nObese', ' adj. Very fat.  obesity n. [latin edo eat]'),
(26131, 'o', '\r\nObey', ' v. 1 a carry out the command of. B carry out (a command). 2 do what one is told to do. 3 be actuated by (a force or impulse). [latin obedio from audio hear]'),
(26132, 'o', '\r\nObfuscate', ' v. (-ting) 1 obscure or confuse (a mind, topic, etc.). 2 stupefy, bewilder.  obfuscation n. [latin fuscus dark]'),
(26133, 'o', '\r\nObituary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 notice of a death or deaths. 2 account of the life of a deceased person. 3 (attrib.) Of or serving as an obituary. [latin obitus death]'),
(26134, 'o', '\r\nObject', ' â€”n. 1 material thing that can be seen or touched. 2 person or thing to which action or feeling is directed (object of attention). 3 thing sought or aimed at. 4 gram. Noun or its equivalent governed by an active transitive verb or by a preposition. 5 philos. Thing external to the thinking mind or subject. â€”v. (often foll. By to, against) 1 express opposition, disapproval, or reluctance. 2 protest.  no object not forming an important or restricting factor (money no object).  objector n. [latin jacio ject- throw]'),
(26135, 'o', '\r\nObjectify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) present as an object; express in concrete form.'),
(26136, 'o', '\r\nObjection', ' n. 1 expression or feeling of opposition or disapproval. 2 objecting. 3 adverse reason or statement. [latin: related to *object]'),
(26137, 'o', '\r\nObjectionable', ' adj. 1 unpleasant, offensive. 2 open to objection.  objectionably adv.'),
(26138, 'o', '\r\nObjective', ' â€”adj. 1 external to the mind; actually existing. 2 dealing with outward things or exhibiting facts uncoloured by feelings or opinions. 3 gram. (of a case or word) in the form appropriate to the object. â€”n. 1 something sought or aimed at. 2 gram. Objective case.  objectively adv. Objectivity n. [medieval latin: related to *object]'),
(26139, 'o', '\r\nObject-lesson', ' n. Striking practical example of some principle.'),
(26140, 'o', '\r\nObjet', 'd''art  n. (pl. Objets d''art pronunc. Same) small decorative object. [french, = object of art]'),
(26141, 'o', '\r\nOblate', ' adj. Geom. (of a spheroid) flattened at the poles. [latin: related to *ob-; cf. *prolate]'),
(26142, 'o', '\r\nOblation', ' n. Thing offered to a divine being. [latin: related to *offer]'),
(26143, 'o', '\r\nObligate', ' v. (-ting) bind (a person) legally or morally (was obligated to attend). [latin: related to *oblige]'),
(26144, 'o', '\r\nObligation', ' n. 1 constraining power of a law, duty, contract, etc. 2 duty, task. 3 binding agreement. 4 indebtedness for a service or benefit (be under an obligation). [latin: related to *oblige]'),
(26145, 'o', '\r\nObligatory', ' adj. 1 binding. 2 compulsory.  obligatorily adv. [latin: related to *oblige]'),
(26146, 'o', '\r\nOblige', ' v. (-ging) 1 constrain, compel. 2 be binding on. 3 do (a person) a small favour, help. 4 (as obliged adj.) Indebted, grateful.  much obliged thank you. [latin obligo bind]'),
(26147, 'o', '\r\nObliging', ' adj. Accommodating, helpful.  obligingly adv.'),
(26148, 'o', '\r\nOblique', ' â€”adj. 1 slanting; at an angle. 2 not going straight to the point; indirect. 3 gram. (of a case) other than nominative or vocative. â€”n. Oblique stroke (/).  obliquely adv. Obliqueness n. Obliquity n. [french from latin]'),
(26149, 'o', '\r\nObliterate', ' v. (-ting) blot out, destroy, leave no clear traces of.  obliteration n. [latin oblitero erase, from litera letter]'),
(26150, 'o', '\r\nOblivion', ' n. State of having or being forgotten. [latin obliviscor forget]'),
(26151, 'o', '\r\nOblivious', ' adj. Unaware or unconscious.  obliviously adv. Obliviousness n.'),
(26152, 'o', '\r\nOblong', ' â€”adj. Rectangular with adjacent sides unequal. â€”n. Oblong figure or object. [latin oblongus longish]'),
(26153, 'o', '\r\nObloquy', ' n. 1 being generally ill spoken of. 2 abuse. [latin obloquium contradiction, from loquor speak]'),
(26154, 'o', '\r\nObnoxious', ' adj. Offensive, objectionable.  obnoxiously adv. Obnoxiousness n. [latin noxa injury]'),
(26155, 'o', '\r\nOboe', ' n. Woodwind double-reed instrument with a piercing plaintive tone.  oboist n. [french hautbois from haut high, bois wood]'),
(26156, 'o', '\r\nObscene', ' adj. 1 offensively indecent. 2 colloq. Highly offensive. 3 law (of a publication) tending to deprave or corrupt.  obscenely adv. Obscenity n. (pl. -ies). [latin obsc(a)enus abominable]'),
(26157, 'o', '\r\nObscurantism', ' n. Opposition to knowledge and enlightenment.  obscurantist n. & adj. [latin obscurus dark]'),
(26158, 'o', '\r\nObscure', ' â€”adj. 1 not clearly expressed or easily understood. 2 unexplained. 3 dark. 4 indistinct. 5 hidden; unnoticed. 6 (of a person) undistinguished, hardly known. â€”v. (-ring) 1 make obscure or unintelligible. 2 conceal.  obscurity n. [french from latin]'),
(26159, 'o', '\r\nObsequies', ' n.pl. Funeral rites. [latin obsequiae]'),
(26160, 'o', '\r\nObsequious', ' adj. Servile, fawning.  obsequiously adv. Obsequiousness n. [latin obsequor comply with]'),
(26161, 'o', '\r\nObservance', ' n. 1 keeping or performing of a law, duty, etc. 2 rite or ceremony.'),
(26162, 'o', '\r\nObservant', ' adj. 1 acute in taking notice. 2 attentive in observance.  observantly adv.'),
(26163, 'o', '\r\nObservation', ' n. 1 observing or being observed. 2 power of perception. 3 remark, comment. 4 thing observed by esp. Scientific study.  observational adj.'),
(26164, 'o', '\r\nObservatory', ' n. (pl. -ies) building for astronomical or other observation.'),
(26165, 'o', '\r\nObserve', ' v. (-ving) 1 perceive, become aware of. 2 watch carefully. 3 a follow or keep (rules etc.). B celebrate or perform (an occasion, rite, etc.). 4 remark. 5 take note of scientifically.  observable adj. [latin servo watch, keep]'),
(26166, 'o', '\r\nObserver', ' n. 1 person who observes. 2 interested spectator. 3 person who attends a meeting etc. To note the proceedings but does not participate.'),
(26167, 'o', '\r\nObsess', ' v. Fill the mind of (a person) continually; preoccupy.  obsessive adj. & n. Obsessively adv. Obsessiveness n. [latin obsideo obsess- besiege]'),
(26168, 'o', '\r\nObsession', ' n. 1 obsessing or being obsessed. 2 persistent idea dominating a person''s mind.  obsessional adj. Obsessionally adv.'),
(26169, 'o', '\r\nObsidian', ' n. Dark glassy rock formed from lava. [latin from obsius, discoverer of a similar stone]'),
(26170, 'o', '\r\nObsolescent', ' adj. Becoming obsolete.  obsolescence n. [latin soleo be accustomed]'),
(26171, 'o', '\r\nObsolete', ' adj. No longer used, antiquated.'),
(26172, 'o', '\r\nObstacle', ' n. Thing that obstructs progress. [latin obsto stand in the way]'),
(26173, 'o', '\r\nObstetrician', ' n. Specialist in obstetrics.'),
(26174, 'o', '\r\nObstetrics', ' n.pl. (treated as sing.) Branch of medicine and surgery dealing with childbirth.  obstetric adj. [latin obstetrix midwife, from obsto be present]'),
(26175, 'o', '\r\nObstinate', ' adj. 1 stubborn, intractable. 2 firmly continuing in one''s action or opinion despite advice.  obstinacy n. Obstinately adv. [latin obstino persist]'),
(26176, 'o', '\r\nObstreperous', ' adj. 1 turbulent, unruly. 2 noisy.  obstreperously adv. Obstreperousness n. [latin obstrepo shout at]'),
(26177, 'o', '\r\nObstruct', ' v. 1 block up; make hard or impossible to pass along or through. 2 prevent or retard the progress of. [latin obstruo obstruct- block up]'),
(26178, 'o', '\r\nObstruction', ' n. 1 obstructing or being obstructed. 2 thing that obstructs, blockage. 3 sport act of unlawfully obstructing another player.'),
(26179, 'o', '\r\nObstructive', ' adj. Causing or intended to cause an obstruction.  obstructively adv. Obstructiveness n.'),
(26180, 'o', '\r\nObtain', ' v. 1 acquire, secure; have granted to one, get. 2 be in vogue, prevail.  obtainable adj. [latin teneo hold]'),
(26181, 'o', '\r\nObtrude', ' v. (-ding) 1 be or become obtrusive. 2 (often foll. By on, upon) thrust (oneself, a matter, etc.) Importunately forward.  obtrusion n. [latin obtrudo thrust against]'),
(26182, 'o', '\r\nObtrusive', ' adj. 1 unpleasantly noticeable. 2 obtruding oneself.  obtrusively adv. Obtrusiveness n.'),
(26183, 'o', '\r\nObtuse', ' adj. 1 dull-witted. 2 (of an angle) between 90Â° and 180Â°. 3 of blunt form; not sharp-pointed or sharp-edged.  obtuseness n. [latin obtundo obtus- beat against, blunt]'),
(26184, 'o', '\r\nObverse', ' n. 1 counterpart, opposite. 2 side of a coin or medal etc. Bearing the head or principal design. 3 front, proper, or top side of a thing. [latin obverto obvers- turn towards]'),
(26185, 'o', '\r\nObviate', ' v. (-ting) get round or do away with (a need, inconvenience, etc.). [latin obvio prevent]'),
(26186, 'o', '\r\nObvious', ' adj. Easily seen, recognized, or understood.  obviously adv. Obviousness n. [latin ob viam in the way]'),
(26187, 'o', '\r\nOc', ' abbr. Officer commanding.'),
(26188, 'o', '\r\nOc-', ' see *ob-.'),
(26189, 'o', '\r\nOcarina', ' n. Small egg-shaped musical wind-instrument. [italian oca goose]'),
(26190, 'o', '\r\nOccasion', ' â€”n. 1 a special event or happening. B time of this. 2 reason, need. 3 suitable juncture, opportunity. 4 immediate but subordinate cause. â€”v. Cause, esp. Incidentally.  on occasion now and then; when the need arises. [latin occido occas- go down]'),
(26191, 'o', '\r\nOccasional', ' adj. 1 happening irregularly and infrequently. 2 made or meant for, or acting on, a special occasion.  occasionally adv.'),
(26192, 'o', '\r\nOccasional', 'table  n. Small table for use as required.'),
(26193, 'o', '\r\nOccident', ' n. Poet. Or rhet. 1 (prec. By the) west. 2 western europe. 3 europe and america as distinct from the orient. [latin occidens -entis setting, sunset, west]'),
(26194, 'o', '\r\nOccidental', ' â€”adj. 1 of the occident. 2 western. â€”n. Native of the occident.'),
(26195, 'o', '\r\nOcciput', ' n. Back of the head.  occipital adj. [latin caput head]'),
(26196, 'o', '\r\nOcclude', ' v. (-ding) 1 stop up or close. 2 chem. Absorb and retain (gases). 3 (as occluded adj.) Meteorol. (of a frontal system) formed when a cold front overtakes a warm front, raising warm air from ground level.  occlusion n. [latin occludo occlus- close up]'),
(26197, 'o', '\r\nOccult', ' adj. 1 involving the supernatural; mystical. 2 esoteric.  the occult occult phenomena generally. [latin occulo occult- hide]'),
(26198, 'o', '\r\nOccupant', ' n. Person who occupies, esp. Lives in, a place etc.  occupancy n. (pl. -ies). [latin: related to *occupy]'),
(26199, 'o', '\r\nOccupation', ' n. 1 person''s employment or profession. 2 pastime. 3 occupying or being occupied. 4 taking or holding of a country etc. By force.'),
(26200, 'o', '\r\nOccupational', ' adj. 1 of or connected with one''s occupation. 2 (of a disease, hazard, etc.) Connected with one''s occupation.'),
(26201, 'o', '\r\nOccupational', 'therapy  n. Programme of mental or physical activity to assist recovery from disease or injury.'),
(26202, 'o', '\r\nOccupier', ' n. Person living in a house etc. As its owner or tenant.'),
(26203, 'o', '\r\nOccupy', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 live in; be the tenant of. 2 take up or fill (space, time, or a place). 3 hold (a position or office). 4 take military possession of. 5 place oneself in (a building etc.) Forcibly or without authority as a protest. 6 keep busy or engaged. [latin occupo seize]'),
(26204, 'o', '\r\nOccur', ' v. (-rr-) 1 come into being as an event or process. 2 exist or be encountered in some place or conditions. 3 (foll. By to) come into the mind of. [latin occurro befall]'),
(26205, 'o', '\r\nOccurrence', ' n. 1 occurring. 2 incident or event.'),
(26206, 'o', '\r\nOcean', ' n. 1 large expanse of sea, esp. Each of the main areas called the atlantic, pacific, indian, arctic, and antarctic oceans. 2 (often in pl.) Colloq. Very large expanse or quantity.  oceanic adj. [greek okeanos]'),
(26207, 'o', '\r\nOcean-going', ' adj. (of a ship) able to cross oceans.'),
(26208, 'o', '\r\nOceanography', ' n. The study of the oceans.  oceanographer n.'),
(26209, 'o', '\r\nOcelot', ' n. Leopard-like cat of s. And central america. [french from nahuatl]'),
(26210, 'o', '\r\nOcher', ' n. (brit. Ochre) 1 earth used as yellow, brown, or red pigment. 2 pale brownish-yellow colour.  ochreous adj. [greek okhra]'),
(26211, 'o', '\r\nOchre', ' n. (us ocher) 1 earth used as yellow, brown, or red pigment. 2 pale brownish-yellow colour.  ochreous adj. [greek okhra]'),
(26212, 'o', '\r\nO''clock', ' adv. Of the clock (used to specify the hour) (6 o''clock).'),
(26213, 'o', '\r\nOct.', ' Abbr. October.'),
(26214, 'o', '\r\nOcta-', ' comb. Form (also oct- before a vowel) eight. [latin octo, greek okto eight]'),
(26215, 'o', '\r\nOctagon', ' n. Plane figure with eight sides and angles.  octagonal adj. [greek: related to *octa-, -gonos -angled]'),
(26216, 'o', '\r\nOctahedron', ' n. (pl. -s) solid figure contained by eight (esp. Triangular) plane faces.  octahedral adj. [greek]'),
(26217, 'o', '\r\nOctane', ' n. Colourless inflammable hydrocarbon occurring in petrol. [from *octa-]'),
(26218, 'o', '\r\nOctane', 'number  n. (also octane rating) figure indicating the antiknock properties of a fuel.'),
(26219, 'o', '\r\nOctave', ' n. 1 mus. A interval between (and including) two notes, one having twice or half the frequency of vibration of the other. B eight notes occupying this interval. C each of the two notes at the extremes of this interval. 2 eight-line stanza. [latin octavus eighth]'),
(26220, 'o', '\r\nOctavo', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 size of a book or page given by folding a sheet of standard size three times to form eight leaves. 2 book or sheet of this size. [latin: related to *octave]'),
(26221, 'o', '\r\nOctet', ' n. (also octette) 1 a musical composition for eight performers. B the performers. 2 group of eight. [italian or german: related to *octa-]'),
(26222, 'o', '\r\nOcto-', ' comb. Form (also oct- before a vowel) eight. [see *octa-]'),
(26223, 'o', '\r\nOctober', ' n. Tenth month of the year. [latin octo eight, originally the 8th month of the roman year]'),
(26224, 'o', '\r\nOctogenarian', ' n. Person from 80 to 89 years old. [latin octogeni 80 each]'),
(26225, 'o', '\r\nOctopus', ' n. (pl. -puses) sea mollusc with eight suckered tentacles. [greek: related to *octo-, pous foot]'),
(26226, 'o', '\r\nOcular', ' adj. Of, for, or by the eyes; visual. [latin oculus eye]'),
(26227, 'o', '\r\nOculist', ' n. Specialist in the treatment of the eyes.'),
(26228, 'o', '\r\nOd', ' slang â€”n. Drug overdose. â€”v. (od''s, od''d, od''ing) take an overdose. [abbreviation]'),
(26229, 'o', '\r\nOdd', ' adj. 1 strange, remarkable, eccentric. 2 casual, occasional (odd jobs; odd moments). 3 not normally considered; unconnected (in some odd corner; picks up odd bargains). 4 a (of numbers) not integrally divisible by two, e.g. 1, 3, 5. B bearing such a number (no parking on odd dates). 5 left over when the rest have been distributed or divided into pairs (odd sock). 6 detached from a set or series (odd volumes). 7 (appended to a number, sum, weight, etc.) Somewhat more than (forty odd; forty-odd people). 8 by which a round number, given sum, etc., is exceeded (we have 102 â€“ do you want the odd 2?).  oddly adv. Oddness n. [old norse oddi angle, point, third or odd number]'),
(26230, 'o', '\r\nOddball', ' n. Colloq. Eccentric person.'),
(26231, 'o', '\r\nOddity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 strange person, thing, or occurrence. 2 peculiar trait. 3 strangeness.'),
(26232, 'o', '\r\nOdd', 'man out  n. Person or thing differing from the others in a group in some respect.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(26233, 'o', '\r\nOddment', ' n. 1 odd article; something left over. 2 (in pl.) Miscellaneous articles.'),
(26234, 'o', '\r\nOdds', ' n.pl. 1 ratio between the amounts staked by the parties to a bet, based on the expected probability either way. 2 balance of probability or advantage (the odds are against it; the odds are in your favour). 3 difference giving an advantage (it makes no odds).  at odds (often foll. By with) in conflict or at variance. Over the odds above the normal price etc. [apparently from *odd]'),
(26235, 'o', '\r\nOdds', 'and ends  n.pl. Miscellaneous articles or remnants.'),
(26236, 'o', '\r\nOdds-on', ' â€”n. State when success is more likely than failure. â€”adj. (of a chance) better than even; likely.'),
(26237, 'o', '\r\nOde', ' n. Lyric poem of exalted style and tone. [greek oide song]'),
(26238, 'o', '\r\nOdious', ' adj. Hateful, repulsive.  odiously adv. Odiousness n. [related to *odium]'),
(26239, 'o', '\r\nOdium', ' n. Widespread dislike or disapproval of a person or action. [latin, = hatred]'),
(26240, 'o', '\r\nOdometer', ' n. Us = *milometer. [greek hodos way]'),
(26241, 'o', '\r\nOdor', ' n. (brit. Odour) 1 smell or fragrance. 2 quality or trace (an odour of intolerance). 3 regard, repute (in bad odour).  odorous adj. Odourless adj. [latin odor]'),
(26242, 'o', '\r\nOdoriferous', ' adj. Diffusing a (usu. Agreeable) odour. [latin: related to *odour]'),
(26243, 'o', '\r\nOdour', ' n. (us odor) 1 smell or fragrance. 2 quality or trace (an odour of intolerance). 3 regard, repute (in bad odour).  odorous adj. Odourless adj. [latin odor]'),
(26244, 'o', '\r\nOdyssey', ' n. (pl. -s) long adventurous journey. [title of the homeric epic poem on the adventures of odysseus]'),
(26245, 'o', '\r\nOecd', ' abbr. Organization for economic cooperation and development.'),
(26246, 'o', '\r\nOedema', ' n. (us edema) accumulation of excess fluid in body tissues, causing swelling. [greek oideo swell]'),
(26247, 'o', '\r\nOedipus', 'complex  n. Child''s, esp. A boy''s, subconscious sexual desire for the parent of the opposite sex.  oedipal adj. [greek oidipous, who unknowingly married his mother]'),
(26248, 'o', '\r\nO''er', ' adv. & prep. Poet. = *over. [contraction]'),
(26249, 'o', '\r\nOesophagus', ' n. (us esophagus) (pl. -gi or -guses) passage from the mouth to the stomach; gullet. [greek]'),
(26250, 'o', '\r\nOestrogen', ' n. (us estrogen) 1 sex hormone developing and maintaining female characteristics of the body. 2 this produced artificially for use in medicine. [greek oistros frenzy, *-gen]'),
(26251, 'o', '\r\nOestrus', ' n. (also oestrum, us estrus) recurring period of sexual receptivity in many female mammals.  oestrous adj. [greek oistros frenzy]'),
(26252, 'o', '\r\nÅ’uvre', ' n. Works of a creative artist regarded collectively. [french, = work, from latin opera]'),
(26253, 'o', '\r\nOf', ' prep. Expressing: 1 origin or cause (paintings of turner; died of cancer). 2 material or substance (house of cards; built of bricks). 3 belonging or connection (thing of the past; articles of clothing; head of the business). 4 identity or close relation (city of rome; a pound of apples; a fool of a man). 5 removal or separation (north of the city; got rid of them; robbed us of Â£1000). 6 reference or direction (beware of the dog; suspected of lying; very good of you; short of money). 7 objective relation (love of music; in search of peace). 8 partition, classification, or inclusion (no more of that; part of the story; this sort of book). 9 description, quality, or condition (the hour of prayer; person of tact; girl of ten; on the point of leaving). 10 us time in relation to the following hour (a quarter of three).  be of possess, give rise to (is of great interest). Of an evening (or morning etc.) Colloq. 1 on most evenings (or mornings etc.). 2 at some time in the evenings (or mornings etc.). Of late recently. Of old formerly. [old english]'),
(26254, 'o', '\r\nOf-', ' see *ob-.'),
(26255, 'o', '\r\nOff.', ' Abbr. 1 office. 2 officer.'),
(26256, 'o', '\r\nOff', ' â€”adv. 1 away; at or to a distance (drove off; 3 miles off). 2 out of position; not on, touching, or attached; loose, separate, gone (has come off; take your coat off). 3 so as to be rid of (sleep it off). 4 so as to break continuity or continuance; discontinued, stopped (turn off the radio; take a day off; the game is off). 5 not available on a menu etc. (chips are off). 6 to the end; entirely; so as to be clear (clear off; finish off; pay off). 7 situated as regards money, supplies, etc. (well off). 8 off stage (noises off). 9 (of food etc.) Beginning to decay. â€”prep. 1 a from; away, down, or up from (fell off the chair; took something off the price). B not on (off the pitch). 2 a temporarily relieved of or abstaining from (off duty). B temporarily not attracted by (off his food). C not achieving (off form). 3 using as a source or means of support (live off the land). 4 leading from; not far from (a street off the strand). 5 at a short distance to sea from (sank off cape horn). â€”adj. 1 far, further (off side of the wall). 2 (of a part of a vehicle, animal, or road) right (the off front wheel). 3 cricket designating the half of the field (as divided lengthways through the pitch) to which the striker''s feet are pointed. 4 colloq. A annoying, unfair (that''s really off). B somewhat unwell (feeling a bit off).  â€”n. 1 the off side in cricket. 2 start of a race.  off and on intermittently; now and then. Off the cuff see *cuff1. Off the peg see *peg. [var. Of *of]'),
(26257, 'o', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of off of for the preposition off (sense 1a), e.g. Picked it up off of the floor, is non-standard and should be avoided.'),
(26258, 'o', '\r\nOffal', ' n. 1 less valuable edible parts of a carcass, esp. The heart, liver, etc. 2 refuse, scraps. [dutch afval: related to *off, *fall]'),
(26259, 'o', '\r\nOffbeat', ' â€”adj. 1 not coinciding with the beat. 2 eccentric, unconventional. â€”n. Any of the unaccented beats in a bar.'),
(26260, 'o', '\r\nOff-centre', ' adj. & adv. Not quite centrally placed.'),
(26261, 'o', '\r\nOff', 'chance  n. (prec. By the) remote possibility.'),
(26262, 'o', '\r\nOff', 'colour  predic. Adj. 1 unwell. 2 us somewhat indecent.'),
(26263, 'o', '\r\nOffcut', ' n. Remnant of timber, paper, etc., after cutting.'),
(26264, 'o', '\r\nOff-day', ' n. Colloq. Day when one is not at one''s best.'),
(26265, 'o', '\r\nOffence', ' n. (us offense) 1 illegal act; transgression. 2 upsetting of feelings, insult; umbrage (give offence; take offence). 3 aggressive action. [related to *offend]'),
(26266, 'o', '\r\nOffend', ' v. 1 cause offence to, upset. 2 displease, anger. 3 (often foll. By against) do wrong; transgress.  offender n. Offending adj. [latin offendo offens- strike against, displease]'),
(26267, 'o', '\r\nOffense', ' n. (brit. Offence) 1 illegal act; transgression. 2 upsetting of feelings, insult; umbrage (give offence; take offence). 3 aggressive action. [related to *offend]'),
(26268, 'o', '\r\nOffensive', ' â€”adj. 1 causing offence; insulting. 2 disgusting. 3 a aggressive, attacking. B (of a weapon) for attacking. â€”n. Aggressive action, attitude, or campaign.  offensively adv. Offensiveness n.'),
(26269, 'o', '\r\nOffer', ' â€”v. 1 present for acceptance, refusal, or consideration. 2 (foll. By to + infin.) Express readiness or show intention. 3 provide; give an opportunity for. 4 make available for sale. 5 present to the attention. 6 present (a sacrifice etc.). 7 present itself; occur (as opportunity offers). 8 attempt (violence, resistance, etc.). â€”n. 1 expression of readiness to do or give if desired, or to buy or sell. 2 amount offered. 3 proposal (esp. Of marriage). 4 bid.  on offer for sale at a certain (esp. Reduced) price. [latin offero oblat-]'),
(26270, 'o', '\r\nOffering', ' n. 1 contribution or gift, esp. Of money. 2 thing offered as a sacrifice etc.'),
(26271, 'o', '\r\nOffertory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 offering of the bread and wine at the eucharist. 2 collection of money at a religious service. [church latin: related to *offer]'),
(26272, 'o', '\r\nOffhand', ' â€”adj. Curt or casual in manner. â€”adv. Without preparation or thought (can''t say offhand).  offhanded adj. Offhandedly adv. Offhandedness n.'),
(26273, 'o', '\r\nOffice', ' n. 1 room or building used as a place of business, esp. For clerical or administrative work. 2 room or area for a particular business (ticket office). 3 local centre of a large business (our london office). 4 position with duties attached to it. 5 tenure of an official position (hold office). 6 (office) quarters, staff, or collective authority of a government department etc. (foreign office). 7 duty, task, function. 8 (usu. In pl.) Piece of kindness; service (esp. Through the good offices of). 9 authorized form of worship. [latin officium from opus work, facio fic- do]'),
(26274, 'o', '\r\nOfficer', ' n. 1 person holding a position of authority or trust, esp. One with a commission in the army, navy, air force, etc. 2 policeman or policewoman. 3 holder of a post in a society (e.g. The president or secretary).'),
