(26399, 'o', '\r\nOmnivorous', ' adj. 1 feeding on both plant and animal material. 2 reading, observing, etc. Everything that comes one''s way.  omnivore n. Omnivorousness n. [latin voro devour]'),
(26400, 'o', '\r\nOn', ' â€”prep. 1 (so as to be) supported by, attached to, covering, or enclosing (sat on a chair; stuck on the wall; rings on her fingers; leaned on his elbow). 2 carried with; about the person of (have you a pen on you?). 3 (of time) exactly at; during (on 29 may; on the hour; on schedule; closed on tuesday). 4 immediately after or before (i saw them on my return). 5 as a result of (on further examination).'),
(26401, 'o', '\r\nOnanism', ' n. Literary masturbation. [onan, biblical person]'),
(26402, 'o', '\r\nOnc', ' abbr. Ordinary national certificate.'),
(26403, 'o', '\r\nOnce', ' â€”adv. 1 on one occasion only. 2 at some point or period in the past. 3 ever or at all (if you once forget it). 4 multiplied by one. â€”conj. As soon as. â€”n. One time or occasion (just the once).  all at once 1 suddenly. 2 all together. At once 1 immediately. 2 simultaneously. For once on this (or that) occasion, even if at no other. Once again (or more) another time. Once and for all (or once for all) in a final manner, esp. After much hesitation. Once (or every once) in a while from time to time. Once or twice a few times. Once upon a time at some unspecified time in the past. [originally genitive of *one]'),
(26404, 'o', '\r\nOnce-over', ' n. Colloq. Rapid inspection.'),
(26405, 'o', '\r\nOncogene', ' n. Gene which can transform a cell into a cancer cell. [greek ogkos mass]'),
(26406, 'o', '\r\nOncology', ' n. The study of tumours. [greek ogkos mass]'),
(26407, 'o', '\r\nOncoming', ' adj. Approaching from the front.'),
(26408, 'o', '\r\nOnd', ' abbr. Ordinary national diploma.'),
(26409, 'o', '\r\nOne', ' â€”adj. 1 single and integral in number. 2 (with a noun implied) a single person or thing of the kind expressed or implied (one of the best; a nasty one). 3 particular but undefined, esp. As contrasted with another (that is one view; one night last week). 4 only such (the one man who can do it). 5 forming a unity (one and undivided). 6 identical; the same (of one opinion). â€”n. 1 a lowest cardinal number. B thing numbered with it. 2 unity; a unit (one is half of two; came in ones and twos).'),
(26410, 'o', '\r\nOne', 'another  pron. Each the other or others (as a formula of reciprocity: love one another).'),
(26411, 'o', '\r\nOne-armed', 'bandit  n. Colloq. Fruit machine with a long handle.'),
(26412, 'o', '\r\nOne-horse', ' attrib. Adj. 1 using a single horse. 2 colloq. Small, poorly equipped.'),
(26413, 'o', '\r\nOne-horse', 'race  n. Contest in which one competitor is far superior to all the others.'),
