(26441, 'o', '\r\nOnside', ' adj. (of a player in a field game) not offside.'),
(26442, 'o', '\r\nOnslaught', ' n. Fierce attack. [dutch: related to *on, slag blow]'),
(26443, 'o', '\r\nOn-street', ' adj. (esp. Of parking) along a street.'),
(26444, 'o', '\r\nOnto', ' prep. = on to.'),
(26445, 'o', '\r\nUsage', ' the form onto is still not fully accepted in the way that into is, although it is in wide use. It is however useful in distinguishing sense as between we drove on to the beach (i.e. In that direction) and we drove onto the beach (i.e. In contact with it).'),
(26446, 'o', '\r\nOntology', ' n. Branch of metaphysics dealing with the nature of being.  ontological adj. Ontologically adv. Ontologist n. [greek ont- being]'),
(26447, 'o', '\r\nOnus', ' n. (pl. Onuses) burden, duty, responsibility. [latin]'),
(26448, 'o', '\r\nOnward', ' â€”adv. (also onwards) 1 forward, advancing. 2 into the future (from 1985 onwards). â€”adj. Forward, advancing.'),
(26449, 'o', '\r\nOnyx', ' n. Semiprecious variety of agate with coloured layers. [greek onux]'),
(26450, 'o', '\r\nOodles', ' n.pl. Colloq. Very great amount. [origin unknown]'),
(26451, 'o', '\r\nOoh', ' int. Expressing surprise, delight, pain, etc. [natural exclamation]'),
(26452, 'o', '\r\nOolite', ' n. Granular limestone.  oolitic adj. [greek oion egg]'),
(26453, 'o', '\r\nOompah', ' n. Colloq. Rhythmical sound of deep brass instruments. [imitative]'),
(26454, 'o', '\r\nOomph', ' n. Slang 1 energy, enthusiasm. 2 attractiveness, esp. Sex appeal. [origin uncertain]'),
(26455, 'o', '\r\nOops', ' int. Colloq. On making an obvious mistake. [natural exclamation]'),
(26456, 'o', '\r\nOoze1', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 trickle or leak slowly out. 2 (of a substance) exude fluid. 3 (often foll. By with) exude (a feeling) freely (oozed (with) charm). â€”n. Sluggish flow.  oozy adj. [old english]'),
(26457, 'o', '\r\nOoze2', ' n. Wet mud.  oozy adj. [old english]'),
(26458, 'o', '\r\nOp', ' n. Colloq. Operation. [abbreviation]'),
(26459, 'o', '\r\nOp.', ' Abbr. Opus.'),
(26460, 'o', '\r\nOp-', ' see *ob-.'),
(26461, 'o', '\r\nOpacity', ' n. Opaqueness. [latin: related to *opaque]'),
(26462, 'o', '\r\nOpal', ' n. Semiprecious stone usu. Of a milky or bluish colour and sometimes showing changing colours. [latin]'),
(26463, 'o', '\r\nOpalescent', ' adj. Iridescent.  opalescence n.'),
(26464, 'o', '\r\nOpaline', ' adj. Opal-like, opalescent.'),
(26465, 'o', '\r\nOpaque', ' adj. (opaquer, opaquest) 1 not transmitting light. 2 impenetrable to sight. 3 unintelligible. 4 unintelligent, stupid.  opaquely adv. Opaqueness n. [latin opacus shaded]'),
(26466, 'o', '\r\nOp', 'art  n. Colloq. = *optical art. [abbreviation]'),
(26467, 'o', '\r\nOp.', 'Cit.  Abbr. In the work already quoted. [latin opere citato]'),
(26468, 'o', '\r\nOpec', ' abbr. Organization of petroleum exporting countries.'),
(26469, 'o', '\r\nOpen', ' â€”adj. 1 not closed, locked, or blocked up; allowing access. 2 unenclosed, unconfined, unobstructed (the open road; open views). 3 a uncovered, bare, exposed (open drain; open wound). B (of a goal etc.) Unprotected, undefended. 4 undisguised, public, manifest (open hostilities). 5 expanded, unfolded, or spread out (had the map open on the table). 6 (of a fabric) not close; with gaps. 7 a frank and communicative. B open-minded. 8 a accessible to visitors or customers; ready for business. B (of a meeting) admitting all, not restricted to members etc. 9 (of a race, competition, scholarship, etc.) Unrestricted as to who may compete. 10 (foll. By to) a willing to receive (is open to offers). B (of a choice, offer, or opportunity) available (three courses open to us). C vulnerable to, allowing of (open to abuse; open to doubt). 11 (of a return ticket) not restricted as to the day of travel. â€”v. 1 make or become open or more open. 2 (foll. By into, on to, etc.) (of a door, room, etc.) Give access as specified (opened on to a patio). 3 a start, establish, or set going (a business, activity, etc.) (opened a new shop; opened fire). B start (conference opens today). 4 (often foll. By with) start; begin speaking, writing, etc. (show opens with a song; he opened with a joke). 5 ceremonially declare (a building etc.) In use. â€”n. 1 (prec. By the) a open space, country, or air. B public notice; general attention (esp. Into the open). 2 open championship or competition etc.  open a person''s eyes enlighten a person. Open out 1 unfold. 2 develop, expand. 3 become communicative. Open up 1 unlock (premises). 2 make accessible. 3 reveal; bring to notice. 4 accelerate. 5 begin shooting or sounding.  openness n. [old english]'),
(26470, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'air  n. Outdoors.  open-air attrib. Adj.'),
(26471, 'o', '\r\nOpen-and-shut', ' adj. Straightforward.'),
(26472, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'book  n. Person who is easily understood.'),
(26473, 'o', '\r\nOpencast', ' adj. (of a mine or mining) with removal of the surface layers and working from above, not from shafts.'),
(26474, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'college  n. College offering training and vocational courses mainly by correspondence.'),
(26475, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'day  n. Day when the public may visit a place normally closed to them.'),
(26476, 'o', '\r\nOpen-door', ' attrib. Adj. Open, accessible.'),
(26477, 'o', '\r\nOpen-ended', ' adj. Having no predetermined limit.'),
(26478, 'o', '\r\nOpener', ' n. 1 device for opening tins, bottles, etc. 2 colloq. First item on a programme etc.'),
(26479, 'o', '\r\nOpen-handed', ' adj. Generous.'),
(26480, 'o', '\r\nOpen-hearted', ' adj. Frank and kindly.'),
(26481, 'o', '\r\nOpen-heart', 'surgery  n. Surgery with the heart exposed and the blood made to bypass it.'),
(26482, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'house  n. Hospitality for all visitors.'),
(26483, 'o', '\r\nOpening', ' â€”n. 1 aperture or gap. 2 opportunity. 3 beginning; initial part. â€”attrib. Adj. Initial, first (opening remarks).'),
(26484, 'o', '\r\nOpening-time', ' n. Time at which public houses may legally open for custom.'),
(26485, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'letter  n. Letter of protest etc. Addressed to an individual and published in a newspaper etc.'),
(26486, 'o', '\r\nOpenly', ' adv. 1 frankly. 2 publicly.'),
(26487, 'o', '\r\nOpen-minded', ' adj. Accessible to new ideas; unprejudiced.'),
(26488, 'o', '\r\nOpen-mouthed', ' adj. Aghast with surprise.'),
(26489, 'o', '\r\nOpen-plan', ' adj. (of a house, office, etc.) Having large undivided rooms.'),
(26490, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'prison  n. Prison with few restraints on prisoners'' movements.'),
(26491, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'question  n. Matter on which different views are legitimate.'),
(26492, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'sandwich  n. Sandwich without a top slice of bread.'),
(26493, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'sea  n. Expanse of sea away from land.'),
(26494, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'secret  n. Supposed secret known to many.'),
(26495, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'society  n. Society with freedom of belief.'),
(26496, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'university  n. University teaching mainly by broadcasting and correspondence, and open to those without academic qualifications.'),
(26497, 'o', '\r\nOpen', 'verdict  n. Verdict affirming that a crime has been committed but not specifying the criminal or (in case of violent death) the cause.'),
(26498, 'o', '\r\nOpenwork', ' n. Pattern with intervening spaces in metal, leather, lace, etc.'),
(26499, 'o', '\r\nOpera1', ' n. 1 a drama set to music for singers and instrumentalists. B this as a genre. 2 opera-house. [italian from latin, = labour, work]'),
(26500, 'o', '\r\nOpera2', ' pl. Of *opus.'),
(26501, 'o', '\r\nOperable', ' adj. 1 that can be operated. 2 suitable for treatment by surgical operation. [latin: related to *operate]'),
(26502, 'o', '\r\nOpera-glasses', ' n.pl. Small binoculars for use at the opera or theatre.'),
(26503, 'o', '\r\nOpera-house', ' n. Theatre for operas.'),
(26504, 'o', '\r\nOperate', ' v. (-ting) 1 work, control. 2 be in action; function. 3 a perform a surgical operation. B conduct a military etc. Action. C be active in business etc. 4 bring about. [latin operor work: related to *opus]'),
(26505, 'o', '\r\nOperatic', ' adj. Of or like an opera or opera singer (an operatic voice).  operatically adv.'),
(26506, 'o', '\r\nOperatics', ' n.pl. Production and performance of operas.'),
(26507, 'o', '\r\nOperating', 'system  n. Basic software that enables the running of a computer program.'),
(26508, 'o', '\r\nOperating', 'theatre  n. Room for surgical operations.'),
(26509, 'o', '\r\nOperation', ' n. 1 action, scope, or method of working or operating. 2 active process. 3 piece of work, esp. One in a series (begin operations). 4 act of surgery on a patient. 5 military manoeuvre. 6 financial transaction. 7 state of functioning (in operation). 8 subjection of a number etc. To a process affecting its value or form, e.g. Multiplication. [latin: related to *operate]'),
(26510, 'o', '\r\nOperational', ' adj. 1 of or engaged in or used for operations. 2 able or ready to function.  operationally adv.'),
(26511, 'o', '\r\nOperational', 'research  n. The application of scientific principles to business etc. Management.'),
(26512, 'o', '\r\nOperations', 'research  n. = *operational research.'),
(26513, 'o', '\r\nOperative', ' â€”adj. 1 in operation; having effect. 2 having the main relevance (â€˜mayâ€™ is the operative word). 3 of or by surgery. â€”n. Worker, esp. A skilled one. [latin: related to *operate]'),
(26514, 'o', '\r\nOperator', ' n. 1 person operating a machine etc., esp. Connecting lines in a telephone exchange. 2 person engaging in business. 3 colloq. Person acting in a specified way (smooth operator). 4 symbol or function denoting an operation in mathematics, computing, etc.'),
(26515, 'o', '\r\nOperculum', ' n. (pl. -cula) 1 fish''s gill-cover. 2 any of various other parts covering or closing an aperture in an animal or plant. [latin operio cover (v.)]'),
(26516, 'o', '\r\nOperetta', ' n. 1 light opera. 2 one-act or short opera. [italian, diminutive of *opera1]'),
(26517, 'o', '\r\nOphidian', ' â€”n. Member of a suborder of reptiles including snakes. â€”adj. 1 of this order. 2 snakelike. [greek ophis snake]'),
(26518, 'o', '\r\nOphthalmia', ' n. Inflammation of the eye. [greek ophthalmos eye]'),
(26519, 'o', '\r\nOphthalmic', ' adj. Of or relating to the eye and its diseases.'),
(26520, 'o', '\r\nOphthalmic', 'optician  n. Optician qualified to prescribe as well as dispense spectacles etc.'),
(26521, 'o', '\r\nOphthalmology', ' n. The study of the eye.  ophthalmologist n.'),
(26522, 'o', '\r\nOphthalmoscope', ' n. Instrument for examining the eye.'),
(26523, 'o', '\r\nOpiate', ' â€”adj. 1 containing opium. 2 narcotic, soporific. â€”n. 1 drug containing opium, usu. To ease pain or induce sleep. 2 soothing influence. [latin: related to *opium]'),
(26524, 'o', '\r\nOpine', ' v. (-ning) (often foll. By that) literary hold or express as an opinion. [latin opinor believe]'),
(26525, 'o', '\r\nOpinion', ' n. 1 unproven belief. 2 view held as probable. 3 what one thinks about something. 4 piece of professional advice (a second opinion). 5 estimation (low opinion of). [latin: related to *opine]'),
(26526, 'o', '\r\nOpinionated', ' adj. Dogmatic in one''s opinions.'),
(26527, 'o', '\r\nOpinion', 'poll  n. Assessment of public opinion by questioning a representative sample.'),
(26528, 'o', '\r\nOpium', ' n. Drug made from the juice of a certain poppy, used esp. As an analgesic and narcotic. [latin from greek opion]'),
(26529, 'o', '\r\nOpossum', ' n. 1 tree-living american marsupial. 2 austral. & nz = *possum 2. [virginian indian]'),
(26530, 'o', '\r\nOpp.', ' Abbr. Opposite.'),
(26531, 'o', '\r\nOpponent', ' n. Person who opposes. [latin oppono opposit- set against]'),
(26532, 'o', '\r\nOpportune', ' adj. 1 well-chosen or especially favourable (opportune moment). 2 (of an action or event) well-timed. [latin opportunus (of the wind) driving towards the *port1]'),
(26533, 'o', '\r\nOpportunism', ' n. Adaptation of one''s policy or judgement to circumstances or opportunity, esp. Regardless of principle.  opportunist n. Opportunistic adj. Opportunistically adv.'),
(26534, 'o', '\r\nOpportunity', ' n. (pl. -ies) favourable chance or opening offered by circumstances.'),
(26535, 'o', '\r\nOpposable', ' adj. Zool. (of the thumb in primates) capable of facing and touching the other digits on the same hand.'),
(26536, 'o', '\r\nOppose', ' v. (-sing) 1 set oneself against; resist; argue or compete against. 2 (foll. By to) place in opposition or contrast.  as opposed to in contrast with.  opposer n. [latin: related to *opponent]'),
(26537, 'o', '\r\nOpposite', ' â€”adj. 1 facing, on the other side (opposite page; the house opposite). 2 (often foll. By to, from) contrary; diametrically different (opposite opinion). â€”n. Opposite thing, person, or term. â€”adv. Facing, on the other side (lives opposite). â€”prep. 1 facing (sat opposite me). 2 in a complementary role to (another actor etc.).'),
(26538, 'o', '\r\nOpposite', 'number  n. Person holding an equivalent position in another group etc.'),
(26539, 'o', '\r\nOpposite', 'sex  n. (prec. By the) either sex in relation to the other.'),
(26540, 'o', '\r\nOpposition', ' n. 1 resistance, antagonism. 2 being hostile or in conflict or disagreement. 3 contrast, antithesis. 4 a group or party of opponents or competitors. B (the opposition) chief parliamentary party opposed to that in office. 5 act of placing opposite. 6 diametrically opposite position of two celestial bodies. [latin: related to *position]'),
(26541, 'o', '\r\nOppress', ' v. 1 keep in subservience. 2 govern or treat cruelly. 3 weigh down (with cares or unhappiness).  oppression n. Oppressor n. [latin: related to *press1]'),
(26542, 'o', '\r\nOppressive', ' adj. 1 oppressing. 2 (of weather) close and sultry.  oppressively adv. Oppressiveness n.'),
(26543, 'o', '\r\nOpprobrious', ' adj. (of language) very scornful; abusive.'),
(26544, 'o', '\r\nOpprobrium', ' n. 1 disgrace. 2 cause of this. [latin, = infamy, reproach]'),
(26545, 'o', '\r\nOppugn', ' v. Literary controvert, call in question. [latin oppugno fight against]'),
(26546, 'o', '\r\nOpt', ' v. (usu. Foll. By for) make a choice, decide.  opt out (often foll. By of) choose not to participate (in). [latin opto choose, wish]'),
(26547, 'o', '\r\nOptative', ' gram. â€”adj. (esp. Of a mood in greek) expressing a wish. â€”n. Optative mood or form. [latin: related to *opt]'),
(26548, 'o', '\r\nOptic', ' adj.  Of the eye or sight (optic nerve). [greek optos seen]'),
(26549, 'o', '\r\nOptical', ' adj. 1 of sight; visual. 2 of or according to optics. 3 aiding sight.  optically adv.'),
(26550, 'o', '\r\nOptical', 'art  n. Art using contrasting colours to create the illusion of movement.'),
(26551, 'o', '\r\nOptical', 'disc  see *disc.'),
(26552, 'o', '\r\nOptical', 'fibre  n. Thin glass fibre through which light can be transmitted to carry signals.'),
(26553, 'o', '\r\nOptical', 'illusion  n. 1 image which deceives the eye. 2 mental misapprehension caused by this.'),
(26554, 'o', '\r\nOptician', ' n. 1 maker, seller, or prescriber of spectacles and contact lenses etc. 2 person trained in the detection and correction of poor eyesight. [medieval latin: related to *optic]'),
(26555, 'o', '\r\nOptics', ' n.pl. (treated as sing.) Science of light and vision.'),
(26556, 'o', '\r\nOptimal', ' adj. Best or most favourable. [latin optimus best]'),
(26557, 'o', '\r\nOptimism', ' n. 1 inclination to hopefulness and confidence. 2 philos. Belief that this world is as good as it could be or that good must ultimately prevail over evil.  optimist n. Optimistic adj. Optimistically adv. [latin optimus best]'),
(26558, 'o', '\r\nOptimize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make the best or most effective use of.  optimization n.'),
(26559, 'o', '\r\nOptimum', ' â€”n. (pl. Optima) 1 most favourable conditions (for growth etc.). 2 best practical solution. â€”adj. = *optimal. [latin, neuter of optimus best]'),
(26560, 'o', '\r\nOption', ' n. 1 a choosing; choice. B thing that is or may be chosen. 2 liberty to choose. 3 right to buy or sell at a specified price within a set time.  keep (or leave) one''s options open not commit oneself. [latin: related to *opt]'),
(26561, 'o', '\r\nOptional', ' adj. Not obligatory. optionally adv.'),
(26562, 'o', '\r\nOptional', 'extra  n. Item costing extra if one chooses to have it.'),
(26563, 'o', '\r\nOpulent', ' adj. 1 wealthy. 2 luxurious. 3 abundant.  opulence n. [latin opes wealth]'),
(26564, 'o', '\r\nOpus', ' n. (pl. Opuses or opera) 1 musical composition numbered as one of a composer''s works (beethoven, opus 15). 2 any artistic work (cf. *magnum opus). [latin, = work]'),
(26565, 'o', '\r\nOr1', ' conj. 1 introducing an alternative (white or black; take it or leave it; whether or not). 2 introducing an alternative name (the lapwing or peewit). 3 introducing an afterthought (came in laughing â€“ or was it crying?). 4 = or else 1 (run or you''ll be late).  or else 1 otherwise (run, or else you will be late). 2 colloq. Expressing a warning or threat (be good or else). [old english]'),
(26566, 'o', '\r\nOr2', ' n. Heraldry gold. [latin aurum gold]'),
(26567, 'o', '\r\n-or', ' suffix forming nouns denoting esp. An agent (actor; escalator) or condition (error; horror). [latin]'),
(26568, 'o', '\r\nOracle', ' n. 1 a place at which divine advice or prophecy was sought in classical antiquity. B response given. C prophet or prophetess at an oracle. 2 person or thing regarded as a source of wisdom etc. 3 (oracle) propr. Teletext service provided by independent television.  oracular adj. [latin oraculum from oro speak]'),
(26569, 'o', '\r\nOral', ' â€”adj. 1 by word of mouth; spoken; not written (oral examination). 2 done or taken by the mouth (oral sex; oral contraceptive). â€”n. Colloq. Spoken examination.  orally adv. [latin os oris mouth]'),
(26570, 'o', '\r\nOrange', ' â€”n. 1 a roundish reddish-yellow juicy citrus fruit. B tree bearing this. 2 its colour. â€”adj. Orange-coloured. [arabic naranj]'),
(26571, 'o', '\r\nOrangeade', ' n. Orange-flavoured, usu. Fizzy, drink.'),
(26572, 'o', '\r\nOrangeman', ' n. Member of a political society formed in 1795 to support protestantism in ireland. [william of orange]'),
(26573, 'o', '\r\nOrangery', ' n. (pl. -ies) place, esp. A building, where orange-trees are cultivated.'),
(26574, 'o', '\r\nOrang-utan', ' n. (also orang-outang) large reddish-haired long-armed anthropoid ape of the e. Indies. [malay, = wild man]'),
(26575, 'o', '\r\nOration', ' n. Formal or ceremonial speech. [latin oratio discourse, prayer, from oro speak, pray]'),
(26576, 'o', '\r\nOrator', ' n. 1 person making a formal speech. 2 eloquent public speaker. [latin: related to *oration]'),
(26577, 'o', '\r\nOratorio', ' n. (pl. -s) semi-dramatic work for orchestra and voices, esp. On a sacred theme. [church latin]'),
(26578, 'o', '\r\nOratory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 art of or skill in public speaking. 2 small private chapel.  oratorical adj. [french and latin oro speak, pray]'),
(26579, 'o', '\r\nOrb', ' n. 1 globe surmounted by a cross as part of coronation regalia. 2 sphere, globe. 3 poet. Celestial body. 4 poet. Eye. [latin orbis ring]'),
(26580, 'o', '\r\nOrbicular', ' adj. Formal circular or spherical. [latin orbiculus diminutive of orbis ring]'),
(26581, 'o', '\r\nOrbit', ' â€”n. 1 a curved course of a planet, satellite, etc. B one complete passage around a body. 2 range or sphere of action. 3 eye socket. â€”v. (-t-) 1 move in orbit round. 2 put into orbit.  orbiter n. [latin orbitus circular]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(26582, 'o', '\r\nOrbital', ' adj. 1 of an orbit or orbits. 2 (of a road) passing round the outside of a town.'),
(26583, 'o', '\r\nOrca', ' n. Any of various cetaceans, esp. The killer whale. [latin]'),
(26584, 'o', '\r\nOrcadian', ' â€”adj. Of orkney. â€”n. Native of orkney. [latin orcades orkney islands]'),
(26585, 'o', '\r\nOrch.', ' Abbr. 1 orchestrated by. 2 orchestra.'),
(26586, 'o', '\r\nOrchard', ' n. Piece of enclosed land with fruit-trees. [latin hortus garden]'),
(26587, 'o', '\r\nOrchestra', ' n. 1 large group of instrumentalists combining strings, woodwinds, brass, and percussion. 2 (in full orchestra pit) part of a theatre etc. Where the orchestra plays, usu. In front of the stage and on a lower level.  orchestral adj. [greek, = area for the chorus in drama]'),
(26588, 'o', '\r\nOrchestrate', ' v. (-ting) 1 arrange or compose for orchestral performance. 2 arrange (elements) to achieve a desired result.  orchestration n.'),
(26589, 'o', '\r\nOrchid', ' n. Any of various plants with brilliant flowers. [greek orkhis, originally = testicle]'),
(26590, 'o', '\r\nOrdain', ' v. 1 confer holy orders on. 2 decree, order. [latin ordino: related to *order]'),
(26591, 'o', '\r\nOrdeal', ' n. 1 painful or horrific experience; severe trial. 2 hist. Test of an accused person by subjection to severe pain, with survival taken as proof of innocence. [old english]'),
(26592, 'o', '\r\nOrder', ' â€”n. 1 a condition in which every part, unit, etc. Is in its right place; tidiness. B specified sequence, succession, etc. (alphabetical order; the order of events). 2 authoritative command, direction, instruction, etc. 3 state of obedience to law, authority, etc. 4 a direction to supply or pay something. B goods etc. To be supplied. 5 social class; its members (the lower orders). 6 kind; sort (talents of a high order). 7 constitution or nature of the world, society, etc. (the moral order; the order of things). 8 taxonomic rank below a class and above a family. 9 religious fraternity with a common rule of life. 10 grade of the christian ministry. 11 any of the five classical styles of architecture (doric order). 12 a company of persons distinguished by a particular honour (order of the garter). B insignia worn by its members. 13 eccl. The stated form of divine service (the order of confirmation). 14 system of rules or procedure (at meetings etc.) (point of order). â€”v. 1 command; bid; prescribe. 2 command or direct (a person) to a specified destination (ordered them home). 3 direct a waiter, tradesman, etc. To supply (ordered dinner; ordered a new suit). 4 (often as ordered adj.) Put in order; regulate (an ordered life). 5 (of god, fate, etc.) Ordain.  in (or out of) order 1 in the correct (or incorrect) sequence or position. 2 fit (or not fit) for use. 3 according (or not according) to the rules at a meeting etc. In order that with the intention; so that. In order to with the purpose of doing; with a view to. Of (or in) the order of approximately. On order ordered but not yet received. Order about command officiously. To order as specified by the customer. [latin ordo ordin- row, command, etc.]'),
(26593, 'o', '\r\nOrder', 'in council  n. Executive order, often approved by parliament but not debated.'),
(26594, 'o', '\r\nOrderly', ' â€”adj. 1 methodically arranged or inclined, tidy. 2 well-behaved. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 male cleaner in a hospital. 2 soldier who carries orders for an officer etc.  orderliness n.'),
(26595, 'o', '\r\nOrderly', 'room  n. Room in a barracks used for company business.'),
(26596, 'o', '\r\nOrder', 'of the day  n. 1 prevailing state of things. 2 principal action, procedure, or programme.'),
(26597, 'o', '\r\nOrder-paper', ' n. Written or printed order of the day''s proceedings, esp. In parliament.'),
(26598, 'o', '\r\nOrdinal', ' n. (in full ordinal number) number defining position in a series, e.g. â€˜firstâ€™, â€˜secondâ€™, â€˜thirdâ€™, etc. [latin: related to *order]'),
(26599, 'o', '\r\nOrdinance', ' n. 1 decree. 2 religious rite. [latin: related to *ordain]'),
(26600, 'o', '\r\nOrdinand', ' n. Candidate for ordination. [latin: related to *ordain]'),
(26601, 'o', '\r\nOrdinary', ' â€”adj. 1 normal, usual. 2 commonplace, unexceptional. â€”n. (pl. -ies) rc ch. 1 parts of a service that do not vary from day to day. 2 rule or book laying down the order of service.  in the ordinary way in normal circumstances. Out of the ordinary unusual.  ordinarily adv. Ordinariness n. [latin: related to *order]'),
(26602, 'o', '\r\nOrdinary', 'level  n. Hist. Lowest level of the gce examination.'),
(26603, 'o', '\r\nOrdinary', 'seaman  n. Sailor of the lowest rank.'),
(26604, 'o', '\r\nOrdinate', ' n. Math. Coordinate measured usu. Vertically. [latin: related to *ordain]'),
(26605, 'o', '\r\nOrdination', ' n. Conferring of holy orders, ordaining.'),
(26606, 'o', '\r\nOrdnance', ' n. 1 artillery; military supplies. 2 government service dealing with these. [contraction of *ordinance]'),
(26607, 'o', '\r\nOrdnance', 'survey  n. Official survey of the uk producing detailed maps.'),
(26608, 'o', '\r\nOrdovician', ' â€”adj. Of the second period in the palaeozoic era. â€”n. This period. [latin ordovices, an ancient british tribe in n. Wales]'),
