(27018, 'p', '\r\nP.a.', ' Abbr. Per annum.'),
(27019, 'p', '\r\nPabulum', ' n. Food, esp. For the mind. [latin]'),
(27020, 'p', '\r\nPace1', ' â€”n. 1 a single step in walking or running. B distance covered in this. 2 speed in walking or running. 3 rate of movement or progression. 4 way of walking or running; gait (ambling pace). â€”v. (-cing) 1 a walk slowly and evenly (pace up and down). B (of a horse) amble. 2 traverse by pacing. 3  set the pace for (a rider, runner, etc.). 4 (foll. By out) measure by pacing.  keep pace (often foll. By with) advance at an equal rate (to). Put a person etc. Through his (or her) paces test a person''s qualities in action etc. Set the pace determine the speed; lead. [french pas from latin passus]'),
(27021, 'p', '\r\nPace2', ' prep. (in stating a contrary opinion) with due respect to (the person named). [latin, ablative of pax peace]'),
(27022, 'p', '\r\nPace', 'bowler  n. Cricket fast bowler.'),
