(27029, 'p', '\r\nPacify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 appease (a person, anger, etc.). 2 bring (a country etc.) To a state of peace.  pacification n. Pacificatory adj.'),
(27030, 'p', '\r\nPack1', ' â€”n. 1 a collection of things wrapped up or tied together for carrying. B = *backpack. 2 set of packaged items. 3 usu. Derog. Lot or set (pack of lies; pack of thieves). 4  set of playing-cards. 5 group of hounds, wild animals, etc. 6 organized group of cub scouts or brownies. 7 rugby team''s forwards. 8 = *face-pack. 9 = *pack ice. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) a fill (a suitcase, bag, etc.) With clothes etc. B put (things) in a bag or suitcase, esp. For travelling. 2 (often foll. By in, into) crowd or cram (packed a lot into a few hours; packed in like sardines). 3 (esp. In passive; often foll. By with) fill (restaurant was packed; fans packed the stadium; packed with information). 4 cover (a thing) with packaging. 5 be suitable for packing. 6 colloq. A carry (a gun etc.). B be capable of delivering (a forceful punch). 7 (of animals or rugby forwards) form a pack.  pack in colloq. Stop, give up (packed in his job). Pack it in (or up) colloq. End or stop it. Pack off send (a person) away, esp. Summarily.  Pack them in fill a theatre etc. With a capacity audience. Pack up colloq. 1 stop functioning; break down. 2 retire from an activity, contest, etc. Send packing colloq. Dismiss summarily. [low german or dutch]'),
(27031, 'p', '\r\nPack2', ' v. Select (a jury etc.) Or fill (a meeting) so as to secure a decision in one''s favour. [probably from *pact]'),
(27032, 'p', '\r\nPackage', ' â€”n. 1 a bundle of things packed. B parcel, box, etc., in which things are packed. 2 (in full package deal) set of proposals or items offered or agreed to as a whole. 3 computing piece of software suitable for a wide range of users. 4 colloq. = *package holiday. â€”v. (-ging) make up into or enclose in a package.  packager n.'),
(27033, 'p', '\r\nPackage', 'holiday  n. (also package tour) holiday (or tour) with travel, hotels, etc. At an inclusive price.'),
(27034, 'p', '\r\nPackaging', ' n. 1 wrapping or container for goods. 2 process of packing goods.'),
(27035, 'p', '\r\nPacked', 'lunch  n. Lunch of sandwiches etc. Prepared and packed to be eaten away from home.'),
(27036, 'p', '\r\nPacked', 'out  adj. Full, crowded.'),
(27037, 'p', '\r\nPacker', ' n. Person or thing that packs, esp. A dealer who prepares and packs food.'),
(27038, 'p', '\r\nPacket', ' n. 1 small package. 2 colloq. Large sum of money won, lost, or spent. 3 (in full packet-boat) hist. Mail-boat or passenger ship.'),
(27039, 'p', '\r\nPackhorse', ' n. Horse for carrying loads.'),
(27040, 'p', '\r\nPack', 'ice  n. Crowded floating ice in the sea.'),
