(27698, 'p', '\r\nPenalty', 'kick  n. Football free kick at the goal resulting from a foul in the penalty area.'),
(27699, 'p', '\r\nPenance', ' n. 1 act of self-punishment as reparation for guilt. 2 a (in the roman catholic and orthodox church) sacrament including confession of and absolution for sins. B penalty imposed, esp. By a priest, for a sin.  do penance perform a penance. [related to *penitent]'),
(27700, 'p', '\r\nPence', ' pl. Of *penny.'),
(27701, 'p', '\r\nPenchant', ' n. (followed by for) inclination or liking. [french]'),
(27702, 'p', '\r\nPencil', ' â€”n. 1 instrument for writing or drawing, usu. A thin rod of graphite etc. Enclosed in a wooden cylinder or metal case. 2 (attrib.) Resembling a pencil in shape (pencil skirt). â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 write, draw, or mark with a pencil. 2 (usu. Foll. By in) write, note, or arrange provisionally. [latin penicillum paintbrush]'),
(27703, 'p', '\r\nPendant', ' n. Hanging jewel etc., esp. One attached to a necklace, bracelet, etc. [french pendre hang]'),
(27704, 'p', '\r\nPendent', ' adj. Formal 1 a hanging. B overhanging. 2 undecided, pending.  pendency n.'),
(27705, 'p', '\r\nPending', ' â€”predic. Adj. 1 awaiting decision or settlement, undecided. 2 about to come into existence (patent pending). â€”prep. 1 during (pending further inquiries). 2 until (bailed pending trial). [after french: see *pendant]'),
(27706, 'p', '\r\nPendulous', ' adj. Hanging down; drooping and swinging.  [latin pendulus from pendeo hang]'),
(27707, 'p', '\r\nPendulum', ' n. (pl. -s) weight suspended so as to swing freely, esp. A rod with a weighted end regulating a clock. [latin neuter adjective: related to *pendulous]'),
(27708, 'p', '\r\nPenetrate', ' v. (-ting) 1 a find access into or through. B (usu. Foll. By with) imbue with; permeate. 2 see into, find out, or discern. 3 see through (darkness, fog, etc.). 4 be absorbed by the mind. 5 (as penetrating adj.) A having or suggesting sensitivity or insight. B (of a voice etc.) Easily heard through or above other sounds; piercing.  penetrable adj. Penetrability n. Penetration n. Penetrative adj. [latin]'),
(27709, 'p', '\r\nPen-friend', ' n. Friend communicated with by letter only.'),
(27710, 'p', '\r\nPenguin', ' n. Flightless black and white sea bird of the southern hemisphere, with wings developed into flippers for swimming underwater. [origin unknown]'),
(27711, 'p', '\r\nPenicillin', ' n. Antibiotic, produced naturally by mould or synthetically. [latin penicillum: related to *pencil]'),
(27712, 'p', '\r\nPeninsula', ' n. Piece of land almost surrounded by water or projecting far into a sea etc.  peninsular adj. [latin paene almost, insula island]'),
(27713, 'p', '\r\nPenis', ' n.  Male organ of copulation and (in mammals) urination. [latin]'),
(27714, 'p', '\r\nPenitent', ' â€”adj. Repentant. â€”n. 1 repentant sinner. 2 person doing penance under the direction of a confessor.  penitence n. Penitently adv.  [latin paeniteo repent]'),
(27715, 'p', '\r\nPenitential', ' adj. Of penitence or penance.'),
(27716, 'p', '\r\nPenitentiary', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) us federal or state prison. â€”adj. 1 of penance. 2 of reformatory treatment. [latin: related to *penitent]'),
(27717, 'p', '\r\nPenknife', ' n. Small folding knife.'),
(27718, 'p', '\r\nPen-name', ' n. Literary pseudonym.'),
(27719, 'p', '\r\nPennant', ' n. 1 tapering flag, esp. That flown at the masthead of a vessel in commission. 2 = *pennon. [blend of *pendant and *pennon]'),
(27720, 'p', '\r\nPenniless', ' adj. Having no money; destitute.'),
(27721, 'p', '\r\nPennon', ' n. 1 long narrow flag, triangular or swallow-tailed. 2 long pointed streamer on a ship. [latin penna feather]'),
(27722, 'p', '\r\nPenny', ' n. (pl. For separate coins -ies, for a sum of money pence) 1 british coin and monetary unit equal to one-hundredth of a pound. 2 hist. British bronze coin and monetary unit equal to one-two-hundred-and-fortieth of a pound.  in for a penny, in for a pound exhortation to total commitment to an undertaking. Pennies from heaven unexpected benefits. The penny drops colloq. One understands at last. Penny wise and pound foolish mean in small expenditures but wasteful of large amounts. A pretty penny a large sum of money. Two a penny easily obtained and so almost worthless. [old english]'),
(27723, 'p', '\r\nPenny', 'black  n. First adhesive postage stamp (1840, price one penny).'),
(27724, 'p', '\r\nPenny', 'farthing  n.  Early type of bicycle with a large front and small rear wheel.'),
(27725, 'p', '\r\nPenny-pinching', ' â€”n. Meanness. â€”adj. Mean.  penny-pincher n.'),
(27726, 'p', '\r\nPennyroyal', ' n. Creeping kind of mint. [anglo-french puliol real royal thyme]'),
(27727, 'p', '\r\nPenny', 'whistle  n. Tin pipe with six finger holes.'),
(27728, 'p', '\r\nPennywort', ' n. Wild plant with rounded leaves, growing esp. In marshy places.'),
(27729, 'p', '\r\nPennyworth', ' n. As much as can be bought for a penny.'),
(27730, 'p', '\r\nPenology', ' n. The study of the punishment of crime and prison management.  penologist n. [latin poena penalty]'),
(27731, 'p', '\r\nPen-pal', ' n. Colloq. = *pen-friend.'),
(27732, 'p', '\r\nPen-pushing', ' n. Colloq. Derog. Clerical work.  pen-pusher n.'),
(27733, 'p', '\r\nPension1', ' â€”n. 1 regular payment made by a government to people above a specified age, to widows, or to the disabled. 2 similar payments made by an employer, private pension fund, etc. On the retirement of an employee. â€”v. Grant a pension to.  pension off 1 dismiss with a pension. 2 cease to employ or use. [latin pendo pens- pay]'),
(27734, 'p', '\r\nPension2', ' n. European, esp. French, boarding-house. [french: related to *pension1]'),
(27735, 'p', '\r\nPensionable', ' adj. 1 entitled to a pension. 2 (of a service, job, etc.) Entitling an employee to a pension.'),
(27736, 'p', '\r\nPensioner', ' n. Recipient of a pension, esp. The retirement pension. [french: related to *pension1]'),
(27737, 'p', '\r\nPensive', ' adj. Deep in thought.  pensively adv. [french penser think]'),
(27738, 'p', '\r\nPent', ' adj. (often foll. By in, up) closely confined; shut in (pent-up feelings). [from *pen2]'),
(27739, 'p', '\r\nPenta-', ' comb. Form five. [greek pente five]'),
(27740, 'p', '\r\nPentacle', ' n. Figure used as a symbol, esp. In magic, e.g. A pentagram. [medieval latin pentaculum: related to *penta-]'),
(27741, 'p', '\r\nPentagon', ' n. 1 plane figure with five sides and angles. 2 (the pentagon) a pentagonal washington headquarters of the us forces. B leaders of the us forces.  pentagonal adj. [greek pentagonon: related to *penta-]'),
(27742, 'p', '\r\nPentagram', ' n. Five-pointed star. [greek: see *penta-, *-gram]'),
(27743, 'p', '\r\nPentameter', ' n. Line of verse with five metrical feet. [greek: see *penta-, *-meter]'),
(27744, 'p', '\r\nPentateuch', ' n. First five books of the old testament. [greek teukhos book]'),
(27745, 'p', '\r\nPentathlon', ' n. Athletic event comprising five different events for each competitor.  pentathlete n. [greek: see *penta-, athlon contest]'),
(27746, 'p', '\r\nPentatonic', ' adj. Consisting of five musical notes.'),
(27747, 'p', '\r\nPentecost', ' n. 1 whit sunday. 2 jewish harvest festival, on the fiftieth day after the second day of passover. [greek pentekoste fiftieth (day)]'),
(27748, 'p', '\r\nPentecostal', ' adj. (of a religious group) emphasizing the divine gifts, esp. The power to heal the sick, and often fundamentalist.'),
(27749, 'p', '\r\nPenthouse', ' n. (esp. Luxurious) flat on the roof or top floor of a tall building. [latin: related to *append]'),
(27750, 'p', '\r\nPenultimate', ' adj. & n. Last but one. [latin paenultimus from paene almost, ultimus last]'),
(27751, 'p', '\r\nPenumbra', ' n. (pl. -s or -brae) 1 partly shaded region around the shadow of an opaque body, esp. That around the shadow of the moon or earth in an eclipse. 2 partial shadow.  penumbral adj. [latin paene almost, *umbra]'),
(27752, 'p', '\r\nPenurious', ' adj. 1 poor. 2 stingy; grudging. 3 scanty. [medieval latin: related to *penury]'),
(27753, 'p', '\r\nPenury', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 destitution; poverty. 2 lack; scarcity. [latin]'),
(27754, 'p', '\r\nPeon', ' n. Spanish american day-labourer. [portuguese and spanish: related to *pawn1]'),
(27755, 'p', '\r\nPeony', ' n. (also paeony) (pl. -ies) plant with large globular red, pink, or white flowers. [greek paionia]'),
(27756, 'p', '\r\nPeople', ' â€”n.pl. Except in sense 2. 1 persons in general or of a specified kind (people don''t like rudeness; famous people). 2 persons composing a community, tribe, race, nation, etc. (a warlike people; peoples of the commonwealth). 3 (the people) a the mass of people in a country etc. Not having special rank or position. B these as an electorate. 4 parents or other relatives (my people disapprove). 5 a subjects, armed followers, etc. B congregation of a parish priest etc. â€”v. (-ling) (usu. Foll. By with) 1 fill with people, animals, etc.; populate. 2 (esp. As peopled adj.) Inhabit. [latin populus]'),
(27757, 'p', '\r\nPep', ' abbr. Personal equity plan.'),
(27758, 'p', '\r\nPep', ' colloq. â€”n. Vigour; spirit. â€”v. (-pp-) (usu. Foll. By up) fill with vigour. [abbreviation of *pepper]'),
(27759, 'p', '\r\nPepper', ' â€”n. 1 hot aromatic condiment from the dried berries of certain plants. 2 anything pungent. 3 a capsicum plant, grown as a vegetable. B its fruit. â€”v. 1 sprinkle or treat with or as if with pepper. 2 pelt with missiles. [sanskrit pippali]'),
(27760, 'p', '\r\nPepper-and-salt', ' adj. With small patches of dark and light colour intermingled.'),
(27761, 'p', '\r\nPeppercorn', ' n. 1  dried pepper berry. 2 (in full peppercorn rent) nominal rent.'),
(27762, 'p', '\r\nPepper-mill', ' n. Device for grinding pepper by hand.'),
(27763, 'p', '\r\nPeppermint', ' n. 1 a mint plant grown for its strong-flavoured oil. B this oil. 2 sweet flavoured with peppermint.'),
(27764, 'p', '\r\nPepperoni', ' n. Beef and pork sausage seasoned with pepper. [italian peperone chilli]'),
(27765, 'p', '\r\nPepper-pot', ' n. Small container with a perforated lid for sprinkling pepper.'),
(27766, 'p', '\r\nPeppery', ' adj. 1 of, like, or containing pepper. 2 hot-tempered. 3 pungent.'),
(27767, 'p', '\r\nPep', 'pill  n. Pill containing a stimulant drug.'),
(27768, 'p', '\r\nPeppy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Vigorous, energetic, bouncy.'),
(27769, 'p', '\r\nPepsin', ' n. Enzyme contained in the gastric juice. [greek pepsis digestion]'),
(27770, 'p', '\r\nPep', 'talk  n. (usu. Short) talk intended to enthuse, encourage, etc.'),
(27771, 'p', '\r\nPeptic', ' adj. Concerning or promoting digestion. [greek peptikos able to digest]'),
(27772, 'p', '\r\nPeptic', 'ulcer  n. Ulcer in the stomach or duodenum.'),
(27773, 'p', '\r\nPeptide', ' n. Biochem. Compound consisting of two or more amino acids bonded in sequence. [greek peptos cooked]'),
(27774, 'p', '\r\nPer', ' prep. 1 for each (two sweets per child; five miles per hour). 2 by means of; by; through (per post). 3 (in full as per) in accordance with (as per instructions).  as per usual colloq. As usual. [latin]'),
