(27793, 'p', '\r\nPercipient', ' adj. Able to perceive; conscious.  percipience n. [latin: related to *perceive]'),
(27794, 'p', '\r\nPercolate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By through) a (of liquid etc.) Filter or ooze gradually. B (of an idea etc.) Permeate gradually. 2 prepare (coffee) in a percolator. 3 strain (a liquid, powder, etc.) Through a fine mesh etc.  percolation n. [latin colum strainer]'),
(27795, 'p', '\r\nPercolator', ' n. Machine making coffee by circulating boiling water through ground beans.'),
(27796, 'p', '\r\nPercussion', ' n. 1  a (often attrib.) Playing of music by striking instruments with sticks etc. (percussion instrument). B such instruments collectively. 2  gentle tapping of the body in medical diagnosis. 3 forcible striking of one esp. Solid body against another.  percussionist n. Percussive adj. [latin percutio -cuss- strike]'),
(27797, 'p', '\r\nPercussion', 'cap  n. Small amount of explosive powder contained in metal or paper and exploded by striking.'),
(27798, 'p', '\r\nPerdition', ' n. Eternal death; damnation. [latin perdo -dit- destroy]'),
(27799, 'p', '\r\nPeregrine', ' n. (in full peregrine falcon) a kind of falcon much used for hawking. [latin peregrinus foreign]'),
(27800, 'p', '\r\nPeremptory', ' adj. 1 (of a statement or command) admitting no denial or refusal. 2 (of a person, manner, etc.) Imperious; dictatorial.  peremptorily adv. Peremptoriness n. [latin peremptorius deadly, decisive]'),
(27801, 'p', '\r\nPerennial', ' â€”adj. 1 lasting through a year or several years. 2 (of a plant) lasting several years. 3 lasting a long time or for ever. â€”n. Perennial plant.  perennially adv. [latin perennis from annus year]'),
(27802, 'p', '\r\nPerestroika', ' n. (in the former ussr) reform of the economic and political system. [russian, = restructuring]'),
(27803, 'p', '\r\nPerfect', ' â€”adj. 1 complete; not deficient. 2 faultless. 3 very enjoyable, excellent (perfect evening). 4 exact, precise (perfect circle). 5 entire, unqualified (perfect stranger). 6 gram. (of a tense) denoting a completed action or event (e.g. He has gone). â€”v. 1 make perfect. 2 complete. â€”n. Gram. The perfect tense.  perfectible adj. Perfectibility n. [latin perficere -fect- complete (v.)]'),
(27804, 'p', '\r\nPerfection', ' n. 1 making, becoming, or being perfect. 2 faultlessness. 3 perfect person, thing, or example.  to perfection exactly; completely. [latin: related to *perfect]'),
(27805, 'p', '\r\nPerfectionism', ' n. Uncompromising pursuit of excellence.  perfectionist n. & adj.'),
(27806, 'p', '\r\nPerfectly', ' adv. 1 completely; quite. 2 in a perfect way.'),
(27807, 'p', '\r\nPerfect', 'pitch  n. = *absolute pitch.'),
(27808, 'p', '\r\nPerfidy', ' n. Breach of faith; treachery.  perfidious adj. [latin perfidia from fides faith]'),
(27809, 'p', '\r\nPerforate', ' v. (-ting) 1 make a hole or holes through; pierce. 2 make a row of small holes in (paper etc.) So that a part may be torn off easily.  perforation n. [latin perforo pierce through]'),
(27810, 'p', '\r\nPerforce', ' adv. Archaic unavoidably; necessarily. [french par force by *force1]'),
(27811, 'p', '\r\nPerform', ' v. 1 (also absol.) Carry into effect; do. 2 execute (a function, play, piece of music, etc.). 3  act in a play; play music, sing, etc.; execute tricks. 4 function.  performer n. [anglo-french: related to *per-, *furnish]'),
