(28162, 'p', '\r\nPied-Ã -terre', ' n. (pl. Pieds-Ã -terre pronunc. Same) (usu. Small) flat, house, etc. Kept for occasional use. [french, literally â€˜foot to earthâ€™]'),
(28163, 'p', '\r\nPie-eyed', ' adj. Slang drunk.'),
(28164, 'p', '\r\nPie', 'in the sky  n. (used without an article) unrealistic prospect of future happiness.'),
(28165, 'p', '\r\nPier', ' n. 1 a structure built out into the sea, a lake, etc., as a promenade and landing-stage. B breakwater. 2 a support of an arch or of the span of a bridge; pillar. B solid masonry between windows etc. [latin pera]'),
(28166, 'p', '\r\nPierce', ' v. (-cing) 1 a (of a sharp instrument etc.) Penetrate. B (often foll. By with) make a hole in or through with a sharp-pointed instrument. C make (a hole etc.). 2 (as piercing adj.) (of a glance, sound, light, pain, cold, etc.) Keen, sharp, or unpleasantly penetrating. 3 (often foll. By through, into) force a way through or into, penetrate. [french percer from latin pertundo bore through]'),
(28167, 'p', '\r\nPier-glass', ' n. Large mirror, used orig. To fill wall-space between windows.'),
(28168, 'p', '\r\nPierrot', ' n. (fem. Pierrette) 1 white-faced entertainer in pier shows etc. With a loose white clown''s costume. 2 french pantomime character so dressed. [french, diminutive of pierre peter]'),
(28169, 'p', '\r\nPietÃ ', ' n. Representation of the virgin mary holding the dead body of christ on her lap. [italian, = *piety]'),
(28170, 'p', '\r\nPietism', ' n. 1 pious sentiment. 2 exaggerated or affected piety. [german: related to *piety]'),
(28171, 'p', '\r\nPiety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 quality of being pious. 2 pious act. [latin: related to *pious]'),
(28172, 'p', '\r\nPiffle', ' colloq. â€”n. Nonsense; empty speech. â€”v. (-ling) talk or act feebly; trifle. [imitative]'),
(28173, 'p', '\r\nPiffling', ' adj. Colloq. Trivial; worthless.'),
(28174, 'p', '\r\nPig', ' â€”n. 1 omnivorous hoofed bristly broad-snouted mammal, esp. A domesticated kind. 2 its flesh as food. 3 colloq. Greedy, dirty, or unpleasant person. 4 oblong mass of metal (esp. Iron or lead) from a smelting-furnace. 5 slang derog. Police officer. â€”v. (-gg-) colloq. Eat (food) greedily.  buy a pig in a poke acquire something without previous sight or knowledge of it. Pig it colloq. Live in a disorderly or filthy fashion. Pig out (often foll. By on) esp. Us slang eat gluttonously. [old english]'),
(28175, 'p', '\r\nPigeon', ' n. Bird of the dove family. [latin pipio -onis]'),
(28176, 'p', '\r\nPigeon-hole', ' â€”n. Each of a set of compartments on a wall etc. For papers, letters, etc. â€”v. 1 assign to a preconceived category. 2 deposit in a pigeon-hole. 3 put aside for future consideration.'),
(28177, 'p', '\r\nPigeon-toed', ' adj. Having the toes turned inwards.'),
(28178, 'p', '\r\nPiggery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 pig farm. 2 = *pigsty.'),
(28179, 'p', '\r\nPiggish', ' adj. Greedy; dirty; mean.'),
(28180, 'p', '\r\nPiggy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Little pig. â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like a pig. 2 (of features etc.) Like those of a pig.'),
(28181, 'p', '\r\nPiggyback', ' (also pickaback) â€”n. Ride on the back and shoulders of another person. â€”adv. On the back and shoulders of another person. [origin unknown]'),
(28182, 'p', '\r\nPiggy', 'bank  n. Pig-shaped money box.'),
(28183, 'p', '\r\nPigheaded', ' adj. Obstinate.  pigheadedness n.'),
(28184, 'p', '\r\nPig-iron', ' n. Crude iron from a smelting-furnace.'),
(28185, 'p', '\r\nPiglet', ' n. Young pig.'),
(28186, 'p', '\r\nPigment', ' â€”n. 1 colouring-matter used as paint or dye. 2 natural colouring-matter of animal or plant tissue. â€”v. Colour with or as if with pigment.  pigmentary adj. [latin pingo paint]'),
(28187, 'p', '\r\nPigmentation', ' n. 1 natural colouring of plants, animals, etc. 2 excessive colouring of tissue by the deposition of pigment.'),
(28188, 'p', '\r\nPigmy', ' var. Of *pygmy.'),
(28189, 'p', '\r\nPigskin', ' n. 1 hide of a pig. 2 leather made from this.'),
(28190, 'p', '\r\nPigsty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 pen for pigs. 2 filthy house, room, etc.'),
(28191, 'p', '\r\nPigswill', ' n. Kitchen refuse and scraps fed to pigs.'),
(28192, 'p', '\r\nPigtail', ' n. Plait of hair hanging from the back of the head.'),
(28193, 'p', '\r\nPike', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 large voracious freshwater fish with a long narrow snout. 2 hist. Weapon with a pointed metal head on a long wooden shaft. [old english]'),
(28194, 'p', '\r\nPikestaff', ' n. Wooden shaft of a pike.  plain as a pikestaff quite plain or obvious.'),
(28195, 'p', '\r\nPilaster', ' n. Rectangular column projecting slightly from a wall.  pilastered adj. [latin pila pillar]'),
(28196, 'p', '\r\nPilau', ' n. (also pilaff, pilaf) middle eastern or indian dish of rice boiled with meat, vegetables, spices, etc. [turkish]'),
(28197, 'p', '\r\nPilchard', ' n. Small marine fish of the herring family. [origin unknown]'),
(28198, 'p', '\r\nPile1', ' â€”n. 1 heap of things laid upon one another. 2 large imposing building. 3 colloq. A large quantity. B large amount of money. 4 a series of plates of dissimilar metals laid one on another alternately to produce an electric current. B = *nuclear reactor. 5 funeral pyre. â€”v. (-ling) 1 a (often foll. By up, on) heap up. B (foll. By with) load. 2 (usu. Foll. By in, into, on, out of, etc.) Crowd hurriedly or tightly.  pile it on colloq. Exaggerate. Pile up 1 accumulate; heap up. 2 colloq. Cause (a vehicle etc.) To crash. [latin pila]'),
(28199, 'p', '\r\nPile2', ' n. 1 heavy beam driven vertically into the ground to support a bridge, the foundations of a house, etc. 2 pointed stake or post. [latin pilum javelin]'),
(28200, 'p', '\r\nPile3', ' n. Soft projecting surface on a carpet, velvet, etc. [latin pilus hair]'),
(28201, 'p', '\r\nPile-driver', ' n. Machine for driving piles into the ground.'),
(28202, 'p', '\r\nPiles', ' n.pl. Colloq. Haemorrhoids. [latin pila ball]'),
(28203, 'p', '\r\nPile-up', ' n. Colloq. Multiple crash of road vehicles.'),
(28204, 'p', '\r\nPilfer', ' v. (also absol.) Steal (objects), esp. In small quantities. [french pelfre]'),
(28205, 'p', '\r\nPilgrim', ' n. 1 person who journeys to a sacred place for religious reasons. 2 traveller. [latin: related to *peregrine]'),
(28206, 'p', '\r\nPilgrimage', ' n. 1 pilgrim''s journey. 2 any journey taken for sentimental reasons.'),
(28207, 'p', '\r\nPilgrim', 'fathers  n.pl. English puritans who founded the colony of plymouth, massachusetts, in 1620.'),
(28208, 'p', '\r\nPill', ' n. 1 a ball or flat disc of solid medicine for swallowing whole. B (usu. Prec. By the) colloq. Contraceptive pill. 2 unpleasant or painful necessity. [latin pila ball]'),
(28209, 'p', '\r\nPillage', ' â€”v. (-ging) (also absol.) Plunder, sack. â€”n. Pillaging, esp. In war. [french piller plunder]'),
(28210, 'p', '\r\nPillar', ' n. 1 slender vertical structure of stone etc. Used as a support or for ornament. 2 person regarded as a mainstay (pillar of the faith). 3 upright mass of air, water, rock, etc.  from pillar to post (rushing etc.) From one place to another. [latin pila pillar]'),
(28211, 'p', '\r\nPillar-box', ' n. Public postbox shaped like a pillar.'),
(28212, 'p', '\r\nPillar-box', 'red  adj. & n. Bright red.'),
(28213, 'p', '\r\nPillbox', ' n. 1 shallow cylindrical box for holding pills. 2 hat of a similar shape. 3 mil. Small partly underground enclosed concrete fort.'),
(28214, 'p', '\r\nPillion', ' n. Seating for a passenger behind a motor cyclist.  ride pillion travel seated behind a motor cyclist. [gaelic pillean small cushion]'),
(28215, 'p', '\r\nPillory', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) hist. Wooden framework with holes for the head and hands, holding a person and allowing him or her to be publicly ridiculed. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 expose to ridicule. 2 hist. Put in the pillory. [french]'),
(28216, 'p', '\r\nPillow', ' â€”n. 1 soft support for the head, esp. In bed. 2 pillow-shaped block or support. â€”v. Rest on or as if on a pillow. [latin pulvinus cushion]'),
(28217, 'p', '\r\nPillowcase', ' n. (also pillowslip) washable cover for a pillow.'),
(28218, 'p', '\r\nPilot', ' â€”n. 1 person who operates the controls of an aircraft. 2 person qualified to take charge of a ship entering or leaving harbour. 3 (usu. Attrib.) Experimental undertaking or test (pilot scheme). 4 guide. â€”v. (-t-) 1 act as a pilot of. 2 conduct or initiate as a pilot. [greek pedon]'),
(28219, 'p', '\r\nPilot-light', ' n. 1 small gas burner kept alight to light another. 2 electric indicator light or control light.'),
(28220, 'p', '\r\nPilot', 'officer  n. Lowest commissioned rank in the raf.'),
(28221, 'p', '\r\nPimento', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 tree native to jamaica. 2 berries of this, usu. Crushed for culinary use; allspice. 3 = *pimiento. [latin: related to *pigment]'),
(28222, 'p', '\r\nPi', 'meson  var. Of *pion.'),
(28223, 'p', '\r\nPimiento', ' n. (pl. -s) = *sweet pepper. [see *pimento]'),
(28224, 'p', '\r\nPimp', ' â€”n. Man who lives off the earnings of a prostitute or a brothel. â€”v. Act as a pimp. [origin unknown]'),
(28225, 'p', '\r\nPimpernel', ' n. = *scarlet pimpernel. [latin piper *pepper]'),
(28226, 'p', '\r\nPimple', ' n. 1 small hard inflamed spot on the skin. 2 anything resembling a pimple.  pimply adj. [old english]'),
(28227, 'p', '\r\nPin', ' abbr. Personal identification number (for use with a cashcard etc.).'),
(28228, 'p', '\r\nPin', ' â€”n. 1 small thin pointed piece of metal with a round or flattened head used (esp. In sewing) for holding things in place, attaching one thing to another, etc. 2 peg of wood or metal for various purposes. 3 (in idioms) something of small value (not worth a pin). 4 (in pl.) Colloq. Legs. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 a (often foll. By to, up, together) fasten with a pin or pins. B transfix with a pin, lance, etc. 2 (usu. Foll. By on) put (blame, responsibility, etc.) On (a person etc.). 3 (often foll. By against, on, etc.) Seize and hold fast.  pin down 1 (often foll. By to) bind (a person etc.) To a promise, arrangement, etc. 2 force (a person) to declare his or her intentions. 3 restrict the actions of (an enemy etc.). 4 specify (a thing) precisely. Pin one''s faith (or hopes etc.) On rely implicitly on. [latin pinna point etc.]'),
(28229, 'p', '\r\nPina', 'colada  n. Cocktail of pineapple juice, rum, and coconut. [spanish]'),
(28230, 'p', '\r\nPinafore', ' n. 1 apron, esp. With a bib. 2 (in full pinafore dress) collarless sleeveless dress worn over a blouse or jumper. [from *pin, *afore]'),
(28231, 'p', '\r\nPinball', ' n. Game in which small metal balls are shot across a board to strike pins.'),
(28232, 'p', '\r\nPince-nez', ' n. (pl. Same) pair of eyeglasses with a nose-clip. [french, = pinch-nose]'),
(28233, 'p', '\r\nPincer', 'movement  n. Movement by two wings of an army converging to surround an enemy.'),
(28234, 'p', '\r\nPincers', ' n.pl. 1 (also pair of pincers) gripping-tool resembling scissors but with blunt jaws. 2 front claws of lobsters and some other crustaceans. [related to *pinch]'),
(28235, 'p', '\r\nPinch', ' â€”v. 1 a squeeze tightly, esp. Between finger and thumb. B (often absol.) (of a shoe etc.) Constrict painfully. 2 (of cold, hunger, etc.) Affect painfully. 3  slang a steal. B arrest. 4 (as pinched adj.) (of the features) drawn. 5 a (usu. Foll. By in, of, for, etc.) Stint. B be niggardly. 6 (usu. Foll. By out, back, down) remove (leaves, buds, etc.) To encourage bushy growth. â€”n. 1 act of pinching. 2 amount that can be taken up with fingers and thumb (pinch of snuff). 3 the stress caused by poverty etc.  at (or in) a pinch in an emergency. [french pincer]'),
(28236, 'p', '\r\nPinchbeck', ' â€”n. Goldlike alloy of copper and zinc used in cheap jewellery etc. â€”adj. Counterfeit, sham. [pinchbeck, name of a watchmaker]'),
(28237, 'p', '\r\nPincushion', ' n. Small pad for holding pins.'),
(28238, 'p', '\r\nPine1', ' n. 1 evergreen coniferous tree with needle-shaped leaves growing in clusters. 2 its wood. 3 (attrib.) Made of pine. [latin pinus]'),
(28239, 'p', '\r\nPine2', ' v. (-ning) 1 (often foll. By away) decline or waste away from grief etc. 2 long eagerly. [old english]'),
(28240, 'p', '\r\nPineal', ' adj. Shaped like a pine cone. [latin pinea: related to *pine1]'),
(28241, 'p', '\r\nPineal', 'body  n. (also pineal gland) conical gland in the brain, secreting a hormone-like substance.'),
(28242, 'p', '\r\nPineapple', ' n. 1 large juicy tropical fruit with yellow flesh and tough segmented skin. 2 plant bearing this. [from *pine1, *apple]'),
(28243, 'p', '\r\nPine', 'cone  n. Fruit of the pine.'),
(28244, 'p', '\r\nPine', 'nut  n. Edible seed of various pines.'),
(28245, 'p', '\r\nPing', ' â€”n. Single short high ringing sound. â€”v. (cause to) make a ping. [imitative]'),
(28246, 'p', '\r\nPing-pong', ' n. Colloq. = *table tennis. [imitative]'),
(28247, 'p', '\r\nPinhead', ' n. 1 head of a pin. 2 very small thing or spot. 3 colloq. Stupid person.'),
(28248, 'p', '\r\nPinhole', ' n. 1 hole made by a pin. 2 hole into which a peg fits.'),
(28249, 'p', '\r\nPinhole', 'camera  n. Camera with a pinhole aperture and no lens.'),
(28250, 'p', '\r\nPinion1', ' â€”n. 1 outer part of a bird''s wing. 2 poet. Wing; flight-feather. â€”v. 1 cut off the pinion of (a wing or bird) to prevent flight. 2 a bind the arms of (a person). B (often foll. By to) bind (the arms, a person, etc.) Fast to a thing. [latin pinna] '),
(28251, 'p', '\r\nPinion2', ' n. 1 small cog-wheel engaging with a larger one. 2 cogged spindle engaging with a wheel. [latin pinea pine-cone: related to *pine1]'),
(28252, 'p', '\r\nPink1', ' â€”n. 1 pale red colour. 2 cultivated plant with fragrant flowers. 3 (prec. By the) the most perfect condition, the peak (the pink of health). 4 person with socialist tendencies. â€”adj. 1 of a pale red colour. 2 tending to socialism.  in the pink colloq. In very good health.  pinkish adj. Pinkness n. Pinky adj. [origin unknown]'),
(28253, 'p', '\r\nPink2', ' v. 1 pierce slightly. 2 cut a scalloped or zigzag edge on. [perhaps from low german or dutch]'),
(28254, 'p', '\r\nPink3', ' v. (of a vehicle engine) emit high-pitched explosive sounds caused by faulty combustion. [imitative]'),
(28255, 'p', '\r\nPink', 'gin  n. Gin flavoured with angostura bitters.'),
(28256, 'p', '\r\nPinking', 'shears  n.pl. Dressmaker''s serrated shears for cutting a zigzag edge.'),
(28257, 'p', '\r\nPinko', ' adj. (pl. -s) esp. Us slang socialist.'),
(28258, 'p', '\r\nPin-money', ' n. 1 hist. Allowance to a woman from her husband. 2 very small sum of money.'),
(28259, 'p', '\r\nPinnace', ' n. Ship''s small boat. [french]'),
(28260, 'p', '\r\nPinnacle', ' n. 1 culmination or climax. 2 natural peak. 3 small ornamental turret crowning a buttress, roof, etc. [latin pinna *pin]'),
(28261, 'p', '\r\nPinnate', ' adj. (of a compound leaf) having leaflets on either side of the leaf-stalk. [latin pinnatus feathered: related to *pinnacle]'),
(28262, 'p', '\r\nPinny', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Pinafore. [abbreviation]'),
(28263, 'p', '\r\nPinpoint', ' â€”n. 1 point of a pin. 2 something very small or sharp. 3 (attrib.) Precise, accurate. â€”v. Locate with precision.'),
(28264, 'p', '\r\nPinprick', ' n. Trifling irritation.'),
(28265, 'p', '\r\nPins', 'and needles  n.pl. Tingling sensation in a limb recovering from numbness.'),
(28266, 'p', '\r\nPinstripe', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Narrow stripe in cloth (pinstripe suit). 2 (in sing. Or pl.) Pinstripe suit (came wearing his pinstripes).  pinstriped adj.'),
(28267, 'p', '\r\nPint', ' n. 1 measure of capacity for liquids etc., 1/8 gal. (0.57, us 0.47, litre). 2  a colloq. Pint of beer. B pint of a liquid, esp. Milk. 3 measure of shellfish containable in a pint mug. [french]'),
(28268, 'p', '\r\nPinta', ' n. Colloq. Pint of milk. [corruption of pint of]'),
(28269, 'p', '\r\nPin-table', ' n. Table used in playing pinball.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(28270, 'p', '\r\nPintail', ' n. Duck or grouse with a pointed tail.'),
(28271, 'p', '\r\nPintle', ' n. Pin or bolt, esp. One on which some other part turns. [old english]'),
(28272, 'p', '\r\nPint-sized', ' adj. Colloq. Very small.'),
(28273, 'p', '\r\nPin-tuck', ' n. Very narrow ornamental tuck.'),
(28274, 'p', '\r\nPin-up', ' n. 1 photograph of a popular or sexually attractive person, hung on the wall. 2 person in such a photograph.'),
(28275, 'p', '\r\nPin-wheel', ' n. Small catherine wheel.'),
(28276, 'p', '\r\nPinyin', ' n. System of romanized spelling for transliterating chinese. [chinese]'),
(28277, 'p', '\r\nPion', ' n. (also pi meson) subatomic particle having a mass many times greater than that of an electron. [from *pi]'),
(28278, 'p', '\r\nPioneer', ' â€”n. 1 initiator of an enterprise; investigator of a subject etc. 2 explorer or settler; colonist. â€”v. 1 initiate (an enterprise etc.) For others to follow. 2 be a pioneer. [french pionnier: related to *pawn1]'),
(28279, 'p', '\r\nPious', ' adj. 1 devout; religious. 2 sanctimonious. 3 dutiful.  piously adv. Piousness n. [latin]'),
(28280, 'p', '\r\nPip1', ' â€”n. Seed of an apple, pear, orange, grape, etc. â€”v. (-pp-) remove the pips from (fruit etc.).  pipless adj. [abbreviation of *pippin]'),
(28281, 'p', '\r\nPip2', ' n. Short high-pitched sound, usu. Electronically produced, esp. As a time signal. [imitative]'),
(28282, 'p', '\r\nPip3', ' v. (-pp-) colloq. 1 hit with a shot. 2 (also pip at the post) defeat narrowly or at the last moment. [origin unknown]'),
(28283, 'p', '\r\nPip4', ' n. 1 any of the spots on a playing-card, dice, or domino. 2  star (1â€“3 according to rank) on the shoulder of an army officer''s uniform. [origin unknown]'),
(28284, 'p', '\r\nPip5', ' n. 1 disease of poultry etc. 2 colloq. Fit of disgust or bad temper (esp. Give one the pip). [low german or dutch]'),
(28285, 'p', '\r\nPipe', ' â€”n. 1 tube of metal, plastic, etc., used to convey water, gas, etc. 2 a narrow tube with a bowl at one end containing tobacco for smoking. B quantity of tobacco held by this. 3  a wind instrument of a single tube. B any of the tubes by which sound is produced in an organ. C (in pl.) = *bagpipes. 4 tubular organ, vessel, etc. In an animal''s body. 5 high note or song, esp. Of a bird. 6 a boatswain''s whistle. B sounding of this. 7 cask for wine, esp. As a measure, usu. = 105 gal. (about 477 litres). â€”v. (-ping) 1 a convey (oil, water, gas, etc.) By pipes. B provide with pipes. 2 play (a tune etc.) On a pipe or pipes. 3 (esp. As piped adj.) Transmit (recorded music etc.) By wire or cable. 4 (usu. Foll. By up, on, to, etc.) Naut. A summon (a crew). B signal the arrival of (an officer etc.) On board. 5 utter in a shrill voice. 6 decorate or trim with piping. 7 lead or bring (a person etc.) By the sound of a pipe or pipes.  pipe down colloq. Be quiet or less insistent. Pipe up begin to play, sing, speak, etc.  pipeful n. (pl. -s). [latin pipo chirp]'),
(28286, 'p', '\r\nPipeclay', ' n. Fine white clay used for tobacco-pipes, whitening leather, etc.'),
(28287, 'p', '\r\nPipe-cleaner', ' n. Piece of flexible tufted wire for cleaning a tobacco-pipe.'),
(28288, 'p', '\r\nPipedream', ' n. Unattainable or fanciful hope or scheme. [originally as experienced when smoking an opium pipe]'),
(28289, 'p', '\r\nPipeline', ' n. 1 long, usu. Underground, pipe for conveying esp. Oil. 2 channel supplying goods, information, etc.  in the pipeline being dealt with or prepared; under discussion, on the way.'),
(28290, 'p', '\r\nPiper', ' n. Person who plays a pipe, esp. The bagpipes.'),
(28291, 'p', '\r\nPipette', ' n. Chem slender tube for transferring or measuring small quantities of liquids. [french diminutive: related to *pipe]'),
(28292, 'p', '\r\nPiping', ' n. 1 pipelike fold or cord for edging or decorating clothing, upholstery, etc. 2 ornamental lines of icing, cream, potato, etc. On a cake etc. 3 lengths of pipe, system of pipes.  piping hot (of food, water, etc.) Very hot.'),
(28293, 'p', '\r\nPipit', ' n. Small bird resembling a lark. [imitative]'),
(28294, 'p', '\r\nPippin', ' n. 1 apple grown from seed. 2 red and yellow eating apple. [french]'),
(28295, 'p', '\r\nPipsqueak', ' n. Colloq. Insignificant or contemptible person or thing. [imitative]'),
(28296, 'p', '\r\nPiquant', ' adj. 1 agreeably pungent, sharp, or appetizing. 2 pleasantly stimulating to the mind.  piquancy n. [french piquer prick]'),
(28297, 'p', '\r\nPique', ' â€”v. (piques, piqued, piquing) 1 wound the pride of, irritate. 2 arouse (curiosity, interest, etc.). â€”n. Resentment; hurt pride. [french: related to *piquant]'),
(28298, 'p', '\r\nPiquet', ' n. Card-game for two players with a pack of 32 cards. [french]'),
(28299, 'p', '\r\nPiracy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 robbery of ships at sea. 2 similar practice, esp. Hijacking. 3 infringement of copyright etc. [related to *pirate]'),
(28300, 'p', '\r\nPiranha', ' n. Voracious s. American freshwater fish. [portuguese]'),
(28301, 'p', '\r\nPirate', ' â€”n. 1 a seafaring robber attacking ships. B ship used by pirates. 2 (often attrib.) Person who infringes another''s copyright or business rights or who broadcasts without official authorization (pirate radio station). â€”v. (-ting) reproduce (a book etc.) Or trade (goods) without permission.  piratical adj. [latin pirata from greek]'),
(28302, 'p', '\r\nPirouette', ' â€”n. Dancer''s spin on one foot or the point of the toe. â€”v. (-tting) perform a pirouette. [french, = spinning-top]'),
(28303, 'p', '\r\nPiscatorial', ' adj. Of fishermen or fishing.  piscatorially adv. [latin piscator angler, from piscis fish]'),
(28304, 'p', '\r\nPisces', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 constellation and twelfth sign of the zodiac (the fish or fishes). 2 person born when the sun is in this sign. [latin, pl. Of piscis fish]'),
(28305, 'p', '\r\nPiscina', ' n. (pl. -nae or -s) 1 stone basin near the altar in a church for draining water used in rinsing the chalice etc. 2 fish-pond. [latin, from piscis fish]'),
(28306, 'p', '\r\nPiss', ' coarse slang â€”v. 1 urinate. 2 discharge (blood etc.) With urine. 3 (as pissed adj.) Drunk. â€”n. 1 urine. 2 act of urinating.  piss about fool or mess about. Piss down rain heavily. Piss off 1 go away. 2 (often as pissed off adj.) Annoy; depress. Piss on (a person or thing) show utter contempt for. Take the piss (often foll. By out of) mock; make fun of. [french, imitative]'),
(28307, 'p', '\r\nPiss', 'artist  n. 1 drunkard. 2 person who fools about.'),
(28308, 'p', '\r\nPiss-taking', ' n. Mockery.  piss-take n. Piss-taker n.'),
(28309, 'p', '\r\nPiss-up', ' n. Drinking spree.'),
(28310, 'p', '\r\nPistachio', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 edible pale-green nut. 2 tree yielding this. [persian pistah]'),
(28311, 'p', '\r\nPiste', ' n. Ski-run of compacted snow. [french, = racetrack]'),
(28312, 'p', '\r\nPistil', ' n. Female organs of a flower, comprising the stigma, style, and ovary.  pistillate adj.  [latin: related to *pestle]'),
(28313, 'p', '\r\nPistol', ' n. Small handgun. [czech pist''al]'),
(28314, 'p', '\r\nPiston', ' n. 1 sliding cylinder fitting closely in a tube in which it moves up and down, used in an internal-combustion engine to impart motion, or in a pump to receive motion. 2 sliding valve in a trumpet etc. [italian: related to *pestle]'),
(28315, 'p', '\r\nPiston-ring', ' n. Ring on a piston sealing the gap between piston and cylinder wall.'),
(28316, 'p', '\r\nPiston-rod', ' n. Rod or crankshaft by which a piston imparts motion.'),
(28317, 'p', '\r\nPit1', ' â€”n. 1 a deep hole in the ground, usu. Large. B coalmine. C covered hole as a trap for animals. 2 hollow on a surface, esp. An indentation of the skin. 3 a = orchestra pit (see *orchestra 2). B usu. Hist. Seating at the back of the stalls. 4 (the pits) slang worst imaginable place, situation, person, etc. 5 a area at the side of a track where racing cars are serviced and refuelled. B sunken area in a workshop floor for access to a car''s underside. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 (usu. Foll. By against) set (one''s wits, strength, etc.) In competition. 2 (usu. As pitted adj.) Make pits, scars, craters, etc. In. 3 put into a pit. [old english from latin puteus well]'),
(28318, 'p', '\r\nPit2', ' v. (-tt-) (usu. As pitted adj.) Remove stones from (fruit). [origin uncertain]'),
(28319, 'p', '\r\nPita', ' var. Of *pitta.'),
(28320, 'p', '\r\nPit-a-pat', ' (also pitter-patter) â€”adv. 1 with a sound like quick light steps. 2 falteringly (heart went pit-a-pat). â€”n. Such a sound. [imitative]'),
(28321, 'p', '\r\nPit', 'bull terrier  n. Small american dog noted for ferocity.'),
(28322, 'p', '\r\nPitch1', ' â€”v. 1 erect and fix (a tent, camp, etc.). 2 throw. 3 fix in a definite position. 4 express in a particular style or at a particular level. 5 (often foll. By against, into, etc.) Fall heavily, esp. Headlong. 6 (of a ship etc.) Plunge backwards and forwards in a lengthwise direction. 7 mus. Set at a particular pitch. 8 cricket a cause (a bowled ball) to strike the ground at a specified point etc. B (of a ball) strike the ground thus. â€”n. 1 area of play in a field-game. 2 height, degree, intensity, etc. (excitement had reached such a pitch). 3 degree of slope, esp. Of a roof. 4 mus. Quality of a sound governed by the rate of vibrations producing it; highness or lowness of a note. 5 act of throwing. 6 pitching motion of a ship etc. 7 colloq.'),
(28323, 'p', '\r\nPitch2', ' â€”n. Dark resinous substance from the distillation of tar or turpentine, used for making ships watertight etc. â€”v. Coat with pitch.  pitchy adj. (-ier, -iest). [latin pix pic-]'),
(28324, 'p', '\r\nPitch-black', ' adj. (also pitch-dark) very or completely dark.'),
(28325, 'p', '\r\nPitchblende', ' n. Uranium oxide occurring in pitchlike masses and yielding radium. [german: related to *pitch2]'),
(28326, 'p', '\r\nPitched', 'battle  n. 1 vigorous argument etc. 2 planned battle between sides in prepared positions and on chosen ground.'),
(28327, 'p', '\r\nPitched', 'roof  n. Sloping roof.'),
(28328, 'p', '\r\nPitcher1', ' n. Large jug with a lip and a handle. [related to *beaker]'),
(28329, 'p', '\r\nPitcher2', ' n. Player who delivers the ball in baseball.'),
(28330, 'p', '\r\nPitchfork', ' â€”n. Long-handled two-pronged fork for pitching hay etc. â€”v. 1 throw with or as if with a pitchfork. 2 (usu. Foll. By into) thrust (a person) forcibly into a position, office, etc.'),
(28331, 'p', '\r\nPitch-pine', ' n. Pine-tree yielding much resin.'),
(28332, 'p', '\r\nPiteous', ' adj. Deserving or arousing pity; wretched.  piteously adv. Piteousness n. [romanic: related to *pity]'),
(28333, 'p', '\r\nPitfall', ' n. 1 unsuspected danger or drawback. 2 covered pit for trapping animals.'),
(28334, 'p', '\r\nPith', ' n. 1 spongy white tissue lining the rind of an orange etc. 2 essential part. 3 spongy tissue in the stems and branches of plants. 4 strength; vigour; energy. [old english]'),
(28335, 'p', '\r\nPit-head', ' n. 1 top of a mineshaft. 2 area surrounding this (also attrib.: pit-head ballot).'),
(28336, 'p', '\r\nPith', 'helmet  n. Protective sun-helmet made of dried pith from plants.'),
(28337, 'p', '\r\nPithy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of style, speech, etc.) Terse and forcible. 2 of or like pith.  pithily adv. Pithiness n.'),
(28338, 'p', '\r\nPitiable', ' adj. Deserving or arousing pity or contempt.  pitiably adv. [french: related to *pity]'),
(28339, 'p', '\r\nPitiful', ' adj. 1 causing pity. 2 contemptible.  pitifully adv.'),
(28340, 'p', '\r\nPitiless', ' adj. Showing no pity (pitiless heat).  pitilessly adv.'),
(28341, 'p', '\r\nPit', 'of the stomach  n. Depression below the breastbone.'),
(28342, 'p', '\r\nPiton', ' n. Peg driven into rock or a crack to support a climber or rope. [french]'),
(28343, 'p', '\r\nPitta', ' n. (also pita) flat hollow unleavened bread which can be split and filled. [modern greek, = a kind of cake]'),
(28344, 'p', '\r\nPittance', ' n. Very small allowance or remuneration. [romanic: related to *pity]'),
(28345, 'p', '\r\nPitter-patter', ' var. Of *pit-a-pat.'),
(28346, 'p', '\r\nPituitary', ' n. (pl. -ies) (also pituitary gland) small ductless gland at the base of the brain. [latin pituita phlegm]'),
(28347, 'p', '\r\nPity', ' â€”n. 1 sorrow and compassion for another''s suffering. 2 cause for regret (what a pity!). â€”v. (-ies, -ied) feel (often contemptuous) pity for.  take pity on help out of pity for.  pitying adj. Pityingly adv. [latin: related to *piety]'),
(28348, 'p', '\r\nPivot', ' â€”n. 1 shaft or pin on which something turns or oscillates. 2 crucial or essential person, point, etc. â€”v. (-t-) 1 turn on or as on a pivot. 2 provide with a pivot.  pivotal adj. [french]'),
(28349, 'p', '\r\nPixel', ' n. Any of the minute areas of uniform illumination of which an image on a display screen is composed. [abbreviation of picture element]'),
(28350, 'p', '\r\nPixie', ' n. (also pixy) (pl. -ies) fairy-like being. [origin unknown]'),
(28351, 'p', '\r\nPizza', ' n. Italian dish of a layer of dough baked with a topping of tomatoes, cheese, etc. [italian, = pie]'),
(28352, 'p', '\r\nPizzeria', ' n. Pizza restaurant.'),
(28353, 'p', '\r\nPizzicato', ' mus. â€”adv. Plucking. â€”adj. (of a note, passage, etc.) Performed pizzicato. â€”n. (pl. -s or -ti) note, passage, etc. Played pizzicato. [italian]'),
(28354, 'p', '\r\nPl.', ' Abbr. 1 plural. 2 (usu. Pl.) Place. 3 plate.'),
(28355, 'p', '\r\nPlacable', ' adj. Easily placated; mild; forgiving.  placability n. [latin placo appease]'),
(28356, 'p', '\r\nPlacard', ' â€”n. Large notice for public display. â€”v. Set up placards on (a wall etc.). [french from dutch placken glue (v.)]'),
(28357, 'p', '\r\nPlacate', ' v. (-ting) pacify; conciliate.  placatory adj. [latin placo appease]'),
(28358, 'p', '\r\nPlace', ' â€”n. 1 a particular portion of space. B portion of space occupied by a person or thing. C proper or natural position. 2 city, town, village, etc. 3 residence, home. 4 group of houses in a town etc., esp. A square. 5 (esp. Large) country house. 6 rank or status. 7 space, esp. A seat, for a person. 8 building or area for a specific purpose (place of work). 9 point reached in a book etc. (lost my place). 10 particular spot on a surface, esp. Of the skin (sore place). 11 a employment or office. B duties or entitlements of office etc. (not my place to criticize). 12 position as a member of a team, student in a college, etc. 13  any of the first three (or four) positions in a race, esp. Other than the winner. 14 position of a digit in a series indicated in decimal or similar notation. â€”v. (-cing) 1 put in a particular or proper place or state or order; arrange. 2 identify, classify, or remember correctly. 3 assign to a particular place, class, or rank; locate. 4 find employment or a living etc. For. 5 make or state (an order or bet etc.). 6 (often foll. By in, on, etc.) Have (confidence etc.). 7 state the position of (any of the first three or four runners) in a race. 8 (as placed adj.) Among the first three (or four) in a race.  give place to 1 make room for. 2 yield precedence to. 3 be succeeded by. Go places colloq. Be successful. In place in the right position; suitable. In place of in exchange for; instead of. In places at only some places or parts. Out of place 1 in the wrong position. 2 unsuitable. Put a person in his (or her) place deflate a person. Take place occur. Take the place of be substituted for.  placement n. [latin platea broad way]'),
(28359, 'p', '\r\nPlacebo', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 medicine with no physiological effect prescribed for psychological reasons. 2 dummy pill etc. Used in a controlled trial. [latin, = i shall be acceptable]'),
(28360, 'p', '\r\nPlace-kick', ' n. Kick in football with the ball placed on the ground.'),
(28361, 'p', '\r\nPlace-mat', ' n. Small table-mat for a person''s plate.'),
(28362, 'p', '\r\nPlace-name', ' n. Name of a town, village, etc.'),
(28363, 'p', '\r\nPlacenta', ' n. (pl. -tae or -s) organ in the uterus of pregnant mammals nourishing the foetus through the umbilical cord and expelled after birth.  placental adj. [greek, = flat cake]'),
(28364, 'p', '\r\nPlacer', ' n. Deposit of sand, gravel, etc. Containing valuable minerals in particles. [american spanish]'),
(28365, 'p', '\r\nPlace-setting', ' n. Set of cutlery etc. For one person at a table.'),
(28366, 'p', '\r\nPlacid', ' adj. 1 calm; not easily excited or irritated. 2 tranquil, serene.  placidity n. Placidly adv. Placidness n. [latin placeo please]'),
(28367, 'p', '\r\nPlacket', ' n. 1 opening or slit in a garment, for fastenings or access to a pocket. 2 flap of fabric under this. [var. Of *placard]'),
(28368, 'p', '\r\nPlagiarize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (also absol.) Take and pass off (another''s thoughts, writings, etc.) As one''s own. 2 pass off the thoughts etc. Of (another person) as one''s own.  plagiarism n. Plagiarist n. Plagiarizer n. [latin plagiarius kidnapper]'),
(28369, 'p', '\r\nPlague', ' â€”n. 1 deadly contagious disease. 2 (foll. By of) colloq. Infestation of a pest etc. 3 great trouble or affliction. 4 colloq. Nuisance. â€”v. (plagues, plagued, plaguing) 1 colloq. Pester, annoy. 2 afflict, hinder (plagued by back pain). 3 affect with plague. [latin plaga stroke, infection]'),
(28370, 'p', '\r\nPlaice', ' n. (pl. Same) marine flat-fish used as food. [latin platessa]'),
(28371, 'p', '\r\nPlaid', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Chequered or tartan, esp. Woollen, twilled cloth (plaid skirt). 2 long piece of this worn over the shoulder in highland scottish costume. [gaelic]'),
(28372, 'p', '\r\nPlain', ' â€”adj. 1 clear, evident. 2 readily understood, simple. 3 (of food, decoration, etc.) Simple. 4 not beautiful or distinguished-looking. 5 outspoken; straightforward. 6 unsophisticated; not luxurious (a plain man; plain living). â€”adv. 1 clearly. 2 simply. â€”n. 1 level tract of country. 2 basic knitting stitch.  plainly adv. Plainness n. [latin planus]'),
(28373, 'p', '\r\nPlainchant', ' n. = *plainsong.'),
(28374, 'p', '\r\nPlain', 'chocolate  n. Dark chocolate without added milk.'),
(28375, 'p', '\r\nPlain', 'clothes  n.pl. Ordinary clothes, not uniform (plain-clothes police).'),
(28376, 'p', '\r\nPlain', 'dealing  n. Candour; straightforwardness.'),
(28377, 'p', '\r\nPlain', 'flour  n. Flour containing no raising agent.'),
(28378, 'p', '\r\nPlain', 'sailing  n. Uncomplicated situation or course of action.'),
(28379, 'p', '\r\nPlainsong', ' n. Unaccompanied church music sung in unison in medieval modes and in free rhythm corresponding to the accentuation of the words.'),
(28380, 'p', '\r\nPlain-spoken', ' adj. Frank.'),
(28381, 'p', '\r\nPlaint', ' n. 1 law accusation; charge. 2 literary complaint, lamentation. [french plainte from latin plango lament]'),
(28382, 'p', '\r\nPlaintiff', ' n. Person who brings a case against another into court. [french plaintif: related to *plaintive]'),
(28383, 'p', '\r\nPlaintive', ' adj. Expressing sorrow; mournful-sounding.  plaintively adv. [french: related to *plaint]'),
(28384, 'p', '\r\nPlait', ' â€”n. Length of hair, straw, etc., in three or more interlaced strands. â€”v. 1 weave (hair etc.) Into a plait. 2 make by interlacing strands (plaited belt). [french pleit from latin plico fold]'),
(28385, 'p', '\r\nPlan', ' â€”n. 1 method or procedure for doing something; design, scheme, or intention. 2 drawing etc. Of a building or structure, made by projection on to a horizontal plane. 3 map of a town or district. 4 scheme of an arrangement (seating plan). â€”v. (-nn-) 1 arrange (a procedure etc.) Beforehand; form a plan; intend. 2 make a plan of or design for. 3 (as planned adj.) In accordance with a plan (planned parenthood). 4 make plans.  plan on (often foll. By pres. Part.) Colloq. Aim at; intend.  planning n. [french]'),
(28386, 'p', '\r\nPlanchette', ' n. Small board on castors with a pencil, said to write spirit messages when a person''s fingers rest lightly on it. [french diminutive: related to *plank]'),
(28387, 'p', '\r\nPlane1', ' â€”n. 1 flat surface such that a straight line joining any two points on it lies wholly in it. 2 level surface. 3 colloq. = *aeroplane. 4 flat surface producing lift by the action of air or water over and under it (usu. In comb.: hydroplane). 5 (often foll. By of) level of attainment, knowledge, etc. â€”adj. 1 (of a surface etc.) Perfectly level. 2 (of an angle, figure, etc.) Lying in a plane. â€”v. (-ning) glide. [latin planus *plain]'),
(28388, 'p', '\r\nPlane2', ' â€”n. Tool for smoothing a usu. Wooden surface by paring shavings from it. â€”v. (-ning) 1 smooth with a plane. 2 (often foll. By away, down) pare with a plane. [latin: related to *plane1]'),
(28389, 'p', '\r\nPlane3', ' n. Tall tree with maple-like leaves and bark which peels in uneven patches. [greek platanos]'),
(28390, 'p', '\r\nPlanet', ' n. Celestial body orbiting round a star.  planetary adj. [greek, = wanderer]'),
(28391, 'p', '\r\nPlanetarium', ' n. (pl. -s or -ria) 1 domed building in which images of stars, planets, constellations, etc. Are projected. 2 device for such projection.'),
(28392, 'p', '\r\nPlangent', ' adj. Literary 1 loud and reverberating. 2 plaintive. [latin: related to *plaint]'),
(28393, 'p', '\r\nPlank', ' â€”n. 1 long flat piece of timber. 2 item in a political or other programme. â€”v. 1 provide or cover with planks. 2 (usu. Foll. By down) colloq. A put down or deposit roughly or violently. B pay (money) on the spot.  walk the plank hist. Be made to walk blindfold along a plank over the side of a ship to one''s death in the sea. [latin planca]'),
(28394, 'p', '\r\nPlanking', ' n. Planks as flooring etc.'),
(28395, 'p', '\r\nPlankton', ' n. Chiefly microscopic organisms drifting in the sea or fresh water. [greek, = wandering]'),
(28396, 'p', '\r\nPlanner', ' n. 1 person who plans new towns etc. 2 person who makes plans. 3 list, table, etc., with information helpful in planning.'),
(28397, 'p', '\r\nPlanning', 'permission  n. Formal permission for building etc., esp. From a local authority.'),
(28398, 'p', '\r\nPlant', ' â€”n. 1 a organism usu. Containing chlorophyll enabling it to live wholly on inorganic substances, and lacking the power of voluntary movement. B small organism of this kind, as distinguished from a shrub or tree. 2 a machinery, fixtures, etc., used in industry. B factory. 3 colloq. Something deliberately placed so as to incriminate another. â€”v. 1 place (seeds, plants, etc.) In soil for growing. 2 (often foll. By in, on, etc.) Put or fix in position. 3 (often refl.) Station (a person etc.), esp. As a spy. 4 cause (an idea etc.) To be established, esp. In another person''s mind. 5 deliver (a blow, kiss, etc.) With a deliberate aim. 6 colloq. Place (something incriminating) for later discovery.   plant out transfer from a pot or frame to the open ground; set out (seedlings) at intervals.  plantlike adj. [latin planta]'),
(28399, 'p', '\r\nPlantain1', ' n. Plant with broad flat leaves spread close to the ground and seeds used as food for birds. [latin plantago]'),
(28400, 'p', '\r\nPlantain2', ' n. 1 a kind of banana plant, grown for its fruit. 2 banana-like fruit of this. [spanish]'),
(28401, 'p', '\r\nPlantation', ' n. 1 estate on which cotton, tobacco, etc. Is cultivated. 2 area planted with trees etc. 3 hist. Colony. [latin: related to *plant]'),
(28402, 'p', '\r\nPlanter', ' n. 1 manager or owner of a plantation. 2 container for house-plants.'),
(28403, 'p', '\r\nPlaque', ' n. 1 commemorative tablet, esp. Fixed to a building. 2 deposit on teeth where bacteria proliferate. [dutch plak tablet: related to *placard]'),
(28404, 'p', '\r\nPlasma', ' n. (also plasm) 1 a colourless fluid part of blood, lymph, or milk, in which corpuscles or fat-globules are suspended. B this taken from blood for transfusions. 2 = *protoplasm. 3 gas of positive ions and free electrons in about equal numbers.  plasmic adj. [greek plasso shape (v.)]'),
(28405, 'p', '\r\nPlaster', ' â€”n. 1 soft mixture of lime, sand, and water etc. Applied to walls, ceilings, etc., to dry into a smooth hard surface. 2  = *sticking-plaster. 3 = *plaster of paris. â€”v. 1 cover (a wall etc.) With plaster. 2 coat, daub, cover thickly. 3 stick or apply (a thing) thickly like plaster. 4 (often foll. By down) smooth (esp. Hair) with water etc. 5 (as plastered adj.) Slang drunk.  plasterer n.  [greek emplastron]'),
(28406, 'p', '\r\nPlasterboard', ' n. Two boards with a filling of plaster for partitions, walls, etc.'),
(28407, 'p', '\r\nPlaster', 'cast  n. 1 bandage stiffened with plaster of paris and applied to a broken limb etc. 2 statue or mould made of plaster.'),
(28408, 'p', '\r\nPlaster', 'of paris  n. Fine white gypsum plaster for plaster casts etc.'),
(28409, 'p', '\r\nPlastic', ' â€”n. 1 synthetic resinous substance that can be given any shape. 2 (in full plastic money) colloq. Credit card(s). â€”adj. 1 made of plastic. 2 capable of being moulded; pliant, supple. 3 giving form to clay, wax, etc.  plasticity n. Plasticize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). Plasticizer n. (also -iser). Plasticky adj. [greek: related to *plasma]'),
(28410, 'p', '\r\nPlastic', 'arts  n.pl. Arts involving modelling or the representation of solid objects.'),
(28411, 'p', '\r\nPlastic', 'bomb  n. Bomb containing plastic explosive.'),
(28412, 'p', '\r\nPlastic', 'explosive  n. Putty-like explosive.'),
(28413, 'p', '\r\nPlasticine', ' n. Propr. Pliant material used for modelling.'),
(28414, 'p', '\r\nPlastic', 'surgery  n. Reconstruction or repair of damaged or unsightly skin, muscle, etc., esp. By the transfer of tissue.  plastic surgeon n.'),
(28415, 'p', '\r\nPlate', ' â€”n. 1 a shallow usu. Circular vessel from which food is eaten or served. B contents of this. 2 similar vessel used for a collection in church etc. 3 (collect.) A utensils of silver, gold, or other metal. B objects of plated metal. 4 piece of metal with a name or inscription for affixing to a door etc. 5 illustration on special paper in a book. 6 thin sheet of metal, glass, etc., coated with a sensitive film for photography. 7 flat thin usu. Rigid sheet of metal etc., often as part of a mechanism. 8 a smooth piece of metal etc. For engraving. B impression from this. 9 a silver or gold cup as a prize for a horse-race etc. B race with this as a prize. 10 a thin piece of plastic material, moulded to the shape of the mouth, on which artificial teeth are mounted. B colloq. Denture. 11 each of several rigid sheets of rock thought to form the earth''s outer crust. 12 thin flat organic structure or formation. â€”v. (-ting) 1 apply a thin coat esp. Of silver, gold, or tin to (another metal). 2 cover (esp. A ship) with plates of metal, for protection.  on a plate colloq. Available with little trouble to the recipient. On one''s plate colloq. For one to deal with.  plateful n. (pl. -s). [latin platta from plattus flat]'),
(28416, 'p', '\r\nPlateau', ' â€”n. (pl. -x or -s) 1 area of fairly level high ground. 2 state of little variation after an increase. â€”v. (plateauing, plateaus, plateaued) (often foll. By out) reach a level or static state after an increase. [french: related to *plate]'),
(28417, 'p', '\r\nPlate', 'glass  n. Thick fine-quality glass for shop windows etc.'),
(28418, 'p', '\r\nPlatelayer', ' n. Person employed in fixing and repairing railway rails.'),
(28419, 'p', '\r\nPlatelet', ' n. Small colourless disc of protoplasm found in blood and involved in clotting.'),
(28420, 'p', '\r\nPlaten', ' n. 1 plate in a printing-press which presses the paper against the type. 2 cylindrical roller in a typewriter etc. Against which the paper is held. [french platine: related to *plate]'),
(28421, 'p', '\r\nPlate-rack', ' n. Rack in which plates are placed to drain.'),
(28422, 'p', '\r\nPlate', 'tectonics  n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) The study of the earth''s surface based on the concept of moving â€˜platesâ€™ (see sense 11 of *plate) forming its structure.'),
(28423, 'p', '\r\nPlatform', ' n. 1 raised level surface, esp. One from which a speaker addresses an audience or one alongside the line at a railway station. 2 floor area at the entrance to a bus etc. 3 thick sole of a shoe. 4 declared policy of a political party. [french: related to *plate, *form]'),
(28424, 'p', '\r\nPlatinum', ' n. Chem. White heavy precious metallic element that does not tarnish. [earlier platina from spanish, diminutive from plata silver]'),
(28425, 'p', '\r\nPlatinum', 'blonde  (also platinum blond) â€”adj. Silvery-blond. â€”n. Person with such hair.'),
(28426, 'p', '\r\nPlatitude', ' n. Commonplace remark, esp. One solemnly delivered.  platitudinous adj. [french: related to *plate]'),
(28427, 'p', '\r\nPlatonic', ' adj. 1 of plato or his ideas. 2 (platonic) (of love or friendship) not sexual.  [greek platon (5th-4th c. Bc), name of a greek philosopher]'),
(28428, 'p', '\r\nPlatonism', ' n. Philosophy of plato or his followers.  platonist n.'),
(28429, 'p', '\r\nPlatoon', ' n. 1 subdivision of a military company. 2 group of persons acting together. [french peloton diminutive of pelote *pellet]'),
(28430, 'p', '\r\nPlatter', ' n. Large flat dish or plate. [anglo-french plater: related to *plate]'),
(28431, 'p', '\r\nPlatypus', ' n. (pl. -puses) australian aquatic egg-laying mammal, with a ducklike bill and flat tail. [greek, = flat foot]'),
(28432, 'p', '\r\nPlaudit', ' n. (usu. In pl.) 1 round of applause. 2 expression of approval. [latin plaudite, imperative of plaudo plaus- clap]'),
(28433, 'p', '\r\nPlausible', ' adj. 1 (of a statement etc.) Reasonable or probable. 2 (of a person) persuasive but deceptive.  plausibility n. Plausibly adv. [latin: related to *plaudit]'),
(28434, 'p', '\r\nPlay', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By with) occupy or amuse oneself pleasantly. 2 (foll. By with) act light-heartedly or flippantly with (a person''s feelings etc.). 3  a perform on or be able to perform on (a musical instrument). B perform (a piece of music etc.). C cause (a record, record-player, etc.) To produce sounds. 4 a (foll. By in) perform a role in (a drama etc.). B perform (a drama or role) on stage etc. C give a dramatic performance at (a particular theatre or place). 5 act in real life the part of (play truant; play the fool). 6 (foll. By on) perform (a trick or joke etc.) On (a person). 7 colloq. Cooperate; do what is wanted (they won''t play). 8  gamble, gamble on. 9  a take part in (a game or recreation). B compete with (another player or team) in a game. C occupy (a specified position) in a team for a game. D assign (a player) to a position. 10  move (a piece) or display (a playing-card) in one''s turn in a game. 11 (also absol.) Strike (a ball etc.) Or execute (a stroke) in a game. 12 move about in a lively manner; flit, dart. 13 (often foll. By on) a touch gently. B emit light, water, etc. (fountains gently playing). 14  allow (a fish) to exhaust itself pulling against a line. 15 (often foll. By at) a engage half-heartedly (in an activity). B pretend to be. â€”n. 1 recreation, amusement, esp. As the spontaneous activity of children. 2 a playing of a game. B action or manner of this. 3 dramatic piece for the stage etc. 4 activity or operation (the play of fancy). 5 a freedom of movement. B space or scope for this. 6 brisk, light, or fitful movement. 7 gambling.  in (or out of) play sport (of the ball etc.) In (or not in) a position to be played according to the rules. Make a play for colloq. Make a conspicuous attempt to acquire. Make play with use ostentatiously. Play about (or around) behave irresponsibly. Play along pretend to cooperate. Play back play (sounds recently recorded). Play ball colloq. Cooperate. Play by ear 1 perform (music) without having seen it written down. 2 (also play it by ear) colloq. Proceed step by step according to results. Play one''s cards right (or well) colloq. Make good use of opportunities; act shrewdly. Play down minimize the importance of. Played out exhausted of energy or usefulness. Play fast and loose act unreliably. Play the field see *field. Play for time seek to gain time by delaying. Play the game observe the rules; behave honourably. Play havoc (or hell) with colloq. Cause great confusion or difficulty to; disrupt. Play into a person''s hands act so as unwittingly to give a person an advantage. Play it cool colloq. Be relaxed or apparently indifferent. Play the market speculate in stocks etc. Play off (usu. Foll. By against) 1 oppose (one person against another), esp. For one''s own advantage. 2 play an extra match to decide a draw or tie. Play on 1 continue to play. 2 take advantage of (a person''s feelings etc.). Play safe (or for safety) avoid risks. Play up 1 behave mischievously. 2 annoy in this way. 3 cause trouble; be irritating. Play up to flatter, esp. To win favour. Play with fire take foolish risks. [old english]'),
(28435, 'p', '\r\nPlay-act', ' v. 1 act in a play. 2 pretend; behave insincerely.  play-acting n.'),
(28436, 'p', '\r\nPlay-back', ' n. Playing back of a sound.'),
(28437, 'p', '\r\nPlaybill', ' n. Poster advertising a play.'),
(28438, 'p', '\r\nPlayboy', ' n. Wealthy pleasure-seeking man.'),
(28439, 'p', '\r\nPlayer', ' n. 1 participant in a game. 2 person playing a musical instrument. 3 actor.'),
(28440, 'p', '\r\nPlayfellow', ' n. Playmate.'),
(28441, 'p', '\r\nPlayful', ' adj. 1 fond of or inclined to play. 2 done in fun.  playfully adv. Playfulness n.'),
(28442, 'p', '\r\nPlaygoer', ' n. Person who goes often to the theatre.'),
(28443, 'p', '\r\nPlayground', ' n. Outdoor area for children to play in.'),
(28444, 'p', '\r\nPlaygroup', ' n. Organized regular meeting of preschool children for supervised play.'),
(28445, 'p', '\r\nPlayhouse', ' n. Theatre.'),
(28446, 'p', '\r\nPlaying-card', ' n. One of a set of usu. 52 oblong cards, divided into four suits and used in games.'),
(28447, 'p', '\r\nPlaying-field', ' n. Field for outdoor games.'),
(28448, 'p', '\r\nPlaylet', ' n. Short play.'),
(28449, 'p', '\r\nPlaymate', ' n. Child''s companion in play.'),
(28450, 'p', '\r\nPlay-off', ' n. Match played to decide a draw or tie.'),
(28451, 'p', '\r\nPlay', 'on words  n. Pun.'),
(28452, 'p', '\r\nPlay-pen', ' n. Portable enclosure for a young child to play in.'),
(28453, 'p', '\r\nPlay', 'school  n. Nursery school or kindergarten.'),
(28454, 'p', '\r\nPlaything', ' n. 1 toy or other thing to play with. 2 person used merely as an object of amusement or pleasure.'),
(28455, 'p', '\r\nPlaytime', ' n. Time for play or recreation.'),
(28456, 'p', '\r\nPlaywright', ' n. Person who writes plays.'),
(28457, 'p', '\r\nPlc', ' abbr. (also plc) public limited company.'),
(28458, 'p', '\r\nPlea', ' n. 1 appeal, entreaty. 2 law formal statement by or on behalf of a defendant. 3 excuse. [latin placitum decree: related to *please]'),
(28459, 'p', '\r\nPleach', ' v. Entwine or interlace (esp. Branches to form a hedge). [latin: related to *plexus]'),
(28460, 'p', '\r\nPlead', ' v. 1 (foll. By with) make an earnest appeal to. 2 (of an advocate) address a lawcourt. 3 maintain (a cause) in a lawcourt. 4 (foll. By guilty or not guilty) declare oneself to be guilty or not guilty of a charge. 5 allege as an excuse (plead insanity). 6 (often as pleading adj.) Make an appeal or entreaty (in a pleading voice). [anglo-french pleder: related to *plea]'),
(28461, 'p', '\r\nPleading', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Formal statement of the cause of an action or defence.'),
(28462, 'p', '\r\nPleasant', ' adj. (-er, -est) pleasing to the mind, feelings, or senses.  pleasantly adv. [french: related to *please]'),
(28463, 'p', '\r\nPleasantry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 amusing or polite remark. 2 humorous speech. 3 jocularity.'),
(28464, 'p', '\r\nPlease', ' v. (-sing) 1 be agreeable to; make glad; give pleasure. 2 (in passive) a (foll. By to + infin.) Be glad or willing to (am pleased to help). B (often foll. By about, at, with) derive pleasure or satisfaction (from). 3 (with it as subject) be the inclination or wish of (it did not please him to attend). 4 think fit (take as many as you please). 5 used in polite requests (come in, please).  if you please if you are willing, esp. Iron. To indicate unreasonableness (then, if you please, we had to pay). Please oneself do as one likes.  pleased adj. Pleasing adj. [french plaisir from latin placeo]'),
(28465, 'p', '\r\nPleasurable', ' adj. Causing pleasure.  pleasurably adv.'),
(28466, 'p', '\r\nPleasure', ' n. 1 feeling of satisfaction or joy. 2 enjoyment. 3 source of pleasure or gratification. 4 one''s will or desire (what is your pleasure?). 5 sensual gratification. 6 (attrib.) Done or used for pleasure. [french: related to *please]'),
(28467, 'p', '\r\nPleat', ' â€”n. Fold or crease, esp. A flattened fold in cloth doubled upon itself. â€”v. Make a pleat or pleats in. [from *plait]'),
(28468, 'p', '\r\nPleb', ' n. Colloq. Usu. Derog. = *plebeian 2.  plebby adj. [abbreviation of *plebeian]'),
(28469, 'p', '\r\nPlebeian', ' â€”n. 1 commoner, esp. In ancient rome. 2 working-class person, esp. An uncultured one. â€”adj. 1 of the common people. 2 uncultured, coarse. [latin plebs plebis common people]'),
(28470, 'p', '\r\nPlebiscite', ' n. Referendum. [latin plebiscitum: related to *plebeian]'),
(28471, 'p', '\r\nPlectrum', ' n. (pl. -s or -tra) thin flat piece of plastic etc. For plucking the strings of a guitar etc. [greek plesso strike]'),
(28472, 'p', '\r\nPledge', ' â€”n. 1 solemn promise. 2 thing given as security against a debt etc. 3 thing put in pawn. 4 thing given as a token of favour etc., or of something to come. 5 drinking of a person''s health, toast. 6 solemn promise to abstain from alcohol (sign the pledge). â€”v. (-ging) 1 a deposit as security. B pawn. 2 promise solemnly by the pledge of (one''s honour, word, etc.). 3 bind by a solemn promise. 4 drink to the health of.  pledge one''s troth see *troth. [french plege]'),
(28473, 'p', '\r\nPleiades', ' n.pl. Cluster of seven stars in the constellation taurus. [latin from greek]'),
(28474, 'p', '\r\nPleistocene', ' geol. â€”adj. Of the first epoch of the quaternary period. â€”n. This epoch. [greek pleistos most, kainos new]'),
(28475, 'p', '\r\nPlenary', ' adj. 1 (of an assembly) to be attended by all members. 2 entire, unqualified (plenary indulgence). [latin plenus full]'),
(28476, 'p', '\r\nPlenipotentiary', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) person (esp. A diplomat) invested with full authority to act. â€”adj. Having this power. [latin: related to *plenary, *potent]'),
(28477, 'p', '\r\nPlenitude', ' n. Literary 1 fullness, completeness. 2 abundance. [latin: related to *plenary]'),
(28478, 'p', '\r\nPlenteous', ' adj. Literary plentiful. [french plentivous: related to *plenty]'),
(28479, 'p', '\r\nPlentiful', ' adj. Abundant, copious.  plentifully adv.'),
(28480, 'p', '\r\nPlenty', ' â€”n. (often foll. By of) abundance, sufficient quantity or number (we have plenty; plenty of time; a time of plenty). â€”adj. Colloq. Plentiful. â€”adv. Colloq. Fully, quite. [latin plenitas: related to *plenary]'),
(28481, 'p', '\r\nPlenum', ' n. Full assembly of people or a committee etc. [latin, neuter of plenus full]'),
(28482, 'p', '\r\nPleonasm', ' n. Use of more words than are needed (e.g. See with one''s eyes).  pleonastic adj. [greek pleon more]'),
(28483, 'p', '\r\nPlethora', ' n. Over-abundance. [greek, = fullness]'),
(28484, 'p', '\r\nPleura', ' n. (pl. -rae) membrane enveloping the lungs.  pleural adj. [greek pleura rib]'),
(28485, 'p', '\r\nPleurisy', ' n. Inflammation of the pleura.  pleuritic adj. [greek: related to *pleura]'),
(28486, 'p', '\r\nPlexus', ' n. (pl. Same or plexuses) anat. Network of nerves or vessels (solar plexus). [latin plecto plex- plait]'),
(28487, 'p', '\r\nPliable', ' adj. 1 bending easily; supple. 2 yielding, compliant.  pliability n. [french: related to *ply1]'),
(28488, 'p', '\r\nPliant', ' adj. = *pliable 1.  pliancy n.'),
(28489, 'p', '\r\nPliers', ' n.pl. Pincers with parallel flat surfaces for holding small objects, bending wire, etc. [from dial. Ply bend: related to *pliable]'),
(28490, 'p', '\r\nPlight1', ' n. Unfortunate condition or state. [anglo-french plit *plait]'),
(28491, 'p', '\r\nPlight2', ' v. Archaic 1 pledge. 2 (foll. By to) engage (oneself) in marriage.  plight one''s troth see *troth. [old english]'),
(28492, 'p', '\r\nPlimsoll', ' n. (also plimsole) rubber-soled canvas sports shoe. [from *plimsoll line]'),
(28493, 'p', '\r\nPlimsoll', 'line  n. (also plimsoll mark) marking on a ship''s side showing the limit of legal submersion under various conditions. [plimsoll, name of a politician]'),
(28494, 'p', '\r\nPlinth', ' n. 1 lower square slab at the base of a column. 2 base supporting a vase or statue etc. [greek, = tile]'),
(28495, 'p', '\r\nPliocene', ' geol. â€”adj. Of the last epoch of the tertiary period. â€”n. This epoch. [greek pleion more, kainos new]'),
(28496, 'p', '\r\nPlo', ' abbr. Palestine liberation organization.'),
(28497, 'p', '\r\nPlod', ' â€”v. (-dd-) 1 walk doggedly or laboriously; trudge. 2 work slowly and steadily. â€”n. Spell of plodding.  plodder n. [probably imitative]'),
(28498, 'p', '\r\nPlonk1', ' â€”v. 1 set down hurriedly or clumsily. 2 (usu. Foll. By down) set down firmly. â€”n. Heavy thud. [imitative]'),
(28499, 'p', '\r\nPlonk2', ' n. Colloq. Cheap or inferior wine. [origin unknown]'),
(28500, 'p', '\r\nPlonker', ' n. Coarse slang 1 fool. 2 penis.'),
(28501, 'p', '\r\nPlop', ' â€”n. Sound as of a smooth object dropping into water without a splash. â€”v. (-pp-) fall or drop with a plop. â€”adv. With a plop. [imitative]'),
(28502, 'p', '\r\nPlosive', ' â€”adj. Pronounced with a sudden release of breath. â€”n. Plosive sound. [from *explosive]'),
(28503, 'p', '\r\nPlot', ' â€”n. 1 defined and usu. Small piece of land. 2 interrelationship of the main events in a play, novel, film, etc. 3 conspiracy or secret plan. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 make a plan or map of. 2 (also absol.) Plan or contrive secretly (a crime etc.). 3 mark on a chart or diagram. 4  make (a curve etc.) By marking out a number of points. 5  provide (a play, novel, film, etc.) With a plot.  plotter n. [old english and french complot]'),
(28504, 'p', '\r\nPlough', ' (us plow) â€”n. 1 implement for cutting furrows in the soil and turning it up. 2 implement resembling this (snowplough). 3 (the plough) the great bear (see *bear2) or its seven bright stars. â€”v. 1 (also absol.) Turn up (the earth) with a plough. 2 (foll. By out, up, etc.) Turn or extract with a plough. 3 furrow or scratch (a surface) as with a plough. 4 produce (a furrow or line) thus. 5 (foll. By through) advance laboriously, esp. Through work, a book, etc. 6 (foll. By through, into) move violently like a plough. 7 colloq. Fail in an examination.  plough back 1 plough (grass etc.) Into the soil to enrich it.'),
(28505, 'p', '\r\nPloughman', ' n. (us plowman) person who uses a plough.'),
(28506, 'p', '\r\nPloughman''s', 'lunch  n. Meal of bread and cheese with pickle and salad.'),
(28507, 'p', '\r\nPloughshare', ' n. (us plowshare) cutting blade of a plough.'),
(28508, 'p', '\r\nPlover', ' n. Plump-breasted wading bird, e.g. The lapwing. [latin pluvia rain]'),
(28509, 'p', '\r\nPlow', ' (us plow) â€”n. 1 implement for cutting furrows in the soil and turning it up. 2 implement resembling this (snowplough). 3 (the plough) the great bear (see *bear2) or its seven bright stars. â€”v. 1 (also absol.) Turn up (the earth) with a plough. 2 (foll. By out, up, etc.) Turn or extract with a plough. 3 furrow or scratch (a surface) as with a plough. 4 produce (a furrow or line) thus. 5 (foll. By through) advance laboriously, esp. Through work, a book, etc. 6 (foll. By through, into) move violently like a plough. 7 colloq. Fail in an examination.  plough back 1 plough (grass etc.) Into the soil to enrich it. 2 reinvest (profits) in the business producing them. [old english]'),
(28510, 'p', '\r\nPlowman', ' n. (us plowman) person who uses a plough.'),
(28511, 'p', '\r\nPlowshare', ' n. (us plowshare) cutting blade of a plough.'),
(28512, 'p', '\r\nPloy', ' n. Cunning manoeuvre to gain advantage. [origin unknown]'),
(28513, 'p', '\r\nPlr', ' abbr. Public lending right.'),
(28514, 'p', '\r\nPluck', ' â€”v. 1 pick or pull out or away. 2 strip (a bird) of feathers. 3 pull at, twitch. 4 (foll. By at) tug or snatch at. 5 sound (the string of a musical instrument) with a finger or plectrum. 6 plunder. â€”n. 1 courage, spirit. 2 plucking; twitch. 3 animal''s heart, liver, and lungs as food.  pluck up summon up (one''s courage etc.). [old english]'),
(28515, 'p', '\r\nPlucky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) brave, spirited.  pluckily adv. Pluckiness n.'),
(28516, 'p', '\r\nPlug', ' â€”n. 1 piece of solid material fitting tightly into a hole, used to fill a gap or cavity or act as a wedge or stopper. 2 a device of metal pins in an insulated casing, fitting into holes in a socket for making an electrical connection. B colloq. Electric socket. 3 = *spark-plug. 4 colloq. Piece of free publicity for an idea, product, etc. 5 cake or stick of tobacco; piece of this for chewing. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 (often foll. By up) stop (a hole etc.) With a plug. 2 slang shoot or hit (a person etc.). 3 colloq. Seek to popularize (an idea, product, etc.) By constant recommendation. 4 colloq. (foll. By away (at)) work steadily (at).  plug in connect electrically by inserting a plug into a socket. [low german or dutch]'),
(28517, 'p', '\r\nPlug-hole', ' n. Hole, esp. In a sink or bath, which can be closed by a plug.'),
(28518, 'p', '\r\nPlug-in', ' attrib. Adj. Designed to be plugged into a socket.'),
(28519, 'p', '\r\nPlum', ' n. 1 a small sweet oval fleshy fruit with a flattish pointed stone. B tree bearing this. 2 reddish-purple colour. 3 raisin used in cooking. 4 colloq. Something prized (often attrib.: plum job).  have a plum in one''s mouth have an affectedly rich voice. [latin: related to *prune1]'),
(28520, 'p', '\r\nPlumage', ' n. Bird''s feathers. [french: related to *plume]'),
(28521, 'p', '\r\nPlumb', ' â€”n. Lead ball, esp. Attached to the end of a line for finding the depth of water or testing whether a wall etc. Is vertical. â€”adv. 1 exactly (plumb in the centre). 2 vertically. 3 us slang quite, utterly (plumb crazy). â€”adj. Vertical. â€”v. 1 a provide with plumbing. B (often foll. By in) fit as part of a plumbing system. C work as a plumber. 2 sound or test with a plumb. 3 reach or experience (an extreme feeling) (plumb the depths of fear).'),
(28522, 'p', '\r\nPlumber', ' n. Person who fits and repairs the apparatus of a water-supply, heating, etc.'),
(28523, 'p', '\r\nPlumbing', ' n. 1 system or apparatus of water-supply etc. 2 work of a plumber. 3 colloq. Lavatory installations.'),
(28524, 'p', '\r\nPlumb-line', ' n. Line with a plumb attached.'),
(28525, 'p', '\r\nPlume', ' â€”n. 1 feather, esp. A large one used for ornament. 2 ornament of feathers etc. Worn on a helmet or hat or in the hair. 3 something resembling this (plume of smoke). â€”v. (-ming) 1 decorate or provide with a plume or plumes. 2 refl. (foll. By on, upon) pride (oneself on esp. Something trivial). 3 (of a bird) preen (itself or its feathers). [latin pluma]'),
(28526, 'p', '\r\nPlummet', ' â€”n. 1 plumb, plumb-line. 2 sounding-line. 3 weight attached to a fishing-line to keep the float upright. â€”v. (-t-) fall or plunge rapidly. [french: related to *plumb]'),
(28527, 'p', '\r\nPlummy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 abounding or rich in plums. 2 colloq. (of a voice) sounding affectedly rich in tone. 3 colloq. Good, desirable.'),
(28528, 'p', '\r\nPlump1', ' â€”adj. Full or rounded in shape; fleshy. â€”v. (often foll. By up, out) make or become plump (plumped up the cushion).  plumpness n. [low german or dutch plomp blunt]'),
(28529, 'p', '\r\nPlump2', ' â€”v. 1 (foll. By for) decide on, choose. 2 (often foll. By down) drop or fall abruptly. â€”n. Abrupt or heavy fall. â€”adv. Colloq. With a plump. [low german or dutch plompen, imitative]'),
(28530, 'p', '\r\nPlum', 'pudding  n. = *christmas pudding.'),
(28531, 'p', '\r\nPlumy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 plumelike, feathery. 2 adorned with plumes.'),
(28532, 'p', '\r\nPlunder', ' â€”v. 1 rob or steal, esp. In wartime; loot. 2 exploit (another person''s or common property) for one''s own profit. â€”n. 1 activity of plundering. 2 property so acquired. [german plÃ¼ndern]'),
(28533, 'p', '\r\nPlunge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 (usu. Foll. By in, into) a thrust forcefully or abruptly. B dive. C (cause to) enter a condition or embark on a course impetuously (they plunged into marriage; the room was plunged into darkness). 2 immerse completely. 3 a move suddenly and dramatically downward. B (foll. By down, into, etc.) Move with a rush (plunged down the stairs). 4 colloq. Run up gambling debts. â€”n. Plunging action or movement; dive.  take the plunge colloq. Take a decisive step. [romanic: related to *plumb]'),
(28534, 'p', '\r\nPlunger', ' n. 1 part of a mechanism that works with a plunging or thrusting movement. 2 rubber cup on a handle for clearing blocked pipes by a plunging and sucking action.'),
(28535, 'p', '\r\nPluperfect', ' gram. â€”adj. (of a tense) denoting an action completed prior to some past point of time (e.g. He had gone by then). â€”n. Pluperfect tense. [latin plus quam perfectum more than perfect]'),
(28536, 'p', '\r\nPlural', ' â€”adj. 1 more than one in number. 2 gram. (of a word or form) denoting more than one. â€”n. Gram. 1 plural word or form. 2 plural number. [latin: related to *plus]'),
(28537, 'p', '\r\nPluralism', ' n. 1 form of society embracing many minority groups and cultural traditions. 2 the holding of more than one office at a time, esp. In the church.  pluralist n. Pluralistic adj.'),
(28538, 'p', '\r\nPlurality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 state of being plural. 2 = *pluralism 2. 3 large number. 4 us majority that is not absolute.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(28539, 'p', '\r\nPluralize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing)  make plural, express in the plural.'),
(28540, 'p', '\r\nPlus', ' â€”prep. 1  with the addition of (symbol +). 2 (of temperature) above zero (plus 2Â°). 3 colloq. With; having gained; newly possessing. â€”adj. 1 (after a number) at least (fifteen plus). 2 (after a grade etc.) Rather better than (beta plus). 3 math. Positive. 4 having a positive electrical charge. 5 (attrib.) Additional, extra. â€”n. 1 the symbol +.'),
(28541, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of plus as a conjunction, as in they arrived late, plus they wanted a meal, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(28542, 'p', '\r\nPlus-fours', ' n.pl. Men''s long wide knickerbockers. [the length was increased by 4 inches to create an overhang]'),
(28543, 'p', '\r\nPlush', ' â€”n. Cloth of silk or cotton etc., with a long soft nap. â€”adj. 1 made of plush. 2 colloq. = *plushy.  plushly adv. Plushness n. [latin: related to *pile3]'),
(28544, 'p', '\r\nPlushy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Stylish, luxurious.  plushiness n.'),
(28545, 'p', '\r\nPluto', ' n. Outermost known planet of the solar system. [greek plouton, god of the underworld]'),
(28546, 'p', '\r\nPlutocracy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a government by the wealthy. B state so governed. 2 wealthy Ã©lite.  plutocratic adj. [greek ploutos wealth]'),
(28547, 'p', '\r\nPlutocrat', ' n. 1 member of a plutocracy. 2 wealthy person.'),
(28548, 'p', '\r\nPlutonic', ' adj.  Formed as igneous rock by solidification below the surface of the earth. [latin pluto, god of the underworld]'),
(28549, 'p', '\r\nPlutonium', ' n. Radioactive metallic element. [pluto, name of a planet]'),
(28550, 'p', '\r\nPluvial', ' adj. 1 of rain; rainy. 2 geol. Caused by rain. [latin pluvia rain]'),
(28551, 'p', '\r\nPly1', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 thickness or layer of cloth or wood etc. 2 strand of yarn or rope etc. [french pli: related to *plait]'),
(28552, 'p', '\r\nPly2', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 use or wield (a tool, weapon, etc.). 2 work steadily at (ply one''s trade). 3 (foll. By with) a supply continuously (with food, drink, etc.). B approach repeatedly (with questions, etc.). 4 a (often foll. By between) (of a vehicle etc.) Travel regularly to and fro. B work (a route) thus. 5 (of a taxi-driver etc.) Attend regularly for custom (ply for hire). [from *apply]'),
(28553, 'p', '\r\nPlymouth', 'brethren  n.pl. Calvinistic religious body with no formal creed and no official order of ministers. [plymouth in devon]'),
(28554, 'p', '\r\nPlywood', ' n. Strong thin board made by gluing layers of wood with the direction of the grain alternating.'),
(28555, 'p', '\r\nPm', ' abbr. 1 prime minister. 2 post-mortem.'),
(28556, 'p', '\r\nPm', ' symb. Promethium.'),
(28557, 'p', '\r\nP.m.', ' Abbr. After noon. [latin post meridiem]'),
(28558, 'p', '\r\nPms', ' abbr. Premenstrual syndrome.'),
(28559, 'p', '\r\nPmt', ' abbr. Premenstrual tension.'),
(28560, 'p', '\r\nPneumatic', ' adj. 1 filled with air or wind (pneumatic tyre). 2 operated by compressed air (pneumatic drill). [greek pneuma wind]'),
(28561, 'p', '\r\nPneumoconiosis', ' n. Lung disease caused by the inhalation of dust or small particles. [greek pneumon lung, konis dust]'),
(28562, 'p', '\r\nPneumonia', ' n. Inflammation of one or both lungs. [greek pneumon lung]'),
(28563, 'p', '\r\nPo', ' abbr. 1 post office. 2 postal order. 3 petty officer. 4 pilot officer.'),
(28564, 'p', '\r\nPo', ' symb. Polonium.'),
(28565, 'p', '\r\nPo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Chamber-pot. [from *pot1]'),
(28566, 'p', '\r\nPoach1', ' v. 1 cook (an egg) without its shell in or over boiling water. 2 cook (fish etc.) By simmering in a small amount of liquid.  poacher n. [french pochier: related to *poke2]'),
(28567, 'p', '\r\nPoach2', ' v. 1 (also absol.) Catch (game or fish) illegally. 2 (often foll. By on) trespass or encroach on (another''s property, territory, etc.). 3 appropriate (another''s ideas, staff, etc.).  poacher n. [earlier poche: related to *poach1]'),
(28568, 'p', '\r\nPock', ' n. (also pock-mark) small pus-filled spot on the skin, esp. Caused by chickenpox or smallpox.  pock-marked adj. [old english]'),
(28569, 'p', '\r\nPocket', ' â€”n. 1 small bag sewn into or on clothing, for carrying small articles. 2 pouchlike compartment in a suitcase, car door, etc. 3 one''s financial resources (beyond my pocket). 4 isolated group or area (pockets of resistance). 5 cavity in the earth containing ore, esp. Gold. 6 pouch at the corner or on the side of a billiard- or snooker-table into which balls are driven. 7 = *air pocket. 8 (attrib.) A small enough or intended for carrying in a pocket.'),
(28570, 'p', '\r\nPocketbook', ' n. 1 notebook. 2 folding case for papers or money carried in a pocket.'),
(28571, 'p', '\r\nPocketful', ' n. (pl. -s) as much as a pocket will hold.'),
(28572, 'p', '\r\nPocket', 'knife  n. = *penknife.'),
(28573, 'p', '\r\nPocket', 'money  n. Money for minor expenses, esp. Given to children.'),
(28574, 'p', '\r\nPod', ' â€”n. Long seed-vessel, esp. Of a pea or bean. â€”v. (-dd-) 1  bear or form pods. 2  remove (peas etc.) From pods. [origin unknown]'),
(28575, 'p', '\r\nPodgy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 short and fat. 2 plump, fleshy.  podginess n. [podge short fat person]'),
(28576, 'p', '\r\nPodium', ' n. (pl. -s or podia) rostrum. [greek podion diminutive of pous pod- foot]'),
(28577, 'p', '\r\nPoem', ' n. 1 metrical composition, usu. Concerned with feeling or imaginative description. 2 elevated composition in verse or prose. 3 something with poetic qualities (a poem in stone). [greek poieo make]'),
(28578, 'p', '\r\nPoesy', ' n. Archaic poetry. [french, ultimately as *poem]'),
(28579, 'p', '\r\nPoet', ' n. (fem. Poetess) 1 writer of poems. 2 highly imaginative or expressive person. [greek poietes: related to *poem]'),
(28580, 'p', '\r\nPoetaster', ' n. Inferior poet. [from *poet, latin -aster derogatory suffix]'),
(28581, 'p', '\r\nPoetic', ' adj. (also poetical) of or like poetry or poets.  poetically adv.'),
(28582, 'p', '\r\nPoetic', 'justice  n. Very appropriate punishment or reward.'),
(28583, 'p', '\r\nPoetic', 'licence  n. Writer''s or artist''s transgression of established rules for effect.'),
(28584, 'p', '\r\nPoet', 'laureate  n. (pl. Poets laureate) poet appointed to write poems for state occasions.'),
(28585, 'p', '\r\nPoetry', ' n. 1 art or work of a poet. 2 poems collectively. 3 poetic or tenderly pleasing quality. [medieval latin: related to *poet]'),
(28586, 'p', '\r\nPo-faced', ' adj. 1 solemn-faced, humourless. 2 smug. [perhaps from *po, influenced by poker-faced]'),
(28587, 'p', '\r\nPogo', ' n. (pl. -s) (also pogo stick) stiltlike toy with a spring, used for jumping about on. [origin uncertain]'),
(28588, 'p', '\r\nPogrom', ' n. Organized massacre (orig. Of jews in russia). [russian]'),
(28589, 'p', '\r\nPoignant', ' adj. 1 painfully sharp to the emotions or senses; deeply moving. 2 arousing sympathy. 3 sharp or pungent in taste or smell. 4 pleasantly piquant.  poignance n. Poignancy n. Poignantly adv. [latin: related to *point]'),
(28590, 'p', '\r\nPoinsettia', ' n. Plant with large scarlet bracts surrounding small yellow flowers. [poinsett, name of a diplomat]'),
(28591, 'p', '\r\nPoint', ' â€”n. 1 sharp or tapered end of a tool, weapon, pencil, etc. 2 tip or extreme end. 3 that which in geometry has position but not magnitude. 4 particular place or position. 5 precise or critical moment (when it came to the point, he refused). 6 very small mark on a surface. 7 dot or other punctuation mark. 8 = *decimal point. 9 stage or degree in progress or increase (abrupt to the point of rudeness). 10 temperature at which a change of state occurs (freezing point). 11 single item or particular (explained it point by point). 12 unit of scoring in games or of measuring value etc. 13 significant or essential thing; what is intended or under discussion (the point of my question; get to the point). 14 sense, purpose; advantage, value (saw no point in staying). 15 characteristic (tact is not his strong point). 16 a each of 32 directions marked at equal distances round a compass. B corresponding direction towards the horizon.'),
(28592, 'p', '\r\nPoint-blank', ' â€”adj. 1 a (of a shot) aimed or fired at very close range. B (of a range) very close. 2 (of a remark etc.) Blunt, direct. â€”adv. 1 at very close range. 2 directly, bluntly.'),
(28593, 'p', '\r\nPoint-duty', ' n. Traffic control by a police officer, esp. At a road junction.'),
(28594, 'p', '\r\nPointed', ' adj. 1 sharpened or tapering to a point. 2 (of a remark etc.) Having point; cutting. 3 emphasized.  pointedly adv.'),
(28595, 'p', '\r\nPointer', ' n. 1 thing that points, e.g. The index hand of a gauge. 2 rod for pointing to features on a chart etc. 3 colloq. Hint. 4 dog of a breed that on scenting game stands rigid looking towards it. 5 (in pl.) Two stars in the great bear in line with the pole star.'),
(28596, 'p', '\r\nPointillism', ' n. Technique of impressionist painting using tiny dots of pure colour which become blended in the viewer''s eye.  pointillist n. & adj. [french pointiller mark with dots]'),
(28597, 'p', '\r\nPointing', ' n. 1 cement filling the joints of brickwork. 2 facing produced by this.'),
(28598, 'p', '\r\nPointless', ' adj. Lacking purpose or meaning; ineffective, fruitless.  pointlessly adv. Pointlessness n.'),
(28599, 'p', '\r\nPoint', 'of honour  n. Thing of great importance to one''s reputation or conscience.'),
(28600, 'p', '\r\nPoint', 'of no return  n. Point in a journey or enterprise at which it becomes essential or more practical to continue to the end.'),
(28601, 'p', '\r\nPoint', 'of order  n. Query in a debate etc. As to whether correct procedure is being followed.'),
(28602, 'p', '\r\nPoint-of-sale', ' adj. (usu. Attrib.) Of the place at which goods are retailed.'),
(28603, 'p', '\r\nPoint', 'of view  n. 1 position from which a thing is viewed. 2 way of considering a matter.'),
(28604, 'p', '\r\nPoint-to-point', ' n. Steeplechase for hunting horses.'),
(28605, 'p', '\r\nPoise', ' â€”n. 1 composure, self-possession. 2 equilibrium. 3 carriage (of the head etc.). â€”v. (-sing) 1 balance; hold suspended or supported. 2 be balanced or suspended. [latin pendo pens- weigh]'),
(28606, 'p', '\r\nPoised', ' adj. 1 a composed, self-assured. B carrying oneself gracefully or with dignity. 2 (often foll. By for, or to + infin.) Ready for action.'),
(28607, 'p', '\r\nPoison', ' â€”n. 1 substance that when introduced into or absorbed by a living organism causes death or injury, esp. One that kills by rapid action even in a small quantity. 2 colloq. Harmful influence. â€”v. 1 administer poison to. 2 kill, injure, or infect with poison. 3 treat (a weapon) with poison. 4 corrupt or pervert (a person or mind). 5 spoil or destroy (a person''s pleasure etc.).  poisoner n. Poisonous adj. [latin: related to *potion]'),
(28608, 'p', '\r\nPoison', 'ivy  n. N. American climbing plant secreting an irritant oil from its leaves.'),
(28609, 'p', '\r\nPoison-pen', 'letter  n. Malicious anonymous letter.'),
(28610, 'p', '\r\nPoke1', ' â€”v. (-king) 1 a thrust or push with the hand, a stick, etc. B (foll. By out, up, etc.) Be thrust forward, protrude. 2 (foll. By at etc.) Make thrusts. 3 thrust the end of a finger etc. Against. 4 (foll. By in) produce (a hole etc. In a thing) by poking. 5 stir (a fire) with a poker. 6 a (often foll. By about, around) potter. B (foll. By about, into) pry; search. 7  coarse slang have sexual intercourse with. â€”n. 1 act of poking. 2 thrust, nudge.  poke fun at ridicule. Poke one''s nose into colloq. Pry or intrude into. [german or dutch]'),
(28611, 'p', '\r\nPoke2', ' n. Dial. Bag, sack.  buy a pig in a poke see *pig. [french dial.]'),
(28612, 'p', '\r\nPoker1', ' n. Metal rod for stirring a fire.'),
(28613, 'p', '\r\nPoker2', ' n. Card-game in which bluff is used as players bet on the value of their hands. [origin unknown]'),
(28614, 'p', '\r\nPoker-face', ' n. Impassive countenance assumed by a poker-player.  poker-faced adj.'),
(28615, 'p', '\r\nPoky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (of a room etc.) Small and cramped.  pokiness n. [from *poke1]'),
(28616, 'p', '\r\nPolar', ' 1 adj. Of or near a pole of the earth or of the celestial sphere. 2 having magnetic or electric polarity. 3 directly opposite in character. [latin: related to *pole2]'),
(28617, 'p', '\r\nPolar', 'bear  n. Large white bear living in the arctic regions.'),
(28618, 'p', '\r\nPolar', 'circle  n. Each of the circles parallel to the equator at 23Â° 27Â´ from either pole.'),
(28619, 'p', '\r\nPolarity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 tendency of a magnet etc. To point with its extremities to the magnetic poles of the earth, or of a body to lie with its axis in a particular direction. 2 state of having two poles with contrary qualities. 3 state of having two opposite tendencies, opinions, etc. 4 electrical condition of a body (positive or negative). 5 attraction towards an object.'),
(28620, 'p', '\r\nPolarize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 restrict the vibrations of (light-waves etc.) To one direction. 2 give magnetic or electric polarity to. 3 divide into two opposing groups.  polarization n.'),
(28621, 'p', '\r\nPolaroid', ' n. Propr. 1 material in thin sheets polarizing light passing through it. 2 camera with internal processing that produces a print rapidly after each exposure. 3 (in pl.) Sunglasses with polaroid lenses.'),
(28622, 'p', '\r\nPolder', ' n. Piece of land reclaimed from the sea or a river, esp. In the netherlands. [dutch]'),
(28623, 'p', '\r\nPole', ' n. 1 native or national of poland. 2 person of polish descent. [german from polish]'),
(28624, 'p', '\r\nPole1', ' n. 1 long slender rounded piece of wood, metal, etc., esp. With the end placed in the ground as a support etc. 2 = *perch1 3.  up the pole slang 1 crazy. 2 in difficulty. [latin palus stake]'),
(28625, 'p', '\r\nPole2', ' n. 1 (in full north pole, south pole) a each of the two points in the celestial sphere about which the stars appear to revolve. B each of the ends of the axis of rotation of the earth (north pole; south pole). 2 each of the two opposite points on the surface of a magnet at which magnetic forces are strongest. 3 each of two terminals (positive and negative) of an electric cell or battery etc. 4 each of two opposed principles.  be poles apart differ greatly. [greek, = axis]'),
(28626, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the spelling is north pole and south pole when used as geographical designations.'),
(28627, 'p', '\r\nPoleax', ' (brit. -axe) â€”n. 1 hist. = *battleaxe 1. 2 butcher''s axe. â€”v. (-xing) 1 hit or kill with a poleaxe. 2 (esp. As poleaxed adj.) Colloq. Dumbfound, overwhelm. [low german or dutch: related to *poll, *axe]'),
(28628, 'p', '\r\nPoleaxe', ' (us -ax) â€”n. 1 hist. = *battleaxe 1. 2 butcher''s axe. â€”v. (-xing) 1 hit or kill with a poleaxe. 2 (esp. As poleaxed adj.) Colloq. Dumbfound, overwhelm. [low german or dutch: related to *poll, *axe]'),
(28629, 'p', '\r\nPolecat', ' n. 1 small brownish-black mammal of the weasel family. 2 us skunk. [origin unknown]'),
(28630, 'p', '\r\nPole-jump', ' var. Of *pole-vault.'),
(28631, 'p', '\r\nPolemic', ' â€”n. 1 forceful verbal or written controversy or argument. 2 (in pl.) Art or practice of controversial discussion. â€”adj. (also polemical) involving dispute; controversial.   polemicist n. [greek polemos war]'),
(28632, 'p', '\r\nPole', 'star  n. 1  star in the little bear, near the north pole in the sky. 2 thing serving as a guide.'),
(28633, 'p', '\r\nPole-vault', ' (also pole-jump) â€”n. Vault, or sport of vaulting, over a high bar with the aid of a pole held in the hands. â€”v. Perform this.  pole-vaulter n.'),
(28634, 'p', '\r\nPolice', ' â€”n. (as pl.) 1 (usu. Prec. By the) the civil force responsible for maintaining public order. 2 its members. 3 force with similar functions (military police). â€”v. (-cing) 1 keep (a place or people) in order by means of police or a similar body. 2 provide with police. 3 keep in order, administer, control (problem of policing the new law). [latin: related to *policy1]'),
(28635, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'constable  see *constable.'),
(28636, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'dog  n. Dog, esp. An alsatian, used in police work.'),
(28637, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'force  n. Body of police of a country, district, or town.'),
(28638, 'p', '\r\nPoliceman', ' n. (fem. Policewoman) member of a police force.'),
(28639, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'officer  n. Member of a police force.'),
(28640, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'state  n. Totalitarian state controlled by political police.'),
(28641, 'p', '\r\nPolice', 'station  n. Office of a local police force.'),
(28642, 'p', '\r\nPolicy1', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 course of action adopted by a government, business, individual, etc. 2 prudent conduct; sagacity. [latin politia *polity]'),
(28643, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' see note at polity.'),
(28644, 'p', '\r\nPolicy2', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 contract of insurance. 2 document containing this. [french police, ultimately from greek apodeixis proof]'),
(28645, 'p', '\r\nPolicyholder', ' n. Person or body holding an insurance policy.'),
(28646, 'p', '\r\nPolio', ' n. = *poliomyelitis. [abbreviation]'),
(28647, 'p', '\r\nPoliomyelitis', ' n.  Infectious viral disease of the grey matter of the central nervous system with temporary or permanent paralysis. [greek polios grey, muelos marrow]'),
(28648, 'p', '\r\nPolish', ' â€”adj. 1 of poland. 2 of the poles or their language. â€”n. Language of poland.'),
(28649, 'p', '\r\nPolish', ' â€”v. (often foll. By up) 1 make or become smooth or glossy by rubbing. 2 (esp. As polished adj.) Refine or improve; add the finishing touches to. â€”n. 1 substance used for polishing. 2 smoothness or glossiness produced by friction. 3 refinement, elegance.  polish off finish (esp. Food) quickly. [latin polio]'),
(28650, 'p', '\r\nPolite', ' adj. (politer, politest) 1 having good manners; courteous. 2 cultivated, refined.  politely adv. Politeness n.  [latin politus: related to *polish]'),
(28651, 'p', '\r\nPolitic', ' â€”adj. 1 (of an action) judicious, expedient. 2 (of a person) prudent, sagacious. 3 political (now only in body politic). â€”v. (-ck-) engage in politics. [greek: related to *polity]'),
(28652, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', ' adj. 1 a of or concerning the state or its government, or public affairs generally. B of or engaged in politics. 2 taking or belonging to a side in politics. 3 concerned with seeking power, status, etc. (political decision).  politically adv. [latin: related to *politic]'),
(28653, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', 'asylum  n. State protection given to a political refugee from another country.'),
(28654, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', 'economy  n. The study of the economic aspects of government.'),
(28655, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', 'geography  n. Geography dealing with boundaries and the possessions of states.'),
(28656, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', 'prisoner  n. Person imprisoned for political reasons.'),
(28657, 'p', '\r\nPolitical', 'science  n. The study of political activity and systems of government.'),
(28658, 'p', '\r\nPolitician', ' n. 1 person involved in politics, esp. Professionally as an mp. 2 esp. Us derog. Person who manoeuvres; schemer, time-server.'),
(28659, 'p', '\r\nPoliticize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 a give a political character to. B make politically aware. 2 engage in or talk politics.  politicization n.'),
(28660, 'p', '\r\nPolitico', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Politician or political enthusiast. [spanish]'),
(28661, 'p', '\r\nPolitics', ' n.pl. 1 (treated as sing. Or pl.) A art and science of government. B public life and affairs. 2 (usu. Treated as pl.) Political principles or practice (what are his politics?). 3 activities concerned with seeking power, status, etc.'),
(28662, 'p', '\r\nPolity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 form or process of civil government. 2 organized society; state. [greek polites citizen, from polis city]'),
(28663, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' this word is sometimes confused with policy.'),
(28664, 'p', '\r\nPolka', ' â€”n. 1 lively dance of bohemian origin. 2 music for this. â€”v. (-kas, -kaed or -ka''d, -kaing) dance the polka. [czech pulka]'),
(28665, 'p', '\r\nPolka', 'dot  n. Round dot as one of many forming a regular pattern on a textile fabric etc.'),
(28666, 'p', '\r\nPoll', ' â€”n. 1 a (often in pl.) Voting or the counting of votes at an election (go to the polls). B result of voting or number of votes recorded. 2 = *opinion poll. 3 human head. â€”v. 1 a take the vote or votes of. B receive (so many votes). C give (a vote). 2 record the opinion of (a person or group) in an opinion poll. 3 cut off the top of (a tree or plant), esp. Make a pollard of. 4 (esp. As polled adj.) Cut the horns off (cattle). [perhaps from low german or dutch]'),
(28667, 'p', '\r\nPollack', ' n. (also pollock) (pl. Same or -s) edible marine fish related to the cod. [origin unknown]'),
(28668, 'p', '\r\nPollard', ' â€”n. 1 animal that has lost or cast its horns; ox, sheep, or goat of a hornless breed. 2 tree whose branches have been cut back to encourage the dense growth of young branches. â€”v. Make (a tree) a pollard. [from *poll]'),
(28669, 'p', '\r\nPollen', ' n. Fine dustlike grains discharged from the male part of a flower, each containing the fertilizing element. [latin]'),
(28670, 'p', '\r\nPollen', 'count  n. Index of the amount of pollen in the air, published as a warning to hay fever sufferers.'),
(28671, 'p', '\r\nPollinate', ' v. (-ting) (also absol.) Convey pollen to or sprinkle (a stigma) with pollen.  pollination n. Pollinator n.'),
(28672, 'p', '\r\nPolling', ' n. Registering or casting of votes.'),
(28673, 'p', '\r\nPolling-booth', ' n. Compartment in which a voter stands to mark the ballot-paper.'),
(28674, 'p', '\r\nPolling-day', ' n. Election day.'),
(28675, 'p', '\r\nPolling-station', ' n. Building, often a school, used for voting at an election.'),
(28676, 'p', '\r\nPollock', ' var. Of *pollack.'),
(28677, 'p', '\r\nPollster', ' n. Person who organizes an opinion poll.'),
(28678, 'p', '\r\nPoll', 'tax  n. 1 informal = *community charge. 2 hist. Tax levied on every adult.'),
(28679, 'p', '\r\nPollute', ' v. (-ting) 1 contaminate (the environment). 2 make foul or impure.  pollutant adj. & n. Polluter n. Pollution n. [latin polluo -lut-]'),
(28680, 'p', '\r\nPolo', ' n. Game like hockey played on horseback with a long-handled mallet. [balti, = ball]'),
(28681, 'p', '\r\nPolonaise', ' n. 1 slow dance of polish origin. 2 music for this. [french: related to *pole]'),
(28682, 'p', '\r\nPolo-neck', ' n. 1 high round turned-over collar. 2 sweater with this.'),
(28683, 'p', '\r\nPolonium', ' n. Radioactive metallic element, occurring naturally in uranium ores. [medieval latin polonia poland]'),
(28684, 'p', '\r\nPoltergeist', ' n. Noisy mischievous ghost, esp. One causing physical damage. [german]'),
(28685, 'p', '\r\nPoltroon', ' n. Spiritless coward.  poltroonery n. [italian poltro sluggard]'),
(28686, 'p', '\r\nPoly', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Polytechnic. [abbreviation]'),
(28687, 'p', '\r\nPoly-', ' comb. Form 1 many (polygamy). 2 polymerized (polyunsaturated; polyester). [greek polus many]'),
(28688, 'p', '\r\nPolyandry', ' n. Polygamy in which a woman has more than one husband.  polyandrous adj. [greek aner andr- male]'),
(28689, 'p', '\r\nPolyanthus', ' n. (pl. -thuses) flowering plant cultivated from hybridized primulas. [greek anthos flower]'),
(28690, 'p', '\r\nPolychromatic', ' adj. 1 many-coloured. 2 (of radiation) containing more than one wavelength.  polychromatism n.'),
(28691, 'p', '\r\nPolychrome', ' â€”adj. In many colours. â€”n. Polychrome work of art. [greek: related to *poly-, *chrome]'),
(28692, 'p', '\r\nPolyester', ' n. Synthetic fibre or resin.'),
(28693, 'p', '\r\nPolyethene', ' n. = *polythene.'),
(28694, 'p', '\r\nPolyethylene', ' n. = *polythene.'),
(28695, 'p', '\r\nPolygamy', ' n. Practice of having more than one wife or (less usu.) Husband at once.  polygamist n. Polygamous adj. [greek gamos marriage]'),
(28696, 'p', '\r\nPolyglot', ' â€”adj. Knowing, using, or written in several languages. â€”n. Polyglot person. [greek glotta tongue]'),
(28697, 'p', '\r\nPolygon', ' n. Figure with many (usu. Five or more) sides and angles.  polygonal adj. [greek -gonos angled]'),
(28698, 'p', '\r\nPolygraph', ' n. Machine for reading physiological characteristics (e.g. Pulse-rate); lie-detector.'),
(28699, 'p', '\r\nPolygyny', ' n. Polygamy in which a man has more than one wife.  polygynous adj. [greek gune woman]'),
(28700, 'p', '\r\nPolyhedron', ' n. (pl. -dra) solid figure with many (usu. More than six) faces.  polyhedral adj. [greek hedra base]'),
(28701, 'p', '\r\nPolymath', ' n. Person of great or varied learning. [greek manthano math- learn]'),
(28702, 'p', '\r\nPolymer', ' n. Compound of one or more large molecules formed from repeated units of smaller molecules.  polymeric adj. Polymerize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). Polymerization n. [greek polumeros having many parts]'),
(28703, 'p', '\r\nPolymorphous', ' adj. (also polymorphic) passing through various forms in successive stages of development.'),
(28704, 'p', '\r\nPolynomial', ' â€”n. Expression of more than two algebraic terms. â€”adj. Of or being a polynomial. [from *poly-, *binomial]'),
(28705, 'p', '\r\nPolyp', ' n. 1 simple organism with a tube-shaped body. 2 small usu. Benign growth on a mucous membrane. [greek pous foot]'),
(28706, 'p', '\r\nPolyphony', ' n. (pl. -ies) mus. Contrapuntal music.  polyphonic adj. [greek phone sound]'),
(28707, 'p', '\r\nPolypropene', ' n. = *polypropylene.'),
(28708, 'p', '\r\nPolypropylene', ' n. Any polymer of propylene, including thermoplastic materials used for films, fibres, or moulding materials.'),
(28709, 'p', '\r\nPolysaccharide', ' n. Any of a group of complex carbohydrates, e.g. Starch. [see *saccharin]'),
(28710, 'p', '\r\nPolystyrene', ' n. A polymer of styrene, a kind of hard plastic, often foamed for packaging. [styrene from greek sturax a resin]'),
(28711, 'p', '\r\nPolysyllabic', ' adj. 1 having many syllables. 2 using words of many syllables. [medieval latin from greek]'),
(28712, 'p', '\r\nPolysyllable', ' n. Polysyllabic word.'),
(28713, 'p', '\r\nPolytechnic', ' â€”n. College offering courses in many (esp. Vocational) subjects up to degree level. â€”adj. Giving instruction in various vocational or technical subjects. [greek tekhne art]'),
(28714, 'p', '\r\nPolytheism', ' n. Belief in or worship of more than one god.  polytheist n. Polytheistic adj. [greek theos god]'),
(28715, 'p', '\r\nPolythene', ' n. A tough light plastic. [from *polyethylene]'),
(28716, 'p', '\r\nPolyunsaturated', ' adj. (of a fat or oil) having a chemical structure capable of further reaction and not contributing to the accumulation of cholesterol in the blood.'),
