(28755, 'p', '\r\nPoop', ' n. Stern of a ship; the deck which is furthest aft and highest. [latin puppis]'),
(28756, 'p', '\r\nPoor', ' adj. 1 without enough money to live comfortably. 2 (foll. By in) deficient in (a possession or quality). 3 a scanty, inadequate. B less good than is usual or expected (poor visibility; is a poor driver). C paltry; inferior (came a poor third). 4 deserving pity or sympathy; unfortunate (you poor thing). 5 spiritless, despicable.  poor man''s inferior or cheaper substitute for. [latin pauper]'),
(28757, 'p', '\r\nPoorhouse', ' n. Hist. = *workhouse.'),
(28758, 'p', '\r\nPoor', 'law  n. Hist. Law concerning public support of the poor.'),
(28759, 'p', '\r\nPoorly', ' â€”adv. In a poor manner, badly. â€”predic. Adj. Unwell.'),
(28760, 'p', '\r\nPoor', 'relation  n. Inferior or subordinate member of a family etc.'),
(28761, 'p', '\r\nPop1', ' â€”n. 1 sudden sharp explosive sound as of a cork when drawn. 2 colloq. Effervescent drink. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (cause to) make a pop. 2 (foll. By in, out, up, etc.) Go, move, come, or put unexpectedly or abruptly (pop out to the shop). 3 slang pawn. â€”adv. With the sound of a pop (go pop).  pop off colloq. Die. Pop the question colloq. Propose marriage. [imitative]'),
