(28844, 'p', '\r\nPose', ' â€”v. (-sing) 1 assume a certain attitude of the body, esp. When being photographed or painted. 2 (foll. By as) pretend to be (another person etc.) (posing as a celebrity). 3 behave affectedly to impress others. 4 put forward or present (a question etc.). 5 place (an artist''s model etc.) In a certain attitude. â€”n. 1 attitude of body or mind. 2 affectation, pretence. [latin pauso *pause, confused with latin pono place]'),
(28845, 'p', '\r\nPoser', ' n. 1 poseur. 2 colloq. Puzzling question or problem.'),
(28846, 'p', '\r\nPoseur', ' n. Person who behaves affectedly. [french poser *pose]'),
(28847, 'p', '\r\nPosh', ' colloq. â€”adj. Smart; upper-class. â€”adv. In an upper-class way (talk posh).  posh up smarten up.  poshly adv. Poshness n. [perhaps from slang posh a dandy, money]'),
(28848, 'p', '\r\nPosit', ' v. (-t-) assume as a fact, postulate. [latin: related to *position]'),
(28849, 'p', '\r\nPosition', ' â€”n. 1 place occupied by a person or thing. 2 way in which a thing or its parts are placed or arranged. 3 proper place (in position). 4 advantage (jockeying for position). 5 attitude; view on a question. 6 situation in relation to others (puts one in an awkward position). 7 rank, status; social standing. 8 paid employment. 9 place where troops etc. Are posted for strategical purposes. â€”v. Place in position.  in a position to able to.  positional adj. [latin pono posit- place]'),
(28850, 'p', '\r\nPositive', ' â€”adj. 1 explicit; definite, unquestionable (positive proof). 2 (of a person) convinced, confident, or overconfident in an opinion. 3 a absolute; not relative. B gram. (of an adjective or adverb) expressing a simple quality without comparison. 4 colloq. Downright (it was a positive miracle). 5 constructive (positive thinking). 6 marked by the presence and not absence of qualities (positive reaction). 7 esp. Philos. Dealing only with matters of fact; practical. 8 tending in a direction naturally or arbitrarily taken as that of increase or progress. 9 greater than zero. 10 electr. Of, containing, or producing the kind of electrical charge produced by rubbing glass with silk; lacking electrons. 11 (of a photographic image) showing lights and shades or colours unreversed. â€”n. Positive adjective, photograph, quantity, etc.  positively adv. Positiveness n. [latin: related to *position]'),
(28851, 'p', '\r\nPositive', 'discrimination  n. Practice of making distinctions in favour of groups considered to be underprivileged.'),
(28852, 'p', '\r\nPositive', 'vetting  n. Inquiry into the background etc. Of a candidate for a post involving national security.'),
(28853, 'p', '\r\nPositivism', ' n. Philosophical system recognizing only facts and observable phenomena.  positivist n. & adj.'),
(28854, 'p', '\r\nPositron', ' n. Physics elementary particle with the same mass as but opposite (positive) charge to an electron. [positive electron]'),
(28855, 'p', '\r\nPosse', ' n. 1 strong force or company. 2 body of law-enforcers. [latin, = be able]'),
(28856, 'p', '\r\nPossess', ' v. 1 hold as property; own. 2 have (a faculty, quality, etc.). 3 occupy or dominate the mind of (possessed by the devil; possessed by fear).  be possessed of own, have. What possessed you? An expression of incredulity.  possessor n. [latin possideo possess-]'),
(28857, 'p', '\r\nPossession', ' n. 1 possessing or being possessed. 2 thing possessed. 3 holding or occupancy. 4 law power or control similar to ownership but which may exist separately from it (prosecuted for possession of drugs). 5 (in pl.) Property, wealth, subject territory, etc. 6 football etc. Control of the ball by a player.'),
(28858, 'p', '\r\nPossessive', ' â€”adj. 1 wanting to retain what one has, reluctant to share. 2 jealous and domineering. 3 gram. Indicating possession. â€”n. (in full possessive case) gram. Case of nouns and pronouns expressing possession.  possessiveness n.'),
(28859, 'p', '\r\nPossibility', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 state or fact of being possible. 2 thing that may exist or happen. 3 (usu. In pl.) Capability of being used; potential (have possibilities). [latin posse be able]'),
(28860, 'p', '\r\nPossible', ' â€”adj. 1 capable of existing, happening, being done, etc. 2 potential (a possible way of doing it). â€”n. 1 possible candidate, member of a team, etc. 2 highest possible score, esp. In shooting.'),
(28861, 'p', '\r\nPossibly', ' adv. 1 perhaps. 2 in accordance with possibility (cannot possibly go).'),
(28862, 'p', '\r\nPossum', ' n. 1 colloq. = *opossum 1. 2 austral. & nz colloq. Marsupial resembling an american opossum.  play possum colloq. Pretend to be unconscious; feign ignorance. [abbreviation]'),
(28863, 'p', '\r\nPost1', ' â€”n. 1 long stout piece of timber or metal set upright in the ground etc. To support something, mark a position or boundary, etc. 2 pole etc. Marking the start or finish of a race. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) attach (a notice etc.) In a prominent place. 2 announce or advertise by poster or list. [latin postis]'),
(28864, 'p', '\r\nPost2', ' â€”n. 1 official conveyance of parcels, letters, etc. (send it by post). 2 single collection or delivery of these; the letters etc. Dispatched (has the post arrived?). 3 place where letters etc.'),
(28865, 'p', '\r\nPost2', ' â€”n. 1 official conveyance of parcels, letters, etc. (send it by post). 2 single collection or delivery of these; the letters etc. Dispatched (has the post arrived?). 3 place where letters etc. Are collected (take it to the post). â€”v. 1 put (a letter etc.) In the post. 2 (esp. As posted adj.) (often foll. By up) supply with information (keep me posted). 3  a enter (an item) in a ledger. B (often foll. By up) complete (a ledger) in this way. [latin: related to *position]'),
(28866, 'p', '\r\nPost3', ' â€”n. 1 place where a soldier is stationed or which he or she patrols. 2 place of duty. 3 a position taken up by a body of soldiers. B force occupying this. C fort. 4 job, paid employment. 5 = *trading post. â€”v. 1 place (soldiers, an employee, etc.). 2 appoint to a post or command. [french: related to *post2]'),
(28867, 'p', '\r\nPost-', ' prefix after, behind. [latin post (adv. And prep.)]'),
(28868, 'p', '\r\nPostage', ' n. Charge for sending a letter etc. By post.'),
