(28968, 'p', '\r\nPow', ' abbr. Prisoner of war.'),
(28969, 'p', '\r\nPow', ' int. Expressing the sound of a blow or explosion. [imitative]'),
(28970, 'p', '\r\nPowder', ' â€”n. 1 mass of fine dry particles. 2 medicine or cosmetic in this form. 3 = *gunpowder. â€”v. 1 apply powder to. 2 (esp. As powdered adj.) Reduce to a fine powder (powdered milk).  powdery adj. [latin pulvis -ver- dust]'),
(28971, 'p', '\r\nPowder', 'blue  adj. & n. (as adj. Often hyphenated) pale blue.'),
(28972, 'p', '\r\nPowder-puff', ' n. Soft pad for applying powder to the skin, esp. The face.'),
(28973, 'p', '\r\nPowder-room', ' n. Euphem. Women''s lavatory in a public building.'),
(28974, 'p', '\r\nPower', ' â€”n. 1 ability to do or act. 2 particular faculty of body or mind. 3 a influence, authority. B ascendancy, control (the party in power). 4 authorization; delegated authority. 5 influential person, body, or thing. 6 state having international influence. 7 vigour, energy. 8 active property or function (heating power). 9 colloq. Large number or amount (did me a power of good). 10 capacity for exerting mechanical force or doing work (horsepower). 11 (often attrib.) Mechanical or electrical energy as distinct from manual labour. 12 a electricity supply. B particular source or form of energy (hydroelectric power). 13 physics rate of energy output. 14 product obtained when a number is multiplied by itself a certain number of times (2 to the power of 3 = 8). 15 magnifying capacity of a lens. 16 deity. â€”v. 1 supply with mechanical or electrical energy. 2 (foll. By up, down) increase or decrease the power supplied to (a device); switch on or off.  the powers that be those in authority. [latin posse be able]'),
(28975, 'p', '\r\nPowerboat', ' n. Powerful motor boat.'),
(28976, 'p', '\r\nPower', 'cut  n. Temporary withdrawal or failure of an electric power supply.'),
(28977, 'p', '\r\nPowerful', ' adj. Having much power or influence.  powerfully adv. Powerfulness n.'),
(28978, 'p', '\r\nPowerhouse', ' n. 1 = *power station. 2 person or thing of great energy.'),
(28979, 'p', '\r\nPowerless', ' adj. 1 without power. 2 wholly unable.  powerlessness n.'),
(28980, 'p', '\r\nPower', 'line  n. Conductor supplying electrical power, esp. One supported by pylons or poles.'),
(28981, 'p', '\r\nPower', 'of attorney  n.  Authority to act for another person in legal and financial matters.'),
