(29173, 'p', '\r\nPrescient', ' adj. Having foreknowledge or foresight.  prescience n. [latin praescio know before]'),
(29174, 'p', '\r\nPrescribe', ' v. (-bing) 1 a advise the use of (a medicine etc.). B recommend, esp. As a benefit. 2 lay down or impose authoritatively. [latin praescribo]'),
(29175, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' prescribe is sometimes confused with proscribe.'),
(29176, 'p', '\r\nPrescript', ' n. Ordinance, law, command. [latin: related to *prescribe]'),
(29177, 'p', '\r\nPrescription', ' n. 1 act of prescribing. 2 a doctor''s (usu. Written) instruction for the supply and use of a medicine. B medicine prescribed.'),
(29178, 'p', '\r\nPrescriptive', ' adj. 1 prescribing, laying down rules. 2 arising from custom.'),
(29179, 'p', '\r\nPresence', ' n. 1 being present. 2 place where a person is (admitted to their presence). 3 person''s appearance or bearing, esp. When imposing. 4 person or spirit that is present (the royal presence; aware of a presence in the room). [latin: related to *present1]'),
(29180, 'p', '\r\nPresence', 'of mind  n. Calmness and quick-wittedness in sudden difficulty etc.'),
(29181, 'p', '\r\nPresent1', ' â€”adj. 1 (usu. Predic.) Being in the place in question. 2 a now existing, occurring, or being such. B now being considered etc. (in the present case). 3 gram. Expressing an action etc. Now going on or habitually performed (present participle). â€”n. (prec. By the) 1 the time now passing (no time like the present). 2 gram. Present tense.  at present now. By these presents law by this document. For the present just now; for the time being. [latin praesens -ent-]'),
(29182, 'p', '\r\nPresent2', ' v. 1 introduce, offer, or exhibit for attention or consideration. 2 a (with a thing as object, foll. By to) offer or give as a gift (to a person). B (with a person as object, foll. By with) make available to; cause to have (that presents us with a problem). 3 a (of a company, producer, etc.) Put (a piece of entertainment) before the public. B (of a performer, compÃ¨re, etc.) Introduce. 4 introduce (a person) formally (may i present my fiancÃ©?).  5 a (of a circumstance) reveal (some quality etc.) (this presents some difficulty). B exhibit (an appearance etc.). 6 (of an idea etc.) Offer or suggest itself. 7 deliver (a cheque, bill, etc.) For acceptance or payment. 8 a (usu. Foll. By at) aim (a weapon). B hold out (a weapon) in position for aiming.  present arms hold a rifle etc. Vertically in front of the body as a salute.  presenter n. (in sense 3b). [latin praesento: related to *present1]'),
(29183, 'p', '\r\nPresent3', ' n. Thing given, gift. [french: related to *present1]'),
(29184, 'p', '\r\nPresentable', ' adj. Of good appearance; fit to be presented.  presentability n. Presentably adv.'),
(29185, 'p', '\r\nPresentation', ' n. 1 a presenting or being presented. B thing presented. 2 manner or quality of presenting. 3 demonstration or display of materials, information, etc.; lecture.'),
(29186, 'p', '\r\nPresent-day', ' attrib. Adj. Of this time; modern.'),
(29187, 'p', '\r\nPresentiment', ' n. Vague expectation; foreboding (esp. Of misfortune).'),
(29188, 'p', '\r\nPresently', ' adv. 1 soon; after a short time. 2 esp. Us & scot. At the present time; now.'),
(29189, 'p', '\r\nPreservative', ' â€”n. Substance for preserving perishable foodstuffs, wood, etc. â€”adj. Tending to preserve.'),
(29190, 'p', '\r\nPreserve', ' â€”v. (-ving) 1 keep safe or free from decay etc. 2 maintain (a thing) in its existing state. 3 retain (a quality or condition). 4 treat (food) to prevent decomposition or fermentation. 5 keep (game etc.) Undisturbed for private use. â€”n. (in sing. Or pl.) 1 preserved fruit; jam. 2 place where game etc. Is preserved. 3 sphere of activity regarded as a person''s own.  preservation n. [latin servo keep]'),
(29191, 'p', '\r\nPre-set', ' v. (-tt-; past and past part. -set) set or fix (a device) in advance of its operation.'),
(29192, 'p', '\r\nPreshrunk', ' adj. (of fabric etc.) Treated so that it shrinks during manufacture and not in use.'),
(29193, 'p', '\r\nPreside', ' v. (-ding) 1 (often foll. By at, over) be chairperson or president of a meeting etc. 2 exercise control or authority. [latin sedeo sit]'),
(29194, 'p', '\r\nPresidency', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 office of president. 2 period of this.'),
(29195, 'p', '\r\nPresident', ' n. 1 head of a republican state. 2 head of a society or council etc. 3 head of certain colleges. 4 us head of a university, company, etc. 5 person in charge of a meeting.  presidential adj.'),
(29196, 'p', '\r\nPresidium', ' n. (also praesidium) standing committee in a communist country. [latin: related to *preside]'),
(29197, 'p', '\r\nPress1', ' â€”v. 1 apply steady force to (a thing in contact). 2 a compress or squeeze a thing to flatten, shape, or smooth it. B squeeze (a fruit etc.) To extract its juice. 3 (foll. By out of, from, etc.) Squeeze (juice etc.). 4 embrace or caress by squeezing (pressed my hand). 5 (foll. By on, against, etc.) Exert pressure. 6 be urgent; demand immediate action. 7 (foll. By for) make an insistent demand. 8 (foll. By up, round, etc.) Crowd. 9 (foll. By on, forward, etc.) Hasten insistently. 10 (often in passive) (of an enemy etc.) Bear heavily on. 11 (often foll. By for, or to + infin.) Urge or entreat (pressed me to stay; pressed me for an answer). 12 (foll. By on, upon) a urge (an opinion, claim, or course of action). B force (an offer, a gift, etc.). 13 insist on (did not press the point). 14 manufacture (a gramophone record, car part, etc.) By using pressure to shape and extract from a sheet of material. â€”n. 1 act of pressing (give it a press). 2 device for compressing, flattening, shaping, extracting juice, etc. 3 = *printing-press. 4 (prec. By the) a art or practice of printing. B newspapers etc. Generally or collectively. 5 notice or publicity in newspapers etc. (got a good press). 6 (press) printing or publishing company. 7 a crowding. B crowd (of people etc.). 8 the pressure of affairs. 9 esp. Ir. & scot. Large usu. Shelved cupboard.  be pressed for have barely enough (time etc.). Go (or send) to press go or send to be printed. [latin premo press-]'),
(29198, 'p', '\r\nPress2', ' v. 1 hist. Force to serve in the army or navy. 2 bring into use as a makeshift (was pressed into service). [obsolete prest from french, = loan]'),
(29199, 'p', '\r\nPress', 'agent  n. Person employed to obtain advertising and press publicity.'),
(29200, 'p', '\r\nPress', 'conference  n. Interview given to a number of journalists.'),
(29201, 'p', '\r\nPress', 'gallery  n. Gallery for reporters, esp. In a legislative assembly.'),
(29202, 'p', '\r\nPress-gang', ' â€”n. 1 hist. Body of men employed to press men into army or navy service. 2 any group using coercive methods. â€”v. Force into service.'),
(29203, 'p', '\r\nPressie', ' n. (also prezzie) colloq. Present, gift. [abbreviation]'),
(29204, 'p', '\r\nPressing', ' â€”adj. 1 urgent. 2 urging strongly (pressing invitation). â€”n. 1 thing made by pressing, e.g. A gramophone record. 2 series of these made at one time. 3 act of pressing (all at one pressing).  pressingly adv.'),
(29205, 'p', '\r\nPress', 'release  n. Statement issued to newspapers.'),
(29206, 'p', '\r\nPress-stud', ' n. Small fastening device engaged by pressing its two halves together.'),
(29207, 'p', '\r\nPress-up', ' n. Exercise in which the prone body is raised from the ground by placing the hands on the floor and straightening the arms.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(29208, 'p', '\r\nPressure', ' â€”n. 1 a exertion of continuous force on or against a body by another in contact with it. B force exerted. C amount of this (expressed by the force on a unit area) (atmospheric pressure). 2 urgency (work under pressure). 3 affliction or difficulty (under financial pressure). 4 constraining influence (put pressure on us). â€”v. (-ring) (often foll. By into) apply (esp. Moral) pressure to; coerce; persuade. [latin: related to *press1]'),
(29209, 'p', '\r\nPressure-cooker', ' n. Airtight pan for cooking quickly under steam pressure.  pressure-cook v.'),
(29210, 'p', '\r\nPressure', 'group  n. Group formed to influence public policy.'),
(29211, 'p', '\r\nPressure', 'point  n. Point where an artery can be pressed against a bone to inhibit bleeding.'),
(29212, 'p', '\r\nPressurize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (esp. As pressurized adj.) Maintain normal atmospheric pressure in (an aircraft cabin etc.) At a high altitude. 2 raise to a high pressure. 3 pressure (a person).  pressurization n.'),
(29213, 'p', '\r\nPressurized-water', 'reactor  n. Nuclear reactor with water at high pressure as the coolant.'),
(29214, 'p', '\r\nPrestel', ' n. Propr. Computerized visual information system operated by british telecom. [from *press1, *telecommunication]'),
(29215, 'p', '\r\nPrestidigitator', ' n. Formal conjuror.  prestidigitation n. [french: related to *presto, *digit]'),
(29216, 'p', '\r\nPrestige', ' n. 1 respect or reputation derived from achievements, power, associations, etc. 2 (attrib.) Having or conferring prestige.  prestigious adj. [latin praestigiae juggler''s tricks]'),
(29217, 'p', '\r\nPresto', ' mus. â€”adv. & adj. In quick tempo. â€”n. (pl. -s) presto passage or movement. [latin praestus quick]'),
(29218, 'p', '\r\nPrestressed', ' adj. (of concrete) strengthened by stretched wires within it.'),
(29219, 'p', '\r\nPresumably', ' adv. As may reasonably be presumed.'),
(29220, 'p', '\r\nPresume', ' v. (-ming) 1 (often foll. By that) suppose to be true; take for granted. 2 (often foll. By to + infin.) A take the liberty, be impudent enough (presumed to question their authority). B dare, venture (may i presume to ask?). 3 be presumptuous. 4 (foll. By on, upon) take advantage of or make unscrupulous use of (a person''s good nature etc.). [latin praesumo]'),
(29221, 'p', '\r\nPresumption', ' n. 1 arrogance, presumptuous behaviour. 2 a presuming a thing to be true. B thing that is or may be presumed to be true. 3 ground for presuming. [latin: related to *presume]'),
(29222, 'p', '\r\nPresumptive', ' adj. Giving grounds for presumption (presumptive evidence).'),
(29223, 'p', '\r\nPresumptuous', ' adj. Unduly or overbearingly confident.  presumptuously adv. Presumptuousness n.'),
(29224, 'p', '\r\nPresuppose', ' v. (-sing) 1 assume beforehand. 2 imply.  presupposition n.'),
(29225, 'p', '\r\nPre-tax', ' adj. (of income etc.) Before deduction of taxes.'),
(29226, 'p', '\r\nPretence', ' n. (us pretense) 1 pretending, make-believe. 2 a pretext, excuse. B false show of intentions or motives. 3 (foll. By to) claim, esp. A false one (to merit etc.). 4 display; ostentation. [anglo-latin: related to *pretend]'),
(29227, 'p', '\r\nPretend', ' â€”v. 1 claim or assert falsely so as to deceive (pretend knowledge; pretended to be rich). 2 imagine to oneself in play (pretended it was night). 3 (as pretended adj.) Falsely claim to be such (a pretended friend). 4 (foll. By to) a lay claim to (a right or title etc.). B profess to have (a quality etc.). â€”adj. Colloq. Pretended; in pretence (pretend money). [latin praetendo: related to *tend1]'),
(29228, 'p', '\r\nPretender', ' n. Person who claims a throne, title, etc.'),
(29229, 'p', '\r\nPretense', ' n. (brit. Pretence) 1 pretending, make-believe. 2 a pretext, excuse. B false show of intentions or motives. 3 (foll. By to) claim, esp. A false one (to merit etc.). 4 display; ostentation. [anglo-latin: related to *pretend]'),
(29230, 'p', '\r\nPretension', ' n. 1 (often foll. By to) a assertion of a claim. B justifiable claim. 2 pretentiousness. [medieval latin: related to *pretend]'),
(29231, 'p', '\r\nPretentious', ' adj. 1 making an excessive claim to merit or importance. 2 ostentatious.  pretentiously adv. Pretentiousness n.'),
(29232, 'p', '\r\nPreterit', ' (brit. Preterite) gram. â€”adj. Expressing a past action or state. â€”n. Preterite tense or form. [latin praeteritum past]'),
(29233, 'p', '\r\nPreterite', ' (us preterit) gram. â€”adj. Expressing a past action or state. â€”n. Preterite tense or form. [latin praeteritum past]'),
(29234, 'p', '\r\nPreternatural', ' adj. Extraordinary, exceptional; supernatural. [latin praeter beyond]'),
(29235, 'p', '\r\nPretext', ' n. Ostensible reason; excuse offered. [latin praetextus: related to *text]'),
(29236, 'p', '\r\nPrettify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make pretty, esp. In an affected way.'),
(29237, 'p', '\r\nPretty', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 attractive in a delicate way (pretty girl; pretty dress). 2 fine or good of its kind. 3 iron. Considerable, fine (a pretty penny). â€”adv. Colloq. Fairly, moderately. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) (often foll. By up) make pretty.  pretty much (or nearly or well) colloq. Almost; very nearly.  prettily adv. Prettiness n. [old english]'),
(29238, 'p', '\r\nPretty-pretty', ' adj. Colloq. Too pretty.'),
(29239, 'p', '\r\nPretzel', ' n. Crisp knot-shaped salted biscuit. [german]'),
(29240, 'p', '\r\nPrevail', ' v. 1 (often foll. By against, over) be victorious or gain mastery. 2 be the more usual or predominant. 3 exist or occur in general use or experience. 4 (foll. By on, upon) persuade. [latin praevaleo: related to *avail]'),
(29241, 'p', '\r\nPrevalent', ' adj. 1 generally existing or occurring. 2 predominant.  prevalence n. [related to *prevail]'),
(29242, 'p', '\r\nPrevaricate', ' v. (-ting) 1 speak or act evasively or misleadingly. 2 quibble, equivocate.  prevarication n. Prevaricator n. [latin, = walk crookedly]'),
(29243, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' prevaricate is often confused with procrastinate, which means â€˜to defer or put off actionâ€™.'),
(29244, 'p', '\r\nPrevent', ' v. (often foll. By from + verbal noun) stop from happening or doing something; hinder; make impossible (the weather prevented me from going).  preventable adj. (also preventible). Prevention n. [latin praevenio -vent- hinder]'),
(29245, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of prevent without â€˜fromâ€™ as in prevented me going is informal. An acceptable alternative is prevented my going.'),
(29246, 'p', '\r\nPreventative', ' adj. & n. = *preventive.'),
(29247, 'p', '\r\nPreventive', ' â€”adj. Serving to prevent, esp. Disease. â€”n. Preventive agent, measure, drug, etc.'),
(29248, 'p', '\r\nPreview', ' â€”n. Showing of a film, play, exhibition, etc., before it is seen by the general public. â€”v. See or show in advance.'),
(29249, 'p', '\r\nPrevious', ' â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By to) coming before in time or order. 2 colloq. Hasty, premature. â€”adv. (foll. By to) before.  previously adv. [latin praevius from via way]'),
(29250, 'p', '\r\nPre-war', ' adj. Existing or occurring before a war.'),
(29251, 'p', '\r\nPrey', ' â€”n. 1 animal that is hunted or killed by another for food. 2 (often foll. By to) person or thing that is influenced by or vulnerable to (something undesirable) (prey to morbid fears). â€”v. (foll. By on, upon) 1 seek or take as prey. 2 (of a disease, emotion, etc.) Exert a harmful influence (it preyed on his mind). [latin praeda]'),
(29252, 'p', '\r\nPrezzie', ' var. Of *pressie.'),
(29253, 'p', '\r\nPrice', ' â€”n. 1 amount of money for which a thing is bought or sold. 2 what is or must be given, done, sacrificed, etc., to obtain or achieve something (peace at any price). 3 odds in betting. â€”v. (-cing)  1 fix or find the price of (a thing for sale). 2 estimate the value of.  at a price at a high cost. Price on a person''s head reward for a person''s capture or death. What price â€¦? (often foll. By verbal noun) colloq. 1 what is the chance of â€¦? (what price your finishing the course?). 2 iron. The much boasted â€¦ proves disappointing (what price your friendship now?). [latin pretium]'),
(29254, 'p', '\r\nPrice-fixing', ' n. Maintaining of prices at a certain level by agreement between competing sellers.'),
(29255, 'p', '\r\nPriceless', ' adj. 1 invaluable. 2 colloq. Very amusing or absurd.'),
(29256, 'p', '\r\nPrice', 'tag  n. 1 label on an item showing its price. 2 cost of an undertaking.'),
(29257, 'p', '\r\nPrice', 'war  n. Period of fierce competition among traders cutting prices.'),
(29258, 'p', '\r\nPricey', ' adj. (-cier, -ciest) colloq. Expensive.'),
(29259, 'p', '\r\nPrick', ' â€”v. 1 pierce slightly; make a small hole in. 2 (foll. By off, out) mark with small holes or dots. 3 trouble mentally (my conscience pricked me). 4 tingle. 5 (foll. By out) plant (seedlings etc.) In small holes pricked in the soil. â€”n. 1 act of pricking. 2 small hole or mark made by pricking. 3 pain caused as by pricking. 4 mental pain. 5 coarse slang a penis. B derog. Contemptible man.  prick up one''s ears 1 (of a dog etc.) Make the ears erect when alert. 2 (of a person) become suddenly attentive. [old english]'),
(29260, 'p', '\r\nPrickle', ' â€”n. 1 small thorn. 2 hard-pointed spine of a hedgehog etc. 3 prickling sensation. â€”v. (-ling) affect or be affected with a sensation of multiple pricking. [old english]'),
(29261, 'p', '\r\nPrickly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having prickles. 2 (of a person) ready to take offence. 3 tingling.  prickliness n.'),
(29262, 'p', '\r\nPrickly', 'heat  n. Itchy inflammation of the skin, causing a tingling sensation and common in hot countries.'),
(29263, 'p', '\r\nPrickly', 'pear  n. 1 cactus with pear-shaped prickly fruit. 2 its fruit.'),
(29264, 'p', '\r\nPride', ' â€”n. 1 a elation or satisfaction at one''s achievements, qualities, possessions, etc. B object of this feeling; the flower or best. 2 high or overbearing opinion of one''s worth or importance. 3 (in full proper pride) proper sense of what befits one''s position; self-respect. 4 group (of certain animals, esp. Lions). 5 best condition, prime. â€”v.refl. (-ding) (foll. By on, upon) be proud of.  take pride (or a pride) in 1 be proud of. 2 maintain in good condition or appearance. [old english: related to *proud]'),
(29265, 'p', '\r\nPride', 'of place  n. Most important or prominent position.'),
(29266, 'p', '\r\nPrie-dieu', ' n. (pl. Prie-dieux pronunc. Same) kneeling-desk for prayer. [french, = pray god]'),
(29267, 'p', '\r\nPriest', ' n. 1 ordained minister of the roman catholic or orthodox church, or of the anglican church (above a deacon and below a bishop). 2 (fem. Priestess) official minister of a non-christian religion.  priesthood n. Priestly adj.  [latin *presbyter]'),
(29268, 'p', '\r\nPrig', ' n. Self-righteous or moralistic person.  priggish adj. Priggishness n. [origin unknown]'),
(29269, 'p', '\r\nPrim', ' adj. (primmer, primmest) stiffly formal and precise; prudish.  primly adv. Primness n.  [french: related to *prime1]'),
(29270, 'p', '\r\nPrima', 'ballerina  n. Chief female dancer in a ballet. [italian]'),
(29271, 'p', '\r\nPrimacy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 pre-eminence. 2 office of a primate. [latin: related to *primate]'),
(29272, 'p', '\r\nPrima', 'donna  n. (pl. Prima donnas) 1 chief female singer in an opera. 2 temperamentally self-important person.  prima donna-ish adj. [italian]'),
(29273, 'p', '\r\nPrima', 'facie  â€”adv. At first sight. â€”adj. (of evidence) based on the first impression. [latin]'),
(29274, 'p', '\r\nPrimal', ' adj. 1 primitive, primeval. 2 chief, fundamental. [latin: related to *prime1]'),
(29275, 'p', '\r\nPrimary', ' â€”adj. 1 a of the first importance; chief. B fundamental, basic. 2 earliest, original; first in a series. 3 of the first rank in a series; not derived. 4 designating any of the colours red, green, and blue, or (for pigments) red, blue, and yellow, of which all other colours are mixtures. 5 (of education) for children below the age of 11. 6 (primary) geol. Of the lowest series of strata. 7 biol. Of the first stage of development. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 thing that is primary. 2 (in full primary election) (in the us) preliminary election to appoint party conference delegates or to select candidates for a principal (esp. Presidential) election. 3 = *primary feather.  primarily adv. [latin: related to *prime1]'),
(29276, 'p', '\r\nPrimary', 'feather  n. Large flight-feather of a bird''s wing.'),
(29277, 'p', '\r\nPrimary', 'school  n. School for children below the age of 11.'),
(29278, 'p', '\r\nPrimate', ' n. 1 member of the highest order of mammals, including apes, monkeys, and man. 2 (also) archbishop. [latin primas -at- chief]'),
(29279, 'p', '\r\nPrime1', ' â€”adj. 1 chief, most important. 2 first-rate, excellent. 3 primary, fundamental. 4 math. A (of a number etc.) Divisible only by itself and unity (e.g. 2, 3, 5, 7, 11). B (of numbers) having no common factor but unity. â€”n. 1 state of the highest perfection (prime of life). 2 (prec. By the; foll. By of) the best part.  [latin primus first]'),
(29280, 'p', '\r\nPrime2', ' v. (-ming) 1 prepare (a thing) for use or action. 2 prepare (a gun) for firing or (an explosive) for detonation. 3 pour (liquid) into a pump to enable it to work. 4 prepare (wood etc.) For painting by applying a substance that prevents paint from being absorbed. 5 equip (a person) with information etc. 6 ply (a person) with food or drink in preparation for something. [origin unknown]'),
(29281, 'p', '\r\nPrime', 'minister  n. Head of an elected government; principal minister.'),
(29282, 'p', '\r\nPrimer1', ' n. Substance used to prime wood etc.'),
(29283, 'p', '\r\nPrimer2', ' n. 1 elementary textbook for teaching children to read. 2 introductory book. [latin: related to *prime1]'),
(29284, 'p', '\r\nPrime', 'time  n. (in broadcasting) time when audiences are largest.'),
(29285, 'p', '\r\nPrimeval', ' adj. 1 of the first age of the world. 2 ancient, primitive.  primevally adv. [latin: related to *prime1, aevum age]'),
(29286, 'p', '\r\nPrimitive', ' â€”adj. 1 at an early stage of civilization (primitive man). 2 undeveloped, crude, simple (primitive methods). â€”n. 1 untutored painter with a direct naÃ¯ve style. 2 picture by such a painter.  primitively adv. Primitiveness n.  [latin: related to *prime1]'),
(29287, 'p', '\r\nPrimogeniture', ' n. 1 fact of being the first-born child. 2 (in full right of primogeniture) right of succession belonging to the first-born. [medieval latin: related to *prime1, latin genitura birth]'),
(29288, 'p', '\r\nPrimordial', ' adj. Existing at or from the beginning, primeval. [latin: related to *prime1, ordior begin]'),
(29289, 'p', '\r\nPrimp', ' v. 1 make (the hair, clothes, etc.) Tidy. 2 refl. Make (oneself) smart. [var. Of *prim]'),
(29290, 'p', '\r\nPrimrose', ' n. 1 a wild plant bearing pale yellow spring flowers. B its flower. 2 pale yellow colour. [french and medieval latin, = first rose]'),
(29291, 'p', '\r\nPrimrose', 'path  n. Pursuit of pleasure.'),
(29292, 'p', '\r\nPrimula', ' n. Cultivated plant bearing primrose-like flowers in a wide variety of colours. [latin diminutive: related to *prime1]'),
(29293, 'p', '\r\nPrimus', ' n. Propr. Portable cooking stove burning vaporized oil. [latin, = first]'),
(29294, 'p', '\r\nPrince', ' n. (as a title usu. Prince) 1 male member of a royal family other than the reigning king. 2 ruler of a small state. 3 noble man in some countries. 4 (often foll. By of) chief or greatest (the prince of novelists). [latin princeps -cip-]'),
(29295, 'p', '\r\nPrince', 'consort  n. (title conferred on) the husband of a reigning queen who is himself a prince.'),
(29296, 'p', '\r\nPrinceling', ' n. Young or petty prince.'),
(29297, 'p', '\r\nPrincely', ' adj. (-ier, iest) 1 of or worthy of a prince. 2 sumptuous, generous, splendid.'),
(29298, 'p', '\r\nPrince', 'of wales  n. (title conferred on) the eldest son and heir apparent of the british monarch.'),
(29299, 'p', '\r\nPrince', 'regent  n. Prince who acts as regent, esp. The future george iv.'),
(29300, 'p', '\r\nPrincess', ' n. (as a title usu. Princess) 1 wife of a prince. 2 female member of a royal family other than a queen. [french: related to *prince]'),
(29301, 'p', '\r\nPrincess', 'royal  n. (title conferred on) the british monarch''s eldest daughter.'),
(29302, 'p', '\r\nPrincipal', ' â€”adj. 1 (usu. Attrib.) First in rank or importance; chief. 2 main, leading. â€”n. 1 chief person. 2 head of some schools, colleges, and universities. 3 leading performer in a concert, play, etc. 4 capital sum as distinct from interest or income. 5 person for whom another is agent etc. 6 civil servant of the grade below secretary. 7 person directly responsible for a crime.  principally adv. [latin: related to *prince]'),
(29303, 'p', '\r\nPrincipal', 'boy  n. Leading male role in a pantomime, usu. Played by a woman.'),
(29304, 'p', '\r\nPrincipality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 state ruled by or government of a prince. 2 (the principality) wales.'),
(29305, 'p', '\r\nPrincipal', 'parts  n.pl. Gram. Parts of a verb from which all other parts can be deduced.'),
(29306, 'p', '\r\nPrinciple', ' n. 1 fundamental truth or law as the basis of reasoning or action. 2 a personal code of conduct (person of high principle). B (in pl.) Personal rules of conduct (has no principles). 3 general law in physics etc. 4 law of nature forming the basis for the construction or working of a machine etc. 5 fundamental source; primary element.  in principle in theory. On principle on the basis of a moral attitude. [latin principium source]'),
(29307, 'p', '\r\nPrincipled', ' adj. Based on or having (esp. Praiseworthy) principles of behaviour.'),
(29308, 'p', '\r\nPrink', ' v. 1 a (usu. Refl.; often foll. By up) smarten (oneself) up. B dress oneself up. 2 (of a bird) preen. [origin unknown]'),
(29309, 'p', '\r\nPrint', ' â€”v. 1 produce or cause (a book, picture, etc.) To be produced by applying inked types, blocks, or plates, to paper, etc. 2 express or publish in print. 3 a (often foll. By on, in) impress or stamp (a mark on a surface). B (often foll. By with) impress or stamp (a surface with a seal, die, etc.). 4 (often absol.) Write (letters) without joining them up. 5 (often foll. By off, out) produce (a photograph) from a negative. 6 (usu. Foll. By out) (of a computer etc.) Produce output in printed form.'),
(29310, 'p', '\r\nPrinted', 'circuit  n. Electric circuit with thin strips of conductor printed on a flat insulating sheet.'),
(29311, 'p', '\r\nPrinter', ' n. 1 person who prints books etc. 2 owner of a printing business. 3 device that prints, esp. From a computer.'),
(29312, 'p', '\r\nPrinting', ' n. 1 production of printed books etc. 2 copies of a book printed at one time. 3 printed letters or writing imitating them.'),
(29313, 'p', '\r\nPrinting-press', ' n. Machine for printing from types or plates etc.'),
(29314, 'p', '\r\nPrintout', ' n. Computer output in printed form.'),
(29315, 'p', '\r\nPrior', ' â€”adj. 1 earlier. 2 (often foll. By to) coming before in time, order, or importance. â€”adv. (foll. By to) before (left prior to his arrival). â€”n. (fem. Prioress) 1 superior of a religious house or order. 2 (in an abbey) deputy of an abbot. [latin, = earlier]'),
(29316, 'p', '\r\nPriority', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 thing that is regarded as more important than others. 2 high(est) place among various things to be done (gave priority to). 3 right to do something before other people. 4 right to proceed ahead of other traffic. 5 (state of) being more important.  prioritize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). [medieval latin: related to *prior]'),
(29317, 'p', '\r\nPriory', ' n. (pl. -ies) monastery governed by a prior or nunnery governed by a prioress. [anglo-french and medieval latin: related to *prior]'),
(29318, 'p', '\r\nPrise', ' v. (also prize) (-sing or -zing) force open or out by leverage. [french: related to *prize2]'),
(29319, 'p', '\r\nPrism', ' n. 1 solid figure whose two ends are equal parallel rectilinear figures, and whose sides are parallelograms. 2 transparent body in this form, usu. Triangular with refracting surfaces at an acute angle with each other, which separates white light into a spectrum of colours. [greek prisma -mat- thing sawn]'),
(29320, 'p', '\r\nPrismatic', ' adj. 1 of, like, or using a prism. 2 (of colours) distributed (as if) by a transparent prism. [greek: related to *prism]'),
(29321, 'p', '\r\nPrison', ' n. 1 place of captivity, esp. A building to which persons are committed while awaiting trial or for punishment. 2 custody, confinement. [latin prehendo seize]'),
(29322, 'p', '\r\nPrisoner', ' n. 1 person kept in prison. 2 (in full prisoner at the bar) person in custody on a criminal charge and on trial. 3 person or thing confined by illness, another''s grasp, etc. 4 (in full prisoner of war) person captured in war.  take prisoner seize and hold as a prisoner. [anglo-french: related to *prison]'),
(29323, 'p', '\r\nPrisoner', 'of conscience  see *conscience.'),
(29324, 'p', '\r\nPrissy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) prim, prudish.  prissily adv. Prissiness n. [perhaps from *prim, *sissy]'),
(29325, 'p', '\r\nPristine', ' adj. 1 in its original condition; unspoilt. 2 spotless; fresh as if new. 3 ancient, primitive. [latin pristinus former]'),
(29326, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of pristine in sense 2 is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(29327, 'p', '\r\nPrivacy', ' n. 1 a being private and undisturbed. B right to this. 2 freedom from intrusion or public attention.'),
(29328, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', ' â€”adj. 1 belonging to an individual, one''s own, personal (private property). 2 confidential, not to be disclosed to others (private talks). 3 kept or removed from public knowledge or observation. 4 not open to the public. 5 (of a place) secluded. 6 (of a person) not holding public office or an official position. 7 (of education or medical treatment) conducted outside the state system, at the individual''s expense. â€”n. 1 private soldier. 2 (in pl.) Colloq. Genitals.  in private privately.  privately adv. [latin privo deprive]'),
(29329, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'bill  n. Parliamentary bill affecting an individual or corporation only.'),
(29330, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'company  n. Company with restricted membership and no public share issue.'),
(29331, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'detective  n. Detective engaged privately, outside an official police force.'),
(29332, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'enterprise  n. Businesses not under state control.'),
(29333, 'p', '\r\nPrivateer', ' n. 1 privately owned and officered warship holding a government commission. 2 its commander.'),
(29334, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'eye  n. Colloq. Private detective.'),
(29335, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'hotel  n. Hotel not obliged to take all comers.'),
(29336, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'means  n.pl. Income from investments etc., apart from earned income.'),
(29337, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'member  n. Mp not holding government office.'),
(29338, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'member''s bill  n. Bill introduced by a private member, not part of government legislation.'),
(29339, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'parts  n.pl. Euphem. Genitals.'),
(29340, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'sector  n. The part of the economy free of direct state control.'),
(29341, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'soldier  n. Ordinary soldier other than the officers.'),
(29342, 'p', '\r\nPrivate', 'view  n. Viewing of an exhibition (esp. Of paintings) before it opens to the public.'),
(29343, 'p', '\r\nPrivation', ' n. Lack of the comforts or necessities of life. [latin: related to *private]'),
(29344, 'p', '\r\nPrivative', ' adj. 1 consisting in or marked by loss or absence. 2 gram. Expressing privation. [latin: related to *private]'),
(29345, 'p', '\r\nPrivatize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) transfer (a business etc.) From state to private ownership.  privatization n.'),
(29346, 'p', '\r\nPrivet', ' n. Bushy evergreen shrub used for hedges. [origin unknown]'),
(29347, 'p', '\r\nPrivilege', ' â€”n. 1 right, advantage, or immunity, belonging to a person, class, or office. 2 special benefit or honour (a privilege to meet you). â€”v. (-ging) invest with a privilege.  privileged adj. [latin: related to *privy, lex leg- law]'),
(29348, 'p', '\r\nPrivy', ' â€”adj. 1 (foll. By to) sharing in the secret of (a person''s plans etc.). 2 archaic hidden, secret. â€”n. (pl. -ies) lavatory, esp. An outside one. [french privÃ© private place]'),
(29349, 'p', '\r\nPrivy', 'council  n. Body of advisers appointed by the sovereign (now chiefly honorary).  privy councillor n. (also privy counsellor).'),
(29350, 'p', '\r\nPrivy', 'purse  n. Allowance from the public revenue for the monarch''s private expenses.'),
(29351, 'p', '\r\nPrivy', 'seal  n. Seal formerly affixed to minor state documents.'),
(29352, 'p', '\r\nPrize1', ' â€”n. 1 something that can be won in a competition, lottery, etc. 2 reward given as a symbol of victory or superiority. 3 something striven for or worth striving for. 4 (attrib.) A to which a prize is awarded (prize poem). B excellent of its kind. â€”v. (-zing) value highly (a much prized possession). [french: related to *praise]'),
(29353, 'p', '\r\nPrize2', ' n. Ship or property captured in naval warfare. [french prise from latin prehendo seize]'),
(29354, 'p', '\r\nPrize3', ' var. Of *prise.'),
(29355, 'p', '\r\nPrizefight', ' n. Boxing-match fought for a prize of money.  prizefighter n.'),
(29356, 'p', '\r\nPrize-giving', ' n. Awarding of prizes, esp. Formally at a school etc.'),
(29357, 'p', '\r\nPrizewinner', ' n. Winner of a prize.  prizewinning attrib. Adj.'),
(29358, 'p', '\r\nPro', ' abbr. 1 public record office. 2 public relations officer.'),
(29359, 'p', '\r\nPro1', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Professional. [abbreviation]'),
(29360, 'p', '\r\nPro2', ' â€”adj. (of an argument or reason) for; in favour. â€”n. (pl. -s) reason in favour. â€”prep. In favour of. [latin, = for, on behalf of]'),
(29361, 'p', '\r\nPro-1', ' prefix 1 favouring or supporting (pro-government). 2 acting as a substitute or deputy for (proconsul). 3 forwards (produce). 4 forwards and downwards (prostrate). 5 onwards (progress). 6 in front of (protect).'),
(29362, 'p', '\r\nPro2', ' â€”adj. (of an argument or reason) for; in favour. â€”n. (pl. -s) reason in favour. â€”prep. In favour of. [latin, = for, on behalf of]'),
(29363, 'p', '\r\nPro-1', ' prefix 1 favouring or supporting (pro-government). 2 acting as a substitute or deputy for (proconsul). 3 forwards (produce). 4 forwards and downwards (prostrate). 5 onwards (progress). 6 in front of (protect). [latin pro in front (of)]'),
(29364, 'p', '\r\nPro-2', ' prefix before in time, place, order, etc. [greek pro before]'),
(29365, 'p', '\r\nProactive', ' adj. (of a person, policy, etc.) Taking the initiative. [from *pro-2, after *reactive]'),
(29366, 'p', '\r\nProbability', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being probable. 2 likelihood of something happening. 3 probable or most probable event. 4 math. Extent to which an event is likely to occur, measured by the ratio of the favourable cases to the total number of possible cases.  in all probability most probably.'),
(29367, 'p', '\r\nProbable', ' â€”adj. (often foll. By that) that may be expected to happen or prove true; likely (the probable explanation; it is probable that they forgot). â€”n. Probable candidate, member of a team, etc.  probably adv. [latin: related to *prove]'),
(29368, 'p', '\r\nProbate', ' n. 1 official proving of a will. 2 verified copy of a will with a certificate as handed to executors. [latin probo *prove]'),
(29369, 'p', '\r\nProbation', ' n. 1 law system of supervising and monitoring the behaviour of (esp. Young) offenders, as an alternative to prison. 2 period of testing the character or abilities of esp. A new employee.  on probation undergoing probation.  probationary adj. [latin: related to *prove]'),
(29370, 'p', '\r\nProbationer', ' n. Person on probation.'),
(29371, 'p', '\r\nProbation', 'officer  n. Official supervising offenders on probation.'),
(29372, 'p', '\r\nProbative', ' adj. Formal affording proof. [latin: related to *prove]'),
(29373, 'p', '\r\nProbe', ' â€”n. 1 penetrating investigation. 2 small device, esp. An electrode, for measuring, testing, etc. 3 blunt-ended surgical instrument for exploring a wound etc. 4 (in full space probe) unmanned exploratory spacecraft transmitting information about its environment. â€”v. (-bing) 1 examine or enquire into closely. 2 explore with a probe. [latin proba: related to *prove]'),
(29374, 'p', '\r\nProbity', ' n. Uprightness, honesty. [latin probus good]'),
(29375, 'p', '\r\nProblem', ' n. 1 doubtful or difficult matter requiring a solution. 2 something hard to understand or accomplish. 3 (attrib.) Causing problems (problem child). 4 puzzle or question for solution; exercise. [greek problema -mat-]'),
(29376, 'p', '\r\nProblematic', ' adj. (also problematical) attended by difficulty; doubtful or questionable.  problematically adv. [greek: related to *problem]'),
(29377, 'p', '\r\nProboscis', ' n. (pl. -sces) 1 long flexible trunk or snout of some mammals, e.g. An elephant or tapir. 2 elongated mouth parts of some insects. [greek bosko feed]'),
(29378, 'p', '\r\nProboscis', 'monkey  n. Monkey of borneo, the male of which has a large pendulous nose.'),
(29379, 'p', '\r\nProcedure', ' n. 1 way of acting or advancing, esp. In business or legal action. 2 way of performing a task. 3 series of actions conducted in a certain order or manner.  procedural adj. [french: related to *proceed]'),
(29380, 'p', '\r\nProceed', ' v. 1 (often foll. By to) go forward or on further; make one''s way. 2 (often foll. By with, or to + infin.) Continue with an activity; go on to do something (proceeded with their work; proceeded to beg me). 3 (of an action) be carried on or continued (the case will now proceed). 4 adopt a course of action (how shall we proceed?). 5 go on to say. 6 (foll. By against) start a lawsuit against (a person). 7 (often foll. By from) originate (trouble proceeded from illness). [latin cedo cess- go]'),
(29381, 'p', '\r\nProceeding', ' n. 1 action or piece of conduct (high-handed proceeding). 2 (in pl.) (in full legal proceedings) lawsuit. 3 (in pl.) Published report of discussions or a conference.'),
(29382, 'p', '\r\nProceeds', ' n.pl. Profits from sale etc. [pl. Of obsolete proceed (n.) From *proceed]'),
(29383, 'p', '\r\nProcess1', ' â€”n. 1 course of action or proceeding, esp. A series of stages in manufacture etc. 2 progress or course (in process of construction). 3 natural or involuntary course or change (process of growing old). 4 action at law; summons or writ. 5 natural projection of a bone, stem, etc. â€”v. 1 deal with by a particular process. 2 (as processed adj.) Treat (food, esp. To prevent decay) (processed cheese). [latin: related to *proceed]'),
(29384, 'p', '\r\nProcess2', ' v. Walk in procession. [back-formation from *procession]'),
(29385, 'p', '\r\nProcession', ' n. 1 people or vehicles etc. Advancing in orderly succession, esp. At a ceremony, demonstration, or festivity. 2 movement of such a group (go in procession). [latin: related to *proceed]'),
(29386, 'p', '\r\nProcessional', ' â€”adj. 1 of processions. 2 used, carried, or sung in processions. â€”n. Eccl. Processional hymn or hymn book.'),
(29387, 'p', '\r\nProcessor', ' n. Machine that processes things, esp.: 1 = *central processor. 2 = *food processor.'),
(29388, 'p', '\r\nProclaim', ' v. 1 (often foll. By that) announce or declare publicly or officially. 2 declare to be (king, a traitor, etc.). proclamation n. [latin: related to *claim]'),
(29389, 'p', '\r\nProclivity', ' n. (pl. -ies) tendency, inclination. [latin clivus slope]'),
(29390, 'p', '\r\nProcrastinate', ' v. (-ting) defer action.  procrastination n. Procrastinator n. [latin cras tomorrow]'),
(29391, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' procrastinate is often confused with prevaricate which means â€˜to be evasive, quibbleâ€™.'),
(29392, 'p', '\r\nProcreate', ' v. (-ting) (often absol.) Produce (offspring) naturally.  procreation n. Procreative adj. [latin: related to *create]'),
(29393, 'p', '\r\nProcrustean', ' adj. Seeking to enforce uniformity ruthlessly or violently. [greek prokroustes, name of a robber who fitted his victims to a bed by stretching them or cutting bits off them]'),
(29394, 'p', '\r\nProctor', ' n.  Disciplinary officer (usu. One of two) at certain universities.  proctorial adj. Proctorship n. [from *procurator]'),
(29395, 'p', '\r\nProcuration', ' n. 1 formal act of procuring. 2 function or authorized action of an attorney. [latin: related to *procure]'),
(29396, 'p', '\r\nProcurator', ' n. Agent or proxy, esp. With power of attorney. [latin procurator agent]'),
(29397, 'p', '\r\nProcurator', 'fiscal  n. (in scotland) local coroner and public prosecutor.'),
(29398, 'p', '\r\nProcure', ' v. (-ring) 1 obtain, esp. By care or effort; acquire (managed to procure a copy). 2 bring about (procured their dismissal). 3 (also absol.) Obtain (women) for prostitution.  procurement n. [latin curo look after]'),
(29399, 'p', '\r\nProcurer', ' n. (fem. Procuress) person who obtains women for prostitution. [latin *procurator]'),
(29400, 'p', '\r\nProd', ' â€”v. (-dd-) 1 poke with a finger, stick, etc. 2 stimulate to action. 3 (foll. By at) make a prodding motion. â€”n. 1 poke, thrust. 2 stimulus to action. [origin unknown]'),
(29401, 'p', '\r\nProdigal', ' â€”adj. 1 recklessly wasteful. 2 (foll. By of) lavish. â€”n. 1 prodigal person. 2 (in full prodigal son) repentant wastrel, returned wanderer, etc. (luke 15:11â€“32).  prodigality n. [latin prodigus lavish]'),
(29402, 'p', '\r\nProdigious', ' adj. 1 marvellous or amazing. 2 enormous. 3 abnormal. [latin: related to *prodigy]'),
(29403, 'p', '\r\nProdigy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 exceptionally gifted or able person, esp. A precocious child. 2 marvellous, esp. Extraordinary, thing. 3 (foll. By of) wonderful example (of a quality). [latin prodigium portent]'),
(29404, 'p', '\r\nProduce', ' â€”v. (-cing) 1 manufacture or prepare (goods etc.). 2 bring forward for consideration, inspection, or use (will produce evidence). 3 bear, yield, or bring into existence (offspring, fruit, a harvest, etc.). 4 cause or bring about (a reaction, sensation, etc.). 5 geom. Extend or continue (a line). 6 supervise the production of (a play, film, broadcast, record, etc.). â€”n. 1 a what is produced, esp. Agricultural products collectively (dairy produce). B amount of this. 2 (often foll. By of) result (of labour, efforts, etc.).  producible adj. [latin duco duct- lead]'),
(29405, 'p', '\r\nProducer', ' n. 1 person who produces goods etc. 2 person who supervises the production of a play, film, broadcast, etc.'),
(29406, 'p', '\r\nProduct', ' n. 1 thing or substance produced, esp. By manufacture. 2 result. 3 quantity obtained by multiplying. [latin: related to *produce]'),
(29407, 'p', '\r\nProduction', ' n. 1 producing or being produced, esp. In large quantities (go into production). 2 total yield. 3 thing produced, esp. A film, play, book, etc. [latin: related to *produce]'),
(29408, 'p', '\r\nProduction', 'line  n. Systematized sequence of operations involved in producing a commodity.'),
(29409, 'p', '\r\nProductive', ' adj. 1 of or engaged in the production of goods. 2 producing much (productive writer). 3 producing commodities of exchangeable value (productive labour). 4 (foll. By of) producing or giving rise to (productive of great annoyance).  productively adv. Productiveness n. [latin: related to *produce]'),
(29410, 'p', '\r\nProductivity', ' n. 1 being productive, capacity to produce. 2 amount produced by an industry, workforce, etc.'),
(29411, 'p', '\r\nProem', ' n. Preface etc. To a book or speech. [latin from greek]'),
(29412, 'p', '\r\nProf.', ' Abbr. Professor.'),
(29413, 'p', '\r\nProfane', ' â€”adj. 1 a irreverent, blasphemous. B (of language) obscene. 2 not sacred or biblical; secular. â€”v. (-ning) 1 treat (esp. A sacred thing) irreverently; disregard. 2 violate or pollute.  profanation n. [latin fanum temple]'),
(29414, 'p', '\r\nProfanity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 profane act or language; blasphemy. 2 swear-word.'),
(29415, 'p', '\r\nProfess', ' v. 1 claim openly to have (a quality or feeling). 2 (often foll. By to + infin.) Pretend, declare (profess ignorance). 3  affirm one''s faith in or allegiance to. [latin profiteor -fess- declare]'),
(29416, 'p', '\r\nProfessed', ' adj. 1 self-acknowledged (professed christian). 2 alleged, ostensible.  professedly adv.'),
(29417, 'p', '\r\nProfession', ' n. 1 vocation or calling, esp. Learned or scientific (medical profession). 2 people in a profession. 3 declaration or avowal.  the oldest profession colloq. Prostitution.'),
(29418, 'p', '\r\nProfessional', ' â€”adj. 1 of, belonging to, or connected with a profession. 2 a skilful, competent. B worthy of a professional (professional conduct). 3 engaged in a specified activity as one''s main paid occupation (professional boxer). 4 derog. Engaged in a specified activity, esp. Fanatically (professional agitator). â€”n. Professional person.  professionally adv.'),
(29419, 'p', '\r\nProfessionalism', ' n. Qualities associated with a profession, esp. Competence, skill, etc.'),
(29420, 'p', '\r\nProfessor', ' n. 1 a (often as a title) highest-ranking academic teaching in a university department; holder of a university chair. B us university teacher. 2 person who professes a religion etc.  professorial adj. Professorship n.'),
(29421, 'p', '\r\nProffer', ' v. Offer. [french: related to *pro-1, *offer]'),
(29422, 'p', '\r\nProficient', ' adj. (often foll. By in, at) adept, expert.  proficiency n. Proficiently adv. [latin proficio -fect- advance]'),
(29423, 'p', '\r\nProfile', ' â€”n. 1 a outline, esp. Of a human face, as seen from one side. B representation of this. 2 short biographical or character sketch. â€”v. (-ling) represent or describe by a profile.  keep a low profile remain inconspicuous. [italian profilare draw in outline]'),
(29424, 'p', '\r\nProfit', ' â€”n. 1 advantage or benefit. 2 financial gain; excess of returns over outlay. â€”v. (-t-) 1 (also absol.) Be beneficial to. 2 obtain advantage or benefit (profited by the experience).  at a profit with financial gain. [latin profectus: related to *proficient]'),
(29425, 'p', '\r\nProfitable', ' adj. 1 yielding profit. 2 beneficial.  profitability n. Profitably adv.'),
(29426, 'p', '\r\nProfit', 'and loss account  n. Account showing net profit or loss at any time.'),
(29427, 'p', '\r\nProfiteer', ' â€”v. Make or seek excessive profits, esp. Illegally or on the black market. â€”n. Person who profiteers.'),
(29428, 'p', '\r\nProfiterole', ' n. Small hollow choux bun, usu. Filled with cream and covered with chocolate. [french diminutive: related to *profit]'),
(29429, 'p', '\r\nProfit', 'margin  n. Profit after the deduction of costs.'),
(29430, 'p', '\r\nProfit-sharing', ' n. Sharing of profits, esp. Between employer and employees.'),
(29431, 'p', '\r\nProfligate', ' â€”adj. 1 recklessly extravagant. 2 licentious, dissolute. â€”n. Profligate person.  profligacy n. Profligately adv. [latin profligo ruin]'),
(29432, 'p', '\r\nPro', 'forma  â€”adv. & adj. As or being a matter of form. â€”n. (in full pro-forma invoice) invoice sent in advance of goods supplied. [latin]'),
(29433, 'p', '\r\nProfound', ' adj. (-er, -est) 1 having or demanding great knowledge, study, or insight (profound treatise; profound doctrines). 2 intense, unqualified, thorough (a profound sleep; profound indifference). 3 deep (profound crevasses).  profoundly adv. Profoundness n. Profundity n. (pl. -ies). [latin profundus]'),
(29434, 'p', '\r\nProfuse', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By in, of) lavish; extravagant. 2 exuberantly plentiful; copious (profuse variety).  profusely adv. Profusion n. [latin fundo fus- pour]'),
(29435, 'p', '\r\nProgenitor', ' n. 1 ancestor. 2 predecessor. 3 original. [latin progigno beget]'),
(29436, 'p', '\r\nProgeny', ' n. 1 offspring; descendant(s). 2 outcome, issue. [latin: related to *progenitor]'),
(29437, 'p', '\r\nProgesterone', ' n. A steroid hormone which stimulates the preparation of the uterus for pregnancy and maintains the uterus in the event of fertilization. [german: related to *pro-1, *gestation]'),
(29438, 'p', '\r\nProgestogen', ' n. 1 steroid hormone (e.g. Progesterone) maintaining pregnancy and preventing further ovulation. 2 similar synthetic hormone.'),
(29439, 'p', '\r\nPrognosis', ' n. (pl. -noses) forecast, esp. Of the course of a disease. [greek gignosko know]'),
(29440, 'p', '\r\nPrognostic', ' â€”n. 1 (often foll. By of) advance indication, esp. Of the course of a disease. 2 prediction, forecast. â€”adj. (often foll. By of) foretelling, predictive. [latin: related to *prognosis]'),
(29441, 'p', '\r\nPrognosticate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By that) foretell, foresee, prophesy. 2 (of a thing) betoken, indicate.  prognostication n. Prognosticator n. [medieval latin: related to *prognostic]'),
(29442, 'p', '\r\nProgram', ' (brit. Programme) â€”n. 1 list of events, performers, etc. At a public function etc. 2 radio or television broadcast. 3 plan of events (programme is dinner and an early night). 4 course or series of studies, lectures, etc. 5 (usu. Program) series of coded instructions for a computer etc. â€”v. (-mm-; us -m-) 1 make a programme of. 2 (usu. Program) express (a problem) or instruct (a computer) by means of a program.  programmable adj. Programmatic adj. Programmer n. (in sense 5 of n.). [greek grapho write]'),
(29443, 'p', '\r\nProgramme', ' (us program) â€”n. 1 list of events, performers, etc. At a public function etc. 2 radio or television broadcast. 3 plan of events (programme is dinner and an early night). 4 course or series of studies, lectures, etc. 5 (usu. Program) series of coded instructions for a computer etc. â€”v. (-mm-; us -m-) 1 make a programme of. 2 (usu. Program) express (a problem) or instruct (a computer) by means of a program.  programmable adj. Programmatic adj. Programmer n. (in sense 5 of n.). [greek grapho write]'),
(29444, 'p', '\r\nProgress', ' â€”n. 1 forward or onward movement towards a destination. 2 advance or development; improvement (made little progress). 3 hist. State tour, esp. By royalty. â€”v. 1 move or be moved forward or onward; continue. 2 advance, develop, or improve (science progresses).  in progress developing; going on. [latin progredior -gress- go forward]'),
(29445, 'p', '\r\nProgression', ' n. 1 progressing. 2 succession; series. [latin: related to *progress]'),
(29446, 'p', '\r\nProgressive', ' â€”adj. 1 moving forward. 2 proceeding step by step; cumulative (progressive drug use). 3 a favouring rapid political or social reform. B modern; efficient (a progressive company). 4 (of disease, violence, etc.) Increasing in severity or extent. 5 (of taxation) increasing with the sum taxed. 6 (of a card-game, dance, etc.) With periodic changes of partners. 7 gram. (of a tense) expressing action in progress, e.g. Am writing, was writing. â€”n. (also progressive) advocate of progressive political policies.  progressively adv. [french or medieval latin: related to *progress]'),
(29447, 'p', '\r\nProhibit', ' v. (-t-) (often foll. By from + verbal noun) 1 forbid. 2 prevent.  prohibitor n. Prohibitory adj. [latin prohibeo -hibit-]'),
(29448, 'p', '\r\nProhibited', 'degrees  var. Of *forbidden degrees.'),
(29449, 'p', '\r\nProhibition', ' n. 1 forbidding or being forbidden. 2 edict or order that forbids. 3 (usu. Prohibition) legal ban on the manufacture and sale of alcohol, esp. In the us (1920â€“33).  prohibitionist n. (in sense 3).'),
(29450, 'p', '\r\nProhibitive', ' adj. 1 prohibiting. 2 (of prices, taxes, etc.) Extremely high (prohibitive price).  prohibitively adv.'),
(29451, 'p', '\r\nProject', ' â€”n. 1 plan; scheme. 2 extensive essay, piece of research, etc. By a student. â€”v. 1 protrude; jut out. 2 throw; cast; impel. 3 extrapolate (results etc.) To a future time; forecast. 4 plan or contrive (a scheme etc.). 5 cause (light, shadow, images, etc.) To fall on a surface. 6 cause (a sound, esp. The voice) to be heard at a distance. 7 (often refl. Or absol.) Express or promote forcefully or effectively. 8  make a projection of (the earth, sky, etc.). 9 a (also absol.) Attribute (an emotion etc.) To an external object or person, esp. Unconsciously. B (refl.) Imagine (oneself) having another''s feelings, being in the future, etc. [latin projicio -ject- throw forth]'),
(29452, 'p', '\r\nProjectile', ' â€”n. 1 missile, esp. Fired by a rocket. 2 bullet, shell, etc. â€”adj. 1 capable of being projected by force, esp. From a gun. 2 projecting or impelling.'),
(29453, 'p', '\r\nProjection', ' n. 1 projecting or being projected. 2 thing that projects or obtrudes. 3 presentation of an image etc. On a surface. 4 forecast or estimate (projection of next year''s profits). 5 a mental image viewed as an objective reality. B unconscious transfer of feelings etc. To external objects or persons. 6 representation on a plane surface of any part of the surface of the earth or a celestial sphere (mercator projection).  projectionist n. (in sense 3).'),
(29454, 'p', '\r\nProjector', ' n. Apparatus for projecting slides or film on to a screen.'),
(29455, 'p', '\r\nProkaryote', ' n. Organism in which the chromosomes are not separated from the cytoplasm by a membrane; bacterium. [from *pro-2, karyo- from greek karuon kernel, -ote as in *zygote]'),
(29456, 'p', '\r\nProlactin', ' n. Hormone that stimulates milk production after childbirth. [from *pro-1, *lactation]'),
(29457, 'p', '\r\nProlapse', ' â€”n. (also prolapsus) 1 forward or downward displacement of a part or organ. 2 prolapsed womb, rectum, etc. â€”v. (-sing) undergo prolapse. [latin: related to *lapse]'),
(29458, 'p', '\r\nProlate', ' adj. Geom. (of a spheroid) lengthened in the direction of a polar diameter. [latin, = brought forward, prolonged]'),
(29459, 'p', '\r\nProle', ' adj. & n. Derog. Colloq. Proletarian. [abbreviation]'),
(29460, 'p', '\r\nProlegomenon', ' n. (pl. -mena) (usu. In pl.) Preface to a book etc., esp. When critical or discursive. [greek lego say]'),
(29461, 'p', '\r\nProletarian', ' â€”adj. Of the proletariat. â€”n. Member of the proletariat. [latin proles offspring]'),
(29462, 'p', '\r\nProletariat', ' n. 1 wage-earners collectively. 2 esp. Derog. Lowest, esp. Uneducated, class. [french: related to *proletarian]'),
(29463, 'p', '\r\nProliferate', ' v. (-ting) 1 reproduce; produce (cells etc.) Rapidly. 2 increase rapidly in numbers.  proliferation n. [latin proles offspring]'),
(29464, 'p', '\r\nProlific', ' adj. 1 producing many offspring or much output. 2 (often foll. By of) abundantly productive. 3 (often foll. By in) abounding, copious.  prolifically adv. [medieval latin: related to *proliferate]'),
(29465, 'p', '\r\nProlix', ' adj. (of speech, writing, etc.) Lengthy; tedious.  prolixity n. [latin]'),
(29466, 'p', '\r\nPrologue', ' n. 1 preliminary speech, poem, etc., esp. Of a play. 2 (usu. Foll. By to) introductory event. [greek logos word]'),
(29467, 'p', '\r\nProlong', ' v. 1 extend in time or space. 2 (as prolonged adj.) Lengthy, esp. Tediously so.  prolongation n. [latin longus long]'),
(29468, 'p', '\r\nProm', ' n. Colloq. 1 = *promenade n. 1. 2  = *promenade concert. [abbreviation]'),
(29469, 'p', '\r\nPromenade', ' â€”n. 1 paved public walk, esp. Along the sea front at a resort. 2 walk, ride, or drive, taken esp. For display or pleasure. â€”v. (-ding) 1 make a promenade (through). 2 lead (a person etc.) About, esp. For display. [french]'),
(29470, 'p', '\r\nPromenade', 'concert  n. Concert with restricted seating and a large area for standing.'),
(29471, 'p', '\r\nPromenade', 'deck  n. Upper deck on a passenger ship.'),
(29472, 'p', '\r\nPromenader', ' n. 1 person who promenades. 2 regular attender at promenade concerts.'),
(29473, 'p', '\r\nPromethean', ' adj. Daring or inventive. [prometheus, a mortal punished by the greek gods for stealing fire]'),
(29474, 'p', '\r\nPromethium', ' n. Radioactive metallic element of the lanthanide series, found in nuclear waste. [prometheus: see *promethean]'),
(29475, 'p', '\r\nProminence', ' n. 1 being prominent. 2 jutting outcrop, mountain, etc. [latin: related to *prominent]'),
(29476, 'p', '\r\nProminent', ' adj. 1 jutting out, projecting. 2 conspicuous. 3 distinguished, important. [latin promineo project]'),
(29477, 'p', '\r\nPromiscuous', ' adj. 1 having frequent, esp. Casual, sexual relationships. 2 mixed and indiscriminate. 3 colloq. Carelessly irregular; casual.  promiscuity n. Promiscuously adv. [latin misceo mix]'),
(29478, 'p', '\r\nPromise', ' â€”n. 1 assurance that one will or will not undertake a certain action etc. (promise of help). 2 sign of future achievements, good results, etc. (writer of great promise). â€”v. (-sing) 1 (usu. Foll. By to + infin., or that + clause; also absol.) Make a promise (promise not to be late). 2 (often foll. By to + infin.) Seem likely (to) (promises to be a good book). 3 colloq. Assure (i promise you, it will not be easy).  promise well (or ill etc.) Hold out good (or bad etc.) Prospects. [latin promissum from mitto miss- send]'),
(29479, 'p', '\r\nPromised', 'land  n. (prec. By the) 1 bibl. Canaan (gen. 12:7 etc.). 2 any desired place, esp. Heaven.'),
(29480, 'p', '\r\nPromising', ' adj. Likely to turn out well; hopeful, full of promise (promising start).  promisingly adv.'),
(29481, 'p', '\r\nPromissory', ' adj. Conveying or implying a promise. [medieval latin: related to *promise]'),
(29482, 'p', '\r\nPromissory', 'note  n. Signed document containing a written promise to pay a stated sum.'),
(29483, 'p', '\r\nPromo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. 1 (often attrib.) Promotion, advertising (promo video). 2 promotional video, trailer, etc. [abbreviation]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(29484, 'p', '\r\nPromontory', ' n. (pl. -ies) point of high land jutting out into the sea etc.; headland. [latin]'),
(29485, 'p', '\r\nPromote', ' v. (-ting) 1 (often foll. By to) raise (a person) to a higher office, rank, etc. (promoted to captain). 2 help forward; encourage (a cause, process, etc.). 3 publicize and sell (a product). 4 chess raise (a pawn) to the rank of queen etc.  promotion n. Promotional adj. [latin promoveo -mot-]'),
(29486, 'p', '\r\nPromoter', ' n. 1 person who promotes, esp. A sporting event, theatrical production, etc. 2 (in full company promoter) person who promotes the formation of a joint-stock company. [medieval latin: related to *promote]'),
(29487, 'p', '\r\nPrompt', ' â€”adj. Acting, made, or done with alacrity; ready (prompt reply). â€”adv. Punctually (at six o''clock prompt). â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By to, or to + infin.) Incite; urge (prompted them to action). 2 a (also absol.) Supply a forgotten word etc. To (an actor etc.). B assist (a hesitating speaker) with a suggestion. 3 give rise to; inspire (feeling, thought, action, etc.). â€”n. 1 a act of prompting. B thing said to prompt an actor etc. C = *prompter. 2 computing sign on a vdu screen to show that the system is waiting for input.  promptitude n. Promptly adv. Promptness n. [latin]'),
(29488, 'p', '\r\nPrompter', ' n. Person who prompts actors.'),
(29489, 'p', '\r\nPromulgate', ' v. (-ting) 1 make known to the public; disseminate; promote. 2 proclaim (a decree, news, etc.).  promulgation n. Promulgator n. [latin]'),
(29490, 'p', '\r\nProne', ' adj. 1 a lying face downwards. B lying flat, prostrate. C having the front part downwards, esp. The palm. 2 (usu. Foll. By to, or to + infin.) Disposed or liable (prone to bite his nails). 3 (usu. In comb.) Likely to suffer (accident-prone).  proneness n. [latin]'),
(29491, 'p', '\r\nProng', ' n. Each of two or more projecting pointed parts at the end of a fork etc. [origin unknown]'),
(29492, 'p', '\r\nPronominal', ' adj. Of, concerning, or being, a pronoun. [latin: related to *pronoun]'),
(29493, 'p', '\r\nPronoun', ' n. Word used instead of and to indicate a noun already mentioned or known, esp. To avoid repetition (e.g. We, their, this, ourselves). [from *pro-1, *noun]'),
(29494, 'p', '\r\nPronounce', ' v. (-cing) 1 (also absol.) Utter or speak (words, sounds, etc.) In a certain, or esp. In the approved, way. 2 utter or proclaim (a judgement, sentence, etc.) Officially, formally, or solemnly (i pronounce you man and wife). 3 state as one''s opinion (pronounced the beef excellent). 4 (usu. Foll. By on, for, against, in favour of) pass judgement (pronounced for the defendant).  pronounceable adj. Pronouncement n. [latin nuntio announce]'),
(29495, 'p', '\r\nPronounced', ' adj. Strongly marked; noticeable (pronounced limp).'),
(29496, 'p', '\r\nPronto', ' adv. Colloq. Promptly, quickly. [latin: related to *prompt]'),
(29497, 'p', '\r\nPronunciation', ' n. 1 pronouncing of a word, esp. With reference to a standard. 2 act of pronouncing. 3 way of pronouncing words etc. [latin: related to *pronounce]'),
(29498, 'p', '\r\nProof', ' â€”n. 1 facts, evidence, reasoning, etc. Establishing or helping to establish a fact (no proof that he was there). 2 demonstration, proving (not capable of proof). 3 test, trial (put them to the proof). 4 standard of strength of distilled alcohol. 5 trial impression from type or film, for correcting before final printing. 6 step by step resolution of a mathematical or philosophical problem. 7 photographic print made for selection etc. â€”adj. 1 (often in comb.) Impervious to penetration, ill effects, etc., esp. By a specified agent (proof against corruption; childproof). 2 being of proof alcoholic strength. â€”v. 1 make proof, esp. Make (fabric) waterproof. 2 make a proof of (a printed work). [latin proba: related to *prove]'),
(29499, 'p', '\r\nProofread', ' v. (past and past part. -read) read and correct (printer''s proofs).  proofreader n.'),
(29500, 'p', '\r\nProp1', ' â€”n. 1 rigid, esp. Separate, support. 2 person or thing that supports, comforts, etc. â€”v. (-pp-) (often foll. By against, up, etc.) Support with or as if with a prop. [low german or dutch]'),
(29501, 'p', '\r\nProp2', ' n. Colloq. = *property 3. [abbreviation]'),
(29502, 'p', '\r\nProp3', ' n. Colloq. Propeller. [abbreviation]'),
(29503, 'p', '\r\nPropaganda', ' n. 1 organized propagation of a doctrine by use of publicity, selected information, etc. 2 usu. Derog. Ideas etc. So propagated.  propagandist n. & adj. Propagandize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). [latin: related to *propagate]'),
(29504, 'p', '\r\nPropagate', ' v. (-ting) 1 a breed (a plant, animal, etc.) From the parent stock. B (refl. Or absol.) (of a plant, animal, etc.) Reproduce itself. 2 disseminate (a belief, theory, etc.). 3 transmit (a vibration, earthquake, etc.).  propagation n. [latin propago]'),
(29505, 'p', '\r\nPropagator', ' n. 1 person or thing that propagates. 2 small heated box for germinating seeds or raising seedlings.'),
(29506, 'p', '\r\nPropane', ' n. Gaseous hydrocarbon used as bottled fuel. [propionic acid: related to *pro-2, greek pion fat]'),
(29507, 'p', '\r\nPropanone', ' n. Chem. = *acetone. [from *propane]'),
(29508, 'p', '\r\nPropel', ' v. (-ll-) drive or push forward; urge on.  propellant n. & adj. [latin pello puls- drive]'),
(29509, 'p', '\r\nPropeller', ' n. Revolving shaft with blades, esp. For propelling a ship or aircraft.'),
(29510, 'p', '\r\nPropene', ' n. Chem. = *propylene. [from *propane, *alkene]'),
(29511, 'p', '\r\nPropensity', ' n. (pl. -ies) inclination, tendency. [latin propensus inclined]'),
(29512, 'p', '\r\nProper', ' adj. 1 a accurate, correct (gave him the proper amount). B fit, suitable, right (at the proper time). 2 decent; respectable, esp. Excessively so (not quite proper). 3 (usu. Foll. By to) belonging or relating (respect proper to them). 4 (usu. Placed after the noun) strictly so called; genuine (this is the crypt, not the cathedral proper). 5 colloq. Thorough; complete (a proper row). [latin proprius one''s own]'),
(29513, 'p', '\r\nProper', 'fraction  n. Fraction less than unity, with the numerator less than the denominator.'),
(29514, 'p', '\r\nProperly', ' adv. 1 fittingly, suitably (do it properly). 2 accurately, correctly (properly speaking). 3 rightly. 4 with decency; respectably (behave properly). 5 colloq. Thoroughly (properly puzzled).'),
(29515, 'p', '\r\nProper', 'noun  n. (also proper name) capitalized name for an individual person, place, animal, country, title, etc., e.g. â€˜janeâ€™, â€˜everestâ€™.'),
(29516, 'p', '\r\nPropertied', ' adj. Having property, esp. Land.'),
(29517, 'p', '\r\nProperty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 thing(s) owned; possession, esp. A house, land, etc. (has money in property). 2 attribute, quality, or characteristic (property of dissolving grease). 3 movable object used on a theatre stage or in a film. [latin proprietas: related to *proper]'),
(29518, 'p', '\r\nProphecy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a prophetic utterance, esp. Biblical. B prediction of future events. 2 faculty, practice, etc. Of prophesying (gift of prophecy). [greek: related to *prophet]'),
(29519, 'p', '\r\nProphesy', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 (usu. Foll. By that, who, etc.) Foretell (an event etc.). 2 speak as a prophet; foretell the future. [french profecier: related to *prophecy]'),
(29520, 'p', '\r\nProphet', ' n. (fem. Prophetess) 1 teacher or interpreter of the supposed will of god. 2 a person who foretells events. B spokesman; advocate (prophet of the new order). 3 (the prophet) muhammad. [greek prophetes spokesman]'),
(29521, 'p', '\r\nProphetic', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By of) containing a prediction; predicting. 2 of a prophet.   prophetically adv. [latin: related to *prophet]'),
(29522, 'p', '\r\nProphylactic', ' â€”adj. Tending to prevent disease etc. â€”n. 1 preventive medicine or action. 2 esp. Us condom. [greek, = keeping guard before]'),
(29523, 'p', '\r\nProphylaxis', ' n. Preventive treatment against disease. [from *pro-2, greek phulaxis guarding]'),
(29524, 'p', '\r\nPropinquity', ' n. 1 nearness in space; proximity. 2 close kinship. 3 similarity. [latin prope near]'),
(29525, 'p', '\r\nPropitiate', ' v. (-ting) appease (an offended person etc.).  propitiable adj. Propitiation n. Propitiator n. Propitiatory adj.  [latin: related to *propitious]'),
(29526, 'p', '\r\nPropitious', ' adj. 1 (of an omen etc.) Favourable, auspicious. 2 (often foll. By for, to) suitable, advantageous. [latin propitius]'),
(29527, 'p', '\r\nProponent', ' n. Person advocating a motion, theory, or proposal. [latin: related to *propose]'),
(29528, 'p', '\r\nProportion', ' â€”n. 1 a comparative part or share (large proportion of the profits). B comparative ratio (proportion of births to deaths). 2 correct or pleasing relation of things or parts of a thing (has fine proportions; exaggerated out of all proportion). 3 (in pl.) Dimensions; size (large proportions). 4 math. Equality of ratios between two pairs of quantities, e.g. 3:5 and 9:15. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By to) make proportionate (proportion the punishment to the crime). [latin: related to *portion]'),
(29529, 'p', '\r\nProportional', ' adj. In due proportion; comparable (proportional increase in the expense).  proportionally adv.'),
(29530, 'p', '\r\nProportional', 'representation  n. Electoral system in which parties gain seats in proportion to the number of votes cast for them.'),
(29531, 'p', '\r\nProportionate', ' adj. = *proportional.  proportionately adv.'),
(29532, 'p', '\r\nProposal', ' n. 1 a act of proposing something. B course of action etc. Proposed. 2 offer of marriage.'),
(29533, 'p', '\r\nPropose', ' v. (-sing) 1 (also absol.) Put forward for consideration or as a plan; suggest. 2 (usu. Foll. By to + infin., or verbal noun) intend; purpose (propose to open a cafÃ©). 3 (usu. Foll. By to) offer oneself in marriage. 4 nominate (a person) as a member of a society, for an office, etc.  propose a toast (or somebody''s health) ask people to drink to someone''s health.  proposer n. [latin pono posit- place]'),
(29534, 'p', '\r\nProposition', ' â€”n. 1 statement, assertion. 2 scheme proposed, proposal. 3 logic statement subject to proof or disproof. 4 colloq. Problem, opponent, prospect, etc. For consideration (difficult proposition). 5 math. Formal statement of a theorem or problem, often including the demonstration. 6 a likely commercial etc. Enterprise etc. B person regarded similarly. 7 colloq. Sexual proposal. â€”v. Colloq. Make a (esp'),
(29535, 'p', '\r\nPropound', ' v. Offer for consideration; propose. [propo(u)ne from latin: related to *propose]'),
(29536, 'p', '\r\nProprietary', ' adj. 1 a of or holding property (proprietary classes). B of a proprietor (proprietary rights). 2 held in private ownership. [latin proprietarius: related to *property]'),
(29537, 'p', '\r\nProprietary', 'medicine  n. Drug, medicine, etc. Produced by a company, usu. Under a patent.'),
(29538, 'p', '\r\nProprietary', 'name  n. (also proprietary term) registered name of a product etc. As a trade mark.'),
(29539, 'p', '\r\nProprietor', ' n. (fem. Proprietress) 1 holder of property. 2 owner of a business etc., esp. Of a hotel.  proprietorial adj. [related to *proprietary]'),
(29540, 'p', '\r\nPropriety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 fitness; rightness. 2 correctness of behaviour or morals. 3 (in pl.) Details or rules of correct conduct. [french: related to *property]'),
(29541, 'p', '\r\nPropulsion', ' n. 1 driving or pushing forward. 2 impelling influence.  propulsive adj. [related to *propel]'),
(29542, 'p', '\r\nPropylene', ' n.  Gaseous hydrocarbon used in the manufacture of chemicals. [from propyl, a univalent radical of propane]'),
(29543, 'p', '\r\nPro', 'rata  â€”adj. Proportional. â€”adv. Proportionally. [latin]'),
(29544, 'p', '\r\nProrogue', ' v. (-gues, -gued, -guing) 1 discontinue the meetings of (a parliament etc.) Without dissolving it. 2 (of a parliament etc.) Be prorogued.  prorogation n. [latin prorogo extend]'),
(29545, 'p', '\r\nProsaic', ' adj. 1 like prose, lacking poetic beauty. 2 unromantic; dull; commonplace.  prosaically adv. [latin: related to *prose]'),
(29546, 'p', '\r\nPros', 'and cons  n.pl. Reasons or considerations for and against a proposition etc.'),
(29547, 'p', '\r\nProscenium', ' n. (pl. -s or -nia) part of the stage in front of the curtain and the enclosing arch. [greek: related to *scene]'),
(29548, 'p', '\r\nProscribe', ' v. (-bing) 1 forbid, esp. By law. 2 reject or denounce (a practice etc.). 3 outlaw (a person).  proscription n. Proscriptive adj. [latin, = publish in writing]'),
(29549, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' proscribe is sometimes confused with prescribe.'),
(29550, 'p', '\r\nProse', ' â€”n. 1 ordinary written or spoken language not in verse. 2 passage of prose, esp. For translation into a foreign language. 3 dull or matter-of-fact quality (prose of existence). â€”v. (-sing) talk tediously. [latin prosa from oratio straightforward (discourse)]'),
(29551, 'p', '\r\nProsecute', ' v. (-ting) 1 (also absol.) Institute legal proceedings against (a person), or with reference to (a claim, crime, etc.) (decided not to prosecute). 2 formal carry on (a trade, pursuit, etc.).  prosecutor n. [latin prosequor -secut- pursue]'),
(29552, 'p', '\r\nProsecution', ' n. 1 a institution and continuation of (esp. Criminal) legal proceedings. B prosecuting party in a court case. 2 prosecuting or being prosecuted (in the prosecution of his hobby).'),
(29553, 'p', '\r\nProselyte', ' n. 1 person converted, esp. Recently, from one opinion, creed, party, etc., to another. 2 convert to judaism.  proselytism n. [latin proselytus from greek]'),
(29554, 'p', '\r\nProselytize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) (also absol.) Convert or seek to convert from one belief etc. To another.'),
(29555, 'p', '\r\nProse', 'poem  n. Piece of poetic writing in prose.'),
(29556, 'p', '\r\nProsody', ' n. 1 science of versification. 2 the study of speech-rhythms.  prosodic adj. Prosodist n. [greek pros to: related to *ode]'),
(29557, 'p', '\r\nProspect', ' â€”n. 1 a (often in pl.) Expectation, esp. Of success in a career etc. (job with no prospects). B something one expects (don''t relish the prospect of meeting him). 2 extensive view of landscape etc. (striking prospect). 3 mental picture. 4 possible or probable customer, subscriber, etc. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By for) explore, search (esp. A region) for gold etc.  prospector n. [latin: related to *prospectus]'),
(29558, 'p', '\r\nProspective', ' adj. Some day to be; expected; future (prospective bridegroom). [latin: related to *prospectus]'),
(29559, 'p', '\r\nProspectus', ' n. (pl. -tuses) printed document advertising or describing a school, commercial enterprise, forthcoming book, etc. [latin, = prospect, from prospicio -spect- look forward]'),
(29560, 'p', '\r\nProsper', ' v. Be successful, thrive. [latin prospero]'),
(29561, 'p', '\r\nProsperity', ' n. Prosperous state; wealth; success.'),
(29562, 'p', '\r\nProsperous', ' adj. 1 successful; rich; thriving. 2 auspicious (prosperous wind).  prosperously adv. [french from latin]'),
(29563, 'p', '\r\nProstate', ' n. (in full prostate gland) gland round the neck of the bladder in male mammals, releasing part of the semen.  prostatic adj. [greek prostates one who stands before]'),
(29564, 'p', '\r\nProsthesis', ' n. (pl. -theses) 1 artificial leg etc; false tooth, breast, etc. 2 branch of surgery dealing with prostheses.  prosthetic adj.  [greek, = placing in addition]'),
(29565, 'p', '\r\nProstitute', ' â€”n. 1 woman who engages in sexual activity for payment. 2 (usu. Male prostitute) man or boy who engages in sexual activity, esp. With homosexual men, for payment. â€”v. (-ting) 1 (esp. Refl.) Make a prostitute of (esp. Oneself). 2 misuse or offer (one''s talents, skills, name, etc.) For money etc.  prostitution n. [latin prostituo -tut- offer for sale]'),
(29566, 'p', '\r\nProstrate', ' â€”adj. 1 a lying face downwards, esp. In submission. B lying horizontally. 2 overcome, esp. By grief, exhaustion, etc. 3 growing along the ground. â€”v. (-ting) 1 lay or throw (esp. A person) flat. 2 refl. Throw (oneself) down in submission etc. 3 overcome; make weak.  prostration n. [latin prosterno -strat- throw in front]'),
(29567, 'p', '\r\nProsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) tedious, commonplace, dull (prosy talk).  prosily adv. Prosiness n.'),
(29568, 'p', '\r\nProtactinium', ' n.  Radioactive metallic element.  [german: related to *actinium]'),
(29569, 'p', '\r\nProtagonist', ' n. 1 chief person in a drama, story, etc. 2 leading person in a contest etc.; principal performer. 3 (usu. Foll. By of, for) advocate or champion of a cause etc. (protagonist of women''s rights). [greek: related to *proto-, agonistes actor]'),
(29570, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of protagonist in sense 3 is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(29571, 'p', '\r\nProtean', ' adj. Variable, taking many forms; versatile. [proteus, greek sea-god who took various shapes]'),
(29572, 'p', '\r\nProtect', ' v. 1 (often foll. By from, against) keep (a person, thing, etc.) Safe; defend, guard. 2  shield (home industry) from competition with import duties. [latin tego tect- cover]'),
(29573, 'p', '\r\nProtection', ' n. 1 a protecting or being protected; defence. B thing, person, or animal that protects. 2 (also protectionism) theory or practice of protecting home industries. 3 colloq. A immunity from violence etc. Obtained by payment to gangsters etc. B (in full protection money) money so paid.  protectionist n. & adj.'),
(29574, 'p', '\r\nProtective', ' adj. Protecting; intended or tending to protect.  protectively adv. Protectiveness n.'),
(29575, 'p', '\r\nProtective', 'custody  n. Detention of a person for his or her own protection.'),
(29576, 'p', '\r\nProtector', ' n. (fem. Protectress) 1 person or thing that protects. 2 hist. Regent ruling during the minority or absence of the sovereign.  protectorship n.'),
(29577, 'p', '\r\nProtectorate', ' n. 1 a state that is controlled and protected by another. B this relation. 2 hist. A office of the protector of a kingdom or state. B period of this, esp. In england 1653â€“9.'),
(29578, 'p', '\r\nProtÃ©gÃ©', ' n. (fem. ProtÃ©gÃ©e pronunc. Same) person under the protection, patronage, tutelage, etc. Of another. [french: related to *protect]'),
(29579, 'p', '\r\nProtein', ' n. Any of a group of organic compounds composed of one or more chains of amino acids and forming an essential part of all living organisms. [greek protos first]'),
(29580, 'p', '\r\nPro', 'tem  adj. & adv. Colloq. = *pro tempore. [abbreviation]'),
(29581, 'p', '\r\nPro', 'tempore  adj. & adv. For the time being. [latin]'),
(29582, 'p', '\r\nProterozoic', ' geol. â€”adj. Of the later part of the precambrian era. â€”n. This time. [greek proteros former, zoe life]'),
(29583, 'p', '\r\nProtest', ' â€”n. 1 statement or act of dissent or disapproval. 2 law written declaration that a bill has been presented and payment or acceptance refused. â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By against, at, about, etc.) Make a protest. 2 affirm (one''s innocence etc.) Solemnly. 3 law write or obtain a protest in regard to (a bill). 4 us object to (a decision etc.).  under protest unwillingly.  protester n. (also protestor). [latin protestor declare formally]'),
(29584, 'p', '\r\nProtestant', ' â€”n. Member or follower of any of the churches separating from the roman catholic church after the reformation. â€”adj. Of the protestant churches or their members etc.  protestantism n. [related to *protest]'),
(29585, 'p', '\r\nProtestation', ' n. 1 strong affirmation. 2 protest. [latin: related to *protest]'),
(29586, 'p', '\r\nProtium', ' n. Ordinary isotope of hydrogen. [latin: related to *proto-]'),
(29587, 'p', '\r\nProto-', ' comb. Form first. [greek protos]'),
(29588, 'p', '\r\nProtocol', ' â€”n. 1 official formality and etiquette, esp. As observed on state occasions etc. 2 original draft of esp. The terms of a treaty. 3 formal statement of a transaction. â€”v. (-ll-) draw up or record in a protocol. [greek kolla glue]'),
(29589, 'p', '\r\nProton', ' n. Elementary particle with a positive electric charge equal to that of an electron, and occurring in all atomic nuclei. [greek protos first]'),
(29590, 'p', '\r\nProtoplasm', ' n. Material comprising the living part of a cell, consisting of a nucleus in membrane-enclosed cytoplasm.  protoplasmic adj. [greek: related to *proto-, *plasma]'),
(29591, 'p', '\r\nPrototype', ' n. 1 original as a pattern for imitations, improved forms, representations, etc. 2 trial model or preliminary version of a vehicle, machine, etc.  prototypic adj. Prototypical adj.  [greek: related to *proto-]'),
(29592, 'p', '\r\nProtozoan', ' â€”n. (also protozoon) (pl. Protozoa or -s) unicellular microscopic organism, e.g. The amoebae. â€”adj. (also protozoic) of this group. [from *proto-, greek zoion animal]'),
(29593, 'p', '\r\nProtract', ' v. (often as protracted adj.) Prolong or lengthen.  protraction n. [latin traho tract- draw]'),
(29594, 'p', '\r\nProtractor', ' n. Instrument for measuring angles, usu. In the form of a graduated semicircle.'),
(29595, 'p', '\r\nProtrude', ' v. (-ding) thrust forward; stick out; project.  protrusion n. Protrusive adj. [latin trudo trus- thrust]'),
(29596, 'p', '\r\nProtuberant', ' adj. Bulging out; prominent.  protuberance n. [latin: related to *tuber]'),
(29597, 'p', '\r\nProud', ' adj. 1 feeling greatly honoured or pleased (proud to know him). 2 a (often foll. By of) haughty, arrogant (too proud to speak to us). B (often in comb.) Having a proper pride; satisfied (house-proud; proud of a job well done). 3 (of an occasion, action, etc.) Justly arousing or showing pride (proud day; proud smile). 4 imposing, splendid. 5 (often foll. By of) slightly projecting (nail stood proud of the plank).  do proud colloq. Treat with lavish generosity or honour (did us proud).  proudly adv. [french prud valiant]'),
(29598, 'p', '\r\nProve', ' v. (-ving; past part. Proved or proven) 1 (often foll. By that) demonstrate the truth of by evidence or argument. 2 a (usu. Foll. By to + infin.) Be found (it proved to be untrue). B emerge as (will prove the winner). 3 test the accuracy of (a calculation). 4  establish the validity of (a will). 5  (of dough) rise in bread-making.  not proven (in scottish law) verdict that there is insufficient evidence to establish guilt or innocence. Prove oneself show one''s abilities, courage, etc.  provable adj. [latin probo test, approve]'),
(29599, 'p', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of proven as the past participle is uncommon except in certain expressions, such as of proven ability. It is, however, standard in scots and american english.'),
(29600, 'p', '\r\nProvenance', ' n. Origin or place of origin; history. [french provenir from latin]'),
(29601, 'p', '\r\nProvenÃ§al', ' â€”adj. Of provence. â€”n. Native or language of provence. [french: related to *province]'),
(29602, 'p', '\r\nProvender', ' n. 1 animal fodder. 2 joc. Food. [latin: related to *prebend]'),
(29603, 'p', '\r\nProverb', ' n. Short pithy saying in general use, held to embody a general truth. [latin proverbium from verbum word]'),
(29604, 'p', '\r\nProverbial', ' adj. 1 (esp. Of a characteristic) well known; notorious (his proverbial honesty). 2 of or referred to in a proverb (proverbial ill wind).  proverbially adv. [latin: related to *proverb]'),
(29605, 'p', '\r\nProvide', ' v. (-ding) 1 supply, furnish (provided me with food; provided a chance). 2 a (usu. Foll. By for, against) make due preparation. B (usu. Foll. By for) take care of a person etc. With money, food, etc. (provides for a large family). 3 (usu. Foll. By that) stipulate in a will, statute, etc.  provider n. [latin provideo -vis- foresee]'),
(29606, 'p', '\r\nProvided', ' conj. (often foll. By that) on the condition or understanding that.'),
(29607, 'p', '\r\nProvidence', ' n. 1 protective care of god or nature. 2 (providence) god in this aspect. 3 foresight; thrift. [latin: related to *provide]'),
(29608, 'p', '\r\nProvident', ' adj. Having or showing foresight; thrifty. [latin: related to *provide]'),
(29609, 'p', '\r\nProvidential', ' adj. 1 of or by divine foresight or interposition. 2 opportune, lucky.  providentially adv.'),
(29610, 'p', '\r\nProvident', 'society  n. = *friendly society.'),
(29611, 'p', '\r\nProviding', ' conj. = *provided.'),
(29612, 'p', '\r\nProvince', ' n. 1 principal administrative division of a country etc. 2 (the provinces) country outside a capital city, esp. Regarded as uncultured or unsophisticated. 3 sphere of action; business (outside my province). 4 branch of learning etc. (in the province of aesthetics). 5 district under an archbishop or metropolitan. 6 territory outside italy under an ancient roman governor. [latin provincia]'),
(29613, 'p', '\r\nProvincial', ' â€”adj. 1 of a province or provinces. 2 unsophisticated or uncultured. â€”n. 1 inhabitant of a province or the provinces. 2 unsophisticated or uncultured person.  provincialism n.'),
(29614, 'p', '\r\nProvision', ' â€”n. 1 a act of providing (provision of nurseries). B preparation, esp. For the future (made provision for their old age). 2 (in pl.) Food, drink, etc., esp. For an expedition. 3 legal or formal stipulation or proviso. â€”v. Supply with provisions. [latin: related to *provide]'),
(29615, 'p', '\r\nProvisional', ' â€”adj. 1 providing for immediate needs only; temporary. 2 (provisional) of the unofficial wing of the ira, using terrorism. â€”n. (provisional) member of the provisional wing of the ira.  provisionally adv.'),
(29616, 'p', '\r\nProviso', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 stipulation. 2 clause containing this.  provisory adj. [latin, = it being provided]'),
(29617, 'p', '\r\nProvo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Provisional. [abbreviation]'),
(29618, 'p', '\r\nProvocation', ' n. 1 provoking or being provoked (did it under severe provocation). 2 cause of annoyance.'),
(29619, 'p', '\r\nProvocative', ' adj. 1 (usu. Foll. By of) tending to provoke, esp. Anger or sexual desire. 2 intentionally annoying or controversial.  provocatively adv. Provocativeness n.'),
(29620, 'p', '\r\nProvoke', ' v. (-king) 1 (often foll. By to, or to + infin.) Rouse or incite (provoked him to fury). 2 call forth; instigate; cause (indignation, an inquiry, process, etc.). 3 (usu. Foll. By into + verbal noun) irritate or stimulate (a person) (provoked him into retaliating). 4 tempt; allure.'),
(29621, 'p', '\r\nProvost', ' n. 1 head of some (esp. Oxbridge) colleges. 2 head of a cathedral chapter. 3 = *provost marshal. [latin propositus from pono place]'),
(29622, 'p', '\r\nProvost', 'marshal  n. Head of military police in camp or on active service.'),
(29623, 'p', '\r\nProw', ' n. 1 fore-part or bow of a ship. 2 pointed or projecting front part. [french proue from greek proira]'),
(29624, 'p', '\r\nProwess', ' n. 1 skill, expertise. 2 valour, gallantry. [french: related to *proud]'),
(29625, 'p', '\r\nProwl', ' â€”v. (often foll. By about, around) roam (a place) esp. Stealthily or restlessly or in search of prey, plunder, etc. â€”n. Act of prowling.  on the prowl prowling.  prowler n. [origin unknown]'),
(29626, 'p', '\r\nProx.', ' Abbr. Proximo.'),
(29627, 'p', '\r\nProximate', ' adj. 1 nearest or next before or after (in place, order, time, causation, thought process, etc.). 2 approximate. [latin proximus nearest]'),
(29628, 'p', '\r\nProximity', ' n. Nearness in space, time, etc. (in close proximity). [latin: related to *proximate]'),
(29629, 'p', '\r\nProximo', ' adj. Commerce of next month (the third proximo). [latin, = in the next (mense month)]'),
(29630, 'p', '\r\nProxy', ' n. (pl. -ies) (also attrib.) 1 authorization given to a substitute or deputy (proxy vote; married by proxy). 2 person authorized to act thus. 3 a written authorization for esp. Proxy voting. B proxy vote. [obsolete procuracy procuration]'),
(29631, 'p', '\r\nPrude', ' n. Excessively (often affectedly) squeamish or sexually modest person.  prudery n. Prudish adj. Prudishly adv. Prudishness n. [french: related to *proud]'),
(29632, 'p', '\r\nPrudent', ' adj. Cautious; politic.  prudence n. Prudently adv. [latin prudens -ent-: related to *provident]'),
(29633, 'p', '\r\nPrudential', ' adj. Of or showing prudence.  prudentially adv.'),
(29634, 'p', '\r\nPrune1', ' n. Dried plum. [latin prunum from greek]'),
(29635, 'p', '\r\nPrune2', ' v. (-ning) 1 a (often foll. By down) trim (a bush etc.) By cutting away dead or overgrown branches etc. B (usu. Foll. By off, away) lop (branches etc.) Thus. 2 reduce (costs etc.) (prune expenses). 3 a (often foll. By of) clear or remove superfluities from. B remove (superfluities). [french prooignier from romanic: related to *round]'),
(29636, 'p', '\r\nPrurient', ' adj. Having or encouraging unhealthy sexual curiosity.  prurience n. [latin prurio itch]'),
(29637, 'p', '\r\nPrussian', ' â€”adj. Of prussia, or esp. Its rigidly militaristic tradition. â€”n. Native of prussia. [prussia, former german state]'),
(29638, 'p', '\r\nPrussian', 'blue  n. & adj. (as adj. Often hyphenated) deep blue (pigment).'),
(29639, 'p', '\r\nPrussic', 'acid  n.  Hydrocyanic acid. [french]'),
(29640, 'p', '\r\nPry', ' v. (pries, pried) 1 (usu. Foll. By into) inquire impertinently. 2 (usu. Foll. By into, about, etc.) Look or peer inquisitively. [origin unknown]'),
(29641, 'p', '\r\nPs', ' abbr. Postscript.'),
(29642, 'p', '\r\nPsalm', ' n. 1 (also psalm) sacred song, esp. From the book of psalms, esp. Metrically chanted in a service. 2 (the psalms or the book of psalms) old testament book containing the psalms. [latin psalmus from greek]'),
(29643, 'p', '\r\nPsalmist', ' n. Composer of a psalm.'),
(29644, 'p', '\r\nPsalmody', ' n. Practice or art of singing psalms, hymns, etc., esp. In public worship. [greek: related to *psalm]'),
(29645, 'p', '\r\nPsalter', ' n. 1 the book of psalms. 2 (psalter) version or copy of this. [old english and french from greek psalterion stringed instrument]'),
(29646, 'p', '\r\nPsaltery', ' n. (pl. -ies) ancient and medieval instrument like a dulcimer but played by plucking the strings. [latin: related to *psalter]'),
(29647, 'p', '\r\nPsephology', ' n. The statistical study of voting etc.  psephologist n. [greek psephos pebble, vote]'),
(29648, 'p', '\r\nPseud', ' colloq. â€”adj. (esp. Intellectually) pretentious; not genuine. â€”n. Such a person; poseur. [from *pseudo-]'),
(29649, 'p', '\r\nPseudo', ' adj. & n. (pl. -s) = *pseud.'),
(29650, 'p', '\r\nPseudo-', ' comb. Form (also pseud- before a vowel) 1 false; not genuine (pseudo-intellectual). 2 resembling or imitating (pseudo-acid). [greek pseudes false]'),
(29651, 'p', '\r\nPseudonym', ' n. Fictitious name, esp. Of an author. [greek: related to *pseudo-, onoma name]'),
(29652, 'p', '\r\nPsi1', ' n. Twenty-third letter of the greek alphabet (y, y). [greek]'),
(29653, 'p', '\r\nPsi2', ' abbr. Pounds per square inch.'),
(29654, 'p', '\r\nPsittacosis', ' n. Contagious viral disease of esp. Parrots, transmissible to human beings. [greek psittakos parrot]'),
(29655, 'p', '\r\nPsoriasis', ' n. Skin disease marked by red scaly patches. [greek psora itch]'),
(29656, 'p', '\r\nPsst', ' int. (also pst) whispered exclamation to attract a person''s attention. [imitative]'),
(29657, 'p', '\r\nPsv', ' abbr.  Public service vehicle.'),
(29658, 'p', '\r\nPsych', ' v. Colloq. 1 (usu. Foll. By up; often refl.) Prepare (oneself or another) mentally for an ordeal etc. 2 (often foll. By out) intimidate or frighten (a person), esp. For one''s own advantage. 3 (usu. Foll. By out) analyse (a person''s motivation etc.) For one''s own advantage (can''t psych him out). [abbreviation]'),
(29659, 'p', '\r\nPsyche', ' n. The soul, spirit, or mind. [latin from greek]'),
(29660, 'p', '\r\nPsychedelia', ' n.pl. 1 psychedelic phenomena. 2 subculture associated with these.'),
(29661, 'p', '\r\nPsychedelic', ' adj. 1 a expanding the mind''s awareness etc., esp. With hallucinogenic drugs. B hallucinatory; bizarre. C (of a drug) producing hallucinations. 2 colloq. A producing a hallucinatory effect; vivid in colour or design etc. B (of colours, patterns, etc.) Bright, bold, and often abstract. [greek psukhe mind, delos clear]'),
(29662, 'p', '\r\nPsychiatry', ' n. The study and treatment of mental disease.  psychiatric adj.  Psychiatrist n. [from *psycho-, greek iatros physician]'),
(29663, 'p', '\r\nPsychic', ' â€”adj. 1 a (of a person) considered to have occult powers such as telepathy, clairvoyance, etc. B supernatural. 2 of the soul or mind. â€”n. Person considered to have psychic powers; medium. [greek psukhe soul, mind]'),
(29664, 'p', '\r\nPsychical', ' adj. 1 concerning psychic phenomena or faculties (psychical research). 2 of the soul or mind.  psychically adv.'),
(29665, 'p', '\r\nPsycho', ' colloq. â€”n. (pl. -s) psychopath. â€”adj. Psychopathic. [abbreviation]'),
(29666, 'p', '\r\nPsycho-', ' comb. Form of the mind or psychology. [greek: related to *psychic]'),
(29667, 'p', '\r\nPsychoanalysis', ' n. Treatment of mental disorders by bringing repressed fears and conflicts into the conscious mind over a long course of interviews.  psychoanalyse v. (-sing). Psychoanalyst n. Psychoanalytic adj. Psychoanalytical adj.'),
(29668, 'p', '\r\nPsychokinesis', ' n. Movement of objects supposedly by telepathy or mental effort.'),
(29669, 'p', '\r\nPsychological', ' adj. 1 of or arising in the mind. 2 of psychology. 3 colloq. (of an ailment etc.) Imaginary (her cold is psychological).  psychologically adv.'),
(29670, 'p', '\r\nPsychological', 'block  n. Mental inhibition caused by emotional factors.'),
(29671, 'p', '\r\nPsychological', 'moment  n. Best time for achieving a particular effect or purpose.'),
(29672, 'p', '\r\nPsychological', 'warfare  n. Campaign directed at reducing enemy morale.'),
(29673, 'p', '\r\nPsychology', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 the study of the human mind. 2 treatise on or theory of this. 3 a mental characteristics etc. Of a person or group. B mental aspects of an activity, situation, etc. (psychology of crime).  psychologist n.'),
(29674, 'p', '\r\nPsychopath', ' n. 1 mentally deranged person, esp. Showing abnormal or violent social behaviour. 2 mentally or emotionally unstable person.  psychopathic adj.'),
(29675, 'p', '\r\nPsychopathology', ' n. 1 the study of mental disorders. 2 mentally or behaviourally disordered state.'),
(29676, 'p', '\r\nPsychopathy', ' n. Psychopathic or psychologically abnormal behaviour.'),
(29677, 'p', '\r\nPsychosis', ' n. (pl. -choses) severe mental disorder with loss of contact with reality. [greek: related to *psyche]'),
(29678, 'p', '\r\nPsychosomatic', ' adj. 1 (of a bodily disorder) mental, not physical, in origin. 2 of the mind and body together.'),
(29679, 'p', '\r\nPsychotherapy', ' n. Treatment of mental disorder by psychological means.  psychotherapeutic adj. Psychotherapist n.'),
(29680, 'p', '\r\nPsychotic', ' â€”adj. Of or suffering from a psychosis. â€”n. Psychotic person.'),
(29681, 'p', '\r\nPt', ' abbr. Physical training.'),
(29682, 'p', '\r\nPt', ' symb.  Platinum.'),
(29683, 'p', '\r\nPt', ' abbr. 1 part. 2 pint. 3 point. 4 naut. Port.'),
(29684, 'p', '\r\nPta', ' abbr. Parent-teacher association.'),
(29685, 'p', '\r\nPtarmigan', ' n. Game-bird with a grouselike appearance. [gaelic]'),
(29686, 'p', '\r\nPte.', ' Abbr. Private (soldier).'),
(29687, 'p', '\r\nPte.', ' Abbr. Private (soldier).'),
(29688, 'p', '\r\nPteridophyte', ' n. Flowerless plant, e.g. Ferns, club-mosses, etc. [greek pteris fern]'),
(29689, 'p', '\r\nPterodactyl', ' n. Large extinct flying reptile. [greek pteron wing, *dactyl]'),
(29690, 'p', '\r\nPterosaur', ' n. Flying reptile with large batlike wings. [greek pteron wing, saura lizard]'),
(29691, 'p', '\r\nPto', ' abbr. Please turn over.'),
(29692, 'p', '\r\nPtolemaic', ' adj. Hist. Of ptolemy or his theories. [greek ptolemaios, name of a 2nd-c. Astronomer]'),
(29693, 'p', '\r\nPtolemaic', 'system  n. Theory that the earth is the stationary centre of the universe.'),
(29694, 'p', '\r\nPtomaine', ' n. Any of various esp. Toxic amine compounds in putrefying matter.  [greek ptoma corpse]'),
(29695, 'p', '\r\nPu', ' symb.  Plutonium.'),
(29696, 'p', '\r\nPub', ' n. Colloq. Public house. [abbreviation]'),
(29697, 'p', '\r\nPub-crawl', ' n.  Colloq. Drinking tour of several pubs.'),
(29698, 'p', '\r\nPuberty', ' n. Period of sexual maturation.  pubertal adj. [latin puber adult]'),
(29699, 'p', '\r\nPubes1', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 lower part of the abdomen at the front of the pelvis. 2 colloq. Pubic hair. [latin]'),
(29700, 'p', '\r\nPubes2', ' pl. Of *pubis.'),
(29701, 'p', '\r\nPubescence', ' n. 1 beginning of puberty. 2  soft down on plants, or on animals, esp. Insects.  pubescent adj. [latin: related to *pubes1]'),
(29702, 'p', '\r\nPubic', ' adj. Of the pubes or pubis.'),
(29703, 'p', '\r\nPubis', ' n. (pl. Pubes) either of a pair of bones forming the two sides of the pelvis. [latin os pubis bone of the *pubes1]'),
(29704, 'p', '\r\nPublic', ' â€”adj. 1 of the people as a whole (public holiday). 2 open to or shared by all (public baths). 3 done or existing openly (public apology). 4 (of a service, funds, etc.) Provided by or concerning government (public money; public records). 5 of or involved in the affairs, esp. The government or entertainment, of the community (distinguished public career; public figures). â€”n. 1 (as sing. Or pl.) Community, or members of it, in general. 2 specified section of the community (reading public; my public).  go public 1 become a public company. 2 reveal one''s plans etc. In public openly, publicly.  publicly adv.  [latin]'),
(29705, 'p', '\r\nPublic-address', 'system  n. Set of loudspeakers, microphones, amplifiers, etc., used in addressing large audiences.'),
(29706, 'p', '\r\nPublican', ' n.  Keeper of a public house. [latin: related to *public]'),
(29707, 'p', '\r\nPublication', ' n. 1 a preparation and issuing of a book, newspaper, etc. To the public. B book etc. So issued. 2 making something publicly known. [latin: related to *public]'),
(29708, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'bar  n. Less or least expensive bar in a public house.'),
(29709, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'company  n.  Company that sells shares on the open market.'),
(29710, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'convenience  n. Public lavatory.'),
(29711, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'enemy  n. Notorious wanted criminal.'),
(29712, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'figure  n. Famous person.'),
(29713, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'health  n. Provision of adequate sanitation, drainage, etc. By government.'),
(29714, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'house  n. Inn providing alcoholic drinks for consumption on the premises.'),
(29715, 'p', '\r\nPublicist', ' n. Publicity agent or public relations officer.'),
(29716, 'p', '\r\nPublicity', ' n. 1 public exposure. 2 a advertising. B material used for this. [french: related to *public]'),
(29717, 'p', '\r\nPublicize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) advertise; make publicly known.'),
(29718, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'lending right  n. Right of authors to payment when their books etc. Are lent by public libraries.'),
(29719, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'opinion  n. Views, esp. Moral, that are generally prevalent.'),
(29720, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'ownership  n. State ownership of the means of production, distribution, or exchange.'),
(29721, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'prosecutor  n. Law officer acting on behalf of the state or in the public interest.'),
(29722, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'relations officer  n. Person employed to promote public relations.'),
(29723, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'relations  n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Professional promotion of a favourable public image, esp. By a company, famous person, etc.'),
(29724, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'school  n. 1 private fee-paying secondary school, esp. For boarders. 2 us, austral., & scot. Non-fee-paying school.'),
(29725, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'sector  n. State-controlled part of an economy, industry, etc.'),
(29726, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'servant  n. State official.'),
(29727, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'spirit  n. Willingness to engage in community action.  public-spirited adj.'),
(29728, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'transport  n. Buses, trains, etc., charging set fares and running on fixed routes.'),
(29729, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'utility  n. Organization supplying water, gas, etc. To the community.'),
(29730, 'p', '\r\nPublic', 'works  n.pl. Building operations etc. Done by or for the state.'),
(29731, 'p', '\r\nPublish', ' v. 1 (also absol.) Prepare and issue (a book, newspaper, etc.) For public sale. 2 make generally known. 3 announce formally. [latin: related to *public]'),
(29732, 'p', '\r\nPublisher', ' n. Person or (esp.) Company that publishes books etc. For sale.'),
(29733, 'p', '\r\nPuce', ' adj. & n. Dark red or purple-brown. [latin pulex flea]'),
(29734, 'p', '\r\nPuck1', ' n. Rubber disc used as a ball in ice hockey. [origin unknown]'),
(29735, 'p', '\r\nPuck2', ' n. Mischievous or evil sprite.  puckish adj. Puckishly adv. Puckishness n. [old english]'),
(29736, 'p', '\r\nPucker', ' â€”v. (often foll. By up) gather into wrinkles, folds, or bulges (this seam is puckered up). â€”n. Such a wrinkle, bulge, fold, etc. [origin unknown]'),
(29737, 'p', '\r\nPud', ' n. Colloq. = *pudding. [abbreviation]'),
(29738, 'p', '\r\nPudding', ' n. 1 a any of various sweet cooked dishes (rice pudding). B savoury dish containing flour, suet, etc. (steak and kidney pudding). C sweet course of a meal. D any of various sausages stuffed with oatmeal, spices, blood, etc. (black pudding). 2 colloq. Plump, stupid, or lazy person.  puddingy adj. [latin botellus sausage]'),
(29739, 'p', '\r\nPuddle', ' â€”n. 1 small pool, esp. Of rainwater. 2 clay and sand worked with water used as a watertight covering for embankments etc. â€”v. (-ling) 1 knead (clay and sand) into puddle. 2  stir (molten iron) to produce wrought iron by expelling carbon.  puddly adj. [old english]'),
(29740, 'p', '\r\nPudendum', ' n. (pl. Pudenda) (usu. In pl.) Genitals, esp. Of a woman. [latin pudeo be ashamed]'),
(29741, 'p', '\r\nPudgy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. (esp. Of a person) plump, podgy.  pudginess n. [cf. *podgy]'),
(29742, 'p', '\r\nPuerile', ' adj. Childish, immature.  puerility n. (pl. -ies). [latin puer boy]'),
(29743, 'p', '\r\nPuerperal', ' adj. Of or caused by childbirth. [latin puer boy, pario bear]'),
(29744, 'p', '\r\nPuerperal', 'fever  n. Fever following childbirth and caused by uterine infection.'),
(29745, 'p', '\r\nPuff', ' â€”n. 1 a short quick blast of breath or wind. B sound of or like this. C small quantity of vapour, smoke, etc., emitted in one blast (puff of smoke). 2 light pastry cake containing jam, cream, etc. 3 gathered material in a dress etc. (puff sleeve). 4 extravagantly enthusiastic review, advertisement, etc., esp. In a newspaper. 5 = *powder-puff. â€”v. 1 emit a puff of air or breath; blow with short blasts. 2 (usu. Foll. By away, out, etc.) Emit or move with puffs (puffing away at his cigar; train puffed out). 3 (usu. In passive; often foll. By out) colloq. Put out of breath (arrived puffed). 4 breathe hard; pant. 5 (usu. Foll. By up, out) inflate; swell (his eye was puffed up). 6 (usu. Foll. By out, up, away) blow or emit (dust, smoke, etc.) With a puff. 7 smoke (a pipe etc.) In puffs. 8 (usu. As puffed up adj.) Elate; make proud or boastful. 9 advertise or promote with exaggerated or false praise.  puff up = sense 8 of v. [imitative]'),
(29746, 'p', '\r\nPuff-adder', ' n. Large venomous african viper which inflates the upper part of its body.'),
(29747, 'p', '\r\nPuffball', ' n. Ball-shaped fungus emitting clouds of spores.'),
(29748, 'p', '\r\nPuffin', ' n. N. Atlantic and n. Pacific sea bird with a large head and brightly coloured triangular bill. [origin unknown]'),
(29749, 'p', '\r\nPuff', 'pastry  n. Leaved pastry made light and flaky by rolling and folding the dough many times.'),
(29750, 'p', '\r\nPuffy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 swollen, puffed out. 2 colloq. Short-winded.  puffily adv. Puffiness n.'),
(29751, 'p', '\r\nPug', ' n. (in full pug-dog) dog of a dwarf breed with a broad flat nose and wrinkled face. [origin unknown]'),
(29752, 'p', '\r\nPugilist', ' n. (esp. Professional) boxer.  pugilism n. Pugilistic adj. [latin pugil boxer]'),
(29753, 'p', '\r\nPugnacious', ' adj. Quarrelsome; disposed to fight.  pugnaciously adv. Pugnacity n. [latin pugnax -acis from pugno fight]'),
(29754, 'p', '\r\nPug-nose', ' n. Short squat or snub nose.  pug-nosed adj.'),
(29755, 'p', '\r\nPuissance', ' n. Competitive jumping of large obstacles in showjumping. [french: related to *puissant]'),
(29756, 'p', '\r\nPuissant', ' adj. Literary or archaic powerful; mighty. [romanic: related to *potent]'),
(29757, 'p', '\r\nPuke', ' v. & n. (-king) slang vomit.  pukey adj. [imitative]'),
(29758, 'p', '\r\nPukka', ' adj. Anglo-ind. Colloq. 1 genuine. 2 of good quality; reliable (a pukka job). [hindi]'),
(29759, 'p', '\r\nPulchritude', ' n. Literary beauty.  pulchritudinous adj. [latin pulcher beautiful]'),
(29760, 'p', '\r\nPule', ' v. (-ling) literary cry querulously or weakly; whimper. [imitative]'),
(29761, 'p', '\r\nPull', ' â€”v. 1 exert force upon (a thing, person, etc.) To move it to oneself or the origin of the force (pulled it nearer). 2 exert a pulling force (engine will not pull). 3 extract (a cork or tooth) by pulling. 4 damage (a muscle etc.) By abnormal strain. 5 a move (a boat) by pulling on the oars. B (of a boat etc.) Be caused to move, esp. In a specified direction. 6 (often foll. By up) proceed with effort (up a hill etc.). 7 (foll. By on) bring out (a weapon) for use against (a person). 8 check the speed of (a horse), esp. To lose a race. 9 attract (custom or support). 10 draw (liquor) from a barrel etc. 11 (foll. By at) tear or pluck at. 12 (often foll. By on, at) inhale or drink deeply; draw or suck (on a pipe etc.). 13 (often foll. By up) remove (a plant) by the root. 14 a cricket strike (the ball) to the leg side. B golf strike (the ball) widely to the left. 15 print (a proof etc.). 16 slang succeed in attracting sexually. â€”n. 1 act of pulling. 2 force exerted by this. 3 influence; advantage. 4 attraction or attention-getter. 5 deep draught of liquor. 6 prolonged effort, e.g. In going up a hill. 7 handle etc. For applying a pull. 8 printer''s rough proof. 9 cricket & golf pulling stroke. 10 suck at a cigarette.  pull about 1 treat roughly. 2 pull from side to side. Pull apart (or to pieces) = take to pieces (see *piece). Pull back (cause to) retreat. Pull down 1 demolish (esp. A building). 2 humiliate. Pull a face distort the features, grimace. Pull a fast one see *fast1. Pull in 1 (of a bus, train, etc.) Arrive to take passengers. 2 (of a vehicle) move to the side of or off the road. 3 colloq. Earn or acquire. 4 colloq. Arrest. Pull a person''s leg deceive playfully. Pull off 1 remove by pulling. 2 succeed in achieving or winning. Pull oneself together recover control of oneself. Pull the other one colloq. Expressing disbelief (with ref. To pull a person''s leg). Pull out 1 take out by pulling. 2 depart. 3 withdraw from an undertaking. 4 (of a bus, train, etc.) Leave a station, stop, etc. 5 (of a vehicle) move out from the side of the road, or to overtake. Pull over (of a vehicle) pull in. Pull one''s punches avoid using one''s full force. Pull the plug on put an end to (by withdrawing resources etc.). Pull rank take unfair advantage of one''s seniority. Pull round (or through) (cause to) recover from an illness. Pull strings exert (esp. Clandestine) influence. Pull together work in harmony. Pull up 1 (cause to) stop moving. 2 pull out of the ground. 3 reprimand. 4 check oneself. Pull one''s weight (often refl.) Do one''s fair share of work. [old english]'),
(29762, 'p', '\r\nPullet', ' n. Young hen, esp. One less than one year old. [latin pullus]'),
(29763, 'p', '\r\nPulley', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 grooved wheel or wheels for a cord etc. To pass over, set in a block and used for changing the direction of a force. 2 wheel or drum fixed on a shaft and turned by a belt, used esp. To increase speed or power. [french polie: related to *pole2]'),
(29764, 'p', '\r\nPull-in', ' n. Roadside cafÃ© or other stopping-place.'),
(29765, 'p', '\r\nPullman', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 luxurious railway carriage or motor coach. 2 sleeping-car. [pullman, name of the designer]'),
(29766, 'p', '\r\nPull-out', ' n. Removable section of a magazine etc.'),
(29767, 'p', '\r\nPullover', ' n. Knitted garment put on over the head and covering the top half of the body.'),
(29768, 'p', '\r\nPullulate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (of a seed, shoot, etc.) Bud, sprout. 2 swarm, teem. 3 develop; spring up. 4 (foll. By with) abound.  pullulation n. [latin pullulo sprout]'),
(29769, 'p', '\r\nPulmonary', ' adj. 1 of the lungs. 2 having lungs or lunglike organs. 3 affected with or susceptible to lung disease. [latin pulmo -onis lung]'),
(29770, 'p', '\r\nPulp', ' â€”n. 1 soft fleshy part of fruit etc. 2 soft thick wet mass, esp. From rags, wood, etc., used in paper-making. 3 (often attrib.) Cheap fiction etc., orig. Printed on rough paper. â€”v. Reduce to or become pulp.  pulpy adj. Pulpiness n. [latin]'),
(29771, 'p', '\r\nPulpit', ' n. 1 raised enclosed platform in a church etc. From which the preacher delivers a sermon. 2 (prec. By the) preachers collectively; preaching. [latin pulpitum platform]'),
(29772, 'p', '\r\nPulpwood', ' n. Timber suitable for making paper-pulp.'),
(29773, 'p', '\r\nPulsar', ' n. Cosmic source of regular rapid pulses of radiation, e.g. A rotating neutron star. [from pulsating star, after quasar]'),
(29774, 'p', '\r\nPulsate', ' v. (-ting) 1 expand and contract rhythmically; throb. 2 vibrate, quiver, thrill.  pulsation n. Pulsatory adj. [latin: related to *pulse1]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(29775, 'p', '\r\nPulse1', ' â€”n. 1 a rhythmical throbbing of the arteries as blood is propelled through them, esp. In the wrists, temples, etc. B each beat of the arteries or heart. 2 throb or thrill of life or emotion. 3 general feeling or opinion. 4 single vibration of sound, electric current, light, etc., esp. As a signal. 5 rhythmical beat, esp. Of music. â€”v. (-sing) pulsate. [latin pello puls- drive, beat]'),
(29776, 'p', '\r\nPulse2', ' n. (as sing. Or pl.) 1 edible seeds of various leguminous plants, e.g. Chick-peas, lentils, beans, etc. 2 plant producing these. [latin puls]'),
(29777, 'p', '\r\nPulverize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 reduce or crumble to fine particles or dust. 2 colloq. Demolish, defeat utterly.  pulverization n. [latin pulvis -ver- dust]'),
(29778, 'p', '\r\nPuma', ' n. Wild american greyish-brown cat. [spanish from quechua]'),
(29779, 'p', '\r\nPumice', ' n. (in full pumice-stone) 1 light porous volcanic rock used in cleaning or polishing. 2 piece of this used for removing hard skin etc. [latin pumex pumic-]'),
(29780, 'p', '\r\nPummel', ' v. (-ll-; us -l-) strike repeatedly, esp. With the fists. [from *pommel]'),
(29781, 'p', '\r\nPump1', ' â€”n. 1 machine or device for raising or moving liquids, compressing gases, inflating tyres, etc. 2 act of pumping; stroke of a pump. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By in, out, into, up, etc.) Raise or remove (liquid, gas, etc.) With a pump. 2 (often foll. By up) fill (a tyre etc.) With air. 3 remove (water etc.) With a pump. 4 work a pump. 5 (often foll. By out) (cause to) move, pour forth, etc., as if by pumping. 6 persistently question (a person) to obtain information. 7 a move vigorously up and down. B shake (a person''s hand) effusively.  pump iron colloq. Exercise with weights. [origin uncertain]'),
(29782, 'p', '\r\nPump2', ' n. 1 plimsoll. 2 light dancing shoe. 3 us court shoe. [origin unknown]'),
(29783, 'p', '\r\nPumpernickel', ' n. German wholemeal rye bread. [german]'),
(29784, 'p', '\r\nPumpkin', ' n. 1 large rounded yellow or orange fruit cooked as a vegetable. 2 large-leaved tendrilled plant bearing this. [greek pepon melon]'),
(29785, 'p', '\r\nPun', ' â€”n. Humorous use of a word or words with two or more meanings; play on words. â€”v. (-nn-) (foll. By on; also absol.) Make a pun or puns with (words). [origin unknown]'),
(29786, 'p', '\r\nPunch1', ' â€”v. 1 strike, esp. With a closed fist. 2 a pierce a hole in (metal, paper, etc.) As or with a punch. B pierce (a hole) thus. â€”n. 1 blow with a fist. 2 ability to deliver this. 3 colloq. Vigour, momentum; effective force. 4 tool, machine, or device for punching holes or impressing a design in leather, metal, etc.  puncher n. [var. Of pounce emboss]'),
(29787, 'p', '\r\nPunch2', ' n. Drink of wine or spirits mixed with water, fruit juices, spices, etc., and usu. Served hot. [origin unknown]'),
(29788, 'p', '\r\nPunch3', ' n. (punch) grotesque humpbacked puppet in punch and judy shows.  as pleased as punch extremely pleased. [abbreviation of punchinello, name of the chief character in an italian puppet-show]'),
(29789, 'p', '\r\nPunchball', ' n. Stuffed or inflated ball on a stand for punching as exercise or training.'),
(29790, 'p', '\r\nPunch-bowl', ' n. 1 bowl for punch. 2 deep round hollow in a hill.'),
(29791, 'p', '\r\nPunch', 'card  n. (also punched card or tape) card etc. Perforated according to a code, for conveying instructions or data to a data processor etc.'),
(29792, 'p', '\r\nPunch-drunk', ' adj. Stupefied from or as if from a series of heavy blows.'),
(29793, 'p', '\r\nPunch-line', ' n. Words giving the point of a joke or story.'),
(29794, 'p', '\r\nPunch-up', ' n. Colloq. Fist-fight; brawl.'),
(29795, 'p', '\r\nPunchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) vigorous; forceful.'),
(29796, 'p', '\r\nPunctilio', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 delicate point of ceremony or honour. 2 etiquette of such points. 3 petty formality. [italian and spanish: related to *point]'),
(29797, 'p', '\r\nPunctilious', ' adj. 1 attentive to formality or etiquette. 2 precise in behaviour.  punctiliously adv. Punctiliousness n. [italian: related to *punctilio]'),
(29798, 'p', '\r\nPunctual', ' adj. Keeping to the appointed time; prompt.  punctuality n. Punctually adv. [medieval latin: related to *point]'),
(29799, 'p', '\r\nPunctuate', ' v. (-ting) 1 insert punctuation marks in. 2 interrupt at intervals (punctuated his tale with heavy sighs). [medieval latin: related to *punctual]'),
(29800, 'p', '\r\nPunctuation', ' n. 1 system of marks used to punctuate a written passage. 2 use of, or skill in using, these.'),
(29801, 'p', '\r\nPunctuation', 'mark  n. Any of the marks (e.g. Full stop and comma) used in writing to separate sentences etc. And clarify meaning.'),
(29802, 'p', '\r\nPuncture', ' â€”n. 1 prick or pricking, esp. The accidental piercing of a pneumatic tyre. 2 hole made in this way. â€”v. (-ring) 1 make or undergo a puncture (in). 2 prick, pierce, or deflate (pomposity etc.). [latin punctura: related to *point]'),
(29803, 'p', '\r\nPundit', ' n. 1 (also pandit) learned hindu. 2 often iron. Expert.  punditry n. [hindustani from sanskrit]'),
(29804, 'p', '\r\nPungent', ' adj. 1 sharp or strong in taste or smell, esp. Producing a smarting or pricking sensation. 2 (of remarks) penetrating, biting, caustic. 3 mentally stimulating.  pungency n. [latin: related to *point]'),
(29805, 'p', '\r\nPunish', ' v. 1 inflict retribution on (an offender) or for (an offence). 2 colloq. Inflict severe blows on (an opponent). 3 tax, abuse, or treat severely or improperly.  punishable adj. Punishing adj. [latin punio]'),
(29806, 'p', '\r\nPunishment', ' n. 1 punishing or being punished. 2 loss or suffering inflicted in this. 3 colloq. Severe treatment or suffering.'),
(29807, 'p', '\r\nPunitive', ' adj. 1 inflicting or intended to inflict punishment. 2 (of taxation etc.) Extremely severe. [french or medieval latin: related to *punish]'),
(29808, 'p', '\r\nPunjabi', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 native of punjab. 2 language of punjab. â€”adj. Of punjab, its people, or language. [punjab, state in india and province in pakistan]'),
(29809, 'p', '\r\nPunk', ' n. 1 a (in full punk rock) anti-establishment and deliberately outrageous style of rock music. B (in full punk rocker) devotee of this. 2 esp. Us young hooligan or petty criminal; lout. 3 soft crumbly fungus-infested wood used as tinder. [origin unknown]'),
(29810, 'p', '\r\nPunkah', ' n. Large swinging cloth fan on a frame, worked by a cord or electrically. [hindi]'),
(29811, 'p', '\r\nPunnet', ' n. Small light basket or container for fruit or vegetables. [origin unknown]'),
(29812, 'p', '\r\nPunster', ' n. Person who makes puns, esp. Habitually.'),
(29813, 'p', '\r\nPunt1', ' â€”n. Square-ended flat-bottomed pleasure boat propelled by a long pole. â€”v. 1 propel (a punt) with a pole. 2 travel or convey in a punt.  punter n. [low german or dutch]'),
(29814, 'p', '\r\nPunt2', ' â€”v. Kick (a ball, esp. In rugby) after it has dropped from the hands and before it reaches the ground. â€”n. Such a kick. [origin unknown]'),
(29815, 'p', '\r\nPunt3', ' v. 1 colloq. A bet on a horse etc. B speculate in shares etc. 2 (in some card-games) lay a stake against the bank. [french ponter]'),
(29816, 'p', '\r\nPunt4', ' n. Chief monetary unit of the republic of ireland. [irish, = pound]'),
(29817, 'p', '\r\nPunter', ' n. Colloq. 1 person who gambles or lays a bet. 2 a customer or client; member of an audience. B prostitute''s client.'),
(29818, 'p', '\r\nPuny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 undersized. 2 weak, feeble. [french puisnÃ© born afterwards]'),
(29819, 'p', '\r\nPup', ' â€”n. Young dog, wolf, rat, seal, etc. â€”v. (-pp-) (also absol.) (of a bitch etc.) Bring forth (young). [from *puppy]'),
(29820, 'p', '\r\nPupa', ' n. (pl. Pupae) insect in the stage between larva and imago.  pupal adj. [latin, = doll]'),
(29821, 'p', '\r\nPupil1', ' n. Person taught by another, esp. A schoolchild or student. [latin pupillus, -illa diminutives of pupus boy, pupa girl]'),
(29822, 'p', '\r\nPupil2', ' n. Dark circular opening in the centre of the iris of the eye. [related to *pupil1]'),
(29823, 'p', '\r\nPuppet', ' n. 1 small figure moved esp. By strings as entertainment. 2 person controlled by another.  puppetry n. [var. Of *poppet]'),
(29824, 'p', '\r\nPuppet', 'state  n. Country that is nominally independent but actually under the control of another power.'),
(29825, 'p', '\r\nPuppy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 young dog. 2 conceited or arrogant young man. [french: related to *poppet]'),
(29826, 'p', '\r\nPuppy-fat', ' n. Temporary fatness of a child or adolescent.'),
(29827, 'p', '\r\nPuppy', 'love  n. = *calf-love.'),
(29828, 'p', '\r\nPurblind', ' adj. 1 partly blind; dim-sighted. 2 obtuse, dim-witted.  purblindness n. [from pur(e) (= â€˜utterlyâ€™) blind]'),
(29829, 'p', '\r\nPurchase', ' â€”v. (-sing) 1 buy. 2 (often foll. By with) obtain or achieve at some cost. â€”n. 1 buying. 2 thing bought. 3 a firm hold to prevent slipping; leverage. B device or tackle for moving heavy objects. 4 annual rent or return from land.  purchaser n. [anglo-french: related to *pro-1, *chase1]'),
(29830, 'p', '\r\nPurdah', ' n. Ind. Screening of women from strangers by a veil or curtain in some muslim and hindu societies. [urdu]'),
(29831, 'p', '\r\nPure', ' adj. 1 unmixed, unadulterated (pure white; pure malice). 2 of unmixed origin or descent (pure-blooded). 3 chaste. 4 not morally corrupt. 5 guiltless. 6 sincere. 7 (of a sound) perfectly in tune. 8 (of a subject of study) abstract, not applied.  pureness n. [latin purus]'),
(29832, 'p', '\r\nPurÃ©e', ' â€”n. Smooth pulp of vegetables or fruit etc. â€”v. (-Ã©es, -Ã©ed) make a purÃ©e of. [french]'),
(29833, 'p', '\r\nPurely', ' adv. 1 in a pure manner. 2 merely, solely, exclusively.'),
(29834, 'p', '\r\nPurgative', ' â€”adj. 1 serving to purify. 2 strongly laxative. â€”n. 1 purgative thing. 2 laxative. [latin: related to *purge]'),
(29835, 'p', '\r\nPurgatory', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 rc ch. Supposed place or state of expiation of petty sins after death and before entering heaven. 2 place or state of temporary suffering or expiation. â€”adj. Purifying.  purgatorial adj. [medieval latin: related to *purge]'),
(29836, 'p', '\r\nPurge', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 (often foll. By of, from) make physically or spiritually clean. 2 remove by cleansing. 3 rid (an organization, party, etc.) Of unacceptable members. 4 a empty (the bowels). B empty the bowels of (a person). 5 law atone for (an offence, esp. Contempt of court). â€”n. 1 act of purging. 2 purgative. [latin purgo purify]'),
(29837, 'p', '\r\nPurify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 clear of extraneous elements; make pure. 2 (often foll. By of, from) make ceremonially pure or clean.  purification n. Purificatory adj. Purifier n.'),
(29838, 'p', '\r\nPurist', ' n. Advocate of scrupulous purity, esp. In language or art.  purism n. Puristic adj.'),
(29839, 'p', '\r\nPuritan', ' â€”n. 1 (puritan) hist. Member of a group of english protestants who sought to simplify and regulate forms of worship after the reformation. 2 purist member of any party. 3 strict observer of religion or morals. â€”adj. 1 (puritan) hist. Of the puritans. 2 scrupulous and austere in religion or morals.  puritanism n. [latin: related to *pure]'),
(29840, 'p', '\r\nPuritanical', ' adj. Strictly religious or moral in behaviour.  puritanically adv.'),
(29841, 'p', '\r\nPurity', ' n. Pureness, cleanness.'),
(29842, 'p', '\r\nPurl1', ' â€”n. 1 knitting stitch made by putting the needle through the front of the previous stitch and passing the yarn round the back of the needle. 2 chain of minute loops decorating the edges of lace etc. â€”v. (also absol.) Knit with a purl stitch. [origin unknown]'),
(29843, 'p', '\r\nPurl2', ' v. (of a brook etc.) Flow with a babbling sound. [imitative]'),
(29844, 'p', '\r\nPurler', ' n. Colloq. Headlong fall. [purl overturn]'),
(29845, 'p', '\r\nPurlieu', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 person''s bounds, limits, or usual haunts. 2 hist. Tract on the border of a forest. 3 (in pl.) Outskirts, outlying region. [anglo-french puralÃ© from aller go]'),
(29846, 'p', '\r\nPurlin', ' n. Horizontal beam along the length of a roof. [anglo-latin perlio]'),
(29847, 'p', '\r\nPurloin', ' v. Formal or joc. Steal, pilfer. [anglo-french purloigner from loign far]'),
(29848, 'p', '\r\nPurple', ' â€”n. 1 colour between red and blue. 2 (in full tyrian purple) crimson dye obtained from some molluscs. 3 purple robe, esp. Of an emperor or senior magistrate. 4 scarlet official dress of a cardinal. 5 (prec. By the) position of rank, authority, or privilege. â€”adj. Of a purple colour. â€”v. (-ling) make or become purple.  purplish adj. [greek porphura, a shellfish yielding dye]'),
(29849, 'p', '\r\nPurple', 'heart  n. Colloq. Heart-shaped stimulant tablet, esp. Of amphetamine.'),
(29850, 'p', '\r\nPurple', 'passage  n. (also purple patch) ornate or elaborate literary passage.'),
(29851, 'p', '\r\nPurport', ' â€”v. 1 profess; be intended to seem (purports to be an officer). 2 (often foll. By that) (of a document or speech) have as its meaning; state. â€”n. 1 ostensible meaning. 2 sense or tenor (of a document or statement).  purportedly adv. [latin: related to *pro-1, porto carry]'),
(29852, 'p', '\r\nPurpose', ' â€”n. 1 object to be attained; thing intended. 2 intention to act. 3 resolution, determination. â€”v. (-sing) have as one''s purpose; design, intend.  on purpose intentionally. To no purpose with no result or effect. To the purpose 1 relevant. 2 useful. [latin propono *propose]'),
(29853, 'p', '\r\nPurpose-built', ' adj. (also purpose-made) built or made for a specific purpose.'),
(29854, 'p', '\r\nPurposeful', ' adj. 1 having or indicating purpose. 2 intentional. 3 resolute.  purposefully adv. Purposefulness n.'),
(29855, 'p', '\r\nPurposeless', ' adj. Having no aim or plan.'),
(29856, 'p', '\r\nPurposely', ' adv. On purpose.'),
(29857, 'p', '\r\nPurpose-made', ' var. Of *purpose-built.'),
(29858, 'p', '\r\nPurposive', ' adj. 1 having, serving, or done with a purpose. 2 purposeful; resolute.'),
(29859, 'p', '\r\nPurr', ' â€”v. 1 (of a cat) make a low vibratory sound expressing contentment. 2  (of machinery etc.) Run smoothly and quietly. 3 (of a person) express pleasure; utter purringly. â€”n. Purring sound. [imitative]'),
(29860, 'p', '\r\nPurse', ' â€”n. 1 small pouch for carrying money on the person. 2 us handbag. 3 money, funds. 4 sum as a present or prize in a contest. â€”v. (-sing) 1 (often foll. By up) pucker or contract (the lips etc.). 2 become wrinkled.  hold the purse-strings have control of expenditure. [greek, = leather bag]'),
(29861, 'p', '\r\nPurser', ' n. Officer on a ship who keeps the accounts, esp. The head steward in a passenger vessel.'),
(29862, 'p', '\r\nPursuance', ' n. (foll. By of) carrying out or observance (of a plan, idea, etc.).'),
(29863, 'p', '\r\nPursuant', ' adv. (foll. By to) in accordance with. [french: related to *pursue]'),
(29864, 'p', '\r\nPursue', ' v. (-sues, -sued, -suing) 1 follow with intent to overtake, capture, or do harm to; go in pursuit. 2 continue or proceed along (a route or course of action). 3 follow or engage in (study or other activity). 4 proceed according to (a plan etc.). 5 seek after, aim at. 6 continue to investigate or discuss (a topic). 7 importune (a person) persistently. 8 (of misfortune etc.) Persistently assail.  pursuer n. [latin sequor follow]'),
(29865, 'p', '\r\nPursuit', ' n. 1 act of pursuing. 2 occupation or activity pursued.  in pursuit of pursuing. [french: related to *suit]'),
(29866, 'p', '\r\nPursuivant', ' n.  Officer of the college of arms below a herald. [french: related to *pursue]'),
(29867, 'p', '\r\nPurulent', ' adj. Of, containing, or discharging pus.  purulence n. [latin: related to *pus]'),
(29868, 'p', '\r\nPurvey', ' v. Provide or supply (food etc.) As one''s business.  purveyor n. [latin: related to *provide]'),
(29869, 'p', '\r\nPurview', ' n. 1 scope or range of a document, scheme, etc. 2 range of physical or mental vision. [anglo-french past part.: related to *purvey]'),
(29870, 'p', '\r\nPus', ' n. Thick yellowish or greenish liquid produced from infected tissue. [latin pus puris]'),
(29871, 'p', '\r\nPush', ' â€”v. 1 exert a force on (a thing) to move it or cause it to move away. 2 exert such a force (do not push against the door). 3 a thrust forward or upward. B (cause to) project (pushes out new roots). 4 move forward or make (one''s way) by force or persistence. 5 exert oneself, esp. To surpass others. 6 (often foll. By to, into, or to + infin.) Urge, impel, or press (a person) hard; harass. 7 (often foll.'),
(29872, 'p', '\r\nPush-button', ' n. 1 button to be pushed, esp. To operate an electrical device. 2 (attrib.) Operated thus.'),
(29873, 'p', '\r\nPushchair', ' n. Folding chair on wheels, for pushing a young child along in.'),
(29874, 'p', '\r\nPusher', ' n. Colloq. Seller of illegal drugs.'),
(29875, 'p', '\r\nPushful', ' adj. Pushy; arrogant.  pushfully adv.'),
(29876, 'p', '\r\nPushing', ' adj. 1 pushy. 2 predic. Colloq. Having nearly reached (a specified age).'),
(29877, 'p', '\r\nPushover', ' n. Colloq. 1 something easily done. 2 person easily persuaded, defeated, etc.'),
(29878, 'p', '\r\nPush-start', ' â€”n. Starting of a vehicle by pushing it to turn the engine. â€”v. Start (a vehicle) in this way.'),
(29879, 'p', '\r\nPushtu', ' n. & adj. = *pashto. [persian]'),
(29880, 'p', '\r\nPush-up', ' n. = *press-up.'),
(29881, 'p', '\r\nPushy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Excessively self-assertive.  pushily adv. Pushiness n.'),
(29882, 'p', '\r\nPusillanimous', ' adj. Formal cowardly, timid.  pusillanimity n. [church latin pusillanimis from pusillus very small, animus mind]'),
(29883, 'p', '\r\nPuss', ' n. Colloq. 1 cat (esp. As a form of address). 2 sly or coquettish girl. [low german or dutch]'),
(29884, 'p', '\r\nPussy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 (also pussy-cat) colloq. Cat. 2 coarse slang vulva.'),
(29885, 'p', '\r\nPussyfoot', ' v. Colloq. 1 move stealthily. 2 equivocate; stall.'),
(29886, 'p', '\r\nPussy', 'willow  n. Willow with furry catkins.'),
(29887, 'p', '\r\nPustulate', ' v. (-ting) form into pustules. [latin: related to *pustule]'),
(29888, 'p', '\r\nPustule', ' n. Pimple containing pus.  pustular adj. [latin pustula]'),
(29889, 'p', '\r\nPut', ' â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Put) 1 move to or cause to be in a specified place or position (put it in your pocket; put the children to bed). 2 bring into a specified condition or state (puts me in great difficulty). 3 (often foll. By on, to) impose, enforce, assign, or apply (put a tax on beer; where do you put the blame?; put a stop to it; put it to good use). 4 place (a person) or (refl.) Imagine (oneself) in a specified position (put them at their ease; put yourself in my shoes). 5 (foll. By for) substitute (one thing) for (another). 6 express in a specified way (to put it mildly). 7 (foll. By at) estimate (an amount etc.) At so much (put the cost at Â£50). 8 (foll. By into) express or translate in (words, or another language). 9 (foll. By into) invest (money in an asset, e.g. Land). 10 (foll. By on) stake (money) on (a horse etc.). 11 (foll. By to) submit for attention (put it to a vote). 12 throw (esp. A shot or weight) as a sport. 13 (foll. By back, off, out to sea, etc.) (of a ship etc.) Proceed in a specified direction. â€”n. Throw of the shot etc.  put about 1 spread (information, a rumour, etc.). 2 naut. Turn round; put (a ship) on the opposite tack. Put across 1 communicate (an idea etc.) Effectively. 2 (often in put it (or one) across) achieve by deceit. Put away 1 restore (a thing) to its usual or former place. 2 lay (money etc.) Aside for future use. 3 imprison or commit to a home etc.'),
(29890, 'p', '\r\nPutative', ' adj. Formal reputed, supposed (his putative father). [latin puto think]'),
(29891, 'p', '\r\nPut-down', ' n. Colloq. Snub.'),
(29892, 'p', '\r\nPut-on', ' n. Colloq. Deception or hoax.'),
(29893, 'p', '\r\nPutrefy', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 become or make putrid; go bad. 2 fester, suppurate. 3 become morally corrupt.  putrefaction n. Putrefactive adj. [latin puter putris rotten]'),
(29894, 'p', '\r\nPutrescent', ' adj. Rotting.  putrescence n. [latin: related to *putrid]'),
(29895, 'p', '\r\nPutrid', ' adj. 1 decomposed, rotten. 2 foul, noxious. 3 corrupt. 4 slang of poor quality; contemptible; very unpleasant.  putridity n. [latin putreo rot (v.)]'),
(29896, 'p', '\r\nPutsch', ' n. Attempt at political revolution; violent uprising. [swiss german]'),
(29897, 'p', '\r\nPutt', ' â€”v. (-tt-) strike (a golf ball) gently on a putting-green. â€”n. Putting stroke. [from *put]'),
(29898, 'p', '\r\nPuttee', ' n. Hist. Long strip of cloth wound round the leg from ankle to knee for protection and support, worn esp. By soldiers. [hindi]'),
(29899, 'p', '\r\nPutter1', ' n. Golf club for putting.'),
(29900, 'p', '\r\nPutter2', ' v. (brit. Potter) 1 (often foll. By about, around) work or occupy oneself in a desultory manner. 2 go slowly, dawdle, loiter (pottered up to the pub). [dial. Pote push]'),
(29901, 'p', '\r\nPutting-green', ' n. (in golf) smooth area of grass round a hole.'),
(29902, 'p', '\r\nPutty', ' â€”n. Cement of whiting and linseed oil, used for fixing panes of glass, filling holes, etc. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) cover, fix, join, or fill with putty. [french potÃ©e: related to *pot1]'),
(29903, 'p', '\r\nPut-up', 'job  n. Colloq. Fraudulent scheme.'),
(29904, 'p', '\r\nPuzzle', ' â€”n. 1 difficult or confusing problem. 2 problem or toy designed to test knowledge or ingenuity. â€”v. (-ling) 1 confound or disconcert mentally. 2 (usu. Foll. By over etc.) Be perplexed (about). 3 (usu. As puzzling adj.) Require much mental effort (puzzling situation). 4 (foll. By out) solve or understand by hard thought.  puzzlement n. [origin unknown]'),
(29905, 'p', '\r\nPuzzler', ' n. Difficult question or problem.'),
(29906, 'p', '\r\nPvc', ' abbr. Polyvinyl chloride.'),
(29907, 'p', '\r\nPw', ' abbr. Policewoman.'),
(29908, 'p', '\r\nPwr', ' abbr. Pressurized-water reactor.'),
(29909, 'p', '\r\nPyaemia', ' n. (us pyemia) blood-poisoning caused by pus-forming bacteria in the bloodstream. [greek puon pus, haima blood]'),
(29910, 'p', '\r\nPyemia', ' n. (brit. Pyaemia) blood-poisoning caused by pus-forming bacteria in the bloodstream. [greek puon pus, haima blood]'),
(29911, 'p', '\r\nPygmy', ' n. (also pigmy) (pl. -ies) (often attrib.) 1 member of a dwarf people of esp. Equatorial africa. 2 very small person, animal, or thing. 3 insignificant person. [latin from greek]'),
(29912, 'p', '\r\nPyjamas', ' n.pl. (us pajamas) 1 suit of loose trousers and jacket for sleeping in. 2 loose trousers worn by both sexes in some asian countries. 3 (pyjama) (attrib.) Of either part of a pair of pyjamas (pyjama jacket). [urdu, = leg-clothing]'),
(29913, 'p', '\r\nPylon', ' n. Tall structure, esp. As a support for electric-power cables etc. [greek pule gate]'),
(29914, 'p', '\r\nPyorrhea', ' n. (brit. Pyorrhaea) 1 gum disease causing loosening of the teeth. 2 discharge of pus. [greek puon pus, rheo flow]'),
