(30206, 'r', '\r\nRag1', ' n. 1 torn, frayed, or worn piece of woven material. 2 (in pl.) Old or worn clothes. 3 (collect.) Scraps of cloth used as material for paper, stuffing, etc. 4 derog. Newspaper.  in rags much torn. Rags to riches poverty to affluence. [probably a back-formation from *ragged]'),
(30207, 'r', '\r\nRag2', ' â€”n. 1 fund-raising programme of stunts, parades, and entertainment organized by students. 2 prank. 3 a rowdy celebration. B noisy disorderly scene. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 tease; play rough jokes on. 2 engage in rough play; be noisy and riotous. [origin unknown]'),
(30208, 'r', '\r\nRag3', ' n. Ragtime composition. [abbreviation]'),
(30209, 'r', '\r\nRagamuffin', ' n. Child in ragged dirty clothes. [probably from *rag1]'),
(30210, 'r', '\r\nRag-and-bone', 'man  n. Itinerant dealer in old clothes, furniture, etc.'),
(30211, 'r', '\r\nRag-bag', ' n. 1 bag for scraps of fabric etc. 2 miscellaneous collection.'),
(30212, 'r', '\r\nRag', 'doll  n. Stuffed cloth doll.'),
(30213, 'r', '\r\nRage', ' â€”n. 1 fierce or violent anger. 2 fit of this. 3 violent action of a natural force. â€”v. (-ging) 1 be full of anger. 2 (often foll. By at, against) speak furiously or madly. 3 (of wind, battle, etc.) Be violent; be at its height. 4 (as raging adj.) Extreme, very painful (raging thirst; raging headache).  all the rage very popular, fashionable. [latin *rabies]'),
(30214, 'r', '\r\nRagged', ' adj. 1 torn; frayed. 2 in ragged clothes. 3 with a broken or jagged outline or surface. 4 faulty, imperfect; lacking finish, smoothness, or uniformity. [old norse]'),
(30215, 'r', '\r\nRagged', 'robin  n. Pink-flowered campion with tattered petals.'),
(30216, 'r', '\r\nRaglan', ' â€”adj. (of a sleeve) running up to the neck of a garment. â€”n. (often attrib.) Overcoat without shoulder seams, the sleeves running up to the neck. [lord raglan]'),
(30217, 'r', '\r\nRagout', ' n. Meat stewed with vegetables and highly seasoned. [french]'),
(30218, 'r', '\r\nRagtag', ' n. (in full ragtag and bobtail) derog. Rabble or common people. [from *rag1]'),
(30219, 'r', '\r\nRagtime', ' n. Form of highly syncopated early jazz, esp. For the piano.'),
(30220, 'r', '\r\nRag', 'trade  n. Colloq. The clothing business.'),
(30221, 'r', '\r\nRagwort', ' n. Yellow-flowered ragged-leaved plant.'),
(30222, 'r', '\r\nRaid', ' â€”n. 1 rapid surprise attack, esp.: a in warfare. B in order to commit a crime, steal, or do harm. 2 surprise attack by police etc. To arrest suspected persons or seize illicit goods. â€”v. Make a raid on.  raider n. [scots form of *road]'),
(30223, 'r', '\r\nRail1', ' â€”n. 1 level or sloping bar or series of bars: a used to hang things on. B as the top of banisters. C forming part of a fence or barrier as protection. 2 steel bar or continuous line of bars laid on the ground, usu. As a railway. 3 (often attrib.) Railway. â€”v. 1 furnish with a rail or rails. 2 (usu. Foll. By in, off) enclose with rails.  off the rails disorganized; out of order; deranged. [french reille from latin regula *rule]'),
(30224, 'r', '\r\nRail2', ' v. (often foll. By at, against) complain or protest strongly; rant. [french railler]'),
(30225, 'r', '\r\nRail3', ' n. Wading bird often inhabiting marshes. [french]'),
(30226, 'r', '\r\nRailcar', ' n. Single powered railway coach.'),
(30227, 'r', '\r\nRailcard', ' n. Pass entitling the holder to reduced rail fares.'),
(30228, 'r', '\r\nRailing', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Fence or barrier made of rails.'),
(30229, 'r', '\r\nRaillery', ' n. Good-humoured ridicule. [french raillerie: related to *rail2]'),
(30230, 'r', '\r\nRailman', ' n. = *railwayman.'),
(30231, 'r', '\r\nRailroad', ' â€”n. Esp. Us = *railway. â€”v. (often foll. By into, through, etc.) Coerce; rush (railroaded into agreeing; railroaded through the cabinet).'),
(30232, 'r', '\r\nRailway', ' n. 1 track or set of tracks of steel rails upon which trains run. 2 such a system worked by a single company. 3 organization and personnel required for its working.'),
(30233, 'r', '\r\nRailwayman', ' n. Railway employee.'),
(30234, 'r', '\r\nRaiment', ' n. Archaic clothing. [arrayment: related to *array]'),
(30235, 'r', '\r\nRain', ' â€”n. 1 a condensed atmospheric moisture falling in drops. B fall of such drops. 2 (in pl.) A (prec. By the) rainy season. B rainfalls. 3 a falling liquid or solid particles or objects. B rainlike descent of these. â€”v. 1 (prec. By it as subject) rain falls. 2 a fall like rain. B (prec. By it as subject) send in large quantities. 3 send down like rain; lavishly bestow (rained blows upon him). 4 (of the sky, clouds, etc.) Send down rain.  rain off (or us out) (esp.'),
(30236, 'r', '\r\nRainbow', ' â€”n. Arch of colours formed in the sky by reflection, refraction, and dispersion of the sun''s rays in falling rain or in spray or mist. â€”adj. Many-coloured. [old english: related to *rain, *bow1]'),
(30237, 'r', '\r\nRainbow', 'trout  n. Large trout orig. Of the pacific coast of n. America.'),
(30238, 'r', '\r\nRain', 'check  n. Esp. Us ticket given for later use when an outdoor event is interrupted or postponed by rain.  take a rain check on reserve the right not to take up (an offer) until convenient.'),
(30239, 'r', '\r\nRaincoat', ' n. Waterproof or water-resistant coat.'),
(30240, 'r', '\r\nRaindrop', ' n. Single drop of rain.'),
(30241, 'r', '\r\nRainfall', ' n. 1 fall of rain. 2 quantity of rain falling within a given area in a given time.'),
(30242, 'r', '\r\nRainforest', ' n. Luxuriant tropical forest with heavy rainfall.'),
(30243, 'r', '\r\nRainproof', ' adj. Impervious to rain.'),
(30244, 'r', '\r\nRainstorm', ' n. Storm with heavy rain.'),
(30245, 'r', '\r\nRainwater', ' n. Water collected from fallen rain.'),
(30246, 'r', '\r\nRainwear', ' n. Clothes for wearing in the rain.'),
(30247, 'r', '\r\nRainy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (of weather, a climate, day, etc.) In or on which rain is falling or much rain usually falls. [old english: related to *rain]'),
(30248, 'r', '\r\nRainy', 'day  n. Time of special need in the future.'),
(30249, 'r', '\r\nRaise', ' â€”v. (-sing) 1 put or take into a higher position. 2 (often foll. By up) cause to rise or stand up or be vertical. 3 increase the amount, value, or strength of. 4 (often foll. By up) construct or build up. 5 levy, collect, or bring together (raise money). 6 cause to be heard or considered (raise an objection). 7 set going or bring into being (raise hopes). 8 bring up, educate. 9 breed, grow. 10 promote to a higher rank. 11 (foll. By to) multiply a quantity to a power. 12 cause (bread) to rise. 13 cards bet more than (another player). 14 end (a siege etc.). 15 remove (a barrier etc.). 16 cause (a ghost etc.) To appear. 17 colloq. Get hold of, find. 18 rouse from sleep or death, or from a lair. â€”n. 1 cards increase in a stake or bid. 2 esp. Us increase in salary.  raise cain colloq. = raise the roof. Raise one''s eyebrows see *eyebrow. Raise from the dead restore to life. Raise a laugh cause others to laugh. Raise the roof be very angry; cause an uproar. [old norse]'),
(30250, 'r', '\r\nRaisin', ' n. Dried grape. [latin: related to *raceme]'),
(30251, 'r', '\r\nRaison', 'd''Ãªtre  n. (pl. Raisons d''Ãªtre pronunc. Same) purpose or reason that accounts for, justifies, or originally caused a thing''s existence. [french]'),
(30252, 'r', '\r\nRaj', ' n. (prec. By the) hist. British sovereignty in india. [hindi]'),
(30253, 'r', '\r\nRaja', ' n. (also rajah) hist. 1 indian king or prince. 2 petty dignitary or noble in india. [hindi from sanskrit]'),
(30254, 'r', '\r\nRake1', ' â€”n. 1 implement consisting of a pole with a toothed crossbar at the end for drawing together hay etc. Or smoothing loose soil or gravel. 2 similar implement used (e.g.) To draw in money at a gaming-table. â€”v. (-king) 1 collect or gather with or as with a rake. 2 make tidy or smooth with a rake. 3 use a rake. 4 search thoroughly, ransack. 5 direct gunfire along (a line) from end to end. 6 scratch or scrape.  rake in colloq. Amass (profits etc.). Rake up revive the (unwelcome) memory of. [old english]'),
(30255, 'r', '\r\nRake2', ' n. Dissolute man of fashion. [rakehell: related to *rake1, *hell]'),
(30256, 'r', '\r\nRake3', ' â€”v. (-king) 1 set or be set at a sloping angle. 2 (of a mast or funnel) incline from the perpendicular towards the stern. â€”n. 1 raking position or build. 2 amount by which a thing rakes. [origin unknown]'),
(30257, 'r', '\r\nRake-off', ' n. Colloq. Commission or share.'),
(30258, 'r', '\r\nRakish', ' adj. 1 dashing; jaunty. 2 dissolute.  rakishly adv. [from *rake2]'),
(30259, 'r', '\r\nRallentando', ' mus. â€”adv. & adj. With a gradual decrease of speed. â€”n. (pl. -s or -di) passage to be performed in this way. [italian]'),
(30260, 'r', '\r\nRally1', ' â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 (often foll. By round) bring or come together as support or for action. 2 bring or come together again after a rout or dispersion. 3 recover after illness etc., revive. 4 revive (courage etc.). 5 (of share-prices etc.) Increase after a fall. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 rallying or being rallied. 2 mass meeting of supporters or persons with a common interest. 3 competition for motor vehicles, mainly over public roads. 4 (in tennis etc.) Extended exchange of strokes. [french rallier: related to *re-, *ally]'),
(30261, 'r', '\r\nRally2', ' v. (-ies, -ied) ridicule good-humouredly. [french railler: related to *rail2]'),
(30262, 'r', '\r\nRallycross', ' n. Motor racing over roads and cross-country.'),
(30263, 'r', '\r\nRam', ' abbr. 1 royal academy of music. 2 random-access memory.'),
