(30284, 'r', '\r\nRancour', ' n. (us rancor) inveterate bitterness, malignant hate.  rancorous adj. [latin rancor: related to *rancid]'),
(30285, 'r', '\r\nRand', ' n. Chief monetary unit of south africa. [the rand, gold-field district near johannesburg]'),
(30286, 'r', '\r\nR', '& b  abbr. Rhythm and blues.'),
(30287, 'r', '\r\nR', '& d  abbr. Research and development.'),
(30288, 'r', '\r\nRandom', ' adj. Made, done, etc., without method or conscious choice.  at random without a particular aim.  randomize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). Randomization n. Randomly adv. Randomness n. [french randon from randir gallop]'),
(30289, 'r', '\r\nRandom-access', ' adj. Computing (of a memory or file) having all parts directly accessible, so that it need not read sequentially.'),
(30290, 'r', '\r\nRandy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) eager for sexual gratification, lustful.  randily adv. Randiness n. [perhaps related to *rant]'),
(30291, 'r', '\r\nRanee', ' n. (also rani) (pl. -s) hist. Raja''s wife or widow. [hindi]'),
(30292, 'r', '\r\nRang', ' past of *ring2.'),
(30293, 'r', '\r\nRange', ' â€”n. 1 a region between limits of variation, esp. Scope of effective operation. B such limits. 2 area relevant to something. 3 a distance attainable by a gun or projectile. B distance between a gun or projectile and its objective. 4 row, series, etc., esp. Of mountains. 5 area with targets for shooting. 6 fireplace with ovens and hotplates for cooking. 7 area over which a thing is distributed. 8 distance that can be covered by a vehicle without refuelling. 9 distance between a camera and the subject to be photographed. 10 large area of open land for grazing or hunting. â€”v. (-ging) 1 reach; lie spread out; extend; be found over a specified district; vary between limits. 2 (usu. In passive or refl.) Line up, arrange. 3 rove, wander. 4 traverse in all directions. [french: related to *rank1]'),
(30294, 'r', '\r\nRangefinder', ' n. Instrument for estimating the distance of an object to be shot at or photographed.'),
(30295, 'r', '\r\nRanger', ' n. 1 keeper of a royal or national park, or of a forest. 2 member of a body of mounted soldiers. 3 (ranger) senior guide.'),
(30296, 'r', '\r\nRangy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) tall and slim.'),
(30297, 'r', '\r\nRani', ' var. Of *ranee.'),
(30298, 'r', '\r\nRank1', ' â€”n. 1 a position in a hierarchy, grade of advancement. B distinct social class; grade of dignity or achievement. C high social position. D place in a scale. 2 row or line. 3 single line of soldiers drawn up abreast. 4 place where taxis await customers. 5 order, array. â€”v. 1 have a rank or place. 2 classify, give a certain grade to. 3 arrange (esp. Soldiers) in rank.  close ranks maintain solidarity. The ranks common soldiers. [french ranc]'),
(30299, 'r', '\r\nRank2', ' adj. 1 luxuriant, coarse; choked with or apt to produce weeds or excessive foliage. 2 a foul-smelling. B loathsome, corrupt. 3 flagrant, virulent, gross, complete (rank outsider). [old english]'),
(30300, 'r', '\r\nRank', 'and file  n. (usu. Treated as pl.) Ordinary members of an organization.'),
(30301, 'r', '\r\nRankle', ' v. (-ling) (of envy, disappointment, etc., or their cause) cause persistent annoyance or resentment. [french (d)rancler fester, from medieval latin dra(cu)nculus little serpent]'),
