(31630, 'r', '\r\nRoly-poly', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) (also roly-poly pudding) pudding made of a rolled strip of suet pastry covered with jam etc. And boiled or baked. â€”adj. Podgy, plump. [probably *roll]'),
(31631, 'r', '\r\nRom', ' n. Computing read-only memory. [abbreviation]'),
(31632, 'r', '\r\nRom.', ' Abbr. Roman (type).'),
(31633, 'r', '\r\nRoman', ' â€”adj. 1 of ancient rome, its territory, people, etc. 2 of medieval or modern rome. 3 = *roman catholic. 4 (roman) (of type) plain and upright, used in ordinary print. 5 (of the alphabet etc.) Based on the ancient roman system with letters aâ€“z. â€”n. 1 citizen or soldier of the ancient roman republic or empire. 2 citizen of modern rome. 3 = *roman catholic. 4 (roman) roman type. [latin]'),
(31634, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'candle  n. Firework discharging flaming coloured balls.'),
(31635, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'catholic  â€”adj. Of the part of the christian church acknowledging the pope as its head. â€”n. Member of this church.  roman catholicism n.'),
(31636, 'r', '\r\nRomance', ' â€”n. 1 idealized, poetic, or unworldly atmosphere or tendency. 2 a love affair. B mutual attraction in this. C sentimental or idealized love. 3 a literary genre concerning romantic love, stirring action, etc. B work of this genre. 4 medieval, esp. Verse, tale of chivalry, common in the romance languages. 5 a exaggeration, lies. B instance of this. 6 (romance) (often attrib.) Languages descended from latin. 7 mus. Short informal piece. â€”v. (-cing) 1 exaggerate, distort the truth, fantasize. 2 court, woo. [romanic: related to *romanic]'),
(31637, 'r', '\r\nUsage', ' the alternative pronunciation given for the noun, with the stress on the first syllable, is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(31638, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'empire  n. Hist. That established by augustus in 27 bc and divided by theodosius in ad 395.'),
(31639, 'r', '\r\nRomanesque', ' â€”n. Style of european architecture c. 900â€“1200, with massive vaulting and round arches. â€”adj. Of this style.'),
(31640, 'r', '\r\nRomanian', ' (also rumanian) â€”n. 1 a native or national of romania. B person of romanian descent. 2 language of romania. â€”adj. Of romania, its people, or language.'),
(31641, 'r', '\r\nRomanic', ' â€”n. = *romance n. 6. â€”adj. 1 a of romance. B romance-speaking. 2 descended from, or inheriting the civilization etc. Of, the ancient romans. [latin romanicus: related to *roman]'),
(31642, 'r', '\r\nRomanize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 make roman or roman catholic in character. 2 put into the roman alphabet or roman type.  romanization n.'),
(31643, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'law  n. Law-code of ancient rome, forming the basis of many modern codes.'),
(31644, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'nose  n. Aquiline high-bridged nose.'),
(31645, 'r', '\r\nRoman', 'numeral  n. Any of the roman letters representing numbers: i = 1, v = 5, x = 10, l = 50, c = 100, d = 500, m = 1000.'),
(31646, 'r', '\r\nRomano-', ' comb. Form roman; roman and (romano-british).'),
(31647, 'r', '\r\nRomantic', ' â€”adj. 1 of, characterized by, or suggestive of romance (romantic picture). 2 inclined towards or suggestive of romance in love (romantic evening; romantic words). 3 (of a person) imaginative, visionary, idealistic. 4 a (of style in art, music, etc.) Concerned more with feeling and emotion than with form and aesthetic qualities. B (also romantic) of the 18thâ€“19th-c. Romantic movement or style in the european arts. 5 (of a project etc.) Unpractical, fantastic. â€”n. 1 romantic person. 2 romanticist.  romantically adv. [french: related to *romance]'),
(31648, 'r', '\r\nRomanticism', ' n. (also romanticism) adherence to a romantic style in art, music, etc.'),
(31649, 'r', '\r\nRomanticist', ' n. (also romanticist) writer or artist of the romantic school.'),
(31650, 'r', '\r\nRomanticize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 make romantic; exaggerate (romanticized account). 2 indulge in romantic thoughts or actions.'),
(31651, 'r', '\r\nRomany', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 gypsy. 2 language of the gypsies. â€”adj. Of gypsies or the romany language. [romany rom gypsy]'),
(31652, 'r', '\r\nRomeo', ' n. (pl. -s) passionate male lover or seducer. [name of a character in shakespeare]'),
(31653, 'r', '\r\nRomp', ' â€”v. 1 play roughly and energetically. 2 (foll. By along, past, etc.) Colloq. Proceed without effort. â€”n. Spell of romping.  romp in (or home) colloq. Win easily. [perhaps from *ramp]'),
(31654, 'r', '\r\nRompers', ' n. Pl. (also romper suit) young child''s one-piece garment covering the legs and trunk.'),
(31655, 'r', '\r\nRondeau', ' n. (pl. Rondeaux pronunc. Same or) poem of ten or thirteen lines with only two rhymes throughout and with the opening words used twice as a refrain. [french: related to *rondel]'),
