(31667, 'r', '\r\nRoof-tree', ' n. Ridge-piece of a roof.'),
(31668, 'r', '\r\nRook1', ' â€”n. Black bird of the crow family nesting in colonies. â€”v. 1 colloq. Charge (a customer) extortionately. 2 win money at cards etc., esp. By swindling. [old english]'),
(31669, 'r', '\r\nRook2', ' n. Chess piece with a battlement-shaped top. [french from arabic]'),
(31670, 'r', '\r\nRookery', ' n. (pl. -ies) colony of rooks, penguins, or seals.'),
(31671, 'r', '\r\nRookie', ' n. Slang new recruit. [corruption of recruit]'),
(31672, 'r', '\r\nRoom', ' â€”n. 1 space for, or occupied by, something; capacity (takes up too much room; room for improvement). 2 a part of a building enclosed by walls, floor, and ceiling. B (in pl.) Apartments or lodgings. C people in a room (room fell silent). â€”v. Us have room(s); lodge, board. [old english]'),
