(31676, 'r', '\r\nRoomy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having much room, spacious.  roominess n.'),
(31677, 'r', '\r\nRoost', ' â€”n. Branch or perch for a bird, esp. To sleep. â€”v. Settle for rest or sleep.  come home to roost (of a scheme etc.) Recoil unfavourably. [old english hrost]'),
(31678, 'r', '\r\nRooster', ' n. Domestic cock.'),
(31679, 'r', '\r\nRoot1', ' â€”n. 1 a part of a plant normally below the ground, conveying nourishment from the soil. B (in pl.) Branches or fibres of this. C small plant with a root for transplanting. 2 a plant with an edible root. B such a root. 3 (in pl.) Emotional attachment or family ties to a place or community. 4 a embedded part of a hair, tooth, nail, etc. B part of a thing attaching it to a greater whole. 5 (often attrib.) Basic cause, source, nature, or origin (root of all evil; roots in the distant past; root cause; the root of things). 6 a number that when multiplied by itself a usu. Specified number of times gives a specified number or quantity (cube root of eight is two). B square root. C value of an unknown quantity satisfying a given equation. 7 core of a word, without prefixes, suffixes, etc. â€”v. 1 (cause to) take root; grow roots (root them firmly). 2 (esp. As rooted adj.) Fix firmly; establish (rooted objection to; reaction rooted in fear). 3 (usu. Foll.'),
(31680, 'r', '\r\nRootstock', ' n. 1 rhizome. 2 plant into which a graft is inserted. 3 primary form from which offshoots have arisen.'),
(31681, 'r', '\r\nRope', ' â€”n. 1 a stout cord made by twisting together strands of hemp, wire, etc. B piece of this. 2 (foll. By of) quantity of onions, pearls, etc. Strung together. 3 (prec. By the) a halter for hanging a person. B execution by hanging. â€”v. (-ping) 1 fasten, secure, or catch with rope. 2 (usu. Foll. By off, in) enclose with rope. 3 mountaineering connect with or attach to a rope.  know (or learn or show) the ropes know (or learn or show) how to do a thing properly. Rope in persuade to take part. Rope into persuade to take part in (roped into washing up). [old english]'),
(31682, 'r', '\r\nRope-ladder', ' n. Two ropes with crosspieces, used as a ladder.'),
(31683, 'r', '\r\nRopy', ' adj. (also ropey) (-ier, -iest) colloq. Poor in quality.  ropiness n.'),
(31684, 'r', '\r\nRoquefort', ' n. Propr. Soft blue cheese made from ewes'' milk. [roquefort in france]'),
(31685, 'r', '\r\nRo-ro', ' attrib. Adj. Roll-on roll-off. [abbreviation]'),
(31686, 'r', '\r\nRorqual', ' n. Whale with a dorsal fin. [french from norwegian]'),
(31687, 'r', '\r\nRorschach', 'test  n. Personality test based on the subject''s interpretation of a standard set of ink-blots. [rorschach, name of a psychiatrist]'),
(31688, 'r', '\r\nRosaceous', ' adj.  Of a large plant family including the rose. [latin: related to *rose1]'),
(31689, 'r', '\r\nRosary', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 rc ch. Repeated sequence of prayers. 2 string of beads for keeping count in this. [latin rosarium rose-garden]'),
(31690, 'r', '\r\nRose1', ' â€”n. 1 prickly bush or shrub bearing usu. Fragrant red, pink, yellow, or white flowers. 2 this flower. 3 flowering plant resembling this (christmas rose). 4 a pinkish-red colour. B (usu. In pl.) Rosy complexion (roses in her cheeks). 5 sprinkling-nozzle of a watering-can etc. 6 circular electric light mounting on a ceiling. 7 a representation of a rose in heraldry etc. B rose-shaped design. 8 (in pl.) Used to express luck, ease, success, etc. (roses all the way; everything''s roses). â€”adj. = *rose-coloured 1. [latin rosa]'),
(31691, 'r', '\r\nRose2', ' past of *rise.'),
(31692, 'r', '\r\nRosÃ©', ' n. Light pink wine. [french]'),
(31693, 'r', '\r\nRosebowl', ' n. Bowl for cut roses, esp. As a prize in a competition.'),
(31694, 'r', '\r\nRosebud', ' n. 1 bud of a rose. 2 pretty young woman.'),
(31695, 'r', '\r\nRose-bush', ' n. Rose plant.'),
(31696, 'r', '\r\nRose-coloured', ' adj. 1 pinkish-red. 2 optimistic, cheerful (wears rose-coloured glasses).'),
(31697, 'r', '\r\nRose-hip', ' n. = *hip2.'),
(31698, 'r', '\r\nRosemary', ' n. Evergreen fragrant shrub used as a herb. [rosmarine from latin ros dew: related to *marine]'),
(31699, 'r', '\r\nRosette', ' n. 1 rose-shaped ornament of ribbon etc., esp. As a supporter''s badge or as a prize in a competition. 2 rose-shaped carving. [french diminutive: related to *rose1]'),
(31700, 'r', '\r\nRose-water', ' n. Perfume made from roses.'),
