(31702, 'r', '\r\nRosewood', ' n. Any of several fragrant close-grained woods used in making furniture.'),
(31703, 'r', '\r\nRosin', ' â€”n. Resin, esp. In solid form. â€”v. (-n-) rub (esp. A violin bow etc.) With rosin. [alteration of *resin]'),
(31704, 'r', '\r\nRospa', ' abbr. Royal society for the prevention of accidents.'),
(31705, 'r', '\r\nRoster', ' â€”n. List or plan of turns of duty etc. â€”v. Place on a roster. [dutch rooster, literally â€˜gridironâ€™]'),
(31706, 'r', '\r\nRostrum', ' n. (pl. Rostra or -s) platform for public speaking, an orchestral conductor, etc. [latin]'),
(31707, 'r', '\r\nRosy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 pink or red. 2 optimistic, hopeful (rosy future).  rosily adv. Rosiness n.'),
(31708, 'r', '\r\nRot', ' â€”v. (-tt-) 1 (of animal or vegetable matter) lose its original form by the chemical action of bacteria, fungi, etc.; decay. 2 gradually perish or waste away (left to rot in prison). 3 cause to rot, make rotten. â€”n. 1 rotting; decay. 2 slang nonsense (talks rot). 3 decline in standards etc. (rot set in). â€”int. Expressing incredulity or ridicule. [old english]'),
(31709, 'r', '\r\nRota', ' n. List of duties to be done or names of people to do them in turn; roster. [latin, = wheel]'),
(31710, 'r', '\r\nRotarian', ' â€”n. Member of rotary. â€”adj. Of rotary.'),
(31711, 'r', '\r\nRotary', ' â€”adj. Acting by rotation (rotary drill). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 rotary machine. 2 (rotary) (in full rotary international) worldwide charitable society of businessmen, orig. Entertaining in rotation. [medieval latin: related to *rota]'),
(31712, 'r', '\r\nRotary', 'club  n. Local branch of rotary.'),
(31713, 'r', '\r\nRotate', ' v. (-ting) 1 move round an axis or centre, revolve. 2 take or arrange (esp. Crops) in rotation. 3 act or take place in rotation (chairmanship will rotate).  rotatable adj. Rotatory adj. [latin: related to *rota]'),
(31714, 'r', '\r\nRotation', ' n. 1 rotating or being rotated. 2 recurrence; recurrent series or period; regular succession. 3 the growing of different crops in regular order to avoid exhausting the soil.  rotational adj.'),
(31715, 'r', '\r\nRotavator', ' n. (also rotovator) propr. Machine with a rotating blade for breaking up or tilling the soil. [from *rotary, *cultivator]'),
(31716, 'r', '\r\nRote', ' n. (usu. Prec. By by; also attrib.) Mechanical or habitual repetition (in order to memorize) (rote learning). [origin unknown]'),
