(31718, 'r', '\r\nRotisserie', ' n. 1 restaurant etc. Where meat is roasted or barbecued. 2 rotating spit for roasting or barbecuing meat. [french: related to *roast]'),
(31719, 'r', '\r\nRotor', ' n. 1 rotary part of a machine. 2 rotary aerofoil on a helicopter, providing lift. [related to *rotate]'),
(31720, 'r', '\r\nRotovator', ' var. Of *rotavator.'),
(31721, 'r', '\r\nRotten', ' adj. (-er, -est) 1 rotting or rotted; fragile from age or use. 2 morally or politically corrupt. 3 slang a disagreeable, unpleasant, bad (had a rotten time). B worthless (rotten idea). C ill (feel rotten).  rottenly adv. Rottenness n. [old norse: related to *rot]'),
(31722, 'r', '\r\nRotten', 'borough  n. Hist. (before 1832) english borough electing an mp though having very few voters.'),
(31723, 'r', '\r\nRotter', ' n. Slang nasty or contemptible person. [from *rot]'),
(31724, 'r', '\r\nRottweiler', ' n. Black-and-tan dog noted for ferocity. [rottweil in germany]'),
(31725, 'r', '\r\nRotund', ' adj. 1 plump, podgy. 2 (of speech etc.) Sonorous, grandiloquent.  rotundity n. [latin rotundus: related to *rota]'),
(31726, 'r', '\r\nRotunda', ' n. Circular building, hall, or room, esp. Domed. [italian rotonda: related to *rotund]'),
(31727, 'r', '\r\nRouble', ' n. (also ruble) chief monetary unit of russia etc. [french from russian]'),
(31728, 'r', '\r\nRouÃ©', ' n. (esp. Elderly) debauchee. [french]'),
(31729, 'r', '\r\nRouge', ' â€”n. Red cosmetic for colouring the cheeks. â€”v. (-ging) 1 colour with or apply rouge. 2 become red, blush. [latin rubeus red]'),
(31730, 'r', '\r\nRough', ' â€”adj. 1 uneven or bumpy, not smooth, level, or polished. 2 shaggy or coarse-haired. 3 boisterous, coarse; violent, not mild, quiet, or gentle (rough fellow; rough play; rough sea). 4 (of wine etc.) Sharp or harsh in taste. 5 harsh, insensitive (rough words; rough treatment). 6 a unpleasant, severe, demanding (had a rough time). B unfortunate; undeserved (had rough luck). C (often foll. By on) hard or unfair (towards). 7 lacking finish etc. 8 incomplete, rudimentary, approximate (rough attempt; rough sketch; rough estimate). 9 (of stationery etc.) Used for rough notes etc. 10 colloq. Unwell; depressed (feeling rough). â€”adv. In a rough manner (play rough). â€”n. 1 (usu. Prec. By the) hardship (take the rough with the smooth). 2 rough ground, esp. On a golf-course (ball went into the rough). 3 violent person (bunch of roughs). 4 unfinished or natural state (written it in rough). â€”v. 1 (foll. By up) ruffle (feathers, hair, etc.), esp. By rubbing. 2 (foll. By out, in) shape, plan, or sketch roughly.  rough it colloq. Do without basic comforts. Rough up slang attack violently.  roughish adj. Roughness n. [old english]'),
(31731, 'r', '\r\nRoughage', ' n. Coarse fibrous material in food, stimulating intestinal action.'),
(31732, 'r', '\r\nRough-and-ready', ' adj. Crude but effective; not over-particular.'),
(31733, 'r', '\r\nRough-and-tumble', ' â€”adj. Irregular, scrambling, disorderly. â€”n. Disorderly fight; scuffle.'),
(31734, 'r', '\r\nRoughcast', ' â€”n. (often attrib.) Plaster of lime and gravel, used on outside walls. â€”adj. (of a plan etc.) Roughly formed, preliminary. â€”v. (past and past part. -cast) 1 coat with roughcast. 2 prepare in outline.'),
(31735, 'r', '\r\nRough', 'diamond  n. 1  uncut diamond. 2 rough-mannered but honest person.'),
(31736, 'r', '\r\nRough-dry', ' v. Dry (clothes) without ironing.'),
(31737, 'r', '\r\nRoughen', ' v. Make or become rough.'),
(31738, 'r', '\r\nRough-hewn', ' adj. Uncouth, unrefined.'),
(31739, 'r', '\r\nRough', 'house  n. Slang disturbance or row; boisterous play.'),
(31740, 'r', '\r\nRough', 'justice  n. 1 treatment that is approximately fair. 2 unjust treatment.'),
(31741, 'r', '\r\nRoughly', ' adv. 1 in a rough manner. 2 approximately (roughly 20 people).  roughly speaking approximately.'),
(31742, 'r', '\r\nRoughneck', ' n. Colloq. 1 worker on an oil rig. 2 rough or rowdy person.'),
(31743, 'r', '\r\nRough-rider', ' n. Person who breaks in or rides unbroken horses.'),
(31744, 'r', '\r\nRoughshod', ' adj. (of a horse) having shoes with nail-heads projecting to prevent slipping.  ride roughshod over treat inconsiderately or arrogantly.'),
(31745, 'r', '\r\nRoulade', ' n. 1 rolled piece of meat, sponge, etc. With a filling. 2 quick succession of notes, usu. Sung to one syllable. [french rouler roll]'),
(31746, 'r', '\r\nRoulette', ' n. Gambling game in which a ball is dropped on to a revolving numbered wheel. [french, = little wheel]'),
(31747, 'r', '\r\nRound', ' â€”adj. 1 shaped like a circle, sphere, or cylinder; convex; circular, curved, not angular. 2 done with or involving circular motion. 3 entire, continuous, complete (round dozen). 4 candid, outspoken. 5 (usu. Attrib.) (of a number) expressed for brevity as a complete number (Â£297.32, or in round figures Â£300). 6 (of a voice, style, etc.) Flowing, sonorous. â€”n. 1 round object or form. 2 a revolving motion or course (yearly round). B recurring series of activities, meetings, etc. (continuous round of pleasure; round of talks). 3 a fixed route for deliveries (milk round). B route etc. For supervision or inspection (watchman''s round; doctor''s rounds). 4 drink etc. For each member of a group. 5 a one bullet, shell, etc. B act of firing this. 6 a slice from a loaf of bread. B sandwich made from two slices. C joint of beef from the haunch. 7 set, series, or sequence of actions in turn, esp.: a one spell of play in a game etc. B one stage in a competition. 8 golf playing of all the holes in a course once. 9 song for unaccompanied voices overlapping at intervals. 10 rung of a ladder. 11 (foll. By of) circumference or extent of (in all the round of nature). â€”adv. 1 with circular motion (wheels go round).'),
(31748, 'r', '\r\nRound', 'brackets  n.pl. Brackets of the form ( ).'),
(31749, 'r', '\r\nRound', 'dance  n. Dance in which couples move in circles or dancers form one large circle.'),
(31750, 'r', '\r\nRoundel', ' n. 1 circular mark, esp. Identifying military aircraft. 2 small disc, esp. A medallion. [french rondel(le): related to *round]'),
(31751, 'r', '\r\nRoundelay', ' n. Short simple song with a refrain. [alteration of french rondelet diminutive: related to *roundel]'),
(31752, 'r', '\r\nRounder', ' n. 1 (in pl.; treated as sing.) Ball game in which players hit the ball and run through a round of bases. 2 complete run as a unit of scoring in rounders.'),
(31753, 'r', '\r\nRoundhead', ' n. Hist. Member of the parliamentary party in the english civil war.'),
(31754, 'r', '\r\nRoundly', ' adv. Bluntly, severely (told them roundly).'),
(31755, 'r', '\r\nRound', 'robin  n. 1 petition, esp. With signatures in a circle to conceal the order of writing. 2 us tournament in which each competitor plays every other.'),
(31756, 'r', '\r\nRound-shouldered', ' adj. Having shoulders bent forward and a rounded back.'),
(31757, 'r', '\r\nRoundsman', ' n.  Tradesman''s employee delivering goods.'),
(31758, 'r', '\r\nRound', 'table  n. 1 international charitable association. 2 (round table) assembly for discussion, esp. At a conference (often attrib.: round-table talks).'),
(31759, 'r', '\r\nRound', 'trip  n. Trip to one or more places and back again.'),
(31760, 'r', '\r\nRound-up', ' n. 1 systematic rounding up. 2 summary or rÃ©sumÃ©.'),
(31761, 'r', '\r\nRoundworm', ' n. Worm with a rounded body.'),
(31762, 'r', '\r\nRouse', ' v. (-sing) 1 (cause to) wake. 2 (often foll. By up, often refl.) Stir up, make or become active or excited (was roused to protest). 3 anger (terrible when roused). 4 evoke (feelings). [origin unknown]'),
(31763, 'r', '\r\nRousing', ' adj. Exciting, stirring (rousing song).'),
(31764, 'r', '\r\nRoustabout', ' n. 1 labourer on an oil rig. 2 unskilled or casual labourer. [roust rout out, rouse]'),
(31765, 'r', '\r\nRout1', ' â€”n. 1 disorderly retreat of defeated troops (put them to rout). 2 overthrow, defeat. â€”v. Put to flight, defeat. [french: related to *route]'),
(31766, 'r', '\r\nRout2', ' v. = *root2. [var. Of *root2]'),
(31767, 'r', '\r\nRoute', ' â€”n. Way or course taken (esp. Regularly) from one place to another. â€”v. (-teing) send, forward, or direct by a particular route. [french route road, from latin rupta (via)]'),
(31768, 'r', '\r\nRoute', 'march  n. Training-march for troops.'),
(31769, 'r', '\r\nRoutine', ' â€”n. 1 regular course or procedure, unvarying performance of certain acts. 2 set sequence in a dance, comedy act, etc. 3 computing sequence of instructions for a particular task. â€”adj. 1 performed as part of a routine (routine duties). 2 of a customary or standard kind.  routinely adv. [french: related to *route]'),
(31770, 'r', '\r\nRoux', ' n. (pl. Same) mixture of fat and flour used in sauces etc. [french]'),
(31771, 'r', '\r\nRove1', ' v. (-ving) 1 wander without settling; roam, ramble. 2 (of eyes) look about. [probably scandinavian]'),
(31772, 'r', '\r\nRove2', ' past of *reeve2.'),
(31773, 'r', '\r\nRover1', ' n. Wanderer.'),
(31774, 'r', '\r\nRover2', ' n. Pirate. [low german or dutch]'),
(31775, 'r', '\r\nRoving', 'eye  n. Tendency to infidelity.'),
(31776, 'r', '\r\nRow1', ' n. 1 line of persons or things. 2 line of seats across a theatre etc. 3 street with houses along one or each side.  in a row 1 forming a row. 2 colloq. In succession (two days in a row). [old english]'),
(31777, 'r', '\r\nRow2', ' â€”v. 1 (often absol.) Propel (a boat) with oars. 2 convey (a passenger) thus. â€”n. 1 spell of rowing. 2 trip in a rowing-boat.  rower n. [old english]'),
(31778, 'r', '\r\nRow3', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 loud noise or commotion. 2 fierce quarrel or dispute. 3 severe reprimand. â€”v. 1 make or engage in a row. 2 reprimand. [origin unknown]'),
(31779, 'r', '\r\nRowan', ' n. (in full rowan-tree) 1 scot. & n.engl. Mountain ash. 2 (in full rowan-berry) its scarlet berry. [scandinavian]'),
(31780, 'r', '\r\nRow-boat', ' n. Us = *rowing-boat.'),
(31781, 'r', '\r\nRowdy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) noisy and disorderly. â€”n. (pl. -ies) rowdy person.  rowdily adv. Rowdiness n. Rowdyism n. [origin unknown]'),
(31782, 'r', '\r\nRowel', ' n. Spiked revolving disc at the end of a spur. [latin rotella diminutive: related to *rota]'),
(31783, 'r', '\r\nRowing-boat', ' n. Small boat propelled by oars.'),
(31784, 'r', '\r\nRowlock', ' n. Device on a boat''s side for holding an oar in place. [oarlock from old english: related to *oar, *lock1]'),
(31785, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', ' â€”adj. 1 of, suited to, or worthy of a king or queen. 2 in the service or under the patronage of a king or queen. 3 of the family of a king or queen. 4 majestic, splendid. 5 exceptional, first-rate (had a royal time). â€”n. Colloq. Member of the royal family.  royally adv. [latin: related to *regal]'),
(31786, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'blue  adj. & n. (as adj. Often hyphenated) deep vivid blue.'),
(31787, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'british legion  n. National association of ex-members of the armed forces, founded in 1921.'),
(31788, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'commission  n. Commission of inquiry appointed by the crown at the request of government.'),
(31789, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'family  n. Family of a sovereign.'),
(31790, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'flush  n. Straight poker flush headed by an ace.'),
(31791, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'icing  n. Hard white icing for cakes.'),
(31792, 'r', '\r\nRoyalist', ' n. Supporter of monarchy, or hist. Of the royal side in the english civil war.  royalism n.'),
(31793, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'jelly  n. Substance secreted by worker bees and fed by them to future queen bees.'),
(31794, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'marine  n. British marine (see *marine n. 1).'),
(31795, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'navy  n. British navy.'),
(31796, 'r', '\r\nRoyalty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 royal office, dignity, or power; being royal. 2 a royal persons. B member of a royal family. 3 percentage of profit from a book, public performance, patent, etc. Paid to the author etc. 4 a royal right (now esp. Over minerals) granted by the sovereign. B payment made by a producer of minerals etc. To the owner of the site etc. [french: related to *royal]'),
(31797, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'warrant  n. Warrant authorizing a tradesperson to supply goods to a specified royal person.'),
(31798, 'r', '\r\nRoyal', 'â€˜weâ€™  n. Use of â€˜weâ€™ instead of â€˜iâ€™ by a single person.'),
(31799, 'r', '\r\nRp', ' abbr. Received pronunciation.'),
(31800, 'r', '\r\nRpi', ' abbr. Retail price index.'),
(31801, 'r', '\r\nRpm', ' abbr. Revolutions per minute.'),
(31802, 'r', '\r\nRpo', ' abbr. Royal philharmonic orchestra.'),
(31803, 'r', '\r\nRsa', ' abbr. 1 royal society of arts. 2 royal scottish academy; royal scottish academician.'),
(31804, 'r', '\r\nRsc', ' abbr. Royal shakespeare company.'),
(31805, 'r', '\r\nRsi', ' abbr. Repetitive strain injury.'),
(31806, 'r', '\r\nRsj', ' abbr. Rolled steel joist.'),
(31807, 'r', '\r\nRsm', ' abbr. Regimental sergeant-major.'),
(31808, 'r', '\r\nRspb', ' abbr. Royal society for the protection of birds.'),
(31809, 'r', '\r\nRspca', ' abbr. Royal society for the prevention of cruelty to animals.'),
(31810, 'r', '\r\nRsv', ' abbr. Revised standard version (of the bible).'),
(31811, 'r', '\r\nRsvp', ' abbr. (in an invitation etc.) Please answer. [french rÃ©pondez s''il vous plaÃ®t]'),
(31812, 'r', '\r\nRt.', ' Abbr. Right.'),
(31813, 'r', '\r\nRt.', 'Hon.  Abbr. Right honourable.'),
(31814, 'r', '\r\nRt.', 'Revd.  Abbr. (also rt. Rev.) Right reverend.'),
(31815, 'r', '\r\nRu', ' abbr. Rugby union.'),
(31816, 'r', '\r\nRu', ' symb. Ruthenium.'),
(31817, 'r', '\r\nRub', ' â€”v. (-bb-) 1 move something, esp. One''s hand, with firm pressure over the surface of. 2 (usu. Foll. By against, in, on, over) apply (one''s hand etc.) In this way. 3 clean, polish, chafe, or make dry, sore, or bare by rubbing. 4 (foll. By in, into, through, over) apply (polish etc.) By rubbing. 5 (often foll. By together, against, on) move with contact or friction or slide (objects) against each other. 6 (of cloth, skin, etc.) Become frayed, worn, sore, or bare with friction. â€”n. 1 act or spell of rubbing (give it a rub). 2 impediment or difficulty (there''s the rub).  rub along colloq. Cope or manage routinely. Rub down dry, smooth, or clean by rubbing. Rub it in (or rub a person''s nose in it) emphasize or repeat an embarrassing fact etc. Rub off 1 (usu. Foll. By on) be transferred by contact, be transmitted (his attitudes have rubbed off on me). 2 remove by rubbing. Rub out erase with a rubber. Rub shoulders with associate with. Rub up 1 polish. 2 brush up (a subject or one''s memory). Rub up the wrong way irritate. [low german]'),
(31818, 'r', '\r\nRubato', ' n. Mus. (pl. -s or -ti) temporary disregarding of strict tempo. [italian, = robbed]'),
(31819, 'r', '\r\nRubber1', ' n. 1 tough elastic substance made from the latex of plants or synthetically. 2 piece of this or a similar substance for erasing esp. Pencil marks. 3 colloq. Condom. 4 (in pl.) Us galoshes.  rubbery adj. Rubberiness n. [from *rub]'),
(31820, 'r', '\r\nRubber2', ' n. Match of esp. Three successive games between the same sides or persons at whist, bridge, cricket, etc. [origin unknown]'),
(31821, 'r', '\r\nRubber', 'band  n. Loop of rubber for holding papers etc. Together.'),
(31822, 'r', '\r\nRubberize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) treat or coat with rubber.'),
(31823, 'r', '\r\nRubberneck', ' colloq. â€”n. Inquisitive person, esp. A tourist or sightseer. â€”v. Behave like a rubberneck.'),
(31824, 'r', '\r\nRubber', 'plant  n. 1 evergreen tropical plant often cultivated as a house-plant. 2 (also rubber tree) tropical tree yielding latex.'),
(31825, 'r', '\r\nRubber', 'stamp  â€”n. 1 device for inking and imprinting on a surface. 2 a person who mechanically copies or endorses others'' actions. B indication of such endorsement. â€”v. (rubber-stamp) approve automatically.'),
(31826, 'r', '\r\nRubbing', ' n.  Impression or copy made by rubbing.'),
(31827, 'r', '\r\nRubbish', ' â€”n. 1 waste material; refuse, litter. 2 worthless material; trash. 3 (often as int.) Nonsense. â€”v. Colloq. Criticize contemptuously.  rubbishy adj. [anglo-french rubbous]'),
(31828, 'r', '\r\nRubble', ' n. Rough fragments of stone, brick, etc., esp. From a demolished building. [french robe spoils]'),
(31829, 'r', '\r\nRub-down', ' n. Rubbing down.'),
(31830, 'r', '\r\nRubella', ' n. Formal german measles. [latin rubellus reddish]'),
(31831, 'r', '\r\nRubicon', ' n. Boundary; point from which there is no going back. [rubicon, river on ancient frontier of italy]'),
(31832, 'r', '\r\nRubicund', ' adj. (of a face, complexion, etc.) Ruddy, high-coloured. [latin rubeo be red]'),
(31833, 'r', '\r\nRubidium', ' n. Soft silvery metallic element. [latin rubidus red]'),
(31834, 'r', '\r\nRubik''s', 'cube  n. Cube-shaped puzzle in which composite faces must be restored to single colours by rotation. [rubik, name of its inventor]'),
(31835, 'r', '\r\nRuble', ' var. Of *rouble.'),
(31836, 'r', '\r\nRubric', ' n. 1 heading or passage in red or special lettering. 2 explanatory words. 3 established custom or rule. 4 direction for the conduct of divine service in a liturgical book. [latin ruber red]'),
(31837, 'r', '\r\nRuby', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 rare precious stone varying in colour from deep crimson to pale rose. 2 deep red colour. â€”adj. Of this colour. [latin rubeus red]'),
(31838, 'r', '\r\nRuby', 'wedding  n. Fortieth wedding anniversary.'),
(31839, 'r', '\r\nRuc', ' abbr. Royal ulster constabulary.'),
(31840, 'r', '\r\nRuche', ' n. Frill or gathering of lace etc.  ruched adj. [french, = beehive]'),
(31841, 'r', '\r\nRuck1', ' n. 1 (prec. By the) main body of competitors not likely to overtake the leaders. 2 undistinguished crowd or group. 3 rugby loose scrum. [apparently scandinavian]'),
(31842, 'r', '\r\nRuck2', ' â€”v. (often foll. By up) make or become creased or wrinkled. â€”n. Crease or wrinkle. [old norse]'),
(31843, 'r', '\r\nRucksack', ' n. Bag carried on the back, esp. By hikers. [german]'),
(31844, 'r', '\r\nRuckus', ' n. Esp. Us informal row, commotion. [perhaps from *ruction or *rumpus]'),
(31845, 'r', '\r\nRuction', ' n. Colloq. 1 disturbance or tumult. 2 (in pl.) Row, heated arguments. [origin unknown]'),
(31846, 'r', '\r\nRudder', ' n. Flat piece hinged vertically to the stern of a ship or on the tailplane of an aircraft etc., for steering.  rudderless adj. [old english]'),
(31847, 'r', '\r\nRuddy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a person, complexion, etc.) Freshly or healthily red. 2 reddish. 3 colloq. Bloody, damnable.  ruddily adv. Ruddiness n. [old english]'),
(31848, 'r', '\r\nRude', ' adj. 1 impolite or offensive. 2 roughly made or done; crude (rude plough). 3 primitive or uneducated (rude simplicity). 4 abrupt, sudden, startling (rude awakening). 5 colloq. Indecent, lewd (rude joke). 6 vigorous or hearty (rude health).  rudely adv. Rudeness n. [latin rudis]'),
(31849, 'r', '\r\nRudiment', ' n. 1 (in pl.) Elements or first principles of a subject. 2 (in pl.) Imperfect beginning of something undeveloped or yet to develop. 3 vestigial or undeveloped part or organ.  rudimentary adj. [latin: related to *rude]'),
(31850, 'r', '\r\nRue1', ' v. (rues, rued, rueing or ruing) repent of; wish to be undone or non-existent (esp. Rue the day). [old english]'),
(31851, 'r', '\r\nRue2', ' n. Evergreen shrub with bitter strong-scented leaves. [greek rhute]'),
(31852, 'r', '\r\nRueful', ' adj. Genuinely or humorously sorrowful.  ruefully adv. Ruefulness n. [from *rue1]'),
(31853, 'r', '\r\nRuff1', ' n. 1 projecting starched frill worn round the neck, esp. In the 16th c. 2 projecting or coloured ring of feathers or hair round a bird''s or animal''s neck. 3 domestic pigeon. 4 (fem. Reeve) wading bird with a ruff. [perhaps = *rough]'),
(31854, 'r', '\r\nRuff2', ' â€”v. Trump at cards. â€”n. Trumping. [french ro(u)ffle]'),
(31855, 'r', '\r\nRuffian', ' n. Violent lawless person. [italian ruffiano]'),
(31856, 'r', '\r\nRuffle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 a disturb the smoothness or tranquillity of. B undergo this. 2 gather (lace etc.) Into a ruffle. 3 (often foll. By up) (of a bird) erect (its feathers) in anger, display, etc. â€”n. Frill of lace etc., esp. Round the wrist or neck. [origin unknown]'),
(31857, 'r', '\r\nRufous', ' adj. (esp. Of animals) reddish-brown. [latin rufus]'),
(31858, 'r', '\r\nRug', ' n. 1 thick floor covering, usu. Smaller than a carpet. 2 thick woollen coverlet or wrap.  pull the rug from under deprive of support; weaken, unsettle. [probably scandinavian]'),
(31859, 'r', '\r\nRugby', ' n. (in full rugby football) team game played with an oval ball that may be kicked or carried. [rugby school, where it was first played]'),
(31860, 'r', '\r\nRugby', 'league  n. Partly professional rugby with teams of 13.'),
(31861, 'r', '\r\nRugby', 'union  n. Amateur rugby with teams of 15.'),
(31862, 'r', '\r\nRugged', ' adj. 1 (esp. Of ground) rough, uneven. 2 (of features) wrinkled, furrowed, irregular. 3 a unpolished; lacking refinement (rugged grandeur). B harsh in sound. 4 robust, hardy.  ruggedly adv. Ruggedness n. [probably scandinavian]'),
(31863, 'r', '\r\nRugger', ' n. Colloq. Rugby.'),
(31864, 'r', '\r\nRuin', ' â€”n. 1 destroyed, wrecked, or spoiled state. 2 downfall or elimination (ruin of my hopes). 3 complete loss of one''s property or position (bring to ruin). 4 (in sing. Or pl.) Remains of a building etc. That has suffered ruin. 5 cause of ruin (the ruin of us). â€”v. 1 a bring to ruin (extravagance has ruined me). B spoil, damage. 2 (esp. As ruined adj.) Reduce to ruins.  in ruins completely wrecked (hopes were in ruins). [latin ruo fall]'),
(31865, 'r', '\r\nRuination', ' n. 1 bringing to ruin. 2 ruining or being ruined.'),
(31866, 'r', '\r\nRuinous', ' adj. 1 bringing ruin, disastrous (ruinous expense). 2 dilapidated.  ruinously adv.'),
(31867, 'r', '\r\nRule', ' â€”n. 1 compulsory principle governing action. 2 prevailing custom or standard; normal state of things. 3 government or dominion (under british rule). 4 graduated straight measure; ruler. 5 code of discipline of a religious order. 6 order made by a judge or court with reference to a particular case only. 7 printing thin line or dash. â€”v. (-ling) 1 dominate; keep under control. 2 (often foll. By over) have sovereign control of (rules over a vast kingdom). 3 (often foll. By that) pronounce authoritatively. 4 a make parallel lines across (paper). B make (a straight line) with a ruler etc.  as a rule usually. Rule out exclude; pronounce irrelevant or ineligible. Rule the roost be in control. [latin regula]'),
(31868, 'r', '\r\nRule', 'of thumb  n. Rule based on experience or practice rather than theory.'),
(31869, 'r', '\r\nRuler', ' n. 1 person exercising government or dominion. 2 straight usu. Graduated strip of wood, metal, or plastic used to draw or measure.'),
(31870, 'r', '\r\nRuling', ' n. Authoritative pronouncement.'),
(31871, 'r', '\r\nRum1', ' n. Spirit distilled from sugar-cane or molasses. [origin unknown]'),
(31872, 'r', '\r\nRum2', ' adj. (rummer, rummest) colloq. Odd, strange, queer. [origin unknown]'),
(31873, 'r', '\r\nRumanian', ' var. Of *romanian.'),
(31874, 'r', '\r\nRumba', ' n. 1 latin american ballroom dance orig. From cuba. 2 music for this. [american spanish]'),
(31875, 'r', '\r\nRum', 'baba  n. Sponge cake soaked in rum syrup.'),
(31876, 'r', '\r\nRumble', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 make a continuous deep resonant sound as of distant thunder. 2 (foll. By along, by, past, etc.) (esp. Of a vehicle) move with a rumbling noise. 3 (often absol.) Slang find out the esp. Discreditable truth about. â€”n. Rumbling sound. [probably dutch rommelen]'),
(31877, 'r', '\r\nRumbustious', ' adj. Colloq. Boisterous, noisy, uproarious. [probably var. Of robustious from *robust]'),
(31878, 'r', '\r\nRuminant', ' â€”n. Animal that chews the cud. â€”adj. 1 of ruminants. 2 meditative. [related to *ruminate]'),
(31879, 'r', '\r\nRuminate', ' v. (-ting) 1 meditate, ponder. 2 chew the cud.  rumination n. Ruminative adj. [latin rumen throat]'),
(31880, 'r', '\r\nRummage', ' â€”v. (-ging) 1 search, esp. Unsystematically. 2 (foll. By out, up) find among other things. â€”n. Rummaging. [french arrumage from arrumer stow cargo]'),
(31881, 'r', '\r\nRummage', 'sale  n. Esp. Us jumble sale.'),
(31882, 'r', '\r\nRummy', ' n. Card-game played usu. With two packs. [origin unknown]'),
(31883, 'r', '\r\nRumor', ' (brit. Rumour) â€”n. (often foll. By of or that) general talk, assertion, or hearsay of doubtful accuracy (heard a rumour that you are leaving). â€”v. (usu. In passive) report by way of rumour (it is rumoured that you are leaving). [latin rumor noise]'),
(31884, 'r', '\r\nRumour', ' (us rumor) â€”n. (often foll. By of or that) general talk, assertion, or hearsay of doubtful accuracy (heard a rumour that you are leaving). â€”v. (usu. In passive) report by way of rumour (it is rumoured that you are leaving).'),
(31885, 'r', '\r\nRump', ' n. 1 hind part of a mammal or bird, esp. The buttocks. 2 remnant of a parliament etc. [probably scandinavian]'),
(31886, 'r', '\r\nRumple', ' v. (-ling) crease, ruffle. [dutch rompelen]'),
(31887, 'r', '\r\nRump', 'steak  n. Cut of beef from the rump.'),
(31888, 'r', '\r\nRumpus', ' n. Colloq. Disturbance, brawl, row, or uproar. [origin unknown]'),
(31889, 'r', '\r\nRun', ' â€”v. (-nn-; past ran; past part. Run) 1 go with quick steps, never having both or all feet on the ground at once. 2 flee, abscond. 3 go or travel hurriedly or briefly (i''ll just run down to the shops). 4 a advance by or as by rolling or on wheels, or smoothly or easily. B (cause to) be in action or operation or go in a specified way (left the engine running; ran the car into a tree). 5 be current or operative (lease runs for 99 years). 6 travel on its route (train is running late). 7 (of a play etc.) Be staged or presented (now running at the apollo). 8 extend; have a course, order, or tendency (road runs by the coast; prices are running high). 9 a (often absol.) Compete in (a race). B finish a race in a specified position. 10 (often foll. By for) seek election (ran for president). 11 flow (with) or be wet; drip (with) (walls running with condensation). 12 a cause (water etc.) To flow. B fill (a bath) thus. 13 spread rapidly (ink ran over the table). 14 traverse (a course, race, or distance). 15 perform (an errand). 16 publish (an article etc.) In a newspaper etc. 17 direct or manage (a business etc.). 18 own and use (a vehicle) regularly. 19 transport in a private vehicle (ran me to the station). 20 enter (a horse etc.) For a race. 21 smuggle (guns etc.). 22 chase or hunt. 23 allow (an account) to accumulate before paying. 24 (of a dyed colour) spread from the dyed parts. 25 a (of a thought, the eye, the memory, etc.) Pass quickly (ideas ran through my mind). B pass (one''s eye) quickly (ran my eye down the page). 26 (of tights etc.) Ladder. 27 (of esp. The eyes or nose) exude liquid. â€”n. 1 running. 2 short excursion. 3 distance travelled. 4 general tendency. 5 regular route. 6 continuous stretch, spell, or course (run of bad luck). 7 (often foll. By on) high general demand (run on the dollar). 8 quantity produced at one time (print run). 9 average type or class (general run of customers). 10 point scored in cricket or baseball. 11 (foll. By of) free use of or access to (run of the house). 12 a animal''s regular track. B enclosure for fowls etc. C range of pasture. 13 ladder in tights etc. 14 mus. Rapid scale passage. 15 (in full the runs) colloq. Diarrhoea.  on the run fleeing. Run about 1 bustle, hurry. 2 (esp. Of children) play freely. Run across happen to meet or find. Run after 1 pursue at a run. 2 pursue, esp. Sexually. Run along colloq. Depart. Run around 1 take from place to place by car etc. 2 (often foll. By with) slang engage in esp. Promiscuous sexual relations. Run away 1 (often foll. By from) flee, abscond. 2 mentally evade (a problem etc.). Run away with 1 carry off. 2 win easily. 3 deprive of self-control, carry away. 4 consume (money etc.). 5 (of a horse) bolt with (a rider etc.). 6 leave home to have a relationship with (esp. Another person''s husband or wife). Run down 1 knock down. 2 reduce the numbers etc. Of. 3 (of an unwound clock etc.) Stop. 4 discover after a search. 5 colloq. Disparage. Run dry 1 cease to flow. 2 = run out 1. Run for it seek safety by fleeing. Run (or good run) for one''s money 1 vigorous or close competition. 2 some return for outlay or effort. Run the gauntlet see *gauntlet2. Run high (of feelings) be strong. Run in 1 run (an engine or vehicle) carefully when new. 2 colloq. Arrest. Run in the family (of a trait) be common in a family. Run into 1 collide with. 2 encounter. 3 reach as many as (a usu. High figure). Run into the ground colloq.'),
(31890, 'r', '\r\nRunabout', ' n. Light car or aircraft.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(31891, 'r', '\r\nRun-around', ' n. (esp. In phr. Give a person the run-around) colloq. Deceit or evasion.'),
(31892, 'r', '\r\nRunaway', ' n. 1 fugitive. 2 bolting animal, vehicle out of control. 3 (attrib.) That is running away or out of control (runaway slave; runaway inflation).'),
(31893, 'r', '\r\nRun-down', ' â€”n. 1 reduction in numbers. 2 detailed analysis. â€”adj. 1 decayed, dilapidated. 2 exhausted (from overwork, illness, etc.).'),
(31894, 'r', '\r\nRune', ' n. 1 letter of the earliest germanic alphabet. 2 similar mark of mysterious or magic significance.  runic adj. [old norse]'),
(31895, 'r', '\r\nRung1', ' n. 1 step of a ladder. 2 strengthening crosspiece in a chair etc. [old english]'),
(31896, 'r', '\r\nRung2', ' past part. Of *ring2.'),
(31897, 'r', '\r\nRun-in', ' n. 1 approach to an action or event. 2 colloq. Quarrel.'),
(31898, 'r', '\r\nRunnel', ' n. 1 brook. 2 gutter. [old english]'),
(31899, 'r', '\r\nRunner', ' n. 1 person, horse, etc. That runs, esp. In a race. 2 creeping rooting plant-stem. 3 rod, groove, roller, or blade on which a thing, e.g. A sledge, slides. 4 sliding ring on a rod etc. 5 messenger. 6 (in full runner bean) twining bean plant with long flat green edible seed pods. 7 long narrow ornamental cloth or rug.  do a runner slang leave hastily; flee.'),
(31900, 'r', '\r\nRunner-up', ' n. (pl. Runners-up or runner-ups) competitor or team taking second place.'),
(31901, 'r', '\r\nRunning', ' â€”n. 1 action of runners in a race etc. 2 way a race etc. Proceeds. â€”adj. 1 continuous (running battle). 2 consecutive (three days running). 3 done with a run (running jump).  in (or out of) the running (of a competitor) with a good (or poor) chance of success. Make (or take up) the running take the lead; set the pace. Take a running jump (esp. As int.) Slang go away.'),
(31902, 'r', '\r\nRunning-board', ' n. Footboard on either side of a vehicle.'),
(31903, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'commentary  n. Verbal description of an esp. Sporting event.'),
(31904, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'knot  n. Knot that slips along a rope etc. To allow tightening etc.'),
(31905, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'mate  n. Us 1 candidate for vice-president etc. 2 horse intended to set the pace for another horse in a race.'),
(31906, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'repairs  n.pl. Minor or temporary repairs etc.'),
(31907, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'sore  n. Suppurating sore; festering situation etc.'),
(31908, 'r', '\r\nRunning', 'water  n. Flowing water, esp. On tap.'),
(31909, 'r', '\r\nRunny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 tending to run or flow. 2 excessively fluid.'),
(31910, 'r', '\r\nRun-off', ' n. Additional election, race, etc., after a tie.'),
(31911, 'r', '\r\nRun-of-the-mill', ' adj. Ordinary, undistinguished.'),
(31912, 'r', '\r\nRun-out', ' n. Dismissal of a batsman by being run out.'),
(31913, 'r', '\r\nRunt', ' n. 1 smallest pig etc. In a litter. 2 weakling; undersized person. [origin unknown]'),
(31914, 'r', '\r\nRun-through', ' n. 1 rehearsal. 2 brief survey.'),
(31915, 'r', '\r\nRun-up', ' n. (often foll. By to) preparatory period.'),
(31916, 'r', '\r\nRunway', ' n. Specially prepared surface for aircraft taking off and landing.'),
(31917, 'r', '\r\nRupee', ' n. Chief monetary unit of india, pakistan, etc. [hindustani]'),
(31918, 'r', '\r\nRupiah', ' n. Chief monetary unit of indonesia. [related to *rupee]'),
(31919, 'r', '\r\nRupture', ' â€”n. 1 breaking; breach. 2 breach in a relationship; disagreement and parting. 3 abdominal hernia. â€”v. (-ring) 1 burst (a cell or membrane etc.). 2 sever (a connection). 3 affect with or suffer a hernia. [latin rumpo rupt- break]'),
(31920, 'r', '\r\nRural', ' adj. In, of, or suggesting the country (rural seclusion). [latin rus rur- the country]'),
(31921, 'r', '\r\nRural', 'dean  see *dean1.'),
(31922, 'r', '\r\nRural', 'district  n. Hist. Group of country parishes with an elected council.'),
(31923, 'r', '\r\nRuse', ' n. Stratagem, trick. [french]'),
(31924, 'r', '\r\nRush1', ' â€”v. 1 go, move, flow, or act precipitately or with great speed. 2 move or transport with great haste (was rushed to hospital). 3 (foll. By at) a move suddenly towards. B begin or attack impetuously. 4 perform or deal with hurriedly (don''t rush your dinner). 5 force or induce (a person) to act hastily. 6 attack or capture by sudden assault. 7 slang overcharge (a customer). â€”n. 1 a rushing; violent or speedy advance or attack. B sudden flow, flood. 2 period of great activity. 3 (attrib.) Done with great haste or speed (a rush job). 4 sudden migration of large numbers. 5 (foll. By on, for) sudden strong demand for a commodity. 6 (in pl.) Colloq. First uncut prints of a film. [french ruser: related to *ruse]'),
(31925, 'r', '\r\nRush2', ' n. 1 marsh plant with slender tapering pith-filled stems, used for making chair-bottoms, baskets, etc. 2 stem of this.  rushy adj. [old english]'),
(31926, 'r', '\r\nRush', 'candle  n. Candle made of rush pith dipped in tallow.'),
(31927, 'r', '\r\nRush', 'hour  n. (often hyphenated when attrib.) Time(s) each day when traffic is heaviest.'),
(31928, 'r', '\r\nRushlight', ' n. Rush candle.'),
(31929, 'r', '\r\nRusk', ' n. Slice of bread rebaked as a light biscuit, esp. As baby food. [spanish or portuguese rosca twist]'),
(31930, 'r', '\r\nRusset', ' â€”adj. Reddish-brown. â€”n. 1 russet colour. 2 rough-skinned russet-coloured apple. [latin russus]'),
(31931, 'r', '\r\nRussian', ' â€”n. 1 a native or national of russia or (loosely) the former soviet union. B person of russian descent. 2 language of russia. â€”adj. 1 of russia or (loosely) the former soviet union or its people. 2 of or in russian.'),
(31932, 'r', '\r\nRussian', 'roulette  n. Firing of a revolver, with one chamber loaded, at one''s head, after spinning the chamber.'),
(31933, 'r', '\r\nRussian', 'salad  n. Salad of mixed diced vegetables with mayonnaise.'),
(31934, 'r', '\r\nRusso-', ' comb. Form russian; russian and.'),
(31935, 'r', '\r\nRust', ' â€”n. 1 reddish corrosive coating formed on iron, steel, etc. By oxidation, esp. When wet. 2 fungal plant-disease with rust-coloured spots. 3 impaired state due to disuse or inactivity. 4 reddish-brown. â€”v. 1 affect or be affected with rust. 2 become impaired through disuse. [old english]'),
(31936, 'r', '\r\nRustic', ' â€”adj. 1 of or like country people or country life. 2 unsophisticated. 3 of rude or rough workmanship. 4 made of untrimmed branches or rough timber (rustic bench). 5 archit. With a roughened or rough-hewn surface. â€”n. Country person, peasant.  rusticity n. [latin rus the country]'),
(31937, 'r', '\r\nRusticate', ' v. (-ting) 1 send down (a student) temporarily from university. 2 retire to or live in the country. 3 make rustic.  rustication n.'),
(31938, 'r', '\r\nRustle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 (cause to) make a gentle sound as of dry blown leaves. 2 (also absol.) Steal (cattle or horses). â€”n. Rustling sound.  rustle up colloq. Produce at short notice.  rustler n. (esp. In sense 2 of v.). [imitative]'),
(31939, 'r', '\r\nRustproof', ' â€”adj. Not susceptible to corrosion by rust. â€”v. Make rustproof.'),
(31940, 'r', '\r\nRusty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 rusted or affected by rust. 2 stiff with age or disuse. 3 (of knowledge etc.) Impaired, esp. By neglect (my french is rusty). 4 rust-coloured. 5 (of black clothes) discoloured by age.  rustiness n.'),
(31941, 'r', '\r\nRut1', ' â€”n. 1 deep track made by the passage of wheels. 2 established (esp. Tedious) practice or routine (in a rut). â€”v. (-tt-) mark with ruts. [probably french: related to *route]'),
(31942, 'r', '\r\nRut2', ' â€”n. Periodic sexual excitement of a male deer etc. â€”v. (-tt-) be affected with rut. [latin rugio roar]'),
(31943, 'r', '\r\nRuthenium', ' n. Rare hard white metallic element from platinum ores. [medieval latin ruthenia russia]'),
(31944, 'r', '\r\nRutherfordium', ' n. Artificial metallic element. [rutherford, name of a physicist]'),
(31945, 'r', '\r\nRuthless', ' adj. Having no pity or compassion.  ruthlessly adv. Ruthlessness n. [ruth pity, from *rue1]'),
(31946, 'r', '\r\nRv', ' abbr. Revised version (of the bible).'),
(31947, 'r', '\r\n-ry', ' suffix = *-ery (infantry; rivalry).'),
(31948, 'r', '\r\nRye', ' n. 1 a cereal plant. B grain of this used for bread and fodder. 2 (in full rye whisky) whisky distilled from fermented rye. [old english]'),
(31949, 'r', '\r\nRyegrass', ' n. Forage or coarse lawn grass. [alteration of ray-grass]'),
(31950, 's', 'S1', ' n. (also s) (pl. Ss or s''s) 1 nineteenth letter of the alphabet. 2 s-shaped thing.'),
(31951, 's', '\r\nS2', ' abbr. (also s.) 1 saint. 2 south, southern.'),
(31952, 's', '\r\nS3', ' symb. Sulphur.'),
(31953, 's', '\r\nS.', ' Abbr. 1 second(s). 2 hist. Shilling(s). 3 son. [sense 2 originally from latin solidus]'),
(31954, 's', '\r\n-s''', ' suffix denoting the possessive case of plural nouns and sometimes of singular nouns ending in s (the boys'' shoes; charles'' book). [old english inflection]'),
(31955, 's', '\r\n''S', ' abbr. 1 is; has (he''s; she''s got it; john''s; charles''s). 2 us (let''s).'),
(31956, 's', '\r\n-''s', ' suffix denoting the possessive case of singular nouns and of plural nouns not ending in -s (john''s book; book''s cover; children''s shoes).'),
(31957, 's', '\r\nSa', ' abbr. 1 salvation army. 2 south africa. 3 south australia.'),
(31958, 's', '\r\nSabbath', ' n. Religious day of rest kept by christians on sunday and jews on saturday. [hebrew, = rest]'),
(31959, 's', '\r\nSabbatical', ' â€”adj. (of leave) granted at intervals to a university teacher for study or travel. â€”n. Period of sabbatical leave. [greek: related to *sabbath]'),
(31960, 's', '\r\nSaber', ' n. (brit. Sabre) 1 curved cavalry sword. 2 light tapering fencing-sword. [french from german sabel]'),
(31961, 's', '\r\nSable', ' â€”n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 small brown-furred mammal of n. Europe and n. Asia. 2 its skin or fur. â€”adj. 1 (usu. Placed after noun) heraldry black. 2 esp. Poet. Gloomy. [slavonic]'),
(31962, 's', '\r\nSabot', ' n. 1 shoe carved from wood. 2 wooden-soled shoe. [french]'),
(31963, 's', '\r\nSabotage', ' â€”n. Deliberate damage to productive capacity, esp. As a political act. â€”v. (-ging) 1 commit sabotage on. 2 destroy, spoil. [french: related to *sabot]'),
