(31978, 's', '\r\nSacral', ' adj. 1 anat. Of the sacrum. 2 of or for sacred rites. [latin sacrum sacred]'),
(31979, 's', '\r\nSacrament', ' n. 1 symbolic christian ceremony, e.g. Baptism and eucharist. 2 (also blessed or holy sacrament) (prec. By the) eucharist. 3 sacred thing.  sacramental adj. [latin: related to *sacred]'),
(31980, 's', '\r\nSacred', ' adj. 1 a (often foll. By to) dedicated to a god. B connected with religion (sacred music). 2 safeguarded or required esp. By tradition; inviolable. [latin sacer holy]'),
(31981, 's', '\r\nSacred', 'cow  n. Colloq. Traditionally hallowed idea or institution.'),
(31982, 's', '\r\nSacrifice', ' â€”n. 1 a voluntary relinquishing of something valued. B thing so relinquished. C the loss entailed. 2 a slaughter of an animal or person or surrender of a possession, as an offering to a deity. B animal, person, or thing so offered. â€”v. (-cing) 1 give up (a thing) as a sacrifice. 2 (foll. By to) devote or give over to. 3 (also absol.) Offer or kill as a sacrifice.  sacrificial adj. [latin: related to *sacred]'),
(31983, 's', '\r\nSacrilege', ' n. Violation of what is regarded as sacred.  sacrilegious adj. [latin: related to *sacred, lego take]'),
