(33624, 's', '\r\nSimoom', ' n. Hot dry dust-laden desert wind. [arabic]'),
(33625, 's', '\r\nSimper', ' â€”v. 1 smile in a silly or affected way. 2 express by or with simpering. â€”n. Such a smile. [origin unknown]'),
(33626, 's', '\r\nSimple', ' adj. (simpler, simplest) 1 understood or done easily and without difficulty. 2 not complicated or elaborate; plain. 3 not compound or complex. 4 absolute, unqualified, straightforward (the simple truth). 5 foolish; gullible, feeble-minded.  simpleness n. [latin simplus]'),
(33627, 's', '\r\nSimple', 'fracture  n. Fracture of the bone only without a wound.'),
(33628, 's', '\r\nSimple', 'interest  n. Interest payable on a capital sum only.'),
(33629, 's', '\r\nSimple-minded', ' adj. Foolish; feeble-minded.  simple-mindedness n.'),
(33630, 's', '\r\nSimpleton', ' n. Gullible or halfwitted person.'),
(33631, 's', '\r\nSimplicity', ' n. Fact or condition of being simple.'),
(33632, 's', '\r\nSimplify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make simple or simpler.  simplification n.'),
(33633, 's', '\r\nSimplistic', ' adj. Excessively or affectedly simple.  simplistically adv.'),
(33634, 's', '\r\nSimply', ' adv. 1 in a simple manner. 2 absolutely (simply astonishing). 3 merely (was simply trying to please).'),
(33635, 's', '\r\nSimulate', ' v. (-ting) 1 pretend to be, have, or feel. 2 imitate or counterfeit. 3 reproduce the conditions of (a situation etc.), e.g. For training. 4 produce a computer model of (a process).  simulation n. Simulator n. [latin: related to *similar]'),
(33636, 's', '\r\nSimultaneous', ' adj. (often foll. By with) occurring or operating at the same time.  simultaneity n. Simultaneously adv. [latin simul at the same time]'),
(33637, 's', '\r\nSin1', ' â€”n. 1 a breaking of divine or moral law, esp. Deliberately. B such an act. 2 offence against good taste or propriety etc. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 commit a sin. 2 (foll. By against) offend. [old english]'),
(33638, 's', '\r\nSin2', ' abbr. Sine.'),
(33639, 's', '\r\nSin', 'bin  n. Colloq. Ice hockey penalty box.'),
(33640, 's', '\r\nSince', ' â€”prep. Throughout or during the period after (has been here since june; happened since yesterday). â€”conj. 1 during or in the time after (what have you done since we met?). 2 because. â€”adv. 1 from that time or event until now (has not seen him since). 2 ago (many years since). [old english, = after that]'),
(33641, 's', '\r\nSincere', ' adj. (sincerer, sincerest) 1 free from pretence. 2 genuine, honest, frank.  sincerity n. [latin]'),
(33642, 's', '\r\nSincerely', ' adv. In a sincere manner.  yours sincerely formula for ending an informal letter.'),
(33643, 's', '\r\nSine', ' n. Ratio of the side opposite a given angle (in a right-angled triangle) to the hypotenuse. [latin *sinus]'),
(33644, 's', '\r\nSinecure', ' n. Profitable or prestigious position requiring little or no work. [latin sine cura without care]'),
(33645, 's', '\r\nSine', 'die  adv. Formal indefinitely (postponed sine die). [latin]'),
(33646, 's', '\r\nSine', 'qua non  n. Indispensable condition or qualification. [latin, = without which not]'),
(33647, 's', '\r\nSinew', ' n. 1 tough fibrous tissue uniting muscle to bone; a tendon. 2 (in pl.) Muscles; bodily strength. 3 (in pl.) Strength or framework of a thing.  sinewy adj. [old english]'),
(33648, 's', '\r\nSinful', ' adj. Committing or involving sin.  sinfully adv. Sinfulness n.'),
(33649, 's', '\r\nSing', ' â€”v. (past sang; past part. Sung) 1 utter musical sounds, esp. Words with a set tune. 2 utter or produce by singing. 3 (of the wind, a kettle, etc.) Hum, buzz, or whistle. 4 (of the ears) hear a humming sound. 5 slang turn informer. 6 (foll. By of) literary celebrate in verse. â€”n. Act or spell of singing.  sing out shout. Sing the praises of praise enthusiastically.  singer n. [old english]'),
(33650, 's', '\r\nSinge', ' â€”v. (-geing) 1 burn superficially; scorch. 2 burn off the tips of (hair). â€”n. Superficial burn. [old english]'),
(33651, 's', '\r\nSinger-songwriter', ' n. Person who sings and writes songs.'),
(33652, 's', '\r\nSinghalese', ' var. Of *sinhalese.'),
(33653, 's', '\r\nSingle', ' â€”adj. 1 one only, not double or multiple. 2 united or undivided. 3 for or done by one person etc. 4 one by itself (a single tree). 5 regarded separately (every single thing). 6 not married. 7 (with neg. Or interrog.) Even one (not a single car). 8 (of a flower) having only one circle of petals. â€”n. 1 single thing, esp. A single room in a hotel. 2 (in full single ticket) ticket valid for an outward journey only. 3 pop record with one item on each side. 4 cricket hit for one run. 5 (usu. In pl.) Game with one player on each side. 6 (in pl.) Unmarried people. â€”v. (foll. By out) choose for special attention etc.  singly adv. [latin singulus]'),
(33654, 's', '\r\nSingle-breasted', ' adj. (of a coat etc.) Having only one vertical row of buttons and overlapping little down the front.'),
(33655, 's', '\r\nSingle', 'combat  n. Duel.'),
(33656, 's', '\r\nSingle', 'cream  n. Thin cream with a relatively low fat content.'),
(33657, 's', '\r\nSingle-decker', ' n. Bus with only one deck.'),
(33658, 's', '\r\nSingle', 'file  â€”n. Line of people one behind another. â€”adv. One behind the other.'),
(33659, 's', '\r\nSingle-handed', ' adv. Without help.  single-handedly adv.'),
(33660, 's', '\r\nSingle-minded', ' adj. Having or intent on only one aim.  single-mindedly adv. Single-mindedness n.'),
(33661, 's', '\r\nSingle', 'parent  n. Person bringing up a child or children alone.'),
(33662, 's', '\r\nSinglet', ' n. Sleeveless vest. [after doublet]'),
(33663, 's', '\r\nSingleton', ' n. 1 one card only of a suit in a player''s hand. 2 single person or thing. [after simpleton]'),
(33664, 's', '\r\nSingsong', ' â€”n. Informal singing party. â€”adj. Monotonously rising and falling. [from *sing, *song]'),
(33665, 's', '\r\nSingular', ' â€”adj. 1 unique; outstanding; extraordinary, strange. 2 gram. (of a word or form) denoting a single person or thing. â€”n. Gram. 1 singular word or form. 2 the singular number.  singularity n. Singularly adv. [latin: related to *single]'),
(33666, 's', '\r\nSinh', ' abbr. Math. Hyperbolic sine. [sine, hyperbolic]'),
(33667, 's', '\r\nSinhalese', ' (also singhalese) â€”n. (pl. Same) 1 member of a n. Indian people now forming the majority of the population of sri lanka. 2 their language. â€”adj. Of this people or language. [sanskrit]'),
(33668, 's', '\r\nSinister', ' adj. 1 evil or villainous in appearance or manner. 2 wicked, criminal. 3 ominous. 4 heraldry of or on the left-hand side of a shield etc. (i.e. To the observer''s right). [latin, = left]'),
(33669, 's', '\r\nSink', ' â€”v. (past sank or sunk; past part. Sunk or as adj. Sunken) 1 fall or come slowly downwards. 2 disappear below the horizon. 3 a go or penetrate below the surface esp. Of a liquid. B (of a ship) go to the bottom of the sea etc. 4 settle comfortably. 5 a decline in strength etc. B (of the voice) descend in pitch or volume. 6 cause or allow to sink or penetrate. 7 cause (a plan, person, etc.) To fail. 8 dig (a well) or bore (a shaft).'),
