(33673, 's', '\r\nSinn', 'fein  n. Political wing of the ira. [irish, = we ourselves]'),
(33674, 's', '\r\nSino-', ' comb. Form chinese; chinese and (sino-american). [greek sinai the chinese]'),
(33675, 's', '\r\nSinology', ' n. The study of the chinese language, chinese history, etc.  sinologist n.'),
(33676, 's', '\r\nSinuous', ' adj. Having many curves; undulating.  sinuosity n. [latin: related to *sinus]'),
(33677, 's', '\r\nSinus', ' n. Cavity of bone or tissue, esp. In the skull connecting with the nostrils. [latin, = bosom, recess]'),
(33678, 's', '\r\nSinusitis', ' n. Inflammation of a sinus.'),
(33679, 's', '\r\n-sion', ' see *-ion.'),
(33680, 's', '\r\nSip', ' â€”v. (-pp-) drink in small mouthfuls. â€”n. 1 small mouthful of liquid. 2 act of taking this. [perhaps var. Of *sup1]'),
(33681, 's', '\r\nSiphon', ' â€”n. 1 tube shaped like an inverted v or u with unequal legs, used to convey liquid from a container to a lower level by atmospheric pressure. 2 bottle from which aerated water is forced by the pressure of gas. â€”v. (often foll. By off) 1 (cause to) flow through a siphon. 2 divert or set aside (funds etc.). [greek, = pipe]'),
(33682, 's', '\r\nSir', ' n. 1 polite form of address or reference to a man. 2 (sir) title prefixed to the forename of a knight or baronet. [from *sire]'),
