(33728, 's', '\r\nSizable', ' var. Of *sizeable.'),
(33729, 's', '\r\nSize1', ' â€”n. 1 relative dimensions, magnitude. 2 each of the classes into which similar things are divided according to size. â€”v. (-zing) sort in sizes or according to size.  the size of it colloq. The truth of the matter. Size up colloq. Form a judgement of.  sized adj. (also in comb.). [french sise]'),
(33730, 's', '\r\nSize2', ' â€”n. Sticky solution used in glazing paper, stiffening textiles, etc. â€”v. (-zing) treat with size. [perhaps = *size1]'),
(33731, 's', '\r\nSizeable', ' adj. (also sizable) large or fairly large.'),
(33732, 's', '\r\nSizzle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 sputter or hiss, esp. In frying. 2 colloq. Be very hot or excited etc. â€”n. Sizzling sound.  sizzling adj. & adv. [imitative]'),
(33733, 's', '\r\nSj', ' abbr. Society of jesus.'),
(33734, 's', '\r\nSka', ' n. A kind of fast orig. Jamaican pop music. [origin unknown]'),
(33735, 's', '\r\nSkate1', ' â€”n. 1 boot with a blade attached for gliding on ice; this blade. 2 = *roller-skate. â€”v. (-ting) 1 a move on skates. B perform (a specified figure) on skates. 2 (foll. By over) refer fleetingly to, disregard.  get one''s skates on slang make haste. Skate on thin ice colloq. Behave rashly, risk danger.  skater n. [dutch schaats from french]'),
(33736, 's', '\r\nSkate2', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) large flat marine fish used as food. [old norse]'),
(33737, 's', '\r\nSkateboard', ' â€”n. Short narrow board on two pairs of trucks, for riding on while standing. â€”v. Ride on a skateboard.  skateboarder n.'),
(33738, 's', '\r\nSkedaddle', ' v. (-ling) colloq. Depart quickly, flee. [origin unknown]'),
(33739, 's', '\r\nSkein', ' n. 1 loosely-coiled bundle of yarn or thread. 2 flock of wild geese etc. In flight. [french escaigne]'),
(33740, 's', '\r\nSkeleton', ' n. 1 hard framework of bones etc. Of an animal. 2 supporting framework or structure of a thing. 3 very thin person or animal. 4 useless or dead remnant. 5 outline sketch, epitome. 6 (attrib.) Having only the essential or minimum number of persons, parts, etc. (skeleton staff).  skeletal adj. [greek skello dry up]'),
(33741, 's', '\r\nSkeleton', 'in the cupboard  n. Discreditable or embarrassing secret.'),
(33742, 's', '\r\nSkeleton', 'key  n. Key designed to fit many locks.'),
(33743, 's', '\r\nSkeptic', ' n. (brit. Sceptic) 1 person inclined to doubt accepted opinions. 2 person who doubts the truth of religions. 3 philosopher who questions the possibility of knowledge.  scepticism n. [greek skeptomai observe]'),
(33744, 's', '\r\nSkeptical', ' adj. (brit. Sceptical) inclined to doubt accepted opinions; critical; incredulous.  sceptically adv.'),
(33745, 's', '\r\nSkerry', ' n. (pl. -ies) scot. Reef, rocky island. [old norse]'),
(33746, 's', '\r\nSketch', ' â€”n. 1 rough or unfinished drawing or painting. 2 rough draft or general outline. 3 short usu. Humorous play. 4 short descriptive essay etc. â€”v. 1 make or give a sketch of. 2 draw sketches. 3 (often foll. By in, out) outline briefly. [greek skhedios extempore]'),
(33747, 's', '\r\nSketch-book', ' n. (also sketch-block) pad of drawing-paper for sketching.'),
(33748, 's', '\r\nSketch-map', ' n. Roughly-drawn map with few details.'),
(33749, 's', '\r\nSketchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 giving only a rough outline, like a sketch. 2 colloq. Unsubstantial or imperfect, esp. Through haste.  sketchily adv. Sketchiness n.'),
(33750, 's', '\r\nSkew', ' â€”adj. Oblique, slanting, set askew. â€”n. Slant. â€”v. 1 make skew. 2 distort. 3 move obliquely.  on the skew askew. [french: related to *eschew]'),
(33751, 's', '\r\nSkewbald', ' â€”adj. (esp. Of a horse) with irregular patches of white and another colour. â€”n. Skewbald animal. [origin uncertain]'),
(33752, 's', '\r\nSkewer', ' â€”n. Long pin designed for holding meat together while cooking. â€”v. Fasten together or pierce (as) with a skewer. [origin uncertain]'),
(33753, 's', '\r\nSkew-whiff', ' adj. & adv. Colloq. Askew.'),
(33754, 's', '\r\nSki', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 each of a pair of long narrow pieces of wood etc., fastened under the feet for travelling over snow. 2 similar device under a vehicle or aircraft. â€”v. (skis, ski''d or skied, skiing) travel on skis.  skier n. [norwegian from old norse] '),
(33755, 's', '\r\nSkid', ' â€”v. (-dd-) 1 (of a vehicle etc.) Slide on slippery ground, esp. Sideways or obliquely. 2 cause (a vehicle) to skid. â€”n. 1 act of skidding. 2 runner beneath an aircraft for use when landing.  on the skids colloq. About to be discarded or defeated. Put the skids under colloq. Hasten the downfall or failure of. [origin unknown]'),
(33756, 's', '\r\nSkid-pan', ' n. Slippery surface for drivers to practise control of skidding.'),
(33757, 's', '\r\nSkid', 'row  n. Us slang part of a town frequented by vagrants etc.'),
(33758, 's', '\r\nSkiff', ' n. Light rowing- or sculling-boat. [french esquif: related to *ship]'),
(33759, 's', '\r\nSki-jump', ' n. Steep slope levelling off before a sharp drop to allow a skier to leap through the air.  ski-jumping n.'),
(33760, 's', '\r\nSkilful', ' adj. (us skillful) (often foll. By at, in) having or showing skill.  skilfully adv.'),
(33761, 's', '\r\nSki-lift', ' n. Device for carrying skiers up a slope, usu. A cable with hanging seats.'),
(33762, 's', '\r\nSkill', ' n. (often foll. By in) ability to do something well; technique, expertise. [old norse, = difference]'),
(33763, 's', '\r\nSkilled', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By in) skilful. 2 (of work or a worker) requiring or having skill or special training.'),
(33764, 's', '\r\nSkillet', ' n. 1 small long-handled metal cooking-pot. 2 us frying-pan. [french]'),
(33765, 's', '\r\nSkillful', ' adj. (brit. Skilful) (often foll. By at, in) having or showing skill.  skilfully adv.'),
(33766, 's', '\r\nSkim', ' â€”v. (-mm-) 1 a take a floating layer from the surface of (a liquid). B take (cream etc.) From the surface of a liquid. 2 a barely touch (a surface) in passing over. B (often followed by over) deal with or treat (a matter) superficially. 3 (often foll. By over, along) go or glide lightly. 4 (often followed by through) read or look over cursorily. â€”n. Skimming. [french: related to *scum]'),
(33767, 's', '\r\nSkimmia', ' n. Evergreen shrub with red berries. [japanese]'),
(33768, 's', '\r\nSkim', 'milk  n. (also skimmed milk) milk from which the cream has been removed.'),
(33769, 's', '\r\nSkimp', ' v. 1 (often followed by on) economize; use a meagre or insufficient amount of, stint. 2 (often foll. By in) supply (a person etc.) Meagrely with food etc. 3 do hastily or carelessly. [cf. *scrimp]'),
(33770, 's', '\r\nSkimpy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) meagre; insufficient.  skimpiness n.'),
(33771, 's', '\r\nSkin', ' â€”n. 1 flexible covering of a body. 2 a skin of a flayed animal with or without the hair etc. B material prepared from skins. 3 complexion of the skin. 4 outer layer or covering, esp. Of a fruit, sausage, etc. 5 film like skin on a liquid etc. 6 container for liquid, made of an animal''s skin. 7 slang skinhead. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 remove the skin from. 2 graze (part of the body). 3 slang swindle.  be skin and bone be very thin. By (or with) the skin of one''s teeth by a very narrow margin. Get under a person''s skin colloq. Interest or annoy a person intensely. Have a thick (or thin) skin be insensitive (or sensitive). No skin off one''s nose colloq. Of no consequence to one.  skinless adj. [old norse]'),
(33772, 's', '\r\nSkin-deep', ' adj. Superficial.'),
(33773, 's', '\r\nSkin-diver', ' n. Underwater swimmer without a diving-suit, usu. With aqualung and flippers.  skin-diving n.'),
(33774, 's', '\r\nSkinflint', ' n. Miser.'),
(33775, 's', '\r\nSkinful', ' n. Colloq. Enough alcohol to make one drunk.'),
(33776, 's', '\r\nSkin-graft', ' n. 1 surgical transplanting of skin. 2 skin transferred in this way.'),
(33777, 's', '\r\nSkinhead', ' n. Youth with a shaven head, esp. One of an aggressive gang.'),
(33778, 's', '\r\nSkinny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) thin or emaciated.  skinniness n.'),
(33779, 's', '\r\nSkint', ' adj. Slang having no money left. [= skinned]'),
(33780, 's', '\r\nSkin-tight', ' adj. (of a garment) very close-fitting.'),
(33781, 's', '\r\nSkip1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 a move along lightly, esp. With alternate hops. B jump lightly, esp. Over a skipping-rope. C gambol, caper, frisk. 2 (often foll. By from, off, to) move quickly from one point, subject, etc. To another. 3 (also absol.) Omit parts of (a text, subject, etc.). 4 colloq. Miss intentionally, not attend. 5 colloq. Leave hurriedly. â€”n. Skipping movement or action.  skip it colloq. Abandon a topic etc. [probably scandinavian]'),
(33782, 's', '\r\nSkip2', ' n. 1 large container for building refuse etc. 2 container for transporting or raising materials in mining etc. [old norse]'),
(33783, 's', '\r\nSkipjack', ' n. (in full skipjack tuna) (pl. Same or -s) small striped pacific tuna used as food. [from *skip1, *jack]'),
(33784, 's', '\r\nSkipper', ' â€”n. 1  captain of a ship or aircraft. 2 captain of a sporting team. â€”v. Be captain of. [low german or dutch schipper]'),
(33785, 's', '\r\nSkipping-rope', ' n. Length of rope turned over the head and under the feet while jumping it as a game or exercise.'),
(33786, 's', '\r\nSkirl', ' â€”n. Shrill sound, esp. Of bagpipes. â€”v. Make a skirl. [probably scandinavian]'),
(33787, 's', '\r\nSkirmish', ' â€”n. 1 minor battle. 2 short argument or contest of wit etc. â€”v. Engage in a skirmish. [french from germanic]'),
(33788, 's', '\r\nSkirt', ' â€”n. 1 woman''s garment hanging from the waist. 2 the part of a coat etc. Hanging below the waist. 3 hanging part at the base of a hovercraft. 4 (in sing. Or pl.) Edge, border, extreme part. 5 (also bit of skirt) slang offens. Woman. 6 (in full skirt of beef etc.) Cut of meat from the flank or diaphragm. â€”v. (often foll. By around) 1 go or lie along or round the edge of. 2 avoid dealing with (an issue etc.). [old norse: related to *shirt]'),
(33789, 's', '\r\nSkirting-board', ' n. Narrow board etc. Along the bottom of a room-wall.'),
(33790, 's', '\r\nSki-run', ' n. Slope prepared for skiing.'),
(33791, 's', '\r\nSkit', ' n. Light, usu. Short, piece of satire or burlesque. [perhaps from old norse: related to *shoot]'),
(33792, 's', '\r\nSkittish', ' adj. 1 lively, playful. 2 (of a horse etc.) Nervous, inclined to shy. [perhaps related to *skit]'),
(33793, 's', '\r\nSkittle', ' n.  1 pin used in skittles. 2 (in pl.; usu. Treated as sing.) Game of trying to bowl down usu. Nine wooden pins. [origin unknown]'),
(33794, 's', '\r\nSkive', ' v. (-ving) (often followed by off) slang evade work; play truant.  skiver n. [old norse]'),
(33795, 's', '\r\nSkivvy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Derog. Female domestic servant. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) work as a skivvy. [origin unknown]'),
(33796, 's', '\r\nSkua', ' n. Large predatory sea bird. [old norse]'),
(33797, 's', '\r\nSkulduggery', ' n. Trickery; unscrupulous behaviour. [origin unknown]'),
(33798, 's', '\r\nSkulk', ' v. Move stealthily; lurk, hide. [scandinavian]'),
(33799, 's', '\r\nSkull', ' n. 1 bony case of the brain of a vertebrate. 2 bony skeleton of the head. 3 head as the seat of intelligence. [origin unknown]'),
(33800, 's', '\r\nSkull', 'and crossbones  n.pl. Representation of a skull with two crossed thigh-bones as an emblem of piracy or death.'),
(33801, 's', '\r\nSkullcap', ' n. Peakless cap covering the crown only.'),
(33802, 's', '\r\nSkunk', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 black and white striped mammal emitting a powerful stench when attacked. 2 colloq. Contemptible person. [american indian]'),
(33803, 's', '\r\nSky', ' â€”n. (pl. Skies) (in sing. Or pl.) Atmosphere and outer space as seen from the earth. â€”v. (skies, skied) cricket etc. Hit (a ball) high.  to the skies without reserve (praise to the skies). [old norse, = cloud]'),
(33804, 's', '\r\nSky', 'blue  adj. & n. (as adj. Often hyphenated) bright clear blue.'),
(33805, 's', '\r\nSkydiving', ' n. Sport of performing acrobatic manoeuvres under free fall before opening a parachute.  skydiver n.'),
(33806, 's', '\r\nSky-high', ' adv. & adj. Very high.'),
(33807, 's', '\r\nSkyjack', ' v. Slang hijack (an aircraft).'),
(33808, 's', '\r\nSkylark', ' â€”n. Lark that sings while soaring. â€”v. Play tricks, frolic.'),
(33809, 's', '\r\nSkylight', ' n. Window in a roof.'),
(33810, 's', '\r\nSkyline', ' n. Outline of hills, buildings, etc. Against the sky.'),
(33811, 's', '\r\nSky-rocket', ' â€”n. = *rocket 1. â€”v. (esp. Of prices) rise very rapidly.'),
(33812, 's', '\r\nSkyscraper', ' n. Very tall building.'),
(33813, 's', '\r\nSkyward', ' â€”adv. (also skywards) towards the sky. â€”adj. Moving skyward.'),
(33814, 's', '\r\nSky-writing', ' n. Writing in aeroplane smoke-trails.'),
(33815, 's', '\r\nSlab', ' n. 1 flat thick esp. Rectangular piece of solid material, esp. Stone. 2 mortuary table. [origin unknown]'),
(33816, 's', '\r\nSlack1', ' â€”adj. 1 (of rope etc.) Not taut. 2 inactive or sluggish. 3 negligent, remiss. 4 (of tide etc.) Neither ebbing nor flowing. â€”n. 1 slack part of a rope (haul in the slack). 2 slack period. 3 (in pl.) Informal trousers. â€”v. 1 loosen (rope etc.). 2 colloq. Take a rest, be lazy.  slack off 1 loosen. 2 (also slack up) reduce one''s level of activity; reduce speed.  slackness n. [old english]'),
(33817, 's', '\r\nSlack2', ' n. Coal-dust or fragments of coal. [probably low german or dutch]'),
(33818, 's', '\r\nSlacken', ' v. Make or become slack.  slacken off = slack off (see *slack1).'),
(33819, 's', '\r\nSlacker', ' n. Shirker.'),
(33820, 's', '\r\nSlag', ' â€”n. 1 refuse left after smelting etc. 2 slang derog. Prostitute; promiscuous woman. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 form slag. 2 (often foll. By off) slang insult, slander.  slaggy adj. [low german]'),
(33821, 's', '\r\nSlag-heap', ' n. Hill of refuse from a coalmine, steelworks, etc.'),
(33822, 's', '\r\nSlain', ' past part. Of *slay.'),
(33823, 's', '\r\nSlake', ' v. (-king) 1 assuage or satisfy (thirst, a desire, etc.). 2 temper (quicklime) by combination with water. [old english: related to *slack1]'),
(33824, 's', '\r\nSlalom', ' n. 1 ski-race down a zigzag obstacle course. 2 obstacle race in canoes etc. [norwegian]'),
(33825, 's', '\r\nSlam1', ' â€”v. (-mm-) 1 shut forcefully and loudly. 2 put down loudly. 3 put or do suddenly (slam the brakes on; car slammed to a halt). 4 slang criticize severely. 5 slang hit. 6 slang conquer easily. â€”n. Sound or action of slamming. [probably scandinavian]'),
(33826, 's', '\r\nSlam2', ' n. Cards winning of every trick in a game. [origin uncertain]'),
(33827, 's', '\r\nSlander', ' â€”n. 1 false and damaging utterance about a person. 2 uttering of this. â€”v. Utter slander about.  slanderous adj. [french esclandre: related to *scandal]'),
(33828, 's', '\r\nSlang', ' â€”n. Very informal words, phrases, or meanings, not regarded as standard and often used by a specific profession, class, etc. â€”v. Use abusive language (to).  slangy adj. [origin unknown]'),
(33829, 's', '\r\nSlanging-match', ' n. Prolonged exchange of insults.'),
(33830, 's', '\r\nSlant', ' â€”v. 1 slope; lie or (cause to) go obliquely. 2 (often as slanted adj.) Present (information) in a biased or particular way. â€”n. 1 slope; oblique position. 2 point of view, esp. A biased one. â€”adj. Sloping, oblique.  on a (or the) slant aslant. [scandinavian]'),
(33831, 's', '\r\nSlantwise', ' adv. Aslant.'),
(33832, 's', '\r\nSlap', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 strike with the palm or a flat object, or so as to make a similar noise. 2 lay forcefully (slapped it down). 3 put hastily or carelessly (slap paint on). 4 (often foll. By down) colloq. Reprimand or snub. â€”n. 1 blow with the palm or a flat object. 2 slapping sound. â€”adv. Suddenly, fully, directly (ran slap into him). [low german, imitative]'),
(33833, 's', '\r\nSlap', 'and tickle  n. Colloq. Sexual horseplay.'),
(33834, 's', '\r\nSlap-bang', ' adv. Colloq. Violently, headlong.'),
(33835, 's', '\r\nSlapdash', ' â€”adj. Hasty and careless. â€”adv. In this manner.'),
(33836, 's', '\r\nSlap-happy', ' adj. Colloq. Cheerfully casual or flippant.'),
(33837, 's', '\r\nSlap', 'in the face  n. Rebuff or affront.'),
(33838, 's', '\r\nSlap', 'on the back  n. Congratulations.'),
(33839, 's', '\r\nSlapstick', ' n. Boisterous comedy.'),
(33840, 's', '\r\nSlap-up', ' attrib. Adj. Colloq. Excellent, lavish.'),
(33841, 's', '\r\nSlash', ' â€”v. 1 cut or gash with a knife etc. 2 (often foll. By at) deliver or aim cutting blows. 3 reduce (prices etc.) Drastically. 4 censure vigorously. â€”n. 1 slashing cut or stroke. 2 printing oblique stroke; solidus. 3 slang act of urinating. [origin unknown]'),
(33842, 's', '\r\nSlat', ' n. Thin narrow piece of wood, plastic, or metal, esp. As in a fence or venetian blind. [french esclat splinter]'),
(33843, 's', '\r\nSlate', ' â€”n. 1 (esp. Bluish-grey) metamorphic rock easily split into flat smooth plates. 2 piece of this as a tile or hist. For writing on. 3 bluish-grey colour of slate. 4 list of nominees for office etc. â€”v. (-ting) 1 roof with slates. 2 colloq. Criticize severely. 3 us make arrangements for (an event etc.). 4 us nominate for office etc. â€”adj.'),
(33844, 's', '\r\nSlattern', ' n. Slovenly woman.  slatternly adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(33845, 's', '\r\nSlaughter', ' â€”v. 1 kill (animals) for food or skins or because of disease. 2 kill (people) ruthlessly or on a great scale. 3 colloq. Defeat utterly. â€”n. Act of slaughtering.  slaughterer n. [old norse: related to *slay]'),
(33846, 's', '\r\nSlaughterhouse', ' n. Place for the slaughter of animals as food.'),
(33847, 's', '\r\nSlav', ' â€”n. Member of a group of peoples in central and eastern europe speaking slavonic languages. â€”adj. Of the slavs. [latin sclavus, ethnic name]'),
(33848, 's', '\r\nSlave', ' â€”n. 1 person who is owned by and has to serve another. 2 drudge, hard worker. 3 (foll. By of, to) obsessive devotee (slave of fashion). 4 machine, or part of one, directly controlled by another. â€”v. (-ving) (often foll. By at, over) work very hard. [french esclave from latin sclavus *slav (captive)]'),
(33849, 's', '\r\nSlave-driver', ' n. 1 overseer of slaves. 2 demanding boss.'),
(33850, 's', '\r\nSlave', 'labour  n. Forced labour.'),
(33851, 's', '\r\nSlaver1', ' n. Hist. Ship or person engaged in the slave-trade.'),
(33852, 's', '\r\nSlaver2', ' â€”v. 1 dribble. 2 (foll. By over) drool over. â€”n. 1 dribbling saliva. 2 a fulsome flattery. B drivel, nonsense. [low german or dutch]'),
(33853, 's', '\r\nSlavery', ' n. 1 condition of a slave. 2 drudgery. 3 practice of having slaves.'),
(33854, 's', '\r\nSlave-trade', ' n. Hist. Dealing in slaves, esp. African blacks.'),
(33855, 's', '\r\nSlavic', ' adj. & n. = *slavonic.'),
(33856, 's', '\r\nSlavish', ' adj. 1 like slaves. 2 without originality.  slavishly adv.'),
(33857, 's', '\r\nSlavonic', ' â€”adj. 1 of the group of languages including russian, polish, and czech. 2 of the slavs. â€”n. Slavonic language-group. [related to *slav]'),
(33858, 's', '\r\nSlay', ' v. (past slew; past part. Slain) 1 literary = *kill 1. 2 = *kill 4. slayer n. [old english]'),
(33859, 's', '\r\nSleaze', ' n. Colloq. Sleaziness. [back-formation from *sleazy]'),
(33860, 's', '\r\nSleazy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) squalid, tawdry.  sleazily adv. Sleaziness n. [origin unknown]'),
(33861, 's', '\r\nSled', ' us â€”n. Sledge. â€”v. (-dd-) ride on a sledge. [low german]'),
(33862, 's', '\r\nSledge', ' â€”n. Vehicle on runners for use on snow. â€”v. (-ging) travel or convey by sledge. [dutch sleedse]'),
(33863, 's', '\r\nSledgehammer', ' n. 1 large heavy long-handled hammer used to break stone etc. 2 (attrib.) Heavy or powerful (sledgehammer blow). [old english slecg: related to *slay]'),
(33864, 's', '\r\nSleek', ' â€”adj. 1 (of hair, skin, etc.) Smooth and glossy. 2 looking well-fed and comfortable. â€”v. Make sleek.  sleekly adv. Sleekness n. [var. Of *slick]'),
(33865, 's', '\r\nSleep', ' â€”n. 1 natural recurring condition of suspended consciousness, with the eyes closed and the muscles relaxed. 2 period of sleep (had a sleep). 3 state like sleep; rest, quiet, death. â€”v. (past and past part. Slept) 1 a be in a state of sleep. B fall asleep. 2 (foll. By at, in, etc.) Spend the night. 3 provide beds etc. For (house sleeps six). 4 (foll. By with, together) have sexual intercourse, esp. In bed. 5 (foll. By on) put off (a decision) until the next day. 6 (foll. By through) fail to be woken by. 7 be inactive or dead. 8 (foll. By off) remedy by sleeping.  get to sleep manage to fall asleep. Go to sleep 1 begin to sleep. 2 (of a limb) become numb. Put to sleep 1 anaesthetize. 2 put down (an animal). Sleep around colloq. Be sexually promiscuous. Sleep in sleep later than usual in the morning. [old english]'),
(33866, 's', '\r\nSleeper', ' n. 1 person or animal that sleeps. 2 horizontal beam supporting a railway track. 3 a sleeping-car. B berth in this. 4 ring or stud worn in a pierced ear to keep the hole open.'),
(33867, 's', '\r\nSleeping-bag', ' n. Padded bag to sleep in when camping etc.'),
(33868, 's', '\r\nSleeping-car', ' n. (also sleeping-carriage) railway coach with berths.'),
(33869, 's', '\r\nSleeping', 'partner  n. Partner not sharing in the actual work of a firm.'),
(33870, 's', '\r\nSleeping-pill', ' n. Pill to induce sleep.'),
(33871, 's', '\r\nSleeping', 'policeman  n. Ramp etc. In the road to make traffic slow down.'),
(33872, 's', '\r\nSleeping', 'sickness  n. Tropical disease causing extreme lethargy.'),
(33873, 's', '\r\nSleepless', ' adj. 1 lacking sleep (sleepless night). 2 unable to sleep. 3 continually active.  sleeplessness n.'),
(33874, 's', '\r\nSleepwalk', ' v. Walk about while asleep.  sleepwalker n.'),
(33875, 's', '\r\nSleepy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 drowsy. 2 quiet, inactive (sleepy town).  sleepily adv. Sleepiness n.'),
(33876, 's', '\r\nSleet', ' â€”n. 1 snow and rain falling together. 2 hail or snow melting as it falls. â€”v. (prec. By it as subject) sleet falls (it is sleeting).  sleety adj. [old english]'),
(33877, 's', '\r\nSleeve', ' n. 1 part of a garment that encloses an arm. 2 cover of a gramophone record. 3 tube enclosing a rod etc.  up one''s sleeve in reserve.  sleeved adj. (also in comb.). Sleeveless adj. [old english]'),
(33878, 's', '\r\nSleigh', ' â€”n. Sledge, esp. For riding on. â€”v. Travel on a sleigh. [dutch slee: related to *sledge]'),
(33879, 's', '\r\nSleight', 'of hand  n. Dexterity, esp. In conjuring. [old norse: related to *sly]'),
(33880, 's', '\r\nSlender', ' adj. (-er, -est) 1 a of small girth or breadth. B gracefully thin. 2 relatively small, scanty, inadequate. [origin unknown]'),
(33881, 's', '\r\nSlept', ' past and past part. Of *sleep.'),
(33882, 's', '\r\nSleuth', ' colloq. â€”n. Detective. â€”v. Investigate crime etc. [old norse]'),
(33883, 's', '\r\nSlew1', ' (also slue) â€”v. (often foll. By round) turn or swing forcibly to a new position. â€”n. Such a turn. [origin unknown]'),
(33884, 's', '\r\nSlew2', ' past of *slay.'),
(33885, 's', '\r\nSlice', ' â€”n. 1 thin flat piece or wedge of esp. Food cut off or out. 2 share; part. 3 long-handled kitchen utensil with a broad flat perforated blade. 4 sport stroke that sends the ball obliquely. â€”v. (-cing) 1 (often foll. By up) cut into slices. 2 (foll. By off) cut off. 3 (foll. By into, through) cut (as) with a knife. 4  strike (a ball) with a slice. [french esclice from germanic]'),
(33886, 's', '\r\nSlick', ' â€”adj. Colloq. 1 a skilful or efficient. B superficially or pretentiously smooth and dexterous; glib. 2 sleek, smooth. â€”n. Large patch of oil etc., esp. On the sea. â€”v. Colloq. 1 (usu. Foll. By back, down) flatten (one''s hair etc.). 2 (usu. Foll. By up) make sleek or smart.  slickly adv. Slickness n. [old english]'),
(33887, 's', '\r\nSlide', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Slid) 1 move along a smooth surface with continuous contact on the same part of the thing moving. 2 move quietly or smoothly; glide. 3 glide over ice without skates. 4 (foll. By over) barely touch upon (a delicate subject etc.). 5 (often foll. By into) move quietly or unobtrusively. â€”n. 1 act of sliding. 2 rapid decline. 3 inclined plane down which children, goods, etc., slide. 4 track made by or for sliding, esp. On ice. 5 part of a machine or instrument that slides. 6 a mounted transparency viewed with a projector. B piece of glass holding an object for a microscope. 7 = *hair-slide.  let things slide be negligent; allow deterioration. [old english]'),
(33888, 's', '\r\nSlide-rule', ' n. Ruler with a sliding central strip, graduated logarithmically for making rapid calculations.'),
(33889, 's', '\r\nSliding', 'scale  n. Scale of fees, taxes, wages, etc., that varies according to some other factor.'),
(33890, 's', '\r\nSlight', ' â€”adj. 1 a small; insignificant. B inadequate. 2 slender, frail-looking. 3 (in superl.) Any whatever (if there were the slightest chance). â€”v. Treat disrespectfully; ignore. â€”n. Act of slighting.  slightly adv. Slightness n. [old norse]'),
(33891, 's', '\r\nSlim', ' â€”adj. (slimmer, slimmest) 1 not fat, slender. 2 small, insufficient (slim chance). â€”v. (-mm-) (often foll. By down) 1 become slimmer by dieting, exercise, etc. 2 make smaller (slimmed it down to 40 pages).  slimmer n. Slimming n. & adj. Slimmish adj. [low german or dutch]'),
(33892, 's', '\r\nSlime', ' n. Thick slippery mud or sticky substance produced by an animal or plant. [old english]'),
(33893, 's', '\r\nSlimline', ' adj. 1 of slender design. 2 (of a drink) not fattening.'),
(33894, 's', '\r\nSlimy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like, covered with, or full of slime. 2 colloq. Disgustingly obsequious.  sliminess n.'),
(33895, 's', '\r\nSling1', ' â€”n. 1 strap etc. Used to support or raise a thing. 2 bandage supporting an injured arm from the neck. 3 strap etc. For firing a stone etc. By hand. â€”v. (past and past part. Slung) 1 colloq. Throw. 2 suspend with a sling.  sling one''s hook slang go away. [old norse or low german or dutch]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(33896, 's', '\r\nSling2', ' n. Sweetened drink of spirits (esp. Gin) and water. [origin unknown]'),
(33897, 's', '\r\nSling-back', ' n. Shoe held in place by a strap above the heel.'),
(33898, 's', '\r\nSlink', ' v. (past and past part. Slunk) (often foll. By off, away, by) move in a stealthy or guilty manner. [old english]'),
(33899, 's', '\r\nSlinky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (of a garment) close-fitting and sinuous.'),
(33900, 's', '\r\nSlip1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 slide unintentionally or momentarily; lose one''s footing or balance. 2 go or move with a sliding motion. 3 escape or fall from being slippery or not being held properly. 4 (often foll. By in, out, away) go unobserved or quietly. 5 a make a careless or slight error. B fall below standard. 6 place or slide stealthily or casually (slipped a coin to him). 7 release from restraint or connection. 8 move (a stitch) to the other needle without knitting it. 9 (foll. By on, off) pull (a garment) easily or hastily on or off. 10 escape from; evade (dog slipped its collar; slipped my mind). â€”n. 1 act of slipping. 2 careless or slight error. 3 a pillowcase. B petticoat. 4 (in sing. Or pl.) = *slipway. 5 cricket a fielder stationed for balls glancing off the bat to the off side. B (in sing. Or pl.) This position.  give a person the slip escape from; evade. Let slip 1 utter inadvertently. 2 miss (an opportunity). 3 release, esp. From a leash. Slip up colloq. Make a mistake. [probably from low german slippen]'),
(33901, 's', '\r\nSlip2', ' n. 1 small piece of paper, esp. For writing on. 2 piece cut from a plant for grafting or planting.  slip of a small and slim (slip of a girl). [low german or dutch]'),
(33902, 's', '\r\nSlip3', ' n. Clay and water mixture for decorating earthenware. [old english, = slime]'),
(33903, 's', '\r\nSlip-knot', ' n. 1 knot that can be undone by a pull. 2 running knot.'),
(33904, 's', '\r\nSlip', 'of the pen  n. (also slip of the tongue) small written (or spoken) mistake.'),
(33905, 's', '\r\nSlip-on', ' â€”attrib. Adj. Easily slipped on and off. â€”n. Slip-on shoe or garment.'),
(33906, 's', '\r\nSlippage', ' n. Act or an instance of slipping.'),
(33907, 's', '\r\nSlipped', 'disc  n. Displaced disc between vertebrae causing lumbar pain.'),
(33908, 's', '\r\nSlipper', ' n. Light loose soft indoor shoe.'),
(33909, 's', '\r\nSlippery', ' adj. 1 difficult to grasp, stand on, etc. Because smooth or wet. 2 unreliable, unscrupulous.  slipperiness n. [old english]'),
(33910, 's', '\r\nSlippery', 'slope  n. Course leading eventually to disaster.'),
(33911, 's', '\r\nSlippy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Slippery.  look (or be) slippy make haste.'),
(33912, 's', '\r\nSlip-road', ' n. Road for entering or leaving a motorway etc.'),
(33913, 's', '\r\nSlipshod', ' adj. Careless, slovenly.'),
(33914, 's', '\r\nSlipstream', ' n. Current of air or water driven back by a revolving propeller or a moving vehicle.'),
(33915, 's', '\r\nSlip-up', ' n. Colloq. Mistake.'),
(33916, 's', '\r\nSlipway', ' n. Ramp for building ships or landing boats.'),
(33917, 's', '\r\nSlit', ' â€”n. Straight narrow incision or opening. â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Slit) 1 make a slit in. 2 cut into strips. [old english]'),
(33918, 's', '\r\nSlither', ' â€”v. Slide unsteadily. â€”n. Act of slithering.  slithery adj. [var. Of slidder: related to *slide]'),
(33919, 's', '\r\nSliver', ' â€”n. Long thin piece cut or split off. â€”v. 1 break off as a sliver. 2 break or form into slivers. [old english]'),
(33920, 's', '\r\nSloane', ' n. (in full sloane ranger) slang fashionable and conventional upper-class young person.  sloaney adj. [sloane square in london, and lone ranger, cowboy hero]'),
(33921, 's', '\r\nSlob', ' n. Colloq. Derog. Lazy, untidy, or fat person. [irish slab mud]'),
(33922, 's', '\r\nSlobber', ' â€”v. 1 dribble. 2 (foll. By over) drool over. â€”n. Dribbling saliver.  slobbery adj. [dutch]'),
(33923, 's', '\r\nSloe', ' n. 1 = *blackthorn. 2 its small sour bluish-black fruit. [old english]'),
(33924, 's', '\r\nSlog', ' â€”v. (-gg-) 1 hit hard and usu. Wildly. 2 work or walk doggedly. â€”n. 1 hard random hit. 2 a hard steady work or walk. B spell of this. [origin unknown]'),
(33925, 's', '\r\nSlogan', ' n. 1 catchy phrase used in advertising etc. 2 party cry; watchword. [gaelic, = war cry]'),
(33926, 's', '\r\nSloop', ' n. Small one-masted fore-and-aft rigged vessel. [dutch sloep]'),
(33927, 's', '\r\nSlop', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (often foll. By over) spill over the edge of a vessel. 2 wet (the floor etc.) By slopping. â€”n. 1 liquid spilled or splashed. 2 sloppy language. 3 (in pl.) Dirty waste water or wine etc. From a kitchen, bedroom, or prison vessels. 4 (in sing. Or pl.) Unappetizing weak liquid food.  slop about move about in a slovenly manner. Slop out carry slops out (in prison etc.). [old english]'),
(33928, 's', '\r\nSlope', ' â€”n. 1 inclined position, direction, or state. 2 piece of rising or falling ground. 3 difference in level between the two ends or sides of a thing. 4 place for skiing on a mountain etc. â€”v. (-ping) 1 have or take a slope, slant. 2 cause to slope.  slope arms place one''s rifle in a sloping position against one''s shoulder. Slope off slang go away, esp. To evade work etc. [aslope crosswise]'),
(33929, 's', '\r\nSloppy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 wet, watery, too liquid. 2 careless, untidy. 3 foolishly sentimental.  sloppily adv. Sloppiness n.'),
(33930, 's', '\r\nSlosh', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By about) splash or flounder. 2 slang hit, esp. Heavily. 3 colloq. A pour (liquid) clumsily. B pour liquid on. â€”n. 1 slush. 2 act or sound of splashing. 3 slang heavy blow. [var. Of *slush]'),
(33931, 's', '\r\nSloshed', ' predic. Adj. Slang drunk.'),
(33932, 's', '\r\nSlot', ' â€”n. 1 slit in a machine etc. For a thing, esp. A coin, to be inserted. 2 slit, groove, etc. For a thing. 3 allotted place in a schedule, esp. In broadcasting. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 (often foll. By in, into) place or be placed (as if) into a slot. 2 provide with slots. [french esclot hollow of breast]'),
(33933, 's', '\r\nSloth', ' n. 1 laziness, indolence. 2 slow-moving s. American mammal that hangs upside down in trees. [from *slow]'),
(33934, 's', '\r\nSlothful', ' adj. Lazy.  slothfully adv.'),
(33935, 's', '\r\nSlot-machine', ' n. Machine worked by the insertion of a coin, esp. Selling small items or providing amusement.'),
(33936, 's', '\r\nSlouch', ' â€”v. Stand, move, or sit in a drooping fashion.  â€”n. 1 slouching posture or movement.  2 slang incompetent or slovenly worker etc. [origin unknown]'),
(33937, 's', '\r\nSlouch', 'hat  n. Hat with a wide flexible brim.'),
(33938, 's', '\r\nSlough1', ' n. Swamp, miry place. [old english]'),
(33939, 's', '\r\nSlough2', ' â€”n. Part that an animal casts or moults, esp. A snake''s cast skin. â€”v. (often foll. By off) cast or drop off as a slough. [origin unknown]'),
(33940, 's', '\r\nSlough', 'of despond  n. State of hopeless depression.'),
(33941, 's', '\r\nSlovak', ' â€”n. 1 native of slovakia in czechoslovakia. 2 language of slovakia, one of the two official languages of czechoslovakia. â€”adj. Of the slovaks or their language. [native name]'),
(33942, 's', '\r\nSloven', ' n. Untidy or careless person. [origin uncertain]'),
(33943, 's', '\r\nSlovenly', ' â€”adj. Careless and untidy; unmethodical. â€”adv. In a slovenly manner.  slovenliness n.'),
(33944, 's', '\r\nSlow', ' â€”adj. 1 a taking a relatively long time to do a thing (also foll. By of: slow of speech). B acting, moving, or done without speed, not quick. 2 not conducive to speed (slow route). 3 (of a clock etc.) Showing a time earlier than is correct. 4 (of a person) not understanding or learning readily. 5 dull, tedious. 6 slack, sluggish (business is slow). 7 (of a fire or oven) giving little heat. 8 photog. (of a film) needing long exposure. 9 reluctant; not hasty (slow to anger). â€”adv. Slowly (also in comb.: slow-moving traffic). â€”v. (usu. Foll. By down, up) 1 reduce one''s speed or the speed of (a vehicle etc.). 2 reduce one''s pace of life.  slowish adj. Slowly adv. Slowness n. [old english]'),
(33945, 's', '\r\nSlowcoach', ' n. Colloq. Slow person.'),
(33946, 's', '\r\nSlow-down', ' n. Action of slowing down.'),
(33947, 's', '\r\nSlow', 'motion  n. 1 speed of a film at which actions etc. Appear much slower than usual. 2 simulation of this in real action.'),
(33948, 's', '\r\nSlow-worm', ' n. Small european legless lizard. [old english slow of uncertain origin]'),
(33949, 's', '\r\nSludge', ' n. 1 thick greasy mud or sediment. 2 sewage.  sludgy adj. [cf. *slush]'),
(33950, 's', '\r\nSlue', ' var. Of *slew1.'),
(33951, 's', '\r\nSlug1', ' n. 1 small shell-less mollusc often destroying plants. 2 a bullet, esp. Of irregular shape. B missile for an airgun. 3 printing metal bar used in spacing. 4 mouthful of drink (esp. Spirits). [scandinavian]'),
(33952, 's', '\r\nSlug2', ' us â€”v. (-gg-) hit hard. â€”n. Hard blow.  slug it out fight it out. [origin unknown]'),
(33953, 's', '\r\nSluggard', ' n. Lazy person. [related to *slug1]'),
(33954, 's', '\r\nSluggish', ' adj. Inert; slow-moving.  sluggishly adv. Sluggishness n.'),
(33955, 's', '\r\nSluice', ' â€”n. 1 (also sluice-gate, sluice-valve) sliding gate or other contrivance for regulating the volume or flow of water. 2 water so regulated. 3 (sluice-way) artificial water-channel, esp. For washing ore. 4 place for rinsing. 5 act of rinsing. â€”v. (-cing) 1 provide or wash with a sluice or sluices. 2 rinse, esp. With running water. 3 (foll. By out, away) wash out or away with a flow of water. 4 (of water) rush out (as if) from a sluice. [french escluse]'),
(33956, 's', '\r\nSlum', ' â€”n. 1 house unfit for human habitation. 2 (often in pl.) Overcrowded and squalid district in a city. â€”v. (-mm-) visit slums, esp. Out of curiosity.  slum it colloq. Put up with conditions less comfortable than usual.  slummy adj. [originally cant] '),
(33957, 's', '\r\nSlumber', ' v. & n. Poet. Or joc. Sleep. [old english]'),
(33958, 's', '\r\nSlump', ' â€”n. Sudden severe or prolonged fall in prices and trade, usu. Bringing widespread unemployment. â€”v. 1 undergo a slump. 2 sit or fall heavily or limply. [imitative]'),
(33959, 's', '\r\nSlung', ' past and past part. Of *sling1.'),
(33960, 's', '\r\nSlunk', ' past and past part. Of *slink.'),
(33961, 's', '\r\nSlur', ' â€”v. (-rr-) 1 pronounce indistinctly with sounds running into one another. 2 mus. Perform (notes) legato. 3 archaic or us put a slur on (a person or a person''s character). 4 (usu. Foll. By over) pass over (a fact, fault, etc.) Lightly. â€”n. 1 imputation of wrongdoing. 2 act of slurring. 3 mus. Curved line joining notes to be slurred. [origin unknown]'),
(33962, 's', '\r\nSlurp', ' colloq. â€”v. Eat or esp. Drink noisily. â€”n. Sound of this. [dutch]'),
(33963, 's', '\r\nSlurry', ' n. Thin semi-liquid cement, mud, manure, etc. [related to dial. Slur thin mud]'),
(33964, 's', '\r\nSlush', ' n. 1 thawing muddy snow. 2 silly sentimentality.  slushy adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin unknown]'),
(33965, 's', '\r\nSlush', 'fund  n. Reserve fund, esp. For political bribery.'),
(33966, 's', '\r\nSlut', ' n. Derog. Slovenly or promiscuous woman.  sluttish adj. [origin unknown]'),
(33967, 's', '\r\nSly', ' adj. (slyer, slyest) 1 cunning, crafty, wily. 2 secretive. 3 knowing; insinuating.  on the sly secretly.  slyly adv. Slyness n. [old norse: related to *slay]'),
(33968, 's', '\r\nSm', ' symb. Samarium.'),
(33969, 's', '\r\nSmack1', ' â€”n. 1 sharp slap or blow. 2 hard hit at cricket etc. 3 loud kiss. 4 loud sharp sound. â€”v. 1 slap. 2 part (one''s lips) noisily in anticipation of food. 3 move, hit, etc., with a smack. â€”adv. Colloq. 1 with a smack. 2 suddenly; directly; violently. 3 exactly (smack in the centre).  a smack in the eye (or face) colloq. Rebuff; setback. [imitative]'),
(33970, 's', '\r\nSmack2', ' (foll. By of) â€”v. 1 have a flavour of; taste of. 2 suggest (smacks of nepotism). â€”n. 1 flavour. 2 barely discernible quality. [old english]'),
(33971, 's', '\r\nSmack3', ' n. Single-masted sailing-boat. [low german or dutch]'),
(33972, 's', '\r\nSmack4', ' n. Slang heroin or other hard drug. [probably alteration of yiddish schmeck sniff]'),
(33973, 's', '\r\nSmacker', ' n. Slang 1 loud kiss. 2 a Â£1. B us $1.'),
(33974, 's', '\r\nSmall', ' â€”adj. 1 not large or big. 2 not great in importance, amount, number, power, etc. 3 not much; little (paid small attention). 4 insignificant (from small beginnings). 5 of small particles (small shot). 6 on a small scale (small farmer). 7 poor or humble. 8 mean; ungenerous. 9 young (small child). â€”n. 1 slenderest part of a thing, esp. Of the back. 2 (in pl.) Colloq. Underwear, esp. As laundry. â€”adv. Into small pieces (chop it small).  feel (or look) small be humiliated or ashamed.  smallish adj. Smallness n. [old english]'),
(33975, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'arms  n.pl. Portable firearms.'),
(33976, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'beer  n. Trifling thing.'),
(33977, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'change  n. Coins, not notes.'),
(33978, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'fry  n. Unimportant people; children.'),
(33979, 's', '\r\nSmallholder', ' n. Farmer of a smallholding.'),
(33980, 's', '\r\nSmallholding', ' n.  Agricultural holding smaller than a farm.'),
(33981, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'hours  n.pl. Period soon after midnight.'),
(33982, 's', '\r\nSmall-minded', ' adj. Petty; narrow in outlook.'),
(33983, 's', '\r\nSmallpox', ' n. Hist. Acute contagious disease with fever and pustules, usu. Leaving scars.'),
(33984, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'print  n. Unfavourable clauses etc. In a contract, usu. Printed small.'),
(33985, 's', '\r\nSmall-scale', ' adj. Made or occurring on a small scale.'),
(33986, 's', '\r\nSmall', 'talk  n. Light social conversation.'),
(33987, 's', '\r\nSmall-time', ' adj. Colloq. Unimportant, petty.'),
(33988, 's', '\r\nSmarm', ' â€”v. Colloq. 1 (often foll. By down) smooth, plaster flat (hair etc.). 2 be ingratiating. â€”n. Colloq. Obsequiousness. [dial.]'),
(33989, 's', '\r\nSmarmy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Ingratiating.  smarmily adv. Smarminess n.'),
(33990, 's', '\r\nSmart', ' â€”adj. 1 well-groomed, neat. 2 brightly coloured, newly painted, etc. 3 stylish, fashionable. 4 (esp. Us) clever, ingenious, quickwitted. 5 quick, brisk. 6 painfully severe; sharp, vigorous. â€”v. 1 feel or give pain. 2 rankle. 3 (foll. By for) suffer the consequences of. â€”n. Sharp pain; stinging sensation. â€”adv. Smartly.  smartish adj. & adv. Smartly adv. Smartness n. [old english]'),
(33991, 's', '\r\nSmart', 'alec  n. (also smart aleck) colloq. Conceited know-all.'),
(33992, 's', '\r\nSmarten', ' v. (usu. Foll. By up) make or become smart.'),
(33993, 's', '\r\nSmart', 'money  n. Money invested by people with expert knowledge.'),
(33994, 's', '\r\nSmash', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) a break into pieces; shatter. B bring or come to sudden destruction, defeat, or disaster. 2 (foll. By into, through) move with great force. 3 (foll. By in) break with a crushing blow. 4  hit (a ball etc.) With great force, esp. Downwards. â€”n. 1 act of smashing, collision. 2 sound of this. 3 (in full smash hit) very successful play, song, performer, etc. â€”adv. With a smash. [imitative]'),
(33995, 's', '\r\nSmash-and-grab', ' n. Robbery in which a shop-window is smashed and goods seized.'),
(33996, 's', '\r\nSmasher', ' n. Colloq. Beautiful or pleasing person or thing.'),
(33997, 's', '\r\nSmashing', ' adj. Colloq. Excellent, wonderful.'),
(33998, 's', '\r\nSmash-up', ' n. Violent collision.'),
(33999, 's', '\r\nSmattering', ' n. Slight superficial knowledge of a language etc. [origin unknown]'),
(34000, 's', '\r\nSmear', ' â€”v. 1 daub or mark with grease etc. 2 smudge. 3 defame. â€”n. 1 act of smearing. 2 med. A material smeared on a microscopic slide etc. For examination. B specimen of this.  smeary adj. [old english]'),
(34001, 's', '\r\nSmear', 'test  n. = *cervical smear.'),
(34002, 's', '\r\nSmell', ' â€”n. 1 faculty of perceiving odours. 2 quality in substances that is perceived by this. 3 unpleasant odour. 4 act of inhaling to ascertain smell. â€”v. (past and past part. Smelt or smelled) 1 perceive or examine by smell. 2 emit an odour; stink. 3 seem by smell to be (smells sour). 4 (foll. By of) a emit the odour of (smells of fish). B be suggestive of (smells of dishonesty). 5 perceive; detect (smell a bargain). 6 have or use a sense of smell.  smell a rat suspect trickery etc. Smell out detect by smell or investigation. [old english]'),
(34003, 's', '\r\nSmelling-salts', ' n.pl. Sharp-smelling substances sniffed to relieve faintness etc.'),
(34004, 's', '\r\nSmelly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having a strong or unpleasant smell.  smelliness n.'),
(34005, 's', '\r\nSmelt1', ' v. 1 extract metal from (ore) by melting. 2 extract (metal) in this way.  smelter n. [low german or dutch smelten]'),
(34006, 's', '\r\nSmelt2', ' past and past part. Of *smell.'),
(34007, 's', '\r\nSmelt3', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) small edible green and silver fish. [old english]'),
(34008, 's', '\r\nSmidgen', ' n. (also smidgin) colloq. Small bit or amount. [perhaps from smitch in the same sense]'),
(34009, 's', '\r\nSmilax', ' n. Any of several climbing shrubs.  [greek, = bindweed]'),
(34010, 's', '\r\nSmile', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 have or assume a happy, kind, or amused expression, with the corners of the mouth turned up. 2 express by smiling (smiled a welcome). 3 give (a smile) of a specified kind (smiled a sardonic smile). 4 (foll. By on, upon) favour (fortune smiled on me). â€”n. 1 act of smiling. 2 smiling expression or aspect. [perhaps from scandinavian]'),
(34011, 's', '\r\nSmirch', ' â€”v. Soil; discredit. â€”n. Spot, stain. [origin unknown]'),
(34012, 's', '\r\nSmirk', ' â€”n. Conceited or silly smile. â€”v. Give a smirk. [old english]'),
(34013, 's', '\r\nSmite', ' v. (-ting; past smote; past part. Smitten) 1 archaic or literary a hit. B chastise; defeat. 2 (in passive) affect strongly; seize (smitten with regret; smitten by her beauty). [old english]'),
(34014, 's', '\r\nSmith', ' n. 1 blacksmith. 2 (esp. In comb.) Worker in metal (goldsmith). 3 (esp. In comb.) Craftsman (wordsmith). [old english]'),
(34015, 's', '\r\nSmithereens', ' n.pl. Small fragments. [dial. Smithers]'),
(34016, 's', '\r\nSmithy', ' n. (pl. -ies) blacksmith''s workshop, forge. [related to *smith]'),
(34017, 's', '\r\nSmitten', ' past part. Of *smite.'),
(34018, 's', '\r\nSmock', ' â€”n. 1 loose shirtlike garment often ornamented with smocking. 2 loose overall. â€”v. Adorn with smocking. [old english]'),
(34019, 's', '\r\nSmocking', ' n. Ornamental effect on cloth made by gathering it tightly with stitches.'),
(34020, 's', '\r\nSmog', ' n. Smoke-laden fog.  smoggy adj. (-ier, -iest). [portmanteau word]'),
(34021, 's', '\r\nSmoke', ' â€”n. 1 visible vapour from a burning substance. 2 act of smoking tobacco. 3 colloq. Cigarette or cigar. â€”v. (-king) 1 a inhale and exhale the smoke of (a cigarette etc.). B do this habitually. 2 emit smoke or visible vapour. 3 darken or preserve with smoke (smoked salmon).  go up in smoke colloq. Come to nothing. Smoke out 1 drive out by means of smoke. 2 drive out of hiding etc. [old english]'),
(34022, 's', '\r\nSmoke', 'bomb  n. Bomb that emits dense smoke on exploding.'),
(34023, 's', '\r\nSmoke-free', ' adj. 1 free from smoke. 2 where smoking is not permitted.'),
(34024, 's', '\r\nSmokeless', ' adj. Producing little or no smoke; free from smoke.'),
(34025, 's', '\r\nSmokeless', 'zone  n. District where only smokeless fuel may be used.'),
(34026, 's', '\r\nSmoker', ' n. 1 person who habitually smokes. 2 compartment on a train where smoking is allowed.'),
(34027, 's', '\r\nSmokescreen', ' n. 1 cloud of smoke concealing (esp. Military) operations. 2 ruse for disguising one''s activities.'),
(34028, 's', '\r\nSmokestack', ' n. 1 chimney or funnel of a locomotive or steamer. 2 tall chimney.'),
(34029, 's', '\r\nSmoky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 emitting, filled with, or obscured by, smoke. 2 stained with or coloured like smoke. 3 having the flavour of smoked food.   smokiness n.'),
(34030, 's', '\r\nSmolder', ' (brit. Smoulder) â€”v. 1 burn slowly without flame or internally. 2 (of emotions) be fierce but suppressed. 3 (of a person) show silent emotion. â€”n. Smouldering. [origin unknown]'),
(34031, 's', '\r\nSmooch', ' colloq. â€”n. 1  period of slow close dancing. 2 period of kissing and caressing. â€”v. Engage in a smooch.  smoochy adj. [imitative]'),
(34032, 's', '\r\nSmooth', ' â€”adj. 1 having an even surface; free from projections, dents, and roughness. 2 that can be traversed without check. 3 (of the sea etc.) Calm, flat. 4 (of a journey etc.) Easy. 5 not harsh in sound or taste. 6 suave, conciliatory; slick. 7 not jerky. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By out, down) make or become smooth. 2 (often foll. By out, down, over, away) reduce or get rid of (differences, faults, difficulties, etc.) In fact or appearance. â€”n. Smoothing touch or stroke. â€”adv. Smoothly.  smoothly adv. Smoothness n. [old english]'),
(34033, 's', '\r\nSmoothie', ' n. Colloq., often derog. Smooth person.'),
(34034, 's', '\r\nSmooth-tongued', ' adj. Insincerely flattering.'),
(34035, 's', '\r\nSmorgasbord', ' n. Various esp. Savoury dishes as hors d''Å“uvres or a buffet meal. [swedish]'),
(34036, 's', '\r\nSmote', ' past of *smite.'),
(34037, 's', '\r\nSmother', ' v. 1 suffocate, stifle. 2 (foll. By in, with) overwhelm or cover with (kisses, gifts, kindness, etc.). 3 extinguish (a fire) by covering it. 4 a die of suffocation. B have difficulty breathing. 5 (often foll. By up) suppress or conceal. [old english]'),
(34038, 's', '\r\nSmoulder', ' (us smolder) â€”v. 1 burn slowly without flame or internally. 2 (of emotions) be fierce but suppressed. 3 (of a person) show silent emotion. â€”n. Smouldering. [origin unknown]'),
(34039, 's', '\r\nSmudge', ' â€”n. Blurred or smeared line, mark, blot, etc. â€”v. (-ging) 1 make a smudge on or of. 2 become smeared or blurred.  smudgy adj. [origin unknown]'),
(34040, 's', '\r\nSmug', ' adj. (smugger, smuggest) self-satisfied.  smugly adv. Smugness n. [low german smuk pretty]'),
(34041, 's', '\r\nSmuggle', ' v. (-ling) 1 (also absol.) Import or export illegally, esp. Without paying duties. 2 (foll. By in, out) convey secretly.  smuggler n. Smuggling n. [low german]'),
(34042, 's', '\r\nSmut', ' â€”n. 1 small flake of soot etc. 2 spot or smudge made by this. 3 obscene talk, pictures, or stories. 4 fungous disease of cereals. â€”v. (-tt-) mark with smuts. smutty adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin unknown]'),
(34043, 's', '\r\nSn', ' symb. Tin. [latin tannun]'),
(34044, 's', '\r\nSnack', ' n. 1 light, casual, or hurried meal. 2 small amount of food eaten between meals. [dutch]'),
(34045, 's', '\r\nSnack', 'bar  n. Place where snacks are sold.'),
(34046, 's', '\r\nSnaffle', ' â€”n. (in full snaffle-bit) simple bridle-bit without a curb. â€”v. (-ling) colloq. Steal; seize. [low german or dutch perhaps from snavel beak]'),
(34047, 's', '\r\nSnafu', ' slang â€”adj. In utter confusion. â€”n. This state. [acronym of â€˜situation normal: all fouled (or fucked) upâ€™]'),
(34048, 's', '\r\nSnag', ' â€”n. 1 unexpected obstacle or drawback. 2 jagged projection. 3 tear in material etc. â€”v. (-gg-) catch or tear on a snag. [probably scandinavian]'),
(34049, 's', '\r\nSnail', ' n. Slow-moving gastropod mollusc with a spiral shell. [old english]'),
(34050, 's', '\r\nSnail''s', 'pace  n. Very slow movement.'),
(34051, 's', '\r\nSnake', ' â€”n. 1 long limbless reptile. 2 (also snake in the grass) traitor; secret enemy. â€”v. (-king) move or twist like a snake. [old english]'),
(34052, 's', '\r\nSnake-charmer', ' n. Person appearing to make snakes move by music etc.'),
(34053, 's', '\r\nSnakes', 'and ladders  n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Board-game with counters moved up â€˜laddersâ€™ and down â€˜snakesâ€™.'),
(34054, 's', '\r\nSnakeskin', ' â€”n. Skin of a snake. â€”adj. Made of snakeskin.'),
(34055, 's', '\r\nSnaky', ' adj. 1 of or like a snake. 2 winding, sinuous. 3 cunning, treacherous.'),
(34056, 's', '\r\nSnap', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 break suddenly or with a cracking sound. 2 (cause to) emit a sudden sharp crack. 3 open or close with a snapping sound. 4 speak or say irritably. 5 (often foll. By at) make a sudden audible bite. 6 move quickly (snap into action). 7  photograph. â€”n. 1 act or sound of snapping. 2 crisp biscuit (brandy snap). 3 snapshot. 4 (in full cold snap) sudden brief spell of cold weather. 5  a card-game in which players call â€˜snapâ€™ when two similar cards are exposed. B (as int.) On noticing an (often unexpected) similarity. 6 vigour, liveliness. â€”adv. With a snap (heard it go snap). â€”adj. Done without forethought (snap decision).  snap out of slang get rid of (a mood, etc.) By a sudden effort. Snap up accept (an offer etc.) Quickly or eagerly. [low german or dutch snappen seize]'),
(34057, 's', '\r\nSnapdragon', ' n. Plant with a two-lipped flower.'),
(34058, 's', '\r\nSnap-fastener', ' n. = *press-stud.'),
(34059, 's', '\r\nSnapper', ' n. Any of several edible marine fish.'),
(34060, 's', '\r\nSnappish', ' adj. 1 curt; ill-tempered; sharp. 2 inclined to snap.'),
(34061, 's', '\r\nSnappy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 brisk, lively. 2 neat and elegant (snappy dresser). 3 snappish.  make it snappy be quick.  snappily adv.'),
(34062, 's', '\r\nSnapshot', ' n. Casual or informal photograph.'),
(34063, 's', '\r\nSnare', ' â€”n. 1 trap, esp. With a noose, for birds or animals. 2 trap, trick, or temptation. 3 (in sing. Or pl.) Twisted strings of gut, hide, or wire stretched across the lower head of a side-drum to produce a rattle. 4 (in full snare drum) drum fitted with snares. â€”v. (-ring) catch in a snare; trap. [old norse]'),
(34064, 's', '\r\nSnarl1', ' â€”v. 1 growl with bared teeth. 2 speak, say, or express angrily. â€”n. Act or sound of snarling. [snar from low german]'),
(34065, 's', '\r\nSnarl2', ' â€”v. (often foll. By up) twist; entangle; hamper the movement of (traffic etc.); become entangled or congested. â€”n. Knot, tangle. [from *snare]'),
(34066, 's', '\r\nSnarl-up', ' n. Colloq. Traffic jam; muddle.'),
(34067, 's', '\r\nSnatch', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By away, from) seize or remove quickly, eagerly, or unexpectedly. 2 a steal (a handbag etc.) By grabbing. B slang kidnap. 3 secure with difficulty. 4 (foll. By at) a try to seize. B take (an offer etc.) Eagerly. â€”n. 1 act of snatching. 2 fragment of a song or talk etc. 3 us slang kidnapping. 4 short spell of activity etc. [related to *snack]'),
(34068, 's', '\r\nSnazzy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang smart, stylish, showy.  snazzily adv. Snazziness n. [origin unknown]'),
(34069, 's', '\r\nSneak', ' â€”v. 1 (foll. By in, out, past, away, etc.) Go or convey furtively. 2 slang steal unobserved. 3 slang tell tales; turn informer. 4 (as sneaking adj.) A furtive (sneaking affection). B persistent and puzzling (sneaking feeling). â€”n. 1 mean-spirited underhand person. 2 slang tell-tale; informer. â€”adj. Acting or done without warning; secret.  sneaky adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin uncertain]'),
(34070, 's', '\r\nSneaker', ' n. Slang soft-soled canvas shoe.'),
(34071, 's', '\r\nSneak-thief', ' n. Thief who steals without breaking in.'),
(34072, 's', '\r\nSneer', ' â€”n. Contemptuous smile or remark. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By at) smile or speak derisively. 2 say with a sneer.  sneering adj. Sneeringly adv. [origin unknown]'),
(34073, 's', '\r\nSneeze', ' â€”n. Sudden loud involuntary expulsion of air from the nose and mouth caused by irritation of the nostrils. â€”v. (-zing) make a sneeze.  not to be sneezed at colloq. Worth having or considering. [old english]'),
(34074, 's', '\r\nSnick', ' â€”v. 1 make a small notch or incision in. 2 cricket deflect (the ball) slightly with the bat. â€”n. 1 small notch or cut. 2 cricket slight deflection of the ball. [snickersnee long knife, ultimately from dutch]'),
(34075, 's', '\r\nSnicker', ' n. & v. = *snigger. [imitative]'),
(34076, 's', '\r\nSnide', ' adj. Sneering; slyly derogatory. [origin unknown]'),
(34077, 's', '\r\nSniff', ' â€”v. 1 inhale air audibly through the nose. 2 (often foll. By up) draw in through the nose. 3 smell the scent of by sniffing. â€”n. 1 act or sound of sniffing. 2 amount of air etc. Sniffed up.  sniff at show contempt for. Sniff out = smell out. [imitative]'),
(34078, 's', '\r\nSniffer', ' n. Person who sniffs, esp. A drug etc. (often in comb.: glue-sniffer).'),
(34079, 's', '\r\nSniffer-dog', ' n. Colloq. Dog trained to sniff out drugs or explosives.'),
(34080, 's', '\r\nSniffle', ' â€”v. (-ling) sniff slightly or repeatedly. â€”n. 1 act of sniffling. 2 (in sing. Or pl.) Cold in the head causing sniffling. [imitative: cf. *snivel]'),
(34081, 's', '\r\nSniffy', ' adj. Colloq. (-ier, -iest) disdainful.  sniffily adv. Sniffiness n.'),
(34082, 's', '\r\nSnifter', ' n. Slang small alcoholic drink. [dial. Snift sniff]'),
(34083, 's', '\r\nSnigger', ' â€”n. Half-suppressed laugh. â€”v. Utter this. [var. Of *snicker]'),
(34084, 's', '\r\nSnip', ' â€”v. (-pp-) (also absol.) Cut with scissors etc., esp. In small quick strokes. â€”n. 1 act of snipping. 2 piece snipped off. 3 slang a something easily done. B  bargain. [low german or dutch snippen]'),
(34085, 's', '\r\nSnipe', ' â€”n. (pl. Same or -s) wading bird with a long straight bill. â€”v. (-ping) 1  fire shots from hiding, usu. At long range. 2 (often foll. By at) make a sly critical attack.  sniper n. (in sense 1 of v.). [probably scandinavian]'),
(34086, 's', '\r\nSnippet', ' n. 1 small piece cut off. 2 (usu. In pl.) A scrap of information etc. B short extract from a book etc.'),
(34087, 's', '\r\nSnitch', ' slang â€”v. 1 steal. 2 (often foll. By on) inform on a person. â€”n. Informer. [origin unknown]'),
(34088, 's', '\r\nSnivel', ' â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 weep with sniffling. 2 run at the nose; sniffle. 3 show weak or tearful sentiment. â€”n. Act of snivelling. [old english]'),
(34089, 's', '\r\nSnob', ' n. Person who despises those inferior in social position, wealth, intellect, taste, etc. (intellectual snob).  snobbery n. Snobbish adj. Snobby adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin unknown]'),
(34090, 's', '\r\nSnog', ' slang â€”v. (-gg-) engage in kissing and caressing. â€”n. Period of this. [origin unknown]'),
(34091, 's', '\r\nSnood', ' n. Ornamental hairnet, worn usu. At the back of the head. [old english]'),
(34092, 's', '\r\nSnook', ' n. Slang contemptuous gesture with the thumb to the nose and the fingers spread.  cock a snook (often foll. By at) 1 make this gesture. 2 register one''s contempt. [origin unknown]'),
(34093, 's', '\r\nSnooker', ' â€”n. 1 game played on an oblong cloth-covered table with a cue-ball, 15 red, and 6 coloured balls. 2 position in this game in which a direct shot would lose points. â€”v. 1 (also refl.) Subject (oneself or an opponent) to a snooker. 2 (esp. As snookered adj.) Slang thwart, defeat. [origin unknown]'),
(34094, 's', '\r\nSnoop', ' colloq. â€”v. 1 pry into another''s affairs. 2 (often foll. By about, around) investigate transgressions of rules, the law, etc. â€”n. Act of snooping.  snooper n. Snoopy adj. [dutch]'),
(34095, 's', '\r\nSnooty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Supercilious; conceited; snobbish.  snootily adv. [origin unknown]'),
(34096, 's', '\r\nSnooze', ' colloq. â€”n. Short sleep, nap. â€”v. (-zing) take a snooze. [origin unknown]'),
(34097, 's', '\r\nSnore', ' â€”n. Snorting or grunting sound of breathing during sleep. â€”v. (-ring) make this sound. [imitative]'),
(34098, 's', '\r\nSnorkel', '  â€”n. 1 breathing-tube for an underwater swimmer. 2 device for supplying air to a submerged submarine. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) use a snorkel. [german schnorchel]'),
(34099, 's', '\r\nSnort', ' â€”n. 1 explosive sound made esp. By horses by the sudden forcing of breath through the nose. 2 similar human sound showing contempt, incredulity, etc. 3 colloq. Small drink of liquor. 4 slang inhaled dose of powdered cocaine etc. â€”v. 1 make a snort. 2 (also absol.) Slang inhale (esp. Cocaine). 3 express or utter with a snort. [imitative]'),
(34100, 's', '\r\nSnot', ' n. Slang nasal mucus. [probably low german or dutch: related to *snout]'),
(34101, 's', '\r\nSnotty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang 1 running or covered with nasal mucus. 2 snooty. 3 mean, contemptible.  snottily adv. Snottiness n.'),
(34102, 's', '\r\nSnout', ' n. 1 projecting nose and mouth of an animal. 2 derog. Person''s nose. 3 pointed front of a thing. [low german or dutch]'),
(34103, 's', '\r\nSnow', ' â€”n. 1 frozen atmospheric vapour falling to earth in light white flakes. 2 fall or layer of this. 3 thing resembling snow in whiteness or texture etc. 4 slang cocaine. â€”v. 1 (prec. By it as subject) snow falls (it is snowing; if it snows). 2 (foll. By in, over, up, etc.) Confine or block with snow.  be snowed under be overwhelmed, esp. With work. [old english]'),
(34104, 's', '\r\nSnowball', ' â€”n. Ball of compressed snow for throwing in play. â€”v. 1 throw or pelt with snowballs. 2 increase rapidly.'),
(34105, 's', '\r\nSnowball-tree', ' n. Guelder rose.'),
(34106, 's', '\r\nSnowberry', ' n. (pl. -ies) shrub with white berries.'),
(34107, 's', '\r\nSnow-blind', ' adj. Temporarily blinded by the glare from snow.'),
(34108, 's', '\r\nSnowblower', ' n. Machine that clears snow by blowing.'),
(34109, 's', '\r\nSnowbound', ' adj. Prevented by snow from going out or travelling.'),
(34110, 's', '\r\nSnowcap', ' n. Snow-covered mountain peak.  snowcapped adj.'),
(34111, 's', '\r\nSnowdrift', ' n. Bank of snow heaped up by the wind.'),
(34112, 's', '\r\nSnowdrop', ' n. Early spring plant with white drooping flowers.'),
(34113, 's', '\r\nSnowfall', ' n. 1 fall of snow. 2 amount of this.'),
(34114, 's', '\r\nSnowflake', ' n. Each of the flakes in which snow falls.'),
(34115, 's', '\r\nSnow', 'goose  n. White arctic goose.'),
(34116, 's', '\r\nSnowline', ' n. Level above which snow never melts entirely.'),
(34117, 's', '\r\nSnowman', ' n. Figure resembling a human, made of compressed snow.'),
(34118, 's', '\r\nSnowmobile', ' n. Motor vehicle, esp. With runners or caterpillar tracks, for travel over snow.'),
(34119, 's', '\r\nSnowplough', ' n. (us snowplow) device or vehicle for clearing roads of thick snow.'),
(34120, 's', '\r\nSnowplow', ' n. (brit. Snowplough) device or vehicle for clearing roads of thick snow.'),
(34121, 's', '\r\nSnowshoe', ' n. Racket-shaped attachment to a boot for walking on snow without sinking in.'),
(34122, 's', '\r\nSnowstorm', ' n. Heavy fall of snow, esp. With a high wind.'),
(34123, 's', '\r\nSnow', 'white  adj. & n. (as adj. Often hyphenated) pure white.'),
(34124, 's', '\r\nSnowy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like snow. 2 (of the weather etc.) With much snow.'),
(34125, 's', '\r\nSnowy', 'owl  n. Large white arctic owl.'),
(34126, 's', '\r\nSnp', ' abbr. Scottish national party.'),
(34127, 's', '\r\nSnr.', ' Abbr. Senior.'),
(34128, 's', '\r\nSnub', ' â€”v. (-bb-) rebuff or humiliate with sharp words or coldness. â€”n. Act of snubbing. â€”adj. Short and blunt in shape. [old norse, = chide]'),
(34129, 's', '\r\nSnub', 'nose  n. Short turned-up nose.  snub-nosed adj.'),
(34130, 's', '\r\nSnuff1', ' â€”n. Charred part of a candle-wick. â€”v. Trim the snuff from (a candle).  snuff it slang die. Snuff out 1 extinguish (a candle flame). 2 put an end to (hopes etc.). [origin unknown]'),
(34131, 's', '\r\nSnuff2', ' â€”n. Powdered tobacco or medicine taken by sniffing. â€”v. Take snuff. [dutch]'),
(34132, 's', '\r\nSnuffbox', ' n. Small box for holding snuff.'),
(34133, 's', '\r\nSnuffer', ' n. Device for snuffing or extinguishing a candle.'),
(34134, 's', '\r\nSnuffle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 make sniffing sounds. 2 speak or say nasally or whiningly. 3 breathe noisily, esp. With a blocked nose. â€”n. Snuffling sound or tone.  snuffly adj. [low german or dutch snuffelen]'),
(34135, 's', '\r\nSnug', ' â€”adj. (snugger, snuggest) 1 cosy, comfortable, sheltered. 2 close-fitting. â€”n. Small room in a pub.  snugly adv. [probably low german or dutch]'),
(34136, 's', '\r\nSnuggery', ' n. (pl. -ies) snug place, den.'),
(34137, 's', '\r\nSnuggle', ' v. (-ling) settle or draw into a warm comfortable position.'),
(34138, 's', '\r\nSo1', ' â€”adv. 1 to such an extent (stop complaining so; so small as to be invisible; not so late as i expected). 2 in this or that way; in the manner, position, or state described or implied (place your feet so; am not cold but may become so). 3 also (he went and so did i). 4 indeed, actually (you said it was good, and so it is). 5 very (i am so glad). 6 (with verbs of saying or thinking etc.) Thus, this, that (i think so; so he said). â€”conj. (often foll. By that) 1 consequently (was ill, so couldn''t come). 2 in order that (came early so that i could see you). 3 and then; as the next step (so then i gave up; and so to bed). 4 (introducing a question) then; after that (so what did you do?).  and so on (or forth) 1 and others of the same kind. 2 and in other similar ways. Or so approximately (50 or so). So as to in order to. So be it expression of acceptance or resignation. So long! Colloq. Goodbye. So much 1 a certain amount (of). 2 nothing but (so much nonsense). So much for that is all that need be done or said about (a thing). So so adj. & adv. Colloq. Only moderately good or well. So what? Colloq. That is not significant. [old english]'),
(34139, 's', '\r\nSo2', ' var. Of *soh.'),
(34140, 's', '\r\n-so', ' comb. Form = *-soever.'),
(34141, 's', '\r\nSoak', ' â€”v. 1 make or become thoroughly wet through saturation. 2 (of rain etc.) Drench. 3 (foll. By in, up) absorb (liquid, knowledge, etc.). 4 refl. (often foll. By in) steep (oneself) in a subject etc. 5 (foll. By in, into, through) (of liquid) go or penetrate by saturation. 6 colloq. Extort money from. 7 colloq. Drink heavily. â€”n. 1 act of soaking; prolonged spell in a bath. 2 colloq. Hard drinker. [old english]'),
(34142, 's', '\r\nSoakaway', ' n. Pit into which liquids may flow and then percolate slowly into the subsoil.'),
(34143, 's', '\r\nSoaking', ' adj. (in full soaking wet) wet through.'),
(34144, 's', '\r\nSo-and-so', ' n. (pl. -so''s) 1 particular but unspecified person or thing. 2 colloq. Objectionable person.'),
(34145, 's', '\r\nSoap', ' â€”n. 1 cleansing agent yielding lather when rubbed in water. 2 colloq. = *soap opera. â€”v. Apply soap to. [old english]'),
(34146, 's', '\r\nSoapbox', ' n. Makeshift stand for a speaker in the street etc.'),
(34147, 's', '\r\nSoap', 'flakes  n.pl. Thin flakes of soap for washing clothes etc.'),
(34148, 's', '\r\nSoap', 'opera  n. Broadcast drama serial with domestic themes (orig. Sponsored in the us by soap manufacturers).'),
(34149, 's', '\r\nSoap', 'powder  n. Powdered soap, esp. With additives, for washing clothes etc.'),
(34150, 's', '\r\nSoapstone', ' n. Steatite.'),
(34151, 's', '\r\nSoapsuds', ' n.pl. = *suds.'),
(34152, 's', '\r\nSoapwort', ' n. Plant with pink or white flowers, and leaves yielding a soapy substance.'),
(34153, 's', '\r\nSoapy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like soap. 2 containing or smeared with soap. 3 unctuous, flattering.  soapily adv. Soapiness n.'),
(34154, 's', '\r\nSoar', ' v. 1 fly or rise high. 2 reach a high level or standard. 3 fly without flapping the wings or using power. [french essorer]'),
(34155, 's', '\r\nSob', ' â€”v. (-bb-) 1 inhale convulsively, usu. With weeping. 2 utter with sobs. â€”n. Act or sound of sobbing. [imitative]'),
(34156, 's', '\r\nSober', ' â€”adj. (soberer, soberest) 1 not drunk. 2 not given to drink. 3 moderate, tranquil, sedate, serious. 4 not exaggerated. 5 (of a colour etc.) Quiet; dull. â€”v. (often foll. By down, up) make or become sober.  soberly adv. [french from latin]'),
(34157, 's', '\r\nSobriety', ' n. Being sober. [latin: related to *sober]'),
(34158, 's', '\r\nSobriquet', ' n. (also soubriquet) nickname. [french]'),
(34159, 's', '\r\nSob', 'story  n. Colloq. Story or explanation appealing for sympathy.'),
(34160, 's', '\r\nSoc.', ' Abbr. 1 socialist. 2 society.'),
(34161, 's', '\r\nSo-called', ' adj. Commonly called, often incorrectly.'),
(34162, 's', '\r\nSoccer', ' n. Association football. [from *assoc.]'),
(34163, 's', '\r\nSociable', ' adj. Liking company, gregarious; friendly.  sociability n. Sociably adv. [latin socius companion]'),
(34164, 's', '\r\nSocial', ' â€”adj. 1 of society or its organization, esp. Of the relations of people or classes of people. 2 living in organized communities. 3 needing companionship; gregarious. â€”n. Social gathering, esp. Of a club.  socially adv. [latin: related to *sociable]'),
(34165, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'climber  n. Person anxious to gain a higher social status.'),
(34166, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'contract  n. Agreement between the state and population for mutual advantage.'),
(34167, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'democracy  n. Political system favouring a mixed economy and democratic social change.  social democrat n.'),
(34168, 's', '\r\nSocialism', ' n. 1 political and economic theory advocating state ownership and control of the means of production, distribution, and exchange. 2 social system based on this.  socialist n. & adj. Socialistic adj. [french: related to *social]'),
(34169, 's', '\r\nSocialite', ' n. Person moving in fashionable society.'),
(34170, 's', '\r\nSocialize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 mix socially. 2 make social. 3 organize on socialistic principles.  socialization n.'),
(34171, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'science  n. The study of society and social relationships.  social scientist n.'),
(34172, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'security  n. State assistance to the poor and unemployed etc.'),
(34173, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'services  n.pl. Welfare services provided by the state, esp. Education, health, and housing.'),
(34174, 's', '\r\nSocial', 'work  n. Professional or voluntary work with disadvantaged groups.  social worker n.'),
(34175, 's', '\r\nSociety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 organized and interdependent community. 2 system and organization of this. 3 aristocratic part of this; its members (polite society; society would not approve). 4 mixing with others; companionship, company. 5 club, association (music society; building society).  societal adj. [latin societas]'),
(34176, 's', '\r\nSociety', 'of friends  n. Pacifist christian sect with no written creed or ordained ministers; quakers.'),
(34177, 's', '\r\nSociety', 'of jesus  see *jesuit.'),
(34178, 's', '\r\nSocio-', ' comb. Form of society or sociology (and) (socio-economic). [latin: related to *social]'),
(34179, 's', '\r\nSociology', ' n. The study of society and social problems.  sociological adj. Sociologist n. [french: related to *social]'),
(34180, 's', '\r\nSock1', ' n. 1 knitted covering for the foot and lower leg. 2 insole.  pull one''s socks up colloq. Make an effort to improve. Put a sock in it slang be quiet. [old english socc from greek sukkhos slipper]'),
(34181, 's', '\r\nSock2', ' colloq. â€”v. Hit hard. â€”n. Hard blow.  sock it to attack or address (a person or people) vigorously. [origin unknown]'),
(34182, 's', '\r\nSocket', ' n. Hollow for something to fit into etc., esp. A device receiving an electric plug, light-bulb, etc. [anglo-french]'),
