(34272, 's', '\r\nSolid', ' â€”adj. (-der, -dest) 1 firm and stable in shape; not liquid or fluid. 2 of such material throughout, not hollow. 3 of the same substance throughout (solid silver). 4 sturdily built; not flimsy or slender. 5 a three-dimensional. B of solids (solid geometry). 6 a sound, reliable (solid arguments). B dependable (solid friend). 7 sound but unexciting (solid piece of work). 8 financially sound. 9 uninterrupted (four solid hours). 10 unanimous, undivided. 11 (of printing) without spaces. â€”n. 1 solid substance or body. 2 (in pl.) Solid food. 3 geom. Three-dimensional body or magnitude. â€”adv. Solidly (jammed solid).  solidly adv. Solidness n. [latin solidus]'),
(34273, 's', '\r\nSolidarity', ' n. 1 unity, esp. Political or in an industrial dispute. 2 mutual dependence. [french: related to *solid]'),
(34274, 's', '\r\nSolidify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make or become solid.  solidification n.'),
(34275, 's', '\r\nSolidity', ' n. Being solid; firmness.'),
(34276, 's', '\r\nSolid-state', ' adj. Using the electronic properties of solids (e.g. A semiconductor) to replace those of valves.'),
(34277, 's', '\r\nSolidus', ' n. (pl. Solidi) oblique stroke (/). [latin: related to *solid]'),
(34278, 's', '\r\nSoliloquy', ' n. (pl. -quies) 1 talking without or regardless of hearers, esp. In a play. 2 this part of a play.  soliloquist n. Soliloquize v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing). [latin solus alone, loquor speak]'),
(34279, 's', '\r\nSolipsism', ' n. Philosophical theory that the self is all that exists or can be known.  solipsist n. [latin solus alone, ipse self]'),
(34280, 's', '\r\nSolitaire', ' n. 1 jewel set by itself. 2 ring etc. With this. 3 game for one player in which pegs etc. Are removed from a board by jumping others over them. 4 us = *patience 3. [french: see *solitary]'),
(34281, 's', '\r\nSolitary', ' â€”adj. 1 living or being alone; not gregarious; lonely. 2 secluded. 3 single, sole. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 recluse. 2 colloq. = *solitary confinement.   solitariness n. [latin solitarius from solus alone]'),
(34282, 's', '\r\nSolitary', 'confinement  n. Isolation in a separate prison cell.'),
(34283, 's', '\r\nSolitude', ' n. 1 being solitary. 2 lonely place. [latin solitudo: related to *solitary]'),
(34284, 's', '\r\nSolo', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 (pl. -s or soli) musical piece or passage, or a dance, performed by one person. 2 thing done by one person, esp. An unaccompanied flight. 3 (in full solo whist) type of whist in which one player may oppose the others. â€”v. (-es, -ed) perform a solo. â€”adv. Unaccompanied, alone. [italian from latin: related to *sole3]'),
