(34505, 's', '\r\nSpasm', ' n. 1 sudden involuntary muscular contraction. 2 convulsive movement or emotion etc. 3 (usu. Foll. By of) colloq. Brief spell. [greek spasma from spao pull]'),
(34506, 's', '\r\nSpasmodic', ' adj. Of or in spasms, intermittent.  spasmodically adv. [greek: related to *spasm]'),
(34507, 's', '\r\nSpastic', ' â€”adj. Of or having cerebral palsy. â€”n. 1 spastic person. 2 slang offens. Stupid or incompetent person. [greek: related to *spasm]'),
(34508, 's', '\r\nSpat1', ' past and past part. Of *spit1.'),
(34509, 's', '\r\nSpat2', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Hist. Short gaiter covering a shoe. [abbreviation of spatterdash: related to *spatter]'),
(34510, 's', '\r\nSpat3', ' n. Colloq. Petty or brief quarrel. [probably imitative]'),
(34511, 's', '\r\nSpat4', ' n. Spawn of shellfish, esp. The oyster. [anglo-french, of unknown origin]'),
(34512, 's', '\r\nSpate', ' n. 1 river-flood (river in spate). 2 unexpected occurrence of similar events (spate of car thefts). [origin unknown]'),
(34513, 's', '\r\nSpathe', ' n. Large bract(s) enveloping a flower-cluster. [greek spathe broad blade]'),
(34514, 's', '\r\nSpatial', ' adj. Of space.  spatially adv. [latin: related to *space]'),
(34515, 's', '\r\nSpatter', ' â€”v. Splash or scatter in drips. â€”n. 1 splash. 2 pattering. [imitative]'),
(34516, 's', '\r\nSpatula', ' n. Broad-bladed flexible implement used for spreading, stirring, mixing paints, etc. [latin diminutive: related to *spathe]'),
(34517, 's', '\r\nSpawn', ' â€”v. 1 a (of a fish, frog, etc.) Produce (eggs). B be produced as eggs or young. 2 produce or generate in large numbers. â€”n. 1 eggs of fish, frogs, etc. 2 mycelium of mushrooms or other fungi. [anglo-french espaundre: related to *expand]'),
(34518, 's', '\r\nSpay', ' v. Sterilize (a female animal) by removing the ovaries. [anglo-french: related to *Ã©pÃ©e]'),
(34519, 's', '\r\nSpeak', ' v. (past spoke; past part. Spoken) 1 utter words in an ordinary voice. 2 utter (words, the truth, etc.). 3 a converse; talk (spoke to her earlier; had to speak to the children about rudeness). B (foll. By of, about) mention in writing etc. C (foll. By for) act as spokesman for. 4 (foll. By to) speak with reference to; support in words (spoke to the resolution). 5 make a speech. 6 use or be able to use (a specified language). 7 a convey an idea (actions speak louder than words). B (usu. Foll. By to) communicate feeling etc.; affect, touch (the sunset spoke to her).  generally (or strictly etc.) Speaking in the general (or strict etc.) Sense. Not (or nothing) to speak of not (or nothing) worth mentioning. On speaking terms friendly enough to converse. Speak for itself be sufficient evidence. Speak out (often followed by against) give one''s opinion courageously. Speak up 1 speak loudly or freely; speak louder. 2 (followed by for) defend. Speak volumes be very significant. [old english]'),
(34520, 's', '\r\n-speak', ' comb. Form jargon (newspeak; computer speak).'),
(34521, 's', '\r\nSpeakeasy', ' n. (pl. -ies) us hist. Slang place where alcoholic liquor was sold illicitly.'),
(34522, 's', '\r\nSpeaker', ' n. 1 person who speaks, esp. In public. 2 person who speaks a specified language (esp. In comb.: a french-speaker). 3 (speaker) presiding officer in a legislative assembly, esp. The house of commons. 4 = *loudspeaker.'),
(34523, 's', '\r\nSpeaking', 'clock  n. Telephone service announcing the correct time.'),
(34524, 's', '\r\nSpear', ' â€”n. 1 thrusting or throwing weapon with a long shaft and a pointed usu. Steel tip. 2 a tip and stem of asparagus, broccoli, etc. B blade of grass etc. â€”v. Pierce or strike (as) with a spear. [old english]'),
(34525, 's', '\r\nSpearhead', ' â€”n. 1 point of a spear. 2 person or group leading an attack etc. â€”v. Act as the spearhead of (an attack etc.).'),
(34526, 's', '\r\nSpearmint', ' n. Common garden mint, used in cookery and to flavour chewing-gum etc.'),
(34527, 's', '\r\nSpearwort', ' n. Aquatic plant with narrow spear-shaped leaves and yellow flowers.'),
(34528, 's', '\r\nSpec1', ' n. Colloq. Speculation.  on spec as a gamble. [abbreviation]'),
(34529, 's', '\r\nSpec2', ' n. Colloq. Detailed working description; specification. [abbreviation of *specification]'),
(34530, 's', '\r\nSpecial', ' â€”adj. 1 a exceptional. B peculiar; specific. 2 for a particular purpose. 3 for children with special needs (special school). â€”n. Special constable, train, edition of a newspaper, dish on a menu, etc.  specially adv. Specialness n. [latin: related to *species]'),
(34531, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'branch  n. Police department dealing with political security.'),
(34532, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'constable  n. Person trained to assist the police in routine duties or in an emergency.'),
(34533, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'correspondent  n. Journalist writing on special events or a special subject.'),
(34534, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'delivery  n. Delivery of mail outside the normal delivery schedule.'),
(34535, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'edition  n. Extra late edition of a newspaper.'),
(34536, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'effects  n.pl. Illusions created by props, camera-work, etc.'),
(34537, 's', '\r\nSpecialist', ' n. 1 person trained in a particular branch of a profession, esp. Medicine. 2 person who specially studies a subject or area.'),
(34538, 's', '\r\nSpeciality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 special subject, product, activity, etc. 2 special feature or skill.'),
(34539, 's', '\r\nSpecialize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (often foll. By in) a be or become a specialist. B devote oneself to an interest, skill, etc. (specializes in insulting people). 2 (esp. In passive) adapt for a particular purpose (specialized organs). 3 (as specialized adj.) Of a specialist (specialized work).  specialization n. [french: related to *special]'),
(34540, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'licence  n. Licence allowing immediate marriage without banns.'),
(34541, 's', '\r\nSpecial', 'pleading  n. Biased reasoning.'),
(34542, 's', '\r\nSpecialty', ' n. (pl. -ies) esp. Us = *speciality.'),
(34543, 's', '\r\nSpecie', ' n. Coin as opposed to paper money. [related to *species]'),
(34544, 's', '\r\nSpecies', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 class of things having some common characteristics. 2 group of animals or plants within a genus, differing only slightly from others and capable of interbreeding. 3 kind, sort. [latin specio look]'),
(34545, 's', '\r\nSpecific', ' â€”adj. 1 clearly defined (a specific purpose). 2 relating to a particular subject; peculiar. 3 exact, giving full details (was specific about his wishes). 4 archaic (of medicine etc.) Having a distinct effect in curing a certain disease. â€”n. 1 archaic specific medicine or remedy. 2 specific aspect or factor (discussed specifics; from the general to the specific).  specifically adv. Specificity n. [latin: related to *species]'),
(34546, 's', '\r\nSpecification', ' n. 1 act of specifying. 2 (esp. In pl.) Detail of the design and materials etc. Of work done or to be done. [medieval latin: related to *specify]'),
(34547, 's', '\r\nSpecific', 'gravity  n. = *relative density.'),
(34548, 's', '\r\nSpecific', 'heat capacity  n. Heat required to raise the temperature of the unit mass of a given substance by a given amount (usu. One degree).'),
(34549, 's', '\r\nSpecify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 (also absol.) Name or mention expressly or as a condition (specified a two-hour limit). 2 include in specifications. [latin: related to *specific]'),
(34550, 's', '\r\nSpecimen', ' n. 1 individual or sample taken as an example of a class or whole, esp. In experiments etc. 2 sample of urine for testing. 3 colloq. Usu. Derog. Person of a specified sort. [latin specio look]'),
(34551, 's', '\r\nSpecious', ' adj. Plausible but wrong (specious argument). [latin: related to *species]'),
(34552, 's', '\r\nSpeck', ' â€”n. 1 small spot or stain. 2 particle. â€”v. (esp. As specked adj.) Marked with specks. [old english]'),
(34553, 's', '\r\nSpeckle', ' â€”n. Speck, esp. One of many markings. â€”v. (-ling) (esp. As speckled adj.) Mark with speckles. [dutch spekkel]'),
(34554, 's', '\r\nSpecs', ' n.pl. Colloq. Spectacles. [abbreviation]'),
(34555, 's', '\r\nSpectacle', ' n. 1 striking, impressive, or ridiculous sight. 2 public show. 3 object of public attention. [latin specio spect- look]'),
(34556, 's', '\r\nSpectacled', ' adj. Wearing spectacles.'),
(34557, 's', '\r\nSpectacles', ' n.pl. Pair of lenses in a frame resting on the nose and ears, used to correct defective eyesight.'),
(34558, 's', '\r\nSpectacular', ' â€”adj. Striking, impressive, lavish. â€”n. Spectacular show.  spectacularly adv.'),
(34559, 's', '\r\nSpectator', ' n. Person who watches a show, game, incident, etc.  spectate v. (-ting) informal. [latin: related to *spectacle]'),
(34560, 's', '\r\nSpectator', 'sport  n. Sport attracting many spectators.'),
(34561, 's', '\r\nSpecter', ' n. (brit. Spectre) 1 ghost. 2 haunting presentiment (spectre of war). [latin spectrum from specio look]'),
(34562, 's', '\r\nSpectra', ' pl. Of *spectrum.'),
(34563, 's', '\r\nSpectral', ' adj. 1 of or like a spectre; ghostly. 2 of the spectrum or spectra.  spectrally adv.'),
(34564, 's', '\r\nSpectre', ' n. (us specter) 1 ghost. 2 haunting presentiment (spectre of war). [latin spectrum from specio look]'),
(34565, 's', '\r\nSpectrometer', ' n. Instrument for measuring observed spectra.'),
(34566, 's', '\r\nSpectroscope', ' n. Instrument for recording spectra for examination.  spectroscopic adj. Spectroscopy n.'),
(34567, 's', '\r\nSpectrum', ' n. (pl. -tra) 1 band of colours as seen in a rainbow etc. 2 entire or wide range of a subject, emotion, etc. 3 distribution of electromagnetic radiation in which the parts are arranged according to wavelength. [latin specio look]'),
(34568, 's', '\r\nSpecula', ' pl. Of *speculum.'),
(34569, 's', '\r\nSpeculate', ' v. (-ting) 1 (usu. Foll. By on, upon, about) theorize, conjecture. 2 deal in a commodity or asset in the hope of profiting from fluctuating prices.  speculation n. Speculative adj. Speculator n. [latin specula watch-tower, from specio look]'),
(34570, 's', '\r\nSpeculum', ' n. (pl. -la) 1 instrument for dilating orifices of the body. 2 mirror of polished metal in a telescope. [latin, = mirror]'),
(34571, 's', '\r\nSped', ' past and past part. Of *speed.'),
(34572, 's', '\r\nSpeech', ' n. 1 faculty, act, or manner of speaking. 2 formal public address. 3 language of a nation, group, etc. [old english: related to *speak]'),
(34573, 's', '\r\nSpeech', 'day  n. School celebration with speeches, prize-giving, etc.'),
(34574, 's', '\r\nSpeechify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) joc. Make esp. Boring or long speeches.'),
(34575, 's', '\r\nSpeechless', ' adj. Temporarily silent because of emotion etc.'),
(34576, 's', '\r\nSpeech', 'therapy  n. Treatment for defective speech.'),
(34577, 's', '\r\nSpeed', ' â€”n. 1 rapidity of movement. 2 rate of progress or motion. 3 gear appropriate to a range of speeds of a bicycle. 4 photog. A sensitivity of film to light. B light-gathering power of a lens. C duration of an exposure. 5 slang amphetamine drug. 6 archaic success, prosperity. â€”v. (past and past part. Sped) 1 go or send quickly. 2 (past and past part. Speeded) travel at an illegal or dangerous speed. 3 archaic be or make prosperous or successful.  at speed moving quickly. Speed up move or work faster.  speeder n. [old english]'),
(34578, 's', '\r\nSpeedboat', ' n. High-speed motor boat.'),
(34579, 's', '\r\nSpeed', 'limit  n. Maximum permitted speed on a road etc.'),
(34580, 's', '\r\nSpeedo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. = *speedometer. [abbreviation]'),
(34581, 's', '\r\nSpeedometer', ' n. Instrument on a vehicle indicating its speed.'),
(34582, 's', '\r\nSpeedway', ' n. 1 a motor-cycle racing. B arena for this. 2 us road or track for fast traffic.'),
(34583, 's', '\r\nSpeedwell', ' n. Small plant with bright blue flowers. [from *speed, *well1]'),
(34584, 's', '\r\nSpeedy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 rapid. 2 done without delay; prompt.  speedily adv. Speediness n.'),
(34585, 's', '\r\nSpeleology', ' n. The study of caves. [greek spelaion cave]'),
(34586, 's', '\r\nSpell1', ' v. (past and past part. Spelt or spelled) 1 (also absol.) Write or name correctly the letters of (a word etc.). 2 a (of letters) form (a word etc.). B result in (spell ruin).  spell out 1 make out (words etc.) Letter by letter. 2 explain in detail.  speller n. [french espeller related to *spell2]'),
(34587, 's', '\r\nSpell2', ' n. 1 words used as a charm or incantation etc. 2 effect of these. 3 fascination exercised by a person, activity, etc. [old english]'),
(34588, 's', '\r\nSpell3', ' n. 1 short or fairly short period (a cold spell). 2 period of some activity or work. [old english, = substitute]'),
(34589, 's', '\r\nSpellbind', ' v. (past and past part. Spellbound) 1 (esp. As spellbinding adj.) Hold the attention as if with a spell; entrance. 2 (as spellbound adj.) Entranced, fascinated.'),
(34590, 's', '\r\nSpelling', ' n. 1 way a word is spelt. 2 ability to spell.'),
(34591, 's', '\r\nSpelt1', ' past and past part. Of *spell1.'),
(34592, 's', '\r\nSpelt2', ' n. A kind of wheat giving very fine flour. [old english]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(34593, 's', '\r\nSpend', ' v. (past and past part. Spent) 1 pay out (money). 2 a use or consume (time or energy). B use up (material etc.). 3 (as spent adj.) Having lost its original force or strength; exhausted.  spend a penny colloq. Go to the lavatory.  spender n. [latin: related to *expend]'),
(34594, 's', '\r\nSpendthrift', ' â€”n. Extravagant person. â€”adj. Extravagant.'),
(34595, 's', '\r\nSperm', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) 1 = *spermatozoon. 2 semen. [greek sperma -mat-]'),
(34596, 's', '\r\nSpermaceti', ' n. White waxy substance from the sperm whale, used for ointments etc. [medieval latin, = whale sperm]'),
(34597, 's', '\r\nSpermatozoon', ' n. (pl. -zoa) mature sex cell in semen. [from *sperm, greek zoion animal]'),
(34598, 's', '\r\nSperm', 'bank  n. Store of semen for artificial insemination.'),
(34599, 's', '\r\nSperm', 'count  n. Number of spermatozoa in one ejaculation or a measured amount of semen.'),
(34600, 's', '\r\nSpermicide', ' n. Substance able to kill spermatozoa.  spermicidal adj.'),
(34601, 's', '\r\nSperm', 'whale  n. Large whale yielding spermaceti.'),
(34602, 's', '\r\nSpew', ' v. (also spue) 1 (often foll. By up) vomit. 2 (often foll. By out) (cause to) gush. [old english]'),
(34603, 's', '\r\nSphagnum', ' n. (pl. -na) (in full sphagnum moss) moss growing in bogs, used as packing etc. [greek sphagnos]'),
(34604, 's', '\r\nSphere', ' n. 1 solid figure with every point on its surface equidistant from its centre; its surface. 2 ball, globe. 3 a field of action, influence, etc. B social class. 4 hist. Each of the revolving shells in which celestial bodies were thought to be set. [greek sphaira ball]'),
(34605, 's', '\r\nSpherical', ' adj. 1 shaped like a sphere. 2 of spheres.  spherically adv.'),
(34606, 's', '\r\nSpheroid', ' n. Spherelike but not perfectly spherical body.  spheroidal adj.'),
(34607, 's', '\r\nSphincter', ' n. Ring of muscle surrounding and closing an opening in the body. [greek sphiggo bind tight]'),
(34608, 's', '\r\nSphinx', ' n. 1 (sphinx) (in greek mythology) winged monster with a woman''s head and a lion''s body, whose riddle oedipus guessed. 2 antiq. A ancient egyptian stone figure with a lion''s body and a human or animal head.'),
(34609, 's', '\r\nSpice', ' â€”n. 1 aromatic or pungent vegetable substance used to flavour food. 2 spices collectively. 3 a piquant quality. B (foll. By of) slight flavour or suggestion. â€”v. (-cing) 1 flavour with spice. 2 (foll. By with) enhance (spiced with wit). [french espice]'),
(34610, 's', '\r\nSpick', 'and span  adj. 1 neat and clean. 2 smart and new. [earlier span and span new, fresh and new like a shaved chip]'),
(34611, 's', '\r\nSpicy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or flavoured with spice. 2 piquant; sensational, improper.  spiciness n.'),
(34612, 's', '\r\nSpider', ' n. Eight-legged arthropod of which many species spin webs esp. To capture insects as food. [old english: related to *spin]'),
(34613, 's', '\r\nSpider', 'crab  n. Crab with long thin legs.'),
(34614, 's', '\r\nSpider', 'monkey  n. Monkey with long limbs and a prehensile tail.'),
(34615, 's', '\r\nSpider', 'plant  n. House plant with long narrow striped leaves.'),
(34616, 's', '\r\nSpidery', ' adj. Elongated and thin (spidery handwriting).'),
(34617, 's', '\r\nSpiel', ' n. Slang glib speech or story; sales pitch. [german, = game]'),
(34618, 's', '\r\nSpigot', ' n. 1 small peg or plug, esp. In a cask. 2 device for controlling the flow of liquid in a tap. [related to *spike2]'),
(34619, 's', '\r\nSpike1', ' â€”n. 1 a sharp point. B pointed piece of metal, esp. The top of an iron railing. 2 a metal point in the sole of a running-shoe to prevent slipping. B (in pl.) Spiked running-shoes. 3 pointed metal rod used for filing rejected news items. 4 large nail. â€”v. (-king) 1 put spikes on or into. 2 fix on a spike. 3 colloq. A lace (a drink) with alcohol etc. B contaminate with something added. 4 colloq. Reject (a newspaper story).  spike a person''s guns spoil his or her plans. [low german or dutch: related to *spoke1]'),
(34620, 's', '\r\nSpike2', ' n. Cluster of flower-heads on a long stem. [latin spica]'),
(34621, 's', '\r\nSpikenard', ' n. 1 tall sweet-smelling indian plant. 2 hist. Perfumed ointment formerly made from this. [medieval latin spica nardi]'),
(34622, 's', '\r\nSpiky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like a spike; having or sticking up in spikes. 2 colloq. Touchy, irritable.  spikily adv. Spikiness n.'),
(34623, 's', '\r\nSpill1', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Spilt or spilled) 1 fall or run or cause (liquid, powder, etc.) To fall or run out of a container, esp. Accidentally. 2 a throw from a vehicle, saddle, etc. B (foll. By into, out etc.) (esp. Of a crowd) leave a place quickly. 3 slang disclose (information etc.). 4 shed (blood). â€”n. 1 spilling or being spilt. 2 tumble, esp. From a horse, bicycle, etc.  spill the beans colloq. Divulge information etc. Spill over overflow.  spillage n. [old english]'),
(34624, 's', '\r\nSpill2', ' n. Thin strip of wood or paper etc. For lighting a fire, pipe, etc. [low german or dutch]'),
(34625, 's', '\r\nSpillikin', ' n. 1 splinter of wood etc. 2 (in pl.) Game in which thin rods are removed one at a time from a heap without moving the others. [from *spill2]'),
(34626, 's', '\r\nSpillway', ' n. Passage for surplus water from a dam.'),
(34627, 's', '\r\nSpin', ' â€”v. (-nn-; past and past part. Spun) 1 (cause to) turn or whirl round quickly. 2 (also absol.) A draw out and twist (wool, cotton, etc.) Into threads. B make (yarn) in this way. 3 (of a spider, silkworm, etc.) Make (a web, cocoon, etc.) By extruding a fine viscous thread. 4 (esp. Of the head) be dizzy through excitement etc. 5 tell or write (a story etc.). 6 (as spun adj.) Made into threads (spun glass; spun gold). 7 toss (a coin). 8 = spin-dry (*spin-drier). â€”n. 1 spinning motion; whirl. 2 rotating dive of an aircraft. 3  secondary twisting motion, e.g. Of a ball in flight. 4 colloq. Brief drive, esp. In a car.  spin out prolong. Spin a yarn tell a story. [old english]'),
(34628, 's', '\r\nSpina', 'bifida  n. Congenital spinal defect in which part of the spinal cord protrudes. [latin, = cleft spine]'),
(34629, 's', '\r\nSpinach', ' n. Green vegetable with edible leaves. [french espinache]'),
(34630, 's', '\r\nSpinal', ' adj. Of the spine. [latin: related to *spine]'),
(34631, 's', '\r\nSpinal', 'column  n. Spine.'),
(34632, 's', '\r\nSpinal', 'cord  n. Cylindrical nervous structure within the spine.'),
(34633, 's', '\r\nSpin', 'bowler  n. Cricket bowler who imparts spin to a ball.'),
(34634, 's', '\r\nSpindle', ' n. 1 slender rod or bar, often tapered, for twisting and winding thread. 2 pin or axis that revolves or on which something revolves. 3 turned piece of wood used as a banister, chair leg, etc. [old english: related to *spin]'),
(34635, 's', '\r\nSpindle', 'tree  n. Tree with hard wood used for spindles.'),
(34636, 's', '\r\nSpindly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) long or tall and thin; thin and weak.'),
(34637, 's', '\r\nSpin-drier', ' n. (also spin-dryer) machine for drying clothes by spinning them in a rapidly revolving drum.  spin-dry v.'),
(34638, 's', '\r\nSpindrift', ' n. Spray on the surface of the sea. [scots var. Of spoondrift from obsolete spoon scud]'),
(34639, 's', '\r\nSpine', ' n. 1 vertebrae extending from the skull to the coccyx; backbone. 2 needle-like outgrowth of an animal or plant. 3 part of a book enclosing the page-fastening. 4 sharp ridge or projection. [latin spina]'),
(34640, 's', '\r\nSpine-chiller', ' n. Frightening and usu. Exciting story, film, etc.  spine-chilling adj.'),
(34641, 's', '\r\nSpineless', ' adj. 1 having no spine; invertebrate. 2 lacking resolve, feeble.'),
(34642, 's', '\r\nSpinet', ' n. Hist. Small harpsichord with oblique strings. [italian spinetta]'),
(34643, 's', '\r\nSpinnaker', ' n. Large triangular sail opposite the mainsail of a racing-yacht. [sphinx, name of the yacht first using it]'),
(34644, 's', '\r\nSpinner', ' n. 1 spin bowler. 2 person or thing that spins, esp. A manufacturer engaged in cotton-spinning. 3 revolving bait.'),
(34645, 's', '\r\nSpinneret', ' n. 1 spinning-organ in a spider etc. 2 device for forming synthetic fibre.'),
(34646, 's', '\r\nSpinney', ' n. (pl. -s) small wood; thicket. [latin spinetum from spina thorn]'),
(34647, 's', '\r\nSpinning-jenny', ' n. Hist. Machine for spinning fibres with more than one spindle at a time.'),
(34648, 's', '\r\nSpinning', 'wheel  n. Household device for spinning yarn or thread, with a spindle driven by a wheel with a crank or treadle.'),
(34649, 's', '\r\nSpin-off', ' n. Incidental result or benefit, esp. From technology.'),
(34650, 's', '\r\nSpinster', ' n. 1 formal unmarried woman. 2 woman, esp. Elderly, thought unlikely to marry.  spinsterish adj. [originally = woman who spins]'),
(34651, 's', '\r\nSpiny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having many spines.'),
(34652, 's', '\r\nSpiny', 'anteater  n.  = *echidna.'),
(34653, 's', '\r\nSpiraea', ' n. (us spirea) shrub with clusters of small white or pink flowers. [greek: related to *spiral]'),
(34654, 's', '\r\nSpiral', ' â€”adj. 1 coiled in a plane or as round a cylinder or cone. 2 having this shape. â€”n. 1 spiral curve or thing (spiral of smoke). 2 progressive rise or fall of two or more quantities alternately because each depends on the other(s). â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 move in a spiral course. 2 (of prices, wages, etc.) Rise or fall continuously.  spirally adv. [greek speira coil]'),
(34655, 's', '\r\nSpiral', 'staircase  n. Circular staircase round a central axis.'),
(34656, 's', '\r\nSpirant', ' â€”adj. Uttered with a continuous expulsion of breath. â€”n. Such a consonant. [latin spiro breathe]'),
(34657, 's', '\r\nSpire', ' n. 1 tapering structure, esp. On a church tower. 2 any tapering thing. [old english]'),
(34658, 's', '\r\nSpirea', ' n. (brit. Spiraea) shrub with clusters of small white or pink flowers. [greek: related to *spiral]'),
(34659, 's', '\r\nSpirit', ' â€”n. 1 person''s essence or intelligence; soul. 2 a rational or intelligent being without a material body. B ghost. 3 a person''s character (an unbending spirit). B attitude (took it in the wrong spirit). C type of person (is a free spirit; a kindred spirit). D prevailing tendency (spirit of the age). 4 a (usu. In pl.) Strong distilled liquor, e.g. Whisky or gin. B distilled volatile liquid (wood spirit). C purified alcohol (methylated spirit). 5 a courage, vivacity. B (in pl.) State of mind, mood (in high spirits; his spirits were dashed). 6 essential as opposed to formal meaning (the spirit of the law). â€”v. (-t-) (usu. Foll. By away, off, etc.) Convey rapidly or mysteriously.  in spirit inwardly. [latin spiritus: related to *spirant]'),
(34660, 's', '\r\nSpirited', ' adj. 1 lively, courageous. 2 (in comb.) In a specified mood (high-spirited).  spiritedly adv.'),
(34661, 's', '\r\nSpirit', 'gum  n. Quick-drying gum for attaching false hair.'),
(34662, 's', '\r\nSpirit-lamp', ' n. Lamp burning methylated spirit etc. Instead of oil.'),
(34663, 's', '\r\nSpiritless', ' adj. Lacking vigour.'),
(34664, 's', '\r\nSpirit-level', ' n. Device with a glass tube nearly filled with alcohol, used to test horizontality.'),
(34665, 's', '\r\nSpiritual', ' â€”adj. 1 of the spirit or soul (spiritual relationship; spiritual home). 2 religious, divine, inspired. 3 refined, sensitive. â€”n. (also negro spiritual) religious song orig. Of american blacks.  spirituality n. Spiritually adv.'),
(34666, 's', '\r\nSpiritualism', ' n. Belief in, and supposed practice of, communication with the dead, esp. Through mediums.  spiritualist n. Spiritualistic adj.'),
(34667, 's', '\r\nSpirituous', ' adj. 1 very alcoholic. 2 distilled as well as fermented.'),
(34668, 's', '\r\nSpirochaete', ' n. Any of various flexible spiral-shaped bacteria. [latin from greek speira coil, khaite long hair]'),
(34669, 's', '\r\nSpirogyra', ' n. Freshwater alga containing spiral bands of chlorophyll. [greek speira coil, guros round]'),
(34670, 's', '\r\nSpit1', ' â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Spat or spit) 1 a (also absol.) Eject (esp. Saliva) from the mouth. B do this in contempt or anger. 2 utter vehemently. 3 (of a fire, gun, etc.) Throw out with an explosion. 4 (of rain) fall lightly. 5 make a spitting noise. â€”n. 1 spittle. 2 act of spitting.  spit it out colloq. Say it quickly and concisely. [old english]'),
(34671, 's', '\r\nSpit2', ' â€”n. 1 rod for skewering meat for roasting on a fire etc. 2 point of land projecting into the sea. â€”v. (-tt-) pierce (as) with a spit. [old english]'),
(34672, 's', '\r\nSpit', 'and polish  n. Colloq. Esp. Military cleaning and polishing.'),
(34673, 's', '\r\nSpite', ' â€”n. Ill will, malice. â€”v. (-ting) hurt, harm, or frustrate (a person) through spite.  in spite of notwithstanding. [french: related to *despite]'),
(34674, 's', '\r\nSpiteful', ' adj. Malicious.  spitefully adv.'),
(34675, 's', '\r\nSpitfire', ' n. Person of fiery temper.'),
(34676, 's', '\r\nSpit-roast', ' v. Roast on a spit.'),
(34677, 's', '\r\nSpitting', 'distance  n. Colloq. Very short distance.'),
(34678, 's', '\r\nSpitting', 'image  n. (foll. By of) colloq. Double of (a person).'),
(34679, 's', '\r\nSpittle', ' n. Saliva. [related to *spit1]'),
(34680, 's', '\r\nSpittoon', ' n. Vessel to spit into.'),
(34681, 's', '\r\nSpiv', ' n. Colloq. Man, esp. A flashily-dressed one, living from shady dealings.  spivvish adj. Spivvy adj. [origin unknown]'),
(34682, 's', '\r\nSplash', ' â€”v. 1 scatter or cause (liquid) to scatter in drops. 2 wet with spattered liquid etc. 3 a (usu. Foll. By across, along, about, etc.) Move while spattering liquid etc. B jump or fall into water etc. With a splash. 4 display (news) prominently. 5 decorate with scattered colour. 6 spend (money) ostentatiously. â€”n. 1 act or noise of splashing. 2 quantity of liquid splashed. 3 mark etc. Made by splashing. 4 prominent news feature, display, etc. 5 patch of colour. 6 colloq. Small quantity of soda water etc. (in drink).  make a splash attract attention. Splash out colloq. Spend money freely.  splashy adj. (-ier, -iest). [imitative]'),
(34683, 's', '\r\nSplashback', ' n. Panel behind a sink etc. To protect the wall from splashes.'),
(34684, 's', '\r\nSplashdown', ' n. Landing of a spacecraft on the sea.  splash down v.'),
(34685, 's', '\r\nSplat', ' colloq. â€”n. Sharp splattering sound. â€”adv. With a splat. â€”v. (-tt-) fall or hit with a splat. [abbreviation of *splatter]'),
(34686, 's', '\r\nSplatter', ' â€”v. Splash esp. With a continuous noisy action; spatter. â€”n. Noisy splashing sound. [imitative]'),
(34687, 's', '\r\nSplay', ' â€”v. 1 spread apart. 2 (of an opening) have its sides diverging. 3 construct (an opening) with divergent sides. â€”n. Surface at an oblique angle to another. â€”adj. Splayed. [from *display]'),
(34688, 's', '\r\nSpleen', ' n. 1 abdominal organ regulating the quality of the blood. 2 moroseness, irritability (from the earlier belief that the spleen was the seat of such feelings). [greek splen]'),
(34689, 's', '\r\nSpleenwort', ' n. Evergreen fern formerly used as a remedy.'),
(34690, 's', '\r\nSplendid', ' adj. 1 magnificent, sumptuous. 2 impressive, glorious, dignified (splendid isolation). 3 excellent; fine.  splendidly adv. [latin: related to *splendour]'),
(34691, 's', '\r\nSplendiferous', ' adj. Colloq. Splendid. [from *splendour]'),
(34692, 's', '\r\nSplendor', ' n. (brit. Splendour) dazzling brightness; magnificence. [latin splendeo shine]'),
(34693, 's', '\r\nSplendour', ' n. (us splendor) dazzling brightness; magnificence. [latin splendeo shine]'),
(34694, 's', '\r\nSplenetic', ' adj. Bad-tempered; peevish.  splenetically adv. [latin: related to *spleen]'),
(34695, 's', '\r\nSplenic', ' adj. Of or in the spleen. [latin from greek: related to *spleen]'),
(34696, 's', '\r\nSplice', ' â€”v. (-cing) 1 join (ropes) by interweaving strands. 2 join (pieces of wood or tape etc.) By overlapping. 3 (esp. As spliced adj.) Colloq. Join in marriage. â€”n. Join made by splicing.  splice the main brace naut. Hist. Slang issue an extra tot of rum. [probably dutch splissen]'),
(34697, 's', '\r\nSplint', ' â€”n. Strip of wood etc. Bound to a broken limb while it sets. â€”v. Secure with a splint. [low german or dutch]'),
(34698, 's', '\r\nSplinter', ' â€”n. Small sharp fragment of wood, stone, glass, etc. â€”v. Break into splinters; shatter.  splintery adj. [dutch: related to *splint]'),
(34699, 's', '\r\nSplinter', 'group  n. Breakaway political group.'),
(34700, 's', '\r\nSplit', ' â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Split) 1 a break, esp. With the grain or into halves; break forcibly. B (often foll. By up) divide into parts, esp. Equal shares (they split the money). 2 (often foll. By off, away) remove or be removed by breaking or dividing. 3 a (usu. Foll. By on, over, etc.) Divide into disagreeing or hostile parties (split on the question of picketing). B (foll. By with) quarrel or cease association with. 4 cause the fission of (an atom). 5 slang leave, esp. Suddenly. 6 (usu. Foll. By on) colloq. Inform. 7 a (as splitting adj.) (of a headache) severe. B (of the head) suffer from a severe headache, noise, etc. â€”n. 1 act or result of splitting. 2 disagreement; schism. 3 (in pl.) Feat of leaping in the air or sitting down with the legs at right angles to the body in front and behind or on either side. 4 dish of split bananas etc. With ice-cream.  split the difference take the average of two proposed amounts. Split hairs make insignificant distinctions. Split one''s sides laugh uncontrollably. Split up separate, end a relationship. [dutch]'),
(34701, 's', '\r\nSplit', 'infinitive  n. Infinitive with an adverb etc. Inserted between to and the verb.'),
(34702, 's', '\r\nSplit-level', ' adj. (of a room etc.) With more than one level.'),
(34703, 's', '\r\nSplit', 'pea  n. Pea dried and split in half for cooking.'),
(34704, 's', '\r\nSplit', 'personality  n. Condition in which a person seems to have two alternating personalities.'),
(34705, 's', '\r\nSplit', 'pin  n. Metal cotter passed through a hole and held by the pressing back of the two ends.'),
(34706, 's', '\r\nSplit-screen', ' n. Screen on which two or more separate images are displayed.'),
(34707, 's', '\r\nSplit', 'second  â€”n. 1 very brief moment. 2 (of timing) very accurate. â€”attrib. Adj. (split-second) 1 very rapid. 2 (of timing) very accurate.'),
(34708, 's', '\r\nSplodge', ' colloq. â€”n. Daub, blot, or smear. â€”v. (-ging) make a splodge on.  splodgy adj. [alteration of *splotch]'),
(34709, 's', '\r\nSplosh', ' colloq. â€”v. Move with a splashing sound. â€”n. 1 splashing sound. 2 splash of water etc. [imitative]'),
(34710, 's', '\r\nSplotch', ' n. & v. = *splodge.  splotchy adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(34711, 's', '\r\nSplurge', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 sudden extravagance. 2 ostentatious display or effort. â€”v. (-ging) (usu. Foll. By on) spend large sums of money or make a great effort. [probably imitative]'),
(34712, 's', '\r\nSplutter', ' â€”v. 1 a speak, say, or express in a choking manner. B emit spitting sounds. 2 speak rapidly or incoherently. â€”n. Spluttering speech or sound. [from *sputter]'),
(34713, 's', '\r\nSpoil', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Spoilt or spoiled) 1 a make or become useless or unsatisfactory. B reduce the enjoyment etc. Of (the news spoiled his dinner). 2 make (esp. A child) unpleasant by over-indulgence. 3 (of food) go bad. 4 render (a ballot-paper) invalid by improper marking. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) 1 plunder, stolen goods. 2 profit or advantage from success or position.  be spoiling for aggressively seek (a fight etc.). Spoilt for choice having so many choices that it is difficult to choose. [latin spolio]'),
(34714, 's', '\r\nSpoilage', ' n. 1 paper spoilt in printing. 2 spoiling of food etc. By decay.'),
(34715, 's', '\r\nSpoiler', ' n. 1 retarding device on an aircraft, interrupting the air flow. 2 similar device on a vehicle to increase contact with the ground at speed.'),
(34716, 's', '\r\nSpoilsport', ' n. Person who spoils others'' enjoyment.'),
(34717, 's', '\r\nSpoilt', ' past and past part. Of *spoil.'),
(34718, 's', '\r\nSpoke1', ' n. Each of the rods running from the hub to the rim of a wheel.  put a spoke in a person''s wheel thwart or hinder a person.  spoked adj. [old english]'),
(34719, 's', '\r\nSpoke2', ' past of *speak.'),
(34720, 's', '\r\nSpoken', ' past part. Of *speak. â€”adj. (in comb.) Speaking in a specified way (well-spoken).  spoken for claimed (this seat is spoken for).'),
(34721, 's', '\r\nSpokeshave', ' n. Tool for planing curved surfaces. [from *spoke1]'),
(34722, 's', '\r\nSpokesman', ' n. (fem. Spokeswoman) person speaking for a group etc. [from *spoke2]'),
(34723, 's', '\r\nSpokesperson', ' n. (pl. -s or -people) spokesman or spokeswoman.'),
(34724, 's', '\r\nSpoliation', ' n. Plundering, pillage. [latin: related to *spoil]'),
(34725, 's', '\r\nSpondee', ' n. Metrical foot consisting of two long syllables (&spondee.).  spondaic adj. [greek sponde libation, with which songs in this metre were associated]'),
(34726, 's', '\r\nSponge', ' â€”n. 1 sea animal with a porous body wall and a rigid internal skeleton. 2 this skeleton or a piece of porous rubber etc. Used in bathing, cleaning, etc. 3 thing like a sponge in consistency etc., esp. A sponge cake. 4 act of sponging. â€”v. (-ging) 1 wipe or cleanse with a sponge. 2 (often foll. By out, away, etc.) Wipe off or efface (as) with a sponge. 3 (often foll. By up) absorb (as) with a sponge. 4 (often foll. By on, off) live as a parasite.  spongiform adj. (esp. In senses 1, 2 of the n.). [latin spongia]'),
(34727, 's', '\r\nSponge', 'bag  n. Waterproof bag for toilet articles.'),
(34728, 's', '\r\nSponge', 'cake  n. Light spongy cake.'),
(34729, 's', '\r\nSponge', 'pudding  n. Light spongy pudding.'),
(34730, 's', '\r\nSponger', ' n. Parasitic person.'),
(34731, 's', '\r\nSponge', 'rubber  n. Porous rubber.'),
(34732, 's', '\r\nSpongy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) like a sponge, porous, elastic, absorbent.  sponginess n.'),
(34733, 's', '\r\nSponsor', ' â€”n. 1 person who pledges money to a charity etc. In return for another person fulfilling a sporting etc. Challenge. 2 a patron of an artistic or sporting activity etc. B company etc. Supporting a broadcast in return for advertising time. 3 person who introduces legislation. 4 godparent at a baptism or (esp. Rc ch.) Person who presents a candidate for baptism. â€”v. Be a sponsor for.  sponsorial adj. Sponsorship n. [latin spondeo spons- pledge]'),
(34734, 's', '\r\nSpontaneous', ' adj. 1 acting, done, or occurring without external cause. 2 instinctive, automatic, natural. 3 (of style or manner) gracefully natural.  spontaneity n. Spontaneously adv. [latin sponte of one''s own accord]'),
(34735, 's', '\r\nSpontaneous', 'combustion  n. Ignition of a substance from internal heat.'),
(34736, 's', '\r\nSpoof', ' n. & v. Colloq. 1 parody. 2 hoax, swindle. [invented word]'),
(34737, 's', '\r\nSpook', ' â€”n. Colloq. Ghost. â€”v. Esp. Us frighten, unnerve. [low german or dutch]'),
(34738, 's', '\r\nSpooky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Ghostly, eerie.  spookily adv. Spookiness n.'),
(34739, 's', '\r\nSpool', ' â€”n. 1 reel for winding magnetic tape, yarn, etc., on. 2 revolving cylinder of an angler''s reel. â€”v. Wind on a spool. [french espole or germanic spole]'),
(34740, 's', '\r\nSpoon', ' â€”n. 1 a utensil with a bowl and a handle for lifting food to the mouth, stirring, etc. B spoonful, esp. Of sugar. 2 spoon-shaped thing, esp. (in full spoon-bait) a revolving metal fish-lure. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up, out) take (liquid etc.) With a spoon. 2 hit (a ball) feebly upwards. 3 colloq. Kiss and cuddle.  spoonful n. (pl. -s). [old english]'),
(34741, 's', '\r\nSpoonbill', ' n. Wading bird with a broad flat-tipped bill.'),
(34742, 's', '\r\nSpoonerism', ' n. (usu. Accidental) transposition of the initial letters etc. Of two or more words. [spooner, name of a scholar]'),
(34743, 's', '\r\nSpoonfeed', ' v. (past and past part. -fed) 1 feed with a spoon. 2 give such extensive help etc. To (a person) that he or she need make no effort.'),
(34744, 's', '\r\nSpoor', ' n. Animal''s track or scent. [dutch]'),
(34745, 's', '\r\nSporadic', ' adj. Occurring only sparsely or occasionally.  sporadically adv. [greek sporas -ad- scattered]'),
(34746, 's', '\r\nSpore', ' n. Reproductive cell of many plants and micro-organisms. [greek spora seed]'),
(34747, 's', '\r\nSporran', ' n. Pouch worn in front of the kilt. [gaelic sporan]'),
(34748, 's', '\r\nSport', ' â€”n. 1 a game or competitive activity, usu. Played outdoors and involving physical exertion, e.g. Cricket, football, racing. B these collectively. 2 (in pl.) Meeting for competing in sports, esp. Athletics. 3 amusement, fun. 4 colloq. A fair, generous, or sporting person. B person with a specified attitude to games, rules, etc. 5 animal or plant deviating from the normal type. â€”v. 1 amuse oneself, play about. 2 wear or exhibit, esp. Ostentatiously.  in sport jestingly. Make sport of ridicule. [from *disport]'),
(34749, 's', '\r\nSporting', ' adj. 1 interested or concerned in sport. 2 generous, fair.  a sporting chance some possibility of success.  sportingly adv.'),
(34750, 's', '\r\nSportive', ' adj. Playful.'),
(34751, 's', '\r\nSports', 'car  n. Low-built fast car.'),
(34752, 's', '\r\nSports', 'coat  n. (also sports jacket) man''s informal jacket.'),
(34753, 's', '\r\nSports', 'ground  n. Piece of land used for sports.'),
(34754, 's', '\r\nSportsman', ' n. (fem. Sportswoman) 1 person who takes part in sport, esp. Professionally. 2 fair and generous person.  sportsmanlike adj. Sportsmanship n.'),
(34755, 's', '\r\nSportswear', ' n. Clothes for sports or informal wear.'),
(34756, 's', '\r\nSporty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 fond of sport. 2 rakish, showy.  sportily adv. Sportiness n.'),
(34757, 's', '\r\nSpot', ' â€”n. 1 small roundish area or mark differing in colour, texture, etc., from the surface it is on. 2 pimple or blemish. 3 moral blemish or stain. 4 particular place, locality. 5 particular part of one''s body or aspect of one''s character. 6 colloq. One''s esp. Regular position in an organization, programme, etc. 7 a colloq. Small quantity (spot of trouble). B drop (spot of rain). 8 = *spotlight. 9 (usu. Attrib.) Money paid or goods delivered immediately after a sale (spot cash). â€”v. (-tt-) 1 colloq. Pick out, recognize, catch sight of. 2 watch for and take note of (trains, talent, etc.). 3 (as spotted adj.) Marked or decorated with spots. 4 make spots, rain slightly.  in a spot (or in a tight etc. Spot) colloq. In difficulty. On the spot 1 at the scene of an event. 2 colloq. In a position demanding response or action. 3 without delay. 4 without moving forwards or backwards (running on the spot). [perhaps from low german or dutch]'),
(34758, 's', '\r\nSpot', 'check  n. Sudden or random check.'),
(34759, 's', '\r\nSpotless', ' adj. Absolutely clean or pure.  spotlessly adv.'),
(34760, 's', '\r\nSpotlight', ' â€”n. 1 beam of light directed on a small area. 2 lamp projecting this. 3 full publicity. â€”v. (past and past part. -lighted or -lit) 1 direct a spotlight on. 2 draw attention to.'),
(34761, 's', '\r\nSpot', 'on  adj. Colloq. Precise; on target.'),
(34762, 's', '\r\nSpotted', 'dick  n. Suet pudding containing currants.'),
(34763, 's', '\r\nSpotter', ' n. 1 (often in comb.) Person who spots people or things (train-spotter). 2 (in full spotter plane) aircraft used to locate enemy positions etc.'),
(34764, 's', '\r\nSpotty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 marked with spots. 2 patchy, irregular.  spottiness n.'),
(34765, 's', '\r\nSpot-weld', ' v. Join (two metal surfaces) by welding at discrete points.  spot weld n. Spot welder n. Spot welding n.'),
(34766, 's', '\r\nSpouse', ' n. Husband or wife. [latin sponsus sponsa betrothed]'),
(34767, 's', '\r\nSpout', ' â€”n. 1 projecting tube or lip used for pouring from a teapot, kettle, jug, etc., or on a fountain, roof-gutter, etc. 2 jet or column of liquid etc. â€”v. 1 discharge or issue forcibly in a jet. 2 utter or speak at length or pompously.  up the spout slang 1 useless, ruined, broken down. 2 pregnant. [dutch]'),
(34768, 's', '\r\nSprain', ' â€”v. Wrench (an ankle, wrist, etc.), causing pain or swelling. â€”n. Such a wrench. [origin unknown]'),
(34769, 's', '\r\nSprang', ' past of *spring.'),
(34770, 's', '\r\nSprat', ' n. Small edible marine fish. [old english]'),
(34771, 's', '\r\nSprawl', ' â€”v. 1 a sit, lie, or fall with limbs flung out untidily. B spread (one''s limbs) thus. 2 (of writing, a plant, a town, etc.) Be irregular or straggling. â€”n. 1 sprawling movement, position, or mass. 2 straggling urban expansion. [old english]'),
(34772, 's', '\r\nSpray1', ' â€”n. 1 water etc. Flying in small drops. 2 liquid sprayed with an aerosol etc. 3 device for this. â€”v. 1 (also absol.) Throw (liquid) as spray. 2 (also absol.) Sprinkle (an object) thus, esp. With insecticide. 3  (of a tom-cat) mark its environment with urine, to attract females.  sprayer n. [origin uncertain]'),
(34773, 's', '\r\nSpray2', ' n. 1 sprig of flowers or leaves, or a small branch; decoratively arranged bunch of flowers. 2 ornament in a similar form. [old english]'),
(34774, 's', '\r\nSpray-gun', ' n. Device for spraying paint etc.'),
(34775, 's', '\r\nSpread', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Spread) 1 (often foll. By out) a open, extend, or unfold. B cause to cover a surface or larger area. C display thus. 2 (often foll. By out) have a wide, specified, or increasing extent. 3 become or make widely known, felt, etc. (rumours are spreading). 4 a cover (spread the wall with paint). B lay (a table). â€”n. 1 act of spreading. 2 capability or extent of spreading (has a large spread). 3 diffusion (spread of learning). 4 breadth. 5 increased girth (middle-aged spread). 6 difference between two rates, prices, etc. 7 colloq. Elaborate meal. 8 paste for spreading on bread etc. 9 bedspread. 10 printed matter spread across more than one column.  spread oneself be lavish or discursive. Spread one''s wings develop one''s powers fully. [old english]'),
(34776, 's', '\r\nSpread', 'eagle  â€”n. Figure of an eagle with legs and wings extended as an emblem. â€”v. (spread-eagle) 1 (usu. As spread-eagled adj.) Place (a person) with arms and legs spread out. 2 defeat utterly.'),
(34777, 's', '\r\nSpreadsheet', ' n. Computer program for the manipulation and retrieval of esp. Tabulated figures, esp. For accounting.'),
(34778, 's', '\r\nSpree', ' n. Colloq. 1 extravagant outing (shopping spree). 2 bout of fun or drinking etc. [origin unknown]'),
(34779, 's', '\r\nSprig', ' â€”n. 1 small branch or shoot. 2 ornament resembling this, esp. On fabric. â€”v. (-gg-) ornament with sprigs (sprigged muslin). [low german sprick]'),
(34780, 's', '\r\nSprightly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) vivacious, lively, brisk.  sprightliness n. [from spright, var. Of *sprite]'),
(34781, 's', '\r\nSpring', ' â€”v. (past sprang; past part. Sprung) 1 rise rapidly or suddenly, leap, jump. 2 move rapidly by or as by the action of a spring. 3 (usu. Foll. By from) originate (from ancestors, a source, etc.). 4 act or appear suddenly or unexpectedly (a breeze sprang up; spring to mind; spring to life). 5 (often foll. By on) present (a thing or circumstance etc.) Suddenly or unexpectedly (sprang it on me). 6 slang contrive the escape of (a person from prison etc.). 7 rouse (game) from a covert etc. 8 (usu. As sprung adj.) Provide (a mattress etc.) With springs. â€”n. 1 jump, leap. 2 recoil. 3 elasticity. 4  elastic device, usu. Of coiled metal, used esp. To drive clockwork or for cushioning in furniture or vehicles. 5 a (often attrib.) The first season of the year, in which new vegetation begins to appear. B (often foll. By of) early stage of life etc. 6 place where water, oil, etc., wells up from the earth; basin or flow so formed. 7 motive for or origin of an action, custom, etc.  spring a leak develop a leak. Spring up come into being, appear.  springlike adj. [old english]'),
(34782, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'balance  n. Balance that measures weight by the tension of a spring.'),
(34783, 's', '\r\nSpringboard', ' n. 1 flexible board for leaping or diving from. 2 source of impetus.'),
(34784, 's', '\r\nSpringbok', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) s. African gazelle. [afrikaans]'),
(34785, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'chicken  n. 1 young fowl for eating. 2 youthful person.'),
(34786, 's', '\r\nSpring-clean', ' â€”n. (also spring-cleaning) thorough cleaning of a house, esp. In spring. â€”v. Clean (a house) thus.'),
(34787, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'equinox  n. (also vernal equinox) equinox about 20 march.'),
(34788, 's', '\r\nSpringer', ' n. Small spaniel of a breed used to spring game.'),
(34789, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'fever  n. Restlessness or lethargy associated with spring.'),
(34790, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'greens  n.pl. Young cabbage leaves.'),
(34791, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'onion  n. Young onion eaten raw.'),
(34792, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'roll  n. Chinese fried pancake filled with vegetables.'),
(34793, 's', '\r\nSpring', 'tide  n. Tide just after the new and the full moon when there is the greatest difference between high and low water.'),
(34794, 's', '\r\nSpringtime', ' n. Season of spring.'),
(34795, 's', '\r\nSpringy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) springing back quickly when squeezed, bent, or stretched; elastic.  springiness n.'),
(34796, 's', '\r\nSprinkle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 scatter in small drops or particles. 2 (often foll. By with) subject to sprinkling with liquid etc. 3 (of liquid etc.) Fall on in this way. 4 distribute in small amounts. â€”n. (usu. Foll. By of) 1 light shower. 2 = *sprinkling. [origin uncertain]'),
(34797, 's', '\r\nSprinkler', ' n. Device for sprinkling a lawn or extinguishing fires.'),
(34798, 's', '\r\nSprinkling', ' n. Small sparse number or amount.'),
(34799, 's', '\r\nSprint', ' â€”v. 1 run a short distance at full speed. 2 run (a specified distance) thus. â€”n. 1 such a run. 2 short burst in cycling, swimming, etc.  sprinter n. [old norse]'),
(34800, 's', '\r\nSprit', ' n. Small diagonal spar from the mast to the upper outer corner of a sail. [old english]'),
(34801, 's', '\r\nSprite', ' n. Elf, fairy. [sprit, contraction of *spirit]'),
(34802, 's', '\r\nSpritsail', ' n. Sail extended by a sprit.'),
(34803, 's', '\r\nSpritzer', ' n. Drink of wine with soda water. [german, = a splash]'),
(34804, 's', '\r\nSprocket', ' n. Each of several teeth on a wheel engaging with links of a chain. [origin unknown]'),
(34805, 's', '\r\nSprout', ' â€”v. 1 put forth (shoots, hair, etc.). 2 begin to grow. â€”n. 1 shoot of a plant. 2 = *brussels sprout. [old english]'),
(34806, 's', '\r\nSpruce1', ' â€”adj. Neatly dressed etc.; smart. â€”v. (-cing) (usu. Foll. By up) make or become smart.  sprucely adv. Spruceness n. [perhaps from *spruce2]'),
(34807, 's', '\r\nSpruce2', ' n. 1 conifer with dense conical foliage. 2 its wood. [obsolete pruce prussia]'),
(34808, 's', '\r\nSprung', ' see *spring.'),
(34809, 's', '\r\nSpry', ' adj. (spryer, spryest) lively, nimble.  spryly adv. [origin unknown]'),
(34810, 's', '\r\nSpud', ' â€”n. 1 colloq. Potato. 2 small narrow spade for weeding. â€”v. (-dd-) (foll. By up, out) remove with a spud. [origin unknown]'),
(34811, 's', '\r\nSpue', ' var. Of *spew.'),
(34812, 's', '\r\nSpumante', ' n. Italian sparkling white wine. [italian, = sparkling]'),
(34813, 's', '\r\nSpume', ' n. & v. (-ming) froth, foam.  spumy adj. (-ier, -iest). [latin spuma]'),
(34814, 's', '\r\nSpun', ' past and past part. Of *spin.'),
(34815, 's', '\r\nSpunk', ' n. 1 colloq. Courage, mettle, spirit. 2 coarse slang semen. 3 touchwood. [origin unknown]'),
(34816, 's', '\r\nSpunky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Brave, spirited.'),
(34817, 's', '\r\nSpun', 'silk  n. Cheap material containing waste silk.'),
(34818, 's', '\r\nSpur', ' â€”n. 1 small spike or spiked wheel worn on a rider''s heel for urging on a horse. 2 stimulus, incentive. 3 spur-shaped thing, esp.: a a projection from a mountain or mountain range. B a branch road or railway. C a hard projection on a cock''s leg. â€”v. (-rr-) 1 prick (a horse) with spurs. 2 incite or stimulate.  on the spur of the moment on impulse. [old english]'),
(34819, 's', '\r\nSpurge', ' n. Plant with an acrid milky juice. [latin expurgare to clean out]'),
(34820, 's', '\r\nSpurious', ' adj. Not genuine, fake. [latin]'),
(34821, 's', '\r\nSpurn', ' v. Reject with disdain or contempt.  [old english]'),
(34822, 's', '\r\nSpurt', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) gush out in a jet or stream. 2 make a sudden effort. â€”n. 1 sudden gushing out, jet. 2 short burst of speed, growth, etc. [origin unknown]'),
(34823, 's', '\r\nSputnik', ' n. Russian artificial satellite orbiting the earth. [russian]'),
(34824, 's', '\r\nSputter', ' â€”v. Make a series of quick explosive sounds, splutter. â€”n. This sound. [dutch (imitative)]'),
(34825, 's', '\r\nSputum', ' n. (pl. Sputa) 1 saliva. 2 expectorated matter, used esp. In diagnosis. [latin]'),
(34826, 's', '\r\nSpy', ' â€”n. (pl. Spies) 1 person who secretly collects and reports information for a government, company, etc. 2 person watching others secretly. â€”v. (spies, spied) 1 discern, see. 2 (often foll. By on) act as a spy. 3 (often foll. By into) pry.  spy out explore or discover, esp. Secretly. [french espie, espier]'),
(34827, 's', '\r\nSpyglass', ' n. Small telescope.'),
(34828, 's', '\r\nSpyhole', ' n. Peep-hole.'),
(34829, 's', '\r\nSq.', ' Abbr. Square.'),
(34830, 's', '\r\nSqn.', 'Ldr.  Abbr. Squadron leader.'),
(34831, 's', '\r\nSquab', ' â€”n. 1 young (esp. Unfledged) pigeon or other bird. 2 short fat person. 3 stuffed cushion, esp. As part of a car-seat. 4 sofa, ottoman. â€”adj. Short and fat, squat. [perhaps from scandinavian]'),
(34832, 's', '\r\nSquabble', ' â€”n. Petty or noisy quarrel. â€”v. (-ling) engage in this. [probably imitative]'),
(34833, 's', '\r\nSquad', ' n. 1 small group sharing a task etc., esp. Of soldiers or policemen (drug squad). 2 sport team. [french escouade]'),
(34834, 's', '\r\nSquad', 'car  n. Police car.'),
(34835, 's', '\r\nSquaddie', ' n. (also squaddy) (pl. -ies) slang recruit; private.'),
(34836, 's', '\r\nSquadron', ' n. 1 unit of the raf with 10â€“18 aircraft. 2 detachment of warships employed on a particular duty. 3 organized group etc., esp. A cavalry division of two troops. [italian squadrone: related to *squad]'),
(34837, 's', '\r\nSquadron', 'leader  n. Commander of an raf squadron, next below wing commander.'),
(34838, 's', '\r\nSqualid', ' adj. 1 filthy, dirty. 2 mean or poor in appearance. [latin]'),
(34839, 's', '\r\nSquall', ' â€”n. 1 sudden or violent wind, esp. With rain, snow, or sleet. 2 discordant cry; scream (esp. Of a baby). â€”v. 1 utter a squall; scream. 2 utter with a squall.  squally adj. [probably alteration of *squeal after *bawl]'),
(34840, 's', '\r\nSqualor', ' n. Filthy or squalid state. [latin]'),
(34841, 's', '\r\nSquander', ' v. Spend wastefully. [origin unknown]'),
(34842, 's', '\r\nSquare', ' â€”n. 1 rectangle with four equal sides. 2 object of (approximately) this shape. 3 open (usu. Four-sided) area surrounded by buildings. 4 product of a number multiplied by itself (16 is the square of 4). 5 l- or t-shaped instrument for obtaining or testing right angles. 6 slang conventional or old-fashioned person. â€”adj. 1 square-shaped. 2 having or in the form of a right angle (square corner). 3 angular, not round. 4 designating a unit of measure equal to the area of a square whose side is one of the unit specified (square metre). 5 (often foll. By with) level, parallel. 6 (usu. Foll. By to) at right angles. 7 sturdy, squat (a man of square frame). 8 arranged; settled (get things square). 9 (also all square) a with no money owed. B (of scores) equal. 10 fair and honest. 11 direct (met with a square refusal). 12 slang conventional or old-fashioned. â€”adv. 1 squarely (hit me square on the jaw). 2 fairly, honestly. â€”v. (-ring) 1 make square. 2 multiply (a number) by itself. 3 (usu. Foll. By to, with) adjust; make or be suitable or consistent; reconcile. 4 mark out in squares. 5 settle or pay (a bill etc.). 6 place (one''s shoulders etc.) Squarely facing forwards. 7 colloq. Pay or bribe (a person). 8 (also absol.) Make the scores of (a match etc.) Equal.  back to square one colloq. Back to the starting-point with no progress made. Out of square not at right angles. Square the circle 1 construct a square equal in area to a given circle. 2 do what is impossible. Square peg in a round hole see *peg. Square up settle an account etc. Square up to 1 move threateningly towards (a person). 2 face and tackle (a difficulty etc.) Resolutely.  squarely adv. Squareness n. Squarish adj. [french esquare, latin quadra]'),
(34843, 's', '\r\nSquare-bashing', ' n. Slang military drill on a barrack-square.'),
(34844, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'brackets  n.pl. Brackets of the form [ ].'),
(34845, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'dance  n. Dance with usu. Four couples facing inwards from four sides.'),
(34846, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'deal  n. Fair bargain or treatment.'),
(34847, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'leg  n. Fielding position in cricket at some distance on the batsman''s leg side and nearly opposite the stumps.'),
(34848, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'meal  n. Substantial meal.'),
(34849, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'measure  n. Measure expressed in square units.'),
(34850, 's', '\r\nSquare-rigged', ' adj. With the principal sails at right angles to the length of the ship.'),
(34851, 's', '\r\nSquare', 'root  n. Number that multiplied by itself gives a specified number.'),
(34852, 's', '\r\nSquash1', ' â€”v. 1 crush or squeeze, esp. Flat or into pulp. 2 (often foll. By into) colloq. Put or make one''s way by squeezing. 3 belittle, bully (a person). 4 suppress (a proposal, allegation, etc.). â€”n. 1 crowd; crowded state. 2 drink made of crushed fruit. 3 (in full squash rackets) game played with rackets and a small ball in a closed court.'),
(34853, 's', '\r\nSquash2', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) 1 trailing annual plant. 2 edible gourd of this. [narragansett]'),
(34854, 's', '\r\nSquat', ' â€”v. (-tt-) 1 sit on one''s heels or on the ground with the knees drawn up. 2 colloq. Sit down. 3 occupy a building as a squatter. â€”adj. (squatter, squattest) short and thick, dumpy. â€”n. 1 squatting posture. 2 place occupied by squatters. [french esquatir flatten]'),
(34855, 's', '\r\nSquatter', ' n. Person who inhabits unoccupied premises without permission.'),
(34856, 's', '\r\nSquaw', ' n. N. American indian woman or wife. [narragansett]'),
(34857, 's', '\r\nSquawk', ' â€”n. 1 loud harsh cry, esp. Of a bird. 2 complaint. â€”v. Utter a squawk. [imitative]'),
(34858, 's', '\r\nSqueak', ' â€”n. 1 short high-pitched cry or sound. 2 (also narrow squeak) narrow escape. â€”v. 1 make a squeak. 2 utter (words) shrilly. 3 (foll. By by, through) colloq. Pass narrowly. 4 slang turn informer. [imitative: related to *squeal, *shriek]'),
(34859, 's', '\r\nSqueaky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) making a squeaking sound.  squeakily adv. Squeakiness n.'),
(34860, 's', '\r\nSqueaky', 'clean  adj. (usu. Hyphenated when attrib.) Colloq. 1 completely clean. 2 above criticism.'),
(34861, 's', '\r\nSqueal', ' â€”n. Prolonged shrill sound or cry. â€”v. 1 make, or utter with, a squeal. 2 slang turn informer. 3 colloq. Protest vociferously. [imitative]'),
(34862, 's', '\r\nSqueamish', ' adj. 1 easily nauseated or disgusted. 2 fastidious.  squeamishly adv. Squeamishness n. [anglo-french escoymos]'),
(34863, 's', '\r\nSqueegee', ' n. Rubber-edged implement on a handle, for cleaning windows etc. [squeege, alteration of *squeeze]'),
(34864, 's', '\r\nSqueeze', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 (often foll. By out) a exert pressure on, esp. To extract moisture etc. B extract (moisture) by squeezing. 2 reduce in size or alter in shape by squeezing. 3 force or push into or through a small or narrow space. 4 a harass or pressure (a person). B (usu. Foll. By out of) obtain by extortion, entreaty, etc. 5 press (a person''s hand) in sympathy etc. â€”n. 1 squeezing or being squeezed. 2 close embrace. 3 crowd, crowded state. 4 small quantity produced by squeezing (squeeze of lemon). 5 restriction on borrowing, investment, etc., in a financial crisis.  put the squeeze on colloq. Coerce or pressure. [origin unknown]'),
(34865, 's', '\r\nSqueeze-box', ' n. Colloq. Accordion or concertina.'),
(34866, 's', '\r\nSquelch', ' â€”v. 1 a make a sucking sound as of treading in thick mud. B move with a squelching sound. 2 disconcert, silence. â€”n. Act or sound of squelching.  squelchy adj. [imitative]'),
(34867, 's', '\r\nSquib', ' n. 1 small hissing firework that finally explodes. 2 satirical essay. [perhaps imitative]'),
(34868, 's', '\r\nSquid', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) ten-armed marine cephalopod used as food. [origin unknown]'),
(34869, 's', '\r\nSquidgy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Squashy, soggy. [imitative]'),
(34870, 's', '\r\nSquiffy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slang slightly drunk. [origin unknown]'),
(34871, 's', '\r\nSquiggle', ' n. Short curly line, esp. In handwriting.  squiggly adj. [imitative]'),
(34872, 's', '\r\nSquill', ' n. Bulbous plant resembling a bluebell. [latin squilla]'),
(34873, 's', '\r\nSquint', ' â€”v. 1 have eyes that do not move together but look in different directions. 2 (often foll. By at) look obliquely or with half-closed eyes. â€”n. 1 condition causing squinting. 2 stealthy or sidelong glance. 3 colloq. Glance, look. 4 oblique opening in a church wall affording a view of the altar. [obsolete asquint, perhaps from dutch schuinte slant]'),
(34874, 's', '\r\nSquire', ' â€”n. 1 country gentleman, esp. The chief landowner of a district. 2 hist. Knight''s attendant. â€”v. (-ring) (of a man) attend or escort (a woman). [related to *esquire]'),
(34875, 's', '\r\nSquirearchy', ' n. (pl. -ies) landowners collectively.'),
(34876, 's', '\r\nSquirm', ' â€”v. 1 wriggle, writhe. 2 show or feel embarrassment. â€”n. Squirming movement. [imitative]'),
(34877, 's', '\r\nSquirrel', ' â€”n. 1 bushy-tailed usu. Tree-living rodent. 2 its fur. 3 hoarder. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 (often foll. By away) hoard. 2 (often foll. By around) bustle about. [greek skiouros, from skia shade, oura tail]'),
(34878, 's', '\r\nSquirt', ' â€”v. 1 eject (liquid etc.) In a jet. 2 be ejected in this way. 3 splash with a squirted substance. â€”n. 1 a jet of water etc. B small quantity squirted. 2 syringe. 3 colloq. Insignificant but self-assertive person. [imitative]'),
(34879, 's', '\r\nSquish', ' colloq. â€”n. Slight squelching sound. â€”v. Move with a squish.  squishy adj. (-ier, -iest). [imitative]'),
(34880, 's', '\r\nSr', ' symb. Strontium.'),
(34881, 's', '\r\nSr.', ' Abbr. 1 senior. 2 seÃ±or. 3 signor.'),
(34882, 's', '\r\nSrn', ' abbr. State registered nurse.'),
(34883, 's', '\r\nSs', ' abbr. 1 steamship. 2 hist. Nazi special police force. 3 saints. [sense 2 from german schutz-staffel]'),
(34884, 's', '\r\nSse', ' abbr. South-south-east.'),
(34885, 's', '\r\nSsw', ' abbr. South-south-west.'),
(34886, 's', '\r\nSt', ' abbr. Saint.'),
(34887, 's', '\r\nSt.', ' Abbr. Street.'),
(34888, 's', '\r\nSt.', ' Abbr. Stone (in weight).'),
(34889, 's', '\r\nStab', ' â€”v. (-bb-) 1 pierce or wound with a knife etc. 2 (often foll. By at) aim a blow with such a weapon. 3 cause a sensation like being stabbed (stabbing pain). 4 hurt or distress (a person, feelings, etc.). â€”n. 1 act of stabbing. 2 wound from this. 3 colloq. Attempt. [origin unknown]'),
(34890, 's', '\r\nStability', ' n. Being stable. [latin: related to *stable]'),
(34891, 's', '\r\nStabilize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make or become stable.  stabilization n.'),
(34892, 's', '\r\nStabilizer', ' n. (also -iser) 1 device used to keep esp. A ship, aircraft, or (in pl.) Child''s bicycle stable. 2 food additive for preserving texture.'),
(34893, 's', '\r\nStab', 'in the back  â€”n. Treacherous attack. â€”v. Betray.'),
(34894, 's', '\r\nStable', ' â€”adj. (-bler, -blest) 1 firmly fixed or established; not likely to move or change. 2 (of a person) not easily upset or disturbed. â€”n. 1 building for keeping horses. 2 establishment for training racehorses. 3 racehorses from one stable. 4 persons, products, etc., having a common origin or affiliation. 5 such an origin or affiliation. â€”v. (-ling) put or keep in a stable.  stably adv. [latin stabilis from sto to stand]'),
(34895, 's', '\r\nStable-companion', ' n. (also stable-mate) 1 horse of the same stable. 2 member of the same organization.'),
(34896, 's', '\r\nStabling', ' n. Accommodation for horses.'),
(34897, 's', '\r\nStaccato', ' esp. Mus. â€”adv. & adj. With each sound or note sharply distinct. â€”n. (pl. -s) staccato passage or delivery. [italian]'),
(34898, 's', '\r\nStack', ' â€”n. 1 (esp. Orderly) pile or heap. 2 = *haystack. 3 colloq. Large quantity (a stack of work; stacks of money). 4 a = *chimney-stack. B = *smokestack. C tall factory chimney. 5 stacked group of aircraft. 6 part of a library where books are compactly stored. 7 high detached rock, esp. Off the coast of scotland. â€”v. 1 pile in a stack or stacks. 2 a arrange (cards) secretly for cheating. B manipulate (circumstances etc.) To suit one. 3 cause (aircraft) to fly in circles while waiting to land. [old norse]'),
(34899, 's', '\r\nStadium', ' n. (pl. -s) athletic or sports ground with tiered seats for spectators. [greek stadion]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(34900, 's', '\r\nStaff', ' â€”n. 1 a stick or pole for use in walking or as a weapon. B stick or rod as a sign of office etc. C person or thing that supports. 2 a people employed in a business etc. B those in authority in a school etc. C body of officers assisting an officer in high command (general staff). 3 (pl. -s or staves) mus. Set of usu. Five parallel lines on or between which notes are placed to indicate their pitch. â€”v. Provide (an institution etc.) With staff. [old english]'),
(34901, 's', '\r\nStaff', 'college  n. College where officers are trained for staff duties.'),
(34902, 's', '\r\nStaff', 'nurse  n.  Nurse ranking just below a sister.'),
(34903, 's', '\r\nStaff', 'sergeant  n. Senior sergeant of a non-infantry company.'),
(34904, 's', '\r\nStag', ' n. 1 adult male deer. 2 stock exch. Slang person who applies for new shares, intending to sell at once for a profit. [old english]'),
(34905, 's', '\r\nStag', 'beetle  n. Beetle with branched mandibles like antlers.'),
(34906, 's', '\r\nStage', ' â€”n. 1 point or period in a process or development. 2 a raised platform, esp. For performing plays etc. On. B (prec. By the) theatrical profession, drama. C scene of action. 3 a regular stopping-place on a route. B distance between two of these. 4 astronaut. Section of a rocket with a separate engine. â€”v. (-ging) 1 present (a play etc.) On stage. 2 arrange, organize (staged a demonstration). [french estage, ultimately from latin sto stand]'),
(34907, 's', '\r\nStagecoach', ' n. Hist. Large closed horse-drawn coach running on a regular route by stages.'),
(34908, 's', '\r\nStagecraft', ' n. Theatrical skill or experience.'),
(34909, 's', '\r\nStage', 'direction  n. Instruction in a play as to actors'' movements, sound effects, etc.'),
(34910, 's', '\r\nStage', 'fright  n. Performer''s fear of an audience.'),
(34911, 's', '\r\nStage-hand', ' n. Person moving stage scenery etc.'),
(34912, 's', '\r\nStage-manage', ' v. 1 be the stage-manager of. 2 arrange and control for effect.'),
(34913, 's', '\r\nStage-manager', ' n. Person responsible for lighting and mechanical arrangements etc. On stage.'),
(34914, 's', '\r\nStage-struck', ' adj. Obsessed with becoming an actor.'),
(34915, 's', '\r\nStage', 'whisper  n. 1 an aside. 2 loud whisper meant to be overheard.'),
(34916, 's', '\r\nStagey', ' var. Of *stagy.'),
(34917, 's', '\r\nStagflation', ' n. Econ. State of inflation without a corresponding increase of demand and employment. [blend of stagnation, inflation]'),
(34918, 's', '\r\nStagger', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) walk unsteadily. 2 shock, confuse. 3 arrange (events etc.) So that they do not coincide. 4 arrange (objects) so that they are not in line. â€”n. 1 tottering movement. 2 (in pl.) Disease, esp. Of horses and cattle, causing staggering. [old norse]'),
(34919, 's', '\r\nStaggering', ' adj. Astonishing, bewildering.  staggeringly adv.'),
(34920, 's', '\r\nStaghound', ' n. Large dog used for hunting deer.'),
(34921, 's', '\r\nStaging', ' n. 1 presentation of a play etc. 2 a platform or support, esp. Temporary. B shelves for plants in a greenhouse.'),
(34922, 's', '\r\nStaging', 'post  n. Regular stopping-place, esp. On an air route.'),
(34923, 's', '\r\nStagnant', ' adj. 1 (of liquid) motionless, having no current. 2 dull, sluggish.  stagnancy n. [latin stagnum pool]'),
(34924, 's', '\r\nStagnate', ' v. (-ting) be or become stagnant.  stagnation n.'),
(34925, 's', '\r\nStag-party', ' n. Colloq. All-male celebration held esp. For a man about to marry.'),
(34926, 's', '\r\nStagy', ' adj. (also stagey) (-ier, -iest) theatrical, artificial, exaggerated.'),
(34927, 's', '\r\nStaid', ' adj. Of quiet and steady character; sedate. [= stayed, past part. Of *stay1]'),
(34928, 's', '\r\nStain', ' â€”v. 1 discolour or be discoloured by the action of liquid sinking in. 2 spoil, damage (a reputation, character, etc.). 3 colour (wood, glass, etc.) With a penetrating substance. 4 impregnate (a specimen) with a colouring agent for microscopic examination. â€”n. 1 discoloration; spot, mark. 2 blot, blemish; damage to a reputation etc. 3 substance used in staining. [earlier distain from french desteindre]'),
(34929, 's', '\r\nStained', 'glass  n. Coloured glass in a leaded window etc.'),
(34930, 's', '\r\nStainless', ' adj. 1 without stains. 2 not liable to stain.'),
(34931, 's', '\r\nStainless', 'steel  n. Chrome steel resisting rust or tarnish.'),
(34932, 's', '\r\nStair', ' n. 1 each of a set of fixed indoor steps. 2 (usu. In pl.) Set of these. [old english]'),
(34933, 's', '\r\nStaircase', ' n. Flight of stairs and the supporting structure.'),
(34934, 's', '\r\nStair-rod', ' n. Rod securing a carpet between two steps.'),
(34935, 's', '\r\nStairway', ' n. = *staircase.'),
(34936, 's', '\r\nStairwell', ' n. Shaft for a staircase.'),
(34937, 's', '\r\nStake1', ' â€”n. 1 stout sharpened stick driven into the ground as a support, boundary mark, etc. 2 hist. A post to which a condemned person was tied to be burnt alive. B (prec. By the) such death as a punishment. â€”v. (-king) 1 secure or support with a stake or stakes. 2 (foll. By off, out) mark off (an area) with stakes. 3 establish (a claim).  stake out colloq. Place under surveillance. [old english]'),
(34938, 's', '\r\nStake2', ' â€”n. 1 sum of money etc. Wagered on an event. 2 (often foll. By in) interest or concern, esp. Financial. 3 (in pl.) A prize-money, esp. In a horse-race. B such a race. â€”v. 1 wager. 2 us colloq. Support, esp. Financially.  at stake risked, to be won or lost. [old english]'),
(34939, 's', '\r\nStakeholder', ' n. Independent party with whom money etc. Wagered is deposited.'),
(34940, 's', '\r\nStake-out', ' n. Esp. Us colloq. Period of surveillance.'),
(34941, 's', '\r\nStakhanovite', ' n. (often attrib.) Exceptionally productive worker. [stakhanov, name of a russian coalminer]'),
(34942, 's', '\r\nStalactite', ' n. Icicle-like deposit of calcium carbonate hanging from the roof of a cave etc. [greek stalaktos dripping]'),
(34943, 's', '\r\nStalagmite', ' n. Icicle-like deposit of calcium carbonate rising from the floor of a cave etc. [greek stalagma a drop]'),
(34944, 's', '\r\nStale', ' â€”adj. 1 a not fresh. B musty, insipid, or otherwise the worse for age or use. 2 trite, unoriginal (stale joke). 3 (of an athlete or performer) impaired by excessive training. â€”v. (-ling) make or become stale.  staleness n. [anglo-french estaler halt]'),
(34945, 's', '\r\nStalemate', ' â€”n. 1 chess position counting as a draw, in which a player cannot move except into check. 2 deadlock. â€”v. (-ting) 1 chess bring (a player) to a stalemate. 2 bring to a deadlock. [obsolete stale: related to *stale, *mate2]'),
(34946, 's', '\r\nStalinism', ' n. Centralized authoritarian form of socialism associated with stalin.  stalinist n. & adj. [stalin, a soviet statesman]'),
(34947, 's', '\r\nStalk1', ' n. 1 main stem of a herbaceous plant. 2 slender attachment or support of a leaf, flower, fruit, etc. 3 similar support for an organ etc. In an animal. [diminutive of (now dial.) Stale rung]'),
(34948, 's', '\r\nStalk2', ' â€”v. 1 pursue (game or an enemy) stealthily. 2 stride, walk in a haughty manner. 3 formal or rhet. Move silently or threateningly through (a place) (fear stalked the land). â€”n. 1 stalking of game. 2 haughty gait. [old english: related to *steal]'),
(34949, 's', '\r\nStalking-horse', ' n. 1 horse concealing a hunter. 2 pretext concealing one''s real intentions or actions. 3 weak political candidate forcing an election in the hope of a more serious contender coming forward.'),
(34950, 's', '\r\nStall1', ' â€”n. 1 trader''s booth or table in a market etc. 2 compartment for one animal in a stable or cowhouse. 3 fixed, usu. Partly enclosed, seat in the choir or chancel of a church. 4 (usu. In pl.) Each of the seats on the ground floor of a theatre. 5 a compartment for one person in a shower-bath etc. B compartment for one horse at the start of a race. 6 a stalling of an engine or aircraft. B condition resulting from this. â€”v. 1 (of a vehicle or its engine) stop because of an overload on the engine or an inadequate supply of fuel to it. 2 (of an aircraft or its pilot) lose control because the speed is too low. 3 cause to stall. [old english]'),
(34951, 's', '\r\nStall2', ' v. 1 play for time when being questioned etc. 2 delay, obstruct. [stall â€˜decoyâ€™: probably related to *stall1]'),
(34952, 's', '\r\nStallholder', ' n. Person in charge of a stall at a market etc.'),
(34953, 's', '\r\nStallion', ' n. Uncastrated adult male horse. [french estalon]'),
(34954, 's', '\r\nStalwart', ' â€”adj. 1 strong, sturdy. 2 courageous, resolute, reliable. â€”n. Stalwart person, esp. A loyal comrade. [old english, = place, *worth]'),
(34955, 's', '\r\nStamen', ' n. Organ producing pollen in a flower. [latin, = warp, thread]'),
(34956, 's', '\r\nStamina', ' n. Physical or mental endurance. [latin, pl. Of *stamen]'),
(34957, 's', '\r\nStammer', ' â€”v. 1 speak haltingly, esp. With pauses or rapid repetitions of the same syllable. 2 (often foll. By out) utter (words) in this way. â€”n. 1 tendency to stammer. 2 instance of stammering. [old english]'),
(34958, 's', '\r\nStamp', ' â€”v. 1 a bring down (one''s foot) heavily, esp. On the ground. B (often foll. By on) crush or flatten in this way. C walk heavily. 2 a impress (a design, mark, etc.) On a surface. B impress (a surface) with a pattern etc. 3 affix a postage or other stamp to. 4 assign a specific character to; mark out. â€”n. 1 instrument for stamping. 2 a mark or design made by this. B impression of an official mark required to be made on deeds, bills of exchange, etc., as evidence of payment of tax. 3 small adhesive piece of paper indicating that payment has been made, esp. A postage stamp. 4 mark or label etc. On a commodity as evidence of quality etc. 5 act or sound of stamping the foot. 6 characteristic mark or quality.  stamp on 1 impress (an idea etc.) On (the memory etc.). 2 suppress.'),
(34959, 's', '\r\nStamp-collector', ' n. Philatelist.'),
(34960, 's', '\r\nStamp-duty', ' n. Duty imposed on certain legal documents.'),
(34961, 's', '\r\nStampede', ' â€”n. 1 sudden flight or hurried movement of animals or people. 2 response of many persons at once to a common impulse. â€”v. (-ding) (cause to) take part in a stampede. [spanish estampida crash, uproar]'),
(34962, 's', '\r\nStamping-ground', ' n. Colloq. Favourite haunt.'),
(34963, 's', '\r\nStance', ' n. 1 standpoint; attitude. 2 attitude or position of the body, esp. When hitting a ball etc. [italian stanza standing]'),
(34964, 's', '\r\nStanch', ' v. (also staunch) 1 restrain the flow of (esp. Blood). 2 restrain the flow from (esp. A wound). [french estanchier]'),
(34965, 's', '\r\nStanchion', ' n. 1 upright post or support. 2 upright bar or frame for confining cattle in a stall. [anglo-french]'),
(34966, 's', '\r\nStand', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Stood) 1 have, take, or maintain an upright position, esp. On the feet or a base. 2 be situated (here once stood a village). 3 be of a specified height. 4 be in a specified state (stands accused; it stands as follows). 5 set in an upright or specified position (stood it against the wall). 6 a move to and remain in a specified position (stand aside). B take a specified attitude (stand aloof). 7 maintain a position; avoid falling, moving, or being moved. 8 assume a stationary position; cease to move. 9 remain valid or unaltered. 10 naut. Hold a specified course. 11 endure, tolerate. 12 provide at one''s own expense (stood him a drink). 13 (often foll. By for) be a candidate (for office etc.) (stood for parliament). 14 act in a specified capacity (stood proxy). 15 undergo (trial). â€”n. 1 cessation from progress, stoppage. 2 a mil. Halt made to repel an attack. B resistance to attack or compulsion (esp. Make a stand). C cricket prolonged period at the wicket by two batsmen. 3 position taken up; attitude adopted. 4 rack, set of shelves, etc. For storage. 5 open-fronted stall or structure for a trader, exhibitor, etc. 6 standing-place for vehicles. 7 a raised structure to sit or stand on. B us witness-box. 8 each halt made for a performance on a tour. 9 group of growing plants (stand of trees).  as it stands 1 in its present condition. 2 in the present circumstances. Stand by 1 stand nearby; look on without interfering. 2 uphold, support (a person). 3 adhere to (a promise etc.). 4 be ready for action. Stand a chance see *chance. Stand corrected accept correction. Stand down withdraw from a position or candidacy. Stand for 1 represent, signify, imply. 2 colloq. Endure, tolerate. Stand one''s ground not yield. Stand in (usu. Foll. By for) deputize. Stand off 1 move or keep away. 2 temporarily dismiss (an employee). Stand on insist on, observe scrupulously. Stand on one''s own feet (or own two feet) be self-reliant or independent. Stand out 1 be prominent or outstanding. 2 (usu. Foll. By against, for) persist in opposition or support. Stand to 1 mil. Stand ready for an attack. 2 abide by. 3 be likely or certain to. Stand to reason be obvious. Stand up 1 a rise to one''s feet. B come to, remain in, or place in a standing position. 2 (of an argument etc.) Be valid. 3 colloq. Fail to keep an appointment with.'),
(34967, 's', '\r\nStandard', ' â€”n. 1 object, quality, or measure serving as a basis, example, or principle to which others conform or should conform or by which others are judged. 2 a level of excellence etc. Required or specified (not up to standard). B average quality (of a low standard). 3 ordinary procedure etc. 4 distinctive flag. 5 a upright support. B upright pipe. 6 a tree or shrub that stands without support. B shrub grafted on an upright stem and trained in tree form. 7 tune or song of established popularity. â€”adj. 1 serving or used as a standard. 2 of a normal or prescribed quality, type, or size. 3 of recognized and permanent value; authoritative (standard book on jazz). 4 (of language) conforming to established educated usage. [anglo-french: related to *extend, and in senses 5 and 6 of n. Influenced by *stand]'),
(34968, 's', '\r\nStandard', 'assessment task  n. Standard test given to schoolchildren.'),
(34969, 's', '\r\nStandard-bearer', ' n. 1 soldier who carries a standard. 2 prominent leader in a cause.'),
(34970, 's', '\r\nStandardize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) cause to conform to a standard.  standardization n.'),
(34971, 's', '\r\nStandard', 'lamp  n. Lamp on a tall upright with a base.'),
(34972, 's', '\r\nStandard', 'of living  n. Degree of material comfort of a person or group.'),
(34973, 's', '\r\nStandard', 'time  n. Uniform time for places in approximately the same longitude, established in a country or region by law or custom.'),
(34974, 's', '\r\nStand-by', ' n. (pl. -bys) 1 (often attrib.) Person or thing ready if needed in an emergency etc. 2 readiness for duty (on stand-by).'),
(34975, 's', '\r\nStand-in', ' n. Deputy or substitute.'),
(34976, 's', '\r\nStanding', ' â€”n. 1 esteem or repute, esp. High; status. 2 duration (of long standing). â€”adj. 1 that stands, upright. 2 established, permanent (a standing rule; a standing army). 3 (of a jump, start, etc.) Performed with no run-up. 4 (of water) stagnant.'),
(34977, 's', '\r\nStanding', 'committee  n. Committee that is permanent during the existence of the appointing body.'),
(34978, 's', '\r\nStanding', 'joke  n. Object of permanent ridicule.'),
(34979, 's', '\r\nStanding', 'order  n. Instruction to a banker to make regular payments, or to a retailer for a regular supply of goods.'),
(34980, 's', '\r\nStanding', 'orders  n.pl. Rules governing procedure in a parliament, council, etc.'),
(34981, 's', '\r\nStanding', 'ovation  n. Prolonged applause from an audience which has risen to its feet.'),
(34982, 's', '\r\nStanding-room', ' n. Space to stand in.'),
(34983, 's', '\r\nStand-off', 'half  n. Rugby half-back forming a link between the scrum-half and the three-quarters.'),
(34984, 's', '\r\nStandoffish', ' adj. Cold or distant in manner.'),
(34985, 's', '\r\nStandpipe', ' n. Vertical pipe extending from a water supply, esp. One connecting a temporary tap to the mains.'),
(34986, 's', '\r\nStandpoint', ' n. Point of view.'),
(34987, 's', '\r\nStandstill', ' n. Stoppage; inability to proceed.'),
(34988, 's', '\r\nStand-up', ' attrib. Adj. 1 (of a meal) eaten standing. 2 (of a fight) violent and thorough. 3 (of a collar) not turned down. 4 (of a comedian) telling jokes to an audience.'),
(34989, 's', '\r\nStank', ' past of *stink.'),
(34990, 's', '\r\nStanza', ' n. Basic metrical unit of a poem etc., typically of four to twelve rhymed lines. [italian]'),
(34991, 's', '\r\nStaphylococcus', ' n. (pl. -cocci) bacterium sometimes forming pus.  staphylococcal adj. [greek staphule bunch of grapes, kokkos berry]'),
(34992, 's', '\r\nStaple1', ' â€”n. U-shaped metal bar or piece of wire with pointed ends for driving into and holding papers together, or holding an electrical wire in place, etc. â€”v. (-ling) fasten or provide with a staple.  stapler n. [old english]'),
(34993, 's', '\r\nStaple2', ' â€”n. 1 principal or important article of commerce (staples of british industry). 2 chief element or main component. 3 fibre of cotton or wool etc. With regard to its quality (cotton of fine staple). â€”attrib. Adj. 1 main or principal (staple diet). 2 important as a product or export. [french estaple market]'),
(34994, 's', '\r\nStar', ' â€”n. 1 celestial body appearing as a luminous point in the night sky. 2 large naturally luminous gaseous body such as the sun. 3 celestial body regarded as influencing fortunes etc. 4 thing like a star in shape or appearance. 5 decoration or mark of rank or excellence etc., usu. With radiating points. 6 a famous or brilliant person; principal performer (star of the show).'),
(34995, 's', '\r\nStarboard', ' â€”n. Right-hand side of a ship or aircraft looking forward. â€”v. (also absol.) Turn (the helm) to starboard. [old english, = steer board]'),
(34996, 's', '\r\nStarch', ' â€”n. 1 polysaccharide obtained chiefly from cereals and potatoes. 2 preparation of this for stiffening fabric. 3 stiffness of manner; formality. â€”v. Stiffen (clothing) with starch. [old english: related to *stark]'),
(34997, 's', '\r\nStarchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of, like, or containing starch. 2 prim, formal.  starchily adv. Starchiness n.'),
(34998, 's', '\r\nStardust', ' n. 1 multitude of stars looking like dust. 2 romance, magic feeling.'),
(34999, 's', '\r\nStare', ' â€”v. (-ring) 1 (usu. Foll. By at) look fixedly, esp. In curiosity, surprise, horror, etc. 2 reduce (a person) to a specified condition by staring (stared me into silence). â€”n. Staring gaze.  stare a person in the face be evident or imminent. Stare a person out stare at a person until he or she looks away. [old english]'),
(35000, 's', '\r\nStarfish', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) echinoderm with five or more radiating arms.'),
(35001, 's', '\r\nStar-gazer', ' n. Colloq. Usu. Derog. Or joc. Astronomer or astrologer.'),
(35002, 's', '\r\nStark', ' â€”adj. 1 sharply evident (in stark contrast). 2 desolate, bare. 3 absolute (stark madness). â€”adv. Completely, wholly (stark naked).  starkly adv. Starkness n. [old english]'),
(35003, 's', '\r\nStarkers', ' predic. Adj. Slang stark naked.'),
(35004, 's', '\r\nStarlet', ' n. Promising young performer, esp. A film actress.'),
(35005, 's', '\r\nStarlight', ' n. Light of the stars.'),
(35006, 's', '\r\nStarling', ' n. Gregarious bird with blackish speckled lustrous plumage. [old english]'),
(35007, 's', '\r\nStarlit', ' adj. 1 lit by stars. 2 with stars visible.'),
(35008, 's', '\r\nStar', 'of david  n. Two interlaced equilateral triangles used as a jewish and israeli symbol.'),
(35009, 's', '\r\nStarry', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of stars. 2 like a star.'),
(35010, 's', '\r\nStarry-eyed', ' adj. Colloq. 1 enthusiastic but impractical. 2 euphoric.'),
(35011, 's', '\r\nStars', 'and stripes  n.pl. National flag of the us.'),
(35012, 's', '\r\nStar-studded', ' adj. Covered with stars; featuring many famous performers.'),
(35013, 's', '\r\nStart', ' abbr. Strategic arms reduction treaty (or talks).'),
(35014, 's', '\r\nStart', ' â€”v. 1 begin. 2 set in motion or action (started a fire). 3 set oneself in motion or action. 4 begin a journey etc. 5 (often foll. By up) (cause to) begin operating. 6 a cause or enable (a person) to make a beginning (started me in business). B (foll. By pres. Part.) Cause (a person) to begin (started me coughing). 7 (often foll. By up) establish. 8 give a signal to (competitors) to start in a race. 9 (often foll. By up, from, etc.) Jump in surprise, pain, etc. 10 spring out, up, etc. 11  conceive (a baby). 12  rouse (game etc.). 13 a (of timbers etc.) Spring out; give way. B cause (timbers etc.) To do this. â€”n. 1 beginning. 2 place from which a race etc. Begins. 3 advantage given at the beginning of a race etc. 4 advantageous initial position in life, business, etc. 5 sudden movement of surprise, pain, etc.  for a start colloq. As a beginning. Start off begin; begin to move. Start out begin a journey. Start up arise; occur. [old english]'),
(35015, 's', '\r\nStarter', ' n. 1 device for starting a vehicle engine etc. 2 first course of a meal. 3 person giving the signal for the start of a race. 4 horse or competitor starting in a race.  for starters colloq. To start with.'),
(35016, 's', '\r\nStarting-block', ' n. Shaped block for a runner''s feet at the start of a race.'),
(35017, 's', '\r\nStarting', 'price  n. Odds ruling at the start of a horse-race.'),
(35018, 's', '\r\nStartle', ' v. (-ling) shock or surprise. [old english]'),
(35019, 's', '\r\nStar', 'turn  n. Main item in an entertainment etc.'),
(35020, 's', '\r\nStarve', ' v. (-ving) 1 (cause to) die of hunger or suffer from malnourishment. 2 colloq. Feel very hungry (i''m starving). 3 a suffer from mental or spiritual want. B (foll. By for) feel a strong craving for. 4 (foll. By of) deprive of. 5 compel by starving (starved into surrender).  starvation n. [old english, = die]'),
(35021, 's', '\r\nStarveling', ' n. Archaic starving person or animal.'),
(35022, 's', '\r\nStar', 'wars  n.pl. Colloq. Strategic defence initiative.'),
(35023, 's', '\r\nStash', ' colloq. â€”v. (often foll. By away) 1 conceal; put in a safe place. 2 hoard. â€”n. 1 hiding-place. 2 thing hidden. [origin unknown]'),
(35024, 's', '\r\nStasis', ' n. (pl. Stases) 1 inactivity; stagnation. 2 stoppage of circulation. [greek]'),
(35025, 's', '\r\nState', ' â€”n. 1 existing condition or position of a person or thing. 2 colloq. A excited or agitated mental condition (esp. In a state). B untidy condition. 3 (usu. State) a political community under one government. B this as part of a federal republic. 4 (usu. State) (attrib.) A of, for, or concerned with the state. B reserved for or done on occasions of ceremony. 5 (usu. State) civil government. 6 pomp. 7 (the states) usa. â€”v. (-ting) 1 express in speech or writing. 2 fix, specify. 3 mus. Play (a theme etc.), esp. For the first time.  in state with all due ceremony. Lie in state be laid in a public place of honour before burial. [partly from *estate, partly from latin *status]'),
(35026, 's', '\r\nStateless', ' adj. Having no nationality or citizenship.'),
(35027, 's', '\r\nStately', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) dignified; imposing.  stateliness n.'),
(35028, 's', '\r\nStately', 'home  n. Large historic house, esp. One open to the public.'),
(35029, 's', '\r\nStatement', ' n. 1 stating or being stated; expression in words. 2 thing stated. 3 formal account of facts. 4 record of transactions in a bank account etc. 5 notification of the amount due to a tradesman etc.'),
(35030, 's', '\r\nState', 'of emergency  n. Condition of danger or disaster in a country, with normal constitutional procedures suspended.'),
(35031, 's', '\r\nState', 'of the art  â€”n. Current stage of esp. Technological development. â€”attrib. Adj. (usu. State-of-the-art) absolutely up-to-date (state-of-the-art weaponry).'),
(35032, 's', '\r\nStateroom', ' n. 1 state apartment. 2 large private cabin in a passenger ship.'),
(35033, 's', '\r\nState', 'school  n. School largely managed and funded by the public authorities.'),
(35034, 's', '\r\nStatesman', ' n. (fem. Stateswoman) distinguished and capable politician or diplomat.  statesmanlike adj. Statesmanship n.'),
(35035, 's', '\r\nStatic', ' â€”adj. 1 stationary; not acting or changing. 2 physics concerned with bodies at rest or forces in equilibrium. â€”n. 1 static electricity. 2 atmospherics.  statically adv. [greek statikos from sta- stand]'),
(35036, 's', '\r\nStatic', 'electricity  n. Electricity not flowing as a current.'),
(35037, 's', '\r\nStatics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) 1 science of bodies at rest or of forces in equilibrium. 2 = *static.'),
(35038, 's', '\r\nStation', ' â€”n. 1 a regular stopping-place on a railway line. B buildings of this. C (in comb.) Centre where vehicles of a specified type depart and arrive (coach station). 2 person or thing''s allotted place or building etc. 3 centre for a particular service or activity. 4 establishment involved in broadcasting. 5 a military or naval base. B inhabitants of this. 6 position in life; rank, status. 7 austral. & nz large sheep or cattle farm. â€”v. 1 assign a station to. 2 put in position. [latin statio from sto stat- stand]'),
(35039, 's', '\r\nStationary', ' adj. 1 not moving. 2 not meant to be moved. 3 unchanging. [latin: related to *station]'),
(35040, 's', '\r\nStationer', ' n. Dealer in stationery.'),
(35041, 's', '\r\nStationery', ' n. Writing-materials, office supplies, etc.'),
(35042, 's', '\r\nStationery', 'office  n. The government''s publishing house.'),
(35043, 's', '\r\nStationmaster', ' n. Official in charge of a railway station.'),
(35044, 's', '\r\nStation', 'of the cross  n. Rc ch. Each of a series of images representing the events in christ''s passion before which prayers are said.'),
(35045, 's', '\r\nStation-wagon', ' n. Esp. Us estate car.'),
(35046, 's', '\r\nStatistic', ' n.  Statistical fact or item. [german: related to *state]'),
(35047, 's', '\r\nStatistical', ' adj. Of statistics.  statistically adv.'),
(35048, 's', '\r\nStatistics', ' n.pl. 1 (usu. Treated as sing.) Science of collecting and analysing significant numerical data. 2 such analysed data.  statistician n.'),
(35049, 's', '\r\nStatuary', ' â€”adj. Of or for statues (statuary art). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 statues collectively. 2 making statues. 3 sculptor. [latin: related to *statue]'),
(35050, 's', '\r\nStatue', ' n. Sculptured figure of a person or animal, esp. Life-size or larger. [latin statua]'),
(35051, 's', '\r\nStatuesque', ' adj. Like, or having the dignity or beauty of, a statue.'),
(35052, 's', '\r\nStatuette', ' n. Small statue.'),
(35053, 's', '\r\nStature', ' n. 1 height of a (esp. Human) body. 2 calibre, esp. Moral; eminence. [latin statura]'),
(35054, 's', '\r\nStatus', ' n. 1 rank, social position, relative importance. 2 superior social etc. Position. [latin: related to *stature]'),
(35055, 's', '\r\nStatus', 'quo  n. Existing state of affairs. [latin]'),
(35056, 's', '\r\nStatus', 'symbol  n. A possession etc. Intended to indicate the owner''s superiority.'),
(35057, 's', '\r\nStatute', ' n. 1 written law passed by a legislative body. 2 rule of a corporation, founder, etc., intended to be permanent. [latin statutum from statuo set up]'),
(35058, 's', '\r\nStatute-book', ' n. 1 book(s) containing the statute law. 2 body of a country''s statutes.'),
(35059, 's', '\r\nStatute', 'law  n. 1 (collect.) Body of principles and rules of law laid down in statutes. 2 a statute.'),
(35060, 's', '\r\nStatute', 'mile  see *mile 1.'),
(35061, 's', '\r\nStatutory', ' adj. Required or enacted by statute.  statutorily adv.'),
(35062, 's', '\r\nStaunch1', ' adj. 1 loyal. 2 (of a ship, joint, etc.) Strong, watertight, airtight, etc.  staunchly adv. [french estanche]'),
(35063, 's', '\r\nStaunch2', ' var. Of *stanch.'),
(35064, 's', '\r\nStave', ' â€”n. 1 each of the curved slats forming the sides of a cask, pail, etc. 2 = *staff n. 3. 3 stanza or verse. â€”v. (-ving; past and past part. Stove or staved) (usu. Foll. By in) break a hole in, damage, crush by forcing inwards.  stave off avert or defer (danger etc.). [from *staff]'),
(35065, 's', '\r\nStay1', ' â€”v. 1 continue in the same place or condition; not depart or change. 2 (often foll. By at, in, with) reside temporarily. 3 archaic or literary a stop or check. B (esp. In imper.) Pause. 4 postpone (judgement etc.). 5 assuage (hunger etc.), esp. Temporarily. â€”n. 1 act or period of staying. 2 suspension or postponement of a sentence, judgement, etc. 3 prop, support. 4 (in pl.) Hist. (esp. Boned) corset.  stay the course endure to the end. Stay in remain indoors. Stay the night remain overnight. Stay put colloq. Remain where it is placed or where one is. Stay up not go to bed (until late). [anglo-french from latin sto stand: sense 3 of n. From french, formed as *stay2]'),
(35066, 's', '\r\nStay2', ' n. 1 naut. Rope or guy supporting a mast, flagstaff, etc. 2 supporting cable on an aircraft etc. [old english from germanic]'),
(35067, 's', '\r\nStay-at-home', ' â€”attrib. Adj. Rarely going out. â€”n. Such a person.'),
(35068, 's', '\r\nStayer', ' n.  Person or animal with great endurance.'),
(35069, 's', '\r\nStaying', 'power  n. Endurance.'),
(35070, 's', '\r\nStaysail', ' n. Sail extended on a stay.'),
(35071, 's', '\r\nStd', ' abbr. Subscriber trunk dialling.'),
(35072, 's', '\r\nStead', ' n.  in a person''s (or thing''s) stead as a substitute; in a person''s or thing''s place. Stand a person in good stead be advantageous or useful to him or her. [old english, = place]'),
(35073, 's', '\r\nSteadfast', ' adj. Constant, firm, unwavering.  steadfastly adv. Steadfastness n. [old english: related to *stead]'),
(35074, 's', '\r\nSteady', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 firmly fixed or supported; unwavering. 2 uniform and regular (steady pace; steady increase). 3 a constant. B persistent. 4 (of a person) serious and dependable. 5 regular, established (steady girlfriend). â€”v. (-ies, -ied) make or become steady. â€”adv. Steadily. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Regular boyfriend or girlfriend.  go steady (often foll. By with) colloq. Have as a regular boyfriend or girlfriend. Steady on! Be careful!  steadily adv. Steadiness n. [from *stead]'),
(35075, 's', '\r\nSteady', 'state  n. Unvarying condition, esp. In a physical process.'),
(35076, 's', '\r\nSteak', ' n. 1 thick slice of meat (esp. Beef) or fish, usu. Grilled or fried. 2 beef cut for stewing or braising. [old norse]'),
(35077, 's', '\r\nSteak-house', ' n. Restaurant specializing in beefsteaks.'),
(35078, 's', '\r\nSteal', ' â€”v. (past stole; past part. Stolen) 1 (also absol.) Take (another''s property) illegally or without right or permission, esp. In secret. 2 obtain surreptitiously, insidiously, or artfully (stole a kiss). 3 (foll. By in, out, away, up, etc.) Move, esp. Silently or stealthily. â€”n. 1 us colloq. Act of stealing or theft. 2 colloq. Easy task or good bargain.  steal a march on get an advantage over by surreptitious means. Steal the show outshine other performers, esp. Unexpectedly. Steal a person''s thunder take away the attention due to someone else by using his or her words, ideas, etc. [old english]'),
(35079, 's', '\r\nStealth', ' n. Secrecy, secret behaviour. [old english: related to *steal]'),
(35080, 's', '\r\nStealthy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) done or moving with stealth; furtive.  stealthily adv. Stealthiness n.'),
(35081, 's', '\r\nSteam', ' â€”n. 1 a gas into which water is changed by boiling. B condensed vapour formed from this. 2 a power obtained from steam. B colloq. Power or energy. â€”v. 1 a cook (food) in steam. B treat with steam. 2 give off steam. 3 a move under steam power. B (foll. By ahead, away, etc.) Colloq. Proceed or travel fast or with vigour. 4 (usu. Foll. By up) a cover or become covered with condensed steam. B (as steamed up adj.) Colloq. Angry or excited. [old english]'),
(35082, 's', '\r\nSteamboat', ' n. Steam-driven boat.'),
(35083, 's', '\r\nSteam', 'engine  n. 1 engine which uses steam to generate power. 2 locomotive powered by this.'),
(35084, 's', '\r\nSteamer', ' n. 1 steamship. 2 vessel for steaming food in.'),
(35085, 's', '\r\nSteam', 'hammer  n. Forging-hammer powered by steam.'),
(35086, 's', '\r\nSteam', 'iron  n. Electric iron that emits steam.'),
(35087, 's', '\r\nSteamroller', ' â€”n. 1 heavy slow-moving vehicle with a roller, used to flatten new-made roads. 2 a crushing power or force. â€”v. Crush or move forcibly or indiscriminately; force.'),
(35088, 's', '\r\nSteamship', ' n. Steam-driven ship.'),
(35089, 's', '\r\nSteam', 'train  n. Train pulled by a steam engine.'),
(35090, 's', '\r\nSteamy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 like or full of steam. 2 colloq. Erotic.  steamily adv. Steaminess n.'),
(35091, 's', '\r\nSteatite', ' n. Impure form of talc, esp. Soapstone. [greek stear steat- tallow]'),
(35092, 's', '\r\nSteed', ' n. Archaic or poet. Horse. [old english]'),
(35093, 's', '\r\nSteel', ' â€”n. 1 strong malleable alloy of iron and carbon, used esp. For making tools, weapons, etc. 2 strength, firmness (nerves of steel). 3 steel rod for sharpening knives. â€”adj. Of or like steel. â€”v. (also refl.) Harden or make resolute. [old english]'),
(35094, 's', '\r\nSteel', 'band  n. Band playing chiefly calypso-style music on percussion instruments made from oil drums.'),
(35095, 's', '\r\nSteel', 'wool  n. Abrasive substance consisting of a mass of fine steel shavings.'),
(35096, 's', '\r\nSteelworks', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Factory producing steel.  steelworker n.'),
(35097, 's', '\r\nSteely', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like steel. 2 severe; resolute.  steeliness n.'),
(35098, 's', '\r\nSteelyard', ' n. Balance with a graduated arm along which a weight is moved.'),
(35099, 's', '\r\nSteep1', ' â€”adj. 1 sloping sharply. 2 (of a rise or fall) rapid. 3 (predic.) Colloq. A exorbitant; unreasonable. B exaggerated; incredible. â€”n. Steep slope; precipice.  steepen v. Steepish adj. Steeply adv. Steepness n. [old english]'),
(35100, 's', '\r\nSteep2', ' â€”v. Soak or bathe in liquid. â€”n. 1 act of steeping. 2 liquid for steeping.  steep in 1 pervade or imbue with. 2 make deeply acquainted with (a subject etc.). [old english]'),
(35101, 's', '\r\nSteeple', ' n. Tall tower, esp. With a spire, above the roof of a church. [old english: related to *steep1]'),
(35102, 's', '\r\nSteeplechase', ' n. 1 horse-race with ditches, hedges, etc., to jump. 2 cross-country foot-race.  steeplechasing n.'),
(35103, 's', '\r\nSteeplejack', ' n. Repairer of tall chimneys, steeples, etc.'),
(35104, 's', '\r\nSteer1', ' v. 1 (also absol.) Guide (a vehicle, ship, etc.) With a wheel or rudder etc. 2 direct or guide (one''s course, other people, a conversation, etc.) In a specified direction.  steer clear of avoid.  steering n. [old english]'),
(35105, 's', '\r\nSteer2', ' n. = *bullock. [old english]'),
(35106, 's', '\r\nSteerage', ' n. 1 act of steering. 2 archaic cheapest part of a ship''s accommodation.'),
(35107, 's', '\r\nSteering-column', ' n. Column on which a steering-wheel is mounted.'),
(35108, 's', '\r\nSteering', 'committee  n. Committee deciding the order of business, the course of operations, etc.'),
(35109, 's', '\r\nSteering-wheel', ' n. Wheel by which a vehicle etc. Is steered.'),
(35110, 's', '\r\nSteersman', ' n. Person who steers a ship.'),
(35111, 's', '\r\nStegosaurus', ' n. (pl. -ruses) plant-eating dinosaur with a double row of bony plates along the spine. [greek stege covering, sauros lizard]'),
(35112, 's', '\r\nStela', ' n. (pl. Stelae) (also stele) archaeol. Upright slab or pillar usu. Inscribed and sculpted, esp. As a gravestone. [latin and greek]'),
(35113, 's', '\r\nStellar', ' adj. Of a star or stars. [latin stella star]'),
(35114, 's', '\r\nStem1', ' â€”n. 1 main body or stalk of a plant. 2 stalk of a fruit, flower, or leaf. 3 stem-shaped part, as: a the slender part of a wineglass. B the tube of a tobacco-pipe. C a vertical stroke in a letter or musical note. 4 gram. Root or main part of a noun, verb, etc., to which inflections are added. 5 main upright timber at the bow of a ship (from stem to stern). â€”v. (-mm-) (foll. By from) spring or originate from. [old english]'),
(35115, 's', '\r\nStem2', ' v. (-mm-) check or stop. [old norse]'),
(35116, 's', '\r\nStench', ' n. Foul smell. [old english: related to *stink]'),
(35117, 's', '\r\nStencil', ' â€”n. 1 (in full stencil-plate) thin sheet in which a pattern is cut, placed on a surface and printed or inked over etc. To reproduce the pattern. 2 pattern so produced. 3 waxed sheet etc. From which a stencil is made by a typewriter. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 (often foll. By on) produce (a pattern) with a stencil. 2 mark (a surface) in this way. [french estanceler sparkle, from latin scintilla spark]'),
(35118, 's', '\r\nSten', 'gun  n. Lightweight sub-machine-gun. [s and t (initials of its inventors'' surnames) + -en after *bren]'),
(35119, 's', '\r\nStenographer', ' n. Esp. Us shorthand typist. [greek stenos narrow]'),
(35120, 's', '\r\nStentorian', ' adj. Loud and powerful. [stentor, name of a herald in homer''s iliad]'),
(35121, 's', '\r\nStep', ' â€”n. 1 a complete movement of one leg in walking or running. B distance so covered. 2 unit of movement in dancing. 3 measure taken, esp. One of several in a course of action. 4 surface of a stair, stepladder, etc.; tread. 5 short distance. 6 sound or mark made by a foot in walking etc. 7 manner of walking etc. 8 degree in the scale of promotion or precedence etc. 9 a stepping in unison or to music (esp. In or out of step). B state of conforming (refuses to keep step with the team). 10 (in pl.) (also pair of steps) = *stepladder. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 lift and set down one''s foot or alternate feet in walking. 2 come or go in a specified direction by stepping. 3 make progress in a specified way (stepped into a new job). 4 (foll. By off, out) measure (distance) by stepping. 5 perform (a dance).  mind (or watch) one''s step be careful. Step by step gradually; cautiously. Step down resign. Step in 1 enter. 2 intervene. Step on it colloq. Accelerate; hurry up. Step out 1 be active socially. 2 take large steps. Step out of line behave inappropriately or disobediently. Step up increase, intensify. [old english]'),
(35122, 's', '\r\nStep-', ' comb. Form denoting a relationship resulting from a parent''s later marriage. [old english, = orphaned]'),
(35123, 's', '\r\nStepbrother', ' n. Son of one''s step-parent by a previous partner.'),
(35124, 's', '\r\nStepchild', ' n. One''s husband''s or wife''s child by a previous partner.'),
(35125, 's', '\r\nStepdaughter', ' n. Female stepchild.'),
(35126, 's', '\r\nStepfather', ' n. Male step-parent.'),
(35127, 's', '\r\nStephanotis', ' n. Fragrant tropical climbing plant. [greek]'),
(35128, 's', '\r\nStepladder', ' n. Short folding ladder with flat steps.'),
(35129, 's', '\r\nStepmother', ' n. Female step-parent.'),
(35130, 's', '\r\nStep-parent', ' n. Mother''s or father''s spouse who is not one''s own parent.'),
(35131, 's', '\r\nSteppe', ' n. Level grassy unforested plain. [russian]'),
(35132, 's', '\r\nStepping-stone', ' n. 1 large stone in a stream etc. Helping one to cross. 2 means of progress.'),
(35133, 's', '\r\nStepsister', ' n. Daughter of one''s step-parent by a previous partner.'),
(35134, 's', '\r\nStepson', ' n. Male stepchild.'),
(35135, 's', '\r\n-ster', ' suffix denoting a person engaged in or associated with a particular activity or quality (brewster; gangster; youngster). [old english]'),
(35136, 's', '\r\nStereo', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 a stereophonic record-player etc. B stereophonic sound reproduction (see *stereophonic). 2 = *stereoscope. â€”adj. 1 = *stereophonic. 2 = stereoscopic (see *stereoscope). [abbreviation]'),
(35137, 's', '\r\nStereo-', ' comb. Form solid; having three dimensions. [greek stereos solid]'),
(35138, 's', '\r\nStereophonic', ' adj. Using two or more channels, giving the effect of naturally distributed sound.'),
(35139, 's', '\r\nStereoscope', ' n. Device for producing a three-dimensional effect by viewing two slightly different photographs together.  stereoscopic adj.'),
(35140, 's', '\r\nStereotype', ' â€”n. 1 a person or thing seeming to conform to a widely accepted type. B such a type, idea, or attitude. 2 printing-plate cast from a mould of composed type. â€”v. (-ping) 1 (esp. As stereotyped adj.) Cause to conform to a type; standardize. 2 a print from a stereotype. B make a stereotype of. [french: related to *stereo-]'),
(35141, 's', '\r\nSterile', ' adj. 1 unable to produce a crop, fruit, or young; barren. 2 unproductive (sterile discussion). 3 free from living micro-organisms etc.  sterility n. [latin]'),
(35142, 's', '\r\nSterilize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 make sterile. 2 deprive of reproductive powers.  sterilization n.'),
(35143, 's', '\r\nSterling', ' â€”adj. 1 of or in british money (pound sterling). 2 (of a coin or precious metal) genuine; of standard value or purity. 3 (of a person etc.) Genuine, reliable. â€”n. British money. [old english, = penny]'),
(35144, 's', '\r\nSterling', 'silver  n. Silver of 921/2% purity.'),
(35145, 's', '\r\nStern1', ' adj. Severe, grim; authoritarian.  sternly adv. Sternness n. [old english]'),
(35146, 's', '\r\nStern2', ' n. Rear part, esp. Of a ship or boat. [old norse: related to *steer1]'),
(35147, 's', '\r\nSternum', ' n. (pl. -na or -nums) breastbone. [greek sternon chest]'),
(35148, 's', '\r\nSteroid', ' n. Any of a group of organic compounds including many hormones, alkaloids, and vitamins. [from *sterol]'),
(35149, 's', '\r\nSterol', ' n. Naturally occurring steroid alcohol. [from *cholesterol, etc.]'),
(35150, 's', '\r\nStertorous', ' adj. (of breathing etc.) Laboured and noisy. [latin sterto snore]'),
(35151, 's', '\r\nStet', ' v. (-tt-) (usu. Written on a proof-sheet etc.) Ignore or cancel (the alteration); let the original stand. [latin, = let it stand]'),
(35152, 's', '\r\nStethoscope', ' n. Instrument used in listening to the heart, lungs, etc. [greek stethos breast]'),
(35153, 's', '\r\nStetson', ' n. Slouch hat with a very wide brim and high crown. [stetson, name of a hat-maker]'),
(35154, 's', '\r\nStevedore', ' n. Person employed in loading and unloading ships. [spanish estivador]'),
(35155, 's', '\r\nStew', ' â€”v. 1  cook by long simmering in a closed vessel. 2 fret, be anxious. 3 colloq. Swelter. 4 (of tea etc.) Become bitter or strong from infusing too long. 5 (as stewed adj.) Colloq. Drunk. â€”n. 1 dish of stewed meat etc. 2 colloq. Agitated or angry state.  stew in one''s own juice suffer the consequences of one''s actions. [french estuver]'),
(35156, 's', '\r\nSteward', ' â€”n. 1 passengers'' attendant on a ship, aircraft, or train. 2 official supervising a meeting, show, etc. 3 person responsible for supplies of food etc. For a college or club etc. 4 property manager. â€”v. Act as a steward (of).  stewardship n. [old english, = house-warden]'),
(35157, 's', '\r\nStewardess', ' n. Female steward, esp. On a ship or aircraft.'),
(35158, 's', '\r\nStick1', ' n. 1 a short slender length of wood. B this as a support or weapon. 2 thin rod of wood etc. For a particular purpose (cocktail stick). 3 implement used to propel the ball in hockey or polo etc. 4 gear lever. 5 conductor''s baton. 6 sticklike piece of celery, dynamite, etc. 7 (often prec. By the) punishment, esp. By beating. 8 colloq. Adverse criticism. 9 colloq. Piece of wood as part of a house or furniture. 10 colloq. Person, esp. When dull or unsociable. [old english]'),
(35159, 's', '\r\nStick2', ' v. (past and past part. Stuck) 1 (foll. By in, into, through) insert or thrust (a thing or its point). 2 stab. 3 (foll. By in, into, on, etc.) A fix or be fixed on a pointed thing. B fix or be fixed (as) by a pointed end. 4 fix or be fixed (as) by adhesive etc. 5 remain (in the mind).'),
(35160, 's', '\r\nSticker', ' n. 1 adhesive label. 2 persistent person.'),
(35161, 's', '\r\nSticking-plaster', ' n. Adhesive plaster for wounds etc.'),
(35162, 's', '\r\nStick', 'insect  n. Insect with a twiglike body.'),
(35163, 's', '\r\nStick-in-the-mud', ' n. Colloq. Unprogressive or old-fashioned person.'),
(35164, 's', '\r\nStickleback', ' n. Small spiny-backed fish. [old english, = thorn-back]'),
(35165, 's', '\r\nStickler', ' n. (foll. By for) person who insists on something (stickler for accuracy). [obsolete stickle be umpire]'),
(35166, 's', '\r\nStick-up', ' n. Colloq. Robbery using a gun.'),
(35167, 's', '\r\nSticky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 tending or intended to stick or adhere. 2 glutinous, viscous. 3 humid. 4 colloq. Difficult, awkward; unpleasant, painful (sticky problem).  stickily adv. Stickiness n.'),
(35168, 's', '\r\nSticky', 'wicket  n. Colloq. Difficult circumstances.'),
(35169, 's', '\r\nStiff', ' â€”adj. 1 rigid; inflexible. 2 hard to bend, move, or turn etc. 3 hard to cope with; needing strength or effort (stiff climb). 4 severe or strong (stiff penalty). 5 formal, constrained. 6 (of a muscle, person, etc.) Aching owing to exertion, injury, etc. 7 (of esp. An alcoholic drink) strong. 8 (foll. By with) colloq. Abounding in. â€”adv. Colloq. Utterly, extremely (bored stiff). â€”n. Slang 1 corpse. 2 foolish or useless person.  stiffish adj. Stiffly adv. Stiffness n. [old english]'),
(35170, 's', '\r\nStiffen', ' v. Make or become stiff.  stiffening n.'),
(35171, 's', '\r\nStiff-necked', ' adj. Obstinate; haughty.'),
(35172, 's', '\r\nStiff', 'upper lip  n. Appearance of being calm in adversity.'),
(35173, 's', '\r\nStifle', ' v. (-ling) 1 suppress. 2 feel or make unable to breathe easily; suffocate. 3 kill by suffocating.  stifling adj. & adv. [origin uncertain]'),
(35174, 's', '\r\nStigma', ' n. (pl. -s or, esp. In sense 3, stigmata) 1 shame, disgrace. 2 part of the pistil that receives the pollen in pollination. 3 (in pl.) (in christian belief) marks like those on christ''s body after the crucifixion, appearing on the bodies of certain saints etc. [greek stigma -mat- brand, dot]'),
(35175, 's', '\r\nStigmatize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) (often foll. By as) brand as unworthy or disgraceful. [greek stigmatizo: related to *stigma]'),
(35176, 's', '\r\nStile', ' n. Steps allowing people but not animals to climb over a fence or wall. [old english]'),
(35177, 's', '\r\nStiletto', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 short dagger. 2 (in full stiletto heel) a long tapering heel of a shoe. B shoe with such a heel. 3 pointed instrument for making eyelets etc. [italian diminutive: related to *style]'),
(35178, 's', '\r\nStill1', ' â€”adj. 1 not or hardly moving. 2 with little or no sound; calm and tranquil. 3 (of a drink) not effervescing. â€”n. 1 deep silence (still of the night). 2 static photograph (as opposed to a motion picture), esp. A single shot from a cinema film. â€”adv. 1 without moving (sit still). 2 even now or at a particular time (is he still here?). 3 nevertheless. 4 (with compar.) Even, yet, increasingly (still greater efforts). â€”v. Make or become still; quieten.  stillness n. [old english]'),
(35179, 's', '\r\nStill2', ' n. Apparatus for distilling spirits etc. [obsolete still (v.) = *distil]'),
(35180, 's', '\r\nStillbirth', ' n. Birth of a dead child.'),
(35181, 's', '\r\nStillborn', ' adj. 1 born dead. 2 abortive.'),
(35182, 's', '\r\nStill', 'life  n. (pl. Lifes) painting or drawing of inanimate objects, e.g. Fruit or flowers.'),
(35183, 's', '\r\nStill-room', ' n. 1 room for distilling. 2 housekeeper''s storeroom or pantry.'),
(35184, 's', '\r\nStilt', ' n. 1 either of a pair of poles with foot supports for walking at a distance above the ground. 2 each of a set of piles or posts supporting a building etc. [low german or dutch]'),
(35185, 's', '\r\nStilted', ' adj. 1 (of literary style etc.) Stiff and unnatural; bombastic. 2 standing on stilts.'),
(35186, 's', '\r\nStilton', ' n. Propr. Strong rich esp. Blue-veined cheese. [stilton in england]'),
(35187, 's', '\r\nStimulant', ' â€”adj. Stimulating, esp. Bodily or mental activity. â€”n. Stimulant substance or influence. [latin: related to *stimulate]'),
(35188, 's', '\r\nStimulate', ' v. (-ting) 1 act as a stimulus to. 2 animate, excite, arouse.  stimulation n. Stimulative adj. Stimulator n. [latin: related to *stimulus]'),
(35189, 's', '\r\nStimulus', ' n. (pl. -li) thing that rouses to activity. [latin, = goad]'),
(35190, 's', '\r\nSting', ' â€”n. 1 sharp wounding organ of an insect, snake, nettle, etc. 2 a act of inflicting a wound with this. B the wound itself or the pain caused by it. 3 painful quality or effect. 4 pungency, vigour. 5 slang swindle. â€”v. (past and past part. Stung) 1 a wound or pierce with a sting. B be able to sting. 2 feel or give a tingling physical or sharp mental pain. 3 (foll. By into) incite, esp. Painfully (stung into replying). 4 slang swindle, charge exorbitantly.  sting in the tail unexpected final pain or difficulty. [old english]'),
(35191, 's', '\r\nStinger', ' n. Stinging animal or thing, esp. A sharp blow.'),
(35192, 's', '\r\nStinging-nettle', ' n. Nettle with stinging hairs.'),
(35193, 's', '\r\nStingray', ' n. Broad flat-fish with a poisonous spine at the base of its tail.'),
(35194, 's', '\r\nStingy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Niggardly, mean.  stingily adv. Stinginess n. [perhaps from *sting]'),
(35195, 's', '\r\nStink', ' â€”v. (past stank or stunk; past part. Stunk) 1 emit a strong offensive smell. 2 (often foll. By out) fill (a place) with a stink. 3 (foll. By out etc.) Drive (a person) out etc. By a stink. 4 colloq. Be or seem very unpleasant. â€”n. 1 strong or offensive smell. 2 colloq. Row or fuss. [old english]'),
(35196, 's', '\r\nStink', 'bomb  n. Device emitting a stink when opened.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(35197, 's', '\r\nStinker', ' n. Slang objectionable or difficult person or thing.'),
(35198, 's', '\r\nStinking', ' â€”adj. 1 that stinks. 2 slang very objectionable. â€”adv. Slang extremely and usu. Objectionably (stinking rich).'),
(35199, 's', '\r\nStint', ' â€”v. 1 supply (food or aid etc.) Meanly or grudgingly. 2 (often refl.) Supply (a person etc.) In this way. â€”n. 1 limitation of supply or effort (without stint). 2 allotted amount of work (do one''s stint). 3 small sandpiper. [old english]'),
(35200, 's', '\r\nStipend', ' n. Salary, esp. Of a clergyman. [latin stipendium]'),
(35201, 's', '\r\nStipendiary', ' â€”adj. Receiving a stipend. â€”n. (pl. -ies) person receiving a stipend. [latin: related to *stipend]'),
(35202, 's', '\r\nStipendiary', 'magistrate  n. Paid professional magistrate.'),
(35203, 's', '\r\nStipple', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 draw or paint or engrave etc. With dots instead of lines. 2 roughen the surface of (paint, cement, etc.). â€”n. 1 stippling. 2 effect of stippling. [dutch]'),
(35204, 's', '\r\nStipulate', ' v. (-ting) demand or specify as part of a bargain etc.  stipulation n. [latin stipulari]'),
(35205, 's', '\r\nStir1', ' â€”v. (-rr-) 1  move a spoon etc. Round and round in (a liquid etc.), esp. To mix ingredients. 2 a cause to move, esp. Slightly. B be or begin to be in motion. 3 rise from sleep. 4 arouse, inspire, or excite (the emotions, a person, etc.). 5 colloq. Cause trouble between people by gossiping etc. â€”n. 1 act of stirring. 2 commotion, excitement.  stir in add (an ingredient) by stirring. Stir up 1 mix thoroughly by stirring. 2 stimulate, excite.  stirrer n. [old english]'),
(35206, 's', '\r\nStir2', ' n. Slang prison. [origin unknown]'),
(35207, 's', '\r\nStir-fry', ' â€”v. Fry rapidly while stirring. â€”n. Stir-fried dish.'),
(35208, 's', '\r\nStirrup', ' n. Metal loop supporting a horse-rider''s foot. [old english, = climbing-rope]'),
(35209, 's', '\r\nStirrup-cup', ' n. Cup of wine etc. Offered to a departing traveller, orig. A rider.'),
(35210, 's', '\r\nStirrup-leather', ' n. (also stirrup-strap) strap attaching a stirrup to a saddle.'),
(35211, 's', '\r\nStirrup-pump', ' n. Hand-operated water-pump with a foot-rest, used to extinguish small fires.'),
(35212, 's', '\r\nStitch', ' â€”n. 1 a (in sewing, knitting, or crocheting) single pass of a needle, or the resulting thread or loop etc. B particular method of sewing etc. 2 least bit of clothing (hadn''t a stitch on). 3 sharp pain in the side induced by running etc. â€”v. Sew; make stitches (in).  in stitches colloq. Laughing uncontrollably. Stitch up 1 join or mend by sewing. 2 slang trick, cheat, betray. [old english: related to *stick2]'),
(35213, 's', '\r\nStitch', 'in time  n. Timely remedy.'),
(35214, 's', '\r\nStoat', ' n. Mammal of the weasel family with brown fur turning mainly white in the winter. [origin unknown]'),
(35215, 's', '\r\nStock', ' â€”n. 1 store of goods etc. Ready for sale or distribution etc. 2 supply or quantity of anything for use. 3 equipment or raw material for manufacture or trade etc. (rolling-stock). 4 farm animals or equipment. 5 a capital of a business. B shares in this. 6 reputation or popularity (his stock is rising). 7 a money lent to a government at fixed interest. B right to receive such interest. 8 line of ancestry (comes of cornish stock). 9 liquid basis for soup etc. Made by stewing bones, vegetables, etc. 10 fragrant-flowered cruciferous cultivated plant. 11 plant into which a graft is inserted. 12 main trunk of a tree etc. 13 (in pl.) Hist. Timber frame with holes for the feet in which offenders were locked as a public punishment. 14 base, support, or handle for an implement or machine. 15 butt of a rifle etc. 16 (in pl.) Supports for a ship during building or repair. 17 band of cloth worn round the neck. â€”attrib. Adj. 1 kept in stock and so regularly available. 2 hackneyed, conventional. â€”v. 1 have (goods) in stock. 2 provide (a shop or a farm etc.) With goods, livestock, etc. 3 fit (a gun etc.) With a stock.  in (or out of) stock available (or not available) immediately for sale etc. Stock up (often foll. By with) provide with or get stocks or supplies (of). Take stock 1 make an inventory of one''s stock. 2 (often foll. By of) review (a situation etc.). [old english]'),
(35216, 's', '\r\nStockade', ' â€”n. Line or enclosure of upright stakes. â€”v. (-ding) fortify with this. [spanish estacada]'),
(35217, 's', '\r\nStockbreeder', ' n. Livestock farmer.'),
(35218, 's', '\r\nStockbroker', ' n. = *broker 2.  stockbroking n.'),
(35219, 's', '\r\nStock-car', ' n. Specially strengthened car for use in racing with deliberate bumping.'),
(35220, 's', '\r\nStock', 'exchange  n. 1 place for dealing in stocks and shares. 2 dealers working there.'),
(35221, 's', '\r\nStockholder', ' n. Owner of stocks or shares.'),
(35222, 's', '\r\nStockinet', ' n. (also stockinette) elastic knitted fabric. [probably from stocking-net]'),
(35223, 's', '\r\nStocking', ' n. 1 long knitted covering for the leg and foot, of nylon, wool, silk, etc. 2 differently-coloured lower leg of a horse etc.  in one''s stocking (or stockinged) feet without shoes. [from *stock]'),
(35224, 's', '\r\nStocking-stitch', ' n. Alternate rows of plain and purl.'),
(35225, 's', '\r\nStock-in-trade', ' n. 1 requisite(s) of a trade or profession. 2 characteristic or essential product; characteristic behaviour, actions, etc.'),
(35226, 's', '\r\nStockist', ' n. Dealer in specified types of goods.'),
(35227, 's', '\r\nStockjobber', ' n.  = *jobber 2.'),
(35228, 's', '\r\nStock', 'market  n. 1 = *stock exchange. 2 transactions on this.'),
(35229, 's', '\r\nStockpile', ' â€”n. Accumulated stock of goods etc. Held in reserve. â€”v. (-ling) accumulate a stockpile of.'),
(35230, 's', '\r\nStockpot', ' n. Pot for making soup stock.'),
(35231, 's', '\r\nStockroom', ' n. Room for storing goods.'),
(35232, 's', '\r\nStock-still', ' adj. Motionless.'),
(35233, 's', '\r\nStocktaking', ' n. 1 making an inventory of stock. 2 review of one''s position etc.'),
(35234, 's', '\r\nStocky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) short and sturdy.  stockily adv. Stockiness n.'),
(35235, 's', '\r\nStockyard', ' n. Enclosure for the sorting or temporary keeping of cattle.'),
(35236, 's', '\r\nStodge', ' n. Colloq. Heavy fattening food. [imitative, after stuff and podge]'),
(35237, 's', '\r\nStodgy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of food) heavy and glutinous. 2 dull and uninteresting.  stodgily adv. Stodginess n.'),
(35238, 's', '\r\nStoic', ' â€”n. 1 member of the ancient greek school of philosophy which sought virtue as the greatest good and taught control of one''s feelings and passions. 2 (stoic) stoical person. â€”adj. 1 of or like the stoics. 2 (stoic) = *stoical. [greek stoa portico]'),
(35239, 's', '\r\nStoical', ' adj. Having or showing great self-control in adversity.  stoically adv.'),
(35240, 's', '\r\nStoicism', ' n. 1 philosophy of the stoics. 2 (stoicism) stoical attitude.'),
(35241, 's', '\r\nStoke', ' v. (-king) (often foll. By up) 1 feed and tend (a fire or furnace etc.). 2 colloq. Fill oneself with food. [back-formation from *stoker]'),
(35242, 's', '\r\nStokehold', ' n. Compartment in a steamship containing its boilers and furnace.'),
(35243, 's', '\r\nStokehole', ' n. Space for stokers in front of a furnace.'),
(35244, 's', '\r\nStoker', ' n. Person who tends a furnace, esp. On a steamship. [dutch]'),
(35245, 's', '\r\nStol', ' abbr. Short take-off and landing.'),
(35246, 's', '\r\nStole1', ' n. 1 woman''s garment like a long wide scarf, worn over the shoulders. 2 strip of silk etc. Worn similarly by a priest. [greek stole equipment, clothing]'),
(35247, 's', '\r\nStole2', ' past of *steal.'),
(35248, 's', '\r\nStolen', ' past part. Of *steal.'),
(35249, 's', '\r\nStolid', ' adj. Not easily excited or moved; impassive, unemotional.  stolidity n. Stolidly adv. [latin]'),
(35250, 's', '\r\nStoma', ' n. (pl. -s or stomata) 1 minute pore in the epidermis of a leaf. 2 small mouthlike artificial orifice made in the stomach. [greek stoma mouth]'),
(35251, 's', '\r\nStomach', ' â€”n. 1 a internal organ in which digestion occurs. B any of several such organs in animals. 2 lower front of the body. 3 (usu. Foll. By for) a appetite. B inclination. â€”v. 1 find palatable. 2 endure (usu. With neg.: cannot stomach it). [greek stoma mouth]'),
(35252, 's', '\r\nStomach-ache', ' n. Pain in the belly or bowels.'),
(35253, 's', '\r\nStomacher', ' n. Hist. Pointed bodice of a dress, often jewelled or embroidered. [probably french: related to *stomach]'),
(35254, 's', '\r\nStomach-pump', ' n. Syringe for forcing liquid etc. Into or out of the stomach.'),
(35255, 's', '\r\nStomach', 'upset  n. Temporary digestive disorder.'),
(35256, 's', '\r\nStomp', ' â€”v. Tread or stamp heavily. â€”n. Lively jazz dance with heavy stamping. [var. Of *stamp]'),
(35257, 's', '\r\nStone', ' â€”n. 1 a solid non-metallic mineral matter; rock. B small piece of this. 2 (often in comb.) Piece of stone of a definite shape or for a particular purpose. 3 a thing resembling stone, e.g. The hard case of the kernel in some fruits. B (often in pl.) Hard morbid concretion in the body. 4 (pl. Same) unit of weight equal to 14 lb. 5 = *precious stone. 6 (attrib.) Made of stone. â€”v. (-ning) 1 pelt with stones. 2 remove the stones from (fruit).  cast (or throw) stones speak ill of a person. Leave no stone unturned try all possible means. A stone''s throw a short distance. [old english]'),
(35258, 's', '\r\nStone', 'age  n. Prehistoric period when weapons and tools were made of stone.'),
(35259, 's', '\r\nStonechat', ' n. Small brown bird with black and white markings.'),
(35260, 's', '\r\nStone-cold', ' adj. Completely cold.'),
(35261, 's', '\r\nStone-cold', 'sober  predic. Adj. Completely sober.'),
(35262, 's', '\r\nStonecrop', ' n. Succulent rock-plant.'),
(35263, 's', '\r\nStoned', ' adj. Slang drunk or drugged.'),
(35264, 's', '\r\nStone-dead', ' adj. Completely dead.'),
(35265, 's', '\r\nStone-deaf', ' adj. Completely deaf.'),
(35266, 's', '\r\nStone-fruit', ' n. Fruit with flesh enclosing a stone.'),
(35267, 's', '\r\nStoneground', ' adj. (of flour) ground with millstones.'),
(35268, 's', '\r\nStonemason', ' n. Person who cuts, prepares, and builds with stone.'),
(35269, 's', '\r\nStonewall', ' v. 1 obstruct (a discussion or investigation) with evasive answers etc. 2 cricket bat with excessive caution.'),
(35270, 's', '\r\nStoneware', ' n. Ceramic ware which is impermeable and partly vitrified but opaque.'),
(35271, 's', '\r\nStonewashed', ' adj. (esp. Of denim) washed with abrasives to give a worn or faded look.'),
(35272, 's', '\r\nStonework', ' n. Masonry.'),
(35273, 's', '\r\nStonker', ' n. Slang excellent person or thing.  stonking adj. [20th c.: origin unknown]'),
(35274, 's', '\r\nStony', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of stones. 2 a hard, rigid. B unfeeling, uncompromising.  stonily adv. Stoniness n.'),
(35275, 's', '\r\nStony-broke', ' adj. Slang entirely without money.'),
(35276, 's', '\r\nStood', ' past and past part. Of *stand.'),
(35277, 's', '\r\nStooge', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 butt or foil, esp. For a comedian. 2 assistant or subordinate, esp. For routine or unpleasant work. â€”v. (-ging) 1 (foll. By for) act as a stooge for. 2 (foll. By about, around, etc.) Move about aimlessly. [origin unknown]'),
(35278, 's', '\r\nStook', ' â€”n. Group of sheaves of grain stood on end in a field. â€”v. Arrange in stooks. [related to low german stuke]'),
(35279, 's', '\r\nStool', ' n. 1 single seat without a back or arms. 2 = *footstool. 3 (usu. In pl.) = *faeces. [old english]'),
(35280, 's', '\r\nStoolball', ' n. Team game with pairs of batters scoring runs between two bases.'),
(35281, 's', '\r\nStool-pigeon', ' n. 1 person acting as a decoy. 2 police informer.'),
(35282, 's', '\r\nStoop1', ' â€”v. 1 lower the body, sometimes bending the knee; bend down. 2 stand or walk with the shoulders habitually bent forward. 3 (foll. By to + infin.) Condescend. 4 (foll. By to) descend to (some conduct). â€”n. Stooping posture. [old english]'),
(35283, 's', '\r\nStoop2', ' n. Us porch, small veranda, or steps in front of a house. [dutch stoep]'),
(35284, 's', '\r\nStop', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 a put an end to the progress, motion, or operation of. B effectively hinder or prevent. C discontinue (stop playing). 2 come to an end (supplies suddenly stopped). 3 cease from motion, speaking, or action. 4 defeat. 5 slang receive (a blow etc.). 6 remain; stay for a short time. 7 (often foll. By up) block or close up (a hole, leak, etc.). 8 not permit or supply as usual (stop their wages). 9 (in full stop payment of or on) instruct a bank to withhold payment on (a cheque). 10 fill (a tooth). 11 press (a violin etc. String) to obtain the required pitch. â€”n. 1 stopping or being stopped. 2 designated stopping-place for a bus or train etc. 3 = *full stop. 4 device for stopping motion at a particular point. 5 change of pitch effected by stopping a string. 6 a (in an organ) row of pipes of one character. B knob etc. Operating these. 7 optics & photog. = *diaphragm 3a. 8 a effective diameter of a lens. B device for reducing this. 9 (of sound) = *plosive.  pull out all the stops make extreme effort. Put a stop to cause to end. Stop at nothing be ruthless. Stop off (or over) break one''s journey. [old english]'),
(35285, 's', '\r\nStopcock', ' n. Externally operated valve regulating the flow through a pipe etc.'),
(35286, 's', '\r\nStopgap', ' n. Temporary substitute.'),
(35287, 's', '\r\nStop-go', ' n. Alternate stopping and restarting, esp. Of the economy.'),
(35288, 's', '\r\nStopoff', ' n. Break in a journey.'),
(35289, 's', '\r\nStopover', ' n. Break in a journey, esp. Overnight.'),
(35290, 's', '\r\nStoppage', ' n. 1 interruption of work owing to a strike etc. 2 (in pl.) Sum deducted from pay, for tax, national insurance, etc. 3 condition of being blocked or stopped.'),
(35291, 's', '\r\nStopper', ' â€”n. Plug for closing a bottle etc. â€”v. Close with this.'),
(35292, 's', '\r\nStop', 'press  n. (often attrib.) Late news inserted in a newspaper after printing has begun.'),
(35293, 's', '\r\nStopwatch', ' n. Watch that can be stopped and started, used to time races etc.'),
(35294, 's', '\r\nStorage', ' n. 1 a storing of goods etc. B method of or space for storing. 2 cost of storing. 3 storing of data in a computer etc.'),
(35295, 's', '\r\nStorage', 'battery  n. (also storage cell) battery (or cell) for storing electricity.'),
(35296, 's', '\r\nStorage', 'heater  n. Electric heater releasing heat stored outside peak hours.'),
(35297, 's', '\r\nStore', ' â€”n. 1 quantity of something kept available for use. 2 (in pl.) A articles gathered for a particular purpose. B supply of, or place for keeping, these. 3 a = *department store. B esp. Us shop. C (often in pl.) Shop selling basic necessities. 4 warehouse for keeping furniture etc. Temporarily. 5 device in a computer for keeping retrievable data. â€”v. (-ring) 1 (often foll. By up, away) accumulate for future use. 2 put (furniture etc.) In a store. 3 stock or provide with something useful. 4 keep (data) for retrieval.  in store 1 kept in readiness. 2 coming in the future. 3 (foll. By for) awaiting. Set store by consider important. [french estore(r) from latin instauro renew]'),
(35298, 's', '\r\nStorehouse', ' n. Storage place.'),
(35299, 's', '\r\nStorekeeper', ' n. 1 storeman. 2 us shopkeeper.'),
(35300, 's', '\r\nStoreman', ' n. Person in charge of a store of goods.'),
(35301, 's', '\r\nStoreroom', ' n. Storage room.'),
(35302, 's', '\r\nStorey', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 = *floor n. 3. 2 thing forming a horizontal division.  -storeyed adj. (in comb.). [anglo-latin: related to *history, perhaps originally meaning a tier of painted windows]'),
(35303, 's', '\r\nStoried', ' adj. Literary celebrated in or associated with stories or legends.'),
(35304, 's', '\r\nStork', ' n. Long-legged usu. White wading bird. [old english]'),
(35305, 's', '\r\nStorm', ' â€”n. 1 violent atmospheric disturbance with strong winds and usu. Thunder, rain, or snow. 2 violent political etc. Disturbance. 3 (foll. By of) a violent shower of missiles or blows. B outbreak of applause, hisses, etc. 4 a direct assault by troops on a fortified place. B capture by such an assault. â€”v. 1 attack or capture by storm. 2 (usu. Foll. By in, out of, etc.) Move violently or angrily (stormed out). 3 (often foll. By at, away) talk violently, rage, bluster.  take by storm 1 capture by direct assault. 2 rapidly captivate. [old english]'),
(35306, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'centre  n. 1 point to which the wind spirals inward in a cyclonic storm. 2 centre of controversy etc.'),
(35307, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'cloud  n. 1 heavy rain-cloud. 2 threatening situation.'),
(35308, 's', '\r\nStorm-door', ' n. Additional outer door.'),
(35309, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'in a teacup  n. Great excitement over a trivial matter.'),
(35310, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'petrel  n. (also stormy petrel) 1 small black and white n. Atlantic petrel. 2 person causing unrest.'),
(35311, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'trooper  n. Member of the storm troops.'),
(35312, 's', '\r\nStorm', 'troops  n.pl. 1 = *shock troops. 2 hist. Nazi political militia.'),
(35313, 's', '\r\nStormy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or affected by storms. 2 (of a wind etc.) Violent. 3 full of angry feeling or outbursts (stormy meeting).  stormily adv. Storminess n.'),
(35314, 's', '\r\nStormy', 'petrel  var. Of *storm petrel.'),
(35315, 's', '\r\nStory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 account of imaginary or past events; tale, anecdote. 2 history of a person or institution etc. 3 (in full story-line) narrative or plot of a novel, play, etc. 4 facts or experiences worthy of narration. 5 colloq. Fib. [anglo-french estorie from latin: related to *history]'),
(35316, 's', '\r\nStoryteller', ' n. 1 person who tells stories. 2 colloq. Liar.  storytelling n. & adj.'),
(35317, 's', '\r\nStoup', ' n. 1 basin for holy-water. 2 archaic flagon, beaker. [old norse]'),
(35318, 's', '\r\nStout', ' â€”adj. 1 rather fat, corpulent, bulky. 2 thick or strong. 3 brave, resolute. â€”n. Strong dark beer.  stoutly adv. Stoutness n. [anglo-french from germanic]'),
(35319, 's', '\r\nStout-hearted', ' adj. Courageous.'),
(35320, 's', '\r\nStove1', ' n. Closed apparatus burning fuel or using electricity for heating or cooking. [low german or dutch]'),
(35321, 's', '\r\nStove2', ' past and past part. Of *stave v.'),
(35322, 's', '\r\nStove-pipe', ' n. Pipe carrying smoke and gases from a stove to a chimney.'),
(35323, 's', '\r\nStow', ' v. Pack (goods, cargo, etc.) Tidily and compactly.  stow away 1 place (a thing) out of the way. 2 be a stowaway on a ship etc. [from *bestow]'),
(35324, 's', '\r\nStowage', ' n. 1 stowing. 2 place for this.'),
(35325, 's', '\r\nStowaway', ' n. Person who hides on a ship or aircraft etc. To travel free.'),
(35326, 's', '\r\nStrabismus', ' n. Med. Squinting, squint. [greek strabos squinting]'),
(35327, 's', '\r\nStraddle', ' v. (-ling) 1 a sit or stand across (a thing) with the legs spread. B be situated on both sides of. 2 part (one''s legs) widely. [from *stride]'),
(35328, 's', '\r\nStrafe', ' v. (-fing) bombard; attack with gunfire. [german, = punish]'),
(35329, 's', '\r\nStraggle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 lack compactness or tidiness. 2 be dispersed or sporadic. 3 trail behind in a race etc. â€”n. Straggling or scattered group.  straggler n. Straggly adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin uncertain]'),
(35330, 's', '\r\nStraight', ' â€”adj. 1 extending uniformly in the same direction; not bent or curved. 2 successive, uninterrupted (three straight wins). 3 ordered; level; tidy (put things straight). 4 honest, candid. 5 (of thinking etc.) Logical. 6 (of theatre, music, etc.) Serious, classical, not popular or comic. 7 a unmodified. B (of a drink) undiluted. 8 colloq. A (of a person etc.) Conventional, respectable. B heterosexual. 9 direct, undeviating. â€”n. 1 straight part, esp. The concluding stretch of a racetrack. 2 straight condition. 3 sequence of five cards in poker. 4 colloq. Conventional person; heterosexual. â€”adv. 1 in a straight line; direct. 2 in the right direction. 3 correctly.  go straight (of a criminal) become honest. Straight away immediately. Straight off colloq. Without hesitation.  straightish adj. Straightness n. [originally a past part. Of *stretch]'),
(35331, 's', '\r\nStraightaway', ' adv. = straight away.'),
(35332, 's', '\r\nStraighten', ' v. 1 (often foll. By out) make or become straight. 2 (foll. By up) stand erect after bending.'),
(35333, 's', '\r\nStraight', 'eye  n. Ability to detect deviation from the straight.'),
(35334, 's', '\r\nStraight', 'face  n. Intentionally expressionless face.  straight-faced adj.'),
(35335, 's', '\r\nStraight', 'fight  n. Polit. Contest between two candidates only.'),
(35336, 's', '\r\nStraight', 'flush  n. Flush in numerical sequence.'),
(35337, 's', '\r\nStraightforward', ' adj. 1 honest or frank. 2 (of a task etc.) Simple.'),
(35338, 's', '\r\nStraight', 'man  n. Comedian''s stooge.'),
(35339, 's', '\r\nStrain1', ' â€”v. 1 stretch tightly; make or become taut or tense. 2 injure by overuse or excessive demands. 3 exercise (oneself, one''s senses, a thing, etc.) Intensely; press to extremes. 4 strive intensively. 5 (foll. By at) tug, pull. 6 distort from the true intention or meaning. 7 a clear (a liquid) of solid matter by passing it through a sieve etc. B (foll. By out) filter (solids) out from a liquid. â€”n. 1 a act of straining. B force exerted in this. 2 injury caused by straining a muscle etc. 3 severe mental or physical demand or exertion (suffering from strain). 4 snatch of music or poetry. 5 tone or tendency in speech or writing (more in the same strain). [french estrei(g)n- from latin stringo]'),
(35340, 's', '\r\nStrain2', ' n. 1 breed or stock of animals, plants, etc. 2 tendency; characteristic. [old english, = begetting]'),
(35341, 's', '\r\nStrained', ' adj. 1 constrained, artificial. 2 (of a relationship) mutually distrustful or tense.'),
(35342, 's', '\r\nStrainer', ' n. Device for straining liquids etc.'),
(35343, 's', '\r\nStrait', ' n. 1 (in sing. Or pl.) Narrow channel connecting two large bodies of water. 2 (usu. In pl.) Difficulty or distress. [french estreit from latin strictus narrow]'),
(35344, 's', '\r\nStraitened', ' adj. Of or marked by poverty.'),
(35345, 's', '\r\nStrait-jacket', ' â€”n. 1 strong garment with long sleeves for confining a violent prisoner etc. 2 restrictive measures. â€”v. (-t-) 1 restrain with a strait-jacket. 2 severely restrict.'),
(35346, 's', '\r\nStrait-laced', ' adj. Puritanical.'),
(35347, 's', '\r\nStrand1', ' â€”v. 1 run aground. 2 (as stranded adj.) In difficulties, esp. Without money or transport. â€”n. Foreshore; beach. [old english]'),
(35348, 's', '\r\nStrand2', ' n. 1 each of the twisted threads or wires making a rope or cable etc. 2 single thread or strip of fibre. 3 lock of hair. 4 element; component. [origin unknown]'),
(35349, 's', '\r\nStrange', ' adj. 1 unusual, peculiar, surprising, eccentric. 2 (often foll. By to) unfamiliar, foreign. 3 (foll. By to) unaccustomed. 4 not at ease.  strangely adv. Strangeness n. [french estrange from latin extraneus]'),
(35350, 's', '\r\nStranger', ' n. 1 person new to a particular place or company. 2 (often foll. By to) person one does not know. 3 (foll. By to) person unaccustomed to (no stranger to controversy).'),
(35351, 's', '\r\nStrangle', ' v. (-ling) 1 squeeze the windpipe or neck of, esp. So as to kill. 2 hamper, suppress.  strangler n. [latin strangulo]'),
(35352, 's', '\r\nStranglehold', ' n. 1 throttling hold in wrestling. 2 deadly grip. 3 complete control.'),
(35353, 's', '\r\nStrangulate', ' v. (-ting) compress (a vein, intestine, etc.), preventing circulation. [latin: related to *strangle]'),
(35354, 's', '\r\nStrangulation', ' n. 1 strangling or being strangled. 2 strangulating.'),
(35355, 's', '\r\nStrap', ' â€”n. 1 strip of leather etc., often with a buckle, for holding things together etc. 2 narrow strip of fabric worn over the shoulders as part of a garment. 3 loop for grasping to steady oneself in a moving vehicle. 4 (the strap) punishment by beating with a leather strap. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (often foll. By down, up, etc.) Secure or bind with a strap. 2 beat with a strap.  strapless adj. [dial., = *strop]'),
(35356, 's', '\r\nStraphanger', ' n. Slang standing passenger in a bus, train, etc.  straphang v.'),
(35357, 's', '\r\nStrapping', ' adj. Large and sturdy.'),
(35358, 's', '\r\nStrata', ' pl. Of *stratum.'),
(35359, 's', '\r\nUsage', ' it is incorrect to use strata as the singular noun instead of stratum.'),
(35360, 's', '\r\nStratagem', ' n. 1 cunning plan or scheme. 2 trickery. [greek strategos a general]'),
(35361, 's', '\r\nStrategic', ' adj. 1 of or promoting strategy. 2 (of materials) essential in war. 3 (of bombing or weapons) done or for use as a longer-term military objective.  strategically adv.'),
(35362, 's', '\r\nStrategy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 long-term plan or policy (economic strategy). 2 art of war. 3 art of moving troops, ships, aircraft, etc. Into favourable positions.  strategist n.'),
(35363, 's', '\r\nStrathspey', ' n. 1 slow scottish dance. 2 music for this. [strathspey, valley of the river spey]'),
(35364, 's', '\r\nStratify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) (esp. As stratified adj.) Arrange in strata or grades etc.  stratification n. [french: related to *stratum]'),
(35365, 's', '\r\nStratigraphy', ' n. Geol. & archaeol. 1 relative position of strata. 2 the study of this.  stratigraphic adj. [from *stratum]'),
(35366, 's', '\r\nStratosphere', ' n. Layer of atmosphere above the troposphere, extending to about 50 km from the earth''s surface.  stratospheric adj. [from *stratum]'),
(35367, 's', '\r\nStratum', ' n. (pl. Strata) 1 layer or set of layers of any deposited substance, esp. Of rock. 2 atmospheric layer. 3 social class. [latin sterno strew]'),
(35368, 's', '\r\nStraw', ' n. 1 dry cut stalks of grain as fodder or material for bedding, packing, etc. 2 single stalk of straw. 3 thin tube for sucking drink through. 4 insignificant thing. 5 pale yellow colour.  clutch at straws try any remedy in desperation. Straw in the wind indication of future developments. [old english]'),
(35369, 's', '\r\nStrawberry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 pulpy red fruit with a seed-studded surface. 2 plant with runners and white flowers bearing this. [old english: related to *straw, for unknown reason]'),
(35370, 's', '\r\nStrawberry', 'mark  n. Reddish birthmark.'),
(35371, 's', '\r\nStraw', 'vote  n. (also straw poll) unofficial ballot as a test of opinion.'),
(35372, 's', '\r\nStray', ' â€”v. 1 wander from the right place or from one''s companions; go astray. 2 deviate morally or mentally. â€”n. Strayed person, animal, or thing. â€”adj. 1 strayed, lost. 2 isolated, occasional. 3 physics wasted or unwanted. [anglo-french strey: related to *astray]'),
(35373, 's', '\r\nStreak', ' â€”n. 1 long thin usu. Irregular line or band, esp. Of colour. 2 strain in a person''s character. 3 spell or series (winning streak). â€”v. 1 mark with streaks. 2 move very rapidly. 3 colloq. Run naked in public.  streaker n. [old english, = pen-stroke]'),
(35374, 's', '\r\nStreaky', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of streaks. 2 (of bacon) with streaks of fat.'),
(35375, 's', '\r\nStream', ' â€”n. 1 flowing body of water, esp. A small river. 2 flow of a fluid or of a mass of people. 3 current or direction in which things are moving or tending (against the stream). 4  group of schoolchildren of similar ability taught together. â€”v. 1 move as a stream. 2 run with liquid. 3 be blown in the wind. 4 emit a stream of (blood etc.). 5  arrange (schoolchildren) in streams.  on stream in operation or production.'),
(35376, 's', '\r\nStreamer', ' n. 1 long narrow strip of ribbon or paper. 2 long narrow flag. 3 banner headline.'),
(35377, 's', '\r\nStreamline', ' v. (-ning) 1 give (a vehicle etc.) The form which presents the least resistance to motion. 2 make simple or more efficient.'),
(35378, 's', '\r\nStreet', ' n. 1 a public road in a city, town, or village. B this with the houses etc. On each side. 2 people who live or work in a particular street.  on the streets living by prostitution. Streets ahead (often foll. By of) colloq. Much superior (to). Up (or right up) one''s street colloq. What one likes, knows about, etc. [old english]'),
(35379, 's', '\r\nStreetcar', ' n. Us tram.'),
(35380, 's', '\r\nStreet', 'credibility  n. (also street cred) slang familiarity with a fashionable urban subculture.'),
(35381, 's', '\r\nStreetwalker', ' n. Prostitute seeking customers in the street.'),
(35382, 's', '\r\nStreetwise', ' n. Knowing how to survive modern urban life.'),
(35383, 's', '\r\nStrength', ' n. 1 being strong; degree or manner of this. 2 a person or thing giving strength. B positive attribute. 3 number of people present or available; full number.  from strength to strength with ever-increasing success. In strength in large numbers. On the strength of on the basis of. [old english: related to *strong]'),
(35384, 's', '\r\nStrengthen', ' v. Make or become stronger.'),
(35385, 's', '\r\nStrenuous', ' adj. 1 requiring or using great effort. 2 energetic.  strenuously adv. [latin]'),
(35386, 's', '\r\nStreptococcus', ' n. (pl. -cocci) bacterium of a type often causing infectious diseases.  streptococcal adj. [greek streptos twisted, kokkos berry]'),
(35387, 's', '\r\nStreptomycin', ' n. Antibiotic effective against many disease-producing bacteria. [greek streptos twisted, mukes fungus]'),
(35388, 's', '\r\nStress', ' â€”n. 1 a pressure or tension. B quantity measuring this. 2 a physical or mental strain. B distress caused by this. 3 a emphasis. B emphasis on a syllable or word. â€”v. 1 emphasize. 2 subject to stress.  lay stress on emphasize. [shortening of *distress]'),
(35389, 's', '\r\nStressful', ' adj. Causing stress.'),
(35390, 's', '\r\nStretch', ' â€”v. 1 draw, be drawn, or be able to be drawn out in length or size. 2 make or become taut. 3 place or lie at full length or spread out. 4 (also absol.) A extend (a limb etc.). B thrust out one''s limbs and tighten one''s muscles after being relaxed. 5 have a specified length or extension; extend. 6 strain or exert extremely; exaggerate (stretch the truth). â€”n. 1 continuous extent, expanse, or period. 2 stretching or being stretched. 3 (attrib.) Elastic (stretch fabric). 4 colloq. Period of imprisonment etc. 5 us straight side of a racetrack.  at a stretch in one period. Stretch one''s legs exercise oneself by walking. Stretch out 1 extend (a limb etc.). 2 last; prolong. Stretch a point agree to something not normally allowed.  stretchy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(35391, 's', '\r\nStretcher', ' n. 1 two poles with canvas etc. Between, for carrying a person in a lying position. 2 brick etc. Laid along the face of a wall.'),
(35392, 's', '\r\nStrew', ' v. (past part. Strewn or strewed) 1 scatter or spread about over a surface. 2 (usu. Foll. By with) spread (a surface) with scattered things. [old english: related to *straw]'),
(35393, 's', '\r\n''Strewth', ' var. Of *''struth.'),
(35394, 's', '\r\nStria', ' n. (pl. Striae) slight ridge or furrow. [latin]'),
(35395, 's', '\r\nStriate', ' â€”adj. (also striated) marked with striae. â€”v. (-ting) mark with slight ridges.  striation n.'),
(35396, 's', '\r\nStricken', ' adj. Overcome with illness or misfortune etc. [archaic past part. Of *strike]'),
(35397, 's', '\r\nStrict', ' adj. 1 precisely limited or defined; undeviating (strict diet). 2 requiring complete obedience or exact performance.  strictly speaking applying words or rules in their strict sense. strictly adv. Strictness n. [latin stringo strict- draw tight] '),
(35398, 's', '\r\nStricture', ' n. (usu. In pl.; often foll. By on, upon) critical or censorious remark. [latin: related to *strict]'),
(35399, 's', '\r\nStride', ' â€”v. (-ding; past strode; past part. Stridden) 1 walk with long firm steps. 2 cross with one step. 3 bestride. â€”n. 1 a single long step. B length of this. 2 gait as determined by the length of stride. 3 (usu. In pl.) Progress (great strides). 4 steady progress (get into one''s stride).  take in one''s stride manage easily. [old english]'),
(35400, 's', '\r\nStrident', ' adj. Loud and harsh.  stridency n. Stridently adv. [latin strido creak]'),
(35401, 's', '\r\nStrife', ' n. Conflict; struggle. [french estrif: related to *strive]'),
(35402, 's', '\r\nStrike', ' â€”v. (-king; past struck; past part. Struck or archaic stricken) 1 deliver (a blow) or inflict a blow on; hit. 2 come or bring sharply into contact with (ship struck a rock). 3 propel or divert with a blow. 4 (cause to) penetrate (struck terror into him). 5 ignite (a match) or produce (sparks etc.) By friction. 6 make (a coin) by stamping. 7  produce (a musical note) by striking. 8 a (also absol.) (of a clock) indicate (the time) with a chime etc. B (of time) be so indicated. 9 a attack suddenly. B (of a disease) afflict. 10 cause to become suddenly (struck dumb). 11 reach or achieve (strike a balance). 12 agree on (a bargain). 13 assume (an attitude) suddenly and dramatically. 14 discover or find (oil etc.) By drilling etc. 15 occur to or appear to (strikes me as silly). 16  (of employees) engage in a strike. 17 lower or take down (a flag or tent etc.). 18 take a specified direction. 19 (also absol.) Secure a hook in the mouth of (a fish) by jerking the tackle. â€”n. 1 act of striking. 2 a organized refusal to work until a grievance is remedied. B similar refusal to participate. 3 sudden find or success. 4 attack, esp. From the air.  on strike taking part in an industrial etc. Strike. Strike home 1 deal an effective blow. 2 have the intended effect. Strike off 1 remove with a stroke. 2 delete (a name etc.) From a list, esp. A professional register. Strike out 1 hit out. 2 act vigorously. 3 delete (an item or name etc.). 4 set off (struck out eastwards). Strike up 1 start (an acquaintance, conversation, etc.), esp. Casually. 2 (also absol.) Begin playing (a tune etc.). Struck on colloq. Infatuated with. [old english, = go, stroke]'),
(35403, 's', '\r\nStrikebreaker', ' n. Person working or employed in place of strikers.'),
(35404, 's', '\r\nStrike', 'pay  n. Allowance paid to strikers by their union.'),
(35405, 's', '\r\nStriker', ' n. 1 employee on strike. 2 football attacking player positioned forward.'),
(35406, 's', '\r\nStriking', ' adj. Impressive; attracting attention.  strikingly adv.'),
(35407, 's', '\r\nStrine', ' n. 1 comic transliteration of australian pronunciation. 2 (esp. Uneducated) australian english. [= australian in strine]'),
(35408, 's', '\r\nString', ' â€”n. 1 twine or narrow cord. 2 piece of this or of similar material used for tying or holding together, pulling, forming the head of a racket, etc. 3 length of catgut or wire etc. On a musical instrument, producing a note by vibration. 4 a (in pl.) Stringed instruments in an orchestra etc. B (attrib.) Of stringed instruments (string quartet). 5 (in pl.) Condition or complication (no strings attached). 6 set of things strung together; series or line. 7 tough side of a bean-pod etc. â€”v. (past and past part. Strung) 1 fit (a racket, violin, archer''s bow, etc.) With a string or strings, or (a violin etc. Bow) with horsehairs etc. 2 tie with string. 3 thread on a string. 4 arrange in or as a string. 5  remove the strings from (a bean).  on a string under one''s control. String along colloq. 1 deceive. 2 (often foll. By with) keep company (with). String out extend; prolong. String up 1 hang up on strings etc. 2 kill by hanging. 3 (usu. As strung up adj.) Make tense. [old english]'),
(35409, 's', '\r\nString-course', ' n. Raised horizontal band of bricks etc. On a building.'),
(35410, 's', '\r\nStringed', ' adj. (of musical instruments) having strings.'),
(35411, 's', '\r\nStringent', ' adj. (of rules etc.) Strict, precise; leaving no loophole for discretion.  stringency n. Stringently adv. [latin: related to *strict]'),
(35412, 's', '\r\nStringer', ' n. 1 longitudinal structural member in a framework, esp. Of a ship or aircraft. 2 colloq. Freelance newspaper correspondent.'),
(35413, 's', '\r\nString', 'vest  n. Vest with large meshes.'),
(35414, 's', '\r\nStringy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) like string, fibrous.  stringiness n.'),
(35415, 's', '\r\nStrip1', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (often foll. By of) remove the clothes or covering from. 2 (often foll. By off) undress oneself. 3 (often foll. By of) deprive (a person) of property or titles. 4 leave bare. 5 (often foll. By down) remove the accessory fittings of or take apart (a machine etc.). 6 damage the thread of (a screw) or the teeth of (a gearwheel). 7 remove (paint) or remove paint from (a surface) with solvent. 8 (often foll. By from) pull (a covering etc.) Off (stripped the masks from their faces). â€”n. 1 act of stripping, esp. In striptease. 2 colloq. Distinctive outfit worn by a sports team. [old english]'),
(35416, 's', '\r\nStrip2', ' n. Long narrow piece.  tear a person off a strip colloq. Rebuke a person. [low german strippe strap]'),
(35417, 's', '\r\nStrip', 'cartoon  n. = *comic strip.'),
(35418, 's', '\r\nStrip', 'club  n. Club at which striptease is performed.'),
(35419, 's', '\r\nStripe', ' n. 1 long narrow band or strip differing in colour or texture from the surface on either side of it. 2 mil. Chevron etc. Denoting military rank. [perhaps from low german or dutch]'),
(35420, 's', '\r\nStriped', ' adj. Marked with stripes.'),
(35421, 's', '\r\nStrip', 'light  n. Tubular fluorescent lamp.'),
(35422, 's', '\r\nStripling', ' n. Youth not yet fully grown. [from *strip2]'),
(35423, 's', '\r\nStripper', ' n. 1 person or thing that strips something. 2 device or solvent for removing paint etc. 3 striptease performer.'),
(35424, 's', '\r\nStrip-search', ' â€”n. Search involving the removal of all a person''s clothes. â€”v. Search in this way.'),
(35425, 's', '\r\nStriptease', ' n. Entertainment in which the performer slowly and erotically undresses.'),
(35426, 's', '\r\nStripy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) striped.'),
(35427, 's', '\r\nStrive', ' v. (-ving; past strove; past part. Striven) 1 try hard (strive to succeed). 2 (often foll. By with, against) struggle. [french estriver]'),
(35428, 's', '\r\nStrobe', ' n. Colloq. Stroboscope. [abbreviation]'),
(35429, 's', '\r\nStroboscope', ' n. 1 physics instrument for determining speeds of rotation etc. By shining a bright light at intervals so that a rotating object appears stationary. 2 lamp made to flash intermittently, esp. For this purpose.  stroboscopic adj. [greek strobos whirling]'),
(35430, 's', '\r\nStrode', ' past of *stride.'),
(35431, 's', '\r\nStroke', ' â€”n. 1 act of striking; blow, hit. 2 sudden disabling attack caused esp. By thrombosis; apoplexy. 3 a action or movement, esp. As one of a series. B slightest action (stroke of work). 4 single complete motion of a wing, oar, etc. 5 (in rowing) the mode or action of moving the oar (row a fast stroke). 6 whole motion of a piston in either direction. 7 specified mode of swimming. 8 specially successful or skilful effort (a stroke of diplomacy).'),
(35432, 's', '\r\nStroll', ' â€”v. Walk in a leisurely way. â€”n. Short leisurely walk. [probably from german strolch vagabond]'),
(35433, 's', '\r\nStrolling', 'players  n.pl. Hist. Travelling actors etc.'),
(35434, 's', '\r\nStrong', ' â€”adj. (stronger; strongest) 1 able to resist; not easily damaged, overcome, or disturbed. 2 healthy. 3 capable of exerting great force or of doing much; muscular, powerful. 4 forceful in effect (strong wind). 5 firmly held (strong suspicion). 6 (of an argument etc.) Convincing. 7 intense (strong light). 8 formidable (strong candidate). 9 (of a solution or drink etc.) Not very diluted. 10 of a specified number (200 strong). 11 gram. (of a verb) forming inflections by a change of vowel within the stem (e.g. Swim, swam). â€”adv. Strongly.  come on strong behave aggressively. Going strong colloq. Continuing vigorously; in good health etc.  strongish adj. Strongly adv. [old english]'),
(35435, 's', '\r\nStrong-arm', ' attrib. Adj. Using force (strong-arm tactics).'),
(35436, 's', '\r\nStrongbox', ' n. Small strongly made chest for valuables.'),
(35437, 's', '\r\nStronghold', ' n. 1 fortified place. 2 secure refuge. 3 centre of support for a cause etc.'),
(35438, 's', '\r\nStrong', 'language  n. Swearing.'),
(35439, 's', '\r\nStrong-minded', ' adj. Determined.'),
(35440, 's', '\r\nStrong', 'point  n. (also strong suit) thing at which one excels.'),
(35441, 's', '\r\nStrongroom', ' n. Room, esp. In a bank, for keeping valuables safe from fire and theft.'),
(35442, 's', '\r\nStrontium', ' n. Soft silver-white metallic element. [strontian in scotland]'),
(35443, 's', '\r\nStrontium-90', ' n. Radioactive isotope of strontium found in nuclear fallout and concentrated in bones and teeth when ingested.'),
(35444, 's', '\r\nStrop', ' â€”n. Device, esp. A strip of leather, for sharpening razors. â€”v. (-pp-) sharpen on a strop. [low german or dutch]'),
(35445, 's', '\r\nStroppy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Bad-tempered; awkward to deal with. [origin uncertain]'),
(35446, 's', '\r\nStrove', ' past of *strive.'),
(35447, 's', '\r\nStruck', ' past and past part. Of *strike.'),
(35448, 's', '\r\nStructural', ' adj. Of a structure.  structurally adv.'),
(35449, 's', '\r\nStructuralism', ' n. Doctrine that structure rather than function is important.  structuralist n. & adj.'),
(35450, 's', '\r\nStructure', ' â€”n. 1 a constructed unit, esp. A building. B way in which a building etc. Is constructed. 2 framework (new wages structure). â€”v. (-ring) give structure to; organize. [latin struo struct- build]'),
(35451, 's', '\r\nStrudel', ' n. Thin leaved pastry rolled round a filling and baked. [german]'),
(35452, 's', '\r\nStruggle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 violently try to get free of restraint. 2 (often foll. By for, or to + infin.) Try hard under difficulties (struggled for power; struggled to win). 3 (foll. By with, against) contend; fight. 4 (foll. By along, up, etc.) Progress with difficulty. 5 (esp. As struggling adj.) Have difficulty in gaining recognition or a living (struggling artist). â€”n. 1 act or spell of struggling. 2 hard or confused contest. [origin uncertain]'),
(35453, 's', '\r\nStrum', ' â€”v. (-mm-) 1 (often foll. By on; also absol.) Play on (a guitar, piano, etc.), esp. Carelessly or unskilfully. 2 play (a tune etc.) In this way. â€”n. Sound or spell of strumming. [imitative: cf. *thrum1]'),
(35454, 's', '\r\nStrumpet', ' n. Archaic or rhet. Prostitute. [origin unknown]'),
(35455, 's', '\r\nStrung', ' past and past part. Of *string.'),
(35456, 's', '\r\nStrung', ' past and past part. Of *string.'),
(35457, 's', '\r\nStrut', ' â€”n. 1 bar in a framework, designed to resist compression. 2 strutting gait. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 walk stiffly and pompously. 2 brace with struts. [old english]'),
(35458, 's', '\r\n''Struth', ' int. (also ''strewth) colloq. Exclamation of surprise. [god''s truth]'),
(35459, 's', '\r\nStrychnine', ' n. Highly poisonous alkaloid used in small doses as a stimulant. [greek strukhnos nightshade]'),
(35460, 's', '\r\nSts', ' abbr. Saints.'),
(35461, 's', '\r\nStub', ' â€”n. 1 remnant of a pencil or cigarette etc. 2 counterfoil of a cheque or receipt etc. 3 stump. â€”v. (-bb-) 1 strike (one''s toe) against something. 2 (usu. Foll. By out) extinguish (a cigarette) by pressure. [old english]'),
(35462, 's', '\r\nStubble', ' n. 1 stalks of corn etc. Left in the ground after the harvest. 2 short stiff hair or bristles.  stubbly adj. [latin stupula]'),
(35463, 's', '\r\nStubborn', ' adj. Obstinate, inflexible.  stubbornly adj. Stubbornness n. [origin unknown]'),
(35464, 's', '\r\nStubby', ' adj. (-ier, -est) short and thick.'),
(35465, 's', '\r\nStucco', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) plaster or cement for coating walls or moulding into decorations. â€”v. (-es, -ed) coat with stucco. [italian]'),
(35466, 's', '\r\nStuck', ' past and past part. Of *stick2.'),
(35467, 's', '\r\nStuck-up', ' adj. Conceited, snobbish. [*stick2]'),
(35468, 's', '\r\nStud1', ' â€”n. 1 large-headed projecting nail, boss, or knob, esp. For ornament. 2 double button, esp. For use with two buttonholes in a shirt-front. â€”v. (-dd-) 1 set with or as with studs. 2 (as studded adj.) (foll. By with) thickly set or strewn with. [old english]'),
(35469, 's', '\r\nStud2', ' n. 1 a number of horses kept for breeding etc. B place where these are kept. 2 stallion. 3 colloq. Young man, esp. One noted for sexual prowess. 4 (in full stud poker) form of poker with betting after the dealing of cards face up.  at stud (of a stallion) hired out for breeding. [old english]'),
(35470, 's', '\r\nStud-book', ' n. Book containing the pedigrees of horses.'),
(35471, 's', '\r\nStudding-sail', ' n. Extra sail set in light winds. [low german or dutch]'),
(35472, 's', '\r\nStudent', ' n. 1 person who is studying, esp. At a place of higher or further education. 2 (attrib.) Studying in order to become (student nurse).  studentship n. [latin: related to *study]'),
(35473, 's', '\r\nStud-farm', ' n. Place where horses are bred.'),
(35474, 's', '\r\nStudio', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 workroom of a painter, photographer, etc. 2 place for making films, recordings, or broadcast programmes. [italian]'),
(35475, 's', '\r\nStudio', 'couch  n. Couch convertible into a bed.'),
(35476, 's', '\r\nStudio', 'flat  n. One-roomed flat.'),
(35477, 's', '\r\nStudious', ' adj. 1 assiduous in study. 2 painstaking.  studiously adv. [latin: related to *study]'),
(35478, 's', '\r\nStudy', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 acquisition of knowledge, esp. From books. 2 (in pl.) Pursuit of academic knowledge. 3 private room used for reading, writing, etc. 4 piece of work, esp. A drawing, done for practice or as an experiment. 5 portrayal in literature etc. Of behaviour or character etc. 6 musical composition designed to develop a player''s skill. 7 thing worth observing (his face was a study). 8 thing that is or deserves to be investigated. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 make a study of; investigate (a subject) (study law). 2 (often foll. By for) apply oneself to study. 3 scrutinize closely (a visible object). 4 learn (one''s role etc.). 5 take pains to achieve (a result) or pay regard to (a subject or principle etc.). 6 (as studied adj.) Deliberate, affected (studied politeness). [latin studium]'),
(35479, 's', '\r\nStuff', ' â€”n. 1 material; fabric. 2 substance or things not needing to be specified (lot of stuff on the news). 3 particular knowledge or activity (know one''s stuff). 4 woollen fabric. 5 trash, nonsense. 6 (prec. By the) a colloq. Supply, esp. Of drink or drugs. B slang money. â€”v. 1 pack (a receptacle) tightly (stuff a cushion with feathers). 2 (foll. By in, into) force or cram (a thing). 3 fill out the skin of (an animal etc.) With material to restore the original shape. 4 fill (food, esp. Poultry) with a mixture, esp. Before cooking. 5 (also refl.) Fill with food; eat greedily. 6 push, esp. Hastily or clumsily. 7 (usu. In passive; foll. By up) block up (the nose etc.).'),
(35480, 's', '\r\nStuffed', 'shirt  n. Colloq. Pompous person.'),
(35481, 's', '\r\nStuffing', ' n. 1 padding for cushions etc. 2 mixture used to stuff food, esp. Before cooking.'),
(35482, 's', '\r\nStuffy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 (of a room etc.) Lacking fresh air. 2 dull or uninteresting. 3 (of the nose etc.) Stuffed up. 4 dull and conventional.  stuffily adv. Stuffiness n.'),
(35483, 's', '\r\nStultify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) make ineffective or useless, esp. By routine.  stultification n. [latin stultus foolish]'),
(35484, 's', '\r\nStumble', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 involuntarily lurch forward or almost fall. 2 (often foll. By along) walk with repeated stumbles. 3 speak haltingly. 4 (foll. By on, upon, across) find by chance. â€”n. Act of stumbling. [related to *stammer]'),
(35485, 's', '\r\nStumbling-block', ' n. Obstacle.'),
(35486, 's', '\r\nStump', ' â€”n. 1 part of a cut or fallen tree still in the ground. 2 similar part (e.g. Of a branch or limb) cut off or worn down. 3 cricket each of the three uprights of a wicket. 4 (in pl.) Joc. Legs. â€”v. 1 (of a question etc.) Be too hard for; baffle. 2 (as stumped adj.) At a loss, baffled. 3 cricket put (a batsman) out by touching the stumps with the ball while he is out of the crease. 4 walk stiffly or noisily. 5 (also absol.) Us traverse (a district) making political speeches.  stump up colloq. Pay or produce (the money required). [low german or dutch]'),
(35487, 's', '\r\nStumpy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) short and thick.  stumpiness n.'),
(35488, 's', '\r\nStun', ' v. (-nn-) 1 knock senseless; stupefy. 2 bewilder, shock. [french: related to *astonish]'),
(35489, 's', '\r\nStung', ' past and past part. Of *sting.'),
(35490, 's', '\r\nStunk', ' past and past part. Of *stink.'),
(35491, 's', '\r\nStunner', ' n. Colloq. Stunning person or thing.'),
(35492, 's', '\r\nStunning', ' adj. Colloq. Extremely attractive or impressive.  stunningly adv.'),
(35493, 's', '\r\nStunt1', ' v. Retard the growth or development of. [obsolete stunt foolish, short]'),
(35494, 's', '\r\nStunt2', ' n. 1 something unusual done for publicity. 2 trick or daring feat. [origin unknown]'),
(35495, 's', '\r\nStunt', 'man  n. Man employed to perform dangerous stunts in place of an actor.'),
(35496, 's', '\r\nStupefy', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 make stupid or insensible. 2 astonish, amaze.  stupefaction n. [french from latin stupeo be amazed]'),
(35497, 's', '\r\nStupendous', ' adj. Amazing or prodigious, esp. In size.  stupendously adv. [latin: related to *stupefy]'),
(35498, 's', '\r\nStupid', ' adj. (stupider, stupidest) 1 unintelligent, foolish (a stupid fellow). 2 typical of stupid persons (stupid mistake). 3 uninteresting, boring. 4 in a stupor.  stupidity n. (pl. -ies). Stupidly adv. [latin: related to *stupendous]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(35499, 's', '\r\nStupor', ' n. Dazed, torpid, or helplessly amazed state. [latin: related to *stupefy]'),
(35500, 's', '\r\nSturdy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 robust; strongly built. 2 vigorous (sturdy resistance).  sturdily adv. Sturdiness n. [french esturdi]'),
(35501, 's', '\r\nSturgeon', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) large sharklike fish yielding caviare. [anglo-french from germanic]'),
(35502, 's', '\r\nStutter', ' â€”v. 1 stammer, esp. By involuntary repetition of the initial consonants of words. 2 (often foll. By out) utter (words) in this way. â€”n. Act or habit of stuttering. [dial. Stut]'),
(35503, 's', '\r\nSty1', ' n. (pl. Sties) = *pigsty. [old english]'),
(35504, 's', '\r\nSty2', ' n. (also stye) (pl. Sties or styes) inflamed swelling on the edge of an eyelid. [old english]'),
(35505, 's', '\r\nSty2', ' n. (also stye) (pl. Sties or styes) inflamed swelling on the edge of an eyelid. [old english]'),
(35506, 's', '\r\nStygian', ' adj. Literary dark, gloomy. [literally = of the styx, a river round hades in greek mythology]'),
(35507, 's', '\r\nStyle', ' â€”n. 1 kind or sort, esp. In regard to appearance and form (elegant style of house). 2 manner of writing, speaking, or performing. 3 distinctive manner of a person, artistic school, or period. 4 correct way of designating a person or thing. 5 superior quality or manner (do it in style). 6 fashion in dress etc. 7 pointed tool for scratching or engraving. 8 bot. Narrow extension of the ovary supporting the stigma. â€”v. (-ling) 1 design or make etc. In a particular (esp. Fashionable) style. 2 designate in a specified way. [latin stilus]'),
(35508, 's', '\r\nStylish', ' adj. 1 fashionable; elegant. 2 superior.  stylishly adv. Stylishness n.'),
(35509, 's', '\r\nStylist', ' n. 1 a designer of fashionable styles etc. B hairdresser. 2 stylish writer or performer.'),
(35510, 's', '\r\nStylistic', ' adj. Of esp. Literary style.  stylistically adv.'),
(35511, 's', '\r\nStylized', ' adj. (also -ised) painted, drawn, etc. In a conventional non-realistic style.'),
(35512, 's', '\r\nStylus', ' n. (pl. -luses) 1 sharp needle following a groove in a gramophone record and transmitting the recorded sound for reproduction. 2 pointed writing tool. [latin: related to *style]'),
(35513, 's', '\r\nStymie', ' (also stimy) â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 golf situation where an opponent''s ball lies between one''s ball and the hole. 2 difficult situation. â€”v. (-mies, -mied, -mying or -mieing) 1 obstruct; thwart. 2 golf block with a stymie. [origin unknown]'),
(35514, 's', '\r\nStyptic', ' â€”adj. Checking bleeding. â€”n. Styptic substance. [greek stupho contract]'),
(35515, 's', '\r\nStyrene', ' n. Liquid hydrocarbon easily polymerized and used in making plastics etc. [greek sturax a resin]'),
(35516, 's', '\r\nSuasion', ' n. Formal persuasion (moral suasion). [latin suadeo suas- urge]'),
(35517, 's', '\r\nSuave', ' adj. Smooth; polite; sophisticated.  suavely adv. Suavity n. [latin suavis]'),
(35518, 's', '\r\nSub', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 submarine. 2 subscription. 3 substitute. 4 sub-editor. â€”v. (-bb-) 1 (usu. Foll. By for) act as a substitute. 2  sub-edit. [abbreviation]'),
(35519, 's', '\r\nSub-', ' prefix 1 at, to, or from a lower position (subordinate; submerge; subtract). 2 secondary or inferior position (subclass; subtotal). 3 nearly; more or less (subarctic). [latin]'),
(35520, 's', '\r\nSubaltern', ' n. Officer below the rank of captain, esp. A second lieutenant. [latin: related to *alternate]'),
(35521, 's', '\r\nSub-aqua', ' adj. Of underwater swimming or diving.'),
(35522, 's', '\r\nSubaquatic', ' adj. Underwater.'),
(35523, 's', '\r\nSubatomic', ' adj. Occurring in, or smaller than, an atom.'),
(35524, 's', '\r\nSubcommittee', ' n. Committee formed from a main committee for a special purpose.'),
(35525, 's', '\r\nSubconscious', ' â€”adj. Of the part of the mind which is not fully conscious but influences actions etc. â€”n. This part of the mind.  subconsciously adv.'),
(35526, 's', '\r\nSubcontinent', ' n. Large land mass, smaller than a continent.'),
(35527, 's', '\r\nSubcontract', ' â€”v. 1 employ another contractor to do (work) as part of a larger project. 2 make or carry out a subcontract. â€”n. Secondary contract.  subcontractor n.'),
(35528, 's', '\r\nSubculture', ' n. Distinct cultural group within a larger culture.'),
(35529, 's', '\r\nSubcutaneous', ' adj. Under the skin.'),
(35530, 's', '\r\nSubdivide', ' v. (-ding) divide again after a first division.  subdivision n.'),
(35531, 's', '\r\nSubdue', ' v. (-dues, -dued, -duing) 1 conquer, subjugate, or tame. 2 (as subdued adj.) Softened; lacking in intensity; toned down. [latin subduco]'),
(35532, 's', '\r\nSub-editor', ' n. 1 assistant editor. 2 person who edits material for printing.  sub-edit v. (-t-).'),
(35533, 's', '\r\nSubfusc', ' â€”adj. Formal dull; dusky. â€”n. Formal clothing at some universities. [latin fuscus dark brown]'),
(35534, 's', '\r\nSubgroup', ' n. Subset of a group.'),
(35535, 's', '\r\nSubheading', ' n. Subordinate heading or title.'),
(35536, 's', '\r\nSubhuman', ' adj. (of behaviour, intelligence, etc.) Less than human.'),
(35537, 's', '\r\nSubject', ' â€”n. 1 a matter, theme, etc. To be discussed, described, represented, etc. B (foll. By for) person, circumstance, etc., giving rise to a specified feeling, action, etc. (subject for congratulation). 2 field of study. 3 logic & gram.'),
(35538, 's', '\r\nSubjective', ' adj. 1 (of art, written history, an opinion, etc.) Not impartial or literal; personal. 2 esp. Philos. Of the individual consciousness or perception; imaginary, partial, or distorted. 3 gram. Of the subject.  subjectively adv. Subjectivity n. [latin: related to *subject]'),
(35539, 's', '\r\nSubjoin', ' v. Add (an illustration, anecdote, etc.) At the end. [latin subjungo -junct-]'),
(35540, 's', '\r\nSub', 'judice  adj. Law under judicial consideration and therefore prohibited from public discussion elsewhere. [latin]'),
(35541, 's', '\r\nSubjugate', ' v. (-ting) bring into subjection; vanquish.  subjugation n. Subjugator n. [latin jugum yoke]'),
(35542, 's', '\r\nSubjunctive', ' gram. â€”adj. (of a mood) expressing what is imagined, wished, or possible (e.g. If i were you; be that as it may). â€”n. This mood or form. [latin: related to *subjoin]'),
(35543, 's', '\r\nSublease', ' â€”n. Lease granted by a tenant to a subtenant. â€”v. (-sing) lease to a subtenant.'),
(35544, 's', '\r\nSublet', ' â€”n. = *sublease n. â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. -let) = *sublease v.'),
(35545, 's', '\r\nSub-lieutenant', ' n. Officer ranking next below lieutenant.'),
(35546, 's', '\r\nSublimate', ' â€”v. (-ting) 1 divert (esp. Sexual energy) into socially more acceptable activity. 2 convert (a substance) from the solid state directly to vapour by heat, and usu. Allow it to solidify again. 3 refine; purify; idealize. â€”n. Sublimated substance.  sublimation n. [latin: related to *sublime]'),
(35547, 's', '\r\nSublime', ' â€”adj. (sublimer, sublimest) 1 of the most exalted or noble kind; awe-inspiring. 2 arrogantly unruffled (sublime indifference). â€”v. 1 = *sublimate v. 2. 2 purify or elevate by or as if by sublimation; make sublime. 3 become pure (as if) by sublimation.  sublimely adv. Sublimity n. [latin sublimis]'),
(35548, 's', '\r\nSubliminal', ' adj. Psychol. (of a stimulus etc.) Below the threshold of sensation or consciousness.  subliminally adv. [latin limen -min- threshold]'),
(35549, 's', '\r\nSub-machine-gun', ' n. Hand-held lightweight machine-gun.'),
(35550, 's', '\r\nSubmarine', ' â€”n. Vessel, esp. An armed warship, capable of operating under water. â€”attrib. Adj. Existing, occurring, done, or used under the sea.  submariner n.'),
(35551, 's', '\r\nSubmerge', ' v. (-ging) 1 place, go, or dive under water. 2 inundate with work, problems, etc.  submergence n. Submersion n. [latin mergo mers- dip]'),
(35552, 's', '\r\nSubmersible', ' â€”n. Submarine operating under water for short periods. â€”adj. Capable of submerging.'),
(35553, 's', '\r\nSubmicroscopic', ' adj. Too small to be seen by an ordinary microscope.'),
(35554, 's', '\r\nSubmission', ' n. 1 a submitting or being submitted. B thing submitted. 2 submissiveness. [latin submissio: related to *submit]'),
(35555, 's', '\r\nSubmissive', ' adj. Humble, obedient.  submissively adv. Submissiveness n.'),
(35556, 's', '\r\nSubmit', ' v. (-tt-) 1 (usu. Foll. By to) a cease resistance; yield. B refl. Surrender (oneself) to the control of another etc. 2 present for consideration. 3 (usu. Foll. By to) subject (a person or thing) to a process, treatment, etc. [latin mitto miss- send]'),
(35557, 's', '\r\nSubnormal', ' adj. Below or less than normal, esp. In intelligence.'),
(35558, 's', '\r\nSuborder', ' n. Taxonomic category between an order and a family.'),
(35559, 's', '\r\nSubordinate', ' â€”adj. (usu. Foll. By to) of inferior importance or rank; secondary, subservient. â€”n. Person working under another. â€”v. (-ting) (usu. Foll. By to) make or treat as subordinate.  subordination n. [latin: related to *ordain]'),
(35560, 's', '\r\nSubordinate', 'clause  n. Clause serving as an adjective, adverb, or noun in a main sentence.'),
(35561, 's', '\r\nSuborn', ' v. Induce by bribery etc. To commit perjury etc. [latin orno equip]'),
(35562, 's', '\r\nSub-plot', ' n. Secondary plot in a play etc.'),
(35563, 's', '\r\nSubpoena', ' â€”n. Writ ordering a person to attend a lawcourt. â€”v. (past and past part. -naed or -na''d) serve a subpoena on. [latin, = under penalty]'),
(35564, 's', '\r\nSub', 'rosa  adj. & adv. In secrecy or confidence. [latin, = under the rose]'),
(35565, 's', '\r\nSubroutine', ' n. Computing routine designed to perform a frequently used operation within a program.'),
(35566, 's', '\r\nSubscribe', ' v. (-bing) 1 (usu. Foll. By to, for) a pay (a specified sum), esp. Regularly, for membership of an organization, receipt of a publication, etc. B contribute money to a fund, for a cause, etc. 2 (usu. Foll. By to) agree with an opinion etc. (i subscribe to that).  subscribe to arrange to receive (a periodical etc.) Regularly. [latin scribo script- write]'),
(35567, 's', '\r\nSubscriber', ' n. 1 person who subscribes. 2 person hiring a telephone line.'),
(35568, 's', '\r\nSubscriber', 'trunk dialling  n. Automatic connection of trunk calls by dialling.'),
(35569, 's', '\r\nSubscript', ' â€”adj. Written or printed below the line. â€”n. Subscript number etc.'),
(35570, 's', '\r\nSubscription', ' â€”n. 1 a act of subscribing. B money subscribed. 2 membership fee, esp. Paid regularly. â€”attrib. Adj. Paid for mainly by advance sales of tickets (subscription concert).'),
(35571, 's', '\r\nSubsection', ' n. Division of a section.'),
(35572, 's', '\r\nSubsequent', ' adj. (usu. Foll. By to) following, esp. As a consequence.  subsequently adv. [latin sequor follow]'),
(35573, 's', '\r\nSubservient', ' adj. 1 servile. 2 (usu. Foll. By to) instrumental. 3 (usu. Foll. By to) subordinate.  subservience n. [latin subservio]'),
