(35817, 's', '\r\nSupply', ' â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 provide (a thing needed). 2 (often foll. By with) provide (a person etc. With a thing). 3 meet or make up for (a deficiency or need etc.). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 providing of what is needed. 2 stock, store, amount, etc., of something provided or obtainable. 3 (in pl.) Provisions and equipment for an army, expedition, etc. 4 (often attrib.) Schoolteacher etc. Acting as a temporary substitute for another.  in short supply scarce. Supply and demand econ. Quantities available and required, as factors regulating price.'),
(35818, 's', '\r\nSupply-side', ' attrib. Adj. Econ. Denoting a policy of low taxation etc. To encourage production and investment.'),
(35819, 's', '\r\nSupport', ' â€”v. 1 carry all or part of the weight of; keep from falling, sinking, or failing. 2 provide for (a family etc.). 3 strengthen, encourage. 4 bear out; tend to substantiate. 5 give help or approval to (a person, team, sport, etc.); further (a cause etc.). 6 speak in favour of (a resolution etc.). 7 (also absol.) Take a secondary part to (a principal actor etc.); perform a secondary act to (the main act) at a pop concert etc. â€”n. 1 supporting or being supported. 2 person or thing that supports. 3 secondary act at a pop concert etc. in support of so as to support. [latin porto carry]'),
(35820, 's', '\r\nSupporter', ' n. Person or thing that supports a cause, team, etc.'),
(35821, 's', '\r\nSupporting', 'film  n. (also supporting picture etc.) Less important film in a cinema programme.'),
(35822, 's', '\r\nSupportive', ' adj. Providing (esp. Emotional) support or encouragement.  supportively adv. Supportiveness n.'),
(35823, 's', '\r\nSuppose', ' v. (-sing) (often foll. By that) 1 assume; be inclined to think. 2 take as a possibility or hypothesis (suppose you are right; supposing you are right). 3 (in imper.) As a formula of proposal (suppose we try again). 4 (of a theory or result etc.) Require as a condition (that supposes we''re on time). 5 (in imper. Or pres. Part. Forming a question) in the circumstances that; if (suppose he won''t let you?). 6 (as supposed adj.) Presumed (his supposed brother). 7 (in passive; foll. By to + infin.) A be expected or required (was supposed to write to you). B (with neg.) Ought not; not be allowed to (you are not supposed to go in there).  i suppose so expression of hesitant agreement. [french: related to *pose]'),
(35824, 's', '\r\nSupposedly', ' adv. Allegedly; as is generally believed.'),
(35825, 's', '\r\nSupposition', ' n. 1 thing supposed. 2 act of supposing.'),
(35826, 's', '\r\nSuppositious', ' adj. Hypothetical.'),
(35827, 's', '\r\nSuppository', ' n. (pl. -ies) medical preparation melting in the rectum or vagina. [latin suppositorius placed underneath]'),
(35828, 's', '\r\nSuppress', ' v. 1 put an end to, esp. Forcibly. 2 prevent (information, feelings, a reaction, etc.) From being seen, heard, or known. 3 a partly or wholly eliminate (electrical interference etc.). B equip (a device) to reduce the interference caused by it.  suppressible adj. Suppression n. Suppressor n. [latin: related to *press1]'),
(35829, 's', '\r\nSuppurate', ' v. (-ting) 1 form pus. 2 fester.  suppuration n. [latin: related to *pus]'),
(35830, 's', '\r\nSupra', ' adv. Above or earlier (in a book etc.). [latin]'),
(35831, 's', '\r\nSupra-', ' prefix above.'),
(35832, 's', '\r\nSupranational', ' adj. Transcending national limits.'),
(35833, 's', '\r\nSupremacy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being supreme. 2 highest authority.'),
(35834, 's', '\r\nSupreme', ' adj. 1 highest in authority or rank. 2 greatest; most important. 3 (of a penalty or sacrifice etc.) Involving death.  supremely adv. [latin]'),
(35835, 's', '\r\nSupreme', 'court  n. Highest judicial court in a state etc.'),
(35836, 's', '\r\nSupremo', ' n. (pl. -s)  person in overall charge. [spanish, = *supreme]'),
(35837, 's', '\r\nSur-1', ' prefix = *super- (surcharge; surrealism). [french]'),
(35838, 's', '\r\nSur-2', ' prefix assim. Form of *sub- before r.'),
(35839, 's', '\r\nSurcease', ' literary â€”n. Cessation. â€”v. (-sing) cease. [french sursis delayed, omitted]'),
(35840, 's', '\r\nSurcharge', ' â€”n. Additional charge or payment. â€”v. (-ging) exact a surcharge from. [french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35841, 's', '\r\nSurd', ' math â€”adj. (of a number) irrational. â€”n. Surd number, esp. The root of an integer. [latin, = deaf]'),
(35842, 's', '\r\nSure', ' â€”adj. 1 (often foll. By of or that) convinced. 2 having adequate reason for a belief or assertion. 3 (foll. By of) confident in anticipation or knowledge of. 4 reliable or unfailing. 5 (foll. By to + infin.) Certain. 6 undoubtedly true or truthful. â€”adv. Colloq. Certainly.  be sure (in imper. Or infin.; foll. By that + clause or to + infin.) Take care to; not fail to. For sure colloq. Certainly. Make sure make or become certain; ensure. Sure enough colloq. In fact; certainly. To be sure admittedly; indeed, certainly.  sureness n. [french from latin securus]'),
(35843, 's', '\r\nSure-fire', ' attrib. Adj. Colloq. Certain to succeed.'),
(35844, 's', '\r\nSure-footed', ' adj. Never stumbling or making a mistake.'),
(35845, 's', '\r\nSurely', ' adv. 1 with certainty or safety (slowly but surely). 2 as an appeal to likelihood or reason (surely that can''t be right).'),
(35846, 's', '\r\nSurety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 money given as a guarantee of performance etc. 2 (esp. In phr. Stand surety for) person who takes responsibility for another''s debt, obligation, etc. [french from latin]'),
(35847, 's', '\r\nSurf', ' â€”n. Foam of the sea breaking on the shore or reefs. â€”v. Practise surfing.  surfer n. [origin unknown]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(35848, 's', '\r\nSurface', ' â€”n. 1 a the outside of a thing. B area of this. 2 any of the limits of a solid. 3 top of a liquid or of the ground etc. 4 outward or superficial aspect. 5 geom. Set of points with length and breadth but no thickness. 6 (attrib.) A of or on the surface. B superficial. â€”v. (-cing) 1 give the required surface to (a road, paper, etc.). 2 rise or bring to the surface. 3 become visible or known. 4 colloq. Wake up; get up.  come to the surface become perceptible. [french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35849, 's', '\r\nSurface', 'mail  n. Mail carried by land or sea.'),
(35850, 's', '\r\nSurface', 'tension  n. Tension of the surface-film of a liquid, tending to minimize its surface area.'),
(35851, 's', '\r\nSurfboard', ' n. Long narrow board used in surfing.'),
(35852, 's', '\r\nSurfeit', ' â€”n. 1 an excess, esp. In eating or drinking. 2 resulting fullness. â€”v. (-t-) 1 overfeed. 2 (foll. By with) (cause to) be wearied through excess. [french: related to *sur-1, *feat]'),
(35853, 's', '\r\nSurfing', ' n. Sport of riding the surf on a board.'),
(35854, 's', '\r\nSurge', ' â€”n. 1 sudden rush. 2 heavy forward or upward motion. 3 sudden increase in price, activity, etc. 4 sudden increase in voltage of an electric current. 5 swell of the sea. â€”v. (-ging) 1 move suddenly and powerfully forwards. 2 (of an electric current etc.) Increase suddenly. 3 (of the sea etc.) Swell. [latin surgo rise]'),
(35855, 's', '\r\nSurgeon', ' n. 1 medical practitioner qualified in surgery. 2 naval or military medical officer.'),
(35856, 's', '\r\nSurgery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 treatment of bodily injuries or disorders by incision or manipulation etc. As opposed to drugs. 2  place where or time when a doctor, dentist, etc., treats patients, or an mp, lawyer, etc., gives advice. [latin chirurgia, from greek kheir hand, ergo work]'),
(35857, 's', '\r\nSurgical', ' adj. 1 of or by surgeons or surgery. 2 a used in surgery. B worn to correct a deformity etc. 3 (esp. Of military action) swift and precise. surgically adv.'),
(35858, 's', '\r\nSurgical', 'spirit  n. Methylated spirit used for cleansing etc.'),
(35859, 's', '\r\nSurly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) bad-tempered; unfriendly.   surliness n. [obsolete sirly haughty: related to *sir]'),
(35860, 's', '\r\nSurmise', ' â€”n. Conjecture. â€”v. (-sing) (often foll. By that) infer doubtfully; guess; suppose. [latin supermitto -miss- accuse]'),
(35861, 's', '\r\nSurmount', ' v. 1 overcome (a difficulty or obstacle). 2 (usu. In passive) cap or crown.  surmountable adj. [french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35862, 's', '\r\nSurname', ' n. Family name, usu. Inherited or acquired by marriage. [obsolete surnoun from anglo-french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35863, 's', '\r\nSurpass', ' v. 1 be greater or better than, outdo. 2 (as surpassing adj.) Pre-eminent. [french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35864, 's', '\r\nSurplice', ' n. Loose white vestment worn by clergy and choristers. [anglo-french surplis]'),
(35865, 's', '\r\nSurplus', ' â€”n. 1 amount left over. 2 excess of revenue over expenditure. â€”adj. Exceeding what is needed or used. [anglo-french]'),
(35866, 's', '\r\nSurprise', ' â€”n. 1 unexpected or astonishing thing. 2 emotion caused by this. 3 catching or being caught unawares. 4 (attrib.) Unexpected; made or done etc. Without warning. â€”v. (-sing) 1 affect with surprise; turn out contrary to the expectations of. 2 (usu. In passive; foll. By at) shock, scandalize. 3 capture or attack by surprise. 4 come upon (a person) unawares. 5 (foll. By into) startle (a person) into an action etc.  take by surprise affect with surprise, esp. By an unexpected encounter or statement.  surprising adj. Surprisingly adv. [french]'),
(35867, 's', '\r\nSurreal', ' adj. Unreal; dreamlike; bizarre. [back-formation from *surrealism]'),
(35868, 's', '\r\nSurrealism', ' n. 20th-c. Movement in art and literature, attempting to express the subconscious mind by dream imagery, bizarre juxtapositions, etc.  surrealist n. & adj. Surrealistic adj. Surrealistically adv. [french: related to *sur-1, *real1]'),
(35869, 's', '\r\nSurrender', ' â€”v. 1 hand over; relinquish. 2 submit, esp. To an enemy. 3 refl. (foll. By to) yield to a habit, emotion, influence, etc. 4  give up rights under (a life-insurance policy) in return for a smaller sum received immediately. 5 abandon (hope etc.). â€”n. Act of surrendering.  surrender to bail duly appear in court after release on bail. [anglo-french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35870, 's', '\r\nSurreptitious', ' adj. Done by stealth; clandestine.  surreptitiously adv. [latin surripio seize secretly]'),
(35871, 's', '\r\nSurrogate', ' n. 1 substitute. 2  deputy, esp. Of a bishop in granting marriage licences.  surrogacy n. [latin rogo ask]'),
(35872, 's', '\r\nSurrogate', 'mother  n. Woman who bears a child on behalf of another woman, usu. By artificial insemination of her own egg by the other woman''s partner.'),
(35873, 's', '\r\nSurround', ' â€”v. Come or be all round; encircle, enclose. â€”n. 1 border or edging, esp. An area of floor between the walls and carpet of a room. 2 surrounding area or substance. [latin: related to *sur-1, unda wave]'),
(35874, 's', '\r\nSurroundings', ' n.pl. Objects or conditions around or affecting a person or thing; environment.'),
(35875, 's', '\r\nSurtax', ' n. Additional tax, esp. On high incomes. [french: related to *sur-1]'),
(35876, 's', '\r\nSurtitle', ' n. Explanatory caption projected on to a screen above the stage during an opera.'),
(35877, 's', '\r\nSurveillance', ' n. Close observation undertaken by the police etc. [french: related to *sur-1, veiller watch]'),
(35878, 's', '\r\nSurvey', ' â€”v. 1 view or consider as a whole. 2 examine the condition of (a building etc.). 3 determine the boundaries, extent, ownership, etc. Of (a district etc.). â€”n. 1 general view or consideration. 2 a act of surveying property. B statement etc. Resulting from this. 3 investigation of public opinion etc. 4 map or plan made by surveying. [latin: related to *super-, video see]'),
(35879, 's', '\r\nSurveyor', ' n. Person who surveys land and buildings, esp. For a living.'),
(35880, 's', '\r\nSurvival', ' n. 1 surviving. 2 relic.'),
(35881, 's', '\r\nSurvive', ' v. (-ving) 1 continue to live or exist. 2 live or exist longer than. 3 remain alive after or continue to exist in spite of (a danger, accident, etc.).  survivor n. [anglo-french survivre from latin supervivo]'),
(35882, 's', '\r\nSus', ' var. Of *suss.'),
(35883, 's', '\r\nSus-', ' prefix assim. Form of *sub- before c, p, t.'),
(35884, 's', '\r\nSusceptibility', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being susceptible. 2 (in pl.) Person''s feelings.'),
(35885, 's', '\r\nSusceptible', ' adj. 1 impressionable, sensitive, emotional. 2 (predic.) A (foll. By to) liable or vulnerable to. B (foll. By of) allowing; admitting of (proof etc.).  susceptibly adv. [latin suscipio -cept- take up]'),
(35886, 's', '\r\nSushi', ' n. Japanese dish of balls of cold rice topped with raw fish etc. [japanese]'),
(35887, 's', '\r\nSuspect', ' â€”v. 1 be inclined to think. 2 have an impression of the existence or presence of. 3 (often foll. By of) mentally accuse. 4 doubt the genuineness or truth of. â€”n. Suspected person. â€”adj. Subject to or deserving suspicion. [latin suspicio -spect-]'),
(35888, 's', '\r\nSuspend', ' v. 1 hang up. 2 keep inoperative or undecided for a time. 3 debar temporarily from a function, office, etc. 4 (as suspended adj.) (of particles or a body in a fluid) floating between the top and bottom. [latin suspendo -pens-]'),
(35889, 's', '\r\nSuspended', 'animation  n. Temporary deathlike condition.'),
(35890, 's', '\r\nSuspended', 'sentence  n. Judicial sentence left unenforced subject to good behaviour during a specified period.'),
(35891, 's', '\r\nSuspender', ' n. 1 attachment to hold up a stocking or sock by its top. 2 (in pl.) Us braces.'),
(35892, 's', '\r\nSuspender', 'belt  n. Woman''s undergarment with suspenders.'),
(35893, 's', '\r\nSuspense', ' n. State of anxious uncertainty or expectation.  suspenseful adj. [french, = delay]'),
(35894, 's', '\r\nSuspension', ' n. 1 suspending or being suspended. 2 springs etc. Supporting a vehicle on its axles. 3 substance consisting of particles suspended in a medium.'),
(35895, 's', '\r\nSuspension', 'bridge  n. Bridge with a roadway suspended from cables supported by towers.'),
(35896, 's', '\r\nSuspicion', ' n. 1 unconfirmed belief; distrust. 2 suspecting or being suspected. 3 (foll. By of) slight trace of.  above suspicion too obviously good etc. To be suspected. Under suspicion suspected. [latin: related to *suspect]'),
(35897, 's', '\r\nSuspicious', ' adj. 1 prone to or feeling suspicion. 2 causing suspicion.  suspiciously adv.'),
(35898, 's', '\r\nSuss', ' v. Slang (also sus) (-ss-) (usu. Foll. By out) 1 investigate, inspect. 2 work out; realize.  on suss on suspicion (of having committed a crime). [abbreviation]'),
(35899, 's', '\r\nSustain', ' v. 1 support, bear the weight of, esp. For a long period. 2 encourage, support. 3 (of food) nourish. 4 endure, stand. 5 suffer (defeat or injury etc.). 6 (of a court etc.) Uphold or decide in favour of (an objection etc.). 7 corroborate (a statement or charge). 8 maintain (effort etc.).  sustainable adj. [latin sustineo keep up]'),
(35900, 's', '\r\nSustenance', ' n. 1 nourishment, food. 2 means of support. [anglo-french: related to *sustain]'),
(35901, 's', '\r\nSuttee', ' n. Esp. Hist. 1 hindu custom of a widow''s suicide on her husband''s funeral pyre. 2 widow undergoing this. [sanskrit sati faithful wife]'),
(35902, 's', '\r\nSuture', ' â€”n. 1 stitching of the edges of a wound or incision. 2 thread or wire used for this. â€”v. (-ring) stitch (a wound or incision). [latin suo sut- sew]'),
(35903, 's', '\r\nSuzerain', ' n. 1 hist. Feudal overlord. 2 archaic sovereign or state partially controlling another state that is internally autonomous.  suzerainty n. [french]'),
(35904, 's', '\r\nSvelte', ' adj. Slender, lissom, graceful. [french from italian]'),
(35905, 's', '\r\nSw', ' abbr. 1 south-west. 2 south-western.'),
(35906, 's', '\r\nSwab', ' â€”n. 1 a absorbent pad used in surgery. B specimen of a secretion taken for examination. 2 mop etc. For cleaning or mopping up. â€”v. (-bb-) 1 clean with a swab. 2 (foll. By up) absorb (moisture) with a swab. 3 mop clean (a ship''s deck) [dutch]'),
(35907, 's', '\r\nSwaddle', ' v. (-ling) wrap (esp. A baby) tightly. [from *swathe]'),
(35908, 's', '\r\nSwaddling-clothes', ' n.pl. Narrow bandages formerly used to wrap and restrain a baby.'),
(35909, 's', '\r\nSwag', ' n. 1 slang booty of burglars etc. 2 austral. & nz traveller''s bundle. 3 festoon of flowers, foliage, drapery, etc.  swagged adj. [probably scandinavian]'),
(35910, 's', '\r\nSwagger', ' â€”v. Walk or behave arrogantly. â€”n. Swaggering gait or manner. [from *swag]'),
(35911, 's', '\r\nSwagger', 'stick  n. Short cane carried by a military officer.'),
(35912, 's', '\r\nSwahili', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 member of a bantu people of zanzibar and adjacent coasts. 2 their language. [arabic]'),
(35913, 's', '\r\nSwain', ' n. 1 archaic country youth. 2 poet. Young lover or suitor. [old norse, = lad]'),
(35914, 's', '\r\nSwallow1', ' â€”v. 1 cause or allow (food etc.) To pass down the throat. 2 perform the muscular movement required to do this. 3 accept meekly or credulously. 4 repress (a feeling etc.) (swallow one''s pride). 5 articulate (words etc.) Indistinctly. 6 (often foll. By up) engulf or absorb; exhaust. â€”n. 1 act of swallowing. 2 amount swallowed. [old english]'),
(35915, 's', '\r\nSwallow2', ' n. Migratory swift-flying bird with a forked tail. [old english]'),
(35916, 's', '\r\nSwallow-dive', ' n. & v. Dive with the arms outspread until close to the water.'),
(35917, 's', '\r\nSwallow-tail', ' n. 1 deeply forked tail. 2 butterfly etc. With this.'),
(35918, 's', '\r\nSwam', ' past of *swim.'),
(35919, 's', '\r\nSwami', ' n. (pl. -s) hindu male religious teacher. [hindi svami]'),
(35920, 's', '\r\nSwamp', ' â€”n. (area of) waterlogged ground. â€”v. 1 overwhelm, flood, or soak with water. 2 overwhelm or make invisible etc. With an excess or large amount of something.  swampy adj. (-ier, -iest). [origin uncertain]'),
(35921, 's', '\r\nSwan', ' â€”n. Large usu. White water-bird with a long flexible neck. â€”v. (-nn-) (usu. Foll. By about, off, etc.) Colloq. Move or go aimlessly, casually, or with a superior air. [old english]'),
(35922, 's', '\r\nSwank', ' colloq. â€”n. Ostentation, swagger. â€”v. Show off.  swanky adj (-ier, -iest). [origin uncertain]'),
(35923, 's', '\r\nSwansong', ' n. Person''s last work or act before death or retirement etc.'),
(35924, 's', '\r\nSwap', ' (also swop) â€”v. (-pp-) exchange or barter. â€”n. 1 act of swapping. 2 thing for swapping or swapped. [originally = â€˜hitâ€™, imitative]'),
(35925, 's', '\r\nSwapo', ' abbr. (also swapo) south west africa people''s organization.'),
(35926, 's', '\r\nSward', ' n. Literary expanse of turf. [old english, = skin]'),
(35927, 's', '\r\nSwarf', ' n. Fine chips or filings of stone, metal, etc. [old norse]'),
(35928, 's', '\r\nSwarm1', ' â€”n. 1 cluster of bees leaving the hive with the queen to establish a new colony. 2 large cluster of insects, birds, or people. 3 (in pl.; foll. By of) great numbers. â€”v. 1 move in or form a swarm. 2 (foll. By with) (of a place) be overrun, crowded, or infested with. [old english]'),
(35929, 's', '\r\nSwarm2', ' v. (foll. By up) climb (a rope or tree etc.) By clinging with the hands and knees etc. [origin unknown]'),
(35930, 's', '\r\nSwarthy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) dark, dark-complexioned. [obsolete swarty from swart black, from old english]'),
(35931, 's', '\r\nSwashbuckler', ' n. Swaggering adventurer.  swashbuckling adj. & n. [swash strike noisily, *buckler]'),
(35932, 's', '\r\nSwastika', ' n. 1 ancient symbol formed by an equal-armed cross with each arm continued at a right angle. 2 this with clockwise continuations as the symbol of nazi germany. [sanskrit]'),
(35933, 's', '\r\nSwat', ' â€”v. (-tt-) 1 crush (a fly etc.) With a sharp blow. 2 hit hard and abruptly. â€”n. Swatting blow. [dial. Var. Of *squat]'),
(35934, 's', '\r\nSwatch', ' n. 1 sample, esp. Of cloth. 2 collection of samples. [origin unknown]'),
(35935, 's', '\r\nSwath', ' n. (also swathe) (pl. -s) 1 ridge of cut grass or corn etc. 2 space left clear by a mower etc. 3 broad strip. [old english]'),
(35936, 's', '\r\nSwathe', ' â€”v. (-thing) bind or wrap in bandages or garments etc. â€”n. Bandage or wrapping. [old english]'),
(35937, 's', '\r\nSway', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) lean or move unsteadily from side to side. 2 oscillate; waver. 3 a control the motion or direction of. B influence; rule over. â€”n. 1 rule, influence, or government (hold sway). 2 swaying motion. [origin uncertain]'),
(35938, 's', '\r\nSwear', ' â€”v. (past swore; past part. Sworn) 1 a (often foll. By to + infin. Or that + clause) state or promise solemnly or on oath. B (cause to) take (an oath) (swore them to secrecy). 2 colloq. Insist (swore he was fit). 3 (often foll. By at) use profane or obscene language. 4 (foll. By by) a appeal to as a witness in taking an oath (swear by almighty god). B colloq. Have great confidence in (swears by yoga). 5 (foll. By to; usu. In neg.) Say certainly (could not swear to it). â€”n. Spell of swearing.  swear blind colloq. Affirm emphatically. Swear in induct into office etc. With an oath. Swear off colloq. Promise to abstain from (drink etc.). [old english]'),
(35939, 's', '\r\nSwear-word', ' n. Profane or indecent word.'),
(35940, 's', '\r\nSweat', ' â€”n. 1 moisture exuded through the pores, esp. From heat or nervousness. 2 state or period of sweating. 3 colloq. State of anxiety (in a sweat). 4 colloq. A drudgery, effort. B laborious task.'),
(35941, 's', '\r\nSweat-band', ' n. Band fitted inside a hat or worn round a wrist etc. To absorb sweat.'),
(35942, 's', '\r\nSweater', ' n. Jersey or pullover.'),
(35943, 's', '\r\nSweatshirt', ' n. Sleeved cotton sweater.'),
(35944, 's', '\r\nSweatshop', ' n. Factory where sweated labour is used.'),
(35945, 's', '\r\nSwede', ' n. 1 a native or national of sweden. B person of swedish descent. 2 (swede) large yellow-fleshed turnip orig. From sweden. [low german or dutch]'),
(35946, 's', '\r\nSwedish', ' â€”adj. Of sweden, its people, or language. â€”n. Language of sweden.'),
(35947, 's', '\r\nSweep', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Swept) 1 clean or clear (a room or area etc.) (as) with a broom. 2 (often foll. By up) clean a room etc. In this way. 3 (often foll. By up) collect or remove (dirt etc.) By sweeping. 4 (foll. By aside, away, etc.) A push (as) with a broom. B dismiss abruptly. 5 (foll. By along, down, etc.) Carry or drive along with force. 6 (foll. By off, away, etc.) Remove or clear forcefully. 7 traverse swiftly or lightly. 8 impart a sweeping motion to. 9 swiftly cover or affect. 10 a glide swiftly; speed along. B go majestically. 11 (of landscape etc.) Be rolling or spacious. â€”n. 1 act or motion of sweeping. 2 curve in the road, sweeping line of a hill, etc. 3 range or scope. 4 = *chimney-sweep. 5 sortie by aircraft. 6 colloq. = *sweepstake.  make a clean sweep of 1 completely abolish or expel. 2 win all the prizes etc. In (a competition etc.). Sweep away abolish swiftly. Sweep the board 1 win all the money at stake. 2 win all possible prizes etc. Sweep under the carpet see *carpet. [old english]'),
(35948, 's', '\r\nSweeper', ' n. 1 person who cleans by sweeping. 2 manual device for sweeping carpets etc. 3 football defensive player positioned close to the goalkeeper.'),
(35949, 's', '\r\nSweeping', ' â€”adj. 1 wide in range or effect (sweeping changes). 2 generalized, arbitrary (sweeping statement). â€”n. (in pl.) Dirt etc. Collected by sweeping.'),
(35950, 's', '\r\nSweepstake', ' n. 1 form of gambling in which all stakes are pooled and paid to the winners. 2 race with betting of this kind. 3 prize(s) won in a sweepstake.'),
(35951, 's', '\r\nSweet', ' â€”adj. 1 tasting of sugar. 2 smelling pleasant like roses or perfume etc.; fragrant. 3 (of sound etc.) Melodious or harmonious. 4 fresh; not salt, sour, or bitter. 5 gratifying or attractive. 6 amiable, pleasant. 7 colloq. Pretty, charming. 8 (foll. By on) colloq. Fond of; in love with. â€”n. 1 small shaped piece of sweet substance, usu. Made with sugar or chocolate. 2 sweet dish or course of a meal.  sweetish adj. Sweetly adv. [old english]'),
(35952, 's', '\r\nSweet-and-sour', ' attrib. Adj. Cooked in a sauce containing sugar and vinegar or lemon etc.'),
(35953, 's', '\r\nSweetbread', ' n. Pancreas or thymus of an animal, esp. As food.'),
(35954, 's', '\r\nSweet-brier', ' n. Wild rose with small fragrant leaves.'),
(35955, 's', '\r\nSweetcorn', ' n. Sweet-flavoured maize kernels.'),
(35956, 's', '\r\nSweeten', ' v. 1 make or become sweet or sweeter. 2 make agreeable or less painful.  sweetening n.'),
(35957, 's', '\r\nSweetener', ' n. 1 substance used to sweeten food or drink. 2 colloq. Bribe or inducement.'),
(35958, 's', '\r\nSweetheart', ' n. 1 lover or darling. 2 term of endearment.'),
(35959, 's', '\r\nSweetie', ' n. Colloq. 1 = *sweet 1. 2 sweetheart.'),
(35960, 's', '\r\nSweetmeal', ' n. Sweetened wholemeal.'),
(35961, 's', '\r\nSweetmeat', ' n. 1 = *sweet 1. 2 small fancy cake.'),
(35962, 's', '\r\nSweetness', ' n. Being sweet; fragrance.  sweetness and light (esp. Uncharacteristic) mildness and reason.'),
(35963, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'pea  n. Climbing plant with fragrant flowers.'),
(35964, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'pepper  n. Mild pepper.'),
(35965, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'potato  n. 1 tropical climbing plant with sweet tuberous roots used for food. 2 root of this.'),
(35966, 's', '\r\nSweetshop', ' n. Confectioner''s shop.'),
(35967, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'talk  colloq. â€”n. Flattery, blandishment. â€”v. (sweet-talk) flatter in order to persuade.'),
(35968, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'tooth  n. Liking for sweet-tasting things.'),
(35969, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'william  n. Cultivated plant with clusters of vivid fragrant flowers.'),
(35970, 's', '\r\nSwell', ' â€”v. (past part. Swollen or swelled) 1 (cause to) grow bigger, louder, or more intense. 2 (often foll. By up) rise or raise up from the surrounding surface. 3 (foll. By out) bulge. 4 (of the heart etc.) Feel full of joy, pride, relief, etc. 5 (foll. By with) be hardly able to restrain (pride etc.). â€”n. 1 act or state of swelling. 2 heaving of the sea with unbreaking waves. 3 a crescendo. B mechanism in an organ etc. For producing a crescendo or diminuendo. 4 colloq. Dandy. 5 protuberance. â€”adj. Colloq. 1 esp. Us fine, excellent. 2 smart, fashionable.  have (or get) a swelled (or swollen) head be (or become) conceited. [old english]'),
(35971, 's', '\r\nSwelling', ' n. Abnormal bodily protuberance.'),
(35972, 's', '\r\nSwelter', ' â€”v. Be uncomfortably hot. â€”n. Sweltering condition. [old english]'),
(35973, 's', '\r\nSwept', ' past and past part. Of *sweep.'),
(35974, 's', '\r\nSwerve', ' â€”v. (-ving) (cause to) change direction, esp. Abruptly. â€”n. Swerving movement. [old english, = scour]'),
(35975, 's', '\r\nSwift', ' â€”adj. 1 quick, rapid. 2 prompt. â€”n. Swift-flying migratory bird with long wings.  swiftly adv. Swiftness n. [old english]'),
(35976, 's', '\r\nSwig', ' â€”v. (-gg-) colloq. Drink in large draughts. â€”n. Swallow of drink, esp. Large. [origin unknown]'),
(35977, 's', '\r\nSwill', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By out) rinse or flush. 2 drink greedily. â€”n. 1 act of rinsing. 2 mainly liquid refuse as pig-food. [old english]'),
(35978, 's', '\r\nSwim', ' â€”v. (-mm-; past swam; past part. Swum) 1 propel the body through water with limbs, fins, or tail. 2 traverse (a stretch of water or distance) by swimming. 3 perform (a stroke) by swimming. 4 float on a liquid. 5 appear to undulate, reel, or whirl. 6 feel dizzy (my head swam). 7 (foll. By in, with) be flooded. â€”n. Period or act of swimming.  in the swim colloq. Involved in or aware of what is going on.  swimmer n. [old english]'),
(35979, 's', '\r\nSwimming-bath', ' n. (also swimming-pool) artificial pool for swimming.'),
(35980, 's', '\r\nSwimming-costume', ' n. = *bathing-costume.'),
(35981, 's', '\r\nSwimmingly', ' adv. Colloq. Smoothly, without impediment.'),
(35982, 's', '\r\nSwimsuit', ' n. Swimming-costume, esp. One-piece for women and girls.'),
(35983, 's', '\r\nSwimwear', ' n. Clothing for swimming in.'),
(35984, 's', '\r\nSwindle', ' â€”v. (-ling) (often foll. By out of) 1 cheat of money etc. 2 cheat a person of (money etc.) (swindled Â£200 out of him). â€”n. 1 act of swindling. 2 fraudulent person or thing.  swindler n. [back-formation from swindler from german]'),
(35985, 's', '\r\nSwine', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 formal or us pig. 2 colloq. (pl. Same or -s) a contemptible person. B unpleasant or difficult thing.  swinish adj. [old english]'),
(35986, 's', '\r\nSwing', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Swung) 1 a (cause to) move with a to-and-fro or curving motion, as of an object attached at one end and hanging free at the other; sway. B hang so as to be free to swing. 2 oscillate or revolve. 3 move by gripping something and leaping etc. (swung from tree to tree). 4 walk with a swing. 5 (foll. By round) move to face the opposite direction. 6 change one''s opinion or mood. 7 (foll. By at) attempt to hit. 8  (also swing it) play (music) with a swing rhythm. 9 colloq. (of a party etc.) Be lively etc. 10 have a decisive influence on (voting etc.). 11 colloq. Achieve, manage. 12 colloq. Be executed by hanging. â€”n. 1 act, motion, or extent of swinging. 2 swinging or smooth gait, rhythm, or action. 3 a seat slung by ropes etc. For swinging on or in. B period of swinging on this. 4 a jazz or dance music with an easy flowing rhythm. B rhythmic feeling or drive of this. 5 discernible change, esp. In votes or points scored etc.  swings and roundabouts situation affording equal gain and loss.  swinger n. [old english]'),
(35987, 's', '\r\nSwing-boat', ' n. Boat-shaped swing at fairs.'),
(35988, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'pepper  n. Mild pepper.'),
(35989, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'potato  n. 1 tropical climbing plant with sweet tuberous roots used for food. 2 root of this.'),
(35990, 's', '\r\nSweetshop', ' n. Confectioner''s shop.'),
(35991, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'talk  colloq. â€”n. Flattery, blandishment. â€”v. (sweet-talk) flatter in order to persuade.'),
(35992, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'tooth  n. Liking for sweet-tasting things.'),
(35993, 's', '\r\nSweet', 'william  n. Cultivated plant with clusters of vivid fragrant flowers.'),
