(36005, 's', '\r\nSwimmingly', ' adv. Colloq. Smoothly, without impediment.'),
(36006, 's', '\r\nSwimsuit', ' n. Swimming-costume, esp. One-piece for women and girls.'),
(36007, 's', '\r\nSwimwear', ' n. Clothing for swimming in.'),
(36008, 's', '\r\nSwindle', ' â€”v. (-ling) (often foll. By out of) 1 cheat of money etc. 2 cheat a person of (money etc.) (swindled Â£200 out of him). â€”n. 1 act of swindling. 2 fraudulent person or thing.  swindler n. [back-formation from swindler from german]'),
(36009, 's', '\r\nSwine', ' n. (pl. Same) 1 formal or us pig. 2 colloq. (pl. Same or -s) a contemptible person. B unpleasant or difficult thing.  swinish adj. [old english]'),
(36010, 's', '\r\nSwing', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Swung) 1 a (cause to) move with a to-and-fro or curving motion, as of an object attached at one end and hanging free at the other; sway. B hang so as to be free to swing. 2 oscillate or revolve. 3 move by gripping something and leaping etc. (swung from tree to tree). 4 walk with a swing. 5 (foll. By round) move to face the opposite direction. 6 change one''s opinion or mood. 7 (foll. By at) attempt to hit. 8  (also swing it) play (music) with a swing rhythm. 9 colloq. (of a party etc.) Be lively etc. 10 have a decisive influence on (voting etc.). 11 colloq. Achieve, manage. 12 colloq. Be executed by hanging. â€”n. 1 act, motion, or extent of swinging. 2 swinging or smooth gait, rhythm, or action. 3 a seat slung by ropes etc. For swinging on or in. B period of swinging on this. 4 a jazz or dance music with an easy flowing rhythm. B rhythmic feeling or drive of this. 5 discernible change, esp. In votes or points scored etc.  swings and roundabouts situation affording equal gain and loss.  swinger n. [old english]'),
(36011, 's', '\r\nSwing-boat', ' n. Boat-shaped swing at fairs.'),
(36012, 's', '\r\nSwing-bridge', ' n. Bridge that can be swung aside to let ships pass.'),
(36013, 's', '\r\nSwing-door', ' n. Self-closing door opening both ways.'),
(36014, 's', '\r\nSwingeing', ' adj. 1 (of a blow) forcible. 2 huge or far-reaching (swingeing economies). [archaic swinge strike hard, from old english]'),
(36015, 's', '\r\nSwing-wing', ' n. Aircraft wing that can move from a right-angled to a swept-back position.'),
(36016, 's', '\r\nSwipe', ' colloq. â€”v. (-ping) 1 (often foll. By at) hit hard and recklessly. 2 steal. â€”n. Reckless hard hit or attempted hit. [perhaps var. Of *sweep]'),
(36017, 's', '\r\nSwirl', ' â€”v. Move, flow, or carry along with a whirling motion. â€”n. 1 swirling motion. 2 twist or curl.  swirly adj. [perhaps from low german or dutch]'),
(36018, 's', '\r\nSwish', ' â€”v. 1 swing (a thing) audibly through the air, grass, etc. 2 move with or make a swishing sound. â€”n. Swishing action or sound. â€”adj. Colloq. Smart, fashionable. [imitative]'),
(36019, 's', '\r\nSwiss', ' â€”adj. Of switzerland or its people. â€”n. (pl. Same) 1 native or national of switzerland. 2 person of swiss descent. [french suisse]'),
(36020, 's', '\r\nSwiss', 'roll  n. Cylindrical sponge cake with a jam etc. Filling.'),
(36021, 's', '\r\nSwitch', ' â€”n. 1 device for completing and breaking an electric circuit. 2 a transfer, change-over, or deviation. B exchange. 3 flexible shoot cut from a tree. 4 light tapering rod. 5 us railway points. â€”v. 1 (foll. By on, off) turn (an electrical device) on or off. 2 change or transfer. 3 exchange. 4 whip or flick with a switch.  switch off colloq. Cease to pay attention. [low german]'),
(36022, 's', '\r\nSwitchback', ' n. 1 ride at a fair etc., with extremely steep ascents and descents. 2 (often attrib.) Such a railway or road.'),
(36023, 's', '\r\nSwitchboard', ' n. Apparatus for making connections between electric circuits, esp. In telephony.'),
(36024, 's', '\r\nSwitched-on', ' adj. Colloq. 1 up to date; aware of what is going on. 2 excited; under the influence of drugs.'),
(36025, 's', '\r\nSwivel', ' â€”n. Coupling between two parts enabling one to revolve without turning the other. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) turn on or as on a swivel. [old english]'),
(36026, 's', '\r\nSwivel', 'chair  n. Chair with a revolving seat.'),
(36027, 's', '\r\nSwizz', ' n. (also swiz) colloq. 1 something unfair or disappointing. 2 swindle. [origin unknown]'),
(36028, 's', '\r\nSwizzle', ' n. 1 colloq. Frothy mixed alcoholic drink esp. Of rum or gin and bitters. 2 slang = *swizz. [origin unknown]'),
(36029, 's', '\r\nSwizzle-stick', ' n. Stick used for frothing or flattening drinks.'),
(36030, 's', '\r\nSwollen', ' past part. Of *swell.'),
(36031, 's', '\r\nSwoon', ' v. & n. Literary faint. [old english]'),
(36032, 's', '\r\nSwoop', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By down) descend rapidly like a bird of prey. 2 (often foll. By on) make a sudden attack. â€”n. Swooping movement or action. [old english]'),
(36033, 's', '\r\nSwop', ' var. Of *swap.'),
(36034, 's', '\r\nSword', ' n. 1 weapon with a long blade and hilt with a handguard. 2 (prec. By the) a war. B military power.  put to the sword kill. [old english]'),
(36035, 's', '\r\nSword', 'dance  n. Dance with the brandishing of swords or with swords laid on the ground.'),
(36036, 's', '\r\nSwordfish', ' n. (pl. Same or -es) large marine fish with swordlike upper jaw.'),
(36037, 's', '\r\nSword', 'of damocles  n. An immediate danger. [from damokles, who had a sword hung by a hair over him]'),
(36038, 's', '\r\nSwordplay', ' n. 1 fencing. 2 repartee; lively argument.'),
(36039, 's', '\r\nSwordsman', ' n. Person of (usu. Specified) skill with a sword.  swordsmanship n.'),
(36040, 's', '\r\nSwordstick', ' n. Hollow walking-stick containing a blade that can be used as a sword.'),
(36041, 's', '\r\nSwore', ' past of *swear.'),
(36042, 's', '\r\nSworn', ' past part. Of *swear. â€”attrib. Adj. Bound (as) by an oath (sworn enemies).'),
(36043, 's', '\r\nSwot', ' colloq. â€”v. (-tt-) 1 study hard. 2 (usu. Foll. By up, up on) study (a subject) hard or hurriedly. â€”n. Usu. Derog. Person who swots. [dial. Var. Of *sweat]'),
(36044, 's', '\r\nSwum', ' past part. Of *swim.'),
(36045, 's', '\r\nSwung', ' past and past part. Of *swing.'),
(36046, 's', '\r\nSybarite', ' n. Self-indulgent or voluptuous person.  sybaritic adj. [sybaris, ancient city in s. Italy]'),
(36047, 's', '\r\nSycamore', ' n. 1 large maple or its wood. 2 us plane-tree or its wood. [greek sukomoros]'),
(36048, 's', '\r\nSycophant', ' n. Flatterer; toady.  sycophancy n. Sycophantic adj. [greek sukophantes]'),
(36049, 's', '\r\nSyl-', ' prefix assim. Form of *syn- before l.'),
(36050, 's', '\r\nSyllabary', ' n. (pl. -ies) list of characters representing syllables. [related to *syllable]'),
(36051, 's', '\r\nSyllabic', ' adj. Of or in syllables.  syllabically adv.'),
(36052, 's', '\r\nSyllable', ' n. 1 unit of pronunciation forming the whole or part of a word and usu. Having one vowel sound often with consonant(s) before or after (e.g. Water has two, inferno three). 2 character(s) representing a syllable. 3 the least amount of speech or writing.  in words of one syllable plainly, bluntly. [greek sullabe]'),
(36053, 's', '\r\nSyllabub', ' n. (also sillabub) dessert of flavoured, sweetened, and whipped cream or milk. [origin unknown]'),
(36054, 's', '\r\nSyllabus', ' n. (pl. -buses or -bi) programme or outline of a course of study, teaching, etc. [misreading of greek sittuba label]'),
(36055, 's', '\r\nSyllepsis', ' n. (pl. Syllepses) figure of speech in which a word is applied to two others in different senses (e.g. Caught the train and a cold) or to two others of which it grammatically suits one only (e.g. Neither you nor he knows) (cf. *zeugma). [greek: related to *syllable]'),
(36056, 's', '\r\nSyllogism', ' n. Reasoning in which a conclusion is drawn from two given or assumed propositions.  syllogistic adj. [greek logos reason]'),
(36057, 's', '\r\nSylph', ' n. 1 elemental spirit of the air. 2 slender graceful woman or girl.  sylphlike adj. [latin]'),
(36058, 's', '\r\nSylvan', ' adj. (also silvan) 1 a of the woods. B having woods. 2 rural. [latin silva a wood]'),
(36059, 's', '\r\nSylviculture', ' var. Of *silviculture.'),
(36060, 's', '\r\nSym-', ' prefix assim. Form of *syn- before b, m, p.'),
(36061, 's', '\r\nSymbiosis', ' n. (pl. -bioses) 1 interaction between two different organisms living in close physical association, usu. To the advantage of both. 2 mutually advantageous association between persons.  symbiotic adj. [greek, = living together]'),
(36062, 's', '\r\nSymbol', ' n. 1 thing regarded as typifying or representing something (white is a symbol of purity). 2 mark, sign, etc. Representing an object, idea, function, or process; logo.  symbolic adj. Symbolically adv. [greek sumbolon]'),
(36063, 's', '\r\nSymbolism', ' n. 1 a use of symbols. B symbols collectively. 2 artistic and poetic movement or style using symbols to express ideas, emotions, etc.  symbolist n.'),
(36064, 's', '\r\nSymbolize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 be a symbol of. 2 represent by symbols. [french: related to *symbol]'),
(36065, 's', '\r\nSymmetry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a correct proportion of parts. B beauty resulting from this. 2 a structure allowing an object to be divided into parts of an equal shape and size. B possession of such a structure. 3 repetition of exactly similar parts facing each other or a centre.  symmetrical adj. Symmetrically adv. [greek summetria]'),
(36066, 's', '\r\nSympathetic', ' adj. 1 of or expressing sympathy. 2 pleasant, likeable. 3 (foll. By to) favouring (a proposal etc.).  sympathetically adv.'),
(36067, 's', '\r\nSympathize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) (often foll. By with) 1 feel or express sympathy. 2 agree.  sympathizer n.'),
(36068, 's', '\r\nSympathy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a sharing of another''s feelings. B capacity for this. 2 a (often foll. By with) sharing or tendency to share (with a person etc.) In an emotion, sensation, or condition. B (in sing. Or pl.) Compassion or commiseration; condolences. 3 (often foll. By for) approval. 4 (in sing. Or pl.; often foll. By with) agreement (with a person etc.) In opinion or desire.  in sympathy (often foll. By with) having, showing, or resulting from sympathy. [greek, = fellow-feeling]'),
(36069, 's', '\r\nSymphony', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 large-scale composition for full orchestra in several movements. 2 instrumental interlude in a large-scale vocal work. 3 = *symphony orchestra.  symphonic adj. [from *syn-, greek phone sound]'),
(36070, 's', '\r\nSymphony', 'orchestra  n. Large orchestra suitable for playing symphonies etc.'),
(36071, 's', '\r\nSymposium', ' n. (pl. -sia) 1 conference, or collection of essays, on a particular subject. 2 philosophical or other friendly discussion. [greek sumpotes fellow-drinker]'),
(36072, 's', '\r\nSymptom', ' n. 1 physical or mental sign of disease. 2 sign of the existence of something.  symptomatic adj. [greek pipto fall]'),
(36073, 's', '\r\nSyn-', ' prefix with, together, alike. [greek sun with]'),
(36074, 's', '\r\nSyn-', ' prefix with, together, alike. [greek sun with]'),
(36075, 's', '\r\nSynagogue', ' n. 1 building for jewish religious observance and instruction. 2 jewish congregation. [greek, = assembly]'),
(36076, 's', '\r\nSynapse', ' n. Anat. Junction of two nerve-cells. [greek hapto join]'),
(36077, 's', '\r\nSync', ' (also synch) colloq. â€”n. Synchronization. â€”v. Synchronize.  in (or out of) sync (often foll. By with) according or agreeing well (or badly). [abbreviation]'),
(36078, 's', '\r\nSynchromesh', ' n. (often attrib.) System of gear-changing, esp. In vehicles, in which the gearwheels revolve at the same speed during engagement. [abbreviation of synchronized mesh]'),
(36079, 's', '\r\nSynchronic', ' adj. Concerned with a subject as it exists at one point in time.  synchronically adv. [from *syn-, greek khronos time]'),
(36080, 's', '\r\nSynchronism', ' n. 1 being or treating as synchronic or synchronous. 2 process of synchronizing sound and picture.'),
(36081, 's', '\r\nSynchronize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 (often foll. By with) make or be synchronous (with). 2 make the sound and picture of (a film etc.) Coincide. 3 cause (clocks etc.) To show the same time.  synchronization n.'),
(36082, 's', '\r\nUsage', ' synchronize should not be used in standard english to mean â€˜coordinateâ€™ or â€˜combineâ€™.'),
(36083, 's', '\r\nSynchronous', ' adj. (often foll. By with) existing or occurring at the same time.'),
(36084, 's', '\r\nSyncopate', ' v. (-ting) 1 displace the beats or accents in (music). 2 shorten (a word) by dropping interior letters.  syncopation n. [latin: related to *syncope]'),
(36085, 's', '\r\nSyncope', ' n. 1 gram. Syncopation. 2 fainting through a fall in blood pressure. [greek sunkope cutting off]'),
(36086, 's', '\r\nSyncretize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) attempt, esp. Inconsistently, to unify or reconcile differing schools of thought.  syncretic adj. Syncretism n. [greek]'),
(36087, 's', '\r\nSyndic', ' n. Any of various university or government officials. [greek sundikos, = advocate]'),
(36088, 's', '\r\nSyndicalism', ' n. Hist. Movement for transferring industrial ownership and control to workers'' unions.  syndicalist n. [french: related to *syndic]'),
(36089, 's', '\r\nSyndicate', ' â€”n. 1 combination of individuals or businesses to promote a common interest. 2 agency supplying material simultaneously to a number of newspapers etc. 3 group of people who gamble, organize crime, etc. 4 committee of syndics. â€”v. (-ting) 1 form into a syndicate. 2 publish (material) through a syndicate.  syndication n. [latin: related to *syndic]'),
(36090, 's', '\r\nSyndrome', ' n. 1 group of concurrent symptoms of a disease. 2 characteristic combination of opinions, emotions, behaviour, etc. [greek sundrome running together]'),
(36091, 's', '\r\nSynecdoche', ' n. Figure of speech in which a part is made to represent the whole or vice versa (e.g. New faces at the club; england lost to india). [greek, = taking together]'),
(36092, 's', '\r\nSynod', ' n. Church council of delegated clergy and sometimes laity. [greek, = meeting]'),
(36093, 's', '\r\nSynonym', ' n. Word or phrase that means the same as another (e.g. Shut and close). [greek onoma name]'),
(36094, 's', '\r\nSynonymous', ' adj. (often foll. By with) 1 having the same meaning. 2 suggestive of; associated with (his name is synonymous with terror).'),
(36095, 's', '\r\nSynopsis', ' n. (pl. Synopses) summary or outline. [greek opsis view]'),
(36096, 's', '\r\nSynoptic', ' adj. Of or giving a synopsis. [greek: related to *synopsis]'),
(36097, 's', '\r\nSynoptic', 'gospels  n.pl. Gospels of matthew, mark, and luke.'),
(36098, 's', '\r\nSynovia', ' n. Physiol. Viscous fluid lubricating joints etc.  synovial adj. [medieval latin]'),
(36099, 's', '\r\nSyntax', ' n. 1 grammatical arrangement of words. 2 rules or analysis of this.  syntactic adj. Syntactically adv. [greek, = arrangement]'),
(36100, 's', '\r\nSynth', ' n. Colloq. = *synthesizer.'),
(36101, 's', '\r\nSynthesis', ' n. (pl. -theses) 1 a combining of elements into a whole. B result of this. 2 chem. Artificial production of compounds from their constituents as distinct from extraction from plants etc. [greek, = placing together]'),
(36102, 's', '\r\nSynthesize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make a synthesis of.'),
(36103, 's', '\r\nSynthesizer', ' n. Electronic, usu. Keyboard, instrument producing a wide variety of sounds.'),
(36104, 's', '\r\nSynthetic', ' â€”adj. 1 made by chemical synthesis, esp. To imitate a natural product. 2 affected, insincere. â€”n. Synthetic substance.  synthetically adv.'),
(36105, 's', '\r\nSyphilis', ' n. Contagious venereal disease.  syphilitic adj. [syphilus, name of a character in a poem of 1530]'),
(36106, 's', '\r\nSyriac', ' â€”n. Language of ancient syria, western aramaic. â€”adj. Of or in syriac.'),
(36107, 's', '\r\nSyrian', ' â€”n. 1 native or national of syria. 2 person of syrian descent. â€”adj. Of syria.'),
(36108, 's', '\r\nSyringa', ' n. 1 = *mock orange. 2 lilac or similar related plant. [related to *syringe]'),
(36109, 's', '\r\nSyringe', ' â€”n. Device for sucking in and ejecting liquid in a fine stream. â€”v. (-ging) sluice or spray with a syringe. [greek surigx pipe]'),
(36110, 's', '\r\nSyrup', ' n. (us sirup) 1 a sweet sauce of sugar dissolved in boiling water. B similar fluid as a drink, medicine, etc. 2 condensed sugar-cane juice; molasses, treacle. 3 excessive sweetness of manner or style.  syrupy adj. [arabic sharab]'),
(36111, 's', '\r\nSystem', ' n. 1 complex whole; set of connected things or parts; organized body of things. 2 a set of organs in the body with a common structure or function. B human or animal body as a whole. 3 method; scheme of action, procedure, or classification. 4 orderliness. 5 (prec. By the) prevailing political or social order, esp. Regarded as oppressive.  get a thing out of one''s system colloq. Get rid of a preoccupation or anxiety. [greek sustema -mat-]'),
(36112, 's', '\r\nSystematic', ' adj. 1 methodical; according to a system. 2 regular, deliberate.  systematically adv.'),
(36113, 's', '\r\nSystematize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) make systematic.  systematization n.'),
(36114, 's', '\r\nSystemic', ' adj. 1 physiol. Of the whole body. 2 (of an insecticide etc.) Entering the plant via the roots or shoots and freely transported within its tissues.  systemically adv.'),
(36115, 's', '\r\nSystems', 'analysis  n. Analysis of a complex process etc. In order to improve its efficiency esp. By using a computer.  systems analyst n.'),
(36116, 't', 'T1', ' n. (also t) (pl. Ts or t''s) 1 twentieth letter of the alphabet. 2 t-shaped thing (esp. Attrib.: t-joint).  to a t exactly; to a nicety.'),
(36117, 't', '\r\nT2', ' symb. Tritium.'),
(36118, 't', '\r\nT.', ' Abbr. (also t) 1 ton(s). 2 tonne(s).'),
(36119, 't', '\r\nTa', ' abbr. Territorial army.'),
(36120, 't', '\r\nTa', ' symb. Tantalum.'),
(36121, 't', '\r\nTa', ' int. Colloq. Thank you. [infantile form]'),
(36122, 't', '\r\nTab1', ' â€”n. 1 small flap or strip of material attached for grasping, fastening, or hanging up, or for identification. 2 us colloq. Bill (picked up the tab). 3  distinguishing mark on a staff officer''s collar. â€”v. (-bb-) provide with a tab or tabs.  keep tabs (or a tab) on colloq. 1 keep account of. 2 have under observation or in check. [probably dial.]'),
(36123, 't', '\r\nTab2', ' n. = *tabulator 2. [abbreviation]'),
(36124, 't', '\r\nTabard', ' n. 1 herald''s official coat emblazoned with royal arms. 2 woman''s or girl''s sleeveless jerkin. 3 hist. Knight''s short emblazoned garment worn over armour. [french]'),
(36125, 't', '\r\nTabasco', ' n. 1 pungent pepper. 2 (tabasco) propr. Sauce made from this. [tabasco in mexico]'),
(36126, 't', '\r\nTabby', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1  grey or brownish cat with dark stripes. 2 a kind of watered silk. [french from arabic]'),
(36127, 't', '\r\nTabernacle', ' n. 1 hist. Tent used as a sanctuary by the israelites during the exodus. 2 niche or receptacle, esp. For the eucharistic elements. 3 nonconformist meeting-house. [latin: related to *tavern]'),
(36128, 't', '\r\nTabla', ' n. Pair of small drums played with the hands, esp. In indian music. [arabic, = drum]'),
(36129, 't', '\r\nTable', ' â€”n. 1 flat surface on a leg or legs, used for eating, working at, etc. 2 a food provided in a household (keeps a good table). B group seated for dinner etc. 3 a set of facts or figures in columns etc. (table of contents). B matter contained in this. C = *multiplication table. â€”v. (-ling) 1 bring forward for discussion etc. At a meeting. 2 esp. Us postpone consideration of (a matter).  at table taking a meal at a table. On the table offered for discussion. Turn the tables (often foll. By on) reverse circumstances to one''s advantage (against). Under the table colloq. 1 very drunk.'),
(36130, 't', '\r\nTableau', ' n. (pl. -x) 1 picturesque presentation. 2 group of silent motionless people representing a scene on stage. [french, = picture, diminutive of *table]'),
(36131, 't', '\r\nTablecloth', ' n. Cloth spread over a table, esp. For meals.'),
(36132, 't', '\r\nTable', 'd''hÃ´te  n. (often attrib.) Meal from a set menu at a fixed price. [french, = host''s table]'),
(36133, 't', '\r\nTableland', ' n. Elevated plateau.'),
(36134, 't', '\r\nTable', 'licence  n. Licence to serve alcoholic drinks with meals only.'),
(36135, 't', '\r\nTable', 'linen  n. Tablecloths, napkins, etc.'),
(36136, 't', '\r\nTablespoon', ' n. 1 large spoon for serving food. 2 amount held by this.  tablespoonful n. (pl. -s).'),
(36137, 't', '\r\nTablet', ' n. 1 small solid dose of a medicine etc. 2 bar of soap etc. 3 flat slab of esp. Stone, usu. Inscribed. 4 us writing-pad. [latin diminutive: related to *table]'),
(36138, 't', '\r\nTable', 'talk  n. Informal talk at table.'),
(36139, 't', '\r\nTable', 'tennis  n. Indoor ball game played with small bats on a table divided by a net.'),
(36140, 't', '\r\nTabletop', ' n. Surface of a table.'),
(36141, 't', '\r\nTableware', ' n. Dishes, plates, etc., for meals.'),
(36142, 't', '\r\nTable', 'wine  n. Wine of ordinary quality.'),
(36143, 't', '\r\nTabloid', ' n. Small-sized, often popular or sensational, newspaper. [from *tablet]'),
(36144, 't', '\r\nTaboo', ' (also tabu) â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 ritual isolation of a person or thing as sacred or accursed. 2 prohibition imposed by social custom. â€”adj. Avoided or prohibited, esp. By social custom (taboo words). â€”v. (-oos, -ooed or -us, -ued) 1 put under taboo. 2 exclude or prohibit, esp. Socially. [tongan]'),
(36145, 't', '\r\nTabor', ' n. Hist. Small drum, esp. Used to accompany a pipe. [french]'),
(36146, 't', '\r\nTabu', ' var. Of *taboo.'),
(36147, 't', '\r\nTabular', ' adj. Of or arranged in tables or lists. [latin: related to *table]'),
(36148, 't', '\r\nTabulate', ' v. (-ting) arrange (figures or facts) in tabular form.  tabulation n.'),
(36149, 't', '\r\nTabulator', ' n. 1 person or thing that tabulates. 2 device on a typewriter etc. For advancing to a sequence of set positions in tabular work.'),
(36150, 't', '\r\nTacho', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. = *tachometer. [abbreviation]'),
(36151, 't', '\r\nTachograph', ' n. Device in a vehicle recording speed and travel time. [greek takhos speed]'),
(36152, 't', '\r\nTachometer', ' n. Instrument measuring velocity or rate of rotation of a shaft (esp. In a vehicle).'),
(36153, 't', '\r\nTacit', ' adj. Understood or implied without being stated (tacit consent).  tacitly adv. [latin taceo be silent]'),
(36154, 't', '\r\nTaciturn', ' adj. Saying little; uncommunicative.  taciturnity n. [latin: related to *tacit]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(36155, 't', '\r\nTack1', ' â€”n. 1 small sharp broad-headed nail. 2 us drawing-pin. 3 long stitch for joining fabrics etc. Lightly or temporarily together. 4 (in sailing) direction, or temporary change of direction, esp. Taking advantage of a side wind (starboard tack). 5 course of action or policy (change tack). 6 sticky condition of varnish etc. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By down etc.) Fasten with tacks. 2 stitch lightly together. 3 (foll. By to, on, on to) add or append. 4  a change a ship''s course by turning its head to the wind. B make a series of such tacks. [probably related to french tache clasp, nail]'),
(36156, 't', '\r\nTack2', ' n. Saddle, bridle, etc., of a horse. [from *tackle]'),
(36157, 't', '\r\nTack3', ' n. Colloq. Cheap or shoddy material; tat, kitsch. [back formation from *tacky2]'),
(36158, 't', '\r\nTackle', ' â€”n. 1 equipment for a task or sport. 2 mechanism, esp. Of ropes, pulley-blocks, hooks, etc., for lifting weights, managing sails, etc. 3 windlass with its ropes and hooks. 4 act of tackling in football etc. â€”v. (-ling) 1 try to deal with (a problem or difficulty). 2 grapple with (an opponent). 3 confront (a person) in discussion or argument. 4 intercept or stop (a player running with the ball).  tackler n. [low german]'),
(36159, 't', '\r\nTackle-block', ' n. Pulley over which a rope runs.'),
(36160, 't', '\r\nTacky1', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) slightly sticky.  tackiness n. [from *tack1]'),
(36161, 't', '\r\nTacky2', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 in poor taste, cheap. 2 tatty, shabby.  tackiness n. [origin unknown]'),
(36162, 't', '\r\nTaco', ' n. (pl. -s) mexican dish of meat etc. In a folded tortilla. [mexican spanish]'),
(36163, 't', '\r\nTact', ' n. 1 skill in dealing with others, esp. In delicate situations. 2 intuitive perception of the right thing to do or say. [latin tango tact- touch]'),
(36164, 't', '\r\nTactful', ' adj. Having or showing tact.  tactfully adv.'),
(36165, 't', '\r\nTactic', ' n. 1 tactical manoeuvre. 2 = *tactics. [greek from tasso arrange]'),
(36166, 't', '\r\nTactical', ' adj. 1 of tactics (tactical retreat). 2 (of bombing etc.) Done in direct support of military or naval operations. 3 adroitly planning or adroitly planned.  tactically adv.'),
(36167, 't', '\r\nTactics', ' n.pl. 1 (also treated as sing.) Disposition of armed forces, esp. In warfare. 2 short-term procedure adopted in carrying out a scheme or achieving an end.  tactician n.'),
(36168, 't', '\r\nTactile', ' adj. 1 of the sense of touch. 2 perceived by touch; tangible.  tactility n. [latin: related to *tact]'),
(36169, 't', '\r\nTactless', ' adj. Having or showing no tact.  tactlessly adv.'),
(36170, 't', '\r\nTadpole', ' n. Larva, esp. Of a frog, toad, or newt. [related to *toad, *poll]'),
(36171, 't', '\r\nTaffeta', ' n. Fine lustrous silk or silklike fabric. [french or medieval latin from persian]'),
(36172, 't', '\r\nTaffrail', ' n. Rail round a ship''s stern. [dutch taffereel panel]'),
(36173, 't', '\r\nTaffy', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Often offens. Welshman. [a supposed pronunciation of davy = david]'),
(36174, 't', '\r\nTag1', ' â€”n. 1 label, esp. On an object to show its address, price, etc. 2 metal etc. Point on a shoelace etc. 3 loop or flap for handling or hanging a thing. 4 loose or ragged end. 5 trite quotation or stock phrase. â€”v. (-gg-) 1 provide with a tag or tags. 2 (often foll. By on, on to) join or attach.  tag along (often foll. By with) go along, accompany passively. [origin unknown]'),
(36175, 't', '\r\nTag2', ' â€”n. Children''s chasing game. â€”v. (-gg-) touch in a game of tag. [origin unknown]'),
(36176, 't', '\r\nTag', 'end  n. Esp. Us last remnant.'),
(36177, 't', '\r\nTagliatelle', ' n. Narrow ribbon-shaped pasta. [italian]'),
(36178, 't', '\r\nT''ai', 'chi  n. (in full t''ai chi ch''uan) chinese martial art and system of callisthenics with slow controlled movements. [chinese, = great ultimate boxing]'),
(36179, 't', '\r\nTail1', ' â€”n. 1 hindmost part of an animal, esp. Extending beyond the body. 2 a thing like a tail, esp. An extension at the rear. B rear of a procession etc. 3 rear part of an aeroplane, vehicle, or rocket. 4 luminous trail following a comet. 5 inferior, weaker, or last part of anything. 6 part of a shirt or coat below the waist at the back. 7 (in pl.) Colloq. A tailcoat. B evening dress including this. 8 (in pl.) Reverse of a coin as a choice when tossing. 9 colloq. Person following another. â€”v. 1 remove the stalks of (fruit). 2 (often foll. By after) colloq. Follow closely.  on a person''s tail closely following a person. Tail off (or away) gradually decrease or diminish; end inconclusively. With one''s tail between one''s legs dejected, humiliated.  tailless adj. [old english]'),
(36180, 't', '\r\nTail2', ' law â€”n. Limitation of ownership, esp. Of an estate limited to a person and that person''s heirs. â€”adj. So limited (estate tail).  in tail under such a limitation. [french taillier cut: related to *tally]'),
(36181, 't', '\r\nTailback', ' n. Long line of traffic caused by an obstruction.'),
(36182, 't', '\r\nTailboard', ' n. Hinged or removable flap at the rear of a lorry etc.'),
(36183, 't', '\r\nTailcoat', ' n. Man''s coat with a long divided flap at the back, worn as part of formal dress.'),
(36184, 't', '\r\nTail-end', ' n. Hindmost, lowest, or last part.'),
(36185, 't', '\r\nTailgate', ' n. 1 esp. Us = *tailboard. 2 rear door of an estate car or hatchback.'),
(36186, 't', '\r\nTail-light', ' n. (also tail-lamp) us rear light on a vehicle etc.'),
(36187, 't', '\r\nTailor', ' â€”n. Maker of clothes, esp. Men''s outer garments to measure. â€”v. 1 make (clothes) as a tailor. 2 make or adapt for a special purpose. 3 work as or be a tailor. [anglo-french taillour: related to *tail2]'),
(36188, 't', '\r\nTailored', ' adj. 1 (of clothing) well or closely fitted. 2 = *tailor-made.'),
(36189, 't', '\r\nTailor-made', ' adj. 1 made to order by a tailor. 2 made or suited for a particular purpose.'),
(36190, 't', '\r\nTailpiece', ' n. 1 rear appendage. 2 final part of a thing. 3 decoration in a blank space at the end of a chapter etc.'),
(36191, 't', '\r\nTailpipe', ' n. Rear section of an exhaust-pipe.'),
(36192, 't', '\r\nTailplane', ' n. Horizontal aerofoil at the tail of an aircraft.'),
(36193, 't', '\r\nTailspin', ' n. 1 spin by an aircraft with the tail spiralling. 2 state of chaos or panic.'),
(36194, 't', '\r\nTail', 'wind  n. Wind blowing in the direction of travel.'),
(36195, 't', '\r\nTaint', ' â€”n. 1 spot or trace of decay, infection, corruption, etc. 2 corrupt condition or infection. â€”v. 1 affect with a taint; become tainted. 2 (foll. By with) affect slightly. [latin: related to *tinge]'),
(36196, 't', '\r\nTake', ' â€”v. (-king; past took; past part. Taken) 1 lay hold of; get into one''s hands. 2 acquire, capture, earn, or win. 3 get by purchase, hire, or formal agreement (take lodgings; took a taxi). 4 (in a recipe) use. 5 regularly buy (a newspaper etc.). 6 obtain after qualifying (take a degree). 7 occupy (take a chair). 8 make use of (take the next turning on the left; take the bus). 9 consume (food or medicine). 10 a be effective (inoculation did not take). B (of a plant, seed, etc.) Begin to grow. 11 require or use up (will only take a minute). 12 carry or accompany (take the book home; bus will take you). 13 remove; steal (someone has taken my pen). 14 catch or be infected with (fire or fever etc.). 15 a experience, seek, or be affected by (take fright; take pleasure). B exert (take no notice). 16 find out and note (took his address; took her temperature). 17 understand; assume (i took you to mean yes). 18 treat, deal with, or regard in a specified way (took it badly; took the corner too fast). 19 (foll. By for) regard as being (do you take me for an idiot?). 20 a accept, receive (take the offer; take a call; takes boarders). B hold (takes 3 pints). C submit to; tolerate (take a joke). 21 wear (takes size 10). 22 choose or assume (took a job; took the initiative). 23 derive (takes its name from the inventor). 24 (foll. By from) subtract (take 3 from 9). 25 perform or effect (take notes; take an oath; take a look). 26 occupy or engage oneself in (take a rest). 27 conduct (took prayers). 28 teach, be taught, or be examined in (a subject). 29 a make (a photograph). B photograph (a person etc.). 30 (in imper.) Use as an example (take napoleon). 31 gram. Have or require as part of a construction (this verb takes an object). 32 have sexual intercourse with (a woman). 33 (in passive; foll. By by, with) be attracted or charmed by. â€”n. 1 amount taken or caught at a time etc. 2 scene or film sequence photographed continuously at one time.  be taken ill become ill, esp. Suddenly. Have what it takes colloq. Have the necessary qualities etc. For success. Take account of see *account. Take advantage of see *advantage. Take after resemble (a parent etc.). Take against begin to dislike. Take aim see *aim. Take apart 1 dismantle. 2 colloq. Beat or defeat. 3 colloq. Criticize severely. Take away 1 remove or carry elsewhere. 2 subtract. 3 buy (hot food etc.) For eating elsewhere. Take back 1 retract (a statement). 2 convey to an original position. 3 carry in thought to a past time. 4 a return (goods) to a shop. B (of a shop) accept such goods. 5 accept (a person) back into one''s affections, into employment, etc. Take the biscuit (or bun or cake) colloq. Be the most remarkable. Take down 1 write down (spoken words). 2 remove or dismantle. 3 lower (a garment worn below the waist). Take effect see *effect. Take for granted see *grant. Take fright see *fright. Take heart be encouraged. Take in 1 receive as a lodger etc. 2 undertake (work) at home. 3 make (a garment etc.) Smaller. 4 understand; observe (did you take that in?). 5 cheat. 6 include. 7 colloq. Visit (a place) on the way to another (took in bath). 8 absorb into the body. Take in hand 1 undertake; start doing or dealing with. 2 undertake to control or reform (a person). Take into account see *account. Take it 1 (often foll. By that) assume. 2 colloq. Endure in a specified way (took it badly). Take it easy see *easy. Take it into one''s head see *head. Take it on one (or oneself) (foll. By to + infin.) Venture or presume. Take it or leave it (esp. In imper.) Accept it or not. Take it out of 1 exhaust the strength of. 2 have revenge on. Take it out on relieve one''s frustration by treating aggressively.'),
(36197, 't', '\r\nTake-away', ' â€”attrib. Adj. (of food) bought cooked for eating elsewhere. â€”n. 1 this food. 2 establishment selling this.'),
(36198, 't', '\r\nTake-home', 'pay  n. Employee''s pay after the deduction of tax etc.'),
(36199, 't', '\r\nTake-off', ' n. 1 act of becoming airborne. 2 act of mimicking.'),
(36200, 't', '\r\nTake-over', ' n. Assumption of control (esp. Of a business); buying-out.'),
(36201, 't', '\r\nTaker', ' n. Person who takes a bet, accepts an offer, etc.'),
(36202, 't', '\r\nTake-up', ' n. Acceptance of a thing offered.'),
(36203, 't', '\r\nTaking', ' â€”adj. Attractive, captivating. â€”n. (in pl.) Amount of money taken at a show, in a shop, etc.'),
(36204, 't', '\r\nTalc', ' n. 1 talcum powder. 2 magnesium silicate formed as soft flat plates, used as a lubricator etc. [arabic from persian talk]'),
(36205, 't', '\r\nTalcum', ' n. 1 = *talc 2. 2 (in full talcum powder) powdered talc for toilet use, usu. Perfumed. [medieval latin: see *talc]'),
(36206, 't', '\r\nTale', ' n. 1 (usu. Fictitious) narrative or story. 2 allegation, often malicious or in breach of confidence. [old english]'),
(36207, 't', '\r\nTalent', ' n. 1 special aptitude or faculty (talent for music). 2 high mental ability. 3 a person or persons of talent. B colloq. Attractive members of the opposite sex (plenty of local talent). 4 ancient esp. Greek weight and unit of currency.  talented adj. [greek talanton]'),
(36208, 't', '\r\nTalent-scout', ' n. (also talent-spotter) person seeking new talent, esp. In sport or entertainment.'),
(36209, 't', '\r\nTalisman', ' n. (pl. -s) ring, stone, etc. Thought to have magic powers, esp. To bring good luck.  talismanic adj. [french and spanish from greek]'),
(36210, 't', '\r\nTalk', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By to, with) converse or communicate verbally. 2 have the power of speech. 3 (often foll. By about) a discuss; express; utter (talked cricket; talking nonsense). B (in imper.) Colloq. As an emphatic statement (talk about expense!). 4 use (a language) in speech (talking spanish). 5 (foll. By at) address pompously. 6 (usu. Foll. By into, out of) bring into a specified condition etc. By talking (talked himself hoarse; did you talk them into it?). 7 betray secrets. 8 gossip (people will talk). 9 have influence (money talks). â€”n. 1 conversation, talking. 2 particular mode of speech (baby-talk). 3 informal address or lecture. 4 a rumour or gossip (talk of a merger). B its theme (the talk was all babies). 5 empty promises; boasting. 6 (often in pl.) Discussions or negotiations.  now you''re talking colloq. I like what you say, suggest, etc. Talk back reply defiantly. Talk down to speak condescendingly to. Talk a person down 1 silence by loudness or persistence. 2 bring (a pilot or aircraft) to landing by radio. Talk of 1 discuss or mention. 2 (often foll. By verbal noun) express some intention of (talked of moving to london). Talk out block (a bill in parliament) by prolonging discussion to the time of adjournment. Talk over discuss at length. Talk a person over (or round) gain agreement by talking. Talk shop talk about one''s occupation etc. Talk to rebuke, scold.  talker n. [from *tale or *tell]'),
(36211, 't', '\r\nTalkative', ' adj. Fond of or given to talking.'),
(36212, 't', '\r\nTalkback', ' n. (often attrib.) System of two-way communication by loudspeaker.'),
(36213, 't', '\r\nTalkie', ' n. Colloq. (esp. Early) film with a soundtrack.'),
(36214, 't', '\r\nTalking', ' â€”adj. 1 that talks, or is able to talk (talking parrot). 2 expressive (talking eyes). â€”n. In senses of *talk v.  talking of while we are discussing.'),
(36215, 't', '\r\nTalking', 'book  n. Recorded reading of a book, esp. For the blind.'),
(36216, 't', '\r\nTalking-point', ' n. Topic for discussion.'),
(36217, 't', '\r\nTalking-shop', ' n. Derog. Arena or opportunity for empty talk.'),
(36218, 't', '\r\nTalking-to', ' n. Colloq. Reproof, reprimand.'),
(36219, 't', '\r\nTall', ' â€”adj. 1 of more than average height. 2 of a specified height (about six feet tall). 3 higher than the surrounding objects (tall building). â€”adv. As if tall; proudly (sit tall).  tallish adj. Tallness n. [old english, = swift]'),
(36220, 't', '\r\nTallboy', ' n. Tall chest of drawers.'),
(36221, 't', '\r\nTall', 'order  n. Unreasonable demand.'),
(36222, 't', '\r\nTallow', ' n. Hard (esp. Animal) fat melted down to make candles, soap, etc.  tallowy adj. [low german]'),
(36223, 't', '\r\nTall', 'ship  n. Sailing-ship with a high mast.'),
(36224, 't', '\r\nTall', 'story  n. Colloq. Extravagant story that is difficult to believe.'),
(36225, 't', '\r\nTally', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 reckoning of a debt or score. 2 total score or amount. 3 mark registering the number of objects delivered or received. 4 hist. A piece of notched wood for keeping account. B account kept thus. 5 identification ticket or label. 6 corresponding thing, counterpart, or duplicate. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) (often foll. By with) agree or correspond. [latin talea rod]'),
(36226, 't', '\r\nTally-ho', ' â€”int. Huntsman''s cry on sighting a fox. â€”n. (pl. -s) cry of this. â€”v. (-hoes, -hoed) 1 utter a cry of â€˜tally-hoâ€™. 2 indicate (a fox) or urge (hounds) with this cry. [cf. French taÃ¯aut]'),
(36227, 't', '\r\nTalmud', ' n. Body of jewish civil and ceremonial law and legend.  talmudic adj. Talmudist n. [hebrew, = instruction]'),
(36228, 't', '\r\nTalon', ' n. Claw, esp. Of a bird of prey. [latin talus ankle]'),
(36229, 't', '\r\nTalus', ' n. (pl. Tali) ankle-bone supporting the tibia. [latin, = ankle]'),
(36230, 't', '\r\nTamarind', ' n. 1 tropical evergreen tree. 2 fruit pulp from this used as food and in drinks. [arabic, = indian date]'),
(36231, 't', '\r\nTamarisk', ' n. Seashore shrub usu. With small pink or white flowers. [latin]'),
(36232, 't', '\r\nTambour', ' n. 1 drum. 2 circular frame holding fabric taut for embroidering. [french: related to *tabor]'),
(36233, 't', '\r\nTambourine', ' n. Small shallow drum with jingling discs in its rim, shaken or banged as an accompaniment. [french, diminutive of *tambour]'),
(36234, 't', '\r\nTame', ' â€”adj. 1 (of an animal) domesticated; not wild or shy. 2 insipid; dull (tame entertainment). 3 (of a person) amenable. â€”v. (-ming) 1 make tame; domesticate. 2 subdue, curb.  tameable adj. Tamely adv. Tameness n. Tamer n. (also in comb.). [old english]'),
(36235, 't', '\r\nTamil', ' â€”n. 1 member of a people of south india and sri lanka. 2 language of this people. â€”adj. Of this people or language. [native name]'),
(36236, 't', '\r\nTam-o''-shanter', ' n. Floppy round esp. Woollen beret, of scottish origin. [hero of a poem by burns]'),
(36237, 't', '\r\nTamp', ' v. Ram down hard or tightly. [tampion stopper for gun-muzzle, from french tampon]'),
(36238, 't', '\r\nTamper', ' v. (foll. By with) 1 meddle with or change illicitly. 2 exert a secret or corrupt influence upon; bribe. [var. Of *temper]'),
(36239, 't', '\r\nTampon', ' n. Plug of soft material used esp. To absorb menstrual blood. [french: related to *tamp]'),
(36240, 't', '\r\nTam-tam', ' n. Large metal gong. [hindi]'),
(36241, 't', '\r\nTan1', ' â€”n. 1 = *suntan. 2 yellowish-brown colour. 3 bark, esp. Of oak, used to tan hides. â€”adj. Yellowish-brown. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 make or become brown by exposure to sunlight. 2 convert (raw hide) into leather. 3 slang beat, thrash. [medieval latin tanno, perhaps from celtic]'),
(36242, 't', '\r\nTan2', ' abbr. Tangent.'),
(36243, 't', '\r\nTandem', ' â€”n. 1 bicycle with two or more seats one behind another. 2 group of two people etc. With one behind or following the other. 3 carriage driven tandem. â€”adv. With two or more horses harnessed one behind another (drive tandem).  in tandem 1 one behind another. 2 alongside each other; together. [latin, = at length]'),
(36244, 't', '\r\nTandoor', ' n. Clay oven. [hindustani]'),
(36245, 't', '\r\nTandoori', ' n. Food spiced and cooked over charcoal in a tandoor (often attrib.: tandoori chicken). [hindustani]'),
(36246, 't', '\r\nTang', ' n. 1 strong taste or smell. 2 characteristic quality. 3 projection on the blade of esp. A knife, by which it is held firm in the handle. [old norse tange point]'),
(36247, 't', '\r\nTangent', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Straight line, curve, or surface that meets a curve at a point, but does not intersect it. 2 ratio of two sides (other than the hypotenuse) opposite and adjacent to an acute angle in a right-angled triangle.  at a tangent diverging from a previous course or from what is relevant or central (go off at a tangent). [latin tango tact- touch]'),
(36248, 't', '\r\nTangential', ' adj. 1 of or along a tangent. 2 divergent. 3 peripheral.  tangentially adv.'),
(36249, 't', '\r\nTangerine', ' n. 1 small sweet thin-skinned citrus fruit like an orange; mandarin. 2 deep orange-yellow colour. [tangier in morocco]'),
(36250, 't', '\r\nTangible', ' adj. 1 perceptible by touch. 2 definite; clearly intelligible; not elusive (tangible proof).  tangibility n. Tangibleness n. Tangibly adv. [latin: related to *tangent]'),
(36251, 't', '\r\nTangle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 intertwine (threads or hairs etc.) Or become entwined in a confused mass; entangle. 2 (foll. By with) colloq. Become involved (esp. In conflict) with (don''t tangle with me). 3 complicate (tangled affair). â€”n. 1 confused mass of intertwined threads etc. 2 confused state. [origin uncertain]'),
(36252, 't', '\r\nTangly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) tangled.'),
(36253, 't', '\r\nTango', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 slow s. American ballroom dance. 2 music for this. â€”v. (-goes, -goed) dance the tango. [american spanish]'),
(36254, 't', '\r\nTangy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having a strong usu. Acid tang.'),
(36255, 't', '\r\nTanh', ' abbr. Hyperbolic tangent.'),
(36256, 't', '\r\nTank', ' â€”n. 1 large container, usu. For liquid or gas. 2 heavy armoured fighting vehicle moving on continuous tracks. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By up) fill the tank of (a vehicle etc.) With fuel.  tankful n. (pl. -s). [originally indian, = pond, from gujarati]'),
(36257, 't', '\r\nTankard', ' n. 1 tall beer mug with a handle. 2 contents of or amount held by this (drank a tankard of ale). [probably dutch tankaert]'),
(36258, 't', '\r\nTanked', 'up  predic. Adj. Colloq. Drunk.'),
(36259, 't', '\r\nTank', 'engine  n. Steam engine with integral fuel and water containers.'),
(36260, 't', '\r\nTanker', ' n. Ship, aircraft, or road vehicle for carrying liquids, esp. Oil, in bulk.'),
(36261, 't', '\r\nTanner', ' n. Person who tans hides.'),
(36262, 't', '\r\nTannery', ' n. (pl. -ies) place where hides are tanned.'),
(36263, 't', '\r\nTannic', ' adj. Of tan (sense 3). [french tannique: related to *tannin]'),
(36264, 't', '\r\nTannic', 'acid  n. Natural yellowish organic compound used as a mordant and astringent.'),
(36265, 't', '\r\nTannin', ' n. Any of various organic compounds found in tree-barks and oak-galls, used in leather production. [french tanin: related to *tan1]'),
(36266, 't', '\r\nTannoy', ' n. Propr. Type of public-address system. [origin uncertain]'),
(36267, 't', '\r\nTansy', ' n. (pl. -ies) plant with yellow flowers and aromatic leaves. [greek athanasia immortality]'),
(36268, 't', '\r\nTantalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 torment or tease by the sight or promise of the unobtainable. 2 raise and then dash the hopes of.  tantalization n. [tantalus, mythical king punished in hades with sight of water and fruit which drew back whenever he tried to reach them]'),
(36269, 't', '\r\nTantalum', ' n. Rare hard white metallic element.  tantalic adj. [related to *tantalize]'),
(36270, 't', '\r\nTantalus', ' n. Stand in which spirit-decanters may be locked up but visible. [see *tantalize]'),
(36271, 't', '\r\nTantamount', ' predic. Adj. (foll. By to) equivalent to. [italian tanto montare amount to so much]'),
(36272, 't', '\r\nTantra', ' n. Any of a class of hindu or buddhist mystical or magical writings. [sanskrit, = doctrine]'),
(36273, 't', '\r\nTantrum', ' n. (esp. Child''s) outburst of bad temper or petulance. [origin unknown]'),
(36274, 't', '\r\nTaoiseach', ' n. Prime minister of the irish republic. [irish, = chief, leader]'),
(36275, 't', '\r\nTaoism', ' n. Chinese philosophy advocating humility and religious piety.  taoist n. [chinese dao right way]'),
(36276, 't', '\r\nTap1', ' â€”n. 1 device by which a flow of liquid or gas from a pipe or vessel can be controlled. 2 tapping of a telephone etc. 3 taproom. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 provide (a cask) or let out (liquid) with a tap. 2 draw sap from (a tree) by cutting into it. 3 obtain information or supplies from. 4 extract or obtain; discover and exploit (mineral wealth waiting to be tapped; tap skills of young people). 5 connect a listening device to (a telephone etc.).  on tap 1 ready to be drawn off by tap. 2 colloq. Freely available. [old english]'),
(36277, 't', '\r\nTap2', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (foll. By at, on) strike a gentle but audible blow. 2 (often foll. By against, on, etc.) Strike or cause (a thing) to strike lightly (tapped me on the shoulder). 3 (often foll. By out) make by a tap or taps (tapped out the rhythm). 4 tap-dance. â€”n. 1 a light blow; rap. B sound of this. 2 a tap-dancing. B metal attachment on a tap-dancer''s shoe. [imitative]'),
(36278, 't', '\r\nTapas', ' n.pl. (often attrib.) Small savoury esp. Spanish dishes. [spanish]'),
(36279, 't', '\r\nTap-dance', ' â€”n. Rhythmic dance performed with shoes with metal taps. â€”v. Perform a tap-dance.  tap-dancer n. Tap-dancing n.'),
(36280, 't', '\r\nTape', ' â€”n. 1 narrow strip of woven material for tying up, fastening, etc. 2 this across the finishing line of a race. 3 (in full adhesive tape) strip of adhesive plastic etc. For fastening, masking, insulating, etc. 4 a = *magnetic tape. B reel or cassette containing this. C tape recording. 5 = *tape-measure. â€”v. (-ping) 1 a fasten or join etc. With tape. B apply tape to. 2 (foll. By off) seal or mark off with tape. 3 record on magnetic tape. 4 measure with tape.  have (or get) a person or thing taped colloq. Understand (him, it, etc.) Fully. [old english]'),
(36281, 't', '\r\nTape', 'deck  n. Machine for using audiotape (separate from the amplifier, speakers, etc.).'),
(36282, 't', '\r\nTape', 'machine  n. 1 machine for recording telegraph messages. 2 = *tape recorder.'),
(36283, 't', '\r\nTape-measure', ' n. Strip of marked tape or flexible metal for measuring.'),
(36284, 't', '\r\nTaper', ' â€”n. 1 wick coated with wax etc. For conveying a flame. 2 slender candle. â€”v. (often foll. By off) 1 diminish or reduce in thickness towards one end. 2 make or become gradually less. [old english]'),
(36285, 't', '\r\nTape', 'recorder  n. Apparatus for recording and replaying sounds on magnetic tape.  tape-record v. Tape recording n.'),
(36286, 't', '\r\nTapestry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a thick fabric in which coloured weft threads are woven to form pictures or designs. B (usu. Wool) embroidery imitating this. C piece of this. 2 events or circumstances etc. Seen as interwoven etc. (life''s rich tapestry).  tapestried adj. [tapissery from french tapis carpet]'),
(36287, 't', '\r\nTapeworm', ' n. Parasitic intestinal flatworm with a segmented body.'),
(36288, 't', '\r\nTapioca', ' n. Starchy substance in hard white grains, obtained from cassava and used for puddings etc. [tupi-guarani]'),
(36289, 't', '\r\nTapir', ' n. Nocturnal central and s. American or malaysian hoofed mammal with a short flexible snout. [tupi]'),
(36290, 't', '\r\nTappet', ' n. Lever or projecting part in machinery giving intermittent motion. [from *tap2]'),
(36291, 't', '\r\nTaproom', ' n. Room in a pub serving drinks on tap.'),
(36292, 't', '\r\nTap', 'root  n. Tapering root growing vertically downwards.'),
(36293, 't', '\r\nTar1', ' â€”n. 1 dark thick inflammable liquid distilled from wood or coal etc., used as a preservative of wood and iron, in making roads, as an antiseptic, etc. 2 similar substance formed in the combustion of tobacco etc. â€”v. (-rr-) cover with tar.  tar and feather smear with tar and then cover with feathers as a punishment. Tarred with the same brush having the same faults. [old english]'),
(36294, 't', '\r\nTar2', ' n. Colloq. Sailor. [from *tarpaulin]'),
(36295, 't', '\r\nTaramasalata', ' n. (also taramosalata) pÃ¢tÃ© made from roe with olive oil, seasoning, etc. [greek taramas roe, salata *salad]'),
(36296, 't', '\r\nTarantella', ' n. 1 whirling s. Italian dance. 2 music for this. [italian from taranto in italy]'),
(36297, 't', '\r\nTarantula', ' n. 1 large hairy tropical spider. 2 large black s. European spider. [medieval latin: related to *tarantella]'),
(36298, 't', '\r\nTarboosh', ' n. Cap like a fez. [arabic from persian]'),
(36299, 't', '\r\nTardy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 slow to act, come, or happen. 2 delaying or delayed.  tardily adv. Tardiness n. [latin tardus slow]'),
(36300, 't', '\r\nTare1', ' n. 1 vetch, esp. As a cornfield weed or fodder. 2 (in pl.) Bibl. An injurious cornfield weed (matt. 13:24-30). [origin unknown]'),
(36301, 't', '\r\nTare2', ' n. 1 allowance made for the weight of packing or wrapping around goods. 2 weight of a vehicle without fuel or load. [arabic tarha]'),
(36302, 't', '\r\nTarget', ' â€”n. 1 mark fired or aimed at, esp. A round object marked with concentric circles. 2 person or thing aimed or fired at etc. (an easy target). 3 objective or result aimed at. 4 butt for criticism, abuse, etc. â€”v. (-t-) 1 identify or single out as a target. 2 aim or direct (missiles targeted on major cities). [french targe shield]'),
(36303, 't', '\r\nTariff', ' n. 1 table of fixed charges (hotel tariff). 2 a duty on a particular class of goods. B list of duties or customs due. [arabic, = notification]'),
(36304, 't', '\r\nTarlatan', ' n. Thin stiff open-weave muslin. [french; probably originally indian]'),
(36305, 't', '\r\nTarmac', ' â€”n. Propr. 1 = *tarmacadam. 2 runway etc. Made of this. â€”v. (tarmac) (-ck-) apply tarmacadam to. [abbreviation]'),
