(36340, 't', '\r\nTasting', ' n. Gathering at which food or drink is tasted and evaluated.'),
(36341, 't', '\r\nTasty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 pleasing in flavour; appetizing. 2 colloq. Attractive.  tastily adv. Tastiness n.'),
(36342, 't', '\r\nTat1', ' n. Colloq. Tatty things; rubbish, junk. [back-formation from *tatty]'),
(36343, 't', '\r\nTat2', ' v. (-tt-) do, or make by, tatting. [origin unknown]'),
(36344, 't', '\r\nTat3', ' see *tit2.'),
(36345, 't', '\r\nTa-ta', ' int. Colloq. Goodbye. [origin unknown]'),
(36346, 't', '\r\nTatter', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Rag; irregularly torn cloth or paper etc.  in tatters colloq. 1 torn in many places. 2 destroyed, ruined. [old norse]'),
(36347, 't', '\r\nTattered', ' adj. In tatters.'),
(36348, 't', '\r\nTatting', ' n. 1 a kind of handmade knotted lace used for trimming etc. 2 process of making this. [origin unknown]'),
(36349, 't', '\r\nTattle', ' â€”v. (-ling) prattle, chatter, gossip. â€”n. Gossip; idle talk. [flemish tatelen, imitative]'),
(36350, 't', '\r\nTattoo1', ' n. 1 evening drum or bugle signal recalling soldiers to quarters. 2 elaboration of this with music and marching as an entertainment. 3 rhythmic tapping or drumming. [earlier tap-too from dutch taptoe, literally â€˜close the tapâ€™ (of the cask)]'),
(36351, 't', '\r\nTattoo2', ' â€”v. (-oos, -ooed) 1 mark (skin) indelibly by puncturing it and inserting pigment. 2 make (a design) in this way. â€”n. Such a design.  tattooer n. Tattooist n. [polynesian]'),
(36352, 't', '\r\nTatty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 tattered; shabby. 2 inferior. 3 tawdry.  tattily adv. Tattiness n. [originally scots, = shaggy, apparently related to *tatter]'),
(36353, 't', '\r\nTau', ' n. Nineteenth letter of the greek alphabet (t, t). [greek]'),
(36354, 't', '\r\nTaught', ' past and past part. Of *teach.'),
(36355, 't', '\r\nTaunt', ' â€”n. Insult; provocation. â€”v. Insult; provoke contemptuously. [french tant pour tant tit for tat, smart rejoinder]'),
(36356, 't', '\r\nTaupe', ' adj. & n. Grey tinged with esp. Brown. [french, = *mole1]'),
(36357, 't', '\r\nTaurus', ' n. (pl. -es) 1 constellation and second sign of the zodiac (the bull). 2 person born when the sun is in this sign.  taurean adj. & n. [latin, = bull]'),
(36358, 't', '\r\nTaut', ' adj. 1 (of a rope etc.) Tight; not slack. 2 (of nerves etc.) Tense. 3 (of a ship etc.) In good condition.  tauten v. Tautly adv. Tautness n. [perhaps = *tough]'),
(36359, 't', '\r\nTautology', ' n. (pl. -ies) repetition using different words, esp. As a fault of style (e.g. Arrived one after the other in succession).  tautological adj. Tautologous adj. [greek tauto the same]'),
(36360, 't', '\r\nTavern', ' n. Archaic or literary inn, pub. [latin taberna]'),
(36361, 't', '\r\nTaverna', ' n. Greek restaurant. [modern greek: related to *tavern]'),
(36362, 't', '\r\nTawdry', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) showy but worthless; gaudy.  tawdrily adv. Tawdriness n. [tawdry lace from st audrey''s lace]'),
(36363, 't', '\r\nTawny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) orange-brown or yellow-brown. [anglo-french taunÃ©: related to *tan1]'),
(36364, 't', '\r\nTawny', 'owl  n. Reddish-brown european owl.'),
(36365, 't', '\r\nTax', ' â€”n. 1 money compulsorily levied by the state or local authorities on individuals, property, or businesses. 2 (usu. Foll. By on, upon) strain, heavy demand, or burdensome obligation. â€”v. 1 impose a tax on. 2 deduct tax from (income etc.).'),
(36366, 't', '\r\nTaxa', ' pl. Of *taxon.'),
(36367, 't', '\r\nTaxation', ' n. Imposition or payment of tax. [latin: related to *tax]'),
(36368, 't', '\r\nTax', 'avoidance  n. Minimizing payment of tax by financial manoeuvring.'),
(36369, 't', '\r\nTax-deductible', ' adj. (of expenditure) legally deductible from income before tax assessment.'),
(36370, 't', '\r\nTax', 'disc  n. Road tax receipt displayed on the windscreen of a vehicle.'),
(36371, 't', '\r\nTax', 'evasion  n. Illegal non-payment or underpayment of tax.'),
(36372, 't', '\r\nTax-free', ' adj. Exempt from tax.'),
(36373, 't', '\r\nTax', 'haven  n. Country etc. Where taxes are low.'),
(36374, 't', '\r\nTaxi', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) (in full taxi-cab) car licensed to ply for hire and usu. Fitted with a taximeter. â€”v. (-xis, -xied, -xiing or -xying) 1 (of an aircraft or pilot) drive on the ground before take-off or after landing. 2 go or convey in a taxi. [abbreviation of taximeter cab]'),
(36375, 't', '\r\nTaxidermy', ' n. Art of preparing, stuffing, and mounting the skins of animals.  taxidermist n. [greek taxis arrangement, derma skin]'),
