(36377, 't', '\r\nTaxi', 'rank  n. (us taxi stand) place where taxis wait to be hired.'),
(36378, 't', '\r\nTaxman', ' n. Colloq. Inspector or collector of taxes.'),
(36379, 't', '\r\nTaxon', ' n. (pl. Taxa) any taxonomic group. [back-formation from *taxonomy]'),
(36380, 't', '\r\nTaxonomy', ' n. Classification of living and extinct organisms.  taxonomic adj. Taxonomical adj. Taxonomically adv. Taxonomist n. [greek taxis arrangement, -nomia distribution]'),
(36381, 't', '\r\nTaxpayer', ' n. Person who pays taxes.'),
(36382, 't', '\r\nTax', 'return  n. Declaration of income for taxation purposes.'),
(36383, 't', '\r\nTayberry', ' n. (pl. -ies) hybrid fruit between the blackberry and raspberry. [river tay in scotland]'),
(36384, 't', '\r\nTb', ' abbr. 1 tubercle bacillus. 2 tuberculosis.'),
(36385, 't', '\r\nTb', ' symb. Terbium.'),
(36386, 't', '\r\nT.b.a.', ' Abbr. To be announced.'),
(36387, 't', '\r\nT-bone', ' n. T-shaped bone, esp. In steak from the thin end of a loin.'),
(36388, 't', '\r\nTbsp.', ' Abbr. Tablespoonful.'),
(36389, 't', '\r\nTc', ' symb. Technetium.'),
(36390, 't', '\r\nTcp', ' abbr. Propr. A disinfectant and germicide. [trichlorophenylmethyliodasalicyl]'),
(36391, 't', '\r\nTe', ' symb. Tellurium.'),
(36392, 't', '\r\nTe', ' n. (also ti) seventh note of a major scale. [earlier si: french from italian]'),
(36393, 't', '\r\nTea', ' n. 1 a (in full tea plant) asian evergreen shrub or small tree. B its dried leaves. 2 drink made by infusing tea-leaves in boiling water. 3 infusion of other leaves etc. (camomile tea; beef tea). 4 a light afternoon meal of tea, bread, cakes, etc. B = *high tea. [probably dutch tee from chinese]'),
(36394, 't', '\r\nTea', 'bag  n. Small perforated bag of tea for infusion.'),
(36395, 't', '\r\nTea', 'break  n. Pause in work etc. To drink tea.'),
(36396, 't', '\r\nTea', 'caddy  n. Container for tea.'),
(36397, 't', '\r\nTeacake', ' n. Light usu. Toasted sweet bun eaten at tea.'),
(36398, 't', '\r\nTeach', ' v. (past and past part. Taught) 1 a give systematic information, instruction, or training to (a person) or about (a subject or skill) (taught me to swim). B (absol.) Practise this professionally. C communicate, instruct in (suffering taught me patience). 2 advocate as a moral etc. Principle (taught forgiveness). 3 (foll. By to + infin.) A instruct (a person) by example or punishment (that will teach you not to disobey). B colloq. Discourage (a person) from (that will teach you to laugh).  teachable adj. [old english]'),
(36399, 't', '\r\nTeacher', ' n. Person who teaches, esp. In a school.'),
(36400, 't', '\r\nTea', 'chest  n. Light metal-lined plywood box for transporting tea.'),
(36401, 't', '\r\nTeaching', ' n. 1 profession of a teacher. 2 (often in pl.) What is taught; doctrine.'),
(36402, 't', '\r\nTea', 'cloth  n. = *tea towel.'),
(36403, 't', '\r\nTea', 'cosy  n. Cover to keep a teapot warm.'),
(36404, 't', '\r\nTeacup', ' n. 1 cup from which tea is drunk. 2 amount held by this.  teacupful n. (pl. -s).'),
(36405, 't', '\r\nTea', 'dance  n. Afternoon tea with dancing.'),
(36406, 't', '\r\nTeak', ' n. 1 a hard durable timber. 2 large indian or se asian deciduous tree yielding this. [portuguese from malayalam]'),
(36407, 't', '\r\nTeal', ' n. (pl. Same) small freshwater duck. [origin unknown]'),
(36408, 't', '\r\nTea', 'lady  n. Woman employed to make tea in offices etc.'),
(36409, 't', '\r\nTea-leaf', ' n. 1 dried leaf of tea. 2 (esp. In pl.) These as dregs. 3 rhyming sl. Thief.'),
(36410, 't', '\r\nTeam', ' â€”n. 1 set of players forming one side in a game. 2 two or more people working together. 3 set of draught animals. â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By up) join in a team or in common action (teamed up with them). 2 (foll. By with) match or coordinate (clothes). [old english]'),
(36411, 't', '\r\nTeam-mate', ' n. Fellow-member of a team.'),
(36412, 't', '\r\nTeam', 'spirit  n. Willingness to act for the communal good.'),
(36413, 't', '\r\nTeamster', ' n. 1 us lorry-driver. 2 driver of a team of animals.'),
(36414, 't', '\r\nTeamwork', ' n. Combined action; co-operation.'),
(36415, 't', '\r\nTea-planter', ' n. Proprietor or cultivator of a tea plantation.'),
(36416, 't', '\r\nTeapot', ' n. Pot with a handle, spout, and lid, for brewing and then pouring tea.'),
(36417, 't', '\r\nTear1', ' â€”v. (past tore; past part. Torn) 1 (often foll. By up) pull apart or to pieces with some force (tore up the letter). 2 a make a hole or rent in this way; undergo this (have torn my coat; curtain tore). B make (a hole or rent). 3 (foll. By away, off, at, etc.) Pull violently (tore off the cover; tore down the notice). 4 violently disrupt or divide (torn by guilt). 5 colloq. Go hurriedly (tore across the road). â€”n. 1 hole etc. Caused by tearing. 2 torn part of cloth etc.  be torn between have difficulty in choosing between. Tear apart 1 search (a place) exhaustively. 2 criticize forcefully. 3 destroy; divide utterly; distress greatly. Tear one''s hair out colloq. Behave with extreme desperation. Tear into colloq. 1 severely reprimand. 2 start (an activity) vigorously. Tear oneself away leave reluctantly. Tear to shreds colloq. Refute or criticize thoroughly. That''s torn it colloq. That has spoiled things etc. [old english]'),
(36418, 't', '\r\nTear2', ' n. 1 drop of clear salty liquid secreted by glands from the eye, and shed esp. In grief. 2 tearlike thing; drop.  in tears crying. [old english]'),
(36419, 't', '\r\nTearaway', ' n. Colloq. Unruly young person.'),
(36420, 't', '\r\nTear-drop', ' n. Single tear.'),
(36421, 't', '\r\nTear-duct', ' n. Drain for carrying tears to or from the eye.'),
(36422, 't', '\r\nTearful', ' adj. 1 crying or inclined to cry. 2 sad (tearful event).  tearfully adv.'),
(36423, 't', '\r\nTear-gas', ' n. Gas causing severe irritation to the eyes.'),
(36424, 't', '\r\nTearing', 'hurry  n. Colloq. Great hurry.'),
(36425, 't', '\r\nTear-jerker', ' n. Colloq. Sentimental story, film, etc.'),
(36426, 't', '\r\nTearoom', ' n. Small unlicensed cafÃ© serving tea etc.'),
(36427, 't', '\r\nTea', 'rose  n. Hybrid shrub with a tealike scent.'),
(36428, 't', '\r\nTease', ' â€”v. (-sing) (also absol.) 1 a make fun of playfully, unkindly, or annoyingly; irritate. B allure, esp. Sexually, while withholding satisfaction. 2 pick (wool etc.) Into separate fibres. 3 dress (cloth) esp. With teasels. â€”n. 1 colloq. Person fond of teasing. 2 act of teasing (only a tease).  tease out separate by disentangling. [old english]'),
(36429, 't', '\r\nTeasel', ' n. (also teazel, teazle) 1 plant with large prickly heads that are dried and used to raise the nap on woven cloth. 2 other device used for this purpose.  teaseler n. [old english: related to *tease]'),
(36430, 't', '\r\nTeaser', ' n. 1 person who teases. 2 colloq. Hard question or task.'),
(36431, 't', '\r\nTeaset', ' n. Set of crockery for serving tea.'),
(36432, 't', '\r\nTeashop', ' n. = *tearoom.'),
(36433, 't', '\r\nTeaspoon', ' n. 1 small spoon for stirring tea. 2 amount held by this.  teaspoonful n. (pl. -s).'),
(36434, 't', '\r\nTeat', ' n. 1 mammary nipple, esp. Of an animal. 2 rubber nipple for sucking from a bottle. [french from germanic]'),
(36435, 't', '\r\nTeatime', ' n. Time in the afternoon when tea is served.'),
(36436, 't', '\r\nTea', 'towel  n. Towel for drying washed crockery etc.'),
(36437, 't', '\r\nTea', 'trolley  n. (us tea wagon) small trolley from which tea is served.'),
(36438, 't', '\r\nTeazel', ' (also teazle) var. Of *teasel.'),
(36439, 't', '\r\nTec', ' abbr. Training and enterprise council.'),
(36440, 't', '\r\nTec', ' n. Colloq. Detective. [abbreviation]'),
(36441, 't', '\r\nTech', ' n. (also tec) colloq. Technical college. [abbreviation]'),
(36442, 't', '\r\nTechnetium', ' n. Artificially produced radioactive metallic element. [greek tekhnetos artificial]'),
(36443, 't', '\r\nTechnic', ' n. 1 (usu. In pl.) A technology. B technical terms, details, methods, etc. 2 technique. [greek tekhne art]'),
(36444, 't', '\r\nTechnical', ' adj. 1 of the mechanical arts and applied sciences (technical college). 2 of a particular subject or craft etc. Or its techniques (technical terms). 3 (of a book or discourse etc.) Using technical language; specialized. 4 due to mechanical failure (technical hitch). 5 strictly or legally interpreted (lost on a technical point).  technically adv.'),
(36445, 't', '\r\nTechnicality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 being technical. 2 technical expression. 3 technical point or detail (acquitted on a technicality).'),
(36446, 't', '\r\nTechnical', 'knockout  n. Ruling by the referee that a boxer has lost because he is not fit to continue.'),
(36447, 't', '\r\nTechnician', ' n. 1 person doing practical or maintenance work in a laboratory etc. 2 person skilled in artistic etc. Technique. 3 expert in practical science.'),
(36448, 't', '\r\nTechnicolor', ' n. (often attrib.) 1 propr. Process of colour cinematography. 2 (usu. Technicolor) colloq. A vivid colour. B artificial brilliance.'),
(36449, 't', '\r\nTechnique', ' n. 1 mechanical skill in art. 2 skilful manipulation of a situation, people, etc. 3 manner of artistic execution in music, painting, etc. [french: related to *technic]'),
(36450, 't', '\r\nTechnocracy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 rule or control by technical experts. 2 instance or application of this. [greek tekhne art]'),
(36451, 't', '\r\nTechnocrat', ' n. Exponent or advocate of technocracy.  technocratic adj.'),
(36452, 't', '\r\nTechnology', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 knowledge or use of the mechanical arts and applied sciences (lacked the technology). 2 these subjects collectively.  technological adj. Technologically adv. Technologist n. [greek tekhnologia systematic treatment, from tekhne art]'),
(36453, 't', '\r\nTectonic', ' adj. 1 of building or construction. 2  of the deformation and subsequent structural changes of the earth''s crust (see *plate tectonics). [greek tekton craftsman]'),
(36454, 't', '\r\nTectonics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Study of the earth''s large-scale structural features (see *plate tectonics).'),
(36455, 't', '\r\nTed', ' n. (also ted) colloq. Teddy boy. [abbreviation]'),
(36456, 't', '\r\nTeddy', ' n. (also teddy) (pl. -ies) (in full teddy bear) soft toy bear. [teddy, pet form of theodore roosevelt]'),
(36457, 't', '\r\nTeddy', 'boy  n. Colloq. Youth, esp. Of the 1950s, wearing edwardian-style clothes, hairstyle, etc. [teddy, pet form of edward]'),
(36458, 't', '\r\nTedious', ' adj. Tiresomely long; wearisome.  tediously adv. Tediousness n. [latin: related to *tedium]'),
(36459, 't', '\r\nTedium', ' n. Tediousness. [latin taedium from taedet it bores]'),
(36460, 't', '\r\nTee1', ' n. = *t1. [phonetic spelling]'),
(36461, 't', '\r\nTee2', ' â€”n. 1  a cleared space from which the golf ball is struck at the start of play for each hole. B small wooden or plastic support for a golf ball used then. 2 mark aimed at in bowls, quoits, curling, etc. â€”v. (tees, teed) (often foll. By up) place (a ball) on a golf tee.  tee off 1  play a ball from a tee. 2 colloq. Start, begin. [origin unknown]'),
(36462, 't', '\r\nTee-hee', ' (also te-hee) â€”int. Expressing esp. Derisive amusement. â€”n. Titter, giggle. â€”v. (-hees, -heed) titter, giggle. [imitative]'),
(36463, 't', '\r\nTeem1', ' v. 1 be abundant. 2 (foll. By with) be full of or swarming with (teeming with ideas). [old english, = give birth to]'),
(36464, 't', '\r\nTeem2', ' v. (often foll. By down) (of water etc.) Flow copiously; pour (teeming with rain). [old norse]'),
(36465, 't', '\r\nTeen', ' attrib. Adj. = *teenage. [abbreviation]'),
(36466, 't', '\r\n-teen', ' suffix forming numerals from 13 to 19. [old english]'),
(36467, 't', '\r\nTeenage', ' attrib. Adj. Of or characteristic of teenagers.  teenaged adj.'),
(36468, 't', '\r\nTeenager', ' n. Person from 13 to 19 years of age.'),
(36469, 't', '\r\nTeens', ' n.pl. Years of one''s age from 13 to 19 (in his teens).'),
(36470, 't', '\r\nTeensy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. = *teeny.'),
(36471, 't', '\r\nTeeny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Tiny. [var. Of *tiny]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(36472, 't', '\r\nTeeny-bopper', ' n. Colloq. Young teenager, usu. A girl, who follows the latest fashions.'),
(36473, 't', '\r\nTeeny-weeny', ' adj. (also teensy-weensy) very tiny.'),
(36474, 't', '\r\nTeepee', ' var. Of *tepee.'),
(36475, 't', '\r\nTeeter', ' v. Totter; move unsteadily. [dial. Titter]'),
(36476, 't', '\r\nTeeth', ' pl. Of *tooth.'),
(36477, 't', '\r\nTeethe', ' v. (-thing) grow or cut teeth, esp. Milk teeth.'),
(36478, 't', '\r\nTeething-ring', ' n. Ring for an infant to bite on while teething.'),
(36479, 't', '\r\nTeething', 'troubles  n.pl. Initial difficulties in an enterprise etc.'),
(36480, 't', '\r\nTeetotal', ' adj. Of or advocating total abstinence from alcohol.  teetotalism n. Teetotaller n. [reduplication of *total]'),
(36481, 't', '\r\nTeff', ' n. An african cereal. [amharic]'),
(36482, 't', '\r\nTefl', ' abbr. Teaching of english as a foreign language.'),
(36483, 't', '\r\nTeflon', ' n. Propr. Non-stick coating for kitchen utensils. [from tetra-, fluor-, -on]'),
(36484, 't', '\r\nTe-hee', ' var. Of *tee-hee.'),
(36485, 't', '\r\nTel.', ' Abbr. (also tel.) Telephone.'),
(36486, 't', '\r\nTele-', ' comb. Form 1 at or to a distance (telekinesis, telescope). 2 television (telecast). 3 by telephone (telesales). [greek tele far off]'),
(36487, 't', '\r\nTele-ad', ' n. Advertisement telephoned to a newspaper etc.'),
(36488, 't', '\r\nTelecast', ' â€”n. Television broadcast. â€”v. Transmit by television.  telecaster n.'),
(36489, 't', '\r\nTelecommunication', ' n. 1 communication over a distance by circuits using cable, fibre optics, satellites, radio etc. 2 (usu. In pl.) Technology of this.'),
(36490, 't', '\r\nTeleconference', ' n. Conference with participants linked by telephone etc.  teleconferencing n.'),
(36491, 't', '\r\nTelefax', ' n. = *fax. [abbreviation of telefacsimile]'),
(36492, 't', '\r\nTelegram', ' n. Message sent by telegraph and delivered in printed form.'),
(36493, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' since 1981 telegram has not been in uk official use, except for international messages. See also telemessage.'),
(36494, 't', '\r\nTelegraph', ' â€”n. (often attrib.) Device or system for transmitting messages or signals to a distance, esp. By making and breaking an electrical connection (telegraph wire). â€”v. 1 (often followed by to) send a message by telegraph to. 2 send or communicate by telegraph (telegraphed my concern). 3 give advance indication of (telegraphed his punch).  telegraphist n.'),
(36495, 't', '\r\nTelegraphic', ' adj. 1 of or by telegraphs or telegrams. 2 economically worded.  telegraphically adv.'),
(36496, 't', '\r\nTelegraphy', ' n. Communication by telegraph.'),
(36497, 't', '\r\nTelekinesis', ' n. Supposed paranormal force moving objects at a distance.  telekinetic adj. [greek kineo move]'),
(36498, 't', '\r\nTelemarketing', ' n. Marketing of goods etc. By unsolicited telephone calls.'),
(36499, 't', '\r\nTelemessage', ' n. Message sent by telephone or telex and delivered in printed form.'),
(36500, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' telemessage has been in uk official use since 1981 for inland messages, replacing telegram.'),
(36501, 't', '\r\nTelemetry', ' n. Process of recording the readings of an instrument and transmitting them by radio.  telemeter n.'),
(36502, 't', '\r\nTeleology', ' n. (pl. -ies) philos. 1 explanation of phenomena by the purpose they serve. 2 theol. Doctrine of design and purpose in the material world.  teleological adj. [greek telos end]'),
(36503, 't', '\r\nTelepathy', ' n. Supposed paranormal communication of thoughts.  telepathic adj. Telepathically adv.'),
(36504, 't', '\r\nTelephone', ' â€”n. 1 apparatus for transmitting sound (esp. Speech) to a distance, esp. By using optical or electrical signals. 2 handset etc. Used in this. 3 system of communication using a network of telephones. â€”v. (-ning) 1 speak to or send (a message) by telephone. 2 make a telephone call.  on the telephone having or using a telephone. Over the telephone using the telephone.  telephonic adj. Telephonically adv.'),
(36505, 't', '\r\nTelephone', 'book  n. = *telephone directory.'),
(36506, 't', '\r\nTelephone', 'booth  n. (also telephone kiosk, telephone box) booth etc. With a telephone for public use.'),
(36507, 't', '\r\nTelephone', 'directory  n. Book listing telephone subscribers and numbers.'),
(36508, 't', '\r\nTelephone', 'number  n. Number used to call a particular telephone.'),
(36509, 't', '\r\nTelephonist', ' n. Operator in a telephone exchange or at a switchboard.'),
(36510, 't', '\r\nTelephony', ' n. Transmission of sound by telephone.'),
(36511, 't', '\r\nTelephoto', ' n. (pl. -s) (in full telephoto lens) lens used in telephotography.'),
(36512, 't', '\r\nTelephotography', ' n. Photographing of distant objects with a system of lenses giving a large image.  telephotographic adj.'),
(36513, 't', '\r\nTeleprinter', ' n. Device for transmitting, receiving, and printing telegraph messages.'),
(36514, 't', '\r\nTeleprompter', ' n. Device beside a television or cinema camera that slowly unrolls a speaker''s script out of sight of the audience.'),
(36515, 't', '\r\nTelesales', ' n.pl. Selling by telephone.'),
(36516, 't', '\r\nTelescope', ' â€”n. 1 optical instrument using lenses or mirrors to magnify distant objects. 2 = *radio telescope. â€”v. (-ping) 1 press or drive (sections of a tube, colliding vehicles, etc.) Together so that one slides into another. 2 close or be capable of closing in this way. 3 compress so as to occupy less space or time.'),
(36517, 't', '\r\nTelescopic', ' adj. 1 of or made with a telescope (telescopic observations). 2 (esp. Of a lens) able to focus on and magnify distant objects. 3 consisting of sections that telescope.  telescopically adv.'),
(36518, 't', '\r\nTelescopic', 'sight  n. Telescope on a rifle etc. Used for sighting.'),
(36519, 't', '\r\nTeletext', ' n. Computerized news and information service transmitted to the televisions of subscribers.'),
(36520, 't', '\r\nTelethon', ' n. Exceptionally long television programme, esp. To raise money for charity. [from *tele-, *marathon]'),
(36521, 't', '\r\nTeletype', ' n. Propr. A kind of teleprinter.'),
(36522, 't', '\r\nTelevise', ' v. (-sing) broadcast on television.'),
(36523, 't', '\r\nTelevision', ' n. 1 system for reproducing on a screen visual images transmitted (usu. With sound) by radio signals or cable. 2 (in full television set) device with a screen for receiving these signals. 3 television broadcasting.  televisual adj.'),
(36524, 't', '\r\nTelex', ' (also telex) â€”n. International system of telegraphy by teleprinters using the public telecommunications network. â€”v. Send, or communicate with, by telex. [from *teleprinter, *exchange]'),
(36525, 't', '\r\nTell', ' v. (past and past part. Told) 1 relate in speech or writing (tell me a story). 2 make known; express in words (tell me your name). 3 reveal or signify to (a person) (your face tells me everything). 4 utter (tell lies). 5 a (often foll. By of, about) divulge information etc.; reveal a secret, the truth etc. (told her about venice; book tells you how to cook; promise you won''t tell; time will tell). B (foll. By on) colloq. Inform against. 6 (foll. By to + infin.) Direct; order (tell them to wait). 7 assure (it''s true, i tell you). 8 decide, determine, distinguish (tell one from the other). 9 (often foll. By on) produce a noticeable effect or influence (strain told on me; evidence tells against you). 10 (often absol.) Count (votes) at a meeting, election, etc.  tell apart distinguish between (could not tell them apart). Tell off colloq. Scold. Tell tales make known another person''s faults etc. Tell the time read the time from a clock or watch. You''re telling me colloq. I agree wholeheartedly. [old english: related to *tale]'),
(36526, 't', '\r\nTeller', ' n. 1 person working at the counter of a bank etc. 2 person who counts votes. 3 person who tells esp. Stories (teller of tales).'),
(36527, 't', '\r\nTelling', ' adj. Having a marked effect; striking; impressive.  tellingly adv.'),
(36528, 't', '\r\nTelling-off', ' n. (pl. Tellings-off) colloq. Scolding.'),
(36529, 't', '\r\nTell-tale', ' n. 1 person who reveals secrets about another. 2 (attrib.) That reveals or betrays (tell-tale smile). 3 automatic monitoring or registering device.'),
(36530, 't', '\r\nTellurium', ' n. Rare lustrous silver-white element used in semiconductors.  telluric adj. [latin tellus -ur- earth]'),
(36531, 't', '\r\nTelly', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. 1 television. 2 television set. [abbreviation]'),
(36532, 't', '\r\nTemerity', ' n. Rashness; audacity. [latin temere rashly]'),
(36533, 't', '\r\nTemp', ' colloq. â€”n. Temporary employee, esp. A secretary. â€”v. Work as a temp. [abbreviation]'),
(36534, 't', '\r\nTemper', ' â€”n. 1 mental disposition, mood (placid temper). 2 irritation or anger (fit of temper). 3 tendency to lose one''s temper (have a temper). 4 composure, calmness (lose one''s temper). 5 hardness or elasticity of metal. â€”v. 1 bring (metal or clay) to a proper hardness or consistency. 2 (foll. By with) moderate, mitigate (temper justice with mercy).  in a bad (or out of) temper irritable, angry. In a good temper amicable, happy. [latin tempero mingle]'),
(36535, 't', '\r\nTempera', ' n. 1 method of painting using an emulsion, e.g. Of pigment with egg-yolk and water, esp. On canvas. 2 this emulsion. [italian]'),
(36536, 't', '\r\nTemperament', ' n. Person''s or animal''s nature and character (nervous temperament). [latin: related to *temper]'),
(36537, 't', '\r\nTemperamental', ' adj. 1 of temperament. 2 a (of a person) unreliable; moody. B colloq. (of esp. A machine) unreliable, unpredictable.  temperamentally adv.'),
(36538, 't', '\r\nTemperance', ' n. 1 moderation, esp. In eating and drinking. 2 (often attrib.) Abstinence, esp. Total, from alcohol (temperance hotel). [latin: related to *temper]'),
(36539, 't', '\r\nTemperate', ' adj. 1 avoiding excess. 2 moderate. 3 (of a region or climate) mild. [latin: related to *temper]'),
(36540, 't', '\r\nTemperature', ' n. 1 measured or perceived degree of heat or cold of a thing, region, etc. 2 colloq. Body temperature above the normal (have a temperature). 3 degree of excitement in a discussion etc. [latin: related to *temper]'),
(36541, 't', '\r\nTempest', ' n. Violent storm. [latin tempus time]'),
(36542, 't', '\r\nTempestuous', ' adj. Stormy; turbulent.  tempestuously adv.'),
(36543, 't', '\r\nTempi', ' pl. Of *tempo.'),
(36544, 't', '\r\nTemplate', ' n. Piece of thin board or metal plate etc., used as a pattern in cutting or drilling etc. [originally templet, diminutive of temple, device in a loom to keep the cloth stretched]'),
(36545, 't', '\r\nTemple1', ' n. Building for the worship, or seen as the dwelling-place, of a god or gods etc. [latin templum]'),
(36546, 't', '\r\nTemple2', ' n. Flat part of either side of the head between the forehead and the ear. [french from latin]'),
(36547, 't', '\r\nTempo', ' n. (pl. -s or -pi) 1 speed at which music is or should be played. 2 speed or pace. [latin tempus -por- time]'),
(36548, 't', '\r\nTemporal', ' adj. 1 worldly as opposed to spiritual; secular. 2 of time. 3 gram. Denoting time or tense (temporal conjunction). 4 of the temples of the head (temporal artery). [latin tempus -por- time]'),
(36549, 't', '\r\nTemporary', ' â€”adj. Lasting or meant to last only for a limited time. â€”n. (pl. -ies) person employed temporarily.  temporarily adv. Temporariness n.'),
(36550, 't', '\r\nTemporize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 avoid committing oneself so as to gain time; procrastinate. 2 comply temporarily; adopt a time-serving policy.'),
(36551, 't', '\r\nTempt', ' v. 1 entice or incite (a person) to do what is wrong or forbidden (tempted him to steal it). 2 allure, attract. 3 risk provoking (fate etc.).  be tempted to be strongly disposed to.  tempter n. Temptress n. [latin tempto, tento try, test]'),
(36552, 't', '\r\nTemptation', ' n. 1 tempting or being tempted; incitement, esp. To wrongdoing. 2 attractive thing or course of action. 3 archaic putting to the test.'),
(36553, 't', '\r\nTempting', ' adj. Attractive, inviting.  temptingly adv.'),
(36554, 't', '\r\nTempura', ' n. Japanese dish of fish, shellfish, etc., fried in batter. [japanese]'),
(36555, 't', '\r\nTen', ' adj. & n. 1 one more than nine. 2 symbol for this (10, x, x). 3 size etc. Denoted by ten. 4 ten o''clock.  ten to one very probably. [old english]'),
(36556, 't', '\r\nTenable', ' adj. 1 maintainable or defensible against attack or objection (tenable position). 2 (foll. By for, by) (of an office etc.) That can be held for (a specified period) or by (a specified class of person).  tenability n. [french tenir hold]'),
(36557, 't', '\r\nTenacious', ' adj. 1 (often foll. By of) keeping a firm hold. 2 persistent, resolute. 3 (of memory) retentive.  tenaciously adv. Tenacity n. [latin tenax -acis from teneo hold]'),
(36558, 't', '\r\nTenancy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 status of or possession as a tenant. 2 duration of this.'),
(36559, 't', '\r\nTenant', ' n. 1 person who rents land or property from a landlord. 2 (often foll. By of) occupant of a place. [french: related to *tenable]'),
(36560, 't', '\r\nTenant', 'farmer  n. Person who farms rented land.'),
(36561, 't', '\r\nTenantry', ' n. Tenants of an estate etc.'),
