(36716, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' with reference to the last example, it is also legitimate to say you are older than him, with than treated as a preposition, esp. In less formal contexts.'),
(36717, 't', '\r\nThane', ' n. Hist. 1 man who held land from an english king or other superior by military service. 2 man who held land from a scottish king and ranked with an earl''s son; chief of a clan. [old english]'),
(36718, 't', '\r\nThank', ' â€”v. 1 express gratitude to (thanked him for the present). 2 hold responsible (you can thank yourself for that). â€”n. (in pl.) 1 gratitude. 2 expression of gratitude. 3 (as a formula) thank you (thanks for your help).  thank goodness (or god or heavens etc.) Colloq. Expression of relief etc. Thanks to as the result of (thanks to my foresight; thanks to your obstinacy). Thank you polite formula expressing gratitude. [old english]'),
(36719, 't', '\r\nThankful', ' adj. 1 grateful, pleased. 2 expressive of thanks.'),
(36720, 't', '\r\nThankfully', ' adv. 1 in a thankful manner. 2 let us be thankful (that) (thankfully, it didn''t rain).'),
(36721, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of thankfully in sense 2 is common, but is considered incorrect by some people.'),
(36722, 't', '\r\nThankless', ' adj. 1 not expressing or feeling gratitude. 2 (of a task etc.) Giving no pleasure or profit; unappreciated.'),
(36723, 't', '\r\nThanksgiving', ' n. 1 expression of gratitude, esp. To god. 2 (thanksgiving or thanksgiving day) fourth thursday in november (a national holiday in the us).'),
(36724, 't', '\r\nThat', ' â€”demons. Pron. (pl. Those) 1 person or thing indicated, named, or understood (i heard that; who is that in the garden?). 2 contrasted with this (this is much better than that). 3 (esp. In relative constructions) the one, the person, etc. (a table like that described above). 4  (pl. That) used instead of which or whom to introduce a defining clause (the book that you sent me; there is nothing here that matters). â€”demons. Adj. (pl. Those) designating the person or thing indicated, named, understood, etc. (cf. Sense 1 of pron.). â€”adv. 1 to such a degree; so (have done that much). 2 colloq. Very (not that good). â€”conj. Introducing a subordinate clause indicating: 1 statement or hypothesis (they say that he is better). 2 purpose (we eat that we may live). 3 result (am so sleepy that i cannot work).  all that very (not all that good). That is (or that is to say) formula introducing or following an explanation of a preceding word or words. That''s that formula indicating conclusion or completion. [old english]'),
(36725, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' in sense 4 of the pronoun, that usually specifies or identifies something referred to, whereas who or which need not: compare the book that you sent me is lost with the book, which i gave you, is lost. That is often omitted in senses 1 and 3 of the conjunction: they say he is ill.'),
(36726, 't', '\r\nThatch', ' â€”n. 1 roof-covering of straw, reeds, etc. 2 colloq. Hair of the head. â€”v. (also absol.) Cover with thatch.  thatcher n. [old english]'),
(36727, 't', '\r\nThaw', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By out) pass from a frozen into a liquid or unfrozen state. 2 (usu. Prec. By it as subject) (of the weather) become warm enough to melt ice etc. 3 become warm enough to lose numbness etc.'),
(36728, 't', '\r\nThe', ' â€”adj. (called the definite article) 1 denoting person(s) or thing(s) already mentioned, under discussion, implied, or familiar (gave the man a wave). 2 describing as unique (the thames). 3 a (foll. By defining adj.) Which is, who are, etc. (edward the seventh). B (foll. By adj. Used absol.) Denoting a class described (from the sublime to the ridiculous). 4 best known or best entitled to the name (with the stressed: do you mean the kipling?). 5 indicating a following defining clause or phrase (the book that you borrowed). 6 a indicating that a singular noun represents a species etc. (the cat is a mammal). B used with a noun which figuratively represents an occupation etc. (went on the stage). C (foll. By the name of a unit) a, per (5p in the pound). â€”adv. (preceding comparatives in expressions of proportional variation) in or by that (or such a) degree; on that account (the more the merrier; the more he has the more he wants). [old english]'),
(36729, 't', '\r\nTheater', ' n. (brit. Theatre) 1 building or outdoor area for dramatic performances. 2 writing and production of plays. 3 room or hall for lectures etc. With seats in tiers. 4 operating theatre. 5 a scene or field of action (the theatre of war). B (attrib.) Designating weapons intermediate between tactical and strategic. [greek theatron]'),
(36730, 't', '\r\nTheatre', ' n. (us theater) 1 building or outdoor area for dramatic performances. 2 writing and production of plays. 3 room or hall for lectures etc. With seats in tiers. 4 operating theatre. 5 a scene or field of action (the theatre of war). B (attrib.) Designating weapons intermediate between tactical and strategic. [greek theatron]'),
(36731, 't', '\r\nTheatrical', ' â€”adj. 1 of or for the theatre or acting. 2 (of a manner or person etc.) Calculated for effect; showy. â€”n. (in pl.) Dramatic performances (amateur theatricals).  theatricality n. Theatrically adv.'),
(36732, 't', '\r\nThee', ' objective case of *thou1.'),
(36733, 't', '\r\nTheft', ' n. Act of stealing. [old english: related to *thief]'),
(36734, 't', '\r\nTheir', ' poss. Pron. (attrib.) Of or belonging to them. [old norse]'),
(36735, 't', '\r\nTheirs', ' poss. Pron. The one or ones of or belonging to them (it is theirs; theirs are over here).  of theirs of or belonging to them (a friend of theirs).'),
(36736, 't', '\r\nTheism', ' n. Belief in gods or a god, esp. A god supernaturally revealed to man.  theist n. Theistic adj. [greek theos god]'),
(36737, 't', '\r\nThem', ' â€”pron. 1 objective case of *they. 2 colloq. They (it''s them again). â€”demons. Adj. Slang or dial. Those. [old norse]'),
(36738, 't', '\r\nTheme', ' n. 1 subject or topic of a talk, book, etc. 2 mus. Prominent melody in a composition. 3 us school exercise on a given subject.  thematic adj. Thematically adv. [greek thema -mat-]'),
(36739, 't', '\r\nTheme', 'park  n. Amusement park organized round a unifying idea.'),
(36740, 't', '\r\nTheme', 'song  n. (also theme tune) 1 recurrent melody in a musical play or film. 2 signature tune.'),
(36741, 't', '\r\nThemselves', ' pron. 1 emphat. Form of *they or *them. 2 refl. Form of *them.  be themselves act in their normal, unconstrained manner. By themselves see by oneself.'),
(36742, 't', '\r\nThen', ' â€”adv. 1 at that time. 2 a next; after that. B and also. 3 a in that case (then you should have said so). B implying grudging or impatient concession (all right then, if you must). C used parenthetically to resume a narrative etc. (the policeman, then, knocked on the door). â€”attrib. Adj. Such at the time in question (the then king). â€”n. That time (until then).  then and there immediately and on the spot. [old english]'),
(36743, 't', '\r\nThence', ' adv. (also from thence) archaic or literary 1 from that place. 2 for that reason. [old english]'),
(36744, 't', '\r\nThenceforth', ' adv. (also thenceforward) archaic or literary from that time onward.'),
(36745, 't', '\r\nTheo-', ' comb. Form god or god(s). [greek theos god]'),
(36746, 't', '\r\nTheocracy', ' n. (pl. -ies) form of government by god or a god directly, or through a priestly order etc.  theocratic adj.'),
(36747, 't', '\r\nTheodolite', ' n. Surveying-instrument for measuring horizontal and vertical angles with a rotating telescope. [origin unknown]'),
(36748, 't', '\r\nTheologian', ' n. Expert in theology. [french: related to *theology]'),
(36749, 't', '\r\nTheology', ' n. (pl. -ies) the study or a system of theistic (esp. Christian) religion.  theological adj. Theologically adv. [greek: related to *theo-]'),
(36750, 't', '\r\nTheorem', ' n. Esp. Math. 1 general proposition that is not self-evident but is proved by reasoning. 2 rule in algebra etc., esp. One expressed by symbols or formulae. [greek theoreo look at]'),
(36751, 't', '\r\nTheoretical', ' adj. 1 concerned with knowledge but not with its practical application. 2 based on theory rather than experience.  theoretically adv.'),
(36752, 't', '\r\nTheoretician', ' n. Person concerned with the theoretical aspects of a subject.'),
(36753, 't', '\r\nTheorist', ' n. Holder or inventor of a theory.'),
(36754, 't', '\r\nTheorize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) evolve or indulge in theories.'),
(36755, 't', '\r\nTheory', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 supposition or system of ideas explaining something, esp. One based on general principles independent of the particular things to be explained (atomic theory; theory of evolution). 2 speculative (esp. Fanciful) view (one of my pet theories). 3 abstract knowledge or speculative thought (all very well in theory). 4 exposition of the principles of a science etc. (the theory of music). 5 collection of propositions to illustrate the principles of a mathematical subject (probability theory). [greek: related to *theorem]'),
(36756, 't', '\r\nTheosophy', ' n. (pl. -ies) any of various philosophies professing to achieve knowledge of god by spiritual ecstasy, direct intuition, or special individual relations, esp. A modern movement following hindu and buddhist teachings and seeking universal brotherhood.  theosophical adj. Theosophist n. [greek theosophos wise concerning god]'),
(36757, 't', '\r\nTherapeutic', ' adj. 1 of, for, or contributing to, the cure of disease. 2 soothing, conducive to well-being.  therapeutically adv. [greek therapeuo wait on, cure]'),
(36758, 't', '\r\nTherapeutics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Branch of medicine concerned with cures and remedies.'),
(36759, 't', '\r\nTherapy', ' n. (pl. -ies) non-surgical treatment of disease or disability.  therapist n. [greek therapeia healing]'),
(36760, 't', '\r\nThere', ' â€”adv. 1 in, at, or to that place or position (lived there for a year; goes there daily). 2 at that point (in speech, performance, writing, etc.). 3 in that respect (i agree with you there). 4 used for emphasis in calling attention (you there!). 5 used to indicate the fact or existence of something (there is a house on the corner). â€”n. That place (lives near there). â€”int. 1 expressing confirmation, triumph, etc. (there! What did i tell you?). 2 used to soothe a child etc. (there, there, never mind).  there and then = then and there. [old english]'),
(36761, 't', '\r\nThereabouts', ' adv. (also thereabout) 1 near that place. 2 near that number, quantity, etc.'),
(36762, 't', '\r\nThereafter', ' adv. Formal after that.'),
(36763, 't', '\r\nThereby', ' adv. By that means, as a result of that.  thereby hangs a tale much could be said about that.'),
(36764, 't', '\r\nTherefore', ' adv. For that reason; accordingly, consequently.'),
(36765, 't', '\r\nTherein', ' adv. Formal 1 in that place etc. 2 in that respect.'),
(36766, 't', '\r\nThereof', ' adv. Formal of that or it.'),
(36767, 't', '\r\nThereto', ' adv. Formal 1 to that or it. 2 in addition.'),
(36768, 't', '\r\nThereupon', ' adv. 1 in consequence of that. 2 immediately after that.'),
(36769, 't', '\r\nTherm', ' n. Unit of heat, esp. As the statutory unit of gas supplied, equivalent to 100,000 british thermal units (1.055 x 108 joules). [greek therme heat]'),
(36770, 't', '\r\nThermal', ' â€”adj. 1 of, for, or producing heat. 2 promoting the retention of heat (thermal underwear). â€”n. Rising current of warm air (used by gliders etc. To gain height).  thermally adv. [french: related to *therm]'),
(36771, 't', '\r\nThermal', 'unit  n. Unit for measuring heat.'),
(36772, 't', '\r\nThermionic', ' adj. Of electrons emitted from a very hot substance. [from *thermo-, *ion]'),
(36773, 't', '\r\nThermionic', 'valve  n. Device giving a flow of thermionic electrons in one direction, used esp. In the rectification of a current and in radio reception.'),
(36774, 't', '\r\nThermo-', ' comb. Form heat. [greek]'),
(36775, 't', '\r\nThermocouple', ' n. Device for measuring temperatures by means of a pair of different metals in contact at a point and generating a thermoelectric voltage.'),
(36776, 't', '\r\nThermodynamics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Science of the relations between heat and other forms of energy.  thermodynamic adj.'),
(36777, 't', '\r\nThermoelectric', ' adj. Producing electricity by a difference of temperatures.'),
(36778, 't', '\r\nThermometer', ' n. Instrument for measuring temperature, esp. A graduated glass tube containing mercury or alcohol. [french: related to *thermo-, *-meter]'),
(36779, 't', '\r\nThermonuclear', ' adj. 1 relating to nuclear reactions that occur only at very high temperatures. 2 (of weapons) using thermonuclear reactions.'),
(36780, 't', '\r\nThermoplastic', ' â€”adj. That becomes plastic on heating and hardens on cooling. â€”n. Thermoplastic substance.'),
(36781, 't', '\r\nThermos', ' n. (in full thermos flask) propr. Vacuum flask. [greek: related to *thermo-]'),
(36782, 't', '\r\nThermosetting', ' adj. (of plastics) setting permanently when heated.'),
(36783, 't', '\r\nThermosphere', ' n. Region of the atmosphere beyond the mesosphere.'),
(36784, 't', '\r\nThermostat', ' n. Device that automatically regulates or responds to temperature.  thermostatic adj. Thermostatically adv. [from *thermo-, greek statos standing]'),
(36785, 't', '\r\nThesaurus', ' n. (pl. -ri or -ruses) book that lists words in groups of synonyms and related concepts. [greek: related to *treasure]'),
(36786, 't', '\r\nThese', ' pl. Of *this.'),
(36787, 't', '\r\nThesis', ' n. (pl. Theses) 1 proposition to be maintained or proved. 2 dissertation, esp. By a candidate for a higher degree. [greek, = putting]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(36788, 't', '\r\nThespian', ' â€”adj. Of drama. â€”n. Actor or actress. [greek thespis, name of a greek tragedian]'),
(36789, 't', '\r\nTheta', ' n. Eighth letter of the greek alphabet (q, q). [greek]'),
(36790, 't', '\r\nThey', ' pron. (obj. Them; poss. Their, theirs) 1 pl. Of *he, *she, *it. 2 people in general (so they say). 3 those in authority (they have raised taxes). [old norse]'),
(36791, 't', '\r\nThey''d', ' contr. 1 they had. 2 they would.'),
(36792, 't', '\r\nThey''ll', ' contr. 1 they will. 2 they shall.'),
(36793, 't', '\r\nThey''re', ' contr. They are.'),
(36794, 't', '\r\nThey''ve', ' contr. They have.'),
(36795, 't', '\r\nThiamine', ' n. (also thiamin) b vitamin found in unrefined cereals, beans, and liver, a deficiency of which causes beriberi. [greek theion sulphur, amin from *vitamin]'),
(36796, 't', '\r\nThick', ' â€”adj. 1 of great or specified extent between opposite surfaces. 2 (of a line etc.) Broad; not fine. 3 arranged closely; crowded together; dense. 4 (usu. Foll. By with) densely covered or filled (air thick with smoke). 5 a firm in consistency; containing much solid matter. B made of thick material (a thick coat). 6 a muddy, cloudy; impenetrable by sight. B (of one''s head) suffering from a hangover, headache, etc. 7 colloq. Stupid. 8 a (of a voice) indistinct. B (of an accent) very marked. 9 colloq. Intimate, very friendly. â€”n. Thick part of anything. â€”adv. Thickly (snow was falling thick).  a bit thick colloq. Unreasonable or intolerable. In the thick of at the busiest part of. Through thick and thin under all conditions; in spite of all difficulties.  thickish adj. Thickly adv. [old english]'),
(36797, 't', '\r\nThicken', ' v. 1 make or become thick or thicker. 2 become more complicated (plot thickens).  thickener n.'),
(36798, 't', '\r\nThickening', ' n. 1 becoming thick or thicker. 2 substance used to thicken liquid. 3 thickened part.'),
(36799, 't', '\r\nThicket', ' n. Tangle of shrubs or trees. [old english: related to *thick]'),
(36800, 't', '\r\nThickhead', ' n. Colloq. Stupid person.  thickheaded adj.'),
(36801, 't', '\r\nThickness', ' n. 1 being thick. 2 extent of this. 3 layer of material (use three thicknesses).'),
(36802, 't', '\r\nThickset', ' adj. 1 heavily or solidly built. 2 set or growing close together.'),
(36803, 't', '\r\nThick-skinned', ' adj. Not sensitive to criticism.'),
(36804, 't', '\r\nThief', ' n. (pl. Thieves) person who steals, esp. Secretly. [old english]'),
(36805, 't', '\r\nThieve', ' v. (-ving) 1 be a thief. 2 steal (a thing). [old english: related to *thief]'),
(36806, 't', '\r\nThievery', ' n. Stealing.'),
(36807, 't', '\r\nThievish', ' adj. Given to stealing.'),
(36808, 't', '\r\nThigh', ' n. Part of the leg between the hip and the knee. [old english]'),
(36809, 't', '\r\nThigh-bone', ' n. = *femur.'),
(36810, 't', '\r\nThimble', ' n. Metal or plastic cap worn to protect the finger and push the needle in sewing. [old english: related to *thumb]'),
(36811, 't', '\r\nThimbleful', ' n. (pl. -s) small quantity, esp. Of drink.'),
(36812, 't', '\r\nThin', ' â€”adj. (thinner, thinnest) 1 having opposite surfaces close together; of small thickness or diameter. 2 (of a line) narrow or fine. 3 made of thin material (thin dress). 4 lean; not plump. 5 not dense or copious (thin hair). 6 of slight consistency. 7 weak; lacking an important ingredient (thin blood; a thin voice). 8 (of an excuse etc.) Flimsy or transparent. â€”adv. Thinly (cut the bread very thin). â€”v. (-nn-) 1 (often foll. By down) make or become thin or thinner. 2 (often foll. By out) make or become less dense or crowded or numerous.  have a thin time colloq. Have a wretched or uncomfortable time. Thin on the ground few in number. Thin on top balding.  thinly adv. Thinness n. Thinnish adj. [old english]'),
(36813, 't', '\r\nThine', ' poss. Pron. Archaic 1 (predic. Or absol.) Of or belonging to thee. 2 (attrib. Before a vowel) = *thy. [old english]'),
(36814, 't', '\r\nThin', 'end of the wedge  see *wedge.'),
(36815, 't', '\r\nThing', ' n. 1 entity, idea, action, etc., that exists or may be thought about or perceived. 2 inanimate material object (take that thing away). 3 unspecified item (a few things to buy). 4 act, idea, or utterance (silly thing to do). 5 event (unfortunate thing to happen). 6 quality (patience is a useful thing). 7 person regarded with pity, contempt, or affection (poor thing!). 8 specimen or type (latest thing in hats). 9 colloq. One''s special interest (not my thing). 10 colloq. Something remarkable (there''s a thing!). 11 (prec. By the) colloq. A what is proper or fashionable. B what is needed (just the thing). C what is to be considered (the thing is, shall we go or not?). D what is important. 12 (in pl.) Personal belongings or clothing (where are my things?). 13 (in pl.) Equipment (painting things). 14 (in pl.) Affairs in general (not in the nature of things). 15 (in pl.) Circumstances, conditions (things look good). 16 (in pl. With a following adjective) all that is so describable (things greek).  do one''s own thing colloq. Pursue one''s own interests or inclinations. Have a thing about colloq. Be obsessed or prejudiced about. Make a thing of colloq. 1 regard as essential. 2 cause a fuss about. [old english]'),
(36816, 't', '\r\nThingummy', ' n. (pl. -ies) (also thingumabob, thingumajig) colloq. Person or thing whose name one has forgotten or does not know.'),
(36817, 't', '\r\nThink', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Thought) 1 be of the opinion (think that they will come). 2 judge or consider (is thought to be a fraud). 3 exercise the mind (let me think for a moment). 4 (foll. By of or about) a consider; be or become aware of. B form or entertain the idea of; imagine. 5 have a half-formed intention (i think i''ll stay). 6 form a conception of. 7 recognize the presence or existence of (thought no harm in it). â€”n. Colloq. Act of thinking (have a think).  think again revise one''s plans or opinions. Think aloud utter one''s thoughts as soon as they occur. Think better of change one''s mind about (an intention) after reconsideration. Think fit see *fit1. Think little (or nothing) of consider to be insignificant. Think much (or a lot or highly) of have a high opinion of. Think out 1 consider carefully. 2 produce (an idea etc.) By thinking. Think over reflect upon in order to reach a decision. Think through reflect fully upon (a problem etc.). Think twice use careful consideration, avoid hasty action, etc. Think up colloq. Devise. [old english]'),
(36818, 't', '\r\nThinker', ' n. 1 person who thinks, esp. In a specified way (an original thinker). 2 person with a skilled or powerful mind.'),
(36819, 't', '\r\nThinking', ' â€”attrib. Adj. Intelligent, rational. â€”n. Opinion or judgement.'),
(36820, 't', '\r\nThink-tank', ' n. Colloq. Body of experts providing advice and ideas on national or commercial problems.'),
(36821, 't', '\r\nThinner', ' n. Solvent for diluting paint etc.'),
(36822, 't', '\r\nThin-skinned', ' adj. Sensitive to criticism.'),
(36823, 't', '\r\nThiosulphate', ' n. Sulphate in which one oxygen atom is replaced by sulphur. [greek theion sulphur]'),
(36824, 't', '\r\nThird', ' adj. & n. 1 next after second. 2 each of three equal parts of a thing.  thirdly adv. [old english: related to *three]'),
(36825, 't', '\r\nThird', 'degree  â€”n. Long and severe questioning, esp. By police to obtain information or a confession. â€”adj. (third-degree) denoting burns of the most severe kind, affecting lower layers of tissue.'),
(36826, 't', '\r\nThird', 'man  n. Fielder positioned near the boundary behind the slips.'),
(36827, 't', '\r\nThird', 'party  â€”n. 1 another party besides the two principals. 2 bystander etc. â€”adj. (third-party) (of insurance) covering damage or injury suffered by a person other than the insured.'),
(36828, 't', '\r\nThird', 'person  n. 1 = *third party. 2 gram. See *person.'),
(36829, 't', '\r\nThird-rate', ' adj. Inferior; very poor.'),
(36830, 't', '\r\nThird', 'reading  n. Third presentation of a bill to a legislative assembly.'),
(36831, 't', '\r\nThird', 'reich  n. Nazi regime, 1933-45.'),
(36832, 't', '\r\nThird', 'world  n. (usu. Prec. By the) developing countries of asia, africa, and latin america.'),
(36833, 't', '\r\nThirst', ' â€”n. 1 need to drink; discomfort caused by this. 2 desire, craving. â€”v. (often foll. By for or after) 1 feel thirst. 2 have a strong desire. [old english]'),
(36834, 't', '\r\nThirsty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 feeling thirst. 2 (of land, a season, etc.) Dry or parched. 3 (often foll. By for or after) eager. 4 colloq. Causing thirst (thirsty work).  thirstily adv. Thirstiness n. [old english: related to *thirst]'),
(36835, 't', '\r\nThirteen', ' adj. & n. 1 one more than twelve. 2 symbol for this (13, xiii, xiii). 3 size etc. Denoted by thirteen.  thirteenth adj. & n. [old english: related to *three]'),
(36836, 't', '\r\nThirty', ' adj. & n. (pl. -ies) 1 three times ten. 2 symbol for this (30, xxx, xxx). 3 (in pl.) Numbers from 30 to 39, esp. The years of a century or of a person''s life.  thirtieth adj. & n. [old english: related to *three]'),
(36837, 't', '\r\nThirty-nine', 'articles  n.pl. Points of doctrine assented to by those taking orders in the church of england.'),
(36838, 't', '\r\nThis', ' â€”demons. Pron. (pl. These) 1 person or thing close at hand or indicated or already named or understood (can you see this?; this is my cousin). 2 (contrasted with that) the person or thing nearer to hand or more immediately in mind. â€”demons. Adj. (pl. These) 1 designating the person or thing close at hand etc. (cf. Senses 1, 2 of pron.). 2 (of time) the present or current (am busy all this week). 3 colloq. (in narrative) designating a person or thing previously unmentioned (then up came this policeman). â€”adv. To the degree or extent indicated (knew him when he was this high).  this and that colloq. Various unspecified things. [old english]'),
(36839, 't', '\r\nThistle', ' n. 1 prickly plant, usu. With globular heads of purple flowers. 2 this as the scottish national emblem. [old english]'),
(36840, 't', '\r\nThistledown', ' n. Light down containing thistle-seeds and blown about in the wind.'),
(36841, 't', '\r\nThistly', ' adj. Overgrown with thistles.'),
(36842, 't', '\r\nThither', ' adv. Archaic or formal to or towards that place. [old english]'),
(36843, 't', '\r\nTho''', ' (also tho) var. Of *though.'),
(36844, 't', '\r\nThole', ' n. (in full thole-pin) 1 pin in the gunwale of a boat as the fulcrum for an oar. 2 each of two such pins forming a rowlock. [old english]'),
(36845, 't', '\r\nThong', ' n. Narrow strip of hide or leather. [old english]'),
(36846, 't', '\r\nThorax', ' n. (pl. -races or -raxes) anat. & zool. Part of the trunk between the neck and the abdomen.  thoracic adj. [latin from greek]'),
(36847, 't', '\r\nThorium', ' n. Chem. Radioactive metallic element. [thor, name of scandinavian god of thunder]'),
(36848, 't', '\r\nThorn', ' n. 1 sharp-pointed projection on a plant. 2 thorn-bearing shrub or tree.  thorn in one''s flesh (or side) constant nuisance.  thornless adj. [old english]'),
(36849, 't', '\r\nThorny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 having many thorns. 2 problematic, causing disagreement.  thornily adv. Thorniness n. [old english: related to *thorn]'),
(36850, 't', '\r\nThorough', ' adj. 1 complete and unqualified; not superficial. 2 acting or done with great care and completeness. 3 absolute (thorough nuisance).  thoroughly adv. Thoroughness n. [related to *through]'),
(36851, 't', '\r\nThoroughbred', ' â€”adj. 1 of pure breed. 2 high-spirited. â€”n. Thoroughbred animal, esp. A horse.'),
(36852, 't', '\r\nThoroughfare', ' n. Road or path open at both ends, esp. For traffic.'),
(36853, 't', '\r\nThoroughgoing', ' attrib. Adj. Thorough; complete.'),
(36854, 't', '\r\nThose', ' pl. Of *that.'),
(36855, 't', '\r\nThou1', ' pron. (obj. Thee; poss. Thy or thine; pl. Ye or you) archaic second person singular pronoun. [old english]'),
(36856, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' thou has now been replaced by you except in some formal, liturgical, dialect, and poetic uses.'),
(36857, 't', '\r\nThou2', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) colloq. 1 thousand. 2 one thousandth. [abbreviation]'),
(36858, 't', '\r\nThough', ' (also tho'') â€”conj. 1 despite the fact that; in spite of being (though it was early we left; though annoyed, i agreed). 2 (introducing a possibility) even if (ask him though he may refuse). 3 and yet; nevertheless. â€”adv. Colloq. However; all the same. [old norse]'),
(36859, 't', '\r\nThought1', ' n. 1 process or power of thinking; faculty of reason. 2 way of thinking associated with a particular time, group, etc. 3 sober reflection or consideration. 4 idea or piece of reasoning produced by thinking. 5 (foll. By of + verbal noun or to + infin.) Partly formed intention (had no thought to go). 6 (usu. In pl.) What one is thinking; one''s opinion. 7 (prec. By a) somewhat (a thought arrogant).  in thought meditating. [old english: related to *think]'),
(36860, 't', '\r\nThought2', ' past and past part. Of *think.'),
(36861, 't', '\r\nThoughtful', ' adj. 1 engaged in or given to meditation. 2 (of a book, writer, etc.) Giving signs of serious thought. 3 (often foll. By of) (of a person or conduct) considerate.  thoughtfully adv. Thoughtfulness n.'),
(36862, 't', '\r\nThoughtless', ' adj. 1 careless of consequences or of others'' feelings. 2 due to lack of thought.  thoughtlessly adv. Thoughtlessness n.'),
(36863, 't', '\r\nThought-reader', ' n. Person supposedly able to perceive another''s thoughts.'),
(36864, 't', '\r\nThousand', ' adj. & n. (pl. Thousands or (in sense 1) thousand) (in sing. Prec. By a or one) 1 ten hundred. 2 symbol for this (1,000, m, m). 3 (in sing. Or pl.) Colloq. Large number.  thousandfold adj. & adv. Thousandth adj. & n. [old english]'),
(36865, 't', '\r\nThrall', ' n. Literary 1 (often foll. By of, to) slave (of a person, or of a power or influence). 2 slavery (in thrall).  thraldom n. [old english from old norse]'),
(36866, 't', '\r\nThrash', ' â€”v. 1 beat or whip severely. 2 defeat thoroughly. 3 deliver repeated blows. 4 (foll. By about, around) move or fling (esp. The limbs) about violently. 5 = *thresh 1. â€”n. 1 act of thrashing. 2 slang (esp. Lavish) party.  thrash out discuss to a conclusion. [old english]'),
(36867, 't', '\r\nThread', ' â€”n. 1 a spun-out cotton, silk, or glass etc.; yarn. B length of this. 2 thin cord of twisted yarns used esp. In sewing and weaving. 3 continuous aspect of a thing (the thread of life; thread of his argument). 4 spiral ridge of a screw. â€”v. 1 pass a thread through (a needle). 2 put (beads) on a thread. 3 insert (a strip of material, e.g. Film or magnetic tape) into equipment. 4 make (one''s way) carefully through a crowded place, over a difficult route, etc. [old english: related to *throw]'),
(36868, 't', '\r\nThreadbare', ' adj. 1 (of cloth) with the nap worn away and the thread visible. 2 (of a person) wearing such clothes. 3 hackneyed.'),
(36869, 't', '\r\nThreadworm', ' n. Parasitic threadlike worm.'),
(36870, 't', '\r\nThreat', ' n. 1 declaration of an intention to punish or hurt if an order etc. Is not obeyed. 2 indication of something undesirable coming (threat of war). 3 person or thing as a likely cause of harm etc. [old english]'),
(36871, 't', '\r\nThreaten', ' v. 1 make a threat or threats against. 2 be a sign of (something undesirable). 3 (foll. By to + infin.) Announce one''s intention to do an undesirable thing. 4 (also absol.) Warn of the infliction of (harm etc.).'),
(36872, 't', '\r\nThree', ' adj. & n. 1 a one more than two. B symbol for this (3, iii, iii). 2 size etc. Denoted by three. [old english]'),
(36873, 't', '\r\nThree-cornered', ' adj. 1 triangular. 2 (of a contest etc.) Between three parties.'),
(36874, 't', '\r\nThree-decker', ' n. 1 warship with three gun-decks. 2 thing with three levels or divisions.'),
(36875, 't', '\r\nThree-dimensional', ' adj. Having or appearing to have length, breadth, and depth.'),
(36876, 't', '\r\nThreefold', ' adj. & adv. 1 three times as much or as many. 2 consisting of three parts.'),
(36877, 't', '\r\nThree-legged', 'race  n. Running-race between pairs, one member of each pair having the left leg tied to the right leg of the other.'),
(36878, 't', '\r\nThree-line', 'whip  n. Written notice to mps from their leader insisting on attendance at a debate and voting a certain way.'),
(36879, 't', '\r\nThreepence', ' n. Sum of three pence.'),
(36880, 't', '\r\nThreepenny', ' attrib. Adj. Costing three pence.'),
(36881, 't', '\r\nThree-piece', ' â€”n. Three-piece suit or suite. â€”attrib. Adj. (esp. Of a suit or suite) consisting of three items.'),
(36882, 't', '\r\nThree-ply', ' â€”adj. Of three strands or layers etc. â€”n. 1 three-ply wool. 2 three-ply wood.'),
(36883, 't', '\r\nThree-point', 'turn  n. Method of turning a vehicle round in a narrow space by moving forwards, backwards, and forwards again.'),
(36884, 't', '\r\nThree-quarter', ' n. (also three-quarter back) rugby any of three or four players just behind the half-backs.'),
(36885, 't', '\r\nThree-quarters', ' n.pl. Three parts out of four.'),
(36886, 't', '\r\nThree', 'rs  n.pl. (prec. By the) reading, writing, and arithmetic.'),
(36887, 't', '\r\nThreescore', ' n. & adj. Archaic sixty.'),
(36888, 't', '\r\nThreesome', ' n. Group of three persons.'),
(36889, 't', '\r\nThree-way', ' adj. Involving three directions or participants.'),
(36890, 't', '\r\nThrenody', ' n. (pl. -ies) song of lamentation or mourning. [greek]'),
(36891, 't', '\r\nThresh', ' v. 1 beat out or separate grain from (corn etc.). 2 = *thrash v. 4.  thresher n. [old english]'),
(36892, 't', '\r\nThreshing-floor', ' n. Hard level floor for threshing esp. With flails.'),
(36893, 't', '\r\nThreshold', ' n. 1 strip of wood or stone forming the bottom of a doorway and crossed in entering a house etc. 2 point of entry or beginning. 3 limit below which a stimulus causes no reaction. [old english: related to *thrash in the sense â€˜treadâ€™]'),
(36894, 't', '\r\nThrew', ' past of *throw.'),
(36895, 't', '\r\nThrice', ' adv. Archaic or literary 1 three times. 2 (esp. In comb.) Highly (thrice-blessed). [related to *three]'),
(36896, 't', '\r\nThrift', ' n. 1 frugality; careful use of money etc. 2 the sea pink. [old norse: related to *thrive]'),
(36897, 't', '\r\nThriftless', ' adj. Wasteful.'),
(36898, 't', '\r\nThrifty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) economical.  thriftily adv. Thriftiness n.'),
(36899, 't', '\r\nThrill', ' â€”n. 1 wave or nervous tremor of emotion or sensation (a thrill of joy). 2 throb, pulsation. â€”v. 1 (cause to) feel a thrill. 2 quiver or throb with or as with emotion. [old english, = pierce: related to *through]'),
(36900, 't', '\r\nThriller', ' n. Exciting or sensational story or play etc., esp. About crime or espionage.'),
(36901, 't', '\r\nThrips', ' n. (pl. Same) an insect harmful to plants. [greek, = woodworm]'),
(36902, 't', '\r\nThrive', ' v. (-ving; past throve or thrived; past part. Thriven or thrived) 1 prosper, flourish. 2 grow rich. 3 (of a child, animal, or plant) grow vigorously. [old norse]'),
(36903, 't', '\r\nThro''', ' var. Of *through.'),
(36904, 't', '\r\nThroat', ' n. 1 a windpipe or gullet. B front part of the neck containing this. 2 literary narrow passage, entrance, or exit.  cut one''s own throat harm oneself or one''s interests. Ram (or thrust) down a person''s throat force on a person''s attention. [old english]'),
(36905, 't', '\r\nThroaty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) (of a voice) hoarsely resonant.  throatily adv. Throatiness n.'),
(36906, 't', '\r\nThrob', ' â€”v. (-bb-) 1 pulsate, esp. With more than the usual force or rapidity. 2 vibrate with a persistent rhythm or with emotion. â€”n. 1 throbbing. 2 (esp. Violent) pulsation. [imitative]'),
(36907, 't', '\r\nThroe', ' n. (usu. In pl.) Violent pang, esp. Of childbirth or death.  in the throes of struggling with the task of. [old english, alteration of original throwe, perhaps by association with woe]'),
(36908, 't', '\r\nThrombosis', ' n. (pl. -boses) coagulation of the blood in a blood-vessel or organ. [greek, = curdling]'),
(36909, 't', '\r\nThrone', ' â€”n. 1 chair of state for a sovereign or bishop etc. 2 sovereign power (came to the throne). â€”v. (-ning) enthrone. [greek thronos]'),
(36910, 't', '\r\nThrong', ' â€”n. (often foll. By of) crowd, esp. Of people. â€”v. 1 come in great numbers (crowds thronged to the stadium). 2 flock into or crowd round; fill with or as with a crowd. [old english]'),
(36911, 't', '\r\nThrostle', ' n. Song thrush. [old english]'),
(36912, 't', '\r\nThrottle', ' â€”n. 1 a valve controlling the flow of fuel or steam etc. In an engine. B (in full throttle-lever) lever or pedal operating this valve. 2 throat, gullet, or windpipe. â€”v. (-ling) 1 choke or strangle. 2 prevent the utterance etc. Of. 3 control (an engine or steam etc.) With a throttle.  throttle back (or down) reduce the speed of (an engine or vehicle) by throttling. [perhaps from *throat]'),
(36913, 't', '\r\nThrough', ' (also thro'', us thru) â€”prep. 1 a from end to end or side to side of. B going in one side or end and out the other of. 2 between or among (swam through the waves). 3 from beginning to end of (read through the letter; went through many difficulties). 4 because of; by the agency, means, or fault of (lost it through carelessness). 5 us up to and including (monday through friday). â€”adv. 1 through a thing; from side to side, end to end, or beginning to end. 2 so as to be connected by telephone (will put you through). â€”attrib. Adj. 1 (of a journey, route, etc.) Done without a change of line or vehicle etc. Or with one ticket. 2 (of traffic) going through a place to its destination. 3 (of a road) open at both ends.  be through colloq. 1 (often foll. By with) have finished. 2 (often foll. By with) cease to have dealings. 3 have no further prospects. Through and through thoroughly, completely. [old english]'),
(36914, 't', '\r\nThroughout', ' â€”prep. Right through; from end to end of. â€”adv. In every part or respect.'),
(36915, 't', '\r\nThroughput', ' n. Amount of material put through a process, esp. In manufacturing or computing.'),
(36916, 't', '\r\nThrove', ' past of *thrive.'),
(36917, 't', '\r\nThrow', ' â€”v. (past threw; past part. Thrown) 1 propel with force through the air. 2 force violently into, or compel to be in, a specified position or state (thrown on the rocks; threw themselves down; thrown out of work). 3 turn or move (part of the body) quickly or suddenly (threw an arm out). 4 project or cast (light, a shadow, etc.). 5 a bring to the ground in wrestling. B (of a horse) unseat (its rider). 6 colloq. Disconcert (the question threw me). 7 (foll. By on, off, etc.) Put (clothes etc.) Hastily on or off etc. 8 a cause (dice) to fall on a table etc. B obtain (a specified number) by throwing dice. 9 cause to pass or extend suddenly to another state or position (threw a bridge across the river). 10 operate (a switch or lever). 11 form on a potter''s wheel. 12 have (a fit or tantrum etc.). 13 give (a party). â€”n. 1 act of throwing or being thrown. 2 distance a thing is or may be thrown. 3 (prec. By a) slang each; per item (sold at Â£10 a throw).  throw away 1 discard as useless or unwanted. 2 waste or fail to make use of (an opportunity etc.). Throw back 1 revert to ancestral character. 2 (usu. In passive; foll. By on) compel to rely on. Throw in 1 interpose (a word or remark). 2 include at no extra cost. 3 throw (a football) from the edge of the pitch where it has gone out of play. Throw in the towel (or sponge) admit defeat. Throw off 1 discard; contrive to get rid of. 2 write or utter in an offhand manner. Throw oneself at seek blatantly as a sexual partner. Throw oneself into engage vigorously in. Throw oneself on (or upon) rely completely on. Throw open (often foll. By to) 1 cause to be suddenly or widely open. 2 make accessible. Throw out 1 put out forcibly or suddenly. 2 discard as unwanted. 3 reject (a proposal).'),
(36918, 't', '\r\nThrow-away', ' attrib. Adj. 1 meant to be thrown away after (one) use. 2 spoken in a deliberately casual way. 3 disposed to throwing things away (throw-away society).'),
(36919, 't', '\r\nThrowback', ' n. 1 reversion to ancestral character. 2 instance of this.'),
(36920, 't', '\r\nThrow-in', ' n. Throwing in of a football during play.'),
(36921, 't', '\r\nThrown', ' past part of *throw.'),
(36922, 't', '\r\nThru', ' us var. Of *through.'),
(36923, 't', '\r\nThrum1', ' â€”v. (-mm-) 1 play (a stringed instrument) monotonously or unskilfully. 2 (often foll. By on) drum idly. â€”n. 1 such playing. 2 resulting sound. [imitative]'),
(36924, 't', '\r\nThrum2', ' n. 1 unwoven end of a warp-thread, or the whole of such ends, left when the finished web is cut away. 2 any short loose thread. [old english]'),
(36925, 't', '\r\nThrush1', ' n. Any of various songbirds, esp. The song thrush and mistle thrush. [old english]'),
(36926, 't', '\r\nThrush2', ' n. 1 fungous disease, esp. Of children, affecting the mouth and throat. 2 similar disease of the vagina. [origin unknown]'),
(36927, 't', '\r\nThrust', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Thrust) 1 push with a sudden impulse or with force. 2 (foll. By on) impose (a thing) forcibly; enforce acceptance of (a thing). 3 (foll. By at, through) pierce, stab; lunge suddenly. 4 make (one''s way) forcibly. 5 (as thrusting adj.) Aggressive, ambitious. â€”n. 1 sudden or forcible push or lunge. 2 propulsive force produced by a jet or rocket engine. 3 strong attempt to penetrate an enemy''s line or territory. 4 remark aimed at a person. 5 stress between the parts of an arch etc. 6 (often foll. By of) chief theme or gist of remarks etc. [old norse]'),
(36928, 't', '\r\nThud', ' â€”n. Low dull sound as of a blow on a non-resonant surface. â€”v. (-dd-) make or fall with a thud. [probably old english]'),
(36929, 't', '\r\nThug', ' n. 1 violent ruffian. 2 (thug) hist. Member of a religious organization of robbers and assassins in india.  thuggery n. Thuggish adj. [hindi]'),
(36930, 't', '\r\nThulium', ' n. Metallic element of the lanthanide series. [latin thule region in the remote north]'),
(36931, 't', '\r\nThumb', ' â€”n. 1 short thick finger on the human hand, set apart from the other four. 2 part of a glove etc. For a thumb. â€”v. 1 wear or soil (pages etc.) With a thumb. 2 turn over pages with or as with a thumb (thumbed through the directory). 3 request or get (a lift) by signalling with a raised thumb. 4 use the thumb in a gesture.  thumb one''s nose = cock a snook (see *snook). Thumbs down indication of rejection. Thumbs up indication of satisfaction or approval. Under a person''s thumb completely dominated by a person. [old english]'),
(36932, 't', '\r\nThumb', 'index  n. Set of lettered grooves cut down the side of a book for easy reference.'),
(36933, 't', '\r\nThumbnail', ' n. 1 nail of a thumb. 2 (attrib.) Concise (thumbnail sketch).'),
(36934, 't', '\r\nThumbprint', ' n. Impression of a thumb esp. For identification.'),
(36935, 't', '\r\nThumbscrew', ' n. Instrument of torture for crushing the thumbs.'),
(36936, 't', '\r\nThump', ' â€”v. 1 beat or strike heavily, esp. With the fist. 2 throb strongly. 3 (foll. By at, on, etc.) Knock loudly. â€”n. 1 heavy blow. 2 dull sound of this. [imitative]'),
(36937, 't', '\r\nThumping', ' adj. Colloq. (esp. As an intensifier) huge (a thumping lie; a thumping great house).'),
(36938, 't', '\r\nThunder', ' â€”n. 1 loud noise caused by lightning and due to the expansion of rapidly heated air. 2 resounding loud deep noise (thunders of applause). 3 strong censure or denunciation. â€”v. 1  (prec. By it as subject) thunder sounds (it is thundering; if it thunders). 2 make or proceed with a noise like thunder. 3 utter (threats, compliments, etc.) Loudly. 4 (foll. By against etc.) Make violent threats etc. Against.  steal a person''s thunder see *steal.  thundery adj. [old english]'),
(36939, 't', '\r\nThunderbolt', ' n. 1 flash of lightning with a simultaneous crash of thunder. 2 unexpected occurrence or announcement. 3 supposed bolt or shaft as a destructive agent, esp. As an attribute of a god.'),
(36940, 't', '\r\nThunderclap', ' n. 1 crash of thunder. 2 something startling or unexpected.'),
(36941, 't', '\r\nThundercloud', ' n. Cumulus cloud charged with electricity and producing thunder and lightning.'),
(36942, 't', '\r\nThunder-fly', ' n. = *thrips.'),
(36943, 't', '\r\nThundering', ' adj. Colloq. (esp. As an intensifier) huge (a thundering nuisance; a thundering great bruise).'),
(36944, 't', '\r\nThunderous', ' adj. 1 like thunder. 2 very loud.'),
(36945, 't', '\r\nThunderstorm', ' n. Storm with thunder and lightning and usu. Heavy rain or hail.'),
(36946, 't', '\r\nThunderstruck', ' predic. Adj. Amazed.'),
(36947, 't', '\r\nThur.', ' Abbr. (also thurs.) Thursday.'),
(36948, 't', '\r\nThurible', ' n. Censer. [latin thus thur- incense]'),
(36949, 't', '\r\nThursday', ' â€”n. Day of the week following wednesday. â€”adv. Colloq. 1 on thursday. 2 (thursdays) on thursdays; each thursday. [old english]'),
(36950, 't', '\r\nThus', ' adv. Formal 1 a in this way. B as indicated. 2 a accordingly. B as a result or inference. 3 to this extent; so (thus far; thus much). [old english]'),
(36951, 't', '\r\nThwack', ' â€”v. Hit with a heavy blow. â€”n. Heavy blow. [imitative]'),
(36952, 't', '\r\nThwart', ' â€”v. Frustrate or foil (a person, plan, etc.). â€”n. Rower''s seat. [old norse, = across]'),
(36953, 't', '\r\nThy', ' poss. Pron. (attrib.) (also thine predic. Or before a vowel) archaic of or belonging to thee. [from *thine]'),
(36954, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' thy has now been replaced by your except in some formal, liturgical, dialect, and poetic uses.'),
(36955, 't', '\r\nThyme', ' n. Any of several herbs with aromatic leaves. [greek thumon]'),
(36956, 't', '\r\nThymol', ' n. Antiseptic obtained from oil of thyme.'),
(36957, 't', '\r\nThymus', ' n. (pl. Thymi) lymphoid organ situated in the neck of vertebrates. [greek]'),
(36958, 't', '\r\nThyroid', ' n. (in full thyroid gland) 1 large ductless gland in the neck of vertebrates, secreting a hormone which regulates growth and development. 2 extract prepared from the thyroid gland of animals and used in treating goitre etc. [greek thureos oblong shield]'),
(36959, 't', '\r\nThyroid', 'cartilage  n. Large cartilage of the larynx, forming the adam''s apple.'),
(36960, 't', '\r\nThyself', ' pron. Archaic emphat. & refl. Form of *thou1, *thee.'),
(36961, 't', '\r\nTi', ' symb. Titanium.'),
(36962, 't', '\r\nTi', ' var. Of *te.'),
(36963, 't', '\r\nTiara', ' n. 1 jewelled ornamental band worn on the front of a woman''s hair. 2 three-crowned diadem worn by a pope.  tiaraed adj. [latin from greek]'),
(36964, 't', '\r\nTibia', ' n. (pl. Tibiae) anat. Inner of two bones extending from the knee to the ankle.  tibial adj. [latin]'),
(36965, 't', '\r\nTic', ' n. (in full nervous tic) occasional involuntary contraction of the muscles, esp. Of the face. [french from italian]'),
(36966, 't', '\r\nTick1', ' â€”n. 1 slight recurring click, esp. That of a watch or clock. 2 colloq. Moment. 3 mark to denote correctness, check items in a list, etc. â€”v. 1 (of a clock etc.) Make ticks. 2 a mark with a tick. B (often foll. By off) mark (an item) with a tick in checking.  tick off colloq. Reprimand. Tick over 1 (of an engine etc.) Idle. 2 (of a person, project, etc.) Be functioning at a basic level. What makes a person tick colloq. Person''s motivation. [probably imitative]'),
(36967, 't', '\r\nTick2', ' n. 1 parasitic arachnid on the skin of dogs, cattle, etc. 2 parasitic insect on sheep and birds etc. [old english]'),
(36968, 't', '\r\nTick3', ' n. Colloq. Credit (buy goods on tick). [apparently an abbreviation of *ticket in on the ticket]'),
(36969, 't', '\r\nTick4', ' n. 1 cover of a mattress or pillow. 2 = *ticking. [greek theke case]'),
(36970, 't', '\r\nTicker', ' n. Colloq. 1 heart. 2 watch. 3 us = *tape machine 1.'),
(36971, 't', '\r\nTicker-tape', ' n. 1 paper strip from a tape machine. 2 this or similar material thrown from windows etc. To greet a celebrity.'),
(36972, 't', '\r\nTicket', ' â€”n. 1 written or printed piece of paper or card entitling the holder to enter a place, participate in an event, travel by public transport, etc. 2 notification of a traffic offence etc. (parking ticket). 3 certificate of discharge from the army. 4 certificate of qualification as a ship''s master, pilot, etc. 5 price etc. Label. 6 esp. Us a list of candidates put forward by one group, esp. A political party. B principles of a party. 7 (prec. By the) colloq. What is correct or needed. â€”v. (-t-) attach a ticket to. [obsolete french Ã©tiquet]'),
(36973, 't', '\r\nTicking', ' n. Stout usu. Striped material used to cover mattresses etc. [from *tick4]'),
(36974, 't', '\r\nTickle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 a touch or stroke (a person etc.) Playfully or lightly so as to produce laughter and spasmodic movement. B produce this sensation. 2 excite agreeably; amuse. 3 catch (a trout etc.) By rubbing it so that it moves backwards into the hand. â€”n.'),
(36975, 't', '\r\nTicklish', ' adj. 1 sensitive to tickling. 2 (of a matter or person) difficult to handle.'),
(36976, 't', '\r\nTick-tack', ' n. A kind of manual semaphore used by racecourse bookmakers.'),
(36977, 't', '\r\nTick-tock', ' n. Ticking of a large clock etc.'),
(36978, 't', '\r\nTidal', ' adj. Relating to, like, or affected by tides.  tidally adv.'),
(36979, 't', '\r\nTidal', 'wave  n. 1 exceptionally large ocean wave, esp. One caused by an underwater earthquake. 2 widespread manifestation of feeling etc.'),
(36980, 't', '\r\nTidbit', ' n. (brit. Titbit) 1 dainty morsel. 2 piquant item of news etc. [perhaps from dial. Tid tender]'),
(36981, 't', '\r\nTiddler', ' n. Colloq. 1 small fish, esp. A stickleback or minnow. 2 unusually small thing. [perhaps related to *tiddly2 and tittlebat, a childish form of stickleback]'),
(36982, 't', '\r\nTiddly1', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Slightly drunk. [origin unknown]'),
(36983, 't', '\r\nTiddly2', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Little. [origin unknown]'),
(36984, 't', '\r\nTiddly-wink', ' n. 1 counter flicked with another into a cup etc. 2 (in pl.) This game. [perhaps related to *tiddly1]'),
(36985, 't', '\r\nTide', ' n. 1 a periodic rise and fall of the sea due to the attraction of the moon and sun. B water as affected by this. 2 time or season (usu. In comb.: whitsuntide). 3 marked trend of opinion, fortune, or events.  tide (-ding) over provide (a person) with what is needed during a difficult period. [old english, = *time]'),
(36986, 't', '\r\nTidemark', ' n. 1 mark made by the tide at high water. 2 a line left round a bath by the dirty water. B colloq. Line between washed and unwashed parts of a person''s body.'),
(36987, 't', '\r\nTidetable', ' n. Table indicating the times of high and low tides.'),
(36988, 't', '\r\nTideway', ' n. Tidal part of a river.'),
(36989, 't', '\r\nTidings', ' n. (as sing. Or pl.) Archaic or joc. News. [old english, probably from old norse]'),
(36990, 't', '\r\nTidy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 neat, orderly. 2 (of a person) methodical. 3 colloq. Considerable (a tidy sum). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 receptacle for holding small objects etc. 2 esp. Us cover for a chair-back etc. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) (also absol.; often foll. By up) put in good order; make (oneself, a room, etc.) Tidy.  tidily adv. Tidiness n. [originally = timely etc., from *tide]'),
(36991, 't', '\r\nTie', ' â€”v. (tying) 1 attach or fasten with string or cord etc. 2 a form (a string, ribbon, shoelace, necktie, etc.) Into a knot or bow. B form (a knot or bow) in this way. 3 (often foll. By down) restrict (a person) in some way (is tied to his job). 4 (often foll. By with) achieve the same score or place as another competitor (tied with her for first place). 5 hold (rafters etc.) Together by a crosspiece etc. 6 mus. Unite (written notes) by a tie. â€”n. 1 cord or wire etc. Used for fastening. 2 strip of material worn round the collar and tied in a knot at the front. 3 thing that unites or restricts persons (family ties). 4 draw, dead heat, or equality of score among competitors. 5 match between any pair from a group of competing players or teams. 6 (also tie-beam etc.) Rod or beam holding parts of a structure together. 7 mus. Curved line above or below two notes of the same pitch indicating that they are to be played without a break between them.  tie in (foll. By with) bring into or have a close association or agreement. Tie up 1 bind securely with cord etc. 2 invest or reserve (capital etc.) So that it is not immediately available for use. 3 (often foll. By with) = tie in. 4 (usu. In passive) fully occupy (a person). 5 bring to a satisfactory conclusion. [old english]'),
(36992, 't', '\r\nTie-break', ' n. (also tie-breaker) means of deciding a winner from competitors who have tied.'),
(36993, 't', '\r\nTied', ' attrib. Adj. 1 (of a house) occupied subject to the tenant''s working for its owner. 2 (of a public house etc.) Bound to supply the products of a particular brewery only.'),
(36994, 't', '\r\nTie-dye', ' n. (also tie and dye) method of producing dyed patterns by tying string etc. To keep the dye away from parts of the fabric.'),
(36995, 't', '\r\nTie-in', ' n. 1 connection or association. 2 joint promotion of related commodities etc. (e.g. A book and a film).'),
(36996, 't', '\r\nTie-pin', ' n. Ornamental pin for holding a tie in place.'),
(36997, 't', '\r\nTier', ' n. Row, rank, or unit of a structure, as one of several placed one above another (tiers of seats).  tiered adj. [french tire from tirer draw, elongate]'),
(36998, 't', '\r\nTiercel', ' var. Of *tercel.'),
(36999, 't', '\r\nTie-up', ' n. Connection, association.'),
(37000, 't', '\r\nTiff', ' n. Slight or petty quarrel. [origin unknown]'),
(37001, 't', '\r\nTiffin', ' n. Ind. Light meal, esp. Lunch. [apparently from tiffing sipping]'),
(37002, 't', '\r\nTiger', ' n. 1 large asian animal of the cat family, with a yellow-brown coat with black stripes. 2 fierce, energetic, or formidable person. [greek tigris]'),
(37003, 't', '\r\nTiger-cat', ' n. Any moderate-sized feline resembling the tiger, e.g. The ocelot.'),
(37004, 't', '\r\nTiger', 'lily  n. Tall garden lily with dark-spotted orange flowers.'),
(37005, 't', '\r\nTiger', 'moth  n. Moth with richly spotted and streaked wings.'),
(37006, 't', '\r\nTight', ' â€”adj. 1 closely held, drawn, fastened, fitting, etc. (tight hold; tight skirt). 2 too closely fitting. 3 impermeable, impervious, esp. (in comb.) To a specified thing (watertight). 4 tense; stretched. 5 colloq. Drunk. 6 colloq. Stingy. 7 (of money or materials) not easily obtainable. 8 a (of precautions, a programme, etc.) Stringent, demanding. B presenting difficulties (tight situation). 9 produced by or requiring great exertion or pressure (tight squeeze). â€”adv. Tightly (hold tight!).  tightly adv. Tightness n. [old norse]'),
(37007, 't', '\r\nTight', 'corner  n. (also tight place or spot) difficult situation.'),
(37008, 't', '\r\nTighten', ' v. Make or become tighter.'),
(37009, 't', '\r\nTight-fisted', ' adj. Stingy.'),
(37010, 't', '\r\nTight-lipped', ' adj. With or as with the lips compressed to restrain emotion or speech; determinedly reticent.'),
(37011, 't', '\r\nTightrope', ' n. Rope stretched tightly high above the ground, on which acrobats perform.'),
(37012, 't', '\r\nTights', ' n.pl. 1 thin close-fitting wool or nylon etc. Garment covering the legs, feet, and the lower part of the torso, worn by women and girls. 2 similar garment worn by a dancer, acrobat, etc.'),
(37013, 't', '\r\nTigress', ' n. Female tiger.'),
(37014, 't', '\r\nTike', ' var. Of *tyke.'),
(37015, 't', '\r\nTilde', ' n. Mark (Ëœ) put over a letter, e.g. Over a spanish n when pronounced ny (as in seÃ±or). [latin: related to *title]'),
(37016, 't', '\r\nTile', '  â€”n. 1 thin slab of concrete or baked clay etc. Used for roofing or paving etc. 2 similar slab of glazed pottery, cork, linoleum, etc., for covering a wall, floor, etc. 3 thin flat piece used in a game (esp. In mah-jong). â€”v. (-ling) cover with tiles.  on the tiles colloq. Having a spree.  tiler n. [latin tegula]'),
(37017, 't', '\r\nTiling', ' n. 1 process of fixing tiles. 2 area of tiles.'),
(37018, 't', '\r\nTill1', ' â€”prep. 1 up to or as late as (wait till six o''clock). 2 up to the time of (faithful till death). â€”conj. 1 up to the time when (wait till i return). 2 so long that (laughed till i cried). [old norse: related to *till3]'),
(37019, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' in all senses, till can be replaced by until which is more formal in style.'),
(37020, 't', '\r\nTill2', ' n. Drawer for money in a shop or bank etc., esp. With a device recording the amount of each purchase. [origin unknown]'),
(37021, 't', '\r\nTill3', ' v. Cultivate (land).  tiller n. [old english, = strive for]'),
(37022, 't', '\r\nTillage', ' n. 1 preparation of land for growing crops. 2 tilled land.'),
(37023, 't', '\r\nTiller', ' n. Bar fitted to a boat''s rudder to turn it in steering. [anglo-french telier weaver''s beam]'),
(37024, 't', '\r\nTilt', ' â€”v. 1 (cause to) assume a sloping position; heel over. 2 (foll. By at) strike, thrust, or run at, with a weapon. 3 (foll. By with) engage in a contest. â€”n. 1 tilting. 2 sloping position. 3 (of medieval knights etc.) Charging with a lance against an opponent or at a mark. 4 attack, esp. With argument or satire (have a tilt at).'),
(37025, 't', '\r\nTilth', ' n. 1 tillage, cultivation. 2 tilled soil. [old english: related to *till3]'),
(37026, 't', '\r\nTimber', ' n. 1 wood prepared for building, carpentry, etc. 2 piece of wood or beam, esp. As the rib of a vessel. 3 large standing trees. 4 (esp. As int.) Warning cry that a tree is about to fall. [old english, = building]'),
(37027, 't', '\r\nTimbered', ' adj. 1 made wholly or partly of timber. 2 (of country) wooded.'),
(37028, 't', '\r\nTimberline', ' n. Line or level above which no trees grow.'),
(37029, 't', '\r\nTimbre', ' n. Distinctive character of a musical sound or voice apart from its pitch and volume. [greek: related to *tympanum]'),
(37030, 't', '\r\nTimbrel', ' n. Archaic tambourine. [french: related to *timbre]'),
(37031, 't', '\r\nTime', ' â€”n. 1 indefinite continued progress of existence, events, etc., in the past, present, and future, regarded as a whole. 2 progress of this as affecting persons or things. 3 portion of time belonging to particular events or circumstances (the time of the plague; prehistoric times). 4 allotted or available portion of time (had no time to eat). 5 point of time, esp. In hours and minutes (the time is 7.30). 6 (prec. By a) indefinite period. 7 time or an amount of time as reckoned by a conventional standard (eight o''clock new york time; the time allowed is one hour). 8 occasion (last time). 9 moment etc. Suitable for a purpose etc. (the time to act). 10 (in pl.) Expressing multiplication (five times six is thirty). 11 lifetime (will last my time). 12 (in sing. Or pl.) Conditions of life or of a period (hard times). 13 slang prison sentence (is doing time). 14 apprenticeship (served his time). 15 period of gestation. 16 date or expected date of childbirth or death. 17 measured time spent in work. 18 a any of several rhythmic patterns of music. B duration of a note. â€”v. (-ming) 1 choose the time for. 2 do at a chosen or correct time. 3 arrange the time of arrival of. 4 ascertain the time taken by.  against time with utmost speed, so as to finish by a specified time. Ahead of time earlier than expected. All the time 1 during the whole of the time referred to (often despite some contrary expectation etc.). 2 constantly. At one time 1 in a known but unspecified past period. 2 simultaneously. At the same time 1 simultaneously. 2 nevertheless. At times intermittently. For the time being until some other arrangement is made. Half the time colloq. As often as not. Have no time for 1 be unable or unwilling to spend time on. 2 dislike. Have a time of it undergo trouble or difficulty. In no time 1 very soon. 2 very quickly. In time 1 not late, punctual. 2 eventually. 3 in accordance with a given rhythm. Keep time move or sing etc. In time. Pass the time of day colloq. Exchange a greeting or casual remarks. Time after time 1 on many occasions. 2 in many instances. Time and (or time and time) again on many occasions. The time of one''s life period of exceptional enjoyment. Time out of mind a longer time than anyone can remember. Time was there was a time. [old english]'),
(37032, 't', '\r\nTime', 'and a half  n. One and a half times the normal rate of payment.'),
(37033, 't', '\r\nTime-and-motion', ' adj. (usu. Attrib.) Measuring the efficiency of industrial and other operations.'),
(37034, 't', '\r\nTime', 'bomb  n. Bomb designed to explode at a pre-set time.'),
(37035, 't', '\r\nTime', 'capsule  n. Box etc. Containing objects typical of the present time, buried for future discovery.'),
(37036, 't', '\r\nTime', 'clock  n. Clock with a device for recording workers'' hours of work.'),
(37037, 't', '\r\nTime', 'exposure  n. Exposure of photographic film for longer than the slowest normal shutter setting.'),
(37038, 't', '\r\nTime-honoured', ' adj. Esteemed by tradition or through custom.'),
(37039, 't', '\r\nTimekeeper', ' n. 1 person who records time, esp. Of workers or in a game. 2 a watch or clock as regards accuracy (a good timekeeper). B person as regards punctuality.  timekeeping n.'),
(37040, 't', '\r\nTime-lag', ' n. Interval of time between a cause and effect.'),
(37041, 't', '\r\nTimeless', ' adj. Not affected by the passage of time.  timelessly adv. Timelessness n.'),
(37042, 't', '\r\nTime-limit', ' n. Limit of time within which a task must be done.'),
(37043, 't', '\r\nTimely', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) opportune; coming at the right time.  timeliness n.'),
(37044, 't', '\r\nTimepiece', ' n. Clock or watch.'),
(37045, 't', '\r\nTimer', ' n. Person or device that measures or records time taken.'),
(37046, 't', '\r\nTime-served', ' adj. Having completed a period of apprenticeship or training.'),
(37047, 't', '\r\nTime-server', ' n. Derog. Person who changes his or her view to suit the prevailing circumstances, fashion, etc.  time-serving adj.'),
(37048, 't', '\r\nTime-share', ' n. Share in a property under a time-sharing scheme.'),
(37049, 't', '\r\nTime-sharing', ' n. 1 use of a holiday home at contractually agreed different times by several joint owners. 2 operation of a computer system by several users for different operations at the same time.'),
(37050, 't', '\r\nTime', 'sheet  n. Sheet of paper for recording hours of work etc.'),
(37051, 't', '\r\nTime-shift', ' â€”v. Move from one time to another, esp. Record (a television programme) for later viewing. â€”n. Movement from one time to another (the continual time-shifts make the plot difficult to follow).'),
(37052, 't', '\r\nTime', 'signal  n. Audible signal of the exact time of day.'),
(37053, 't', '\r\nTime', 'signature  n. Mus. Indication of tempo following a clef.'),
(37054, 't', '\r\nTime', 'switch  n. Switch acting automatically at a pre-set time.'),
(37055, 't', '\r\nTimetable', ' â€”n. List of times at which events are scheduled to take place, esp. The arrival and departure of transport or a sequence of lessons. â€”v. (-ling) include in or arrange to a timetable; schedule.'),
(37056, 't', '\r\nTime', 'zone  n. Range of longitudes where a common standard time is used.'),
(37057, 't', '\r\nTimid', ' adj. (timider, timidest) easily frightened; apprehensive.  timidity n. Timidly adv. [latin timeo fear]'),
(37058, 't', '\r\nTiming', ' n. 1 way an action or process is timed. 2 regulation of the opening and closing of valves in an internal-combustion engine.'),
(37059, 't', '\r\nTimorous', ' adj. 1 timid. 2 frightened.  timorously adv. [medieval latin: related to *timid]'),
(37060, 't', '\r\nTimpani', ' n.pl. (also tympani) kettledrums.  timpanist n. [italian, pl. Of timpano = *tympanum]'),
(37061, 't', '\r\nTin', ' â€”n. 1  silvery-white metallic element, used esp. In alloys and in making tin plate. 2 container made of tin or tinned iron, esp. Airtight for preserving food. 3 = *tin plate. â€”v. (-nn-) 1 seal (food) in a tin for preservation. 2 cover or coat with tin. [old english]'),
(37062, 't', '\r\nTin', 'can  n. Tin container, esp. An empty one.'),
(37063, 't', '\r\nTincture', ' â€”n. (often foll. By of) 1 slight flavour or trace. 2 tinge (of a colour). 3 medicinal solution (of a drug) in alcohol (tincture of quinine). â€”v. (-ring) 1 colour slightly; tinge, flavour. 2 (often foll. By with) affect slightly (with a quality). [latin: related to *tinge]'),
(37064, 't', '\r\nTinder', ' n. Dry substance that readily catches fire from a spark.  tindery adj. [old english]'),
(37065, 't', '\r\nTinder-box', ' n. Hist. Box containing tinder, flint, and steel, formerly used for kindling fires.'),
(37066, 't', '\r\nTine', ' n. Prong, tooth, or point of a fork, comb, antler, etc. [old english]'),
(37067, 't', '\r\nTin', 'foil  n. Foil made of tin, aluminium, or tin alloy, used for wrapping food.'),
(37068, 't', '\r\nTing', ' â€”n. Tinkling sound as of a bell. â€”v. (cause to) emit this sound. [imitative]'),
(37069, 't', '\r\nTinge', ' â€”v. (-ging) (often foll. By with; often in passive) 1 colour slightly. 2 affect slightly. â€”n. 1 tendency towards or trace of some colour. 2 slight admixture of a feeling or quality. [latin tingo tinct- dye]'),
(37070, 't', '\r\nTingle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 feel a slight prickling, stinging, or throbbing sensation. 2 cause this (the reply tingled in my ears). â€”n. Tingling sensation.  tingly adj. [probably from *tinkle]'),
(37071, 't', '\r\nTin', 'hat  n. Colloq. Military steel helmet.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(37072, 't', '\r\nTinker', ' â€”n. 1 itinerant mender of kettles and pans etc. 2 scot. & ir. Gypsy. 3 colloq. Mischievous person or animal. 4 spell of tinkering. â€”v. 1 (foll. By at, with) work in an amateurish or desultory way. 2 work as a tinker. [origin unknown]'),
(37073, 't', '\r\nTinkle', ' â€”v. (-ling) (cause to) make a succession of short light ringing sounds. â€”n. 1 tinkling sound. 2 colloq. Telephone call.  tinkly adj. [imitative]'),
(37074, 't', '\r\nTinnitus', ' n. Med. Condition with ringing in the ears. [latin tinnio tinnit- ring, tinkle]'),
(37075, 't', '\r\nTinny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of or like tin. 2 flimsy, insubstantial. 3 (of sound) thin and metallic.'),
(37076, 't', '\r\nTin-opener', ' n. Tool for opening tins.'),
(37077, 't', '\r\nTin-pan', 'alley  n. World of composers and publishers of popular music.'),
(37078, 't', '\r\nTin', 'plate  n. Sheet iron or sheet steel coated with tin.'),
(37079, 't', '\r\nTinpot', ' attrib. Adj. Cheap, inferior.'),
(37080, 't', '\r\nTinsel', ' n. 1 glittering metallic strips, threads, etc., used as decoration. 2 superficial brilliance or splendour. 3 (attrib.) Gaudy, flashy.  tinselled adj. Tinselly adj. [latin scintilla spark]'),
(37081, 't', '\r\nTinsmith', ' n. Worker in tin and tin plate.'),
(37082, 't', '\r\nTinsnips', ' n. Clippers for cutting sheet metal.'),
(37083, 't', '\r\nTint', ' â€”n. 1 variety of a colour, esp. Made by adding white. 2 tendency towards or admixture of a different colour (red with a blue tint). 3 faint colour spread over a surface. â€”v. Apply a tint to; colour. [tinct: related to *tinge]'),
(37084, 't', '\r\nTin-tack', ' n. Iron tack.'),
(37085, 't', '\r\nTintinnabulation', ' n. Ringing or tinkling of bells. [latin tintinnabulum bell]'),
(37086, 't', '\r\nTin', 'whistle  n. = *penny whistle.'),
(37087, 't', '\r\nTiny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) very small or slight.  tinily adv. Tininess n. [origin unknown]'),
(37088, 't', '\r\n-tion', ' see *-ion.'),
(37089, 't', '\r\nTip1', ' â€”n. 1 extremity or end, esp. Of a small or tapering thing. 2 small piece or part attached to the end of a thing. 3 leaf-bud of tea. â€”v. (-pp-) provide with a tip.  on the tip of one''s tongue about to be said or remembered. Tip of the iceberg small evident part of something much larger. [old norse]'),
(37090, 't', '\r\nTip2', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (often foll. By over, up) a lean or slant. B cause to do this. 2 (foll. By into etc.) A overturn or cause to overbalance. B discharge the contents of (a container etc.) In this way. â€”n. 1 a slight push or tilt. B light stroke. 2 place where material (esp. Refuse) is tipped.  tip the scales see *scale2. [origin uncertain]'),
(37091, 't', '\r\nTip3', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 make a small present of money to, esp. For a service given. 2 name as the likely winner of a race or contest etc. 3 strike or touch lightly. â€”n. 1 small money present, esp. For a service given. 2 piece of private or special information, esp. Regarding betting or investment. 3 small or casual piece of advice.  tip off give (a person) a hint or piece of special information or warning. Tip a person the wink give a person private information. [origin uncertain]'),
(37092, 't', '\r\nTip-off', ' n. Hint or warning etc.'),
(37093, 't', '\r\nTipper', ' n. (often attrib.) Road haulage vehicle that tips at the back to discharge its load.'),
(37094, 't', '\r\nTippet', ' n. 1 long piece of fur etc. Worn by a woman round the shoulders. 2 similar garment worn by judges, clergy, etc. [probably from *tip1]'),
(37095, 't', '\r\nTipple', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 drink intoxicating liquor habitually. 2 drink (liquor) repeatedly in small amounts. â€”n. Colloq. Alcoholic drink.  tippler n. [origin unknown]'),
(37096, 't', '\r\nTipstaff', ' n. 1 sheriff''s officer. 2 metal-tipped staff carried as a symbol of office. [from *tip1]'),
(37097, 't', '\r\nTipster', ' n. Person who gives tips, esp. About betting at horse-races.'),
(37098, 't', '\r\nTipsy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 slightly drunk. 2 caused by or showing intoxication (a tipsy lurch).  tipsily adv. Tipsiness n. [from *tip2]'),
(37099, 't', '\r\nTiptoe', ' â€”n. The tips of the toes. â€”v. (-toes, -toed, -toeing) walk on tiptoe, or very stealthily. â€”adv. (also on tiptoe) with the heels off the ground.'),
(37100, 't', '\r\nTiptop', ' colloq. â€”adj. Highest in excellence. â€”n. Highest point of excellence. â€”adv. Most excellently.'),
(37101, 't', '\r\nTip-up', ' attrib. Adj. Able to be tipped, e.g. Of a theatre seat.'),
(37102, 't', '\r\nTir', ' abbr. International road transport. [french transport international routier]'),
(37103, 't', '\r\nTirade', ' n. Long vehement denunciation or declamation. [french from italian]'),
(37104, 't', '\r\nTire1', ' v. (-ring) 1 make or grow weary. 2 exhaust the patience or interest of; bore. 3 (in passive; foll. By of) have had enough of; be fed up with. [old english]'),
(37105, 't', '\r\nTire2', ' n. Band of metal placed round the rim of a wheel to strengthen it.  [perhaps = archaic tire â€˜headdressâ€™]'),
(37106, 't', '\r\nTire3', ' n. (brit. Tyre) rubber covering, usu. Inflated, placed round a wheel to form a soft contact with the road. [var. Of *tire2]'),
(37107, 't', '\r\nTired', ' adj. 1 weary; ready for sleep. 2 (of an idea etc.) Hackneyed.  tiredly adv. Tiredness n.'),
(37108, 't', '\r\nTireless', ' adj. Not tiring easily, energetic.  tirelessly adv. Tirelessness n.'),
(37109, 't', '\r\nTiresome', ' adj. 1 wearisome, tedious. 2 colloq. Annoying.  tiresomely adv. Tiresomeness n.'),
(37110, 't', '\r\nTiro', ' n. (also tyro) (pl. -s) beginner, novice. [latin, = recruit]'),
(37111, 't', '\r\n''Tis', ' archaic it is. [contraction]'),
(37112, 't', '\r\nTissue', ' n. 1 any of the coherent collections of specialized cells of which animals or plants are made (muscular tissue). 2 = *tissue-paper. 3 disposable piece of thin soft absorbent paper for wiping, drying, etc. 4 fine woven esp. Gauzy fabric. 5 (foll. By of) connected series (tissue of lies). [french tissu woven cloth]'),
(37113, 't', '\r\nTissue-paper', ' n. Thin soft paper for wrapping etc.'),
(37114, 't', '\r\nTit1', ' n. Any of various small birds. [probably from scandinavian]'),
(37115, 't', '\r\nTit2', ' n.  tit for tat blow for blow; retaliation. [= earlier tip in tip for tap: see *tip2]'),
(37116, 't', '\r\nTit3', ' n. 1 coarse slang woman''s breast. 2 colloq. Nipple. [old english]'),
(37117, 't', '\r\nTitan', ' n. (often titan) person of very great strength, intellect, or importance. [greek, = member of a race of giants]'),
(37118, 't', '\r\nTitanic', ' adj. Gigantic, colossal.  titanically adv. [greek: related to *titan]'),
(37119, 't', '\r\nTitanium', ' n. Grey metallic element. [greek: related to *titan]'),
(37120, 't', '\r\nTitbit', ' n. (us tidbit) 1 dainty morsel. 2 piquant item of news etc. [perhaps from dial. Tid tender]'),
(37121, 't', '\r\nTitchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Very small. [titch small person, from tich, name of a comedian]'),
(37122, 't', '\r\nTitfer', ' n. Slang hat. [abbreviation of tit for tat, rhyming slang]'),
(37123, 't', '\r\nTithe', ' â€”n. 1 one-tenth of the annual produce of land or labour, formerly taken as a tax for the church. 2 tenth part. â€”v. (-thing) 1 subject to tithes. 2 pay tithes. [old english, = tenth]'),
(37124, 't', '\r\nTithe', 'barn  n. Barn built to hold tithes paid in kind.'),
(37125, 't', '\r\nTitian', ' adj. (of hair) bright auburn. [titian, name of a painter]'),
(37126, 't', '\r\nTitillate', ' v. (-ting) 1 excite, esp. Sexually. 2 tickle.  titillation n. [latin]'),
(37127, 't', '\r\nTitivate', ' v. (-ting) (often refl.) Colloq. Smarten up; put the finishing touches to.  titivation n. [earlier tidivate, perhaps from *tidy after cultivate]'),
(37128, 't', '\r\nTitle', ' n. 1 name of a book, work of art, etc. 2 heading of a chapter, document, etc. 3 a = *title-page. B book, magazine, etc., in terms of its title (brought out two new titles). 4 (usu. In pl.) Caption or credit in a film etc. 5 name indicating a person''s status (e.g. Queen, professor) or used as a form of address or reference (e.g. Lord, mr, your grace). 6 championship in sport. 7 law a right to ownership of property with or without possession. B facts constituting this. C (foll. By to) just or recognized claim. [latin titulus]'),
(37129, 't', '\r\nTitled', ' adj. Having a title of nobility or rank.'),
(37130, 't', '\r\nTitle-deed', ' n. Legal instrument as evidence of a right.'),
(37131, 't', '\r\nTitle-holder', ' n. Person who holds a title, esp. A sporting champion.'),
(37132, 't', '\r\nTitle-page', ' n. Page at the beginning of a book giving the title, author, etc.'),
(37133, 't', '\r\nTitle', 'role  n. Part in a play etc. That gives it its name (e.g. Othello).'),
(37134, 't', '\r\nTitmouse', ' n. (pl. Titmice) small active tit. [old english tit little, mase titmouse, assimilated to *mouse]'),
(37135, 't', '\r\nTitrate', ' v. (-ting) chem. Ascertain the amount of a constituent in (a solution) by reaction with a known concentration of reagent.  titration n. [french titre title]'),
(37136, 't', '\r\nTitter', ' â€”v. Laugh covertly; giggle. â€”n. Covert laugh. [imitative]'),
(37137, 't', '\r\nTitter', ' â€”v. Laugh covertly; giggle. â€”n. Covert laugh. [imitative]'),
(37138, 't', '\r\nTittle', ' n. 1 small written or printed stroke or dot. 2 particle; whit (not one jot or tittle). [latin: related to *title]'),
(37139, 't', '\r\nTittle-tattle', ' â€”n. Petty gossip. â€”v. (-ling) gossip, chatter. [reduplication of *tattle]'),
(37140, 't', '\r\nTittup', ' â€”v. (-p- or -pp-) go about friskily or jerkily; bob up and down; canter. â€”n. Such a gait or movement. [perhaps imitative]'),
(37141, 't', '\r\nTitular', ' adj. 1 of or relating to a title. 2 existing, or being, in name or title only (titular ruler). [french: related to *title]'),
(37142, 't', '\r\nTizzy', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. State of agitation (in a tizzy). [origin unknown]'),
(37143, 't', '\r\nT-junction', ' n. Road junction at which one road joins another at right angles without crossing it.'),
(37144, 't', '\r\nTl', ' symb. Thallium.'),
(37145, 't', '\r\nTm', ' symb. Thulium.'),
(37146, 't', '\r\nTnt', ' abbr. Trinitrotoluene, a high explosive formed from toluene.'),
(37147, 't', '\r\nTo', ', before a vowel, when stressed â€”prep. 1 introducing a noun expressing: a what is reached, approached, or touched (fell to the ground; went to paris; five minutes to six). B what is aimed at (throw it to me). C as far as (went on to the end). D what is followed (made to order). E what is considered or affected (am used to that; that is nothing to me). F what is caused or produced (turn to stone). G what is compared (nothing to what it once was; equal to the occasion). H what is increased (add it to mine). I what is involved or composed as specified (there is nothing to it). 2 introducing the infinitive: a as a verbal noun (to get there is the priority). B expressing purpose, consequence, or cause (we eat to live; left him to starve; i''m sorry to hear that). C as a substitute for to + infinitive (wanted to come but was unable to). â€”adv. 1 in the normal or required position or condition (come to; heave to). 2 (of a door) in a nearly closed position.  to and fro 1 backwards and forwards. 2 repeatedly between the same points. [old english]'),
(37148, 't', '\r\nToad', ' n. 1 froglike amphibian breeding in water but living chiefly on land. 2 repulsive person. [old english]'),
(37149, 't', '\r\nToadflax', ' n. Plant with yellow or purple flowers.'),
(37150, 't', '\r\nToad-in-the-hole', ' n. Sausages baked in batter.'),
(37151, 't', '\r\nToadstool', ' n. Fungus, usu. Poisonous, with a round top and slender stalk.'),
(37152, 't', '\r\nToady', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) sycophant. â€”v. (foll. By to) (-ies, -ied) behave servilely to; fawn upon.  toadyism n. [contraction of toad-eater]'),
(37153, 't', '\r\nToast', ' â€”n. 1 sliced bread browned on both sides by radiant heat. 2 a person or thing in whose honour a company is requested to drink. B call to drink or an instance of drinking in this way. â€”v. 1 brown by radiant heat. 2 warm (one''s feet, oneself, etc.) At a fire etc. 3 drink to the health or in honour of (a person or thing). [french toster roast]'),
(37154, 't', '\r\nToaster', ' n. Electrical device for making toast.'),
(37155, 't', '\r\nToasting-fork', ' n. Long-handled fork for making toast.'),
(37156, 't', '\r\nToastmaster', ' n. (fem. Toastmistress) person responsible for announcing toasts at a public occasion.'),
(37157, 't', '\r\nToast', 'rack  n. Rack for holding slices of toast at table.'),
(37158, 't', '\r\nTobacco', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 plant of american origin with narcotic leaves used for smoking, chewing, or snuff. 2 its leaves, esp. As prepared for smoking. [spanish tabaco, of american indian origin]'),
(37159, 't', '\r\nTobacconist', ' n. Dealer in tobacco, cigarettes, etc.'),
(37160, 't', '\r\nToboggan', ' â€”n. Long light narrow sledge for sliding downhill over snow or ice. â€”v. Ride on a toboggan. [canadian french from algonquian]'),
(37161, 't', '\r\nToby', 'jug  n. Jug or mug in the form of a stout man wearing a three-cornered hat. [familiar form of the name tobias]'),
(37162, 't', '\r\nToccata', ' n. Musical composition for a keyboard instrument, designed to exhibit the performer''s touch and technique. [italian, = touched]'),
(37163, 't', '\r\nTocsin', ' n. Alarm bell or signal. [provenÃ§al tocasenh]'),
(37164, 't', '\r\nTod', ' n. Slang  on one''s tod alone; on one''s own. [rhyming slang on one''s tod sloan]'),
(37165, 't', '\r\nToday', ' â€”adv. 1 on this present day. 2 nowadays. â€”n. 1 this present day. 2 modern times. [old english]'),
(37166, 't', '\r\nToddle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 walk with short unsteady steps like a small child. 2 colloq. A walk, stroll. B (usu. Foll. By off or along) depart. â€”n. Act of toddling. [origin unknown]'),
(37167, 't', '\r\nToddler', ' n. Child who is just learning to walk.'),
(37168, 't', '\r\nToddy', ' n. (pl. -ies) drink of spirits with hot water and sugar etc. [hindustani tar palm]'),
(37169, 't', '\r\nTo-do', ' n. (pl. -s) commotion or fuss.'),
(37170, 't', '\r\nToe', ' â€”n. 1 any of the five terminal projections of the foot. 2 corresponding part of an animal. 3 part of a shoe etc. That covers the toes. 4 lower end or tip of an implement etc. â€”v. (toes, toed, toeing) touch (a starting-line etc.) With the toes.  on one''s toes alert. Toe the line conform, esp. Under pressure. [old english]'),
(37171, 't', '\r\nToecap', ' n. (usu. Strengthened) outer covering of the toe of a boot or shoe.'),
(37172, 't', '\r\nToe-hold', ' n. 1 small foothold. 2 small beginning or advantage.'),
(37173, 't', '\r\nToenail', ' n. Nail of each toe.'),
(37174, 't', '\r\nToff', ' n. Slang upper-class person. [perhaps from tuft, = titled undergraduate]'),
(37175, 't', '\r\nToffee', ' n. 1 firm or hard sweet made by boiling sugar, butter, etc. 2 this substance.  for toffee slang (prec. By can''t etc.) (denoting incompetence) at all (they couldn''t sing for toffee). [origin unknown]'),
(37176, 't', '\r\nToffee-apple', ' n. Apple with a coating of toffee.'),
(37177, 't', '\r\nToffee-nosed', ' adj. Slang snobbish, superior.'),
(37178, 't', '\r\nTofu', ' n. Curd of mashed soya beans. [japanese]'),
(37179, 't', '\r\nTog1', ' colloq. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Item of clothing. â€”v. (-gg-) (foll. By out, up) dress. [apparently originally cant: ultimately related to latin *toga]'),
(37180, 't', '\r\nTog2', ' n. Unit of thermal resistance used to express the insulating properties of clothes and quilts. [arbitrary, probably from *tog1]'),
(37181, 't', '\r\nToga', ' n. Hist. Ancient roman citizen''s loose flowing outer garment.  togaed adj. (also toga''d). [latin]'),
(37182, 't', '\r\nTogether', ' â€”adv. 1 in company or conjunction (walking together; were at school together). 2 simultaneously (both shouted together). 3 one with another (talking together). 4 into conjunction; so as to unite (tied them together; put two and two together). 5 into company or companionship. 6 uninterruptedly (he could talk for three hours together). â€”adj. Colloq. Well-organized; self-assured; emotionally stable. together with as well as. [old english: related to *to, *gather]'),
(37183, 't', '\r\nTogetherness', ' n. 1 being together. 2 feeling of comfort from this.'),
(37184, 't', '\r\nToggle', ' n. 1 fastener for a garment consisting of a crosspiece which passes through a hole or loop. 2 computing switch action that is operated the same way but with opposite effect on successive occasions. [origin unknown]'),
(37185, 't', '\r\nToggle', 'switch  n. Electric switch with a lever to be moved usu. Up and down.'),
(37186, 't', '\r\nToil', ' â€”v. 1 work laboriously or incessantly. 2 make slow painful progress. â€”n. Intensive labour; drudgery. [anglo-french toil(er) dispute]'),
(37187, 't', '\r\nToilet', ' n. 1 = *lavatory. 2 process of washing oneself, dressing, etc. (at one''s toilet). [french toilette diminutive of toile cloth]'),
(37188, 't', '\r\nToilet', 'paper  n. Paper for cleaning oneself after excreting.'),
(37189, 't', '\r\nToilet', 'roll  n. Roll of toilet paper.'),
(37190, 't', '\r\nToiletry', ' n. (pl. -ies) (usu. In pl.) Article or cosmetic used in washing, dressing, etc.'),
(37191, 't', '\r\nToilet', 'soap  n. Soap for washing oneself.'),
(37192, 't', '\r\nToilette', ' n. = *toilet 2. [french]'),
(37193, 't', '\r\nToilet-training', ' n. Training of a young child to use the lavatory.  toilet-train v.'),
(37194, 't', '\r\nToilet', 'water  n. Dilute perfume used after washing.'),
(37195, 't', '\r\nToils', ' n.pl. Net, snare. [toil from french: related to *toilet]'),
(37196, 't', '\r\nToilsome', ' adj. Involving toil.'),
(37197, 't', '\r\nToing', 'and froing  n. Constant movement to and fro; bustle; dispersed activity. [from *to, *fro]'),
(37198, 't', '\r\nTokay', ' n. A sweet hungarian wine. [tokaj in hungary]'),
(37199, 't', '\r\nToken', ' n. 1 thing serving as a symbol, reminder, or mark (as a token of affection; in token of my esteem). 2 voucher. 3 thing equivalent to something else, esp. Money. 4 (attrib.) A perfunctory (token effort). B conducted briefly to demonstrate strength of feeling (token strike). C chosen by tokenism to represent a group (token woman).'),
(37200, 't', '\r\nTokenism', ' n. 1 granting of minimum concessions, esp. To minority groups. 2 making of only a token effort.'),
(37201, 't', '\r\nTold', ' past and past part. Of *tell.'),
(37202, 't', '\r\nTolerable', ' adj. 1 endurable. 2 fairly good.  tolerably adv. [latin: related to *tolerate]'),
(37203, 't', '\r\nTolerance', ' n. 1 willingness or ability to tolerate; forbearance. 2 allowable variation in any measurable property.'),
(37204, 't', '\r\nTolerant', ' adj. 1 disposed to tolerate others or their acts or opinions. 2 (foll. By of) enduring or patient.'),
(37205, 't', '\r\nTolerate', ' v. (-ting) 1 allow the existence or occurrence of without authoritative interference. 2 endure (suffering etc.). 3 find or treat as endurable. 4 be able to take or undergo (drugs, treatment, etc.) Without adverse effects. [latin tolero]'),
(37206, 't', '\r\nToleration', ' n. Tolerating or being tolerated, esp. The allowing of religious differences without discrimination. [latin: related to *tolerate]'),
(37207, 't', '\r\nToll1', ' n. 1 charge to use a bridge, road, etc. 2 cost or damage caused by a disaster etc.  take its toll be accompanied by loss, injury, etc.'),
(37208, 't', '\r\nToll2', ' â€”v. 1 a (of a bell) sound with slow uniform strokes. B ring (a bell) in this way. C (of a bell) announce or mark (a death etc.) In this way. 2 strike (the hour). â€”n. 1 tolling. 2 stroke of a bell. [(now dial.) Toll entice, pull, from an old english root]'),
(37209, 't', '\r\nToll-bridge', ' n. Bridge at which a toll is charged.'),
(37210, 't', '\r\nToll-gate', ' n. Gate preventing passage until a toll is paid.'),
(37211, 't', '\r\nToll-road', ' n. Road maintained by the tolls collected on it.'),
(37212, 't', '\r\nToluene', ' n. Colourless aromatic liquid hydrocarbon derivative of benzene, used in the manufacture of explosives etc. [tolu in colombia]'),
(37213, 't', '\r\nTom', ' n. (in full tom-cat) male cat. [abbreviation of the name thomas]'),
(37214, 't', '\r\nTomahawk', ' n. N. American indian war-axe. [renape]'),
(37215, 't', '\r\nTomato', ' n. (pl. -es) 1 glossy red or yellow pulpy edible fruit. 2 plant bearing this. [ultimately from mexican tomatl]'),
(37216, 't', '\r\nTomb', ' n. 1 burial-vault. 2 grave. 3 sepulchral monument. [greek tumbos]'),
(37217, 't', '\r\nTombola', ' n. Lottery with tickets drawn from a drum for immediate prizes. [french or italian]'),
(37218, 't', '\r\nTomboy', ' n. Boisterous girl who enjoys activities traditionally associated with boys.  tomboyish adj. [from *tom]'),
(37219, 't', '\r\nTombstone', ' n. Memorial stone over a grave, usu. With an epitaph.'),
(37220, 't', '\r\nTom,', 'dick, and harry  n. (also tom, dick, or harry) (usu. Prec. By any or every) person taken at random (any tom, dick, or harry can walk in).'),
(37221, 't', '\r\nTome', ' n. Heavy book or volume. [greek temno cut]'),
(37222, 't', '\r\nTomfool', ' â€”n. Foolish person. â€”attrib. Adj. Silly, foolish.'),
(37223, 't', '\r\nTomfoolery', ' n. Foolish behaviour.'),
(37224, 't', '\r\nTommy', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. British private soldier. [tommy atkins, name used in specimens of completed official forms]'),
(37225, 't', '\r\nTommy-gun', ' n. Sub-machine-gun. [thompson, name of its co-inventor]'),
(37226, 't', '\r\nTommy-rot', ' n. Slang nonsense. [from *tom]'),
(37227, 't', '\r\nTomography', ' n. Method of radiography displaying details in a selected plane within the body. [greek tome a cutting]'),
(37228, 't', '\r\nTomorrow', ' â€”adv. 1 on the day after today. 2 at some future time. â€”n. 1 the day after today. 2 the near future. [from *to, *morrow]'),
(37229, 't', '\r\nTomtit', ' n. Tit, esp. A blue tit.'),
(37230, 't', '\r\nTom-tom', ' n. 1 primitive drum beaten with the hands. 2 tall drum used in jazz bands etc. [hindi tamtam, imitative]'),
(37231, 't', '\r\nTon', ' n. 1 (in full long ton) unit of weight equal to 2,240 lb (1016.05 kg). 2 (in full short ton) unit of weight equal to 2,000 lb (907.19 kg). 3 = *metric ton. 4 (in full displacement ton) unit of measurement of a ship''s weight or volume. 5 (usu. In pl.) Colloq. Large number or amount (tons of people). 6 slang a speed of 100 m.p.h. B Â£100.  weigh a ton colloq. Be very heavy. [originally the same word as *tun]'),
(37232, 't', '\r\nTonal', ' adj. Of or relating to tone or tonality.  tonally adv. [medieval latin: related to *tone]'),
(37233, 't', '\r\nTonality', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 mus. A relationship between the tones of a musical scale. B observance of a single tonic key as the basis of a composition. 2 colour scheme of a picture.'),
(37234, 't', '\r\nTone', ' â€”n. 1 musical or vocal sound, esp. With reference to its pitch, quality, and strength. 2 (often in pl.) Modulation of the voice expressing a particular feeling or mood (a cheerful tone). 3 manner of expression in writing or speaking. 4 mus. A musical sound, esp. Of a definite pitch and character. B interval of a major second, e.g. Câ€“d. 5 a general effect of colour or of light and shade in a picture. B tint or shade of a colour. 6 prevailing character of the morals and sentiments etc. In a group. 7 proper firmness of the body. 8 state of good or specified health. â€”v. (-ning) 1 give the desired tone to. 2 modify the tone of. 3 (often foll. By to) attune. 4 (foll. By with) (esp. Of colour) be in harmony with.  tone down make or become softer in tone. Tone up make or become stronger in tone.  toneless adj. Tonelessly adv. Toner n. [greek tonos from teino stretch]'),
(37235, 't', '\r\nTone-deaf', ' adj. Unable to perceive differences of musical pitch accurately.'),
(37236, 't', '\r\nTone', 'poem  n. Orchestral composition with a descriptive or rhapsodic theme.'),
(37237, 't', '\r\nTongs', ' n.pl. Implement with two arms for grasping coal, sugar, etc. [old english]'),
(37238, 't', '\r\nTongue', ' â€”n. 1 fleshy muscular organ in the mouth used in tasting, licking, and swallowing, and (in man) for speech. 2 tongue of an ox etc. As food. 3 faculty of or tendency in speech (a sharp tongue). 4 particular language (the german tongue). 5 thing like a tongue in shape or position, esp.: a a long low promontory. B a strip of leather etc. Under the laces in a shoe. C the clapper of a bell. D the pin of a buckle. E a projecting strip on a board etc. Fitting into the groove of another. â€”v. (-guing) use the tongue to articulate (notes) in playing a wind instrument. find (or lose) one''s tongue be able (or unable) to express oneself after a shock etc. Hold one''s tongue see *hold1. With one''s tongue in one''s cheek insincerely or ironically. [old english]'),
(37239, 't', '\r\nTongue-and-groove', ' â€”n. (often attrib.) Planking etc. With a projecting strip down one side and a groove down the other. â€”v. 1 panel with tongue-and-groove. 2 (as tongued and grooved adj.) Having a tongue-and-groove joint.'),
(37240, 't', '\r\nTongue-in-cheek', ' â€”adj. Ironic. â€”adv. Insincerely or ironically.'),
(37241, 't', '\r\nTongue-tie', ' n. Speech impediment due to a malformation of the tongue.'),
(37242, 't', '\r\nTongue-tied', ' adj. 1 too shy or embarrassed to speak. 2 having a tongue-tie.'),
(37243, 't', '\r\nTongue-twister', ' n. Sequence of words difficult to pronounce quickly and correctly.'),
(37244, 't', '\r\nTonic', ' â€”n. 1 invigorating medicine. 2 anything serving to invigorate. 3 = *tonic water. 4 mus. Keynote. â€”adj. Invigorating. [greek: related to *tone]'),
(37245, 't', '\r\nTonic', 'sol-fa  n. Mus. System of notation used esp. In teaching singing.'),
(37246, 't', '\r\nTonic', 'water  n. Carbonated water flavoured with quinine.'),
(37247, 't', '\r\nTonight', ' â€”adv. On the present or approaching evening or night. â€”n. The evening or night of the present day. [old english]'),
(37248, 't', '\r\nTonnage', ' n. 1 ship''s internal cubic capacity or freight-carrying capacity. 2 charge per ton on freight or cargo. [related to *ton]'),
(37249, 't', '\r\nTonne', ' n. = *metric ton. [french: related to *ton]'),
(37250, 't', '\r\nTonsil', ' n. Either of two small organs, one on each side of the root of the tongue. [latin]'),
(37251, 't', '\r\nTonsillectomy', ' n. (pl. -ies) surgical removal of the tonsils.'),
(37252, 't', '\r\nTonsillitis', ' n. Inflammation of the tonsils.'),
(37253, 't', '\r\nTonsorial', ' adj. Usu. Joc. Of a hairdresser or hairdressing. [latin tondeo tons- shave]'),
(37254, 't', '\r\nTonsure', ' â€”n. 1 shaving of the crown of the head or the entire head, esp. Of a person entering the priesthood or a monastic order. 2 bare patch made in this way. â€”v. (-ring) give a tonsure to. [latin: related to *tonsorial]'),
(37255, 't', '\r\nTon-up', ' attrib. Adj. Slang (of a motor cyclist) achieving a speed of 100 m.p.h., esp. Habitually.'),
(37256, 't', '\r\nToo', ' adv. 1 to a greater extent than is desirable or permissible (too large). 2 colloq. Very (not too sure). 3 in addition (i''m coming too). 4 moreover (food was bad, and expensive too).  none too rather less than (feeling none too good). Too bad see *bad. Too much intolerable. Too much for 1 more than a match for. 2 beyond what is endurable by. Too right see *right. [stressed form of *to]'),
(37257, 't', '\r\nTook', ' past of *take.'),
(37258, 't', '\r\nTool', ' â€”n. 1 implement used to carry out mechanical functions by hand or by machine. 2 thing used in an occupation or pursuit (tools of one''s trade). 3 person merely used by another. 4 coarse slang penis. â€”v. 1 dress (stone) with a chisel. 2 impress a design on (leather). 3 (foll. By along, around, etc.) Slang drive or ride, esp. In a casual or leisurely manner. [old english]'),
(37259, 't', '\r\nToolmaker', ' n. Person who makes precision tools.  toolmaking n.'),
(37260, 't', '\r\nTool-pusher', ' n. Worker directing the drilling on an oil rig.'),
(37261, 't', '\r\nToot', ' â€”n. Short sharp sound as made by a trumpet. â€”v. 1 sound (a trumpet etc.) With a short sharp sound. 2 give out such a sound. [probably imitative]'),
(37262, 't', '\r\nTooth', ' n. (pl. Teeth) 1 each of a set of hard bony enamel-coated structures in the jaws of most vertebrates, used for biting and chewing. 2 toothlike part or projection, e.g. The cog of a gearwheel, the point of a saw or comb, etc. 3 (often foll. By for) taste; appetite. 4 (in pl.) Force, effectiveness.  armed to the teeth completely and elaborately armed. Fight tooth and nail fight very fiercely. Get one''s teeth into devote oneself seriously to. In the teeth of 1 in spite of (opposition or difficulty etc.). 2 contrary to (instructions etc.). 3 directly against (the wind etc.).  toothed adj. (also in comb.). Toothless adj. [old english]'),
(37263, 't', '\r\nToothache', ' n. Pain in a tooth or teeth.'),
(37264, 't', '\r\nToothbrush', ' n. Brush for cleaning the teeth.'),
(37265, 't', '\r\nTooth-comb', ' n. = *fine-tooth comb.'),
(37266, 't', '\r\nToothpaste', ' n. Paste for cleaning the teeth.'),
(37267, 't', '\r\nToothpick', ' n. Small sharp stick for removing food lodged between the teeth.'),
(37268, 't', '\r\nTooth', 'powder  n. Powder for cleaning the teeth.'),
(37269, 't', '\r\nToothsome', ' adj. (of food) delicious.'),
(37270, 't', '\r\nToothy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having large, numerous, or prominent teeth.'),
(37271, 't', '\r\nTootle', ' v. (-ling) 1 toot gently or repeatedly. 2 (usu. Foll. By along, around, etc.) Colloq. Move casually.'),
(37272, 't', '\r\nTootsy', ' n. (pl. -ies) slang usu. Joc. Foot. [origin uncertain]'),
(37273, 't', '\r\nTop1', ' â€”n. 1 highest point or part. 2 a highest rank or place. B person occupying this. C upper end or head (top of the table). 3 upper surface or part of a thing. 4 stopper of a bottle, lid of a jar, etc. 5 garment for the upper part of the body. 6 utmost degree; height (at the top of his voice). 7 (in pl.) Colloq. Person or thing of the best quality. 8 (esp. In pl.) Leaves etc. Of a plant grown esp. For its root (turnip-tops). 9 naut. Platform round the head of the lower mast. 10 = *top gear (climbed the hill in top). â€”attrib. Adj. 1 highest in position. 2 highest in degree or importance. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 provide with a top, cap, etc. 2 be higher or better than; surpass; be at the top of (topped the list). 3 reach the top of (a hill etc.). 4 slang kill. 5 golf hit (a ball) above the centre.   off the top of one''s head see *head. On top in a superior position; above. On top of 1 fully in command of. 2 in close proximity to. 3 in addition to. On top of the world colloq. Exuberant. Over the top 1 over the parapet of a trench (and into battle). 2 beyond what is normally acceptable (that joke was over the top). Top off (or up) put an end or the finishing touch to.'),
(37274, 't', '\r\nTop2', ' n. Toy spinning on a point when set in motion. [old english]'),
(37275, 't', '\r\nTopaz', ' n. Transparent mineral, usu. Yellow, used as a gem. [greek topazos]'),
(37276, 't', '\r\nTop', 'brass  n. Colloq. Highest-ranking officers.'),
(37277, 't', '\r\nTopcoat', ' n. 1 overcoat. 2 outer coat of paint etc.'),
(37278, 't', '\r\nTop', 'dog  n. Colloq. Victor; master.'),
(37279, 't', '\r\nTop', 'drawer  n. Colloq. High social position or origin.'),
(37280, 't', '\r\nTop-dress', ' v. Apply fertilizer on the top of (earth) instead of ploughing it in.  top-dressing n.'),
(37281, 't', '\r\nTope', ' v. (-ping) archaic or literary drink alcohol to excess, esp. Habitually.  toper n. [origin uncertain]'),
(37282, 't', '\r\nTopee', ' var. Of *topi.'),
(37283, 't', '\r\nTop-flight', ' adj. Of the highest rank of achievement.'),
(37284, 't', '\r\nTopgallant', ' n. Mast, sail, yard, or rigging immediately above the topmast and topsail.'),
(37285, 't', '\r\nTop', 'gear  n. Highest gear.'),
(37286, 't', '\r\nTop', 'hat  n. Tall silk hat.'),
(37287, 't', '\r\nTop-heavy', ' adj. Disproportionately heavy at the top.'),
(37288, 't', '\r\nTopi', ' n. (also topee) (pl. -s) hat, esp. A sola topi. [hindi, = hat]'),
(37289, 't', '\r\nTopiary', ' â€”adj. Concerned with or formed by clipping shrubs, trees, etc. Into ornamental shapes. â€”n. Topiary art. [greek topos place]'),
(37290, 't', '\r\nTopic', ' n. Subject of a discourse, conversation, or argument. [greek topos place, commonplace]'),
(37291, 't', '\r\nTopical', ' adj. Dealing with the news, current affairs, etc.  topicality n. Topically adv.'),
(37292, 't', '\r\nTopknot', ' n. Knot, tuft, crest, or bow of ribbon, worn or growing on the head.'),
(37293, 't', '\r\nTopless', ' adj. 1 without a top. 2 a (of clothes) having no upper part. B (of esp. A woman) bare-breasted. C (of a place) where women go topless; employing bare-breasted women.'),
(37294, 't', '\r\nTop-level', ' adj. Of the highest level of importance, prestige, etc.'),
(37295, 't', '\r\nTopmast', ' n. Mast next above the lower mast.'),
(37296, 't', '\r\nTop-notch', ' adj. Colloq. First-rate.'),
(37297, 't', '\r\nTopography', ' n. 1 detailed description, representation on a map, etc., of the features of a town, district, etc. 2 such features.  topographer n. Topographical adj. [greek topos place]'),
(37298, 't', '\r\nTopology', ' n. The study of geometrical properties unaffected by changes of shape or size.  topological adj. [greek topos place]'),
(37299, 't', '\r\nTopper', ' n. Colloq. = *top hat.'),
(37300, 't', '\r\nTopping', ' â€”adj. Archaic slang excellent. â€”n. Thing that tops another thing, esp. Sauce on a dessert etc.'),
(37301, 't', '\r\nTopple', ' v. (-ling) 1 (often foll. By over, down) (cause to) fall as if top-heavy. 2 overthrow. [from *top1]'),
(37302, 't', '\r\nTopsail', ' n. Square sail next above the lowest; fore-and-aft sail on a gaff.'),
(37303, 't', '\r\nTop', 'secret  adj. Of the highest secrecy.'),
(37304, 't', '\r\nTopside', ' n. 1 outer side of a round of beef. 2 side of a ship above the water-line.'),
(37305, 't', '\r\nTopsoil', ' n. Top layer of soil.'),
(37306, 't', '\r\nTopspin', ' n. Spinning motion imparted to a ball in tennis etc. By hitting it forward and upward.'),
(37307, 't', '\r\nTopsy-turvy', ' adv. & adj. 1 upside down. 2 in utter confusion. [from *top1, obsolete terve overturn]'),
(37308, 't', '\r\nTop-up', ' n. Addition; something that serves to top up.'),
(37309, 't', '\r\nToque', ' n. Woman''s small brimless hat. [french]'),
(37310, 't', '\r\nTor', ' n. Hill or rocky peak. [old english]'),
(37311, 't', '\r\nTorch', ' n. 1 portable battery-powered electric lamp. 2 thing lit for illumination. 3 source of heat, illumination, or enlightenment.  carry a torch for suffer from unrequited love for. [latin: related to *tort]'),
(37312, 't', '\r\nTorchlight', ' n. Light of a torch or torches.  torchlit adj.'),
(37313, 't', '\r\nTorch', 'song  n. Popular song of unrequited love.'),
(37314, 't', '\r\nTore', ' past of *tear1.'),
(37315, 't', '\r\nToreador', ' n. Bullfighter, esp. On horseback. [latin taurus]'),
(37316, 't', '\r\nTorment', ' â€”n. 1 severe physical or mental suffering. 2 cause of this. â€”v. 1 subject to torment. 2 tease or worry excessively.  tormentor n. [latin tormentum: related to *tort]'),
(37317, 't', '\r\nTormentil', ' n. Low-growing plant with bright yellow flowers. [french from medieval latin]'),
(37318, 't', '\r\nTorn', ' past part. Of *tear1.'),
(37319, 't', '\r\nTornado', ' n. (pl. -es) violent storm of small extent with whirling winds.  [spanish tronada thunderstorm]'),
(37320, 't', '\r\nTorpedo', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) 1 cigar-shaped self-propelled underwater missile that explodes on impact with a ship. 2 similar device dropped from an aircraft. â€”v. (-es, -ed) 1 destroy or attack with a torpedo. 2 destroy or damage (a policy, institution, plan, etc.). [latin, = electric ray: related to *torpor]'),
(37321, 't', '\r\nTorpedo-boat', ' n. Small fast warship armed with torpedoes.'),
(37322, 't', '\r\nTorpid', ' adj. 1 sluggish, inactive, apathetic. 2 numb. 3 (of a hibernating animal) dormant.  torpidity n. [latin: related to *torpor]'),
(37323, 't', '\r\nTorpor', ' n. Torpid condition. [latin torpeo be sluggish]'),
(37324, 't', '\r\nTorque', ' n. 1 mech. Twisting or rotating force, esp. In a machine. 2 hist. Necklace of twisted metal, esp. Of the ancient gauls and britons. [latin: related to *tort]'),
(37325, 't', '\r\nTorr', ' n. (pl. Same) unit of pressure equal to 133.32 pascals (1/760 of one atmosphere). [torricelli, name of a physicist]'),
(37326, 't', '\r\nTorrent', ' n. 1 rushing stream of liquid. 2 (in pl.) Great downpour of rain. 3 (usu. Foll. By of) violent or copious flow (torrent of abuse).  torrential adj. [french from italian]'),
(37327, 't', '\r\nTorrid', ' adj. 1 a (of the weather) very hot and dry. B (of land etc.) Parched by such weather. 2 passionate, intense. [latin torreo tost- parch]'),
(37328, 't', '\r\nTorrid', 'zone  n. The part of the earth between the tropics of cancer and capricorn.'),
(37329, 't', '\r\nTorsion', ' n. Twisting, esp. Of one end of a body while the other is held fixed.  torsional adj. [latin: related to *tort]'),
(37330, 't', '\r\nTorso', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 trunk of the human body. 2 statue of this. [latin thyrsus rod]'),
(37331, 't', '\r\nTort', ' n. Law breach of duty (other than under contract) leading to liability for damages.  tortious adj. [latin torqueo tort- twist]'),
(37332, 't', '\r\nTortilla', ' n. Thin flat orig. Mexican maize cake eaten hot. [spanish diminutive of torta cake]'),
(37333, 't', '\r\nTortoise', ' n. Slow-moving reptile with a horny domed shell. [medieval latin tortuca]'),
(37334, 't', '\r\nTortoiseshell', ' â€”n. 1 yellowish-brown mottled or clouded outer shell of some turtles. 2 a = *tortoiseshell cat. B = *tortoiseshell butterfly. â€”adj. Having the colouring or appearance of tortoiseshell.'),
(37335, 't', '\r\nTortoiseshell', 'butterfly  n. Butterfly with wings mottled like tortoiseshell.'),
(37336, 't', '\r\nTortoiseshell', 'cat  n. Domestic cat with markings resembling tortoiseshell.'),
(37337, 't', '\r\nTortuous', ' adj. 1 full of twists and turns. 2 devious, circuitous.   tortuously adv. [latin: related to *tort]'),
(37338, 't', '\r\nUsage', ' tortuous should not be confused with torturous which means â€˜involving torture, excruciatingâ€™.'),
(37339, 't', '\r\nTorture', ' â€”n. 1 infliction of severe bodily pain, esp. As a punishment or means of persuasion. 2 severe physical or mental suffering. â€”v. (-ring) subject to torture.  torturer n. Torturous adj. [latin tortura twisting: related to *tort]'),
(37340, 't', '\r\nTory', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 colloq. = *conservative n. 2. 2 hist. Member of the party that gave rise to the conservative party (opp. *whig). â€”adj. Colloq. = *conservative adj. 3.  toryism n. [originally = irish outlaw]'),
(37341, 't', '\r\nTosa', ' n. Dog of a breed of mastiff, orig. Kept for dog-fighting. [japanese]'),
(37342, 't', '\r\nTosh', ' n. Colloq. Rubbish, nonsense. [origin unknown]'),
(37343, 't', '\r\nToss', ' â€”v. 1 throw up (a ball etc.), esp. With the hand. 2 roll about, throw, or be thrown, restlessly or from side to side. 3 (usu. Foll. By to, away, aside, out, etc.) Throw (a thing) lightly or carelessly. 4 a throw (a coin) into the air to decide a choice etc. By the side on which it lands. B (also absol.; often foll. By for) settle a question or dispute with (a person) in this way. 5 (of a bull etc.) Throw (a person etc.) Up with the horns. 6 coat (food) with dressing etc. By shaking it. â€”n. 1 act of tossing (a coin, the head, etc.). 2  fall, esp. From a horse.  toss one''s head throw it back esp. In anger, impatience, etc. Toss off 1 drink off at a draught. 2 dispatch (work) rapidly or without effort. 3 coarse slang masturbate. Toss up toss a coin. [origin unknown]'),
(37344, 't', '\r\nToss-up', ' n. 1 doubtful matter. 2 tossing of a coin.'),
(37345, 't', '\r\nTot1', ' n. 1 small child. 2 dram of liquor. [originally dial.]'),
(37346, 't', '\r\nTot2', ' v. (-tt-) 1 (usu. Foll. By up) add (figures etc.). 2 (foll. By up) (of items) mount up.  tot up to amount to. [abbreviation of *total or of latin totum the whole]'),
(37347, 't', '\r\nTotal', ' â€”adj. 1 complete, comprising the whole (total number of votes). 2 absolute, unqualified (in total ignorance). â€”n. Total number or amount. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 a amount in number to. B find the total of. 2 (foll. By to, up to) amount to. [medieval latin totus entire]'),
(37348, 't', '\r\nTotalitarian', ' adj. Of a one-party form of government requiring complete subservience to the state.  totalitarianism n.'),
(37349, 't', '\r\nTotality', ' n. 1 complete amount. 2 time during which an eclipse is total.'),
(37350, 't', '\r\nTotalizator', ' n. (also totalisator) 1 device showing the number and amount of bets staked on a race, to facilitate the division of the total among those backing the winner. 2 system of betting based on this.'),
(37351, 't', '\r\nTotalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) collect into a total; find the total of.'),
(37352, 't', '\r\nTotally', ' adv. Completely.'),
(37353, 't', '\r\nTote1', ' n. Slang totalizator. [abbreviation]'),
(37354, 't', '\r\nTote2', ' v. (-ting) esp. Us colloq. Carry, convey (toting a gun). [originally us, probably of dial. Origin]'),
(37355, 't', '\r\nTote', 'bag  n. Woman''s large bag for shopping etc.'),
(37356, 't', '\r\nTotem', ' n. 1 natural object, esp. An animal, adopted esp. By n. American indians as an emblem of a clan or individual. 2 image of this.  totemic adj. [algonquian]'),
(37357, 't', '\r\nTotem-pole', ' n. Pole on which totems are carved or hung.'),
(37358, 't', '\r\nT''other', ' adj. & pron. Dial. Or joc. The other. [thet other â€˜that otherâ€™]'),
(37359, 't', '\r\nTotter', ' â€”v. 1 stand or walk unsteadily or feebly. 2 a (of a building etc.) Shake as if about to collapse. B (of a system of government etc.) Be about to fall. â€”n. Unsteady or shaky movement or gait.  tottery adj. [dutch]'),
(37360, 't', '\r\nTotting-up', ' n. 1 adding of separate items. 2 adding up of convictions for driving offences, possibly resulting in disqualification.'),
(37361, 't', '\r\nToucan', ' n. Tropical american fruit-eating bird with an immense beak. [tupi]'),
(37362, 't', '\r\nTouch', ' â€”v. 1 come into or be in physical contact with (a thing, each other, etc.). 2 (often foll. By with) bring the hand etc. Into contact with. 3 bring (two things) into mutual contact. 4 rouse tender or painful feelings in. 5 strike lightly. 6 (usu. With neg.) A disturb, harm, or affect. B have any dealings with. C consume, use (i don''t touch alcohol). 7 concern. 8 a reach as far as, esp. Momentarily. B (usu. With neg.) Approach in excellence etc. (can''t touch him for style). 9 modify (pity touched with fear). 10 (as touched adj.) Colloq. Slightly mad. 11 (usu. Foll.'),
(37363, 't', '\r\nTouch-and-go', ' adj. Critical, risky.'),
(37364, 't', '\r\nTouchdown', ' n. Act of touching down by an aircraft.'),
(37365, 't', '\r\nTouchÃ©', ' int. 1 acknowledgement of a justified accusation or retort. 2 acknowledgement of a hit by a fencing-opponent. [french, = touched]'),
(37366, 't', '\r\nTouching', ' â€”adj. Moving; pathetic. â€”prep. Literary concerning.  touchingly adv.'),
(37367, 't', '\r\nTouch-line', ' n. (in various sports) either of the lines marking the side boundaries of the pitch.'),
(37368, 't', '\r\nTouch-paper', ' n. Paper impregnated with nitre, for igniting etc.'),
(37369, 't', '\r\nTouchstone', ' n. 1 dark schist or jasper used for testing alloys by marking it with them. 2 criterion.'),
(37370, 't', '\r\nTouch-type', ' v. Type without looking at the keys.  touch-typist n.'),
(37371, 't', '\r\nTouchwood', ' n. Readily inflammable wood etc., esp. When made soft by fungi.'),
(37372, 't', '\r\nTouchy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) apt to take offence; over-sensitive.  touchily adv. Touchiness n.'),
(37373, 't', '\r\nTough', ' â€”adj. 1 hard to break, cut, tear, or chew. 2 able to endure hardship; hardy. 3 unyielding, stubborn, difficult (it was a tough job). 4 colloq. A acting sternly; hard (get tough with). B (of circumstances, luck, etc.) Severe, hard. 5 colloq. Criminal or violent. â€”n. Tough person, esp. A ruffian.  toughen v. Toughness n. [old english]'),
(37374, 't', '\r\nToupee', ' n. Hairpiece to cover a bald spot. [french]'),
(37375, 't', '\r\nTour', ' â€”n. 1 a journey from place to place as a holiday; sightseeing excursion. B a walk round; inspection (made a tour of the garden). 2 spell of duty on military or diplomatic service. 3 series of performances, matches, etc., at different places. â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By through) make a tour. 2 make a tour of (a country etc.).  on tour (esp. Of a team, theatre company, etc.) Touring. [latin: related to *turn]'),
(37376, 't', '\r\nTour', 'de force  n. (pl. Tours de force) outstanding feat or performance. [french]'),
(37377, 't', '\r\nTourer', ' n. Car or caravan for touring in.'),
(37378, 't', '\r\nTourism', ' n. Commercial organization and operation of holidays.'),
(37379, 't', '\r\nTourist', ' n. 1 holiday-maker, esp. Abroad (often attrib.: tourist season). 2 member of a touring sports team.'),
(37380, 't', '\r\nTourist', 'class  n. Lowest class of passenger accommodation in a ship, aircraft, etc.'),
(37381, 't', '\r\nTouristy', ' adj. Usu. Derog. Appealing to or visited by many tourists.'),
(37382, 't', '\r\nTourmaline', ' n. Mineral of various colours used as a gemstone. [french from sinhalese]'),
(37383, 't', '\r\nTournament', ' n. 1 large contest of many rounds (chess tournament). 2 display of military exercises etc. (royal tournament). 3 hist. Pageant with jousting. [french: related to *tourney]'),
(37384, 't', '\r\nTournedos', ' n. (pl. Same) small round thick cut from a fillet of beef. [french]'),
(37385, 't', '\r\nTourney', ' â€”n. (pl. -s) tournament. â€”v. (-eys, -eyed) take part in a tournament. [french: related to *turn]'),
(37386, 't', '\r\nTourniquet', ' n. Device for stopping the flow of blood through an artery by constriction. [french]'),
(37387, 't', '\r\nTour', 'operator  n. Travel agent specializing in package holidays.'),
(37388, 't', '\r\nTousle', ' v. (-ling) 1 make (esp. The hair) untidy. 2 handle roughly. [dial. Touse]'),
(37389, 't', '\r\nTout', ' â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By for) solicit custom persistently; pester customers. 2 solicit the custom of (a person) or for (a thing), esp. Sell (tickets) at a price higher than the official one. 3  spy out the movements and condition of racehorses in training. â€”n. Person who touts, esp. Tickets. [old english, = peep]'),
(37390, 't', '\r\nTow1', ' â€”v. Pull (a boat, vehicle, etc.) Along by a rope etc. â€”n. Towing or being towed.  have in (or on) tow 1 be towing. 2 be accompanied by and often in charge of (a person). On tow being towed. [old english]'),
(37391, 't', '\r\nTow2', ' n. Coarse part of flax or hemp prepared for spinning. [low german touw]'),
(37392, 't', '\r\nToward', ' prep. = *towards.'),
(37393, 't', '\r\nTowards', ' prep. 1 in the direction of (set out towards town). 2 as regards; in relation to (attitude towards death). 3 as a contribution to; for (put it towards her holiday). 4 near (towards the end of our journey). [old english, = future: related to *to, *-ward]'),
(37394, 't', '\r\nTow-bar', ' n. Bar for towing esp. A caravan.'),
(37395, 't', '\r\nTowel', ' â€”n. Absorbent cloth or paper etc. Used for drying after washing. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) (often refl.) Wipe or dry with a towel. [french toail(l)e from germanic]'),
(37396, 't', '\r\nTowelling', ' n. Thick soft absorbent cloth, used esp. For towels.'),
(37397, 't', '\r\nTower', ' â€”n. 1 tall structure, often part of a church, castle, etc. 2 fortress etc. With a tower. 3 tall structure housing machinery etc. (cooling tower; control tower). â€”v. 1 (usu. Foll. By above, up) reach or be high or above; be superior. 2 (as towering adj.) A high, lofty (towering intellect). B violent (towering rage). [greek turris]'),
(37398, 't', '\r\nTower', 'block  n. Tall building containing offices or flats.'),
(37399, 't', '\r\nTower', 'of strength  n. Person who gives strong emotional support.'),
(37400, 't', '\r\nTow-headed', ' adj. Having very light or unkempt hair.'),
(37401, 't', '\r\nTown', ' n. 1 a densely populated built-up defined area, between a city and a village in size. B densely populated area, esp. As opposed to the country. 2 a london or the chief city or town in an area (went up to town). B central business area in a neighbourhood.  go to town colloq. Do something with great energy or enthusiasm. On the town colloq. Enjoying night-life in a town. [old english]'),
(37402, 't', '\r\nTown', 'clerk  n. Us & hist. Official in charge of the records etc. Of a town.'),
(37403, 't', '\r\nTown', 'crier  n. = *crier 2.'),
(37404, 't', '\r\nTownee', ' var. Of *townie.'),
(37405, 't', '\r\nTown', 'gas  n. Manufactured gas for domestic and commercial use.'),
(37406, 't', '\r\nTown', 'hall  n. Headquarters of local government, with public meeting rooms etc.'),
(37407, 't', '\r\nTown', 'house  n. 1 town residence, esp. Of a person with a house in the country. 2 terrace house. 3 house in a planned group in a town.'),
(37408, 't', '\r\nTownie', ' n. (also townee) derog. Town inhabitant ignorant of country life.'),
(37409, 't', '\r\nTown', 'planning  n. Planning of the construction and growth of towns.  town planner n.'),
(37410, 't', '\r\nTownscape', ' n. 1 visual appearance of a town or towns. 2 picture of a town.'),
(37411, 't', '\r\nTownsfolk', ' n. Inhabitants of a town or towns.'),
(37412, 't', '\r\nTownship', ' n. 1 s.afr. Urban area set aside for black occupation. 2 us & can. A division of a county. B district six miles square. 3 hist. Small town or village forming part of a large parish. 4 austral. & nz small town.'),
(37413, 't', '\r\nTownsman', ' n. (fem. Townswoman) inhabitant of a town.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(37414, 't', '\r\nTownspeople', ' n.pl. People of a town.'),
(37415, 't', '\r\nTow-path', ' n. Path by a river or canal, orig. Used for towing a boat by horse.'),
(37416, 't', '\r\nToxaemia', ' n. (us toxemia) 1 blood-poisoning. 2 increased blood pressure in pregnancy. [related to *toxic, greek haima blood]'),
(37417, 't', '\r\nToxemia', ' n. (brit. Toxaemia) 1 blood-poisoning. 2 increased blood pressure in pregnancy. [related to *toxic, greek haima blood]'),
(37418, 't', '\r\nToxic', ' adj. 1 poisonous. 2 of poison.  toxicity n. [greek toxikon poison for arrows]'),
(37419, 't', '\r\nToxicology', ' n. The study of poisons.  toxicological adj. Toxicologist n.'),
(37420, 't', '\r\nToxin', ' n. Poison produced by a living organism.'),
(37421, 't', '\r\nToxocara', ' n. Parasitic worm in dogs and cats.'),
(37422, 't', '\r\nToxocariasis', ' n. Disease resulting from infection by the toxocara.'),
(37423, 't', '\r\nToy', ' â€”n. 1 plaything. 2 thing regarded as providing amusement. 3 (usu. Attrib.) Diminutive breed of dog etc. â€”v. (usu. Foll. By with) 1 trifle, amuse oneself, flirt. 2 move a thing idly. [origin unknown]'),
(37424, 't', '\r\nToy', 'boy  n. Colloq. Woman''s much younger boyfriend.'),
(37425, 't', '\r\nToyshop', ' n. Shop selling toys.'),
(37426, 't', '\r\nTrace1', ' â€”v. (-cing) 1 a observe or find vestiges or signs of by investigation. B (often foll. By along, through, to, etc.) Follow or mark the track or position of. C (often foll. By back) follow to its origins. 2 copy (a drawing etc.) By drawing over its lines on superimposed translucent paper. 3 (often foll. By out) mark out, delineate, sketch, or write, esp. Laboriously. 4 make one''s way along (a path etc.). â€”n. 1 a indication of something having existed; vestige. B very small quantity. 2 track or footprint. 3 track left by the moving pen of an instrument etc.  traceable adj. [latin traho draw]'),
(37427, 't', '\r\nTrace2', ' n. Each of the two side-straps, chains, or ropes by which a horse draws a vehicle.  kick over the traces become insubordinate or reckless. [french trais, pl. Of *trait]'),
(37428, 't', '\r\nTrace', 'element  n. Chemical element required only in minute amounts by living organisms for normal growth.'),
(37429, 't', '\r\nTracer', ' n. 1 bullet etc. That is visible in flight because of flames etc. Emitted. 2 artificial radioactive isotope which can be followed through the body by the radiation it produces.'),
(37430, 't', '\r\nTracery', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 ornamental stone openwork, esp. In the upper part of a gothic window. 2 fine decorative pattern.'),
(37431, 't', '\r\nTrachea', ' n. (pl. -cheae) windpipe. [latin from greek]'),
(37432, 't', '\r\nTracheotomy', ' n. (pl. -ies) incision of the trachea to relieve an obstruction.'),
(37433, 't', '\r\nTracing', ' n. 1 traced copy of a drawing etc. 2 act of tracing.'),
(37434, 't', '\r\nTracing-paper', ' n. Translucent paper for making tracings.'),
(37435, 't', '\r\nTrack', ' â€”n. 1 a mark(s) left by a person, animal, vehicle, etc. B (in pl.) Such marks, esp. Footprints. 2 rough path, esp. One beaten by use. 3 continuous railway line. 4 a racecourse; circuit. B prepared course for runners etc. 5 a groove on a gramophone record. B section of a record, cd, or magnetic tape containing one song etc. C lengthwise strip of magnetic tape containing a single sequence of signals. 6 line of travel (track of the comet). 7 band round the wheels of a tank etc. 8 line of thought or action. â€”v. 1 follow the track of. 2 trace (a course, development, etc.) By vestiges. 3 (often foll. By back, in, etc.) (of a film or television camera) move in relation to the subject being filmed.  in one''s tracks colloq. Where one stands, instantly (stopped him in his tracks). Keep (or lose) track of follow (or fail to follow) the course of. Make tracks colloq. Depart. Make tracks for colloq. Go in pursuit of or towards. Off the track away from the subject. Track down reach or capture by tracking.  tracker n. [french trac]'),
(37436, 't', '\r\nTracker', 'dog  n. Police dog tracking by scent.'),
(37437, 't', '\r\nTrack', 'events  n.pl. Running-races as opposed to jumping etc.'),
(37438, 't', '\r\nTrack', 'record  n. Person''s past performance.'),
(37439, 't', '\r\nTrack', 'shoe  n. Runner''s spiked shoe.'),
(37440, 't', '\r\nTrack', 'suit  n. Loose warm suit worn for exercising etc.'),
(37441, 't', '\r\nTract1', ' n. 1 stretch or extent of territory, esp. Large. 2  bodily organ or system (digestive tract). [latin traho tract- pull]'),
(37442, 't', '\r\nTract2', ' n. Pamphlet, esp. Propagandist. [apparently latin tractatus from tracto handle]'),
(37443, 't', '\r\nTractable', ' adj. (of a person or material) easily handled; manageable.  tractability n. [latin tracto handle]'),
(37444, 't', '\r\nTraction', ' n. 1 act of hauling or pulling a thing over a surface. 2 sustained therapeutic pulling on a limb etc. With pulleys, weights, etc. [french or medieval latin: related to *tract1]'),
(37445, 't', '\r\nTraction-engine', ' n. Steam or diesel engine for drawing heavy loads on roads, fields, etc.'),
(37446, 't', '\r\nTractor', ' n. 1 vehicle used for pulling farm machinery etc. 2 traction-engine. [related to *traction]'),
(37447, 't', '\r\nTrad', ' colloq. â€”n. Traditional jazz. â€”adj. Traditional. [abbreviation]'),
(37448, 't', '\r\nTrade', ' â€”n. 1 a buying and selling. B this between nations etc. C business conducted for profit (esp. As distinct from a profession). D business of a specified nature or time (christmas trade; tourist trade). 2 skilled craft practised professionally. 3 (usu. Prec. By the) people engaged in a specific trade (the trade will never agree). 4 us transaction, esp. A swap. 5 (usu. In pl.) Trade wind. â€”v. (-ding) 1 (often foll. By in, with) engage in trade; buy and sell. 2 a exchange in commerce. B exchange (insults, blows, etc.). C us swap. 3 (usu. Foll. By with, for) have a transaction with a person for a thing.  trade in (often foll. By for) exchange (esp. A used car) in part payment for another. Trade off exchange, esp. As a compromise. Trade on take advantage of.  tradable adj. Tradeable adj. [low german, = track: related to *tread]'),
(37449, 't', '\r\nTrade-in', ' n. Thing given in part exchange for another.'),
(37450, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'mark  n. 1 device or name secured by law or custom as representing a company, product, etc. 2 distinctive characteristic etc.'),
(37451, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'name  n. 1 name by which a thing is called in a trade. 2 name given to a product. 3 name under which a business trades.'),
(37452, 't', '\r\nTrade-off', ' n. Balance, compromise.'),
(37453, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'price  n. Price charged to the retailer.'),
(37454, 't', '\r\nTrader', ' n. 1 person engaged in trade. 2 merchant ship.'),
(37455, 't', '\r\nTradescantia', ' n. (usu. Trailing) plant with large blue, white, or pink flowers. [tradescant, name of a naturalist]'),
(37456, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'secret  n. 1 secret device or technique used esp. In a trade. 2 joc. Any secret.'),
(37457, 't', '\r\nTradesman', ' n. (fem. Tradeswoman) person engaged in trade, esp. A shopkeeper.'),
(37458, 't', '\r\nTradespeople', ' n.pl. People engaged in trade.'),
(37459, 't', '\r\nTrades', 'union congress  n. Official representative body of british trade unions.'),
(37460, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'union  n. (also trades union) organized association of workers in a trade, profession, etc., formed to protect and further their rights and interests.  trade-unionism n. Trade-unionist n.'),
(37461, 't', '\r\nTrade', 'wind  n. Wind blowing continually towards the equator and deflected westward.'),
(37462, 't', '\r\nTrading', ' n. Act of engaging in trade.'),
(37463, 't', '\r\nTrading', 'estate  n. Specially-designed industrial and commercial area.'),
(37464, 't', '\r\nTrading', 'post  n. Store etc. In a remote or unsettled region.'),
(37465, 't', '\r\nTrading-stamp', ' n. Stamp given to customers by some shops and exchangeable in large numbers for goods or cash.'),
(37466, 't', '\r\nTradition', ' n. 1 a custom, opinion, or belief handed down to posterity. B this process of handing down. 2 artistic, literary, etc. Principles based on experience and practice; any one of these. [latin trado -dit- hand on, betray]'),
(37467, 't', '\r\nTraditional', ' adj. 1 of, based on, or obtained by tradition. 2 (of jazz) in the style of the early 20th c.  traditionally adv.'),
(37468, 't', '\r\nTraditionalism', ' n. Respect or support for tradition.  traditionalist n. & adj.'),
(37469, 't', '\r\nTraduce', ' v. (-cing) speak ill of; misrepresent.  traducement n. Traducer n. [latin, = disgrace]'),
(37470, 't', '\r\nTraffic', ' â€”n. 1 vehicles moving on a public highway or in the air or at sea. 2 (usu. Foll. By in) trade, esp. Illegal (drugs traffic). 3 coming and going of people or goods by road, rail, air, sea, etc. 4 dealings between people etc. (had no traffic with them). 5 messages etc. Transmitted through a communications system; volume of this. â€”v. (-ck-) 1 (usu. Foll. By in) deal in something, esp. Illegally. 2 deal in; barter.  trafficker n. [french from italian]'),
(37471, 't', '\r\nTraffic', 'island  n. Raised area in a road to divide traffic streams and for pedestrians to use in crossing.'),
(37472, 't', '\r\nTraffic', 'jam  n. Traffic at a standstill because of roadworks, an accident, etc.'),
(37473, 't', '\r\nTraffic-light', ' n. (also traffic-lights n.pl.) Signal controlling road traffic by coloured lights.'),
(37474, 't', '\r\nTraffic', 'warden  n. Official employed to help control road traffic and esp. Parking.'),
(37475, 't', '\r\nTragedian', ' n. 1 writer of tragedies. 2 (fem. Tragedienne) actor in tragedy. [french: related to *tragedy]'),
(37476, 't', '\r\nTragedy', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 serious accident, disaster, etc.; sad event. 2 a play dealing with tragic events and ending unhappily, esp. With the downfall of the protagonist. B such plays as a genre. [greek tragoidia]'),
(37477, 't', '\r\nTragic', ' adj. 1 disastrous; greatly distressing; very sad. 2 of tragedy.  tragically adv.'),
(37478, 't', '\r\nTragicomedy', ' n. (pl. -ies) play or situation with a mixture of comedy and tragedy.  tragicomic adj.'),
(37479, 't', '\r\nTrail', ' â€”n. 1 track or scent left by a moving thing, person, etc. 2 beaten path, esp. Through a wild region. 3 long line of people or things following behind something. 4 part dragging behind a thing or person. â€”v. 1 draw or be drawn along behind, esp. On the ground. 2 (often foll. By behind) walk wearily. 3 follow the trail of; pursue. 4 be losing in a contest (trailing by three points). 5 (usu. Foll. By away, off) peter out; tail off. 6 a (of a plant etc.) Grow or hang over a wall, along the ground, etc. B hang loosely. 7 (often refl.) Drag (oneself, one''s limbs, etc.) Along wearily etc. [french or low german]'),
(37480, 't', '\r\nTrail-blazer', ' n. 1 person who marks a new track through wild country. 2 pioneer.  trail-blazing n.'),
(37481, 't', '\r\nTrailer', ' n. 1 set of brief extracts from a film etc., used to advertise it in advance. 2 vehicle towed by another, esp.: a the rear section of an articulated lorry. B an open cart. C a platform for transporting a boat etc. D us a caravan.'),
(37482, 't', '\r\nTrailing', 'edge  n. Rear edge of an aircraft''s wing etc.'),
(37483, 't', '\r\nTrain', ' â€”v. 1 a (often foll. By to + infin.) Teach (a person, animal, oneself, etc.) A specified skill, esp. By practice. B undergo this process (trained as a teacher). 2 bring or come to physical efficiency by exercise, diet, etc. 3 (often foll. By along, up) guide the growth of (a plant). 4 (usu. As trained adj.) Make (the mind, eye, etc.) Discerning through practice etc. 5 (often foll. By on) point or aim (a gun, camera, etc.) At an object etc. â€”n. 1 series of railway carriages or trucks drawn by an engine. 2 thing dragged along behind or forming the back part of a dress, robe, etc. 3 succession or series of people, things, events, etc. (train of thought). 4 body of followers; retinue.  in train properly arranged or directed.  trainee n. [latin traho draw]'),
(37484, 't', '\r\nTrain-bearer', ' n. Person holding up the train of a robe etc.'),
(37485, 't', '\r\nTrainer', ' n. 1 person who trains horses, athletes, footballers, etc. 2 aircraft or simulator used to train pilots. 3  soft running shoe.'),
(37486, 't', '\r\nTraining', ' n. Process of teaching or learning a skill etc.'),
(37487, 't', '\r\nTrain-spotter', ' n. Person who collects locomotive numbers as a hobby.  train-spotting n.'),
(37488, 't', '\r\nTraipse', ' colloq. â€”v. (-sing) tramp or trudge wearily. â€”n. Tedious journey on foot. [origin unknown]'),
(37489, 't', '\r\nTrait', ' n. Characteristic. [latin tractus: related to *tract1]'),
(37490, 't', '\r\nTraitor', ' n. (fem. Traitress) (often foll. By to) person who is treacherous or disloyal, esp. To his or her country.  traitorous adj. [latin traditor: related to *tradition]'),
(37491, 't', '\r\nTrajectory', ' n. (pl. -ies) path of an object moving under given forces. [latin traicio -ject- throw across]'),
(37492, 't', '\r\nTram', ' n. 1 (also tramcar) electrically-powered passenger road vehicle running on rails. 2 four-wheeled vehicle used in coalmines. [low german and dutch trame beam]'),
(37493, 't', '\r\nTramlines', ' n.pl. 1 rails for a tramcar. 2 colloq.  Pair of long parallel lines at the sides of a tennis or badminton court.'),
(37494, 't', '\r\nTrammel', ' â€”n. 1 (usu. In pl.) Impediment; hindrance (trammels of domesticity). 2 triple drag-net for fishing. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) hamper. [medieval latin tremaculum]'),
(37495, 't', '\r\nTramp', ' â€”v. 1 a walk heavily and firmly. B go on foot, esp. A distance. 2 a cross on foot, esp. Wearily or reluctantly. B cover (a distance) in this way. 3 (often foll. By down) tread on; trample; stamp on. 4 live as a tramp. â€”n. 1 itinerant vagrant or beggar. 2 sound of a person, or esp. People, walking, marching, etc. 3 long walk. 4 slang derog. Promiscuous woman. [germanic]'),
(37496, 't', '\r\nTrample', ' v. (-ling) 1 tread under foot. 2 press down or crush in this way.  trample on 1 tread heavily on. 2 treat roughly or with contempt. [from *tramp]'),
(37497, 't', '\r\nTrampoline', ' â€”n. Strong fabric sheet connected by springs to a horizontal frame, used for gymnastic jumping. â€”v. (-ning) use a trampoline. [italian trampolino]'),
(37498, 't', '\r\nTramway', ' n. Rails for a tram.'),
(37499, 't', '\r\nTrance', ' n. 1 a sleeplike state without response to stimuli. B hypnotic or cataleptic state. 2 such a state as entered into by a medium. 3 rapture, ecstasy. [latin transeo pass over]'),
(37500, 't', '\r\nTranny', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Transistor radio. [abbreviation]'),
(37501, 't', '\r\nTranquil', ' adj. Calm, serene, undisturbed.  tranquillity n. Tranquilly adv. [latin]'),
(37502, 't', '\r\nTranquilize', ' v. (brit. Tranquillize, -ise) (-zing or -sing) make tranquil, esp. By a drug etc.'),
(37503, 't', '\r\nTranquilizer', ' n. (brit. Tranquillizer, -iser) drug used to diminish anxiety.'),
(37504, 't', '\r\nTranquillize', ' v. (us tranquilize, -ise) (-zing or -sing) make tranquil, esp. By a drug etc.'),
(37505, 't', '\r\nTranquillizer', ' n. (us tranquilizer, -iser) drug used to diminish anxiety.'),
(37506, 't', '\r\nTrans-', ' prefix 1 across, beyond. 2 on or to the other side of. 3 through. [latin]'),
(37507, 't', '\r\nTransact', ' v. Perform or carry through (business). [latin: related to *act]'),
(37508, 't', '\r\nTransaction', ' n. 1 a piece of esp. Commercial business done. B transacting of business etc. 2 (in pl.) Published reports of discussions, papers read, etc., at the meetings of a learned society.'),
(37509, 't', '\r\nTransalpine', ' adj. On the north side of the alps. [latin]'),
(37510, 't', '\r\nTransatlantic', ' adj. 1 beyond the atlantic, esp.: a american. B us european. 2 crossing the atlantic.'),
(37511, 't', '\r\nTransceiver', ' n. Combined radio transmitter and receiver.'),
(37512, 't', '\r\nTranscend', ' v. 1 be beyond the range or grasp of (human experience, reason, belief, etc.). 2 excel; surpass. [latin scando climb]'),
