(37868, 't', '\r\nTuc', ' abbr. Trades union congress.'),
(37869, 't', '\r\nTuck', ' â€”v. 1 (often foll. By in, up) a draw, fold, or turn the outer or end parts of (cloth or clothes etc.) Close together so as to be held; push in the edge of (a thing) so as to confine it (tucked his shirt into his trousers). B push in the edges of bedclothes around (a person) (came to tuck me in). 2 draw together into a small space (tucked its head under its wing). 3 stow (a thing) away in a specified place or way (tucked it in a corner; tucked it out of sight). 4 make a stitched fold in (cloth etc.). â€”n. 1 flattened usu. Stitched fold in cloth etc. 2 colloq. Food, esp. Cakes and sweets (also attrib.: tuck box).  tuck in colloq. Eat heartily. Tuck into (or away) colloq. Eat (food) heartily (tucked into their dinner; could really tuck it away). [low german or dutch]'),
(37870, 't', '\r\nTucker', ' â€”n. 1 hist. Piece of lace or linen etc. In or on a woman''s bodice. 2 austral. & nz slang food. â€”v. (esp. In passive; often foll. By out) us & austral. Colloq. Tire.'),
(37871, 't', '\r\nTuck-in', ' n. Colloq. Large meal.'),
(37872, 't', '\r\nTuck', 'shop  n. Small shop selling sweets etc. To schoolchildren.'),
(37873, 't', '\r\n-tude', ' suffix forming abstract nouns (altitude; solitude). [latin -tudo]'),
(37874, 't', '\r\nTudor', ' adj. 1 of the royal family of england 1485â€“1603 or this period. 2 of the architectural style of this period, esp. With half-timbering. [owen tudor, name of the grandfather of henry vii]'),
(37875, 't', '\r\nTues.', ' Abbr. (also tue.) Tuesday.'),
(37876, 't', '\r\nTuesday', ' â€”n. Day of the week following monday. â€”adv. 1 colloq. On tuesday. 2 (tuesdays) on tuesdays; each tuesday. [old english]'),
