(38139, 'u', '\r\nUlsterman', ' n. (fem. Ulsterwoman) native of ulster.'),
(38140, 'u', '\r\nUlt.', ' Abbr. Ultimo.'),
(38141, 'u', '\r\nUlterior', ' adj. Not evident or admitted; hidden, secret (esp. Ulterior motive). [latin, = further]'),
(38142, 'u', '\r\nUltimate', ' â€”adj. 1 last or last possible, final. 2 fundamental, primary, basic (ultimate truths). â€”n. 1 (prec. By the) best achievable or imaginable. 2 final or fundamental fact or principle.  ultimately adv. [latin ultimus last]'),
(38143, 'u', '\r\nUltimatum', ' n. (pl. -s) final statement of terms, the rejection of which could cause hostility etc. [latin: related to *ultimate]'),
(38144, 'u', '\r\nUltimo', ' adj. Commerce of last month (the 28th ultimo). [latin, = in the last (mense month)]'),
(38145, 'u', '\r\nUltra', ' â€”adj. Extreme, esp. In religion or politics. â€”n. Extremist. [see *ultra-]'),
(38146, 'u', '\r\nUltra-', ' comb. Form 1 extreme(ly), excessive(ly) (ultra-modern). 2 beyond. [latin ultra beyond]'),
(38147, 'u', '\r\nUltra-high', ' adj. (of a frequency) in the range 300 to 3000 megahertz.'),
(38148, 'u', '\r\nUltramarine', ' â€”n. 1 brilliant blue pigment orig. From lapis lazuli. 2 colour of this. â€”adj. Of this colour. [italian and medieval latin, = beyond the sea, from where lapis lazuli was brought]'),
(38149, 'u', '\r\nUltramicroscopic', ' adj. Too small to be seen by an ordinary optical microscope.'),
(38150, 'u', '\r\nUltramontane', ' â€”adj. 1 situated beyond the alps. 2 advocating supreme papal authority. â€”n. 1 person living beyond the alps. 2 advocate of supreme papal authority. [medieval latin: related to *mountain]'),
(38151, 'u', '\r\nUltrasonic', ' adj. Of or using sound waves pitched above the range of human hearing.  ultrasonically adv.'),
(38152, 'u', '\r\nUltrasonics', ' n.pl. (usu. Treated as sing.) Science of ultrasonic waves.'),
(38153, 'u', '\r\nUltrasound', ' n. Ultrasonic waves.'),
(38154, 'u', '\r\nUltraviolet', ' adj. Of or using radiation with a frequency just beyond that of the violet end of the visible spectrum.'),
(38155, 'u', '\r\nUltra', 'vires  adv. & predic.adj. Beyond one''s legal power or authority. [latin]'),
(38156, 'u', '\r\nUlulate', ' v. (-ting) howl, wail.  ululation n. [latin]'),
(38157, 'u', '\r\nUm', ' int. Expressing hesitation or a pause in speech. [imitative]'),
(38158, 'u', '\r\nUmbel', ' n. Flower-cluster with stalks springing from a common centre and forming a flat or curved surface.  umbellate adj. [latin umbella sunshade]'),
(38159, 'u', '\r\nUmbelliferous', ' adj. (of a plant) bearing umbels, such as parsley and carrot.'),
(38160, 'u', '\r\nUmber', ' â€”n. 1 natural pigment like ochre but darker and browner. 2 colour of this. â€”adj. Of this colour. [latin umbra shadow]'),
(38161, 'u', '\r\nUmbilical', ' adj. Of the navel. [from *umbilicus]'),
(38162, 'u', '\r\nUmbilical', 'cord  n. Cordlike structure attaching a foetus to the placenta.'),
(38163, 'u', '\r\nUmbilicus', ' n. (pl. -ci or -cuses) navel. [latin]'),
(38164, 'u', '\r\nUmbra', ' n. (pl. -s or -brae) total shadow, esp. That cast on the earth by the moon during a solar eclipse. [latin, = shadow]'),
(38165, 'u', '\r\nUmbrage', ' n. Offence taken (esp. Take umbrage at). [latin: related to *umbra]'),
(38166, 'u', '\r\nUmbrella', ' n. 1 collapsible cloth canopy on a central stick, used against rain, strong sun, etc. 2 protection, patronage. 3 (often attrib.) Coordinating agency (umbrella organization). [italian diminutive: related to *umbra]'),
(38167, 'u', '\r\nUmlaut', ' n. 1 mark (Â¨) used over a vowel, esp. In germanic languages, to indicate a vowel change. 2 such a vowel change, e.g. German mann, mÃ¤nner, english man, men. [german]'),
(38168, 'u', '\r\nUmpire', ' â€”n. Person enforcing rules and settling disputes in esp. Cricket or between disputants. â€”v. (-ring) (often foll. By for, in, etc.) Act as umpire (in). [french nonper not equal: related to *peer2]'),
(38169, 'u', '\r\nUmpteen', ' colloq. â€”adj. Indefinitely many; a lot of. â€”pron. Indefinitely many.  umpteenth adj. [jocular formation on *-teen]'),
(38170, 'u', '\r\nUn', ' abbr. United nations.'),
(38171, 'u', '\r\nUn-1', ' prefix 1 added to adjectives and participles and their derivative nouns and adverbs, meaning: a not (unusable). B reverse of (esp. With implied approval etc.) (unselfish; unsociable). 2 (less often) added to nouns, meaning â€˜a lack ofâ€™, â€˜the reverse ofâ€™ (unrest; untruth). [old english]'),
(38172, 'u', '\r\nUsage', ' the number of words that can be formed with this prefix (and with un-2) is virtually unlimited; consequently only a selection can be given here.'),
(38173, 'u', '\r\nUn-2', ' prefix added to verbs and (less often) nouns, forming verbs denoting: 1 reversal (undress; unsettle). 2 deprivation (unmask). 3 release from (unburden; uncage). 4 causing to be no longer (unman). [old english]'),
