(39287, 'v', '\r\nVale', ' n. (archaic except in place-names) valley. [latin vallis]'),
(39288, 'v', '\r\nValediction', ' n. Formal 1 bidding farewell. 2 words used in this.  valedictory adj. & n. (pl. -ies). [latin vale farewell]'),
(39289, 'v', '\r\nValence1', ' n. = *valency.'),
(39290, 'v', '\r\nValence2', ' var. Of *valance.'),
(39291, 'v', '\r\nValency', ' n. (pl. -ies) combining power of an atom measured by the number of hydrogen atoms it can displace or combine with. [latin valentia power]'),
(39292, 'v', '\r\nValentine', ' n. 1 card sent, often anonymously, as a mark of love on st valentine''s day (14 feb.). 2 sweetheart chosen on this day. [valentine, name of two saints]'),
(39293, 'v', '\r\nValerian', ' n. Any of various flowering herbs, esp. Used as a sedative. [french from medieval latin]'),
(39294, 'v', '\r\nValet', ' â€”n. Gentleman''s personal servant. â€”v. (-t-) 1 work as a valet (for). 2 clean or clean out (a car). [french va(s)let, related to *varlet, *vassal]'),
(39295, 'v', '\r\nValetudinarian', ' â€”n. Person of poor health or who is unduly anxious about health. â€”adj. Of a valetudinarian.  valetudinarianism n. [latin valetudo health]'),
(39296, 'v', '\r\nValiant', ' adj. Brave.  valiantly adv. [latin valeo be strong]'),
(39297, 'v', '\r\nValid', ' adj. 1 (of a reason, objection, etc.) Sound, defensible. 2 a executed with the proper formalities, legally acceptable (valid contract; valid passport). B not yet expired.  validity n. [latin validus strong: related to *valiant]'),
(39298, 'v', '\r\nValidate', ' v. (-ting) make valid; ratify.  validation n.'),
(39299, 'v', '\r\nValise', ' n. Us small portmanteau. [french from italian]'),
(39300, 'v', '\r\nValium', ' n. Propr. Drug diazepam used as a tranquillizer. [origin uncertain]'),
(39301, 'v', '\r\nValley', ' n. (pl. -s) low area between hills, usu. With a stream or river flowing through it. [french: related to *vale]'),
(39302, 'v', '\r\nValor', ' n. (brit. Valour) courage, esp. In battle.  valorous adj. [latin valeo be strong]'),
(39303, 'v', '\r\nValour', ' n. (us valor) courage, esp. In battle.  valorous adj. [latin valeo be strong]'),
(39304, 'v', '\r\nValuable', ' â€”adj. Of great value, price, or worth. â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Valuable thing.  valuably adv.'),
(39305, 'v', '\r\nValuation', ' n. 1 estimation (esp. Professional) of a thing''s worth. 2 worth so estimated.'),
(39306, 'v', '\r\nValue', ' â€”n. 1 worth, desirability, or utility, or the qualities on which these depend. 2 worth as estimated (set a high value on my time). 3 amount for which a thing can be exchanged in the open market. 4 equivalent of a thing. 5 (in full value for money) something well worth the money spent. 6 effectiveness (news value). 7 (in pl.) One''s principles, priorities, or standards. 8 mus. Duration of a note. 9 math. Amount denoted by an algebraic term. â€”v. (-ues, -ued, -uing) 1 estimate the value of, esp. Professionally. 2 have a high or specified opinion of.  valueless adj. Valuer n. [french past part. Of valoir be worth, from latin valeo]'),
(39307, 'v', '\r\nValue', 'added tax  n. Tax levied on the rise in value of services and goods at each stage of production.'),
(39308, 'v', '\r\nValue', 'judgement  n. Subjective estimate of worth etc.'),
(39309, 'v', '\r\nValve', ' n. 1 device controlling flow through a pipe etc., esp. Allowing movement in one direction only. 2 structure in an organ etc. Allowing a flow of blood etc. In one direction only. 3 = *thermionic valve. 4 device to vary the effective length of the tube in a trumpet etc. 5 half-shell of an oyster, mussel, etc.  valvular adj. [latin valva leaf of a folding door]'),
(39310, 'v', '\r\nVamoose', ' v. Us slang depart hurriedly. [spanish vamos let us go]'),
(39311, 'v', '\r\nVamp1', ' â€”n. Upper front part of a boot or shoe. â€”v. 1 (often foll. By up) repair or furbish. 2 (foll. By up) make by patching or from odds and ends. 3 improvise a musical accompaniment. [french avantpiÃ© front of the foot]'),
(39312, 'v', '\r\nVamp2', ' colloq. â€”n. Woman who uses sexual attraction to exploit men. â€”v. Allure and exploit (a man). [abbreviation of *vampire]'),
(39313, 'v', '\r\nVampire', ' n. 1 supposed ghost or reanimated corpse sucking the blood of sleeping persons. 2 person who preys ruthlessly on others. 3 (in full vampire bat) tropical (esp. South american) bloodsucking bat. [french or german from magyar]'),
(39314, 'v', '\r\nVan1', ' n. 1 small covered goods vehicle. 2 railway carriage for luggage and for the guard. [abbreviation of *caravan]'),
(39315, 'v', '\r\nVan2', ' n. Vanguard, forefront. [abbreviation]'),
(39316, 'v', '\r\nVanadium', ' n. Hard grey metallic element used to strengthen steel. [old norse vanadÃ­s name of the scandinavian goddess freyja]'),
(39317, 'v', '\r\nVandal', ' n. Person who wilfully or maliciously damages property.  vandalism n. [vandals, name of a germanic people that sacked rome and destroyed works of art in the 5th c.: latin from germanic]'),
(39318, 'v', '\r\nVandalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) wilfully or maliciously destroy or damage (esp. Public property).'),
(39319, 'v', '\r\nVane', ' n. 1 weather-vane. 2 blade of a screw propeller or windmill etc. [dial. Var. Of obsolete fane banner]'),
(39320, 'v', '\r\nVanguard', ' n. 1 foremost part of an advancing army etc. 2 leaders of a movement etc. [french avan(t)garde from avant before: related to *guard]'),
(39321, 'v', '\r\nVanilla', ' n. 1 a tropical fragrant climbing orchid. B (in full vanilla-pod) fruit of this. 2 extract from the vanilla-pod, or a synthetic substance, used as flavouring. [spanish diminutive of vaina pod]'),
(39322, 'v', '\r\nVanish', ' v. 1 disappear. 2 cease to exist. [latin: related to *vain]'),
(39323, 'v', '\r\nVanishing', 'cream  n. Skin ointment that leaves no visible trace.'),
(39324, 'v', '\r\nVanishing-point', ' n. 1 point at which receding parallel lines appear to meet. 2 stage of complete disappearance.'),
(39325, 'v', '\r\nVanity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 conceit about one''s appearance or attainments. 2 futility, unsubstantiality, unreal thing (the vanity of human achievement). 3 ostentatious display. [latin: related to *vain]'),
(39326, 'v', '\r\nVanity', 'bag  n. (also vanity case) woman''s make-up bag or case.'),
(39327, 'v', '\r\nVanity', 'unit  n. Wash-basin set into a unit with cupboards beneath.'),
(39328, 'v', '\r\nVanquish', ' v. Literary conquer, overcome. [latin vinco]'),
(39329, 'v', '\r\nVantage', ' n. 1 (also vantage point) place giving a good view. 2 tennis = *advantage. [french: related to *advantage]'),
(39330, 'v', '\r\nVapid', ' adj. Insipid; dull; flat.  vapidity n. [latin vapidus]'),
(39331, 'v', '\r\nVapor', ' n. (brit. Vapour) 1 moisture or other substance diffused or suspended in air, e.g. Mist, smoke. 2  gaseous form of a substance. 3 medicinal inhalant.  vaporous adj. Vapoury adj. [latin vapor steam]'),
(39332, 'v', '\r\nVaporize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) change into vapour.  vaporization n.'),
(39333, 'v', '\r\nVapour', ' n. (us vapor) 1 moisture or other substance diffused or suspended in air, e.g. Mist, smoke. 2  gaseous form of a substance. 3 medicinal inhalant.  vaporous adj. Vapoury adj. [latin vapor steam]'),
(39334, 'v', '\r\nVapour', 'trail  n. Trail of condensed water from an aircraft etc.'),
(39335, 'v', '\r\nVariable', ' â€”adj. 1 changeable, adaptable. 2 apt to vary; not constant. 3 math. (of a quantity) indeterminate; able to assume different numerical values. â€”n. Variable thing or quantity.  variability n. Variably adv.'),
(39336, 'v', '\r\nVariance', ' n. 1 (usu. Prec. By at) difference of opinion; dispute (we were at variance). 2 discrepancy.'),
(39337, 'v', '\r\nVariant', ' â€”adj. 1 differing in form or details from a standard (variant spelling). 2 having different forms (forty variant types). â€”n. Variant form, spelling, type, etc.'),
(39338, 'v', '\r\nVariation', ' n. 1 varying. 2 departure from the normal kind, amount, a standard, etc. (prices are subject to variation). 3 extent of this. 4 variant thing. 5 mus. Theme in a changed or elaborated form.'),
(39339, 'v', '\r\nVaricolored', ' adj. (brit. Varicoloured) 1 variegated in colour. 2 of various colours. [latin varius *various]'),
(39340, 'v', '\r\nVaricoloured', ' adj. (us varicolored) 1 variegated in colour. 2 of various colours. [latin varius *various]'),
(39341, 'v', '\r\nVaricose', ' adj. (esp. Of a vein etc.) Permanently and abnormally dilated. [latin varix varicose vein]'),
(39342, 'v', '\r\nVaried', ' adj. Showing variety.'),
(39343, 'v', '\r\nVariegated', ' adj. 1 with irregular patches of different colours. 2 having leaves of two or more colours.  variegation n. [latin: related to *various]'),
(39344, 'v', '\r\nVariety', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 diversity; absence of uniformity; many-sidedness. 2 quantity or collection of different things (for a variety of reasons). 3 a class of things that differ from the rest in the same general class. B member of such a class. 4 (foll. By of) different form of a thing, quality, etc. 5 biol. Subdivision of a species. 6 series of dances, songs, comedy acts, etc. (variety show). [latin: related to *various]'),
(39345, 'v', '\r\nVarious', ' adj. 1 different, diverse (from various backgrounds). 2 several (for various reasons).  variously adv. [latin varius]'),
(39346, 'v', '\r\nUsage', ' various (unlike several) cannot be used with of, as (wrongly) in various of the guests arrived late.'),
(39347, 'v', '\r\nVarlet', ' n. Archaic menial; rascal. [french var. Of vaslet *valet]'),
(39348, 'v', '\r\nVarnish', ' â€”n. 1  resinous solution used to give a hard shiny transparent coating. 2 similar preparation (nail varnish). 3 deceptive outward appearance or show. â€”v. 1 apply varnish to. 2 give a deceptively attractive appearance to. [french vernis, probably ultimately from berenice in cyrenaica]'),
(39349, 'v', '\r\nVarsity', ' n. (pl. -ies) colloq. (esp. With ref. To sports) university. [abbreviation]'),
(39350, 'v', '\r\nVary', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 be or become different; be of different kinds; change. 2 make different; modify. [latin vario: related to *various]'),
(39351, 'v', '\r\nVas', ' n. (pl. Vasa) vessel or duct. [latin, = vessel]'),
(39352, 'v', '\r\nVascular', ' adj. Of or containing vessels for conveying blood, sap, etc. [latin vasculum diminutive of *vas]'),
(39353, 'v', '\r\nVas', 'deferens  n. (pl. Vasa deferentia) sperm duct of the testicle.'),
(39354, 'v', '\r\nVase', ' n. Vessel used as an ornament or container for flowers. [latin: related to *vas]'),
(39355, 'v', '\r\nVasectomy', ' n. (pl. -ies) removal of part of each vas deferens, esp. For sterilization.'),
(39356, 'v', '\r\nVaseline', ' n. Propr. Type of petroleum jelly used as an ointment etc. [german wasser water, greek elaion oil]'),
(39357, 'v', '\r\nVassal', ' n. 1 hist. Feudal tenant of land. 2 humble dependant.  vassalage n. [medieval latin vassallus retainer]'),
(39358, 'v', '\r\nVast', ' adj. Immense, huge.  vastly adv. Vastness n. [latin]'),
(39359, 'v', '\r\nVat', ' abbr. Value added tax.'),
(39360, 'v', '\r\nVat', ' n. Tank, esp. For holding liquids in brewing, distilling, food manufacture, dyeing, and tanning. [dial. Var. Of fat, from old english]'),
(39361, 'v', '\r\nVatican', ' n. Palace or government of the pope in rome. [name of a hill in rome]'),
(39362, 'v', '\r\nVaudeville', ' n. Esp. Us 1 variety entertainment. 2 light stage play with interspersed songs.  vaudevillian adj. & n.  [french]'),
(39363, 'v', '\r\nVault', ' â€”n. 1 arched roof. 2 vaultlike covering (vault of heaven). 3 underground storage chamber or place of interment beneath a church or in a cemetery etc. 4 act of vaulting. â€”v. 1 leap, esp. Using the hands or a pole. 2 spring over in this way. 3 (esp. As vaulted) a make in the form of a vault. B provide with a vault or vaults. [latin volvo roll]'),
(39364, 'v', '\r\nVaulting', ' n. Arched work in a vaulted roof or ceiling.'),
(39365, 'v', '\r\nVaulting-horse', ' n. Wooden box for vaulting over.'),
(39366, 'v', '\r\nVaunt', ' v. & n. Literary boast. [latin: related to *vain]'),
(39367, 'v', '\r\nVc', ' abbr. Victoria cross.'),
(39368, 'v', '\r\nVcr', ' abbr. Video cassette recorder.'),
(39369, 'v', '\r\nVd', ' abbr. Venereal disease.'),
(39370, 'v', '\r\nVdu', ' abbr. Visual display unit.'),
(39371, 'v', '\r\nVe', ' abbr. Victory in europe (in 1945).'),
(39372, 'v', '\r\n''Ve', ' abbr. (usu. After pronouns) have (i''ve; they''ve).'),
(39373, 'v', '\r\nVeal', ' n. Calf''s flesh as food. [french from latin vitulus calf]'),
(39374, 'v', '\r\nVector', ' n. 1 math. & physics quantity having direction as well as magnitude. 2 carrier of disease. [latin veho vect- convey]'),
(39375, 'v', '\r\nVeda', ' n. (in sing. Or pl.) Oldest hindu scriptures.  vedic adj. [sanskrit, = knowledge]'),
(39376, 'v', '\r\nVe', 'day  n. 8 may, the day marking victory in europe (in 1945).'),
(39377, 'v', '\r\nVeer', ' â€”v. 1 change direction, esp. (of the wind) clockwise. 2 change in course or opinion etc. â€”n. Change of direction. [french virer]'),
(39378, 'v', '\r\nVeg', ' n. Colloq. Vegetable(s). [abbreviation]'),
(39379, 'v', '\r\nVegan', ' â€”n. Person who does not eat animals or animal products. â€”adj. Using or containing no animal products. [shortening of *vegetarian]'),
(39380, 'v', '\r\nVegeburger', ' var. Of *veggie burger.'),
(39381, 'v', '\r\nVegetable', ' â€”n. 1 plant, esp. A herbaceous plant used for food, e.g. A cabbage, potato, or bean. 2 colloq. Derog. A offens. Person who is severely mentally incapacitated, esp. Through brain injury etc. B dull or inactive person. â€”adj. Of, derived from, or relating to plant life or vegetables as food. [latin: related to *vegetate]'),
