(39383, 'v', '\r\nVegetal', ' adj. Of or like plants. [medieval latin: related to *vegetate]'),
(39384, 'v', '\r\nVegetarian', ' â€”n. Person who does not eat meat or fish. â€”adj. Excluding animal food, esp. Meat (vegetarian diet).  vegetarianism n. [from *vegetable]'),
(39385, 'v', '\r\nVegetate', ' v. (-ting) 1 live an uneventful or monotonous life. 2 grow as plants do. [latin vegeto animate]'),
(39386, 'v', '\r\nVegetation', ' n. Plants collectively; plant life. [medieval latin: related to *vegetate]'),
(39387, 'v', '\r\nVegetative', ' adj. 1 concerned with growth and development as distinct from sexual reproduction. 2 of vegetation. [french or medieval latin: related to *vegetate]'),
(39388, 'v', '\r\nVeggie', ' n. (also vegie) colloq. Vegetarian. [abbreviation]'),
(39389, 'v', '\r\nVeggie', 'burger  n. (also vegeburger) flat cake like a hamburger but containing vegetables or soya protein instead of meat.'),
(39390, 'v', '\r\nVehement', ' adj. Showing or caused by strong feeling; ardent (vehement protest).  vehemence n. Vehemently adv. [latin]'),
(39391, 'v', '\r\nVehicle', ' n. 1 conveyance used on land or in space. 2 thing or person as a medium for expression or action. 3 liquid etc. As a medium for suspending pigments, drugs, etc.  vehicular adj. [latin veho carry]'),
(39392, 'v', '\r\nVeil', ' â€”n. 1 piece of usu. Transparent fabric attached to a woman''s hat etc., esp. To conceal or protect the face. 2 piece of linen etc. As part of a nun''s headdress. 3 thing that hides or disguises (a veil of silence). â€”v. 1 cover with a veil. 2 (esp. As veiled adj.) Partly conceal (veiled threats).  beyond the veil in the unknown state of life after death. Draw a veil over avoid discussing; hush up. Take the veil become a nun. [latin velum]'),
(39393, 'v', '\r\nVein', ' n. 1 a any of the tubes conveying blood to the heart. B (in general use) any blood-vessel. 2 rib of an insect''s wing or leaf. 3 streak of a different colour in wood, marble, cheese, etc. 4 fissure in rock filled with ore. 5 specified character or tendency; mood (spoke in a sarcastic vein).  veined adj. Veiny adj. (-ier, -iest). [latin vena]'),
(39394, 'v', '\r\nVelcro', ' n. Propr. Fastener consisting of two strips of fabric which cling when pressed together. [french velours crochÃ© hooked velvet]'),
(39395, 'v', '\r\nVeld', ' n. (also veldt) s.afr. Open country. [afrikaans: related to *field]'),
(39396, 'v', '\r\nVeleta', ' n. Ballroom dance in triple time. [spanish, = weather-vane]'),
(39397, 'v', '\r\nVellum', ' n. 1 a fine parchment, orig. Calfskin. B manuscript on this. 2 smooth writing-paper imitating vellum. [french velin: related to *veal]'),
(39398, 'v', '\r\nVelocity', ' n. (pl. -ies) speed, esp. Of inanimate things (wind velocity; velocity of light). [latin velox swift]'),
(39399, 'v', '\r\nVelodrome', ' n. Place or building with a track for cycle-racing. [french vÃ©lo bicycle]'),
(39400, 'v', '\r\nVelour', ' n. (also velours pronunc. Same) plushlike fabric. [french]'),
(39401, 'v', '\r\nVelvet', ' â€”n. 1 soft fabric with a thick short pile on one side. 2 furry skin on a growing antler. â€”adj. Of, like, or soft as velvet.  on velvet in an advantageous or prosperous position.  velvety adj. [latin villus tuft, down]'),
(39402, 'v', '\r\nVelveteen', ' n. Cotton fabric with a pile like velvet.'),
(39403, 'v', '\r\nVelvet', 'glove  n. Outward gentleness, esp. Cloaking firmness.'),
(39404, 'v', '\r\nVen.', ' Abbr. Venerable (as the title of an archdeacon).'),
(39405, 'v', '\r\nVenal', ' adj. Corrupt; able to be bribed; involving bribery.  venality n. Venally adv. [latin venum thing for sale]'),
(39406, 'v', '\r\nUsage', ' venal is sometimes confused with venial, which means â€˜pardonableâ€™.'),
(39407, 'v', '\r\nVend', ' v. Offer (small wares) for sale.  vendible adj. [latin vendo sell]'),
(39408, 'v', '\r\nVendetta', ' n. 1 blood feud. 2 prolonged bitter quarrel. [latin: related to *vindictive]'),
(39409, 'v', '\r\nVending-machine', ' n. Slot-machine selling small items.'),
(39410, 'v', '\r\nVendor', ' n. Law seller, esp. Of property. [anglo-french: related to *vend]'),
(39411, 'v', '\r\nVeneer', ' â€”n. 1 thin covering of fine wood etc. 2 (often foll. By of) deceptively pleasing appearance. â€”v. 1 apply a veneer to (wood etc.). 2 disguise. [german furnieren to furnish]'),
(39412, 'v', '\r\nVenerable', ' adj. 1 entitled to deep respect on account of character, age, associations, etc. (venerable priest; venerable relics). 2 title of an archdeacon in the church of england. [latin: related to *venerate]'),
(39413, 'v', '\r\nVenerate', ' v. (-ting) respect deeply.  veneration n. Venerator n. [latin veneror revere]'),
(39414, 'v', '\r\nVenereal', ' adj. 1 of sexual desire or intercourse. 2 of venereal disease. [latin venus veneris sexual love]'),
(39415, 'v', '\r\nVenereal', 'disease  n. Disease contracted by sexual intercourse with an infected person or congenitally.'),
(39416, 'v', '\r\nVenetian', ' â€”n. Native, citizen, or dialect of venice. â€”adj. Of venice. [from french or medieval latin venetia venice]'),
(39417, 'v', '\r\nVenetian', 'blind  n. Window-blind of adjustable horizontal slats.'),
(39418, 'v', '\r\nVengeance', ' n. Punishment inflicted for wrong to oneself or one''s cause.  with a vengeance to a high or excessive degree (punctuality with a vengeance). [french venger from latin vindico avenge]'),
(39419, 'v', '\r\nVengeful', ' adj. Vindictive; seeking vengeance.  vengefully adv. [obsolete venge avenge: related to *vengeance]'),
(39420, 'v', '\r\nVenial', ' adj. (of a sin or fault) pardonable; not mortal.  veniality n. Venially adv. [latin venia forgiveness]'),
(39421, 'v', '\r\nUsage', ' venial is sometimes confused with venal, which means â€˜corruptâ€™.'),
(39422, 'v', '\r\nVenison', ' n. Deer''s flesh as food. [latin venatio hunting]'),
(39423, 'v', '\r\nVenn', 'diagram  n. Diagram using overlapping and intersecting circles etc. To show the relationships between mathematical sets. [venn, name of a logician]'),
(39424, 'v', '\r\nVenom', ' n. 1 poisonous fluid of esp. Snakes. 2 malignity; virulence.  venomous adj. Venomously adv. [latin venenum]'),
(39425, 'v', '\r\nVenous', ' adj. Of, full of, or contained in, veins. [latin: related to *vein]'),
(39426, 'v', '\r\nVent1', ' â€”n. 1 opening allowing the passage of air etc. 2 outlet; free expression (gave vent to my anger). 3 anus, esp. Of a lower animal. â€”v. 1 make a vent in (a cask etc.). 2 give free expression to.  vent one''s spleen on scold or ill-treat without cause. [latin ventus wind]'),
(39427, 'v', '\r\nVent2', ' n. Slit in a garment, esp. In the lower edge of the back of a jacket. [french fente from latin findo cleave]'),
(39428, 'v', '\r\nVentilate', ' v. (-ting) 1 cause air to circulate freely in (a room etc.). 2 air (a question, grievance, etc.). 3 med. A oxygenate (the blood). B admit or force air into (the lungs).  ventilation n. [latin ventilo blow, winnow: related to *vent1]'),
(39429, 'v', '\r\nVentilator', ' n. 1 appliance or aperture for ventilating a room etc. 2 med. = *respirator 2.'),
(39430, 'v', '\r\nVentral', ' adj. Of or on the abdomen. [venter abdomen, from latin]'),
(39431, 'v', '\r\nVentricle', ' n. 1 cavity in the body. 2 hollow part of an organ, esp. The brain or heart.  ventricular adj. [latin ventriculus diminutive of venter belly]'),
(39432, 'v', '\r\nVentriloquism', ' n. (also ventriloquy) skill of speaking without moving the lips, esp. As entertainment with a dummy.  ventriloquist n. [latin venter belly, loquor speak]'),
(39433, 'v', '\r\nVenture', ' â€”n. 1 risky undertaking. 2 commercial speculation. â€”v. (-ring) 1 dare; not be afraid. 2 dare to go, make, or put forward (venture out; venture an opinion). 3 a expose to risk; stake. B take risks. [from *adventure]'),
(39434, 'v', '\r\nVenture', 'scout  n. Senior scout.'),
(39435, 'v', '\r\nVenturesome', ' adj. 1 disposed to take risks. 2 risky.'),
(39436, 'v', '\r\nVenue', ' n. Place for a match, meeting, concert, etc. [french, from venir come]'),
(39437, 'v', '\r\nVenus', 'fly-trap  n. Insectivorous plant. [latin venus goddess of love]'),
(39438, 'v', '\r\nVeracious', ' adj. Formal 1 truthful by nature. 2 (of a statement etc.) True.  veracity n. [latin verax from verus true]'),
(39439, 'v', '\r\nVeranda', ' n. (usu. Covered) platform along the side of a house. [hindi from portuguese varanda]'),
(39440, 'v', '\r\nVerb', ' n. Word used to indicate action, a state, or an occurrence (e.g. Hear, be, happen). [latin verbum word]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(39441, 'v', '\r\nVerbal', ' â€”adj. 1 of words. 2 oral, not written. 3 of a verb. 4 (of a translation) literal. 5 talkative. â€”n. 1 slang verbal statement to the police. 2 slang stream of abuse.  verbally adv. [latin: related to *verb]'),
(39442, 'v', '\r\nUsage', ' some people reject sense 2 of verbal as illogical, and prefer oral. However, verbal is the usual term in expressions such as verbal communication, verbal contract, and verbal evidence.'),
(39443, 'v', '\r\nVerbalize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) put into words.'),
(39444, 'v', '\r\nVerbal', 'noun  n. Noun derived from a verb (e.g. Smoking in smoking is forbidden: see *-ing1).'),
(39445, 'v', '\r\nVerbatim', ' adv. & adj. In exactly the same words. [medieval latin: related to *verb]'),
(39446, 'v', '\r\nVerbena', ' n. (pl. Same) plant of a genus of usu. Annual or biennial plants with clusters of fragrant flowers. [latin]'),
(39447, 'v', '\r\nVerbiage', ' n. Derog. Too many words or unnecessarily difficult words. [french: related to *verb]'),
(39448, 'v', '\r\nVerbose', ' adj. Using more words than are needed.  verbosity n. [latin verbosus from verbum word]'),
(39449, 'v', '\r\nVerdant', ' adj. 1 (of grass, a field, etc.) Green, lush. 2 (of a person) unsophisticated, green.  verdancy n. [perhaps from french verdeant from viridis green]'),
(39450, 'v', '\r\nVerdict', ' n. 1 decision of a jury in a civil or criminal case. 2 decision; judgement. [anglo-french verdit from ver true, dit saying]'),
(39451, 'v', '\r\nVerdigris', ' n. Greenish-blue substance that forms on copper or brass. [french, = green of greece]'),
(39452, 'v', '\r\nVerdure', ' n. Literary green vegetation or its colour. [french verd green]'),
(39453, 'v', '\r\nVerge1', ' n. 1 edge or border. 2 brink (on the verge of tears). 3 grass edging of a road etc. [latin virga rod]'),
(39454, 'v', '\r\nVerge2', ' v. (-ging) 1 (foll. By on) border on. 2 incline downwards or in a specified direction. [latin vergo bend]'),
(39455, 'v', '\r\nVerger', ' n. 1 church caretaker and attendant. 2 officer preceding a bishop etc. With a staff. [anglo-french: related to *verge1]'),
(39456, 'v', '\r\nVerify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 establish the truth, correctness, or validity of by examination etc. (verified my figures). 2 (of an event etc.) Bear out (a prediction or promise).  verifiable adj. Verification n. [medieval latin: related to *very]'),
(39457, 'v', '\r\nVerily', ' adv. Archaic really, truly. [from *very]'),
(39458, 'v', '\r\nVerisimilitude', ' n. Appearance of being true or real. [latin verus true, similis like]'),
(39459, 'v', '\r\nVeritable', ' adj. Real; rightly so called (a veritable feast).  veritably adv. [french: related to *verity]'),
(39460, 'v', '\r\nVerity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 a fundamental truth. 2 archaic truth. [latin veritas truth]'),
(39461, 'v', '\r\nVermicelli', ' n. 1 pasta in long slender threads. 2 shreds of chocolate as cake decoration etc. [latin vermis worm]'),
(39462, 'v', '\r\nVermicide', ' n. Drug that kills intestinal worms. [latin vermis worm]'),
(39463, 'v', '\r\nVermiculite', ' n. A hydrous silicate mineral used esp. As a moisture-holding medium for plant growth. [latin vermiculatus worm-eaten, from vermis worm]'),
(39464, 'v', '\r\nVermiform', ' adj. Worm-shaped. [medieval latin: related to *vermicide]'),
(39465, 'v', '\r\nVermiform', 'appendix  n. Small blind tube extending from the caecum in man and some other mammals.'),
(39466, 'v', '\r\nVermilion', ' â€”n. 1 cinnabar. 2 a brilliant red pigment made esp. From this. B colour of this. â€”adj. Of this colour. [latin vermiculus diminutive of vermis worm]'),
(39467, 'v', '\r\nVermin', ' n. (usu. Treated as pl.) 1 mammals and birds harmful to game, crops, etc., e.g. Foxes and rats. 2 parasitic worms or insects. 3 vile people.  verminous adj. [latin vermis worm]'),
(39468, 'v', '\r\nVermouth', ' n. Wine flavoured with aromatic herbs. [german: related to *wormwood]'),
(39469, 'v', '\r\nVernacular', ' â€”n. 1 language or dialect of a particular country. 2 language of a particular class or group. 3 homely speech. â€”adj. (of language) native; not foreign or formal. [latin vernaculus native]'),
(39470, 'v', '\r\nVernal', ' adj. Of or in spring. [latin ver spring]'),
(39471, 'v', '\r\nVernal', 'equinox  var. Of *spring equinox.'),
(39472, 'v', '\r\nVernier', ' n. Small movable graduated scale for obtaining fractional parts of subdivisions on a fixed scale. [vernier, name of a mathematician]'),
(39473, 'v', '\r\nVeronal', ' n. Sedative drug. [german from verona in italy]'),
(39474, 'v', '\r\nVeronica', ' n. Speedwell. [medieval latin, probably from st veronica]'),
(39475, 'v', '\r\nVerruca', ' n. (pl. Verrucae or -s) wart or similar growth, esp. On the foot. [latin]'),
(39476, 'v', '\r\nVersatile', ' adj. 1 adapting easily to different subjects or occupations; skilled in many subjects or occupations. 2 having many uses.  versatility n. [latin verto vers- turn]'),
(39477, 'v', '\r\nVerse', ' n. 1 poetry. 2 stanza of a poem or song. 3 each of the short numbered divisions of the bible. 4 poem. [latin versus: related to *versatile]'),
(39478, 'v', '\r\nVersed', ' adj. (foll. By in) experienced or skilled in. [latin versor be engaged in]'),
(39479, 'v', '\r\nVersicle', ' n. Each of a priest''s short sentences in a liturgy, answered by the congregation. [latin diminutive: related to *verse]'),
(39480, 'v', '\r\nVersify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) 1 turn into or express in verse. 2 compose verses.  versification n. Versifier n.'),
(39481, 'v', '\r\nVersion', ' n. 1 account of a matter from a particular point of view. 2 book etc. In a particular edition or translation (authorized version). 3 form or variant. [latin verto vers- turn]'),
(39482, 'v', '\r\nVerso', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 left-hand page of an open book. 2 back of a printed leaf. [latin verso (folio) on the turned (leaf)]'),
(39483, 'v', '\r\nVersus', ' prep. Against (esp. In law and sport). [latin: related to *verse]'),
(39484, 'v', '\r\nVertebra', ' n. (pl. -brae) each segment of a backbone.  vertebral adj. [latin verto turn]'),
(39485, 'v', '\r\nVertebrate', ' â€”adj. (of an animal) having a backbone. â€”n. Vertebrate animal. [latin vertebratus jointed: related to *vertebra]'),
(39486, 'v', '\r\nVertex', ' n. (pl. -tices or -texes) 1 highest point; top, apex. 2 a each angular point of a triangle, polygon, etc. B meeting-point of lines that form an angle. [latin, = whirlpool, crown of a head, from verto turn]'),
(39487, 'v', '\r\nVertical', ' â€”adj. 1 at right angles to a horizontal plane. 2 in a direction from top to bottom of a picture etc. 3 of or at the vertex. â€”n. Vertical line or plane.  vertically adv. [latin: related to *vertex]'),
(39488, 'v', '\r\nVertical', 'take-off  n. Take-off of an aircraft directly upwards.'),
(39489, 'v', '\r\nVertiginous', ' adj. Of or causing vertigo. [latin: related to *vertigo]'),
(39490, 'v', '\r\nVertigo', ' n. Dizziness caused esp. By heights. [latin, = whirling, from verto turn]'),
(39491, 'v', '\r\nVervain', ' n. Any of several verbenas, esp. One with small blue, white, or purple flowers. [latin: related to *verbena]'),
(39492, 'v', '\r\nVerve', ' n. Enthusiasm, vigour, spirit. [french]'),
(39493, 'v', '\r\nVery', ' â€”adv. 1 in a high degree (did it very easily). 2 in the fullest sense (foll. By own or superl. Adj.: do your very best; my very own room). â€”adj. Actual; truly such (the very thing we need; his very words; the very same).  not very in a low degree, far from being. Very good (or well) formula of consent or approval. Very high frequency (in radio) 30-300 megahertz. Very reverend title of a dean. [latin verus true]'),
(39494, 'v', '\r\nVery', 'light  n. Flare projected from a pistol for signalling or illuminating part of a battlefield etc. [very, name of its inventor]'),
(39495, 'v', '\r\nVesicle', ' n. Small bladder, bubble, or blister. [latin]'),
(39496, 'v', '\r\nVespers', ' n.pl. Evensong. [latin vesper evening]'),
(39497, 'v', '\r\nVessel', ' n. 1 hollow receptacle, esp. For liquid. 2 ship or boat, esp. A large one. 3 duct or canal etc. Holding or conveying blood or sap, etc., esp. = *blood-vessel. [latin diminutive: related to *vas]'),
(39498, 'v', '\r\nVest', ' â€”n. 1 undergarment worn on the trunk. 2 us & austral. Waistcoat. â€”v. 1 (foll. By with) bestow (powers, authority, etc.) On. 2 (foll. By in) confer (property or power) on (a person) with an immediate fixed right of future possession. 3 clothe (oneself), esp. In vestments. [latin vestis garment]'),
(39499, 'v', '\r\nVestal', 'virgin  n. Rom. Antiq. Virgin consecrated to vesta and vowed to chastity. [vesta, roman goddess of the hearth and home]'),
(39500, 'v', '\r\nVested', 'interest  n. 1 personal interest in a state of affairs, usu. With an expectation of gain. 2 law interest (usu. In land or money held in trust) recognized as belonging to a person.'),
(39501, 'v', '\r\nVestibule', ' n. 1 hall or lobby of a building. 2 us enclosed space between railway-carriages. [latin]'),
(39502, 'v', '\r\nVestige', ' n. 1 trace; sign. 2 slight amount; particle. 3 atrophied part or organ of an animal or plant that was well developed in ancestors.  vestigial adj. [latin vestigium footprint]'),
(39503, 'v', '\r\nVestment', ' n. Ceremonial garment, esp. A chasuble. [latin: related to *vest]'),
(39504, 'v', '\r\nVestry', ' n. (pl. -ies) church room or building for keeping vestments etc. In.'),
(39505, 'v', '\r\nVet1', ' â€”n. Colloq. Veterinary surgeon. â€”v. (-tt-) make a careful and critical examination of (a scheme, work, candidate, etc.). [abbreviation]'),
(39506, 'v', '\r\nVet2', ' n. Us veteran. [abbreviation]'),
(39507, 'v', '\r\nVetch', ' n. Plant of the pea family used largely for fodder. [latin vicia]'),
(39508, 'v', '\r\nVeteran', ' n. 1 (often attrib.) Old soldier or long-serving member of any group (war veteran; veteran actor). 2 us ex-serviceman or servicewoman. [latin vetus -er- old]'),
(39509, 'v', '\r\nVeteran', 'car  n. Car made before 1916, or (strictly) before 1905.'),
(39510, 'v', '\r\nVeterinarian', ' n. Formal veterinary surgeon.'),
(39511, 'v', '\r\nVeterinary', ' â€”adj. Of or for the diseases and injuries of animals. â€”n. (pl. -ies) veterinary surgeon. [latin veterinae cattle]'),
(39512, 'v', '\r\nVeterinary', 'surgeon  n. Person qualified to treat animals.'),
(39513, 'v', '\r\nVeto', ' â€”n. (pl. -es) 1 right to reject a measure, resolution, etc. Unilaterally. 2 rejection, prohibition. â€”v. (-oes, -oed) 1 reject (a measure etc.). 2 forbid, prohibit. [latin, = i forbid]'),
(39514, 'v', '\r\nVex', ' v. 1 anger, irritate. 2 archaic grieve, afflict. [latin vexo afflict]'),
(39515, 'v', '\r\nVexation', ' n. 1 vexing or being vexed. 2 annoying or distressing thing.'),
(39516, 'v', '\r\nVexatious', ' adj. 1 causing vexation. 2 law (of litigation) lacking sufficient grounds and seeking only to annoy the defendant.'),
(39517, 'v', '\r\nVexed', ' adj. (of a question) much discussed; problematic.'),
(39518, 'v', '\r\nV.g.c.', ' Abbr. Very good condition.'),
(39519, 'v', '\r\nVhf', ' abbr. Very high frequency.'),
(39520, 'v', '\r\nVia', ' prep. Through (london to rome via paris; send it via your son). [latin, ablative of via way]'),
(39521, 'v', '\r\nViable', ' adj. 1 (of a plan etc.) Feasible, esp. Economically. 2 (esp. Of a foetus) capable of developing and surviving independently.  viability n. [french vie life]'),
(39522, 'v', '\r\nViaduct', ' n. Long bridge, esp. A series of arches, carrying a road or railway across a valley or hollow. [latin via way, after *aqueduct]'),
(39523, 'v', '\r\nVial', ' n. Small (usu. Cylindrical glass) vessel, esp. For holding medicines. [related to *phial]'),
(39524, 'v', '\r\nViand', ' n. Formal (usu. In pl.) Article of food. [latin vivo live]'),
(39525, 'v', '\r\nViaticum', ' n. (pl. -ca) eucharist given to a dying person. [latin via road]'),
(39526, 'v', '\r\nVibes', ' n.pl. Colloq. 1 vibrations, esp. Feelings communicated. 2 = *vibraphone. [abbreviation]'),
(39527, 'v', '\r\nVibrant', ' adj. 1 vibrating. 2 (often foll. By with) thrilling, lively. 3 (of sound) resonant. 4 (of colours) bright and striking.  vibrancy n. Vibrantly adv. [latin: related to *vibrate]'),
(39528, 'v', '\r\nVibraphone', ' n. Instrument like a xylophone but with motor-driven resonators under the metal bars giving a vibrato effect. [from *vibrato]'),
(39529, 'v', '\r\nVibrate', ' v. (-ting) 1 move rapidly to and fro. 2 (of a sound) throb; resonate. 3 (foll. By with) quiver, thrill. 4 swing to and fro, oscillate. [latin vibro shake]'),
(39530, 'v', '\r\nVibration', ' n. 1 vibrating. 2 (in pl.) A mental, esp. Occult, influence. B atmosphere or feeling communicated.'),
(39531, 'v', '\r\nVibrato', ' n. Rapid slight variation in musical pitch producing a tremulous effect. [italian: related to *vibrate]'),
(39532, 'v', '\r\nVibrator', ' n. Device that vibrates, esp. An instrument for massage or sexual stimulation.  vibratory adj.'),
(39533, 'v', '\r\nViburnum', ' n. A shrub, usu. With white flowers. [latin, = wayfaring-tree]'),
(39534, 'v', '\r\nVicar', ' n. Clergyman of a church of england parish where he formerly received a stipend rather than tithes: cf. *rector 1. [latin vicarius substitute: related to *vice3]'),
(39535, 'v', '\r\nVicarage', ' n. Vicar''s house.'),
(39536, 'v', '\r\nVicarious', ' adj. 1 experienced indirectly or second-hand. 2 acting or done for another. 3 deputed, delegated.  vicariously adv. [latin: related to *vicar]'),
(39537, 'v', '\r\nVice1', ' n. 1 immoral conduct. 2 form of this (the vice of gluttony). 3 weakness; indulgence (brandy is my one vice). [latin vitium]'),
(39538, 'v', '\r\nVice2', ' n. (us vise) clamp with two jaws holding an object so as to leave the hands free to work on it. [vis screw, from latin vitis vine]'),
(39539, 'v', '\r\nVice3', ' prep. In the place of; succeeding. [latin, ablative of (vix) vicis change]'),
(39540, 'v', '\r\nVice-', ' comb. Form forming nouns meaning: 1 substitute, deputy (vice-president). 2 next in rank to (vice admiral). [related to *vice3]'),
(39541, 'v', '\r\nVice-chancellor', ' n. Deputy chancellor (esp. Administrator of a university).'),
(39542, 'v', '\r\nVice-president', ' n. Official ranking below and deputizing for a president.  vice-presidency n. (pl. -ies). Vice-presidential adj.'),
(39543, 'v', '\r\nViceregal', ' adj. Of a viceroy.'),
(39544, 'v', '\r\nVicereine', ' n. 1 viceroy''s wife. 2 woman viceroy. [french: related to *vice-, reine queen]'),
(39545, 'v', '\r\nVice', 'ring  n. Group of criminals organizing prostitution.'),
(39546, 'v', '\r\nViceroy', ' n. Sovereign''s deputy ruler in a colony, province, etc. [french: related to *vice-, roy king]'),
(39547, 'v', '\r\nVice', 'squad  n. Police department concerned with prostitution etc.'),
(39548, 'v', '\r\nVice', 'versa  adj. With the order of the terms changed; the other way round. [latin, = the position being reversed]'),
(39549, 'v', '\r\nVichyssoise', ' n. (usu. Chilled) creamy soup of leeks and potatoes. [french, = of vichy]'),
(39550, 'v', '\r\nVichy', 'water  n. Effervescent mineral water from vichy in france.'),
(39551, 'v', '\r\nVicinity', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 surrounding district. 2 (foll. By to) nearness.  in the vicinity (often foll. By of) near (to). [latin vicinus neighbour]'),
(39552, 'v', '\r\nVicious', ' adj. 1 bad-tempered, spiteful (vicious dog, remark). 2 violent (vicious attack). 3 corrupt, depraved. 4 (of reasoning etc.) Faulty, unsound.  viciously adv. Viciousness n. [latin: related to *vice1]'),
(39553, 'v', '\r\nVicious', 'circle  n. Self-perpetuating, harmful sequence of cause and effect.'),
(39554, 'v', '\r\nVicious', 'spiral  n. Vicious circle, esp. As causing inflation.'),
(39555, 'v', '\r\nVicissitude', ' n. Literary change, esp. Of fortune. [latin: related to *vice3]'),
(39556, 'v', '\r\nVictim', ' n. 1 person or thing injured or destroyed (road victim; victim of greed). 2 prey; dupe (fell victim to his charm). 3 creature sacrificed to a deity or in a religious rite. [latin]'),
(39557, 'v', '\r\nVictimize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) 1 single out for punishment or discrimination. 2 make (a person etc.) A victim.  victimization n.'),
(39558, 'v', '\r\nVictor', ' n. Winner in a battle or contest. [latin vinco vict- conquer]'),
(39559, 'v', '\r\nVictoria', 'cross  n. Highest decoration for conspicuous bravery in the armed services. [queen victoria]'),
(39560, 'v', '\r\nVictorian', ' â€”adj. 1 of the time of queen victoria. 2 prudish; strict. â€”n. Person of this time.'),
(39561, 'v', '\r\nVictoriana', ' n.pl. Articles, esp. Collectors'' items, of the victorian period.'),
(39562, 'v', '\r\nVictoria', 'sponge  n. Sandwich sponge cake with a jam filling.'),
(39563, 'v', '\r\nVictorious', ' adj. 1 conquering, triumphant. 2 marked by victory.  victoriously adv. [latin: related to *victor]'),
(39564, 'v', '\r\nVictory', ' n. (pl. -ies) defeat of an enemy or opponent.'),
(39565, 'v', '\r\nVictual', ' â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Food, provisions. â€”v. (-ll-; us -l-) 1 supply with victuals. 2 obtain stores. 3 eat victuals. [latin victus food]'),
(39566, 'v', '\r\nVictualer', ' n. (brit. Victualler) 1 person etc. Who supplies victuals. 2 (in full licensed victualler) publican etc. Licensed to sell alcohol.'),
(39567, 'v', '\r\nVictualler', ' n. (us victualer) 1 person etc. Who supplies victuals. 2 (in full licensed victualler) publican etc. Licensed to sell alcohol.'),
(39568, 'v', '\r\nVicuÃ±a', ' n. 1 s. American mammal like a llama, with fine silky wool. 2 a cloth from its wool. B imitation of this. [spanish from quechua]'),
(39569, 'v', '\r\nVide', ' v. (in imper.) See, consult (a passage in a book etc.). [latin video see]'),
(39570, 'v', '\r\nVidelicet', ' adv. = *viz. [latin video see, licet allowed]'),
(39571, 'v', '\r\nVideo', ' â€”adj. 1 of the recording (or reproduction) of moving pictures on magnetic tape. 2 of the broadcasting of television pictures. â€”n. (pl. -s) 1 such recording or broadcasting. 2 colloq. = *video recorder. 3 colloq. A film on videotape. â€”v. (-oes, -oed) record on videotape. [latin, = i see]'),
(39572, 'v', '\r\nVideo', 'cassette  n. Cassette of videotape.'),
(39573, 'v', '\r\nVideodisc', ' n. Disc for recording moving pictures and sound.'),
(39574, 'v', '\r\nVideo', 'game  n. Computer game played on a television screen.'),
(39575, 'v', '\r\nVideo', 'nasty  n. Colloq. Horrific or pornographic video film.'),
(39576, 'v', '\r\nVideo', 'recorder  n. (also video cassette recorder) apparatus for recording and playing videotapes.'),
(39577, 'v', '\r\nVideo', 'shop  n. Shop hiring out or selling video films etc.'),
(39578, 'v', '\r\nVideotape', ' â€”n. Magnetic tape for recording moving pictures and sound. â€”v. (-ping) record on this.'),
(39579, 'v', '\r\nVideotape', 'recorder  n. = *video recorder.'),
(39580, 'v', '\r\nVideotex', ' n. (also videotext) any electronic information system, esp. Teletext or viewdata.'),
(39581, 'v', '\r\nVie', ' v. (vies; vied; vying) (often foll. By with) compete; strive for superiority. [probably french: related to *envy]'),
(39582, 'v', '\r\nVietnamese', ' â€”adj. Of vietnam. â€”n. (pl. Same) native or language of vietnam.'),
(39583, 'v', '\r\nView', ' â€”n. 1 range of vision (came into view). 2 a what is seen; prospect, scene, etc. B picture etc. Of this. 3 a opinion. B manner of considering a thing (took a long-term view). 4 inspection by the eye or mind (private view). â€”v. 1 look at; inspect with the idea of purchasing; survey visually or mentally. 2 form a mental impression or opinion of; consider. 3 watch television.  have in view 1 have as one''s object. 2 bear (a circumstance) in mind. In view of considering. On view being shown or exhibited. With a view to with the hope or intention of. [latin video see]'),
(39584, 'v', '\r\nViewdata', ' n. News and information service from a computer source, connected to a television screen by a telephone link.'),
(39585, 'v', '\r\nViewer', ' n. 1 person who views, esp. Television. 2 device for looking at film transparencies etc.'),
(39586, 'v', '\r\nViewfinder', ' n. Device on a camera showing the borders of the proposed photograph.'),
(39587, 'v', '\r\nViewpoint', ' n. Point of view.'),
(39588, 'v', '\r\nVigil', ' n. 1 keeping awake during the night etc., esp. To keep watch or pray. 2 eve of a festival or holy day. [latin vigilia]'),
(39589, 'v', '\r\nVigilance', ' n. Watchfulness, caution.  vigilant adj. [latin: related to *vigil]'),
(39590, 'v', '\r\nVigilante', ' n. Member of a self-appointed group maintaining order etc. [spanish, = vigilant]'),
(39591, 'v', '\r\nVignette', ' n. 1 short description, character sketch. 2 book illustration not in a definite border. 3 photograph etc. With the background shaded off. [french, diminutive: related to *vine]'),
(39592, 'v', '\r\nVigor', ' n. (brit. Vigour) 1 physical or mental strength or energy. 2 healthy growth. 3 forcefulness; trenchancy, animation.  vigorous adj. Vigorously adv. [french from latin vigeo be lively]'),
(39593, 'v', '\r\nVigour', ' n. (us vigor) 1 physical or mental strength or energy. 2 healthy growth. 3 forcefulness; trenchancy, animation.  vigorous adj. Vigorously adv. [french from latin vigeo be lively]'),
(39594, 'v', '\r\nViking', ' n.  Scandinavian pirate and raider of the 8thâ€“11th c. [old norse]'),
(39595, 'v', '\r\nVile', ' adj. 1 disgusting. 2 depraved. 3 colloq. Abominable (vile weather).  vilely adv. Vileness n. [latin vilis cheap, base]'),
(39596, 'v', '\r\nVilify', ' v. (-ies, -ied) defame; malign.  vilification n. [latin: related to *vile]'),
(39597, 'v', '\r\nVilla', ' n. 1 country house; mansion. 2 rented holiday home, esp. Abroad. 3 (usu. As part of an address) detached or semi-detached house in a residential district. [italian and latin]'),
(39598, 'v', '\r\nVillage', ' n. 1 country settlement, larger than a hamlet and smaller than a town. 2 self-contained village-like community within a city etc. (greenwich village; olympic village).  villager n. [latin: related to *villa]'),
(39599, 'v', '\r\nVillain', ' n. 1 wicked person. 2 chief evil character in a play, story, etc. 3 colloq. Professional criminal. 4 colloq. Rascal. [latin: related to *villa]'),
(39600, 'v', '\r\nVillainous', ' adj. Wicked.'),
(39601, 'v', '\r\nVillainy', ' n. (pl. -ies) wicked behaviour or act. [french: related to *villain]'),
(39602, 'v', '\r\nVillein', ' n. Hist. Feudal tenant entirely subject to a lord or attached to a manor.  villeinage n. [var. Of *villain]'),
(39603, 'v', '\r\nVim', ' n. Colloq. Vigour. [perhaps from latin, accusative of vis energy]'),
(39604, 'v', '\r\nVinaigrette', ' n. 1 salad dressing of oil, wine vinegar, and seasoning. 2 small bottle for smelling-salts. [french, diminutive: related to *vinegar]'),
