(39914, 'w', '\r\nWarlock', ' n. Archaic sorcerer. [old english, = traitor]'),
(39915, 'w', '\r\nWarlord', ' n. Military commander or commander-in-chief.'),
(39916, 'w', '\r\nWarm', ' â€”adj. 1 of or at a fairly high temperature. 2 (of clothes etc.) Affording warmth. 3 a sympathetic, friendly, loving. B hearty, enthusiastic. 4 colloq. Iron. Dangerous, difficult, hostile. 5 colloq. A (in a game) close to the object etc. Sought. B near to guessing. 6 (of a colour etc.) Reddish or yellowish; suggestive of warmth. 7 hunting (of a scent) fresh and strong. â€”v. 1 make warm. 2 a (often foll. By up) warm oneself. B (often foll. By to) become animated or sympathetic. â€”n. 1 act of warming. 2 warmth of the atmosphere etc.  warm up 1 make or become warm. 2 prepare for a performance etc. By practising. 3 reach a temperature for efficient working. 4 reheat (food).  warmly adv. Warmth n. [old english]'),
(39917, 'w', '\r\nWarm-blooded', ' adj. 1 having blood temperature well above that of the environment. 2 ardent.'),
(39918, 'w', '\r\nWar', 'memorial  n. Monument to those killed in a war.'),
(39919, 'w', '\r\nWarm-hearted', ' adj. Kind, friendly.  warm-heartedness n.'),
(39920, 'w', '\r\nWarming-pan', ' n. Hist. Container for live coals with a flat body and a long handle, used for warming a bed.'),
(39921, 'w', '\r\nWarmonger', ' n. Person who promotes war.  warmongering n. & adj.'),
(39922, 'w', '\r\nWarm-up', ' n. Period of preparatory exercise.'),
(39923, 'w', '\r\nWarm', 'work  n. 1 work etc. That makes one warm through exertion. 2 dangerous conflict etc.'),
(39924, 'w', '\r\nWarn', ' v. 1 (also absol.) A (often foll. By of or that) inform of danger, unknown circumstances, etc. B (foll. By to + infin.) Advise (a person) to take certain action. C (often foll. By against) inform (a person etc.) About a specific danger. 2 (usu. With neg.) Admonish.  warn off tell (a person) to keep away (from). [old english]'),
(39925, 'w', '\r\nWarning', ' n. 1 in senses of *warn. 2 thing that warns. [old english]'),
(39926, 'w', '\r\nWar', 'of nerves  n. Attempt to wear down an opponent psychologically.'),
(39927, 'w', '\r\nWarp', ' â€”v. 1 a make or become distorted, esp. Through heat, damp, etc. B make or become perverted or strange (warped sense of humour). 2 haul (a ship) by a rope attached to a fixed point. â€”n. 1 a warped state, esp. Of timber. B perversion of the mind. 2 lengthwise threads in a loom. 3 rope used in warping a ship. [old english]'),
(39928, 'w', '\r\nWarpaint', ' n. 1 paint used to adorn the body before battle, esp. By n. American indians. 2 colloq. Make-up.'),
(39929, 'w', '\r\nWarpath', ' n.  on the warpath 1 (of n. American indians) going to war. 2 colloq. Seeking a confrontation.'),
(39930, 'w', '\r\nWarrant', ' â€”n. 1 thing that authorizes an action. 2 a written authorization, money voucher, etc. B written authorization allowing police to search premises, arrest a suspect, etc. 3 certificate of service rank held by a warrant-officer. â€”v. 1 serve as a warrant for; justify. 2 guarantee or attest to esp. The genuineness of.  i (or i''ll) warrant i am certain; no doubt. [french warant, from germanic]'),
(39931, 'w', '\r\nWarrant-officer', ' n. Officer ranking between commissioned officers and ncos.'),
(39932, 'w', '\r\nWarranty', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 undertaking as to the ownership or quality of a thing sold etc., often accepting responsibility for defects or repairs over a specified period. 2 (usu. Foll. By for + verbal noun) authority or justification. [anglo-french warantie: related to *warrant]'),
(39933, 'w', '\r\nWarren', ' n. 1 network of rabbit burrows. 2 densely populated or labyrinthine building or district. [anglo-french warenne from germanic]'),
(39934, 'w', '\r\nWarring', ' see *war v.'),
(39935, 'w', '\r\nWarrior', ' n. 1 person experienced or distinguished in fighting. 2 fighting man, esp. Of primitive peoples. 3 (attrib.) Martial (warrior nation). [french werreior: related to *war]'),
(39936, 'w', '\r\nWarship', ' n. Ship used in war.'),
(39937, 'w', '\r\nWart', ' n. 1 small hard round growth on the skin. 2 protuberance on the skin of an animal, surface of a plant, etc.  warts and all colloq. With no attempt to conceal blemishes.  warty adj. [old english]'),
(39938, 'w', '\r\nWart-hog', ' n. African wild pig.'),
