(39940, 'w', '\r\nWary', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 on one''s guard; circumspect. 2 (foll. By of) cautious. 3 showing caution.  warily adv. Wariness n. [ware look out for, avoid]'),
(39941, 'w', '\r\nWas', ' 1st & 3rd sing. Past of *be.'),
(39942, 'w', '\r\nWash', ' â€”v. 1 cleanse with liquid, esp. Water. 2 (foll. By out, off, away, etc.) A remove (a stain) in this way. B (of a stain etc.) Be removed by washing. 3 wash oneself or one''s hands and face. 4 wash clothes, dishes, etc. 5 (of fabric or dye) bear washing without damage. 6 (of an argument etc.) Stand scrutiny; be believed or acceptable. 7  (of a river, waters, etc.) Touch. 8 (of liquid) carry along in a specified direction (was washed overboard; washed up on the shore). 9 (foll. By over, along, etc.) Sweep, move, or splash. 10 (foll. By over) occur all around without greatly affecting (a person). 11 sift (ore) by the action of water. 12 brush watery paint or ink over. 13 poet. Moisten, water. â€”n. 1 a washing or being washed. B (prec. By the) a laundry etc. (sent them to the wash). 2 clothes etc. For washing or just washed. 3 motion of agitated water or air, esp.'),
(39943, 'w', '\r\nWash-basin', ' n. Plumbed-in basin for washing one''s hands etc.'),
(39944, 'w', '\r\nWashboard', ' n. 1 ribbed board on which clothes are scrubbed. 2 this as a percussion instrument.'),
(39945, 'w', '\r\nWashed', 'out  adj. (also washed-out) 1 faded; pale. 2 colloq. Pale, exhausted.'),
(39946, 'w', '\r\nWashed', 'up  adj. (also washed-up) esp. Us slang defeated, having failed.'),
(39947, 'w', '\r\nWasher', ' n. 1 person or machine that washes. 2 flat ring inserted at a joint to tighten it and prevent leakage or under the head of a screw etc., or under a nut, to disperse its pressure.'),
(39948, 'w', '\r\nWasher-up', ' n. (pl. Washers-up) (also washer-upper) person who washes up dishes etc.'),
(39949, 'w', '\r\nWasherwoman', ' n. Laundress.'),
(39950, 'w', '\r\nWashing', ' n. Clothes etc. For washing or just washed.'),
(39951, 'w', '\r\nWashing-machine', ' n. Machine for washing clothes.'),
(39952, 'w', '\r\nWashing-powder', ' n. Soap powder or detergent for washing clothes.'),
(39953, 'w', '\r\nWashing-soda', ' n. Sodium carbonate, used dissolved in water for washing and cleaning.'),
(39954, 'w', '\r\nWashing-up', ' n. 1 process of washing dishes etc. 2 used dishes etc. For washing.'),
(39955, 'w', '\r\nWash-out', ' n. Colloq. Complete failure, non-event.'),
(39956, 'w', '\r\nWashroom', ' n. Esp. Us public toilet.'),
(39957, 'w', '\r\nWashstand', ' n. Piece of furniture to hold a basin, jug, soap, etc.'),
(39958, 'w', '\r\nWashy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 too watery or weak. 2 lacking vigour or intensity.  washily adv. Washiness n.'),
(39959, 'w', '\r\nWasn''t', ' contr. Was not.'),
(39960, 'w', '\r\nWasp', ' n. (also wasp) us usu. Derog. Middle-class american white protestant. [white anglo-saxon protestant]'),
(39961, 'w', '\r\nWasp', ' n. Stinging insect with black and yellow stripes. [old english]'),
(39962, 'w', '\r\nWaspish', ' adj. Irritable, snappish.'),
(39963, 'w', '\r\nWasp-waist', ' n. Very slender waist.'),
(39964, 'w', '\r\nWassail', ' archaic â€”n. Festive occasion; drinking-bout. â€”v. Make merry. [old norse ves heill be in health: related to *whole]'),
(39965, 'w', '\r\nWastage', ' n. 1 amount wasted. 2 loss by use, wear, or leakage. 3 (also natural wastage) loss of employees other than by redundancy.'),
(39966, 'w', '\r\nWaste', ' â€”v. (-ting) 1 use to no purpose or with inadequate result or extravagantly. 2 fail to use (esp. An opportunity). 3 (often foll. By on) a give (advice etc.) Without effect. B (often in passive) fail to be appreciated or used properly (she was wasted on him; feel wasted in this job). 4 wear gradually away; make or become weak. 5 devastate. â€”adj. 1 superfluous; no longer needed. 2 not inhabited or cultivated. â€”n. 1 act of wasting. 2 waste material. 3 waste region. 4 being used up; diminution by wear. 5 = *waste pipe.'),
(39967, 'w', '\r\nWaste', 'disposal unit  n. Device fitted to a sink etc. For disposing of household waste.'),
(39968, 'w', '\r\nWasteful', ' adj. 1 extravagant. 2 causing or showing waste.  wastefully adv.'),
(39969, 'w', '\r\nWasteland', ' n. 1 unproductive or useless area of land. 2 place or time considered spiritually or intellectually barren.'),
(39970, 'w', '\r\nWaste', 'paper  n. Used or valueless paper.'),
(39971, 'w', '\r\nWaste-paper', 'basket  n. Receptacle for waste paper.'),
(39972, 'w', '\r\nWaste', 'pipe  n. Pipe to carry off waste material.'),
(39973, 'w', '\r\nWaste', 'product  n. Useless by-product of manufacture or of an organism.'),
(39974, 'w', '\r\nWaster', ' n. 1 wasteful person. 2 colloq. Wastrel.'),
(39975, 'w', '\r\nWastrel', ' n. Good-for-nothing person.'),
(39976, 'w', '\r\nWatch', ' â€”v. 1 keep the eyes fixed on. 2 keep under observation; follow observantly. 3 (often foll. By for) be in an alert state; be vigilant. 4 (foll. By over) look after; take care of. â€”n. 1 small portable timepiece for carrying on the wrist or in a pocket. 2 state of alert or constant observation or attention. 3 naut. A usu. Four-hour spell of duty. B (in full starboard or port watch) each of the halves into which a ship''s crew is divided to take alternate watches. 4 hist. Watchman or watchmen.  on the watch for waiting for (an anticipated occurrence). Watch it (or oneself) colloq. Be careful. Watch out (often foll. By for) be on one''s guard.  watcher n. (also in comb.). [old english: related to *wake1]'),
(39977, 'w', '\r\nWatchdog', ' n. 1 dog guarding property etc. 2 person or body monitoring others'' rights etc.'),
(39978, 'w', '\r\nWatchful', ' adj. 1 accustomed to watching, alert. 2 on the watch.  watchfully adv. Watchfulness n.'),
(39979, 'w', '\r\nWatching', 'brief  n. Brief of a barrister who follows a case for a client not directly concerned.'),
(39980, 'w', '\r\nWatchmaker', ' n. Person who makes and repairs watches and clocks.'),
(39981, 'w', '\r\nWatchman', ' n. Man employed to look after an empty building etc. At night.'),
(39982, 'w', '\r\nWatch-night', 'service  n. Religious service held on the last night of the year.'),
(39983, 'w', '\r\nWatch-tower', ' n. Tower for keeping watch from.'),
(39984, 'w', '\r\nWatchword', ' n. Phrase summarizing a guiding principle.'),
(39985, 'w', '\r\nWater', ' â€”n. 1 colourless transparent liquid compound of oxygen and hydrogen. 2 liquid consisting chiefly of this and found in seas and rivers, in rain, and in secretions of organisms. 3 expanse of water; a sea, lake, river, etc. 4 (in pl.) Part of a sea or river. 5 (often as the waters) mineral water at a spa etc. 6 state of a tide. 7 solution of a specified substance in water (lavender-water). 8 transparency and brilliance of a gem. 9 (attrib.) A found in or near water. B of, for, or worked by water. C involving, using, or yielding water. 10 (usu. In pl.) Amniotic fluid, released during labour. â€”v. 1 sprinkle or soak with water. 2 supply (a plant) with water. 3 give water to (an animal). 4 secrete water. 5 (as watered adj.) (of silk etc.) Having irregular wavy glossy markings. 6 take in a supply of water.  by water using a ship etc. For transport. Like water in great quantity, profusely. Make one''s mouth water cause one''s saliva to flow, stimulate one''s appetite or anticipation. Of the first water of the finest quality or extreme degree. Water down 1 dilute. 2 make less forceful or horrifying. Water under the bridge past events accepted as irrevocable. [old english]'),
(39986, 'w', '\r\nWater-bearer', ' var. Of *water-carrier.'),
(39987, 'w', '\r\nWater-bed', ' n. Mattress filled with water.'),
(39988, 'w', '\r\nWater-biscuit', ' n. Thin crisp unsweetened biscuit.'),
(39989, 'w', '\r\nWater-buffalo', ' n. Common domestic indian buffalo.'),
(39990, 'w', '\r\nWater', 'bus  n. Boat carrying passengers on a regular run on a river, lake, etc.'),
(39991, 'w', '\r\nWater-cannon', ' n. Device using a jet of water to disperse a crowd etc.'),
(39992, 'w', '\r\nWater-carrier', ' n. (also water-bearer) (prec. By the) zodiacal sign or constellation aquarius.'),
(39993, 'w', '\r\nWater', 'chestnut  n. Corm from a sedge, used in chinese cookery.'),
