(40042, 'w', '\r\nWaterworks', ' n. 1 establishment for managing a water-supply. 2 colloq. Shedding of tears. 3 colloq. Urinary system.'),
(40043, 'w', '\r\nWatery', ' adj. 1 containing too much water. 2 too thin in consistency. 3 of or consisting of water. 4 vapid, uninteresting. 5 (of colour) pale. 6 (of the sun, moon, or sky) rainy-looking. 7 (of eyes) moist; tearful. wateriness n.'),
(40044, 'w', '\r\nWatt', ' n. Si unit of power, equivalent to one joule per second, corresponding to the rate of energy in an electric circuit where the potential difference is one volt and the current one ampere. [watt, name of an engineer]'),
(40045, 'w', '\r\nWattage', ' n. Amount of electrical power expressed in watts.'),
(40046, 'w', '\r\nWatt-hour', ' n. Energy used when one watt is applied for one hour.'),
(40047, 'w', '\r\nWattle1', ' n. 1 structure of interlaced rods and sticks used for fences etc. 2 australian acacia with pliant branches and golden flowers used as the national emblem. [old english]'),
(40048, 'w', '\r\nWattle2', ' n. Fleshy appendage on the head or throat of a turkey or other birds. [origin unknown]'),
(40049, 'w', '\r\nWattle', 'and daub  n. Network of rods and twigs plastered with clay or mud as a building material.'),
(40050, 'w', '\r\nWave', ' â€”v. (-ving) 1 a (often foll. By to) move a hand etc. To and fro in greeting or as a signal. B move (a hand etc.) In this way. 2 a show a sinuous or sweeping motion as of a flag, tree, corn, etc. In the wind. B impart a waving motion to. 3 direct (a person) by waving (waved them away; waved them to follow). 4 express (a greeting etc.) By waving. 5 give an undulating form to (hair etc.). 6 (of hair etc.) Have such a form. â€”n. 1 ridge of water between two depressions. 2 long body of water curling into an arch and breaking on the shore. 3 thing compared to this, e.g. A body of persons in one of successive advancing groups. 4 gesture of waving. 5 a process of waving the hair. B undulating form produced by this. 6 temporary occurrence or increase of a condition or influence (wave of enthusiasm; heat wave). 7 physics a disturbance of the particles of esp. A fluid medium for the propagation or direction of motion, heat, light, sound, etc. B single curve in this motion. 8 undulating line or outline.  make waves colloq. Cause trouble. Wave aside dismiss as intrusive or irrelevant. Wave down wave to (a vehicle or driver) to stop. [old english]'),
(40051, 'w', '\r\nWaveband', ' n. Range of radio wavelengths between certain limits.'),
(40052, 'w', '\r\nWave-form', ' n. Physics curve showing the shape of a wave at a given time.'),
(40053, 'w', '\r\nWavelength', ' n. 1 distance between successive crests of a wave. 2 this as a distinctive feature of radio waves from a transmitter. 3 colloq. Particular mode or range of thought.'),
(40054, 'w', '\r\nWavelet', ' n. Small wave.'),
(40055, 'w', '\r\nWave', 'machine  n. Device at a swimming-pool producing waves.'),
(40056, 'w', '\r\nWaver', ' v. 1 be or become unsteady; begin to give way. 2 be irresolute. 3 (of a light) flicker. [old norse: related to *wave]'),
(40057, 'w', '\r\nWavy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having waves or alternate contrary curves.  waviness n.'),
(40058, 'w', '\r\nWax1', ' â€”n. 1 sticky plastic yellowish substance secreted by bees as the material of honeycomb. 2 this bleached and purified, used for candles, modelling, etc. 3 any similar substance, e.g. The yellow substance secreted by the ear. â€”v. 1 cover or treat with wax. 2 remove unwanted hair from (legs etc.) Using wax.  waxy adj. (-ier, -iest). [old english]'),
(40059, 'w', '\r\nWax2', ' v. 1 (of the moon) increase in apparent size. 2 become larger or stronger. 3 pass into a specified state or mood (wax lyrical).  wax and wane undergo alternate increases and decreases. [old english]'),
(40060, 'w', '\r\nWaxen', ' adj. 1 smooth or pale like wax. 2 archaic made of wax.'),
(40061, 'w', '\r\nWaxwing', ' n. Any of various birds with tips like red sealing-wax to some wing-feathers.'),
(40062, 'w', '\r\nWaxwork', ' n. 1 object, esp. A lifelike dummy, modelled in wax. 2 (in pl.) Exhibition of wax dummies.'),
(40063, 'w', '\r\nWay', ' â€”n. 1 road, track, path, etc., for passing along. 2 course or route for reaching a place (asked the way to london; the way out). 3 method or plan for attaining an object. 4 style, manner (i like the way you dress). 5 person''s chosen or habitual course of action. 6 normal course of events (that is always the way). 7 travelling distance; length traversed or to be traversed. 8 unimpeded opportunity or space to advance (make way). 9 advance in some direction; impetus, progress (under way). 10 being engaged in movement from place to place; time spent in this (on the way home). 11 specified direction (step this way). 12 colloq. Scope or range. 13 line of occupation or business. 14 specified condition or state (things are in a bad way). 15 respect (is useful in some ways). 16 (in pl.) Part into which a thing is divided (split it three ways). 17 (in pl.) Structure of timber etc. Down which a new ship is launched. â€”adv. Colloq. Far (way off).  by the way incidentally. By way of 1 by means of. 2 as a form of. 3 passing through. Come one''s way become available to one. Get out of the (or my etc.) Way stop obstructing a person. Go out of one''s way make a special effort. In a way to some extent. In the (or one''s) way forming an obstruction. Lead the way act as guide or leader. Look the other way ignore what one should notice. On the (or one''s) way 1 in the course of a journey etc. 2 having progressed. 3 colloq. (of a child) conceived but not yet born. On the way out colloq. Going out of fashion or favour. Out of the way 1 no longer an obstacle. 2 disposed of. 3 unusual. 4 (of a place) remote. [old english]'),
(40064, 'w', '\r\nWay', 'back  adv. Colloq. Long ago.'),
(40065, 'w', '\r\nWaybill', ' n. List of passengers or parcels on a vehicle.'),
(40066, 'w', '\r\nWayfarer', ' n. Traveller, esp. On foot.  wayfaring n. & adj.'),
(40067, 'w', '\r\nWaylay', ' v. (past and past part. Waylaid) 1 lie in wait for. 2 stop to talk to or rob.'),
(40068, 'w', '\r\nWay', 'of life  n. Principles or habits governing all one''s actions etc.'),
(40069, 'w', '\r\nWay-out', ' adj. Colloq. Unusual; eccentric.'),
(40070, 'w', '\r\n-ways', ' suffix forming adjectives and adverbs of direction or manner (sideways).'),
(40071, 'w', '\r\nWays', 'and means  n.pl. 1 methods of achieving something. 2 methods of raising government revenue.'),
(40072, 'w', '\r\nWayside', ' n. 1 side of a road. 2 land at the side of a road.'),
(40073, 'w', '\r\nWayward', ' adj. Childishly self-willed; capricious.  waywardness n. [from *away, *-ward]'),
(40074, 'w', '\r\nWb', ' abbr. Weber(s).'),
(40075, 'w', '\r\nWc', ' abbr. 1 water-closet. 2 west central.'),
(40076, 'w', '\r\nW/cdr.', ' Abbr. Wing commander.'),
(40077, 'w', '\r\nWe', ' pron. (obj. Us; poss. Our, ours) 1 pl. Of *i2. 2 used for or by a royal person in a proclamation etc. Or by an editor etc. In a formal context. [old english]'),
(40078, 'w', '\r\nWea', ' abbr. Workers'' educational association.'),
(40079, 'w', '\r\nWeak', ' adj. 1 deficient in strength, power, vigour, resolution, or number. 2 unconvincing. 3 gram. (of a verb) forming inflections by the addition of a suffix to the stem.  weakish adj. [old norse]'),
(40080, 'w', '\r\nWeaken', ' v. Make or become weak or weaker.'),
(40081, 'w', '\r\nWeak-kneed', ' adj. Colloq. Lacking resolution.'),
(40082, 'w', '\r\nWeakling', ' n. Feeble person or animal.'),
(40083, 'w', '\r\nWeakly', ' â€”adv. In a weak manner. â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) sickly, not robust.'),
(40084, 'w', '\r\nWeak-minded', ' adj. 1 mentally deficient. 2 lacking in resolution.'),
(40085, 'w', '\r\nWeak', 'moment  n. Time when one is unusually compliant or susceptible.'),
(40086, 'w', '\r\nWeakness', ' n. 1 being weak. 2 weak point. 3 (foll. By for) self-indulgent liking (weakness for chocolate).'),
(40087, 'w', '\r\nWeak', 'point  n. (also weak spot) 1 place where defences are assailable. 2 flaw in an argument or character or in resistance to temptation.'),
(40088, 'w', '\r\nWeal1', ' â€”n. Ridge raised on the flesh by a stroke of a rod or whip. â€”v. Mark with a weal. [var. Of *wale]'),
(40089, 'w', '\r\nWeal2', ' n. Literary welfare. [old english]'),
(40090, 'w', '\r\nWealth', ' n. 1 riches. 2 being rich. 3 (foll. By of) abundance. [old english]'),
(40091, 'w', '\r\nWealthy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) having an abundance, esp. Of money.'),
(40092, 'w', '\r\nWean', ' v. 1 accustom (an infant or other young mammal) to food other than (esp. Its mother''s) milk. 2 (often foll. By from, away from) disengage (from a habit etc.) By enforced discontinuance. [old english, = accustom]'),
(40093, 'w', '\r\nWeapon', ' n. 1 thing designed, used, or usable for inflicting bodily harm. 2 means for gaining the advantage in a conflict. [old english]'),
(40094, 'w', '\r\nWeaponry', ' n. Weapons collectively.'),
(40095, 'w', '\r\nWear', ' â€”v. (past wore; past part. Worn) 1 have on one''s person as clothing or an ornament etc. 2 exhibit or present (a facial expression etc.) (wore a frown). 3 colloq. (usu. With neg.) Tolerate. 4 (often foll. By away, down) a injure the surface of, or partly obliterate or alter, by rubbing, stress, or use. B undergo such injury or change. 5 (foll. By off, away) rub or be rubbed off. 6 make (a hole etc.) By constant rubbing or dripping etc. 7 (often foll. By out) exhaust. 8 (foll. By down) overcome by persistence. 9 (foll. By well etc.) Endure continued use or life. 10 (of time) pass, esp. Tediously. 11 (of a ship) fly (a flag). â€”n. 1 wearing or being worn. 2 things worn; fashionable or suitable clothing (sportswear; footwear). 3 (in full wear and tear) damage from continuous use.  wear one''s heart on one''s sleeve show one''s feelings openly. Wear off lose effectiveness or intensity. Wear out 1 use or be used until useless. 2 tire or be tired out. Wear thin (of patience, excuses, etc.) Begin to fail. Wear the trousers see *trousers.  wearer n. [old english]'),
(40096, 'w', '\r\nWearisome', ' adj. Tedious; tiring by monotony or length.'),
(40097, 'w', '\r\nWeary', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 very tired after exertion or endurance. 2 (foll. By of) no longer interested in, tired of. 3 tiring, tedious. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) make or grow weary.  wearily adv. Weariness n. [old english]'),
(40098, 'w', '\r\nWeasel', ' n. Small flesh-eating mammal related to the stoat and ferret. [old english]'),
(40099, 'w', '\r\nWeasel', 'word  n. (usu. In pl.) Word that is intentionally ambiguous or misleading.'),
(40100, 'w', '\r\nWeather', ' â€”n. 1 state of the atmosphere at a place and time as regards heat, cloudiness, dryness, sunshine, wind, and rain etc. 2 (attrib.) Naut. Windward. â€”v. 1 expose to or affect by atmospheric changes; season (wood). 2 be discoloured or worn in this way. 3 a come safely through (a storm). B survive (a difficult period etc.). 4 get to the windward of (a cape etc.).  keep a weather eye open be watchful. Make heavy weather of colloq. Exaggerate the difficulty presented by. Under the weather colloq. Indisposed. [old english]'),
(40101, 'w', '\r\nWeather-beaten', ' adj. Affected by exposure to the weather.'),
(40102, 'w', '\r\nWeatherboard', ' n. 1 sloping board attached to the bottom of an outside door to keep out the rain etc. 2 each of a series of overlapping horizontal boards on a wall.  weatherboarding n. (in sense 2 of n.).'),
(40103, 'w', '\r\nWeathercock', ' n. 1 weather-vane in the form of a cock. 2 inconstant person.'),
(40104, 'w', '\r\nWeather', 'forecast  n. Assessment of likely weather.'),
(40105, 'w', '\r\nWeatherman', ' n. Meteorologist, esp. One who broadcasts a weather forecast.'),
(40106, 'w', '\r\nWeatherproof', ' adj. Resistant to the effects of bad weather, esp. Rain.'),
(40107, 'w', '\r\nWeather-vane', ' n. 1 revolving pointer on a church spire etc. To show the direction of the wind. 2 inconstant person.'),
(40108, 'w', '\r\nWeave1', ' â€”v. (-ving; past wove; past part. Woven or wove) 1 a form (fabric) by interlacing long threads in two directions. B form (thread) into fabric in this way. 2 make fabric in this way. 3 a (foll. By into) make (facts etc.) Into a story or connected whole. B make (a story) in this way. â€”n. Style of weaving. [old english]'),
(40109, 'w', '\r\nWeave2', ' v. (-ving) move repeatedly from side to side; take an intricate course to avoid obstructions.  get weaving slang begin action; hurry. [old norse: related to *wave]'),
(40110, 'w', '\r\nWeaver', ' n. 1 person who weaves fabric. 2 (in full weaver-bird) tropical bird building elaborately woven nests.'),
(40111, 'w', '\r\nWeb', ' n. 1 a woven fabric. B amount woven in one piece. 2 complex series (web of lies). 3 cobweb, gossamer, or a similar product of a spinning creature. 4 membrane between the toes of a swimming animal or bird. 5 large roll of paper used in printing. 6 thin flat connecting part in machinery etc.  webbed adj. [old english]'),
(40112, 'w', '\r\nWebbing', ' n. Strong narrow closely-woven fabric used for belts etc.'),
(40113, 'w', '\r\nWeber', ' n. The si unit of magnetic flux. [weber, name of a physicist]'),
(40114, 'w', '\r\nWeb-footed', ' adj. Having the toes connected by webs.'),
(40115, 'w', '\r\nWed.', ' Abbr. (also weds.) Wednesday.'),
(40116, 'w', '\r\nWed', ' v. (-dd-; past and past part. Wedded or wed) 1 usu. Formal or literary marry. 2 unite. 3 (as wedded adj.) Of or in marriage (wedded bliss). 4 (as wedded adj.) (foll. By to) obstinately attached or devoted to (a pursuit etc.). [old english, = pledge]'),
(40117, 'w', '\r\nWe''d', ' contr. 1 we had. 2 we should; we would.'),
(40118, 'w', '\r\nWedding', ' n. Marriage ceremony. [old english: related to *wed]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(40119, 'w', '\r\nWedding', 'breakfast  n. Meal etc. Between a wedding and departure for the honeymoon.'),
(40120, 'w', '\r\nWedding', 'cake  n. Rich iced cake served at a wedding reception.'),
(40121, 'w', '\r\nWedding', 'ring  n. Ring worn by a married person.'),
(40122, 'w', '\r\nWedge', ' â€”n. 1 piece of tapering wood or metal etc. Driven between two objects or parts to secure or separate them. 2 anything resembling a wedge. 3 golf club with a wedge-shaped head. â€”v. (-ging) 1 secure or fasten with a wedge. 2 force open or apart with a wedge. 3 (foll. By in, into) pack or thrust (a thing or oneself) tightly in or into.  thin end of the wedge colloq. Thing of little importance in itself, but likely to lead to more serious developments. [old english]'),
(40123, 'w', '\r\nWedgwood', ' n. Propr. 1 a kind of fine stoneware usu. With a white cameo design. 2 its characteristic blue colour. [wedgwood, name of a potter]'),
(40124, 'w', '\r\nWedlock', ' n. The married state.  born in (or out of) wedlock born of married (or unmarried) parents. [old english, = marriage vow]'),
(40125, 'w', '\r\nWednesday', ' â€”n. Day of the week following tuesday. â€”adv. Colloq. 1 on wednesday. 2 (wednesdays) on wednesdays; each wednesday. [old english]'),
(40126, 'w', '\r\nWeds.', ' Abbr. Var. Of *wed.'),
(40127, 'w', '\r\nWee1', ' adj. (weer; weest) 1 esp. Scot. Little. 2 colloq. Tiny. [old english]'),
(40128, 'w', '\r\nWee2', ' n. Colloq. = *wee-wee.'),
(40129, 'w', '\r\nWeed', ' â€”n. 1 wild plant growing where it is not wanted. 2 thin weak-looking person or horse. 3 (prec. By the) slang a marijuana. B tobacco. â€”v. 1 a clear (an area) of weeds. B remove unwanted parts from. 2 (foll. By out) a sort out and remove (inferior or unwanted parts etc.). B rid of inferior parts, unwanted members, etc. 3 cut off or uproot weeds. [old english]'),
(40130, 'w', '\r\nWeed-killer', ' n. Chemical used to destroy weeds.'),
(40131, 'w', '\r\nWeeds', ' n.pl. (in full widow''s weeds) archaic deep mourning worn by a widow. [old english, = garment]'),
(40132, 'w', '\r\nWeedy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 weak, feeble. 2 having many weeds.'),
(40133, 'w', '\r\nWeek', ' n. 1 period of seven days reckoned usu. From midnight on saturday. 2 any period of seven days. 3 the six days between sundays. 4 a the five days monday to friday. B time spent working in this period (35-hour week; three-day week). [old english]'),
(40134, 'w', '\r\nWeekday', ' n. Day other than sunday or saturday and sunday.'),
(40135, 'w', '\r\nWeekend', ' n. 1 sunday and saturday or part of saturday. 2 this period extended slightly esp. For a holiday or visit etc.'),
(40136, 'w', '\r\nWeekender', ' n. Person who spends the weekend away from home; weekend visitor.'),
(40137, 'w', '\r\nWeekly', ' â€”adj. Done, produced, or occurring once a week. â€”adv. Once a week. â€”n. (pl. -ies) weekly newspaper or periodical.'),
(40138, 'w', '\r\nWeeny', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Tiny. [from *wee1]'),
(40139, 'w', '\r\nWeep', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Wept) 1 shed tears. 2 (often foll. By for) bewail, lament over. 3 a be covered with or send forth drops. B come or send forth in drops; exude liquid. 4 (as weeping adj.) (of a tree) having drooping branches. â€”n. Spell of weeping. [old english]'),
(40140, 'w', '\r\nWeepie', ' n. Colloq. Sentimental or emotional film, play, etc.'),
(40141, 'w', '\r\nWeepy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. Inclined to weep; tearful.'),
(40142, 'w', '\r\nWeevil', ' n. Destructive beetle feeding esp. On grain. [low german]'),
(40143, 'w', '\r\nWee-wee', ' colloq. â€”n. 1 act of urinating. 2 urine. â€”v. (-wees, -weed) urinate. [origin unknown]'),
(40144, 'w', '\r\nWeft', ' n. 1 threads woven across a warp to make fabric. 2 yarn for these. 3 thing woven. [old english: related to *weave1]'),
(40145, 'w', '\r\nWeigh', ' v. 1 find the weight of. 2 balance in the hands to guess or as if to guess the weight of. 3 (often foll. By out) take a definite weight of (a substance); measure out (a specified weight) (weigh out the flour; weigh out 6 oz). 4 a estimate the relative value, importance, or desirability of. B (foll. By with, against) compare. 5 be equal to (a specified weight). 6 have (esp. A specified) importance; exert an influence. 7 (often foll. By on) be heavy or burdensome (to); be depressing (to).  weigh down 1 bring down by exerting weight. 2 be oppressive to. Weigh in (of a boxer before a contest, or a jockey after a race) be weighed. Weigh in with colloq. Advance (an argument etc.) Boldly. Weigh out (of a jockey) be weighed before a race. Weigh up colloq. Form an estimate of. Weigh one''s words carefully choose the way one expresses something. [old english, = carry]'),
(40146, 'w', '\r\nWeighbridge', ' n. Weighing-machine for vehicles.'),
(40147, 'w', '\r\nWeigh-in', ' n. Weighing of a boxer before a fight.'),
(40148, 'w', '\r\nWeight', ' â€”n. 1 force experienced by a body as a result of the earth''s gravitation. 2 heaviness of a body regarded as a property of it. 3 a quantitative expression of a body''s weight. B scale of such weights (troy weight). 4 body of a known weight for use in weighing or weight training. 5 heavy body, esp. As used in a mechanism etc. 6 load or burden. 7 influence, importance. 8 athletics = *shot1 7. â€”v. 1 a attach a weight to. B hold down with a weight. 2 (foll. By with) impede or burden.  throw one''s weight about (or around) colloq. Be unpleasantly self-assertive. Worth one''s weight in gold very useful or helpful. [old english]'),
(40149, 'w', '\r\nWeighting', ' n. Extra allowance paid in special cases.'),
(40150, 'w', '\r\nWeightless', ' adj. (of a body, esp. In an orbiting spacecraft etc.) Not apparently acted on by gravity.  weightlessness n.'),
(40151, 'w', '\r\nWeightlifting', ' n. Sport of lifting heavy weights.  weightlifter n.'),
(40152, 'w', '\r\nWeight', 'training  n. Physical training using weights.'),
(40153, 'w', '\r\nWeighty', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 heavy. 2 momentous. 3 (of utterances etc.) Deserving consideration. 4 influential, authoritative.  weightily adv. Weightiness n.'),
(40154, 'w', '\r\nWeir', ' n. Dam across a river to raise the level of water upstream or regulate its flow. [old english]'),
(40155, 'w', '\r\nWeird', ' adj. 1 uncanny, supernatural. 2 colloq. Queer, incomprehensible.  weirdly adv. Weirdness n. [old english wyrd destiny]'),
(40156, 'w', '\r\nWeirdo', ' n. (pl. -s) colloq. Odd or eccentric person.'),
(40157, 'w', '\r\nWelch', ' var. Of *welsh.'),
(40158, 'w', '\r\nWelcome', ' â€”n. Act of greeting or receiving gladly; kind or glad reception. â€”int. Expressing such a greeting. â€”v. (-ming) receive with a welcome. â€”adj. 1 that one receives with pleasure (welcome guest; welcome news). 2 (foll. By to, or to + infin.) Cordially allowed or invited (you are welcome to use my car).  make welcome receive hospitably. Outstay one''s welcome stay too long as a visitor etc. You are welcome there is no need for thanks. [old english]'),
(40159, 'w', '\r\nWeld', ' â€”v. 1 a hammer or press (pieces of iron or other metal usu. Heated but not melted) into one piece. B join by fusion with an electric arc etc. C form by welding into some article. 2 fashion into an effectual or homogeneous whole. â€”n. Welded joint.  welder n. [alteration of *well2, probably influenced by the form welled]'),
(40160, 'w', '\r\nWelfare', ' n. 1 well-being, happiness; health and prosperity (of a person or community etc.). 2 (welfare) a welfare centre or office. B financial support given by the state. [from *well1, *fare]'),
(40161, 'w', '\r\nWelfare', 'state  n. 1 system whereby the state undertakes to protect the health and well-being of its citizens by means of grants, pensions, etc. 2 country practising this system.'),
(40162, 'w', '\r\nWelfare', 'work  n. Organized effort for the welfare of the poor, disabled, etc.'),
(40163, 'w', '\r\nWelkin', ' n. Poet. Sky. [old english, = cloud]'),
(40164, 'w', '\r\nWell1', ' â€”adv. (better, best) 1 in a satisfactory way (works well). 2 with some distinction (plays the piano well). 3 in a kind way (treated me well). 4 thoroughly, carefully (polish it well). 5 with heartiness or approval (speak well of). 6 probably, reasonably (you may well be right). 7 to a considerable extent (is well over forty). â€”adj. (better, best) 1 (usu. Predic.) In good health. 2 (predic.) A in a satisfactory state or position. B advisable (it would be well to enquire). â€”int. Expressing surprise, resignation, etc., or used to introduce speech.  leave well alone avoid needless change or disturbance. Well and truly decisively, completely. Well away 1 having made considerable progress. 2 colloq. Fast asleep or drunk. Well done! Expressing praise for something done. Well worth certainly worth. [old english]'),
(40165, 'w', '\r\nWell2', ' â€”n. 1 shaft sunk into the ground to obtain water, oil, etc. 2 enclosed space like a well-shaft, e.g. In the middle of a building for stairs or a lift, or for light or ventilation. 3 (foll. By of) source. 4 (in pl.) Spa. 5 = *ink-well. 6 archaic water-spring. 7 railed space in a lawcourt. â€”v. (foll. By out, up) spring as from a fountain. [old english]'),
(40166, 'w', '\r\nWe''ll', ' contr. We shall; we will.'),
(40167, 'w', '\r\nWell-adjusted', ' adj. 1 mentally and emotionally stable. 2 in a good state of adjustment.'),
(40168, 'w', '\r\nWell-advised', ' adj. (usu. Foll. By to + infin.) Prudent.'),
(40169, 'w', '\r\nWell-appointed', ' adj. Having all the necessary equipment.'),
(40170, 'w', '\r\nWell-attended', ' adj. Attended by a large number of people.'),
(40171, 'w', '\r\nWell-balanced', ' adj. Sane, sensible.'),
(40172, 'w', '\r\nWell-behaved', ' adj. Habitually behaving well.'),
(40173, 'w', '\r\nWell-being', ' n. State of being contented, healthy, etc.'),
(40174, 'w', '\r\nWell-born', ' adj. Of noble family.'),
(40175, 'w', '\r\nWell-bred', ' adj. Having or showing good breeding or manners.'),
(40176, 'w', '\r\nWell-built', ' adj. Big, strong, and well-proportioned.'),
(40177, 'w', '\r\nWell-connected', ' adj. Associated, esp. By birth, with persons of good social position.'),
(40178, 'w', '\r\nWell-disposed', ' adj. (often foll. By towards) friendly or sympathetic.'),
(40179, 'w', '\r\nWell-dressed', ' adj. Fashionably smart.'),
(40180, 'w', '\r\nWell-earned', ' adj. Fully deserved.'),
(40181, 'w', '\r\nWell-founded', ' adj. (of suspicions etc.) Based on good evidence.'),
(40182, 'w', '\r\nWell-groomed', ' adj. With carefully tended hair, clothes, etc.'),
(40183, 'w', '\r\nWell-head', ' n. Source.'),
(40184, 'w', '\r\nWell-heeled', ' adj. Colloq. Wealthy.'),
(40185, 'w', '\r\nWellies', ' n.pl. Colloq. Wellingtons. [abbreviation]'),
(40186, 'w', '\r\nWell-informed', ' adj. Having much knowledge or information about a subject.'),
(40187, 'w', '\r\nWellington', ' n. (in full wellington boot) waterproof boot usu. Reaching the knee. [duke of wellington]'),
(40188, 'w', '\r\nWell-intentioned', ' adj. Having or showing good intentions.'),
(40189, 'w', '\r\nWell-judged', ' adj. Opportunely, skilfully, or discreetly done.'),
(40190, 'w', '\r\nWell-kept', ' adj. Kept in good order or condition.'),
(40191, 'w', '\r\nWell-known', ' adj. Known to many.'),
(40192, 'w', '\r\nWell-made', ' adj. 1 strongly manufactured. 2 having a good build.'),
(40193, 'w', '\r\nWell-mannered', ' adj. Having good manners.'),
(40194, 'w', '\r\nWell-meaning', ' adj. (also well-meant) well-intentioned (but ineffective).'),
(40195, 'w', '\r\nWellnigh', ' adv. Almost (wellnigh impossible).'),
(40196, 'w', '\r\nWell', 'off  adj. (also well-off) 1 having plenty of money. 2 in a fortunate situation.'),
(40197, 'w', '\r\nWell-oiled', ' adj. Colloq. Very drunk.'),
(40198, 'w', '\r\nWell-paid', ' adj. 1 (of a job) that pays well. 2 (of a person) amply rewarded for a job.'),
(40199, 'w', '\r\nWell-preserved', ' adj. 1 in good condition. 2 (of an old person) showing little sign of age.'),
(40200, 'w', '\r\nWell-read', ' adj. Knowledgeable through much reading.'),
(40201, 'w', '\r\nWell-received', ' adj. Welcomed; favourably received.'),
(40202, 'w', '\r\nWell-rounded', ' adj. Complete and symmetrical.'),
(40203, 'w', '\r\nWell-spoken', ' adj. Articulate or refined in speech.'),
(40204, 'w', '\r\nWell-spring', ' n. = *well-head.'),
(40205, 'w', '\r\nWell-to-do', ' adj. Prosperous.'),
(40206, 'w', '\r\nWell-tried', ' adj. Often tested with good results.'),
(40207, 'w', '\r\nWell-trodden', ' adj. Much frequented.'),
(40208, 'w', '\r\nWell-wisher', ' n. Person who wishes one well.'),
(40209, 'w', '\r\nWell-worn', ' adj. 1 much worn by use. 2 (of a phrase etc.) Trite.'),
(40210, 'w', '\r\nWelsh', ' â€”adj. Of or relating to wales or its people or language. â€”n. 1 the celtic language of wales. 2 (prec. By the; treated as pl.) The people of wales. [old english, ultimately from latin volcae, name of a celtic people]'),
(40211, 'w', '\r\nWelsh', ' v. (also welch) 1 (of a loser of a bet, esp. A bookmaker) decamp without paying. 2 evade an obligation. 3 (foll. By on) a fail to carry out a promise to (a person). B fail to honour (an obligation). [origin unknown]'),
(40212, 'w', '\r\nWelshman', ' n. Man who is welsh by birth or descent.'),
(40213, 'w', '\r\nWelsh', 'rabbit  n. (also, by folk etymology, welsh rarebit) dish of melted cheese etc. On toast.'),
(40214, 'w', '\r\nWelshwoman', ' n. Woman who is welsh by birth or descent.'),
(40215, 'w', '\r\nWelt', ' â€”n. 1 leather rim sewn round the edge of a shoe-upper for the sole to be attached to. 2 = *weal1. 3 ribbed or reinforced border of a garment. 4 heavy blow. â€”v. 1 provide with a welt. 2 raise weals on; thrash. [origin unknown]'),
(40216, 'w', '\r\nWelter1', ' â€”v. 1 roll, wallow. 2 (foll. By in) lie prostrate or be soaked in. â€”n. 1 general confusion. 2 (foll. By of) disorderly mixture or contrast. [low german or dutch]'),
(40217, 'w', '\r\nWelter2', ' n. Heavy rider or boxer. [origin unknown]'),
(40218, 'w', '\r\nWelterweight', ' n. 1 weight in certain sports intermediate between lightweight and middleweight, in the amateur boxing scale 63.5â€“67 kg. 2 sportsman of this weight.'),
(40219, 'w', '\r\nWen', ' n. Benign tumour on the skin, esp. On the scalp. [old english]'),
(40220, 'w', '\r\nWench', ' n. Joc. Girl or young woman. [abbreviation of wenchel, from old english, = child]'),
(40221, 'w', '\r\nWend', ' v.  wend one''s way make one''s way. [old english, = turn]'),
(40222, 'w', '\r\nWendy', 'house  n. Children''s small houselike tent or structure for playing in. [wendy, name of a character in barrie''s peter pan]'),
(40223, 'w', '\r\nWent', ' past of *go1.'),
(40224, 'w', '\r\nWept', ' past of *weep.'),
(40225, 'w', '\r\nWere', ' 2nd sing. Past, pl. Past, and past subjunctive of *be.'),
(40226, 'w', '\r\nWe''re', ' contr. We are.'),
(40227, 'w', '\r\nWeren''t', ' contr. Were not.'),
(40228, 'w', '\r\nWerewolf', ' n. (pl. -wolves) mythical being who at times changes from a person to a wolf. [old english]'),
(40229, 'w', '\r\nWesleyan', ' â€”adj. Of or relating to a protestant denomination founded by john wesley. â€”n. Member of this denomination.'),
(40230, 'w', '\r\nWest', ' â€”n. 1 a point of the horizon where the sun sets at the equinoxes. B compass point corresponding to this. C direction in which this lies. 2 (usu. The west) a european civilization. B states of western europe and n. America. C western part of a country, town, etc. â€”adj. 1 towards, at, near, or facing the west. 2 from the west (west wind). â€”adv. 1 towards, at, or near the west. 2 (foll. By of) further west than.  go west slang be killed or destroyed etc. To the west (often followed by of) in a westerly direction. [old english]'),
(40231, 'w', '\r\nWestbound', ' adj. Travelling or leading westwards.'),
(40232, 'w', '\r\nWest', 'country  n. South-western england.'),
(40233, 'w', '\r\nWest', 'end  n. Main entertainment and shopping area of london.'),
(40234, 'w', '\r\nWestering', ' adj. (of the sun) nearing the west.'),
(40235, 'w', '\r\nWesterly', ' â€”adj. & adv. 1 in a western position or direction. 2 (of a wind) from the west. â€”n. (pl. -ies) such a wind.'),
(40236, 'w', '\r\nWestern', ' â€”adj. Of or in the west. â€”n. Film or novel about cowboys in western north america.  westernmost adj.'),
(40237, 'w', '\r\nWesterner', ' n. Native or inhabitant of the west.'),
(40238, 'w', '\r\nWesternize', ' v. (also -ise) (-zing or -sing) influence with, or convert to, the ideas and customs etc. Of the west.'),
(40239, 'w', '\r\nWest', 'indian  n. 1 native or national of the west indies. 2 person of west indian descent.'),
(40240, 'w', '\r\nWest-north-west', ' n. Point or direction midway between west and north-west.'),
(40241, 'w', '\r\nWest', 'side  n. Us western part of manhattan.'),
(40242, 'w', '\r\nWest-south-west', ' n. Point or direction midway between west and south-west.'),
(40243, 'w', '\r\nWestward', ' â€”adj. & adv. (also westwards) towards the west. â€”n. Westward direction or region.'),
(40244, 'w', '\r\nWet', ' â€”adj. (wetter, wettest) 1 soaked or covered with water or other liquid. 2 (of the weather etc.) Rainy. 3 (of paint etc.) Not yet dried. 4 used with water (wet shampoo). 5 colloq. Feeble, inept. â€”v. (-tt-; past and past part. Wet or wetted) 1 make wet. 2 a urinate in or on (wet the bed).'),
(40245, 'w', '\r\nWet', 'blanket  n. Colloq. Gloomy person hindering others'' enjoyment.'),
(40246, 'w', '\r\nWet', 'dream  n. Erotic dream with the involuntary ejaculation of semen.'),
(40247, 'w', '\r\nWether', ' n. Castrated ram. [old english]'),
(40248, 'w', '\r\nWetland', ' n. (often in pl.) Swamps and other damp areas of land.'),
(40249, 'w', '\r\nWet-nurse', ' â€”n. Woman employed to suckle another''s child. â€”v. 1 act as a wet-nurse to. 2 colloq. Treat as if helpless.'),
(40250, 'w', '\r\nWet', 'suit  n. Rubber garment worn by skin-divers etc. To keep warm.'),
(40251, 'w', '\r\nWe''ve', ' contr. We have.'),
(40252, 'w', '\r\nWg.', 'Cdr.  Abbr. Wing commander.'),
(40253, 'w', '\r\nWhack', ' colloq. â€”v. 1 strike or beat forcefully. 2 (as whacked adj.) Tired out. â€”n. 1 sharp or resounding blow. 2 slang share.  have a whack at slang attempt. [imitative]'),
(40254, 'w', '\r\nWhacking', ' colloq. â€”adj. Very large. â€”adv. Very.'),
(40255, 'w', '\r\nWhale', ' â€”n. (pl. Same or -s) very large marine mammal with a streamlined body and horizontal tail. â€”v. (-ling) hunt whales.  a whale of a colloq. An exceedingly good or fine etc. [old english]'),
(40256, 'w', '\r\nWhalebone', ' n. Elastic horny substance in the upper jaw of some whales.'),
(40257, 'w', '\r\nWhale-oil', ' n. Oil from the blubber of whales.'),
(40258, 'w', '\r\nWhaler', ' n. Whaling ship or seaman.'),
(40259, 'w', '\r\nWham', ' int. Colloq. Expressing forcible impact. [imitative]'),
(40260, 'w', '\r\nWharf', ' â€”n. (pl. Wharves or -s) quayside area to which a ship may be moored to load and unload. â€”v. 1 moor (a ship) at a wharf. 2 store (goods) on a wharf. [old english]'),
(40261, 'w', '\r\nWharfage', ' n. 1 accommodation at a wharf. 2 fee for this.'),
(40262, 'w', '\r\nWhat', ' â€”interrog. Adj. 1 asking for a choice from an indefinite number or for a statement of amount, number, or kind (what books have you read?). 2 colloq. = *which interrog.adj. (what book have you chosen?). â€”adj. (usu. In an exclamation) how great or remarkable (what luck!). â€”rel. Adj. The or any â€¦ that (will give you what help i can). â€”pron. (corresponding to the functions of the adj.) 1 what thing or things? (what is your name?; i don''t know what you mean). 2 (asking for a remark to be repeated) = what did you say? 3 how much (what you must have suffered!). 4 (as rel. Pron.) That or those which; a or the or any thing which (what followed was worse; tell me what you think). â€”adv. To what extent (what does it matter?).  what about what is the news or your opinion of. What-d''you-call-it colloq. Substitute for a name not recalled. What ever what at all or in any way (what ever do you mean?) (see also *whatever). What for colloq. 1 for what reason? 2 severe reprimand (esp. Give a person what for). What have you (prec. By or or and) colloq. Anything else similar. What not (prec. By and) other similar things. What''s-his (or -her or -its) -name colloq. Substitute for a name not recalled. What''s what colloq. What is useful or important etc. What with colloq. Because of (usu. Several things). [old english]'),
(40263, 'w', '\r\nWhatever', ' adj. & pron. 1 = *what (in relative uses) with the emphasis on indefiniteness (lend me whatever you can; whatever money you have). 2 though anything (we are safe whatever happens). 3 (with neg. Or interrog.) At all; of any kind (there is no doubt whatever).'),
(40264, 'w', '\r\nWhatnot', ' n. Colloq. Indefinite or trivial thing.'),
(40265, 'w', '\r\nWhatsoever', ' adj. & pron. = *whatever.'),
(40266, 'w', '\r\nWheat', ' n. 1 cereal plant bearing dense four-sided seed-spikes. 2 its grain, used in making flour etc. [old english]'),
(40267, 'w', '\r\nWheatear', ' n. Small migratory bird. [related to *white, *arse]'),
(40268, 'w', '\r\nWheaten', ' adj. Made of wheat.'),
(40269, 'w', '\r\nWheat', 'germ  n. Embryo of the wheat grain, extracted as a source of vitamins.'),
(40270, 'w', '\r\nWheatmeal', ' n. Flour made from wheat with some of the bran and germ removed.'),
(40271, 'w', '\r\nWheedle', ' v. (-ling) 1 coax by flattery or endearments. 2 (foll. By out) get (a thing) out of a person or cheat (a person) out of a thing by wheedling. [origin uncertain]'),
(40272, 'w', '\r\nWheel', ' â€”n. 1 circular frame or disc which revolves on an axle and is used for vehicular or other mechanical motion. 2 wheel-like thing. 3 motion as of a wheel, esp. The movement of a line of soldiers with one end as a pivot. 4 (in pl.) Slang car. 5 = *steering-wheel. â€”v. 1 a turn on an axis or pivot. B swing round in line with one end as a pivot. 2 a (often foll. By about, round) change direction or face another way. B cause to do this. 3 push or pull (a wheeled thing, or its load or occupant). 4 go in circles or curves.  at the wheel 1 driving a vehicle. 2 directing a ship. 3 in control. On wheels (or oiled wheels) smoothly. Wheel and deal engage in political or commercial scheming. Wheels within wheels 1 intricate machinery. 2 colloq. Indirect or secret agencies.  wheeled adj. (also in comb.). [old english]'),
(40273, 'w', '\r\nWheelbarrow', ' n. Small handcart with one wheel and two shafts.'),
(40274, 'w', '\r\nWheelbase', ' n. Distance between the axles of a vehicle.'),
(40275, 'w', '\r\nWheelchair', ' n. Chair on wheels for an invalid or disabled person.'),
(40276, 'w', '\r\nWheel-clamp', ' n. = *clamp1 n. 2.'),
(40277, 'w', '\r\n-wheeler', ' comb. Form vehicle with a specified number of wheels (three-wheeler).'),
(40278, 'w', '\r\nWheeler-dealer', ' n. Person who wheels and deals.'),
(40279, 'w', '\r\nWheel-house', ' n. Steersman''s shelter.'),
(40280, 'w', '\r\nWheelie', ' n. Slang stunt of riding a bicycle or motor cycle with the front wheel off the ground.'),
(40281, 'w', '\r\nWheel-spin', ' n. Rotation of a vehicle''s wheels without traction.'),
(40282, 'w', '\r\nWheelwright', ' n. Person who makes or repairs wheels.'),
(40283, 'w', '\r\nWheeze', ' â€”v. (-zing) 1 breathe with an audible whistling sound. 2 utter with this sound. â€”n. 1 sound of wheezing. 2 colloq. Clever scheme.  wheezy adj. (-ier, -iest). Wheezily adv. Wheeziness n. [probably from old norse, = hiss]'),
(40284, 'w', '\r\nWhelk', ' n. Marine mollusc with a spiral shell. [old english]'),
(40285, 'w', '\r\nWhelm', ' v. Poet. 1 engulf. 2 crush with weight. [old english]'),
(40286, 'w', '\r\nWhelp', ' â€”n. 1 young dog; puppy. 2 archaic cub. 3 ill-mannered child or youth. â€”v. (also absol.) Give birth to (a whelp or whelps or (derog.) A child). [old english]'),
(40287, 'w', '\r\nWhen', ' â€”interrog. Adv. 1 at what time? 2 on what occasion? 3 how soon? â€”rel. Adv. (prec. By time etc.) At or on which (there are times when i could cry). â€”conj. 1 at the or any time that; as soon as (come when you like; come when ready). 2 although (why stand when you could sit?). 3 after which; and then; but just then (was nearly asleep when the bell rang). â€”pron. What time?; which time (till when can you stay?; since when it has improved). â€”n. Time, occasion (fixed the where and when). [old english]'),
(40288, 'w', '\r\nWhence', ' formal â€”interrog. Adv. From what place? â€”conj. 1 to the place from which (return whence you came). 2 (often prec. By place etc.) From which. 3 and thence (whence it follows that). [old english: related to *when]'),
(40289, 'w', '\r\nUsage', ' the use of from whence rather than simply whence (as in the place from whence they came), though common, is generally considered incorrect.'),
(40290, 'w', '\r\nWhenever', ' conj. & adv. 1 at whatever time; on whatever occasion. 2 every time that.'),
(40291, 'w', '\r\nWhensoever', ' conj. & adv. Formal = *whenever.'),
(40292, 'w', '\r\nWhere', ' â€”interrog. Adv. 1 in or to what place or position? 2 in what respect? (where does it concern us?). â€”rel. Adv. (prec. By place etc.) In or to which (places where they meet). â€”conj. 1 in or to the or any place, direction, or respect in which (go where you like; tick where applicable). 2 and there (reached crewe, where the car broke down). â€”pron. What place? (where do you come from?). â€”n. Place; scene of something (see *when n.). [old english]'),
(40293, 'w', '\r\nWhereabouts', ' â€”interrog. Adv. Approximately where? â€”n. (as sing. Or pl.) Person''s or thing''s location.'),
(40294, 'w', '\r\nWhereas', ' conj. 1 in contrast or comparison with the fact that. 2 (esp. In legal preambles) taking into consideration the fact that.'),
(40295, 'w', '\r\nWhereby', ' conj. By what or which means.'),
(40296, 'w', '\r\nWherefore', ' â€”adv. Archaic 1 for what reason? 2 for which reason. â€”n. See *why.'),
(40297, 'w', '\r\nWherein', ' conj. Formal in what or which place or respect.'),
(40298, 'w', '\r\nWhereof', ' conj. Formal of what or which.'),
(40299, 'w', '\r\nWhereupon', ' conj. Immediately after which.'),
(40300, 'w', '\r\nWherever', ' â€”adv. In or to whatever place. â€”conj. In every place that.'),
(40301, 'w', '\r\nWherewithal', ' n. Colloq. Money etc. Needed for a purpose.'),
(40302, 'w', '\r\nWherry', ' n. (pl. -ies) 1 light rowing-boat usu. For carrying passengers. 2 large light barge. [origin unknown]'),
(40303, 'w', '\r\nWhet', ' v. (-tt-) 1 sharpen (a tool). 2 stimulate (the appetite or a desire etc.). [old english]'),
(40304, 'w', '\r\nWhether', ' conj. Introducing the first or both of alternative possibilities (i doubt whether it matters; i do not know whether they have arrived or not).  whether or no whether it is so or not. [old english]'),
(40305, 'w', '\r\nWhetstone', ' n. Tapered stone used with water to sharpen tools.'),
(40306, 'w', '\r\nWhew', ' int. Expressing surprise, consternation, or relief. [imitative]'),
(40307, 'w', '\r\nWhey', ' n. Watery liquid left when milk forms curds. [old english]'),
(40308, 'w', '\r\nWhich', ' â€”interrog. Adj. Asking for choice from a definite set of alternatives (which john do you mean?; say which book you prefer). â€”rel. Adj. Being the one just referred to; and this or these (ten years, during which time they admitted nothing). â€”interrog. Pron. 1 which person or persons? (which of you is responsible?). 2 which thing or things? (say which you prefer). â€”rel. Pron. (poss. Of which, whose) 1 which thing or things, usu. Introducing a clause not essential for identification (the house, which is empty, has been damaged). 2 used in place of that after in or that (there is the house in which i was born; that which you have just seen). [old english]'),
(40309, 'w', '\r\nWhichever', ' adj. & pron. Any which (take whichever you like).'),
(40310, 'w', '\r\nWhiff', ' n. 1 puff or breath of air, smoke, etc. 2 smell. 3 (foll. By of) trace of scandal etc. 4 small cigar. [imitative]'),
(40311, 'w', '\r\nWhig', ' n. Hist. Member of the british reforming and constitutional party succeeded in the 19th c. By the liberal party.  whiggery n. Whiggish adj. Whiggism n. [whiggamer, -more, nickname of 17th-c. Scots rebels]'),
(40312, 'w', '\r\nWhile', ' â€”n. Period of time (a long while ago; waited a while; all this while). â€”conj. 1 during the time that; for as long as; at the same time as (while i was away, the house was burgled; fell asleep while reading). 2 in spite of the fact that; whereas (while i want to believe it, i cannot). â€”v. (-ling) (foll. By away) pass (time etc.) In a leisurely or interesting way. â€”rel. Adv. (prec. By time etc.) During which (the summer while i was abroad).  between whiles in the intervals. For a while for some time. In a while soon. The while in the meantime. Worth while (or worth one''s while) worth the time or effort spent. [old english]'),
(40313, 'w', '\r\nUsage', ' worth while (two words) is used only predicatively, as in thought it worth while to ring the police, whereas worthwhile is used both predicatively and attributively.'),
(40314, 'w', '\r\nWhilst', ' adv. & conj. While. [from *while]'),
(40315, 'w', '\r\nWhim', ' n. 1 sudden fancy; caprice. 2 capriciousness. [origin unknown]'),
(40316, 'w', '\r\nWhimper', ' â€”v. Make feeble, querulous, or frightened sounds. â€”n. Such a sound. [imitative]'),
(40317, 'w', '\r\nWhimsical', ' adj. Capricious, fantastic.  whimsicality n. Whimsically adv.'),
(40318, 'w', '\r\nWhimsy', ' n. (pl. -ies) = *whim. [origin uncertain]'),
(40319, 'w', '\r\nWhin', ' n. (in sing. Or pl.) Gorse. [scandinavian]'),
(40320, 'w', '\r\nWhinchat', ' n. Small songbird.'),
(40321, 'w', '\r\nWhine', ' â€”n. 1 complaining long-drawn wail as of a dog. 2 similar shrill prolonged sound. 3 querulous tone or complaint. â€”v. (-ning) emit or utter a whine; complain. [old english]'),
(40322, 'w', '\r\nWhinge', ' v. (-geing or -ging) colloq. Whine; grumble peevishly. [old english]'),
(40323, 'w', '\r\nWhinny', ' â€”n. (pl. -ies) gentle or joyful neigh. â€”v. (-ies, -ied) give a whinny. [imitative]'),
(40324, 'w', '\r\nWhip', ' â€”n. 1 lash attached to a stick for urging on animals or punishing etc. 2 a member of a political party in parliament appointed to control its discipline and tactics. B whips'' written notice requesting or requiring attendance for voting at a division etc., variously underlined according to the degree of urgency (three-line whip). C (prec. By the) party discipline and instructions (asked for the labour whip). 3 dessert made with whipped cream etc. 4 = *whipper-in. â€”v. (-pp-) 1 beat or urge on with a whip. 2 beat (cream or eggs etc.) Into a froth. 3 take or move suddenly, unexpectedly, or rapidly (whipped out a knife; whipped behind the door). 4 slang steal. 5 slang a excel. B defeat. 6 bind with spirally wound twine. 7 sew with overcast stitches.  whip in bring (hounds) together. Whip on urge into action. Whip up excite or stir up. [low german or dutch]'),
(40325, 'w', '\r\nWhipcord', ' n. Tightly twisted cord.'),
(40326, 'w', '\r\nWhip', 'hand  n. 1 hand that holds the whip (in riding etc.). 2 (usu. Prec. By the) advantage or control in a situation.'),
(40327, 'w', '\r\nWhiplash', ' n. Flexible end of a whip.'),
(40328, 'w', '\r\nWhiplash', 'injury  n. Injury to the neck caused by a jerk of the head, esp. As in a motor accident.'),
(40329, 'w', '\r\nWhipper-in', ' n. (pl. Whippers-in) huntsman''s assistant who manages the hounds.'),
(40330, 'w', '\r\nWhippersnapper', ' n. 1 small child. 2 insignificant but presumptuous person.'),
(40331, 'w', '\r\nWhippet', ' n. Crossbred dog of the greyhound type used for racing. [probably from obsolete whippet move briskly, from whip it]'),
(40332, 'w', '\r\nWhipping', 'boy  n. Scapegoat.'),
(40333, 'w', '\r\nWhipping-top', ' n. Top kept spinning by blows of a lash.'),
(40334, 'w', '\r\nWhippoorwill', ' n. American nightjar. [imitative]'),
(40335, 'w', '\r\nWhip-round', ' n. Colloq. Informal collection of money among a group of people.'),
(40336, 'w', '\r\nWhipstock', ' n. Handle of a whip.'),
(40337, 'w', '\r\nWhirl', ' â€”v. 1 swing round and round; revolve rapidly. 2 (foll. By away) convey or go rapidly in a vehicle etc. 3 send or travel swiftly in an orbit or a curve. 4 (of the brain etc.) Seem to spin round. â€”n. 1 whirling movement. 2 state of intense activity (the social whirl). 3 state of confusion (in a whirl).  give it a whirl colloq. Attempt it. [old norse, and low german or dutch]'),
(40338, 'w', '\r\nWhirligig', ' n. 1 spinning or whirling toy. 2 merry-go-round. 3 revolving motion.'),
(40339, 'w', '\r\nWhirlpool', ' n. Powerful circular eddy of water.'),
(40340, 'w', '\r\nWhirlwind', ' n. 1 rapidly whirling mass or column of air. 2 (attrib.) Very rapid.'),
(40341, 'w', '\r\nWhirr', ' â€”n. Continuous rapid buzz or soft clicking sound. â€”v. (-rr-) make this sound. [scandinavian]'),
(40342, 'w', '\r\nWhisk', ' â€”v. 1 (foll. By away, off) a brush with a sweeping movement. B take suddenly. 2 whip (cream, eggs, etc.). 3 convey or go (esp. Out of sight) lightly or quickly. 4 wave or lightly brandish. â€”n. 1 whisking action or motion. 2 utensil for whisking eggs or cream etc. 3 bunch of grass, twigs, bristles, etc., for removing dust or flies. [scandinavian]'),
(40343, 'w', '\r\nWhisker', ' n. 1 (usu. In pl.) Hair growing on a man''s face, esp. On the cheek. 2 each of the bristles on the face of a cat etc. 3 colloq. Small distance (within a whisker of).  whiskered adj. Whiskery adj. [from *whisk]'),
(40344, 'w', '\r\nWhiskey', ' n. (brit. Whisky) (pl. -eys) spirit distilled esp. From malted grain, esp. Barley or rye. [abbreviation of usquebaugh from gaelic, = water of life]'),
(40345, 'w', '\r\nWhisky', ' n. (ir. & us whiskey) (pl. -ies or -eys) spirit distilled esp. From malted grain, esp. Barley or rye. [abbreviation of usquebaugh from gaelic, = water of life]'),
(40346, 'w', '\r\nWhisper', ' â€”v. 1 a speak very softly without vibration of the vocal cords. B talk or say in a barely audible tone or in a secret or confidential way. 2 rustle or murmur. â€”n. 1 whispering speech or sound. 2 thing whispered.  it is whispered there is a rumour. [old english]'),
(40347, 'w', '\r\nWhist', ' n. Card-game usu. For two pairs of players. [earlier whisk, perhaps from *whisk (with ref. To whisking away the tricks): perhaps associated with whist! (= silence)]'),
(40348, 'w', '\r\nWhist', 'drive  n. Social occasion with the playing of progressive whist.'),
(40349, 'w', '\r\nWhistle', ' â€”n. 1 clear shrill sound made by forcing breath through a small hole between nearly closed lips. 2 similar sound made by a bird, the wind, a missile, etc. 3 instrument used to produce such a sound. â€”v. (-ling) 1 emit a whistle. 2 a give a signal or express surprise or derision by whistling. B (often foll. By up) summon or give a signal to (a dog etc.) By whistling. 3 (also absol.) Produce (a tune) by whistling. 4 (foll. By for) vainly seek or desire. [old english]'),
(40350, 'w', '\r\nWhistle-stop', ' n. 1 us small unimportant town on a railway. 2 politician''s brief pause for an electioneering speech on tour.'),
(40351, 'w', '\r\nWhit', ' â€”n. = *whitsuntide. â€”attrib. Adj. Of whitsuntide or whit sunday. [old english, = white]'),
(40352, 'w', '\r\nWhit', ' n. Particle; least possible amount (not a whit better). [apparently = *wight]'),
(40353, 'w', '\r\nWhite', ' â€”adj. 1 resembling a surface reflecting sunlight without absorbing any of the visible rays; of the colour of milk or snow. 2 nearly this colour; pale, esp. In the face. 3 (white) a of the human group having light-coloured skin. B of or relating to white people. 4 albino (white mouse). 5 (of hair) having lost its colour, esp. In old age. 6 (of coffee) with milk or cream. â€”n. 1 white colour or pigment. 2 a white clothes or material. B (in pl.) White garments as worn in cricket, tennis, etc. 3 a (in a game or sport) white piece, ball, etc. B player using these. 4 = *egg-white. 5 whitish part of the eyeball round the iris. 6 (white) member of a light-skinned race.  bleed white drain of wealth etc.  whiteness n. Whitish adj. [old english]'),
(40354, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'ant  n. Termite.'),
(40355, 'w', '\r\nWhitebait', ' n. (pl. Same) (usu. In pl.) Small silvery-white young of herrings and sprats, esp. As food.'),
(40356, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'cell  n. Leucocyte.'),
(40357, 'w', '\r\nWhite-collar', ' attrib. Adj. (of a worker or work) non-manual; clerical, professional.'),
(40358, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'corpuscle  n. = *white cell.'),
(40359, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'elephant  n. Useless possession.'),
(40360, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'feather  n. Symbol of cowardice.'),
(40361, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'flag  n. Symbol of surrender.'),
(40362, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'friar  n. Carmelite.'),
(40363, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'goods  n.pl. Large domestic electrical equipment.'),
(40364, 'w', '\r\nWhitehead', ' n. Colloq. White or white-topped skin-pustule.'),
(40365, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'heat  n. 1 temperature at which metal emits white light. 2 state of intense passion or activity.  white-hot adj.'),
(40366, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'hope  n. Person expected to achieve much.'),
(40367, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'horses  n.pl. White-crested waves at sea.'),
(40368, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'lead  n. Mixture of lead carbonate and hydrated lead oxide used as pigment.'),
(40369, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'lie  n. Harmless or trivial untruth.'),
(40370, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'light  n. Apparently colourless light, e.g. Ordinary daylight.'),
(40371, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'magic  n. Magic used for beneficent purposes.'),
(40372, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'meat  n. Poultry, veal, rabbit, and pork.'),
(40373, 'w', '\r\nWhiten', ' v. Make or become white.  whitener n.'),
(40374, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'noise  n. Noise containing many frequencies with equal intensities.'),
(40375, 'w', '\r\nWhite-out', ' n. Dense blizzard, esp. In polar regions.'),
(40376, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'paper  n. Government report giving information.'),
(40377, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'pepper  n. Pepper made by grinding a ripe or husked berry.'),
(40378, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'russian  n. = *byelorussian.'),
(40379, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'sauce  n. Sauce of flour, melted butter, and milk or cream.'),
(40380, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'slave  n. Woman tricked or forced into prostitution.'),
(40381, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'spirit  n. Light petroleum as a solvent.'),
(40382, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'sugar  n. Purified sugar.'),
(40383, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'tie  n. Man''s white bow-tie as part of full evening dress.'),
(40384, 'w', '\r\nWhitewash', ' â€”n. 1 solution of quicklime or whiting for whitening walls etc. 2 means employed to conceal mistakes or faults. â€”v. 1 cover with whitewash. 2 attempt to clear the reputation of by concealing facts.'),
(40385, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'wedding  n. Wedding at which the bride wears a formal white wedding dress.'),
(40386, 'w', '\r\nWhite', 'whale  n. Northern cetacean, white when adult.'),
(40387, 'w', '\r\nWhitewood', ' n. Pale wood, esp. Prepared for staining etc.'),
(40388, 'w', '\r\nWhither', ' archaic â€”adv. 1 to what place or state? 2 (prec. By place etc.) To which. â€”conj. 1 to the or any place to which (go whither you will). 2 and thither. [old english]'),
(40389, 'w', '\r\nWhiting1', ' n. (pl. Same) small white-fleshed fish used as food. [dutch: related to *white]'),
(40390, 'w', '\r\nWhiting2', ' n. Ground chalk used in whitewashing etc.'),
(40391, 'w', '\r\nWhitlow', ' n. Inflammation near a fingernail or toenail. [originally white *flaw1]'),
(40392, 'w', '\r\nWhitsun', ' â€”n. = *whitsuntide. â€”adj. = *whit. [whitsun day = whit sunday]'),
(40393, 'w', '\r\nWhit', 'sunday  n. Seventh sunday after easter, commemorating pentecost.'),
(40394, 'w', '\r\nWhitsuntide', ' n. Weekend or week including whit sunday.'),
(40395, 'w', '\r\nWhittle', ' v. (-ling) 1 (often foll. By at) pare (wood etc.) With repeated slicing with a knife. 2 (often foll. By away, down) reduce by repeated subtractions. [dial. Thwittle]'),
(40396, 'w', '\r\nWhiz', ' (also whizz) â€”n. Sound made by a body moving through the air at great speed. â€”v. (-zz-) move with or make a whiz. [imitative]'),
(40397, 'w', '\r\nWhiz-kid', ' n. Colloq. Brilliant or highly successful young person.'),
(40398, 'w', '\r\nWho', ' abbr. World health organization.'),
(40399, 'w', '\r\nWho', ' pron. (obj. Whom or colloq. Who; poss. Whose) 1 a what or which person or persons? (who called?; you know who it was). B what sort of person or persons? (who am i to object?). 2 (a person) that (anyone who wishes can come; the woman whom you met; the man who you saw). 3 and or but he, they, etc. (gave it to tom, who sold it to jim). [old english]'),
(40400, 'w', '\r\nUsage', ' in the last two examples of sense 2 whom is correct, but who is common in less formal contexts.'),
(40401, 'w', '\r\nWhoa', ' int. Used to stop or slow a horse etc. [var. Of *ho]'),
(40402, 'w', '\r\nWho''d', ' contr. 1 who had. 2 who would.'),
(40403, 'w', '\r\nWhodunit', ' n. (also whodunnit) colloq. Detective story, play, or film. [= who done (illiterate for did) it?]'),
(40404, 'w', '\r\nWhoever', ' pron. (obj. Whoever or formal whomever; poss. Whosever) 1 the or any person or persons who (whoever comes is welcome). 2 though anyone (whoever else objects, i do not).'),
(40405, 'w', '\r\nWhole', ' â€”adj. 1 uninjured, unbroken, intact, or undiminished. 2 not less than; all there is of. 3 (of blood or milk etc.) With no part removed. â€”n. 1 thing complete in itself. 2 all there is of a thing. 3 (foll. By of) all members etc. Of (the whole of london knows it).  as a whole as a unity; not as separate parts. On the whole taking everything relevant into account. Whole lot see *lot.  wholeness n. [old english]'),
(40406, 'w', '\r\nWholefood', ' n. Food which has not been unnecessarily processed or refined.'),
(40407, 'w', '\r\nWholegrain', ' attrib. Adj. Made with or containing whole grains (wholegrain rice).'),
(40408, 'w', '\r\nWholehearted', ' adj. 1 completely devoted. 2 done with all possible effort or sincerity.  wholeheartedly adv.'),
(40409, 'w', '\r\nWholemeal', ' n. (usu. Attrib.) Meal or flour with none of the bran or germ removed.'),
(40410, 'w', '\r\nWhole', 'number  n. Number without fractions; integer.'),
(40411, 'w', '\r\nWholesale', ' â€”n. Selling of goods in large quantities to be retailed by others. â€”adj. & adv. 1 by wholesale. 2 on a large scale. â€”v. (-ling) sell wholesale.  wholesaler n. [by whole sale]'),
(40412, 'w', '\r\nWholesome', ' adj. 1 promoting physical, mental, or moral health. 2 prudent (wholesome respect). [old english: related to *whole]'),
(40413, 'w', '\r\nWholewheat', ' n. (usu. Attrib.) Wheat with none of the bran or germ removed.'),
(40414, 'w', '\r\nWholism', ' var. Of *holism.'),
(40415, 'w', '\r\nWholly', ' adv. 1 entirely; without limitation. 2 purely.'),
(40416, 'w', '\r\nWhom', ' objective case of *who.'),
(40417, 'w', '\r\nWhomever', ' objective case of *whoever.'),
(40418, 'w', '\r\nWhomsoever', ' objective case of *whosoever.'),
(40419, 'w', '\r\nWhoop', ' â€”n. 1 loud cry of or as of excitement etc. 2 long rasping indrawn breath in whooping cough. â€”v. Utter a whoop.  whoop it up colloq. 1 engage in revelry. 2 us make a stir. [imitative]'),
(40420, 'w', '\r\nWhoopee', ' int. Expressing exuberant joy.  make whoopee colloq. 1 have fun, make merry. 2 make love. [imitative]'),
(40421, 'w', '\r\nWhooping', 'cough  n. Infectious bacterial disease, esp. Of children, with a series of short violent coughs followed by a whoop.'),
(40422, 'w', '\r\nWhoops', ' int. Colloq. Expressing surprise or apology, esp. On losing balance or making an obvious mistake. [var. Of *oops]'),
(40423, 'w', '\r\nWhop', ' v. (-pp-) slang 1 thrash. 2 defeat. [origin unknown]'),
(40424, 'w', '\r\nWhopper', ' n. Slang 1 something big of its kind. 2 great lie.'),
(40425, 'w', '\r\nWhopping', ' adj. Colloq. (esp. As an intensifier) huge (a whopping success; a whopping great lie).'),
(40426, 'w', '\r\nWhore', ' n. 1 prostitute. 2 derog. Promiscuous woman. [old english]'),
(40427, 'w', '\r\nWhore-house', ' n. Brothel.'),
(40428, 'w', '\r\nWhorl', ' n. 1 ring of leaves etc. Round a stem. 2 one turn of a spiral. [apparently var. Of *whirl]'),
(40429, 'w', '\r\nWhortleberry', ' n. (pl. -ies) bilberry. [origin unknown]'),
(40430, 'w', '\r\nWhose', ' â€”interrog. Pron. Of or belonging to which person (whose is this book?). â€”interrog. Adj. Of whom or which (whose book is this?) â€”rel. Pron. Of whom; of which (the man, whose name was tim; the house whose roof was damaged).'),
(40431, 'w', '\r\nWhosoever', ' pron. (obj. Whomsoever; poss. Whosesoever) archaic = *whoever.'),
(40432, 'w', '\r\nWho''s', 'who  n. 1 who or what each person is (know who''s who). 2 list with facts about notable persons.'),
(40433, 'w', '\r\nWhy', ' â€”adv. 1 for what reason or purpose (why did you do it?; i do not know why you came). 2 (prec. By reason etc.) For which (the reasons why i did it). â€”int. Expressing: 1 surprised discovery or recognition (why, it''s you!). 2 impatience (why, of course i do!). 3 reflection (why, yes, i think so). 4 objection (why, what is wrong with it?).  whys and wherefores reasons; explanation. [old english: related to *what]'),
(40434, 'w', '\r\nWi', ' abbr. 1 west indies. 2  women''s institute.'),
(40435, 'w', '\r\nWick', ' n. Strip or thread feeding a flame with fuel.  get on a person''s wick colloq. Annoy a person. [old english]'),
(40436, 'w', '\r\nWicked', ' adj. (-er, -est) 1 sinful, iniquitous, immoral. 2 spiteful. 3 playfully malicious. 4 colloq. Very bad. 5 slang excellent.  wickedly adv. Wickedness n. [origin uncertain]'),
(40437, 'w', '\r\nWicker', ' n. Plaited osiers etc. As material for baskets etc. [scandinavian]'),
(40438, 'w', '\r\nWickerwork', ' n. 1 wicker. 2 things made of wicker.'),
(40439, 'w', '\r\nWicket', ' n. 1 cricket a three stumps with the bails in position defended by a batsman. B ground between two wickets. C state of this. D instance of a batsman being got out (bowler has taken four wickets). 2 (in full wicket-door or -gate) small door or gate, esp. Beside or in a larger one or closing the lower part only of a doorway. [anglo-french wiket = french guichet]'),
(40440, 'w', '\r\nWicket-keeper', ' n. Fieldsman stationed close behind a batsman''s wicket.'),
(40441, 'w', '\r\nWiddershins', ' adv. (also withershins) esp. Scot. 1 in a direction contrary to the sun''s course (considered unlucky). 2 anticlockwise. [german, = contrary]'),
(40442, 'w', '\r\nWide', ' â€”adj. 1 having sides far apart, broad, not narrow (wide river; wide sleeve; wide angle). 2 (following a measurement) in width (a metre wide). 3 a extending far (wide range; wide experience). B considerable (wide margin). 4 not restricted (a wide public). 5 a liberal; unprejudiced (takes wide views). B not specialized; general. 6 open to the full extent (wide eyes). 7 (foll. By of) not within a reasonable distance of, far from (wide shot; wide of the target). 8 (in comb.) Extending over the whole of (nationwide). â€”adv. 1 widely. 2 to the full extent. 3 far from the target etc. (shooting wide). â€”n. = *wide ball.  give a wide berth to see *berth. Wide of the mark see *mark1. Wide open (often foll. By to) exposed (to attack etc.). The wide world all the world, great as it is. [old english]'),
(40443, 'w', '\r\nWide', 'awake  adj. 1 fully awake. 2 colloq. Wary, knowing.'),
(40444, 'w', '\r\nWide', 'ball  n. Cricket ball judged to be beyond the batsman''s reach, so scoring a run.'),
(40445, 'w', '\r\nWide-eyed', ' adj. Surprised; naÃ¯ve.'),
(40446, 'w', '\r\nWidely', ' adv. 1 to a wide extent; far apart. 2 extensively. 3 by many people (it is widely thought that). 4 considerably; to a large degree (holds a widely different view).'),
(40447, 'w', '\r\nWiden', ' v. Make or become wider.'),
(40448, 'w', '\r\nWidespread', ' adj. Widely distributed.'),
(40449, 'w', '\r\nWidgeon', ' n. (also wigeon) a kind of wild duck. [origin uncertain]'),
(40450, 'w', '\r\nWidow', ' â€”n. 1 woman who has lost her husband by death and not married again. 2 woman whose husband is often away on a specified activity (golf widow). â€”v. 1 make into a widow or widower. 2 (as widowed adj.) Bereft by the death of a spouse.  widowhood n. [old english]'),
(40451, 'w', '\r\nWidower', ' n. Man who has lost his wife by death and not married again.'),
(40452, 'w', '\r\nWidow''s', 'peak  n. V-shaped growth of hair towards the centre of the forehead.'),
(40453, 'w', '\r\nWidth', ' n. 1 measurement from side to side. 2 large extent. 3 liberality of views etc. 4 strip of material of full width.  widthways adv. [from *wide]'),
(40454, 'w', '\r\nWield', ' v. Hold and use; command, exert (a weapon, tool, power, etc.). [old english]'),
(40455, 'w', '\r\nWiener', 'schnitzel  n. Veal cutlet breaded, fried, and garnished. [german]'),
(40456, 'w', '\r\nWife', ' n. (pl. Wives) 1 married woman, esp. In relation to her husband. 2 archaic woman.  wifely adj. [old english, = woman]'),
(40457, 'w', '\r\nWig', ' n. Artificial head of hair. [abbreviation of *periwig]'),
(40458, 'w', '\r\nWigeon', ' var. Of *widgeon.'),
(40459, 'w', '\r\nWigging', ' n. Colloq. Reprimand. [origin uncertain]'),
(40460, 'w', '\r\nWiggle', ' colloq. â€”v. (-ling) move from side to side etc. â€”n. Act of wiggling; kink in a line etc.  wiggly adj. (-ier, -iest). [low german or dutch wiggelen]'),
(40461, 'w', '\r\nWight', ' n. Archaic person. [old english, = thing, creature]'),
(40462, 'w', '\r\nWigwam', ' n. N. American indian''s hut or tent. [ojibwa]'),
(40463, 'w', '\r\nWilco', ' int. Colloq. Expressing compliance or agreement. [abbreviation of will comply]');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(40464, 'w', '\r\nWild', ' â€”adj. 1 in its original natural state; not domesticated, cultivated, or civilized (wild cat; wild strawberry). 2 unrestrained, disorderly, uncontrolled (wild youth; wild hair). 3 tempestuous (wild night). 4 intensely eager, frantic (wild excitement; wild delight). 5 (foll. By about) colloq. Enthusiastically devoted to. 6 colloq. Infuriated. 7 haphazard, ill-aimed, rash (wild guess; wild venture). 8 colloq. Exciting, delightful. â€”adv. In a wild manner. â€”n. 1 wild tract of land. 2 desert.  in the wild in an uncultivated etc. State. In the wilds colloq. Far from towns etc. Run wild grow or stray unchecked or undisciplined.  wildly adv. Wildness n. [old english]'),
(40465, 'w', '\r\nWild', 'card  n. 1 card having any rank chosen by the player holding it. 2 computing character that will match any character or combination of characters. 3 person or thing that can be used in several different ways.'),
(40466, 'w', '\r\nWildcat', ' â€”n. 1 hot-tempered or violent person. 2 exploratory oil well. â€”adj. (attrib.) 1 (of a strike) sudden and unofficial. 2 reckless; financially unsound.'),
(40467, 'w', '\r\nWildebeest', ' n. (pl. Same or -s) = *gnu. [afrikaans: related to *wild, *beast]'),
(40468, 'w', '\r\nWilderness', ' n. 1 desert; uncultivated region or garden area. 2 (foll. By of) confused assemblage. [old english: related to *wild, *deer]'),
(40469, 'w', '\r\nWildfire', ' n. Hist. Combustible liquid used in war.  spread like wildfire spread with great speed.'),
(40470, 'w', '\r\nWildfowl', ' n. (pl. Same)  game-bird.'),
(40471, 'w', '\r\nWild-goose', 'chase  n. Foolish or hopeless quest.'),
(40472, 'w', '\r\nWild', 'hyacinth  n. = *bluebell.'),
(40473, 'w', '\r\nWildlife', ' n. Wild animals collectively.'),
(40474, 'w', '\r\nWild', 'west  n. Western us before the establishment of law and order.'),
(40475, 'w', '\r\nWile', ' â€”n. (usu. In pl.) Stratagem, trick. â€”v. (-ling) (foll. By away, into, etc.) Lure. [perhaps from scandinavian]'),
(40476, 'w', '\r\nWilful', ' adj. (us willful) 1 intentional, deliberate (wilful murder; wilful neglect). 2 obstinate.  wilfully adv. [from *will2]'),
(40477, 'w', '\r\nWill1', ' v.aux. (3rd sing. Present will; past would) 1 (strictly only in the 2nd and 3rd persons: see *shall) expressing a future statement, command, etc. (you will regret this; they will leave at once). 2 expressing the speaker''s intention (i will return soon). 3 wish or desire (will you have a drink?; come when you will). 4 expressing a request as a question (will you please open the window?). 5 be able to (the jar will hold a kilo). 6 have a habit or tendency to (accidents will happen; will sit there for hours). 7 expressing probability or expectation (that will be my wife). [old english]'),
(40478, 'w', '\r\nWill2', ' â€”n. 1 faculty by which a person decides what to do. 2 strong desire or intention (will to live). 3 determination, will-power (has a strong will). 4 legal written directions for the disposal of one''s property after death. 5 disposition towards others (good will). 6 archaic what one desires or ordains. â€”v. 1 try to cause by will-power (willed her to win). 2 intend; desire. 3 bequeath by a will.  at will whenever one wishes. With a will energetically or resolutely. [old english]'),
(40479, 'w', '\r\nWillful', ' adj. (brit. Wilful) 1 intentional, deliberate (wilful murder; wilful neglect). 2 obstinate.  wilfully adv. [from *will2]'),
(40480, 'w', '\r\nWillie', ' var. Of *willy.'),
(40481, 'w', '\r\nWillies', ' n.pl. Colloq. Nervous discomfort (gives me the willies). [origin unknown]'),
(40482, 'w', '\r\nWilling', ' adj. 1 ready to consent or undertake. 2 given or done etc. By a willing person.  willingly adv. Willingness n.'),
(40483, 'w', '\r\nWill-o''-the-wisp', ' n. 1 phosphorescent light seen on marshy ground. 2 elusive person. [= william of the torch]'),
(40484, 'w', '\r\nWillow', ' n. Tree with pliant branches yielding osiers and timber for cricket-bats etc., usu. Growing near water. [old english]'),
(40485, 'w', '\r\nWillow-herb', ' n. Plant with leaves like a willow.'),
(40486, 'w', '\r\nWillow-pattern', ' n. Conventional chinese design of blue on white porcelain etc.'),
(40487, 'w', '\r\nWillow-warbler', ' n. Small woodland bird with a tuneful song.'),
(40488, 'w', '\r\nWillowy', ' adj. 1 lithe and slender. 2 having willows.'),
(40489, 'w', '\r\nWill-power', ' n. Control by deliberate purpose over impulse.'),
(40490, 'w', '\r\nWilly', ' n. (also willie) (pl. -ies) colloq. Penis. [diminutive of william]'),
(40491, 'w', '\r\nWilly-nilly', ' adv. Whether one likes it or not. [later spelling of will i, nill i i am willing, i am unwilling]'),
(40492, 'w', '\r\nWilt', ' â€”v. 1 wither, droop. 2 lose energy, flag. â€”n. Plant-disease causing wilting. [originally dial.]'),
(40493, 'w', '\r\nWily', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) crafty, cunning.  wiliness n.'),
(40494, 'w', '\r\nWimp', ' n. Colloq. Feeble or ineffectual person.  wimpish adj. [origin uncertain]'),
(40495, 'w', '\r\nWimple', ' n. Headdress also covering the neck and the sides of the face, worn by some nuns. [old english]'),
(40496, 'w', '\r\nWin', ' â€”v. (-nn-; past and past part. Won) 1 secure as a result of a fight, contest, bet, effort, etc. 2 be the victor; be victorious in. â€”n. Victory in a game etc.  win the day be victorious in battle, argument, etc. Win over persuade, gain the support of. Win one''s spurs colloq. Gain distinction or fame.  Win through (or out) overcome obstacles. You can''t win colloq. There is no way to succeed or to please.  winnable adj. [old english, = toil]'),
(40497, 'w', '\r\nWince', ' â€”n. Start or involuntary shrinking movement of the face, showing pain or distress. â€”v. (-cing) give a wince. [germanic: related to *wink]'),
(40498, 'w', '\r\nWincey', ' n. (pl. -s) lightweight fabric of wool and cotton or linen. [apparently an alteration of woolsey in *linsey-woolsey]'),
(40499, 'w', '\r\nWinceyette', ' n. Lightweight flannelette.'),
(40500, 'w', '\r\nWinch', ' â€”n. 1 crank of a wheel or axle. 2 windlass. â€”v. Lift with a winch. [old english]'),
(40501, 'w', '\r\nWind1', ' â€”n. 1 air in natural motion, esp. A current of this. 2 a breath, esp. As needed in exercise or playing a wind instrument. B power of breathing easily. 3 empty talk. 4 gas generated in the bowels etc. 5 wind instruments of an orchestra etc. 6 scent carried by the wind. â€”v. 1 cause to be out of breath by exertion or a blow. 2 make (a baby) bring up wind after feeding. 3 detect the presence of by a scent.'),
(40502, 'w', '\r\nWind2', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Wound) 1 (often as winding adj.) Go in a spiral, curved, or crooked course. 2 make (one''s way) thus. 3 wrap closely; coil. 4 a provide with a coiled thread etc. B surround with or as with a coil. 5 wind up (a clock etc.). â€”n. 1 bend or turn in a course. 2 single turn when winding.  wind down 1 lower by winding. 2 unwind. 3 draw gradually to a close. Wind off unwind. Wind up 1 coil the whole of. 2 tighten the coiling or coiled spring of (esp. A clock). 3 colloq. A increase the intensity of (feelings etc.), excite. B provoke (a person) to anger etc. 4 bring to a conclusion; end. 5 a arrange the affairs of and dissolve (a company). B cease business and go into liquidation. 6 colloq. Arrive finally. [old english]'),
(40503, 'w', '\r\nWindbag', ' n. Colloq. Person who talks a lot but says little of any value.'),
(40504, 'w', '\r\nWind-break', ' n. Thing serving to break the force of the wind.'),
(40505, 'w', '\r\nWindburn', ' n. Inflammation of the skin caused by exposure to the wind.'),
(40506, 'w', '\r\nWindcheater', ' n. Wind-resistant jacket.'),
(40507, 'w', '\r\nWind-cone', ' n. = *wind-sock.'),
(40508, 'w', '\r\nWind-down', ' n. Colloq. Gradual lessening of excitement or activity.'),
(40509, 'w', '\r\nWinder', ' n. Winding mechanism, esp. Of a clock or watch.'),
(40510, 'w', '\r\nWindfall', ' n. 1 fruit, esp. An apple, blown to the ground by the wind. 2 unexpected good fortune, esp. A legacy.'),
(40511, 'w', '\r\nWinding-sheet', ' n. Sheet in which a corpse is wrapped for burial.'),
(40512, 'w', '\r\nWind', 'instrument  n. Musical instrument sounded by an air-current, esp. The breath.'),
(40513, 'w', '\r\nWind-jammer', ' n. Merchant sailing-ship.'),
(40514, 'w', '\r\nWindlass', ' n. Machine with a horizontal axle for hauling or hoisting. [old norse, = winding-pole]'),
(40515, 'w', '\r\nWindmill', ' n. 1 mill worked by the wind acting on its sails. 2 toy consisting of a stick with curved vanes that revolve in a wind.  tilt at windmills attack an imaginary enemy.'),
(40516, 'w', '\r\nWindow', ' n. 1 a opening in a wall etc., usu. With glass to admit light etc. B the glass itself. 2 space for display behind the window of a shop. 3 window-like opening. 4 opportunity to learn from observation. 5 transparent part in an envelope showing an address. 6 vdu display showing a particular part of the data.  windowless adj. [old norse, = wind-eye]'),
(40517, 'w', '\r\nWindow-box', ' n. Box placed outside a window for growing flowers.'),
(40518, 'w', '\r\nWindow-dressing', ' n. 1 art of arranging a display in a shop-window etc. 2 adroit presentation of facts etc. To give a deceptively favourable impression.'),
(40519, 'w', '\r\nWindow-pane', ' n. Pane of glass in a window.'),
(40520, 'w', '\r\nWindow-seat', ' n. 1 seat below a window, esp. In an alcove. 2 seat next to a window in an aircraft, train, etc.'),
(40521, 'w', '\r\nWindow-shop', ' v. Look at goods displayed in shop-windows, without buying anything.'),
(40522, 'w', '\r\nWindow-sill', ' n. Sill below a window.'),
(40523, 'w', '\r\nWindpipe', ' n. Air-passage from the throat to the lungs.'),
(40524, 'w', '\r\nWindscreen', ' n. Screen of glass at the front of a motor vehicle.'),
(40525, 'w', '\r\nWindscreen', 'wiper  n. Blade moving in an arc to keep a windscreen clear of rain etc.'),
(40526, 'w', '\r\nWindshield', ' n. Us = *windscreen.'),
(40527, 'w', '\r\nWind-sock', ' n. Canvas cylinder or cone on a mast to show the direction of the wind at an airfield etc.'),
