(40691, 'w', '\r\nWoodlouse', ' n. (pl. -lice) small land crustacean with many legs.'),
(40692, 'w', '\r\nWoodman', ' n. Forester.'),
(40693, 'w', '\r\nWoodpecker', ' n. Bird that taps tree-trunks in search of insects.'),
(40694, 'w', '\r\nWoodpigeon', ' n. Dove with white patches like a ring round its neck.'),
(40695, 'w', '\r\nWoodpile', ' n. Pile of wood, esp. For fuel.'),
(40696, 'w', '\r\nWood', 'pulp  n. Wood-fibre prepared for paper-making.'),
(40697, 'w', '\r\nWoodruff', ' n. White-flowered plant with fragrant leaves.'),
(40698, 'w', '\r\nWoodshed', ' n. Shed where wood for fuel is stored.  something nasty in the woodshed colloq. Shocking thing kept secret.'),
(40699, 'w', '\r\nWoodwind', ' n. 1 wind instruments that were (mostly) orig. Made of wood, e.g. The flute, clarinet, oboe, and saxophone. 2 one such instrument.'),
(40700, 'w', '\r\nWoodwork', ' n. 1 making of things in wood. 2 things made of wood.  crawl out of the woodwork colloq. (of something distasteful) appear.'),
(40701, 'w', '\r\nWoodworm', ' n. 1 wood-boring larva of a kind of beetle. 2 condition of wood affected by this.'),
(40702, 'w', '\r\nWoody', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 wooded. 2 like or of wood.  woodiness n.'),
(40703, 'w', '\r\nWoodyard', ' n. Yard where wood is used or stored.'),
(40704, 'w', '\r\nWoody', 'nightshade  n. A kind of nightshade with poisonous red berries.'),
(40705, 'w', '\r\nWoof1', ' â€”n. Gruff bark of a dog. â€”v. Give a woof. [imitative]'),
(40706, 'w', '\r\nWoof2', ' n. = *weft 1. [old english: related to *web]'),
(40707, 'w', '\r\nWoofer', ' n. Loudspeaker for low frequencies. [from *woof1]'),
(40708, 'w', '\r\nWool', ' n. 1 fine soft wavy hair from the fleece of sheep etc. 2 woollen yarn or cloth or clothing. 3 wool-like substance (steel wool).  pull the wool over a person''s eyes deceive a person. [old english]'),
(40709, 'w', '\r\nWoolen', ' (brit. Woollen) â€”adj. Made wholly or partly of wool. â€”n. 1 woollen fabric. 2 (in pl.) Woollen garments. [old english]'),
(40710, 'w', '\r\nWool-gathering', ' n. Absent-mindedness.'),
(40711, 'w', '\r\nWoollen', ' (us woolen) â€”adj. Made wholly or partly of wool. â€”n. 1 woollen fabric. 2 (in pl.) Woollen garments. [old english]'),
(40712, 'w', '\r\nWoolly', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 bearing wool. 2 like wool. 3 woollen (a woolly cardigan). 4 (of a sound) indistinct. 5 (of thought) vague or confused. â€”n. (pl. -ies) colloq. Woollen garment, esp. A pullover.  woolliness n.'),
(40713, 'w', '\r\nWoolsack', ' n. 1 lord chancellor''s wool-stuffed seat in the house of lords. 2 his position.'),
(40714, 'w', '\r\nWoozy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) colloq. 1 dizzy or unsteady. 2 slightly drunk.  woozily adv. Wooziness n. [origin unknown]'),
(40715, 'w', '\r\nWop', ' n. Slang offens. Italian or other s. European. [origin uncertain]'),
(40716, 'w', '\r\nWorcester', 'sauce  n. A pungent sauce. [worcester in england]'),
(40717, 'w', '\r\nWord', ' â€”n. 1 meaningful element of speech, usu. Shown with a space on either side of it when written or printed. 2 speech, esp. As distinct from action. 3 one''s promise or assurance. 4 (in sing. Or pl.) Thing said, remark, conversation. 5 (in pl.) Text of a song or an actor''s part. 6 (in pl.) Angry talk (have words). 7 news, message (send word). 8 command (gave the word to begin). â€”v. Put into words; select words to express.  in other words expressing the same thing differently. In so many words in those very words; explicitly. In a (or one) word briefly. My (or upon my) word exclamation of surprise etc. Take a person at his or her word interpret a person''s words literally. Take a person''s word for it believe a person''s statement without investigation etc. The word (or word of god) the bible. Word for word in exactly the same or (of translation) corresponding words.  wordless adj. [old english]'),
(40718, 'w', '\r\nWord-blindness', ' n. = *dyslexia.'),
(40719, 'w', '\r\nWord-game', ' n. Game involving the making or selection etc. Of words.'),
(40720, 'w', '\r\nWording', ' n. Form of words used.'),
(40721, 'w', '\r\nWord', 'of mouth  n. Speech (only).'),
(40722, 'w', '\r\nWord-perfect', ' adj. Knowing one''s part etc. By heart.'),
(40723, 'w', '\r\nWordplay', ' n. Witty use of words, esp. Punning.'),
(40724, 'w', '\r\nWord', 'processor  n. Computer program, or device incorporating a computer, used for storing text entered from a keyboard, making corrections, and providing a printout.  word-process v. Word processing n.'),
(40725, 'w', '\r\nWordy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) using or expressed in too many words.  wordily adv. Wordiness n.'),
(40726, 'w', '\r\nWore', ' past of *wear.'),
(40727, 'w', '\r\nWork', ' â€”n. 1 application of mental or physical effort to a purpose; use of energy. 2 task to be undertaken. 3 thing done or made by work; result of an action. 4 employment or occupation etc., esp. As a means of earning income. 5 literary or musical composition. 6 actions or experiences of a specified kind (nice work!). 7 (in comb.) Things made of a specified material or with specified tools etc. (ironwork; needlework).'),
(40728, 'w', '\r\nWorkable', ' adj. That can be worked, will work, or is worth working.  workability n.'),
(40729, 'w', '\r\nWorkaday', ' adj. Ordinary, everyday, practical.'),
(40730, 'w', '\r\nWorkaholic', ' n. Colloq. Person addicted to working.'),
(40731, 'w', '\r\nWork-basket', ' n. Basket for sewing materials.'),
(40732, 'w', '\r\nWorkbench', ' n. Bench for manual work, esp. Carpentry.'),
(40733, 'w', '\r\nWorkbook', ' n. Student''s book with exercises.'),
(40734, 'w', '\r\nWorkbox', ' n. Box for tools, needlework, etc.'),
(40735, 'w', '\r\nWork', 'camp  n. Camp at which community work is done, esp. By young volunteers.'),
(40736, 'w', '\r\nWorkday', ' n. Day on which work is usually done.'),
(40737, 'w', '\r\nWorker', ' n. 1 person who works, esp. For an employer. 2 neuter bee or ant. 3 person who works hard.'),
(40738, 'w', '\r\nWork', 'experience  n. Scheme intended to give young people temporary experience of employment.'),
(40739, 'w', '\r\nWorkforce', ' n. 1 workers engaged or available. 2 number of these.'),
(40740, 'w', '\r\nWorkhouse', ' n. Hist. Public institution for the poor of a parish.'),
(40741, 'w', '\r\nWorking', ' â€”attrib. Adj. 1 a engaged in work (working mother; working man). B while so engaged (all his working life; in working hours). 2 functioning or able to function (working model). â€”n. 1 activity of work. 2 functioning. 3 mine or quarry. 4 (usu. In pl.) Machinery, mechanism.'),
(40742, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'capital  n. Capital actually used in a business.'),
(40743, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'class  n. Social class employed, esp. In manual or industrial work, for wages.  working-class adj.'),
(40744, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'day  n. 1 workday. 2 part of the day devoted to work.'),
(40745, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'hypothesis  n. Hypothesis as a basis for action.'),
(40746, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'knowledge  n. Knowledge adequate to work with.'),
(40747, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'lunch  n. Lunch at which business is conducted.'),
(40748, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'order  n. Condition in which a machine works.'),
(40749, 'w', '\r\nWorking', 'party  n. Group of people appointed to study and advise on a particular problem.'),
(40750, 'w', '\r\nWorkload', ' n. Amount of work to be done.'),
(40751, 'w', '\r\nWorkman', ' n. 1 man employed to do manual labour. 2 person with regard to skill in a job (a good workman).'),
(40752, 'w', '\r\nWorkmanlike', ' adj. Competent, showing practised skill.'),
(40753, 'w', '\r\nWorkmanship', ' n. Degree of skill in doing a task or of finish in the product made.'),
(40754, 'w', '\r\nWorkmate', ' n. Person working alongside another.'),
(40755, 'w', '\r\nWork', 'of art  n. Fine picture, poem, building, etc.'),
(40756, 'w', '\r\nWorkout', ' n. Session of physical exercise or training.'),
(40757, 'w', '\r\nWorkpiece', ' n. Thing worked on with a tool or machine.'),
(40758, 'w', '\r\nWorkplace', ' n. Place at which a person works.'),
(40759, 'w', '\r\nWorkroom', ' n. Room for working in.'),
(40760, 'w', '\r\nWorksheet', ' n. 1 paper for recording work done or in progress. 2 paper listing questions or activities for students etc. To work through.'),
(40761, 'w', '\r\nWorkshop', ' n. 1 room or building in which goods are manufactured. 2 place or meeting for concerted discussion or activity (dance workshop).'),
(40762, 'w', '\r\nWork-shy', ' adj. Disinclined to work.'),
(40763, 'w', '\r\nWorkstation', ' n. 1 location of a stage in a manufacturing process. 2 computer terminal or the desk etc. Where this is located.'),
(40764, 'w', '\r\nWork', 'study  n. Assessment of methods of working so as to achieve maximum productivity.'),
(40765, 'w', '\r\nWork', 'table  n. Table for working at.'),
(40766, 'w', '\r\nWorktop', ' n. Flat surface for working on, esp. In a kitchen.'),
(40767, 'w', '\r\nWork-to-rule', ' n. Working to rule.'),
(40768, 'w', '\r\nWorld', ' n. 1 a the earth, or a planetary body like it. B its countries and people. 2 the universe, all that exists. 3 a the time, state, or scene of human existence. B (prec. By the, this) mortal life. 4 secular interests and affairs. 5 human affairs; active life. 6 average, respectable, or fashionable people or their customs or opinions. 7 all that concerns or all who belong to a specified class or sphere of activity (the world of sport). 8 (foll. By of) vast amount. 9 (attrib.) Affecting many nations, of all nations (world politics; world champion).  bring (or come) into the world give birth (or be born). For all the world (foll. By like, as if) precisely. In the world of all; at all (what in the world is it?). Man (or woman) of the world person experienced and practical in human affairs. Out of this world colloq. Extremely good etc. Think the world of have a very high regard for. [old english]'),
(40769, 'w', '\r\nWorld-beater', ' n. Person or thing surpassing all others.'),
(40770, 'w', '\r\nWorld-class', ' adj. Of a quality or standard regarded as high throughout the world.'),
(40771, 'w', '\r\nWorld', 'cup  n. Competition between football teams from various countries.'),
(40772, 'w', '\r\nWorld-famous', ' adj. Known throughout the world.'),
(40773, 'w', '\r\nWorldly', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 of the affairs of the world, temporal, earthly (worldly goods). 2 experienced in life, sophisticated, practical.  worldliness n.'),
(40774, 'w', '\r\nWorldly-wise', ' adj. Prudent or shrewd in one''s dealings with the world.'),
(40775, 'w', '\r\nWorld', 'music  n. Pop music that incorporates local or ethnic elements (esp. From the developing world).'),
(40776, 'w', '\r\nWorld', 'war  n. War involving many major nations.'),
(40777, 'w', '\r\nWorld-weary', ' adj. Bored with human affairs.  world-weariness n.'),
(40778, 'w', '\r\nWorldwide', ' â€”adj. Occurring in or known in all parts of the world. â€”adv. Throughout the world.'),
(40779, 'w', '\r\nWorm', ' â€”n. 1 any of various types of creeping invertebrate animals with long slender bodies and no limbs. 2 larva of an insect, esp. In fruit or wood. 3 (in pl.) Intestinal parasites. 4 insignificant or contemptible person. 5 spiral part of a screw. â€”v. 1 (often refl.) Move with a crawling motion. 2 refl. (foll. By into) insinuate oneself into favour etc. 3 (foll. By out) obtain (a secret etc.) By cunning persistence. 4 rid (a dog etc.) Of worms. [old english]'),
(40780, 'w', '\r\nWorm-cast', ' n. Convoluted mass of earth left on the surface by a burrowing earthworm.'),
(40781, 'w', '\r\nWormeaten', ' adj. 1 eaten into by worms; decayed. 2 old and dilapidated.'),
(40782, 'w', '\r\nWorm-hole', ' n. Hole left by the passage of a worm.'),
(40783, 'w', '\r\nWorm''s-eye', 'view  n. View from below or from a humble position.'),
(40784, 'w', '\r\nWormwood', ' n. 1 plant with a bitter aromatic taste. 2 bitter mortification; source of this. [old english: cf. *vermouth]'),
(40785, 'w', '\r\nWormy', ' adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 full of worms. 2 wormeaten.  worminess n.'),
(40786, 'w', '\r\nWorn', ' past part. Of *wear. â€”adj. 1 damaged by use or wear. 2 looking tired and exhausted.'),
(40787, 'w', '\r\nWorrisome', ' adj. Causing worry.'),
(40788, 'w', '\r\nWorry', ' â€”v. (-ies, -ied) 1 give way to anxiety. 2 harass, importune; be a trouble or anxiety to. 3 (of a dog etc.) Shake or pull about with the teeth. 4 (as worried adj.) Uneasy. â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 thing that causes anxiety or disturbs tranquility. 2 disturbed state of mind; anxiety.  worrier n. [old english, = strangle]'),
(40789, 'w', '\r\nWorry', 'beads  n.pl. String of beads manipulated with the fingers to occupy or calm oneself.'),
(40790, 'w', '\r\nWorse', ' â€”adj. 1 more bad. 2 (predic.) In or into worse health or a worse condition (is getting worse). â€”adv. More badly; more ill. â€”n. 1 worse thing or things (you might do worse than accept). 2 (prec. By the) worse condition (a change for the worse).  none the worse (often foll. By for) not adversely affected (by). The worse for wear 1 damaged by use. 2 injured. Worse luck unfortunately. Worse off in a worse (esp. Financial) position. [old english]'),
(40791, 'w', '\r\nWorsen', ' v. Make or become worse.'),
(40792, 'w', '\r\nWorship', ' â€”n. 1 a homage or service to a deity. B acts, rites, or ceremonies of this. 2 adoration, devotion. 3 (worship) (prec. By his, her, your) forms of description or address for a mayor, certain magistrates, etc. â€”v. (-pp-; us -p-) 1 adore as divine; honour with religious rites. 2 idolize or regard with adoration. 3 attend public worship. 4 be full of adoration.  worshipper n. [old english: related to *worth, *-ship]'),
(40793, 'w', '\r\nWorshipful', ' adj. (also worshipful) archaic (esp. In old titles of companies or officers) honourable, distinguished.'),
(40794, 'w', '\r\nWorst', ' â€”adj. Most bad. â€”adv. Most badly. â€”n. Worst part or possibility (prepare for the worst). â€”v. Get the better of; defeat.  at its etc. Worst in the worst state. At worst (or the worst) in the worst possible case. Do your worst expression of defiance. Get the worst of it be defeated. If the worst comes to the worst if the worst happens. [old english: related to *worse]'),
(40795, 'w', '\r\nWorsted', ' n. 1 fine woollen yarn. 2 fabric made from this. [worste(a)d in norfolk]'),
(40796, 'w', '\r\nWort', ' n. 1 archaic (except in names) plant (liverwort). 2 infusion of malt before it is fermented into beer. [old english]'),
(40797, 'w', '\r\nWorth', ' â€”predic. Adj. (used like a preposition) 1 of a value equivalent to (is worth Â£50; is worth very little). 2 such as to justify or repay (worth doing; not worth the trouble). 3 possessing or having property amounting to (is worth a million pounds). â€”n. 1 what a person or thing is worth; the (usu. High) merit of (of great worth). 2 equivalent of money in a commodity (ten pounds'' worth of petrol).  for all one is worth colloq. With one''s utmost efforts. For what it is worth without a guarantee of its truth or value. Worth it colloq. Worth while. Worth one''s salt see *salt. Worth one''s weight in gold see *weight. Worth while (or one''s while) see *while. [old english]'),
(40798, 'w', '\r\nWorthless', ' adj. Without value or merit.  worthlessness n.'),
(40799, 'w', '\r\nWorthwhile', ' adj. That is worth the time, effort, or money spent.'),
(40800, 'w', '\r\nUsage', ' see note at while.'),
(40801, 'w', '\r\nWorthy', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) 1 deserving respect, estimable (lived a worthy life). 2 entitled to (esp. Condescending) recognition (a worthy old couple). 3 a (foll. By of or to + infin.) Deserving (worthy of a mention). B (foll. By of) adequate or suitable to the dignity etc. Of (words worthy of the occasion). â€”n. (pl. -ies) 1 worthy person. 2 person of some distinction.  worthily adv. Worthiness n.'),
(40802, 'w', '\r\n-worthy', ' comb. Form forming adjectives meaning: 1 deserving of (noteworthy). 2 suitable for (roadworthy).'),
(40803, 'w', '\r\nWould', ' v.aux. (3rd sing. Would) past of *will1, used esp.: 1 in reported speech (he said he would be home by evening). 2 to express a condition (they would have been killed if they had gone). 3 to express habitual action (would wait every evening). 4 to express a question or polite request (would they like it?; would you come in, please?). 5 to express probability (she would be over fifty by now). 6 to express consent (they would not help).'),
(40804, 'w', '\r\nWould-be', ' attrib. Adj. Desiring or aspiring to be.'),
(40805, 'w', '\r\nWouldn''t', ' contr. Would not.'),
(40806, 'w', '\r\nWound1', ' â€”n. 1 injury done to living tissue by a deep cut or heavy blow etc. 2 pain inflicted on one''s feelings; injury to one''s reputation. â€”v. Inflict a wound on. [old english]'),
(40807, 'w', '\r\nWound2', ' past and past part. Of *wind2.'),
(40808, 'w', '\r\nWound', 'up  adj. Excited; tense; angry.'),
(40809, 'w', '\r\nWove', ' past of *weave1.'),
(40810, 'w', '\r\nWoven', ' past part. Of *weave1.'),
(40811, 'w', '\r\nWow1', ' â€”int. Expressing astonishment or admiration. â€”n. Slang sensational success. â€”v. Slang impress greatly. [imitative]'),
(40812, 'w', '\r\nWow2', ' n. Slow pitch-fluctuation in sound-reproduction, perceptible in long notes. [imitative]'),
(40813, 'w', '\r\nWp', ' abbr. Word processor.'),
(40814, 'w', '\r\nWpc', ' abbr. Woman police constable.'),
(40815, 'w', '\r\nW.p.m.', ' Abbr. Words per minute.'),
(40816, 'w', '\r\nWrac', ' abbr. Women''s royal army corps.');
INSERT INTO `oedict` (`word_id`, `letter`, `word`, `meaning`) VALUES
(40817, 'w', '\r\nWrack', ' n. 1 seaweed cast up or growing on the shore. 2 destruction. [low german or dutch wrak: cf. *wreck]'),
(40818, 'w', '\r\nWraf', ' abbr. Women''s royal air force.'),
(40819, 'w', '\r\nWraith', ' n. 1 ghost. 2 spectral appearance of a living person supposed to portend that person''s death. [origin unknown]'),
(40820, 'w', '\r\nWrangle', ' â€”n. Noisy argument or dispute. â€”v. (-ling) engage in a wrangle. [low german or dutch]'),
(40821, 'w', '\r\nWrap', ' â€”v. (-pp-) 1 (often foll. By up) envelop in folded or soft encircling material. 2 (foll. By round, about) arrange or draw (a pliant covering) round (a person). 3 (foll. By round) slang crash (a vehicle) into (a stationary object). â€”n. 1 shawl, scarf, etc. 2 esp. Us wrapping material.  take the wraps off disclose. Under wraps in secrecy. Wrapped up in engrossed or absorbed in. Wrap up 1 colloq. Finish off (a matter). 2 put on warm clothes (wrap up well). 3 (in imper.) Slang be quiet. [origin unknown] '),
(40822, 'w', '\r\nWraparound', ' adj. (also wrapround) 1 (esp. Of clothing) designed to wrap round. 2 curving or extending round at the edges.'),
(40823, 'w', '\r\nWrap-over', ' â€”attrib. Adj. (of a garment) overlapping when worn. â€”n. Such a garment.'),
(40824, 'w', '\r\nWrapper', ' n. 1 cover for a sweet, book, posted newspaper, etc. 2 loose enveloping robe or gown.'),
(40825, 'w', '\r\nWrapping', ' n. (esp. In pl.) Material used to wrap; wraps, wrappers.'),
(40826, 'w', '\r\nWrapping', 'paper  n. Strong or decorative paper for wrapping parcels.'),
(40827, 'w', '\r\nWrapround', ' var. Of *wraparound.'),
(40828, 'w', '\r\nWrasse', ' n. Bright-coloured marine fish. [cornish wrach]'),
(40829, 'w', '\r\nWrath', ' n. Literary extreme anger. [old english: related to *wroth]'),
(40830, 'w', '\r\nWrathful', ' adj. Literary extremely angry.  wrathfully adv.'),
(40831, 'w', '\r\nWreak', ' v. 1 (usu. Foll. By upon) give play to (vengeance or one''s anger etc.). 2 cause (damage etc.) (wreak havoc). [old english, = avenge]'),
(40832, 'w', '\r\nWreath', ' n. (pl. -s) 1 flowers or leaves fastened in a ring, esp. As an ornament for the head or for laying on a grave etc. 2 curl or ring of smoke, cloud, or soft fabric. [old english: related to *writhe]'),
(40833, 'w', '\r\nWreathe', ' v. (-thing) 1 encircle or cover as, with, or like a wreath. 2 (foll. By round) wind (one''s arms etc.) Round (a person etc.). 3 (of smoke etc.) Move in wreaths.'),
(40834, 'w', '\r\nWreck', ' â€”n. 1 the sinking or running aground of a ship. 2 ship that has suffered a wreck. 3 greatly damaged building, thing, or person. 4 (foll. By of) wretched remnant. â€”v. 1 a seriously damage (a vehicle etc.). B ruin (hopes, a life, etc.). 2 cause the wreck of (a ship). [anglo-french wrec from germanic]'),
(40835, 'w', '\r\nWreckage', ' n. 1 wrecked material. 2 remnants of a wreck. 3 act of wrecking.'),
(40836, 'w', '\r\nWrecker', ' n. 1 person or thing that wrecks or destroys. 2 esp. Us person employed in demolition or breaking up damaged vehicles. 3 esp. Hist. Person on the shore who tries to bring about a shipwreck for plunder or profit.'),
(40837, 'w', '\r\nWren', ' n. Member of the women''s royal naval service. [from the abbreviation wrns]'),
(40838, 'w', '\r\nWren', ' n. Small usu. Brown short-winged songbird with an erect tail. [old english]'),
(40839, 'w', '\r\nWrench', ' â€”n. 1 violent twist or oblique pull or tearing off. 2 tool like a spanner for gripping and turning nuts etc. 3 painful uprooting or parting. â€”v. 1 twist or pull violently round or sideways. 2 (often foll. By off, away, etc.) Pull off with a wrench. [old english]'),
(40840, 'w', '\r\nWrest', ' v. 1 wrench away from a person''s grasp. 2 (foll. By from) obtain by effort or with difficulty. [old english]'),
(40841, 'w', '\r\nWrestle', ' â€”n. 1 contest in which two opponents grapple and try to throw each other to the ground, esp. As an athletic sport. 2 hard struggle. â€”v. (-ling) 1 (often foll. By with) take part or fight in a wrestle. 2 a (foll. By with, against) struggle. B (foll. By with) do one''s utmost to deal with (a task, difficulty, etc.).  wrestler n. Wrestling n. [old english]'),
(40842, 'w', '\r\nWretch', ' n. 1 unfortunate or pitiable person. 2 (often as a playful term of depreciation) reprehensible person. [old english, = outcast]'),
(40843, 'w', '\r\nWretched', ' adj. (wretcheder, wretchedest) 1 unhappy, miserable; unwell. 2 of bad quality; contemptible. 3 displeasing, hateful.  wretchedly adv. Wretchedness n.'),
(40844, 'w', '\r\nWrick', ' var. Of *rick2.'),
(40845, 'w', '\r\nWriggle', ' â€”v. (-ling) 1 (of a worm etc.) Twist or turn its body with short writhing movements. 2 make wriggling motions. 3 (foll. By along, through, etc.) Go thus (wriggled through the gap). 4 be evasive. â€”n. Act of wriggling.  wriggle out of colloq. Avoid on a pretext.  wriggly adj. [low german wriggelen]'),
(40846, 'w', '\r\nWright', ' n. Maker or builder (usu. In comb.: playwright; shipwright). [old english: related to *work]'),
(40847, 'w', '\r\nWring', ' â€”v. (past and past part. Wrung) 1 a squeeze tightly. B (often foll. By out) squeeze and twist, esp. To remove liquid. 2 break by twisting. 3 distress, torture. 4 extract by squeezing. 5 (foll. By out, from) obtain by pressure or importunity; extort. â€”n. Act of wringing.  wring one''s hands clasp them as a gesture of distress. Wring the neck of kill (a chicken etc.) By twisting its neck. [old english]'),
(40848, 'w', '\r\nWringer', ' n. Device for wringing water from washed clothes etc.'),
(40849, 'w', '\r\nWringing', ' adj. (in full wringing wet) so wet that water can be wrung out.'),
(40850, 'w', '\r\nWrinkle', ' â€”n. 1 crease in the skin, esp. Caused by age. 2 similar mark in another flexible surface. 3 colloq. Useful tip or clever expedient. â€”v. (-ling) 1 make wrinkles in. 2 form wrinkles. [probably related to old english gewrinclod sinuous]'),
(40851, 'w', '\r\nWrinkly', ' â€”adj. (-ier, -iest) having wrinkles. â€”n. Slang offens. Old or middle-aged person.'),
(40852, 'w', '\r\nWrist', ' n. 1 joint connecting the hand with the arm. 2 part of a garment covering this. [old english]'),
